var e=JSON.parse(`[{"slug":"cacio-e-pepe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g spaghetti or tonnarelli"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g Pecorino Romano, finely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast coarsely cracked pepper in a dry pan 1 min until fragrant.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":60,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil pasta in lightly salted water; the cheese is salty. Reserve 1 cup starchy water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off heat, whisk pecorino with a splash of warm (not boiling) pasta water into a thick paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss pasta with pepper and paste, adding pasta water until glossy and creamy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Three ingredients and nowhere to hide. The sauce is an emulsion of cheese, starch and rendered pepper oil, and it breaks above ~65°C: hotter, and pecorino's proteins seize into chewing-gum clumps around the strands. So the work happens off the heat, with warm pasta water, and the starch does the emulsifying for you. Rome's shepherds built the dish from the three things that survived months of transhumance; it remains the best proof in the repertoire of how far technique can carry a bare pantry.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","gently hot","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cacio%20e%20Pepe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cacio e Pepe"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Cacio e Pepe","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Cacio%20e%20Pepe","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cheese-families","term":"Cheese Families"},{"slug":"pecorino-romano","term":"Pecorino Romano"},{"slug":"black-pepper-and-the-pepper-family","term":"Black Pepper & the Pepper Family"}],"pantryItems":["Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Pasta"],"standard":{"slug":"cacio-e-pepe","marks":[{"id":"cacio-e-pepe#even","text":"Even gloss along every strand; no chalky white grit on the pasta, no cheese welded to the tongs or fused to the bottom of the bowl."},{"id":"cacio-e-pepe#lift","text":"Lift a forkful and the sauce hangs on it; the sauce is still loose and glossy when the bowl reaches the table, and the emptied bowl keeps a thin clinging film rather than a watery pool that separates within a minute."},{"id":"cacio-e-pepe#pepper","text":"Pepper is cracked coarse enough that individual fragments are visible in the sauce, and it is worked through the coating rather than dusted over the top. No dry black powder left on the surface."},{"id":"cacio-e-pepe#bitten","text":"Bitten, a strand resists at the centre with no chalky raw core."},{"id":"cacio-e-pepe#fully","text":"Fully seasoned from the pecorino alone: nothing is reached for at the table, and it is not so salty it sends you to the water glass."}],"fault":"The cheese met heat, the paste was built with boiling water or tossed in a pan still on the flame, and the pecorino has seized into stringy pellets sitting in a thin grey puddle."}},{"slug":"ragu-alla-bolognese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g ground beef + 200g ground pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pancetta, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 1 carrot, 1 celery rib, fine dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tagliatelle, Parmigiano"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render pancetta; sweat the soffritto 10 min until soft, not browned.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brown the meats hard, breaking them up; stir in tomato paste and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deglaze with wine, add milk and stock; simmer partly covered 2–3 hours, topping up with stock.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss with tagliatelle and a splash of pasta water; finish with Parmigiano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Milk isn't optional: its proteins buffer the wine's acidity and keep three-hours-cooked meat tender instead of grainy, which is why the recipe the Accademia Italiana della Cucina deposited at Bologna's Chamber of Commerce in 1982 includes it without apology. The failure mode is impatience: a hard boil squeezes the meat dry, while the barest tremble of a simmer dissolves it into the sauce. And in Bologna it goes on tagliatelle: the ragù clings to a rough egg noodle in a way it never will to slick spaghetti.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Keeping the cooking liquid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rag%C3%B9%20alla%20Bolognese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ragù alla Bolognese"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Ragù alla Bolognese","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Rag%C3%B9%20alla%20Bolognese","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pancetta-tesa-and-arrotolata","term":"Pancetta (Tesa & Arrotolata)"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Milk","Parmesan","Pasta","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"standard":{"slug":"ragu-alla-bolognese","marks":[{"id":"ragu-alla-bolognese#finished","text":"The finished colour is a dull brick-brown, not red. Still red at three hours means the paste was never cooked out or the pot never went long enough."},{"id":"ragu-alla-bolognese#risen","text":"Fat has risen to a clear orange film across the top and stirs back in without separating again. Tilt the pan: solids and liquid move together, with no water running ahead."},{"id":"ragu-alla-bolognese#grain","text":"A grain of meat crushes to nothing between finger and thumb. Springy pellets mean the simmer was a boil."},{"id":"ragu-alla-bolognese#white","text":"No white flecks or curdled grain anywhere. The milk has gone invisible into the sauce, which is the hard mark to hit when the wine went in ahead of it."},{"id":"ragu-alla-bolognese#tossed","text":"Tossed with tagliatelle and a splash of pasta water, the sauce clings the length of each ribbon and the bowl bottom stays clean."}],"fault":"A bitter, ashy line under everything, because the pot was left to catch on the bottom between top-ups and the catch got stirred back in. The ragù still looks right, only darker than it should be, so the mistake is usually found on the tongue rather than in the pan."}},{"slug":"pizza-margherita","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"325g water (65%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt, 3g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crushed San Marzano tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fresh mozzarella, torn and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"Basil, olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix dough, rest 20 min, knead briefly until smooth; cold-ferment 24–72 h for flavor (or 2 h warm).","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":37200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 4 balls; proof 2 h at room temp. Preheat a steel or stone at max heat 45 min.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stretch from the center out, keeping a rim. Top sparingly: tomato, mozzarella, oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 6–8 min until leopard-spotted; finish with basil.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wet, sparse toppings are why home pizza goes soggy: less is structural, not aesthetic, because a domestic oven gives you eight minutes where Naples gives ninety seconds, and every extra spoon of tomato is water the crust must boil off. The long cold ferment is where the flavor lives; the screaming-hot steel is what stands in for the wood floor. The tricolore of tomato, mozzarella and basil was, the story goes, assembled for Queen Margherita in 1889; history or not, the restraint it teaches is real.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","rich","briny","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, richness that coats and comforts, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"dish":{"label":"Civitiello’s Neapolitan pizza, filmed","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cq90lUQUCUo","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pizza%20Margherita%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pizza Margherita"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Pizza Margherita","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Pizza%20Margherita","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fermentation-in-bread-yeast-levain-preferments","term":"Fermentation in Bread: Yeast, Levain, Preferments"},{"slug":"mozzarella-di-bufala-campana","term":"Mozzarella di Bufala Campana"},{"slug":"dehydrator-ferment-station-and-the-pantry-machines","term":"Dehydrator, Ferment Station & the Pantry Machines"},{"slug":"tomatoes-the-argument-for-august","term":"Tomatoes: The Argument for August"}],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Basil","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Bread","Flour"],"standard":{"slug":"pizza-margherita","marks":[{"id":"pizza-margherita#risen","text":"The rim has risen to roughly a thumb's width and is brown-gold with scattered darker blisters across it. A home oven will not give Naples' full leopard char in six to eight minutes; what you do not accept is a flat, uniformly tan rim with no blistering anywhere: that is a steel that never got hot enough."},{"id":"pizza-margherita#slice","text":"A slice held by the rim tips but does not fold back on itself; the underside is spotted brown through the dry flour, with no raw white centre."},{"id":"pizza-margherita#toppings","text":"The toppings are sparse enough to read as separate things: bare dough visible between the pieces of mozzarella, tomato a thin skim rather than a lid. The centre is dry when it lands: no standing water, no ring of whey around the cheese."},{"id":"pizza-margherita#torn","text":"Torn open, the rim is glossy and open with holes running up into the crust; a tight, even crumb in the rim means it was pressed flat during the stretch."},{"id":"pizza-margherita#basil","text":"The basil is bright green and merely wilted, never blackened or frazzled, and the mozzarella has just begun to spot and blister rather than browning into a sheet."}],"fault":"The ball is stretched cold and tight straight from the fridge before it has relaxed: it fights back, springs closed at the rim and thins to a tear at the centre, and it bakes with a pale, springy, undersized cornicione and a bare patch of scorched sauce where the hole opened."}},{"slug":"risotto-alla-milanese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"320g carnaroli or arborio rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2L hot chicken or veg stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Big pinch saffron"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g butter, 60g Parmigiano"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat onion in half the butter; toast rice 2 min until edges turn translucent.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deglaze with wine. Add hot stock a ladle at a time, stirring, ~18 min.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bloom saffron in a ladle of stock and add halfway through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off heat, beat in cold butter and Parmigiano (the mantecatura) until it flows like lava.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Stirring rubs starch off the grain (that, not cream, is where the creaminess comes from), but stop at al dente, because the rice keeps cooking on the plate and the texture Milan wants is all'onda, loose enough to move in waves when you shake the pan. The mantecatura is non-negotiable: cold butter and Parmigiano beaten in off the heat, an emulsion finished with your wrist. Milanese legend credits the saffron to a Duomo glassworker's apprentice in 1574, gilding his wedding rice with the pigment from his day job.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Risotto method","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","floral","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Risotto%20alla%20Milanese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Risotto alla Milanese"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Risotto alla Milanese","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Risotto%20alla%20Milanese","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"saffron","term":"Saffron"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Butter","Parmesan","Rice","Wine","Stock/Broth","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"chicken-piccata","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 chicken breasts, halved and pounded thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour for dredging"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter, 1 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp capers"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season and flour the cutlets; sear 2–3 min per side in oil + 1 tbsp butter; remove.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deglaze with wine, scraping the fond; reduce by half.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add lemon juice and capers; swirl in remaining butter off heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return chicken to coat; shower with parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The flour dredge is doing three jobs: browning, protecting the thin cutlet from the pan's full violence, and, the quiet one, leaving starch behind to thicken the sauce the moment wine hits the fond. The sauce is built in minutes and ruined in seconds: swirl the final butter in off the heat, because on a boil it splits to grease. Piccata as Americans know it is an Italian-American amplification of veal piccata al limone, louder, brinier, and none the worse for the journey.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","briny","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Piccata%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Piccata"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Chicken Piccata","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Chicken%20Piccata","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"deglaze-and-fond","term":"Deglaze & Fond"},{"slug":"reverse-sear-and-the-gray-band","term":"Reverse Sear & the Gray Band"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Capers","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Flour","Wine"],"standard":{"slug":"chicken-piccata","marks":[{"id":"chicken-piccata#cutlets","text":"Cutlets of even thickness, around 1cm, golden edge to edge on both faces with no pale patches of unbrowned flour."},{"id":"chicken-piccata#sauce","text":"Sauce is the brown of lifted fond rather than floury-pale, the pan floor scraped clean, and nothing on the plate tastes of raw flour."},{"id":"chicken-piccata#sauce2","text":"Sauce is opaque and glossy, coats the back of a spoon and holds together on the plate: no yellow butter ring creeping out at the rim."},{"id":"chicken-piccata#roughly","text":"Roughly two tablespoons per cutlet, clinging to the meat rather than pooling: cut at the thickest point the chicken is just opaque with clear juice, and the coating is still on the cutlet rather than shed into the sauce."},{"id":"chicken-piccata#sharp","text":"Sharp before rich (lemon and caper brine land first, the butter behind them) and nobody reaches for more lemon at the table."}],"fault":"The final butter went in over live heat and the sauce split: a greasy yellow slick with the capers stranded in it, and cutlets sitting in oil instead of wearing a glaze."}},{"slug":"pasta-e-fagioli","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 cans cannellini or borlotti beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 1 carrot, 1 celery, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g ditalini"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rosemary, Parmigiano rind if you have it"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat vegetables in olive oil; add garlic, rosemary, tomato paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add beans, stock, and the cheese rind; simmer 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend a third of the soup for body; return and cook pasta directly in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with good oil and grated cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cooking the pasta directly in the soup is the whole dish: the starch it sheds turns bean broth into velvet, which is why pasta boiled separately leaves you with beans in dishwater. The Parmigiano rind is the poor kitchen's stock cube, collagen and glutamate simmered back into service. This is cucina povera at its most instructive, and it punishes the make-ahead cook honestly, because the ditalini keep drinking broth as they stand. Eat it the day it's made, or hold the pasta back.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","floral","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20e%20Fagioli%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pasta e Fagioli"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Pasta e Fagioli","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Pasta%20e%20Fagioli","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Rosemary","Parmesan","Pasta","White beans","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"pasta-e-fagioli","marks":[{"id":"pasta-e-fagioli#ladled","text":"Ladled, it falls in a slow ribbon and mounds slightly in the bowl rather than spreading flat to the rim."},{"id":"pasta-e-fagioli#broth","text":"Broth is opaque and velvety; no clear liquid separates out around the beans after a minute standing."},{"id":"pasta-e-fagioli#beans","text":"Most beans are still whole with their skins intact rather than the pot gone to paste, and the ditalini is still firm at the tooth when the bowl reaches the table: pasta is distinguishable from bean by bite."},{"id":"pasta-e-fagioli#surface","text":"Surface carries a slick of raw finishing oil, beaded and green-smelling rather than cooked in, with grated cheese that has not yet melted flat."},{"id":"pasta-e-fagioli#tasted","text":"Tasted at the bowl it needs no salt at the table and is not sharp with it: the rind and the finishing cheese are already in the total. Rosemary reads as background perfume, with no loose needles in the spoon."}],"fault":"It sat on the stove or was reheated, and the ditalini has drunk the broth: what arrives is a stiff bean porridge with soft grey pasta and no liquid left to call soup."}},{"slug":"eggplant-parmigiana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 large eggplants, sliced 1cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, basil"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mozzarella, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g Parmigiano"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt eggplant 30 min; pat dry. Fry or roast at 220°C until deeply golden.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer a quick garlic-basil tomato sauce, 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Layer sauce, eggplant, both cheeses; repeat, ending with Parmigiano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 190°C for 30 min; rest 15 min before cutting.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true}],"note":"Resting is the difference between clean layers and a delicious landslide: the molten mozzarella and sauce need fifteen minutes to set from liquid back to structure, exactly like a lasagne. Salting the eggplant matters less for bitterness (modern varieties have had it bred out) than for collapsing the sponge: unsalted slices drink oil by the ladleful. Naples, Sicily and Parma all claim the dish; the safest position is to claim a second helping and stay out of it.","equipment":["Thermometer"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eggplant%20Parmigiana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Eggplant Parmigiana"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Eggplant Parmigiana","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Eggplant%20Parmigiana","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Eggplant","Basil","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Mozzarella","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"tiramisu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg yolks, 80g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g mascarpone"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml strong espresso, cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp marsala or rum (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"~24 savoiardi"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cocoa powder"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk yolks and sugar over a bain-marie until pale and thick; cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in mascarpone until just smooth: don't overbeat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip biscuits in espresso for one second per side; layer with cream twice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 6+ hours; dust cocoa just before serving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A one-second dip, each side, no more: savoiardi are engineered to wick liquid by capillary action and they finish softening in the fridge; a soaked biscuit has already collapsed before assembly. The other trap is the mascarpone: at 75% fat it churns toward butter if overbeaten, so fold until just smooth and no further. For all its air of antiquity, tiramisù is younger than the moon landing: Treviso's Le Beccherie popularised it in the early 1970s, and the name promises what the espresso delivers: a pick-me-up.","equipment":["Bain-marie"],"techniques":["The water bath"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tiramisu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tiramisu"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Tiramisu","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Tiramisu","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mascarpone","term":"Mascarpone"},{"slug":"espresso-and-the-coffee-program","term":"Espresso & the Coffee Program"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Wine","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Coffee/Espresso"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath"]},{"slug":"rosemary-focaccia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g water (80%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt, 4g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"50ml olive oil plus more for the pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rosemary, flaky salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix to a wet, shaggy dough; four sets of folds over 2 hours (or overnight cold).","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into an oiled pan; let relax and rise 1 hour until bubbly.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil your fingers and dimple firmly to the bottom; top with rosemary and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 220°C, 22–25 min, until deep gold.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"The dimples aren't decoration. They trap pools of oil that fry the crust from above, and they selectively degas the dough so it bakes level instead of ballooning into one giant tunnel. Press to the bottom of the pan, with conviction. At 80% hydration the dough will feel alarmingly wet; that water is the open crumb. This is Liguria's bread, where olive oil is an ingredient measured in glugs, not a greasing agent: skimp on it and you've made a different, sadder bread.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rosemary%20Focaccia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rosemary Focaccia"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Rosemary Focaccia","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Rosemary%20Focaccia","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fermentation-in-bread-yeast-levain-preferments","term":"Fermentation in Bread: Yeast, Levain, Preferments"}],"pantryItems":["Rosemary","Bread","Flour"],"standard":{"slug":"rosemary-focaccia","marks":[{"id":"rosemary-focaccia#stands","text":"Oil still stands in the craters at the pass and the top is an even deep gold; the flaky salt has survived as separate crystals that crunch, and the rosemary is toasted olive-green and still smells of rosemary when you lean over the pan, not blackened to ash."},{"id":"rosemary-focaccia#slab","text":"The slab is level: no more than a finger's width between the high points and the dimple floors, and no single dome risen across the middle."},{"id":"rosemary-focaccia#lift","text":"Lift a corner with a spatula. The base releases clean, is fried gold, and rattles on the pan; no dough sticks and there is no pale wet patch."},{"id":"rosemary-focaccia#face","text":"The cut face stands about 3cm from a 20x30cm pan, with holes of many sizes running right to the edges; the crumb is glossy and springs back, and there is no dense seam along the bottom."},{"id":"rosemary-focaccia#corner","text":"Eat a corner plain. It is seasoned all the way through, not just on the salted top, and the olive oil reads as flavour, green and peppery, not as grease left on the fingers."}],"fault":"The pan rise is given the printed hour rather than the state: in a cool kitchen the dough is still sluggish and short of the corners when it goes in, so it bakes tight and low, the dimples close over during the spring, and the crumb comes out uniform and bready instead of open."}},{"slug":"shrimp-scampi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g large shrimp, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch chili flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon, parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Linguine or crusty bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear shrimp 1 min per side in olive oil; remove before fully cooked.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften garlic and chili gently; deglaze with wine and reduce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mount with butter, add lemon; return shrimp 1 min to finish.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss with linguine and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Shrimp overcook in the time it takes to find your tongs; they're done at 50-something degrees and rubber by 70, so they leave the pan early and return only for the final minute, finishing in the sauce's gentler heat. The sauce itself is a butter mount: wine reduced, then cold butter swirled in off the boil so it emulsifies instead of splitting. The name is an Italian-American tautology: scampo is a langoustine, so 'shrimp scampi' really means shrimp cooked the way the old country cooked something better.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shrimp%20Scampi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shrimp Scampi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Shrimp Scampi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Shrimp%20Scampi","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"shrimp-sizing-and-prawn-confusion","term":"Shrimp Sizing & Prawn Confusion"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Pasta","Bread","Wine","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"shrimp-scampi","marks":[{"id":"shrimp-scampi#garlic","text":"The garlic is straw-pale and slack, every slice the same colour. One browned edge shows in a sauce this shallow, and tastes of it."},{"id":"shrimp-scampi#sauce","text":"The sauce is opaque and clinging rather than clear: a spoon drawn through the pan leaves a track that closes slowly, no free butter rings the pan edge, and once the linguine goes in every strand is slick with nothing pooling in the bottom of the bowl."},{"id":"shrimp-scampi#shrimp","text":"Every shrimp is a loose C. Tight closed rings mean they sat in the pan through the reduction instead of leaving it early."},{"id":"shrimp-scampi#thickest","text":"Cut across the thickest part of the back: opaque to the centre, no grey translucent core, the flesh parting with a clean snap rather than compressing."},{"id":"shrimp-scampi#taste","text":"Taste the sauce before the shrimp go back in: the wine reads cooked, with no raw-alcohol sharpness on the front of the tongue, and the lemon lands behind the garlic rather than in front of it."}],"fault":"Rubbery shrimp in a good sauce, because they were cooked through on the first pass and then returned to the pan anyway. They come out tight, closed rings that bounce off the tooth and shed a little water into the sauce as they sit, and no amount of butter reads as anything but a wasted mount around them."}},{"slug":"the-french-omelette","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives (classic), Gruyère (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat eggs completely smooth with salt; no streaks of white.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt butter over medium in a nonstick pan until foaming, not brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add eggs; stir rapidly with a fork or spatula while shaking the pan until softly set curds form.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off heat, let the base set 10 seconds, then roll onto the plate seam-side down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pale gold outside, custard inside: browning is a flavor choice, and in Paris it's a mistake, because the Maillard notes bully the butter and eggs out of the room. The whole dish is heat management: rapid stirring makes the curds small and cream-fine, and the ten still seconds at the end knit them into a skin that can roll. This is the classic audition piece for a French kitchen precisely because there are three ingredients and nothing in the pan to hide behind.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"dish":{"label":"Pépin’s omelet lesson","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1XoCQm5JSQ","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Omelette%20recipe","sub":"Search films of The French Omelette"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on The French Omelette","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20The%20French%20Omelette","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"nonstick-honestly","term":"Nonstick, Honestly"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Chives","Butter","Cheese (any)","Gruyère/Alpine"],"standard":{"slug":"the-french-omelette","marks":[{"id":"the-french-omelette#uniform","text":"Uniform pale blond over the whole surface, seam and both ends included; turn it under the light and no tan or freckled patch appears anywhere."},{"id":"the-french-omelette#cigar-shaped","text":"Cigar-shaped, closed at both ends, seam underneath; it holds its roll on the plate and does not slacken open."},{"id":"the-french-omelette#skin","text":"Skin unbroken and dry to the eye: no curd bursting through a tear, and no butter or water weeping into a ring around it within thirty seconds of plating."},{"id":"the-french-omelette#cream-fine","text":"Cut across it: cream-fine moist curds top to bottom with no ropey white streaks of unbeaten albumen, the set base no thicker than a coin, and when the plate is tilted the centre slumps once and stops rather than running."},{"id":"the-french-omelette#seasoned","text":"Seasoned evenly through: first bite and last taste the same, with no grains of salt sitting on the outside and no flat unsalted mouthful in the middle."}],"fault":"The pan ran hot and the eggs were left to sit between stirs, so the omelette comes up freckled tan on the seam side with the curds fused into one rubbery sheet instead of custard."}},{"slug":"coq-au-vin","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs, skin-on"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g bacon lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g mushrooms, 20 pearl onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bottle red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cups stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Thyme, bay, garlic"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render lardons; brown chicken hard in the fat; set both aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown onions and mushrooms; add garlic and flour, cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deglaze with wine, add stock and herbs; return chicken, simmer 45–60 min.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reduce the sauce until it coats a spoon; finish with a knob of butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Reduce the wine by half before the stock goes in and the sauce arrives deeper and less boozy: raw wine needs long simmering to shed its edge, and the chicken will be done first. The dish was built for an old rooster whose sinew wanted hours of wet heat; supermarket thighs cook faster than the flavor develops, which is why the reduction has to do the work the bird no longer can. The finishing knob of butter isn't garnish: swirled in off the heat, it ties the sauce to a gloss.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Coq%20au%20Vin%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Coq au Vin"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Coq au Vin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Coq%20au%20Vin","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Bacon","Onion","Garlic","Mushrooms","Thyme","Bay leaf","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"standard":{"slug":"coq-au-vin","marks":[{"id":"coq-au-vin#sauce","text":"The sauce is opaque brown-red, coats a spoon, and still shines at the table. A mounted sauce gone matte was boiled after the butter went in."},{"id":"coq-au-vin#thigh","text":"Each thigh comes out of the pot in one piece with its skin still attached and stained wine-dark, not sloughing off as a grey rag. Nobody should expect crisp skin after an hour in liquid, but it should be whole and on the meat."},{"id":"coq-au-vin#meat","text":"The meat gives at the fork but stays on the piece; pulled apart, it is wet through, not dry-shredding at the outer edge."},{"id":"coq-au-vin#lardons","text":"Lardons still have bite and the pearl onions hold their shape. Neither has gone waterlogged and grey in an hour of wine."},{"id":"coq-au-vin#boozy","text":"No boozy sting: the wine tastes cooked through, with no alcohol heat at the back of the throat and no sharpness on the front of the tongue."}],"fault":"Dry chicken in an excellent sauce, because the thighs sat in the pot while it reduced. Done at fifty minutes and held to eighty for the sake of the gloss, which is a trade nobody at the table agreed to."}},{"slug":"soupe-a-loignon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg yellow onions, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Baguette, Gruyère"},{"kind":"item","text":"Thyme"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook onions in butter over medium-low with a pinch of salt 45–60 min to deep mahogany.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in flour; deglaze with wine, add stock and thyme; simmer 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle into ovenproof bowls; top with toasted baguette and a blanket of Gruyère.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Broil until molten and bronzed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"If the onions catch, deglaze with a splash of water and keep going: those dark patches dissolving off the pan floor ARE the soup. What you cannot do is hurry: caramelizing a kilo of onions to mahogany takes the better part of an hour, and high heat gives you bitter black edges on raw centers instead. Les Halles market workers ate this at dawn against the cold; the Gruyère lid broiled onto the bowl is the only roof the dish needs.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Caramelizing onions","The broiler: heat from above"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Soupe%20%C3%A0%20l'Oignon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Soupe à l'Oignon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Soupe à l'Oignon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Soupe%20%C3%A0%20l'Oignon","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"gruyere","term":"Gruyère"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Thyme","Butter","Cheese (any)","Gruyère/Alpine","Bread","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"standard":{"slug":"soupe-a-loignon","marks":[{"id":"soupe-a-loignon#spoonful","text":"A spoonful lifted from any depth of the pot shows mahogany strands throughout, with no pale or yellow ones hiding near the top."},{"id":"soupe-a-loignon#kilo","text":"The kilo of raw onions has collapsed to roughly a fifth of its volume: a jammy tangle with no rings still holding their shape."},{"id":"soupe-a-loignon#broth","text":"Broth is dark and clean-bodied, just filming the back of a spoon; never gluey, never pale, and with no raw-flour taste."},{"id":"soupe-a-loignon#gruy","text":"The Gruyère lid is bronzed and blistered, sealed to the rim of the bowl, and lifts off in one sheet with the crouton beneath it still holding under spoon pressure rather than dissolved to mush."},{"id":"soupe-a-loignon#sweet","text":"Sweet first, beef-savoury behind it, and no bitterness in the finish: a scorched edge at the back of the throat is the tell that the hour was rushed."}],"fault":"The heat was pushed to save the hour, so the onions carry black scorched shreds around pale undercooked centres and the finished bowl tastes bitter where it should taste sweet."}},{"slug":"ratatouille","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 eggplant, 2 zucchini, 2 bell peppers"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tomatoes or 400g canned"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Herbes de Provence, basil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut everything to equal 2cm dice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauté each vegetable separately until browned; this is the whole secret.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine with tomatoes, garlic, herbs; simmer gently 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest: it's better warm or next day, with torn basil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Crowded vegetables steam; separate batches taste like Provence, one batch tastes like cafeteria. Each vegetable has its own water content and its own browning schedule, and only a solo turn in the pan gets eggplant creamy, zucchini nutty and peppers sweet before they meet. The reward for the patience is a dish genuinely better the next day, once the juices in the bottom of the pot have had a night to argue themselves into agreement.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ratatouille%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ratatouille"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Ratatouille","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Ratatouille","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"peppers-and-eggplant-the-nightshade-late-summer","term":"Peppers & Eggplant: The Nightshade Late Summer"},{"slug":"tomatoes-the-argument-for-august","term":"Tomatoes: The Argument for August"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Zucchini","Eggplant","Basil","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"ratatouille","marks":[{"id":"ratatouille#piece","text":"Every piece is still recognisable as its own vegetable at roughly 2cm; nothing has collapsed into a common pulp."},{"id":"ratatouille#spoon","text":"Spoon up a few pieces of each and the eggplant, zucchini and pepper all carry browned faces: the pan colour is on every vegetable, not just one, and none of it reads uniform grey."},{"id":"ratatouille#eggplant","text":"Eggplant is creamy through with no squeak; zucchini yields but holds its square and has not watered out."},{"id":"ratatouille#liquid","text":"Liquid in the pot is a small amount of glossy, oil-slicked juice, not a thin wash; a spoon dragged across the bottom leaves a track that fills back slowly."},{"id":"ratatouille#taste","text":"Taste an eggplant cube and a piece of tomato back to back (both are seasoned, neither bland against the other), and the garlic and herbs taste cooked-sweet and perfumed with no raw grassy bite."}],"fault":"Everything went into one crowded pan, the vegetables released their water and stewed instead of browning, so the dish arrives grey and slack in a thin pool with the zucchini gone to pulp."}},{"slug":"sole-meuniere","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 sole or flounder fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour for dredging"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley, capers (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the fish obsessively; season and dredge in flour, shaking off excess.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear in half the butter 2 min per side until golden; plate.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add remaining butter; cook to beurre noisette, nutty and hazelnut-brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add lemon juice off heat (it will spit), pour over fish, add parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Watch the milk solids: foam, then quiet, then brown: quiet is your ten-second warning before beurre noisette becomes beurre brûlé. The dredge-and-butter formula is the miller's wife's namesake trick (à la meunière, floured like her hands), and it made Julia Child's first meal in France, the one she credited with her conversion. Dry the fish obsessively; surface water is why fillets glue themselves to the pan and why crusts turn to paste.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brown butter","Searing: the hard crust","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brown butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brown%20butter%20beurre%20noisette%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sole%20Meuni%C3%A8re%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sole Meunière"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Sole Meunière","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Sole%20Meuni%C3%A8re","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Capers","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Flour"],"standard":{"slug":"sole-meuniere","marks":[{"id":"sole-meuniere#fillet","text":"The fillet leaves the pan whole and arrives whole: an even tan crust across the top face, no white floury patches at the thin tail end, and no pale grey paste welded to the pan behind it."},{"id":"sole-meuniere#butter","text":"The butter solids in the sauce are brown specks, not black grit, and the butter itself is the colour of a hazelnut skin and no darker."},{"id":"sole-meuniere#crust","text":"The crust is still audible under the sauce. Press a fork to the floured face on the plate and it gives a faint crackle, with the coating still dry-edged where the butter has not pooled. A fillet that waited on the plate while the noisette was fussed over arrives uniformly slack."},{"id":"sole-meuniere#crust2","text":"The crust holds when a fork goes in, and the flesh beneath separates into opaque white sheets with no translucent seam at the thickest point."},{"id":"sole-meuniere#taste","text":"Taste a corner: the crust is seasoned in itself, with no salt grains left sitting on the surface, and the lemon reads as brightness through the butter rather than as a sour edge sitting on top of it."}],"fault":"A black, acrid sauce, because the noisette was made in the pan that just cooked the flour. The scorched dredge was already sitting in there, so the butter went from foam to bitter with no nutty stage in between, and the specks in it are carbon rather than browned milk solids."}},{"slug":"boeuf-bourguignon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg beef chuck, large cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bottle Burgundy or pinot noir"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml stock, 2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"Carrots, pearl onions, mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour, thyme, bay"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown beef in batches until crusted; render lardons.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauté aromatics, add flour and paste; deglaze with wine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add stock and herbs; braise covered at 160°C for 2.5–3 h until fork-tender.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauté mushrooms and onions separately; fold in and reduce sauce to gloss.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Batches, always: gray meat makes gray stew. A crowded pan drops below searing temperature, the beef exhales its moisture, and everything boils in its own juice instead of browning; the fond those batches leave behind is most of the sauce's spine. Burgundy's farmers built the dish to turn a workhorse cut and an open bottle into something aristocratic, and the finish still matters: mushrooms and pearl onions cooked separately and folded in late, so they arrive as themselves and not as casualties.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boeuf%20Bourguignon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boeuf Bourguignon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Boeuf Bourguignon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Boeuf%20Bourguignon","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chuck","term":"Chuck"},{"slug":"pinot-noir-and-burgundy","term":"Pinot Noir & Burgundy"},{"slug":"boudin-noir-and-boudin-blanc","term":"Boudin Noir & Boudin Blanc"},{"slug":"pinot-noir","term":"Pinot Noir"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Mushrooms","Thyme","Flour","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"standard":{"slug":"boeuf-bourguignon","marks":[{"id":"boeuf-bourguignon#sauce","text":"The sauce is glossy and near-black red, thick enough to coat the back of a spoon and hold a parted line, with no orange fat standing on the surface and no thin purple liquid at the pot's edge."},{"id":"boeuf-bourguignon#cube","text":"A cube lifted out shows a dark browned exterior rather than pale grey, splits under the side of a fork with no sawing, and still holds its corners. Meat collapsed to threads in the pot went past the mark."},{"id":"boeuf-bourguignon#open","text":"Cut one open: moist and uniform to the centre, with no dry grey ring of squeezed fibre inside a tender exterior."},{"id":"boeuf-bourguignon#pearl","text":"Pearl onions are whole with their layers intact and the mushrooms are firm at the centre; bite one of each and it still tastes of itself rather than only of sauce."},{"id":"boeuf-bourguignon#wine","text":"The wine reads as depth, not acid: round at the finish, no tannic scrape on the teeth. Salt lands square with the sauce at its final thickness, with no flat middle and no hard salty edge from a pot seasoned at the strength it had an hour earlier."}],"fault":"Dry, stringy beef inside a good sauce, because the pot ran at a boil instead of a tick. The fibres wrung themselves out long before the collagen melted, so the meat shreds under the fork and still eats like rope."}},{"slug":"quiche-lorraine","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 blind-baked tart shell (200g flour, 100g butter, pinch salt, cold water)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g bacon lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs + 2 yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nutmeg, pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Gruyère (modern, optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blind-bake the shell fully: pale pastry never recovers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render lardons; scatter in the shell with cheese if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk custard; season with nutmeg. Pour into shell on the oven rack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 160°C ~30 min until it barely trembles at the center.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"A quiche is a custard in a crust: treat it like crème brûlée, not a frittata. Pull it while the center still trembles; carryover sets it, and an extra five minutes turns silk into scrambled egg in a box. The blind bake is non-negotiable because pale pastry under wet custard never recovers. Lorraine's original had no cheese at all (lardons, cream and eggs only), so the Gruyère is a modern courtesy, welcome but on notice.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Pie crust & blind baking","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The brûlée torch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=creme%20brulee%20torch%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Quiche%20Lorraine%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Quiche Lorraine"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Quiche Lorraine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Quiche%20Lorraine","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"blind-baking-and-pie-doctrine","term":"Blind Baking & Pie Doctrine"},{"slug":"cheese-families","term":"Cheese Families"},{"slug":"nutmeg-and-mace","term":"Nutmeg & Mace"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Eggs","Butter","Cream","Cheese (any)","Gruyère/Alpine","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"crepes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch salt, 1 tbsp sugar for sweet"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend everything smooth; rest 30 min so the flour hydrates.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a lightly buttered pan; pour a thin layer, swirling immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip when the edges lace and lift, ~1 min; 20 seconds more.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack under a towel; fill with lemon-sugar, jam, or ham and Gruyère.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The first crêpe belongs to the cook; every French grandmother agrees, and there is chemistry inside the folklore: the first one calibrates the pan's temperature and oil film, and it is nearly always wrong. The 30-minute rest matters just as much, letting the flour hydrate and the gluten relax so the batter runs paper-thin instead of pulling back. Once the edges lace and lift, you have your timing; from there it is a production line.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Crêpes"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Crêpes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=crepe%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cr%C3%AApes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Crêpes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Crêpes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Cr%C3%AApes","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"gruyere","term":"Gruyère"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"standard":{"slug":"crepes","marks":[{"id":"crepes#cooked","text":"The cooked face is lace-mottled: irregular brown blotches over pale gold; the second side is barely coloured and matte, and that side faces in."},{"id":"crepes#full","text":"The crêpe is a full circle out to the pan's edge and even across it, under 2mm and translucent when held to the light, with no thick lip where the batter stalled."},{"id":"crepes#folds","text":"It folds into quarters without cracking: the lacy edge is crisp, the body supple, never leathery."},{"id":"crepes#stacked","text":"Stacked crêpes peel apart one at a time without fusing or tearing, and chew soft with no rubber."},{"id":"crepes#batch","text":"The batch yields at least a dozen from a 24cm pan. Eight or nine fat ones means they were poured thick, whatever they look like individually."}],"fault":"The batter keeps thickening on the bench while the first ones cook and is never loosened back to thin cream: the later crêpes stop running to the edge, set thick and matte, chew like pancakes and fold into a wedge instead of lying flat."}},{"slug":"creme-brulee","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla bean or 2 tsp paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g sugar + more for the crust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm cream with vanilla. Whisk yolks and sugar; temper in the cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain into ramekins; bake in a water bath at 150°C, 30–35 min, until just wobbly.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 4+ hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle a thin, even layer of sugar; torch to amber glass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two thin torched layers beat one thick one: better crack, no burnt patches, because a deep bed of sugar scorches on top before the bottom melts. The custard itself is an exercise in restraint: yolks only, a water bath to hold the oven's violence at arm's length, and out at the barely-wobbly stage since carryover finishes the set. The point of the dish is contrast, cold silk under hot glass, which is why the torching happens at the last minute, never ahead.","equipment":["Ramekins","Torch","Bain-marie"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","The brûlée torch","The water bath","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","charred","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The brûlée torch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=creme%20brulee%20torch%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cr%C3%A8me%20Br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Crème Brûlée"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Crème Brûlée","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Cr%C3%A8me%20Br%C3%BBl%C3%A9e","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-kitchen-torch","term":"The Kitchen Torch"},{"slug":"vanilla","term":"Vanilla"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"],"standard":{"slug":"creme-brulee","marks":[{"id":"creme-brulee#sugar","text":"The sugar is one unbroken amber sheet edge to edge, right up to the ramekin wall: no black scorched spots, no white grains left unmelted."},{"id":"creme-brulee#back","text":"The back of a spoon breaks it in a single crack and the shards come away large; the sugar layer is under 2mm."},{"id":"creme-brulee#nudged","text":"Nudged at the end of the bake, the custard moves as one slow mass under a set surface: no loose ripple or liquid slack at the centre, and no ramekin that has stopped moving altogether. Chilled, it is set edge to edge with nothing sloshing."},{"id":"creme-brulee#first","text":"The first spoonful leaves a clean-walled trench that holds its shape, with no watery pool collecting in it and no grain or bubbles in the custard."},{"id":"creme-brulee#custard","text":"The custard is still fridge-cold to the spoon while the caramel over it is warm; if both are the same temperature, it was torched too early."}],"fault":"It is baked to firm instead of to the wobble: held in until nothing moves at all, so carryover pushes it past the set: the custard puffs, tightens grainy at the edges and weeps a ring of watery whey that pools in the first spoonful."}},{"slug":"salade-nicoise","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Good jarred tuna in oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, boiled 8 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g small potatoes, 150g green beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tomatoes, olives, anchovies"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dijon vinaigrette: 1 tbsp mustard, 2 tbsp vinegar, 6 tbsp olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil potatoes; blanch beans until snappy and shock in ice water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the vinaigrette; season assertively.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress potatoes while warm: they drink it in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Compose everything on a platter; never toss into confetti.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Warm potatoes drink vinaigrette, a technique you'll reuse for every potato salad forever, because starch cells open with heat and close as they cool, carrying the dressing in with them. The other law is compositional: a Niçoise is arranged, never tossed, so every element keeps its own temperature and texture. Nice's purists famously bar cooked vegetables entirely; the potato-and-bean version is the one the rest of the world won, but the anchovies and good oil-packed tuna are not negotiable anywhere.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salade%20Ni%C3%A7oise%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salade Niçoise"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Salade Niçoise","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Salade%20Ni%C3%A7oise","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tuna-grades-akami-chutoro-otoro","term":"Tuna Grades (Akami, Chūtoro, Ōtoro)"},{"slug":"blanch-and-shock","term":"Blanch & Shock"},{"slug":"tuna-and-winter-fat","term":"Tuna & Winter Fat"}],"pantryItems":["Tuna","Anchovy","Eggs","Tomato","Potato","Green beans","Olives","Vinegar","Mustard"],"standard":{"slug":"salade-nicoise","marks":[{"id":"salade-nicoise#arranged","text":"Arranged in distinct groups on the platter, each element liftable on its own: tuna in large hand-broken flakes rather than shredded to floss, nothing tossed into confetti, no ingredient buried."},{"id":"salade-nicoise#beans","text":"Beans are bright green and snap when bent, not olive-drab and floppy."},{"id":"salade-nicoise#halve","text":"Halve a potato: the interior is glossy and seasoned to the centre, not dry and white under a merely wet skin."},{"id":"salade-nicoise#halve2","text":"Halve an egg: white fully set, yolk bright and just tacky at the middle, no grey-green ring at the boundary."},{"id":"salade-nicoise#tasted","text":"Tasted on its own the vinaigrette is sharp enough to sting, and it stays joined through service: the platter floor carries a light gloss, not a split pool under the potatoes."}],"fault":"The potatoes were dressed after they went cold, so the vinaigrette slides straight off them into a puddle under the platter and the salad reads bland while the bottom of the dish is oily."}},{"slug":"miso-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10cm piece kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g katsuobushi (or skip for vegan)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp miso"},{"kind":"item","text":"Silken tofu, wakame, scallion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep kombu in barely-simmering water 10 min; remove before it boils.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add katsuobushi, cut heat, steep 3 min; strain. This is dashi.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm dashi with tofu and rehydrated wakame.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve miso through a strainer off the boil; add scallions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boiled miso goes flat and gritty: its aroma compounds are volatile and its koji enzymes die, which is why it dissolves through a strainer off the boil, a finishing seasoning rather than an ingredient to cook. The real recipe here is the dashi underneath: kombu pulled before the simmer (boiled, it turns slimy and bitter), katsuobushi steeped a bare three minutes. Ichiban dashi takes less time than toast and is the difference between miso soup and salty cloud water.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","herb-fresh","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, melting away silken."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Miso%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Miso Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Miso Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Miso%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tofu","Scallion","Miso","Seaweed/Nori"],"standard":{"slug":"miso-soup","marks":[{"id":"miso-soup#judge","text":"Judge the dashi before any miso goes near it: clear pale gold, you can see the bottom of the pot through it, and savoury enough to drink unseasoned. If it tastes like hot water, no amount of miso will rescue the bowl."},{"id":"miso-soup#nothing","text":"Nothing solid strayed through: no scum, no shreds of kombu or katsuobushi, no undissolved lumps of miso, no grit on the tongue."},{"id":"miso-soup#finished","text":"The finished bowl is an even cloudy tan and stays even for the first minute or two, not a clear layer sitting above a sludge of settled miso."},{"id":"miso-soup#tofu","text":"Tofu cubes arrive whole and hot through, not broken into curds by stirring; wakame is fully unfurled and silky rather than rubbery or leathery."},{"id":"miso-soup#enough","text":"Hot enough to steam but sippable straight from the bowl without a spoon, around 75°C, and the miso still smells sweet and malty at the nose. Flat and merely salty means it went back to the boil."}],"fault":"The pot was brought back to a boil after the miso went in, so the aroma has blown off and what arrives is gritty salt water, the dashi's work erased in the last thirty seconds."}},{"slug":"chicken-teriyaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 chicken thighs, boneless, skin-on"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sake"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Crisp the thighs skin-down over medium, pressing, 8 min; flip 3 min. Drain fat.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":660,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the mixed sauce; simmer and turn to glaze, 2–3 min, until syrupy.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest, slice, spoon over the glaze; serve with rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Real teriyaki is a pan glaze, not a marinade: teri means luster, and the luster comes from soy, mirin and sake reducing onto already-crisp skin in the final minutes. Marinated first, the sugars burn before the skin ever crisps, which is the take-out version's chewy fate. Render the thighs patiently, drain the fat so the glaze grips instead of sliding, and pull it while the syrup still flows; it tightens as it cools.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Teriyaki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Teriyaki"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Chicken Teriyaki","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Chicken%20Teriyaki","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sake-fundamentals-the-polishing-ladder","term":"Sake Fundamentals: The Polishing Ladder"},{"slug":"sake-service-and-pairing","term":"Sake Service & Pairing"},{"slug":"baste-arroser-and-glaze","term":"Baste, Arroser & Glaze"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar"],"standard":{"slug":"chicken-teriyaki","marks":[{"id":"chicken-teriyaki#skin","text":"The skin is one continuous lacquered sheet, mahogany and flat against the meat, with no shrunken lifted edges and no black patches where the sugar caught."},{"id":"chicken-teriyaki#glaze","text":"The glaze is thin enough to see the skin through and thick enough to hang: a drip off the spoon stretches before it falls, and no thin brown puddle spreads from under the thigh. On the tongue it is savoury and salty first with the sweetness behind, not candy."},{"id":"chicken-teriyaki#skin2","text":"The skin holds its own under the knife. It parts with the meat rather than sliding off as a soft sheet, and the glaze sits on it as a film instead of soaking into a spongy layer."},{"id":"chicken-teriyaki#sliced","text":"Sliced across after resting, the meat is uniformly opaque and the board takes a trace of clear juice, not a flood."},{"id":"chicken-teriyaki#slick","text":"No fat slick anywhere: what sits under the chicken and around the slices is glaze, not oil, and it does not separate into a rim as the plate stands."}],"fault":"Soft skin under a sticky coat, because the sauce went into the pan before the fat had finished rendering and before the pan was drained. The glaze steams the crust it was meant to sit on, and the thigh arrives sweet, slippery and flabby with the skin loose from the meat."}},{"slug":"onigiri","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 cups short-grain rice, cooked and warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fillings: umeboshi, tuna-mayo, salmon flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nori strips"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wet your hands, salt your palms.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take a handful of warm rice, tuck a spoon of filling in the center.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press into a triangle with firm, gentle turns: 3 presses, rotate, repeat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap with nori just before eating so it stays crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Compress just enough to hold: an onigiri should fall apart in the mouth, not in the hand, and the difference is three firm-but-gentle presses versus a squeeze that welds the grains into a plug. Wet, salted palms are the whole seasoning system: the salt lives on the surface where the tongue meets it first. Nori goes on at the last second so it snaps rather than sogs. This is Japan's oldest portable meal: samurai carried them into the field a thousand years ago.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Onigiri%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Onigiri"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Onigiri","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Onigiri","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"wild-salmon-the-run-calendar","term":"Wild Salmon: The Run Calendar"},{"slug":"tuna-grades-akami-chutoro-otoro","term":"Tuna Grades (Akami, Chūtoro, Ōtoro)"},{"slug":"tuna-and-winter-fat","term":"Tuna & Winter Fat"}],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Tuna","Rice","Mayonnaise","Seaweed/Nori"]},{"slug":"japanese-curry","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g chicken thigh or beef, chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, 2 carrots, 2 potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Curry roux blocks (or make roux + curry powder + garam masala)"},{"kind":"item","text":"900ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Secret weapons: grated apple, splash soy"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the meat; sweat onions until golden.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add vegetables and water; simmer 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off heat, dissolve roux fully; return to a gentle simmer to thicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add apple and soy; serve over rice with pickles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Roux clumps in boiling liquid, the flour's starch gels into a skin around each block before the center melts, so kill the heat, melt it through, then return to a simmer to thicken. The grated apple and splash of soy are the home cook's signature moves, sweetness and depth the box alone doesn't carry. Curry came to Japan via the British navy in the Meiji era and became kokumin-shoku, the national dish; ask any schoolchild their favorite dinner and this is the statistical answer.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Japanese%20Curry%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Japanese Curry"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Japanese Curry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Japanese%20Curry","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"roux-slurry-and-liaison","term":"Roux, Slurry & Liaison"},{"slug":"riesling-the-sommeliers-secret-weapon","term":"Riesling: The Sommelier's Secret Weapon"},{"slug":"curry-powder-the-colonial-invention","term":"Curry Powder: The Colonial Invention"},{"slug":"chaat-masala","term":"Chaat Masala"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Onion","Potato","Carrot","Apple","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"gyoza","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g ground pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cups cabbage, minced and salted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic, ginger, scallion"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp soy, 1 tsp sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 gyoza wrappers"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dip: soy + rice vinegar + chili oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Squeeze the salted cabbage dry; mix with pork and aromatics until sticky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill, wet the edge, pleat one side against the flat side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry flat-side down until golden; add 60ml water, cover, steam 3 min.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Uncover, crisp the bottoms again; serve dark side up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fry-steam-fry is the whole game: the first fry sets the base, the water steams the filling through, and the second fry, after the lid comes off and the water burns away, restores the shattering bottom. Squeezing the salted cabbage truly dry is the other hinge; skipped, that water ends up steaming the wrapper into glue from the inside. The pleats are one-sided on purpose, curving the dumpling so it stands on its crisp keel.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pleating dumplings","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gyoza%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gyoza"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Gyoza","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Gyoza","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Chiles","Cabbage","Wrappers","Soy sauce","Sesame","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["pleating-dumplings","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"oyakodon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 chicken thighs, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, barely beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp each soy and mirin, 1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice, mitsuba or scallion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer onion in the seasoned dashi until soft; add chicken, cook through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour egg in two stages: first two-thirds, swirl gently, cover 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the rest; kill heat while still runny.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide over hot rice: the egg finishes on the way to the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The eggs should be three textures at once (set at the edges, soft in the middle, glossy-raw on top), which is why they go in two stages and the heat dies while the second pour still trembles; the bowl finishes the cooking on the way to the table. Oyako means parent-and-child, the chicken and the egg in one bowl, a name whose dark little joke Japan has enjoyed since the 1890s. Barely beat the eggs: visible streaks of white are correct.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oyakodon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oyakodon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Oyakodon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Oyakodon","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Rice","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["dashi"]},{"slug":"yakisoba","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g yakisoba noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g pork belly or chicken, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cabbage, carrot, onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 2 tbsp Worcestershire, 1 tbsp each oyster sauce, ketchup, soy"},{"kind":"item","text":"Aonori, pickled ginger"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear pork hard; add vegetables, stir-fry 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add loosened noodles with a splash of water; toss.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour sauce around the edge of the pan so it sizzles and caramelizes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with aonori and beni shoga.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sauce hitting hot steel, not noodles, is where the street-stall flavor comes from: poured around the pan's edge, the Worcestershire-based blend scorches and caramelizes in the second before it reaches the food. That toasted tang is the taste of every Japanese festival midway. Loosen the noodles with a splash of water first, or they tear; and don't skip the beni shoga, whose pickled sharpness is the dish's off-switch for richness.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yakisoba%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yakisoba"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Yakisoba","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Yakisoba","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"wok-hei-and-stir-fry-discipline","term":"Wok Hei & Stir-Fry Discipline"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Ginger","Carrot","Cabbage","Noodles","Oyster sauce","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"tamagoyaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp dashi (kombu dashi keeps it vegetarian)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp each sugar, mirin, soy"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk everything; strain for silkiness.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil a pan (rectangular ideal); pour a thin layer, roll it to one end when barely set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil again, pour more egg, lifting the roll so it flows underneath; roll again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Repeat until used up; shape in a sushi mat if you have one, slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Each layer glues to the last while still wet: patience loses, momentum wins. Wait until a layer sets fully and the roll becomes a stack of loose crêpes; roll while the surface still shines and the layers fuse into one sliceable block. Straining the egg is the difference between silk and scramble. Every sushi counter is judged on this omelette: regulars traditionally order tamago first, because it announces exactly how careful the hands behind the counter are.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Crêpes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=crepe%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamagoyaki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tamagoyaki"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Tamagoyaki","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Tamagoyaki","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi"]},{"slug":"weeknight-shoyu-ramen","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2L good chicken stock + 10cm kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tare per bowl: 1.5 tbsp soy, 1 tsp mirin, ½ tsp sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fresh ramen noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"Soft egg (6.5 min), scallion, nori"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chashu or shredded rotisserie chicken"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep kombu in stock 20 min at a bare simmer; season lightly.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix tare in each bowl: the bowl seasons the soup, not the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook noodles separately; never in the broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Assemble: tare, broth, noodles, toppings. Serve screaming hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ramen is assembly, not soup-making: broth, tare, noodles and toppings are four separate projects that meet in the bowl, which is why the tare seasons the BOWL and not the pot; every serving lands calibrated instead of drifting saltier as the broth reduces. Noodles never cook in the broth; their starch would cloud in minutes what the kombu spent twenty building. Get the sequence right and a weeknight bowl stands shockingly close to the shop version.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Ramen broth","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Weeknight%20Shoyu%20Ramen%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Weeknight Shoyu Ramen"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Weeknight Shoyu Ramen","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Weeknight%20Shoyu%20Ramen","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"stocks-blanc-brun-broth-and-demi","term":"Stocks: Blanc, Brun, Broth & Demi"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Scallion","Noodles","Soy sauce","Sesame","Wine","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"salmon-donburi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 sushi-grade salmon fillets or hot-seared salmon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Warm sushi rice (rice + 2 tbsp seasoned vinegar)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Avocado, cucumber"},{"kind":"item","text":"Soy, wasabi, sesame, nori"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: spicy mayo (mayo + sriracha)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season warm rice with sushi vinegar; fan to glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice salmon against the grain (raw) or sear 2 min a side and flake.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bowl the rice; arrange salmon, avocado, cucumber.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with sesame, nori, and sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Body-temperature rice, cool fish: the temperature contrast is the luxury, and it is the entire reason sushi rice is fanned rather than refrigerated; cold rice turns hard and dull as its starch retrogrades. Season the rice while warm so the vinegar penetrates. Slice the salmon against the grain in single confident strokes, sawing shreds the flesh, and if searing instead, two minutes a side leaves the center blushing, which is where the richness lives.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sushi rice & rolling","Searing: the hard crust","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sushi rice & rolling","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20sushi%20rice%20rolling%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salmon%20Donburi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salmon Donburi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Salmon Donburi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Salmon%20Donburi","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sashimi-grade-and-ikejime","term":"Sashimi-Grade & Ikejime"}],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Cucumber","Avocado","Rice","Soy sauce","Sesame","Vinegar","Mayonnaise","Seaweed/Nori","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"perfect-fried-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 cups day-old rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 scallions"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp soy, 1 tsp sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: char siu, shrimp, peas"}],"steps":[{"text":"Break the cold rice apart with wet hands.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scramble eggs in hot oil until barely set; remove.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blast rice over the highest heat, pressing and tossing until it dances and smells toasty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return eggs, add soy around the rim, scallions, sesame oil. Done in 90 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Day-old rice isn't tradition, it's chemistry: chilled overnight, the starch retrogrades into firm crystalline grains that fry and stay separate, where fresh rice steams into mush on contact. From there the dish is heat: the highest flame you own, rice pressed against the metal until it dances and smells toasty, soy added around the rim so it toasts on the steel before it touches a grain. Ninety seconds, and the pan does more of the cooking than the recipe does.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","nutty-toasted","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"dish":{"label":"CCD: how to make any fried rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Perfect%20Fried%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Perfect Fried Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Perfect Fried Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Perfect%20Fried%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"shrimp-sizing-and-prawn-confusion","term":"Shrimp Sizing & Prawn Confusion"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Eggs","Scallion","Peas","Rice","Soy sauce","Sesame"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"mapo-tofu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450g soft tofu, cubed and steeped in hot salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g ground pork (or shiitake for veg)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp doubanjiang"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp fermented black beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic, ginger, 300ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sichuan peppercorn, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cornstarch slurry, scallion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry pork crisp; add doubanjiang and beans, fry until the oil turns red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add aromatics, then stock; slide in tofu, simmer 5 min without stirring hard.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thicken with slurry in two additions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with ground Sichuan peppercorn and scallion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Steeping the tofu in hot salted water firms and seasons it so it survives the wok: soft tofu handled cold shatters into curds before it reaches the table. The dish's engine is doubanjiang fried until the oil runs red, which is Sichuan's way of saying the paste is awake; the ground Sichuan peppercorn goes on at the end, where its citrus-electric numbing stays vivid. Named, the story goes, for the pockmarked grandmother, má pó, who sold it to Chengdu's oil porters.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","herb-fresh","aromatic","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mapo%20Tofu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mapo Tofu"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Mapo Tofu","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Mapo%20Tofu","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sichuan-peppercorn-and-mala","term":"Sichuan Peppercorn & Málà"},{"slug":"roux-slurry-and-liaison","term":"Roux, Slurry & Liaison"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Tofu","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Mushrooms","Black beans","Stock/Broth","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"char-siu-pork","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g pork shoulder, cut into long strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp hoisin, 2 tbsp soy, 2 tbsp honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp five-spice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Red fermented tofu (optional, for the classic hue)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate pork 4–24 hours; reserve marinade.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Roast at 200°C on a rack, 30–40 min, turning once.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil the marinade with extra honey into a glaze; brush every 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish under the broiler for charred, lacquered edges. Rest, slice thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Shoulder over tenderloin: you want fat cap and char, not lean and dry, because the glaze's honey needs fat underneath to lacquer against, and the burnt edges are the prize the whole roast exists for. Brushing every ten minutes builds the coat in layers the way one thick slather never will. The red fermented tofu is the traditional colorant and a quiet umami engine; without it you'll still eat well, but the Cantonese barbecue-shop hue is its signature.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Roasting in a hot oven","The broiler: heat from above","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","charred","tangy-ferment","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, char used as punctuation, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Char%20Siu%20Pork%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Char Siu Pork"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Char Siu Pork","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Char%20Siu%20Pork","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chinese-five-spice","term":"Chinese Five-Spice"},{"slug":"salamander-broiler-and-top-down-fire","term":"Salamander, Broiler & Top-Down Fire"},{"slug":"pork-shoulder-butt-vs-picnic","term":"Pork Shoulder: Butt vs. Picnic"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Tofu","Soy sauce","Wine","Honey","Five-spice"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","fermentation"]},{"slug":"kung-pao-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g chicken thigh, diced, velveted (1 tsp each soy, wine, cornstarch)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dried chilies, 1 tsp Sichuan peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Roasted peanuts, scallion whites"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 1 tbsp each soy, black vinegar, sugar; 1 tsp dark soy; slurry"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sizzle chilies and peppercorns in oil until fragrant, not black.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add chicken; stir-fry until golden at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add scallions, then the sauce; it should thicken in seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss in peanuts off heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kung pao is sweet, sour and numbing in balance: taste the sauce before it hits the wok, because there is no adjusting a stir-fry after the fact. The chilies are sizzled until fragrant and mahogany, never black; scorched chilies turn the whole dish acrid. Velveting the thigh meat in soy, wine and cornstarch is what keeps it silk under high heat. The dish carries a Qing governor's title (gongbao, palace guardian), and the name survived even the era when politics tried to erase it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Wok technique","Velveting"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","nutty-toasted","fiery","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Velveting","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=velveting%20meat%20chinese%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kung%20Pao%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kung Pao Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Kung Pao Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Kung%20Pao%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Scallion","Chiles","Soy sauce","Peanut","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique"]},{"slug":"beef-and-broccoli","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g flank steak, thin against the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"Velvet: 1 tsp baking soda + 1 tbsp soy + 1 tbsp cornstarch, 15 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head broccoli, blanched 1 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 2 tbsp oyster sauce, 1 tbsp soy, 1 tsp sugar, 120ml stock, slurry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic, ginger"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear velveted beef in a ripping-hot pan 1 min; remove.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry garlic and ginger; add broccoli and sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return beef as the sauce thickens to a gloss.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately over rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Baking-soda velveting is the takeout secret: fifteen minutes raises the meat's surface pH so its proteins can't knot tight, and flank turns silk-soft instead of chewy. Slice thin and against the grain; the soda can't rescue long fibers. Blanching the broccoli first means the stir-fry only has to finish it, so the florets stay green and snappy while the oyster-sauce gloss comes together in the last thirty seconds. The dish is Cantonese-American engineering, and none the lesser for it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Velveting","Searing: the hard crust","Blanching & shocking","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","briny","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a briny, maritime salinity, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Velveting","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=velveting%20meat%20chinese%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beef%20%26%20Broccoli%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beef & Broccoli"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Beef & Broccoli","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Beef%20%26%20Broccoli","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fillet-steak-loin-and-collar","term":"Fillet, Steak, Loin & Collar"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Broccoli","Soy sauce","Oyster sauce","Stock/Broth","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"scallion-pancakes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml just-boiled water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil + 2 tbsp flour (paste)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Big bunch scallions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt; dip: soy + vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a hot-water dough; rest 30 min: it becomes stretchy and forgiving.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll thin, brush with oil-flour paste, blanket with scallions and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into a snake, coil into a snail, flatten again. This builds the layers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pan-fry in shallow oil, pressing and rotating, until shattering-crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hot water kills gluten's bounce: that's why these roll thin without fighting back, where a cold-water dough would snap back like elastic and tear. The lamination is the other half: oil paste, roll, coil, flatten, so the pancake fries into distinct shattering layers instead of one dense disc. Press and rotate as it fries: the spin crisps the surface evenly, and it is the street-stall tell of someone who has made ten thousand of them.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scallion%20Pancakes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Scallion Pancakes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Scallion Pancakes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Scallion%20Pancakes","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Scallion","Flour","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"wonton-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g ground pork + 150g shrimp, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp soy, 1 tsp sesame oil, white pepper, ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 wonton wrappers"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2L chicken stock + a slice of ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bok choy, scallion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the filling in one direction until sticky and bouncy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dot filling, wet edges, fold triangle, then pinch corners together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil wontons separately until they float + 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve in hot seasoned stock with greens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boil the wontons in water, not the broth: wrapper flour clouds a stock you spent hours keeping clean. The filling is beaten in one direction until sticky, which cross-links the proteins into the springy bite Cantonese cooks prize; lazily mixed filling crumbles. Float-plus-one-minute is the doneness rule. The name means 'swallowing clouds' in Cantonese, and that is the standard the folds are being asked to live up to.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wonton%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wonton Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Wonton Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Wonton%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Ground meat","Shrimp","Ginger","Scallion","Cabbage","Wrappers","Soy sauce","Sesame","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"dan-dan-noodles","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g fresh wheat noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g ground pork fried crisp with 1 tbsp sweet bean or hoisin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Per bowl: 1 tbsp sesame paste or tahini, 1 tbsp chili oil, 2 tsp soy, 1 tsp black vinegar, ½ tsp sugar, ground Sichuan pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot noodle water to loosen"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions, crushed peanuts, wilted greens"}],"steps":[{"text":"Build the sauce in each serving bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the pork topping until crispy and dark.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook noodles; loosen each sauce bowl with 3 tbsp noodle water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Noodles in, pork on top: everyone mixes their own.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The dish is a sauce ratio, not a recipe: sesame paste, chili oil, soy, black vinegar, sugar and Sichuan pepper built in the bottom of each bowl, loosened with noodle water, learned once and riffed on forever. The crisp pork is a topping, not a sauce; its crunch against the slick noodles is the point. Named for the dan-dan shoulder pole Chengdu street vendors carried (noodles swinging from one end, sauce from the other), it was fast food a century before the term existed.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","fiery","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, real fire that builds and stays, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dan%20Dan%20Noodles%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dan Dan Noodles"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Dan Dan Noodles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Dan%20Dan%20Noodles","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sesame-seeds-tahini-and-beyond","term":"Sesame: Seeds, Tahini & Beyond"},{"slug":"black-pepper-and-the-pepper-family","term":"Black Pepper & the Pepper Family"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Scallion","Chiles","Noodles","Soy sauce","Tahini","Peanut","Sesame","Vinegar","Sugar","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"steamed-fish-ginger-and-scallion","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole sea bass or 4 fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ginger, half julienned, half sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions, julienned"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp soy + 1 tsp sugar + 2 tbsp water, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp smoking-hot oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steam fish over sliced ginger: whole ~10 min, fillets 6–7 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":1020,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Discard steaming liquid; blanket with raw ginger and scallion julienne.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour smoking oil over: it should crackle violently.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drizzle the seasoned soy around, not on, the fish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The smoking-oil pour flash-cooks the raw ginger and scallion julienne in a half-second of contact: if it doesn't crackle violently, the oil wasn't ready and the aromatics just sit there raw. Discarding the steaming liquid matters: it carries the fish's muddier notes, which is also why the seasoned soy is poured around, not on, so the skin stays taut. This is the Cantonese test of a fish's freshness: ten minutes of steam and nowhere to hide.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Steamed%20Fish%2C%20Ginger%20%26%20Scallion%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Steamed Fish, Ginger & Scallion"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Steamed Fish, Ginger & Scallion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Steamed%20Fish%2C%20Ginger%20%26%20Scallion","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"grilling-vs-barbecue-vs-smoking","term":"Grilling vs. Barbecue vs. Smoking"},{"slug":"steaming-and-en-papillote","term":"Steaming & En Papillote"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Ginger","Scallion","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"chicken-congee","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 cup jasmine rice, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L chicken stock or water + bone-in chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"Big knob ginger, smashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toppings: scallion, fried shallots, white pepper, soy, chili crisp, century egg if you're brave"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer rice, ginger, and chicken gently, lid ajar, 60–90 min, stirring now and then.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull chicken when cooked; shred and return.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until the grains burst into silk; thin with stock to taste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season lightly; let the toppings do the talking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Freeze the rinsed rice overnight and it breaks down in half the time: ice crystals shatter the grain from the inside, which is the entire trick. Congee wants indifference, not attention: the barest simmer, lid ajar, stirred just often enough to keep the bottom honest, until the grains burst into silk. Season it lightly and late; the bowl is a canvas, and the toppings (white pepper, fried shallots, chili crisp, a century egg for the brave) are where each eater signs it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Congee"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Congee","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20congee%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Congee%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Congee"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Chicken Congee","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Chicken%20Congee","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Ginger","Scallion","Shallot","Chiles","Rice","Soy sauce","Stock/Broth"]},{"slug":"tacos-de-pollo-asado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken thighs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade: juice of 2 limes + 1 orange, 2 tsp each cumin and smoked paprika, oregano, garlic, chipotle in adobo"},{"kind":"item","text":"Corn tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"White onion, cilantro, lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salsa of choice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate chicken 30 min to overnight.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill or sear hard until charred at the edges; rest, chop.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast tortillas on the open flame or dry pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve street-style: meat, onion, cilantro, lime. Nothing else needed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Double-stack thin tortillas: the inner one catches the juice, the outer one stays whole; taquerias figured out the failure mode centuries before the hand-wash station. Citrus marinades run on a clock: thirty minutes brightens, overnight works because chicken thighs forgive, but the acid is already denaturing the surface. Char hard, rest, chop. Then stop: onion, cilantro, lime is the complete street specification, and everything added past it is subtraction.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Asado fire","Searing: the hard crust","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","smoky","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Asado fire","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=argentine%20asado%20fire%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tacos%20de%20Pollo%20Asado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tacos de Pollo Asado"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Tacos de Pollo Asado","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Tacos%20de%20Pollo%20Asado","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"paprika-and-pimenton","term":"Paprika & Pimentón"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Lime","Orange","Cilantro","Oregano","Bread","Cumin","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"guacamole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 ripe avocados"},{"kind":"item","text":"½ white onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 serrano or jalapeño, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Big handful cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of 1–2 limes, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tomato optional, controversial"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mash onion, chili, cilantro, and salt into a rough paste first, a molcajete if you have one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in avocado, leaving real chunks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lime and salt until it tastes bright, then one more pinch of salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately with the pit in (it doesn't help; it just looks right).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt suppresses bitterness and makes avocado taste more like itself: season past timid, tasting as you go, because under-salted guacamole reads as bland fat. Grinding the onion, chile, cilantro and salt into a paste first is the molcajete method: it distributes the aromatics as flavor rather than as chunks. Leave real pieces of avocado. And the pit in the bowl does nothing: oxidation is an air problem, solved by plastic pressed to the surface, not folklore.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The molcajete","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","herb-fresh","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":21,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The molcajete","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=molcajete%20salsa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Guacamole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Guacamole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Guacamole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Guacamole","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"jamon-serrano","term":"Jamón Serrano"}],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Avocado","Lime","Cilantro"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"chicken-tinga","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken breasts or thighs, poached and shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g tomatoes or canned"},{"kind":"item","text":"2–3 chipotles in adobo + 1 tbsp sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic, oregano, splash of poaching broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tostadas, crema, queso fresco"}],"steps":[{"text":"Caramelize onion; blend tomatoes with chipotles and garlic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the purée in the onion pan until darkened and thick: this is 'frying the salsa'.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add chicken and broth; simmer 15 min until juicy, not soupy.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile onto tostadas with crema, lettuce, queso.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Frying the blended salsa is the step that makes it Mexican cooking: a raw purée tastes like tomato juice; seared in hot fat until it darkens and thickens, it concentrates into something with a past. The chipotles are smoked-then-dried jalapeños, so the dish carries fire and smoke without a grill. Simmer only until juicy (tinga on a tostada should sit, not soak), and remember the poaching broth is free flavor waiting to be used.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Caramelizing onions","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","aromatic","silky","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":21,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Tinga%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Tinga"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Chicken Tinga","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Chicken%20Tinga","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"queso-oaxaca-quesillo","term":"Queso Oaxaca (Quesillo)"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Oregano","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"carne-asada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g skirt or flank steak"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of 3 limes + 1 orange"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, 1 jalapeño"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro, cumin, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tortillas and salsa verde to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate 30 min: citrus starts 'cooking' the meat beyond that.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill over the hottest fire you can make, 3–4 min per side.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 5 min; slice thin against the grain.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve as tacos or with beans and grilled onions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Skirt steak has an obvious grain: cut across it and it's butter, with it and it's a chew toy; the muscle fibers run the width of the strip, so slicing means shortening them. The marinade runs on the same clock as all citrus: thirty minutes is seasoning, hours is surface mush. What the dish really wants is fire beyond your comfort zone: skirt is thin, so only violent heat can char the outside before the inside overshoots.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","gently hot","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":21,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carne%20Asada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carne Asada"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Carne Asada","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Carne%20Asada","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"flank-skirt-and-hanger","term":"Flank, Skirt & Hanger"},{"slug":"pressure-and-slow-cooking","term":"Pressure & Slow Cooking"},{"slug":"salamander-broiler-and-top-down-fire","term":"Salamander, Broiler & Top-Down Fire"},{"slug":"fillet-steak-loin-and-collar","term":"Fillet, Steak, Loin & Collar"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Orange","Cilantro","Bread","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"enchiladas-verdes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 corn tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g tomatillos, husked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 2 serranos, garlic, cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Shredded cooked chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crema, queso fresco"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for softening tortillas"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil tomatillos and chilies until olive-drab; blend with aromatics; fry the salsa 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flash-fry each tortilla 5 seconds a side to waterproof it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip, fill with chicken, roll seam-down; sauce generously.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 10 min; finish with crema, cheese, raw onion rings.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"The five-second fry waterproofs each tortilla; skip it and your enchiladas dissolve into casserole, because naked corn tortillas drink salsa until they surrender. Tomatillos are boiled just to olive-drab; past that they burst and turn bitter. Then the salsa is fried, like every serious Mexican sauce, until it deepens. Verdes are assembled and eaten, not held: ten minutes in the oven to marry, crema and queso fresco on top, and straight to the table.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","fiery","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, real fire that builds and stays, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":21,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Enchiladas%20Verdes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Enchiladas Verdes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Enchiladas Verdes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Enchiladas%20Verdes","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Tomatillo","Cilantro","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Bread"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"pozole-rojo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g pork shoulder, chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cans hominy, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 guajillo + 2 ancho chilies, seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic, onion, oregano, bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garnish bar: cabbage, radish, lime, onion, oregano, tostadas"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer pork with onion, garlic, bay until tender, ~90 min.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toast chilies 30 seconds; soak, blend with garlic, strain; fry the purée.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add chili base and hominy to the pot; simmer 30 min more.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with the garnish bar: the toppings are half the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Toast the chilies until fragrant, never smoking: burnt chile is bitter forever, and no amount of simmering apologizes for it. Guajillo brings the color, ancho the raisin depth; strained and fried, they become the pot's red engine. The garnish bar is not optional: pozole arrives at the table half-finished by design, and the cabbage, radish, lime and oregano are each eater's own seasoning. The dish predates the conquest: hominy was sacred maize, and pozole was a feast food when Tenochtitlan still stood.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","aromatic","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":21,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pozole%20Rojo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pozole Rojo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Pozole Rojo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Pozole%20Rojo","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"garnish-expression-twists-and-the-cherry-question","term":"Garnish: Expression, Twists & the Cherry Question"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Cabbage","Corn","Radish/Turnip","Lime","Oregano"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"elote","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 ears corn"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp mayo + 2 tbsp crema"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g cotija, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chile powder or Tajín"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lime"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char corn directly over flame or a screaming grill pan, turning, ~8 min.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Paint with the mayo-crema while hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll in cotija; dust with chile.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze lime over everything.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The mayo is glue and gloss: it's what makes the cheese and chile stick to every kernel, and its fat carries the chile's flavor in a way naked corn can't. Char matters more than cooking: the kernels are edible raw, so the flame's only job is blisters and smoke. Cotija is the right cheese because it refuses to melt, staying a salty crumble. This is Mexico City street food eaten standing up; the lime at the end is what makes the richness legal.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Charring over open flame"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","smoky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":21,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Elote%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Elote"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Elote","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Elote","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cotija","term":"Cotija"}],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Corn","Lime","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"chiles-rellenos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 poblano chilies"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g Oaxaca or Monterey Jack"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salsa roja for serving"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char poblanos until fully blackened; steam in a bag, peel, slit, seed carefully.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stuff with cheese; seal with a toothpick; dust with flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip whites to soft peaks, fold in yolks; coat chilies in this capeado.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 180°C oil, basting the top, until puffed gold. Serve in warm salsa.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The flour dust is primer: egg batter slides straight off a naked chile, and the capeado is the dish: whites whipped to soft peaks, yolks folded back in, a soufflé worn as a jacket. Char the poblanos fully black and steam them in a bag; hesitant charring means peeling skin off raw flesh in shreds. Seed gently through the slit: the chile must stay whole to hold its molten heart. Basting the top as it fries sets the batter before you dare to flip.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Charring over open flame","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":21,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The soufflé","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=souffle%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chiles%20Rellenos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chiles Rellenos"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Chiles Rellenos","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Chiles%20Rellenos","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"monterey-jack-and-pepper-jack","term":"Monterey Jack & Pepper Jack"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Chiles","Cheese (any)","Flour"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"salsa-roja-de-molcajete","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 Roma tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 serranos or jalapeños"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"¼ white onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, cilantro"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry-char tomatoes, chilies, garlic, onion on a comal or cast iron until blistered black in spots.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peel the garlic; grind chilies and garlic with salt first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in tomatoes and onion to a rustic, textured crush.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with cilantro. Never refrigerate what you'll eat today.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Char is the seasoning: a pale salsa needs a confession, not more salt. Blistering the tomatoes, chilies and garlic black in spots on dry metal builds the smoke the recipe otherwise has no source for. Grind chilies, garlic and salt first: the coarse salt is an abrasive that pulls the aromatics into paste before the wet tomatoes flood in. The molcajete's basalt leaves a texture no blender blade can counterfeit (a crush, not a purée), and today's salsa never sees the refrigerator.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["The molcajete","Tortillas on the comal","Charring over open flame"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","aromatic","herb-fresh","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":21,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The molcajete","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=molcajete%20salsa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salsa%20Roja%20de%20Molcajete%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salsa Roja de Molcajete"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Salsa Roja de Molcajete","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Salsa%20Roja%20de%20Molcajete","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"copper-and-enameled-cast-iron","term":"Copper & Enameled Cast Iron"},{"slug":"comal-plancha-and-griddles","term":"Comal, Plancha & Griddles"},{"slug":"cast-iron","term":"Cast Iron"}],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Cilantro","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"tres-leches","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Sponge: 5 eggs, 150g sugar, 120g flour, splash vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 can condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 can evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream (in the soak) + more whipped on top"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whip eggs and sugar to ribbon stage; fold in flour. Bake 175°C, 25 min.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool slightly; stab everywhere with a skewer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the whisked three milks over slowly, in stages. Chill 4+ hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crown with whipped cream and cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A fatless sponge is a sponge in the literal sense: butter cakes refuse to drink, because fat waterproofs the crumb; the whipped-egg sponge is all open structure, and it swallows three milks without collapsing. Stab it everywhere and pour in stages, letting each addition find its level. The chill is structural: four hours turns soaked cake into cold custard-cake, which is the dessert's whole argument. Every Latin American bakery case runs on this physics.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tres%20Leches%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tres Leches"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Tres Leches","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Tres%20Leches","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"butter-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken thighs, in yogurt + ginger-garlic + chili powder 1h"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g tomato purée"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g butter, 100ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp garam masala, 1 tsp each kasuri methi, sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ginger, garlic, green chili"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char the marinated chicken hard under a broiler or in a dry pan; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer tomatoes with aromatics 20 min; blend silky; strain if you're fancy.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mount with butter and cream; season with garam masala and crushed kasuri methi.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in chicken and its juices; simmer 5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kasuri methi (dried fenugreek leaf, crushed between your palms into the finished sauce) is the flavor people can't name but always miss; without it this is just chicken in tomato cream. The char matters equally: murgh makhani was invented at Delhi's Moti Mahal in the 1950s as a way to revive day-old tandoori chicken in butter and tomato, so the smoky edges aren't garnish, they're the dish's origin story. Balance the tomatoes' acidity with sugar until the sauce stops arguing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","The broiler: heat from above","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":22,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tandoor breads","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=naan%20tandoor%20technique%20home%20oven","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Butter%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Butter Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Butter Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Butter%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"garam-masala","term":"Garam Masala"},{"slug":"salamander-broiler-and-top-down-fire","term":"Salamander, Broiler & Top-Down Fire"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Butter","Cream","Yogurt","Tomato paste","Sugar","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"chana-masala","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 cans chickpeas (or 250g dried, cooked)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tomatoes or 1 can, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ginger-garlic paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp coriander, 1 tsp cumin, ½ tsp turmeric, chili, 1 tsp amchur or lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro, ghee or oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown onions properly: 12 patient minutes, deep gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add ginger-garlic, then spices; fry 30 seconds in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add tomatoes; cook until the masala pulls away from the pan and the oil rises.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chickpeas + a cup of water; simmer 15 min, mashing a few. Finish with amchur and cilantro.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"The oil separating is India's version of reading the fond: when the masala pulls from the pan and fat beads at its edges, the water is gone and the onions and tomatoes have actually fried instead of stewed. Everything before that moment is preamble; everything after is assembly. Amchur, the dried-mango powder at the finish, is the sour spine street vendors use; lemon substitutes, but the funky-tart depth is the real signature. Mash a few chickpeas; they thicken the gravy from inside.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","herb-fresh","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chana%20Masala%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chana Masala"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Chana Masala","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Chana%20Masala","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-souring-spices-amchur-loomi-anardana-and-tamarind","term":"The Souring Spices: Amchur, Loomi, Anardana & Tamarind"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Lemon","Cilantro","Chickpeas","Cumin","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"dal-tadka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g toor or red lentils"},{"kind":"item","text":"Turmeric, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tadka: 3 tbsp ghee, 1 tsp cumin seeds, 3 garlic cloves sliced, 2 dried chilies, pinch hing, curry leaves if you have them"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tomato, cilantro, lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer lentils with turmeric until collapsing; whisk to your preferred texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with salt; stir in chopped tomato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make the tadka: bloom spices in hot ghee until the garlic is bronzed. Seconds matter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour it over the dal at the table. Listen to it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The tadka goes on last because fat-bloomed spices lose their perfume in a long simmer: the whole technique exists to deliver cumin, garlic and chili at the moment of maximum aroma, hot ghee poured hissing over the finished dal at the table. Seconds matter in the pan: bronzed garlic is nutty, brown garlic is bitter, and the difference is one breath. The lentils themselves want collapse, whisked until they can't decide if they're soup. Listen to the sizzle; that sound is the recipe working.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":22,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tadka: blooming spices in fat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tadka%20tempering%20spices%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dal%20Tadka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dal Tadka"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Dal Tadka","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Dal%20Tadka","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"turmeric","term":"Turmeric"},{"slug":"toasting-blooming-and-the-tadka-doctrine","term":"Toasting, Blooming & the Tadka Doctrine"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lemon","Cilantro","Curry/Makrut leaves","Lentils","Cumin","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat"]},{"slug":"palak-paneer","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g spinach, blanched 60 sec and shocked"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g paneer, cubed, briefly pan-fried"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 2 tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ginger, garlic, green chili"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp each cumin and garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cream, ghee, kasuri methi"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the shocked spinach coarsely: it should stay emerald.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry onion, then ginger-garlic-chili, then tomato until the fat rises.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add spinach; simmer only 5 min to keep the color.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold in paneer, cream, methi; done.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Blanch-and-shock keeps chlorophyll bright: sixty seconds in boiling water, straight into ice, and the purée stays emerald; long-simmered spinach turns army green because heat converts chlorophyll's magnesium out of the molecule. So the spinach joins the masala for only five minutes, at the end. Pan-frying the paneer first gives it a skin that survives the sauce. The cream and kasuri methi finish is what moves it from vegetable purée to Punjabi Sunday lunch.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","gently hot","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, melting away silken."},"pairingId":22,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Palak%20Paneer%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Palak Paneer"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Palak Paneer","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Palak%20Paneer","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"paneer","term":"Paneer"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Spinach","Cream","Cheese (any)","Cumin","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"weeknight-chicken-biryani","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken thighs in yogurt + biryani masala + ginger-garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g basmati, soaked 30 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, fried to birista (crisp brown)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Saffron in warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mint, cilantro, ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"Whole spices: bay, cardamom, cinnamon, clove"}],"steps":[{"text":"Parboil rice with whole spices to 70%; a grain should snap, not squash.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer: marinated chicken on the bottom, rice, birista, herbs, saffron milk, ghee.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal the lid (foil or dough); 10 min medium, then 25 min lowest heat (dum).","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 10 min. Dig from the bottom up.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Seventy-percent rice is the whole exam: a parboiled grain should snap between the fingers, not squash, because it finishes cooking in the dum steam above the chicken; fully cooked rice becomes porridge upstairs. The sealed lid is the oven: trap the steam and the pot becomes a pressure chamber where saffron, birista and marinade juices distill through the layers. Dig from the bottom up at the table, so every serving carries the full stratigraphy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Biryani & dum","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","warm-spiced","floral","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, warm spice humming through the middle, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Biryani & dum","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=biryani%20dum%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Weeknight%20Chicken%20Biryani%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Weeknight Chicken Biryani"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Weeknight Chicken Biryani","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Weeknight%20Chicken%20Biryani","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"saffron","term":"Saffron"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Cilantro","Mint","Bay leaf","Milk","Yogurt","Rice","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Saffron","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"aloo-gobi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 cauliflower, florets"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 2 tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin seeds, ½ tsp turmeric, 1 tsp coriander, garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ginger, green chili, cilantro"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear cauliflower and potato in oil until spotted brown; remove.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bloom cumin; fry onion, ginger, chili; add ground spices and tomato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return vegetables; splash of water, lid on, 12 min.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Uncover, dry it out, finish with garam masala and cilantro.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a dry curry: steam to cook, then evaporate; a wet aloo gobi is a stew that lost its way. The lid-on phase cooks the vegetables through, the lid-off phase burns off the water so the spices concentrate back onto them. Searing the cauliflower and potato first, until spotted brown, is what keeps them distinct instead of dissolving: the florets' brown spots are flavor the simmer can't provide. Garam masala at the end, always: it's a finishing perfume, not a base note.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","warm-spiced","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, warm spice humming through the middle, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":22,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aloo%20Gobi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Aloo Gobi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Aloo Gobi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Aloo%20Gobi","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cumin","term":"Cumin"},{"slug":"garam-masala","term":"Garam Masala"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Tomato","Potato","Chiles","Cauliflower","Cilantro","Cumin","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"raita","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g whole-milk yogurt, whisked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber, grated and squeezed"},{"kind":"item","text":"½ tsp roasted cumin, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mint or cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pinch chili powder"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk yogurt smooth with a splash of water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze the cucumber like you mean it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold everything together; dust with cumin and chili.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Roast whole cumin in a dry pan, then grind: pre-ground is a photocopy of the smell; the toasting rebuilds the volatile oils that left the jar months ago. Squeeze the cucumber like you mean it, because its water is the difference between raita and yogurt soup by the time dinner is served. The dish's job at the table is thermal: yogurt's proteins bind capsaicin in a way water can't, which is why it sits beside every fiery plate on the subcontinent.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":22,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Raita%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Raita"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Raita","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Raita","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cumin","term":"Cumin"}],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Cucumber","Cilantro","Mint","Milk","Yogurt","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"garlic-naan-stovetop","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g yogurt + 60ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp each sugar, salt; ½ tsp baking soda + 1 tsp baking powder (or 4g yeast)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic butter, cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cast-iron skillet"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead a soft, slightly tacky dough; rest 1 hour covered.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll teardrops; wet one side with water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slap wet-side down onto a ripping-hot dry cast iron; when bubbled, flip the pan over the open flame to char the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush with garlic butter immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The water-slap makes the naan stick like a tandoor wall. That's the entire trick: a wet underside welds to ripping-hot cast iron, and when you flip the whole pan over the flame the top blisters exactly as it would inside the clay oven's throat. Yogurt in the dough keeps it tender and slightly sour. Brush with garlic butter the second it leaves the pan, while the surface is still thirsty. Stack under a towel; naan hardens as it cools alone.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Tandoor breads","Charring over open flame","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tandoor breads","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=naan%20tandoor%20technique%20home%20oven","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garlic%20Naan%20(Stovetop)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Garlic Naan (Stovetop)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Garlic Naan (Stovetop)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Garlic%20Naan%20(Stovetop)","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"copper-and-enameled-cast-iron","term":"Copper & Enameled Cast Iron"},{"slug":"cast-iron","term":"Cast Iron"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Cilantro","Butter","Milk","Yogurt","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"tandoori-style-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken drumsticks/thighs, slashed to the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade 1: lemon + salt + chili, 15 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade 2: thick yogurt, ginger-garlic, garam masala, cumin, turmeric, kashmiri chili, mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for basting, onion + lime to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Two-stage marinade: acid first, then yogurt mix 4–24 h.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Roast at 240°C on a rack ~25 min, or grill.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Baste with butter; finish under the broiler until charred at the ridges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest; serve with lime, raw onion, mint chutney.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Slashes to the bone let marinade and heat in: unslashed tandoori is just baked chicken in costume. The two-stage marinade is the classic sequence: salt, lemon and chili first to season the flesh, then the yogurt mix, whose lactic acid tenderizes gently where straight citrus would mush the surface. Mustard oil in the marinade is the northern signature, sharp and faintly wasabi-like. The broiler finish stands in for the tandoor's 480-degree throat: you want char at the ridges, not an even tan.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Tandoor breads","Roasting in a hot oven","The broiler: heat from above","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","charred","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, char used as punctuation, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tandoor breads","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=naan%20tandoor%20technique%20home%20oven","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tandoori-Style%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tandoori-Style Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Tandoori-Style Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Tandoori-Style%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Lemon","Lime","Butter","Yogurt","Mustard","Cumin","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"kheer","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100g basmati rice, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cardamom, saffron"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pistachios, almonds, raisins"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer rice in milk, stirring often, scraping the sides in, 40 min.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2400,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"It's ready when a spoon leaves a trail.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweeten at the end; add cardamom and saffron.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill or serve warm, heavy with nuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The browned milk solids on the pot wall are flavor: scrape them back in, don't fight them; that slow toasting is where kheer gets its faint caramel depth, the same chemistry that makes rabri. Sugar goes in at the end because it interferes with the rice's softening if added early. The spoon-trail test is the doneness call: thick enough to hold a line, loose enough to relax on the plate. Every festival table on the subcontinent seats a version of this pudding.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","warm-spiced","floral","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, warm spice humming through the middle, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kheer%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kheer"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Kheer","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Kheer","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Milk","Rice","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"pad-thai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g dried rice noodles, soaked in warm water 30 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g shrimp or chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 3 tbsp tamarind paste, 3 tbsp fish sauce, 3 tbsp palm sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Firm tofu, dried shrimp, garlic chives, bean sprouts"},{"kind":"item","text":"Peanuts, lime, chili flakes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the sauce until it tastes equally sweet, sour, salty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wok in two batches: sear protein and tofu, push aside, scramble egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add noodles and sauce; toss until noodles absorb it and turn supple.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprouts and chives at the last second; peanuts and lime at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Soak, don't boil, the noodles: they finish cooking in the sauce and arrive chewy instead of mushy; boiled noodles have already spent their starch and can only overcook. The sauce is a three-way argument (tamarind sour, palm sugar sweet, fish sauce salt), settled in the pan before the noodles ever hear it. Work in batches: a crowded wok stews. The dish was standardised and promoted by the Thai government in the 1940s as an act of nation-building, which is why one noodle dish carries the country's name.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Searing: the hard crust","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"dish":{"label":"Pailin’s definitive Pad Thai","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kBBnBF3sOws","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pad%20Thai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pad Thai"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Pad Thai","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Pad%20Thai","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Shrimp","Tofu","Eggs","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Bean sprouts","Chives","Noodles","Fish sauce","Peanut","Sugar","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"green-curry-gaeng-keow-wan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp green curry paste (jarred is fine, doctor with fresh chili and lime leaf)"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml coconut milk, unshaken"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g chicken thigh, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Thai eggplant or bamboo shoots"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fish sauce, palm sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Thai basil, makrut lime leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the paste in the thick coconut cream from the can's top until the oil splits out, 5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add chicken; coat in the paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add remaining coconut milk, eggplant, torn lime leaves; simmer 12 min.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with fish sauce and palm sugar; rain in basil off heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cracking the coconut cream, frying the paste in the can's thick top layer until the oil visibly splits out, is the step that separates Thai kitchens from timid ones. That separated fat is the cooking medium; paste simmered in whole coconut milk merely poaches, and the curry tastes boiled instead of fried. Green is the hottest of the classic pastes, its color from fresh chilies rather than dried. Basil goes in off the heat, where its anise perfume survives the trip to the table.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","gently hot","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pounding a curry paste","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=thai%20curry%20paste%20mortar%20pestle%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Green%20Curry%20(Gaeng%20Keow%20Wan)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Green Curry (Gaeng Keow Wan)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Green Curry (Gaeng Keow Wan)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Green%20Curry%20(Gaeng%20Keow%20Wan)","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"curry-leaf","term":"Curry Leaf"},{"slug":"the-fresh-aromatic-trio-galangal-lemongrass-and-makrut-lime","term":"The Fresh Aromatic Trio: Galangal, Lemongrass & Makrut Lime"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Chiles","Eggplant","Lime","Coconut","Basil","Curry/Makrut leaves","Milk","Coconut milk","Fish sauce","Sugar","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"tom-yum-goong","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1L stock (shrimp shells make it best)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 lemongrass stalks, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 slices galangal, 5 makrut lime leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g shrimp, mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp fish sauce, 3 tbsp lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Thai chilies, cilantro, chili paste (nam prik pao) optional"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the aromatics in stock 10 min: they flavor, you don't eat them.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add mushrooms, then shrimp for the last 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Kill the heat, THEN add lime juice and fish sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Adjust to hot-sour-salty balance; cilantro to finish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lime juice boiled turns bitter and flat: off-heat acid is the rule for the whole cuisine, and this soup is its clearest test: kill the flame, then season, and the broth stays electric. The aromatics are infrastructure, not food; lemongrass, galangal and makrut leaves are bruised, simmered and left in the bowl unchewed. Shrimp shells simmered into the stock are where the depth comes from. The name is the recipe: tom, to boil; yum, the hot-sour balance the last thirty seconds decides.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tom%20Yum%20Goong%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tom Yum Goong"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Tom Yum Goong","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Tom%20Yum%20Goong","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-fresh-aromatic-trio-galangal-lemongrass-and-makrut-lime","term":"The Fresh Aromatic Trio: Galangal, Lemongrass & Makrut Lime"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Ginger","Chiles","Mushrooms","Lime","Cilantro","Lemongrass","Curry/Makrut leaves","Fish sauce","Stock/Broth"]},{"slug":"larb-gai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g ground chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp uncooked sticky rice, toasted and ground (khao khua)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp lime juice, 2 tbsp fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chili flakes, shallots, scallions"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mint by the handful"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce cups, sticky rice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast rice in a dry pan to deep gold; grind coarse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook chicken with a splash of water, breaking fine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress warm with lime, fish sauce, chili.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in herbs and rice powder just before serving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Khao khua, raw sticky rice toasted to deep gold and ground coarse, is nutty perfume plus grip: it's why larb clings to lettuce instead of raining out, and its faint smoke is the dish's backbone. Make it fresh; it fades in a jar within days. The chicken cooks with a splash of water, gently, because larb is a warm salad, not a stir-fry, dressed while warm so the meat drinks the lime and fish sauce. Mint by the handful, not the sprig. Isaan eats this with sticky rice and eats it daily.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","umami-deep","tangy-ferment","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Larb%20Gai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Larb Gai"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Larb Gai","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Larb%20Gai","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Scallion","Shallot","Chiles","Lettuce/Greens","Lime","Mint","Rice","Fish sauce"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"pad-krapow-moo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g ground pork (or chicken)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves + 4 Thai chilies, pounded"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp each oyster sauce, fish sauce; 1 tsp dark soy, 1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Big bunch holy basil (Thai basil works)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fried egg per person, jasmine rice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scream-hot wok: fry the garlic-chili paste 15 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add pork, pressing into the wok to sear, not stew.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauces in; toss 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off heat, wilt in the basil. Crown rice with it and a crispy-edged fried egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is Thailand's ten-minute answer to a bad day, and the crispy-laced fried egg on top is mandatory; its yolk is the sauce's co-signer. Press the pork into the screaming wok and let it sear; constant stirring stews it gray. Holy basil is the dish's name and soul, peppery and clove-like where sweet basil is soft; if you substitute, the dish forgives but notices. The fifteen-second garlic-chili fry at the start is where the heat infuses the oil that carries everything else.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","aromatic","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pad%20Krapow%20Moo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pad Krapow Moo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Pad Krapow Moo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Pad%20Krapow%20Moo","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-wok","term":"The Wok"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Garlic","Chiles","Basil","Rice","Soy sauce","Fish sauce","Oyster sauce","Sugar","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"pad-krapow-moo","marks":[{"id":"pad-krapow-moo#pork","text":"The pork is in separate browned crumbles with crisp edges and dark caught faces, not a uniform pale mince, and no grey liquid sits in the bottom of the wok."},{"id":"pad-krapow-moo#sauce","text":"The sauce is a glaze clinging to the meat: tilt the plate and nothing runs from under the pork."},{"id":"pad-krapow-moo#holy","text":"Holy basil is wilted but whole-leafed and still green, no leaf blackened or dissolved into the sauce, leaves show in every spoonful rather than as scattered flecks, and its clove-pepper smell rises off the plate."},{"id":"pad-krapow-moo#fried","text":"The fried egg has brittle brown lace at the rim that shatters under a spoon, and the yolk breaks and runs when pierced."},{"id":"pad-krapow-moo#fish","text":"Fish and oyster salt lead, sugar is barely traceable, and the garlic tastes toasted rather than raw or acrid."}],"fault":"Cold pork tipped in all at once, dropping the wok below searing heat, so the meat poaches in the water it releases and the sauce joins that water into a grey-brown broth the rice drinks."}},{"slug":"massaman-curry","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g beef chuck, chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp massaman paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Potatoes, onions, roasted peanuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tamarind, palm sugar, fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cinnamon stick, star anise, cardamom"}],"steps":[{"text":"Crack the coconut cream; fry the paste and whole spices until fragrant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add beef, remaining coconut milk, water to cover; simmer 90 min.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add potatoes and onion, 25 min more.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Balance: tamarind sour, palm sugar sweet, fish sauce salt. Peanuts in at the end.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Massaman is Thai-Persian fusion three centuries old: the cinnamon, star anise and cardamom aren't a mistake, they're the history, carried into Ayutthaya by Muslim traders and folded into a coconut curry that still bears their name ('matsaman', the Muslim curry). It behaves like a braise, not a stir-fry: beef chuck wants its ninety minutes, and the potatoes drink the sauce as they finish. Balance at the end (tamarind, palm sugar, fish sauce) and let the roasted peanuts land last, still crunchy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","umami-deep","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Massaman%20Curry%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Massaman Curry"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Massaman Curry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Massaman%20Curry","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chuck","term":"Chuck"},{"slug":"the-souring-spices-amchur-loomi-anardana-and-tamarind","term":"The Souring Spices: Amchur, Loomi, Anardana & Tamarind"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Potato","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Fish sauce","Peanut","Sugar","Tamarind","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"som-tum","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g green papaya, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, 2–5 Thai chilies"},{"kind":"item","text":"Long beans, cherry tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp fish sauce, 2 tbsp lime, 1 tbsp palm sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dried shrimp, peanuts"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound garlic and chilies in a mortar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add beans and tomatoes; bruise, don't purée.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add papaya; pound-and-scoop with a spoon in the other hand to mix.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress and taste; it should slap: hot, sour, funky, a little sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pound to bruise: bruised cells drink dressing; chopped ones just wear it. The mortar is the whole apparatus: one hand pestles while the other folds with a spoon, so every strand of green papaya gets both damage and dressing. Taste at the end and be honest: som tum should slap, hot-sour-funky-sweet in that order, and a polite version is a coleslaw. Green papaya is crunch with no agenda, which is exactly why it can carry that much aggression.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","aromatic","umami-deep","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Som%20Tum%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Som Tum"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Som Tum","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Som%20Tum","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"garnish-expression-twists-and-the-cherry-question","term":"Garnish: Expression, Twists & the Cherry Question"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Green beans","Lime","Stone fruit","Fish sauce","Peanut","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"khao-pad-thai-fried-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 cups day-old jasmine rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g shrimp or chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"1½ tbsp fish sauce, 1 tsp soy, pinch sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chinese broccoli or tomato, onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cucumber, lime, prik nam pla (fish sauce + chilies)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear protein; scramble eggs in the same wok.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rice in; press, flip, press over max heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with fish sauce and soy around the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with cucumber slices, lime, and prik nam pla on the side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jasmine rice and fish sauce are what make it Thai rather than Chinese. That, and the lime you squeeze over at the end, plus the prik nam pla alongside so each eater tunes their own salt and fire. The wok work is the same physics as every fried rice: day-old grains, maximum flame, press-flip-press so the rice toasts against the steel. The cucumber slices aren't garnish; they're the cooling system, and Thai plates almost always ship with one.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"dish":{"label":"CCD: how to make any fried rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Khao%20Pad%20(Thai%20Fried%20Rice)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Khao Pad (Thai Fried Rice)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Khao Pad (Thai Fried Rice)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Khao%20Pad%20(Thai%20Fried%20Rice)","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Shrimp","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Broccoli","Cucumber","Lime","Rice","Soy sauce","Fish sauce","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"chicken-satay-peanut-sauce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken thigh, strips, on soaked skewers"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade: coconut milk, turmeric, coriander, cumin, lemongrass, sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 4 tbsp peanut butter, 200ml coconut milk, 1 tbsp red curry paste, tamarind, palm sugar, fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cucumber relish (ajad)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate 2–24 h.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer sauce ingredients 10 min until it thickens; loosen with water.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill skewers hot and fast, brushing with coconut milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with sauce and quick-pickled cucumber.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fry the red curry paste into the cracked coconut milk first: that one step takes the sauce from peanut-butter dip to satay sauce, blooming the paste's chilies and aromatics in fat before the peanuts thicken everything. Turmeric in the marinade is why the skewers glow gold. Grill hot and fast, brushing with coconut milk to baste and flare the fire. The cucumber ajad on the side is structural: quick-pickled relief between rich bites.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","earthy","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pounding a curry paste","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=thai%20curry%20paste%20mortar%20pestle%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Satay%2C%20Peanut%20Sauce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Satay, Peanut Sauce"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Chicken Satay, Peanut Sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Chicken%20Satay%2C%20Peanut%20Sauce","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Cucumber","Coconut","Lemongrass","Butter","Milk","Coconut milk","Fish sauce","Peanut","Sugar","Tamarind","Curry paste","Cumin","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"mango-sticky-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g glutinous (sweet) rice, soaked 4h+"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g sugar, ½ tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ripe mangoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toasted mung beans or sesame"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steam soaked rice over cloth 25 min: steam, never boil.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Dissolve sugar and salt into warm coconut milk; pour ⅔ over the hot rice; rest 20 min covered.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the remaining coconut milk lightly for the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve warm rice, cold mango, sauce over everything.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The salt isn't optional: Thai desserts run on the sweet-salty axis, and the lightly salted coconut sauce is what keeps the dish from cloying. Steam the rice over cloth, never boil it: glutinous rice cooked in water bursts into paste, while steam leaves each grain whole and chewy. Pour the sweetened coconut milk over the rice while hot: the twenty-minute covered rest is when it drinks. Warm rice, cold mango: the season's best dessert is a temperature contrast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mango%20Sticky%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mango Sticky Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Mango Sticky Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Mango%20Sticky%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mango-the-monsoon-herald","term":"Mango: The Monsoon Herald"}],"pantryItems":["Mango","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"hummus","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 cans chickpeas (or 250g dried, cooked soft with ½ tsp baking soda)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g good tahini"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of 1–2 lemons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cumin, olive oil, paprika"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer canned chickpeas 15 min with a pinch of baking soda until squashable; a few skins will float off.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend hot chickpeas with garlic and lemon first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add tahini; stream in ice water until pale, whipped, and pourable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Plate in swooshes; flood with olive oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hot chickpeas plus ice water is the fluffiness hack: the temperature shock whips the tahini into a pale emulsion the way no amount of blending cold ingredients can. The baking-soda simmer matters too: it raises the pH so the chickpeas' pectin dissolves and they blend to silk instead of grit, shedding their skins as a bonus. Be generous with the tahini; hummus bi tahina is a sesame dish wearing a chickpea coat, and the timid-tahini version is why homemade so often disappoints.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","bright","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"dish":{"label":"Middle Eats’ silkiest hummus","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_1KYLNtXNEs","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hummus%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hummus"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Hummus","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Hummus","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"paprika-and-pimenton","term":"Paprika & Pimentón"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Lemon","Chickpeas","Tahini","Cumin","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"falafel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g DRIED chickpeas, soaked 18h (never canned)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 5 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Big bunches parsley and cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp each cumin, coriander; chili"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder, 2 tbsp flour if needed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying; tahini sauce, pita, pickles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grind soaked (uncooked!) chickpeas with everything to coarse couscous texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 30 min; stir in baking powder.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Form balls; fry at 175°C until deep brown, ~4 min.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stuff into pita with salad, pickles, tahini.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Canned chickpeas make falafel that explodes into the oil; dried-and-soaked is a law, not a preference, because cooked chickpeas carry water that flashes to steam inside the crust. The soaked-raw grind should look like coarse couscous and hold when squeezed. Baking powder goes in at the last minute for lift. Fry at a true 175°C: cooler and they drink oil, hotter and the crust browns before the raw chickpea center cooks. Egypt and the Levant both claim the invention; the argument is older than the fryer.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Falafel","Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","nutty-toasted","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Falafel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=falafel%20frying%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Falafel%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Falafel"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Falafel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Falafel","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Cilantro","Parsley","Bread","Flour","Chickpeas","Tahini","Pickles","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"shakshuka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 1 red pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp each cumin and paprika, pinch cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Feta, cilantro, crusty bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soften onion and pepper 10 min; add garlic and spices.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add tomatoes; simmer 10 min until thick enough to hold a spoon-well.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack eggs into wells; cover and cook 6–8 min to runny yolks.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Feta, herbs, bread. Serve in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Thicken the sauce before the eggs go in: eggs poached in watery sauce turn the whole pan to soup, because they release moisture as they set. The spoon-well test is the gate: if a crater holds its shape, the sauce is ready to cradle. Cover the pan so the whites steam while the yolks stay molten, and pull it a minute early; the pan keeps cooking at the table, which is where this dish belongs, eaten straight from the skillet with bread doing the work of cutlery.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","briny","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a briny, maritime salinity, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shakshuka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shakshuka"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Shakshuka","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Shakshuka","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"feta","term":"Feta"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Cilantro","Cheese (any)","Feta","Bread","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"oven-chicken-shawarma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g chicken thighs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade: 4 tbsp olive oil, lemon, 6 garlic cloves, 2 tsp each cumin and paprika, 1 tsp turmeric, ½ tsp cinnamon, pinch allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pita, tahini or garlic sauce (toum), pickles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate 1–24 h.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Roast at 220°C with the onions, 30–35 min, until charred at the edges.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest, slice thin, then crisp the slices in the pan juices under the broiler 3 min.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile into pita with sauce and pickles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The slice-then-broil step fakes the rotating spit: a real shawarma cone crisps only at its carved surface, and re-crisping your slices in the pan juices recreates exactly that maximum-edge-per-bite economy. The marinade's warm spices (cinnamon, allspice, turmeric) are what tell your nose this is shawarma and not just roast chicken. Thighs only: the spit was never a lean-meat machine, and breast meat leaves the broiler as chalk.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","The broiler: heat from above","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","charred","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, char used as punctuation, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oven%20Chicken%20Shawarma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oven Chicken Shawarma"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Oven Chicken Shawarma","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Oven%20Chicken%20Shawarma","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Lemon","Bread","Tahini","Pickles","Cumin","Paprika","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"tabbouleh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 big bunches flat parsley, chopped fine and dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp fine bulgur, soaked in the tomato juices"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 ripe tomatoes, small dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mint, scallion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon juice, olive oil, salt, pinch allspice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash and DRY the parsley completely before chopping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dice tomatoes; let bulgur soak in their juice 15 min, no cooking.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop parsley with a sharp knife; bruised parsley turns to lawn clippings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine; dress sharply with lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Real tabbouleh is a parsley salad with bulgur in it, not the other way around: the Lebanese original counts the grain by spoonfuls. Two rules protect it: the parsley must be bone-dry before chopping (wet leaves bruise into paste), and the knife must be sharp, because crushed parsley tastes like lawn clippings. The bulgur never cooks; it swells in tomato juice and lemon, staying nubbly. Dress it sharper than feels polite: this salad is the acid on a rich table.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tabbouleh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tabbouleh"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Tabbouleh","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Tabbouleh","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pressure-and-slow-cooking","term":"Pressure & Slow Cooking"}],"pantryItems":["Scallion","Tomato","Lemon","Parsley","Mint","Couscous/Bulgur","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"fattoush","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 pitas, torn and fried or toasted crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"Romaine, cucumber, tomatoes, radish, scallion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Purslane or mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dressing: lemon, olive oil, garlic, 2 tsp sumac"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pomegranate molasses (optional but transformative)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Crisp the pita in olive oil; salt it like chips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop vegetables chunky; herbs whole-leaf.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk dressing heavy on sumac and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss with pita at the last moment.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sumac is the identity of this salad (lemony, winey, brick-red), and substituting it out leaves a pleasant chopped salad that has lost its passport. The fried pita is the second signature: fattoush belongs to the fattat family of dishes built on repurposed bread, and the shards must be salted like chips and added at the last second so they crackle against the vegetables. Pomegranate molasses in the dressing is optional and transformative: sweet-sour depth for one spoonful of effort.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","sweet-edged","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fattoush%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fattoush"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Fattoush","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Fattoush","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sumac","term":"Sumac"},{"slug":"pomegranate-and-quince-autumns-perfume","term":"Pomegranate & Quince: Autumn's Perfume"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Cucumber","Lettuce/Greens","Radish/Turnip","Lemon","Pomegranate","Mint","Bread","Sugar","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"kofta-kebabs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground lamb or beef (20% fat)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated and squeezed dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley, mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp each cumin, coriander, allspice; ½ tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flat metal skewers ideal"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yogurt sauce, flatbread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead meat, onion, herbs, spices until tacky: the protein binds itself.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 30 min; form onto skewers, pressing finger-dents.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill or broil hot, 3–4 min per side.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve on flatbread with yogurt and sumac onions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Grate-and-squeeze the onion: flavor without the water that makes kofta slump off the skewer and fall through the grill. Kneading until tacky is the binder: the salt-dissolved proteins glue the meat to itself, no egg or breadcrumb needed. Twenty percent fat is structural; lean kofta is a meatloaf lollipop. The finger-dents aren't decoration either: they add surface for char and keep the cylinder gripping the flat skewer as it turns.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The broiler: heat from above","Salting to draw the water out","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","charred","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kofta%20Kebabs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kofta Kebabs"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Kofta Kebabs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Kofta%20Kebabs","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lamb-primals","term":"Lamb Primals"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Ground meat","Onion","Parsley","Mint","Yogurt","Cumin","Cinnamon","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"baba-ghanoush","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 large eggplants"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp tahini"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pomegranate seeds, parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Burn the eggplants (whole, directly on flame or under broiler) until collapsed and blackened all over, ~20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain the flesh in a colander 15 min; discard the bitter liquid.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop-and-stir with tahini, lemon, garlic. Fork, not blender.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pool olive oil on top; jewel with pomegranate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The smoke IS the recipe: un-charred eggplant makes tahini-flavored baby food. Burn the eggplants whole until fully collapsed; the blackened skin perfumes the flesh through and through, which is the entire flavor architecture of the dish. Draining the flesh afterward discards the bitter, watery liquid that would dilute everything. Then a fork, not a blender: baba ghanoush wants strands and texture, and a machine purées away the evidence that a fire was ever involved.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","The broiler: heat from above"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","nutty-toasted","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baba%20Ghanoush%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baba Ghanoush"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Baba Ghanoush","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Baba%20Ghanoush","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sesame-seeds-tahini-and-beyond","term":"Sesame: Seeds, Tahini & Beyond"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Eggplant","Lemon","Pomegranate","Parsley","Tahini","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"mujadara","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g green or brown lentils"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 large onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lots of olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yogurt, chopped salad"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry onions low and slow in generous oil until deep mahogany and half-crisp, 25 min. Reserve half.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer lentils 12 min; add rice, cumin, water to cover by 2cm.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in half the onions; cook covered 15 min, rest 10.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crown with the crispy onions and cold yogurt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This peasant dish lives or dies on onion patience: mahogany, not blond, because those twenty-five slow minutes are the only place its depth can come from; lentils and rice bring bulk, the onions bring the cuisine. Half get folded through, half go on top still crisp, and the contrast is the dish. Mujadara is medieval (it appears in Arabic cookbooks from the 13th century), and it remains the proof that three cheap ingredients plus discipline outrank ten expensive ones without it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Caramelizing onions","Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mujadara%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mujadara"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Mujadara","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Mujadara","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Yogurt","Rice","Lentils","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"quick-baklava-rolls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 pack phyllo, thawed properly"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g walnuts or pistachios, ground with cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Syrup: 200g sugar, 150ml water, honey, lemon, made FIRST and cooled"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter 2 sheets, scatter nuts, roll around a dowel or loosely by hand; scrunch into the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Repeat until the pan is full; butter the tops; slice now, not later.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 170°C, 40 min, to deep gold.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour COLD syrup over HOT pastry: listen to it crackle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold syrup over hot pastry equals crisp; hot syrup over hot pastry equals soggy: it's physics, not opinion, which is why the syrup is made first and cooled completely. Slice before baking, never after; baked phyllo shatters under a knife. Keep the unused sheets under a barely-damp towel as you work, because phyllo dries to tissue paper in minutes. The crackle when the syrup pours is the dish telling you the sequence was right.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Handling phyllo"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Handling phyllo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=working%20with%20phyllo%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Quick%20Baklava%20Rolls%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Quick Baklava Rolls"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Quick Baklava Rolls","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Quick%20Baklava%20Rolls","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Butter","Bread","Phyllo/Puff pastry","Honey","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"]},{"slug":"tortilla-espanola","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g waxy potatoes, sliced 3mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml olive oil (reused after)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach, not fry, potatoes and onion in the oil over medium-low until tender, 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain well; fold into beaten, well-salted eggs; rest 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook in a nonstick pan over medium 4 min; plate-flip; 3 min more.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":660,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve jiggly in the center, at room temperature, with pride.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The plate-flip is a rite of passage: commit fully, hesitation is how tortillas hit the floor. Everything before it is oil management: the potatoes poach, never fry, going tender without color, and the ten-minute rest in the egg lets starch and custard agree before the pan. Spain's eternal argument is onion or no onion (con cebolla wins in most kitchens, including this one), but the jiggly center is not up for debate: sobremesa-warm, not hot, is how it's served everywhere from Cádiz to Bilbao.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tortilla%20Espa%C3%B1ola%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tortilla Española"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Tortilla Española","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Tortilla%20Espa%C3%B1ola","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"starchy-vs-waxy-potatoes","term":"Starchy vs. Waxy Potatoes"},{"slug":"nonstick-honestly","term":"Nonstick, Honestly"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Potato","Bread"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"weeknight-paella","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"320g bomba or calasparra rice (arborio in a pinch)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L hot chicken stock + saffron"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chicken thighs, chorizo optional (heresy but delicious), shrimp"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sofrito: onion, garlic, grated tomato, 1 tsp pimentón"},{"kind":"item","text":"Widest shallowest pan you own"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown chicken; make the sofrito in the same pan until jammy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add rice; toast 1 min. Add all the hot saffron stock. NOW STOP STIRRING.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 18 min, rotating the pan; add shrimp for the last 5.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crank the heat 90 seconds for socarrat, the crackling bottom crust. Rest 5 min under a towel.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Stirring makes risotto; not stirring makes paella: the socarrat, that crackling caramelized bottom crust, only forms on rice left alone against hot metal, and it is the reward for restraint. Bomba rice matters because it drinks three times its volume without bursting. The chorizo is heresy by Valencian law and delicious by every other jurisdiction; commit either way. Rotate the pan for even heat, listen for the faint crackle in the last ninety seconds, and rest it under a towel before serving.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Paella & socarrat","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","smoky","sweet-edged","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, wood-smoke running underneath, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Paella & socarrat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=paella%20socarrat%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Risotto method","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=risotto%20technique%20mantecatura","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Weeknight%20Paella%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Weeknight Paella"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Weeknight Paella","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Weeknight%20Paella","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chorizo-spanish-vs-mexican","term":"Chorizo: Spanish vs. Mexican"},{"slug":"mirepoix-trinity-sofrito-and-friends","term":"Mirepoix, Trinity, Sofrito & Friends"},{"slug":"paprika-and-pimenton","term":"Paprika & Pimentón"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Shrimp","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Rice","Stock/Broth","Paprika","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"gambas-al-ajillo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g shrimp, peeled, salted 10 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 garlic cloves, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried guindilla or chile de árbol"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sherry or brandy splash, parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bread. Mandatory."}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm oil, garlic, and chili from cold: the garlic should toast slowly to pale gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the heat; shrimp in a single layer, 45 seconds a side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Splash of sherry; parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve bubbling with bread for the oil, which is the actual point.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Starting garlic in cold oil is how you get eight cloves of flavor with zero bitterness: the slow climb toasts it evenly to pale gold, while garlic dropped into hot oil goes from raw to acrid in seconds. The shrimp want less time than seems reasonable: forty-five seconds a side in oil that hot. But the true product of this tapa is the oil itself, garlicky, chili-warmed, sherry-splashed: the bread isn't a side, it's the second course.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","herb-fresh","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gambas%20al%20Ajillo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gambas al Ajillo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Gambas al Ajillo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Gambas%20al%20Ajillo","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-sherry-spectrum","term":"The Sherry Spectrum"},{"slug":"chile-flavor-beyond-heat","term":"Chile Flavor Beyond Heat"},{"slug":"brandy-cognac-armagnac-calvados-and-pisco","term":"Brandy: Cognac, Armagnac, Calvados & Pisco"},{"slug":"chile-fluency-fresh-and-dried","term":"Chile Fluency (Fresh & Dried)"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Chiles","Parsley","Bread","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"patatas-bravas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g potatoes, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Brava sauce: olive oil, 2 tsp pimentón (mix sweet+hot), 1 tbsp flour, 250ml stock, splash vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional allioli: garlic + oil emulsion or garlicky mayo"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer potatoes 8 min; dry completely; chill if you have time.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry twice: 150°C until tender, then 190°C until shattering.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brava: bloom pimentón in oil, stir in flour, whisk in stock until it coats a spoon; vinegar to finish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauce over, never under: the crunch must survive.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Double-frying is the same physics as great French fries: the first fry cooks the interior at low temperature, the second builds the shattering crust at high, and chilling between the two dries the surface so it crisps harder. The brava sauce is pimentón's showcase, sweet and hot paprika bloomed in oil; tomato-based versions are a Madrid-vs-everywhere argument, but the vinegar tang is universal. Sauce over, never under: drowned potatoes surrender the crunch you just engineered twice.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Building an emulsion"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","bright","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Patatas%20Bravas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Patatas Bravas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Patatas Bravas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Patatas%20Bravas","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Potato","Flour","Vinegar","Stock/Broth","Mayonnaise","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"gazpacho","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg dead-ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber, ½ green pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g stale bread, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml good olive oil, 2 tbsp sherry vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend vegetables, bread, vinegar until totally smooth, minutes, not seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With the motor running, stream in the oil; watch it turn pale coral and creamy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 2 hours; adjust salt and vinegar cold (cold mutes both).","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve in a glass. It's a drink that ate a salad.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The slow oil stream is what separates gazpacho from cold salsa: blended in gradually with the motor running, the oil emulsifies and the soup turns pale coral, creamy without a drop of cream. Stale bread is the ancient thickener; gazpacho descends from Roman and Moorish bread-and-vinegar soups that met the tomato only after the Columbian exchange. Season it again once cold: chill mutes both salt and acid, and a gazpacho seasoned warm tastes flat from the refrigerator.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gazpacho%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gazpacho"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Gazpacho","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Gazpacho","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Cucumber","Bread","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"pan-con-tomate","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Good crusty bread, thick slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dead-ripe tomatoes, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"Best olive oil you own"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grill or toast the bread hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ghost the garlic clove across the hot surface once or twice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the tomato halves on a box grater; discard skins. Spoon over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil generously; salt; eat immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Four ingredients means zero hiding places: this dish is a quality test for all of them, which is why Catalonia treats it as a barometer of any kitchen. The box-grater move is the technique worth stealing: grated dead-ripe tomato gives you pure pulp and leaves the skin behind in your hand, no blanching, no peeling. Ghost the garlic across the hot toast once, not five times; pa amb tomàquet whispers garlic where lesser breads shout it.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","briny","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pan%20con%20Tomate%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pan con Tomate"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Pan con Tomate","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Pan%20con%20Tomate","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Bread"]},{"slug":"albondigas-in-salsa-espanola","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef+pork mix"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bread soaked in milk, 1 egg, garlic, parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour for rolling"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: onion, carrot, garlic, 150ml white wine, 400ml stock, saffron or pimentón"},{"kind":"item","text":"Almond picada (optional): ground almonds + fried bread + garlic"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix meatballs gently; roll in flour; brown, don't cook through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build the sauce in the same pan; blend it smooth if you like.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer meatballs in the sauce 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Thicken with the picada, Spain's ancient answer to cornstarch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The flour coating browns into the fond that becomes your sauce: nothing is wasted; the meatballs are rolled in flour, seared, and the crust they leave on the pan is the sauce's foundation. Brown, don't cook through: they finish their cooking in the simmer, staying juicy. The almond picada at the end is Spain's ancient answer to cornstarch: ground almonds, fried bread and garlic pounded together, thickening the sauce while seasoning it, a technique running straight back to medieval Catalan cooking.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Searing: the hard crust","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","smoky","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alb%C3%B3ndigas%20in%20Salsa%20Espa%C3%B1ola%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Albóndigas in Salsa Española"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Albóndigas in Salsa Española","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Alb%C3%B3ndigas%20in%20Salsa%20Espa%C3%B1ola","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Parsley","Milk","Bread","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Nuts","Paprika","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"pollo-al-ajillo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut in small pieces (or 8 thighs)"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 garlic cloves, smashed, skins on"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml dry sherry or white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bay, rosemary or thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil, salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown chicken hard in olive oil with the garlic cloves loitering around it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"When garlic is bronzed and chicken golden, add sherry and herbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered 20 min, turning, until the sauce is a garlicky glaze.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve with bread and the confit garlic to squeeze out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Skin-on smashed garlic perfumes without burning: naked minced garlic would be charcoal by minute five of the chicken's browning, but whole smashed cloves in their jackets toast gently into sweet confit you squeeze out at the table like treasure. The sherry deglaze is Andalusia in a splash: it lifts the fond and reduces with the chicken's juices into a garlicky glaze rather than a sauce. Small pieces, hard sear, bread on the table: this is Spanish grandmother food with nothing to prove.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Confit"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","briny","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pollo%20al%20Ajillo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pollo al Ajillo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Pollo al Ajillo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Pollo%20al%20Ajillo","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Thyme","Rosemary","Bay leaf","Wine","Clove"]},{"slug":"croquetas-de-jamon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"80g butter, 100g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"800ml whole milk, warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g jamón or ham, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nutmeg, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour, 2 eggs, panko for breading"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a blond roux; whisk in milk gradually into a THICK béchamel, 15 min of stirring.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in ham; spread in a tray, press film onto the surface; chill 4h+.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape logs; bread twice (egg, panko, egg, panko).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 185°C in small batches until deep gold, ~2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"The béchamel should be almost too thick to stir: it loosens into molten cream inside the crust, which is the entire trick of a croqueta: a sauce impersonating a solid just long enough to be fried. The fifteen minutes of stirring is where the flour taste cooks out. Double-breading builds a wall that holds the lava in, and small batches keep the oil at temperature: a crowded pot cools, and cool oil is how croquetas burst. Jamón scraps are the classic economy; the dish exists to waste nothing.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Béchamel: the mother sauce","Making a roux","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Béchamel: the mother sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bechamel%20mother%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Croquetas%20de%20Jam%C3%B3n%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Croquetas de Jamón"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Croquetas de Jamón","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Croquetas%20de%20Jam%C3%B3n","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"jamon-iberico-and-the-bellota-grades","term":"Jamón Ibérico (and the Bellota Grades)"},{"slug":"roux-slurry-and-liaison","term":"Roux, Slurry & Liaison"},{"slug":"nutmeg-and-mace","term":"Nutmeg & Mace"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"crema-catalana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon peel, orange peel, cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 egg yolks, 100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sugar for the burnt crust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Infuse milk with citrus peel and cinnamon 15 min; strain.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk yolks, sugar, cornstarch; temper in milk; return to the stove.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir until it thickens to loose pudding: first bubble, then off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill in shallow dishes; burn a sugar crust with a torch or screaming-hot spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Crème brûlée's older cousin, stovetop, citrus-and-cinnamon, done in half the time with no oven and no water bath, and Catalonia will tell you which came first. Cornstarch is the safety net that lets it thicken over direct flame where the French custard would curdle: first bubble, then straight off the heat. The infusion is the identity (lemon peel, orange peel, cinnamon), so give it its full fifteen minutes. Traditionally burnt with a hot iron on Saint Joseph's Day; a torch carries no penalty.","equipment":["Torch"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","The brûlée torch"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","bright","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The brûlée torch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=creme%20brulee%20torch%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Crema%20Catalana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Crema Catalana"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Crema Catalana","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Crema%20Catalana","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-kitchen-torch","term":"The Kitchen Torch"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Orange","Milk","Sour cream/Crema","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"horiatiki-greek-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 ripe tomatoes, wedged"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber, thick half-moons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, rings; ½ red onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Kalamata olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 slab feta, never crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oregano, red wine vinegar, lots of olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chunk everything rustic; no lettuce, ever.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the tomatoes first and let them weep a little.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress with vinegar and a fearless pour of oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the feta slab on top whole; dust with oregano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The tomato juice, oil and vinegar pooling at the bottom is the sauce: bread goes in it, and that's the meal; Greeks call the ritual papara and consider the bowl unfinished until it's wiped. No lettuce, ever: horiatiki means 'village-style', and the village didn't grow lettuce for salads. Salting the tomatoes first starts the weeping that builds the pool. The feta rides on top as a slab because crumbling it is how you tell a tourist kitchen from a Greek one.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salting to draw the water out","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Horiatiki%20(Greek%20Salad)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Horiatiki (Greek Salad)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Horiatiki (Greek Salad)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Horiatiki%20(Greek%20Salad)","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"feta","term":"Feta"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Cucumber","Olives","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Feta","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"tzatziki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g full-fat Greek yogurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber, grated, salted, and strangled dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp olive oil, splash red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dill or mint"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the grated cucumber 10 min, then squeeze in a towel until it gives up.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold everything into the yogurt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 30 min: the garlic needs time to bloom.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Trench of olive oil on top to serve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Watery tzatziki is a squeezing failure, never a recipe failure: cucumber is 96% water and every gram you don't wring out ends up loosening the yogurt within the hour. Salt the gratings first so osmosis does half the strangling for you. The thirty-minute rest matters because raw grated garlic needs time to mellow and distribute; tzatziki eaten immediately bites, rested it hums. Full-fat yogurt only: this sauce's whole job is richness against char.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tzatziki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tzatziki"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Tzatziki","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Tzatziki","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Cucumber","Mint","Dill","Yogurt","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"chicken-souvlaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g chicken thigh, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade: olive oil, lemon, garlic, 2 tsp oregano, 1 tsp honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pita, tzatziki, tomato, onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Skewers, soaked if wooden"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate 30 min–8 h.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":30600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Thread snugly; grill or broil hot, turning, ~12 min, until charred at the corners.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Warm pitas over the same flame.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build: pita, tzatziki, meat, tomato, onion, more oregano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oregano, lemon and char is the entire flavor thesis of the Greek grill, and the honey's job is helping the char along: its sugars caramelize at the corners faster than the meat alone would. Thighs, threaded snugly so they baste each other. Souvlaki is Greece's oldest fast food by an absurd margin: skewer-and-firedog sets survive from Bronze Age Santorini, seventeen centuries before anyone wrote a cookbook. Warm the pita over the same flame; it should smell faintly of the fire.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["The broiler: heat from above","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","sweet-edged","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, a sweet edge rounding the corners, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Souvlaki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Souvlaki"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Chicken Souvlaki","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Chicken%20Souvlaki","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Lemon","Oregano","Bread","Honey"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"moussaka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggplants + 2 potatoes, sliced, roasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ragù: 600g lamb or beef, onion, garlic, tomato, red wine, cinnamon stick, allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Béchamel: 80g butter, 80g flour, 800ml milk, nutmeg, 2 yolks, grated cheese"}],"steps":[{"text":"Roast the sliced vegetables until golden instead of frying: lighter, faster.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the ragù until thick and jammy; cinnamon is the signature, don't fear it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a thick béchamel; enrich with yolks and cheese off heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer potato, eggplant, ragù, béchamel deep on top. Bake 180°C, 45 min. REST 30 MIN.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":true}],"note":"Moussaka cut hot is soup: the thirty-minute rest is written in stone on an island somewhere, and it's the same physics as lasagne: molten béchamel and juicy ragù need time to set back into layers. The cinnamon stick in the meat is the signature; Greek cooking spices its meats the way others spice desserts, and this is the dish where you learn to stop flinching. Roasting the eggplant instead of frying isn't just lighter: fried eggplant brings oil the béchamel doesn't need.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Béchamel: the mother sauce","Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Béchamel: the mother sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bechamel%20mother%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moussaka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moussaka"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Moussaka","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Moussaka","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lamb-primals","term":"Lamb Primals"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Eggplant","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Flour","Wine","Cinnamon","Nutmeg","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"spanakopita","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g spinach, wilted and squeezed bone-dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g feta, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 scallions, dill by the fistful"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pack phyllo, 150g melted butter or olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix filling; it should be dry enough to mound.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer half the phyllo sheets in a buttered pan, brushing each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Filling in; layer remaining sheets, brushing each. Score the top now.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 180°C, 45 min, to deep gold. Cool 15 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Wet spinach is the enemy of crisp phyllo: squeeze, then squeeze again, then once more, because 500 grams of spinach hides a coffee cup of water and every drop steams the pastry from within. Score the top before baking; afterward the sheets shatter under the knife. Brushing every layer is what builds the shatter: each film of fat fries its sheet in place. The feta-dill-scallion trinity is fixed; the dish forgives nothing missing and needs nothing more.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Handling phyllo","Scoring","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","briny","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a briny, maritime salinity, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Handling phyllo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=working%20with%20phyllo%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Spanakopita%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Spanakopita"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Spanakopita","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Spanakopita","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Scallion","Spinach","Dill","Butter","Cheese (any)","Feta","Phyllo/Puff pastry"],"judgedBy":["scoring","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"avgolemono","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L good chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g orzo or rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cups shredded chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs + juice of 2 lemons"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dill, pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook orzo in the stock; add chicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk eggs and lemon until frothy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Temper: whisk hot broth into the eggs one ladle at a time, then pour back off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm gently, never boil again, until it turns velvet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tempering is the technique; the reward is a soup thickened by nothing but egg, lemon and nerve. Whisk the hot broth into the eggs one ladle at a time, patience here is the entire recipe, then return it off the heat and never let it boil again, because boiled avgolemono is lemon-scented egg-drop soup. Done right it turns velvet, pale as ivory. The sauce version of the same trick dresses dolmades and artichokes; learn it once, use it across the whole cuisine.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Rolling dolmas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20roll%20dolmas%20grape%20leaves","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Avgolemono%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Avgolemono"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Avgolemono","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Avgolemono","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Lemon","Dill","Rice","Pasta","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"briam","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 zucchini, 2 eggplants, 4 potatoes, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tomatoes, grated, + 2 sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml olive oil (yes, really)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oregano, parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss everything with the grated tomato, oil, and seasoning.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer or jumble into a baking dish; sliced tomato on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 190°C for 60–75 min, until collapsed, blistered, and syrupy at the edges.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Room temperature with feta and bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Greek vegetable dishes use oil as an ingredient, not a coating; lathera means 'the oily ones', and briam leans in with 150ml of it; that oil emulsifies with the grated tomato and vegetable juices into the syrupy pan sauce that IS the dish. The long bake is deliberate collapse: you want blistered edges and vegetables that have surrendered their borders to each other. Room temperature is the serving law, with feta and bread: hot briam hasn't finished becoming itself.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","charred","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, char used as punctuation, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Briam%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Briam"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Briam","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Briam","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Zucchini","Eggplant","Parsley","Oregano"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"greek-lemon-potatoes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg potatoes, thick wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml stock or water"},{"kind":"item","text":"80ml olive oil, juice of 2 lemons"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, 2 tsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dijon ½ tsp (secret binder)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the liquid, oil, lemon, mustard; toss with potatoes in a roasting pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast 200°C for 40 min in the braise.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Liquid gone? Now they fry in the leftover oil: 20 more minutes, turning once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"They should be creamy inside, bronzed and lemon-lacquered outside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Braise-then-roast is the trick: the potatoes drink the lemon-stock first, going creamy and seasoned to the center, then fry in the oil that remains once the liquid is gone. Twenty more minutes for the bronzed, lacquered outside. The half-teaspoon of Dijon is the quiet binder holding lemon and oil together in the pan. Crowding is the failure mode: wedges stacked two-deep steam forever and never reach the frying phase.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Braising","Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Greek%20Lemon%20Potatoes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Greek Lemon Potatoes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Greek Lemon Potatoes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Greek%20Lemon%20Potatoes","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"riesling-the-sommeliers-secret-weapon","term":"Riesling: The Sommelier's Secret Weapon"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Potato","Lemon","Oregano","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"keftedes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground beef or lamb"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated; 2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Stale bread soaked in milk (or 3 tbsp breadcrumbs)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Big handfuls mint and parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, oregano, cumin pinch"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour, oil for frying; lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead everything; rest the mix 30 min in the fridge.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll walnut-size; dust in flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shallow-fry in olive oil until crusty and deep brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lemon over the top. Serve with tzatziki.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mint is what makes them Greek: skip it and you've made good meatballs from nowhere in particular. The milk-soaked bread is a panade, holding moisture through the fry the way breadcrumbs alone can't, and the half-hour chill firms the mix so the balls hold their shape in the oil. Shallow-fried in olive oil to a deep crust, hit with lemon while hot; keftedes are mezze, built to be eaten by the handful an hour later at room temperature, and they're arguably better that way.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","earthy","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Keftedes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Keftedes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Keftedes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Keftedes","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"deep-and-shallow-frying","term":"Deep & Shallow Frying"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Lemon","Parsley","Mint","Oregano","Milk","Bread","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"bougiourdi-baked-feta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 slab feta"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"½ green chili or pinch chili flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oregano, olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Foil or a small clay dish; bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Layer tomato, feta, chili in a small dish; oil and oregano over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 200°C for 12–15 min until slumped and bubbling at the edges.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Optional 1-min broil for color.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Attack with bread while molten.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Feta doesn't melt like mozzarella: it softens to a spreadable cloud, and that's the texture you want; brined sheep's-milk cheese holds its structure where stretched-curd cheeses run. Bougiourdi is northern Greek taverna food, Thessaloniki's answer to the question of what to do with feta, a tomato and fifteen minutes. The chili is not optional: the name roughly promises a kick. Attack it molten, with bread; it stiffens as it cools and the moment passes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The broiler: heat from above"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","gently hot","rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, a warm current of chile, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bougiourdi%20(Baked%20Feta)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bougiourdi (Baked Feta)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Bougiourdi (Baked Feta)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Bougiourdi%20(Baked%20Feta)","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"feta","term":"Feta"},{"slug":"clay-and-earthenware-tagine-donabe-cazuela","term":"Clay & Earthenware: Tagine, Donabe, Cazuela"}],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Chiles","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Feta","Bread"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above"]},{"slug":"chicken-and-andouille-gumbo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120ml oil + 120g flour (the roux)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The trinity: 2 onions, 2 celery ribs, 1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g chicken thighs, 400g andouille, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cajun seasoning, bay, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions, filé powder optional, rice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a dark roux: whisk oil and flour over medium 30–45 min to milk-chocolate brown. Do not walk away.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Trinity into the hot roux: it will roar and stop the cooking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in stock gradually; add browned sausage and chicken; simmer 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Skim, season, scallions. Serve over rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The roux is a color, not a time: chocolate roux makes deep gumbo, blond roux makes étouffée, burnt roux means start over with no appeals; thirty-plus minutes of whisking with no walking away, because the difference between chocolate and burnt is about ninety seconds of inattention. The trinity going into the hot roux roars and stops the cooking at exactly the color you chose. Filé at the table, never in the pot. Gumbo is West African okra cooking, French roux and Choctaw filé in one bowl, Louisiana's whole story, simmered.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20%26%20Andouille%20Gumbo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken & Andouille Gumbo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Chicken & Andouille Gumbo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Chicken%20%26%20Andouille%20Gumbo","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tasso-and-cajun-andouille","term":"Tasso & Cajun Andouille"},{"slug":"sweetness-as-seasoning","term":"Sweetness as Seasoning"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Onion","Scallion","Celery","Bell pepper","Thyme","Bay leaf","Flour","Stock/Broth","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"red-beans-and-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried red kidney beans, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 smoked ham hock or 300g pickled pork/smoked sausage"},{"kind":"item","text":"The trinity + 4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bay, thyme, cayenne, black + white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Louisiana hot sauce; long-grain rice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the trinity; add garlic, seasoning, beans, hock, water to cover well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 2–3 hours until beans surrender.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mash a cupful against the pot wall to cream the gravy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve over rice with sliced sausage seared crisp and hot sauce on the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Monday food: it simmers while you live your life; the dish exists because Monday was wash day in New Orleans and the beans minded themselves while the laundry didn't. Creaminess comes from the mash, not from cream: a cupful of beans crushed against the pot wall thickens the gravy from inside. The smoked hock is the seasoning engine; give it the full simmer. Louis Armstrong signed his letters 'Red beans and ricely yours', which is all the endorsement a recipe ever needs.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Red%20Beans%20%26%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Red Beans & Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Red Beans & Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Red%20Beans%20%26%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ham-and-hock","term":"Ham & Hock"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Sausage","Ham/Prosciutto","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Chiles","Thyme","Bay leaf","Rice","Kidney/Pinto beans","Pickles","Hot sauce","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"chicken-and-sausage-jambalaya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g chicken thighs, 300g andouille"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cups long-grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"The trinity + garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"800ml stock (Cajun) + optional 200g crushed tomato (Creole)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cajun seasoning, bay, scallions"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown sausage, then chicken, hard: the crust on the pot bottom is the flavor bank.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Trinity in; scrape up every brown bit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rice, stock, seasoning; boil, then lid on, lowest heat, 25 min. NO PEEKING.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 10, fold from the bottom, scallions over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brown equals Cajun, red equals Creole: both are correct and both camps will tell you the other is wrong; the tomato line runs straight through Louisiana and this recipe lets you pick a side. The crust the sausage and chicken leave on the pot bottom is the flavor bank, and the trinity's job is to rob it. Then the rice discipline: lid on, lowest heat, twenty-five minutes, no peeking: every lift of the lid bleeds the steam the rice is counting on. Fold from the bottom; the best grains live there.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20%26%20Sausage%20Jambalaya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken & Sausage Jambalaya"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Chicken & Sausage Jambalaya","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Chicken%20%26%20Sausage%20Jambalaya","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tasso-and-cajun-andouille","term":"Tasso & Cajun Andouille"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Bay leaf","Rice","Stock/Broth","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"shrimp-etouffee","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g shrimp (make quick stock from the shells)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g butter + 60g flour (blond-to-peanut roux)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The trinity + garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml shrimp stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cajun seasoning, hot sauce, lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions, rice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer shells in water 15 min; strain. Free flavor.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter roux to peanut-butter color; trinity in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in stock to a gravy; season; simmer 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shrimp in for the final 3 minutes only. Over rice.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Étouffée means 'smothered': the shrimp poach in the gravy, so they go in last or they go in vain; three minutes in the smother is the whole of their cooking. The shell stock is fifteen minutes of free flavor most kitchens throw away. Blond-to-peanut roux, butter-based, is what separates étouffée from gumbo's dark oil roux: this is the gentler, richer end of the roux spectrum, crawfish country's Sunday dish translated to shrimp for the rest of the map.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shrimp%20%C3%89touff%C3%A9e%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shrimp Étouffée"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Shrimp Étouffée","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Shrimp%20%C3%89touff%C3%A9e","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Scallion","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Peanut","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux"]},{"slug":"dirty-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g chicken livers + 250g ground pork (or all pork)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cups cooked rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"The trinity + garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cajun seasoning, cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Stock splash, scallions, parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear the pork; add finely chopped livers and cook until deeply browned.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Trinity in the same fat; scrape the fond.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the rice with splashes of stock so it stains 'dirty'.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scallions and parsley heavy at the end.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The livers are the 'dirt': mineral depth people love until you tell them what it is, so tell them after. Browning them hard is what converts liver's tinny edge into something dark and savory; pale livers taste like the argument against the dish. Day-old rice folds without breaking. This is boucherie cooking: the Cajun hog-butchering tradition where nothing was wasted and the odd bits became the best plate on the table, which is exactly the lesson the dish still teaches.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dirty%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dirty Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Dirty Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Dirty%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Ground meat","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Scallion","Chiles","Parsley","Rice","Stock/Broth","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"shrimp-creole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g shrimp"},{"kind":"item","text":"The trinity + garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bay, thyme, cayenne, Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot sauce, lemon, scallions"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the trinity in butter; garlic and seasonings after.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tomatoes in; simmer 20 min to a bright, loose sauce.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Balance: hot sauce, Worcestershire, pinch of sugar if the tomatoes bite.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shrimp for 4 minutes at the end; over rice.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"No roux here: Creole cooking shows its French-Spanish-African braid with tomato instead, and the sauce stays bright and loose where gumbo goes dark and deep; that's the city-versus-country line in Louisiana cooking, drawn in a single pot. Simmer the tomatoes only twenty minutes: long enough to marry, short enough to stay vivid. The shrimp get four minutes at the end, full stop. A pinch of sugar is legal if the tomatoes bite back.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shrimp%20Creole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shrimp Creole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Shrimp Creole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Shrimp%20Creole","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Lemon","Thyme","Bay leaf","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"blackened-redfish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 redfish, drum, or snapper fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Blackening mix: 1 tbsp paprika, 2 tsp cayenne, thyme, oregano, garlic + onion powder, black + white pepper, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cast iron, outdoors or with the windows open"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat dry cast iron until it's genuinely frightening, 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Dip fillets in butter; pack the spice on both sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay in the pan: 2–3 min per side. It will smoke like a rite.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"More butter and lemon over the crust to serve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Paul Prudhomme's dish nearly wiped out the redfish it made famous: the 1980s craze hit the Gulf so hard that commercial redfishing had to be shut down, a recipe with its own conservation history. The 'blackening' is butter and spice charring on ferociously hot cast iron, not fish burning; the fillet under the crust stays sweet and just-cooked. Ten minutes of preheat is the honest minimum, and the smoke is biblical. Outdoors, or with every window open and the smoke alarm's battery in your pocket.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky","bright","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Blackened%20Redfish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Blackened Redfish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Blackened Redfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Blackened%20Redfish","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cast-iron","term":"Cast Iron"},{"slug":"copper-and-enameled-cast-iron","term":"Copper & Enameled Cast Iron"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Thyme","Oregano","Butter","Paprika"]},{"slug":"muffuletta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 round seeded muffuletta loaf or sturdy Italian bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive salad: green olives, giardiniera, celery, capers, garlic, oregano, olive oil: chopped, ideally rested overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mortadella, salami, ham"},{"kind":"item","text":"Provolone, mozzarella"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chop the olive salad fine; let it marinate as long as you can stand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split the loaf; spoon oil and salad onto BOTH cut faces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer meats and cheeses generously.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press under a weight 30 min; cut in quarters. Room temperature is correct.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pressing lets the olive oil soak the crumb: a fresh muffuletta is good, a pressed one is legendary, and the half-hour under a weight is what welds sandwich into monument. The olive salad is the actual recipe; the meats are its delivery system, and an overnight rest in the jar makes it sing. Born at Central Grocery in 1906, built by Sicilian immigrants around Sicilian bread. Room temperature is correct: cold clenches the oil and mutes the whole orchestra.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Muffuletta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Muffuletta"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Muffuletta","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Muffuletta","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"teliszalami-hungarian-winter-salami","term":"Téliszalámi (Hungarian Winter Salami)"},{"slug":"mozzarella-di-bufala-campana","term":"Mozzarella di Bufala Campana"},{"slug":"provolone-dolce-and-piccante","term":"Provolone (Dolce & Piccante)"},{"slug":"mortadella-bologna","term":"Mortadella Bologna"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Celery","Olives","Capers","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Bread","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"beignets","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml warm milk + 60ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, 60g sugar, 40g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g yeast, pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying; an avalanche of powdered sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a soft, slightly sticky dough; rise 1–2 h (or overnight cold, better).","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 1cm thick; cut rectangles; no second proof needed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 180°C, flipping, until puffed and gold, ~2 min per side.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bury in powdered sugar while hot. No restraint permitted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"They puff into pillows because of trapped gas layers in the rolled dough: fry immediately after cutting, no second proof, and the steam does the rest. The overnight cold rise is better than the warm one: slower fermentation, better flavor, and dough that rolls without a fight. Powdered sugar in an avalanche, no restraint permitted: the Café du Monde serving standard is 'geological'. Eat them hot; a cold beignet is just fried bread with regrets.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beignets%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beignets"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Beignets","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Beignets","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"bananas-foster","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 bananas, halved lengthwise"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g butter, 100g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"½ tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml dark rum + 30ml banana liqueur (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Vanilla ice cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt butter and sugar into a bubbling caramel; add cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bananas in, 1 min per side, basting.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off heat, add rum; return to flame and tilt to ignite (or just simmer 1 min, still delicious).","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Over cold ice cream immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born at Brennan's in 1951, when New Orleans was the port where the bananas landed and the restaurant needed a dessert for a customs official named Foster. If you flambé: long lighter, pan off the burner, nothing you love above the pan, and know that skipping the flame entirely still leaves caramel, rum and butter doing all the real work; the fire burns off showmanship, not flavor. One minute per side keeps the bananas intact. Cold ice cream is non-negotiable: the dish IS the temperature clash.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Flambé","Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Flambé","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=flambe%20technique%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bananas%20Foster%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bananas Foster"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Bananas Foster","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Bananas%20Foster","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Banana","Butter","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"kimchi-fried-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 cups day-old rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 cups sour, well-fermented kimchi, chopped + 3 tbsp of its juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g bacon or spam (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp gochujang, 1 tsp soy, sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fried egg per person, scallions, sesame, nori"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the pork; fry kimchi in the fat 3 min until its edges catch.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rice in; press and toss with gochujang and kimchi juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sesame oil off heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fried egg on top, yolk runny; scissors on the nori.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Old, sour kimchi is the good stuff for frying; fresh kimchi is for eating cold; fermentation past its crisp prime is exactly when the funk and acidity concentrate into something worth caramelizing. Frying it in the pork fat until the edges catch is where the flavor turns. The kimchi juice is the seasoning liquid, not a garnish: three tablespoons of it stain the rice through. Runny yolk on top, scissors on the nori, and Korea's definitive leftovers dish is complete.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment","briny","nutty-toasted","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it, a briny, maritime salinity, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":31,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"dish":{"label":"CCD: how to make any fried rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kimchi%20Fried%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kimchi Fried Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Kimchi Fried Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Kimchi%20Fried%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Scallion","Chiles","Cabbage","Rice","Soy sauce","Sesame","Curry paste","Seaweed/Nori","Kimchi"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"bulgogi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g ribeye or sirloin, shaved thin (freeze 1h to slice)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade: 4 tbsp soy, 2 tbsp sugar, 1 tbsp sesame oil, ½ grated Asian pear or apple, garlic, ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"Onion, scallion, mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice, lettuce leaves, ssamjang"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the shaved beef 30 min–4 h; the pear tenderizes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":16200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear in a blazing pan in thin batches so it caramelizes instead of boils.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear the onions and mushrooms in the drippings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve as ssam: lettuce, rice, meat, dab of ssamjang, one perfect bite.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Grated pear is Korea's ancient meat tenderizer: enzymes plus sweetness in one ingredient; the calpains in Asian pear soften the shaved beef while its sugar feeds the caramelization. Freeze the beef an hour first: firm meat shaves into the paper-thin slices the marinade needs. Then the crucial pan discipline: thin batches in a blazing pan, because crowded bulgogi boils in its own marinade and arrives gray. 'Fire meat' is the name; earn it.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bulgogi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bulgogi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Bulgogi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Bulgogi","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ribeye-prime-rib","term":"Ribeye / Prime Rib"},{"slug":"sirloin-tri-tip-and-picanha","term":"Sirloin, Tri-Tip & Picanha"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Mushrooms","Lettuce/Greens","Apple","Rice","Soy sauce","Sesame","Sugar"],"standard":{"slug":"bulgogi","marks":[{"id":"bulgogi#slices","text":"The slices are 1 to 2mm, thin enough to pass light before cooking, and they lie on the plate as loose separate ribbons rather than a fused clump."},{"id":"bulgogi#ribbon","text":"Each ribbon shows dark caramelized edges against a lighter centre, and the last batch is the same colour as the first: anything uniformly grey-brown never touched hot metal, and anything speckled with black flecks picked up burnt sugar left in the pan."},{"id":"bulgogi#finishes","text":"The pan finishes each batch nearly dry, a spoonful of syrupy drippings and no pool of simmering marinade."},{"id":"bulgogi#onion","text":"Onion and mushroom come out browned and collapsed but still holding shape, tasting of the drippings rather than of raw marinade."},{"id":"bulgogi#closed","text":"A closed ssam is bitten through in one go without the meat dragging out of the leaf, the slices parting under the teeth rather than needing to be chewed apart."}],"fault":"The bowl tipped into the pan, marinade and all, so the soy and sugar liquid boils and the beef poaches grey in it, leaving sweet sticky ribbons with no caramelization anywhere."}},{"slug":"bibimbap","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 bowls hot rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Namul: spinach, bean sprouts, carrots, mushrooms, zucchini; each seasoned separately with sesame oil, garlic, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g bulgogi beef or fried tofu"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fried eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 3 tbsp gochujang, 1 tbsp each sesame oil, sugar, water, vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch or sauté each vegetable alone; season each alone. This is the discipline of the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange spokes of color over the rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Egg in the center; sauce on the side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Destroy the arrangement completely before eating: mixing IS the dish (bibim = mix).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Season each namul alone: that is the discipline of the dish; vegetables cooked and dressed together taste beige, while separate seasoning keeps every spoke of the color wheel distinct until the moment of destruction. And destruction is the point: bibim means mix, and the arranged bowl is a photograph waiting to be eaten properly. For dolsot-style, rub a hot stone bowl with sesame oil and let the rice crackle into nurungji crust before anything else touches it: the crunch is half the dish's reputation.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","gently hot","umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":31,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bibimbap%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bibimbap"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Bibimbap","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Bibimbap","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"blanch-and-shock","term":"Blanch & Shock"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Tofu","Eggs","Garlic","Carrot","Chiles","Mushrooms","Spinach","Zucchini","Bean sprouts","Rice","Sesame","Vinegar","Sugar","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"japchae","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g sweet potato glass noodles (dangmyeon)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Spinach, carrots, onion, mushrooms, each cooked separately"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g beef strips (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 4 tbsp soy, 2 tbsp sugar, 2 tbsp sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sesame seeds, scallion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil noodles 6 min; rinse, snip a few times with scissors, toss with a little sauce.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook each vegetable and the beef separately, seasoning as you go.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine everything with the remaining sauce: hands in gloves work best.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve warm or room temp, glistening.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Separate sautéing keeps each color and texture distinct; japchae made in one pan tastes beige, because everything shares its water and nothing browns. The dangmyeon are the other half: sweet-potato starch noodles that stay glassy and chewy where wheat noodles would slump, snipped a few times so the dish serves without a wrestling match. Gloved hands are the honest mixing tool. Royal-court food originally, the banquet dish that became everyone's holiday standard.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","umami-deep","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Japchae%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Japchae"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Japchae","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Japchae","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"by-the-glass-economics-coravin-and-corkage","term":"By-the-Glass Economics, Coravin & Corkage"},{"slug":"sesame-seeds-tahini-and-beyond","term":"Sesame: Seeds, Tahini & Beyond"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Scallion","Potato","Sweet potato","Carrot","Mushrooms","Spinach","Noodles","Soy sauce","Sesame","Sugar"]},{"slug":"kimchi-jjigae","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 cups very ripe kimchi + juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g pork belly, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp gochujang, 1 tbsp gochugaru"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g tofu, thick slabs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions, onion, 500ml water or anchovy stock"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry pork belly until its fat runs; add kimchi and fry 5 min more.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add pastes, water, onion; boil hard 15 min: jjigae likes violence.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tofu slabs in gently for the last 5.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scallions; serve volcanic with rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The frying step turns sour kimchi's sharpness into depth: boiled-only jjigae tastes thin, because the pork fat and the hard fry mellow the acid into something rounder before the water ever arrives. Then jjigae likes violence: a hard boil, not a polite simmer, is what emulsifies the fat and pastes into the broth's body. Over-the-hill kimchi is the correct kimchi. Tofu slides in last and gently, to arrive at the table as slabs rather than rubble.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment","rich","herb-fresh","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":31,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"dish":{"label":"Maangchi’s tongbaechu kimchi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eTucCw1w6Ak","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kimchi%20Jjigae%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kimchi Jjigae"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Kimchi Jjigae","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Kimchi%20Jjigae","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pork-belly","term":"Pork Belly"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Anchovy","Tofu","Onion","Scallion","Chiles","Cabbage","Stock/Broth","Curry paste","Kimchi"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"korean-fried-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg chicken wings or boneless thigh chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dry brine: salt, pepper, ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"Batter: ½ cup potato starch + ¼ cup flour + ½ tsp baking powder + cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 3 tbsp gochujang, 2 tbsp honey, 1 tbsp each soy, rice vinegar; garlic, butter knob"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying, sesame"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss chicken in a thin, drippy batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry once at 165°C, 8 min; rest 5.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry again at 190°C until shattering, 4 min.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toss in the warmed sauce or serve it alongside to preserve maximum crunch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Potato starch plus the double fry is why Korean fried chicken stays crisp under sauce for an hour; starch crusts fry glassier than flour ones, and the two-stage fry (low to cook, rest, high to crust) drives out the moisture that would soften everything from inside. The thin, drippy batter is intentional: KFC's shell is a shatter, not a bread-crumb coat. Toss in sauce at the last moment, or serve it alongside and let each eater choose their own structural risk.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":31,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Korean%20Fried%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Korean Fried Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Korean Fried Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Korean%20Fried%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Ginger","Potato","Chiles","Butter","Flour","Soy sauce","Sesame","Vinegar","Honey","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"pajeon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 cup flour + ¼ cup potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cup ICE water, 1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Whole bunch of scallions, split lengthwise"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: shrimp, squid (haemul pajeon)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dip: soy + vinegar + gochugaru + sesame"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk a thin batter: lumps are fine, cold is critical.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay scallions in a hot oiled pan like lumber; pour batter over and around.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press seafood in; drizzle beaten egg at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip when deeply crisp; crisp side two. Cut with scissors.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ice water and minimal mixing equal crisp; warm water and enthusiasm equal pancake sadness: cold suppresses gluten development, and lumps in the batter are a feature. The scallions aren't in the pancake, they ARE the pancake: laid like lumber and merely held together by batter. Press, don't flood. The classic pairing is pajeon and makgeolli on a rainy day: Koreans will tell you the sizzle of the pan sounds like rain, and the tradition is older than the explanation.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","fiery","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, real fire that builds and stays, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":31,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pajeon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pajeon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Pajeon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Pajeon","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cephalopods-squid-octopus-cuttlefish","term":"Cephalopods (Squid, Octopus, Cuttlefish)"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Squid/Octopus","Eggs","Scallion","Potato","Chiles","Flour","Sesame","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"tteokbokki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g cylindrical rice cakes (soaked 20 min if refrigerated)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp gochujang, 1 tbsp gochugaru, 1 tbsp sugar, 1 tbsp soy"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml anchovy stock or water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fish cakes, cabbage, boiled eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions, sesame"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the pastes into the stock; bring to a boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rice cakes and fish cakes in; simmer 10–12 min, stirring so nothing welds to the pot.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"The sauce should reduce to a glossy, spicy lava that coats.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Eggs and scallions in the final minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The dish is done when the sauce clings; watery tteokbokki just means three more minutes of patience, because the rice cakes are shedding starch the whole time and the reduction finishes the job on its own schedule. Keep stirring the bottom; tteok weld themselves to a neglected pot. Anchovy stock under the gochujang is the street-cart depth most home versions miss. Royal-court tteokbokki was once a soy-braised dish; the fire-red version conquered Korea from Seoul's snack stalls in the 1950s.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","fiery","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":31,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tteokbokki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tteokbokki"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Tteokbokki","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Tteokbokki","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Eggs","Scallion","Chiles","Cabbage","Rice","Sesame","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"la-galbi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg flanken-cut beef short ribs (across the bone, thin)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade: 6 tbsp soy, 3 tbsp brown sugar, 2 tbsp mirin, ½ pear grated, garlic, ginger, sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions, sesame"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot grill or broiler"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse ribs briefly to shed bone dust; pat dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Marinate 4–24 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill over high flame 3–4 min per side until lacquered and charred at the edges.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Scissors into segments; wrap in lettuce or attack directly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Flanken cut means each slice owns three little bone medallions, and the meat nearest them is the prize, richer and chewier where it worked against the bone. The cut is named for Los Angeles, where Korean immigrant butchers ran short ribs through the bandsaw crosswise and invented a faster galbi that Korea then imported back. Rinse off the bone dust, marinate long, grill violently short: thin meat plus high flame is the whole timing model.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","The broiler: heat from above","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, a sweet edge rounding the corners, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=LA%20Galbi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of LA Galbi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on LA Galbi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20LA%20Galbi","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"short-ribs-english-vs-flanken","term":"Short Ribs (English vs. Flanken)"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Soy sauce","Sesame","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"doenjang-jjigae","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp doenjang (fermented soybean paste)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp gochugaru"},{"kind":"item","text":"Zucchini, potato, onion, mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g tofu"},{"kind":"item","text":"600ml anchovy-kelp stock (or water)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Green chili, scallion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve doenjang into the stock; add potato first, 5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Everything else except tofu; simmer 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tofu and chili last 3 min.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve bubbling with rice; this is Korea's daily default dinner.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Doenjang is Korea's miso but louder: funkier, chunkier, and it can take a real boil, unlike miso, whose delicacy dies at a simmer; that ruggedness is why this stew is Korea's daily default dinner, bubbling volcanic at the center of the table. Potato goes in first because it needs the head start. The anchovy-kelp stock underneath is quiet infrastructure: water works, but the stock is the difference between weeknight and someone's-grandmother.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","fiery","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":31,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Doenjang%20Jjigae%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Doenjang Jjigae"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Doenjang Jjigae","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Doenjang%20Jjigae","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Tofu","Onion","Scallion","Potato","Chiles","Mushrooms","Zucchini","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"quick-pho-ga","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L good chicken stock + 2 chicken breasts poached in it"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion + big knob ginger, charred over flame"},{"kind":"item","text":"Spices, toasted: 2 star anise, 1 cinnamon stick, 3 cloves, 1 tsp coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fish sauce, rock sugar or pinch sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flat rice noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"Herb plate: Thai basil, cilantro, lime, sprouts, chilies, hoisin, sriracha"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char onion and ginger until blackened; toast spices; add both to stock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 30 min; strain; season with fish sauce and sugar until it tastes complete alone.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook noodles separately; slice the poached chicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bowl: noodles, chicken, boiling broth over. Herbs at the table, each to their own.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Charring the onion and ginger is pho's signature: sweet smoke dissolved into the broth that no shortcut replaces; blacken them properly over open flame and don't apologize. Toast the spices until they wake. Then season the broth until it tastes complete ALONE, before it ever meets a noodle, because everything added later dilutes. The herb plate is the eater's half of the contract: pho arrives unfinished on purpose, and the table finishes it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","warm-spiced","aromatic","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, warm spice humming through the middle, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Quick%20Pho%20Ga%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Quick Pho Ga"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Quick Pho Ga","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Quick%20Pho%20Ga","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Ginger","Chiles","Lime","Cilantro","Basil","Noodles","Fish sauce","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Hot sauce","Coriander seed","Cinnamon","Clove","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"banh-mi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 light, crisp baguette rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grilled lemongrass pork or chicken (marinade: lemongrass, fish sauce, sugar, garlic, shallot)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Do chua: carrot + daikon, quick-pickled in vinegar-sugar-salt 30 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pâté (optional, traditional), mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cucumber spears, cilantro sprigs, sliced chili, Maggi or soy"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pickle the vegetables first: they need 30 minutes minimum.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill the marinated meat until caramelized at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the roll hollow-hot; paint with mayo (and pâté).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer meat, pickles, cucumber, whole cilantro sprigs, chilies, a shake of Maggi.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The architecture is contrast (warm against cool, rich against pickled, crisp against soft), and missing pickles collapses the whole thesis; the do chua's sharpness is the load-bearing wall. The bread is the other non-negotiable: a true banh mi roll is light and shatter-crusted, rice flour thinning the wheat, the colonial baguette rebuilt for the tropics. Toast it hollow-hot. Whole cilantro sprigs, stems and all; chopped cilantro is a different, lesser herb.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","gently hot","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Banh%20Mi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Banh Mi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Banh Mi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Banh%20Mi","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pate-and-terrine","term":"Pâté & Terrine"},{"slug":"pate-en-croute","term":"Pâté en Croûte"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Garlic","Shallot","Carrot","Chiles","Cucumber","Radish/Turnip","Cilantro","Lemongrass","Bread","Fish sauce","Vinegar","Mayonnaise","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"bun-thit-nuong-vietnamese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork shoulder, thin slices, marinated: lemongrass, fish sauce, sugar, garlic, shallot, sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g rice vermicelli, cooked and cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, cucumber, herbs (mint, perilla, cilantro), pickled carrot-daikon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crushed peanuts, fried shallots"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nuoc cham to drown it"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grill or sear the pork hard until the sugar chars, in batches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build bowls: lettuce and herbs at the bottom, noodles, pork on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peanuts and fried shallots over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Nuoc cham poured over everything at the table; toss like a salad.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"It's a noodle salad, not a noodle soup: cold noodles, hot meat, cool herbs, and the nuoc cham poured over at the table ties the room together; toss it like a salad and eat it like a race. The pork's marinade is engineered to burn a little: the sugar chars at the edges into the bitter-sweet crust that perfumes the whole bowl. Batches on the grill, never a crowd. The bowl is southern Vietnam's answer to lunch heat: everything about it works at ninety degrees in the shade.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","nutty-toasted","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bun%20Thit%20Nuong%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bun Thit Nuong"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Bun Thit Nuong","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Bun%20Thit%20Nuong","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Shallot","Carrot","Cucumber","Lettuce/Greens","Radish/Turnip","Cilantro","Mint","Lemongrass","Shiso","Noodles","Bread","Fish sauce","Peanut","Sesame","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"goi-cuon-summer-rolls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Rice paper rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cooked shrimp, halved lengthwise (and/or pork belly slices)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice vermicelli, cooked"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, mint, cilantro, chives"},{"kind":"item","text":"Peanut dip: hoisin + peanut butter + a little water, chili, crushed peanuts"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dip rice paper in warm water 2 seconds; it keeps softening after, so undershoot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay shrimp pink-side-down first, then herbs, lettuce, noodles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold sides, then roll tight like a burrito.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve seam-down with the peanut sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two-second dip: the paper should still feel slightly stiff when you start rolling, because it keeps softening from residual water and a fully soft sheet tears and welds to itself. Shrimp go pink-side-down first so the finished roll shows them off through the translucent skin; that window is the dish's signature. Roll tight, burrito-discipline. Goi cuon means 'salad roll', which is the honest name: this is a salad wearing rice paper, eaten for the dip as much as the roll.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","nutty-toasted","rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Goi%20Cuon%20(Summer%20Rolls)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Goi Cuon (Summer Rolls)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Goi Cuon (Summer Rolls)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Goi%20Cuon%20(Summer%20Rolls)","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Shrimp","Chiles","Lettuce/Greens","Cilantro","Mint","Chives","Butter","Noodles","Bread","Wrappers","Peanut"]},{"slug":"ca-kho-to-caramel-fish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g catfish or salmon steaks"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp sugar (for the caramel, nuoc mau)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Shallots, garlic, ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coconut water or plain water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper, chilies, scallion; clay pot ideal"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt sugar dry to a dark amber caramel, darker than feels safe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add fish sauce (stand back), aromatics, and a splash of coconut water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Nestle in the fish; simmer uncovered 20–25 min, spooning the sauce over.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish aggressive with black pepper. Rice is not optional.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bitter-edged caramel plus fish sauce is the backbone of Vietnamese kho (sweet, salty, smoky, profound), and the caramel (nuoc mau) must go darker than feels safe, to the edge of burnt, or the dish tastes like candy instead of dinner. Stand back when the fish sauce hits; the pan will shout. Clay pot preferred but not required. Spoon the glaze over as it reduces, finish aggressive with black pepper, and accept that rice is not optional; the sauce demands it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","umami-deep","tangy-ferment","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ca%20Kho%20To%20(Caramel%20Fish)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ca Kho To (Caramel Fish)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Ca Kho To (Caramel Fish)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Ca%20Kho%20To%20(Caramel%20Fish)","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"clay-and-earthenware-tagine-donabe-cazuela","term":"Clay & Earthenware: Tagine, Donabe, Cazuela"},{"slug":"wild-salmon-the-run-calendar","term":"Wild Salmon: The Run Calendar"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","White fish","Salmon","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Shallot","Chiles","Coconut","Fish sauce","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"lemongrass-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken thighs, bite-size"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 stalks lemongrass, tender parts minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp fish sauce, 1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic, shallot, chili"},{"kind":"item","text":"Turmeric ½ tsp (optional, for the golden version)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate chicken 15 min in everything.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sear in a hot pan without crowding until the edges caramelize.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add a splash of water to glaze; reduce until sticky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Over rice with cucumber and extra chili.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mince the lemongrass like you're angry at it: big pieces are woody splinters, fine mince is pure perfume, and only the pale tender third of the stalk is worth the knife. The fish-sauce-and-sugar marinade is a caramelization kit: the sear should build sticky charred edges, so don't crowd the pan. The final splash of water deglazes those sugars into a glaze rather than leaving them on the pan. Fifteen minutes of marinade genuinely suffices; lemongrass works fast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","sweet-edged","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a sweet edge rounding the corners, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lemongrass%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lemongrass Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Lemongrass Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Lemongrass%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-negroni-and-equal-parts-architecture","term":"The Negroni & Equal-Parts Architecture"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Shallot","Chiles","Lemongrass","Fish sauce","Sugar","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"nuoc-cham","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 tbsp water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic, minced; bird's eye chili; shreds of carrot"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve sugar in warm water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add fish sauce and lime; adjust until no single note dominates.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Garlic and chili float on top when it's mixed right.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keeps a week; you'll use it on everything.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is the master sauce of the cuisine: learn the ratio (fish sauce, lime, sugar at 1:1:⅔, water at 2) and you can dress a nation of dishes: bun bowls, rolls, grilled anything, fried everything. Balance is the entire skill; adjust until no single note wins, and taste it with something bland, not off a spoon. Garlic and chili floating on the surface is the visual doneness test: they only float when the sugar is fully dissolved and the density is right.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nuoc%20Cham%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nuoc Cham"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Nuoc Cham","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Nuoc%20Cham","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Carrot","Chiles","Lime","Fish sauce","Sugar"]},{"slug":"ca-phe-sua-da","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp dark roast coffee, coarse (robusta traditional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2–3 tbsp sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Phin filter (or moka pot / strong French press)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A glass of ice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Condensed milk in the glass first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brew the coffee slowly through the phin directly on top: 4–5 minutes of drips.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir hard to marry them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over ice. Sit with it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Robusta's brutal strength is the point: condensed milk was built to argue with it, and the argument is the drink. Vietnam grows robusta almost exclusively, twice the caffeine and none of arabica's manners; French colonists brought coffee and canned milk stood in for scarce fresh, and the workaround outlived the empire. The phin's slow four-minute drip extracts it dense as espresso. Stir hard before the ice, or the bottom half is dessert and the top half is punishment.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ca%20Phe%20Sua%20Da%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ca Phe Sua Da"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Ca Phe Sua Da","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Ca%20Phe%20Sua%20Da","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"espresso-and-the-coffee-program","term":"Espresso & the Coffee Program"}],"pantryItems":["Milk","Coffee/Espresso"]},{"slug":"rau-muong-xao-toi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 big bunch morning glory (water spinach), or substitute spinach/broccolini"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, smashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp fish sauce (or soy for veg)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch sugar, oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split thick stems; keep leaves whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blast garlic in hot oil 10 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Greens in, tossing violently; fish sauce and sugar around the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"90 seconds total. Out before it weeps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The wok should be hot enough that the greens hiss like rain on pavement: anything gentler stews them, and stewed morning glory goes limp and metallic. Ninety seconds total: garlic blasted ten seconds, greens tossed violently, sauce around the rim, out before the leaves weep. Split the hollow stems so they cook in the same window as the leaves. This is Vietnam's daily green: the test of a cook's fire more than of any recipe.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Wok technique"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","tangy-ferment","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rau%20Muong%20Xao%20Toi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rau Muong Xao Toi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Rau Muong Xao Toi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Rau%20Muong%20Xao%20Toi","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Spinach","Broccoli","Fish sauce","Sugar","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"rau-muong-xao-toi","marks":[{"id":"rau-muong-xao-toi#leaves","text":"Leaves are dark and glossy green with none gone olive or grey, and the split stems have kept their pale jade colour."},{"id":"rau-muong-xao-toi#stems","text":"Stems snap under the tooth and the leaves are tender in the same mouthful: no raw crunch at the thick ends, no collapse in the leaves."},{"id":"rau-muong-xao-toi#bowl","text":"The bowl is nearly dry, under a tablespoon of liquid after standing a minute, and the greens are coated in oil rather than sitting in a puddle."},{"id":"rau-muong-xao-toi#smashed","text":"The smashed cloves have gone gold and soft through, sweet rather than raw-sharp, with none scorched black or bitter, and one turns up in most mouthfuls."},{"id":"rau-muong-xao-toi#fish-sauce","text":"No raw fish-sauce smell comes off the bowl: it reads as savoury salt rather than as fish, and no sweetness is identifiable as sugar."}],"fault":"The greens go in still wet from the wash, the water crashes the wok temperature, and the bunch simmers in its own steam: limp olive leaves in a grey puddle, tasting faintly metallic."}},{"slug":"ga-kho-gung-ginger-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g bone-in chicken pieces (or thighs)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Huge knob ginger, half julienned, half smashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar for caramel"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Shallots, black pepper, scallion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the dark caramel; add fish sauce and shallots.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chicken and smashed ginger in; coat, splash of water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered 25 min, turning, until the sauce is a dark glaze.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Julienned ginger and pepper in the last 5 minutes so it stays fiery.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Two ginger additions, two jobs: the early smashed ginger mellows into the caramel sauce, sweet and rounded; the late julienne keeps its bite, fierce and clean at the end. That layering is the whole dish. The kho method is the same as the fish version (dark caramel, fish sauce, patient uncovered reduction to a glaze) because kho is a verb, not a recipe: to braise in caramel. Bone-in pieces reward the effort; the marrow feeds the sauce.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","sweet-edged","umami-deep","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a sweet edge rounding the corners, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ga%20Kho%20Gung%20(Ginger%20Chicken)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ga Kho Gung (Ginger Chicken)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Ga Kho Gung (Ginger Chicken)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Ga%20Kho%20Gung%20(Ginger%20Chicken)","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Ginger","Scallion","Shallot","Fish sauce","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"chicken-tagine-preserved-lemon-and-olives","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, 1 tsp each ginger and turmeric, pinch saffron"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 preserved lemon, rind slivered"},{"kind":"item","text":"Green or violet olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro, parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Coat chicken in the spices and grated onion; rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown gently; add water halfway up; simmer covered 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Uncover, reduce; add olives and preserved lemon for the last 10.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The sauce should be thick, yellow, and glossy with onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Grated onion melts into the sauce entirely: that thick, glossy body is the tagine's signature, no flour needed; sliced onion would leave you with pieces where Morocco expects velvet. The preserved lemon is the dish's voice: rind only, slivered, its fermented salinity nothing like fresh lemon. Daqq is the name of the sauce's final texture, reduced until it clings. The conical lid, or a tight ordinary one, recycles the steam so the chicken braises in barely any added water.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The tagine"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","herb-fresh","aromatic","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The tagine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tagine%20cooking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Tagine%2C%20Preserved%20Lemon%20%26%20Olives%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Tagine, Preserved Lemon & Olives"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Chicken Tagine, Preserved Lemon & Olives","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Chicken%20Tagine%2C%20Preserved%20Lemon%20%26%20Olives","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"clay-and-earthenware-tagine-donabe-cazuela","term":"Clay & Earthenware: Tagine, Donabe, Cazuela"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Olives","Lemon","Cilantro","Parsley","Pickles","Turmeric","Clove","Saffron"]},{"slug":"lamb-and-apricot-tagine","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g lamb shoulder, chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, 1 tsp each ginger, cinnamon; ½ tsp turmeric; saffron"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g dried apricots + honey 1 tbsp"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toasted almonds, sesame"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro; couscous or bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown lamb; soften onions with the spices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Water to nearly cover; simmer very low 2 hours until spoon-tender.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Apricots and honey in for the last 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Scatter almonds and sesame; serve from the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sweet-savory is Morocco's home key: the apricots should read as depth, not dessert, and salt is what holds the line; season the sauce properly and the honey becomes architecture instead of frosting. Lamb shoulder wants its two slow hours to spoon-tenderness. This is the mrouzia family of dishes, descended from banquet cooking where sugar and meat shared a pan centuries before anyone called it fusion. The almonds go on at the end, still crunchy: texture is the dish's last course.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The tagine","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","sweet-edged","herb-fresh","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, a sweet edge rounding the corners, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The tagine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tagine%20cooking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lamb%20%26%20Apricot%20Tagine%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lamb & Apricot Tagine"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Lamb & Apricot Tagine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Lamb%20%26%20Apricot%20Tagine","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lamb-primals","term":"Lamb Primals"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Ginger","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Cilantro","Bread","Couscous/Bulgur","Sesame","Honey","Nuts","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"seven-vegetable-couscous","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g couscous, steamed or good instant"},{"kind":"item","text":"Vegetables: carrot, turnip, zucchini, pumpkin, cabbage, tomato, onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 can chickpeas"},{"kind":"item","text":"Broth: ginger, turmeric, cinnamon, saffron, olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Raisins, smen or butter, harissa on the side"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the sturdier vegetables in the spiced broth 20 min; softer ones in the final 10.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam or hydrate the couscous; fluff with butter, grain by grain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mound couscous, crater the top, vegetables in and around.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Broth over; harissa thinned with broth for those who dare.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Friday couscous is a ritual across the Maghreb (the mosque, then the mound) and the broth-soaked grain matters more than any single vegetable; seven is tradition's number, but the spiced broth is the recipe. Fluff the couscous grain by grain with butter or smen; clumped couscous is the visible sign of a rushed hand. Serve it cratered like a volcano with the vegetables in and around, harissa thinned with broth on the side so each eater sets their own fire.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Seven-Vegetable%20Couscous%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Seven-Vegetable Couscous"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Seven-Vegetable Couscous","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Seven-Vegetable%20Couscous","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"baharat-and-the-seven-spice-family","term":"Baharat & the Seven-Spice Family"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Tomato","Carrot","Cabbage","Zucchini","Squash/Pumpkin","Radish/Turnip","Dried fruit","Butter","Couscous/Bulgur","Chickpeas","Stock/Broth","Curry paste","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"harira","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g lamb, small dice (or leave out)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cup lentils + 1 can chickpeas"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, celery with leaves, tomatoes 600g"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp each ginger, turmeric, cinnamon; pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Big handfuls cilantro + parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tedouira: 2 tbsp flour whisked in water; lemon, dates on the side"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown lamb with onion, celery, spices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tomatoes, lentils, water; simmer 40 min; chickpeas in.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stream in the flour slurry, stirring, to a silky, faintly thick body.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Herb avalanche at the end; serve with lemon and dates, Ramadan-style.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The tedouira, flour whisked into water and streamed in at the end, gives harira its signature velvet; thin harira is missing the step, not the point. The herb avalanche goes in last, cilantro and parsley by the double handful. This is the soup that breaks the Ramadan fast across Morocco, served with lemon and dates in a pairing older than anyone's grandmother: the date's sugar for the empty stomach, the soup's warmth for everything else. The celery leaves are flavor, not garnish.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Harira%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Harira"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Harira","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Harira","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Ginger","Tomato","Celery","Lemon","Dried fruit","Cilantro","Parsley","Flour","Lentils","Chickpeas","Turmeric","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"chermoula-roast-fish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 white fish fillets or 1 whole fish"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chermoula: big bunch cilantro + parsley, 4 garlic cloves, 2 tsp cumin, 1 tsp paprika, pinch cayenne, lemon juice, olive oil, blended"},{"kind":"item","text":"Potatoes, tomatoes, peppers, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olives, preserved lemon optional"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the chermoula; use a third to marinate the fish 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer sliced vegetables in a dish; toss with another third; roast 20 min at 210°C.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fish on top, remaining chermoula over; back in for 12–15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve from the dish with bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chermoula is Morocco's pesto: marinade, sauce and dressing in one, and you make double, always, because everything it touches improves. The three-way split is the method: a third marinates the fish, a third seasons the vegetables, a third arrives fresh at the table, so the same sauce shows up raw and cooked in one dish. Cumin and cilantro carry it; the cayenne is a suggestion, not a dare. The roasting vegetables build a rack for the fish and a side dish simultaneously.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chermoula%20Roast%20Fish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chermoula Roast Fish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Chermoula Roast Fish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Chermoula%20Roast%20Fish","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"salt-cod-and-the-preserved-fish-pantry","term":"Salt Cod & the Preserved Fish Pantry"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Chiles","Olives","Lemon","Cilantro","Parsley","Pickles","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"zaalouk","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggplants, charred whole or cubed and steamed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tomatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp each cumin and paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil, lemon, cilantro"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook garlic in olive oil; add tomatoes and spices; reduce to a jam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash in the eggplant flesh; keep cooking until thick enough to stand a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with lemon and cilantro.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm or room temp, with bread, as part of a salad spread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Moroccan 'salads' are mostly cooked, served warm or room-temperature in a mosaic of small plates, and zaalouk anchors the table, eggplant and tomato reduced to a smoky jam thick enough to stand a spoon. The reduction is the recipe: keep cooking past 'sauce' to 'spread', because watery zaalouk is just ratatouille with doubts. Char the eggplants whole if you can; the smoke earns its place. Bread is the only utensil the dish recognizes.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zaalouk%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zaalouk"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Zaalouk","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Zaalouk","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Eggplant","Lemon","Cilantro","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"kefta-mkaouara","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground beef or lamb, kneaded with grated onion, cumin, paprika, parsley, cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 800g tomatoes, garlic, 1 tsp paprika, ½ tsp cumin, pinch cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bread, always bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Roll small marble-size kefta.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the tomato sauce 15 min until thick; drop kefta straight in raw; 15 min more.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack eggs into the bubbling sauce; cover 5 min to just-set.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve in the tagine or pan; eat with bread only, no cutlery.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Poaching the raw meatballs in the sauce trades a browning step for tenderness, and one less pan; the kefta stay plush and the sauce takes their seasoning in exchange. Marble-size is correct: bigger kefta poach unevenly. The eggs cracked into the bubbling sauce at the end are the dish's flourish, whites just set, yolks molten, the tagine arriving at the table still sputtering. Bread only, no cutlery: the dish is designed for tearing and scooping, and a fork misses the point.","equipment":["Tagine","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The tagine"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The tagine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tagine%20cooking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kefta%20Mkaouara%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kefta Mkaouara"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Kefta Mkaouara","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Kefta%20Mkaouara","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Cilantro","Parsley","Bread","Cumin","Paprika"]},{"slug":"moroccan-carrot-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g carrots, sliced coins, boiled just-tender"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin, ½ tsp paprika, pinch cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon juice, olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro, parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil carrots 6–8 min; drain hot.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toss immediately with garlic, spices, lemon, oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 30 min minimum; better after a night.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shower with herbs to serve, room temperature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Warm vegetables drink dressing: the same trick as the Niçoise potatoes, wearing a djellaba; toss the carrots with the cumin, lemon and oil straight from the colander and the seasoning penetrates instead of coating. The overnight rest is where good becomes serious. Cumin and carrot is one of the old reliable marriages of the spice road. Serve it room temperature among other little salads; cold from the fridge, half its perfume stays asleep.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moroccan%20Carrot%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moroccan Carrot Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Moroccan Carrot Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Moroccan%20Carrot%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Carrot","Chiles","Lemon","Cilantro","Parsley","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"atay-moroccan-mint-tea","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Chinese gunpowder green tea"},{"kind":"item","text":"Enormous bunch fresh spearmint"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sugar to a level that would alarm your dentist (adjust)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Boiling water; a pot with a long spout"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the tea leaves with a splash of boiling water; discard the rinse (it removes bitterness).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add mint and sugar; fill with boiling water; steep 3–4 min.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour a glass and return it to the pot, twice, to mix.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour from a height for the crown of foam. That foam is the honor of the house.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The high pour aerates and cools, and hospitality is measured in the foam it leaves; a glass without its crown is a host without manners. Rinsing the gunpowder tea first discards the bitter dust so the sugar and mint argue only with clean leaves. The pour-and-return mixing ritual matters more than stirring ever could. Refusing the third glass is technically permitted and socially inadvisable; the proverb assigns the three glasses their meanings: gentle as life, strong as love, bitter as death.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Atay%20(Moroccan%20Mint%20Tea)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Atay (Moroccan Mint Tea)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Atay (Moroccan Mint Tea)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Atay%20(Moroccan%20Mint%20Tea)","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mint","Sugar"]},{"slug":"orange-and-cinnamon-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 oranges"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp orange-blossom water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Powdered sugar or honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mint, chopped dates or almonds optional"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel oranges to the flesh; slice into wheels, catching juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange; sprinkle with orange-blossom water and sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust cinnamon in stripes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 20 min; mint on top.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Five minutes, four ingredients, and it ends a heavy tagine like a window opening, which is precisely its job; Moroccan meals close on freshness, not richness. Orange-blossom water is the ingredient that makes it Moroccan rather than merely fruit: a teaspoon of distilled perfume, and more is not better. Slice into wheels over a bowl so the juice is captured, not lost. The cinnamon goes on in stripes because the eyes eat first, even at the end of dinner.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","sweet-edged","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The tagine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tagine%20cooking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Orange%20%26%20Cinnamon%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Orange & Cinnamon Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Orange & Cinnamon Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Orange%20%26%20Cinnamon%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Orange","Dried fruit","Mint","Honey","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"buttermilk-fried-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut in 10, or 8 thighs/drums"},{"kind":"item","text":"Brine: 500ml buttermilk, 2 tbsp salt, hot sauce, 4–24 h"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dredge: 300g flour + 50g cornstarch, paprika, garlic powder, cayenne, black pepper, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying (cast iron, 2cm deep, or a pot)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pull chicken from buttermilk; dredge hard, pressing craggy bits on. Rest 15 min so the crust hydrates.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 160–165°C, turning, 14–18 min to 74°C internal at the bone.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1080,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest on a rack, never paper towels.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the second it leaves the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The fifteen-minute post-dredge rest glues the crust on: the flour hydrates into a paste that welds to the skin, and skipping it is why crusts secede from the chicken in the fryer. The buttermilk brine is doing two jobs: salt seasons to the bone while the acid tenderizes overnight. Fry at a true 160-165°C and trust the thermometer at the bone, not the clock. Rest on a rack: paper towels steam the bottom crust soft, undoing twenty minutes of work in two.","equipment":["Skillet","Thermometer","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Buttermilk%20Fried%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Buttermilk Fried Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Buttermilk Fried Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Buttermilk%20Fried%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Chiles","Buttermilk","Flour","Hot sauce","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"smash-burger","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g 80/20 ground beef, loosely rolled into 8 balls, never pressed in advance"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices American cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"Potato buns, toasted in butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Minced onion optional (smashed into the patty)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: mayo + mustard + pickle brine + paprika; pickles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Ripping-hot flat surface. Ball on; smash FLAT with a heavy spatula for 10 firm seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season; scrape up at 90 seconds with the whole crust attached.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip, cheese, 30 seconds. Stack doubles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauced toasted bun. Eat standing over the sink like a person of culture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Smash once, immediately, then never press again: the early smash maximizes beef-to-steel contact while the fat is still solid, building the lacy Maillard crust that IS the style; late pressing just ejects juice onto the griddle. Loose-packed balls, never preformed pucks. Scrape up with the whole crust attached; the crust was the point of everything. Doubles beat singles because two crusts outrank one thick middle: the math of the smash burger is all surface area.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Smash%20Burger%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Smash Burger"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Smash Burger","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Smash%20Burger","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"american-cheese-defended","term":"American Cheese, Defended"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Potato","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mustard","Mayonnaise","Pickles","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"baked-mac-and-cheese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g elbow macaroni, cooked 2 min shy"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g butter + 60g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"900ml milk, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sharp cheddar + 150g gruyère or monterey jack, grated (never pre-shredded)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mustard powder, cayenne, nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Topping: buttered panko or more cheese"}],"steps":[{"text":"Roux 2 min; whisk in milk to a sauce that coats a spoon.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cheese in off heat, one handful at a time; season boldly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in pasta; into a buttered dish; top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 200°C, 20–25 min, to bronze and bubble. Rest 10.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"Pre-shredded cheese is dusted with anti-caking starch that makes gritty sauce. Grate your own; it's the whole game. Cheese joins the béchamel OFF the heat, a handful at a time, because boiled cheese proteins seize and the sauce breaks to grease. Undercook the pasta by two minutes; it finishes in the oven drinking sauce. Season the béchamel past polite (mustard powder, cayenne, nutmeg) because a quarter of the seasoning gets lost inside all that dairy.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Making a roux"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Béchamel: the mother sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bechamel%20mother%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baked%20Mac%20%26%20Cheese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baked Mac & Cheese"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Baked Mac & Cheese","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Baked%20Mac%20%26%20Cheese","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"west-country-farmhouse-cheddar","term":"West Country Farmhouse Cheddar"},{"slug":"monterey-jack-and-pepper-jack","term":"Monterey Jack & Pepper Jack"}],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Gruyère/Alpine","Pasta","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Mustard","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"oven-pulled-pork","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg pork shoulder (Boston butt), bone-in"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rub: 3 tbsp brown sugar, 2 tbsp paprika, 1 tbsp each salt, pepper, garlic powder; cumin, cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Splash cider vinegar + liquid smoke (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Buns, slaw, vinegar-based BBQ sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the shoulder the night before if you can.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast at 135°C, uncovered, 5–7 hours to 95°C internal, until the bone waves goodbye.","durationSec":25200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":25200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 45 min wrapped; shred, folding the crusty bark back in.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Splash with vinegar sauce; pile onto buns under slaw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"95°C internal seems insane until you learn collagen's schedule: the meat is 'done' at 70°C but pullable only 25 degrees later, when the connective tissue has fully melted to gelatin; pulling early gets you sliced pork that fights back. The long, low, uncovered roast builds bark, and the bark gets folded back through the shred where it belongs. The 45-minute wrapped rest lets the meat reabsorb what the oven tried to take. Vinegar sauce, not sweet: the fat needs an argument, not a dessert.","equipment":["Thermometer"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","smoky","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, wood-smoke running underneath, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oven%20Pulled%20Pork%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oven Pulled Pork"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Oven Pulled Pork","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Oven%20Pulled%20Pork","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pork-shoulder-butt-vs-picnic","term":"Pork Shoulder: Butt vs. Picnic"},{"slug":"oils-and-smoke-points","term":"Oils & Smoke Points"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Chiles","Bread","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Cumin","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"buttermilk-biscuits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour (soft Southern flour if you can get it)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder, ½ tsp baking soda, 1 tsp salt, 1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g FROZEN butter, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"240ml cold buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Melted butter for the tops"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss grated frozen butter through the dry mix.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add buttermilk; stir until it barely holds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the shaggy mass over itself 5 times: those are your layers. Pat 2.5cm thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut straight down, no twisting; bake at 230°C, 12–14 min, touching each other.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":840,"estimated":true}],"note":"Twisting the cutter seals the edges shut, and touching biscuits climb each other and rise taller, the physics of breakfast. Frozen grated butter is the shortcut to flaky: distinct shards that steam into layers, no pastry cutter required. The five folds ARE the lamination. Soft Southern flour (lower protein) is why the same recipe rises higher below the Mason-Dixon; buttermilk's acid fires the baking soda the moment they meet, so the oven should be waiting, not preheating.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Buttermilk%20Biscuits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Buttermilk Biscuits"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Buttermilk Biscuits","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Buttermilk%20Biscuits","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"frozen-dessert-machines-churners-and-pacojet","term":"Frozen Dessert Machines: Churners & Pacojet"}],"pantryItems":["Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"skillet-cornbread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"240g cornmeal (+ 60g flour, or go 100% corn, Southern-style)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder, ½ tsp soda, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, 400ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g butter or bacon fat, melted in the skillet"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sugar: 0–2 tbsp, depending which side of the Mason-Dixon you cook for"}],"steps":[{"text":"Put the empty cast-iron skillet in a 220°C oven with the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk wet into dry just barely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour batter into the screaming-hot fat: it must sizzle on contact.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 20–22 min; flip out crust-side up.","durationSec":1320,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1320,"estimated":true}],"note":"The preheated skillet fries the bottom into a crust. Cornbread poured into a cold pan is just corn cake; the batter must sizzle on contact, which is why the empty cast iron heats in the oven with the fat already in it. Bacon fat over butter if the question ever arises. The sugar line is the true Mason-Dixon: Northern cornbread is sweet cake, Southern is savory bread, and the recipe's 0-2 tablespoon range is a diplomatic instrument. Flip it out crust-side up and serve it proud.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Skillet%20Cornbread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Skillet Cornbread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Skillet Cornbread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Skillet%20Cornbread","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"corn-sugar-against-the-clock","term":"Corn: Sugar Against the Clock"}],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Corn","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"]},{"slug":"braised-collard-greens","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 big bunches collards, ribs stripped, leaves torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 smoked ham hock or turkey wing (or smoked paprika + olive oil for veg)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cider vinegar, hot sauce, pinch sugar, chili flakes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the hock in the stock 30 min head start.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sweat onion and garlic; add greens in armfuls as they collapse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer low, partly covered, 45–60 min.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season the potlikker with vinegar and hot sauce; then guard it. People fight over potlikker.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Potlikker, the broth left behind, was once the most nutritious thing on the table, the pot's minerals and smoke dissolved into liquid, and cornbread exists to be dunked in it; guard it accordingly. The ham hock's half-hour head start extracts its smoke before the greens ever arrive. Collards want the full hour: this is one of the few greens that improves with surrender. The vinegar and hot sauce at the end aren't garnish: acid is what makes long-cooked greens taste alive again.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Braising","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Braised%20Collard%20Greens%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Braised Collard Greens"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Braised Collard Greens","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Braised%20Collard%20Greens","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ham-and-hock","term":"Ham & Hock"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Turkey","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Kale/Collards","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Hot sauce","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"apple-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Double crust: 300g flour, 230g cold butter, 1 tsp salt, ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg mixed apples (Granny Smith + Honeycrisp), sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g sugar + brown sugar, cinnamon, nutmeg, lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cornstarch or flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"Egg wash, coarse sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the crust with visible butter shards; chill 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Macerate apples with sugar 30 min; drain and REDUCE the juices to a syrup; toss back with starch.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill, lid or lattice, vent, wash, sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 220°C for 20 min then 180°C for 40. Cool 3 hours (yes, three).","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":12000,"estimated":false}],"note":"Reducing the drained maceration juice is the anti-soup insurance every soggy pie was missing: the apples give up their water BEFORE baking, and the syrup goes back in as concentrated flavor instead of steam. Two apple varieties, one for structure and one for sauce. The three-hour cool is structural, not ceremonial: the starch-thickened juices set as they cool, and a hot-cut pie bleeds everything you reduced. Visible butter shards in the crust are the flake: overworked dough is a cracker.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Apple%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Apple Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Apple Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Apple%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"apples-and-pears-the-autumn-keepers","term":"Apples & Pears: The Autumn Keepers"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Apple","Butter","Flour","Honey","Sugar","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"chocolate-chip-cookies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"225g butter, browned and cooled slightly"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar + 100g white"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, 2 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour, 1 tsp baking soda, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g chocolate, half chips, half chopped bar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the butter: nutty, amber, sediment and all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk with sugars, then eggs and vanilla until glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in dry, then chocolate. REST the dough 1–24 h in the fridge.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 190°C, 11–12 min, until edges set and centers look slightly undone. Flaky salt while warm.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true}],"note":"Resting the dough hydrates the flour and deepens the toffee notes: a 24-hour cookie genuinely tastes like a different recipe, which is the pastry-counter secret hiding in plain sight. Browning the butter first stacks nutty Maillard flavor under the sugar. Half chips, half chopped bar: the bar shards spread into strata while the chips hold their shape. Pull them looking slightly underdone at the center; carryover finishes what the oven started, and flaky salt lands while they're warm.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chocolate%20Chip%20Cookies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chocolate Chip Cookies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Chocolate Chip Cookies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Chocolate%20Chip%20Cookies","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"vanilla","term":"Vanilla"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"new-england-clam-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg littleneck clams (or 3 cans + juice, no shame)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g bacon or salt pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 2 celery ribs, 500g waxy potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml clam juice + 400ml milk/cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bay, thyme, oyster crackers"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steam clams open in a splash of water; chop meat, strain and keep every drop of liquor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render bacon; sweat vegetables; flour in for 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add clam liquor and potatoes; simmer until tender.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream and clams in at the end: clams toughen if boiled. Crackers on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Clams are done the moment they open: everything after that is punishment, which is why they leave the pot as soon as they gape and return only at the very end, off the boil. Their strained liquor is the soup's entire identity; guard every drop. Salt pork or bacon is the old Yankee foundation. A proper chowder is creamy but never thick enough to hold a spoon upright (the flour is a whisper, not a wall), and tomatoes remain illegal in several New England jurisdictions, at least emotionally.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20England%20Clam%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New England Clam Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on New England Clam Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20New%20England%20Clam%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"starchy-vs-waxy-potatoes","term":"Starchy vs. Waxy Potatoes"},{"slug":"bivalves-oysters-clams-mussels-scallops","term":"Bivalves (Oysters, Clams, Mussels, Scallops)"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Mussels/Clams","Onion","Potato","Celery","Thyme","Bay leaf","Cream","Flour"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"adana-kebab","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g lamb, hand-chopped or coarsely ground, ~20% fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Turkish red pepper flakes (pul biber) + 1 tsp Urfa if you have it"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, grated and squeezed dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, no other seasoning: that is the point"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flat metal skewers, lavash, sumac onions"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead meat, peppers and salt 5 min until tacky and pale: the knead is the binder, there is no egg.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 1 h. Press onto wide flat skewers with wet hands, ridging with your fingers.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot coals, turning often, 8–10 min, fat dripping and flaring.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pull onto lavash so the bread catches the juices; pile with sumac onions and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Adana is licensed by law in its home city: lamb, tail fat, pepper, salt, nothing else: the knead and the coals do everything a marinade pretends to. The five-minute knead is the actual binder; salt-dissolved proteins turn the mince tacky enough to grip a flat skewer with no egg and no breadcrumb. Hand-chopped beats ground because intact fat pockets baste from inside as they drip and flare. Pulling it onto lavash isn't plating, it's engineering: the bread is the sauce's retirement plan.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Salting to draw the water out","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","bright","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Adana%20Kebab%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Adana Kebab"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on Adana Kebab","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Adana%20Kebab","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sumac","term":"Sumac"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Bell pepper","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"lahmacun","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 300g flour, 180ml water, 5g yeast, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g finely minced lamb or beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, 1 tomato, ½ red pepper; all minced to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste, pul biber, parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon, more parsley, sumac onions to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a soft dough; rest 1 h. Divide into 6.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the topping almost to a wet paste: it must spread thin, not sit in chunks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll each round paper-thin; smear a thin skim of topping to the very edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at max heat on a steel or upside-down tray, 4–6 min. Roll around parsley and a squeeze of lemon; eat like a cigar.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":360,"estimated":true}],"note":"Thin is the whole game: the meat should cook in the same minutes as the dough, so the topping is chopped nearly to a wet paste and smeared in a skim; if it sits in chunks, the dough burns waiting for it. Bake on the hottest surface you own. Rolled around parsley and a squeeze of lemon and eaten like a cigar, it's Anatolia's flatbread fast food with centuries of practice behind it, often translated 'Turkish pizza', which undersells a dish that never needed cheese.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lahmacun%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lahmacun"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on Lahmacun","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Lahmacun","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Lemon","Parsley","Flour","Tomato paste","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"imam-bayildi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 slim aubergines"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced into half-moons"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tomatoes, grated; 6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml good olive oil; do not measure with fear"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sugar, salt, parsley, lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel aubergines in stripes; fry whole in olive oil until soft and bronzed; slit open like a canoe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stew onions slowly in the same oil until sweet; add garlic, tomato, a pinch of sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stuff the slits generously; nestle in a pan with a splash of water and more oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and braise gently 30 min. Cool to room temperature: never serve hot.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The name means 'the imam fainted', from the pleasure or from the olive oil bill, and no one has settled it since Ottoman times. Room temperature is when the oil, sugar and aubergine finally speak in one voice; hot, the dish is merely good, which is why the zeytinyağlı family of olive-oil dishes is always served cooled. Do not measure the oil with fear: it is an ingredient here, the braising medium and the sauce, and the aubergine is a sponge built to hold it.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Braising","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","aromatic","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=%C4%B0mam%20Bay%C4%B1ld%C4%B1%20recipe","sub":"Search films of İmam Bayıldı"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on İmam Bayıldı","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20%C4%B0mam%20Bay%C4%B1ld%C4%B1","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Eggplant","Lemon","Parsley","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"mercimek-corbasi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g red lentils, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 1 carrot, 1 small potato"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp tomato paste, 1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2L water or light stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Finish: butter bloomed with pul biber, dried mint, lemon wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat vegetables; stir in tomato paste and cumin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add lentils and water; simmer 20 min until everything collapses.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend completely smooth: Turkish lentil soup is silk, not rubble.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sizzle butter with pepper flakes and mint; ribbon it over each bowl. Lemon at the table, always.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This soup is on every Turkish table from truck stop to wedding, and the lemon is not garnish: the soup is designed unfinished until you squeeze it, the acid switching on everything the lentils muted. Blend it completely: Turkish lentil soup is silk, not rubble, and a stick blender's extra minute is the difference. The sizzled-butter finish, pul biber and dried mint bloomed ten seconds, is the çorba signature move: aroma poured on top rather than stirred away.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mercimek%20%C3%87orbas%C4%B1%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mercimek Çorbası"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on Mercimek Çorbası","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Mercimek%20%C3%87orbas%C4%B1","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Lemon","Mint","Butter","Lentils","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"menemen","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 ripe tomatoes, grated (skins discarded)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green Turkish peppers (or ½ green bell + a hot chili), sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter + 1 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pul biber, salt; crusty bread mandatory"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soften peppers in the butter and oil 3–4 min.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add grated tomato; cook down until saucy and no longer watery, 6–8 min.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack eggs straight in; stir lazily off and on: big soft curds, not scramble.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull off heat while still glossy and loose; dust with pul biber. Serve in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The eternal Turkish argument is onions or no onions, and both camps agree on the part that matters: menemen keeps cooking in the pan, so stop one minute before your nerve says so; glossy and loose on the stove is perfect on the table. Grated tomato, skins left behind in your hand, is the texture the dish wants. Stir lazily; big soft curds, never a scramble. The bread is mandatory because the pan juices are half the meal, and menemen is eaten from the pan it was made in.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","gently hot","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Menemen%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Menemen"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on Menemen","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Menemen","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Tomato","Chiles","Butter","Bread"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"kiymali-pide","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 350g flour, 210ml warm water, 7g yeast, 1 tsp salt, 1 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g minced lamb or beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion + 1 pepper + 1 tomato, all finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pul biber, black pepper, parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Egg yolk + butter for the crust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a soft dough; rise 1 h. Divide in 3.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix meat and vegetables raw: they cook in the oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll long ovals; spread filling; fold the edges in and pinch the ends into a boat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush rims with yolk; bake at 250°C, 10–12 min; butter the crusts as they land.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true}],"note":"The boat shape is function: the rim is a handle, the middle is a plate. Slice crosswise and eat the ends first: that is where the crust-to-filling ratio peaks. The filling goes on raw and wet, cooking in the oven's blast and feeding its juices into the dough beneath, which is why pide bakers roll thin and bake violently hot, 250°C or better. Butter brushed on the rims as they land is the pide oven's goodbye. Turkey's answer to pizza predates the comparison and doesn't need it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=K%C4%B1ymal%C4%B1%20Pide%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kıymalı Pide"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on Kıymalı Pide","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20K%C4%B1ymal%C4%B1%20Pide","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Parsley","Butter","Flour"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"manti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 300g flour, 1 egg, 120ml water, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g minced lamb, ½ grated onion, salt, pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic yogurt: 400g yogurt, 2 crushed cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 60g butter, 1 tbsp tomato paste, pul biber"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dried mint, sumac"}],"steps":[{"text":"Firm dough; rest 30 min. Roll thin; cut 3cm squares.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dot each with a chickpea-sized bit of filling; pinch corners into tiny parcels.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil 8–10 min in salted water.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Plate on garlic yogurt; pour over the sizzling paste-butter; dust mint and sumac.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Anatolian grandmothers judge a bride by how small her mantı are: forty on one spoon is the legend; yours can be big, and the yogurt forgives everything. The trinity of toppings is fixed: garlic yogurt for cool, paste-stained butter for heat and color, dried mint and sumac for perfume. Tiny parcels matter because the dough-to-filling ratio is the dish: mantı is pasta engineering, kin to manti and momo along the whole Silk Road, and Kayseri claims the smallest.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mant%C4%B1%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mantı"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on Mantı","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Mant%C4%B1","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sumac","term":"Sumac"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Mint","Butter","Yogurt","Flour","Tomato paste","Clove","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"cilbir","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, poached"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g thick yogurt at room temperature, 2 garlic cloves crushed in"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp pul biber + ½ tsp dried mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toasted bread or simit"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whip garlic and salt into the yogurt: room temperature, or it will chill the eggs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach eggs soft, 3 min.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Foam the butter; bloom pepper flakes and mint 10 seconds off heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Yogurt in a shallow bowl, eggs on top, red butter over everything.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fifteenth-century Ottoman palace food that costs pennies: çılbır appears in Topkapı kitchen records, and the combination has not been improved since: molten yolk, garlic yogurt and chili butter making a sauce no chef has bettered. Room-temperature yogurt is the one technical rule; cold yogurt chills the eggs and stiffens instead of flowing. Bloom the pul biber and mint off the heat: ten seconds in foaming butter, no more, or the spice scorches bitter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=%C3%87%C4%B1lb%C4%B1r%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Çılbır"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on Çılbır","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20%C3%87%C4%B1lb%C4%B1r","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Garlic","Mint","Butter","Yogurt","Bread","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"izgara-kofte","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g minced beef-lamb mix"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 grated onion, squeezed dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 slices stale bread, soaked and squeezed, or 3 tbsp fine bulgur"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp pul biber, salt, parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grilled peppers, rice or bread, cacık alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead everything 3–4 min until sticky and uniform.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 30 min in the fridge: köfte hold their shape only when cold.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape flattened fingers; grill or sear in a cast pan 3 min a side.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with charred green peppers and cool cacık.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Squeeze the onion like you mean it: onion water is the number one cause of köfte that fall apart and steam instead of char; the grated pulp brings flavor, the juice brings failure. The knead-until-sticky and the cold half-hour rest are the other two legs of the tripod: warm, loose köfte slump through the grill grates. The bread panade keeps them plush through the char. Serve with blistered green peppers and cool cacık, the Turkish grill's eternal supporting cast.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","herb-fresh","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=%C4%B0zgara%20K%C3%B6fte%20recipe","sub":"Search films of İzgara Köfte"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on İzgara Köfte","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20%C4%B0zgara%20K%C3%B6fte","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Ground meat","Onion","Parsley","Rice","Bread","Couscous/Bulgur","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"fistikli-baklava","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 pack filo (fresh if you can find it), ~30 sheets"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g melted clarified butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g Antep pistachios, finely ground (bright green)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Syrup: 400g sugar, 300ml water, squeeze of lemon: NO honey in Turkish baklava"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of ground pistachio to crown"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a tray; lay 12 buttered sheets, then half the nuts, 6 sheets, remaining nuts, 12 sheets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut all the way through into diamonds BEFORE baking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 170°C, 45–50 min, until deep gold to the bottom.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil syrup 10 min; pour COLD syrup over HOT baklava: listen to it crackle. Rest 4 h.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hot pastry, cold syrup (or the reverse) never both hot: that is the difference between crisp layers and a sweet sponge, and the crackle when the syrup pours is the sound of it going right. Gaziantep would also like a word about honey: no. Turkish baklava syrup is sugar and lemon, and the flavor is the Antep pistachio's, bright green and ground fine. Cut all the way through before baking; baked filo shatters under a knife. The four-hour rest lets the syrup finish its slow descent.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Handling phyllo"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Handling phyllo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=working%20with%20phyllo%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=F%C4%B1st%C4%B1kl%C4%B1%20Baklava%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fıstıklı Baklava"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on Fıstıklı Baklava","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20F%C4%B1st%C4%B1kl%C4%B1%20Baklava","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Butter","Phyllo/Puff pastry","Honey","Sugar","Nuts"]},{"slug":"jerk-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut in quarters, slashed to the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"Jerk paste: 4–6 scotch bonnets, 6 spring onions, thumb of ginger, 6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp allspice berries, thyme, cinnamon, nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Soy sauce, brown sugar, lime, oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pimento wood or a handful of allspice berries for the coals"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blitz the paste; massage into the slashes; marinate overnight, no shortcuts here.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over medium coals, lid down, turning every 10 min, ~40 min total.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Throw allspice berries on the coals for the last 10: that smoke is the signature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest, chop through the bone with a cleaver, serve with rice and peas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jerk is a smoking technique, not a spice mix; allspice (pimento) wood smoke is the soul, and the scotch bonnet is just the sermon. The method is Maroon cooking: escaped slaves in Jamaica's Blue Mountains slow-smoked meat over pimento wood in covered pits, and the covered grill with allspice berries on the coals is the home translation. The overnight marinade in the slashes is non-negotiable: jerk seasons to the bone or it isn't jerk. Chop through the bone to serve; the cleaver is traditional.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Jerk","Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","fiery","charred","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, real fire that builds and stays, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Jerk","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=jamaican%20jerk%20technique%20pimento","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jerk%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jerk Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Jerk Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Jerk%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"allspice","term":"Allspice"},{"slug":"scotch-irish-and-world-whisky","term":"Scotch, Irish & World Whisky"},{"slug":"berries-the-late-spring-sprint","term":"Berries: The Late-Spring Sprint"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Chiles","Lime","Berries","Thyme","Soy sauce","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Nutmeg","Allspice"],"standard":{"slug":"jerk-chicken","marks":[{"id":"jerk-chicken#skin","text":"Skin is burnished dark mahogany with char confined to the slash edges and the raised ridges; the flats read brown, and no soft yellow fat is still sitting under the skin anywhere on the quarter."},{"id":"jerk-chicken#slashes","text":"The slashes have opened and stayed open with paste visible sitting in them, and the meat lining each cut is stained the same rust-brown all the way down to the bone, with no pale ring under the surface."},{"id":"jerk-chicken#thigh","text":"Thigh joint reads 75C, the drumstick twists free of the socket in one turn, and juice at the bone runs clear."},{"id":"jerk-chicken#cleaver","text":"The cleaver passes through each bone in a single strike, leaving a clean flat face: no splinters driven into the meat, no piece needing a second blow."},{"id":"jerk-chicken#taste","text":"Taste a piece of meat from inside a slash rather than the skin: it carries allspice and pimento smoke on its own, and nothing on the quarter tastes of scorched sugar or burnt soy."}],"fault":"Coals too hot with the lid up, so brown sugar and soy carbonize into a bitter black shell within twenty minutes while the joint is still bloody at the bone; the plate reads scorched and raw at once."}},{"slug":"rice-and-peas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g dried kidney beans (the 'peas'), soaked, or 2 cans"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml coconut milk + 400ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 spring onions, 2 garlic cloves, big sprig thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole scotch bonnet, UNBROKEN"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g long-grain rice, salt, allspice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer beans in coconut milk and water with aromatics until tender.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add rice, salt, and float the whole scotch bonnet on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover tight, lowest heat, 20 min; do not stir, do not peek.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fish out the bonnet whole; fluff. If it burst, well, say a prayer and taste carefully.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The unbroken scotch bonnet perfumes without punishing: floated whole on the pot, it gives its fruit-forward aroma and keeps its violence to itself, provided nobody bursts it; Sunday dinner across Jamaica is measured against this pot. The 'peas' are kidney beans, and the coconut milk they simmer in becomes the rice's cooking liquid, which is the whole flavor scheme. Lid on, lowest heat, no stirring, no peeking, pilaf discipline with an island accent.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","rich","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rice%20and%20Peas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rice and Peas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Rice and Peas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Rice%20and%20Peas","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Chiles","Peas","Coconut","Thyme","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Kidney/Pinto beans","Clove","Allspice"]},{"slug":"curry-goat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg goat (or mutton), bone-in, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp Jamaican curry powder (turmeric-heavy)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, 6 spring onions, garlic, ginger, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 potatoes, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil, salt, pimento berries"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season meat with curry powder and aromatics; marinate overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"'Burn the curry': fry another spoon of curry powder in oil until it darkens and smells toasty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown meat in that; add water to barely cover; simmer 2–2.5 h until sliding off the bone.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Potatoes in for the last 30: they thicken the gravy as they fray at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Burning the curry, blooming an extra spoonful in hot oil until it darkens and smells toasty, is the step that separates curry goat from stew with curry powder in it; raw curry powder tastes like dust, burnt curry tastes like Sunday. Jamaican curry is turmeric-heavy, the Indian spice box rebuilt by indentured laborers who came after emancipation. Bone-in is mandatory: the bones feed the gravy, and the potatoes fraying at the edges in the last half hour are the thickener.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","fiery","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Curry%20Goat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Curry Goat"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Curry Goat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Curry%20Goat","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mutton-and-hogget","term":"Mutton & Hogget"},{"slug":"lamb-spring-hogget-and-mutton","term":"Lamb: Spring, Hogget & Mutton"},{"slug":"curry-powder-the-colonial-invention","term":"Curry Powder: The Colonial Invention"},{"slug":"berries-the-late-spring-sprint","term":"Berries: The Late-Spring Sprint"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Potato","Chiles","Berries","Thyme","Turmeric","Curry powder","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"ropa-vieja","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg flank steak"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, 2 bell peppers, sliced; 6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes, 120ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cumin, 2 tsp oregano, 2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pimento-stuffed olives + capers; white rice and black beans to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer flank with bay and half an onion until it surrenders, ~2 h. Shred into rags; keep the broth.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sofrito: slow-fry onions, peppers, garlic; add cumin and oregano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wine, tomatoes, a ladle of broth; simmer 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the shredded beef and olives; simmer until the 'old clothes' soak up the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The name means 'old rags': the beef must be shredded along the grain into threads, never cubed, because the rags are what soak up the sofrito sauce; and the olives are Cuba's little salt-bombs, non-negotiable. Keep the first braising broth: it is the stock the sauce is built on. The dish sailed from the Canary Islands with a legend attached, a poor man who shredded his own clothes into the pot and found them turned to meat, and Cuba made it the national supper.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","earthy","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, an earthy, grounding base, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ropa%20Vieja%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ropa Vieja"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Ropa Vieja","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Ropa%20Vieja","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Olives","Capers","Oregano","Bay leaf","Rice","Black beans","Wine","Cumin","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"mofongo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 green plantains"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g chicharrón (crisp pork skin) or crispy bacon"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, mashed with olive oil and salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chicken broth or garlic-butter shrimp to serve over"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel plantains; slice thick; fry at 170°C until tender and gold, not crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In a pilón (mortar), smash hot plantains with the garlic paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in chicharrón as you pound: crunch through the mash.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into a bowl, unmold, moat with hot broth or crown with shrimp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mofongo waits for no one: plantains seize as they cool, so you pound hot, serve hotter, and keep the broth on the side to loosen every bite. The pilón's wooden pounding is the texture: a mash with intent, chicharrón folded through so there is crunch inside the soft. Green plantains only; sweet ones make dessert. The dish is Puerto Rico's plate-sized monument to its African inheritance: fufu pounding translated to the plantain, garlic and pork of the island.","equipment":["Thermometer","Mortar & pestle","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mofongo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mofongo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Mofongo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Mofongo","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Bacon","Shrimp","Garlic","Plantain","Butter","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"jamaican-beef-patties","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry: 350g flour, 1 tbsp turmeric + curry powder, 200g cold butter/suet, ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g minced beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scotch bonnet, spring onion, garlic, thyme, allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Breadcrumbs + splash of stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Egg wash"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the golden pastry; rest 1 h: turmeric is for the famous yellow.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the filling; add crumbs and stock so it sets soft and spoonable, not dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll, cut circles, fill, fold, crimp with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 200°C, 25–30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"The filling must be almost porridge-soft going in: it firms as it bakes, and a dry patty filling is a crime the crust cannot pardon; the breadcrumbs-and-stock slurry is the insurance. The turmeric-gold pastry is the patty's flag, flaky from cold fat worked barely in. Scotch bonnet to your nerve, but a patty with no heat at all is a pastry with an identity crisis. Jamaica's lunch counter standard, eaten from a paper bag, ideally inside coco bread for the full carbohydrate embrace.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","fiery","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, real fire that builds and stays, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20Beef%20Patties%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jamaican Beef Patties"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Jamaican Beef Patties","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Jamaican%20Beef%20Patties","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"turmeric","term":"Turmeric"},{"slug":"curry-powder-the-colonial-invention","term":"Curry Powder: The Colonial Invention"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Chiles","Thyme","Butter","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Stock/Broth","Turmeric","Curry powder","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking"]},{"slug":"ackee-and-saltfish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g salt cod, soaked overnight, water changed twice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 can ackee, drained gently (fresh if you are lucky and careful)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 1 tomato, spring onions, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scotch bonnet to taste, black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fried dumplings or boiled green banana alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil soaked cod 15 min; flake, discarding skin and bone.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauté onion, thyme, bonnet, tomato in a generous pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the fish; heat through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add ackee last; fold ONCE or twice: it crumbles like scrambled eggs if bullied.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jamaica's national dish, and a lesson in gentleness: ackee is buttery and fragile: treat it like a soft scramble, folded once or twice, never stirred, and it rewards you; bullied, it dissolves into the pan. The salt cod's overnight soak with water changes is where the dish is decided: under-soaked fish shouts down everything else. Ackee arrived on slave ships from West Africa and became the island's breakfast; canned is the safe and standard route abroad, since fresh ackee must be opened by the sun before it's picked.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ackee%20and%20Saltfish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ackee and Saltfish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Ackee and Saltfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Ackee%20and%20Saltfish","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Salt cod","Onion","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Banana","Thyme"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"trinidad-doubles","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Bara: 250g flour, 1 tsp yeast, ½ tsp turmeric, 1 tsp ground cumin, water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Channa: 2 cans chickpeas, 2 tbsp curry powder, garlic, onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pepper sauce, cucumber chutney"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tamarind sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soft, slack dough; rise 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Curry the chickpeas until tender and saucy, crushing a few to thicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With oiled hands, stretch dough into thin rounds; fry seconds a side: they should puff and stay soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Two bara, channa between, sauces on top. Eat standing up, leaning forward.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The national street breakfast of Trinidad: 'slight pepper' is the standard order, and the lean-forward stance is structural, not optional, because the bara are engineered soft and the channa runs. Fry the bara seconds a side; they should puff and stay pillowy, never crisp. The dish is Indo-Trinidadian to the bone, chana and fried bread from indentured workers' kitchens turned into the island's morning ritual. Two bara always, hence doubles, and the tamarind and cucumber sauces are the full dress code.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidad%20Doubles%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Trinidad Doubles"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Trinidad Doubles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Trinidad%20Doubles","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Cucumber","Flour","Chickpeas","Tamarind","Cumin","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"tostones","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 green plantains, green as a traffic light, no yellow"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garlic dipping sauce: mashed garlic, oil, lime (mojo)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: hot sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel (score the skin lengthwise, lever it off); slice 2.5cm rounds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"First fry: 160°C until tender and pale gold, 3–4 min. Drain.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Smash each flat with a cup bottom or tostonera.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Second fry: 190°C until shatter-crisp. Salt in the air the second they land.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The double fry is non-negotiable: the first fry cooks the plantain through, the smash creates the surface, the second fry crisps it, the same two-stage physics as great French fries, with a flatter middle step. Dip the smashed rounds in salted garlic water before round two for the Puerto Rican abuela upgrade; the brine seasons the cracks and steams the inside tender. Green as a traffic light, no yellow: sweetness is for maduros, and a tostón's whole job is starch and salt.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Scoring"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tostones%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tostones"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Tostones","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Tostones","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Plantain","Lime","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["scoring"]},{"slug":"rum-punch","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 part lime juice ('one of sour')"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 parts sugar syrup or grenadine ('two of sweet')"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 parts dark Caribbean rum ('three of strong')"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 parts fruit juice, orange + pineapple ('four of weak')"},{"kind":"item","text":"Angostura bitters, nutmeg, ice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir sour, sweet, strong, weak in a jug.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add a good dash of Angostura.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill properly: punch is best after an hour making friends with itself.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve over ice; grate nutmeg on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The recipe is a 300-year-old rhyme (one of sour, two of sweet, three of strong, four of weak) and once you say it aloud you will never look it up again; planter's punch built the Caribbean bar on that meter. The hour's chill is real technique: punch mellows as it sits, the rum's edges rounding into the fruit. Angostura and fresh-grated nutmeg are the two finishing moves that separate island punch from fruit juice with ambitions. Scale it by the jug; punch was never a single-serving idea.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rum%20Punch%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rum Punch"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Rum Punch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Rum%20Punch","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lime","Orange","Pineapple","Sugar","Nutmeg"]},{"slug":"jollof-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g long-grain parboiled rice, rinsed hard"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 tomatoes + 2 red bell peppers + 1 scotch bonnet + 1 onion, blended"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp tomato paste, 100ml oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp curry powder, thyme, bay, stock cubes (this is tradition, not shame)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Extra onion, sliced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Reduce the blended base in oil with tomato paste until it darkens and the oil rises: 20 min, do not rush the fry.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season hard: curry powder, thyme, bay, stock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in rice; add water just level with the rice; cover with foil then lid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lowest heat 30 min. Let the bottom catch a little: that smoky base note is the point.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The slightly burnt bottom layer is prized from Lagos to Accra: party jollof tastes of smoke on purpose, and cooks chase that flavor deliberately with a hard final blast of heat under a closed lid. The base is a fried-down blend of tomato, red pepper and scotch bonnet, reduced until the oil stains red before the rice ever arrives: rushed base, flat jollof. As for Ghana versus Nigeria: the jollof wars are real, the trash talk is international sport, and I cook both and stay out of it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Jollof & party smoke","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","umami-deep","smoky","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Jollof & party smoke","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=jollof%20rice%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jollof%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jollof Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Jollof Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Jollof%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"scotch-irish-and-world-whisky","term":"Scotch, Irish & World Whisky"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Thyme","Bay leaf","Rice","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"egusi-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g ground egusi (melon seeds)"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g assorted meat/stockfish/tripe, or beef chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp palm oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Blended pepper mix: tomato, red bell, scotch bonnet, onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 big handfuls spinach or bitterleaf; crayfish powder, stock"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer meats seasoned until tender; keep the stock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry pepper mix in palm oil; mix egusi with a little water into a paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add egusi in clumps; let them set 10 min before breaking into curds.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Loosen with stock, return meat, wilt in the greens. Serve with pounded yam or fufu.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Let the egusi lumps steam-set before stirring: those curds are the texture people fight over, and stirring too soon collapses them into porridge instead of soup. The ground melon seeds are the protein engine of a dish that predates every imported thickener; palm oil gives the color and the bitter leaf (or spinach standing in) the counterweight. Serve with fufu or pounded yam doing the work of cutlery: the soup is built for scooping, not spooning.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","fiery","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, real fire that builds and stays, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egusi%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Egusi Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Egusi Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Egusi%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Crawfish","Liver & Offal","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Spinach","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"suya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef sirloin, sliced thin against the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yaji: 4 tbsp ground roasted peanuts (oil pressed out), 1 tbsp ginger, 1 tbsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne, 1 tsp garlic powder, 1 stock cube, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil, skewers"},{"kind":"item","text":"Raw onion rings, tomato, extra yaji to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make yaji: peanuts must be roasted, ground, and squeezed in paper until almost dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil the beef; press yaji into every face; thread ribbons on skewers; rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill hot and fast, 3 min a side, brushing with oil.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve wrapped in paper with raw onion and a dusting of fresh yaji.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nigerian street mastery: the peanut in the yaji must be dry or the crust turns to paste on the grill: kuli-kuli, the defatted groundnut cake, is the traditional base precisely because raw ground peanuts weep oil into mush. Suya without extra yaji dusted on at the end is just kebab; the double application, before and after the fire, is the mallam's signature. Thin-sliced beef, screaming coals, and raw onion on the side: the full Kaduna night-market specification.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","charred","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, char used as punctuation, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Suya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Suya"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Suya","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Suya","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sirloin-tri-tip-and-picanha","term":"Sirloin, Tri-Tip & Picanha"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Peanut","Stock/Broth","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"chicken-yassa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut up"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 onions (yes, six) sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of 4 limes + 4 tbsp Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, whole; 4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil, bay, green olives, rice to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate chicken in lime, mustard, garlic and a third of the onions, overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Char the chicken hard on a grill or in a pan; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stew ALL the onions in oil, low and slow, 30 min, until melting and sweet.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add marinade, bonnet and chicken; simmer 30 min. Olives at the end.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Senegal's gift: the onions are the sauce: six of them collapse into something between confit and gravy, sharpened by lime and mustard, and the rule is melt, never brown; browned onions make a different, lesser dish. The mustard is the colonial French inheritance turned to local advantage, and the long lime marinade does double duty as tenderizer and seasoning. Casamance claims the original, made with the limes the region grows in absurd abundance. Serve over broken rice like Dakar does.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Caramelizing onions","Charring over open flame","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Yassa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Yassa"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Chicken Yassa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Chicken%20Yassa","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Olives","Lime","Bay leaf","Rice","Mustard","Clove"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"mafe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g beef or lamb chunks (or sweet potato + cabbage for veg)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g natural peanut butter, unsweetened"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp tomato paste, 400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 4 garlic, 1 scotch bonnet"},{"kind":"item","text":"Carrots, sweet potato, cabbage wedges; rice to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown meat; fry onion, garlic, tomato paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add tomatoes and water; simmer meat 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk peanut butter with hot broth; stir in; add vegetables.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered 30 min until the oil rises to the top and the sauce clings.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"When the peanut oil breaks and beads on the surface, the mafe is done; that sheen is West Africa's version of a reduced French sauce, the visual proof that the peanut butter has cooked from raw paste into gravy. Undercooked mafe tastes like satay that lost its way; broken mafe tastes like Sunday in Bamako. The stew tolerates almost any meat or none, which is why it feeds the whole Sahel. Scotch bonnet floated whole, sweet potatoes drinking the sauce: patience is the only rare ingredient.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mafe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mafe"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Mafe","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Mafe","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Sweet potato","Carrot","Chiles","Cabbage","Butter","Rice","Peanut","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"fufu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g cassava (or plantain + cassava mix; or cassava flour for the fast road)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt (controversial; many say none)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A strong wooden spoon and stronger forearms"},{"kind":"item","text":"Any soup with 'swallow' status alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil peeled cassava until completely soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound (or beat in a stand mixer with paddle, modern kitchen truce) adding splashes of hot water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work until stretchy, smooth and lump-free: it should slump slowly, not hold peaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape into smooth mounds with wet hands. Serve with egusi or mafe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fufu is cutlery: pinch, roll, dent, scoop, swallow; chewing is technically allowed but culturally discouraged, and the dent is functional, a spoon shaped from the starch itself. The pounding (or the modern flour shortcut) aims for one texture: smooth, stretchy, and firm enough to hold its scoop of soup. It has no flavor ambitions of its own, and that is the design: fufu is the vehicle, the soup is the journey, and eating it with utensils misses the entire point of its existence.","equipment":["Stand mixer"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fufu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fufu"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Fufu","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Fufu","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"stand-mixer-and-attachments","term":"Stand Mixer & Attachments"},{"slug":"the-kitchen-torch","term":"The Kitchen Torch"}],"pantryItems":["Plantain","Flour"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"kelewele","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 ripe plantains, yellow with black spots"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp grated ginger, 2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne, ½ tsp nutmeg, pinch of cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, splash of water to make a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying; roasted peanuts to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cube plantains.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toss in the ginger-spice paste; sit 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 180°C until mahogany at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt, scatter peanuts. Best eaten from paper at dusk, Accra-style.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ripe plantain caramelizes where green plantain crisps: kelewele is candy-edged and fluffy inside, the opposite argument to tostones, and both are right. The ginger-chili-spice bath is what separates it from plain fried plantain: Ghana's night-market vendors marinate the cubes so the edges char with spice rather than just sugar. The plantains should be yellow-black and past their polite prime: the uglier the peel, the better the kelewele. Sold in newspaper cones after dark, eaten walking.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kelewele%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kelewele"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Kelewele","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Kelewele","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Plantain","Peanut","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"thieboudienne","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick fish steaks (grouper/snapper); stuffing: parsley, garlic, chili pounded to a paste (rof)"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g broken rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp tomato paste, 1 onion, dried fish or shrimp (optional funk)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Vegetables: cassava, carrot, cabbage, aubergine, okra"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tamarind or lime"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slit fish; pack with rof paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry tomato paste and onion in oil; add water, vegetables, fish; simmer: fish out at 15 min, veg when tender.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook rice IN that red broth until it drinks it all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Platter: red rice, fish, vegetables arranged like a market stall; scrape the crust from the pot for the elders.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Senegal's national dish and jollof's acknowledged ancestor, the one-pot fish-and-rice that traveled the coast and became a continent's argument. The crusty rice at the pot's bottom (xoon) is served with ceremony; burn it a little on purpose, because the household's best eater gets first claim. The stuffed fish, herbs and garlic pounded into slits, is the technique that marks a serious cook. UNESCO listed the dish itself as cultural heritage, which surprises nobody in Dakar.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Jollof & party smoke","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=jollof%20rice%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Thieboudienne%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Thieboudienne"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Thieboudienne","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Thieboudienne","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Shrimp","White fish","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Chiles","Cabbage","Eggplant","Okra","Lime","Parsley","Rice","Tomato paste","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste"]},{"slug":"moin-moin","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g black-eyed peas, soaked and SKINNED (rub in water, skins float off)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red bell pepper, ½ onion, 1 scotch bonnet"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil or palm oil, stock cube, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: boiled egg quarters, flaked fish"},{"kind":"item","text":"Banana leaves, ramekins or foil cups"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend skinned peas with pepper, onion and minimal water: thick as pancake batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in oil and seasoning until airy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into leaves or cups; tuck in egg if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam 45–50 min until set like firm custard.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false}],"note":"Skinning the beans is the tedious step everyone tries to skip and always regrets: skins make it coarse and bitter; rub, float, discard, thank yourself. The batter steams into something between a savory custard and a pudding, and the leaf-wrapped version (banana or uma leaves) perfumes what the foil version merely contains. Moin moin's texture is the whole exam: silk-smooth batter, gentle steam, and patience. Nigeria serves it beside jollof at every party that respects itself.","equipment":["Ramekins"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Jollof & party smoke","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=jollof%20rice%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moin%20Moin%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moin Moin"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Moin Moin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Moin%20Moin","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Bell pepper","Chiles","Peas","Banana","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"puff-puff","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp yeast, 80g sugar, ½ tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"¼ tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"~280ml warm water: batter, not dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying; sugar to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk everything into a thick, stretchy batter; rise 1 h until bubbly.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat oil to 170°C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scoop with your hand: squeeze batter through your fist and thumb into balls (or use two spoons, coward's route).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry, rolling, until deep gold; dust with sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The hand-squeeze drop is the true technique and worth learning: batter gripped in the fist, a ball extruded between thumb and forefinger, flicked into the oil: perfectly round puff puff marks you as someone who was taught, not someone who watched a video. The batter is a wet yeast dough that doubles into sourness if you let it: one rise, no more. Rolled in sugar while hot, sold in bags at every Nigerian gathering, and structurally identical cousins span the continent: mikate, bofrot, mandazi.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puff%20Puff%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Puff Puff"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ndudu by Fafa on Puff Puff","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndudu%20by%20Fafa%20Puff%20Puff","sub":"A YouTube search for Ndudu by Fafa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour","Sugar","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"feijoada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g black beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g mixed pork: shoulder, ribs, smoked sausage, bacon (traditionally ears and trotters too)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, 6 garlic cloves, 3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Orange slices, white rice, collard ribbons, farofa"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer beans with bay; separately brown the meats in stages.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Unite everything; simmer 2–3 h, lazy bubbles, until the broth turns black velvet.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mash a ladle of beans against the pot wall to thicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with rice, quick-fried collards, farofa and orange slices: the orange is digestive strategy, not decoration.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brazil's Saturday ritual: feijoada is eaten at noon because nobody is expected to function afterward, and the full apparatus (rice, collards, farofa, orange) is the dish; the pot alone is only half the ceremony. The orange cuts the richness and the old wisdom says it helps digest the pork. Trust two centuries of precedent. Build it on the cheap smoked and salted cuts; the black beans want their fat and smoke. The crackling farofa on top is texture insurance no serious table skips.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Soaking dried goods back to life","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Feijoada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Feijoada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Sausage","Onion","Garlic","Kale/Collards","Orange","Bay leaf","Rice","Black beans","Hot sauce","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"pao-de-queijo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g sour tapioca starch (polvilho azedo, the sour matters)"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk + 60ml oil + 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g grated cheese: half parmesan, half mozzarella (Minas cheese if you can dream it)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nothing else: gluten free by birth, not by marketing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil milk, oil and salt; pour over the starch and beat: it will look wrong; continue.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in eggs one at a time, then cheese, into a sticky dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll golf balls with oiled hands; freeze the extras raw: they bake perfectly from frozen.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 180°C, 20–25 min: hollow, chewy, crackle-skinned.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"The chew comes from tapioca's long starch: no gluten involved, which is why the crumb stretches and squeaks in a way wheat can't imitate; sour tapioca starch (polvilho azedo) adds the faint tang Minas Gerais considers correct. The scalded-starch dough is the technique: hot milk and oil poured over the tapioca gelatinizes it before the cheese and eggs arrive. Brazilians keep frozen dough balls the way others keep ice cubes: adopt this immediately; they bake from frozen without complaint.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=P%C3%A3o%20de%20Queijo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pão de Queijo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"marketing-and-the-regulars-math","term":"Marketing & the Regulars Math"},{"slug":"gluten-and-hydration","term":"Gluten & Hydration"},{"slug":"mozzarella-di-bufala-campana","term":"Mozzarella di Bufala Campana"},{"slug":"frozen-dessert-machines-churners-and-pacojet","term":"Frozen Dessert Machines: Churners & Pacojet"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Mozzarella"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"moqueca-baiana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g firm white fish, thick chunks, lime-and-salted 15 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 1 red + 1 yellow pepper, 3 tomatoes, all in rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp dendê (red palm oil), the flavor of Bahia"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro, lime; rice and farofa to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Layer vegetables then fish in a wide clay or heavy pan: moqueca is built, not stirred.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour coconut milk over; simmer gently 15 min, spooning sauce over the fish.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ribbon in the dendê at the end; it should marble the surface orange.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bury in cilantro; serve bubbling, in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dendê oil is non-negotiable for Bahian moqueca: it is the difference between a coconut fish stew and the coast of Bahia in a pot, red palm oil carrying the Afro-Brazilian signature the dish is named for. No stirring: the fish stays in proud pieces, and the pot is rocked, not spooned. Layer everything raw and let it braise: moqueca is assembly plus restraint. The rival capixaba version from Espírito Santo uses annatto and no coconut; Bahia considers this a rough draft.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moqueca%20Baiana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moqueca Baiana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Tomato","Lime","Coconut","Cilantro","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"brigadeiro","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 can (395g) sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp good cocoa powder, sifted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chocolate sprinkles for rolling"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir everything (except sprinkles) in a nonstick pan over medium-low, constantly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until the mass pulls away and you can see the pan bottom for a full second when you drag the spoon, 10–12 min.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely; butter your palms; roll into balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll through sprinkles; serve in little paper cups.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The drag-test is everything: too soon and they slump, too far and they turn to fudge cement. Brazilians also eat it warm from the pan with a spoon, 'brigadeiro de colher', which is not a failure, it is a lifestyle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brigadeiro%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Brigadeiro"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"nonstick-honestly","term":"Nonstick, Honestly"}],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Bread","Chocolate/Cocoa"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"ceviche-clasico","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g impeccably fresh white fish (sole, sea bass), 2cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"10–12 limes, juiced at the last minute"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, shaved thin, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ají limo or habanero, minced; cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sweet potato and choclo corn to serve; salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the fish 2 min: it firms the flesh.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Douse in lime; steep 5–10 minutes only, stirring once. Milky-opaque edges, raw heart.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in onion, chili, cilantro.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately, with the leche de tigre (the milky lime broth) pooling, sweet potato alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Modern Lima cured the old hours-long soak: ceviche is a ten-minute dish: the fish 'cooks' in citric acid only at its surface, staying silk at the center, and the old marathon marinades were making ceviche jerky. The leche de tigre, the milky cure left in the bowl, is drunk as a hangover remedy with a straight face and sold in glasses on its own. Bitter orange was the original acid before limes arrived; the ají and red onion are not garnish but load-bearing. Cold plates, cold fish, no waiting.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Ceviche","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","fiery","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, real fire that builds and stays, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Ceviche","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ceviche%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ceviche%20Cl%C3%A1sico%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ceviche Clásico"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Potato","Sweet potato","Chiles","Corn","Lime","Cilantro"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"lomo-saltado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef sirloin strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, thick wedges; 2 tomatoes, wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp soy sauce + 1 tbsp vinegar (Peru's Chinese heritage, chifa)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ají amarillo, sliced; garlic, cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Thick-cut fries + white rice, yes, both"}],"steps":[{"text":"Get a wok or pan violently hot; sear beef in batches; remove.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flash onion 1 min; tomato 1 min: they must stay standing.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":60,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beef back in; soy and vinegar down the pan wall so they hit steel and flame.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in fried potatoes, cilantro; serve with rice. Double carbs are the identity.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lomo saltado is Cantonese wok technique speaking Spanish: chifa cooking, born when Chinese railroad and plantation workers married the wok and soy sauce to Peru's beef and ají. The fries go IN the stir-fry, soaking soy juices while staying half-crisp: the best bite on any Peruvian table and the detail that separates the dish from beef stir-fry with a side. Violent heat, small batches, and the tomatoes barely kissed by the pan so they keep their shape.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lomo%20Saltado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lomo Saltado"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lomo-embuchado","term":"Lomo Embuchado"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Cilantro","Rice","Soy sauce","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"aji-de-gallina","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g poached chicken, shredded; keep the broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp ají amarillo paste: the yellow soul of Peru"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices white bread, soaked in 200ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g walnuts + 40g parmesan"},{"kind":"item","text":"Onion, garlic; boiled potatoes, rice, black olives, egg to plate"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry onion, garlic and ají amarillo paste until it deepens to gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the milk-soaked bread with walnuts; add to the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Loosen with broth to a thick, spoon-coating cream; melt in the parmesan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in chicken; serve over potato slices with rice, olives, and quartered egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bread and walnuts thickening a chili cream, a colonial-era technique still unbeaten, descended from Moorish-Spanish white sauces and rebuilt around Peru's pepper. Ají amarillo is fruity before it is hot; there is no substitute, only compromise, and the jarred paste is honest when fresh pods aren't. Poached and shredded chicken drinks the sauce in a way cubes never do. Boiled potato and a black olive underneath and on top: the Lima plating grammar, older than anyone asking why.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","briny","nutty-toasted","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, a briny, maritime salinity, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aj%C3%AD%20de%20Gallina%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ají de Gallina"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Olives","Milk","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Rice","Bread","Stock/Broth","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"arepas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g precooked white corn meal (masarepa, NOT masa harina, not polenta)"},{"kind":"item","text":"~400ml warm water, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp oil or butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fillings: shredded beef, black beans and cheese, reina pepiada (chicken-avocado)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the pan"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix meal, salt, water; rest 5 min; knead 1 min into soft, crack-free dough.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat into 2cm-thick discs, smoothing the edges with wet fingers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle in a little butter, 5–6 min a side, until spotted and hollow-sounding.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Split like pita; stuff while steaming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Colombia eats them plain with butter and cheese; Venezuela splits and stuffs them like sandwiches. Make both, declare no winner, keep the peace at your table. Masarepa (precooked corn meal) is the non-negotiable ingredient: masa harina is a different corn process and makes tortillas, not arepas. The griddle-then-oven two-step gets you a crust that knocks hollow with a steamy middle. Reina pepiada (avocado and chicken) is Venezuela's finest argument for the stuffed camp.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Arepas","Working with masa","Griddle & plancha work","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Shaping by hand","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Arepas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20arepas%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arepas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arepas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"steaming-and-en-papillote","term":"Steaming & En Papillote"},{"slug":"line-slang-86-in-the-weeds-and-family-meal","term":"Line Slang: 86, In the Weeds & Family Meal"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Corn","Avocado","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cornmeal/Polenta","Black beans"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"bife-con-chimichurri","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 thick ribeyes or a 700g flank, room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse salt: that is the entire steak seasoning"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chimichurri: big bunch parsley, 4 garlic cloves, 1 tbsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp red wine vinegar, 120ml olive oil, chili flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Patience and good coals if possible"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chop chimichurri by hand: a blender makes pesto sludge; rest it 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the beef; grill over steady heat, turning ONCE, to a proper crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the meat as long as you cooked it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice against the grain; spoon chimichurri over: never marinate with it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Argentine asado rules: salt only on the meat, sauce only after: chimichurri is a finishing sauce and a table sauce, and cooking it kills the parsley and the argument. The sauce is an emulsion of nothing: oil, vinegar, dried oregano, fresh parsley and garlic, loose by design, spooned over char. Asado is slow fire and patience worn as identity; a bife at home honors it with a violent sear and a proper rest. The vinegar in the chimichurri is doing the work lemon does elsewhere, acid against fat.","equipment":["Blender","Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","charred","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Asado fire","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=argentine%20asado%20fire%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bife%20con%20Chimichurri%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bife con Chimichurri"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Chiles","Parsley","Oregano","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"empanadas-saltenas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 400g flour, 100g lard or butter, 1 tsp salt, ~180ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g beef, hand-chopped small (never minced, says Salta)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, spring onion tops, cumin, paprika, chili"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 boiled eggs, chopped; green olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"Egg wash if baking; oil if frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Supple dough; rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cook onions soft; add beef briefly: juicy, underdone slightly; cool COLD (the fat sets and stays inside).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill rounds with meat, egg and an olive; seal with the repulgue braid-fold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 220°C 15 min or fry until blistered.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"The repulgue (rope crimp) is regional ID: each Argentine province folds differently so you know what filling you grabbed before the first bite; twelve folds is the Salta standard and bakers count. Cold filling is the anti-leak law: warm filling melts the fat, soaks the dough and bursts the seam in the oven. Salta's empanadas run juicy on purpose (hand-cut beef, potato, scallion), and the correct posture is the doubles lean: elbows out, empanada tilted, juice hitting pavement instead of shirt.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Empanada folding","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Empanada folding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=empanada%20repulgue%20folding%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Empanadas%20Salte%C3%B1as%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Empanadas Salteñas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20American%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South American kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Chiles","Olives","Butter","Flour","Cumin","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"pierogi-ruskie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 400g flour, 200ml warm water, 1 egg, 2 tbsp sour cream, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filling: 500g potatoes, boiled; 200g farmer cheese or twaróg; fried onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter, a scandalous amount"},{"kind":"item","text":"More onions, fried gold; sour cream to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: bacon lardons for the top"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soft, tender dough; rest under a bowl 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash potatoes with cheese and fried onion; season boldly: cold filling mutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll thin; cut circles; fill, fold, pinch a tight seam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil until they float + 1 min; then fry in butter until gold-edged. Bury in onions and sour cream.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"'Ruskie' means Ruthenian, not Russian, a distinction Poland will happily explain at length, the name pointing to the old eastern borderlands where the potato-and-cheese filling was born. Boil-then-butter-fry is the only correct final form: the boil cooks the dough, the fry in butter (ideally with onions) gives the blistered edges that make the dish. Seal the edges dry and pinch hard: a pierogi that opens in the pot donates its filling to the water.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pierogi","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Salted water & the float test","Mashing & puréeing","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pierogi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20pierogi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pierogi%20Ruskie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pierogi Ruskie"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Flour"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"barszcz","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"5 beets, 3 roasted or raw-grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Good vegetable or beef stock, 1.5L"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 2 garlic cloves, bay, allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp vinegar or beet sour (kwas), 1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sour cream, dill; uszka dumplings if it is Christmas"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer beets and aromatics in stock 40 min.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain for clear barszcz (Polish, ruby consommé) or blend for the thick road.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Balance sharply: vinegar and sugar until it bites and hums.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with sour cream and dill, or crystal-clear with tiny mushroom uszka.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The soup must be SOUR: that is what barszcz means, and the old method built the sourness from fermented beet kvass before vinegar became the shortcut. Add the acid off the boil to keep the color garnet; long boiling turns beets the color of regret, because the pigment breaks down with heat and time. Clear barszcz with uszka dumplings is the Christmas Eve formal version; the everyday bowl takes cream and a boiled potato without apology.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","rich","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Barszcz%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Barszcz"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"consomme-and-clarification","term":"Consommé & Clarification"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Beets","Berries","Dill","Bay leaf","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Vinegar","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Clove","Allspice"],"standard":{"slug":"barszcz","marks":[{"id":"barszcz#deep","text":"Deep luminous garnet; a spoonful held to the light reads red-purple, not brick, brown or dusty pink."},{"id":"barszcz#strained","text":"The strained version is genuinely clear: light passes through the bowl with no cloud or sediment; the blended version is uniformly smooth with no fibrous beet threads."},{"id":"barszcz#nothing","text":"Nothing rides the surface and nothing strays into the bowl: no fat beads, no scum, no loose allspice berry or bay fragment; the only solids present are the ones put there deliberately."},{"id":"barszcz#enough","text":"Hot enough to steam but off the boil: a spoon of sour cream stirred through blends to an even pink without curdling into flecks or beading at the rim."},{"id":"barszcz#sour","text":"Sour first and hard enough to tighten the jaw, sugar arriving a beat behind to round it, salt last; if the first spoon reads sweet earth before it reads sour, it is not barszcz yet."}],"fault":"The vinegar went in early and the pot kept boiling, so the soup arrives a dull brownish brick with the sourness cooked flat, tasting merely of sweet earth."}},{"slug":"chicken-paprikash","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs, bone-in"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp sweet Hungarian paprika, fresh tin, not the dusty relic"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, minced fine; 1 tomato; 1 Hungarian wax pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml sour cream + 1 tbsp flour, whisked; nokedli or noodles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown chicken; set aside. Melt onions slowly in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"OFF THE HEAT stir in paprika: 30 seconds, no more, it burns bitter fast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chicken back with stock, tomato, pepper; simmer 35 min.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Temper the flour-sour cream with hot sauce, then stir in; do not boil again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two Hungarian laws: paprika never touches direct heat, and sour cream never boils. Obey both and the sauce is velvet; break either and you will taste your mistake, scorched-bitter in the first case, curdled in the second. The paprika goes into the fat off the flame, blooming without burning, and the sour cream gets tempered in at the end. Sweet Hungarian paprika by the spoonful, not the pinch: it is the sauce's body, not its decoration. Nokedli dumplings underneath are the full citizenship.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Paprikash%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Paprikash"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"teliszalami-hungarian-winter-salami","term":"Téliszalámi (Hungarian Winter Salami)"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Tomato","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Noodles","Flour","Stock/Broth","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"gulyas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g beef shin or chuck, 2cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, minced, equal in weight to your courage"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp sweet paprika + 1 tsp hot; caraway seeds, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, 2 parsnips, 2 potatoes; 1 pepper, 1 tomato"},{"kind":"item","text":"Csipetke: pinched flour-egg noodles (optional but loved)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the mountain of onions in lard until gold, 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off heat: paprika. Then beef, caraway, garlic; stew in its own juice 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Water to soup depth; simmer 1.5 h.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Root vegetables and potatoes for the last 30; pinch in csipetke at the end.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Gulyás is a herdsman's SOUP in Hungary: the thick 'goulash' abroad is a different animal (that one is pörkölt), and the confusion has annoyed Budapest for a century. The onion mass dissolving into the broth is your thickener; no flour needed, just patience while a mountain of onions melts into the paprika fat. Csipetke, pinched noodles dropped in at the end, are the traditional finish. The dish rode out of the Hungarian plains with the cattle drivers whose name it keeps.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Guly%C3%A1s%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gulyás"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"caraway-and-nigella","term":"Caraway & Nigella"},{"slug":"scales-and-measuring-by-weight","term":"Scales & Measuring by Weight"},{"slug":"chuck","term":"Chuck"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Parsnip","Noodles","Flour","Paprika","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"beef-stroganoff","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef fillet or sirloin, finger strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g mushrooms, thick slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion; 1 tbsp flour; 1 tsp mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml beef stock, 150ml sour cream (smetana)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter; fried shoestring potatoes or buttered noodles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear beef in butter fast and hard, in batches, pink inside; remove.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown mushrooms properly; add onion; dust with flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stock and mustard; reduce to a light gravy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sour cream in, beef back, one minute, done. Never boil, never overcook the fillet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A 19th-century Russian count's dish: the beef is barely cooked twice, which means barely cooked once each time: seared in ripping heat, removed, and returned only to warm through in the sauce; gray stroganoff beef is the most common tragedy in home kitchens. The mustard in the sauce is the old Petersburg signature, there in the earliest printed recipe. Sour cream off the boil, always. Egg noodles or shoestring potatoes underneath, depending which side of the emigration you learned it from.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beef%20Stroganoff%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beef Stroganoff"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"boning-fillet-and-the-butchery-blades","term":"Boning, Fillet & the Butchery Blades"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Potato","Mushrooms","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Noodles","Flour","Stock/Broth","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"golubtsy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 large cabbage, cored"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g minced pork-beef mix + 150g cooked rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated carrot, garlic, dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 400g tomatoes + 200ml stock + 2 tbsp sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bay, allspice, butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soften cabbage: freeze whole overnight then thaw (the lazy genius method), or blanch and peel leaves.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix filling; roll burrito-tight in leaves, shaving thick ribs flat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Nestle seam-down in a pot lined with spare leaves.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with sauce; braise low 1–1.5 h. Better tomorrow, best the day after.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true}],"note":"The freezer trick replaces twenty minutes of scalding your fingers peeling blanched leaves: freeze the whole cabbage overnight, thaw it, and the cell walls have collapsed so the leaves fold like silk. Roll them seam-down and pack the pot tight; loose golubtsy unroll and become soup. The tomato-sour cream braise wants low heat and time, and the dish is better the next day, like most of its relatives from the Baltic to the Black Sea: every family east of Vienna owns a version and a grandmother who made it best.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Braising","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Golubtsy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Golubtsy"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"blanch-and-shock","term":"Blanch & Shock"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Cabbage","Dill","Bay leaf","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Rice","Stock/Broth","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["braising","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"adjaruli-khachapuri","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 350g flour, 200ml warm milk-water, 5g yeast, 1 tsp sugar, salt, oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g cheese: mozzarella + feta + sulguni if the gods provide"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nothing else needs to exist"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soft dough; rise 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Roll ovals; pile cheese down the middle; roll the long sides in and twist the ends into a boat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 240°C, 12–14 min until the crust bronzes and cheese bubbles.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":840,"estimated":false},{"text":"Crater the center; drop in a yolk and butter; return 1 min. Stir at the table; tear crust and drag.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Georgia's cheese boat: the yolk cooks in residual heat as you stir it into the molten cheese with a chunk of butter, and the crust is your spoon. Tear from the pointed ends and drag through the middle. Sharing is technically possible. The Adjaruli, from the Black Sea coast, is shaped like the boats in its harbors; the sulguni-imeruli cheese blend is the target, approximated abroad with mozzarella-plus-feta. Georgia treats khachapuri seriously enough to track its price as an inflation index.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Khachapuri shaping","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Khachapuri shaping","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=khachapuri%20adjaruli%20shaping%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Adjaruli%20Khachapuri%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Adjaruli Khachapuri"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sulguni","term":"Sulguni"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Feta","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"kartoffelpuffer-latkes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g starchy potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, 2 tbsp flour or matzo meal, salt, pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil, generous"},{"kind":"item","text":"Apple sauce AND sour cream: serve both, let people reveal themselves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate potatoes and onion; SQUEEZE in a towel like you are wringing out a grudge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the starch settle in the squeezed liquid 5 min; pour off water, scrape the white starch paste back in.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix with egg and flour; fry thin patties in 1cm of properly hot oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, never paper. Salt immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Reclaiming the settled starch is the old trick that glues the latke without extra flour: pour off the pink water from the squeezed shreds, keep the white paste at the bottom, and fold it back in. Wet shreds are the enemy: squeeze twice, apologize to no one. Fry in enough fat to float the lace edges, and hold finished ones in a warm oven on a rack, never stacked. Applesauce or sour cream is a border-crossing argument the potato itself declines to settle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kartoffelpuffer%20%2F%20Latkes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kartoffelpuffer / Latkes"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"starchy-vs-waxy-potatoes","term":"Starchy vs. Waxy Potatoes"},{"slug":"line-slang-86-in-the-weeds-and-family-meal","term":"Line Slang: 86, In the Weeds & Family Meal"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Potato","Apple","Sour cream/Crema","Flour"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"syrniki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g dry farmer cheese / twaróg (drain quark overnight if that is your lot)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour + more for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar, vanilla, pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter; sour cream, jam or condensed milk to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mash cheese smooth; mix in yolk, sugar, flour: minimum flour for maximum cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape thick pucks with floured hands; dust the sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in butter, medium-low, 3–4 min a side, gold and slightly puffed.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve warm, with sour cream and jam fighting for territory.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The wetter the cheese, the more flour you need, the worse the syrniki: drain the cheese, not the recipe. A proper syrnik is 80% cheese and tastes like cheesecake's country cousin.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Syrniki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Syrniki"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"bigos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g sauerkraut, drained (rinse only if brutal) + 300g fresh cabbage"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g mixed meats: pork shoulder, kielbasa, bacon, leftover roast; bigos forgives"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g dried forest mushrooms, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, prunes 6–8, juniper berries, bay, allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Splash of red wine or the mushroom water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the meats in stages; soften onion in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Everything in the pot: krauts, mushrooms and their liquor, prunes, spices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer, barely, 2–3 h, stirring from the bottom.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool, sleep, reheat: bigos is legally required to be better the next day, and the day after that.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Poland's hunter's stew has no fixed recipe, only a law: at least two cabbages, at least two meats, and never eaten on the day it was made. Freeze and rewarm it: it improves like an argument you finally won.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","warm-spiced","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, warm spice humming through the middle, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bigos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bigos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"kabanosy-and-the-kielbasa-family","term":"Kabanosy & the Kielbasa Family"},{"slug":"wild-mushrooms-the-forest-calendar","term":"Wild Mushrooms: The Forest Calendar"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Sausage","Onion","Mushrooms","Cabbage","Berries","Dried fruit","Bay leaf","Wine","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"fish-and-chips","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick cod or haddock fillets, skinned, patted bone-dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Batter: 200g flour + 50g rice flour, 1 tsp baking powder, 300ml ICE-COLD beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg floury potatoes (Maris Piper), cut fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"Beef dripping or neutral oil for the fryer"},{"kind":"item","text":"Malt vinegar, flaky salt, mushy peas, tartare"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chips first, twice: blanch at 130°C until tender and pale; drain and cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the batter at the last second: cold, barely mixed, lumps welcome.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flour the fish, then batter; fry at 180°C, 6–8 min, until amber and rigid.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chips back in at 190°C until bronzed. Salt everything; vinegar like you mean it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold beer and rice flour are the crunch conspiracy: cold slows gluten development, rice flour never softens, and together they make a batter that cracks like an eggshell over steaming fish. Dust the fillets in flour first or the batter slides off in the fryer. Chips want the double fry; the fish wants one violent one. Beef dripping is the traditional northern medium and worth the pilgrimage. Malt vinegar, over-salting, and eating from the paper are all part of the specification.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fish%20and%20Chips%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fish and Chips"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Fish and Chips","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Fish%20and%20Chips","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"beer-styles-the-two-family-map","term":"Beer Styles: The Two-Family Map"},{"slug":"fryer-setups-and-oil-management","term":"Fryer Setups & Oil Management"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","White fish","Potato","Peas","Flour","Vinegar","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"sunday-roast-beef-and-yorkshire-puddings","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg rib of beef on the bone, room temperature, salted an hour ahead"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yorkshire batter: 140g flour, 4 eggs, 200ml milk, pinch salt, rested 1 h minimum"},{"kind":"item","text":"Beef dripping"},{"kind":"item","text":"Roast potatoes, carrots, greens"},{"kind":"item","text":"Gravy from the pan and real horseradish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blast beef at 240°C for 20 min, then 160°C to 52°C core for rare-medium; rest 30 min under foil.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Meanwhile get dripping SMOKING in a muffin tin, 220°C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour cold batter into screaming fat: half-depth; shut the door 22 min. No peeking; a glance costs an inch of rise.","durationSec":1320,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1320,"estimated":true},{"text":"Carve at the table; puddings serve as gravy boats.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yorkshire law: hot fat, cold batter, closed door: the fat must smoke, the batter must pour cold from the fridge, and the oven door stays shut until the puddings have climbed; the rest of the roast is logistics, the pudding is the theatre. Rest the batter an hour minimum. Rest the beef as long as you dare, tented, because the juices you save become the gravy you serve. The pudding's original job was filling the family up before the meat, economics that became beloved architecture.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sunday%20Roast%20Beef%20and%20Yorkshire%20Puddings%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sunday Roast Beef and Yorkshire Puddings"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Sunday Roast Beef and Yorkshire Puddings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Sunday%20Roast%20Beef%20and%20Yorkshire%20Puddings","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"startup-costs-the-real-number","term":"Startup Costs: The Real Number"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Potato","Carrot","Milk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"shepherds-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g minced lamb (beef makes it cottage pie: the distinction is enforced)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 2 carrots, 2 celery ribs, diced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato purée, 1 tbsp flour, 300ml stock, Worcestershire, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1kg floury potatoes, 100ml milk, 75g butter, egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"A little cheddar for the crust, if your family allows"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown lamb hard; remove. Soften vegetables in the fat; purée and flour in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stock, Worcestershire, thyme; lamb back; simmer 30 min to a thick, glossy ragù.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash potatoes with butter, milk, yolk: firm, not sloppy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile mash over; furrow with a fork (crisping ridges); bake 200°C, 25–30 min until bubbling at the borders.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The fork-furrows are function: ridges brown, valleys stay creamy, and the filling must be thick before baking, because mash cannot fix a soupy foundation; reduce until a spoon dragged through leaves a trail. Shepherd's pie is lamb by definition: the beef version is cottage pie, and the distinction survives every attempt to blur it. Worcestershire and a little tomato paste in the filling are the umami spine. Rest ten minutes out of the oven or serve it as soup with a crust.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shepherd's%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shepherd's Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Shepherd's Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Shepherd's%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Thyme","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"chicken-tikka-masala","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken thighs; marinade: yogurt, lemon, garlic, ginger, garam masala, paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 2 onions, 4 garlic, thumb ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp each cumin, coriander, paprika; 1 tsp turmeric, garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes, 150ml cream, butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sugar, salt, cilantro; rice or naan"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate chicken 2 h+; grill or broil on high till charred at the edges.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry onions gold; add garlic, ginger, spices; cook till fragrant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tomatoes; simmer 15 min; blend silky; back in pan with cream and butter.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Charred chicken in; simmer 10 min; balance with sugar, salt, lemon.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born in Britain of Punjabi parents: legend says a Glasgow chef rescued a dry order with tomato soup and cream. It is now Britain's true national dish, and the char on the chicken is where the tikka still lives.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Tandoor breads","The broiler: heat from above","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","charred","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tandoor breads","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=naan%20tandoor%20technique%20home%20oven","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Tikka%20Masala%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Tikka Masala"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Chicken Tikka Masala","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Chicken%20Tikka%20Masala","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Lemon","Cilantro","Butter","Cream","Yogurt","Rice","Bread","Sugar","Cumin","Paprika","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"bangers-and-mash","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 proper pork sausages (Cumberland or Lincolnshire, high meat content)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1kg floury potatoes, 100g butter, 100ml hot milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Onion gravy: 3 onions, sliced; 1 tbsp flour; 400ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Splash of Worcestershire, tsp of mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"Peas, if your heart says so"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry sausages LOW and slow, 20–25 min, turning often: never prick them.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Meanwhile melt onions in butter 20 min to deep gold; flour in; stock gradually to a glossy gravy.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash: rice or mash the potatoes dry, then beat in butter first, hot milk second.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash in a wave, bangers at anchor, gravy over everything.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pricking sausages drains the juice the casing was protecting: a banger should arrive taut and glossy, cooked gently so the skin never splits; the name comes from wartime sausages so watery they burst, a defect the modern banger honors in name only. Butter before milk in the mash: fat coats the starch first, then milk loosens it, and the order genuinely matters. Onion gravy is the third citizen of the plate: slow onions, proper stock, and patience.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bangers%20and%20Mash%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bangers and Mash"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Bangers and Mash","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Bangers%20and%20Mash","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Sausage","Onion","Potato","Peas","Butter","Milk","Flour","Stock/Broth","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"full-english-breakfast","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 rashers back bacon, 4 good sausages"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, 2 tomatoes halved, big handful mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 can baked beans, warmed (in a pan, have some pride)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pudding, 4 slices (negotiable but recommended)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toast or fried bread; strong tea, mandatory"}],"steps":[{"text":"Order of operations: sausages first (slow), then bacon, tomatoes and black pudding in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mushrooms in butter in their own corner till their water is gone and they brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the beans; keep everything in a low oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Eggs last, basted in the bacon fat; assemble everything on one heroic warm plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The full English is a logistics exam, not a recipe: one pan's fat feeds the next ingredient, and the egg goes down when everyone is seated. Cold toast is the only unforgivable failure.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Full%20English%20Breakfast%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Full English Breakfast"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Full English Breakfast","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Full%20English%20Breakfast","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Sausage","Eggs","Tomato","Mushrooms","Bread"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"cornish-pasty","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry: 500g strong flour, 120g lard + 120g butter, cold, 1 tsp salt, ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g beef skirt, chopped small, never minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 potato + ½ swede (rutabaga), sliced thin; 1 onion, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, heavy black pepper, knob of butter per pasty"},{"kind":"item","text":"Egg wash"}],"steps":[{"text":"Firm pastry: it must survive a mineshaft; rest 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer RAW filling on rounds: potato, swede, onion, beef, hard seasoning, butter on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold and crimp along the side, 20 twists: the Cornish rope.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 200°C 10 min then 170°C 40 min. Rest 15: the inside is lava and gravy.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Protected by law like Champagne: raw filling baked slowly makes its own gravy in a sealed pastry pot. The thick crimp was the miner's handle: arsenic-dusted hands never touched lunch.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cornish%20Pasty%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cornish Pasty"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Cornish Pasty","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Cornish%20Pasty","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Butter","Flour"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking"]},{"slug":"sticky-toffee-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g pitted dates, chopped + 250ml boiling water + 1 tsp bicarb"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter, 150g dark brown sugar, 2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"175g self-raising flour, 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 150g butter, 200g dark brown sugar, 250ml double cream, pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Vanilla ice cream or more cream: both is correct"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak dates in boiling water with the bicarb: they dissolve into toffee sludge. Do not skip the bicarb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream butter and sugar; eggs; fold flour; stir in dates, liquid and all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 180°C, 30–35 min in a tray.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sauce: melt everything, boil 3 min. Stab the warm sponge all over; drown; grill 1 min till it bubbles.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The bicarb breaks the dates down so the sponge is damp and dark, not fruity: that is the whole secret, dissolving the fruit into the crumb itself; poke-and-pour while warm, because a dry sticky toffee is a contradiction in terms. Make more sauce than seems decent: half soaks in, half floods the plate. The Lake District claims the dish through the Sharrow Bay hotel, and Cartmel sells it as a civic identity. Cold cream against hot toffee is the closing argument.","equipment":["Grill","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sticky%20Toffee%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sticky Toffee Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Sticky Toffee Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Sticky%20Toffee%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"victoria-sponge","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"225g soft butter, 225g caster sugar, 4 eggs, 225g self-raising flour, the sacred equal weights"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder, splash of milk, vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Good raspberry jam, 4 big spoonfuls"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml double cream, whipped soft (or classic buttercream)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Caster sugar for dusting, never icing sugar, says the WI"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream butter and sugar until genuinely pale: 5 full minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Eggs one at a time with a spoon of flour riding shotgun against curdling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold remaining flour; divide between two lined 20cm tins; bake 180°C, 22–25 min.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool completely; jam on one, cream on the other; unite. Dust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Weigh your eggs in shells and match butter, sugar and flour to them, the Victorian method that never fails. The Women's Institute judges a sponge on the sides: straight, gold, no shoulder.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Victoria%20Sponge%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Victoria Sponge"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Victoria Sponge","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Victoria%20Sponge","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Berries","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","creaming-butter-and-sugar"]},{"slug":"scones-with-jam-and-clotted-cream","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"350g self-raising flour + 1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g cold butter, cubed; 3 tbsp caster sugar, pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"175ml milk, squeeze of lemon in it, warmed slightly"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten, for the tops"},{"kind":"item","text":"Strawberry jam + clotted cream: order of application is a border dispute"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub butter into flour to crumbs; stir in sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the soured warm milk; mix with a knife blade, barely, 20 seconds; the dough should be rough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat 3cm thick; stamp with a floured cutter, straight down, NO twisting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 220°C, 10–12 min. Serve warm, split by hand.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true}],"note":"Handle it like it owes you money and you will get rocks; handle it like a soap bubble and you will get clouds. Devon puts cream first, Cornwall jam first: serve both and referee nothing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scones%20with%20Jam%20and%20Clotted%20Cream%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Scones with Jam and Clotted Cream"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Scones with Jam and Clotted Cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Scones%20with%20Jam%20and%20Clotted%20Cream","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Berries","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"irish-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg lamb neck or shoulder on the bone, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1kg potatoes: half sliced thin, half left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, sliced; 3 carrots (contested by purists, beloved by everyone else)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L water or light lamb stock, thyme, bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley, butter, and brown soda bread alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Layer in a heavy pot: sliced potatoes, onions, lamb, seasoning; repeat; whole potatoes on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Water to two-thirds up; bare simmer, lid on, 2 h. Resist stirring.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"The bottom potatoes dissolve into the broth; the top ones steam whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Parsley and a knob of butter to finish; serve in deep bowls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The oldest Irish trick: potatoes as both thickener and dumpling: the sliced layer melts to velvet while the whole ones hold, one vegetable doing two jobs, peasant genius. Purists hold the line at mutton, onion and potato; carrots are tolerated, browning is not traditional (the pale stew is authentic), and the long, low simmer is where a cheap cut becomes Sunday. Skim as it goes and salt late: the reduction concentrates everything, including your early mistakes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Irish%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Irish Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Irish Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Irish%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"scotch-irish-and-world-whisky","term":"Scotch, Irish & World Whisky"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Potato","Carrot","Parsley","Thyme","Butter","Bread","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"soda-bread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450g plain flour (or half wholemeal for brown soda)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nothing else. No yeast, no sugar, no kneading, no waiting."}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk dry ingredients; make a well; pour in buttermilk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix with an open hand, claw-shaped, 30 seconds, a soft, shaggy dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape a round on a floured tray; cut a deep cross fully across the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 220°C, 15 min, then 200°C, 25 min: hollow knock underneath means done.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Buttermilk meets bicarbonate and the clock starts: into the oven within minutes, because the carbon dioxide is escaping from the moment they touch; soda bread is chemistry with a deadline. Knead barely, shape roughly: worked dough makes a tough loaf. The cross lets the fairies out, says tradition; lets the heat into the dense center, says physics: both are satisfied by one cut. Day-old soda bread is toast's finest hour, and the butter should be visible from across the room.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Soda%20Bread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Soda Bread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Soda Bread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Soda%20Bread","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Buttermilk","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"colcannon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg floury potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g curly kale or savoy cabbage, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 spring onions, sliced, steeped in 150ml warm milk or cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"125g butter: this is not a misprint"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil potatoes in skins; peel hot (a fork and a towel); mash dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wilt kale in butter until glossy and tender.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the onion-warmed milk and most of the butter; fold through the greens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with a crater in each mound and the last of the butter melting in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The butter lake in the middle is the correct service: each forkful travels from the edge, through the pool, to the mouth. Halloween colcannon once hid a ring, a coin and a thimble: marriage, wealth, or spinsterhood in one bowl.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colcannon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colcannon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Colcannon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Colcannon","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Scallion","Potato","Kale/Collards","Cabbage","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"boxty","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g raw potato, grated + 250g cooked mashed potato"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour, 1 tsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"~200ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"Smoked salmon and crème fraîche, or bacon and eggs, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Squeeze grated raw potato in a towel; save the liquid, let starch settle, add starch back.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix raw, mash, flour and buttermilk into a thick batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry pancakes in butter, medium heat, 4 min a side.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve stacked with the topping of your allegiance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boxty on the griddle, boxty in the pan: the old rhyme promises no husband for the woman who cannot make it; the stakes have relaxed, the recipe hasn't. Half raw grated potato, half mash is the identity: the raw half fries into a crisp lace edge, the mash keeps the heart tender, and neither works alone. Squeeze the raw shreds hard and reclaim the starch, latke-style. Leitrim and the borderlands claim it; butter and sugar or bacon and eggs complete it, era depending.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boxty%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boxty"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Boxty","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Boxty","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"wild-salmon-the-run-calendar","term":"Wild Salmon: The Run Calendar"}],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Salmon","Eggs","Smoked fish","Potato","Butter","Sour cream/Crema","Buttermilk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"dublin-coddle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 good pork sausages"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g thick bacon rashers, cut in ribbons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1kg potatoes, thickly sliced; 3 onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml ham or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley, black pepper, and a bottle of stout for the cook"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch sausages and bacon 2 min (traditional; also washes off nothing important; argue among yourselves).","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Layer onions, potatoes, meats in a pot with pepper and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stock to just cover; lid on; bare simmer or low oven 1.5–2 h.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve pale and proud in bowls with soda bread. It is not pretty. It is perfect.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coddle is Dublin's Saturday-night insurance: it sits happily on the lowest heat for hours waiting for whoever comes home late, which was always its job; Joyce and Behan both put it in print, and the Liberties still defend it. The pale boiled sausages horrify outsiders; the flavor converts them, because everything the sausages and rashers surrender goes into the potatoes and broth. Brown the sausages first if you must: Dublin will know, but Dublin will still eat it with you.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dublin%20Coddle%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dublin Coddle"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Dublin Coddle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Dublin%20Coddle","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Bacon","Sausage","Onion","Potato","Parsley","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"beef-and-guinness-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg beef chuck, big chunks, patted dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flour; 3 onions, 4 carrots, 2 celery ribs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml Guinness + 300ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato purée, brown sugar 1 tsp, thyme, bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mash or colcannon to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown beef HARD in batches: gray meat makes gray stew.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften vegetables; purée in; flour in; scrape the good crust up with a splash of stout.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Everything back with Guinness, stock, thyme, bay; oven 150°C, 2.5 h, lid ajar.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Skim, season, and add the pinch of sugar: stout turns bitter as it reduces; the sugar squares the account.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Guinness gives roasted-coffee depth but leaves a bitter tab; the teaspoon of brown sugar at the end settles it, and skipping that balance is why some pots taste like burnt toast. Brown the beef in batches like every braise demands, and give the stout its full simmer to mellow. If the spoon stands briefly in the gravy, you have arrived. Serve with mash or colcannon and nothing green that isn't legally required; this is pub food, and the pub knows best.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","silky","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a silken, spoon-slow texture, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beef%20and%20Guinness%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beef and Guinness Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Beef and Guinness Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Beef%20and%20Guinness%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Thyme","Flour","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"seafood-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g mixed fish: salmon, smoked haddock, white fish, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g mussels or prawns"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g smoked bacon, diced; 1 onion, 2 celery ribs, 1 leek"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g potatoes, diced; 2 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"600ml fish stock + 300ml cream; dill or chives, brown bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render bacon; sweat vegetables in the fat; flour in for 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stock gradually; potatoes; simmer till tender.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream in; then fish, gentlest heat, 4–5 min: smoked haddock first, delicate fish last.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mussels or prawns for the final 2 min; herbs; black pepper. Brown bread and butter are non-negotiable.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Every pub on the Wild Atlantic Way claims the best chowder, and the smoked fish is the common law: a few ounces of smoked haddock or coley season the whole pot the way bacon seasons beans, and a chowder without it tastes like it never saw the coast. Add the fish in stages by firmness so nothing overcooks. Loose enough to call soup, rich enough to call dinner, and brown soda bread alongside is not a suggestion: it's the other half of the dish.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Seafood%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Seafood Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Seafood Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Seafood%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Shrimp","White fish","Salmon","Mussels/Clams","Smoked fish","Onion","Leek","Potato","Celery","Dill","Chives","Bread","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"champ","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg floury potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 big bunch spring onions (scallions), sliced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil potatoes; drain and steam-dry in the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer scallions in the milk 3 min: the milk turns green-sweet.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mash potatoes; beat in the scallion milk and half the butter till fluffy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mound, crater, remaining butter in the crater. Eat from the outside in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ulster's answer to colcannon: scallions instead of cabbage, and the same butter-well ritual. Simmering the onions in the milk is the difference between champ and mash with bits in.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Champ%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Champ"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Champ","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Champ","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Scallion","Potato","Butter","Milk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"barmbrack","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"350g mixed dried fruit, soaked OVERNIGHT in 300ml strong black tea (+ whiskey, optional but wise)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g self-raising flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"125g brown sugar, 1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp mixed spice (cinnamon, nutmeg, clove)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter, thickly applied, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the fruit in tea until swollen and boozy-plump.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix flour, sugar, spice; beat in egg and the fruit with its remaining tea.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape into a lined loaf tin; bake 170°C, 60–75 min.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap and keep a day before slicing: brack improves in the tin. Toast the ends.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Halloween loaf of fortunes: a ring baked inside foretold marriage within the year; coins, rags and thimbles carried harsher prophecies, and Ireland still sells bracks with the ring in. Tea-soaked fruit needs no butter in the batter; overnight in strong sweet tea plumps the sultanas into little flavor grenades, and the butter belongs on top, in slabs, on a toasted slice. Báirín breac, speckled loaf, is the name and the appearance both.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Barmbrack%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Barmbrack"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Barmbrack","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Barmbrack","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mandoline-and-the-slicing-machines","term":"Mandoline & the Slicing Machines"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"irish-coffee","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"50ml Irish whiskey"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml strong, fresh, HOT coffee"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp demerara sugar: not optional; it holds the cream up"},{"kind":"item","text":"50ml double cream, lightly whipped to pouring-thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"A stemmed, warmed glass"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the glass with hot water; empty it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Coffee and sugar in; stir till dissolved; whiskey in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip cream only until it ribbons, barely thickened.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour cream over the back of a warm spoon to float. Do NOT stir. Drink the coffee through the cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented at Foynes flying-boat terminal to console frozen transatlantic passengers. The sugar raises the coffee's density so the cream floats: it is engineering, not sweetening. Hot lips, cold sip, or you have built it wrong.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Irish%20Coffee%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Irish Coffee"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Donal Skehan on Irish Coffee","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Donal%20Skehan%20Irish%20Coffee","sub":"A YouTube search for Donal Skehan and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"hot-drinks-toddy-irish-coffee-and-the-warm-service","term":"Hot Drinks: Toddy, Irish Coffee & the Warm Service"}],"pantryItems":["Cream","Sugar","Coffee/Espresso"]},{"slug":"chicken-adobo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg chicken thighs and drumsticks, bone-in, skin-on"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml soy sauce + 120ml cane or white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole head of garlic, smashed (yes, the whole head)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves, 1 tsp black peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: 1 tbsp brown sugar, coconut milk for adobo sa gata; rice, always rice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate chicken in soy, vinegar, garlic, bay, pepper, 30 min to overnight.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Everything into a pot; bring to a simmer WITHOUT stirring; the vinegar must cook out first, 10 min uncovered.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and simmer 30 min; then fish out the chicken and crisp the skin in a hot pan or under a broiler.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reduce the sauce to a glossy glaze; reunite; serve over mountains of rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The no-stir rule until the vinegar mellows is the oldest adobo law: raw vinegar bites, cooked vinegar sings. Every Filipino family's adobo is different and every one is correct; this is the national dish precisely because it refuses to be standardized.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","The broiler: heat from above","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","gently hot","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"dish":{"label":"Panlasang Pinoy’s killer adobo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aoc1_LwKaUg","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Adobo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Adobo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Chicken Adobo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Chicken%20Adobo","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Coconut","Bay leaf","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"sinigang-na-baboy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g pork belly or ribs, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 packet sinigang mix, or the honest road: 300g fresh tamarind simmered and strained"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, 1 onion, 1 daikon radish, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Long green beans, taro chunks (they thicken), water spinach (kangkong)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1–2 long green chilies; fish sauce to season"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer pork with onion and tomato until tender, 45–60 min, skimming.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add taro; cook 15 min until it starts dissolving at the edges: that is your body.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sour it: tamarind until your eyes want to close; season with fish sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Radish and beans; then kangkong and chilies for the final 2 min, barely wilted.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sinigang is judged on pampaasim, the sourness. It should make you flinch slightly on the first spoon and crave it by the third. On a rainy Manila day this outranks adobo, and Filipinos will say so quietly.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","umami-deep","tangy-ferment","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sinigang%20na%20Baboy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sinigang na Baboy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Sinigang na Baboy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Sinigang%20na%20Baboy","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"why-restaurants-fail-the-honest-list","term":"Why Restaurants Fail: The Honest List"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Spinach","Green beans","Radish/Turnip","Fish sauce","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"kare-kare","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg oxtail (or ox tripe + oxtail, traditional), or beef shank"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g peanut butter or ground toasted peanuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp toasted ground rice (dry-toast raw rice till gold, grind)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Banana blossom, long beans, aubergine, bok choy"},{"kind":"item","text":"Annatto oil; bagoong (fermented shrimp paste), the required partner"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer oxtail 2.5–3 h (or pressure-cook 45 min) until wobbling off the bone; keep the broth.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bloom annatto in oil; whisk in peanut butter and toasted rice with ladles of broth into a thick, orange-gold sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return meat; simmer 15 min; add vegetables in order of toughness.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with rice and a defiant spoon of bagoong on the side: the dish is seasoned AT the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kare-kare's secret is that the stew itself is nearly unsalted: the funky, salty bagoong on the side completes it, bite by bite. Toasted rice powder is the ancestral thickener; skip it and the sauce is merely peanut, not kare-kare.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","tangy-ferment","rich","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, ferment tang with real depth behind it, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kare-Kare%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kare-Kare"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Kare-Kare","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Kare-Kare","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"shank-oxtail-and-cheek","term":"Shank, Oxtail & Cheek"},{"slug":"annatto-achiote","term":"Annatto (Achiote)"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Shrimp","Liver & Offal","Cabbage","Eggplant","Green beans","Banana","Butter","Rice","Fish sauce","Peanut"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"lumpiang-shanghai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot + ½ onion, minced fine; 4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg; 2 tbsp soy sauce, big pinch pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"25–30 lumpia wrappers (thin spring roll wrappers), thawed under a damp towel"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying; sweet chili or banana ketchup for dunking"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix filling until sticky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread a thin log of filling along each wrapper; roll TIGHT and skinny: finger-width, no fatter; seal with water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut long rolls in half; freeze extras raw in a single layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 170°C until deep gold and blistered, 4–5 min. They must audibly crunch.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Skinny is the whole art: a fat lumpia steams its own wrapper soggy from inside. No Filipino party in history has produced leftover lumpia; make double and hide a plate for yourself before serving.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","umami-deep","warm-spiced","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lumpiang%20Shanghai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lumpiang Shanghai"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Lumpiang Shanghai","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Lumpiang%20Shanghai","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Chiles","Banana","Bread","Wrappers","Soy sauce","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"pancit-canton","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g pancit canton noodles (dried wheat-egg noodles)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g chicken, sliced; 150g shrimp; Chinese sausage if you have it"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cabbage, carrot, snow peas, celery, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves; 4 tbsp soy + 2 tbsp oyster sauce; 400ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Calamansi or lemon wedges, the finishing acid"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear chicken and sausage; add shrimp briefly; remove all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry garlic; stir-fry vegetables 2 min, still crisp.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stock and sauces to a boil; add dry noodles, turning as they drink the liquid, 4–5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Everything back in; toss; serve with calamansi squeezed over at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pancit is served at every Filipino birthday: long noodles for long life, and cutting them is considered bad luck. The noodles cook IN the seasoned stock, not water: they absorb the flavor instead of wearing it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pancit%20Canton%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pancit Canton"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Pancit Canton","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Pancit%20Canton","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"acid-architecture","term":"Acid Architecture"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Shrimp","Eggs","Garlic","Carrot","Celery","Cabbage","Peas","Lemon","Noodles","Oyster sauce","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"lechon-kawali","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg pork belly, whole slab, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bay leaves, peppercorns, 1 head garlic, salt, for the simmer"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sarsa: Mang Tomas liver sauce, or spiced vinegar with garlic and chili"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the belly in seasoned water 45–60 min until tender; drain.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Dry UNCOVERED in the fridge overnight: the skin must be desert-dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deep-fry at 180°C, standing back (it spits like a personal insult), until the skin blisters and crackles, 12–15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 10 min; chop through with a cleaver; salt; serve with sarsa and rice.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boil, dry, fry: the trinity. The overnight uncovered drying is the step everyone skips and the exact reason their skin chews instead of shatters. Proper lechon kawali crackles loud enough to pause a conversation.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lechon%20Kawali%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lechon Kawali"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Lechon Kawali","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Lechon%20Kawali","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Chiles","Bay leaf","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"sizzling-sisig","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g pork face/ears/jowl (or all belly, the accessible road)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 big onion, minced; 4–6 bird's eye chilies"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp calamansi or lemon juice; 2 tbsp soy"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mayonnaise or 1 raw egg yolk for the sizzling plate"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chicken liver 100g, optional but traditional; cast-iron plate, screaming hot"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer pork till tender; then grill or broil until edges char and crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop everything FINE: sisig means to snack on something sour; the chop is the texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss with onion, chili, calamansi, soy, mashed cooked liver.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Onto a smoking cast-iron plate; crack the egg on top; stir at the table while it roars.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born in Pampanga from American air-base surplus pig heads: Anthony Bourdain called it the best thing ever eaten with beer, and he was not wrong. The plate must sizzle on arrival; silent sisig is just pork salad.","equipment":["Skillet","Grill"],"techniques":["The broiler: heat from above"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred","fiery","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sizzling%20Sisig%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sizzling Sisig"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Sizzling Sisig","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Sizzling%20Sisig","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"guanciale-jowl-and-lardo","term":"Guanciale, Jowl & Lardo"},{"slug":"grilling-vs-barbecue-vs-smoking","term":"Grilling vs. Barbecue vs. Smoking"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Eggs","Liver & Offal","Onion","Chiles","Lemon","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above"]},{"slug":"tapsilog","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g beef sirloin, sliced thin; marinade: soy, calamansi or lemon, garlic, sugar, pepper (that is the tapa)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cups day-old rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head garlic, chopped, for the sinangag (garlic rice)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2–4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Spiced vinegar on the side"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate beef overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sinangag: fry the garlic gold in oil; scoop half out; toss the rice through the garlic oil, salt, then return the crisp bits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear the tapa hard and fast in a hot pan until the sugar edges caramelize.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry eggs sunny with lacy edges. Assemble: tapa + sinangag + itlog = tapsilog.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The -silog suffix is a national breakfast operating system: longsilog, tocilog, bangsilog: any protein + garlic rice + fried egg. Day-old rice is mandatory; fresh rice turns garlic fried rice into porridge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tapsilog%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tapsilog"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Tapsilog","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Tapsilog","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Garlic","Lemon","Rice","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"leche-flan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"10 egg YOLKS (the whites go to another life)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 can (395g) condensed milk + 1 can (370ml) evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla or dayap/lime zest"},{"kind":"item","text":"Caramel: 150g sugar + 3 tbsp water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Llanera molds or a loaf tin; a steamer or bain-marie"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt sugar to deep amber; pour into molds, tilting to coat; let harden.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk yolks and milks GENTLY: stir, don't beat; bubbles become holes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain twice through a fine sieve; pour over the caramel; cover with foil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam 30–35 min or bake in a water bath at 160°C, 45 min, until it barely trembles. Chill overnight; invert.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":33600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Ten yolks and no whites is what separates leche flan from crème caramel, denser, richer, closer to gold. The double strain and the gentle stir are why a proper flan is glass-smooth with not one pore.","equipment":["Steamer","Bain-marie","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The water bath","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","fiery","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Leche%20Flan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Leche Flan"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Leche Flan","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Leche%20Flan","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"microplane-graters-and-the-zest-doctrine","term":"Microplane, Graters & the Zest Doctrine"},{"slug":"casings-molds-and-the-curing-chamber","term":"Casings, Molds & the Curing Chamber"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lime","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"halo-halo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Sweet beans + garbanzos, nata de coco, kaong (palm fruit), macapuno strings"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sweetened saba banana + jackfruit strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"Leche flan (see the recipe above) + ube halaya (purple yam jam)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A mountain of finely shaved ice + 150ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scoop ube ice cream; pinipig (toasted rice crisps) to crown"}],"steps":[{"text":"Layer the sweets and fruits in a tall glass: heaviest at the bottom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack shaved ice to a peak above the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour evaporated milk over; crown with flan, ube halaya, ice cream, pinipig.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with a long spoon and one instruction: halo means mix; stir it ALL before eating.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Halo-halo means mix-mix, and the stirring is the recipe: fifteen ingredients become one lavender-colored, crunchy-creamy glass of joy. It is the edible portrait of the Philippines: many islands, one bowl.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Halo-Halo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Halo-Halo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Halo-Halo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Halo-Halo","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sweet potato","Banana","Milk","Rice","Chickpeas"]},{"slug":"rinderrouladen","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 wide, thin slices beef topside (ask the butcher for roulade cuts)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tsp sharp German mustard; 8 bacon rashers"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dill pickles, quartered lengthwise; 1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, 1 leek, tomato paste for the braise; 500ml beef stock + 200ml red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter, flour; red cabbage and spätzle alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay beef flat; paint with mustard; layer bacon, onion, a pickle spear; roll tight and pin or tie.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the rolls hard on all sides; remove; brown the vegetables and paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wine, stock; rouladen back; braise covered at 160°C, 1.5–2 h.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift out; strain and reduce the sauce; whisk in cold butter. Slice one open to admire the spiral.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mustard, bacon, pickle: the trinity inside every German grandmother's roulade, and the pickle is not garnish; its brine keeps the lean beef juicy through the long braise, basting from within. Roll tight, secure well, brown hard: the fond those spirals leave is the gravy's foundation, finished with the strained braising liquid. Sunday food, rotkohl and knödel alongside by law of custom. Thin-cut topside is the correct canvas: the filling is the actual painting.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Braising","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rinderrouladen%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rinderrouladen"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Rinderrouladen","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Rinderrouladen","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Bacon","Onion","Leek","Tomato","Carrot","Cabbage","Dill","Butter","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["braising","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"kasespatzle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g flour, 4 eggs, ~150ml sparkling water, 1 tsp salt, nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g grated mountain cheese: Emmentaler + Bergkäse mix"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, sliced into rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter, generously"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives; a green salad to pretend at balance"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat batter until it blisters and pulls: it should tear slowly off the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape or press through a spätzle board into simmering salted water; scoop as they float.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onions in butter, slow, until deep bronze: this is half the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer hot spätzle and cheese in a warm pan until molten; crown with onions and chives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Swabia's answer to mac and cheese, and the batter test is everything: beat until it throws bubbles and fights back. Sparkling water keeps the noodles light; the burnished onions are non-negotiable.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=K%C3%A4sesp%C3%A4tzle%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Käsespätzle"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Käsespätzle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20K%C3%A4sesp%C3%A4tzle","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"emmentaler","term":"Emmentaler"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Chives","Butter","Cheese (any)","Gruyère/Alpine","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"sauerbraten","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg beef rump, marinated 3 TO 5 DAYS in advance"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade: 500ml red wine + 250ml red wine vinegar, onion, carrot, bay, cloves, juniper, peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste; 60g crushed gingersnap cookies (Lebkuchen if you can find them)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Handful of raisins (Rhineland-style, contested elsewhere)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Red cabbage and potato dumplings, the classic escort"}],"steps":[{"text":"Submerge beef in the cold marinade; refrigerate 3–5 days, turning daily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dry the beef; brown hard; sauté the strained marinade vegetables; add liquid and braise 2.5–3 h at 150°C.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain the braise; whisk in crushed gingersnaps: they melt into a sweet-sour velvet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raisins in (or not, per your region); slice the beef thick against the grain and drown politely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The gingersnap thickener sounds like a prank and is actually genius: spice, sweetness and body in one move, balancing days of vinegar. Plan ahead: sauerbraten is a calendar commitment, not a craving.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Straining & passing through a sieve","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","sweet-edged","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauerbraten%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauerbraten"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Sauerbraten","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Sauerbraten","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Ginger","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Cabbage","Dried fruit","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Wine","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"schweinshaxe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 pork knuckles (hind hocks), skin on, scored in a diamond pattern"},{"kind":"item","text":"Caraway seeds, salt, pepper, 1 head garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, quartered; 500ml dark beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"Potato dumplings and mustard to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"A cold beer for the cook, Bavarian law"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub knuckles with salt, caraway, crushed garlic; rest overnight uncovered in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast on the onion bed at 160°C, 2.5 h, adding beer to the pan as it evaporates.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Crank to 230°C (or broil) for the final 20–30 min, basting with beer, until the skin blisters into amber glass.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest briefly; serve with the beer-onion pan gravy, strained.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oktoberfest engineering: low heat renders, high heat detonates the crackling: hours of gentle roasting melt the collagen, then a final blast makes the skin blister into glass. The overnight dry salt-rub is what makes it possible; wet skin merely toughens, and scoring lets the fat escape and the salt descend. Rest the meat, not the skin: tent the flesh loosely and leave the crackling proud. A liter of beer alongside is not decoration: it is the dish's native habitat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","The broiler: heat from above","Straining & passing through a sieve","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":35,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Schweinshaxe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Schweinshaxe"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Schweinshaxe","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Schweinshaxe","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"beer-styles-the-two-family-map","term":"Beer Styles: The Two-Family Map"},{"slug":"caraway-and-nigella","term":"Caraway & Nigella"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"currywurst","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 bratwurst or bockwurst"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 300g ketchup, 1 tbsp curry powder + more for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp paprika, dash Worcestershire, splash of cola (Berlin kiosk secret)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fries or a soft roll (Schrippe); a wooden fork for authenticity"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer ketchup with grated onion, curry powder, paprika, Worcestershire and cola, 10 min, to a glossy sauce.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill or pan-fry the sausages until snappy and browned.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice into coins, the ceremonial cut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauce over, extra curry powder dusted on top, fries alongside. Eat standing up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented in 1949 Berlin by Herta Heuwer at a street kiosk: she got British ketchup and curry powder in trade and fed a rebuilding city. There is a museum, there are wars over the sauce, and it must be eaten with a tiny fork from a paper tray.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":35,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Currywurst%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Currywurst"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Currywurst","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Currywurst","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Onion","Bread","Paprika","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"konigsberger-klopse","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground veal-pork mix; 1 stale roll soaked in milk; 1 egg; 1 minced onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 anchovy fillets, minced fine (the historic secret; do not tell the children)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L light beef or vegetable broth for poaching"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 40g butter, 40g flour, poaching broth, 100ml cream, 1 egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp capers + a splash of their brine; lemon; boiled potatoes and beets alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix meatballs gently with the soaked roll, egg, onion, anchovy; shape golf balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach, never fry, in barely simmering broth 15 min; keep the broth.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roux; whisk in the broth to a silky white sauce; cream, capers, brine, lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Temper in the yolk off heat; return the klopse; dust with parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"East Prussia's elegant meatball: poached pale, sauced white, sharpened with capers and hidden anchovy: it looks like nursery food and tastes like a diplomat wrote it. The anchovy dissolves entirely, leaving depth nobody can place; the capers and lemon cut the cream's richness with precision. Poach gently, never boil, or the klopse toughen. The dish outlived its city: Königsberg is Kaliningrad now, but the recipe kept the old name and every German menu knows it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Making a roux","Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","briny","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=K%C3%B6nigsberger%20Klopse%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Königsberger Klopse"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Königsberger Klopse","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20K%C3%B6nigsberger%20Klopse","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-brine-and-sea-register","term":"The Brine & Sea Register"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Anchovy","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Beets","Capers","Lemon","Butter","Cream","Bread","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"schwabischer-kartoffelsalat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg waxy potatoes, boiled in skins"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml hot beef or vegetable broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp white wine vinegar + 1 tsp mustard + 1 finely minced onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives, salt, plenty of black pepper, and NO mayonnaise, this is the South"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel potatoes while hot (fork and knife, stoic expression); slice thin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the HOT broth with vinegar, mustard and onion over the warm slices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 30 min: the potatoes drink the broth and turn glossy (schlonzig, the untranslatable goal).","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold in oil last; chives; serve at room temperature, never cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"North Germany binds with mayonnaise; Swabia binds with hot broth and calls it schlonzig: slippery, glossy, spoon-clinging. Warm potatoes absorb; cold potatoes refuse. That is the entire science.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Schw%C3%A4bischer%20Kartoffelsalat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Schwäbischer Kartoffelsalat"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Schwäbischer Kartoffelsalat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Schw%C3%A4bischer%20Kartoffelsalat","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Potato","Chives","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Mayonnaise"]},{"slug":"rotkohl","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 red cabbage (~1.2kg), quartered, cored, shredded fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 apples, grated; 1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp goose fat or butter; 2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml red wine vinegar + 150ml red wine or apple juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, 4 cloves, 2 juniper berries, cinnamon stick; redcurrant jelly to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt sugar in the fat until it just caramelizes; add onion, then cabbage and apple, turning to coat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Vinegar immediately: the acid fixes the violet color before it turns sad blue-gray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wine, spices; cover; stew gently 60–75 min, stirring now and then.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":4500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Finish with a spoon of redcurrant jelly; season sweet-sour to a knife's-edge balance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Acid first or the color dies: red cabbage is a pH indicator wearing a dinner jacket. Like bigos, it is better the next day, and it freezes so well that German households batch it for the whole holiday season.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Braising"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","warm-spiced","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rotkohl%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rotkohl"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Rotkohl","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Rotkohl","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"apples-and-pears-the-autumn-keepers","term":"Apples & Pears: The Autumn Keepers"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Cabbage","Apple","Berries","Bay leaf","Butter","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"laugenbrezeln","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour, 10g salt, 7g yeast, 280ml water, 30g soft butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lye bath: 30g food-grade lye (NaOH) in 1L cold water, gloves and care, or the timid road: 80g baked baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse pretzel salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nothing else; the shape and the bath are the recipe"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for Butterbrezeln, the Bavarian breakfast"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stiff dough; knead well; rest 30 min. Roll long ropes, fat-bellied, thin-armed; twist into pretzels.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill uncovered 1 h: a dry skin takes the lye evenly.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Dip 10 seconds in the lye bath (gloves!); salt; slash the fat belly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 220°C, 14–16 min to mahogany. The crumb inside stays snow-white.","durationSec":960,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":960,"estimated":true}],"note":"The lye is the flavor and the shine: that deep bronze, faintly mineral crust cannot be faked, though baked baking soda gets you 80% there without the chemistry gloves. In Bavaria, split and buttered, this is breakfast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The pretzel bath"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":35,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The pretzel bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pretzel%20lye%20baking%20soda%20bath%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Laugenbrezeln%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Laugenbrezeln"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Laugenbrezeln","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Laugenbrezeln","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Flour"]},{"slug":"schwarzwalder-kirschtorte","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Chocolate sponge: 6 eggs, 150g sugar, 100g flour + 50g cocoa, 50g melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 jar sour cherries + juice; 2 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml kirschwasser: the cherry brandy IS the cake's legal identity"},{"kind":"item","text":"800ml whipping cream, stabilized with a little gelatin or cream stiffener"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dark chocolate shavings, whole cherries to crown"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bake the sponge; cool; slice into three layers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thicken cherry juice with starch; fold in cherries. Soak each sponge layer shamelessly with kirsch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack: sponge, cherry filling, cream (twice); coat the whole in cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shave a chocolate bar into curls over everything; pipe rosettes; cherry on each. Chill 4 h.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"German law is not joking: without real kirschwasser it may not be sold as Schwarzwälder Kirschtorte. The cherry brandy is the legal and actual soul of the cake, and the soak should worry you slightly as you pour; that is the correct amount. Stabilized whipped cream holds the architecture through an evening. Sour cherries, never sweet, against the bitter cocoa sponge. Build it a day ahead: the layers need the night to negotiate, and the kirsch needs time to travel.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":["Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Schwarzw%C3%A4lder%20Kirschtorte%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Schwarzwälder Kirschtorte"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Schwarzwälder Kirschtorte","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Schwarzw%C3%A4lder%20Kirschtorte","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"legal-entity-and-partnership-agreements","term":"Legal Entity & Partnership Agreements"},{"slug":"brandy-cognac-armagnac-calvados-and-pisco","term":"Brandy: Cognac, Armagnac, Calvados & Pisco"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Stone fruit","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting"]},{"slug":"wiener-schnitzel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 veal cutlets (pork makes it Schnitzel Wiener Art; Vienna checks the paperwork)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour, 2 beaten eggs + splash of cream, fine breadcrumbs from stale rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"Clarified butter (butterschmalz), 2cm deep: not oil, if you want the true taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges; lingonberry jam"},{"kind":"item","text":"Erdäpfelsalat (potato-cucumber salad) alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound cutlets between plastic to 4mm: even, thin, unbroken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flour, egg, crumbs: pressing NEVER; the crumbs should barely cling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in deep clarified butter, SWIRLING the pan constantly so butter waves over the top, 90 seconds a side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The crust should souffléd away from the meat in golden waves. Lemon, immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The wrinkled, billowed crust (soufflieren) is the mark of a Vienna kitchen: loose crumbs plus swirling fat inflate it like a duvet. Press the crumbs on and you have made a fine schnitzel and failed a Viennese exam.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":35,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wiener%20Schnitzel%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wiener Schnitzel"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Potato","Cucumber","Lemon","Butter","Cream","Bread","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"tafelspitz","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg beef tafelspitz (top of the rump cap), in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 marrow bones; 1 onion, halved and CHARRED cut-side down"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, ½ celeriac, 1 leek, parsley root if found"},{"kind":"item","text":"Peppercorns, bay, lovage"},{"kind":"item","text":"Apple-horseradish (Apfelkren) + chive sauce; roasted potatoes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char the onion halves black in a dry pan: they bronze the broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Start bones and beef in COLD water; bring to a whisper; skim like a monk, never boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Vegetables in for the final hour; simmer 3 h total until a fork slides free.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve in courses, Vienna-style: first the broth with the marrow, then sliced beef with both sauces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Emperor Franz Joseph ate this daily, so Vienna built a whole restaurant liturgy around boiled beef. The charred onion and the never-boil rule are why the broth is amber and clear instead of gray and cloudy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tafelspitz%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tafelspitz"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Leek","Potato","Carrot","Apple","Celeriac","Parsley","Bay leaf","Chives"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"wiener-saftgulasch","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg beef shin; 1kg onions: equal weights, that is the law of Saftgulasch"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp sweet paprika + 1 tsp hot; 1 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp vinegar, 1 tsp caraway ground with lemon zest and garlic (the Viennese perfume)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp marjoram; lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"Semmelknödel or crusty rolls; fried sausage + egg on top makes it Fiakergulasch"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the onion mountain in lard, 30 patient minutes, to deep gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off heat: paprika + vinegar together (the acid guards the color); paste, spices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beef in with a little water; stew low 2.5 h, adding splashes, never drowning; the onions are the sauce.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest overnight if you can. Serve with dumplings to mop the sauce (Saft) it is named for.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Vienna took Hungary's soup and reduced it to gravy: equal onions to beef, no flour, no cream. The caraway-lemon-garlic grind stirred in at the end is the secret Viennese signature.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","aromatic","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wiener%20Saftgulasch%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wiener Saftgulasch"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"floral-notes-and-perfume-dosage","term":"Floral Notes & Perfume Dosage"},{"slug":"pomegranate-and-quince-autumns-perfume","term":"Pomegranate & Quince: Autumn's Perfume"},{"slug":"caraway-and-nigella","term":"Caraway & Nigella"},{"slug":"microplane-graters-and-the-zest-doctrine","term":"Microplane, Graters & the Zest Doctrine"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Sausage","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Lemon","Oregano","Bread","Vinegar","Tomato paste","Paprika","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"frittatensuppe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1L strong clear beef broth (the Tafelspitz broth, ideally; Vienna wastes nothing)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crêpe batter: 100g flour, 2 eggs, 200ml milk, salt, chives"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"That is all: restraint is the recipe"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make thin, pale crêpes; cool them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll each into a cigar; slice into fine ribbons, the frittaten.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the broth to just under a boil; season precisely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ribbons into warm bowls; broth over at the table; chives. Serve immediately, before they soften.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Every Viennese lunch menu opens with this: yesterday's crêpes, yesterday's broth, and somehow the sum is elegant. Pour the broth AT the table: frittaten hold their bite for exactly three minutes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Crêpes"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Crêpes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=crepe%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Frittatensuppe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Frittatensuppe"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Chives","Butter","Milk","Flour","Stock/Broth"]},{"slug":"semmelknodel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g stale white rolls (Semmeln), cubed: 2 days old is perfect"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml warm milk; 2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, minced, sweated in butter; parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nutmeg, salt; 2 tbsp flour only if the dough betrays you"},{"kind":"item","text":"Brown butter for serving, or a gulasch to sit beside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour warm milk over the bread cubes; rest 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add eggs, onion, parsley, nutmeg; squeeze together: cohesive, still lumpy with bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With wet hands, form tight orange-sized spheres; test-boil ONE.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer (never boil) in salted water 15 min. Slice leftovers and fry in butter tomorrow, arguably the better dish.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"The single test dumpling is Alpine law: if it dissolves, knead in flour; if it bounces, add milk. And know that fried leftover knödel with egg (Knödel mit Ei) is the secret dish Austrians actually crave.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Brown butter","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Sautéing & the shallow fry","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brown butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brown%20butter%20beurre%20noisette%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Semmelkn%C3%B6del%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Semmelknödel"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Parsley","Butter","Bread","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"tiroler-grostl","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g boiled waxy potatoes from yesterday, sliced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g smoked bacon or leftover roast pork, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced; butter or lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"2–4 eggs, fried sunny"},{"kind":"item","text":"Caraway, marjoram, chives, black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render bacon in a wide iron pan; scoop out, leaving the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry potatoes in one layer, patiently, until crusted gold: turn seldom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Onion in till bronze; bacon back; caraway and marjoram.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fried eggs on top, yolks loose; chives; serve in the pan with a mountain view if available.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ski-hut alchemy: yesterday's potatoes and Sunday's leftover roast become the alpine breakfast of record: Gröstl was never a recipe so much as a policy about leftovers, which is why every Tyrolean hut's version differs and every one is right. The discipline is not stirring: every flip you resist buys more crust, and the crust is the dish. A fried egg on top, sunny side, is mandatory; caraway and marjoram are the Tyrolean accent. Eaten at altitude with a view, ideally, but a kitchen table works.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tiroler%20Gr%C3%B6stl%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tiroler Gröstl"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Oregano","Chives","Butter","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"kaiserschmarrn","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, SEPARATED; 250ml milk; 120g flour; pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar + more for caramelizing; vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g raisins soaked in rum (imperial faction) or not (heretic faction)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Plenty of butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar blizzard; Zwetschkenröster (stewed plums) or apple sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Batter from yolks, milk, flour; whip whites with sugar to soft peaks; fold in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into foaming butter; scatter raisins; cook like a thick pancake till the base sets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Quarter it, flip the quarters, then TEAR into ragged bites with two forks in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"More butter and sugar over; toss till the edges caramelize. Bury in icing sugar; serve with plums.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Named for Emperor Franz Joseph. Schmarrn means nonsense, and the tearing is the technique: ragged edges catch butter and caramel that a tidy pancake never could. It is scrambled on purpose, imperial by accident.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kaiserschmarrn%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kaiserschmarrn"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Apple","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"apfelstrudel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Strudel dough: 250g strong flour, 125ml warm water, 2 tbsp oil, pinch salt, 1 tsp vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1kg tart apples, sliced thin; lemon; 80g sugar, cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g breadcrumbs toasted in 80g butter; 50g raisins, rum-soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"More melted butter for brushing: strudel drinks it"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar; vanilla sauce or a cloud of whipped cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the dough smooth and slappy; rest under a warm bowl 1 h: the rest IS the stretchiness.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"On a floured tablecloth, roll then STRETCH with the backs of your hands until you can read a newspaper through it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Buttered crumbs, then apples, sugar, raisins along one edge; trim thick borders; roll using the cloth like a wave.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Curve onto a tray; butter; bake 190°C, 35–40 min, buttering once more mid-bake.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"The newspaper test is the real Viennese standard: dough stretched to reading-thinness bakes into dozens of shattering layers. The tablecloth does the rolling; your hands just lift and let gravity stretch.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Kneading dough"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Apfelstrudel%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Apfelstrudel"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"hot-drinks-toddy-irish-coffee-and-the-warm-service","term":"Hot Drinks: Toddy, Irish Coffee & the Warm Service"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Apple","Dried fruit","Butter","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Vinegar","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"sachertorte","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Batter: 130g dark chocolate melted, 130g butter, 100g icing sugar, 6 eggs separated, 130g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"110g caster sugar for the whites"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g good apricot jam, warmed and sieved"},{"kind":"item","text":"Glaze: 200g sugar, 125ml water, 150g dark chocolate, boiled to a pourable gloss"},{"kind":"item","text":"Unsweetened whipped cream (Schlagobers), mandatory escort"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream butter, icing sugar, yolks; add cooled chocolate. Whip whites with caster sugar; fold in with the flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 170°C, ~55 min; cool upside down for a flat top.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice in half; apricot jam in the middle AND painted over the entire outside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the glaze to 108°C; cool slightly; pour over in one confident motion. Do not touch it again. Rest overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Franz Sacher improvised it in 1832 as a sixteen-year-old apprentice when the head chef fell ill; Vienna later fought a seven-year court war over who owns the 'original.' The apricot layer is not filling: it is the waterproofing that keeps the cake moist under its chocolate armor.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The overnight rest: letting a dish marry","Creaming butter & sugar"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sachertorte%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sachertorte"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"doneness-by-touch-sight-and-sound","term":"Doneness by Touch, Sight & Sound"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"vanillekipferl","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"210g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g ground walnuts or almonds (walnuts are the old Vienna way)"},{"kind":"item","text":"160g cold butter, cubed; 70g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks; pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Vanilla sugar mixed with icing sugar, a deep snowdrift of it"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub everything into a dough quickly: warm hands are the enemy; chill 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll thin ropes; cut thumb-lengths; bend each into a crescent with tapered tips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 170°C, 10–12 min: pale gold only; the tips brown first and burn second.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll in vanilla sugar while WARM (they drink it), then again when cool (they wear it).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The crescent shape toasts the tips before the middle: pull them when they look one minute underdone. Double-sugaring, warm then cold, is the Austrian grandmother move: the first coat melts in, the second one snows.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shaping by hand","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","earthy","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vanillekipferl%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vanillekipferl"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"kottbullar","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g ground beef + 200g ground pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, grated to a pulp; 60g breadcrumbs soaked in 100ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg; ½ tsp allspice + ¼ tsp white pepper, the Swedish accent"},{"kind":"item","text":"Gravy: butter, 2 tbsp flour, 400ml beef stock, 100ml cream, splash soy"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lingonberry jam, pressed cucumber, boiled or mashed potatoes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak crumbs in cream 10 min; mix everything gently: overworked meatballs bounce.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll walnut-sized with wet hands; chill 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown in butter in batches, rolling the pan so they stay spherical; remove.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roux in the same pan; stock, cream, a whisper of soy for color; balls back to finish in the gravy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Allspice is what makes a meatball Swedish: that warm, faintly Christmas note. And lingonberries are not garnish; the sweet-tart against cream gravy is the entire national argument for the dish.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","fiery","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=K%C3%B6ttbullar%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Köttbullar"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Cucumber","Butter","Cream","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Stock/Broth","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"gravlax","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g impeccably fresh salmon, skin on, pin-boned, in two matching pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp salt + 3 tbsp sugar + 1 tbsp crushed white peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 huge bunches of dill: a forest, not a sprig"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: 2 tbsp aquavit or gin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hovmästarsås: mustard, sugar, vinegar, oil, chopped dill; dark rye alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the cure into the flesh; splash the spirit; bury both fillets in dill and sandwich flesh-to-flesh.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap tight; weight with a plate and cans; refrigerate 48 h, flipping every 12 and basting with its own brine.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Unwrap, scrape off the cure and dill, pat dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice paper-thin on the bias, knife almost flat; serve with mustard sauce and rye.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Gravlax means 'buried salmon': medieval fishermen cured it in beach sand. The flip-every-12-hours ritual cures it evenly; the 48-hour wait is why it slices like silk instead of sashimi.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Curing gravlax","Brining & curing","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Curing gravlax","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=gravlax%20curing%20salmon%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gravlax%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gravlax"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"body-alcohol-and-weight-matching","term":"Body, Alcohol & Weight-Matching"},{"slug":"scales-and-measuring-by-weight","term":"Scales & Measuring by Weight"}],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Dill","Vinegar","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"smorrebrod","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices dense Danish rye (rugbrød): the dark, seeded, sour kind"},{"kind":"item","text":"Good butter, applied to the edges like waterproofing"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toppings, pick your classics: pickled herring + egg + dill; roast beef + remoulade + crispy onions; shrimp piled high with mayo and lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garnishes with intent: dill, chives, radish coins, lemon twists"},{"kind":"item","text":"A knife and fork: hands are for amateurs"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter the rye completely: it is the moisture barrier, not a courtesy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build tall and deliberate: base protein, then sauce, then texture, then the fresh green flourish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Every topping must be visible from above: Danish rules state the bread should disappear under the load.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately; eat with cutlery, herring before meat, in the traditional order.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Copenhagen turned the open sandwich into architecture: the butter seals, the rye carries, and the topping order (herring first, then fish, then meat, then cheese) is a lunch liturgy older than the bicycle lanes.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sm%C3%B8rrebr%C3%B8d%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Smørrebrød"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Shrimp","Eggs","Onion","Radish/Turnip","Lemon","Dill","Chives","Butter","Mayonnaise","Pickles"]},{"slug":"janssons-frestelse","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg firm potatoes, cut in matchsticks, never grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced, softened in butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"125g Swedish anchovy-spiced sprats (ansjovis) + 3 tbsp of their brine, NOT Mediterranean anchovies"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml cream (half in at the start, half mid-bake)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Breadcrumbs and butter for the lid"}],"steps":[{"text":"Layer: potatoes, onions, sprats, potatoes; season lightly: the brine is salty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over half the cream and the sprat brine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 200°C, 30 min; add remaining cream; crumbs and butter on top.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Back in for 25–30 min until bronzed and bubbling at the edges. Rest 10 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Jansson's Temptation guards Sweden's Christmas table, and the trap is the tin: Swedish ansjovis are sweet-spiced sprats (cinnamon, sandalwood), not salty anchovies. Use the wrong tin and Swedes will know, and they will be kind about it, and that is worse.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Janssons%20Frestelse%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Janssons Frestelse"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Onion","Potato","Butter","Cream","Breadcrumbs/Panko"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"smulpaj-med-kardemumma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g apples or 500g bilberries/blueberries, or rhubarb in spring"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar + 1 tbsp cornstarch tossed with the fruit; squeeze of lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crumb: 150g flour + 100g rolled oats + 120g cold butter + 90g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cardamom, the Nordic signature, plus a pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Vaniljsås (thin vanilla custard) or vanilla ice cream, mandatory"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss fruit with sugar, starch, lemon straight into the dish: no pre-cooking, no bottom crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the crumb ingredients to rubble with visible butter pebbles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanket the fruit loosely: do not press; air is what crisps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 190°C, 35–40 min until the juices bubble through the crust like lava at the borders. Serve warm, drowned in vanilla sauce.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sweden skipped the pastry-crust struggle entirely: smulpaj is all topping, no rolling pin, and cardamom instead of cinnamon is what makes it taste like a Stockholm kitchen. The bubbling-through-the-edges is not mess; it is the doneness indicator.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","floral","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Smulpaj%20med%20Kardemumma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Smulpaj med Kardemumma"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"proofing-oven-spring-and-the-crumb","term":"Proofing, Oven Spring & the Crumb"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Apple","Berries","Butter","Cream","Bread","Flour","Oats","Sugar","Vanilla","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"fiskesuppe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g mixed fish: salmon + cod, chunked; 150g shrimp"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, 1 carrot, ½ celeriac, cut in fine batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L good fish stock; 200ml cream + 100ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter + 2 tbsp flour; white wine splash"},{"kind":"item","text":"The Bergen signature: 1–2 tsp white vinegar + 1 tsp sugar; dill and chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat vegetables in butter, keeping their color; dust with flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stock gradually; simmer 10 min; wine, cream, milk.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with the sweet-sour: vinegar and sugar until the soup brightens: it should taste faintly, elegantly tangy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fish in at a bare shiver, 3–4 min; shrimp last minute; herbs; serve with crusty bread.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bergen fish soup's identity is the sweet-and-sour finish, a Norwegian trick that lifts cream and fish out of heaviness. The fish poaches in the residual heat; if the soup boils after the fish goes in, Bergen disowns you.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fiskesuppe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fiskesuppe"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","White fish","Salmon","Leek","Carrot","Celeriac","Dill","Chives","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"kanelbullar","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour, 250ml warm milk, 75g butter, 60g sugar, 7g yeast, ½ tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ground cardamom in the dough, non-negotiable, this is Sweden"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filling: 100g soft butter, 90g sugar, 2 tbsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg for brushing; pearl sugar to crown"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coffee, because these exist for fika"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rich dough with the cardamom; knead 10 min; rise 1 h.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll a rectangle; spread the cinnamon butter; fold in thirds like a letter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut strips; twist and knot each one into a rough turban (or roll classic spirals, no shame).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Proof 45 min; egg wash, pearl sugar; bake 220°C, 8–10 min. Eat warm within the hour, as intended.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cardamom in the dough is what separates a kanelbulle from a mall cinnamon roll: Sweden imports more cardamom per head than almost anywhere on earth. October 4th is the official national Cinnamon Bun Day; attendance is essentially compulsory.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Letter-folding dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Letter-folding dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=letter%20fold%20dough%20lamination%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kanelbullar%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kanelbullar"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Coffee/Espresso","Cinnamon","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"frikadeller-med-agurkesalat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground pork (or pork-veal)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated; 3 tbsp flour or oats; 1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml sparkling water or milk: the batter should be almost too soft to shape"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Agurkesalat: 1 cucumber sliced paper-thin, brined in vinegar-sugar-water; boiled potatoes, brown gravy"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the meat with salt first (it binds), then onion, flour, egg, and liquid until soft and spoonable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 30 min in the fridge: the oats or flour drink the liquid.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Shape with a spoon dipped in the melted butter: oval dollops straight into the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry medium, 4–5 min a side, to deep brown. Serve with the icy-crisp pickled cucumbers.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Danish frikadeller are dropped from a spoon, never rolled: the batter is deliberately too loose for hands, which is exactly why they fry up feather-light. The cucumber salad is the required cold-crisp counterweight.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Frikadeller%20med%20Agurkesalat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Frikadeller med Agurkesalat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Cucumber","Butter","Flour","Oats","Vinegar","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"toast-skagen","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g cooked cold-water shrimp, peeled (the tiny sweet ones)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp mayonnaise + 2 tbsp crème fraîche"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Dijon, plenty of chopped dill, lemon juice, white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices white bread, crusts off, fried gold in butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Vendace or lumpfish roe to crown; a lemon wedge and a glass of something cold"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the shrimp seriously: wet shrimp drown the dressing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold shrimp with mayo, crème fraîche, mustard, dill, lemon; season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter-fry the bread to an even gold; cool 1 min so it stays crisp.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":60,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the skagenröra generously; crown with a spoon of roe and a dill sprig.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented by restaurateur Tore Wretman, who named it after a fishing port mid-regatta. The butter-fried bread is the difference between an elegant Swedish starter and shrimp salad on toast. Never skip the fry.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Toast%20Skagen%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Toast Skagen"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Lemon","Dill","Butter","Sour cream/Crema","Bread","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"aebleskiver","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour, 1 tsp baking powder, ½ tsp bicarb, pinch salt, 1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs, SEPARATED; 300ml buttermilk; zest of 1 lemon; ½ tsp cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for every single hollow of the pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"The æbleskive pan, cast iron with half-sphere wells (a fine excuse to own one)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Raspberry jam and icing sugar for dunking and dusting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Batter from yolks, buttermilk, dry ingredients; whip whites stiff and fold in gently.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter each well of the hot pan; fill three-quarters.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"When the edges set, rotate each ball a quarter-turn with a knitting needle or skewer; repeat until sealed spheres.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll until deep gold all over and a skewer comes out clean. Dust; dunk; repeat until the batter is gone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The quarter-turn rotation is the whole craft: the uncooked batter flows into the gap and builds the sphere layer by layer, like throwing a pot in reverse. Danes make them at Christmas with gløgg, and the first batch always belongs to the cook.","equipment":["Skillet","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=%C3%86bleskiver%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Æbleskiver"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scandinavian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Scandinavian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Berries","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"injera","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g teff flour (the tiny Ethiopian grain, brown or ivory)"},{"kind":"item","text":"~750ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 3–5 DAY head start: the batter ferments itself with wild yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional starter shortcut: 1 tbsp sourdough starter to launch it"},{"kind":"item","text":"A wide nonstick pan or a mitad if the ancestors are smiling"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk teff and water to a thin batter; cover loosely; leave at room temperature 3–5 days until sour and bubbling, pouring off the dark liquid daily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thin with water to crêpe-batter consistency; some cooks boil a cupful and stir it back (absit) for flexibility.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour onto a hot dry pan in a spiral from the outside in; do not flip, ever.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover; steam 2–3 min until the surface erupts in 'eyes' and the edges lift. Cool on a cloth, never stacked hot.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"The eyes, thousands of little craters, are the proof of proper fermentation and the entire point: they grip the wat. Injera is plate, fork and bread in one; the last piece soaked in sauce (the injera under everything) is the best bite of the meal.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sourdough starter","Injera","Crêpes","Fermentation","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sourdough starter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Injera%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Injera"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"doro-wat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken drumsticks, skinned, slashed; lemon-salt bath first"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 HUGE red onions, minced to a paste: no oil yet, this is the secret"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp berbere (the deep red Ethiopian spice armory), more if you are brave"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp niter kibbeh (spiced clarified butter); garlic, ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 boiled eggs, pricked; injera to serve, obviously"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry-cook the onion paste in a heavy pot, no fat, stirring, 20–30 min until it collapses and browns, the wat's foundation.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add niter kibbeh, garlic, ginger; then berbere; fry the paste 10 min, adding splashes of water so it deepens without burning.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chicken in, turning to coat; simmer low, 45 min, the sauce thick as tar and glossy with red oil.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pricked eggs in for the last 15 min to drink the sauce. Serve on injera, eggs crowning.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dry-frying the onions before any fat touches them is the Ethiopian move nobody expects: it builds a sweetness and body no sautéed onion can reach. Doro wat is the dish of holidays and homecomings; the slashed chicken and pricked eggs exist so the berbere can get all the way in.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Injera","Caramelizing onions","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","tangy-ferment","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Doro%20Wat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Doro Wat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"berbere-and-mitmita","term":"Berbere & Mitmita"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Lemon","Butter","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"misir-wat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g red lentils, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 red onions, minced; 4 garlic cloves; thumb of ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp berbere"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp niter kibbeh or oil (oil keeps it vegan, Ethiopia fasts expertly)"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml water; salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry-toast the onions in the pot 10 min (the doro wat trick, abbreviated), then add fat, garlic, ginger.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Berbere in; fry 3–4 min with splashes of water until brick red and fragrant.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lentils and water; simmer 25 min, stirring more as it thickens.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until the lentils dissolve into a thick, spoon-standing red porridge. Salt; serve on injera.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Over 200 fasting days a year make Ethiopia a quiet vegan superpower, and misir wat is the crown jewel, meatless by design, not compromise. Done right it holds its shape on injera without running; if it pours, keep cooking.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Injera","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","earthy","warm-spiced","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, an earthy, grounding base, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Misir%20Wat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Misir Wat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Lentils","Curry paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"shiro-wat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g shiro powder (roasted, spiced chickpea flour: buy from an Ethiopian shop; it is already seasoned by someone's grandmother)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, minced fine; 3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp niter kibbeh or oil; 1 tomato, grated (optional, Gurage-style)"},{"kind":"item","text":"~600ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Jalapeño rings and a knob of butter to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soften onion and garlic in the fat; add tomato if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk shiro powder with the warm water into a smooth slurry: no lumps enter the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in; simmer, whisking, 10–15 min as it thickens to double-cream body.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with butter and fresh chili; it should still slowly spread on the injera, not stand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Shiro is Ethiopia's daily bread-and-butter dish: the powder itself carries the spice, so the cooking takes minutes. The texture debate is regional and eternal: thick (tegabino, served bubbling in a clay pot) or pourable. Both camps are certain.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Injera","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shiro%20Wat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shiro Wat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Butter","Flour","Chickpeas","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"awaze-tibs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g beef sirloin or lamb leg, in bite-size cubes, some fat left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp niter kibbeh"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion in wedges, 2 green chilies, 1 tomato, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"Awaze: 2 tbsp berbere mixed with honey wine (tej), red wine or water to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rosemary sprig, the Ethiopian tibs signature; injera or bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Get a heavy pan brutally hot; sear the meat in niter kibbeh in one layer, brown, not gray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Onion wedges and rosemary in; toss 2 min, keeping crunch.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Awaze paste in; flame down; toss 1–2 min so it coats and caramelizes at the edges.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tomato and chili last, barely warmed. Serve sizzling, ideally in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tibs is Ethiopia's celebration sear: meat cut small so every cube gets crust. The rosemary is the tell of a proper tibs house, and awaze (berbere loosened with honey wine) is the difference between spiced meat and the real thing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Injera","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","floral","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a floral thread lifting the aroma, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Awaze%20Tibs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Awaze Tibs"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"reverse-sear-and-the-gray-band","term":"Reverse Sear & the Gray Band"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Rosemary","Bread","Wine","Honey","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"gomen-wat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 big bunches collard greens or kale, ribs out, rolled and sliced into ribbons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion; 4 garlic cloves; thumb of ginger, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp niter kibbeh or oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chilies, split; ½ tsp korerima (Ethiopian cardamom) if you can find it"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon squeeze; salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soften onion in the fat; garlic, ginger, chilies.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Greens in with a splash of water; cover; steam-sauté 20–25 min, stirring occasionally.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook past bright green to deep, tender olive: this is not a crisp-tender dish; it is a surrendered one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Korerima and lemon at the end; serve alongside the red wats for contrast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Gomen is the cool green counterweight on the injera platter: mild by design, sitting between the berbere-red wats like a rest between chords. The gebeta (shared platter) is composed like music: hot, mild, sour, soft, and everyone eats from the same score.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gomen%20Wat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gomen Wat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Kale/Collards","Lemon","Bread","Cardamom","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"kitfo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g leanest beef (eye of round), impeccably fresh, minced fine by hand or a trusted butcher, twice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp niter kibbeh (Ethiopian spiced clarified butter), warmed to liquid"},{"kind":"item","text":"2–3 tsp mitmita (the fiery orange chili-cardamom powder, hotter than berbere)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ayib (fresh crumbly cheese, like dry cottage cheese) and gomen alongside"},{"kind":"item","text":"Injera or kocho; korerima pinch"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the niter kibbeh gently: it must be liquid but never hot enough to cook.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the raw minced beef with the butter and mitmita until glossy and uniform.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately: traditional is tere (fully raw and body-warm), leb leb is lightly warmed in the pan for the cautious.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Plate with cool ayib and gomen: the mild cheese is the fire brigade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kitfo is the Gurage people's pride and Ethiopia's steak tartare: butter-warm, mitmita-hot, eaten the day the animal is butchered. Order it leb leb (barely warmed) your first time; the ayib on the side is not decoration, it is the antidote.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Injera"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","gently hot","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kitfo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kitfo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"berbere-and-mitmita","term":"Berbere & Mitmita"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Chiles","Orange","Butter","Cheese (any)","Curry paste","Cardamom"]},{"slug":"ugali","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g white maize flour (fine cornmeal)"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt (contested: many Kenyan kitchens say none)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A strong wooden spoon (mwiko) and a firmer forearm"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sukuma wiki, nyama choma, or any saucy stew alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the water; rain in a third of the flour, whisking, into a porridge; cook 3 min.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the rest of the flour in stages, switching to the wooden spoon, folding and pressing hard against the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 8–10 min, turning the mass, until it pulls cleanly off the sides and smells toasty.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Turn out into a bowl; shape a dome. Tear with hands, dent with a thumb, scoop the stew.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"From Nairobi to Dar es Salaam, ugali is the table's foundation: judged entirely by texture: firm enough to hold its dome, soft enough to press a thumbprint. The toasted layer stuck to the pot (the crust) is fought over by those who know.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ugali%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ugali"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"kachumbari","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 ripe tomatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, shaved thin, soaked 10 min in salted lime water (the taming bath)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1–2 green chilies, minced; big handful cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of 2 limes; 2 tbsp oil (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt; ½ avocado, cubed, if serving with nyama choma"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the onion: raw onion untamed will conquer the whole salad.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss tomatoes, drained onion, chili, cilantro.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lime and salt just before serving; oil only if the meal is lean.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve cold against hot grilled meat, its entire purpose.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kachumbari is East Africa's fresh counterstrike, born to sit beside smoky nyama choma: the raw salad's acid and crunch against grilled meat's char is the whole point of the pairing, from Nairobi to Dar es Salaam. The salted-lime onion bath is the one technique that matters: bite without brutality, the harsh sulfur drawn out while the crunch stays. Tomatoes ripe, chilies to taste, cilantro if your family is a cilantro family. It is ready in ten minutes and better after twenty.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","charred","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kachumbari%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kachumbari"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Avocado","Lime","Cilantro"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"mandazi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g flour; 7g yeast; 80g sugar; ½ tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml coconut milk, warmed (the Swahili coast signature)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg; 1 tsp ground cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar or a dip in chai; that is the whole ceremony"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead everything into a soft dough; rise 1 h until pillowy.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 1cm thick; cut triangles: the traditional shape, no scraps wasted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 170°C, turning, until they puff hollow and turn deep gold, 2–3 min a side.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain; dust or dunk. Best warm, with spiced tea, watching the Indian Ocean if possible.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mandazi are the Swahili coast's answer to the doughnut: coconut milk and cardamom instead of glaze, triangular so the cutting wastes nothing. In Mombasa they are breakfast, snack and currency of hospitality in one.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mandazi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mandazi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Flour","Sugar","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"bacalhau-a-bras","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g salt cod, soaked 24–36 h, water changed thrice, then shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g shoestring potato matchsticks, fried crisp (or good store-bought batata palha, Lisbon's open secret)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced thin; 3 garlic cloves; bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs, barely beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black olives, flat parsley, black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the onions with garlic and bay in olive oil, slow, 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cod in; toss 3–4 min until it drinks the oil.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold in the crisp potatoes, then pour the eggs over LOW heat, folding lazily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off while the eggs are still glossy and creamy: they finish on the walk to the table. Olives and parsley over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Portugal claims 365 bacalhau recipes, one per day, and Brás is the one every Lisbon tasca is judged by. The eggs must stay loose: set eggs make cod scramble; creamy eggs make à Brás. The fried potato holds its whisper of crunch for exactly one serving: eat immediately.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bacalhau%20%C3%A0%20Br%C3%A1s%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bacalhau à Brás"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Salt cod","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Olives","Parsley","Bay leaf","Clove"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"caldo-verde","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g floury potatoes; 1 onion; 3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2L water; 100ml good olive oil, divided"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g couve galega or collard greens/kale, rolled and sliced into the finest ribbons you can manage"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g chouriço, sliced in coins"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt; broa (corn bread) alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer potatoes, onion, garlic in the water until collapsing; blend smooth with half the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the chouriço coins; keep them and their paprika-stained oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the green ribbons into the simmering purée for 3–4 minutes ONLY: they must stay bright and slightly resistant.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve with chouriço and its oil floated on top, and the rest of the olive oil poured with confidence.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Portugal's national soup, from the Minho north: the potato purée is the canvas, the barely-cooked green ribbons the identity. The knife work is the recipe: the greens should be cut so fine they drape like silk on the spoon.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","aromatic","silky","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":36,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caldo%20Verde%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Caldo Verde"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Potato","Kale/Collards","Corn","Bread","Flour","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"frango-piri-piri","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, spatchcocked flat (backbone out, pressed with authority)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Piri-piri marinade: 6–10 African bird's eye chilies, 6 garlic cloves, 1 tbsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml olive oil, 60ml lemon juice, 2 tbsp red wine vinegar, bay, oregano, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Extra sauce reserved BEFORE the raw chicken touches any of it"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fries and a tomato-onion salad, churrasqueira style"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blitz the marinade; reserve a third for basting and the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slash the chicken to the bone; massage the rest in; marinate 4 h to overnight.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over medium coals (or roast 220°C), skin-side up first, turning every 10 min, ~40 min, basting with the reserved sauce.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 10; cut with scissors, churrasqueira style; more sauce over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The chili sailed from the Americas to Africa on Portuguese ships and came home as piri-piri, the empire's spiciest souvenir. Every churrasqueira guards its sauce recipe; the constant flipping and basting builds the lacquered, blistered skin no single turn can.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Butchery basics","Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","bright","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Butchery basics","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i_ZkAHCR1D0","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Frango%20Piri-Piri%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Frango Piri-Piri"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lemon","Oregano","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Wine","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg small clams, purged 2 h in salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil; 100ml white wine (vinho verde keeps the theme)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 enormous handful cilantro, chopped: the Portuguese herb, not a garnish"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon; bread for the broth, mandatory"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the garlic in the olive oil until fragrant and barely gold, never brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Clams in; wine in; lid on; high heat 3–4 min, shaking the pan.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"The moment the shells open, cilantro in by the fistful; toss once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lemon over; straight to the table in the pan. Discard any shell that stayed shut. Bread into the broth immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Named for a 19th-century poet who understood priorities. The broth (olive oil, wine, clam liquor, cilantro) outranks the clams themselves; Lisbon provides bread for a reason. Cilantro separates Portugal from every garlic-clam dish in Spain: fistfuls, not sprigs.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","herb-fresh","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Am%C3%AAijoas%20%C3%A0%20Bulh%C3%A3o%20Pato%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Garlic","Lemon","Cilantro","Bread","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"bifana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork loin or shoulder, sliced thin as a coin"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, mashed; 1 tbsp sweet paprika; 1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml white wine + 1 tbsp white wine vinegar; 50g lard or olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 papo secos (crusty Portuguese rolls), or the crustiest rolls in reach"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yellow mustard and piri-piri sauce at the table; a cold beer beside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the pork slices in garlic, paprika, wine, vinegar, bay: 1 h minimum.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Melt the lard in a wide pan; fry the pork fast in batches, then return all with the marinade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 10 min until the liquid becomes a scant, garlicky jus.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pile into split rolls, dredging each slice back through the pan juices on the way. Mustard stripe; squeeze of piri-piri.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The bifana is Portugal's sandwich of record: football matches, festivals, 2am. The roll must be dipped-not-drowned: enough jus to stain the crumb, never enough to collapse the crust. Vendas Novas claims the throne and defends it annually.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bifana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bifana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"beer-at-the-table-and-the-carbonation-advantage","term":"Beer at the Table & the Carbonation Advantage"},{"slug":"pork-loin-vs-tenderloin","term":"Pork Loin vs. Tenderloin"},{"slug":"beer-styles-the-two-family-map","term":"Beer Styles: The Two-Family Map"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Bay leaf","Bread","Vinegar","Wine","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"francesinha","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Per sandwich: 2 slices soft white bread, 1 fresh sausage, 1 linguiça, 1 thin steak, 2 slices ham"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices melting cheese to shroud it entirely"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 300ml beer, 200ml tomato passata, 1 tbsp piri-piri, 30ml port, beef stock, butter, flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg on top (a la Francesinha moderna); fries around the moat"},{"kind":"item","text":"A nap scheduled for afterward"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the meats; build the tower: bread, steak, sausages, ham, bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shroud the whole sandwich in cheese slices like a blanket.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill or bake until the cheese melts into a seamless coat; crown with a fried egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sauce ingredients 15 min to a spicy beer-tomato velvet; pour over EVERYTHING at the table; fries into the moat.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Porto's 'little French girl': a croque monsieur that emigrated and came back armored. Every tasca guards its sauce like a state secret (the beer-port-piri-piri base is the known perimeter). It is eaten with knife, fork, and humility.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Francesinha%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Francesinha"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"beer-at-the-table-and-the-carbonation-advantage","term":"Beer at the Table & the Carbonation Advantage"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Sausage","Eggs","Tomato","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread","Flour","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth"]},{"slug":"polvo-a-lagareiro","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 octopus, 1.5–2kg, cleaned (frozen and thawed is BETTER: the ice does the tenderizing)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1kg small potatoes, skin on, batatas a murro, 'punched potatoes'"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head of garlic, cloves smashed; bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml of your best olive oil: lagareiro means the oil-press man; this dish is his tribute"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro or parsley; coarse salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the octopus with bay and an onion, gently, 50–60 min until a knife slides into the thick tentacle; rest in its liquid.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast salted potatoes until tender; give each a gentle punch to crack it open.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Octopus and potatoes into a roasting dish; bury in garlic; flood with olive oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast 220°C, 15–20 min until the tentacle tips char and curl. Serve in the oil: it is the sauce.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Freeze-thaw beats every wine-cork legend for tenderizing octopus: ice crystals do the work no simmering trick can. The punched potatoes drink the garlicky oil through their cracks; lagareiro is really a dish about olive oil wearing an octopus costume.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","aromatic","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polvo%20%C3%A0%20Lagareiro%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Polvo à Lagareiro"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cephalopods-squid-octopus-cuttlefish","term":"Cephalopods (Squid, Octopus, Cuttlefish)"}],"pantryItems":["Squid/Octopus","Garlic","Potato","Cilantro","Parsley","Bay leaf","Clove"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"sardinhas-assadas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 fat fresh sardines: June sardines, when they are oily and proud; scaled, GUTS IN (tradition) or out (peace at home)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse sea salt, applied 30 min ahead"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices broa or crusty bread; roasted green pepper salad"},{"kind":"item","text":"Charcoal: a grill pan is the apology, coals are the answer"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the sardines heavily 30 min ahead; brush the grill, not the fish, with oil.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot coals 3–4 min a side without fiddling: the skin releases when it is ready and not before.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slide each sardine onto a slice of bread: the bread is the plate and the sponge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Eat with fingers, discarding the spine like a comb. The bread, now soaked in sardine drippings, is the final course.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"During Lisbon's Santo António festival in June the whole city smells of this: sardines are grilled by the million and eaten on bread in the street. The drip-soaked bread eaten last is the ritual's quiet reward; some elders skip the fish and negotiate directly for the bread.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","smoky","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, wood-smoke running underneath, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sardinhas%20Assadas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sardinhas Assadas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-oily-fish-calendar","term":"The Oily Fish Calendar"},{"slug":"building-the-home-and-starting-bar","term":"Building the Home & Starting Bar"}],"pantryItems":["Sardines","Bell pepper","Bread"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"pasteis-de-nata","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 sheet all-butter puff pastry (or laminate your own if the saints move you)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Syrup: 200g sugar, 120ml water, cinnamon stick, lemon peel, boiled 2 min, cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g flour + 250ml milk, whisked smooth, cooked to a thick white base"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cinnamon and icing sugar for dusting; an espresso beside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Roll the pastry tight like a cigar; slice into 12 coins; press each into muffin tins with wet thumbs, spiraling the layers UP the sides thin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the syrup into the milk base, then the yolks; strain into a jug.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill cups three-quarters; bake at your oven's absolute maximum, 270°C+, 10–12 min.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"The tops must scorch in leopard spots while the pastry shatters. Cool 10 min only; dust; eat warm.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"The monks of Belém invented them to use yolks left from starching habits with egg whites, and the 1837 original recipe is still locked away, known to three people. The blistered black spots are not a flaw: an unscorched nata is an unfinished one. Maximum heat, brief time: the custard should tremble, the pastry should audibly crackle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Lamination","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","charred","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Past%C3%A9is%20de%20Nata%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pastéis de Nata"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Flour","Phyllo/Puff pastry","Sugar","Coffee/Espresso","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"arroz-doce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g short-grain rice (carolino, Portugal's pearl)"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml water with a pinch of salt, lemon peel in wide strips, 1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"700ml whole milk, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar; 3 egg yolks (the Portuguese enrichment: this is not plain rice pudding)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ground cinnamon for the decorative crown"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook rice in the lemon-cinnamon water until absorbed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add warm milk in stages, stirring, risotto-patience, 25 min, until creamy but still loose.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sugar in; then temper in the yolks off the heat: they turn it golden and silken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour onto shallow platters; when cool, dust cinnamon through a stencil in diamonds and lattices, the grandmother's signature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cinnamon lattice on top is a folk art: Portuguese grandmothers sign their arroz doce in patterns, and at weddings the designs get ambitious. The yolks separate it from every other rice pudding in Europe: richer, golden, and properly loose; it should relax on the plate, never stand in a scoop.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Risotto method"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","bright","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Risotto method","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=risotto%20technique%20mantecatura","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arroz%20Doce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arroz Doce"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Milk","Rice","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"nasi-goreng","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 cups day-old rice, cold, clumps broken"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp kecap manis: the sweet soy that IS the flavor of Indonesia"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sambal or 3 red chilies + 3 shallots + 2 garlic, pounded"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g chicken, shrimp, or leftover anything; 2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"The crown: a fried egg per person, prawn crackers, cucumber, fried shallots"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the pounded paste in generous oil until it smells like an Indonesian street at dusk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Protein in; then rice, pressing and tossing over maximum heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Kecap manis down the side of the wok: let it scorch slightly before tossing; that caramel char is the taste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Well in the middle; scramble the eggs there; toss through. Crown each plate with a wobbling fried egg and crackers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Indonesia's breakfast of champions is built on last night's rice: fresh rice steams into mush; day-old rice fries into distinct, chewy grains. The kecap manis must touch hot metal before it touches rice: scorched sweet soy is nasi goreng's soul.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Sugar stages & caramel","Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nasi%20Goreng%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nasi Goreng"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"shrimp-sizing-and-prawn-confusion","term":"Shrimp Sizing & Prawn Confusion"},{"slug":"the-wok","term":"The Wok"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Shrimp","Eggs","Garlic","Shallot","Chiles","Cucumber","Rice","Soy sauce","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"beef-rendang","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg beef chuck or shin, large cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paste: 10 shallots, 6 garlic, 5 red chilies, thumb galangal, thumb ginger, 3 lemongrass stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"800ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 makrut lime leaves, 1 turmeric leaf if the gods provide, 1 tbsp tamarind"},{"kind":"item","text":"Kerisik: 80g toasted grated coconut, pounded to an oily paste, the secret weight"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blitz the paste; combine EVERYTHING cold in a wide pot: no browning, no sautéing; rendang starts as a swimming pool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered on medium-low, stirring occasionally, 2 h, as it thickens from soup (gulai) to stew (kalio).","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep going: the coconut milk breaks, the oil returns, and the beef begins to FRY in its own sauce. Stir constantly now, 30–45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Kerisik in near the end. Done when the sauce is a dark, clinging paste and the kitchen smells like a Padang restaurant. Better tomorrow, immortal by day three.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rendang is not a curry; it is a slow-motion transformation with names for each stage, ending when the dish essentially deep-fries itself in its own split coconut oil. Minangkabau cooks made it to survive weeks of travel without refrigeration; the dryness is preservation wearing the costume of flavor.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beef%20Rendang%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beef Rendang"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-fresh-aromatic-trio-galangal-lemongrass-and-makrut-lime","term":"The Fresh Aromatic Trio: Galangal, Lemongrass & Makrut Lime"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Lemongrass","Curry/Makrut leaves","Milk","Coconut milk","Tamarind","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"sate-ayam","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken thighs, small flat slices; bamboo skewers, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade: 3 tbsp kecap manis, 2 garlic, 1 tbsp coriander seed, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"Peanut sauce: 150g fried peanuts pounded (or chunky peanut butter, forgiven), 2 red chilies, 2 garlic, 1 tbsp palm sugar, tamarind, kecap manis"},{"kind":"item","text":"Thin kecap manis + lime + shallot for the final drizzle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lontong (rice cakes) or plain rice; cucumber"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate chicken 1 h; thread flat and close on skewers.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauce: fry pounded chili and garlic; add peanuts, palm sugar, tamarind, water; simmer to spoon-thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot coals, fanning, brushing with marinade, 3–4 min a side, until edges char and glisten.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pool sauce on the plate, satay on top, drizzle of kecap manis and lime; shallots over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Street sate men fan the coals with woven fans in a rhythm you can hear across the street: high heat, small meat, constant motion. The peanut sauce should taste of roasted peanut first, sweet second, chili third; drowning satay in sweet sauce is how you spot a tourist stall.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sate%20Ayam%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sate Ayam"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Shallot","Chiles","Cucumber","Lime","Butter","Rice","Soy sauce","Peanut","Sugar","Tamarind","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"gado-gado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Blanched: green beans, cabbage, bean sprouts, spinach or kangkung"},{"kind":"item","text":"Boiled potatoes, halved boiled eggs; cucumber, raw"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fried tofu and tempeh, sliced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 200g fried peanuts, 3 chilies, 2 garlic, 2 tbsp palm sugar, 1 tbsp tamarind, salt; pounded or blitzed with warm water to a thick dressing"},{"kind":"item","text":"Krupuk (crackers) and fried shallots on top, the mandatory crunch"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch each vegetable separately, seconds each, into ice water, color and bite intact.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the tofu and tempeh golden.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make the sauce thick enough to hold a spoon upright, loosened just to pouring.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange everything in generous piles; flood with warm sauce; crown with krupuk and shallots. Toss at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Gado-gado means mix-mix: Indonesia's answer to salad is boiled, fried, and dressed in peanut sauce, and it out-satisfies most stews. The sauce is the dish: warm, thick, sweet-sour-hot in that order; a thin gado-gado sauce is a personal failing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gado-Gado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gado-Gado"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tofu","Eggs","Garlic","Shallot","Potato","Chiles","Spinach","Cabbage","Green beans","Cucumber","Bean sprouts","Peanut","Sugar","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"soto-ayam","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 small chicken or 6 thighs; 1.5L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paste: 6 shallots, 4 garlic, 3 candlenuts (or macadamias), thumb turmeric, the golden engine, thumb ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 lemongrass stalks bruised, 4 lime leaves, 2 bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"Glass noodles, bean sprouts, shredded cabbage, boiled egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sambal, fried shallots, celery leaf, lime: the ritual table of garnishes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer chicken to a light broth, 40 min; shred the meat; strain the broth.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the pounded turmeric paste until the oil turns gold and the rawness leaves.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Paste into broth with lemongrass and lime leaves; simmer 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build bowls: noodles, sprouts, chicken, egg; ladle the golden broth; let each eater garnish: lime and sambal are not optional.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Every Indonesian region has its soto and defends it; the turmeric-gold Lamongan style is the great unifier. Candlenuts are the quiet thickener: they melt into the broth and give it body without cream. The garnish table is the point: soto arrives unfinished on purpose.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","earthy","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Soto%20Ayam%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Soto Ayam"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-sommeliers-service-ritual-and-floor-fluency","term":"The Sommelier's Service Ritual & Floor Fluency"},{"slug":"turmeric","term":"Turmeric"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Celery","Cabbage","Lime","Bean sprouts","Lemongrass","Curry/Makrut leaves","Noodles","Nuts","Curry paste","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"mie-goreng","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g fresh yellow noodles (or instant noodles, parboiled, the honest midnight road)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp kecap manis + 1 tbsp soy + 1 tsp oyster sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic, 3 shallots, 2 chilies, pounded; 150g chicken or shrimp"},{"kind":"item","text":"Handful of choy sum or cabbage; 2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fried shallots, lime, pickled chilies; krupuk if they are near"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the paste; protein in until just done.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push aside; scramble eggs in the gap.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Noodles and greens in; sauces down the hot wok wall; toss violently over maximum flame 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The noodles should catch and char in spots: wok breath, Indonesian accent. Shallots and lime over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mie goreng is nasi goreng's noodle sibling and Indonesia's 2am ambassador. Same law applies: the kecap manis must scorch on the wok steel, and the slight char spots are the flavor: a pale mie goreng is a steamed one.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mie%20Goreng%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mie Goreng"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"why-restaurants-fail-the-honest-list","term":"Why Restaurants Fail: The Honest List"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Shrimp","Eggs","Garlic","Shallot","Chiles","Cabbage","Lime","Noodles","Soy sauce","Oyster sauce","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"sambal-terasi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"10 red chilies + 5 bird's eye (adjust courage accordingly)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 shallots, 3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp terasi (fermented shrimp paste), toasted in foil until it announces itself to the neighborhood"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato; 1 tbsp palm sugar; lime juice; salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the terasi in foil in a dry pan: 2 min a side; it will smell alarming and taste essential.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry chilies, shallots, garlic, tomato until soft and blistered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound everything in a stone mortar (ulekan) to a rough, glistening paste; a blender makes it too polite.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the paste in the same oil 5 min; season with palm sugar, salt, lime. Keeps a week; improves for three days.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Indonesia runs on sambal: hundreds of varieties, and terasi is the backbone of the classic. The mortar matters: pounding ruptures cells and marries oils in a way blades cannot. A meal without sambal, say the Javanese, is a conversation without warmth.","equipment":["Blender","Mortar & pestle","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Fermentation","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","sweet-edged","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a sweet edge rounding the corners, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sambal%20Terasi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sambal Terasi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-essential-smallwares","term":"The Essential Smallwares"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Shallot","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Fish sauce","Sugar","Curry paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","fermentation"]},{"slug":"tempe-orek","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g tempeh, cut in matchsticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp kecap manis; 1 tbsp palm sugar; 2 tbsp tamarind water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 shallots, 2 garlic, 2 red chilies, sliced; 1 lime leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for shallow frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sesame or fried peanuts, optional crunch reinforcement"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the tempeh sticks in hot oil until deep gold and rigid; drain well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry shallots, garlic, chili, lime leaf until fragrant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Kecap manis, palm sugar, tamarind in; bubble to a sticky glaze, 1–2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tempeh back; toss fast to lacquer every stick. Serve at room temperature: it stays crisp for days.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tempeh is Java's 400-year-old gift to the world, fermented soy with actual character. The kering (dry) style glaze must be cooked to the hard-sticky stage so the tempeh stays crunchy; Indonesians pack it for travel because it refuses to go stale.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","aromatic","gently hot","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, aromatics blooming from the first bite, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tempe%20Orek%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tempe Orek"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"deep-and-shallow-frying","term":"Deep & Shallow Frying"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Shallot","Chiles","Lime","Soy sauce","Peanut","Sesame","Sugar","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"pisang-goreng","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 ripe saba or plantain-adjacent bananas (firm-ripe; spotted, not blackened)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Batter: 120g flour + 30g rice flour, 1 tbsp sugar, pinch salt, ~180ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sesame seeds in the batter, optional but loved"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grated cheese + condensed milk drizzle (Indonesian genius) or plain with coffee"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the batter smooth and cold: rice flour is the crunch insurance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Halve bananas lengthwise or fan them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip and fry at 175°C until deep gold with lacy edges, 3–4 min.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain on a rack; serve hot: with the cheese-and-condensed-milk treatment if you trust me. You should.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Every Indonesian street corner has a pisang goreng cart at 4pm, and the cheese-on-banana move sounds wrong to every foreigner exactly once. Rice flour in the batter keeps it crackling past cooling: the wheat-only versions go soft while your back is turned.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pisang%20Goreng%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pisang Goreng"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Plantain","Banana","Milk","Cheese (any)","Flour","Sesame","Sugar","Coffee/Espresso"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"nasi-lemak","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g rice cooked in 300ml coconut milk + 200ml water, 3 pandan leaves knotted, pinch salt, slice of ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sambal: 15 dried chilies soaked, 6 shallots, 1 tsp belacan, tamarind, palm sugar: fried down dark and sweet-hot"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crisp anchovies (ikan bilis) + roasted peanuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"Boiled egg halves, cucumber coins"},{"kind":"item","text":"Banana leaf, for serving and for honor"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the rice with coconut milk, pandan, ginger: it should emerge rich and perfumed, every grain separate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the sambal paste patiently, 20 min, until it darkens and the oil splits: sweet first, then heat.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry anchovies until glass-crisp; toast peanuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mound rice on banana leaf; sambal beside; ring with anchovies, peanuts, egg, cucumber. Mix as you eat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Malaysia's national breakfast, named for the rice ('fatty rice') not the extras. The sambal is the exam: it must balance sweet and heat so precisely that argument is impossible, which in Malaysia means argument is constant. Pandan is the perfume that no shortcut replaces.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","umami-deep","floral","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nasi%20Lemak%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nasi Lemak"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Eggs","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Cucumber","Banana","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Fish sauce","Peanut","Sugar","Tamarind","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"char-kway-teow","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g fresh flat rice noodles, room temperature, gently separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g shrimp + Chinese sausage (lap cheong), sliced; cockles if you are in Penang"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs; big handful bean sprouts; Chinese chives"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves; 2 tbsp lard: yes, lard; this dish keeps score"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 2 tbsp light soy + 1 tbsp dark soy + 1 tsp kecap manis, chili paste"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wok to maximum violence; lard in; garlic 5 seconds; sausage and shrimp 30 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Noodles in; sauce over; toss with pulling motions, letting the noodles sit and CHAR between tosses.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push aside; crack eggs; break and drag through the noodles half-set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprouts and chives; 20 seconds; out. Total wok time under 4 minutes, one portion at a time.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Penang hawkers cook char kway teow ONE PLATE AT A TIME over jet flames: crowding the wok kills the wok hei, the smoky breath that is the entire dish. Lard is traditional and audible in the flavor; the best stalls are run by someone's grandmother who has made nothing else for 40 years.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Char%20Kway%20Teow%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Char Kway Teow"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lap-cheong-and-the-chinese-cured-pantry","term":"Lap Cheong & the Chinese Cured Pantry"}],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Shrimp","Mussels/Clams","Eggs","Garlic","Chiles","Bean sprouts","Chives","Noodles","Soy sauce","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"char-kway-teow","marks":[{"id":"char-kway-teow#noodles","text":"Noodles lift as individual strands, glossy and separate, with no torn fragments and no mat of stuck-together ribbons."},{"id":"char-kway-teow#colour","text":"Colour is deliberately uneven: dark soy stains some strands more than others and scattered noodles carry black-brown char blisters. Even mahogany throughout means it was stirred rather than charred."},{"id":"char-kway-teow#held","text":"Held at arm's length the plate smells of scorch and smoke before it smells of soy, and that smell is still on it when it reaches the table."},{"id":"char-kway-teow#ragged","text":"Egg is in ragged folds gripping the noodles, set but soft, with no separate scrambled lumps and no wet gloss of raw white."},{"id":"char-kway-teow#bean","text":"Bean sprouts snap between the teeth, the chives are bright green, the shrimp are curled once and just opaque, and the plate rim carries no pooled oil or sauce."}],"fault":"Fridge-cold noodles pulled apart in a hurry, so the strands break before they meet the lard and the dish arrives as short stubs bound in leached starch, however hot the wok was."}},{"slug":"curry-laksa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Paste: 8 dried chilies, 6 shallots, 4 garlic, thumb galangal, 2 lemongrass, 1 tbsp belacan, 3 candlenuts, 1 tbsp curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"800ml coconut milk + 600ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g yellow noodles + rice vermicelli, mixed, the two-noodle law"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toppings: poached chicken, prawns, tofu puffs (the broth sponges), fish cake, bean sprouts"},{"kind":"item","text":"Half a boiled egg, laksa leaf (Vietnamese mint), sambal, calamansi lime"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the pounded paste in oil 10 full minutes: it must darken and split before any liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stock in, simmer 10; coconut milk in, never boiling hard, 10 more.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanch both noodles; poach the toppings in the broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build bowls: noodles, toppings, tofu puffs; flood with broth; laksa leaf and sambal on top; lime on the side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The tofu puffs are the secret MVPs: they drink the curry broth and burst in the mouth. Kuala Lumpur curry laksa vs Penang assam laksa is the eternal derby; this is the coconut side of the river. Fry the paste until the oil returns or the whole bowl tastes raw.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Curry%20Laksa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Curry Laksa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"curry-leaf","term":"Curry Leaf"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Shrimp","Tofu","Eggs","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Bean sprouts","Mint","Lemongrass","Milk","Coconut milk","Noodles","Fish sauce","Stock/Broth","Curry paste","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"assam-laksa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g mackerel, simmered, flesh flaked, broth reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paste: 8 dried chilies, 6 shallots, 1 tbsp belacan, thumb galangal, 2 lemongrass"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp tamarind pulp + 2 pieces asam keping (dried tamarind slices), the sour engine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Torch ginger flower (bunga kantan), shredded, the perfume that defines Penang"},{"kind":"item","text":"Thick rice noodles; garnish: cucumber, pineapple, red onion, mint, and hae ko (black shrimp paste) to swirl in"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer mackerel 15 min; flake; return bones to the broth briefly for depth, then strain.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the paste; add to the broth with tamarind, asam keping, torch ginger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 20 min; return the flaked fish; the broth should be sour first, fishy-sweet second, hot third.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bowls: thick noodles, broth, then the raw garnish garden on top; a teaspoon of hae ko stirred in at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"CNN once ranked this among the best foods on earth, and Penang shrugged; they knew. No coconut here: assam laksa is sour, herbal, and defiantly fishy, and the torch ginger flower is non-negotiable perfume. The hae ko swirl at the end is the handshake that completes it.","equipment":["Torch"],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","umami-deep","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Assam%20Laksa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Assam Laksa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"floral-notes-and-perfume-dosage","term":"Floral Notes & Perfume Dosage"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Cucumber","Pineapple","Mint","Lemongrass","Noodles","Bread","Fish sauce","Stock/Broth","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"roti-canai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour; 1 tsp salt; 1 tbsp sugar; 1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml water + 3 tbsp condensed milk (the mamak softness secret)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ghee or oil: for the dough, the rest, the flip, and your hands"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 h to overnight resting, dough balls submerged in oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dhal curry or chicken curry for the mandatory dunk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead a soft dough; divide into balls; coat in oil; rest MINIMUM 6 hours: the rest does the stretching for you.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"On an oiled counter: flatten, then flip-slap-stretch the dough tissue-thin (or gently pull to tablecloth thinness, the coward-friendly road; the holes forgive).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ripple the sheet like a curtain, coil into a snail, tuck the tail; rest 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flatten the coil; griddle with ghee until gold and blistered. Then CLAP the hot roti between both hands from the sides: the layers shatter and bloom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The final clap is not theatre: compressing the hot coiled layers makes them separate and flake. Mamak stall masters flip dough like matadors, but the long oily rest means gravity can do it for you at home. Score the flakiness by the sound: a proper roti canai crackles when clapped.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Roti%20Canai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Roti Canai"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"resting-carryover-and-the-thermometer","term":"Resting, Carryover & the Thermometer"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"hainanese-chicken-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole chicken (1.5kg), best you can find: the dish is nowhere to hide"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ginger, scallions, salt; iced water bath standing by"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice: 400g jasmine, cooked in the poaching broth with garlic, ginger, rendered chicken fat, pandan if you have it"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chili sauce: red chilies, ginger, garlic, lime, a spoon of the broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dark soy, cucumber; the broth served as soup: nothing is wasted"}],"steps":[{"text":"Submerge chicken in barely-simmering salted water with ginger and scallion; poach 30–35 min at a shiver, never a boil.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Straight into iced water 10 min: the skin tightens into its signature jelly-silk layer.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cook the rice in the skimmed broth with garlic, ginger and chicken fat: the rice is secretly the star.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop chicken through the bone; dress with soy and sesame; serve with rice, chili-ginger sauce, cucumber, and a bowl of the broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The ice bath is the entire technique: the collagen under the skin sets into that translucent silk layer prized from Penang to Singapore. And every hawker will tell you the truth quietly: people come back for the rice. Fat, broth, garlic, pandan: judged on the rice alone.","equipment":["Bain-marie"],"techniques":["The water bath","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","herb-fresh","floral","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hainanese%20Chicken%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hainanese Chicken Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Chiles","Cucumber","Lime","Rice","Soy sauce","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"kaya-toast","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Kaya: 4 eggs, 200ml coconut milk, 150g sugar (30g caramelized for the brown hainanese style), 4 pandan leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Thin white bread or traditional charcoal-grilled bun"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cold butter, sliced THICK: it must be a slab, not a spread"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 soft-boiled eggs (90 seconds in boiled-then-off water, Malaysian style)"},{"kind":"item","text":"White pepper, dark soy; strong kopi to drink"}],"steps":[{"text":"Kaya: whisk eggs, sugar, coconut milk; cook in a double boiler with knotted pandan, stirring, 45 min to a thick amber curd. Patience is the ingredient.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the bread dry and crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Kaya thick on one slice, the cold butter SLAB on the other; press together: hot toast, melting edge, cold heart.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack the barely-set eggs into a saucer; season with soy and white pepper; dunk the toast. That is the law.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The kopitiam breakfast of Malaysia and Singapore: the butter must still be cold and visible when you bite: the contrast is the dish. Kaya means 'rich' in Malay, and the pandan-coconut curd earns the name; jarred versions are a postcard of a place this recipe actually takes you.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["The water bath"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","silky","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kaya%20Toast%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kaya Toast"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Butter","Milk","Coconut milk","Bread","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath"]},{"slug":"sambal-udang","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g large prawns, shells off, tails on (keep 4 heads for the oil: flavor lives there)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paste: 12 dried chilies soaked, 8 shallots, 4 garlic, 1 tbsp belacan, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tamarind water; 1 tbsp palm sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion in petals; oil, generous"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice, and more rice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the prawn heads in the oil 2 min; press and discard: the oil is now amber gold.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the pounded paste in that oil, patiently, 10–12 min until it darkens and the oil returns to the surface (pecah minyak, the oil breaks).","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tamarind, palm sugar, splash water; taste for the sweet-sour-heat triangle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prawns and onion petals in; 3–4 min only, glossed and just curled. Over rice, spooning every drop of the red oil.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pecah minyak, 'the oil breaks', is the Malay checkpoint every sambal must pass: when the red oil separates and floats, the paste is cooked; before that, it is raw and it will tell on you. The prawn-head oil is the professional's head start.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","sweet-edged","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":24,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sambal%20Udang%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sambal Udang"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Garlic","Shallot","Chiles","Rice","Fish sauce","Sugar","Tamarind","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"cendol","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Cendol jellies: 100g rice flour + 2 tbsp mung bean starch, 400ml pandan juice (blitz 10 leaves with water, strain), pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Gula melaka syrup: 200g palm sugar + 100ml water + 1 pandan leaf, melted to dark silk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml thick coconut milk, pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Shaved ice, a mountain of it"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sweetened red beans or creamed corn, the traditional reinforcements"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the pandan batter, stirring hard, until thick and glossy like green polenta, 5–6 min.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Press through a colander with large holes (or a cendol press) directly into iced water; worms of green jelly form and set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the gula melaka to a dark syrup; salt the coconut milk lightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tall glass: jellies, beans, ice mountain, coconut milk flood, gula melaka poured last in dark ribbons. Stir; drink-eat with a spoon-straw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The salt in the coconut milk is the professional touch; it makes the palm sugar taste twice as deep. Real gula melaka from Malacca is smoky and almost licorice-dark; the dish is a duet between that syrup and the pandan worms, and everything else is chorus.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cendol%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cendol"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Corn","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Flour","Kidney/Pinto beans","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"teh-tarik","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp strong black tea dust (or 4 robust teabags)"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4–6 tbsp condensed milk (the sweetness IS the point; this is not the moment for restraint)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 metal jugs with handles"},{"kind":"item","text":"A willingness to spill twice before you learn"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brew the tea strong, 5 min; strain onto the condensed milk; stir.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Now pull: pour the tea from one jug to the other from as high as your nerve allows, a meter is the goal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Repeat 5–7 times; the stream aerates into a microfoam crown and the temperature lands exactly drinkable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately, foam intact, with roti canai or kaya toast. Practice over the sink; mastery over the counter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Teh tarik means 'pulled tea', and the pull is engineering: aeration rounds the tannins, blooms the condensed milk, and cools the tea to drinking temperature in seconds. Mamak stall masters pull in long silver arcs without looking: Malaysia holds actual competitions, with trophies.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Teh%20Tarik%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Teh Tarik"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk"]},{"slug":"ayam-goreng-lengkuas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken pieces, bone-in"},{"kind":"item","text":"Spice bath: 4 tbsp grated galangal (lengkuas, the hero), 4 garlic, 4 shallots, 2 tsp ground coriander, thumb turmeric, 2 candlenuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml coconut water or water; 2 bay-like salam leaves, 1 lemongrass stalk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sambal terasi and cucumber alongside: see two cards over"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the spices; simmer the chicken IN the spice bath, uncovered, 30–40 min until the liquid is nearly gone and the chicken is cooked and stained gold.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reserve the spice sediment left in the pot: that is the kremes treasure.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dry the chicken 15 min; deep-fry at 180°C until deep bronze and crackled, 4–5 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry spoonfuls of the reserved spice sediment in the oil until they bloom into crisp golden flakes; shower over the chicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Java fries chicken twice-cooked: braised in spice first so the seasoning reaches the bone, then flash-fried for the shell. The kremes, the fried crumbs of the braising spices, are the part people order extra of, and now you know they were never a separate recipe.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ayam%20Goreng%20Lengkuas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ayam Goreng Lengkuas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indonesian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Indonesian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Cucumber","Coconut","Lemongrass","Curry paste","Coriander seed","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"sibirskie-pelmeni","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 400g flour, 1 egg, 150ml ice water, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filling: 250g beef + 250g pork, ground together twice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated to pulp; 3 tbsp ice water worked into the meat; black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter, sour cream (smetana), black pepper AND white vinegar: the two serving factions"},{"kind":"item","text":"A freezing balcony or a freezer: pelmeni are made by the hundred"}],"steps":[{"text":"Firm dough; rest 30 min under a bowl. Mix filling with the ice water until it slaps.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll thin; cut small circles; a hazelnut of filling each; fold half-moons, then pinch the tips together into little hats.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Freeze most of them on trays: Siberian families make thousands before winter; you may make sixty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil from frozen in salted water; when they float, give them 2 min more. Butter and smetana, or the vinegar-pepper road.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Siberian hunters carried frozen pelmeni as instant meals a century before the freezer aisle. The ice water in the filling steams inside the sealed dough: that is the juice. Small is correct: one pelmen, one bite, no negotiation.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sibirskie%20Pelmeni%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sibirskie Pelmeni"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Eggs","Onion","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"varenyky-z-vyshneyu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 400g flour, 200ml kefir or sour milk, 1 egg, ½ tsp soda, pinch salt, the soft Ukrainian style"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g sour cherries, pitted (frozen work beautifully), tossed with 3 tbsp sugar + 1 tbsp starch just before filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"More sugar for serving"},{"kind":"item","text":"Smetana or thick sour cream, a bowl of it"},{"kind":"item","text":"A summer evening, ideally in Poltava"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soft, pillowy dough: kefir and soda make it tender where Polish pierogi dough is snappy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss cherries with sugar and starch AT THE LAST MOMENT: sugared cherries weep and drown the seams.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill generously; seal with a rope-pinch; the juice will try to escape; deny it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil 3–4 min after they float; lift gently; serve buried in smetana and the escaped crimson juices.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ukraine's beloved summer varenyky: the cherry-sugar timing is the whole craft, and even grandmothers lose a few to burst seams (those are the cook's tax, eaten at the stove). The kefir dough stays soft even cold, which matters, because cold cherry varenyky at midnight are a documented phenomenon.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pierogi","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pierogi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20pierogi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Varenyky%20z%20Vyshneyu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Varenyky z Vyshneyu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Stone fruit","Milk","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"blini","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour; 7g yeast; 1 tbsp sugar; ½ tsp salt: yeast is what makes them blini, not pancakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml warm milk; 2 eggs, separated; 50g melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"More butter for the pan and for brushing every single finished blin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toppings in rank: smetana, jam, honey, smoked salmon, or caviar if the occasion earns it"},{"kind":"item","text":"A stack plate under a towel"}],"steps":[{"text":"Yeasted batter from milk, yolks, flour; rise 1 h until it breathes and bubbles.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in whipped whites, lightness insurance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry thin in a buttered pan, swirling; lace-edged, golden, one minute a side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush each with butter and stack under the towel. Serve the stack whole; let the table demolish it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Blini are older than Russia's written history: pagan sun symbols eaten by the stack at Maslenitsa, the butter festival before Lent. The yeast gives them a sour-tender crumb no baking-powder pancake reaches; the mandatory butter-brushing between layers is why the stack glistens like the sun it represents.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Blini%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Blini"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"roe-caviar-ikura-tobiko-bottarga","term":"Roe: Caviar, Ikura, Tobiko, Bottarga"},{"slug":"insurance-the-policy-stack","term":"Insurance: The Policy Stack"}],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Eggs","Smoked fish","Butter","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Honey","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"salat-olivier","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 waxy potatoes + 3 carrots, boiled in skins, cooled, peeled, diced FINE"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, hard-boiled, diced; 1 can peas, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 dill pickles (brined, not vinegar, if you can), diced; 300g bologna-style sausage or poached chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mayonnaise: proper full-fat, folded not drowned"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dill; and everything diced the SAME size, this is the exam"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dice everything to neat pea-sized cubes: uniformity is the aesthetic and the texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold together with pickles and peas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mayonnaise in stages until bound but not swimming; salt, pepper, dill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 2 h minimum: Olivier improves as the pickle brine wanders. Serve in a heap, at every New Year table, by law of tradition.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented by a French chef in 1860s Moscow with capers and grouse; Soviet scarcity rewrote it with bologna and peas, and that version conquered from Kyiv to Vladivostok. No New Year happens without it: the salad outlived the empire, the union, and every diet that challenged it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salat%20Olivier%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salat Olivier"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mortadella-bologna","term":"Mortadella Bologna"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Eggs","Potato","Carrot","Peas","Dill","Vinegar","Mayonnaise","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"shchi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef brisket on the bone (or mushroom broth for the fasting version)"},{"kind":"item","text":"½ fresh cabbage, shredded, or 400g sauerkraut for sour shchi, the winter king"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 potatoes, 1 carrot, 1 onion, 1 parsley root"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bay, peppercorns, garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"Smetana and black bread, the constitutionally required escorts"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the beef to a clean broth, 1.5 h, skimming.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Vegetables in by hardness: potatoes, carrot, then cabbage (fresh cooks brief; kraut goes early and long).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Garlic and bay near the end; the broth should stay light: shchi is not a stew.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest a day if you can (sutochnye shchi, day-old shchi, is the connoisseur's version). Smetana in the bowl, never the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A thousand years of Russian dinner in one word. The proverb says 'Shchi da kasha: pishcha nasha': cabbage soup and porridge are our food. Freezing it was traditional, not convenient: carters carried frozen blocks of shchi and axed off portions to melt over fires.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","umami-deep","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shchi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shchi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mushroom-field-guide","term":"Mushroom Field Guide"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Carrot","Mushrooms","Cabbage","Parsley","Bay leaf","Sour cream/Crema","Bread","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"solyanka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g mixed smoked and cured meats: sausage, ham, smoked ribs, the deli drawer's last stand"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion; 2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 dill pickles, diced + 100ml pickle brine, the signature sourness"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp capers + green olives with brine; bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon slices and smetana on every serving, non-negotiable"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render and brown the meats; soften onion in the fat; tomato paste fried dark.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Broth or water in; simmer 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pickles, brine, capers, olives; simmer 10 more, taste: it must be salty-sour-smoky in bold strokes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bowl it with a lemon wheel floating and a white island of smetana. Serve to anyone recovering from a long night.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Solyanka is the great Russian leftovers amnesty: every ending sausage earns a second life. The pickle brine is the backbone; the lemon is the flourish; and its reputation as the definitive morning-after soup is earned, clinical, and cross-generational.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","smoky","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Solyanka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Solyanka"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Tomato","Olives","Capers","Lemon","Dill","Sour cream/Crema","Tomato paste","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"kotleta-po-kyivsky","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 chicken breasts, wing bone left on if you want the classic silhouette"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g butter mashed with garlic, dill, parsley, lemon, rolled into 4 cold logs, then FROZEN"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour, 3 beaten eggs, fine white breadcrumbs: double-breaded, no gaps"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil + butter for deep frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mashed potatoes; a warning for your dinner guests"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butterfly and pound the breasts thin between plastic: no tears; a tear is a leak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap each frozen butter log completely in chicken; tuck like a burrito; chill 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flour, egg, crumbs; then egg and crumbs AGAIN; the double coat is the dam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 170°C, 8–10 min to deep gold, finish 5 min in a 180°C oven. Warn the table: the first cut sprays.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kyiv's cutlet, and Ukraine will tell you so. The frozen butter log and the double breading are the two seals that keep the molten herb-butter core in until the knife releases it. Restaurants once served it with a paper frill on the bone as a handle; the geyser at first cut remains the dish's signature and its hazard.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kotleta%20po-Kyivsky%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kotleta po-Kyivsky"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Garlic","Potato","Lemon","Parsley","Dill","Butter","Bread","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"okroshka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1L cold kvass (bread kvass, unsweetened), or kefir cut with sparkling water, the modern schism"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 boiled potatoes, 4 eggs, diced; 1 long cucumber; a bunch of radishes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g cooked ham, doctor's sausage, or poached chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"A FOREST of dill and green onions, the whites mashed with salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Smetana, mustard, ice cubes for serving"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dice everything fine and even, salad-style.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash the onion whites with salt in the bowl bottom, the aromatic base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine solids; chill thoroughly: the soup is only as cold as its parts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the cold kvass over at the table; a spoon of smetana and a dab of mustard per bowl; an ice cube in the heat of July.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold soup for the dacha summer: okroshka means 'crumbled things', and the kvass-versus-kefir divide splits families annually. The vegetables must be dry and cold before the liquid arrives: okroshka is a salad that decided to swim, not a soup that got confused.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okroshka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Okroshka"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Scallion","Potato","Cucumber","Radish/Turnip","Dill","Sour cream/Crema","Bread","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"pirozhki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 500g flour, 250ml warm milk, 7g yeast, 1 egg, 60g butter, 1 tbsp sugar, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filling one: braised cabbage with egg. Filling two: potato and fried onion. Filling three: seasoned ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg for glazing (baked) or oil for frying, the two schools"},{"kind":"item","text":"Each filling cooked, seasoned boldly, and fully cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"A kitchen and an afternoon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rich yeasted dough; rise 1 h until doubled.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 16; flatten rounds; a generous spoon of COLD filling each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pinch into plump ovals seam-side down; proof 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake glazed at 200°C, 20 min, or fry seam-down in a finger of oil. Cool under a towel; the towel-softened crust is traditional.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The train-station pirozhok, the grandmother pirozhok, the theater-buffet pirozhok, a portable civilization. Hot filling murders the dough from inside, so patience between steps is the recipe. Fried is the street; baked is the home; the towel rest is the secret third ingredient.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Braising","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pirozhki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pirozhki"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"kitchen-design-and-flow","term":"Kitchen Design & Flow"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Cabbage","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["braising","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"medovik","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Layers: 100g honey, 100g butter, 150g sugar, 2 eggs, 1 tsp soda; cooked warm, then 450g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"The soda goes into the warm honey and FOAMS: that caramel foam is the flavor"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cream: 500g smetana + 200g condensed milk, or smetana whipped with sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8–10 paper-thin baked layers + one sacrificed for crumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"24 hours of patience, mandatory"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm honey, butter, sugar; whisk in soda and watch it foam pale and triple; eggs; flour to a soft warm dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide in 9; roll each paper-thin; bake 180°C, 4–5 min per layer to gold biscuit.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Trim hot against a plate; save every scrap and grind to crumbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack with generous smetana cream; coat; press crumbs over everything. Refrigerate 24 h: the layers drink the cream and turn to velvet.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Legend claims a young chef made it for a tsarina who claimed to hate honey; she demanded it daily. The soda foaming in warm honey creates a caramelized depth no raw honey gives. And the overnight wait is not serving advice, it is the recipe: day-one medovik is crackers and cream; day-two medovik is the cake.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Medovik%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Medovik"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"building-flavor-in-layers","term":"Building Flavor in Layers"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Honey","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"uzbek-plov","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g lamb shoulder, large chunks, some fat; 100g lamb tail fat if you can find the real thing"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g medium-grain rice (devzira if the bazaar smiles), soaked in warm salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 yellow carrots (or orange), cut in thick matchsticks BY HAND, never grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions; 2 whole heads of garlic, unpeeled; 2 tbsp cumin seeds, 1 tbsp barberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"A kazan or heavy dutch oven; this dish has a vessel opinion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the fat; brown lamb hard; onions to deep gold; carrots in, folded not stirred, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Water to cover, whole garlic heads and spices in: this is the zirvak: simmer 40 min; it must taste over-seasoned; the rice will drink the excess.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain rice; lay it OVER the zirvak: never stir from this moment; add water two knuckles above the rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"High heat until the water vanishes; pierce holes to the bottom with a spoon handle; lid on tight; lowest flame 25 min. Fluff layers together only at serving.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"Plov is the Uzbek national ceremony: wedding plov is cooked by masters (oshpaz) for a thousand guests from a single kazan. The zirvak is the soul and the no-stir law is absolute: the rice steams above the meat like a congregation above a sermon. Hand-cut carrots survive; grated ones vanish and take the dish's honor with them.","equipment":["Dutch oven","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20Plov%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Uzbek Plov"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"startup-costs-the-real-number","term":"Startup Costs: The Real Number"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Orange","Rice","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"lagman","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Hand-pulled noodles: 500g flour, 250ml water, 1 tsp salt, plus oil for the pulling ritual"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g lamb or beef, small cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 peppers, 2 tomatoes, 1 onion, celery, green beans, garlic, the vegetable crowd (kovurma)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste; 1 tsp ground cumin + coriander; a star anise"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black rice vinegar and chili oil (laza) at the table, the Uyghur signature"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rest the dough 1 h; roll ropes, oil them, coil and rest again; then pull (swing, fold, slap) into long noodles (or roll and cut, and keep your dignity).","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear meat hard in a kazan; onions, then vegetables in waves, keeping crunch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tomato paste, spices, water to a loose sauce; simmer 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil noodles; bowl them; ladle the kovurma over; vinegar and chili oil to taste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lagman traveled the Silk Road with the Uyghur people, the same noodle that became ramen east and spaghetti west, still hand-pulled in Tashkent doorways. One unbroken noodle per portion is the masters' flex; the vinegar-and-chili-oil finish is what makes it lagman and not stew on pasta.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Hand-pulled noodles","Searing: the hard crust","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","umami-deep","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Hand-pulled noodles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=hand%20pulled%20noodles%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lagman%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lagman"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"malbec-zinfandel-and-the-new-world-crowd-pleasers","term":"Malbec, Zinfandel & the New World Crowd-Pleasers"},{"slug":"the-sommeliers-service-ritual-and-floor-fluency","term":"The Sommelier's Service Ritual & Floor Fluency"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Celery","Chiles","Green beans","Noodles","Flour","Vinegar","Tomato paste","Cumin","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"shashlik","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg lamb shoulder or pork neck, in generous 4cm cubes: big is correct"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions: 2 grated to pulp for the marinade, 1 sliced in rings for serving"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, maybe a splash of sparkling water, and NOTHING acidic; no vinegar, no lemon, no kiwi sorcery"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flat metal skewers (shampury); real wood coals, burned to gray"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lavash, fresh tomatoes, sumac onions, and strong opinions"}],"steps":[{"text":"Massage meat with grated onion pulp, salt, pepper; marinate 4 h: the onion enzymes tenderize without pickling.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Burn wood down to breathing gray coals: flames are for amateurs and lost eyebrows.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thread meat snug on flat skewers; grill 12–15 min, turning on a called rhythm, fat hissing.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 3 min; slide off with lavash as your glove; bury in onion rings and herbs.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"From Tbilisi to Tashkent, shashlik is a weekend religion and the marinade wars are eternal, but the old masters agree: onion juice and salt, never acid; vinegar cooks the outside into leather before the coals get a word in. The man tending the skewers may not be interrupted except with compliments.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","charred","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, char used as punctuation, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shashlik%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shashlik"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fermentation-and-pickling","term":"Fermentation & Pickling"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Lamb","Onion","Tomato","Lemon","Vinegar","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"samsa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 400g flour, 200ml warm water, 1 tsp salt, 50g melted butter (or true tandyr-style with layers of fat)"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g lamb, hand-chopped with 150g lamb fat: chopped, never ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, diced fine, nearly equal to the meat; this is the juice engine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cumin, black pepper, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Egg yolk, sesame and nigella seeds for the tops"}],"steps":[{"text":"Firm dough; rest; roll thin, butter, roll into a log, coil, chill: the lazy lamination.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix filling: the mountain of onion is what melts into gravy inside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut coils, flatten showing the spiral, fill, pinch into triangles seam-down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Yolk wash, seeds; bake at 220°C, 25–30 min (a pizza stone imitates the tandyr wall).","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"In Uzbekistan samsa is slapped onto the inner wall of the tandyr oven and pried off with a hook when bronzed. The hand-chopped meat and the absurd quantity of onion are the law: grinding makes meatball filling; chopping makes juicy pockets. Eat with a bite from the corner first: steam management is a learned skill.","equipment":["Pizza steel"],"techniques":["Lamination","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Samsa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Samsa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lamination","term":"Lamination"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Butter","Flour","Sesame","Cumin","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"beshbarmak","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg lamb or beef on the bone (horse meat is the true Kazakh honor, but we work with the butcher we have)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Noodle sheets: 300g flour, 1 egg, 120ml broth or water, salt, rolled thin, cut in wide diamonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 onions, sliced in rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"The broth (sorpa), skimmed clean and guarded"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper, parsley; bowls of sorpa served alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the meat 2–2.5 h to a clean, strong broth; lift out; keep the sorpa barely simmering.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice meat; smother the onion rings in a ladle of hot fatty broth off the heat: they soften into silk (tuzdyk).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the noodle diamonds in the sorpa itself, 3–4 min.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Great platter: noodles, then meat, then the broth-wilted onions; pepper over. Bowls of hot sorpa on the side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beshbarmak means five fingers: it is eaten by hand from a shared platter, and the honored guest is served the meat from the head. The onions never touch a pan: hot broth alone melts them, which is the dish's quiet genius. Nomad food scaled for hospitality: one animal, one platter, everyone fed.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beshbarmak%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beshbarmak"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Parsley","Noodles","Bread","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"khinkali","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 500g flour, 250ml warm water, 1 tsp salt, no egg; supple and strong"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g beef + pork (or lamb in the mountains), hand-chopped or coarse ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion pulped; ½ tsp caraway, chili flakes; big handful cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml warm broth or water kneaded INTO the meat: the future soup"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper for dusting; nothing else; no sauce; Georgia is watching"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the meat with the warm liquid until sloshy: it will drink it all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll thick coins, then thin the edges; a ball of filling; pleat 18–24 folds around it and twist a topknot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil in salted water; when they float belly-up, 3–4 min more.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve dusted with black pepper. Hold by the knot, bite the side, DRINK the broth, then eat: the doughy knots pile on the plate as your scorecard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tbilisi counts your pleats: 19 is respectable, 28 is a marriage proposal. Using a fork is legal grounds for gentle mockery: khinkali are eaten by hand, and spilling the broth is the only real crime. The topknots left on the plate announce your total to the table; eat them and lose the count, keep them and keep the honor.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Khinkali%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Khinkali"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Chiles","Cilantro","Flour","Stock/Broth","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"lobio","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g dark red kidney beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion; 3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g walnuts, ground, Georgia's cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp blue fenugreek (utskho suneli) + 1 tsp coriander + hint of marigold (or khmeli suneli blend)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro storm, pomegranate seeds; mchadi (corn cakes) or crusty bread; pickles alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer beans until fully soft, 1.5 h; drain, keeping a cup of the liquor.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry onion; pound garlic with salt; grind walnuts with the spices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crush half the beans roughly in the pot; leave half whole; fold in onion, walnut paste, garlic, and liquor to a thick rumble.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 20 min off heat; cilantro and pomegranate over. Serve warm or room temperature in clay if you have it.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Georgia cooks with walnuts the way France cooks with cream: lobio is beans elevated to a national dish by that one idea plus blue fenugreek, the spice nowhere else uses. Half-crushed, half-whole is the texture law; and lobio with pickles and corn bread is a supra (feast) table in miniature.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","nutty-toasted","aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lobio%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lobio"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Corn","Pomegranate","Cilantro","Cream","Bread","Kidney/Pinto beans","Nuts","Pickles","Clove"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"pkhali-trio","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g spinach, blanched + 300g beets, roasted + 300g green beans, boiled, three colors, one method"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g walnuts, ground fine, divided among the three"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves; 1 tsp blue fenugreek; 1 tsp coriander; chili"},{"kind":"item","text":"White wine vinegar, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pomegranate seeds pressed into each ball, the ruby signature"}],"steps":[{"text":"Squeeze each vegetable absolutely dry: water is the enemy of pkhali.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind walnuts with garlic, spices, vinegar into a paste (the bazha logic).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mince or grind each vegetable; knead a third of the walnut paste into each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into balls or pat into mounds; dimple; pomegranate seeds in the dimples. Serve room temperature at the head of the feast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pkhali is the overture of every Georgian supra: the same walnut-fenugreek paste transforms three vegetables into three different jewels. The squeeze-dry step is the craft: wet vegetables make sad pâté. Any vegetable that holds still long enough becomes pkhali; that is the recipe's real secret.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","nutty-toasted","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pkhali%20Trio%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pkhali Trio"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Spinach","Green beans","Beets","Pomegranate","Vinegar","Wine","Nuts","Clove"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"adjika","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"10 red chilies (mix hot and mild to steer the fire), seeded or not, your call"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head of garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g walnuts (Abkhaz style), optional but persuasive"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp blue fenugreek + 2 tsp ground coriander + 1 tsp dried dill; 1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp white wine vinegar; NO tomatoes: the red is chili, not sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Traditional: pound chilies, garlic and salt on a flat stone until oily and furious. Modern: pulse in a processor, coarse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the walnuts and spices to a dense, spreadable paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Vinegar to loosen; rest a day for the heat to settle into flavor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keeps a month refrigerated. Spread on meat, cheese, bread, eggs. Georgia puts it near everything.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Real Abkhaz adjika contains no tomato; that is a northern rumor. It began as salt stretched with chilies by shepherds paid in salt; the name means simply 'salt' in Abkhaz. A jar in the fridge upgrades a month of meals: it is less a condiment than a point of view.","equipment":["Food processor"],"techniques":["The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","nutty-toasted","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Adjika%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Adjika"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"head-cheese-and-the-aspic-arts","term":"Head Cheese & the Aspic Arts"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Dill","Vinegar","Wine","Nuts","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"chak-chak","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 3 eggs, 300g flour, pinch of salt, 1 tbsp vodka or milk (the crispness whisper)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Syrup: 250g honey + 80g sugar + 2 tbsp water"},{"kind":"item","text":"A large plate, buttered hands, and witnesses"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: crushed nuts or dried fruit worked in"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stiff egg dough; rest 30 min; roll thin, cut matchstick strips or pea-sized nuggets.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in batches at 170°C seconds only: pale gold, puffed, crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil honey and sugar 3–4 min to a thread stage.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour over the fried pieces; toss; with buttered hands shape a mountain (or ring) while warm. Set 2 h; break off shards to serve with tea.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chak-chak is the wedding crown of Tatar and Bashkir tables: brides were once judged on the fineness of their strands. The honey must reach thread stage or the mountain slumps; beyond it, the mountain becomes geology. Served with tea, broken by hand, and defended fiercely as Kazan's true souvenir.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chak-Chak%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chak-Chak"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"vodka-honestly","term":"Vodka, Honestly"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Honey","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"country-sourdough-loaf","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour (450g white + 50g whole wheat for flavor)"},{"kind":"item","text":"375ml water (75% hydration, honest but manageable)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g active sourdough starter, bubbly and doubled after feeding"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"A dutch oven, the home baker's steam engine"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix flour and water; rest 1 h (autolyse). Add starter and salt; squeeze through.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bulk ferment 4–6 h at room temperature, folding the dough over itself every 45 min: four folds replace all kneading.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape a tight round; seam-up in a floured, cloth-lined bowl; refrigerate overnight: the cold builds the sour and the schedule builds your sleep.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Score decisively; bake in a preheated covered dutch oven at 240°C, 20 min lidded, 20–25 uncovered to deep mahogany. Wait an hour before cutting: the crumb is still cooking.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The overnight cold proof is the great equalizer: flavor deepens, timing bends to your life, and cold dough scores like a dream. And the cruel truth: the crackling you hear as it cools is the bread finishing itself; cut hot and you gum the crumb you waited two days for.","equipment":["Dutch oven"],"techniques":["Sourdough starter","Fermentation","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sourdough starter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"dish":{"label":"Weissman’s starter guide","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Country%20Sourdough%20Loaf%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Country Sourdough Loaf"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on Country Sourdough Loaf","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20Country%20Sourdough%20Loaf","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"dehydrator-ferment-station-and-the-pantry-machines","term":"Dehydrator, Ferment Station & the Pantry Machines"},{"slug":"why-restaurants-fail-the-honest-list","term":"Why Restaurants Fail: The Honest List"}],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour"],"standard":{"slug":"country-sourdough-loaf","marks":[{"id":"country-sourdough-loaf#score","text":"The score has opened and lifted a raised ear, and the loaf has burst nowhere else: no blowout at the side, no split creeping around the base. It went up where it was told, not out."},{"id":"country-sourdough-loaf#crust","text":"The crust is deep mahogany over the whole dome and down the sides with no pale, soft band around the foot; the opened cut face may read lighter but must be dry and set, never doughy. Cooled, the crust is rigid under a press and the base sounds hollow under a knock."},{"id":"country-sourdough-loaf#board","text":"On the board the loaf stands at least half as tall as it is wide, shoulders rounded, not spread to a disc."},{"id":"country-sourdough-loaf#crumb","text":"The crumb is irregular with glossy hole walls from top to bottom and no dense stripe along the base; a finger press springs back and leaves no dent or tack."},{"id":"country-sourdough-loaf#tastes","text":"It tastes of wheat first with a clean, faint sourness behind it and the salt clearly present. A sharp vinegar bite, or no acidity at all under a bland crumb, means the retard was mistimed rather than the bake."}],"fault":"Bulk runs long in a warm kitchen and the dough is already spent by shaping: it will not hold tension, flows out flat when turned from the banneton, the score stays shut instead of lifting, and the loaf bakes wide and pale-footed with a fine, even crumb and a tacky base."}},{"slug":"baguette-de-tradition","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g French T65 or all-purpose flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"350ml cool water; 3g yeast, almost nothing; time is the yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"A poolish head start: 150g of the flour + 150ml water + pinch of yeast, fermented overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"A baking steel or upturned tray + a pan for steam"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the overnight poolish with everything else; knead briefly; bulk 2 h with two folds.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide in 3; rest 20 min; shape logs, then roll to length with open palms, tapering the tips (the pointage is a signature).","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Proof seam-up in a floured couche or towel folds, 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2700,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Flip, score 3–5 overlapping slashes at a shallow ANGLE; bake at 250°C with a cup of water thrown in the steam pan, 22–25 min to deep gold.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"French law defines a tradition baguette: flour, water, salt, yeast, nothing else, so technique is the entire recipe. The blade must be nearly flat to the loaf; vertical cuts open like wounds, angled cuts bloom into ears. Steam in the first 10 minutes is what turns crust into glass.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Pizza steel"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baguette%20de%20Tradition%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baguette de Tradition"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on Baguette de Tradition","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20Baguette%20de%20Tradition","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour"],"standard":{"slug":"baguette-de-tradition","marks":[{"id":"baguette-de-tradition#three","text":"Three to five slashes have each raised a distinct ear, overlapping by about a third, so they read as one continuous seam down the loaf rather than a ladder of separate rungs."},{"id":"baguette-de-tradition#crust","text":"The crust is thin and russet-gold with a faint sheen rather than a matte, floury dullness; the loaf crackles audibly on the rack as it cools, and squeezed it cracks and sheds shards onto the board."},{"id":"baguette-de-tradition#body","text":"The body is straight and even in diameter with tapered points, and the underside seam is closed, no blowout on the flank or the base."},{"id":"baguette-de-tradition#loaf","text":"Each loaf weighs roughly 230–250g baked and feels light for its size; a heavy loaf for its length means it went in underproofed or came out short of colour."},{"id":"baguette-de-tradition#crumb","text":"The crumb is cream rather than white, irregular, with glossy hole walls. A cottony, chalk-white crumb means the poolish or the bulk was cut short."}],"fault":"The log is rolled out to length before any tension has been built into the shape: the seam never seals and the skin stays slack, so the loaf spreads on the couche, and in the oven it blows open along the bottom or one flank instead of at the scores."}},{"slug":"japanese-shokupan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Tangzhong: 20g of the flour + 100ml milk, cooked to a pudding paste, the softness engine"},{"kind":"item","text":"320g bread flour (total, including tangzhong's share); 7g yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml warm milk; 30g sugar; 6g salt; 1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g soft butter, kneaded in last"},{"kind":"item","text":"A lidded pullman tin for perfect squares, or an open loaf tin for the domed cloud"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the tangzhong to thick paste; cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead everything except butter until smooth; butter in; knead to a full windowpane: this dough should stretch to translucent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rise 1 h; divide in 3; roll each flat, fold in thirds, roll up into a spiral; nest the spirals side by side in the tin.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Proof to 80% of the tin; bake 180°C, 30–35 min. The pull-apart seams and the cotton crumb are the reward.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tangzhong, pre-cooking a little flour in liquid, pre-gelatinizes starch so the bread holds more moisture and stays soft for days without a single additive. It is the open secret of every Asian bakery, and the triple-spiral shaping is why shokupan tears in clouds instead of slices.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Letter-folding dough","Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Letter-folding dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=letter%20fold%20dough%20lamination%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Japanese%20Shokupan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Japanese Shokupan"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on Japanese Shokupan","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20Japanese%20Shokupan","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tangzhong-and-scalds","term":"Tangzhong & Scalds"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"flour-tortillas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g all-purpose flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g lard (the flavor of the north of Mexico) or butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt; ½ tsp baking powder (optional, Sonoran-style puff)"},{"kind":"item","text":"~160ml very warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"A rolling pin and a dry, hot comal or skillet"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub lard into flour and salt; add warm water; knead 3 min to a soft, earlobe dough.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 12 balls; REST 30 min covered: un-rested dough snaps back and mocks you.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll paper-thin from the center out, rotating; the goal is translucence.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a dry hot pan: 30 seconds, flip when bubbles rise, 30 more, pressing gently so it balloons. Stack in a towel: the steam finishes them soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The rest is the recipe: gluten that has relaxed rolls thin and stays tender; gluten that hasn't fights back like elastic. Warm water and lard make the difference between a wrap and a Sonoran flour tortilla, and the towel-steamed stack is why the first one is good but the fifth one is perfect.","equipment":["Skillet","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Flour%20Tortillas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Flour Tortillas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on Flour Tortillas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20Flour%20Tortillas","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"comal-plancha-and-griddles","term":"Comal, Plancha & Griddles"}],"pantryItems":["Butter","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"pita","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour (or half whole wheat, the Levant standard)"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml warm water; 7g yeast; 1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp olive oil; 10g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"A baking steel, stone, or upturned cast-iron pan: ferocious bottom heat is the pocket-maker"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nothing to fill them with yet; patience"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead a smooth, supple dough; rise 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide in 8; ball tightly; rest 20 min (the rest, again, is the secret).","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll even 5mm rounds: thin spots tear, thick spots refuse to pocket; even is everything.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake on the screaming-hot steel at 260°C: 2–3 min, they balloon like startled pufferfish. Wrap in a towel to fall soft.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pocket is physics: even thickness + violent bottom heat = steam trapped between two skins that inflates the bread in seconds. An oven that isn't hot enough gives you flatbread and an explanation; 260°C on steel gives you the pocket, every time.","equipment":["Skillet","Pizza steel"],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pita%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pita"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on Pita","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20Pita","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"bagels","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g high-gluten or bread flour; 8g yeast; 10g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"260ml water, a LOW hydration; bagel dough should feel like a workout"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp barley malt syrup (or honey) in the dough + 2 tbsp in the boiling pot"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toppings: sesame, poppy, everything mix, coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"A pot of water and a hot oven in sequence: the boil is the birthright"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the stiff dough well: it will resist; win. Rise 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide in 8; roll ropes and join into rings (or poke-and-stretch); proof 20 min, then refrigerate overnight uncovered: the cold ferment builds the shine and the tang.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil straight from the fridge: 45 seconds a side in malty water, longer boil, chewier crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top generously; bake at 230°C, 18–20 min to burnished bronze.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"The boil sets the crust before the oven can spring the crumb: that is the chew, the shine, and the entire difference between a bagel and a roll with a hole. New York credits its water; bakers credit the overnight cold proof and the malt. Both are half right.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Boiling bagels","Fermentation","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bagels%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bagels"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on Bagels","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20Bagels","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"gluten-and-hydration","term":"Gluten & Hydration"}],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour","Couscous/Bulgur","Sesame","Honey"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"challah","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour; 7g yeast; 80g sugar or honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs + 2 yolks (whites reserved for the wash); 180ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"80ml oil; 10g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sesame or poppy seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"A six-strand ambition or a three-strand peace"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rich, smooth dough; knead well; rise 1.5 h.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into strands; roll long ropes, fatter in the middle, tapered at the ends.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Braid: three strands for confidence, six for glory (over two, under one, repeat); tuck the ends under.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Proof 45 min; egg wash TWICE (once now, once in 20 min, the double coat is the lacquer); seeds; bake 180°C, 30–35 min to deep amber.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The double egg wash is the bakery secret behind that mirror shine. Braided challah crowns the Shabbat table, and stale challah has the best second act in bread history: it was born to become French toast; some bakers quietly make an extra loaf just to let it age for Sunday.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Challah%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Challah"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on Challah","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20Challah","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Bread","Flour","Sesame","Honey","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"ciabatta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml water: 80% hydration; yes, it will feel like wallpaper paste; hold your nerve"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g yeast; 10g salt; 1 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"A biga head start: 150g flour + 100ml water + pinch yeast, overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"A bench scraper, wet hands, and acceptance"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix biga with everything; no kneading: instead, four sets of stretch-and-folds in the bowl over 2 h with wet hands.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour (yes, pour) onto a heavily floured surface; do not degas; cut rough slippers with the scraper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Proof 45 min on floured cloth, seam-up; flip onto a hot steel with conviction.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 240°C with steam, 20–22 min. The open, glossy, irregular crumb is the entire point of your suffering.","durationSec":1320,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1320,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ciabatta was invented in 1982, a young bread with an old soul, engineered by Italian bakers to fight the baguette invasion. The wet dough IS the recipe: every hole in the crumb is a bubble you didn't press out. Handle it like something sleeping that you'd rather not wake.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ciabatta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ciabatta"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on Ciabatta","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20Ciabatta","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"paratha","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g atta (chapati flour) or half whole wheat, half all-purpose"},{"kind":"item","text":"~220ml warm water; 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ghee: for the dough, the layers, the pan, and philosophically"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional stuffing for another day: spiced potato (aloo), grated paneer"},{"kind":"item","text":"A tawa or heavy flat pan"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead a soft dough with a spoon of ghee; rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll a round; brush with ghee; dust with flour (the release agent between layers); roll into a rope, coil into a snail, rest 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the coil flat again gently: the layers are inside now.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on the tawa: dry side one, flip, ghee the top, flip and press the edges with a cloth, rotating, until blistered gold. CRUSH lightly between palms to bloom the flakes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The coil-and-crush is North India's lamination: ghee and a dusting of flour keep the layers separate, and the final clap between palms (same instinct as roti canai's) shatters them open. A paratha that doesn't flake was rolled too hard: the layers are pressed to death, not born.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Paratha%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Paratha"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on Paratha","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20Paratha","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"paneer","term":"Paneer"}],"pantryItems":["Potato","Cheese (any)","Flour"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"english-muffins","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450g bread flour; 7g yeast; 1 tbsp sugar; 8g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"280ml warm milk; 1 egg; 30g soft butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Semolina or cornmeal for dusting, the signature gritty jacket"},{"kind":"item","text":"A griddle or heavy skillet; the oven is optional and slightly shameful"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter and jam standing by for the fork-split moment"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soft, slightly sticky dough; knead well; rise 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 2cm thick; cut rounds; dust both faces generously with semolina; proof 30–40 min until puffy.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle on LOW, 6–7 min a side: they bake through on the stovetop, patience over heat; they should sound hollow.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":420,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool; then split with a FORK around the equator, never a knife. Toast; watch the craters hold the butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The fork split is the whole religion: a knife slices the crumb flat, a fork tears it open into the nooks and crannies that hold butter like tidepools. Griddled bread is older than ovens: the English muffin is a survivor from that era wearing a semolina coat.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work","Proofing: the rise before the oven","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=English%20Muffins%20recipe","sub":"Search films of English Muffins"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: King Arthur Baking on English Muffins","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Arthur%20Baking%20English%20Muffins","sub":"A YouTube search for King Arthur Baking and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Bread","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Semolina","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"sauce-bechamel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"50g butter + 50g flour: equal weights, the white roux"},{"kind":"item","text":"600ml whole milk, warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf, ½ onion studded with 2 cloves (the onion piqué, old school, still correct)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nutmeg, a whisper; salt, white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"This is Mother #1: lasagne, moussaka, croque monsieur, gratins all call her"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt butter; flour in; cook the roux 2 min, foaming, NOT coloring: white sauce, white roux.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Warm milk in thirds, whisking each addition smooth before the next: lumps are just dry pockets ambushed by liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Onion piqué and bay in; simmer gently 8–10 min, stirring the corners: flour needs time to lose its rawness.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with nutmeg, salt, white pepper; strain. Add gruyère and it becomes Mornay; press film to the surface if holding.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The whole roux family obeys one law: cold-into-hot or hot-into-cold, never hot-into-hot: warm milk into warm roux, whisked in stages, cannot lump. And the 10-minute simmer is not optional: béchamel that tastes of flour was rushed, not ruined.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Béchamel: the mother sauce","Making a roux"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Béchamel: the mother sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bechamel%20mother%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauce%20B%C3%A9chamel%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauce Béchamel"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Sauce Béchamel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Sauce%20B%C3%A9chamel","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"syrah-grenache-and-the-rhone-school","term":"Syrah, Grenache & the Rhône School"},{"slug":"gruyere","term":"Gruyère"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Bay leaf","Butter","Milk","Flour","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux"]},{"slug":"sauce-veloute","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"50g butter + 50g flour, blond roux this time"},{"kind":"item","text":"700ml excellent chicken, fish, or veal stock, warm: the stock IS the sauce; this mother has no makeup"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mushroom trimmings and a parsley stem if they are near"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper, squeeze of lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mother #2, and the gateway to suprême, allemande, bercy, and every 'pan sauce' instinct"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the roux 3–4 min to pale straw, blond, nutty, still light.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Warm stock in stages, whisking; simmer 15–20 min, skimming the skin that rises (dépouillage, the old discipline).","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"It should coat a spoon and hold a drawn line (nappe).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season late: the stock reduces and concentrates. Finish with lemon; add cream and it becomes sauce suprême.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Velouté means velvety, and it is a mirror: made with thin stock it is library paste; made with gelatinous, golden stock it needs almost nothing else. Learn it and you learn every gravy, every chowder base, every 'add flour then broth' move in the Western songbook.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Making a roux","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","silky","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauce%20Velout%C3%A9%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauce Velouté"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Sauce Velouté","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Sauce%20Velout%C3%A9","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mother-sauce-fish-stock-fumet","term":"Mother Sauce Fish Stock: Fumet"},{"slug":"reduction-nappe-and-mounting","term":"Reduction, Nappe & Mounting"},{"slug":"the-nutty-register","term":"The Nutty Register"},{"slug":"mushroom-field-guide","term":"Mushroom Field Guide"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Mushrooms","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"sauce-espagnole-and-demi-glace","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60g butter + 60g flour, cooked to a BROWN roux: patience, it smells of hazelnuts when ready"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L rich brown veal or beef stock (roasted bones: the color and body live there)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mirepoix: 2 carrots, 1 onion, 2 celery ribs, browned hard; 3 tbsp tomato paste, caramelized"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bay, thyme, parsley stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mother #3, and demi-glace, its famous child, is just espagnole + stock, halved by fire"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the mirepoix in butter; add paste and darken it; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make the brown roux separately, stirring 8–10 min to café-au-lait; whisk in warm stock.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Unite with mirepoix and herbs; simmer 1.5–2 h, skimming relentlessly; strain through fine mesh.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"For demi-glace: equal parts espagnole and fresh brown stock, reduced by half again, until it coats a spoon like dark satin and sets to jelly when cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is the cathedral build of classical cuisine: hours of skimming for a sauce used by the spoonful. Modern kitchens often skip the flour and just reduce great stock to glace; both roads end at the same truth: brown sauce is concentrated time. A tray of demi-glace cubes in the freezer is a superpower.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","umami-deep","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauce%20Espagnole%20%26%20Demi-Glace%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauce Espagnole & Demi-Glace"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Sauce Espagnole & Demi-Glace","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Sauce%20Espagnole%20%26%20Demi-Glace","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Parsley","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Flour","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Nuts"],"standard":{"slug":"sauce-espagnole-and-demi-glace","marks":[{"id":"sauce-espagnole-and-demi-glace#roux","text":"The roux before the stock goes in: café-au-lait to hazelnut brown, smelling of toasted nuts, with no black specks and no acrid catch at the back of the nose. Burnt roux cannot be skimmed, strained or reduced out afterwards, so it is stopped here."},{"id":"sauce-espagnole-and-demi-glace#ladle","text":"A ladle drawn across the surface comes up dry: no fat eyes, no grey scum, and no ring of it on the pot wall stirred back in."},{"id":"sauce-espagnole-and-demi-glace#passed","text":"Passed through fine mesh and smooth on the lip: no specks of mirepoix, no herb, no grain from the roux."},{"id":"sauce-espagnole-and-demi-glace#coats","text":"It coats the back of a spoon and holds a finger-drawn line without closing, and the film on the spoon reads deep translucent brown against the light rather than grey or muddy. Chilled demi-glace sets firm enough to cut into cubes that keep their edges."},{"id":"sauce-espagnole-and-demi-glace#tastes","text":"It tastes of roasted bone with no chalk of raw flour anywhere in it, and it is deliberately short of salt: seasoned to the palate now, it becomes brine when the demi is reduced by half."}],"fault":"Skimming was let go in the first half hour, so fat and albumin were reduced back into the sauce: it comes out cloudy, greasy on the lip, and tasting of scorched flour instead of roasted bone."}},{"slug":"sauce-tomate-classical","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100g salt pork or pancetta, diced (Escoffier's opening move, omit for meatless, add 2 tbsp olive oil)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion + 1 carrot, diced; 3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg ripe tomatoes, chopped, or 2 cans best-quality whole peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp flour (the classical thickener; heresy in Naples, law in Paris); bay, thyme, pinch of sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mother #4: the only mother with two passports, French and Italian"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the pork; sweat onion and carrot in the fat; garlic briefly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust with flour; tomatoes in with herbs and sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Classical road: cover and BAKE at 150°C, 1 h: oven heat cooks it evenly with no scorching, Escoffier's own trick.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pass through a food mill (not a blender: the mill leaves body, the blades make foam); season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Escoffier baked his tomato sauce, and the trick still wins: no stirring, no burnt pot corners, deeper flavor. The food mill matters: milled sauce is silky with substance; blended sauce is aerated and orange. Italy's version drops the flour and pork and simmers instead; keep both in your pocket and deploy by occasion.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","herb-fresh","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauce%20Tomate%20(Classical)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauce Tomate (Classical)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Sauce Tomate (Classical)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Sauce%20Tomate%20(Classical)","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"meringues-french-swiss-italian","term":"Meringues: French, Swiss, Italian"},{"slug":"sieves-chinois-tamis-and-food-mill","term":"Sieves: Chinois, Tamis & Food Mill"},{"slug":"ff-and-e-smallwares-and-opening-inventory","term":"FF&E, Smallwares & Opening Inventory"},{"slug":"boning-fillet-and-the-butchery-blades","term":"Boning, Fillet & the Butchery Blades"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Thyme","Bay leaf","Flour","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"sauce-hollandaise","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 egg yolks; 1 tbsp water; 1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g butter, melted warm (clarified for the classical stiff sauce; whole for a looser, tastier one)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cayenne pinch; salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"A bowl over barely-simmering water, the bain-marie tightrope"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mother #5: eggs Benedict, asparagus, and the entire béarnaise family await"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk yolks with water over the bain-marie until pale, doubled and ribboned, the sabayon stage; the bowl should never be too hot to touch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off and on the heat as needed, drizzle in the warm butter thread by thread, whisking always: an emulsion is built drop by drop and lost all at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lemon, salt, cayenne; it should hold soft peaks and pour reluctantly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold warm (not hot) up to an hour. If it breaks: new yolk in a clean bowl, whisk the broken sauce into IT, resurrection in ninety seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hollandaise fails at two temperatures: too hot (scrambled) and too cold (split). Between them lies a wide, forgiving valley, wider than its reputation admits. The broken-sauce rescue (fresh yolk, rebuild) works every time and separates the calm from the panicked; add tarragon reduction instead of lemon and you have made béarnaise, the steak's soulmate.","equipment":["Bain-marie"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Hollandaise","Building an emulsion","The water bath"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Hollandaise","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=hollandaise%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauce%20Hollandaise%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauce Hollandaise"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Sauce Hollandaise","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Sauce%20Hollandaise","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Chiles","Asparagus","Lemon","Butter"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath","whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"beurre-blanc","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 shallots, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml dry white wine + 50ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g COLD butter, cubed: the cold is structural, not preference"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional insurance: 1 tbsp cream (the classicists sniff; the sauce holds)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper; fish, scallops, or asparagus waiting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Reduce shallots, wine and vinegar to 2 tablespoons of syrupy essence, the flavor base and the acid scaffold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lowest heat: whisk in cold butter cube by cube, each melting creamily into the last: never fully melting, always emulsifying.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The sauce should thicken to double-cream body, ivory and glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season; strain if you want silk, don't if you like the shallot. Hold warm, never hot: it lives between 50–60°C and dies outside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beurre blanc is butter convinced to be a sauce: the acid reduction holds the emulsion the way lemon holds mayonnaise. Legend says it was a failed béarnaise (a Loire cook forgot the egg yolks) served anyway to acclaim: the best argument in history for plating your mistakes with confidence.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Beurre blanc","Building an emulsion"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","aromatic","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, melting away silken."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Beurre blanc","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=beurre%20blanc%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beurre%20Blanc%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beurre Blanc"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Beurre Blanc","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Beurre%20Blanc","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"scallops-dry-vs-wet-u-10","term":"Scallops: Dry vs. Wet, U-10"}],"pantryItems":["Scallops","Shallot","Asparagus","Butter","Cream","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion"]},{"slug":"salsa-verde-italian","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 huge bunch flat parsley, leaves only"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 anchovy fillets; 1 tbsp capers, rinsed; 1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 slice stale bread soaked in 2 tbsp red wine vinegar (the old thickener)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml olive oil; black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Boiled meats, grilled fish, roast vegetables, or a spoon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chop parsley, anchovy, capers, garlic together on one board until fine and mingled: knife, not machine, for texture with a spine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze the vinegar-soaked bread; chop it through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the oil; season with pepper (the anchovy and capers carry the salt).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 30 min. It should be spoonable, rough, and insistently green.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is the sauce Italy serves with bollito misto and the sauce that fixes anything plain: the vinegar-soaked bread is the medieval emulsifier that predates the blender and beats it. Machine-blitzed salsa verde turns to army-green paste; the knife keeps every element speaking its own line.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","umami-deep","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salsa%20Verde%20(Italian)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salsa Verde (Italian)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Salsa Verde (Italian)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Salsa%20Verde%20(Italian)","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"knife-care-and-the-cutting-board-question","term":"Knife Care & the Cutting Board Question"}],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Garlic","Capers","Parsley","Bread","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"romesco","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 dried ñora peppers, soaked and flesh scraped (or 2 tbsp good sweet paprika, the honest shortcut)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes + 1 head of garlic, roasted until slumped"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g toasted almonds + 40g hazelnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 slice bread fried in olive oil; 2 tbsp sherry vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml olive oil; salt; grilled anything, famously calçots, to receive it"}],"steps":[{"text":"Roast tomatoes and garlic at 200°C until collapsed and sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the nuts; fry the bread crouton-crisp in oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound or pulse: nuts and bread first (the body), then peppers, tomato flesh, squeezed garlic, vinegar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stream in oil to a thick, rusty, spoonable sauce, coarse enough to remember the nuts. Rest an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tarragona built romesco for fishermen and perfected it for calçot season, when Catalonia eats charred onions by the meter, dredged through bowls of this. Nuts and fried bread are the thickeners, no cream anywhere in sight, and the sauce keeps a week, improving daily, ready to rescue any grilled thing in the house.","equipment":["Thermometer"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","charred","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, char used as punctuation, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romesco%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Romesco"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Romesco","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Romesco","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-sherry-spectrum","term":"The Sherry Spectrum"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Bread","Vinegar","Wine","Nuts","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"chimichurri-rojo-and-aji-verde","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Rojo: the green chimichurri's kit (parsley, garlic, oregano, vinegar, oil) PLUS 2 tbsp sweet paprika + 1 tsp smoked + chili flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ají verde: 3 jalapeños, 100g cotija or parmesan, 100ml mayo, huacatay if the universe provides, cilantro storm, garlic, lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, patience of at least one hour for the rojo"},{"kind":"item","text":"A blender for the ají (this one WANTS the machine), a knife for the rojo"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grilled meats, roast chicken, fries, eggs: they all apply"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rojo: hand-chop as for green chimichurri; bloom the paprikas in 2 tbsp warmed oil first (30 seconds, off heat) and fold through with the rest of the oil and vinegar; rest 1 h.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ají verde: blitz jalapeños, cheese, mayo, garlic, cilantro, lime to a smooth pale-green cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt both; loosen the ají with water if needed to pouring-cream body.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the rojo on beef and pork; the ají on rotisserie chicken, fries, and everything else: Lima's green sauce takes prisoners.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two South American finishing sauces, two philosophies: the rojo is rustic and knife-cut with bloomed paprika doing the smoky work; Peru's ají verde is a creamy ambush: mild at first taste, insistent by the third. Peruvian chicken shops guard their exact ratios like state secrets, and now you hold a working key.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chimichurri%20Rojo%20%26%20Aj%C3%AD%20Verde%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chimichurri Rojo & Ají Verde"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Chimichurri Rojo & Ají Verde","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Chimichurri%20Rojo%20%26%20Aj%C3%AD%20Verde","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cotija","term":"Cotija"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Cilantro","Parsley","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Vinegar","Mayonnaise","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat"]},{"slug":"peanut-satay-sauce-master","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g roasted peanuts, ground (or 180g natural peanut butter, no sugar added)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paste: 3 red chilies, 3 shallots, 2 garlic, 1 lemongrass stalk, 1 tsp galangal or ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp palm sugar; 1 tbsp tamarind water; 1 tbsp kecap manis; 200ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime leaf if near; salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Satay, gado-gado, noodles, grilled chicken, raw vegetables, your spoon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound or blitz the paste; fry in 2 tbsp oil until fragrant and split, 4–5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peanuts, coconut milk, palm sugar, tamarind, kecap manis in; simmer, stirring, 8–10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"It will thicken and the oil will bead at the surface, the doneness signal across all of Southeast Asia.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Adjust the triangle: sweet (palm sugar), sour (tamarind), heat (chili). Loosen with water for a dressing; keep thick for a dip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Learn this once and you own a dozen dishes: the sauce moves from Indonesian satay to gado-gado to Malaysian rojak with tiny adjustments. The oil-bead is the universal Southeast Asian checkpoint (pecah minyak again): before it, the paste is raw; after it, the sauce is alive and keeps a week.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","sweet-edged","aromatic","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peanut%20Satay%20Sauce%20(Master)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Peanut Satay Sauce (Master)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: French Cooking Academy on Peanut Satay Sauce (Master)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=French%20Cooking%20Academy%20Peanut%20Satay%20Sauce%20(Master)","sub":"A YouTube search for French Cooking Academy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"congruent-vs-contrast-the-master-key","term":"Congruent vs. Contrast (The Master Key)"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Lemongrass","Butter","Milk","Coconut milk","Noodles","Soy sauce","Peanut","Sugar","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"tarte-tatin","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 firm apples (Braeburn, Cox, or Reine des Reinettes if France loves you): peeled, halved, cored"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar; 100g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 disc all-butter puff pastry or shortcrust, rolled slightly larger than your pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 24cm ovenproof skillet: the tarte is built and born in one vessel"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crème fraîche, cold, for the hot-cold collision"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the apple halves uncovered in the fridge overnight if you can: drier apples, deeper caramel, no flood.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt sugar in the skillet to deep amber; whisk in butter; off heat, pack the apples in TIGHT standing circles, rounded side down: they shrink; overcrowd them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 15 min on the stove so the apples drink caramel from below; drape the pastry over, tucking the edges down inside like a duvet.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 200°C, 25–30 min. Rest FIVE minutes only; place a plate over, breathe once, and flip with conviction. Rearrange any deserters.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born at the Hôtel Tatin when one of the sisters (legend argues which) botched an apple pie and baked it upside down anyway. The five-minute rest is the window: sooner and the caramel is lava, later and it welds to the pan. Flip like you mean it: hesitation, not heat, is what ruins a Tatin.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tarte%20Tatin%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tarte Tatin"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Tarte Tatin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Tarte%20Tatin","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Apple","Butter","Sour cream/Crema","Bread","Phyllo/Puff pastry","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"basque-burnt-cheesecake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900g cream cheese, fully room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g sugar; 6 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"450ml heavy cream; 1 tsp vanilla; ½ tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g flour, sifted: the only structure this cake believes in"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 23cm springform lined with two rough sheets of parchment, pleats, wrinkles and all"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat cheese and sugar smooth; eggs one by one; cream, vanilla, salt; flour last. The batter is pourable; trust it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the rough-lined pan; the crumpled parchment IS the aesthetic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 230°C, 45–50 min: the top must go genuinely DARK, mahogany to near-black at the edges, while the center still sways like a water bed.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely in the pan; it collapses into its famous crater. Serve at room temperature, in slumping slices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"La Viña in San Sebastián bakes these by the dozen and started a world movement with what classical bakers would call three mistakes: too hot, too dark, underbaked. The burn is bitter caramel doing the work of a crust, and the center should still tremble when it leaves the oven; it sets as it cools into custard-silk. The ugliness is the recipe.","equipment":["Springform","Parchment"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20Burnt%20Cheesecake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Basque Burnt Cheesecake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Basque Burnt Cheesecake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Basque%20Burnt%20Cheesecake","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"creaming-foaming-and-cake-structure","term":"Creaming, Foaming & Cake Structure"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Cream","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"cannoli-siciliani","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Shells: 250g flour, 30g sugar, 30g lard or butter, 1 egg, 60ml Marsala, 1 tbsp vinegar, pinch cocoa and cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g sheep's-milk ricotta: drained OVERNIGHT in a sieve; this is the entire ballgame"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g icing sugar; candied orange peel, chopped; dark chocolate chips"},{"kind":"item","text":"Metal cannoli tubes; oil or lard for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pistachios, crushed, for the ends; icing sugar snowfall"}],"steps":[{"text":"Firm dough with the Marsala and vinegar (the blistering agents); rest 1 h; roll paper-thin.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut ovals; wrap around tubes, sealing with egg white; fry at 180°C until blistered and bronzed, 90 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool; slide off tubes gently. Shells keep a week in a tin: this is a make-ahead in disguise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the drained ricotta with sugar until silken; fold in peel and chocolate; pipe into shells AT THE MOMENT of serving. Dip ends in pistachio; dust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Sicilian commandment: fill at the last second, for a shell filled early is soggy by the time it crosses the room. Wet ricotta is the other assassin: overnight draining turns grainy dampness into cream. The blisters on the shell are the mark of the vinegar and Marsala; smooth shells mark a recipe that lost its nerve.","equipment":["Thermometer","Piping bag"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","nutty-toasted","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cannoli%20Siciliani%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cannoli Siciliani"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Cannoli Siciliani","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Cannoli%20Siciliani","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ricotta","term":"Ricotta"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Orange","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Ricotta","Flour","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"pavlova","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg whites, room temperature, not a speck of yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"220g caster sugar, added a spoonful at a time; patience is the leavening"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cornstarch + 1 tsp white vinegar + ½ tsp vanilla, the marshmallow-middle trinity"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml cream, softly whipped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Passionfruit (the antipodean birthright), kiwi, berries: something SHARP; the meringue brings all the sugar needed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whip whites to soft peaks; sugar in gradually until stiff, glossy, and grit-free when rubbed between fingers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in cornstarch, vinegar, vanilla; mound onto parchment in a crown with slightly raised walls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 120°C, 75–90 min; then oven OFF, door cracked, and leave it to cool completely inside, an hour minimum; sudden cold is what cracks it (though cracks are honest).","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crown with cream and tart fruit only at serving; the shell is a one-act structure.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Australia and New Zealand have fought over the pavlova for a century (the dessert is named for a Russian ballerina and claimed by two other nations, very antipodean). The cornstarch-vinegar duo is what makes it a pavlova and not a meringue: crisp shell, marshmallow heart. The fruit must bite: passionfruit's acidity is structural, not decorative.","equipment":["Parchment"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pavlova%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pavlova"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Pavlova","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Pavlova","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"speck-alto-adige","term":"Speck Alto Adige"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Berries","Cream","Vinegar","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"gulab-jamun","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g milk powder + 40g flour + ¼ tsp baking soda, the modern khoya stand-in"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ghee; ~70ml milk, added teaspoon by teaspoon to a soft, JUST-combined dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"Syrup: 300g sugar, 300ml water, 4 cardamom pods crushed, few saffron threads, 1 tsp rose water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ghee or oil for frying at a strictly moderate temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pistachios slivered over; served warm, always warm"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the syrup first; keep it warm, not hot: one-string-consistency is not needed; it should stay thin enough to soak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Barely knead the dough (kneading = rubber); rest 10 min; roll into small, CRACK-FREE balls with greased palms: cracks become canyons in the fryer.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 140–150°C, low, rolling constantly, 7–8 min to deep even brown; high heat browns the outside and leaves a raw heart.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Straight from oil into warm syrup; soak MINIMUM 2 hours. They double in size and turn to velvet.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The three commandments carved above every Indian sweet shop: dough barely mixed, oil kept low, syrup merely warm. Violate any one and the jamun tells on you: dense center, dark shell, or wrinkled skin. Done right they are darker than you expect, softer than seems possible, and warm rose-cardamom syrup runs out at the first press of the spoon.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","rich","warm-spiced","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gulab%20Jamun%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gulab Jamun"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Gulab Jamun","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Gulab%20Jamun","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fryer-setups-and-oil-management","term":"Fryer Setups & Oil Management"}],"pantryItems":["Milk","Flour","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"knafeh-nabulseyeh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g kataifi (shredded filo), thawed, chopped short and tossed with 150g melted butter or ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g akkawi or a mozzarella + a little ricotta blend, sliced and DESALTED if using akkawi (soak, change water thrice)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Syrup (qatr): 300g sugar, 200ml water, squeeze of lemon, 1 tsp orange-blossom water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ground pistachios, a generous green blizzard"},{"kind":"item","text":"A round metal tray; the flip is part of the ceremony"}],"steps":[{"text":"Press half the buttered kataifi firmly into the tray (tint the tray with a little orange food color or safflower for the classic sunset, or don't).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cheese layer edge to edge; remaining kataifi pressed on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 200°C, 30–35 min (or the stovetop-rotation method of the Nablus masters) until the base is deep gold.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Flip onto a serving tray; pour the ROOM-TEMPERATURE syrup over the HOT knafeh: the hiss is the applause; pistachio over everything. Serve within the hour, while the cheese pulls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nablus is the Jerusalem of knafeh, and the physics are strict: hot pastry, cooler syrup (the baklava law again), and cheese that has been de-salted into sweetness. The cheese-pull window is real and short; knafeh shops in the Levant sell it by the slab from trays the size of truck wheels because it never has time to sit.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Handling phyllo"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Handling phyllo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=working%20with%20phyllo%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Knafeh%20Nabulseyeh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Knafeh Nabulseyeh"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Knafeh Nabulseyeh","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Knafeh%20Nabulseyeh","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"akkawi-and-nabulsi","term":"Akkawi & Nabulsi"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Orange","Butter","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Ricotta","Phyllo/Puff pastry","Sugar","Nuts"]},{"slug":"ichigo-daifuku","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100g shiratamako (glutinous rice flour: shiratamako over mochiko for silkiness)"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml water; 60g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g anko (sweet red bean paste), chilled, divided into 8 balls"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 small ripe strawberries, hulled, dried well"},{"kind":"item","text":"A LOT of cornstarch or katakuriko for dusting; mochi takes hostages"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wrap each strawberry, point down, in a thin coat of anko: the double-center is the architecture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk shiratamako, sugar and water; microwave in bursts (or steam), stirring between, until translucent, glossy and stretchy, 2–3 min total.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn onto a starch-blizzarded surface; cool 2 min to merely hot; divide in 8 with a starched scraper.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flatten each piece thin at the edges, thicker at center; wrap around the anko-berry, pinching closed at the bottom. Eat the same day: mochi waits for no one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The strawberry daifuku is barely a century old and already canon; the trick is thermal: mochi is only workable hot, so starch your hands like a gymnast and move with intention. Bite one in half to see the reward: red fruit, dark anko, white mochi, the Japanese flag's unofficial dessert.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ichigo%20Daifuku%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ichigo Daifuku"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Ichigo Daifuku","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Ichigo%20Daifuku","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Berries","Rice","Flour","Kidney/Pinto beans","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"black-sesame-tang-yuan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Filling: 80g black sesame seeds, toasted and ground + 60g sugar + 60g softened butter or lard, chilled until scoopable"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 200g glutinous rice flour + ~160ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"A knob of cooked dough (boil a small piece 1 min and knead it back in, the elasticity secret)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ginger syrup: 1L water, 80g sliced ginger, 100g brown sugar or rock sugar, simmered 15 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"A slotted spoon and someone to share the bowl"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the filling; chill firm; roll into 16 small balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm-water dough, soft as an earlobe; knead in the cooked-dough piece: it makes the wrapper stretchy and crack-proof.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap each filling ball, rolling smooth between palms: no seams, no cracks; cracks leak in the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil in water until they float + 2 min; transfer into the warm ginger syrup and serve them swimming.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Eaten at Lantern Festival and winter solstice, because the round shape spoken aloud (tuanyuan) sounds like 'family reunion'; the dessert is a homophone you can eat. The molten sesame center should flow, not gush; and the cooked-dough knead-back is the grandmother technology that keeps the skins supple where plain dough tears.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Salted water & the float test","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Black%20Sesame%20Tang%20Yuan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Black Sesame Tang Yuan"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Black Sesame Tang Yuan","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Black%20Sesame%20Tang%20Yuan","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Butter","Rice","Flour","Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"alfajores-de-maicena","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g cornstarch + 130g flour, the maicena ratio; it is why they melt"},{"kind":"item","text":"130g soft butter; 100g sugar; 3 egg yolks; 1 tsp vanilla + zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder; splash of cognac or pisco, optional and traditional"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g dulce de leche, repostero (thick) grade, or reduce regular in a pan until it holds a peak"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grated coconut for rolling the edges; icing sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream butter and sugar; yolks; fold in the sifted cornstarch, flour and baking powder to a tender dough. Chill 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 5mm; cut small rounds; bake 170°C, 10–12 min; they must NOT color; pale is the law; golden is a failure.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool completely: they are fragile as gossip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sandwich with a thick coin of dulce de leche; roll the exposed edge in coconut; dust or don't.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cornstarch is the melt: alfajores de maicena should dissolve on the tongue like sweet chalk in the best possible sense, which is why baking them past pallor is the cardinal sin. Argentina consumes alfajores at a rate no other nation approaches: the cookie is a food group, a currency, and a love language, packed in every lunchbox from Salta to Ushuaia.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Creaming butter & sugar","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alfajores%20de%20Maicena%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alfajores de Maicena"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Alfajores de Maicena","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Alfajores%20de%20Maicena","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"brandy-cognac-armagnac-calvados-and-pisco","term":"Brandy: Cognac, Armagnac, Calvados & Pisco"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Coconut","Butter","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"malva-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg; 150g sugar; 1 tbsp apricot jam, the secret signature"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour; 1 tsp baking soda; pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter, melted; 1 tsp vinegar; 125ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 200ml cream, 100g butter, 100g sugar, 60ml hot water, 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Custard or vanilla ice cream; in South Africa, usually both"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat egg, sugar and jam until pale and doubled: the jam is flavor and moisture insurance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in flour, soda, salt alternately with the milk, butter and vinegar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in a buttered dish, 180°C, 35–40 min to a deep brown sponge.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the sauce ingredients to melting; STAB the hot pudding all over and pour it ALL on: it looks like too much; it is exactly enough. Rest 15 min while it drinks.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"South Africa's Sunday-lunch closer, Cape Dutch by ancestry, with apricot jam doing quiet work in the crumb. Like its cousin sticky toffee, the poke-and-drown is the recipe: the sponge should arrive at the table saturated, sitting in a shallow moat, keeping a caramel secret in every slice. Oprah's chef once served it to millions; the Karoo shrugged: they knew.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malva%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Malva Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Malva Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Malva%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Vinegar","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"tira-de-asado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg beef short ribs, cut ACROSS the bones in 3cm strips (flanken cut; ask for tira de asado)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse salt (sal parrillera), the only seasoning; Argentina is firm on this"},{"kind":"item","text":"Real wood or lump charcoal, burned to steady gray embers"},{"kind":"item","text":"A parrilla or grill grate set HIGH above the coals"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chimichurri and salad criolla at the table; a Malbec breathing nearby"}],"steps":[{"text":"Light the fire an hour ahead; the asador's rule is embers, never flame: you should be able to hold your hand over the grate for 7–8 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the tira generously; lay it BONE SIDE DOWN first and leave it there 25–30 min: the bones are the roasting rack and the heat shield.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip once. Only once. 15–20 min on the meat side until deep-crusted and bronze.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 5 min; cut between the bones; serve on a wooden board, standing around the fire, as intended.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Argentine asado is slow-grilling as liturgy: low embers, high grate, one flip, and the asador is never rushed and never questioned. Bone-side-down first lets the fat render and the meat baste itself: tira eaten straight from the parrilla with just salt is the argument for the whole national method.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Asado fire","Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, wood-smoke running underneath."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Asado fire","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=argentine%20asado%20fire%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tira%20de%20Asado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tira de Asado"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Tira de Asado","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Tira%20de%20Asado","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"malbec","term":"Malbec"},{"slug":"malbec-zinfandel-and-the-new-world-crowd-pleasers","term":"Malbec, Zinfandel & the New World Crowd-Pleasers"},{"slug":"short-ribs-english-vs-flanken","term":"Short Ribs (English vs. Flanken)"}],"pantryItems":["Beef"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"provoleta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 thick rounds (2cm) of provoleta cheese, provolone made for the fire; regular provolone works if dried"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dried oregano and chili flakes, a confident shower"},{"kind":"item","text":"A drizzle of olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"A small cast-iron skillet or provoletera, set on the grill"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crusty bread: the cheese will not wait for ceremony"}],"steps":[{"text":"Leave the cheese rounds uncovered in the fridge overnight to dry the surface: dry cheese crusts; moist cheese escapes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the little skillet on the parrilla or over medium-high flame until properly hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cheese in: 2–3 min a side, until a golden crust forms outside and the heart turns molten: the flip takes nerve and a thin spatula.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oregano, chili, oil over; serve IN the skillet, bubbling, with bread for the drag.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Provoleta opens the asado while the main event cooks: the drying step is the difference between a disc with a crisp shell and a puddle with regrets. The correct texture is a contradiction: crunchy outside, pull-apart inside, eaten so hot that everyone burns their mouth slightly and no one complains.","equipment":["Skillet","Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","briny","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Asado fire","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=argentine%20asado%20fire%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provoleta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Provoleta"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Provoleta","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Provoleta","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"provolone-dolce-and-piccante","term":"Provolone (Dolce & Piccante)"}],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Bread"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"choripan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 fresh chorizo criollo sausages (coarse, garlicky, NOT the Spanish cured kind)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 crusty rolls or a baguette in lengths, pan francés"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chimichurri, a generous bowl (see The Saucier for the rojo; the classic verde lives with the bife)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: salsa criolla: diced tomato, onion, pepper in vinegar and oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Napkins are a courtesy; drips are the signature"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grill the chorizos over medium embers, turning patiently, 15–18 min: never pricked, never rushed; a burst casing is a small national tragedy.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split the rolls; toast the cut faces over the fire until kissed with char.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butterfly each sausage lengthwise (mariposa style) and give the cut face one minute on the grate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Into the bread; chimichurri spooned over with abandon; salsa criolla for the maximalists. Eat standing, leaning forward: the choripán stance is universal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The choripán is Argentina's street handshake, eaten outside every fútbol stadium and at every asado before the real meat lands, and the butterfly cut is the pro move: it doubles the char surface and makes a stable sandwich platform. Chori + pan; the name is the recipe, and the chimichurri is the constitution.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","charred","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Asado fire","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=argentine%20asado%20fire%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chorip%C3%A1n%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Choripán"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Choripán","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Chorip%C3%A1n","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chorizo-spanish-vs-mexican","term":"Chorizo: Spanish vs. Mexican"}],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bread","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"milanesa-a-la-napolitana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 thin beef escalopes (nalga or peceto), pounded wide and even"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour; 3 eggs beaten with garlic, parsley and a splash of milk; fine breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for shallow frying, a finger deep"},{"kind":"item","text":"The 'napolitana' armor: tomato sauce, ham slices, mozzarella, oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fries underneath or a fried egg on top (a caballo), or both; restraint is not the theme"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the escalopes; flour, garlicky egg, crumbs: press the crumbs on firmly this time; milanesa crust should be a jacket, not a soufflé.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 175°C, 2–3 min a side, to even deep gold; drain.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Blanket each with tomato sauce, ham, mozzarella, oregano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Under a hot broiler until the cheese bubbles and spots. Serve over fries, immediately, in portions that look like a dare.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The 'napolitana' has nothing to do with Naples; it was born at Restaurante Nápoli in Buenos Aires, allegedly to rescue a slightly burnt milanesa under sauce and cheese. Argentina eats milanesas the way other nations eat sandwiches: daily, in every home, and the leftover milanesa sandwich (sánguche de milanesa) may quietly outrank the original.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The broiler: heat from above","Sautéing & the shallow fry","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Milanesa%20a%20la%20Napolitana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Milanesa a la Napolitana"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Milanesa a la Napolitana","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Milanesa%20a%20la%20Napolitana","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"deep-and-shallow-frying","term":"Deep & Shallow Frying"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Garlic","Tomato","Parsley","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"locro","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g dried white corn (maíz blanco), soaked overnight, the body of the nation"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g white beans, soaked; 500g pumpkin, cubed (half will dissolve, by design)"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g beef brisket + 200g pork ribs + 150g pancetta + 1 chorizo, the meat parade"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, 2 spring onions; paprika, cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"The quiquirimichi: 4 tbsp fat or oil bloomed with 2 tbsp paprika, 1 tsp chili, spooned over each bowl"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained corn and beans in ample water 1 h before anything else joins.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the meats in order of toughness; simmer 1.5 h more, stirring from the bottom: locro loves to stick.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pumpkin in for the final 40 min, half mashed into the broth until the stew turns golden and thick enough to hold a story.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve in deep bowls with the crimson quiquirimichi drizzled over, the fire on the gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Locro is what Argentina eats on May 25th and July 9th, the patriotic stew, Andean by ancestry, simmered since before the republic existed. The dissolved pumpkin is the thickener and the color; the paprika fat on top is the flourish; and the pot must be watched like a newborn, because scorched locro on Independence Day is a family scandal.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locro%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Locro"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Locro","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Locro","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Sausage","Onion","Scallion","Leek","Chiles","Squash/Pumpkin","Corn","White beans","Cumin","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"matambre-arrollado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole matambre (thin flank/rose meat, ~1kg), trimmed to even thinness, the name means 'hunger killer'"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinade: garlic, oregano, chili flakes, vinegar, 2 h minimum"},{"kind":"item","text":"The filling mosaic: 3 hard-boiled eggs, roasted red pepper strips, blanched carrot batons, spinach leaves, olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butcher's twine and a confident rolling technique"},{"kind":"item","text":"Simmering broth (or a covered roasting tray with stock), then cold, pressed, and sliced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay the marinated matambre flat, fat side down; carpet with spinach; arrange carrots, peppers and whole eggs in lanes ACROSS the rolling direction.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll tight as a sleeping bag; tie snug at intervals; wrap in cloth or foil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer submerged in seasoned broth 1.5 h (or braise covered); then press under a weighted board in the fridge overnight.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":34200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Unwrap; slice thick rounds to reveal the stained-glass cross-section. Serve cold or room temp with chimichurri and bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Matambre is the picada's centerpiece and the picnic's crown: every slice a mosaic of egg, pepper and green. The overnight press is what makes it sliceable; skip it and the mosaic crumbles. Rolled, tied and pressed, the 'hunger killer' earns its name served cold off a cutting board while the asado fire is still just getting started.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Braising","Blanching & shocking","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","floral","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Asado fire","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=argentine%20asado%20fire%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Matambre%20Arrollado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Matambre Arrollado"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Matambre Arrollado","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Matambre%20Arrollado","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"searing-and-pan-technique","term":"Searing & Pan Technique"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Garlic","Carrot","Bell pepper","Chiles","Spinach","Olives","Oregano","Bread","Vinegar","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["braising","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"vitel-tone","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg peceto (eye of round), tied"},{"kind":"item","text":"Poaching bath: onion, carrot, celery, bay, peppercorns, splash of white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"The salsa: 200g canned tuna in oil, 4 anchovies, 3 tbsp capers, 2 hard-boiled yolks, 250ml mayo, 100ml cream, splash of the poaching broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"More capers for the top; black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"24 hours of fridge patience: this is a Christmas dish that must be made before Christmas"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the beef gently in the aromatic bath 60–75 min to just-cooked; cool IN the broth (the anti-dryness insurance), then chill.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend tuna, anchovies, capers, yolks, mayo, cream and broth to a smooth, pourable sauce; it should taste boldly of the sea against the quiet beef.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the cold beef paper-thin across the grain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shingle on a platter; flood completely with the salsa; capers over; rest overnight. Serve cold at the head of the Christmas table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Piedmont sent vitello tonnato to Argentina with the immigrants, and Buenos Aires made it mandatory: December without vitel toné is legally suspicious. The overnight rest under the sauce is the recipe: the beef drinks the tonnato and the two become one dish. It is eaten in 35°C heat at midnight on Nochebuena, and no one has ever suggested changing this.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","umami-deep","rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vitel%20Ton%C3%A9%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vitel Toné"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Vitel Toné","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Vitel%20Ton%C3%A9","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tuna-grades-akami-chutoro-otoro","term":"Tuna Grades (Akami, Chūtoro, Ōtoro)"}],"pantryItems":["Tuna","Anchovy","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Capers","Bay leaf","Cream","Wine","Stock/Broth","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"medialunas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour; 10g yeast; 100g sugar; 10g salt; 2 eggs; 180ml warm milk; 1 tbsp honey; vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cold butter for laminating (the manteca faction, the true café medialuna)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Almíbar glaze: 100g sugar + 100ml water + 1 tbsp honey, boiled 3 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"Three letter-folds with chilling between, croissant technique wearing a Buenos Aires accent"},{"kind":"item","text":"A cortado or café con leche; this pastry does not exist alone"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rich sweet dough; chill 1 h. Butter block flattened to a square.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lock the butter in the dough; roll and letter-fold three times, chilling 30 min between folds: the layers are built, not hoped for.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 4mm; cut tall triangles; notch the base; roll into snug crescents with the tips tucked (medialunas are curled tighter than croissants, and smaller).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Proof until wobbly, 1.5–2 h; egg wash; bake 180°C, 15–18 min; paint IMMEDIATELY with the warm almíbar: the sticky shine is the signature.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":8280,"estimated":true}],"note":"The medialuna is a croissant that emigrated and got sweeter and stickier, and Buenos Aires consumes them by the dozen: 'seis medialunas' is a standard breakfast order, not a party platter. The honey-syrup glaze applied hot out of the oven is what separates them from their French ancestor: a café medialuna should very gently glue your fingers together.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Lamination","Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","gently hot","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Medialunas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Medialunas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Medialunas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Medialunas","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Chiles","Butter","Flour","Honey","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"dulce-de-leche-casero","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2L whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla; ½ tsp baking soda dissolved in a splash of milk, the color and anti-curdle agent"},{"kind":"item","text":"A heavy, wide pot (wide = faster evaporation) and a wooden spoon with your name on it"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt at the end; jars"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve sugar in milk over medium heat; add the baking soda solution.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer steadily, stirring OFTEN at first and CONSTANTLY once it thickens and turns gold, 2–2.5 hours: the last 20 minutes are where reputations are made and pots are lost.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":false},{"text":"It is ready at repostero stage when a spoon dragged across the bottom leaves a slow-closing path; earlier for a pourable salsa stage.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt; vanilla; jar warm. Keeps a month refrigerated, which is theoretical, because it will not last a week.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Legend dates it to 1829: a maid left milk and sugar on the fire during a political summit and returned to find the accord already browning in the pot. The baking soda is the professionals' secret: it nudges the Maillard browning to that deep amber and keeps the milk from splitting. Stir with patience; dulce de leche is not made, it is attended.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","rich","sweet-edged","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dulce%20de%20Leche%20Casero%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dulce de Leche Casero"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Dulce de Leche Casero","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Dulce%20de%20Leche%20Casero","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"mate","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"50g yerba mate (con palo for beginners, sin palo for the committed)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A mate gourd (calabaza) or wooden/silicone cup, and a bombilla (filter straw)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Water heated to 70–80°C: NEVER boiling; boiled water burns the yerba and the friendship"},{"kind":"item","text":"A thermos, because one serving is never the plan"},{"kind":"item","text":"A circle of people; mate alone is legal but mate shared is the point"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fill the gourd two-thirds with yerba; cover with your palm, flip and shake: the fine dust migrates up, away from the bombilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tilt the yerba into a slope against one wall; pour a splash of COOL water into the hollow low side; wait a minute: the yerba wakes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Plant the bombilla into the wet hollow, thumb over the mouthpiece; do not stir it, ever again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour hot water into the hollow only, drink to the gurgle, refill, and pass: same server (cebador), same order, clockwise. Saying 'gracias' means you are done, so do not say it until you mean it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mate is not a beverage; it is a protocol for friendship with caffeine attached. The slope keeps half the yerba dry so the flavor releases in chapters instead of all at once, and the cebador's honor rides on every refill. Argentina, Uruguay, Paraguay and southern Brazil all claim it and all agree on one thing: moving the bombilla is a crime.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mate%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mate"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Mate","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Mate","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-concept-and-business-plan","term":"The Concept & Business Plan"},{"slug":"legal-entity-and-partnership-agreements","term":"Legal Entity & Partnership Agreements"}],"pantryItems":["Squash/Pumpkin"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"ahi-poke","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g sashimi-grade ahi tuna, cut in confident 2cm cubes: the cube size is the texture"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp soy sauce (shoyu) + 1 tsp sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"½ sweet Maui onion, slivered thin; 2 green onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp toasted sesame seeds; ½ tsp red chili flakes or a spoon of chili crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"Limu (seaweed) or crumbled roasted nori; inamona (roasted crushed kukui nut) or a few crushed macadamias standing in"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill everything: bowl, fish, hands' patience; poke is a cold-chain dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the tuna gently with shoyu and sesame oil first, the glaze coat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Onions, sesame, chili, limu in; fold, don't stir; taste: it should be ocean-first, seasoning second.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 10 min in the fridge and serve cold, alone as pupu or over hot rice as a poke bowl: the temperature contrast is the modern classic.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Poke means 'to cut crosswise,' and the dish predates every trend by centuries: fishermen seasoning the day's cut with sea salt, limu and crushed kukui nut. The mainland bowl craze added everything; Hawaii kept it simple, and the rule holds: great fish, few moves. If the tuna is beautiful, everything else is a whisper.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","gently hot","briny","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, a warm current of chile, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ahi%20Poke%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ahi Poke"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ahi Poke","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ahi%20Poke","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sashimi-grade-and-ikejime","term":"Sashimi-Grade & Ikejime"}],"pantryItems":["Tuna","Onion","Scallion","Chiles","Soy sauce","Sesame","Nuts","Seaweed/Nori"]},{"slug":"kalua-pig-oven-style","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2.5kg pork shoulder (butt), skin scored if present"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tbsp coarse Hawaiian sea salt (alaea red salt if you can find it, the earth flavor)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp liquid smoke: the honest imu substitute; no shame, only physics"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ti leaves or banana leaves for wrapping (foil works, leaves perfume)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Steamed rice, mac salad, and cabbage to sauté in the drippings"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pierce the pork deeply all over; rub with the salt and liquid smoke.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap in leaves, then tightly in two layers of foil: you are building a miniature imu (underground oven) on a baking tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast at 150°C for 5–6 hours. Do not open it. The package does the work.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shred with forks INTO the collected juices: never drain them; sauté cabbage in a skillet, fold the pork through, and serve over rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The real thing cooks all day in an imu, an earth oven of kiawe embers and hot stones, for luaus that feed hundreds. The oven version is a respectful forgery: salt, smoke, wrap, patience. The juices are the dish; kalua pig served dry is a solvable crime, and the cabbage fold-through is how every plate lunch spot stretches perfection.","equipment":["Skillet","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kalua%20Pig%20(Oven%20Style)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kalua Pig (Oven Style)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kalua Pig (Oven Style)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kalua%20Pig%20(Oven%20Style)","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pork-shoulder-butt-vs-picnic","term":"Pork Shoulder: Butt vs. Picnic"},{"slug":"floral-notes-and-perfume-dosage","term":"Floral Notes & Perfume Dosage"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Cabbage","Banana","Rice"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"lomi-lomi-salmon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g salted salmon (or fresh salmon cured 2 h in 2 tbsp salt, then rinsed), diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 ripe tomatoes, diced the same size"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sweet Maui onion, minced; 3 green onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crushed ice, a small handful, folded in before serving, the traditional chill"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nothing else; the salt cure is the seasoning"}],"steps":[{"text":"If curing your own: salt the salmon 2 h, rinse well, soak 10 min if still fierce, pat dry, dice.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Massage (lomi means to knead) the salmon gently with the tomatoes so the juices marry and stain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in both onions; chill thoroughly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crushed ice folded through at the last moment; serve cold beside kalua pig and poi, as the luau's bright note.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lomi lomi is the massage in the name: you work the salted fish and tomato together by hand until they become one cool, briny relish. It arrived with New England sailors' salt salmon and became Hawaiian by technique. On the luau plate it is the acid and the chill against the smoke; never skip it, and never serve it warm.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lomi%20Lomi%20Salmon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lomi Lomi Salmon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Lomi Lomi Salmon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Lomi%20Lomi%20Salmon","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Onion","Scallion","Tomato"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"loco-moco","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 cups hot steamed white rice, the foundation, packed in a bowl"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 beef patties (200g each), seasoned simply, smashed thin-edged"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, fried sunny with lacy edges and loose yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"Brown gravy: pan drippings + 1 tbsp flour + 300ml beef stock + splash of soy and Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"Green onions; hot sauce within reach"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear the patties hard in a skillet for a real crust; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Gravy in the same pan: flour into the drippings, stock whisked in, soy and Worcestershire, simmered to spoon-coating.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the eggs in a corner of the pan, basting with the gravy pan's fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack in order: rice, patty, gravy poured generously, egg crowning; scallions. Break the yolk into the gravy: that is the moment the dish is named for.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born in 1949 at a Hilo diner when teenagers wanted something cheaper than a sandwich and bigger than breakfast: the Lincoln Grill obliged, and Hawaii adopted it statewide. The loco moco is gravy engineering: rice below to catch, yolk above to enrich, and the whole thing eaten with a spoon. It is breakfast, lunch, and post-surf medicine in one bowl.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Loco%20Moco%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Loco Moco"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Loco Moco","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Loco%20Moco","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Rice","Flour","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"spam-musubi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 can Spam, sliced in 8 even planks"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cups cooked short-grain rice, warm, lightly salted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Glaze: 2 tbsp soy + 2 tbsp sugar + 1 tbsp mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 nori sheets, halved into wide bands"},{"kind":"item","text":"The musubi mold, or the cleaned Spam can itself, the original tool"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the Spam planks until crisp-edged; add the glaze and let it lacquer both sides, 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press warm rice into the mold (or the can, plastic-lined) in a firm 2cm layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Glazed Spam on top; press again; unmold the brick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap the nori band around the middle, seam down, sealing with a damp finger. Serve at room temperature; gas stations set the standard here, genuinely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Spam landed with the military in WWII and Hawaii, uniquely, decided to love it; the islands eat more per capita than anywhere in America, and the musubi is the proof: onigiri logic, plantation history, 7-Eleven distribution. The teriyaki lacquer is the pro move, and room temperature is correct; a musubi is built for the beach bag, not the plate.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Spam%20Musubi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Spam Musubi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Spam Musubi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Spam%20Musubi","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Rice","Wine","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"]},{"slug":"saimin","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g fresh saimin noodles (or fresh ramen noodles: wavy, eggy)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Broth: 1.5L dashi (kombu + shrimp shells or dried shrimp, the plantation signature) + 2 tbsp soy + 1 tsp mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Char siu slices, kamaboko (pink-swirl fish cake), a scrambled-egg ribbon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Green onions, a handful; won ton if you are feeling deluxe"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot mustard and shoyu on the side: the local dip for the char siu"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep kombu; add dried shrimp or shells; simmer gently 20 min; strain and season: the broth should taste of the sea more than the soy.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the egg ribbon: thin omelet, rolled and sliced.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil noodles just to tender-springy; never let them sit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Assemble hot: noodles, broth, char siu, kamaboko, egg, green onion. Serve with the mustard-shoyu dip and eat immediately, loudly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Saimin is Hawaii's own noodle soup, born on the plantations where Japanese, Chinese, Filipino and Portuguese workers pooled their pantries; the shrimp-dashi broth is the fingerprint no ramen shares. It is so local that McDonald's in Hawaii once sold it. The mustard dip for the char siu is the old-timer move that marks you as someone who knows.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","umami-deep","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Saimin%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Saimin"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Saimin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Saimin","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Noodles","Wine","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"huli-huli-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg chicken thighs, bone-in, or a spatchcocked whole bird"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 120ml pineapple juice, 80ml soy, 60g brown sugar, 2 tbsp ketchup, ginger and garlic grated, splash of sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Reserve a third of the sauce BEFORE the chicken touches it, the basting law"},{"kind":"item","text":"Charcoal, ideally with a chunk of kiawe (mesquite) for the roadside smoke"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice and mac salad; a smoke plume visible from the highway, traditionally"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the chicken in two-thirds of the sauce, 4 h to overnight.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill over medium coals, turning (huli means turn, and the dish is named for the shout) every 5–7 min.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Baste with the reserved sauce on each turn; the pineapple sugars want to burn, so keep the chicken moving and the heat honest.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"30–40 min total to lacquered mahogany and juices running clear. Rest; chop; serve over rice.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ernest Morgado grilled chicken for a farmers' gathering in 1955, and the crowd shouting 'huli!' (turn!) as the racks flipped named the dish; he trademarked it and sold millions of halves at fundraisers. The constant turning is the technique: pineapple-soy glaze caramelizes in thin coats instead of burning in one. Roadside smoke is the traditional advertisement.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Butchery basics","Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","charred","sweet-edged","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, char used as punctuation, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Butchery basics","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i_ZkAHCR1D0","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Huli%20Huli%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Huli Huli Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Huli Huli Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Huli%20Huli%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Ginger","Pineapple","Stone fruit","Rice","Soy sauce","Sesame","Sugar"],"standard":{"slug":"huli-huli-chicken","marks":[{"id":"huli-huli-chicken#lacquer","text":"The lacquer is even and glossy over the whole piece, dark amber to mahogany, thick enough to hold a fingernail track, with no blistered black patches on the thin edges and no beads of carbon in the skin folds."},{"id":"huli-huli-chicken#glaze","text":"The glaze is set and tacky rather than wet: a rested piece leaves no sauce puddle on the board after a minute, and the coating stays put when the piece is turned."},{"id":"huli-huli-chicken#lift","text":"Lift the skin at the edge of a thigh: the fat beneath has rendered away and the skin lies tight on the meat, not floating on a soft white layer."},{"id":"huli-huli-chicken#thigh","text":"Thigh reads 75C at the bone, juices run clear, and the meat beside the bone shows no translucent pink."},{"id":"huli-huli-chicken#last","text":"The last coat has had fire time on it: no thin slick of uncooked sauce anywhere on the piece, no raw garlic or ketchup edge in the taste, and the sweetness reads caramelized rather than stirred in."}],"fault":"Left too long between turns, so the pineapple sugars run past lacquer into carbon on the down face while the up face sits pale and slack: one side black and bitter, the other barely coloured."}},{"slug":"haupia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800ml coconut milk (two cans, full-fat: this dessert is coconut wearing a suit)"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g sugar; pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g cornstarch (the modern standard; pia, Polynesian arrowroot, is the ancestor)"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml water to slurry the starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"A square pan; cut into cubes when set, per luau law"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm coconut milk with sugar and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the cornstarch slurry in; cook over medium, whisking constantly, 5–8 min until it thickens to a heavy pudding that holds a trail.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the pan; smooth; press film to the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 3 h until firm; cut into neat squares. Serve cold, on ti leaf if you have it, or layer over chocolate in a pie shell for the famous chocolate haupia pie.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Haupia sits at every luau in modest white squares and outlasts every fancier dessert on the table, firm enough to eat by hand, soft enough to vanish. The whisking window matters: undercooked slumps, overcooked bounces. And the chocolate haupia pie (a Ted's Bakery legend on Oahu's North Shore) is the officially sanctioned way to gild it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haupia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Haupia"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Haupia","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Haupia","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"oka-ia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g very fresh firm white fish (mahi-mahi, snapper), cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of 6–8 limes (about 150ml): the cooking fire, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml thick coconut cream, the Pacific signature that separates oka from ceviche"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato, diced; ½ cucumber, seeded and diced; ½ small onion, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt; a chili, minced, optional; served cold in bowls, often in a half coconut shell"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the fish with lime juice and a good pinch of salt; chill 15–20 min until the outside turns opaque while the center stays tender.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off about half the lime juice: the coconut needs room to speak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the coconut cream, tomato, cucumber, onion, chili.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste for salt-lime balance; serve cold and soon, with taro, breadfruit, or just a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Samoa calls it oka, Fiji calls it kokoda, Tahiti calls it poisson cru: the Pacific's great citrus-and-coconut fish family, and the coconut cream is the move that makes it gentler and rounder than any Latin ceviche. The half-drain of lime is the balance point: too much acid and the coconut curdles into argument; drained right, they make peace in the bowl.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Ceviche"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","fiery","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Ceviche","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ceviche%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oka%20i'a%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oka i'a"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oka i'a","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oka%20i'a","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Cucumber","Lime","Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk"]},{"slug":"hawaiian-mac-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g elbow macaroni: boiled PAST al dente on purpose; soft is correct here"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g mayonnaise (Best Foods/Hellmann's, per plate-lunch orthodoxy), plus more next day"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, grated fine; ½ sweet onion, grated to pulp with its juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp milk; 1 tsp apple cider vinegar; ½ tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and a heavy hand of black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the macaroni 2 min past the package: plate-lunch mac is tender, not springy; drain and toss hot with the vinegar.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool 10 min; fold in the grated onion with its juice, carrot, milk, and most of the mayo.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with sugar, salt, and real pepper presence.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill at least 2 h; refresh with the reserved mayo before serving: the pasta drinks overnight. Scoop with an ice cream scoop beside rice and something smoky.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"The two-scoop plate lunch (rice, mac salad, protein) is Hawaii's food pyramid, and the mac salad is deliberately, proudly soft: overcooked pasta and grated onion juice are the technique, not the accident. The vinegar toss on hot pasta and the next-day mayo refresh are the plate-lunch counter secrets; al dente here would be a category error.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","fiery","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, real fire that builds and stays, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hawaiian%20Mac%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hawaiian Mac Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Hawaiian Mac Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Hawaiian%20Mac%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Carrot","Apple","Pasta","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mayonnaise","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"fried-shrimp-poboy-dressed","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g Gulf shrimp, peeled: size 21/25, big enough to matter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Wet: 250ml buttermilk + hot sauce; Dry: 150g corn flour (fish fry) + 50g flour, seasoned hard with cayenne, garlic, paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 loaf real po'boy French bread: the shattering-crust, cotton-crumb kind; this bread cannot be substituted, only approximated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dressed means: shredded iceberg, tomato slices, dill pickle rounds, mayonnaise; all four, no debate"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot sauce and lemon at the table"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak shrimp in the buttermilk-hot sauce bath 20 min; dredge through the seasoned corn flour with dry hands.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 180°C in small batches, 2 min, to gold and crunch; salt in the air.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split the loaf keeping a hinge; drag mayo edge to edge; build the dressing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the shrimp shamelessly; close; press gently; cut on the bias. Lean forward when you eat it: the drip line is part of the design.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The po'boy was born in 1929 when the Martin brothers fed striking streetcar workers free sandwiches: 'here comes another poor boy.' The bread is the whole argument: airy Leidenheimer-style loaves that shatter and vanish, holding fried shrimp like a paper bag holds fireworks. 'Dressed' is a complete sentence in this city; answer it correctly.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fried%20Shrimp%20Po'boy%20(Dressed)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fried Shrimp Po'boy (Dressed)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Fried Shrimp Po'boy (Dressed)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Fried%20Shrimp%20Po'boy%20(Dressed)","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Corn","Lemon","Dill","Buttermilk","Bread","Flour","Mayonnaise","Pickles","Hot sauce","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"oysters-rockefeller","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"24 oysters on the half shell, liquor saved, shells nested in rock salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g butter; 1 bunch green onions + celery heart + parsley, minced to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g watercress or spinach (the Antoine's original is famously green but famously NOT spinach, the recipe is still a secret)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp breadcrumbs; 1 tbsp Herbsaint or Pernod, the anise whisper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cayenne; lemon wedges; a broiler at full temper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the green paste in butter until it collapses into velvet, 8–10 min; crumbs in; Herbsaint in: it should hold a soft shape.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Nestle the shells into rock-salt-lined pans: the salt levels and insulates.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"A generous spoon of the green blanket over each oyster, sealing to the shell edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Broil 4–5 min until bubbling and browned at the peaks. Serve in the pan, on the salt, with lemon.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented at Antoine's in 1899 when snails ran short and named Rockefeller because the sauce was so rich: the true recipe has never left the family, and every green-sauced oyster in the world is an educated guess. The Herbsaint is the local tell; the rock salt is not garnish, it is the oven rack. Jules Alciatore took the formula to his grave, which in this town counts as good manners.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The broiler: heat from above","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","briny","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a briny, maritime salinity, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":37,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oysters%20Rockefeller%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oysters Rockefeller"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Oysters Rockefeller","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Oysters%20Rockefeller","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sheet-pans-racks-and-hotel-pans","term":"Sheet Pans, Racks & Hotel Pans"},{"slug":"oysters-and-the-r-month-rule","term":"Oysters & the R-Month Rule"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Scallion","Celery","Chiles","Spinach","Lettuce/Greens","Lemon","Arugula/Watercress","Parsley","Butter","Breadcrumbs/Panko"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"new-orleans-bbq-shrimp","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg large head-on Gulf shrimp: the heads are the sauce; do not remove them"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cold butter, cubed; 6 garlic cloves, smashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Worcestershire; 1 tbsp black pepper, coarsely cracked: this dish is a pepper delivery system"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon; 2 sprigs rosemary; splash of amber beer or shrimp stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"A loaf of French bread and a stack of napkins; both are structural"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sizzle garlic, pepper, rosemary in a little butter; Worcestershire, beer, lemon in; simmer 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shrimp in a single layer; 2 min a side, spooning constantly.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off heat: whisk in the cold butter cube by cube, beurre blanc logic in a Creole accent, until the sauce turns glossy and clings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Straight to the table in the skillet. Peel with your hands, suck the heads, and drag bread through the sauce until the pan reflects light.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No barbecue, no grill, no smoke: the name is pure New Orleans misdirection for shrimp drowned in peppered Worcestershire butter, born at Pascal's Manale in the 1950s. The head-on shrimp are non-negotiable: the fat in the heads finishes the sauce from the inside. Dry cleaning bills are the traditional review of a proper batch.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Beurre blanc","Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Beurre blanc","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=beurre%20blanc%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Orleans%20BBQ%20Shrimp%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Orleans BBQ Shrimp"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on New Orleans BBQ Shrimp","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20New%20Orleans%20BBQ%20Shrimp","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"beer-at-the-table-and-the-carbonation-advantage","term":"Beer at the Table & the Carbonation Advantage"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Lemon","Rosemary","Butter","Bread","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"backyard-crawfish-boil","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"15kg live crawfish (a sack), purged in fresh water, spring ritual quantities; scale at your peril, not ours"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bag + 1 jar crawfish boil seasoning, plus extra cayenne, salt, lemons halved, garlic heads halved, onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"Small red potatoes, corn cobs halved, smoked sausage links, the classic passengers"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional modern passengers: mushrooms, artichokes, whole garlic braids, a pineapple for the bold"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 60L pot with basket, a propane burner, a newspaper-covered table, and everyone you know"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the seasoned water: it should taste aggressively over-salted and spicy; the crawfish only borrow from it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Potatoes and aromatics first, 10 min; sausage and corn, 5 more.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crawfish in; return to a boil for 3 minutes ONLY, then kill the fire.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"THE SOAK is the recipe: 20–30 min off heat; they sink as they drink the spice. Taste one at 15 min. Dump the basket down the table; no plates; pinch the tail, suck the head.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The boil is not cooked, it is soaked: three minutes of fire and half an hour of patience, and the spice lives in the soak, not the boil. Crawfish season runs roughly January to June, peaking around Easter, and the table etiquette is fixed: newspaper, no plates, and the person who says they don't suck heads gets exactly one season of grace.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The seafood boil","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","smoky","gently hot","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, wood-smoke running underneath, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The seafood boil","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=crawfish%20boil%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Backyard%20Crawfish%20Boil%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Backyard Crawfish Boil"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Backyard Crawfish Boil","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Backyard%20Crawfish%20Boil","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Crawfish","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Chiles","Mushrooms","Corn","Lemon","Pineapple","Artichoke"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"yakamein","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g beef chuck, simmered in 2L water with garlic, onion, and a heavy hand of Creole seasoning until shreddable"},{"kind":"item","text":"The seasoning spine: soy sauce + Worcestershire + hot sauce + cayenne, until the broth is dark and insistent"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g spaghetti: yes, spaghetti; this is the correct noodle and the point"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 boiled eggs, halved; a storm of chopped green onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ketchup and hot sauce bottles on the table, per tradition"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the beef 2 h; shred it into the broth; season the broth boldly: it should ride the line between soup and sauce.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the spaghetti separately; portion into deep cups or bowls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle beef and broth over the noodles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Half an egg on top, green onions like confetti, and let each eater adjust with the bottles. Serve in a styrofoam cup for full authenticity.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"'Old Sober': sold at second lines, corner stores and Jazz Fest, and famous as the city's hangover ambulance. Its origin is argued (Chinese immigrants, Black soldiers returning from Korea, or both meeting in the middle), but its jurisdiction is settled: New Orleans only. Miss Linda the Yakamein Lady made it famous; the boiled egg is the badge and the spaghetti is the law.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yakamein%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yakamein"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Yakamein","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Yakamein","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tasting-and-seasoning-like-a-chef","term":"Tasting & Seasoning Like a Chef"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Chiles","Pasta","Noodles","Soy sauce","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce","Old Bay/Creole"]},{"slug":"grillades-and-grits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g beef or veal rounds, pounded thin, cut in palm-size squares"},{"kind":"item","text":"Seasoned flour for dredging; oil for browning + the roux after"},{"kind":"item","text":"The trinity: 1 onion, 1 bell pepper, 2 celery ribs, diced; 4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes + 500ml beef stock; bay, thyme, Worcestershire, hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grits: 200g stone-ground, cooked slow in milk-water with butter and a little cheese"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dredge and brown the meat hard in batches; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a peanut-butter-colored roux in the drippings; trinity in until it sighs; garlic, tomatoes, stock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meat back; simmer low and lidded 1–1.5 h until fork-tender and the gravy is deep russet.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Start the grits at the hour mark: slow, stirred, enriched. Grillades over grits, gravy over everything, green onions over that.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The grand dame of the New Orleans brunch table: debutante breakfasts, Christmas morning, and every white-tablecloth Jazz brunch runs on grillades. It is pot roast logic at breakfast tempo: thin meat, dark roux, trinity, time. The grits must be stone-ground and patient; instant grits under grillades is how you get quietly disinvited.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","rich","aromatic","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, richness that coats and comforts, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Grillades%20and%20Grits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Grillades and Grits"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Grillades and Grits","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Grillades%20and%20Grits","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Celery","Bell pepper","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Cheese (any)","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce","Clove"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"gumbo-zherbes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"An ODD number of greens, 7 or 9 for luck: collards, mustard, turnip, spinach, watercress, cabbage, beet tops, carrot tops, parsley: the more, the more friends"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 smoked ham hock + 300g smoked sausage + 200g brisket or ham (Lenten version: omit and go maroon-style meatless)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dark roux: 100ml oil + 100g flour; the trinity; garlic generously"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filé powder, thyme, bay, cayenne; the strained pot likker from the greens, every drop"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice; and Holy Thursday on the calendar, traditionally"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil all the greens together in ample water 30 min; RESERVE the likker; chop or grind the greens fine.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a milk-chocolate roux; trinity; garlic; then the meats, browned.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Greens back in with their likker; simmer low 1.5–2 h: the gumbo should turn deep green-brown and thick as a sermon.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Filé off the heat; serve over rice. Count your greens; each one is a friend you'll make this year, says the tradition.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The green gumbo of Holy Thursday: Leah Chase served it at Dooky Chase's once a year and the city planned around it. The odd number of greens is the superstition (each green, a new friend), the pot likker is the soul, and the dish carries the whole Creole genealogy in one pot: African, French, German, Choctaw. One day a year was the tradition; your kitchen may be more generous.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","aromatic","gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gumbo%20Z'herbes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gumbo Z'herbes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Gumbo Z'herbes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Gumbo%20Z'herbes","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mirepoix-trinity-sofrito-and-friends","term":"Mirepoix, Trinity, Sofrito & Friends"},{"slug":"prime-cost-the-number-that-runs-the-building","term":"Prime Cost: The Number That Runs the Building"},{"slug":"ham-and-hock","term":"Ham & Hock"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Sausage","Ham/Prosciutto","Garlic","Carrot","Chiles","Spinach","Kale/Collards","Cabbage","Beets","Lettuce/Greens","Radish/Turnip","Arugula/Watercress","Parsley","Thyme","Bay leaf","Flour","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"creole-pralines","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g white sugar + 200g light brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml evaporated milk (the Creole signature) + 60g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g pecan halves, lightly toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla; pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"A candy thermometer and a sheet of parchment ready BEFORE you start: pralines wait for no one"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil sugars and milk, stirring, to soft-ball stage, 114–116°C; butter and pecans in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off heat: rest 2 min, then STIR with purpose 2–3 min as it turns from glossy to matte and thickens: this is the crystallization you are conducting.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":false},{"text":"Vanilla and salt; drop in quick spoonfuls onto parchment: they set as you work, so move like you mean it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool 30 min to a creamy, fudge-adjacent set with a gentle sparkle. Store airtight, humidity permitting, and in this town, humidity rarely permits.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"French pralines were almonds in hard caramel; New Orleans rewrote them with pecans and cream into something softer and entirely its own, sold by the praline ladies of the Quarter whose recipes built businesses. The matte-turn is the moment of truth: stop stirring too early and they never set, too late and they set in the pot. Humid days genuinely sabotage them; even the candy here consults the weather.","equipment":["Thermometer","Parchment"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Creole%20Pralines%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Creole Pralines"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Creole Pralines","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Creole%20Pralines","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"resting-carryover-and-the-thermometer","term":"Resting, Carryover & the Thermometer"}],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"king-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Brioche-style dough: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 80g sugar, 120ml warm milk, 3 eggs, 100g soft butter, zest of a lemon, fresh-grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filling: 120g brown sugar + 2 tbsp cinnamon + 60g soft butter (or a cream cheese ribbon, the modern favorite)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing: 200g powdered sugar + milk + vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sanding sugar in purple, green and gold: justice, faith, and power, in that order"},{"kind":"item","text":"A tiny plastic baby, hidden AFTER baking; whoever finds it buys the next cake"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rich dough; rise 1.5 h. Roll a long rectangle; spread the cinnamon butter; roll into a log and join into an oval ring, sealing well.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Proof 45 min; bake 180°C, 25–30 min to deep gold; cool completely.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tuck the baby into the underside. Ice generously while the icing still flows.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Band the three sugars in bold stripes around the ring. Serve from Twelfth Night (January 6) to Mardi Gras and NOT ONE DAY OUTSIDE that window, per local law.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The king cake calendar is real: it appears on Epiphany and vanishes at midnight when Lent begins, and eating one in July marks you as a tourist or a rebel. The baby (baby Jesus, by way of a mid-century plastics vendor) assigns the next cake's buyer, an engine of perpetual cake. Purple, green and gold have meant justice, faith and power since Rex declared it in 1872; the cake is a parade you can eat.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of King Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on King Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20King%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"sazerac","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60ml rye whiskey (the modern standard; cognac was the 1850s original before the vines failed)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sugar cube + a few drops of water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 dashes Peychaud's bitters: the non-negotiable; Angostura is a different cocktail"},{"kind":"item","text":"Herbsaint or absinthe, a bar-spoon, for rinsing the glass"},{"kind":"item","text":"A lemon peel; a chilled old-fashioned glass; NO ice in the final drink"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill one glass with ice; in a SECOND glass, muddle the sugar cube with water and Peychaud's.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the rye and ice to the mixing glass; stir 30 seconds: stirred, never shaken; this drink is silk, not foam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Empty the chilled glass; rinse it with Herbsaint, rolling to coat, and discard the excess like a true believer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain the rye in; express the lemon peel over the surface, wipe the rim, and, per the old ritual, discard the peel or rest it on the edge. Serve neat, cold, and unhurried.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Antoine Peychaud mixed his bitters into brandy at his Royal Street apothecary in the 1830s, and the drink that grew from it is now the official cocktail of New Orleans by actual legislative act. The absinthe rinse-and-discard is the signature gesture, perfume, not ingredient. One is a civilized evening; the old Quarter wisdom on a second Sazerac remains: proceed, but cancel your morning.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sazerac%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sazerac"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Isaac Toups on Sazerac","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Isaac%20Toups%20Sazerac","sub":"A YouTube search for Isaac Toups and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"absinthe-and-the-rinse","term":"Absinthe & the Rinse"},{"slug":"the-old-fashioned-template","term":"The Old Fashioned Template"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"nashville-hot-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, quartered, or 4 leg quarters: dark meat carries the heat best"},{"kind":"item","text":"Brine/dredge: buttermilk + hot sauce bath; seasoned flour, double-dipped"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE PASTE: 6 tbsp of the frying oil whisked with 3–4 tbsp cayenne, 2 tbsp brown sugar, 1 tsp each garlic powder, paprika, chili powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying, some sacrificed to the paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"White bread slices and dill pickle chips, the mandatory, sauce-soaked altar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak chicken in buttermilk-hot sauce 4 h; dredge flour-buttermilk-flour for a craggy shell.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 160°C, 15–18 min for quarters, to deep gold and 74°C at the bone; rest on a rack.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle hot frying oil into the cayenne-sugar bowl; whisk into a brick-red paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"BRUSH or dunk every surface of the hot chicken. Serve on white bread with pickles on top; the bread's job is to catch the run-off and become the second course.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Legend says Thornton Prince's scorned sweetheart doctored his Sunday fried chicken with a punitive dose of pepper, and he loved it, and Prince's Hot Chicken was born. The genius is the oil paste: cayenne bloomed in hot frying fat clings like lacquer and burns in slow motion. Ask for 'medium' your first time at any Nashville shack; 'hot' is a decision, and 'shut the cluck up' tiers are legally binding.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nashville%20Hot%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nashville Hot Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nashville Hot Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nashville%20Hot%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Chiles","Dill","Buttermilk","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Pickles","Hot sauce","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"memphis-dry-rub-ribs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 racks pork spare ribs or loin backs, membrane peeled off the bone side"},{"kind":"item","text":"The rub: 3 tbsp paprika, 2 tbsp brown sugar, 1 tbsp each garlic powder, onion powder, black pepper, 2 tsp cayenne, 1 tbsp celery salt, 1 tsp dry mustard, oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mop: 100ml cider vinegar + 100ml water + 1 tbsp of the rub"},{"kind":"item","text":"A smoker or kettle grill set up indirect at 120°C, hickory chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce served ON THE SIDE if at all: this is the dry side of the great Memphis divide"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the racks generously the night before; refrigerate uncovered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smoke indirect at 120°C, meat side up, hickory smoke thin and blue, 4.5–5.5 h.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":19800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mop lightly every 45 min after the second hour, moisture, not sauce.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"They are done when the rack bends and cracks at the surface when lifted with tongs. Dust with a final pinch of fresh rub (the Rendezvous signature) and serve dry, proudly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Memphis split barbecue into wet and dry, and the dry rib (crusted in spice, sauced by nobody) is the city's dissenting opinion made delicious, perfected in the basement of the Rendezvous since 1948. The fresh dusting of rub at the end wakes the whole bark up. The bend test beats every thermometer: ribs that bend and crack are done; ribs that fall clean off the bone are overdone, whatever the internet says.","equipment":["Grill","Smoker"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","gently hot","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, a warm current of chile, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Low & slow smoking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Memphis%20Dry-Rub%20Ribs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Memphis Dry-Rub Ribs"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Memphis Dry-Rub Ribs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Memphis%20Dry-Rub%20Ribs","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"grills-and-smokers-kettle-kamado-offset-pellet","term":"Grills & Smokers: Kettle, Kamado, Offset, Pellet"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Celery","Chiles","Oregano","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Sugar","Paprika"],"standard":{"slug":"memphis-dry-rub-ribs","marks":[{"id":"memphis-dry-rub-ribs#rack","text":"The rack is matte and dry, brick red to mahogany; a fingertip drawn across it lifts spice dust rather than glaze, and nothing tacky transfers to the board."},{"id":"memphis-dry-rub-ribs#lifted","text":"Lifted with tongs a third of the way along, the rack bends toward 90 degrees and the surface cracks open between the bones without the meat tearing free."},{"id":"memphis-dry-rub-ribs#turn","text":"Turn the rack over: no papery membrane is left anywhere on the bone side, and the fat between the bones has rendered soft rather than sitting in rubbery seams."},{"id":"memphis-dry-rub-ribs#bite","text":"A bite pulls meat cleanly away leaving a clean bite mark, and the bone stays in the rack; bones that slide out grey and bare are past the standard."},{"id":"memphis-dry-rub-ribs#final","text":"The final dusting reads as raw spice on the lips, paprika and celery salt distinct from the cooked bark beneath, and no sauce has touched the meat."}],"fault":"Mopped like a sauce rather than a wetting, heavy and often, so the vinegar dissolves the rub into a red slurry that never sets: the rack comes off streaked and sticky with most of the bark in the drip pan."}},{"slug":"memphis-bbq-spaghetti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g spaghetti, cooked PAST al dente: soft is traditional and correct here"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g smoked pulled pork (yesterday's, ideally; this dish exists to give it a second act)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion + 1 green bell pepper, diced, sweated in butter or drippings"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml barbecue sauce (Memphis-style: tomato and vinegar, sweet-tangy) + 200ml crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"A shot of the rub from the ribs recipe; hot sauce to taste"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat onion and pepper in drippings until soft and sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauce, tomatoes and rub in; simmer 15 min to a loose, smoky ragù; fold in the pulled pork.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the spaghetti soft; drain and fold into the sauce until stained and glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 10 minutes: BBQ spaghetti is better once it settles. Serve as a side to ribs (the Memphis way) or a bowl of its own.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"A pitmaster's invention from Memphis' Brady and Lil's: smoke-shop pasta that scandalized purists and conquered the city; the Bar-B-Q Shop still sells it daily. It is barbecue logic applied to Monday leftovers: soft noodles, smoky pork, sweet-tangy sauce. Al dente would miss the point entirely: this is a barbecue side wearing Italian clothes, not the reverse.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Memphis%20BBQ%20Spaghetti%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Memphis BBQ Spaghetti"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Memphis BBQ Spaghetti","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Memphis%20BBQ%20Spaghetti","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Butter","Pasta","Vinegar","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"country-ham-with-redeye-gravy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices dry-cured country ham, ¼-inch thick (Tennessee's salt-cured, aged pride, Benton's if you can get it)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter or just the ham's own fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml strong black coffee: yes, coffee; that is the gravy"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp brown sugar, optional heresy that many kitchens quietly commit"},{"kind":"item","text":"Biscuits and grits: see the American shelf for the biscuits; you will need them"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the ham slices in a hot cast-iron skillet, 2–3 min a side, until the edges brown and the fat renders; remove.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the black coffee straight into the screaming pan: stand back; the deglaze is the show.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape every browned bit loose; reduce 2 min; the brown fat pools in the center like a red eye.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the ham or plate it; spoon the thin, salty-bitter gravy over ham, biscuits, and grits without prejudice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Redeye gravy is the leanest recipe in the South (ham drippings and coffee, nothing else), and it exists because country ham is too salty and too proud to need more. The 'red eye' is the coil of fat in the middle of the coffee pool. Andrew Jackson gets a folk-etymology credit involving a hungover cook; the gravy, like the story, is thin, strong, and Tennessean.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Country%20Ham%20with%20Redeye%20Gravy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Country Ham with Redeye Gravy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Country Ham with Redeye Gravy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Country%20Ham%20with%20Redeye%20Gravy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"aged-gouda","term":"Aged Gouda"},{"slug":"american-country-ham","term":"American Country Ham"},{"slug":"vermouth-and-the-fortified-aperitif-shelf","term":"Vermouth & the Fortified Aperitif Shelf"}],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Butter","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar","Coffee/Espresso"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"fried-catfish-and-hushpuppies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 catfish fillets, soaked 30 min in buttermilk with hot sauce (the mud-cutter bath)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dredge: 200g fine cornmeal + 2 tbsp flour, seasoned with salt, pepper, cayenne, garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hushpuppies: 150g cornmeal + 60g flour, 1 tsp baking powder, 1 minced onion, 1 egg, ~150ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying; lemon, tartar sauce, hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coleslaw and white bread, the fish-fry constitution"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the hushpuppy batter thick; rest while the fish soaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the drained fillets in seasoned cornmeal, pressing lightly; fry at 175°C, 4–5 min to golden crunch; drain on a rack.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the hushpuppy batter by spoonfuls into the same oil; fry rolling, 3 min, to deep bronze spheres.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt everything in the air; pile the platter; lemon over the fish. Serve loud, with slaw, pickles, and no ceremony.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Tennessee and Mississippi River fish fry is a standing institution: cornmeal-crusted catfish and hushpuppies from the same oil, and the buttermilk soak is what retires any river taste. The hushpuppy name-legend (frying batter to quiet the dogs) is folklore, and everyone tells it anyway. Fine cornmeal, hot oil, no breadcrumbs in sight: the crust should whisper, crackle, and vanish.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fried%20Catfish%20and%20Hushpuppies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fried Catfish and Hushpuppies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Fried Catfish and Hushpuppies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Fried%20Catfish%20and%20Hushpuppies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Buttermilk","Bread","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"fried-green-tomatoes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 firm green (unripe) tomatoes, sliced a confident ½-inch: thin slices weep, thick slices steam"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt for a 15-minute preliminary sweat on a rack"},{"kind":"item","text":"The three-station dredge: seasoned flour → buttermilk + egg → cornmeal mixed with a little flour and cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bacon fat or oil, ¼-inch deep in cast iron"},{"kind":"item","text":"Comeback sauce or remoulade: mayo, ketchup, hot sauce, garlic, lemon, whisked while the tomatoes sweat"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the slices and rest them on a rack 15 min; pat dry: this concentrates the tang and defends the crust.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Run the three stations with one wet hand, one dry, the classic assembly-line discipline.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shallow-fry in bacon fat at a steady sizzle, 3 min a side, to deep gold; drain on the rack, never paper.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt hot; stack with the sauce for dunking, or crown with pimento cheese if it is that kind of afternoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The green tomato is not an unripe mistake: it is a tart, firm vegetable in its own right, and frying it in cornmeal is how the South eats the end of summer and the start of it. The salt-sweat is the professional step everyone skips: it pulls the water that would otherwise blow the crust off. Bacon fat is traditional, audible, and correct.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fried%20Green%20Tomatoes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fried Green Tomatoes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Fried Green Tomatoes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Fried%20Green%20Tomatoes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"dehydrator-ferment-station-and-the-pantry-machines","term":"Dehydrator, Ferment Station & the Pantry Machines"}],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lemon","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Mayonnaise","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"squash-casserole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg yellow crookneck squash, sliced in coins"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, sliced; 60g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs; 150ml sour cream or mayo (both factions exist; both win); 150g sharp cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, a shake of cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"The crown: 100g crushed butter crackers (the round buttery kind) tossed in 40g melted butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sauté squash and onion in butter until collapsed and lightly golden, 12–15 min; drain in a colander and PRESS: watery squash is the casserole's one enemy.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold with eggs, sour cream, most of the cheddar; season with intent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Into a buttered dish; remaining cheese, then the buttered cracker rubble edge to edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 180°C, 30–35 min until set at the center and bronzed on top. Rest 10. Casserole logic: it slices tomorrow, it spoons today.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true}],"note":"No meat-and-three steam table in Tennessee operates without squash casserole, and the cracker crust is non-negotiable: it is the textural apology for how soft and sweet the middle is. The press-drain is the technique: squash is mostly water and will flood the dish given any opening. This is a 'vegetable' by Southern definition, which is to say butter, cheese, and a memory of squash.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Squash%20Casserole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Squash Casserole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Squash Casserole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Squash%20Casserole","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Chiles","Squash/Pumpkin","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"chess-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 blind-baked 23cm pie shell (all-butter, edges crimped high, the filling rises then settles)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar + 60g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs; 120g melted butter; 60ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp fine cornmeal + 1 tsp flour, the signature grit; 1 tbsp vinegar + 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nothing fancy; the whole pie costs a dollar in ingredients, which is the point"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk sugars, eggs, butter, buttermilk smooth; whisk in cornmeal, flour, vinegar, vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the warm blind-baked shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 170°C, 40–45 min: the top forms its famous crackled sugar skin; the center should still shimmy slightly.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool COMPLETELY: hours; chess pie sets on the counter, not in the oven. Slice thin; it is sweeter than it looks and it looks sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The name has three folk etymologies: 'chest pie' (it kept in the pie chest), 'jes' pie' (just pie, as the cook allegedly said), or the English 'cheese pie' lineage, and Tennessee tells all three, usually at once. The cornmeal top-skin and the vinegar's quiet counterweight are the whole recipe: pantry-bare ingredients, crackled crust, transcendent result. The buttermilk version leans tangy; the lemon version is another church entirely.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chess%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chess Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Chess Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Chess%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Vinegar","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking"]},{"slug":"appalachian-apple-stack-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dried apples: 300g, simmered in water 45 min with ½ tsp cinnamon, cloves and allspice, then mashed to a thick, dark butter. Fresh apples are the wrong flavor; dried is the identity"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cake layers: 350g flour, 150g sugar, 120g butter, 120ml sorghum or molasses, 1 egg, 120ml buttermilk, 1 tsp soda, ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"5–7 THIN layers, baked in a skillet or cake pans: more crust than crumb, closer to soft gingerbread cookies"},{"kind":"item","text":"Patience: assembled, wrapped, and RESTED 2 days minimum"},{"kind":"item","text":"Only a dusting of powdered sugar on serving day, no icing; the apples are the frosting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer and mash the dried apples with spices to a spreadable dark butter; cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a stiff sorghum dough; divide in 6; pat each thin into a greased skillet and bake 180°C, 10–12 min: the layers come out firm and unpromising; trust the process.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack: layer, thick apple spread, layer, until crowned; wrap the whole cake tight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Refrigerate 2–3 DAYS: the apple butter migrates and the firm layers soften into velvet. Dust and slice thin: it is richer than it admits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"The East Tennessee mountain wedding cake: neighbors each baked a layer and the bride's family provided the apples; the taller the stack, the more beloved the couple. Day one it is inedible discs and jam; day three it is one unified, spiced, apple-soaked marvel, which is either a baking lesson or a marriage metaphor, and in the mountains they will tell you it is both.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","aromatic","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Appalachian%20Apple%20Stack%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Appalachian Apple Stack Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Appalachian Apple Stack Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Appalachian%20Apple%20Stack%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"building-flavor-in-layers","term":"Building Flavor in Layers"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ginger","Apple","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"lynchburg-lemonade","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"45ml Tennessee whiskey (charcoal-mellowed: the Lincoln County process is what makes it Tennessee and not bourbon)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml triple sec or orange liqueur"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml fresh lemon juice + 1 tsp sugar (or 30ml sour mix if honesty is off tonight)"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml lemon-lime soda, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lots of ice, a tall glass, lemon wheel and a cherry; a porch strongly recommended"}],"steps":[{"text":"Shake whiskey, triple sec, lemon and sugar hard with ice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain into a tall glass packed with fresh ice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with the soda; one gentle stir: keep the sparkle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lemon wheel, cherry, porch. Batch it in a pitcher for company: 1 part each of the first three, 4 of soda, stirred over a mountain of ice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Named for Lynchburg, the tiny town where Jack Daniel's is distilled: famously located in a dry county, a fact every tour guide delivers with a straight face. The charcoal-mellowing (dripped through sugar-maple charcoal before barreling) is the legal and flavor line between Tennessee whiskey and bourbon. The drink itself is porch engineering: whiskey made agreeable to everyone, in quantities that sneak up accordingly.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","smoky","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, wood-smoke running underneath, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lynchburg%20Lemonade%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lynchburg Lemonade"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Lynchburg Lemonade","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Lynchburg%20Lemonade","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"bourbon-rye-and-american-whiskey","term":"Bourbon, Rye & American Whiskey"},{"slug":"the-liqueur-shelf-orange-maraschino-chartreuse-and-friends","term":"The Liqueur Shelf: Orange, Maraschino, Chartreuse & Friends"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Lime","Orange","Stone fruit","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"chilaquiles-verdes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 corn tortillas, cut in triangles, fried crisp (or thick good-quality chips, the honest weekday road)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salsa verde: 500g tomatillos, 2 serranos, ½ onion, 2 garlic cloves, cilantro, charred and blended"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs per person, fried; or shredded chicken for the fuller house"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crema, crumbled queso fresco, sliced raw onion, avocado"},{"kind":"item","text":"Refried beans on the side; strong coffee non-negotiable"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char tomatillos, chiles, onion and garlic under a broiler until blistered; blend with cilantro and salt; simmer the salsa 10 min in a wide pan until it darkens slightly.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the tortilla triangles to full crispness: they are about to be rained on and must fight back.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the chips into the simmering salsa 60–90 seconds ONLY: the eternal chilaquiles question is crisp-edged or fully soft, and the answer is a timer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Plate immediately; crema zigzag, queso, onion, avocado, fried egg on the summit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chilaquiles are Mexico's official answer to yesterday's tortillas and last night's decisions: the name is Nahuatl, the dish is medicinal. The 90-second salsa bath is the entire skill: count it out loud. And the crisp-vs-soft debate splits families evenly; serve it at the edge of crisp and let everyone's plate finish the argument.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","The broiler: heat from above","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","herb-fresh","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilaquiles%20Verdes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chilaquiles Verdes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Chilaquiles Verdes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Chilaquiles%20Verdes","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"service-styles-and-front-of-house-fluency","term":"Service Styles & Front-of-House Fluency"},{"slug":"queso-oaxaca-quesillo","term":"Queso Oaxaca (Quesillo)"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Avocado","Tomatillo","Cilantro","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Bread","Coffee/Espresso","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"huevos-rancheros","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 corn tortillas, 2 per plate, lightly fried until pliable-crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salsa ranchera: 6 ripe tomatoes, 2 jalapeños, ½ onion, 2 garlic; charred, blended coarse, simmered with a pinch of cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 eggs, fried sunny, whites set and yolks loose"},{"kind":"item","text":"Refried black beans, warm; queso fresco, cilantro, avocado"},{"kind":"item","text":"A hot comal and the confidence of a ranch cook"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char and blend the salsa coarse: ranchera is rustic; a smooth salsa is a different breakfast; simmer 10 min until it thickens and tastes of the griddle.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry tortillas 20 seconds a side in a little oil: soft with crisped edges, the raft consistency.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the eggs in the same pan, basting the whites.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build: tortillas, a smear of beans, eggs riding on top, salsa ladled around and over the whites but NOT drowning the yolks; cheese and cilantro.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is mid-morning ranch food (almuerzo, the second breakfast after early chores), and the architecture is fixed: tortilla raft, bean mortar, egg cargo, salsa weather. The yolks stay visible because they are the sauce's second act. Charring the vegetables before blending is the fifteen seconds of extra work that makes it taste like a ranch and not a jar.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Griddle & plancha work","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Huevos%20Rancheros%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Huevos Rancheros"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Huevos Rancheros","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Huevos%20Rancheros","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"comal-plancha-and-griddles","term":"Comal, Plancha & Griddles"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Corn","Avocado","Cilantro","Cheese (any)","Bread","Black beans","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"ful-medames","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 cans fava beans (or 400g dried, soaked and cooked to collapse) with their liquid"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves mashed to paste with salt; juice of 1–2 lemons"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp good olive oil, plus a defiant pour at the end; 1 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"The garnish table: chopped tomato, onion, parsley, chili, boiled eggs, tahini"},{"kind":"item","text":"Warm pita or Egyptian baladi bread, the utensil and the plate"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the beans in their liquid; mash PARTIALLY with a fork or the traditional smash: rubble, not purée; some beans whole, some cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in garlic paste, lemon, cumin and half the oil while hot: the beans drink while warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste and push it: ful should be assertively lemony and garlicky; timid ful is porridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a shallow bowl; flood with olive oil; let everyone build their own bite with the garnishes and torn bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Egypt's national breakfast, eaten from pharaonic times to this morning's street carts, where it is ladled from giant buried copper pots (the idra) that simmer overnight. The partial mash is the texture law and the toppings are personal sovereignty. It is the cheapest complete breakfast on earth and among the most beloved: a bowl of ful and bread will carry a person to mid-afternoon with opinions.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","earthy","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ful%20Medames%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ful Medames"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Ful Medames","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Ful%20Medames","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lemon","Parsley","Bread","White beans","Tahini","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"jian-bing","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Batter: 60g mung bean flour + 60g wheat flour + 30g millet or rice flour, ~250ml water, thin as heavy cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Per crepe: 1 egg; hoisin + fermented bean paste thinned with water; chili sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions, cilantro, black sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"The crunch heart: bao cui (a fried cracker sheet), or a big handful of crushed wonton crisps, the home substitute"},{"kind":"item","text":"A wide flat nonstick pan and an offset spatula playing the role of the street griddle and rake"}],"steps":[{"text":"Ladle batter onto the medium-hot pan and spread thin in a spiral with the back of the ladle, the street-corner rake move.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack an egg straight onto the crepe; scramble-spread it across the surface; sesame and scallions into the wet egg; flip the whole crepe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Paint the cooked side with the bean sauce and chili; land the cracker sheet in the middle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the sides over the crunch, fold in half, fold again; slide into a bag or onto a plate. Eat walking, ideally toward a subway.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beijing's breakfast of record, made in under two minutes by street masters whose ladle-spiral is muscle memory measured in decades; the egg is cooked ON the crepe, not beside it, and the buried cracker is the engineering: soft, saucy, then a shattering center. A jian bing without the crunch is a crepe with regrets; guard the crisp until the moment of folding.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking","Crêpes","Fermentation","Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","nutty-toasted","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Low & slow smoking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Crêpes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=crepe%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jian%20Bing%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jian Bing"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Jian Bing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Jian%20Bing","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"whole-muscle-vs-fermented-salami-the-two-churches","term":"Whole-Muscle vs. Fermented Salami: The Two Churches"},{"slug":"grills-and-smokers-kettle-kamado-offset-pellet","term":"Grills & Smokers: Kettle, Kamado, Offset, Pellet"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Scallion","Chiles","Cilantro","Cream","Flour","Sesame"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking","griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"masala-dosa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Batter (start 2 days out): 300g idli rice + 100g urad dal + 1 tsp fenugreek seeds: soaked separately, ground silky, mixed and fermented overnight until bubbly and faintly sour"},{"kind":"item","text":"Potato masala: 600g potatoes boiled and crushed, 1 onion, mustard seeds, curry leaves, turmeric, green chili, ginger, tempered in oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ghee or oil for the pan; a flat dosa tawa or wide nonstick"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coconut chutney: fresh coconut, green chili, ginger, tempered mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sambar if the morning is ambitious; this is a project with a payoff"}],"steps":[{"text":"Ferment the ground batter overnight in a warm spot: it should double and smell like a bakery with opinions; salt after fermenting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make the masala: bloom mustard seeds and curry leaves in oil; turmeric, onion, chili; fold in the crushed potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle batter onto the hot greased tawa; spread in expanding spirals to paper-thin; drizzle ghee at the edges: it lifts and lace-crisps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"When deep gold and crisp, lay a bar of masala across; fold into a cylinder or triangle; serve at once with chutney. Repeat until the batter or the family surrenders.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"South India's fermented masterpiece: the overnight wild fermentation is what gives dosa its sour depth and its lacework crispness; no shortcut batter has ever fooled a grandmother from Karnataka. The spiral spread is a learned hand (your first three are the cook's tax), and the ghee at the edge is the difference between a crepe and a dosa that crackles like paper when folded.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Crêpes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=crepe%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Masala%20Dosa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Masala Dosa"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Masala Dosa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Masala%20Dosa","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Potato","Chiles","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Rice","Lentils","Mustard","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"diner-buttermilk-pancakes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour; 2 tbsp sugar; 1 tsp baking powder + ½ tsp baking soda; ½ tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml buttermilk; 2 eggs; 40g melted butter; 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"More butter for the griddle and an indecent pat for the stack"},{"kind":"item","text":"Real maple syrup, warmed: the imitation stuff is a separate hobby"},{"kind":"item","text":"A griddle at a steady, patient medium"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk dry; whisk wet; combine with TEN LAZY STROKES maximum: lumps are the leavening's life insurance; smooth batter makes rubber.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 5 min; the batter will thicken and bubble on its own.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle onto the buttered griddle; flip ONCE, when bubbles pierce the surface and stay open: the pancake announces itself.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Never press the flipped cake. Stack three; butter between the floors; syrup poured with commitment.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The buttermilk pancake is American diner scripture, and every rule is anti-gluten: barely mix, rest, one flip, no pressing. The soda-buttermilk reaction gives the tang and the lift; the open bubbles are the flip signal older than any timer. A pressed pancake sighs out its steam and dies flat: the spatula is for turning, not for enforcement.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Diner%20Buttermilk%20Pancakes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Diner Buttermilk Pancakes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Diner Buttermilk Pancakes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Diner%20Buttermilk%20Pancakes","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"insurance-the-policy-stack","term":"Insurance: The Policy Stack"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"gaufres-de-liege","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"375g flour; 7g yeast; 140ml warm milk; 2 eggs; pinch of salt; 1 tbsp vanilla sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g soft butter, kneaded in: this is a brioche pretending to be a waffle"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g PEARL SUGAR, folded in last: the entire identity; regular sugar is not a substitute, it is a different food"},{"kind":"item","text":"A waffle iron, preferably sturdy; the sugar will caramelize and test it"},{"kind":"item","text":"No toppings required: the waffle IS the topping"}],"steps":[{"text":"Yeasted dough (not batter, dough); rise 1 h until puffed.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Knead in the soft butter, then fold in the pearl sugar nuggets; divide into 8 balls; rest 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in a medium-hot iron 3–4 min: the pearl sugar melts and caramelizes into glassy pockets and a lacquered shell.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool 2 min: the caramel is molten jewelry. Eat warm from the hand, street-corner style; no plate, no fork, no improvements.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Liège waffle is dough, not batter: a brioche studded with pearl sugar that caramelizes in the iron into crunchy amber pockets, allegedly born when a prince-bishop's cook baked sugar into the waffle itself. The Brussels waffle (light, crisp, rectangular, topped) is its formal cousin; the Liège is the one Belgians actually eat walking. Clean the iron while warm, and forgive it: caramel is the cost of doing business.","equipment":["Waffle iron"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gaufres%20de%20Li%C3%A8ge%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gaufres de Liège"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Gaufres de Liège","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Gaufres%20de%20Li%C3%A8ge","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"banh-mi-op-la","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs per skillet: ốp la means sunny-side, and the dish arrives in the pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small crusty baguette per person, warmed to shatter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter or oil; 3 spring onions, sliced; a few slices of pâté or chả lụa if the pantry is Vietnamese today"},{"kind":"item","text":"Maggi seasoning sauce (the bottle is the soul of this breakfast) plus soy and chili sauce on the table"},{"kind":"item","text":"Quick pickled carrot-daikon and cucumber spears, optional but advised"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat a small skillet (the one you will serve in); butter; crack in the eggs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook gently until the whites set glossy and the yolks tremble; scatter spring onions into the whites in the last minute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the baguette until the crust crackles; add pâté alongside in the pan if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the skillet on a trivet: Maggi shaken over the eggs at the table, bread torn and dragged through yolk and seasoning until the pan is polished.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Saigon's sidewalk breakfast: eggs served in the little pan they were cooked in, a hot baguette, and the ceremonial shake of Maggi, the French colonial bottle Vietnam adopted and never returned. The bread-drag through Maggi-stained yolk is the entire dish; cutlery is available and beside the point. It is the bánh mì sandwich deconstructed into its happiest form.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=B%C3%A1nh%20M%C3%AC%20%E1%BB%90p%20La%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bánh Mì Ốp La"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Bánh Mì Ốp La","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20B%C3%A1nh%20M%C3%AC%20%E1%BB%90p%20La","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pate-and-terrine","term":"Pâté & Terrine"},{"slug":"pate-en-croute","term":"Pâté en Croûte"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Carrot","Chiles","Cucumber","Radish/Turnip","Butter","Bread","Pickles"]},{"slug":"manoushe-zaatar","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 400g flour, 7g yeast, 1 tsp sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm water, 2 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"The paint: 6 tbsp za'atar (wild thyme, sumac, toasted sesame) whisked with 8 tbsp olive oil to a loose slurry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional second act: akkawi or mozzarella for a cheese man'oushe, or both halves (cocktail style)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fresh sides for the fold: tomato, cucumber, mint leaves, olives, labneh"},{"kind":"item","text":"A baking steel at maximum heat, playing the role of the saj dome"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soft dough; rise 1 h; divide into 6 balls; rest 15 min.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press and stretch each into a thin round, leaving a modest rim; dimple the center with fingertips so the topping pools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the za'atar-oil slurry generously edge to edge of the dimpled field.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake on the screaming steel 4–5 min until the rim blisters. Fold around mint, tomato and cucumber, and eat standing, or roll it for the walk to school.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lebanon's morning flag: bakeries (forn) open before dawn and the whole neighborhood carries man'oushe home or eats it folded on the corner; children take it rolled in paper as the original packed lunch. The za'atar-to-oil ratio should look excessive and taste correct: the herb blend is tangy from sumac and nutty from sesame, and a pale, timid man'oushe is a flatbread that lost its nationality.","equipment":["Pizza steel"],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","nutty-toasted","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Man'oushe%20Za'atar%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Man'oushe Za'atar"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Man'oushe Za'atar","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Man'oushe%20Za'atar","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"akkawi-and-nabulsi","term":"Akkawi & Nabulsi"},{"slug":"labneh","term":"Labneh"},{"slug":"zaatar-the-blend","term":"Za'atar (The Blend)"}],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Cucumber","Olives","Mint","Thyme","Yogurt","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Flour","Sesame","Sugar","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"bircher-muesli","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100g rolled oats, soaked OVERNIGHT; this is the original overnight oats, with a century of seniority"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml milk (or half milk, half apple juice: the sanatorium original) + 150g yogurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 apples, grated at serving time, SKIN ON, straight into the bowl"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice; 1 tbsp honey; 40g chopped hazelnuts or almonds, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Seasonal fruit and berries over the top; cream if the doctor is not looking"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the oats in the milk (and juice, if going classical) overnight in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"In the morning: stir in yogurt, lemon and honey: the mixture should be loose and spoonable, not spackle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the apples directly into the bowl at the last minute and fold: the lemon keeps them bright.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Nuts and fruit over; serve cold. It keeps three days, improving in texture and losing only its apple freshness, hence the last-minute grate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dr. Bircher-Benner served this to patients at his Zurich sanatorium around 1900, apple-forward, oat-supported, and decades ahead of every overnight-oats trend that thinks it invented soaking. The fresh-grated apple at serving is the non-negotiable: pre-grated apple browns and sulks. The Swiss eat muesli for supper as often as breakfast, which is the quiet endorsement of a nation that reads labels.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","nutty-toasted","sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bircher%20Muesli%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bircher Muesli"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Bircher Muesli","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Bircher%20Muesli","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Apple","Berries","Milk","Cream","Yogurt","Bread","Oats","Honey","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"takoyaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Batter: 150g flour, 2 eggs, 500ml cold dashi, 1 tsp soy, thin, almost alarming"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cooked octopus, cut in bite chunks; pickled red ginger (beni shoga), minced; tenkasu (tempura crumbs); scallions"},{"kind":"item","text":"The takoyaki pan: half-sphere wells; the æbleskiver pan moonlights honorably here"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toppings in order: takoyaki sauce, Japanese mayo in stripes, aonori, and a snowfall of katsuobushi"},{"kind":"item","text":"Two thin skewers or picks, the turning tools of the trade"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat and oil every well generously; pour batter to the BRIM and then over it: flooding the flat top is correct, not sloppy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop octopus, ginger, tenkasu and scallion into each well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"When the edges set, cut the flooded batter into squares with your pick and TUCK the flaps in as you rotate each ball a quarter-turn: the overflow becomes the sphere.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep rolling until bronzed and crisp all over. Sauce, mayo stripes, aonori, katsuobushi: serve while the flakes still dance and the centers are still molten. Warn people. They never listen.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Osaka's street sphere: crisp shell, molten savory custard, a bite of octopus at the core, and the flood-and-tuck turn is the entire craft, same physics as the Danish æbleskiver a few chapters over. The bonito flakes waving on top are reacting to heat, not wind. First-timers always bite too soon; the burnt-mouth initiation is a documented Osaka tradition.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Takoyaki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Takoyaki"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cephalopods-squid-octopus-cuttlefish","term":"Cephalopods (Squid, Octopus, Cuttlefish)"}],"pantryItems":["Squid/Octopus","Eggs","Ginger","Scallion","Flour","Soy sauce","Stock/Broth","Mayonnaise","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["dashi"]},{"slug":"pani-puri","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"40 puri shells, semolina dough rolled thin, fried to hollow globes (or the store-bought box, used without shame and with better consistency)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The pani: big bunch mint + cilantro, green chili, ginger, blitzed with 750ml cold water, 2 tbsp tamarind, 1 tsp chaat masala, black salt (kala namak, the sulfurous soul)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filling: boiled potato cubes, boiled chickpeas or sprouted moong, minced onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sweet tamarind-date chutney"},{"kind":"item","text":"Assembly-line speed; pani puri is eaten at the pace it is made"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill the spiced green water thoroughly: pani puri means water-bread, and the water must be COLD, spicy, sour and faintly funky from the black salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the potato-chickpea filling; keep the sweet chutney beside it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thumb-crack the top of each puri; a spoon of filling, a drip of sweet chutney.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dunk the whole shell in the pani, lift dripping, and eat in ONE BITE, immediately: hesitation is structural failure. Repeat until someone declares a winner.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"India's greatest one-bite: crisp shell, warm filling, sweet chutney and ice-cold spiced water detonating in strict sequence: golgappa in Delhi, phuchka in Kolkata, pani puri in Mumbai, and the vendor's speed is part of the recipe (he cracks, fills and hands them faster than you can eat). The one-bite rule is physics, not etiquette: a bitten puri empties itself down your wrist.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pani%20Puri%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pani Puri"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chaat-masala","term":"Chaat Masala"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Potato","Chiles","Dried fruit","Cilantro","Mint","Bread","Chickpeas","Semolina","Tamarind","Curry powder"]},{"slug":"koshari","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g brown lentils; 200g rice; 150g small macaroni or broken vermicelli: the three-carb foundation, cooked separately"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 can chickpeas, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"The sauce: 500g crushed tomatoes, 4 garlic cloves, 1 tbsp vinegar, 1 tsp cumin, chili to taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE CROWN: 3 onions, sliced thin, dusted in flour, fried patiently to dark crisp tangles: make double; half will disappear"},{"kind":"item","text":"Da'a: garlic-vinegar-cumin dressing (4 tbsp vinegar, 2 garlic, 1 tsp cumin, splash of water) for the table"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook lentils, rice and pasta each to its own doneness: the discipline of the dish is respecting three textures.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the spiced tomato sauce 15 min; sharpen with vinegar.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion tangles low and patient to mahogany crunch; salt them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer in bowls: rice, lentils, pasta, chickpeas, sauce ladled over, onions piled recklessly on top; da'a shaken over at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cairo's working lunch: carbs three ways under spiced tomato, born of Egyptian, Italian and Indian threads braided in one bowl, sold from shops that serve nothing else and never need to. The fried onions are not garnish, they are the argument; and the da'a (garlic-vinegar) is the pivot that keeps three starches tasting bright. Egypt's cheapest full meal and one of its proudest.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","bright","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, acid holding everything up to the light, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Koshari%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Koshari"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Pasta","Noodles","Flour","Lentils","Chickpeas","Vinegar","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"pupusas-con-curtido","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g masa harina + ~500ml warm water + ½ tsp salt: a soft, pliable masa, wetter than tortilla dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fillings: grated quesillo or mozzarella; refried beans; chicharrón paste, or all three (revueltas)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Curtido, made FIRST: ½ cabbage shredded, 1 carrot grated, ½ onion, oregano, brined in hot water + vinegar + salt, resting minimum 2 h (overnight is respect)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salsa roja: blended tomato, simmered thin and mild"},{"kind":"item","text":"A hot comal and a small bowl of oiled water for your palms"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the curtido first: pupusas without curtido are unaccompanied minors.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ball the masa; press a well with your thumb; pack in filling; close the masa over and pat between oiled palms into a thick disc, rotating; leaks are patched with a pinch of masa, not mourned.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a medium-hot comal 4–5 min a side until freckled brown and puffed slightly; the cheese should threaten the edges.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve hot, curtido piled on top or beside, salsa spooned over. Eat by hand, tearing, in the pupusería tradition.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"El Salvador's national dish, so national there is a legislated Pupusa Day (second Sunday of November), and the vendor's palm-slap rhythm is the street's soundtrack. The curtido is the load-bearing condiment: its fermented crunch and vinegar cut the griddled cheese perfectly. Cheese escaping and lacing crisp on the comal is not a mistake; that fried-cheese skirt is the connoisseur's corner.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Working with masa","Tortillas on the comal","Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pupusas%20con%20Curtido%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pupusas con Curtido"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"resting-carryover-and-the-thermometer","term":"Resting, Carryover & the Thermometer"},{"slug":"queso-oaxaca-quesillo","term":"Queso Oaxaca (Quesillo)"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Cabbage","Corn","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Bread","Cornmeal/Polenta","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","tortillas-on-the-comal"]},{"slug":"tamales-rojos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g masa harina for tamales (coarser grind) beaten with 200g lard or softened butter, 1 tsp baking powder, salt, and ~400ml warm broth, until a spoonful of masa FLOATS in water; that is the test"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filling: 600g braised pork or chicken, shredded into salsa roja (guajillo + ancho chiles, garlic, cumin, blended and simmered)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 dried corn husks, soaked pliable in hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"A steamer pot with coins in the bottom water, the old rattle alarm"},{"kind":"item","text":"Patience and, traditionally, several relatives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the masa with the fat until genuinely fluffy: the float test is pass/fail and non-negotiable; a sinking masa steams into a brick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread masa on the smooth side of each husk in a thin rectangle; a stripe of the red-sauced meat down the center.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold sides over, fold the tail up; stack UPRIGHT, open ends up, packed snugly in the steamer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam 75–90 min. The coins rattle while there is water; silence means refill. Done when the tamal releases cleanly from the husk. Rest 15 min; they set as they stand.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":6300,"estimated":true}],"note":"The tamalada, the assembly-line family tamale day, is the real recipe: one person spreads, one fills, one folds, everyone talks. Masa that floats is aerated enough to steam light; the coin-in-the-pot alarm is grandmother engineering; and the husk releasing cleanly is the only doneness test that matters. Tamales freeze beautifully, which is why no one has ever made a small batch.","equipment":["Steamer"],"techniques":["Working with masa","Braising","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamales%20Rojos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tamales Rojos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Butter","Cornmeal/Polenta","Stock/Broth","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","braising"]},{"slug":"arancini-al-ragu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Risotto base, made and CHILLED overnight: 400g arborio cooked with saffron, butter and parmesan: yesterday's risotto is the origin story and the best version"},{"kind":"item","text":"Heart: 200g thick beef ragù with peas, cold and scoopable; cubes of mozzarella or caciocavallo"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coating: flour paste (flour + water, crepe-thin) or flour-egg, then fine breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying at 170°C"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges; a paper cone if you are doing it properly"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wet your hands; flatten a palm of cold saffron rice; pocket a spoon of ragù and a cheese cube; close into a tight ball or the Catania cone shape.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill the formed arancini 30 min: cold centers survive the fryer.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Coat: through the flour slurry, then rolled firmly in breadcrumbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 170°C, 4–5 min to deep orange-gold. Rest 3 min: the name means 'little oranges' and the center is molten citrus-colored lava. Lemon over; eat standing.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":false}],"note":"Sicily's portable risotto: round in Palermo, pointed like Etna in Catania, and the east-west 'arancino vs arancina' gender war is fought annually with genuine passion. The overnight-chilled rice is structural: fresh risotto collapses; day-old rice, its starch set, holds the sphere. Bar counters across the island sell them at ten in the morning, and no one has ever needed a reason.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Risotto method","Crêpes","Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Risotto method","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=risotto%20technique%20mantecatura","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Crêpes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=crepe%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arancini%20al%20Rag%C3%B9%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arancini al Ragù"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fryer-setups-and-oil-management","term":"Fryer Setups & Oil Management"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Peas","Lemon","Butter","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Mozzarella","Rice","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"churros-con-chocolate","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 250ml water, 40g butter, pinch salt, 1 tbsp sugar, boiled; 150g flour beaten in off heat; 1 egg optional (Spain mostly says no; the egg makes them softer, the eggless are crunchier)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A piping bag with a large STAR tip: the ridges are function, not decoration"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying at 180°C; scissors for cutting the ropes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cinnamon sugar for rolling (the Latin American road) or plain sugar (the Madrid road)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The chocolate: 200g dark chocolate melted into 400ml milk thickened with 1 tsp cornstarch, spoon-coating, almost pudding"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the panade until it pulls from the pan in a ball; cool 5 min; beat smooth (with the egg if using).","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe ropes directly into the hot oil, cutting with scissors: loops, sticks, or the Madrid lazo spiral.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry 3–4 min, turning, to even deep gold; the ridges crisp first and protect the tender middle.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain; roll in sugar while hot; serve with the thick chocolate standing upright enough to embarrass coffee. Dunk deep. This is breakfast in Madrid and dessert everywhere else.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The star tip is the physics of the churro: ridges multiply surface area so the outside crisps before the inside overcooks: pipe them smooth and they can burst in the oil. Madrid eats churros con chocolate at dawn after a long night (the churrerías open when the bars close), and the chocolate is legally distinct from drinking cocoa: it must hold the churro upright for a beat when planted.","equipment":["Piping bag","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Churros"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Churros","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=churros%20piping%20frying%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Churros%20con%20Chocolate%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Churros con Chocolate"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Cinnamon"]},{"slug":"hotteok","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 300g flour + 50g glutinous rice flour (the chew insurance), 7g yeast, 1 tbsp sugar, 1 tsp salt, 220ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"The molten filling: 100g brown sugar + 2 tbsp chopped walnuts and peanuts + 1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for the pan, a generous slick"},{"kind":"item","text":"The hotteok press, or any flat-bottomed cup, oiled"},{"kind":"item","text":"Napkins and restraint you will not use"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soft, sticky dough; rise 1 h until doubled and lazy.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil your hands; flatten a portion on your palm; spoon in the sugar-nut mix; gather and pinch closed like a soup dumpling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seam-down into the oiled pan; 30 seconds; flip and PRESS flat and wide with the oiled press, the signature move; the filling spreads into a molten disc.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry 2–3 min a side to deep gold. Cool ONE full minute: the interior is cinnamon magma with a nut aggregate. Serve in a paper cup, folded, street style.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Korea's winter street heater: vendors press and flip in a hypnotic rhythm, and the queue is longest when the wind is worst. The rice flour is the chew; the press is the craft (it thins the dough so the sugar melts fully and laces to the edges); and the molten-center burn is such a known hazard that vendors hand hotteok over pre-folded in a cup, seam up, with a look that says wait.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","nutty-toasted","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hotteok%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hotteok"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Rice","Flour","Peanut","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"banh-xeo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Batter: 200g rice flour + 1 tbsp cornstarch, 1 tsp turmeric (the sizzle is the name, the yellow is the flag), 400ml water + 100ml coconut milk, scallions sliced in"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filling: 200g shrimp, 150g thin pork slices, 2 handfuls bean sprouts"},{"kind":"item","text":"A mountain of lettuce leaves, mustard greens, mint, perilla, the wrapping garden"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nuoc cham for dunking (the Vietnamese shelf has the recipe)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A wide nonstick or carbon steel pan, hot, and oil at the ready"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rest the batter 30 min: it should be thin as light cream; thick batter makes a pancake, thin makes a shatter.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear a few shrimp and pork slices in the oiled pan; ladle in a THIN swirl of batter, tilting to lace the edges; sprouts on one half; LID ON 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lid off; drizzle oil at the rim; cook until the edges lift, lace and audibly crisp: the xèo (sizzle) should be constant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold; slide out. To eat: tear a piece, bundle it in lettuce with herbs, dunk in nuoc cham. The wrap is not optional; it is the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bánh xèo is named for its own sound (xèo is the sizzle of batter hitting hot metal), and the crepe is judged entirely at the rim: lacy, blistered, shattering. The lid-then-open sequence steams the sprouts and then re-crisps the shell. Eating it unwrapped marks the tourist; the lettuce-herb bundle with the fish-sauce dunk is where the hot-cold, crisp-fresh architecture completes itself.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","rich","earthy","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, richness that coats and comforts, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Crêpes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=crepe%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=B%C3%A1nh%20X%C3%A8o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bánh Xèo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"carbon-steel-pans","term":"Carbon Steel Pans"},{"slug":"steel-science-carbon-stainless-and-hrc","term":"Steel Science: Carbon, Stainless & HRC"},{"slug":"vermouth-and-the-fortified-aperitif-shelf","term":"Vermouth & the Fortified Aperitif Shelf"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Shrimp","Scallion","Lettuce/Greens","Coconut","Bean sprouts","Mint","Shiso","Milk","Coconut milk","Flour","Mustard","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"hane-tsuki-gyoza","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"30 round gyoza wrappers (store-bought is standard even in Japan; your energy belongs to the filling and the fry)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Filling: 300g ground pork, 150g cabbage salted-squeezed dry, 3 garlic cloves, thumb of ginger, 2 scallions, 1 tbsp soy + 1 tsp sesame oil + 1 tsp sake"},{"kind":"item","text":"The crisp lace (hane): 1 tsp flour whisked into 100ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dip: soy + rice vinegar 1:1, chili oil (rayu) to taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"A lidded nonstick skillet: the steam-fry demands both"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the chopped cabbage 10 min; wring it like laundry: water is the enemy of a juicy-not-soggy filling; knead with pork and aromatics until tacky.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"A teaspoon of filling per wrapper; wet the rim; pleat 5–7 folds on ONE side, pressing to the flat side: the crescent stands on its own seam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange standing in the oiled hot pan; fry 2 min to brown bottoms; pour in the flour-water; LID ON, steam 4–5 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lid off; let the water vanish and the flour set into a golden lace web connecting the dumplings. Flip the whole raft onto a plate, lace-side up. Dip; repeat; regret the batch size, always.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Japan took the Chinese jiaozi, moved the garlic to the front row, and perfected the yaki (steam-fry) method: brown, steam, crisp, in one pan, one sequence. The hane (wing), that shared lattice of crisped flour-water, is the modern flourish that turns a plate of dumplings into a single shatterable raft. Pleats on one side only is the tell of practiced hands, and thirty is famously never enough.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hane-tsuki%20Gyoza%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hane-tsuki Gyoza"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Street%20Food%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Street Food Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sake-fundamentals-the-polishing-ladder","term":"Sake Fundamentals: The Polishing Ladder"},{"slug":"sake-service-and-pairing","term":"Sake Service & Pairing"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Chiles","Cabbage","Flour","Wrappers","Sesame","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"the-cubano","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 loaf Cuban bread (soft, lard-enriched, thin-crusted, a sturdy soft baguette approximates)"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g mojo roast pork, sliced (leftover pernil is the sandwich's reason for existing)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g good sliced ham; 150g Swiss cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dill pickle slices, a full deck; yellow mustard: plain, ballpark yellow; this is canon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the press; a plancha, panini press, or a skillet plus a foil-wrapped brick"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the bread; mustard on both faces, no mayo; Tampa and Miami agree on almost nothing but they agree on this.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer: ham, mojo pork, Swiss, pickles; close.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter the outside; press under weight in a medium skillet, flipping once, 4–5 min a side.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"It is done when flattened to half height, the crust is gold glass, and the cheese has committed. Cut on a hard diagonal: the cut is traditional and non-negotiable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born in the cigar-factory lunchrooms of Tampa's Ybor City and perfected along Miami's Calle Ocho: the two cities still litigate custody (Tampa adds salami, Miami calls that a scandal). The press is the technique: compression fuses meat, cheese, pickle and mustard into a single laminated material. A cold unpressed cubano is just a ham sandwich with ambitions.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cubano%20recipe","sub":"Search films of The Cubano"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on The Cubano","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20The%20Cubano","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tiki-and-the-rum-canon","term":"Tiki & the Rum Canon"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Dill","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"the-reuben","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices real rye bread, seeded, sliced from a loaf with a crust"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corned beef, sliced thin and piled warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sauerkraut, squeezed ALMOST dry and warmed: wet kraut is the Reuben's only enemy"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices Swiss cheese; Russian dressing: mayo + ketchup + horseradish + relish + Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the griddle; a deli pickle spear standing guard"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the corned beef gently (a steam over simmering water is the deli way); squeeze and warm the kraut separately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress both inner faces of the bread; build: cheese against both breads, beef and kraut in the middle: the cheese walls waterproof the rye.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter the outsides; griddle over medium, pressing lightly, 3–4 min a side until the rye crisps and the Swiss slumps.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest one minute; cut on the diagonal; serve with the pickle and more dressing for dipping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Omaha and New York both claim the Reuben, and the dispute is unwinnable, which is fitting for a sandwich this good. The engineering is real: cheese on BOTH sides as a moisture barrier, kraut squeezed to surrender, everything warm before assembly: a Reuben built cold and grilled fast arrives soggy in the middle and burnt at the edges, and deserves its fate.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","charred","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Reuben%20recipe","sub":"Search films of The Reuben"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on The Reuben","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20The%20Reuben","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Cabbage","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mayonnaise","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"caesar-salad-table-side-rules","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 heads romaine, hearts and inner leaves, cold, dry, LEFT IN LARGE PIECES or whole leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"The dressing, built in the bowl: 2 garlic cloves, 4 anchovy fillets, 2 egg yolks (or coddled egg, the original), 1 tsp Dijon, juice of 1 lemon, dash Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil, streamed; 60g parmesan, half in, half over"},{"kind":"item","text":"Croutons: torn bread fried in olive oil and garlic until gold, salted hot"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper, with drama"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mash garlic and anchovy to paste against the bowl with a fork: the bowl is the mortar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Yolks, mustard, lemon, Worcestershire; whisk; stream in the oil to a loose emulsion; half the cheese in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the whole leaves through with your hands until every leaf is lacquered, not drowned.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Croutons, remaining parmesan shaved over, pepper cracked from a height. Serve immediately on cold plates; a Caesar waits for no one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented not in Rome but in Tijuana (Caesar Cardini, July 4th weekend 1924, a packed dining room and a raided pantry) and originally eaten by hand, whole leaves as scoops for the dressing. The anchovy was allegedly the later addition (Cardini used only Worcestershire), but the modern consensus is settled. Grilled chicken is a fine lunch and a historical footnote; the leaf, the emulsion and the crouton are the dish.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caesar%20Salad%20(Table-Side%20Rules)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Caesar Salad (Table-Side Rules)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Caesar Salad (Table-Side Rules)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Caesar%20Salad%20(Table-Side%20Rules)","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Eggs","Garlic","Lettuce/Greens","Lemon","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Bread","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"katsu-sando","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 thick pork loin chops (2.5cm), fat cap scored, pounded slightly even"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour, 1 beaten egg, PANKO: the coarse Japanese crumb is the architecture; fine crumbs need not apply"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices shokupan (Japanese milk bread, see The Bakery), crusts OFF, unapologetically soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tonkatsu sauce (or: ketchup + Worcestershire + oyster sauce + a little sugar, whisked); finely shredded cabbage, optional but classic"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot mustard, a thin swipe; oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the chops; flour, egg, panko. Press the panko on so it stands up shaggy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 170°C, 5–6 min, to deep gold and just-blushing at the center; rest 3 min on a rack; the crumbs must stay vertical.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Paint both cut faces of the katsu with sauce; mustard on the bread; cabbage down if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sandwich; press GENTLY under a plate 2 min; trim the edges square with a sharp knife and cut into neat thirds: the geometry is the presentation.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The katsu sando is a study in engineered contrast: shatteringly crisp panko against cloud-soft crustless milk bread, sweet-tangy sauce mediating. The edge-trim is not vanity: konbini and depachika counters sell them in perfect windowed boxes, and the clean cross-section is the genre's signature. Rest the katsu before building or the steam will sog the crumb from inside; the three-minute wait is the whole difference.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Scoring","Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Katsu%20Sando%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Katsu Sando"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Katsu Sando","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Katsu%20Sando","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Cabbage","Milk","Bread","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Oyster sauce","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["scoring","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"torta-ahogada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 birote rolls (Guadalajara's sour, hard-crusted torpedo: the crustiest sour roll you can find; it must survive drowning)"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g carnitas, warm and rough-chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Refried beans, a thin structural smear"},{"kind":"item","text":"The drowning sauce: 8 ripe tomatoes simmered with garlic, oregano and clove, blended and kept hot; plus the OPTIONAL widow-maker: chile de árbol salsa, fiercely hot"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pickled red onions with lime; napkins beyond reason"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the rolls keeping a hinge; smear beans; pack with warm carnitas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle the hot tomato sauce over the WHOLE sandwich: ahogada means drowned; half-drowned (media) is permitted for beginners.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the árbol salsa by the spoon according to your relationship with consequence.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crown with pickled onions; serve immediately in a shallow bowl with a fork and your sleeves rolled. Eat fast; the birote holds, but not forever.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Guadalajara's hangover institution and point of civic pride, the birote roll, sour and hard-crusted from the city's particular fermentation, is the only bread on earth that can be submerged in hot salsa and still fight back. Legend credits a vendor who dropped a torta in sauce and a customer who said serve it anyway. 'Bien ahogada' (fully drowned, full árbol) is a rite of passage with a countdown.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Confit"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","fiery","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, real fire that builds and stays, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Torta%20Ahogada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Torta Ahogada"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Torta Ahogada","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Torta%20Ahogada","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Oregano","Bread","Pickles","Clove"]},{"slug":"ploughmans-lunch","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"A wedge of proper farmhouse cheddar (aged, cloth-bound if the shop loves you) + a second cheese, stilton or a soft one, for the argument"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crusty bread or a cottage roll, thick butter; a hard-boiled egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Branston-style pickle (the sweet dark chutney) AND pickled onions, both; they do different jobs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ham off the bone, a few slices, optional; an apple, quartered; celery sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"A pint of bitter or strong tea, per conviction"}],"steps":[{"text":"There is no cooking; there is only assembly with respect: cheese at room temperature. Cold cheddar is muted cheddar; take it out an hour ahead.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange on a board with the pickle in a pot, onions beside, butter thick enough to show teeth marks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the egg 8 minutes if from scratch; halve it; salt it.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Build each bite by hand: bread, butter, cheese, a smear of pickle. The apple resets the palate; the celery pretends at virtue; the pint completes the scene.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The ploughman's is a pub menu invention polished into tradition: a marketing campaign from the 1950s cheese board that Britain adopted so thoroughly it now feels medieval, which is its own kind of authenticity. The rules that matter: room-temperature cheese, real chutney, and no cutlery ambition. It is lunch as landscape: everything visible, nothing hidden, assembled bite by bite at your own pace.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ploughman's%20Lunch%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ploughman's Lunch"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ploughman's Lunch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ploughman's%20Lunch","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"west-country-farmhouse-cheddar","term":"West Country Farmhouse Cheddar"},{"slug":"stilton","term":"Stilton"},{"slug":"aged-gouda","term":"Aged Gouda"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Celery","Apple","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Blue cheese","Bread","Pickles"]},{"slug":"pan-bagnat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 round crusty rolls or 1 country loaf: sturdy crumb; this bread has a job to do"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cans best olive-oil-packed tuna; 4 boiled eggs, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tomatoes, sliced and salted; cucumber, radishes, green pepper strips, red onion shaved"},{"kind":"item","text":"Niçoise olives, capers, basil leaves; NO mayonnaise: Nice has bylaws about this, nearly literally"},{"kind":"item","text":"Good olive oil and red wine vinegar, applied like weather"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the rolls; pull out a little crumb to make room; douse both faces with oil and a whisper of vinegar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer: tomatoes against the bread (their juice is the point), tuna, egg, vegetables, olives, capers, basil; oil again over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Close; wrap TIGHT in paper or film; weight under a plate and something heavy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 1 hour minimum, up to 4: pan bagnat means 'bathed bread'; the soak is the recipe. Unwrap, halve, eat outdoors, ideally within sight of water.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is the salade niçoise (three shelves over, in the French chapter) engineered for the beach, and the pressing rest is the entire dish: the tomato juice and oil migrate into the crumb until sandwich and dressing become one object. A pan bagnat eaten fresh-made is an error of impatience; Nice's guardians of the recipe (a real association) permit no mayonnaise, no cooked vegetables, and no shortcuts on the bath.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pan%20Bagnat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pan Bagnat"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Pan Bagnat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Pan%20Bagnat","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tuna","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Cucumber","Radish/Turnip","Olives","Capers","Basil","Bread","Vinegar","Wine","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"sabich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 fresh pitas with proper pockets"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 aubergines, sliced in 1cm rounds, salted 20 min, fried in olive oil to bronze silk (the heart of the dish)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, hard-boiled, traditionally haminados: simmered hours to caramel-brown (a shortcut: boil with onion skins and a splash of coffee)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Israeli salad (tomato-cucumber, fine dice), hummus or tahini sauce, amba (pickled mango sauce, the Iraqi signature), zhug for heat"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pickles, chopped parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the drained aubergine rounds unhurried until deeply golden and creamy inside; drain on a rack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the eggs your chosen way; slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the pitas; swipe hummus or tahini inside the pocket.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer: aubergine, egg, salad, pickles; sauce with tahini, streak with amba, dot with zhug. Eat over a plate, leaning: sabich drips by design.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brought to Israel by Iraqi Jews: this was the traditional Shabbat morning spread (fried aubergine, slow eggs, amba, all prepared ahead) folded into a pita and sold from a Ramat Gan stand in the 1960s. The amba is the fingerprint: fenugreek-funky pickled mango that no substitute touches. Sabich is the falafel's worldlier cousin, and in Tel Aviv the partisans of each maintain diplomatic but firm positions.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","bright","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Falafel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=falafel%20frying%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sabich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sabich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sabich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sabich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mango-the-monsoon-herald","term":"Mango: The Monsoon Herald"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Eggplant","Cucumber","Mango","Parsley","Bread","Tahini","Pickles","Coffee/Espresso"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"vada-pav","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 pav (soft square dinner rolls), pillowy, split with a hinge"},{"kind":"item","text":"The vada: 500g potatoes boiled and crushed, tempered with mustard seeds, curry leaves, turmeric, green chili, ginger, asafoetida; rolled into 8 balls"},{"kind":"item","text":"Batter: 150g chickpea flour (besan), pinch turmeric and soda, water to a thick coat"},{"kind":"item","text":"The chutneys, all three: dry garlic-peanut-chili powder (the signature), green chutney, sweet tamarind"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fried green chilies, salted, on the side; oil for deep frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Temper the spices in oil; fold into the crushed potato; cool and ball.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip each ball in besan batter; fry at 175°C to deep gold, 3–4 min: the shell should be thin and crisp, the inside steamy.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the split pav briefly, cut-face down, with a little butter if you like.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smear green and tamarind chutney inside, dust the dry garlic chutney generously, seat the hot vada, press gently. Fried chili on the side. Eat in four bites, standing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mumbai's beating heart, the 'Bombay burger', born outside Dadar station in 1966 to feed mill workers fast and cheap, and now the city's food in the way pizza is New York's. The dry garlic chutney is the non-negotiable: coarse, red, fierce, it seasons every bite the wet chutneys miss. A vada pav costs pocket change, takes two hands, and at rush hour the vendor's rhythm is public theater.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","earthy","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vada%20Pav%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vada Pav"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Vada Pav","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Vada%20Pav","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Ginger","Potato","Chiles","Curry/Makrut leaves","Bread","Flour","Chickpeas","Peanut","Mustard","Tamarind","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"bun-thit-nuong-lunch-atlas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork shoulder, sliced thin; marinade: lemongrass minced fine, garlic, shallot, fish sauce, sugar, honey, pepper, a spoon of oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g rice vermicelli (bún), cooked, rinsed cool, drained in loose nests"},{"kind":"item","text":"The salad floor: lettuce ribbons, cucumber matchsticks, bean sprouts, mint, perilla, cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nuoc cham, a generous cup (the Vietnamese shelf holds the formula); crushed roasted peanuts, fried shallots, pickled carrot-daikon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional upgrade: a crisp fried spring roll per bowl, sliced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the pork minimum 2 h: the sugar and honey are for the char as much as the flavor.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill over coals or sear in a screaming pan in a single layer until the edges caramelize and catch, 2–3 min a side.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build bowls cool-to-hot: herbs and salad at the bottom, noodle nests, warm pork and its juices on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peanuts, shallots, pickles over; nuoc cham poured around the edges at the table. TOSS before eating: the bowl arrives as strata and is eaten as a unified whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The genius of the Vietnamese noodle bowl is thermal: cool noodles, cold herbs, hot charred pork, room-temperature sauce: every temperature present in every bite after the toss. The caramelized lemongrass char is the flavor engine (a grill pan works; actual coals convert). The toss is not optional: an untossed bún bowl is a photograph; a tossed one is lunch.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","charred","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, char used as punctuation, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=B%C3%BAn%20Th%E1%BB%8Bt%20N%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Bng%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bún Thịt Nướng"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Bún Thịt Nướng","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20B%C3%BAn%20Th%E1%BB%8Bt%20N%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Bng","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"vermouth-and-the-fortified-aperitif-shelf","term":"Vermouth & the Fortified Aperitif Shelf"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Shallot","Carrot","Cucumber","Lettuce/Greens","Radish/Turnip","Bean sprouts","Cilantro","Mint","Lemongrass","Shiso","Noodles","Bread","Fish sauce","Peanut","Honey","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"birria-de-res-con-consome","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg beef chuck + short rib (or the traditional goat), big chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"The adobo: 6 guajillo + 3 ancho + 2 chipotle chiles, toasted and soaked; 4 garlic, ½ onion, 1 tbsp vinegar, cumin, oregano, clove, cinnamon, blended smooth"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, 1L stock or water; salt boldly"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the tacos: corn tortillas, melting cheese, minced onion + cilantro, lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"The pot's own consomé, skimmed, served alongside in cups: the dish is a soup AND its own condiment"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the adobo; strain it; marinate the beef in it overnight if you can.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear the meat; pour over the adobo and stock; braise covered at 150°C or a bare simmer 3–3.5 h until it shreds at a glance.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":12600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shred the meat; skim the consomé and keep it hot: the red fat on top is treasure, not waste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For quesabirria: dip tortillas IN the fatty consomé, griddle, fill with meat and cheese, fold, crisp both sides. Serve with cups of consomé for dunking, onion-cilantro, lime.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jalisco's wedding stew that conquered the world as a taco: birria means something like 'a glorious mess,' and the consomé-dipped, griddle-crisped tortilla is why the internet lost its mind. The technique to keep: the braising liquid is dip, sauce, soup and frying medium all at once. Nothing is discarded; the red-stained fingers are the receipt.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Griddle & plancha work","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Birria%20de%20Res%20con%20Consom%C3%A9%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Birria de Res con Consomé"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Birria de Res con Consomé","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Birria%20de%20Res%20con%20Consom%C3%A9","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Lime","Cilantro","Oregano","Bay leaf","Cheese (any)","Bread","Vinegar","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Cinnamon","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"birria-de-res-con-consome","marks":[{"id":"birria-de-res-con-consome#distinct","text":"In the cup, a distinct red-orange fat cap sits on a deep russet broth. Grey fat means the chiles gave up nothing."},{"id":"birria-de-res-con-consome#consom","text":"The consomé is smooth on the tongue, with no chile-skin fragments and no grit settling in the bottom of the cup."},{"id":"birria-de-res-con-consome#meat","text":"The meat falls apart under a single fork with no pull, and a shred pulled open is wet through rather than dry and cottony at the core. Shredded back into the pot it goes red throughout, and that colour comes from the tossing, not from waiting for a marinade to reach the middle of a chunk."},{"id":"birria-de-res-con-consome#dipped","text":"A dipped and griddled tortilla is stained edge to edge, crisp on both faces, and folds without cracking along the crease; torn open, the cheese pulls in strings."},{"id":"birria-de-res-con-consome#taste","text":"Taste the consomé from a cup rather than off a spoon at the pot: it should be seasoned to be drunk on its own. Lime adds lift to it and should never be needed to rescue it."}],"fault":"Burnt-coffee bitterness through the whole pot, because the chiles went a few seconds past fragrant on the comal and blistered black. Three hours of braising deepens that bitterness rather than burying it, and neither salt nor lime lifts it out."}},{"slug":"bouillabaisse","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg mixed rockfish and firm fish (red mullet, monkfish, sea bass, gurnard, variety IS the recipe), plus heads and bones for the base"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 leeks, 1 fennel bulb, 4 tomatoes, 6 garlic cloves, orange zest strip, big pinch saffron, thyme, bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml olive oil; 100ml pastis, optional and traditional"},{"kind":"item","text":"The rouille: garlic, saffron, egg yolk, olive oil, a soaked bread slice, cayenne, a rust-colored mayonnaise with intent"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toasted baguette slices rubbed with garlic; the broth and the fish served as TWO courses, Marseille rules"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the base: sweat leeks, fennel, tomato and garlic in olive oil; add fish heads/bones, saffron, zest, herbs, water to cover; boil HARD 25 min: bouillabaisse is the rare stock you boil violently; the oil emulsifies and the broth turns cloudy gold on purpose.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain, pressing every drop; season; splash of pastis.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach the fish in the broth in order of firmness, minutes each: monkfish first, delicate last.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve course one: broth over garlic toasts spread with rouille. Course two: the fish, moistened with more broth. Argue about which course is better; that is also traditional.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Marseille codified this fisherman's pot with an actual charter (the Charte de la Bouillabaisse) dictating the fish and the two-course service, and the violent boil is the founding technique: bouillir + abaisser, boil then settle, emulsifying olive oil into the saffron broth. The rouille is not garnish; it is the saffron mayonnaise that finishes every spoonful. No lobster necessary; the rockfish variety does the talking.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","aromatic","briny","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, aromatics blooming from the first bite, melting away silken."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bouillabaisse%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bouillabaisse"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Bouillabaisse","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Bouillabaisse","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Eggs","Garlic","Leek","Tomato","Chiles","Fennel","Orange","Thyme","Bread","Stock/Broth","Mayonnaise","Clove","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"sancocho-de-siete-carnes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"A parade of meats, pick at least three (the Dominican grand version uses seven): beef shank, pork shoulder, chicken thighs, smoked bones, longaniza"},{"kind":"item","text":"The root cellar: yuca, ñame or taro, green plantain AND ripe plantain, corn cobs in rounds, auyama (kabocha-style squash)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sofrito: onion, garlic, bell pepper, cilantro, oregano; a spoon of bitter orange or lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5L water or light stock; salt in stages"},{"kind":"item","text":"White rice, avocado slices and hot sauce alongside: the mandatory trinity"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the meats with the sofrito; brown the sturdiest ones; add water and simmer the toughest first, adding the rest by tenderness rank over the first hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roots in by hardness: yuca and ñame first, plantains and corn next, squash last.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer until the squash and some yuca dissolve, thickening the broth to a rich gold: 45 min more; adjust salt and acid.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve in deep bowls: broth, meats and roots together, rice and avocado on the side, each eater composing bites of soup, rice and avocado in rotation.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Every Latin country has a sancocho and every one insists theirs is the reference: the Dominican siete carnes is the celebration flagship, cooked in pots that assume a crowd. The engineering is the staggered entry: seven meats and six roots, each landing at its own tenderness. The dissolved squash and yuca ARE the thickener, and the avocado-rice sidecar isn't garnish: it is the second half of the dish.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sancocho%20de%20Siete%20Carnes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sancocho de Siete Carnes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Sancocho de Siete Carnes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Sancocho%20de%20Siete%20Carnes","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"shank-oxtail-and-cheek","term":"Shank, Oxtail & Cheek"},{"slug":"mirepoix-trinity-sofrito-and-friends","term":"Mirepoix, Trinity, Sofrito & Friends"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Bell pepper","Squash/Pumpkin","Corn","Avocado","Plantain","Lime","Orange","Cilantro","Oregano","Rice","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"ajiaco-santafereno","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg chicken breasts and thighs, bone-in"},{"kind":"item","text":"The three-potato law: 400g papas criollas (small yellow: frozen imports work; they DISSOLVE into the broth), 400g waxy red, 400g floury white: each does a different job"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 corn cobs, cut in rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"Guascas, the herb that IS ajiaco (dried, from a Latin market; oregano-ish but its own thing entirely)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The service: capers, thick cream (crema), avocado, white rice: added at the table, each spoonful composed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chicken with the white floury potatoes and half the criollas 40 min: the dissolvers start immediately.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift out the chicken; shred; keep warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add red potatoes, corn and guascas; simmer 30 min more until the broth turns thick, golden and faintly herbal: the criollas should have vanished into velvet.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the chicken; serve with the caper-cream-avocado ritual: a spoon of capers and cream stirred into each bowl, avocado alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bogotá's cold-plateau signature: a soup thickened by nothing but its own dissolving yellow potatoes, perfumed with guascas, a herb so tied to the dish that its absence renames the soup. The three potatoes each hold a role: one melts, one holds, one mediates. And the capers-and-cream stirred in at the table are the Andean finish that turns a chicken-potato soup into a capital city's identity.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ajiaco%20Santafere%C3%B1o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ajiaco Santafereño"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Ajiaco Santafereño","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Ajiaco%20Santafere%C3%B1o","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"feasibility-and-market-analysis","term":"Feasibility & Market Analysis"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Potato","Corn","Avocado","Capers","Oregano","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Stock/Broth"]},{"slug":"hot-and-sour-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2L good chicken stock; 150g pork loin in fine shreds (or leave it out and lean on mushrooms)"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 dried shiitake, soaked and sliced (liquid reserved into the pot); 50g wood ear fungus, soaked, slivered; ½ block firm tofu in matchsticks; bamboo shoot slivers"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE HOT: 1–2 tsp ground WHITE pepper: the heat here is pepper, not chili. THE SOUR: 4–5 tbsp Chinkiang black vinegar, added at the end"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp soy; 3 tbsp cornstarch slurry; 2 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sesame oil, scallions, more white pepper at the table"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer stock with mushrooms, their soaking liquor, pork, tofu and bamboo, 10 min; season with soy.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thicken with the slurry to a glossy, spoon-coating body: hot and sour soup should have weight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stream in the beaten egg in a thin ribbon while stirring SLOWLY in one direction, wide silk banners, not confetti.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"OFF the heat: the vinegar and white pepper, both fade if boiled; this is why restaurant versions taste brighter than tired homemade ones. Sesame oil; scallions; serve immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The two namesake elements are both added last and off the heat: vinegar's aroma and white pepper's punch are volatile, and boiling them into the pot is the single most common failure. The white-pepper heat is the dish's fingerprint: a slow burn in the throat rather than chili on the lips. The egg-ribbon speed rule is universal: slow stir, wide silk; fast stir, sad snow.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","umami-deep","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hot%20and%20Sour%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hot and Sour Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Hot and Sour Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Hot%20and%20Sour%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Tofu","Eggs","Scallion","Chiles","Mushrooms","Sesame","Vinegar","Stock/Broth","Dried mushrooms"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"cullen-skink","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g undyed smoked haddock, finnan haddie if the fishmonger is a poet"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, minced; 1 leek, sliced fine; 40g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g floury potatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml whole milk + the haddock's poaching liquor; 100ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives; black pepper; crusty bread with more butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the haddock in the milk with a bay leaf, 4–5 min; lift out; flake, discarding skin; KEEP the smoky milk.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat onion and leek in butter without color; add potatoes and just enough water; simmer until tender.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crush a third of the potatoes against the pot: the traditional thickener; no flour here.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the smoky poaching milk and cream; return the flaked fish gently; warm through without boiling; chives and pepper over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"From the fishing village of Cullen on the Moray Firth: 'skink' is old Scots for a shin-beef soup, and the name transferred when the fishing fleet's haddock replaced the beef. The smoked haddock poach IS the stock: the milk drinks the smoke and carries it through the whole pot. It routinely defeats fancier chowders in blind tastings, which the Scots report without surprise.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cullen%20Skink%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cullen Skink"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Cullen Skink","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Cullen%20Skink","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"bay-leaf","term":"Bay Leaf"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Smoked fish","Onion","Leek","Potato","Chives","Butter","Milk","Cream","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"snert-erwtensoep","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g green split peas, rinsed: no soak needed; they are eager"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pork hock or 300g bacon in a piece + 1 rookworst (Dutch smoked sausage) or kielbasa, added late"},{"kind":"item","text":"The winter cellar: 1 leek, 2 celery ribs + celeriac chunk, 2 carrots, 1 potato, 1 onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L water; bay; celery leaves saved for the top"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dark rye bread with butter, or the full tradition: rye with katenspek (smoked bacon) alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer split peas with the hock and 2L water, skimming, 45 min, until the peas begin dissolving.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2700,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"All the vegetables in; simmer 45 min more, stirring the bottom: split peas love to stick.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the hock; shred the meat back in; add the whole sausage for the last 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The test: snert is ready when a spoon stands upright in it; thin snert is just soup. Slice the sausage into coins; celery leaves over. Better tomorrow; correct the day after skating.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Netherlands measures winter in snert: it is the official food of the ice-skating season, ladled at outdoor rinks, and the standing-spoon test is the national doneness standard, cited in earnest. Day-old is canonically superior: Dutch families make it specifically to reheat. The rookworst added late keeps its snap; boiled from the start it surrenders, and a surrendered rookworst is quietly mourned.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","smoky","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, wood-smoke running underneath, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Snert%20(Erwtensoep)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Snert (Erwtensoep)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Snert (Erwtensoep)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Snert%20(Erwtensoep)","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Sausage","Onion","Leek","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Peas","Celeriac","Butter","Bread"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"nihari","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg beef shank with marrow bones (the marrow is the finish), or the traditional slow lamb shank"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nihari masala: fennel, cumin, coriander, black cardamom, cloves, cinnamon, black pepper, dried ginger, a little nutmeg and mace, toasted and ground (or a trusted boxed masala, widely used and unashamed)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, fried to deep brown; garlic-ginger paste, generous"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g ghee; 4 tbsp atta (wheat flour) slurried in water: the silken thickener"},{"kind":"item","text":"The garnish court: julienned fresh ginger, green chilies, cilantro, lemon wedges, crisp fried onions; naan for the drag"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the onions to mahogany in ghee; add garlic-ginger, then the meat, searing; masala in, bloomed 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Water to cover generously; the lowest possible simmer, lid on, 4–6 hours (overnight in tradition) until the shank collapses and the marrow loosens.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the atta slurry; simmer 20 min uncovered: the broth turns glossy, thick and clinging, its signature body.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest; skim some of the spiced fat and pour it BACK over the top (the tarka crown). Serve in shallow bowls buried in ginger, chili, cilantro and lemon, naan in hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nihari comes from 'nihar' (morning) because Delhi and Lucknow's old cooks simmered it overnight for laborers' dawn breakfast; Karachi later claimed it so completely that Pakistan considers it a national dish. The atta slurry is the identity: a wheat-silk body no cornstarch replicates. And the old shops seed each day's pot with a ladle of yesterday's (the taar): some claim their lineage is unbroken for generations.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Tandoor breads","Searing: the hard crust","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","warm-spiced","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tadka: blooming spices in fat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tadka%20tempering%20spices%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tandoor breads","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=naan%20tandoor%20technique%20home%20oven","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nihari%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nihari"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Nihari","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Nihari","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cardamom-green-and-black","term":"Cardamom: Green & Black"},{"slug":"nutmeg-and-mace","term":"Nutmeg & Mace"},{"slug":"searing-and-pan-technique","term":"Searing & Pan Technique"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Fennel","Lemon","Cilantro","Bread","Flour","Cumin","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Nutmeg","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"cioppino","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"The catch, flexible by market: 500g firm white fish chunks, 300g shrimp, 500g mussels and/or clams, 1 dungeness crab in sections if the day is glorious"},{"kind":"item","text":"The base: 1 onion, 1 fennel bulb, 6 garlic cloves, 1 tsp chili flakes, 3 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes + 250ml dry white or red wine + 500ml fish stock or clam juice; bay, oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil; parsley; lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"SOURDOUGH, toasted and garlic-rubbed; in San Francisco this is not a suggestion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat onion and fennel in olive oil; garlic and chili; caramelize the tomato paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wine, reduced by half; tomatoes, stock, herbs; simmer 20 min to a loose, brick-red stew base: it should taste bold before any seafood enters.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the seafood by clock: crab and fish first, 4 min; shrimp, 2 min; mussels and clams last, lid on, until they open.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Parsley storm, lemon, black pepper; serve in wide bowls with sourdough for the two-handed mop. Bibs are dignity, not defeat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"San Francisco's Italian immigrant fishermen built cioppino on the wharf from whatever the boats brought (the name likely from Ligurian 'ciuppin,' to chop), and the recipe's soul is flexibility with discipline: any seafood, but staged by cooking time, never dumped at once. The fennel is the quiet signature separating it from marinara stews, and day-old sourdough dragged through the dregs is the meal's true final course.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","herb-fresh","tangy-ferment","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cioppino%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cioppino"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Cioppino","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Cioppino","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"bivalves-oysters-clams-mussels-scallops","term":"Bivalves (Oysters, Clams, Mussels, Scallops)"},{"slug":"crab-vocabulary","term":"Crab Vocabulary"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","White fish","Crab","Mussels/Clams","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Fennel","Lemon","Parsley","Oregano","Bay leaf","Bread","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"jamaican-oxtail-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg oxtail, trimmed, seasoned overnight: salt, pepper, allspice, garlic, ginger, thyme, scallions, a whole scotch bonnet's worth of respect"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp brown sugar + 2 tbsp oil, for the browning, the Jamaican caramel opening"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp soy or browning sauce; 1 onion, 1 carrot; 750ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 can butter beans (broad beans), drained, the required companions, added late"},{"kind":"item","text":"'Spinners': small flour-and-water dumplings rolled between palms; rice and peas alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Burn the sugar: heat it in oil until it froths deep mahogany, the caramel base that stains and flavors everything after; sear the oxtail in it in batches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Aromatics in; stock to nearly cover; the lowest simmer or a 150°C oven, lid on, 3–3.5 h (a pressure cooker earns its keep here at 50 min).","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":12600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Skim the fat honestly: oxtail is generous. The gravy should reduce to a glossy, sticky, spoon-clinging brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter beans and spinners in for the final 20 min. Serve over rice and peas; instruct guests that the bones are meant to be picked up. They will not need telling twice.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oxtail went from butcher's discard to Jamaica's most expensive plate, and the burnt-sugar opening is the island's founding technique, the same caramel move that starts half the repertoire. The collagen is the point: hours of simmer turn tail into gravy that sets like jelly when cold and glosses lips when hot. Butter beans are non-negotiable, and sucking the marrow from the bones is not optional etiquette; it is the reason for the dish.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sugar stages & caramel","Searing: the hard crust","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20Oxtail%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jamaican Oxtail Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Jamaican Oxtail Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Jamaican%20Oxtail%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"rice-cooker-and-donabe-rice","term":"Rice Cooker & Donabe Rice"},{"slug":"ff-and-e-smallwares-and-opening-inventory","term":"FF&E, Smallwares & Opening Inventory"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Carrot","Chiles","Peas","Thyme","Butter","Rice","Bread","Flour","White beans","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Allspice"],"standard":{"slug":"jamaican-oxtail-stew","marks":[{"id":"jamaican-oxtail-stew#gravy","text":"The gravy is opaque mahogany with a shine, and carries no black specks of carbon from the sugar."},{"id":"jamaican-oxtail-stew#hangs","text":"It hangs off the ladle before it drops and sheets the back of a spoon; a spoon through the surface comes up with gravy, not oil, and no orange fat layer floats on top."},{"id":"jamaican-oxtail-stew#taste","text":"Taste it: the caramel reads deep and faintly bittersweet under the allspice and thyme, with no acrid catch at the back of the throat, and the salt sits right at the gravy's reduced strength. An overnight cure plus soy plus three hours of reduction is how this pot goes over."},{"id":"jamaican-oxtail-stew#meat","text":"Meat leaves the bone under light pressure from a spoon but arrives on the plate in pieces rather than as a pile of shreds, and the bones come out clean with marrow soft enough to draw."},{"id":"jamaican-oxtail-stew#butter","text":"Butter beans are whole and unbroken, and a spinner torn in half shows cooked dough to the middle with no chalky raw flour core."}],"fault":"The whole pot tastes of ash, because the sugar was taken past mahogany to black in the first minute. The gravy still looks correct, which is why the mistake usually gets found at the table."}},{"slug":"fresh-egg-pasta-tagliatelle-master","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g 00 flour (or all-purpose, honestly fine), the Emilian ratio: 100g flour per egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole eggs, the best you can buy; the yolks are the color and the character"},{"kind":"item","text":"Semolina for dusting; nothing else: no oil, no water, no salt in the dough (salt the water later)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A rolling pin or pasta machine; a bench scraper"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 minutes of kneading-and-resting patience; ragù or butter and parmesan waiting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Flour in a mound, well in the center, eggs in the well; beat with a fork, pulling in flour gradually until a shaggy mass forms; knead 8–10 min until smooth as an earlobe and springing back from a thumb press.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"REST 30 min wrapped: unrested dough fights the pin and wins.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll thin enough to read a newspaper through (the same Emilian test as strudel, chapters away); flour the sheet, roll it into a loose flat cigar, and cut 8mm ribbons; shake out the nests.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil in aggressively salted water 60–90 seconds: fresh pasta is done almost on arrival. Sauce it in the pan with a splash of pasta water; never rinse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sfogline of Bologna, the rolling-pin masters, judge a sheet by holding it to the window: light through the dough, no thin spots. One rule carries the whole craft: 100 grams of flour, one egg, repeat to scale. Fresh tagliatelle exists for ragù; the rough, porous surface of hand-rolled pasta grips sauce the way extruded dried pasta never will.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Fresh pasta","Salted water & the float test","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fresh pasta","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fresh%20pasta%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fresh%20Egg%20Pasta%20(Tagliatelle%20Master)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fresh Egg Pasta (Tagliatelle Master)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Fresh Egg Pasta (Tagliatelle Master)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Fresh%20Egg%20Pasta%20(Tagliatelle%20Master)","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Pasta","Bread","Flour","Semolina"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"spaghetti-alla-carbonara","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g spaghetti or rigatoni"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g guanciale, cut in thick batons (pancetta is the accepted understudy; bacon changes the play)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg yolks + 1 whole egg; 80g Pecorino Romano, finely grated + more for serving"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper, toasted in the pan and coarsely cracked: a full teaspoon; it is a lead actor"},{"kind":"item","text":"NOTHING ELSE. No cream, no garlic, no onion, no parsley. Rome is listening."}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the guanciale slowly until glass-crisp, fat pooled; kill the heat; toast the pepper in that fat 30 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk yolks, egg and pecorino into a thick paste; loosen with a ladle of starchy pasta water into a pourable cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the pasta 1 min shy; transfer wet into the guanciale pan (off heat); toss.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the egg-cheese cream, tossing constantly, adding pasta water splash by splash until a glossy sauce clings to every strand: the residual heat cooks it; direct heat scrambles it. Serve instantly with more pecorino and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Carbonara is a technique wearing a recipe's clothes: an egg emulsion cooked by residual heat alone, stabilized by starch water. The pan must be off the flame when the eggs arrive; scrambled carbonara is the world's most common Italian heartbreak. And the no-cream rule is not snobbery; cream mutes the pecorino-pepper argument that IS the dish. Guanciale's funk is the soul; find it if you can.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Spaghetti%20alla%20Carbonara%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Spaghetti alla Carbonara"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Spaghetti alla Carbonara","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Spaghetti%20alla%20Carbonara","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pecorino-romano","term":"Pecorino Romano"},{"slug":"guanciale-jowl-and-lardo","term":"Guanciale, Jowl & Lardo"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Parsley","Cream","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Pasta"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"biang-biang-mian","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Dough: 400g bread flour, 210ml water, 1 tsp salt; kneaded smooth, then rested LONG: 2 hours minimum, oiled, in flat oval slabs"},{"kind":"item","text":"The topping (you tiao zi): 3 tbsp coarse chili flakes + 1 tbsp Sichuan pepper + minced garlic + scallions piled on the noodles, then 4 tbsp SMOKING oil poured directly over: the sizzle is the sauce being born"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp black vinegar + 2 tbsp soy per bowl"},{"kind":"item","text":"Blanched bok choy or bean sprouts; optional braised pork or nothing at all"},{"kind":"item","text":"Confidence and a clear counter: these noodles are made by slapping"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rest the oiled dough slabs 2 h: the rest does 90% of the stretching; this cannot be rushed and cannot be skipped.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press each slab flat; hold the ends and pull, SLAPPING the middle against the counter rhythmically (biang! biang!: the name is the sound) until it stretches to a meter-long belt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tear down the middle lengthwise (leave the ends joined for the classic loop); boil 60–90 seconds with the greens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Noodles into bowls over the vinegar-soy; pile on garlic, chili, scallion; pour the smoking oil over the pile at the table. Toss until every belt is stained red. Slurp with intent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Xi'an's belt noodle, named onomatopoeically for the slap of dough on counter, and written with a character so famously complex (50-plus strokes) that it fits in no standard font and is taught as a rhyme. The pour-over of smoking oil onto raw aromatics is the master move: it blooms the chili and garlic in one hiss, sauce made in the bowl, three seconds, no pan.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Hand-pulled noodles","Braising","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","herb-fresh","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Biang%20Biang%20Mian%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Biang Biang Mian"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Biang Biang Mian","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Biang%20Biang%20Mian","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sichuan-peppercorn-and-mala","term":"Sichuan Peppercorn & Málà"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Scallion","Chiles","Cabbage","Bean sprouts","Noodles","Bread","Flour","Vinegar","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["braising","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"taiwanese-beef-noodle-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg beef shank and/or chuck in large chunks, blanched from cold water and rinsed, the clean-broth opening"},{"kind":"item","text":"The braise: 3 tbsp doubanjiang (fermented chili-bean paste, the soul), 2 tbsp soy + 1 tbsp dark soy, 1 tbsp sugar, 5 slices ginger, 6 garlic, 2 scallions, 1 tomato, star anise, cinnamon, bay, Sichuan peppercorns in a sachet"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L water or light stock; splash of rice wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g fresh wheat noodles, medium-thick and chewy"},{"kind":"item","text":"The service: blanched greens, suan cai (pickled mustard greens), chopped scallion, chili oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry ginger, garlic, scallion in oil; add doubanjiang and fry until the oil turns brick red; tomato, sugar, soys.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beef in with water, wine, and the spice sachet; simmer at a whisper 2.5–3 h until the shank's tendon turns to amber glass.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Remove the sachet; season the broth to a deep red-brown, savory-first, faintly sweet, gently numbing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil noodles fresh per bowl; ladle beef and broth over; top with greens, a spoon of suan cai, scallion. The suan cai is the counterpoint: salty-sour against the deep braise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Taipei holds an annual festival and genuine championships for this bowl, the migrant dish of 1949 that became the island's culinary flag. Doubanjiang fried until the oil breaks red is the non-negotiable foundation, and shank is chosen for its tendon: three hours turns it translucent and yielding, the texture the whole dish is secretly about. The pickled mustard greens on top are not garnish; they are the reset button between rich spoonfuls.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Braising","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Taiwanese%20Beef%20Noodle%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Taiwanese Beef Noodle Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Taiwanese Beef Noodle Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Taiwanese%20Beef%20Noodle%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"shank-oxtail-and-cheek","term":"Shank, Oxtail & Cheek"},{"slug":"ff-and-e-smallwares-and-opening-inventory","term":"FF&E, Smallwares & Opening Inventory"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Bay leaf","Noodles","Soy sauce","Wine","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Sugar","Pickles","Cinnamon","Star anise","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["braising","fermentation"]},{"slug":"jajangmyeon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp chunjang (Korean black bean paste): fried FIRST in oil, alone; raw chunjang is bitter and unrepentant"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g pork belly or shoulder, small dice; 1 large onion + 1 zucchini + ½ cabbage wedge, all in chunky dice; potato optional"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar; 2 tbsp oyster sauce; 350ml stock or water; cornstarch slurry"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g fresh thick wheat noodles (jajang or udon-style)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Julienned cucumber to crown; danmuji (yellow pickled radish) on the side, the required companion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the chunjang in 3 tbsp oil over medium, stirring, 3–4 min until glossy and nutty: this step converts bitterness to depth and cannot be skipped; set the fried paste aside.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the pork; add vegetables and stir-fry until edges soften.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the paste with sugar, oyster sauce and stock; simmer 5 min; thicken with slurry to a lava-thick black gravy.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil noodles, rinse briefly, bowl them; ladle the black sauce over in a generous crater-fill; cucumber on top. Mix until every strand is night-colored. Eat with the radish alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Korea's Chinese-restaurant icon, delivered by the million (it is THE moving-day and payday meal, and famously eaten alone on Black Day, April 14th, by the unattached). The fried-paste step is the entire secret: chunjang goes from acrid to chocolate-deep in three minutes of oil. Slurp loudly, wear the splatter, and know that a white shirt near jajangmyeon is a decision, not an accident.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Wok technique","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jajangmyeon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jajangmyeon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Jajangmyeon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Jajangmyeon","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Potato","Cabbage","Zucchini","Cucumber","Radish/Turnip","Noodles","Black beans","Oyster sauce","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"khao-soi-gai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 chicken legs or 6 thighs, bone-in"},{"kind":"item","text":"The paste: 3 tbsp red curry paste + 1 tbsp curry powder + ½ tsp turmeric + 2 crushed cardamom pods (the Burmese accent) + minced ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"800ml coconut milk (thick cream separated and reserved) + 400ml chicken stock; 2 tbsp fish sauce, 1 tbsp palm sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g fresh egg noodles: two-thirds boiled for the bowls, ONE-THIRD DEEP-FRIED into crispy nests for the crowns"},{"kind":"item","text":"The ritual plate: pickled mustard greens, shallot wedges, lime, roasted chili oil (naam prik pao)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the paste in the thick coconut cream until the oil splits red and fragrant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chicken in, turned in the paste; add remaining coconut milk and stock; simmer 30–35 min until tender; season with fish sauce and palm sugar: the broth should be rich, gently sweet, curry-deep.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry a third of the raw noodles in hot oil until puffed and golden; drain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the rest; bowl them; chicken and broth over; crown with the crispy nest. Serve the pickle-shallot-lime-chili plate alongside: each eater cuts the richness to taste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chiang Mai's signature: a Burmese-Yunnanese caravan dish that settled in the north and became the city's one-bowl résumé. The soft-and-crispy double noodle is the identity: the fried nest wilts slowly into the coconut broth, giving every stage of the bowl a different texture. The sour pickled greens and raw shallot are structural, not optional; khao soi without them is sweet and unfinished.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","gently hot","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pounding a curry paste","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=thai%20curry%20paste%20mortar%20pestle%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Khao%20Soi%20Gai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Khao Soi Gai"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Khao Soi Gai","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Khao%20Soi%20Gai","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Milk","Cream","Coconut milk","Noodles","Fish sauce","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Sugar","Curry paste","Pickles","Turmeric","Cardamom","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"pad-see-ew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g fresh wide rice noodles (sen yai), separated gently at room temperature: cold fridge noodles crack; warm them 20 seconds if needed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g chicken thighs or pork, thin slices; 2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 big handfuls Chinese broccoli (gai lan), stems bias-cut, leaves separate"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 2 tbsp dark sweet soy + 1.5 tbsp oyster sauce + 1 tbsp light soy + 1 tsp sugar + splash of white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"High-smoke-point oil; a wok and the hottest burner in the house; white pepper and chili-vinegar (prik nam som) at the table"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wok to maximum; sear the meat; push aside; scramble the eggs hard against the steel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Gai lan stems in for 30 seconds; then noodles and sauce, poured around the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Now the discipline: TOSS, then LET THEM SIT 20–30 seconds untouched to char, toss, sit again: the noodles must kiss the steel long enough to catch smoke and dark-soy caramel at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leaves in last, one toss, out. Serve immediately with white pepper and the chili vinegar: the sour cuts the sweet soy; Thais add it at the table by reflex.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pad see ew is pad thai's quieter sibling and the wok-hei exam: sweet dark soy caramelizes on hot steel only if you stop stirring: the sit-and-char rhythm is everything, and constant tossing steams the noodles pale. One portion at a time in a home wok, same law as char kway teow two shelves over. The chili vinegar on the table is the dish's missing half; try it once and you will never eat it plain again.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Sugar stages & caramel","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","sweet-edged","gently hot","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pad%20See%20Ew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pad See Ew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Pad See Ew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Pad%20See%20Ew","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"smoke-char-and-the-burnt-edge","term":"Smoke, Char & the Burnt Edge"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Eggs","Chiles","Broccoli","Noodles","Oyster sauce","Vinegar","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"zaru-soba","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g good dried soba: at least 40% buckwheat; the ni-hachi (80/20 buckwheat-wheat) standard is the sweet spot"},{"kind":"item","text":"The tsuyu: 500ml dashi (kombu-shiitake dashi for vegetarian) + 100ml soy + 100ml mirin, simmered 2 min and CHILLED completely"},{"kind":"item","text":"The yakumi (accents): finely sliced scallion, grated fresh wasabi or a dab of paste, shredded nori"},{"kind":"item","text":"A bamboo mat or strainer plate; ice water for the shock"},{"kind":"item","text":"The finale: the sobayu, the cloudy noodle-cooking water, served warm in a small pot"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil soba in generous unsalted water per the packet minus 30 seconds; stir once at the start.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and WASH under cold running water, rubbing the noodles with your hands to strip surface starch: this rinse is the difference between slick, distinct strands and a gummy nest; finish in ice water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain well; mound on the mat; nori over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip a third of each mouthful into the cold tsuyu (mixed with scallion and wasabi to taste) and slurp decisively. When the noodles are gone, pour the warm sobayu into the remaining tsuyu and drink it as the closing soup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold soba is judged naked, no sauce blankets a bad noodle, so the wash-and-shock is the craft, and slurping is functional: air carries the buckwheat's aroma. Dip only partway; drowning the noodle drowns the buckwheat. The sobayu ritual at the end is the mark of someone raised on this: the cooking water carries the buckwheat's nutrients and turns the leftover dip into a farewell broth. Waste nothing; that is the meal.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","umami-deep","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zaru%20Soba%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zaru Soba"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Zaru Soba","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Zaru%20Soba","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Scallion","Mushrooms","Noodles","Wine","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"kitsune-udon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g udon: frozen sanuki udon is genuinely the connoisseur's home choice (better chew than most fresh packs)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The broth (kakejiru): 1L dashi (kombu-shiitake dashi for vegetarians) + 3 tbsp light soy (usukuchi, for the pale Kansai gold) + 2 tbsp mirin + 1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 pieces aburaage (fried tofu pouches), simmered 10 min in 200ml dashi + 2 tbsp each soy, mirin, sugar until glossy and swollen, the 'fox' of the name"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions, sliced fine; shichimi togarashi at the table"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: a slice of kamaboko for the pink swirl"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the aburaage in its sweet braise until it drinks the liquid and turns amber; set aside in its syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the broth to just under a boil: kakejiru is never boiled hard; it goes cloudy and sharp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the udon (from frozen, straight into boiling water 60–90 seconds); rinse briefly in hot water to clean the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bowl the noodles; ladle the hot gold broth; lay the sweet fox pouches on top; scallions; shichimi to taste. Slurp the noodles, then drink the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kitsune means fox: Japanese folklore holds that foxes, messengers of the harvest deity Inari, adore fried tofu, and the sweet-simmered pouch on top is their tribute. The dish is a Kansai gentleness exam: pale broth, thick chewy noodle, sweet tofu; nothing shouts. The frozen-udon endorsement is sincere: flash-frozen sanuki udon keeps the dense, bouncy koshi (chew) that defines the noodle better than most refrigerated packs ever manage.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","Braising","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kitsune%20Udon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kitsune Udon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Kitsune Udon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Kitsune%20Udon","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"shichimi-togarashi","term":"Shichimi Tōgarashi"}],"pantryItems":["Tofu","Scallion","Mushrooms","Noodles","Soy sauce","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","braising"]},{"slug":"singapore-noodles","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g thin rice vermicelli, soaked in warm water 10 min until pliable, drained WELL and left to dry-fluff: wet noodles clump and steam"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g shrimp; 150g char siu or ham, slivered; 2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion + 1 red pepper, slivered; 2 handfuls bean sprouts; 3 scallions in lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE SIGNATURE: 1.5 tbsp good curry powder + ½ tsp turmeric, bloomed in oil, not dusted on dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sauce: 1 tbsp light soy + 1 tsp fish sauce + ½ tsp sugar; white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scramble the eggs first in the hot wok; out. Sear shrimp and char siu; out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry onion and pepper 1 min; then bloom the curry powder and turmeric in a little fresh oil in the center 15 seconds until fragrant: raw curry powder tastes dusty; bloomed, it tastes golden.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Noodles in; toss with tongs, lifting and dropping, adding the sauce around the rim; everything else back in with the sprouts and scallions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss high and fast 90 seconds: every strand evenly yellow, nothing clumped, sprouts still snapping. White pepper; serve immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The great joke of the dish: it is unknown in Singapore, a Cantonese invention from Hong Kong's cha chaan tengs, named for exotic flair, and now beloved from London takeaways to New York. The two techniques that matter: dry noodles (soaked, drained, rested) and bloomed curry powder: the fifteen-second oil bloom is what turns the spice from chalk to gold. Tongs beat spatulas here; vermicelli wants to be lifted, not shoved.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Searing: the hard crust","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","herb-fresh","earthy","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Singapore%20Noodles%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Singapore Noodles"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Singapore Noodles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Singapore%20Noodles","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Scallion","Bell pepper","Bean sprouts","Noodles","Fish sauce","Sugar","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"dry-brined-roast-turkey-with-real-gravy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 turkey, 5–6kg: smaller birds cook truer; two small beat one giant"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dry brine: 1 tbsp salt per 2kg + 1 tsp baking powder + herbs, rubbed on THREE DAYS ahead, bird left UNCOVERED in the fridge"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g butter, softened with sage and thyme, worked under the breast skin"},{"kind":"item","text":"The gravy engine: neck and giblets simmered into stock; the roasting pan's drippings; 40g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, carrots and celery under the bird as the rack; the actual rack retired"}],"steps":[{"text":"Three days out: dry-brine and refrigerate uncovered: the salt penetrates, the skin dries to parchment, and the wait does what basting pretends to.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast day: herb butter under the breast skin; bird on the vegetable bed, legs toward the oven's back (the hot corner), at 220°C for 30 min, then 165°C.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull at 65°C in the breast, 74°C in the thigh: carryover finishes it; rest 45 MINUTES uncovered; the rest is not optional, it is half the recipe.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Gravy: pan on the burner, flour into the drippings, giblet stock whisked in, simmered 10 min and strained. Carve off the bone in the kitchen; platter the slices over a ladle of gravy.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Three truths the holiday table deserves: the dry brine beats the wet bucket (seasoned meat, crackling skin, zero cooler logistics); basting is theater that opens the oven and lengthens the wait; and the 45-minute rest is where the juice you fought for decides to stay. Legs to the back exploits the oven's hot zone: dark meat needs the heat the breast fears.","equipment":["Parchment"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Roasting in a hot oven","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dry-Brined%20Roast%20Turkey%20with%20Real%20Gravy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dry-Brined Roast Turkey with Real Gravy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Dry-Brined Roast Turkey with Real Gravy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Dry-Brined%20Roast%20Turkey%20with%20Real%20Gravy","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"roasting-baking-and-carryover","term":"Roasting, Baking & Carryover"}],"pantryItems":["Turkey","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Thyme","Sage","Butter","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"standard":{"slug":"dry-brined-roast-turkey-with-real-gravy","marks":[{"id":"dry-brined-roast-turkey-with-real-gravy#skin","text":"Skin uniformly deep brown, taut and dry enough to click under a fingernail, unbroken over the breast where the herb butter went under."},{"id":"dry-brined-roast-turkey-with-real-gravy#bird","text":"The bird came out on two thermometer readings, not a time: 65°C at the deepest part of the breast and 74°C at the thigh joint, the probe kept off the bone."},{"id":"dry-brined-roast-turkey-with-real-gravy#carved","text":"Carved at a full 45 minutes: a slice weeps at the cut edge and the board stays nearly dry. A flood on the board says the bird was cut early."},{"id":"dry-brined-roast-turkey-with-real-gravy#breast","text":"Breast slices hold together coming off the knife and read moist white through their whole thickness; at the thigh the meat pulls easily from the bone and the juices there run clear rather than pink."},{"id":"dry-brined-roast-turkey-with-real-gravy#slice","text":"A slice of breast eaten alone, no gravy, is seasoned to its centre. The gravy is strained, coats a spoon, and carries no fat slick on top and no floury edge underneath."}],"fault":"The bird is timed rather than probed and the breast rides up to thigh temperature: white meat comes off the knife in dry crumbling slabs, and the gravy ends up doing the work the roast was meant to do."}},{"slug":"christmas-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g mixed dried fruit + 100g candied peel, soaked OVERNIGHT (traditionally weeks) in 150ml brandy or stout"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g suet or cold grated butter; 120g dark brown sugar; 100g breadcrumbs + 80g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs; 1 grated apple + 1 grated carrot (the moisture insurance); black treacle 1 tbsp; mixed spice, generous"},{"kind":"item","text":"Silver coin or charm, sterilized, hidden inside, per tradition and dental waiver"},{"kind":"item","text":"A pudding basin, parchment-and-foil lid pleated for expansion, string handle; more brandy for the flames"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir-up Sunday protocol: everyone in the house stirs the mix east-to-west and makes a wish; then pack into the buttered basin, lid it with pleated parchment and foil, tie with string.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"STEAM 6 hours, topping up the water: long steam is the color and the depth; there is no shortcut oven.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool; re-lid; store in a cold cupboard 4–6 weeks, feeding it a spoon of brandy weekly: the pudding matures like the season's patience.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Christmas Day: steam 2 hours to reheat; unmold; warm brandy in a ladle, ignite, and pour the blue flames over at the table, lights dimmed. Serve with brandy butter or custard.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pudding is a calendar: mixed on Stir-up Sunday (five weeks before Christmas), fed weekly, flamed once. The six-hour steam converts fruit and crumb into the dense mahogany the British defend against all fashion, and the flaming entrance is the point: brandy at 40%+ lights reliably if warmed first. The hidden coin brings luck to its finder and a warning to everyone else; both are traditional.","equipment":["Parchment","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Flambé","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pleating dumplings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20pleat%20dumplings%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Christmas%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Christmas Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Christmas Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Christmas%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Carrot","Apple","Butter","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Beer/Cider","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"dresdner-stollen","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour; 14g yeast; 200ml warm milk; 100g sugar; 1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g butter IN the dough, plus 100g melted for after; stollen is a butter delivery system with raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g raisins + 60g candied orange and lemon peel + 60g almonds, soaked overnight in 80ml rum"},{"kind":"item","text":"Zest of a lemon, cardamom, mace, vanilla; 150g marzipan rolled into a log for the center (the Marzipanstollen faction: join it)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar for the burial, two full coats"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rich yeasted dough; knead well; rise 1 h. Work in the drained rum fruit gently (wet fruit tears the gluten).","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll an oval; lay the marzipan log off-center; fold the dough over in the signature hump: the shape represents the swaddled child and is older than the recipe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Proof 45 min; bake 170°C, 50–60 min to deep gold; the loaf will be heavy and unglamorous. Trust it.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":6300,"estimated":true},{"text":"THE RITUAL: brush the hot loaf with all the melted butter until it drinks every drop; bury in icing sugar; cool; sugar again. Wrap and REST 2–3 weeks: young stollen is bread; aged stollen is Christmas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dresden has baked stollen since the 1400s and once petitioned the Pope to allow butter in it (the 'Butter Letter' of 1491, a real document); the city still holds a Stollenfest with a loaf the size of a car. The butter-bath-and-sugar-crust seals the crumb so the weeks of aging mellow instead of staling; slice thin from the MIDDLE and push the halves back together: the mark of a household that knows.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dresdner%20Stollen%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dresdner Stollen"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Dresdner Stollen","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Dresdner%20Stollen","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Orange","Dried fruit","Butter","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts","Cardamom","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"pan-de-muerto","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour; 10g yeast; 100g sugar; 4 eggs + 2 yolks; 120ml warm milk; pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g soft butter, kneaded in slowly, a brioche in spirit"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE PERFUME: zest of 2 oranges + 1 tbsp orange blossom water (the scent is the offering; do not substitute it away)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dough reserved for the bones: ropes rolled with knuckle-bumps, and a small ball for the skull, crossed atop each loaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"The finish: melted butter + a snowfall of sugar (or sesame in some regions)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rich dough with the zest and azahar; knead until silk; rise 1.5–2 h: enriched dough is slow and unhurried, like the season.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reserve a fist of dough. Shape the rest into round loaves; roll bone ropes with your fingers spread (the gaps make the knuckles) and drape two crossed bones plus the top knob (the skull, or the tear) on each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Proof 1 h; egg wash gently along the bones.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 170°C, 25–30 min; brush warm with butter and dredge in sugar. Place one on the ofrenda first; eat the rest with hot chocolate or café de olla.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Baked for Día de Muertos and placed on the ofrenda so returning souls can enjoy its essence: the orange-blossom scent is literally the point; the bread perfumes the altar. The crossed ropes are bones, the knob a skull or a fallen tear, the circle the cycle itself. The living eat it dunked in hot chocolate, which the dead are said not to mind, having better access to the aroma than anyone.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pan%20de%20Muerto%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pan de Muerto"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Pan de Muerto","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Pan%20de%20Muerto","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Orange","Butter","Bread","Flour","Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"hallacas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"The guiso, made a day ahead: 500g beef + 300g pork + 200g chicken, diced small, stewed with peppers, onion, garlic, olives, capers, raisins, a spoon of papelón (raw cane sugar) and wine, sweet-savory and thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"Masa: 500g precooked corn flour + 700ml broth tinted gold with annatto-infused oil; salted well"},{"kind":"item","text":"The adornos: strips of pepper, onion rings, olives, capers, raisins, almonds, laid on TOP of the filling like jewelry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Plantain leaves, passed over a flame until supple, wiped, cut in large squares; string for tying"},{"kind":"item","text":"The family: hallacas are made in assembly line or not at all: one spreads, one fills, one adorns, one ties"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the guiso the day before; chill: cold filling handles cleanly and tastes married.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead the annatto broth into the corn flour until smooth and spreadable; oil each leaf square and spread a thin masa circle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"A spoon of guiso, then the adornos arranged deliberately; fold the leaf into a neat packet, wrap in a second leaf, and tie with string in the crosshatch that marks your household.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the packets 60 min; cool; refrigerate: hallacas are made by the dozens in December and reheated (boiled 20 min) throughout the season. They improve daily.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Venezuela's Christmas in a leaf: the whole country makes them in family production lines each December, and every household's tie-pattern and guiso are identity documents (the standing joke: the best hallaca is always your mother's). The sweet-savory guiso (olives, raisins, capers in one filling) carries the colonial pantry's whole story. Making a single hallaca is technically possible and spiritually incorrect.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Scoring","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","sweet-edged","tangy-ferment","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hallacas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hallacas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Hallacas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Hallacas","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"butchery-verbs-frenching-butterflying-tying","term":"Butchery Verbs: Frenching, Butterflying, Tying"},{"slug":"line-slang-86-in-the-weeds-and-family-meal","term":"Line Slang: 86, In the Weeds & Family Meal"},{"slug":"annatto-achiote","term":"Annatto (Achiote)"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Corn","Plantain","Olives","Capers","Dried fruit","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["scoring","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"matzo-ball-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"The golden broth: 1 whole chicken or 1.5kg wings and backs, 2 onions (one halved and CHARRED), carrots, celery, parsnip, dill stems, peppercorns; simmered 2.5 h at a whisper and strained clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"Matzo balls: 120g matzo meal, 4 eggs, 4 tbsp schmaltz (chicken fat, the flavor is the point) or oil, 4 tbsp seltzer, salt, pinch of ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"The eternal question, answered by household: floaters (add ½ tsp baking powder, more seltzer) or sinkers (denser, defiant)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Carrot coins simmered soft; fresh dill, a forest"},{"kind":"item","text":"REST the matzo mixture 30 min in the fridge, non-negotiable"}],"steps":[{"text":"Build the broth low and slow, skimming; the charred onion gives the amber; strain and season it until it tastes like the answer to a question.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the matzo balls gently: overmixing makes cannonballs regardless of leavening politics; chill 30 min so the meal hydrates.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll with wet hands into walnut-sized spheres (they double); simmer LIDDED in salted water or spare broth 35–40 min: no peeking; steam is doing the work.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Balls into bowls with carrot coins; golden broth over; dill with abandon. Serve to anyone in need of repair.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The knaidel crossed Europe in steerage and became the centerpiece of the Passover seder and the cure-all of the deli counter; 'Jewish penicillin' is affectionate and, per the broth's steam and salt, mildly clinical fact. Schmaltz is the flavor spine; seltzer is the airiness hack elder generations swore by before science agreed. Floaters versus sinkers is a genuine hereditary trait; marriages have negotiated it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","rich","earthy","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, richness that coats and comforts, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Matzo%20Ball%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Matzo Ball Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Matzo Ball Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Matzo%20Ball%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"schmaltz-and-rendering","term":"Schmaltz & Rendering"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Ginger","Carrot","Celery","Parsnip","Dill","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"holiday-braised-brisket","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg beef brisket, flat cut with the fat cap ON: trimmed to a ½cm blanket, never removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 onions, sliced, a genuine mountain; they are the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml stock + 250ml red wine (or the sweeter road: a can of tomatoes + a spoon of brown sugar and vinegar); 3 tbsp tomato paste; garlic, bay, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Carrots in fat chunks for the final hour"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE SCHEDULE: cooked TODAY, sliced and re-sauced TOMORROW: the two-day method is the recipe"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear the brisket hard on both sides in a wide pot; out. Brown the onion mountain patiently in the drippings, 20 min; paste and garlic in.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wine and stock; brisket back, fat cap UP; braise covered at 150°C, 3.5–4 h until a fork twists in with mild resistance: collapsing-soft slices badly.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"OVERNIGHT: chill the whole pot. The fat lifts off in a sheet; the meat firms.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Next day: slice the COLD brisket across the grain in neat ribbons; shingle back into the defatted sauce; reheat covered at 160°C, 40 min. The slices drink the gravy and arrive intact and yielding.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Every Jewish holiday table (Rosh Hashanah, Passover, Hanukkah) runs on brisket, and the grandmothers' two-day method is pure technique dressed as tradition: cold brisket slices cleanly where warm brisket shreds, the fat removes itself, and the reheat marries slice to sauce. Against the grain is the entire game on this cut; mark the grain direction BEFORE cooking, because the sear will hide it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Holiday%20Braised%20Brisket%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Holiday Braised Brisket"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Holiday Braised Brisket","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Holiday%20Braised%20Brisket","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"brisket-point-vs-flat","term":"Brisket (Point vs. Flat)"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Thyme","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["braising","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"zongzi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g glutinous rice, soaked 4 h, drained, seasoned with 2 tbsp soy + 1 tsp five-spice (the savory southern road)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The heart: 300g pork belly marinated overnight in soy, shaoxing and sugar; salted duck egg yolks, halved; dried shiitake, soaked; optional chestnuts and dried shrimp"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 dried bamboo leaves, boiled 5 min and wiped, plus kitchen string"},{"kind":"item","text":"The fold: two leaves overlapped into a cone, filled, capped, folded, and bound, a craft with a learning curve measured in generations"},{"kind":"item","text":"A deep pot and 2.5–3 hours of gentle boiling"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the pork overnight; soak and season the rice; prep all fillings into an assembly line.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold two leaves into a watertight cone; a layer of rice, then pork, yolk, mushroom, chestnut; rice to cap; fold the leaf tails over and around into a tight tetrahedron and bind with string, snug, not strangled; rice swells.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil submerged (weight them with a plate) 2.5–3 h; top up with boiling water as needed.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 15 min; snip, unwrap, and eat from the leaf. They freeze impeccably; the season's labor feeds months.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wrapped each year for the Dragon Boat Festival in memory of the poet Qu Yuan: rice cast into the river so the fish would spare him, or so the two-thousand-year story goes. The north-south divide is real and festive: sweet red-date zongzi above the Yangtze, savory pork-and-yolk below, and each side finds the other's version baffling. The leaf fold is the inheritance; the first ten leak, and everyone's first ten leaked.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zongzi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zongzi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Zongzi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Zongzi","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"duck-prosciutto-magret-seche","term":"Duck Prosciutto (Magret Séché)"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Duck","Shrimp","Eggs","Mushrooms","Rice","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar","Dried mushrooms","Five-spice"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"hoppin-john","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g dried black-eyed peas (or field peas, the Lowcountry heart), soaked or not: they are forgiving"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 smoked ham hock or 200g smoked bacon (or smoked paprika + oil for the meatless table)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 1 celery rib, 1 small green pepper; 2 garlic cloves; bay, thyme, chili flake"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g long-grain rice, Carolina Gold if the pantry is blessed"},{"kind":"item","text":"The required escorts: braised greens (collards: the folded money) and cornbread (the gold); a splash of vinegar and hot sauce at the table"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the hock in 1.5L water 30 min; add peas and aromatics; cook 45–60 min until tender but standing, in broth that has turned smoky and potlikker-rich.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull and shred the hock meat back in; season boldly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The classic road: cook the rice IN a measured portion of the pea broth (the true Hoppin' John, one pot, every grain stained); the easy road: serve the peas ladled over rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Eat on January 1st with greens and cornbread; leave three peas on the plate for luck, and call the leftovers by their proper name: Skippin' Jenny.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The South's New Year contract: peas for coins, greens for folding money, cornbread for gold. Eat them on the first day and the year owes you. The dish is Carolina Lowcountry by way of West African rice culture (the pea-and-rice grammar of the whole Atlantic diaspora), and cooking the rice in the pea broth is the old way that makes it a dish instead of a topping. Day-two Skippin' Jenny demonstrates thrift, which brings its own luck.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Braising"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","gently hot","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hoppin'%20John%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hoppin' John"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Hoppin' John","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Hoppin'%20John","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Celery","Bell pepper","Chiles","Kale/Collards","Peas","Thyme","Bay leaf","Rice","Vinegar","Hot sauce","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising"]},{"slug":"tourtiere-du-reveillon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Double crust: 400g flour, 200g cold lard or butter (lard is the Québécois answer), 1 tsp salt, ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground pork (or the Lac-Saint-Jean way: mixed pork, beef and game)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, minced; 2 garlic cloves; 250ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE SPICE SIGNATURE: ½ tsp each cinnamon, clove and allspice + savory or thyme: the scent that says December in Québec"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 grated potato (or ½ cup mashed), the binder; egg wash for the lid"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the pork with onion, garlic, spices and stock, breaking it fine, 30–40 min until the liquid is nearly gone; stir in the grated potato to bind; cool COMPLETELY: hot filling melts pastry from below.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the bottom crust into a deep plate; fill generously.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lid it; crimp; cut a chimney vent (some houses cut a fir tree or a star: the vent is the signature line).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Egg wash; bake 200°C 20 min, then 180°C 30–35 min to deep gold. Rest 15. Serve with ketchup aux fruits (fruit ketchup) or green tomato chutney: the sweet-sour partner is traditional and correct.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"The centerpiece of the réveillon, the feast after Midnight Mass on Christmas Eve, and every Québécois family's spicing is a quiet fingerprint: always warm spices, never hot ones; the cinnamon-clove hum against pork is the taste of the holiday itself. The cooled-filling rule protects the crust; the fruit ketchup is not a condiment eccentricity but the dish's appointed partner, defended across generations. Freeze one unbaked; January will thank December.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tourti%C3%A8re%20du%20R%C3%A9veillon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tourtière du Réveillon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: America’s Test Kitchen on Tourtière du Réveillon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=America%E2%80%99s%20Test%20Kitchen%20Tourti%C3%A8re%20du%20R%C3%A9veillon","sub":"A YouTube search for America’s Test Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Thyme","Butter","Flour","Stock/Broth","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine"]},{"slug":"central-texas-smoked-brisket","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole packer brisket, 5–6kg, trimmed to a ¼-inch fat cap, edges rounded (aerodynamics against drying)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The rub, in its entirety: coarse salt and coarse black pepper, 50/50. Central Texas has spoken; everything else is another state"},{"kind":"item","text":"Post oak if you can get it; oak or hickory otherwise; a smoker or kettle rigged for indirect at a steady 120°C"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butcher paper for the wrap: paper breathes where foil braises"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 10–14 hour day, a chair, and something to read"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the night before. Smoke fat-side toward the heat at 120°C, thin blue smoke only: white billowing smoke is bitterness in progress.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ride it to the STALL: around 70°C internal the temperature freezes for hours as the meat sweats. This is normal. Do not panic. Do not crank the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"When the bark is set and mahogany (usually mid-stall), wrap tightly in butcher paper; continue to 95°C internal, when a probe slides in like warm butter: feel outranks number.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"REST 1–2 hours minimum, wrapped, in a cooler. Slice the flat across the grain in pencil-width slices; turn the point 90 degrees and slice again. The slice should drape, not fall apart.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brisket is a patience exam with a meat certificate: the stall is evaporative cooling doing its work, the paper wrap (the Franklin method) is the compromise between bark and speed, and the long rest redistributes what twelve hours of smoke fought for. Salt, pepper, oak, time: the Central Texas liturgy has four words, and juiciness is the sermon.","equipment":["Thermometer","Smoker"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking","Braising","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Low & slow smoking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"dish":{"label":"BBQ with Franklin: The Brisket","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Central%20Texas%20Smoked%20Brisket%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Central Texas Smoked Brisket"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Central Texas Smoked Brisket","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20Central%20Texas%20Smoked%20Brisket","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"brisket-point-vs-flat","term":"Brisket (Point vs. Flat)"},{"slug":"grills-and-smokers-kettle-kamado-offset-pellet","term":"Grills & Smokers: Kettle, Kamado, Offset, Pellet"}],"pantryItems":["Beef"],"standard":{"slug":"central-texas-smoked-brisket","marks":[{"id":"central-texas-smoked-brisket#bark","text":"Bark is uniform near-black mahogany and stays put: a thumb drawn across it neither smears nor lifts, and the coarse pepper is still visible as grain in the crust."},{"id":"central-texas-smoked-brisket#resistance","text":"Resistance to the probe is equal at three points along the flat, thick end included, at 95C internal; one soft spot and two firm ones means it goes back on."},{"id":"central-texas-smoked-brisket#rendered","text":"The fat cap has rendered translucent and spreadable, and the seam fat inside the point gives under finger pressure with no hard white core left in it."},{"id":"central-texas-smoked-brisket#slices","text":"Slices are pencil-width, about 6mm, cut across the grain on the flat and again after the point is turned 90 degrees; a slice held at one end drapes over a finger, parts under a gentle tug, and does not shred under its own weight or spring back."},{"id":"central-texas-smoked-brisket#slice","text":"A slice from the thin end of the flat, not the point, beads moisture immediately when squeezed between two fingers, and the board carries beads and smears rather than a spreading pool; the crust tastes of pepper first with the salt behind it, and no slice needs sauce."}],"fault":"Panic at the stall and the pit pushed to 150C, so the brisket hits 95C hours early with the collagen only half converted: the flat slices firm and dry, the bark tastes scorched rather than smoked, and no rest recovers it."}},{"slug":"yakitori","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken thighs, cut in even 3cm pieces; the full izakaya spread adds skin (kawa), meatballs (tsukune), and liver for the brave"},{"kind":"item","text":"Negi: thick scallion or baby leek segments, skewered between the meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"The tare: 100ml soy + 100ml mirin + 50ml sake + 2 tbsp sugar + chicken trimmings, simmered 20 min to a glossy lacquer, kept and re-fed forever"},{"kind":"item","text":"Or the purist's other road: shio, just good salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bamboo skewers soaked 30 min; a narrow charcoal bed (binchotan is the dream) so the skewer ends hang off the heat"}],"steps":[{"text":"Thread meat and negi snugly: gaps dry out, crowding steams; the skewer should look like a tidy train.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot coals, turning every 45–60 seconds: yakitori is constant motion, small adjustments, no walking away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tare skewers: dip into the sauce and RETURN to the grill; repeat twice more: layered dips lacquer; a single late dunk merely wets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with a final gloss of tare or a pinch of salt; sansho pepper on the fatty ones. Serve immediately, two skewers at a time, with something cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tokyo's yakitori masters guard tare pots that have been topped up, never emptied, for decades; every dipped skewer feeds chicken essence back into the mother sauce. The dip-grill-dip rhythm caramelizes in coats, the same logic as the huli huli baste chapters away. Shio versus tare is the eternal counter question; order both and betray no allegiance.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","sweet-edged","smoky","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, a sweet edge rounding the corners, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yakitori%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yakitori"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Yakitori","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20Yakitori","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sake-fundamentals-the-polishing-ladder","term":"Sake Fundamentals: The Polishing Ladder"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Liver & Offal","Scallion","Leek","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"tacos-al-pastor-home-trompo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg pork shoulder, sliced into wide ½cm sheets (freeze 30 min for clean slicing)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The adobo: 4 guajillo + 2 ancho chiles soaked, 3 tbsp achiote paste, ¼ cup pineapple juice, ¼ cup vinegar, garlic, oregano, cumin, clove, blended thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE HOME TROMPO: a vertical skewer stood in a pan (or two skewers through the stacked meat), crowned with a thick pineapple slab; or a hot cast-iron sear for the honest weeknight"},{"kind":"item","text":"Corn tortillas; minced onion, cilantro, lime; salsa of your conviction"},{"kind":"item","text":"The taquero's flourish: a thin wedge of roasted pineapple flicked onto each taco"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the pork sheets in adobo overnight: every face painted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack the sheets onto the vertical skewer, alternating orientation, packing tight into a rough cone; pineapple on top; roast at 200°C about 90 min until the edges char, carving as the outside crisps and returning the trompo to the oven between rounds.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"(Skillet road: sear the marinated sheets hard, then chop.) Crisp the shaved edges briefly on a hot plancha with a little of the drippings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm tortillas; meat, onion, cilantro, the pineapple wedge, lime, salsa. Small tacos, eaten in twos and threes, standing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Al pastor is shawarma that emigrated: Lebanese immigrants brought the vertical spit to Puebla and Mexico City in the 1930s, and the swap from lamb to adobo-stained pork with pineapple made it Mexican forever. The trompo's genius is self-basting rotation and edge-crisping in thin shaves; the home stack captures most of it. The pineapple flick from spit to taco, mid-air, remains the taquero's signature and the amateur's aspiration.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Roasting in a hot oven","Griddle & plancha work","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","gently hot","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tacos%20al%20Pastor%20(Home%20Trompo)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tacos al Pastor (Home Trompo)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Tacos al Pastor (Home Trompo)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20Tacos%20al%20Pastor%20(Home%20Trompo)","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mandoline-and-the-slicing-machines","term":"Mandoline & the Slicing Machines"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Lime","Pineapple","Cilantro","Oregano","Bread","Flour","Vinegar","Cumin","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"tacos-al-pastor-home-trompo","marks":[{"id":"tacos-al-pastor-home-trompo#shaves","text":"Shaves come off in thin strips 2 to 3mm across, ragged and short, never cubes or slabs."},{"id":"tacos-al-pastor-home-trompo#shaved","text":"The shaved meat is brick red from achiote through the full half-centimetre of each sheet, not stained on the outer face with pale pork underneath."},{"id":"tacos-al-pastor-home-trompo#spoonful","text":"Every spoonful holds two textures: lacquered crisp edges that crackle between the fingers against a soft interior, and no piece is soft the whole way through. Uniform tenderness is a failed pass."},{"id":"tacos-al-pastor-home-trompo#pineapple","text":"The pineapple wedge is charred at the edge, still holds its shape when lifted, and releases juice when bitten; it is a thin wedge on each taco, not a dice stirred through."},{"id":"tacos-al-pastor-home-trompo#tortilla","text":"The tortilla is warm and pliable enough to fold twice without cracking, and a 40 to 50g portion rides in it held in one hand without soaking through or spilling out the fold."}],"fault":"Seared and chopped and sent straight out, the meat never returning to the hot plancha, so there are no crisp edges anywhere and the taco tastes of marinated pork instead of al pastor."}},{"slug":"anticuchos-de-corazon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g beef heart, cleaned of silverskin and valves, cut in 3cm cubes (or beef sirloin while you gather nerve, but the heart is the dish)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The marinade: 4 tbsp ají panca paste (the mild, fruity dark-red Peruvian chile), 4 garlic cloves, 3 tbsp red wine vinegar, 1 tbsp cumin, oregano, salt, splash of beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flat skewers; the marinade doubles as the basting sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Boiled potatoes and giant corn (choclo) for the plate"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ají verde or a rocoto salsa alongside; The Saucier's green sauce moonlights perfectly here"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the heart cubes 4 h to overnight: ají panca stains them brick red and does the tenderizing.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Thread 4–5 cubes per flat skewer, snug.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over HIGH coals fast: 2–3 min a side, basting with the marinade: heart is lean muscle; medium is perfect, well-done is rubber and regret.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 2 min; serve skewers over potato slices with choclo and the green sauce. Eat from the stick, standing at the cart in spirit.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lima's anticucheras grill hearts at street corners each evening, and the smoke plume is the address; the dish is Afro-Peruvian heritage, born of enslaved cooks transforming discarded cuts into the country's beloved nightcap. Heart is the tenderest offal there is when cooked fast and hot: it is pure working muscle, no funk, all beef. The two-minute rule is the whole craft: anticuchos wait for no conversation.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","smoky","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, wood-smoke running underneath, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Anticuchos%20de%20Coraz%C3%B3n%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Anticuchos de Corazón"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Anticuchos de Corazón","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20Anticuchos%20de%20Coraz%C3%B3n","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Potato","Chiles","Corn","Oregano","Vinegar","Wine","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"pollo-a-la-brasa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole chicken (1.6kg), spatchcocked or trussed for the rotisserie"},{"kind":"item","text":"The black marinade: 3 tbsp soy sauce (yes, the chifa influence), 2 tbsp ají panca paste, 1 tbsp cumin, 1 tbsp dried oregano, 4 garlic cloves, 1 tbsp vinegar, 1 tsp black pepper, splash of dark beer or pisco"},{"kind":"item","text":"12–24 hours of marinating: the depth is non-negotiable"},{"kind":"item","text":"A rotisserie over coals if life is generous; otherwise a rack at 200°C with a flip and a final blast"},{"kind":"item","text":"The rite of service: fries, a simple salad, and ají verde in quantities that seem excessive and are not"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the marinade; massage over and UNDER the skin; refrigerate overnight: the soy and panca stain the bird to its name ('a la brasa' birds are famously mahogany-black at the edges).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rotisserie over steady coals ~75 min, or roast: skin-up at 200°C for 45 min, then 230°C for the final 15 to blister.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 15 min; the juices under that lacquered skin are the reputation.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Quarter with poultry shears; serve with fries, salad, and the green sauce within everyone's reach. Eating it with hands is standard; eating it without ají verde is unheard of.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Peru classifies pollo a la brasa as cultural patrimony with its own national day (the third Sunday of July), and the country eats it weekly, by the millions; the soy in the marinade is the fingerprint of Peru's Chinese-Peruvian (chifa) kitchens folded into a Swiss immigrant's 1950s rotisserie idea. The overnight marinade and the char-edged lacquer are the identity; the ají verde is the covenant. This is arguably the best roast chicken formula on earth, and Peru will not argue because Peru already knows.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Butchery basics","Trussing","Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","umami-deep","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Butchery basics","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i_ZkAHCR1D0","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Trussing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20truss%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pollo%20a%20la%20Brasa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pollo a la Brasa"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Pollo a la Brasa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20Pollo%20a%20la%20Brasa","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Oregano","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"picanha-na-brasa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole picanha (top sirloin cap / culotte), 1.2–1.5kg, fat cap fully intact: the cap IS the cut; a trimmed picanha is a demotion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse rock salt (sal grosso), the churrasco seasoning in its entirety"},{"kind":"item","text":"Long skewers if going rodízio-style: cut with the grain into thick bands and fold into C-shapes, fat outward"},{"kind":"item","text":"Or the whole-roast road: fat cap scored in a diamond, seared cap-down first"},{"kind":"item","text":"Farofa, vinagrete (tomato-onion salsa), and grilled bread alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt generously just before the fire: rock salt crusts; excess brushes off after.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Skewer road: fold the bands into Cs, fat on the outside curve; grill over medium-high coals, fat side catching first, turning until the crust is deep and the center rosy; SLICE the cooked outer layer off at the table and return the skewer to the fire, the rodízio rhythm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whole road: sear cap-down until the fat renders and bronzes, 8–10 min; flip, move to indirect heat to 52–54°C internal; rest 10.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice ACROSS the grain (the bands changed the grain direction: attend to it), each slice wearing its ribbon of golden fat. Vinagrete over; farofa for the juices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brazil's crown cut, the sirloin cap that US butchers long buried inside 'top sirloin' until churrascarias taught everyone better. The fat cap bastes the meat from above as it renders, which is why removing it is treated as vandalism from Porto Alegre to São Paulo. The slice-and-return rodízio method means every serving is an edge piece; there is no polite way to say the middle-slice people were wrong, but they were.","equipment":["Thermometer","Grill"],"techniques":["Scoring","Searing: the hard crust","Grilling over live coals","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Picanha%20na%20Brasa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Picanha na Brasa"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Picanha na Brasa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20Picanha%20na%20Brasa","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sirloin-tri-tip-and-picanha","term":"Sirloin, Tri-Tip & Picanha"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Bread"],"judgedBy":["scoring","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"braai-boerewors-with-chakalaka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg boerewors in its traditional single coil (beef-and-pork sausage with coriander seed, clove and nutmeg); by South African regulation, at least 90% meat, and the butcher's blend is his reputation"},{"kind":"item","text":"Two long skewers or a hinged grid (rooster) to keep the coil intact: the coil is never cut before cooking"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chakalaka: 1 onion, 2 carrots grated, 1 pepper, 2 tbsp curry powder, 1 can chopped tomatoes, 1 can baked beans, chili to taste, simmered 20 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pap (stiff white maize porridge, ugali's southern cousin, see the East African shelf) or fresh rolls (then it is a boerewors roll)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Wood fire burned to coals; the braai is wood-fire culture, and gas is tolerated the way decaf is tolerated"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pin the coil flat with crossed skewers or clamp it in the grid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over medium coals 15–20 min, turning the whole coil as one piece, until deep brown and just cooked through. NEVER prick it; the coriander-scented fat is the flavor, and a pricked wors weeps its soul into the fire.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chakalaka: fry onion and curry powder, add carrots, pepper, tomatoes, beans; simmer to a relish both saucy and chunky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the coil 5 min; cut into lengths at the table; serve with pap and chakalaka, or thumbed into a roll with the relish as the sauce.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"The braai is South Africa's fire religion: National Braai Day shares September 24th with Heritage Day by design, the argument being that the fire is the heritage. Boerewors ('farmer's sausage') is legally defined, coriander-forward, and judged by the snap; the unbroken coil turned in one piece is the braaier's dexterity exam. Chakalaka, born in Johannesburg's townships, is the spiced counterpoint no braai plate goes without.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","warm-spiced","gently hot","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, warm spice humming through the middle, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Braai%20Boerewors%20with%20Chakalaka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Braai Boerewors with Chakalaka"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Braai Boerewors with Chakalaka","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20Braai%20Boerewors%20with%20Chakalaka","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"culture-and-the-owners-job","term":"Culture & the Owner's Job"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Sausage","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Chiles","Bread","Coriander seed","Nutmeg","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"cevapi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground beef + 250g ground lamb (Sarajevo leans beef; the mix is the diaspora consensus), 20% fat, ground fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves grated to paste; 1 tsp salt; ½ tsp baking soda dissolved in 3 tbsp sparkling water, the bounce secret"},{"kind":"item","text":"NO breadcrumbs, NO egg: ćevapi bind by kneading alone"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lepinja or somun flatbread (a sturdy pita stands in), split and warmed IN the meat drippings"},{"kind":"item","text":"The court: raw onion, finely chopped; kajmak (clotted cream: mascarpone cut with butter and salt approximates) ; ajvar (roasted pepper relish)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the meat with garlic, salt and the soda water 5 full minutes until sticky and pale: the knead builds the spring; rest overnight in the fridge (non-negotiable; the texture forms in the cold).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into finger-sized cylinders, uniform as soldiers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot coals 8–10 min, rolling for even char: they should blister outside and stay juicy-bouncy within.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Steam-warm the split lepinja over the grill (dragged through drippings if the fire allows); pack 5–10 ćevapi inside with a snowdrift of raw onion; kajmak and ajvar alongside. Count is ordered by number; ten is a statement.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Balkans' shared flame and shared argument: Sarajevo, Banja Luka and Belgrade each claim the definitive ćevapi, differing on meat, size and bread, and agreeing only that everyone else is wrong. The overnight rest and the soda-water knead are the twin secrets behind the signature bounce (no filler, all spring). The bread wiped through drippings is the quiet best bite of the Balkans; the raw onion is not optional garnish but a load-bearing wall.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","rich","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=%C4%86evapi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ćevapi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Ćevapi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20%C4%86evapi","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mascarpone","term":"Mascarpone"},{"slug":"lamb-spring-hogget-and-mutton","term":"Lamb: Spring, Hogget & Mutton"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Bread","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"home-gyros","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg boneless pork shoulder (the Greek standard; chicken thighs also canonical), sliced in ½cm sheets"},{"kind":"item","text":"The marinade: 3 tbsp yogurt, 2 tbsp olive oil, 4 garlic cloves, 1 tbsp oregano, 2 tsp sweet paprika, 1 tsp cumin, lemon juice, salt, black pepper; overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE HOME SPIT: sheets threaded onto two vertical skewers anchored in half an onion, packed into a tight loaf: the loaf mimics the cone"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pita, warmed and lightly oiled; tzatziki (the Greek shelf holds it); tomato, red onion, and (per the Athens street standard) a few FRENCH FRIES tucked inside"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paprika-dusted paper wrap if you want the full corner-shop effect"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the sheets overnight; thread and pack them onto the skewer-and-onion stand into a dense cylinder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast at 200°C about 75–90 min until the exterior is deeply browned; rest 10.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Carve DOWNWARD in thin shavings, rotating the loaf: every pass takes the crisped edge; briefly crisp the shavings in a hot pan with the tray drippings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm pita; tzatziki dragged wide; meat, tomato, onion, and the fries INSIDE; wrap tight in paper, twist the base. Eat over the paper, leaning: the Athens posture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Gyros means 'turn': the same vertical-spit family as shawarma and al pastor, each stop on the migration wearing local dress; Greece claimed it with pork, oregano and tzatziki. The stacked-loaf home method is the honest cheat: pressed sheets carve into the same edge-crisped shavings the cone produces. And the fries inside are not a tourist corruption but the street standard: starch, sauce and char in one bite, which is the entire argument for the wrap.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Home%20Gyros%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Home Gyros"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Home Gyros","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20Home%20Gyros","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Corn","Lemon","Oregano","Yogurt","Bread","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"nyama-choma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg goat leg or shoulder in large bone-in pieces (or beef short ribs, but goat is the word's meaning in practice)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, the traditional seasoning in full; a baste of salt water is the old way"},{"kind":"item","text":"The modern option: a light rub of garlic, ginger, lemon and a little oil (purists abstain and are correct to)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Kachumbari (the East African shelf holds it) and ugali, the holy trinity of the choma joint"},{"kind":"item","text":"Steady charcoal, a grill grate, and no hurry whatsoever: choma is an afternoon, not a meal"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the meat; let it stand while the coals burn to steady gray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over MEDIUM coals, turning every 10 min and basting with salt water, 45–75 min depending on the cut; nyama choma is cooked past medium on purpose: firm, chewy at the edges, deeply browned; this is the texture the tradition wants, not a failure of it.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":5220,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest briefly; chop through the bone into chunks on a board. The board often comes to the table as the platter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with kachumbari heaped alongside, ugali for the hands, and more salt on the board. Eat with fingers, unhurried, over hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kenya and Tanzania's social contract in meat form. Nyama choma simply means 'roasted meat,' and the choma joint (butcher, grill, and bar in one) is where afternoons dissolve: you pick the cut at the counter, it arrives chopped on a board an hour later, and the kachumbari's lime bite against the salt-crusted goat is the whole architecture. The chew is canonical: this is meat for conversation pacing, and the last pieces gnawed from the bone are considered the best ones.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","bright","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, acid holding everything up to the light, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nyama%20Choma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nyama Choma"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Aaron Franklin on Nyama Choma","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aaron%20Franklin%20Nyama%20Choma","sub":"A YouTube search for Aaron Franklin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Lemon"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"maine-lobster-roll","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 live lobsters, 600g each (or 400g picked cooked meat; knuckle and claw are the sweet spots)"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE SCHISM. Maine style: meat lightly bound with 3 tbsp mayo, lemon, pinch of celery salt; Connecticut style: meat warmed in 60g melted butter, nothing else"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 top-split New England buns: the flat-sided kind exists to be griddled"},{"kind":"item","text":"More butter for griddling the bun sides"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives; potato chips alongside, per the shack constitution"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil lobsters in heavily salted water 8–9 min; ice bath; crack and pick, keeping the pieces LARGE: a lobster roll is judged by chunk size.","durationSec":540,"handsOnSec":780,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Choose your state: fold with cool mayo (Maine) or swirl through warm butter (Connecticut). There is no third option and no peace summit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter the flat sides of the buns; griddle to gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the meat in beyond reason; chives over. Serve immediately with chips and a view of water, real or imagined.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The top-split bun is structural genius (flat griddled walls, open top, meat displayed like jewelry), and the Maine-versus-Connecticut divide (cold mayo vs warm butter) is the rare food war where both sides are simply right on different afternoons. The one law both states share: the lobster is the dish; anything beyond fat, salt and lemon is embezzlement.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maine%20Lobster%20Roll%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maine Lobster Roll"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Maine Lobster Roll","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Maine%20Lobster%20Roll","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lobster","Potato","Celery","Lemon","Chives","Butter","Bread","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"maryland-crab-cakes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g jumbo lump blue crab, picked over GENTLY for shell: the lumps are the entire point; treat them like they're borrowed"},{"kind":"item","text":"The binder, minimal by law: 1 egg, 3 tbsp mayo, 1 tsp Dijon, 1 tsp Worcestershire, 2 tbsp crushed saltines ONLY"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp Old Bay, the Chesapeake's fingerprint, plus parsley and lemon zest"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for broiling or pan-searing"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges, tartar or remoulade; saltines and a cold beer complete the deckhouse setting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the wet binder with Old Bay; FOLD the crab through with your fingertips, maybe six folds total: the cakes should barely hold together; that fragility is the credential.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape 4 fat pucks without compressing; chill 1 h: the fridge is the real binder.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Broil with a brush of butter 10–12 min, or sear in butter 4 min a side, until bronzed.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lemon over; sauce beside. A fork should meet no resistance and find no filler.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Maryland judges a crab cake by an inverse law: the less it holds together, the better it is: filler-heavy cakes are dismissed as 'bread cakes' with a politeness that fools no one. Jumbo lump means the two big swimmer-fin muscles, and breaking them during mixing is the cardinal sin. The one-hour chill replaces the flour other states lean on; the cake is mostly crab, barely persuaded.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","The broiler: heat from above"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","charred","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maryland%20Crab%20Cakes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maryland Crab Cakes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Maryland Crab Cakes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Maryland%20Crab%20Cakes","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"crab-vocabulary","term":"Crab Vocabulary"},{"slug":"searing-and-pan-technique","term":"Searing & Pan Technique"}],"pantryItems":["Crab","Eggs","Lemon","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter","Beer/Cider","Mayonnaise","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"lowcountry-shrimp-and-grits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g large shrimp, peeled; SHELLS KEPT and simmered 15 min in 400ml water for the pan-sauce stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g stone-ground grits, cooked slow in milk-water 40 min with butter and a handful of sharp cheddar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g andouille or smoked sausage, sliced (or bacon, the older road), rendered crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 shallot, 2 garlic cloves, splash of lemon; 2 tbsp butter for mounting; scallions"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot sauce at the table: Charleston assumes it"}],"steps":[{"text":"Start the grits first and stir them like they owe you: 40 patient minutes; season boldly; hold warm and loose.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the sausage; sear the shrimp in the drippings 1 min a side; remove all.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shallot and garlic in the pan; the shrimp-shell stock poured in and reduced by half, scraping every browned promise off the steel; mount with cold butter and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shrimp and sausage back for 30 seconds; ladle over deep bowls of grits; scallions; hot sauce per conscience.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This was Charleston shrimp-boat breakfast, 'breakfast shrimp' cooked in their own quick shell stock over grits, decades before it conquered dinner menus, and the shell stock remains the dividing line: it is the difference between shrimp ON grits and shrimp AND grits, a marriage with a sauce as the vow. Stone-ground and forty minutes: instant grits under this pan sauce would be a resignation letter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lowcountry%20Shrimp%20and%20Grits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lowcountry Shrimp and Grits"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Lowcountry Shrimp and Grits","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Lowcountry%20Shrimp%20and%20Grits","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"reduction-nappe-and-mounting","term":"Reduction, Nappe & Mounting"},{"slug":"tasso-and-cajun-andouille","term":"Tasso & Cajun Andouille"}],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Sausage","Shrimp","Garlic","Scallion","Shallot","Lemon","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Cornmeal/Polenta","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"moules-frites","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg mussels, scrubbed, beards pulled, any gapers that refuse to close discarded without appeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE MARINIÈRE: 60g butter, 3 shallots, 3 garlic cloves, 250ml dry white wine (or Belgian blonde ale, the Brussels road), thyme, bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml cream, optional (the Normande upgrade); a storm of parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Frites: 1kg potatoes, cut thick, DOUBLE-FRIED; 150°C to blond, rest, then 190°C to bronze"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mayonnaise for the frites, Belgium is watching, and bread for the broth"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry round one on the potatoes; rest them while the mussels cook: the timing interlocks by design.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat shallots and garlic in butter; wine in, boiled hard 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mussels in; LID ON; high heat 4–6 min, shaking the pot twice, until they open, then off, immediately; every extra minute shrinks them.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream and parsley if using; frites into round two. Serve the pot at the table, an empty-shell bowl beside; use a hinged shell as tongs, dunk frites in mayo, and finish by drinking the broth like the sauce it is.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Belgium's national table setting (the pot, the shell-bowl, the cone of frites) and the empty-shell-as-tweezers technique is the local handshake that marks you as someone who has eaten these before. The mussels steam in minutes; the frites take two fries; the broth (wine, butter, mussel liquor) is the best sauce nobody had to make. Discard any mussel that stays shut after cooking, and any restaurant that serves them without frites.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moules%20Frites%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moules Frites"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Moules Frites","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Moules%20Frites","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Garlic","Shallot","Potato","Parsley","Thyme","Butter","Cream","Bread","Wine","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Mayonnaise","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"baja-fish-tacos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g firm white fish (cod, mahi), cut in finger-length strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"Beer batter: 150g flour + 1 tbsp cornstarch, 1 tsp baking powder, salt, cold Mexican lager to pancake-batter thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"The white sauce: 4 tbsp mayo + 4 tbsp crema or sour cream, lime juice, pinch of cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Shredded cabbage, never lettuce; the crunch must survive the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Corn tortillas, warmed and doubled; pico de gallo, pickled onions, lime, a smoky salsa"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the batter cold and thin at the last minute; the cornstarch and the lager's bubbles are the shatter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust the fish strips in flour; through the batter; fry at 185°C, 3–4 min, until amber and audibly crisp; drain on a rack.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm and stack the tortillas in pairs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build in strict order: cabbage floor, fish, white sauce ribboned over, pico, pickled onion, lime squeezed at the last second. Two bites of contrast (hot crisp, cool cream, sharp lime) or it isn't Baja.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born on the streets of Ensenada, the cart stands still argue over which one was first, where Japanese tempura technique met Mexican street logistics and made the fried fish taco. The architecture is thermal: hot shattering fish against cold cabbage and cool crema, assembled seconds before eating. A fish taco that sits is a fish taco that steams; the cart hands them over one at a time for exactly this reason.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baja%20Fish%20Tacos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baja Fish Tacos"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Baja Fish Tacos","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Baja%20Fish%20Tacos","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Cabbage","Corn","Lettuce/Greens","Lime","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Bread","Flour","Beer/Cider","Mayonnaise","Pickles","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"singapore-chilli-crab","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 mud crabs (~1kg each), cleaned and cracked into sections, claws pre-cracked for mercy (your fishmonger will do this; let them)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The rempah: 8 dried chilies soaked + 4 fresh, 6 shallots, 4 garlic, thumb of ginger, 1 tbsp belacan, blended"},{"kind":"item","text":"The sauce: 4 tbsp tomato ketchup + 2 tbsp chili sauce, 1 tbsp sugar, 500ml stock, 2 tbsp tamarind water"},{"kind":"item","text":"The finish: 1 beaten egg swirled in off the heat; the silken ribbons are the signature"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fried mantou buns (or thick toast): the sauce outranks the crab and demands a vehicle"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the rempah in oil until it splits and smells like the hawker centre, 8–10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Ketchup, chili sauce, sugar, stock, tamarind; simmer to a loose gravy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crab sections in; lid on; 8–10 min, tossing twice, until the shells blaze orange and the meat is just set.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"OFF the heat: stream in the egg, folding once. Ribbons, not scramble. Pile into a platter, sauce flooding; mantou standing by. Bibs are issued, not offered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented in 1956 by Cher Yam Tian from a pushcart: she swapped tomato sauce into stir-fried crab and Singapore built a national dish and a tourism pillar on it. The egg ribbon finish and the sweet-hot ketchup-chili gravy are the identity; the crab is arguably the delivery mechanism for the sauce, which is why the fried mantou for mopping are not a side but a load-bearing course. The mess is mandatory and the shirt is a calculated loss.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Singapore%20Chilli%20Crab%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Singapore Chilli Crab"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Singapore Chilli Crab","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Singapore%20Chilli%20Crab","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Eggs","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Tomato","Chiles","Bread","Fish sauce","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"pla-neung-manao","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole sea bass or barramundi (~800g), cleaned, scored to the bone three times a side"},{"kind":"item","text":"The dressing, made raw: 6 garlic cloves and 6–10 bird's eye chilies chopped fine, 5 tbsp lime juice, 3 tbsp fish sauce, 1 tbsp palm sugar, 3 tbsp of the fish's own steaming liquor"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemongrass stalks, bruised, laid under and inside the fish"},{"kind":"item","text":"A big handful of cilantro; thin lime slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"A steamer large enough, or a foil tent over a rack, and jasmine rice, non-negotiable"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay the fish on lemongrass in a deep plate that fits your steamer (the plate catches the precious liquor); steam 12–15 min until the flesh flakes at the bone.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"While it steams, stir the dressing: raw garlic, chili, lime, fish sauce, palm sugar; it should be sour FIRST, then hot, then sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Loosen the dressing with the hot steaming juices from the plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour it all over the fish; blanket with cilantro and lime slices; serve in the steaming plate. Spoon the pooled sauce over every serving of rice like it is the point, because it is.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bangkok's lime-steamed fish is the Thai counterargument to heavy sauces: the fish steams naked, and the raw lime-garlic-chili dressing hits it hot, cooking nothing and waking everything. Sour-first is the calibration: Thai tables rate this dish by how hard the first spoonful makes you sit up. The steaming liquor folded back into the dressing is the quiet professional move that ties fish and sauce into one voice.","equipment":["Steamer"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pla%20Neung%20Manao%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pla Neung Manao"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Pla Neung Manao","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Pla%20Neung%20Manao","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"steaming-and-en-papillote","term":"Steaming & En Papillote"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Cilantro","Lemongrass","Rice","Fish sauce","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"cantonese-steamed-fish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole fish, immaculately fresh (~700g: sea bass, flounder, tilapia), cleaned; freshness IS the recipe"},{"kind":"item","text":"3cm ginger: half in fine julienne for after, half in coins under the fish; 4 scallions: whites under, greens in long fine shreds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp light soy + 1 tbsp water + 1 tsp sugar, warmed, the seasoned soy"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp neutral oil, heated until it SHIMMERS and just smokes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro sprigs; rice, inevitably"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay the fish on ginger coins and scallion whites; steam over rolling water 8–10 min (8 per 500g is the old rule) until a chopstick slides into the thickest part.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"POUR OFF the fishy steaming liquid: unlike the Thai school, Cantonese doctrine discards it; the flavor arrives fresh in the next two steps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanket the fish with the ginger julienne and scallion greens; pour the warmed seasoned soy around (not over) the fish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The finale: the shimmering oil poured directly over the aromatics: the CRACKLE is the dish's applause; the herbs flash-fry against the fish's skin. Cilantro; serve whole, head toward the guest of honor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Cantonese steamed fish is a freshness verdict delivered in under half an hour: banquet cooks say the recipe has three ingredients: the fish, the fish, and the fish. The hot-oil pour is the signature technique (it blooms ginger and scallion in one hiss, cousin to the biang biang and gomen finishes elsewhere in this guide), and the timing rule is absolute: an extra ninety seconds of steam is the difference between silk and cotton.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20Steamed%20Fish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cantonese Steamed Fish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Cantonese Steamed Fish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Cantonese%20Steamed%20Fish","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Ginger","Scallion","Cilantro","Rice","Sugar"],"standard":{"slug":"cantonese-steamed-fish","marks":[{"id":"cantonese-steamed-fish#near","text":"Before it goes near the steamer: eyes clear and domed rather than sunken and cloudy, gills wet and red, the flesh springing back when pressed, and the smell nothing but clean seawater. This is the one fault that cannot be corrected downstream, so it is checked first and out loud."},{"id":"cantonese-steamed-fish#chopstick","text":"A chopstick into the thickest part behind the head meets no resistance, and the flesh parts from the backbone in whole sheets. At the bone it reads just-set white, faintly translucent, never chalky and never pink."},{"id":"cantonese-steamed-fish#steaming","text":"The steaming liquid has been poured off: no grey pool under the fish. The seasoned soy sits clear around it, at a level below the flesh, so the skin stays unglazed."},{"id":"cantonese-steamed-fish#aromatics","text":"The aromatics were cooked by the oil, not the steam: ginger julienne curled and translucent at the tips, scallion greens collapsed and shining, and there was an audible crackle at the pour. Slack raw-green shreds sitting in oil mean the oil went on below smoking."},{"id":"cantonese-steamed-fish#whole","text":"Whole and unbroken on the platter: skin intact from head to tail, no flesh torn where the fish was lifted off the steaming plate, head to the guest of honour. A bite from the middle of the fillet tastes of fish first; the soy registers only when the flesh is dragged through it."}],"fault":"It is steamed to the timer instead of to the chopstick and comes out ninety seconds long; the fillet shreds into dry cotton fibre when lifted, and a milky pool of squeezed-out juice spreads under the fish and dilutes the soy."}},{"slug":"miso-glazed-black-cod","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 black cod (sablefish) fillets, 180g each, skin on: the fish's buttery fat is why this dish became famous"},{"kind":"item","text":"The saikyo marinade: 150g white miso + 4 tbsp mirin + 4 tbsp sake + 3 tbsp sugar, whisked over low heat and COOLED"},{"kind":"item","text":"48–72 HOURS of marinating: the number is the recipe"},{"kind":"item","text":"A broiler or 230°C oven; the sugar wants to burn, so watch like a hawk in the final minutes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pickled ginger shoot or simple steamed rice and greens"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the marinade warm until the sugar dissolves; cool completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bury the fillets in it; refrigerate 2–3 days, turning once a day: the miso cures, seasons and sweetens to the bone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe (don't rinse) the excess; broil on oiled foil 8–10 min until the surface caramelizes to lacquered mahogany with charred corners and the flesh flakes in large petals.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 2 min; serve with rice. The fish should taste seasoned throughout, not sauced; that is the 72 hours talking.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36120,"estimated":true}],"note":"Saikyo-yaki is a centuries-old Kyoto preservation technique (sweet white miso curing rich fish) that Nobu Matsuhisa turned into arguably the most imitated restaurant dish of the modern era. The multi-day cure is non-negotiable: overnight gives you glazed fish; three days gives you transformed fish. Black cod's luxurious fat is the perfect canvas, and the charred edges are not a flaw but the caramelized signature the marinade exists to produce.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The broiler: heat from above","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","charred","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Miso-Glazed%20Black%20Cod%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Miso-Glazed Black Cod"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Miso-Glazed Black Cod","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Miso-Glazed%20Black%20Cod","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"brining-wet-dry-and-cures","term":"Brining: Wet, Dry & Cures"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Ginger","Butter","Rice","Miso","Wine","Sugar","Pickles"],"standard":{"slug":"miso-glazed-black-cod","marks":[{"id":"miso-glazed-black-cod#lacquered","text":"Lacquered mahogany across the surface with genuine black at the corners and the raised edges; a flat even gold means it never sat close enough to the element, or the cure was too short to give the sugars anything to work with."},{"id":"miso-glazed-black-cod#thin","text":"A thin skin of glaze, wiped and not rinsed: no clods of scraped miso baked hard onto the fillet, and equally no washed-looking bare patches that took no colour at all."},{"id":"miso-glazed-black-cod#flesh","text":"The flesh breaks into large petals a centimetre thick under the edge of a spoon, the fillet lying flat rather than buckled, with no white curd of albumin squeezed out along the seams."},{"id":"miso-glazed-black-cod#white","text":"White and glistening to the centre with the fat visibly running, and no dry chalky seam sitting under the crust."},{"id":"miso-glazed-black-cod#bite","text":"A bite taken from the middle, with no glaze on it, still tastes salted and faintly sweet: an unseasoned centre means the fish went in at 24 hours, not 48 to 72."}],"fault":"Black before it is cooked, because the excess marinade was left on and the fillet sat too close to the element: bitter carbon on top, cool untransformed flesh under it."}},{"slug":"linguine-alle-vongole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg small clams (vongole veraci, manila or littlenecks), purged 1 h in salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g linguine or spaghetti"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 garlic cloves, sliced thin; big pinch chili flakes; 150ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil, divided: the finish pour is part of the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"A mountain of parsley; NO CHEESE: the Neapolitan line in the sand"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm garlic and chili in half the oil until fragrant, never brown; wine in; clams in; lid on; 3–4 min until they open. Pull the clams; STRAIN and keep every drop of the liquor; shuck half, leave half in shell.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the pasta THREE minutes short in barely-salted water (the clam liquor is saline enough).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish the pasta IN the pan: clam liquor plus splashes of pasta water, tossing over heat as the starch and oil emulsify into a clinging, glossy brodetto.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Clams back, parsley, the remaining raw oil swirled in off heat. Toss; serve instantly in warm bowls. Cheese offered here is a test; declining is the answer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The dish is a masterclass in emulsione: the sauce is nothing but clam liquor, starch, oil and motion, built in the final three minutes of the pasta's cooking, which is why the pasta must finish IN the pan, drinking the sea. Bianco (no tomato) is the purist's Naples standard. The half-shucked, half-shelled service is both practical and honest: some bites are work, and those are the ones you remember.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Shucking shellfish","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","gently hot","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Shucking shellfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20shuck%20oysters%20clams%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Linguine%20alle%20Vongole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Linguine alle Vongole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Linguine alle Vongole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Linguine%20alle%20Vongole","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Garlic","Chiles","Parsley","Cheese (any)","Pasta","Wine","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"linguine-alle-vongole","marks":[{"id":"linguine-alle-vongole#garlic","text":"Garlic slices are pale ivory in the sauce and still hold their shape; tan flecks mean the base was browned before the wine arrived."},{"id":"linguine-alle-vongole#shell","text":"Every shell on the plate is fully open (a shut clam is a fault, not a garnish), half in shell and half shucked through the pasta, and the meats are still plump enough to fill their shells rather than shrunk to grey nubs from sitting in the pan through the reduction."},{"id":"linguine-alle-vongole#sauce","text":"The sauce is emulsified, not oiled: it wears the strands with a cloudy gloss, and tilting the bowl leaves a clinging film rather than a clear ring of oil at the rim."},{"id":"linguine-alle-vongole#strand","text":"A strand bitten through shows a hairline of firmer paste at its centre and no white chalk core: the last three minutes happened in the pan, not the water."},{"id":"linguine-alle-vongole#nothing","text":"Nothing grits against the teeth in the final spoonful, proof the clams were purged and the liquor strained, and the bowl needs no salt at the table, because the salt came from the liquor."}],"fault":"A broken or watery sauce, from finishing the pasta in the water and pouring the clam liquor over it at the end rather than tossing the two together over heat; the oil separates into a slick at the rim and the pasta sits in clam water instead of wearing it."}},{"slug":"proper-fish-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g mixed fish: salmon, smoked haddock (the flavor spine), firm white fish; poached 5 min in 500ml milk with bay and onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g raw prawns; 3 boiled eggs, quartered, the traditional punctuation"},{"kind":"item","text":"The sauce: 50g butter, 50g flour, the strained poaching milk, 1 tsp Dijon, big handful parsley, squeeze of lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg floury potatoes, mashed with 100g butter and hot milk, seasoned like you mean it"},{"kind":"item","text":"A grating of cheddar over the mash; peas alongside, per national instinct"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the fish gently in the milk; lift out and flake into large pieces into the baking dish, evicting any bones; scatter prawns and eggs among them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roux, then the smoky poaching milk, whisked to a silken parsley sauce; mustard and lemon; pour over: the sauce should hug, not drown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crown with mash, sealing to the dish edges (the seal prevents eruptions); furrow with a fork; cheese over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 200°C, 30–35 min until bronzed at the ridges and bubbling at the borders. Rest 10 minutes: fish pie lava is a known domestic hazard.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false}],"note":"The British fish pie is comfort engineering: the smoked haddock seasons the poaching milk, the milk becomes the sauce, and nothing is wasted, one flavor circulating through the whole dish. The fork furrows brown, the valleys stay soft (shepherd's pie logic, chapters away), and the boiled egg quarters are the old-fashioned touch that divides generations and should win. Assemble it a day ahead; it bakes better from cold.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Proper%20Fish%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Proper Fish Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Proper Fish Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Proper%20Fish%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","White fish","Salmon","Eggs","Smoked fish","Onion","Potato","Peas","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"salt-baked-whole-fish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole fish (1–1.2kg, branzino, dorade, snapper), gutted and scaled, skin ON and intact: the skin is the wrapper's inner wall"},{"kind":"item","text":"2kg coarse sea salt + 3 egg whites + 2 tbsp water, mixed to wet-sand consistency"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon slices, fennel fronds or thyme, tucked in the cavity only"},{"kind":"item","text":"A serving-worthy baking tray and a spoon and knife for the tableside excavation"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ladolemono or just olive oil and lemon: the fish emerges needing almost nothing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bed a 1cm layer of the salt mixture on the tray; lay the herbed fish on it; pack the rest over and around into a complete white tomb, sealing to the tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 200°C: 25–30 min for 1kg (an inserted skewer, held 5 seconds, should come out warm to the lip).","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 5 min; then the theater: CRACK the hardened dome with a spoon handle and lift the crust away in plates: the skin peels off with it, leaving steamed-in-its-own-moisture flesh.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the fillets from the bone; oil and lemon over. The fish will not be salty; it will be the most evenly cooked fish of your life.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The salt crust is not a seasoning but an oven within the oven, a hermetic clay that steams the fish in its own juices at perfect, gentle pressure; the technique is ancient Mediterranean and essentially foolproof, which is why restaurants charge theater prices for it. The egg white sets the dome so it cracks in satisfying slabs. Guests assume sorcery; the cook knows it is the easiest whole fish in this entire guide.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salt-Baked%20Whole%20Fish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salt-Baked Whole Fish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Salt-Baked Whole Fish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Salt-Baked%20Whole%20Fish","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Eggs","Lemon","Thyme"],"standard":{"slug":"salt-baked-whole-fish","marks":[{"id":"salt-baked-whole-fish#salt","text":"Before the salt goes on: skin whole and unbroken, no knife nicks along the flank or at the vent, and the cavity holding lemon and herbs only. Not one grain of salt goes inside the fish."},{"id":"salt-baked-whole-fish#dome","text":"The dome comes out hard, matte and pale, still sealed to the tray, with no split that steamed open and no browned damp patch where the pack ran thin, and it breaks into slabs under a spoon handle, the skin lifting away stuck to the crust and leaving the flesh clean, unmarked and carrying no salt grains."},{"id":"salt-baked-whole-fish#skewer","text":"A skewer into the thickest part, held five seconds, comes out warm on the lip: cold means it goes back in before the crust is touched, hot means it has gone past."},{"id":"salt-baked-whole-fish#tail","text":"The tail end is as moist as the shoulder: evenness is the whole claim of the crust, and a dry tail says the pack was thin at that end."},{"id":"salt-baked-whole-fish#taste","text":"Taste at the belly seam first: that is where salt gets in if the skin was nicked, and one blown bite there condemns the fish."}],"fault":"Salt packed into the cavity as well as over the fish: it cures straight through the thin belly wall while the crust is doing its work, and the fillet arrives brined rather than steamed."}},{"slug":"salmon-en-papillote","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 salmon fillets, 160g each, pin-boned; skin off for this method"},{"kind":"item","text":"The bed: fennel shaved thin, leek julienne, or asparagus: quick-cooking vegetables only; the packet forgives nothing slow"},{"kind":"item","text":"Per packet: splash of white wine or vermouth, 1 tsp butter, lemon slices, thyme or dill, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 large squares of parchment, folded and crimped into half-moon seals"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scissors at the table: each diner opens their own cloud"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fold each parchment square in half; open; vegetables on one side of the crease, salmon on top, seasoning, lemon, butter, wine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the paper over and crimp the edge in small overlapping pleats, twisting the final corner tight: the seal IS the oven; a leaky packet is a tray-bake.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200°C, 10–12 min: the packets balloon like proud little zeppelins.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve the packets sealed and puffed; snip open at the table: the escaping herb-lemon steam is the dish's introduction, and the pooled juices at the bottom are its sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Papillote is steam-poaching wearing gift wrap: the sealed paper traps wine, butter and the fish's own moisture into a self-basting micro-climate, and the tableside opening delivers the aroma at its absolute peak: a trick restaurants have leaned on for a century because it works on every sense at once. The rules are two: fast vegetables only, and a genuinely crimped seal. Master the pleats and you own the most impressive ten-minute dinner in existence.","equipment":["Parchment","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salmon%20en%20Papillote%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salmon en Papillote"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Salmon en Papillote","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Salmon%20en%20Papillote","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pressure-and-slow-cooking","term":"Pressure & Slow Cooking"}],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Leek","Asparagus","Lemon","Dill","Thyme","Butter","Wine"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"pescado-a-la-veracruzana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick fillets or 1 whole red snapper (huachinango, the classic), salted and rubbed with lime 15 min ahead"},{"kind":"item","text":"The sauce: 6 ripe tomatoes chopped, 1 onion, 4 garlic cloves, simmered down"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE MEDITERRANEAN-MEXICAN HANDSHAKE: ½ cup green olives, 3 tbsp capers, 2 tbsp raisins, 3–4 pickled güero chilies (or pickled jalapeños), bay, oregano, cinnamon pinch"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml olive oil; splash of the chile pickling liquid"},{"kind":"item","text":"White rice and warm tortillas"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stew onion and garlic in olive oil; tomatoes in; simmer 15 min to a loose, bright sauce.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Olives, capers, raisins, pickled chilies, herbs and the splash of brine; the sauce should ring salty, sweet, briny and gently hot all at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Nestle the lime-cured fish into the sauce; spoon it over; lid on; poach gently 10–12 min until the flesh flakes.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 5 min in the pan; serve from it, sauce ladled generously, rice catching the excess.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Veracruz was Spain's front door into Mexico, and this dish is the meeting minutes: Mediterranean olives, capers and olive oil folded into a New World tomato sauce with pickled chiles casting the deciding vote. The raisins are the old Moorish echo most recipes north of the port forget. It is Mexico's great poached-fish dish (gentle heat, briny-bright sauce) and proof that fusion cooking is not a trend but a four-century-old habit.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pescado%20a%20la%20Veracruzana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pescado a la Veracruzana"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Pescado a la Veracruzana","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Pescado%20a%20la%20Veracruzana","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fermentation-and-pickling","term":"Fermentation & Pickling"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Olives","Capers","Lime","Dried fruit","Oregano","Bay leaf","Rice","Bread","Pickles","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"garides-saganaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g large shrimp, peeled, tails on (shells simmered 10 min in a splash of water if you have the patience: the liquor deepens the sauce)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 ripe tomatoes, grated (skins left behind in your hand) or 1 can crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 3 garlic cloves, big pinch chili flakes; 1 tsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml ouzo, the flambé moment and the anise thread"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g feta in large chunks; olive oil; parsley; bread with a mission"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soften onion in olive oil; garlic and chili; tomatoes in; simmer 10 min to a thick, sweet sauce (shell liquor in if made).","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shrimp in for 1 min; ouzo poured over and, if the stove allows, touched with flame for ten dramatic seconds; otherwise simmered 1 min to burn off.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":60,"estimated":true},{"text":"Feta chunks nestled on top; lid on 2 min: the cheese should slump and half-melt, never dissolve.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oregano and parsley over; the pan goes straight to the table with bread. The feta-tomato-ouzo pockets are the prize; distribute them justly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Saganaki names the two-handled pan, not the dish: anything served bubbling in one earns the title, and the shrimp version is the taverna's seaside flex. The ouzo is structural: its anise bridges sweet tomato and briny feta the way pastis serves bouillabaisse a few shelves over. Undercook the shrimp slightly before the lid goes on; they finish in the steam, and rubbery shrimp under perfect feta is a tragedy in one act.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Flambé","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","herb-fresh","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Flambé","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=flambe%20technique%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garides%20Saganaki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Garides Saganaki"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Garides Saganaki","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Garides%20Saganaki","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Parsley","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Feta","Bread","Clove"],"standard":{"slug":"garides-saganaki","marks":[{"id":"garides-saganaki#colour","text":"No colour on the aromatics. Check before the tomatoes go in: the onion is translucent and slack with no gold at the edges and the garlic has not taken any colour. In the finished pan there are no brown flecks showing against the red."},{"id":"garides-saganaki#sauce","text":"The sauce is reduced far enough that a spoon dragged across the pan bottom leaves a track that holds a beat, and a film of orange oil has come to the surface."},{"id":"garides-saganaki#feta","text":"The feta is still in liftable chunks with slumped, glossy shoulders. Nothing has dissolved; the tomato around it is smooth and red, not pink and grainy."},{"id":"garides-saganaki#shrimp","text":"The shrimp are loose C-curls, opaque through the back, tails bright. They finished under the lid, so none has tightened into a ring."},{"id":"garides-saganaki#ouzo","text":"The ouzo reads as a thread of anise behind the tomato with no alcohol sting at the back of the throat. Taste the sauce with a crumb of feta on the spoon rather than without: the pan should land on the right side of salty once the cheese is in, and never before it."}],"fault":"A thin, weeping pan, because the tomatoes went in short of reduced. The shrimp liquor and the lid's condensation finish the job, the feta floats instead of nestling, and the bread comes away wet rather than loaded."}},{"slug":"kedgeree","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g smoked haddock, poached 5 min in 400ml milk with a bay leaf, milk kept, per this guide's standing tradition"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g basmati rice; 1 onion, sliced, cooked sweet in 50g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp curry powder + ½ tsp turmeric, bloomed in the butter (the fifteen-second bloom, the Singapore-noodle law applies here too)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 boiled eggs, quartered, jammy, not chalky; big handful parsley + cilantro"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges; a pot of tea; optionally a spoon of cream stirred through"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach and flake the haddock; cook the basmati in the strained poaching milk topped up with water: the rice absorbs the smoke.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the onion in butter; bloom the spices until the kitchen smells like the East India Company's ledger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold rice, fish and half the herbs through the spiced onion gently: large flakes intact, everything stained gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Eggs on top, remaining herbs, lemon squeezed over. Serve warm, not piping: kedgeree is a breakfast that refuses to be rushed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Khichri, India's rice-and-lentil comfort, sailed home with colonial officers, traded its lentils for smoked haddock and eggs, and settled onto the Victorian breakfast sideboard as kedgeree; the dish is empire history you can eat, with the smoked-milk-into-rice trick doing the same one-flavor-circulating work as the fish pie two recipes over. Anglo-Indian families kept the truest versions; the jammy egg and the spice bloom are the two details that separate revival from relic.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","herb-fresh","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kedgeree%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kedgeree"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Kedgeree","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Kedgeree","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"curry-leaf","term":"Curry Leaf"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Eggs","Smoked fish","Onion","Lemon","Cilantro","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter","Milk","Cream","Rice","Noodles","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"chargrilled-oysters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"24 fresh oysters on the half shell, liquor retained, Gulf oysters if you can get them; this is their dish"},{"kind":"item","text":"The butter: 200g softened with 6 garlic cloves grated, 1 tsp Creole seasoning, lemon juice, parsley, a shake of hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g finely grated parmesan-romano mix for the crust"},{"kind":"item","text":"A grill hot enough to be taken seriously: the flare-up is part of the method"},{"kind":"item","text":"French bread for the shells' buttery aftermath; this round stays in New Orleans"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the compound butter smooth; the parmesan mix stands separate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oysters over the hottest part of the grill; a generous spoon of butter onto each: it will melt, pool, and overflow into the fire; the FLAMES licking the shells are seasoning, not accident.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cheese over each as the butter bubbles; lid down 2–3 min until the edges of the oysters curl and the cheese freckles.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off with tongs (the shells hold heat like grudges); more bread than seems dignified. The post-oyster ritual: bread wiped through each empty shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Drago's in Metairie invented these in the 1990s and half the Gulf Coast followed: the charred-butter flare is the actual flavor engine, smoking the garlic butter as it overflows. It is the cooked-oyster gateway for the raw-averse and a hometown answer to Rockefeller's guarded secrecy: everything visible, everything on fire, bread mandatory. Since this guide already holds the Rockefeller, consider the set complete: one oyster in a tuxedo, one in flip-flops, both correct.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny","charred","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":37,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chargrilled%20Oysters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chargrilled Oysters"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Chargrilled Oysters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Chargrilled%20Oysters","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Bread","Hot sauce","Clove","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"escabecheng-isda","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole tilapia, snapper or pompano (~800g), scored, salted, and fried WHOLE in hot oil until the skin crisps and the tail goes glassy"},{"kind":"item","text":"The sweet-sour: 100ml cane vinegar + 3 tbsp sugar + 2 tbsp soy + 150ml water, simmered"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ginger in fine matchsticks, generous; 1 onion in petals; 1 red pepper + 1 carrot in ribbons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cornstarch slurry to gloss; scallions"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice, and the understanding that the sauce is going on the rice too"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the whole fish in 2cm of hot oil, 5–6 min a side, until deeply golden and structurally proud; drain and stage on the platter.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In a fresh pan: sizzle the ginger, then onion, pepper and carrot 2 min, bright and still snapping.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Vinegar mixture in; boil 1 min (the boil mellows the vinegar's edge); gloss with the slurry to a pourable sheen.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the hot sweet-sour and its vegetable ribbons over the crisp fish AT the table: the hiss where sauce meets fried skin is the dish's opening line. Serve immediately: half the fish crisp, half sauce-softened, both correct.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Escabeche crossed from Spain through the galleon trade and the Philippines rewired it: where the Mediterranean original is a cold vinegar preservation, the Filipino version is served hot, sweetened with cane sugar, and built on the crisp-versus-sauced tension of a whole fried fish dressed at the last second. The ginger is the Filipino signature; the pour-over timing is the craft. It is the same word wearing a completely different climate.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Escabecheng%20Isda%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Escabecheng Isda"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Escabecheng Isda","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Escabecheng%20Isda","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Carrot","Bell pepper","Rice","Vinegar","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"whole-grilled-fish-with-ladolemono","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole fish (dorade, branzino, ~500g each), gutted, scaled, patted DESERT-DRY inside and out; dry skin is the entire anti-sticking strategy"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse salt; olive oil for a light rub; lemon halves and oregano sprigs in the cavities"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE LADOLEMONO: 100ml best olive oil + juice of 1½ lemons + 1 tsp dried Greek oregano + pinch of salt, whisked to a loose emulsion at the last minute"},{"kind":"item","text":"A grill grate scrubbed clean and oiled at the final moment; real coals if the day allows"},{"kind":"item","text":"Patience at the flip: the fish releases itself when ready and not one second before"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry, salt and lightly oil the fish; stuff the cavities; let them come toward room temperature while the coals settle to medium-high.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay diagonal on the hot clean grate; DO NOT TOUCH for 6–8 min; attempting an early flip is how skin dies; when ready, the fish lifts freely.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"One flip; 6–8 min more, until the dorsal fin pulls out with no resistance, the Aegean doneness test.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the ladolemono; fillet the fish tableside or serve whole; spoon the emulsion over generously. The sauce is four ingredients and the fish is one; that ratio is the philosophy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Greek island taverna's entire seafood program is this recipe executed with conviction: pristine fish, live fire, and ladolemono, the oil-lemon emulsion that is less a sauce than a magnifying glass. The two techniques that matter travel to every grilled fish you will ever make: bone-dry skin on a clean hot oiled grate, and the no-touch rule until the fish volunteers. The fin-pull test predates thermometers and has never been wrong.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Filleting fish","Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","charred","briny","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, char used as punctuation, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Filleting fish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20fillet%20a%20fish%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Whole%20Grilled%20Fish%20with%20Ladolemono%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Whole Grilled Fish with Ladolemono"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Whole Grilled Fish with Ladolemono","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Whole%20Grilled%20Fish%20with%20Ladolemono","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"doneness-by-touch-sight-and-sound","term":"Doneness by Touch, Sight & Sound"},{"slug":"boning-fillet-and-the-butchery-blades","term":"Boning, Fillet & the Butchery Blades"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Lemon","Oregano"],"standard":{"slug":"whole-grilled-fish-with-ladolemono","marks":[{"id":"whole-grilled-fish-with-ladolemono#skin","text":"Skin whole from gill to tail, striped with dark bars, and the grate clean when the fish comes off: nothing of the fish left welded to the bars."},{"id":"whole-grilled-fish-with-ladolemono#dorsal","text":"The dorsal fin pulls out with no resistance before the fish leaves the coals, and the reading holds up when it is opened: at the shoulder the flesh is opaque and slips off the pin bones, and the line along the spine has set from red to pale grey."},{"id":"whole-grilled-fish-with-ladolemono#fillet","text":"The top fillet lifts away in one piece and the backbone comes out whole, leaving the underside fillet intact."},{"id":"whole-grilled-fish-with-ladolemono#ladolemono","text":"The ladolemono reaches the plate as one liquid: cloudy, loose enough to pour, thick enough to coat, and spooned over within a minute of the whisk. Clear oil floating on a lemon layer means it was made ahead and sat."},{"id":"whole-grilled-fish-with-ladolemono#piece","text":"A piece of fish eaten before any sauce touches it is already seasoned to the middle, skin and cavity both: the ladolemono is bringing acid and oil, not the salt."}],"fault":"Turned before it let go: half the skin stays on the grate, the flank tears open, and the exposed flesh dries over the coals through the second half of the cook."}},{"slug":"calamari-fritti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g squid, cleaned: bodies in 1cm rings, tentacles left whole (the tentacles are the connoisseur's share)"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE TENDERNESS INSURANCE: 30 min soak in 250ml milk or buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"The dredge: 150g flour + 50g fine semolina or rice flour (the lasting-crunch conspiracy, once again), salt, pepper, pinch of cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying at a strict 190°C; work in small batches"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges, mandatory, and a lemon-caper aioli or marinara for the table"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the squid in milk; drain and shake off: damp, not wet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge in the seasoned flour mix, tossing in a bag or bowl; sieve off the excess: a thin coat fries lacy; a thick one fries leaden.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 190°C in small batches NINETY SECONDS ONLY: golden, curled, and out; crowding drops the temperature and the whole batch pays.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rack, never paper; salt in the air; lemon squeezed over while hissing. Serve within five minutes of the fryer; calamari holds a grudge against waiting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Squid obeys the two-clock law: under two minutes or over forty: everything between is rubber bands. Fritti lives on the first clock, which is why the ninety-second batch discipline is the entire recipe, and why restaurant calamari fails the moment it queues under a heat lamp. The milk soak and the semolina cut are the two quiet upgrades; the tentacles crisp into frilled lace and should be distributed by seniority or seized by speed.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Calamari%20Fritti%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Calamari Fritti"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen Rennie on Calamari Fritti","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%20Rennie%20Calamari%20Fritti","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen Rennie and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Squid/Octopus","Chiles","Capers","Lemon","Milk","Buttermilk","Flour","Semolina","Mayonnaise"],"standard":{"slug":"calamari-fritti","marks":[{"id":"calamari-fritti#reads","text":"The oil reads a true 190°C as the squid goes in and climbs back to it before the next batch; a batch that drags it below about 170°C is already lost and no amount of extra time will recover it."},{"id":"calamari-fritti#pale","text":"Pale straw gold, never tan. The dredge sits as a lacy film that still shows the shape of the ring beneath it, with no shaggy clumps of loose flour fried alongside."},{"id":"calamari-fritti#rings","text":"Rings hold an open curl and the tentacles are frilled and separate; a fused clump on the rack means the batch went in crowded."},{"id":"calamari-fritti#ring","text":"A ring parts in a single bite with no rebound against the teeth, the flesh inside white and moist rather than chalky."},{"id":"calamari-fritti#undersides","text":"Undersides as crisp as tops with a dry plate under the pile, salt clinging to the crust and none loose in the bottom of the bowl, and the plate on the table within five minutes of the fryer, nothing waiting under a lamp."}],"fault":"The oil fell below 190°C because too much went in at once: the coating drinks fat instead of setting, and the squid stews long enough in the lukewarm bath to tighten, so the same bite is both greasy and rubbery."}},{"slug":"new-york-cheesecake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900g cream cheese, GENUINELY room temperature; cold cheese is the number one cause of lumps and cracks"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g sugar; 4 eggs + 1 yolk; 200ml sour cream; 1 tbsp vanilla; 2 tbsp flour; lemon zest"},{"kind":"item","text":"The crust: 200g graham crackers or digestives crushed + 90g melted butter + pinch salt, pressed and pre-baked 10 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 23cm springform, base wrapped in a double foil skirt"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE WATER BATH, and an overnight in the fridge; both are the recipe"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat cheese and sugar on LOW until just smooth; eggs one at a time, scraping constantly: every minute of high-speed beating is air that becomes a crack later.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sour cream, vanilla, flour, zest; pour over the baked crust; set the wrapped pan in a roasting tray of hot water halfway up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 160°C, 60–70 min: the edges set, the center jiggles like a water bed in a 5cm circle: pull it there; oven OFF, door cracked, one hour of slow descent.","durationSec":4200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill overnight, uncovered. Slice with a hot wet knife, wiped between cuts. Dense, ivory, level as a lake: that is New York.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Basque cheesecake three cards over breaks every rule on purpose; the New York breaks none: low speed, water bath, slow cool, long chill, each step an anti-crack insurance policy, because the New York standard is a flat, dense, unburnt plateau. Lindy's and Junior's built empires on the difference. The overnight chill is not patience theater: the texture literally does not exist until hour twelve.","equipment":["Springform","Sheet pan","Bain-marie","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The water bath","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","fiery","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20York%20Cheesecake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New York Cheesecake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on New York Cheesecake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20New%20York%20Cheesecake","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"knife-cuts-the-classical-ladder","term":"Knife Cuts: The Classical Ladder"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath","the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"profiteroles-pate-a-choux-master","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"The choux: 125ml water + 125ml milk, 100g butter, pinch salt, 1 tsp sugar, 150g flour, 4 eggs (about: the batter tells you)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Vanilla ice cream or crème pâtissière for filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"The sauce: 150g dark chocolate + 150ml hot cream, whisked to a pourable ganache"},{"kind":"item","text":"A piping bag; a wet finger for the peaks"},{"kind":"item","text":"An oven you will NOT open for 25 minutes, on your honor"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil water, milk, butter, salt, sugar; dump in the flour and beat over heat 2 min until the panade pulls from the pan and films the bottom: this dry-out step is the puff's foundation.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off heat, cool 3 min; beat in eggs one at a time; STOP when the batter falls from the spoon in a slow V. You may not need all of the last egg.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe walnut-sized mounds; wet-finger the peaks flat; bake 200°C 25 min WITHOUT OPENING, then 170°C 10 min to dry the walls; pierce each and cool.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split; a scoop of ice cream inside; stack in a bowl; warm ganache poured over at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Choux is the only pastry leavened almost purely by steam, which is why the two disciplines are absolute: cook the panade dry so it can drink the eggs, and never open the oven while the steam is doing the lifting; one peek and the puffs sigh flat forever. The V-test beats every egg count ever printed. Master this dough and you own profiteroles, éclairs, gougères and the croquembouche; it is four doors behind one handle.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":["Choux pastry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","earthy","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Choux pastry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=choux%20pastry%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Profiteroles%20(P%C3%A2te%20%C3%A0%20Choux%20Master)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Profiteroles (Pâte à Choux Master)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Profiteroles (Pâte à Choux Master)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Profiteroles%20(P%C3%A2te%20%C3%A0%20Choux%20Master)","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ganache-bloom-and-chocolate-craft","term":"Ganache, Bloom & Chocolate Craft"},{"slug":"pate-and-terrine","term":"Pâté & Terrine"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"standard":{"slug":"profiteroles-pate-a-choux-master","marks":[{"id":"profiteroles-pate-a-choux-master#oven","text":"Before the oven: the batter falls from the spoon in a slow V rather than a blob or a ribbon, and each piped mound holds a defined dome that settles under a wet finger without spreading toward its neighbour."},{"id":"profiteroles-pate-a-choux-master#puff","text":"Each puff is round and roughly double its piped height, with no baked-on peak and no flat sunken side; the tray reads as one size."},{"id":"profiteroles-pate-a-choux-master#colour","text":"Colour is even gold-brown over the dome and carries down to the crease where it meets the base: a blond, soft-looking waist there means it came out early."},{"id":"profiteroles-pate-a-choux-master#minutes","text":"Ten minutes on the rack and nothing has sunk or gone soft: pressed at the side the wall resists, then cracks. Split one and it is a single hollow cavity with a few wisps of webbing, walls dry to the touch, no wet dough sitting in the base."},{"id":"profiteroles-pate-a-choux-master#ganache","text":"The ganache coats the back of a spoon and drapes over the stack in one glossy sheet; two minutes under it, the shells still crack."}],"fault":"They come out at the 25-minute mark without the low dry-out leg and are left unpierced: the walls are still damp inside, their own trapped steam soaks them on the rack, and within ten minutes crisp shells have gone to chewy leather that turns to sponge under the ganache."}},{"slug":"tarte-au-citron","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pâte sucrée: 250g flour, 100g icing sugar, 125g cold butter, 1 egg, pinch salt; blind-baked to full biscuit gold"},{"kind":"item","text":"The cream: zest of 3 lemons rubbed into 180g sugar (the perfume step), 160ml lemon juice, 4 eggs + 2 yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g butter, cubed, whisked in off the heat: the French curd method; silk is the target"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional crown: nothing (Paris), or Italian meringue torched (the tarte au citron meringuée faction)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 24cm tart ring; a microplane; conviction about sourness"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub zest into sugar until it looks like wet sand and smells like a grove; whisk with juice, eggs and yolks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over a bain-marie, whisking, to 82°C: thick ribbons, spoon-coating; strain immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the cold butter piece by piece off the heat until the curd turns to lemon silk; pour into the fully baked shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 130°C just 8–10 min to set the surface (or simply chill 4 h for the no-bake-set school). The top should be mirror-flat. Slice with a hot knife; the cut face should stand glossy and clean.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"The great tarte au citron is an argument between sugar and acid that acid must narrowly win: a polite lemon tart is a failed one, per every French pastry chef who ever scored one. The zest-into-sugar rub extracts the oils no juice contains, and the late cold butter is what turns curd into cream. Fully bake the shell before filling: a pale, soggy base under perfect curd is the most common heartbreak in the genre.","equipment":["Torch","Bain-marie","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The water bath","Pie crust & blind baking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","silky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tarte%20au%20Citron%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tarte au Citron"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Tarte au Citron","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Tarte%20au%20Citron","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"microplane-graters-and-the-zest-doctrine","term":"Microplane, Graters & the Zest Doctrine"},{"slug":"citrus-zest-juice-and-pith","term":"Citrus: Zest, Juice & Pith"},{"slug":"meringues-french-swiss-italian","term":"Meringues: French, Swiss, Italian"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"standard":{"slug":"tarte-au-citron","marks":[{"id":"tarte-au-citron#surface","text":"The surface is mirror-flat and one even deep yellow: no bubbles risen through it, no crazed or wrinkled skin, no browned rim where the curd met the pastry."},{"id":"tarte-au-citron#lift","text":"Lift a slice on the knife: the base carries its own weight without drooping, and the underside is an even biscuit brown right to the centre with no pale, damp patch."},{"id":"tarte-au-citron#knife","text":"A hot knife leaves a clean vertical cut face, glossy and square, and it has not crept or slumped after five minutes on the plate."},{"id":"tarte-au-citron#curd","text":"The curd is silk on the tongue (no grain, no thread of cooked egg, no specks of zest caught in it), and the cut face is glossy rather than dull and matte."},{"id":"tarte-au-citron#sour","text":"Sour arrives before sweet and the swallow makes the mouth water. If the sugar lands first, it does not go out."}],"fault":"The curd is pushed past 82°C, or cooked over direct heat instead of the bain-marie: it scrambles at the pan wall, and even strained it sets dull and grainy and weeps a thin syrup onto the surface overnight."}},{"slug":"panna-cotta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml cream + 150ml milk; 70g sugar; 1 vanilla bean, split and scraped (or 2 tsp paste)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2¼ tsp powdered gelatin (or 3½ sheets; agar-agar for a vegetarian set), bloomed in 3 tbsp cold water: THE MINIMUM THAT HOLDS; the number is the dish"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ramekins or pretty glasses (the no-unmold insurance)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The classic escorts: macerated berries, or a spoon of honey and nothing else"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm cream, milk, sugar, vanilla and salt to steaming, never boiling; boiled cream tastes cooked.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off heat: whisk in the bloomed gelatin until utterly dissolved (an undissolved granule is a rubber pearl in someone's spoonful).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain into ramekins; chill 4–6 h.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Unmold by dipping the ramekin 5 seconds in hot water and inverting with one decisive shake: the panna cotta should land with a tremble and keep trembling. Berries beside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Piedmont's 'cooked cream' is a two-ingredient philosophy exam: the entire craft is using the LEAST gelatin that still stands; the wobble test is canonical, and a panna cotta that doesn't shimmy when the table is bumped was set by a coward. Restaurant versions fail firm far more often than they fail loose. Serve it in the glass if nerves run high; the tremble matters more than the unmolding theater.","equipment":["Ramekins"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panna%20Cotta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Panna Cotta"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Panna Cotta","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Panna%20Cotta","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Berries","Cream","Honey","Sugar","Vanilla","Nutmeg"],"standard":{"slug":"panna-cotta","marks":[{"id":"panna-cotta#unmolds","text":"It unmolds in one piece with sharp, defined edges top and bottom and smooth sides: no drag marks, no torn skin, no collapsed shoulder."},{"id":"panna-cotta#stands","text":"It stands ten minutes at room temperature without slumping or throwing off liquid: the loose end of the set is a shiver, not a spread."},{"id":"panna-cotta#spoon","text":"The spoon goes through with no resistance and no snap, and the cut wall sags gently rather than standing square."},{"id":"panna-cotta#melts","text":"It melts at body heat and is gone: nothing left to chew, no rubbery skin to work through, no film on the palate."},{"id":"panna-cotta#vanilla","text":"Vanilla seeds are spread through the whole cross-section rather than banded at the top, and the cut face is one even ivory: no paler, waxier layer along the plate side."}],"fault":"It is poured hot and left undisturbed to set, so it separates as it chills: the seeds sink to what becomes the top and the fat rises to what becomes the plate side, leaving a pale waxy layer there that coats the mouth before the cream ever arrives."}},{"slug":"baked-alaska","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 disc of sponge or pound cake, 20cm, brushed with a little liqueur or syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L ice cream: one flavor or layered in a bowl-mold (the dome is traditional); FROZEN ROCK-HARD, 24 h"},{"kind":"item","text":"Italian meringue: 4 egg whites, 220g sugar cooked to 118°C with 60ml water, whipped in as syrup until stiff, glossy and cool"},{"kind":"item","text":"A piping bag or palette knife for the meringue armor: full coverage, no gaps; the meringue is the insulation"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 240°C oven for 3–4 minutes, or a torch, or (for the tableside legend) warmed rum and a match"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mold the ice cream in a bowl lined with film; freeze solid; unmold onto the cake disc; back into the freezer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make the Italian meringue: the hot syrup cooks the whites, which is what lets the dessert survive the oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work fast: armor the entire dome and cake edge in thick meringue swoops, sealing to the tray; freeze again 1 h (or up to 2 days, the make-ahead secret).","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blast at 240°C 3–4 min until the peaks bronze, or torch it, and serve immediately, cutting with a hot knife: hot toasted shell, frozen heart, cake foundation, all in one slice.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The dessert is a physics demonstration with a dessert license: meringue is mostly trapped air, and air is such a poor conductor that the oven never learns there is ice cream inside. Delmonico's named it for the Alaska Purchase in 1867, and the flaming-rum service is the showman's cadenza. The whole thing waits fully assembled in the freezer for days: the most theatrical dessert in this guide is secretly the best make-ahead in it.","equipment":["Piping bag","Sheet pan","Torch"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baked%20Alaska%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baked Alaska"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Baked Alaska","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Baked%20Alaska","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"frozen-dessert-machines-churners-and-pacojet","term":"Frozen Dessert Machines: Churners & Pacojet"},{"slug":"knife-care-and-the-cutting-board-question","term":"Knife Care & the Cutting Board Question"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Sugar"],"standard":{"slug":"baked-alaska","marks":[{"id":"baked-alaska#no-go","text":"The go/no-go at the oven door: the meringue held a straight peak off the whisk and the bowl was cool to the palm before any of it went on, and the assembled dome is hard to a knuckle-rap everywhere, base included. A dome that gives anywhere goes back to the freezer, not into the oven."},{"id":"baked-alaska#sealed","text":"Sealed all round: at least 2cm of meringue over the dome, covering the cake edge and meeting the tray, with no window of ice cream showing and no crack at the base."},{"id":"baked-alaska#peaks","text":"Peaks bronzed with the valleys still cream-white after 3 to 4 minutes; an evenly tanned dome means it stayed in and the heat reached the ice cream."},{"id":"baked-alaska#knife","text":"Cut with a hot knife, the face shows three bands with hard borders: hot toasted shell, ice cream holding its own edge, cake beneath. A melted ring under the meringue and the oven won."},{"id":"baked-alaska#beads","text":"No beads of syrup on the meringue and no liquid on the plate a minute after service, and the cake base is still dry enough to lift the slice on a spatula."}],"fault":"The meringue goes onto ice cream that has softened at the surface, or goes on still warm from the syrup: the two meet as slush, the dome slides and the seal splits at the base, and the slice arrives as cream soup under browned foam."}},{"slug":"banana-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"The custard, from scratch and non-negotiable: 500ml whole milk, 100g sugar, 3 tbsp cornstarch, 3 egg yolks, 2 tbsp butter, 1 tbsp vanilla, pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 box vanilla wafers: the industrial cookie is canonical here; this is the wafer's finest hour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4–5 ripe bananas, sliced at assembly (not before)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Topping schism: meringue baked gold (the church-cookbook original) or sweetened whipped cream (the potluck majority)"},{"kind":"item","text":"A glass dish or trifle bowl (the layers are meant to be seen) and 4+ hours of fridge time"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the custard: whisk milk, sugar, starch, yolks over medium, stirring always, until it thickens and one big bubble breaks the surface; butter and vanilla off heat; press film to the surface and cool slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer while the custard is still warm: wafers, bananas, custard: three stories high, custard flooding every floor; the warm custard is what softens the wafers into their famous cake-like second life.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crown with meringue and bake 10 min at 180°C, or chill first and pipe cream later, per allegiance.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Refrigerate 4 h minimum, overnight for the devout. Serve in great scoops that show the strata.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"The dish is secretly about time: the wafers are engineered crisp specifically so the custard can transform them, and hour-four banana pudding and hour-twelve banana pudding are two different desserts, both correct. Southern churches and barbecue joints treat it as the mandatory closer (a plate of brisket without banana pudding is legally incomplete in several states of mind). Boxed pudding mix is the shortcut everyone takes and the difference everyone tastes.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","rich","floral","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Banana%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Banana Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Banana Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Banana%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Banana","Butter","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"key-lime-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"The filling, complete: 1 can (395g) sweetened condensed milk, 4 egg yolks, 120ml key lime juice (about 20 key limes, or standard limes, honestly fine, and Florida will survive it), zest of 2"},{"kind":"item","text":"Graham crust: 200g crumbs + 90g melted butter + 2 tbsp sugar, pressed and baked 10 min"},{"kind":"item","text":"Softly whipped cream for the crown: never meringue, say the Keys; meringue, say other parts of the Keys"},{"kind":"item","text":"NO GREEN FOOD COLORING: the filling is pale yellow, and green is the mark of an impostor"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 23cm pie plate; 4 hours of chill"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk yolks with zest 2 min until pale; whisk in the condensed milk, then the lime juice: watch it THICKEN in the bowl as the acid sets the milk; this is the original no-bake chemistry.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the warm baked crust; bake 160°C just 12–15 min until the center barely wobbles, a brief bake for food-safety peace of mind that the original sponge-fishermen skipped entirely.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool, then chill 4 h minimum.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cream over; lime zest; serve cold in thin slices: the pie is rich beyond its pale looks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born on Key West sponge boats with no ovens and no refrigeration: canned milk plus lime acid sets the filling by chemistry alone, which is why the ingredient list is three items and a crust. Florida statute genuinely addresses the pie (it is the official state pie), and the no-green-dye rule is enforced by public opinion. Key limes are more aromatic and more trouble; the honest bottled juice from the Keys splits the difference and no one at the table has ever known.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Key%20Lime%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Key Lime Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Key Lime Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Key%20Lime%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sous-vide-hardware-circulators-sealers-and-safety-kit","term":"Sous Vide Hardware: Circulators, Sealers & Safety Kit"},{"slug":"citrus-zest-juice-and-pith","term":"Citrus: Zest, Juice & Pith"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lime","Butter","Milk","Cream","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"eton-mess","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g strawberries: 400g quartered and macerated 30 min with 2 tbsp sugar and lemon squeeze; 100g crushed to a rough sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml double cream, whipped to SOFT peaks only: stiff cream makes it a trifle with regrets"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g crisp meringues, store-bought without shame or baked from the pavlova recipe a few cards over"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional: a splash of elderflower cordial into the berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"Glasses or one grand bowl; assembly at the final moment"}],"steps":[{"text":"Macerate the quartered berries: the sugar pulls a ruby syrup that becomes the dessert's bloodstream; crush the reserved handful into it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the cream soft; it should slump, not stand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"THE FOLD, seconds before serving: cream, broken meringue in thumb-sized shards, berries and their syrup: three lazy strokes; streaks and lumps ARE the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately, while the meringue still crunches. An Eton mess that sits becomes a different (worse) English pudding.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Legend blames a dropped pavlova at an Eton–Harrow cricket match; the school itself has served it since the 1930s, and the name is the recipe review. The entire craft is restraint at the fold (three strokes, soft cream, big shards) because the pleasure is the argument between crunch, cream and syrup, and overmixing ends the argument. It is the highest reward-to-effort ratio in this entire dessert atlas, and it knows it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eton%20Mess%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Eton Mess"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Eton Mess","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Eton%20Mess","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Berries","Cream","Sugar","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"kulfi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L whole milk: reduced by two-thirds; the reduction IS the recipe; there is no shortcut that is also kulfi"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar; 6 green cardamom pods, crushed; big pinch of saffron bloomed in warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g pistachios + 20g almonds, chopped, half in and half for the crown"},{"kind":"item","text":"Kulfi molds, steel cups, or paper cups with sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cream, optional insurance; NO eggs, NO churning: kulfi is proudly still"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the milk in a wide heavy pan, stirring and scraping the sides and bottom constantly, 60–75 min, until reduced to a third and the color of pale caramel: the browned milk solids you scrape back in (rabri) are the flavor.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":4740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sugar, cardamom, saffron in the final 10 min; cool completely; stir in half the nuts.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into molds; freeze 6 h minimum.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Unmold with a 10-second warm-water dip; roll in the remaining nuts. Slice discs or serve on the stick; kulfi melts slower than ice cream: that density is the point, not a flaw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kulfi predates the ice cream churn by centuries, Mughal kitchens froze reduced milk in sealed cones packed in Himalayan ice and saltpeter, and it remains the un-ice-cream: no air whipped in, so it is dense, slow-melting, and tastes overwhelmingly of what it is, which is milk transformed by an hour of patient fire. The caramelized-milk note from the long reduction is the flavor no condensed-milk shortcut reaches; the stirring hour is the tuition.","equipment":["Ice-cream churner"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Churning ice cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ice%20cream%20churning%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kulfi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kulfi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Kulfi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Kulfi","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"reduction-nappe-and-mounting","term":"Reduction, Nappe & Mounting"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom","Saffron"]},{"slug":"umm-ali","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g puff pastry, baked crisp and torn into shards (or croissants, or the traditional roqaq flatbread; the dish forgives and adopts)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L whole milk + 200ml cream, heated with 120g sugar and 1 tbsp vanilla or a cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"The treasure layer: 60g each pistachios, almonds, hazelnuts + 50g raisins + 40g shredded coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"More cream (100ml) spooned over the top for the broiler"},{"kind":"item","text":"A wide baking dish; a broiler; a crowd, because Umm Ali refuses to be made small"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bake the pastry to full crisp gold; tear into a buttered dish; scatter the nuts, raisins and coconut through the shards.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the hot sweetened milk over everything: the pastry drinks and slumps; press gently so all of it bathes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the extra cream across the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Broil 5–8 min until the surface blisters bronze and the corners bubble. Rest 10 minutes; serve warm in deep spoonfuls: crisp top, custard-soft middle, treasure throughout.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"'Umm Ali' means Mother of Ali, and the dish carries Egypt's most cheerfully sinister dessert legend: a 13th-century sultana who celebrated a palace rival's demise by ordering a sweet distributed to the whole country; the dessert outlived the politics by 700 years. It is bread pudding's sun-drenched cousin: no eggs, just hot milk, crisp pastry and a nut trove, finished under fire. Every Egyptian family scales it up, never down.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","The broiler: heat from above"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","charred","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Umm%20Ali%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Umm Ali"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Umm Ali","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Umm%20Ali","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"malbec-zinfandel-and-the-new-world-crowd-pleasers","term":"Malbec, Zinfandel & the New World Crowd-Pleasers"}],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Dried fruit","Milk","Cream","Phyllo/Puff pastry","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"lamingtons","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 butter sponge (225g each butter, sugar, self-raising flour; 4 eggs; splash milk) baked in a square tin, and STALED overnight; fresh sponge disintegrates in the dip"},{"kind":"item","text":"The icing bath: 400g icing sugar + 50g cocoa + 30g butter melted in 150ml hot milk, kept warm and RUNNY over a pan of water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g desiccated coconut in a wide tray"},{"kind":"item","text":"Two forks for the dipping ritual; a rack for the setting"},{"kind":"item","text":"The contested upgrade: split and filled with jam and cream (Queensland approves; purists abstain)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bake the sponge a day ahead; cut into 16 neat cubes with a serrated knife.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the assembly line: warm chocolate bath, coconut tray, rack, in that order; the line IS the recipe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop a cube in the icing; roll with forks until coated on all six faces; let excess drain a beat; then roll through coconut immediately: the coconut only grips wet icing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rack-dry 30 min. Serve with tea, at a fundraiser, per the national custom.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Named for a Lord Lamington, Governor of Queensland, and allegedly born when his cook rescued stale sponge with chocolate and coconut, which remains the operating instruction: day-old cake is structural, not thrifty. Australia and New Zealand both claim it (a familiar ANZAC pattern, see pavlova), and the 'lamington drive', schools and clubs funding themselves by the tray, made the cube a civic institution. The two-fork roll keeps fingerprints out of the chocolate; speed keeps the coconut on.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lamingtons%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lamingtons"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Lamingtons","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Lamingtons","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting"]},{"slug":"nanaimo-bars","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Base: 200g graham or digestive crumbs + 80g desiccated coconut + 60g chopped walnuts, bound with 120g melted butter + 50g sugar + 5 tbsp cocoa + 1 beaten egg, cooked 1 min to thicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE MIDDLE, the whole point: 100g soft butter beaten with 3 tbsp custard powder (Bird's: the yellow tin is canonical), 3 tbsp cream, 250g icing sugar, a pale gold buttercream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Top: 150g dark chocolate melted with 2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 20cm square pan, lined; a fridge; NO OVEN anywhere in this recipe"},{"kind":"item","text":"A hot knife for the clean-cut flex"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the crumb base briefly until glossy; press firmly and evenly into the pan; chill 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the custard buttercream fluffy and spread level: the yellow stripe must be generous and straight; it is the bar's flag.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the chocolate over; tilt to the corners; chill until ALMOST set, then score the top: cutting fully-set chocolate shatters the stripe's clean edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill firm; cut small squares with a hot wiped knife. They keep a week refrigerated and freeze like champions, which is why Canadian freezers are never without.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Named for the city of Nanaimo, BC, and enshrined in a 1950s hospital-fundraiser cookbook: the city later held an official contest to fix the definitive recipe, which Canada treats with constitutional seriousness. The Bird's custard powder middle is the non-negotiable identity (vanilla buttercream is a lookalike, not a Nanaimo), and the no-bake trinity (crumb, custard, chocolate) travels to every potluck between the Pacific and the Atlantic. The score-before-set trick is the difference between bakery bars and cracked ones.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Scoring","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","earthy","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nanaimo%20Bars%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nanaimo Bars"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Nanaimo Bars","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Nanaimo%20Bars","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"knife-care-and-the-cutting-board-question","term":"Knife Care & the Cutting Board Question"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Butter","Cream","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","scoring"]},{"slug":"tarta-de-santiago","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g ground almonds, the cake entire; there is NO flour, and there never was"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g sugar; 5 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Zest of 1 lemon + ½ tsp cinnamon; 1 tbsp sweet wine or brandy, optional and traditional"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the tin only (23cm)"},{"kind":"item","text":"The stencil: a Cross of Saint James (print one) and icing sugar for the emblem"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk eggs and sugar 3 min until pale and slightly thickened: this is the only lift the cake gets.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold in almonds, zest, cinnamon, wine; the batter is dense and proud of it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 175°C, 30–35 min until deep gold and a skewer comes out with moist crumbs; cool fully in the tin: almond cakes firm as they cool.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the cross stencil on top; snow icing sugar over; lift to reveal the emblem. Serve in slim wedges with sherry or coffee.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pilgrims' cake of Santiago de Compostela, documented since the 1500s and now protection-stamped: by regulation a true tarta is at least a third almond, and the flourlessness is medieval fact, not modern diet: making it accidentally the oldest gluten-free showpiece in Europe. The sword-cross of Saint James stenciled in sugar is the finish line of the Camino rendered in dessert form; a slice at the end of the walk is tradition, but the cake asks no proof of pilgrimage.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","warm-spiced","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, warm spice humming through the middle, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tarta%20de%20Santiago%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tarta de Santiago"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Tarta de Santiago","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Tarta%20de%20Santiago","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Wine","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"jian-dui","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g glutinous rice flour; 60g sugar dissolved in ~220ml just-boiled water (the hot-water dough is the stretch)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g sweet red bean paste (or lotus paste), chilled and rolled into 12 balls"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g raw white sesame seeds in a bowl; a second bowl of water for wet-rolling"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for deep frying: starting LOW at 130°C and finishing at 160°C; the two-stage fry is the inflation mechanism"},{"kind":"item","text":"A slotted spoon and gentle pressure: you will be massaging them as they fry"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the hot-water dough; knead smooth; rest 10 min. Wrap each chilled paste ball in dough, sealing seamlessly: cracks become blowouts.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wet each ball's surface; roll through sesame and press the seeds home between your palms.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 130°C, rolling constantly; as they firm, press each gently against the pot with the spoon; they rebound and BEGIN TO INFLATE; keep pressing and rolling as the shell expands hollow.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise to 160°C for the final minutes until deep gold and audibly crisp. Drain upright; eat warm: crackling sesame shell, chewy wall, soft sweet heart.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sesame ball's magic trick is the press-and-puff: trapped steam plus glutinous stretch means every gentle squeeze against the pot inflates the ball a little more, until a modest dumpling becomes a golden balloon; dim sum masters can blow them to softball size with a fist-sized heart of air. Tang-dynasty palace records mention them; Chinatown bakeries sell them at dawn; and the two-temperature fry is the entire difference between a dense sphere and a hollow marvel.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jian%20Dui%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jian Dui"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Jian Dui","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Jian%20Dui","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"building-the-home-and-starting-bar","term":"Building the Home & Starting Bar"}],"pantryItems":["Rice","Bread","Flour","Kidney/Pinto beans","Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"molten-chocolate-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g dark chocolate (60–70%) + 200g butter, melted together and cooled slightly"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs + 4 yolks, whisked with 150g sugar until pale, thick and ribboning: the aeration is the cake's only flour-adjacent structure"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g flour, folded in last; pinch of salt; 1 tbsp espresso or rum, optional"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 buttered-and-cocoa-dusted ramekins: the cocoa dust is the release mechanism"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE MAKE-AHEAD SECRET: the filled ramekins hold 24 h in the fridge; bake from cold, add 2 minutes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk eggs, yolks and sugar to the thick ribbon; fold in the chocolate-butter, then the flour and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the prepared ramekins three-quarters; chill at least 30 min (the chilled center is the molten-core insurance).","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220°C: 11–13 min from the fridge: the edges rise and set, the top forms a thin crust, the CENTER still shivers when nudged.","durationSec":780,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":780,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 60 seconds; run a thin knife around; invert onto plates with one confident flip. The first spoon-breach and the flow is the entire dessert. Vanilla ice cream is the appointed witness.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jean-Georges Vongerichten's famous 1987 'mistake', a chocolate sponge pulled early, became the most copied restaurant dessert of its generation, though Michel Bras had engineered a deliberate frozen-center coulant a few years prior; the dispute is friendly and unresolved. The home truths: the ribbon stage is the structure, the underbake is the recipe, and the do-ahead ramekins make it the rare showstopper that improves your evening instead of consuming it. The 60-second rest lets the walls firm just enough to survive the flip.","equipment":["Ramekins"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Folding: keeping the air in","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Molten%20Chocolate%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Molten Chocolate Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Molten Chocolate Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Molten%20Chocolate%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"creaming-foaming-and-cake-structure","term":"Creaming, Foaming & Cake Structure"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Coffee/Espresso"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"fudgy-brownies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g dark chocolate + 175g butter, melted; PLUS 30g cocoa powder, the double-chocolate structure of the fudgy school"},{"kind":"item","text":"220g sugar + 60g brown sugar; 3 eggs + 1 yolk; 1 tbsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"90g flour ONLY: low flour is the fudge line; ½ tsp salt + flaky salt for the top"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE SHINY-CRUST SECRET: whisk the eggs and sugars a full 2 minutes until thick; the dissolved sugar is what bakes into that crackled tissue top"},{"kind":"item","text":"A 20cm square pan, lined with an overhang; the discipline to underbake"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt chocolate and butter; cool 5 min. Whisk eggs and sugars the full 2 minutes. Do not negotiate; the crust depends on it.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stream in the chocolate; fold in cocoa, flour, salt until JUST combined; into the pan; flaky salt over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 175°C, 22–26 min: the tester should come out with dark moist crumbs and a smear; a clean tester means you have baked cake.","durationSec":1560,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1560,"estimated":true},{"text":"COOL COMPLETELY in the pan (hours; the center sets as it cools), lift by the overhang, and cut with a hot wiped knife. Corner people and middle people may now negotiate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The brownie is a hundred-year American argument between cakey and fudgy, and this recipe votes fudgy without apology: low flour, extra yolk, double chocolate. The two techniques that separate bakery brownies from box memories are the long sugar-egg whisk (the sole source of the shiny crackle crust) and the disciplined underbake-then-full-cool: a warm brownie is a delicious failure of nerve; a cooled one slices into the dense squares the argument was always about.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fudgy%20Brownies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fudgy Brownies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Fudgy Brownies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Fudgy%20Brownies","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"carrot-cake-with-cream-cheese-frosting","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour, 2 tsp baking soda, 2 tsp cinnamon + ½ tsp each ginger and nutmeg, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs; 200g sugar + 150g brown sugar; 250ml neutral OIL: oil, not butter; it is why carrot cake stays moist for days"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g carrots, grated FINE on the small holes: coarse ribbons make wet, structural chaos"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional congregation: 100g toasted walnuts, 80g raisins, 50g coconut, crushed pineapple (drained hard), each addition has a lobby"},{"kind":"item","text":"Frosting: 250g cream cheese COLD + 100g soft butter + 300g icing sugar + vanilla + pinch salt: beaten just until smooth"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk eggs, sugars and oil 2 min; fold in the dry, then the fine-grated carrots and any of the congregation.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide between two lined 20cm tins; bake 175°C, 30–35 min; cool COMPLETELY: cream cheese frosting on a warm cake is a landslide with witnesses.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Frosting: beat butter smooth first, then the cold cream cheese briefly, then sugar on low: overbeating cream cheese thins it; the frosting should hold a proud swoosh.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack, fill, and frost rustically: the swoosh is the style; walnuts pressed to the sides if using. Refrigerate 30 min to settle; serve at cool room temperature.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Carrot cake is medieval thrift (carrots as sugar substitute) that America crowned in the 1970s with cream cheese frosting, and the frosting is now legally inseparable from the cake in the public imagination. The two technique truths: fine-grated carrots vanish into moist crumb where coarse ones puddle, and cold cream cheese into already-creamed butter is the anti-soup order of operations. The add-ins are a genuine national referendum; the only unelectable position is frosting from a tub.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carrot%20Cake%20with%20Cream%20Cheese%20Frosting%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carrot Cake with Cream Cheese Frosting"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Carrot Cake with Cream Cheese Frosting","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Carrot%20Cake%20with%20Cream%20Cheese%20Frosting","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-opening-soft-launch-and-the-first-90-days","term":"The Opening: Soft Launch & the First 90 Days"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ginger","Carrot","Pineapple","Coconut","Dried fruit","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"kurtoskalacs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Rich dough: 350g flour, 7g yeast, 60g sugar, 150ml warm milk, 1 egg + 1 yolk, 60g soft butter, pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar for the rolling coat + 100g melted butter for basting"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ground walnuts, cinnamon sugar, or coconut for the after-roll"},{"kind":"item","text":"THE SPIT: the traditional wooden dorong, or the home rig: thick rolling pin or cardboard tubes triple-wrapped in foil, buttered"},{"kind":"item","text":"An oven with a rack to suspend the spit (or a rotisserie, or a grill, turning by hand like your Transylvanian ancestors)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soft supple dough; rise 1 h. Roll into a rectangle; cut long 1.5cm ribbons.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wind each ribbon in a spiral up the buttered spit, edges just touching; roll the wrapped spit over the counter to fuse the coils, then roll it through sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190°C suspended over a tray, TURNING every 4–5 min and basting with butter, 20–25 min: the sugar caramelizes into a glassy amber shell as it rotates.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tap off the spit while warm (it releases as a hollow chimney); roll immediately in cinnamon sugar or walnuts. Tear off spiral ribbons to eat: the chimney unwinds the way it was wound.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Transylvania's 'chimney cake' (Hungarian by language, Székely by heartland) is the oldest of Europe's spit cakes, fair-ground food since the 1700s: dough ribbon, rotating spit, caramelizing sugar, live fire. The rotation is the recipe; the sugar must keep moving to lacquer instead of scorch. The steam that puffs from the hollow chimney when you tear the first ribbon is the moment street vendors from Budapest to Prague have monetized for three centuries, and it is worth every forint.","equipment":["Grill","Sheet pan","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","earthy","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=K%C3%BCrt%C5%91skal%C3%A1cs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kürtőskalács"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Kürtőskalács","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20K%C3%BCrt%C5%91skal%C3%A1cs","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"sugar-pie-tarte-au-sucre","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked pie shell, edges crimped high"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g dark brown sugar (or the Québec ideal: half maple sugar) whisked with 3 tbsp flour and a pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs; 250ml heavy cream + 60ml milk; 2 tbsp melted butter; 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"A whisk and nothing else: the filling takes four minutes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cold pouring cream or vanilla ice cream at the table; the pie is sweet and refuses to pretend otherwise"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk sugar, flour and salt in the shell itself or a bowl; whisk in eggs, cream, milk, butter, vanilla until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the shell: it will look thin; the oven fixes everything.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 180°C, 40–45 min until the top is glossy caramel-brown, puffed at the edges, and the center wobbles like the panna cotta a few cards north.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool at least 2 h: the filling sets into a soft caramel custard as it cools. Serve barely warm or room temperature, in slim slices, with cold cream as the mandatory counterweight.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The réveillon table that gave this guide its tourtière closes with tarte au sucre, Québec's brown-sugar-and-cream declaration, descended from northern France's sugar tarts and upgraded whenever the maple harvest allows. The recipe is pantry-bare on purpose (settler kitchens, winter months, no fruit for hundreds of miles), and the thin-slice rule is engineering, not manners: the pie is a caramel in pastry clothing, and the cold cream exists to argue with it. It wins the argument.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Sugar stages & caramel","Pie crust & blind baking","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, melting away silken."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sugar%20Pie%20(Tarte%20au%20Sucre)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sugar Pie (Tarte au Sucre)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Sugar Pie (Tarte au Sucre)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Sugar%20Pie%20(Tarte%20au%20Sucre)","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"affogato-al-caffe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 generous scoops of the best vanilla or fior di latte gelato you can obtain: the quality ceiling of the dish is the quality of this scoop"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 shots of ESPRESSO, freshly pulled and screaming hot: the temperature collision is the recipe"},{"kind":"item","text":"Optional accessories, each with precedent: a thimble of amaretto or Frangelico; a crumbled amaretti biscuit; shaved dark chocolate"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chilled glasses or small bowls: cold vessel, hot coffee, the contrast preserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"A small spoon and no delay whatsoever"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill the glasses; scoop the gelato into them and return to the freezer while the espresso brews.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the espresso at the last possible second.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the hot shots directly over the cold scoops AT THE TABLE: the name means 'drowned,' and the drowning is the ceremony.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Eat immediately with the spoon, chasing the moving border where espresso melts gelato into cream, then drink the end like the coffee it has become. Total elapsed time: four minutes. Total regret: zero.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Affogato is the shortest recipe in this entire five-hundred-dish guide and among the most complete: hot and cold, bitter and sweet, drink and dessert, all in one glass with a two-minute window. Italy files it under coffee as much as dolce, and the only rule that matters is simultaneity: pre-poured affogato is just coffee-flavored milk. As the five-hundredth door in this collection, it makes the guide's closing argument: technique is knowing exactly how little a great thing needs.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Affogato%20al%20Caff%C3%A8%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Affogato al Caffè"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Claire Saffitz on Affogato al Caffè","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Claire%20Saffitz%20Affogato%20al%20Caff%C3%A8","sub":"A YouTube search for Claire Saffitz and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"espresso-and-the-coffee-program","term":"Espresso & the Coffee Program"}],"pantryItems":["Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla","Coffee/Espresso"]},{"slug":"downeast-lobster-roll","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 cooked lobsters (about 700g meat), chilled and cut in bite-size pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 tbsp mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 celery rib, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp chives, snipped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 New England split-top hot dog buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fold lobster with mayonnaise, celery, lemon, and chives. Season carefully and keep cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter both flat sides of each split-top bun.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle the buns over medium heat until deep gold and crisp on both sides, 2 min a side.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Line each bun with a lettuce leaf if you like, then heap the salad in generously.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately with chips and a pickle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Maine means cold and mayonnaise-dressed; Connecticut means warm and buttered: the border between them is real and patrolled. The claw meat is sweeter than the tail, so use both and never drown either: a proper roll is lobster with a suggestion of dressing, not salad with a rumor of lobster. The bun is a top-split New England frankfurter roll, buttered and griddled on its flat sides: the wrong bun is disqualifying in either state. Chips and a view of the water complete the specification.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Downeast%20Lobster%20Roll%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Downeast Lobster Roll"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Downeast Lobster Roll","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Downeast%20Lobster%20Roll","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lobster","Celery","Lemon","Chives","Butter","Bread","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"steamers-with-drawn-butter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg soft-shell steamer clams"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml water or light beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the clams well and let them sit in cold salted water 20 min to purge sand; lift them out rather than pouring.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the water to a boil in a wide pot, add the clams, cover, and steam 5-8 min until they open.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false},{"text":"Melt the butter gently and skim the foam to draw it clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain the clam broth through a cloth into cups: the sand settles at the bottom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the clams with the broth for rinsing and the butter for dipping. Pull off the black neck sheath as you eat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The broth is not garnish, it is procedure: swish each clam in it to rinse the last grit, then dip in butter. The two-cup ritual is the whole table manner of the dish. Any clam that stays shut goes in the bin, no appeals. Soft-shells purge best with a long soak in seawater-salty brine, some say with cornmeal. The broth itself gets drunk at the end, sand settled to the bottom, which is the closest thing a Maine clam feed has to a benediction.","equipment":["Steamer"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Steamers%20with%20Drawn%20Butter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Steamers with Drawn Butter"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Steamers with Drawn Butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Steamers%20with%20Drawn%20Butter","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Lemon","Butter","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"maine-wild-blueberry-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry for a double-crust 23cm pie"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g wild Maine blueberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of cinnamon and salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter, in bits"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten, for the wash"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the berries with sugar, cornstarch, lemon, cinnamon, and salt; let stand 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line the pie plate with pastry and fill with the berries. Dot with butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the top crust or a lattice, seal and crimp, then cut vents. Brush with egg wash and dust with sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 20 min, then lower to 190C for 35-40 min until the juices bubble thickly through the vents.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool at least 4 hours: a warm blueberry pie runs.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Wild Maine berries are small, dark, and intense; the big cultivated ones carry more water and need an extra spoon of cornstarch: the recipe changes with the berry, and the wild ones barely need help. Never wash them until the moment they go in; water on stored berries is how mold wins. Maine harvests its wild barrens with rakes, a crop that was never planted by anyone; the pie is the state dessert by statute, one of the few pieces of pie legislation on any American book.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maine%20Wild%20Blueberry%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maine Wild Blueberry Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Maine Wild Blueberry Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Maine%20Wild%20Blueberry%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Berries","Butter","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine"]},{"slug":"whoopie-pies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g cocoa powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"240ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 115g butter, 200g icing sugar, 175g marshmallow creme, 1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk flour, cocoa, soda, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream butter and brown sugar until light; beat in egg and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Alternate the dry ingredients and buttermilk into the butter in three additions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop heaped tablespoons onto lined trays, well spaced, and bake at 190C for 10-12 min until they spring back. Cool completely.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat the filling ingredients smooth, then sandwich pairs of cakes generously.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Maine calls this the official state treat and Pennsylvania disputes the birth certificate. Either way the cakes must be cake-soft, never cookie-crisp: pull them the moment they spring back.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Whoopie%20Pies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Whoopie Pies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Whoopie Pies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Whoopie%20Pies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"sauteed-fiddleheads","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450g fiddlehead ferns, brown papery husk rubbed off"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash the fiddleheads in several changes of water, rubbing away the husk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil them in well-salted water 10 min; this step is not optional, raw fiddleheads make people ill. Drain.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter in a skillet, add the garlic, and cook 30 seconds until fragrant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the fiddleheads and saute over high heat 3-4 min until they take some color.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Finish with lemon, salt, pepper, and flaky salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"They are a three-week May crop and taste like asparagus crossed with green beans: the coiled fronds of the ostrich fern, foraged along Maine riverbanks since long before anyone wrote it down. Boil first, always: a quick sauté alone is not enough to make them safe, and the boil also strips the bitterness. Then treat them simply: butter, garlic, lemon, salt. Rushing fiddleheads to the pan is the one mistake the season doesn't give you time to repeat.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauteed%20Fiddleheads%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauteed Fiddleheads"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sauteed Fiddleheads","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sauteed%20Fiddleheads","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Lemon","Butter","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"maine-lobster-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Meat from 2 lobsters, cut in chunks, shells reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml heavy cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sweet paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the butter over low heat and sweat the onion until soft without color.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the lobster meat and cook gently 5 min so the butter turns coral: this is the color of the stew.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Warm the milk and cream separately, then add slowly to the lobster, stirring.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat until steaming but never boiling; boiling toughens the meat and can break the milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with salt, white pepper, and a dusting of paprika. Rest overnight in the fridge if you can.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The old Maine rule is that lobster stew must age a day before serving: the butter and lobster essence marry into something the fresh pot cannot match, and the overnight rest is written into every coastal community cookbook since the 1800s. Cook the lobster meat gently in butter first; that infused butter is the stew's entire flavor engine. Milk and cream, salt, patience: nothing else. A stew this plain has nowhere to hide, which is exactly the Down East point.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maine%20Lobster%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maine Lobster Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Maine Lobster Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Maine%20Lobster%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lobster","Onion","Butter","Milk","Cream","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"saturday-night-baked-beans","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried yellow-eye or navy beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g salt pork, scored"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, whole, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dry mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the beans, cover with fresh water, and simmer 30 min until the skins split when you blow on a spoonful. Drain, reserving the liquid.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the onion in the bottom of a bean pot, add the beans, and bury the salt pork in the middle with its rind up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix molasses, sugar, mustard, salt, and pepper with 500ml of the bean liquid and pour over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and bake at 130C for 6 hours, checking every hour and topping up with bean liquid so they never go dry.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Uncover for the last 45 min to let the pork crisp and the top glaze.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false}],"note":"Saturday night beans with brown bread was the New England Sabbath dinner: the oven did the work while nobody had to. Yellow-eye beans are the Maine choice; navy beans are the compromise.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Saturday%20Night%20Baked%20Beans%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Saturday Night Baked Beans"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Saturday Night Baked Beans","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Saturday%20Night%20Baked%20Beans","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mustard-seeds-yellow-brown-and-black","term":"Mustard Seeds: Yellow, Brown & Black"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","White beans","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"needhams","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g mashed potato, plain, cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"450g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g shredded coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g dark chocolate"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp coconut oil or paraffin-free shortening"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the mashed potato with the melted butter, vanilla, and salt until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the icing sugar a little at a time, then the coconut, until you have a stiff paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press into a lined 20x20cm pan, smooth the top, and chill until firm, about 2 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into small bars and freeze 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the chocolate with the coconut oil, dip each bar, and set on parchment until firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yes, potato: it carries the sugar and coconut without any flavor of its own, a thrift-born trick from the 1870s that produces something better than the sum of its parts; the mashed potato binds the filling into a fondant no one can reverse-engineer by taste. Named, the story goes, for a popular evangelist of the day. Chill the filling firm before dipping, and temper the chocolate or accept the streaks. Maine's candy counters still stock them beside the fudge, where they belong.","equipment":["Parchment"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering chocolate","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tempering%20chocolate%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Needhams%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Needhams"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Needhams","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Needhams","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Coconut","Butter","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"downeast-haddock-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g haddock fillet, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g salt pork or bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g potatoes, in 2cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml water or fish stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml heavy cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and a pinch of thyme"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the salt pork in the pot until crisp; lift out the bits and reserve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion in the fat until translucent, then add potatoes and the water. Simmer 12 min until nearly tender.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the haddock on top, cover, and poach gently 6-8 min until it flakes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the milk and cream and heat through without boiling. Season and finish with butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the pot sit an hour off the heat, then rewarm gently to serve, scattered with the pork bits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A New England chowder is thickened by potato starch and patience, not flour: the potatoes shed what the pot needs, and resting it before serving is the difference between soup and chowder; the classic instruction is to make it in the morning for supper. Add the haddock late and let residual heat cook it in big flakes. Salt pork is the old foundation, evaporated milk the old enrichment, and a chowder that stands a day improves, the rare seafood dish that rewards waiting.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Downeast%20Haddock%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Downeast Haddock Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Downeast Haddock Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Downeast%20Haddock%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","White fish","Onion","Potato","Thyme","Butter","Milk","Cream","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"maine-italian-sandwich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft submarine rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g boiled ham, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g American cheese, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sour pickles, sliced lengthwise"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the rolls most of the way through without separating the halves.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay in the ham and cheese first, then the tomato, pepper, onion, pickle, and olives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Douse generously with olive oil: this is the point of the sandwich, not a garnish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season hard with salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap in paper and let it sit 10 min so the bread drinks the oil.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Invented in Portland in 1902 and unchanged since: Giovanni Amato's cart fed dockworkers a soft roll of ham, cheese, and vegetables, and Maine has defended the formula ever since. The roll must be soft (crusty bread is a different, wrong sandwich), the oil must be excessive, and it must be wrapped and rested before eating so the oil and brine soak in. It is Maine's 'Italian', a sandwich that exists nowhere in Italy and everywhere in Maine, which bothers exactly no one.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maine%20Italian%20Sandwich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maine Italian Sandwich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Maine Italian Sandwich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Maine%20Italian%20Sandwich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Olives","Cheese (any)","Bread","Pickles"]},{"slug":"apple-cider-donuts","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml apple cider, boiled down to 120ml and cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"375g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For coating: 200g sugar with 2 tbsp cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the cider until reduced to a scant half cup: this is where the apple flavor lives. Cool it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the dry ingredients. Separately whisk sugar, butter, eggs, buttermilk, and the cider reduction.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine gently into a soft, sticky dough. Chill 1 hour, then roll on a floured board to 1.5cm and cut.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 175C for 90 seconds a side until deep gold. Drain briefly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss in cinnamon sugar while still hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Unreduced cider makes a bland donut. The boiled-down syrup is the whole recipe, a cup of cider concentrated to a few spoonfuls of orchard essence; every White Mountain orchard sells these in September and none of them skip that step. The cake-donut batter should be soft and a little sticky, chilled so it can be handled. Fry, then straight into cinnamon sugar while hot enough that it sticks: a cooled donut sheds its coat, and an apple cider donut without the sugar jacket is out of uniform.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Apple%20Cider%20Donuts%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Apple Cider Donuts"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Apple Cider Donuts","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Apple%20Cider%20Donuts","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Apple","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"new-england-boiled-dinner","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg corned beef brisket"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cabbage, cut in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 carrots, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 potatoes, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 turnips, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 beets, cooked separately"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the corned beef with cold water, add bay and peppercorns, and simmer very gently 2.5 hours until a fork slides in easily. Never let it boil hard.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the beets separately so they do not stain the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add potatoes, carrots, and turnips to the beef broth and cook 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the cabbage wedges for the last 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slice the beef across the grain and serve on a platter with the vegetables around it, butter and pepper on the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cook the beets apart or the whole dinner turns pink: they bleed into everything they simmer with, and a rose-tinted corned beef alarms even the tolerant. Next-day leftovers get chopped and fried into red flannel hash, which is the point of making too much; the hash is arguably the better meal and the beets finally get to dye everything on purpose. Add the vegetables in stages by density. This is Yankee thrift cooking that fed the region for two centuries and apologizes to no one.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","gently hot","rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, a warm current of chile, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20England%20Boiled%20Dinner%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New England Boiled Dinner"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New England Boiled Dinner","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20England%20Boiled%20Dinner","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Potato","Carrot","Cabbage","Beets","Radish/Turnip","Bay leaf","Butter"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"cider-braised-pork-shoulder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg pork shoulder, skin scored"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml hard cider"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 apples, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sage leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp whole-grain mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the pork generously the night before if you can, and pat dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown all sides hard in a Dutch oven, then set aside and pour off excess fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions in the pot, add cider, and scrape up the fond. Return the pork, add sage, cover, and braise at 150C for 3 hours.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the apples for the last 40 min.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the pork, then whisk mustard and butter into the reduced braising liquid for the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hard cider brings acid and orchard sweetness that wine cannot: apples and pork being the oldest marriage in the northern larder. If the sauce tastes thin at the end, reduce it hard before the butter goes in: never after, because mounted butter breaks if you boil it. The dry cider matters; sweet cider turns the braise to dessert. Brown the shoulder patiently, give it its slow hours, and the sauce finishes as essence of orchard-and-pig, needing nothing else.","equipment":["Dutch oven"],"techniques":["Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cider-Braised%20Pork%20Shoulder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cider-Braised Pork Shoulder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cider-Braised Pork Shoulder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cider-Braised%20Pork%20Shoulder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"braising-and-stewing","term":"Braising & Stewing"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Apple","Sage","Butter","Beer/Cider","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["braising","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"north-country-corn-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 ears fresh corn, kernels cut off, cobs reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the stripped cobs in the milk for 20 min to draw out their sweetness, then discard them.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Render the bacon; lift out the bits and sweat the onion in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle in the flour, cook 1 min, then add the potatoes and the corn milk. Simmer 15 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the corn kernels and cream and cook 5 min more: no longer, or the corn goes tired.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season, finish with butter, and scatter bacon and chives over each bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cobs hold as much flavor as the kernels: simmering them in the milk first is the whole difference between corn chowder and potato soup with corn in it; scrape the stripped cobs with the back of the knife for the milky pulp too. Salt pork or bacon is the traditional foundation. Late-summer corn needs almost nothing else, which is the dish's argument: the chowder frame (pork, potato, dairy) exists to display whatever the field just handed you.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Country%20Corn%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Country Corn Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Country Corn Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Country%20Corn%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Onion","Potato","Corn","Chives","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"sugar-house-pancakes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted, plus more for the griddle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Real maple syrup, warmed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the dry ingredients in a wide bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat eggs, buttermilk, and melted butter together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold wet into dry with a light hand: lumps are correct, overmixing gives you rubber.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the batter 10 min while the griddle heats to medium.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cook in butter until bubbles break across the surface, flip once, and serve with warm syrup, never the corn syrup impostor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sugaring season runs six weeks in late winter, and every sugar house in the state serves pancakes to prove the syrup. Warm the syrup: cold syrup chills the plate and mutes the maple.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sugar%20House%20Pancakes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sugar House Pancakes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sugar House Pancakes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sugar%20House%20Pancakes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"hampton-beach-fried-clams","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g shucked soft-shell clams with bellies"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g corn flour or fine cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges and tartar sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the clams in buttermilk 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the corn flour, flour, salt, and pepper in a wide bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C. Lift the clams from the buttermilk, dredge in the flour mix, and shake off the excess.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in small batches 60-90 seconds until pale gold: they cook faster than you expect.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt immediately, and serve with lemon and tartar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Whole-belly clams, not strips: the belly carries the flavor (briny, sweet, faintly mineral), and the strip is the compromise sold to people who fear the good part. Corn flour gives the sandy, craggy crust that plain wheat flour cannot. The oil must recover fast between batches, so fry few at a time. Born on the North Shore (Woodman's of Essex claims the 1916 invention) and perfected along every clam shack from there to Hampton Beach, eaten from a paper box, standing up, in the wind.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Shucking shellfish","Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Shucking shellfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20shuck%20oysters%20clams%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hampton%20Beach%20Fried%20Clams%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hampton Beach Fried Clams"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Hampton Beach Fried Clams","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Hampton%20Beach%20Fried%20Clams","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Corn","Lemon","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"blueberry-buckle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g blueberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the crumb: 100g sugar, 75g flour, 1 tsp cinnamon, 55g cold butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream butter and sugar, beat in the egg, then alternate the dry ingredients with the milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the blueberries and spread in a buttered 23cm square pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the crumb ingredients together to coarse pebbles and scatter over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C for 45-50 min until a skewer comes out clean.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool 20 min and cut in the pan.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"It is called a buckle because the heavy crumb topping makes the cake buckle as it bakes: the batter rises, the streusel bears down, and the surface folds into valleys that hold pockets of crumb. Toss the berries in a spoon of the flour first if yours are large and wet, or they sink to the bottom in a soggy stratum. Buckles, slumps, grunts and crisps are the whole New England family of fruit-and-batter improvisations; the buckle is the one that travels best to breakfast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Rubbing fat into flour","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Blueberry%20Buckle%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Blueberry Buckle"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Blueberry Buckle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Blueberry%20Buckle","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Berries","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"white-mountain-venison-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg venison shoulder, in 4cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp bacon fat or oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 carrots, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g potatoes, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf, thyme, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the venison well, season, and toss in flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown hard in batches in the fat; crowding steams the meat and costs you the crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions, add tomato paste and cook it dark, then deglaze with the wine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the meat with stock, juniper, bay, and thyme. Cover and braise at 150C for 2 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add carrots and potatoes for the last 40 min. Season again before serving: venison takes more salt than beef.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wild venison is lean to the point of austerity: the bacon fat, the juniper, and the long slow heat are all there to supply what the animal does not: fat, aromatics, and time being the three things a whitetail never carries. Never let it boil hard; lean meat toughens at a gallop. The juniper is the north-woods signature, resinous against the venison's iron. Deer camp cooking at its most honest: what the hunt provides, the pot completes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=White%20Mountain%20Venison%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of White Mountain Venison Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on White Mountain Venison Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20White%20Mountain%20Venison%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"juniper","term":"Juniper"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Bacon","Venison/Game","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Berries","Thyme","Bay leaf","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["braising","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"maple-cream-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 blind-baked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml dark maple syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream, whipped, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the maple syrup 5 min to concentrate it, then whisk in the milk and heat to steaming.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks, cornstarch, and salt smooth in a bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Temper the hot maple milk into the yolks in a thin stream, then return everything to the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over medium heat, whisking constantly, until it thickens and bubbles once or twice. Off heat, beat in butter and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the shell, press cling film on the surface, and chill 4 hours. Top with whipped cream.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Use Grade A dark syrup: the pale early-season syrup vanishes into the dairy, and the pie's whole argument is that maple should taste like maple. Whisking through the first thick bubble is what cooks out the starch; stop early and the filling tastes of raw cornstarch under all that sugar. New Hampshire sugarhouses run on exactly this pie every March, when the sap runs and the season justifies dessert twice a day. Unsweetened whipped cream on top: the pie brings sugar enough.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Pie crust & blind baking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maple%20Cream%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maple Cream Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Maple Cream Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Maple%20Cream%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"farm-turkey-pot-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g cooked turkey, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml turkey or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry or biscuit topping"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot, and celery in the butter until soft, 8 min.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the flour and cook 2 min, then add the stock slowly, whisking, until smooth and thickened.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add cream, thyme, turkey, and peas. Season well and cool slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a deep dish, lay the pastry over, seal the edges, and cut vents. Brush with egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 30-35 min until the crust is deep gold and the filling bubbles at the vents.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true}],"note":"The filling must be seasoned past the point that seems right in the pot: the pastry absorbs seasoning as it bakes and a timid gravy tastes like paste under a crust; salt until it argues back, then stop. Thicken it properly too: a pot pie filling should mound on a spoon before the lid ever goes on. Vent the crust generously. This is the after-Thanksgiving economy of every New England farm kitchen, the bird's second act, and for many families the better one.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Farm%20Turkey%20Pot%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Farm Turkey Pot Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Farm Turkey Pot Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Farm%20Turkey%20Pot%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Turkey","Eggs","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Peas","Thyme","Butter","Cream","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"vermont-maple-syrup-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml dark maple syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g walnuts, toasted and chopped (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the syrup, brown sugar, and melted butter together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs one at a time, then the flour, vanilla, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the walnuts in the shell if using and pour the filling over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C for 45-50 min until the edges are set and the center still wobbles slightly.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely before slicing: it sets as it cools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is sugar-house pie: maple in place of corn syrup, which is why it tastes of something. Pull it while the middle still moves; a fully firm pie in the oven is a cracked pie on the table.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vermont%20Maple%20Syrup%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vermont Maple Syrup Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Vermont Maple Syrup Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Vermont%20Maple%20Syrup%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mandoline-and-the-slicing-machines","term":"Mandoline & the Slicing Machines"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"cheddar-crusted-apple-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the crust: 375g flour, 1 tsp salt, 225g cold butter, 150g sharp Vermont cheddar, grated, 6-8 tbsp ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg firm apples, peeled and sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of nutmeg and salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the butter into the flour and salt to coarse crumbs, work in the grated cheddar, then bring together with ice water. Chill 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the apples with sugar, flour, spices, lemon, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll out two-thirds of the pastry for the base, fill with apples, and dot with butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with the remaining pastry, seal, vent, and brush with cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 20 min, then 190C for 40 min until deep gold and bubbling. Cool 3 hours.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Apple pie without cheese is like a kiss without a squeeze, as the Vermont saying goes, and grating the cheddar into the crust beats laying a slice on top, because the fat goes everywhere and the sharpness bakes into every flake instead of sitting on one corner. Use a real aged Vermont cheddar with some bite; mild cheese vanishes into the butter. The cheese crust browns faster than plain pastry, so shield the edge if it races ahead. Sweet-sharp is the whole thesis of the pie.","equipment":["Rolling pin","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cheddar-Crusted%20Apple%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cheddar-Crusted Apple Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cheddar-Crusted Apple Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cheddar-Crusted%20Apple%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Apple","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"sugar-on-snow","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml pure maple syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"A tray of clean packed snow or crushed ice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sour pickles and plain doughnuts, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pack a tray with clean snow and set it in the freezer while you work.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the syrup in a deep pot, it foams high, until a candy thermometer reads 115C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the tray out and drizzle the hot syrup in ribbons across the snow.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"It sets in seconds into chewy taffy; lift it with a fork and twirl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with a sour pickle and a plain doughnut on the side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pickle is not a joke: the sourness cuts the sugar so you can eat more of it, which is the entire point of a sugaring party: sugar on snow, pickle, plain donut, repeat, in that order, until the season's syrup is properly honored. Boil the syrup to the thread stage and ribbon it over packed clean snow, where it sets into taffy you eat off a fork. Vermont has thrown these parties every March since the sugarhouses had neighbors, and the menu has not changed because it is already right.","equipment":["Thermometer","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sugar%20on%20Snow%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sugar on Snow"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sugar on Snow","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sugar%20on%20Snow","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sugar","Pickles","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"vermont-cheddar-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml vegetable stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sharp Vermont cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dry mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dash of hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the vegetables in butter until soft without color, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the flour and cook 2 min to a blond roux.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the stock slowly, whisking, then the milk. Simmer 15 min until smooth and lightly thickened.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, add the cheese in handfuls, stirring until each melts before adding more. Never let it boil after the cheese goes in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with mustard, hot sauce, salt, and white pepper. Serve with chives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cheese added to a boiling pot goes stringy and greasy: the pan comes off the heat first, always. Grate the cheddar yourself; the anti-caking dust on pre-grated cheese makes the soup gritty.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vermont%20Cheddar%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vermont Cheddar Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Vermont Cheddar Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Vermont%20Cheddar%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Carrot","Celery","Chives","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"maple-creemee","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml heavy cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml dark maple syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the milk, cream, and syrup together until steaming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with the salt, temper in the hot dairy, and return to the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook to 82C, stirring, until it coats the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain and chill thoroughly, ideally overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Churn in an ice cream machine and serve immediately, soft, in a cone: a creemee is never hard-frozen.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Every Vermont roadside stand runs a maple creemee machine in summer, and the texture is the point: serve it straight from the churn, soft enough to curl; a creemee that holds a stiff peak has waited too long. Real syrup in the base, dark grade for flavor, not maple extract; Vermonters can taste the difference across a parking lot. The name is Vermont's own, defended casually but firmly against 'soft serve', which is what everyone else calls the lesser thing.","equipment":["Ice-cream churner"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Churning ice cream","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Churning ice cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ice%20cream%20churning%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maple%20Creemee%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maple Creemee"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Maple Creemee","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Maple%20Creemee","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"maple-glazed-ham","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4kg bone-in smoked ham"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml maple syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Whole cloves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the ham fat in a diamond pattern and stud with cloves. Set on a rack with a cup of water in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 160C, covered loosely with foil, for about 90 min.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the syrup, mustard, vinegar, and pepper together 5 min into a glaze.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Uncover the ham and brush with glaze every 15 min for the last 45 min, until lacquered and deep amber.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":2880,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 20 min before carving; boil down the pan juices with any leftover glaze for the table.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Repeated thin coats build lacquer; one thick coat at the start just burns. Sugar's schedule is unforgiving, so the glazing starts in the last hour and repeats every fifteen minutes. Score the fat in diamonds so the glaze has channels to follow. Save the bone for the bean pot; it is the second dinner hiding inside the first, and Yankee thrift considers discarding it a moral failure. Dark syrup again: the pale grades disappear against smoke and salt.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Scoring"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","bright","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maple-Glazed%20Ham%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maple-Glazed Ham"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Maple-Glazed Ham","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Maple-Glazed%20Ham","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Sugar","Maple syrup","Clove"],"judgedBy":["scoring"]},{"slug":"cheddar-and-chive-biscuits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"375g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"170g cold butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sharp cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp chives, snipped"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml cold buttermilk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the dry ingredients, then cut in the cold butter until the pieces are pea-sized.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss through the cheddar and chives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the buttermilk and stir just until it comes together: the dough should look shaggy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat to 3cm thick on a floured board, fold in thirds twice for layers, then cut straight down with a floured cutter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 15-18 min until risen and golden.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":false}],"note":"Cutting straight down without twisting is what lets the layers climb; a twisted cutter seals the edges and the biscuit stays squat, the same physics as every biscuit, with cheese raising the stakes because the fat load is higher. Freeze the butter, keep the cheddar coarse so it forms pockets, and get them into the oven cold. Sharp Vermont cheddar or don't bother; the chives are the freshness that keeps the richness honest. Split and butter while the steam still escapes.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Letter-folding dough","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Letter-folding dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=letter%20fold%20dough%20lamination%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cheddar%20and%20Chive%20Biscuits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cheddar and Chive Biscuits"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cheddar and Chive Biscuits","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cheddar%20and%20Chive%20Biscuits","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chives","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Buttermilk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"sugarhouse-baked-beans","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried soldier or navy beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g salt pork, scored"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml maple syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dry mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained beans in fresh water 30 min until the skins split. Drain and keep the liquid.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the onion, beans, and salt pork in a bean pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the syrup, sugar, mustard, salt, and pepper into 500ml bean liquid and pour over the beans.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and bake at 130C for 6 hours, adding bean liquid whenever the surface looks dry.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Uncover for the last 45 min to glaze.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false}],"note":"Maple in place of molasses is the Vermont signature: lighter, more floral, and it wants a full hour uncovered at the end so the top can set into a glaze; covered to the finish, the beans stay soupy and the maple never concentrates. Salt pork in the pot, an onion buried whole, and the long slow oven doing what it has always done. This is the northern fork of the Boston bean road, argued across a state line for two centuries with no armistice expected.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sugarhouse%20Baked%20Beans%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sugarhouse Baked Beans"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sugarhouse Baked Beans","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sugarhouse%20Baked%20Beans","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","White beans","Mustard","Sugar","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"chicken-and-cheddar-pot-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g cooked chicken, torn in pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g mushrooms, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sharp cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Puff pastry sheet"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the leek, carrot, and mushrooms in butter until the mushroom liquid cooks away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the flour, cook 2 min, then whisk in the stock gradually until thickened.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add cream, thyme, and chicken; off the heat, stir in the cheddar until melted. Season well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill a deep dish, top with pastry, seal, vent, and brush with egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 30 min until the pastry is puffed and deep gold.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"Cook the mushrooms until they squeak and their water has gone before the flour goes in, or that water will thin the sauce inside the crust where you cannot fix it, the one unfixable failure in a dish that forgives most things. The cheddar joins off the heat so it melts smooth instead of breaking. Season past shy, as with every pot pie; pastry eats seasoning. Vermont's twist on the farm standard earns its place because cheddar and chicken were always neighbors here.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20and%20Cheddar%20Pot%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken and Cheddar Pot Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Chicken and Cheddar Pot Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Chicken%20and%20Cheddar%20Pot%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Leek","Carrot","Mushrooms","Thyme","Butter","Cream","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour","Phyllo/Puff pastry","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"vermont-maple-brined-pork-chops","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick bone-in pork chops"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the brine: 1L water, 60g salt, 120ml maple syrup, 2 bay leaves, 1 tbsp peppercorns, 4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp maple syrup, for the glaze"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm a third of the water with the salt and syrup until dissolved, then add the rest cold along with the bay, peppercorns, and garlic. Chill completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brine the chops 4-6 hours, no longer, or the texture turns spongy and the meat reads salty.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the chops out, rinse, and dry them thoroughly: a wet chop steams instead of searing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear in the oil over high heat 3-4 min a side, then add the butter and baste until they read 62C at the bone.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 8 min. Deglaze the pan with the vinegar, whisk in the glaze syrup and thyme, and spoon over the rested chops.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sugaring country puts maple in the brine as well as the glaze, where its sugar helps the crust brown and its sweetness balances the salt. Four to six hours is the window: pork left overnight in a brine this strong goes spongy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Brining & curing","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","gently hot","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vermont%20Maple-Brined%20Pork%20Chops%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vermont Maple-Brined Pork Chops"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Vermont Maple-Brined Pork Chops","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Vermont%20Maple-Brined%20Pork%20Chops","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"baste-arroser-and-glaze","term":"Baste, Arroser & Glaze"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Maple syrup","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"boston-clam-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3kg quahogs or 900g chopped clams with their juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g salt pork, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g floury potatoes, in 2cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml clam juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml heavy cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper and thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oyster crackers to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"If using whole quahogs, steam them open in a little water, chop the meat, and strain the liquor through cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the salt pork slowly until crisp; lift out the cracklings and reserve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions in the fat until translucent, then add the potatoes, clam juice, and bay. Simmer 15 min until the potatoes are tender and starting to break at the edges.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the milk and cream and heat until steaming, never boil. Add the clams and cook 2 min only.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with pepper and thyme, finish with butter, and rest off the heat 30 min before rewarming to serve, topped with cracklings.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Flour has no place here: a proper Boston chowder is thickened by potato starch alone, and the flour-thickened wallpaper-paste version is the tourist tell. Clams go in last: two minutes is cooked, five is rubber, and their strained liquor should have gone in long before. Salt pork to start, cream at the end, oyster crackers by the fistful. Boston takes this seriously enough that a 1939 bill tried to outlaw tomatoes in chowder; it failed as law and succeeded as culture.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boston%20Clam%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boston Clam Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Boston Clam Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Boston%20Clam%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Mussels/Clams","Onion","Potato","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"boston-baked-beans","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried navy beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g salt pork, scored"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dry mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained beans in fresh water 30 min until the skins curl back when you blow on them. Drain and save the liquid.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the onion in the base of a bean pot, add beans, and bury the salt pork with the rind standing proud.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk molasses, sugar, mustard, salt, and pepper into 600ml of the bean liquid and pour in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and bake at 130C for 6-7 hours, topping up with bean liquid every hour so they never dry out.","durationSec":25200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":25200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Uncover the last hour to darken the top and crisp the pork rind.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"These beans named the city (Boston, the Bean Town) and molasses came up from the Caribbean trade that Boston's ships ran, its slight bitterness keeping six hours of sugar from cloying. Navy beans, salt pork, an onion, and an oven you're not in a hurry to reclaim. Never salt early; salt toughens bean skins and adds an hour. Saturday beans with brown bread was the Puritan rhythm (cooked before the Sabbath, eaten without labor), and the pot still keeps that schedule happily.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boston%20Baked%20Beans%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boston Baked Beans"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Boston Baked Beans","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Boston%20Baked%20Beans","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","White beans","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"boston-cream-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the sponge: 250g flour, 2 tsp baking powder, 1/2 tsp salt, 115g butter, 250g sugar, 3 eggs, 180ml milk, 2 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the pastry cream: 500ml milk, 6 yolks, 100g sugar, 50g cornstarch, 2 tbsp butter, 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the glaze: 175g dark chocolate, 175ml cream, 1 tbsp corn syrup"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the pastry cream first: heat the milk, whisk yolks with sugar and cornstarch, temper, return to the pan, and cook to a thick boil. Beat in butter and vanilla, cover the surface, and chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For the cake, cream butter and sugar, add eggs one at a time, then alternate dry ingredients with milk. Divide between two 23cm pans.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C for 25 min until a skewer comes clean. Cool completely.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Spread the cold pastry cream over one layer, thick, and set the second on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the cream, pour over the chocolate, add the syrup, stir smooth, and pour over the cake so it drips down the sides. Chill 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"It is a cake, not a pie: the name survives from the 1850s when cakes were baked in pie tins, and the Parker House hotel claims the original, chocolate glaze and all, from 1856. It is also the official dessert of Massachusetts, and disputes are settled by eating. The custard must be thick enough to stay in its layer under the weight of cake and ganache; a runny pastry cream turns the state dessert into a landslide. Chill before slicing, and use a hot knife for clean strata.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Creaming butter & sugar","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, melting away silken."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boston%20Cream%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boston Cream Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Boston Cream Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Boston%20Cream%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"parker-house-rolls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter, softened, plus 85g melted for brushing"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix flour, sugar, salt, and yeast. Add the milk, eggs, and softened butter and knead 10 min to a smooth, elastic dough.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rise, covered, 1 hour until doubled.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into 16 pieces, roll each into a ball, then flatten into an oval. Brush with melted butter, crease the middle with a knife handle, and fold not quite in half.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange overlapping in a buttered pan, cover, and rise 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 18-20 min until deep gold, brushing with more melted butter as they come out. Finish with flaky salt.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented at the Parker House hotel in the 1870s. The off-center fold is the signature: it gives the roll its lip, and the butter in the crease is what makes it pull apart in sheets.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Parker%20House%20Rolls%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Parker House Rolls"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Parker House Rolls","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Parker%20House%20Rolls","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"whole-belly-fried-clams","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g shucked soft-shell clams, bellies intact"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corn flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pastry flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon and tartar sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the clams in evaporated milk 20 min: the classic Ipswich step.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Combine corn flour, pastry flour, salt, and pepper in a wide dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C. Lift clams from the milk a handful at a time and toss in the flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry 60-90 seconds until pale gold and crisp; they darken fast, so pull early.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt at once, serve with lemon and tartar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chubby Woodman fried the first ones on Ipswich beach in 1916: the clam shack origin story New England actually agrees on. Evaporated milk, not buttermilk, is the North Shore secret: it browns faster and clings better, and the corn-flour dredge finishes the sandy crust. Whole bellies, never strips; the belly is the flavor and the strip is the apology. Fry in small batches so the oil never sulks, and eat them within sight of salt water if at all possible.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Shucking shellfish","Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Shucking shellfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20shuck%20oysters%20clams%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Whole-Belly%20Fried%20Clams%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Whole-Belly Fried Clams"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Whole-Belly Fried Clams","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Whole-Belly%20Fried%20Clams","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Mussels/Clams","Corn","Lemon","Milk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"boston-brown-bread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"125g rye flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"125g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"125g whole wheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g raisins (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter two 500ml cans or a pudding mold well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flours, cornmeal, soda, and salt together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the molasses and buttermilk until just combined, then the raisins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the molds two-thirds full, cover tightly with buttered foil, and set on a rack in a deep pot. Pour in boiling water to halfway up the molds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and steam over low heat 2 hours, topping up the water. Turn out and slice thick with butter.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Steamed, not baked: the moist heat gives it the dense pudding crumb, three flours (rye, cornmeal, wheat) and molasses in a can standing in simmering water for two hours. It is the traditional partner to Saturday beans, the pair sold door-to-door together in living memory, and it slices best the next day, ideally with cream cheese or more molasses. The can is not an affectation; it is the mold that makes the shape, and the string-cut rounds are the correct service.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boston%20Brown%20Bread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boston Brown Bread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Boston Brown Bread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Boston%20Brown%20Bread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Buttermilk","Bread","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"baked-scrod","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 pieces young cod or haddock, about 180g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cracker crumbs (Ritz style)"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the fish dry, season, and lay in a buttered baking dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the cracker crumbs with the melted butter, parsley, and paprika.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze lemon over the fish and pack the crumb mixture on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 12-15 min until the crumbs are gold and the fish flakes at the thickest point.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve at once with lemon wedges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Scrod is not a species: it is Boston market slang for a young cod or haddock, whatever came in that morning. Buttered cracker crumbs are the traditional coat and they beat breadcrumbs for richness.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baked%20Scrod%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baked Scrod"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Baked Scrod","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Baked%20Scrod","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Paprika"]},{"slug":"toll-house-chocolate-chip-cookies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"280g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"225g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g granulated sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"165g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g semisweet chocolate chips"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g walnuts, chopped (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk flour, soda, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream the butter with both sugars until light, 3 min, then beat in the eggs one at a time and the vanilla.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the dry ingredients, then the chips and nuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the dough in the fridge at least an hour; overnight is better.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in spoonfuls at 190C for 9-11 min until set at the edges and still soft in the middle. Cool on the tray 5 min.","durationSec":660,"handsOnSec":660,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"Ruth Wakefield invented these at the Toll House Inn in Whitman in 1938. Resting the dough hydrates the flour and deepens the flavor, the single biggest improvement to the original recipe.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Creaming butter & sugar","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Toll%20House%20Chocolate%20Chip%20Cookies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Toll House Chocolate Chip Cookies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Toll House Chocolate Chip Cookies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Toll%20House%20Chocolate%20Chip%20Cookies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"cape-cod-cranberry-relish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"350g fresh cranberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 orange, zest and juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp port or water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Combine the cranberries, sugar, orange juice and zest, cinnamon, salt, and port in a saucepan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring to a simmer over medium heat, stirring until the sugar dissolves.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 10-12 min until most of the berries have popped and the syrup runs thick off a spoon.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Taste and adjust: it should be sharp, not candied.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool completely; it thickens as it sets. Keeps two weeks refrigerated.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Massachusetts bogs grow a third of the country's cranberries, and this relish is the shortest distance between the bog and the table. Pull the pot while a few berries still hold their shape; a fully collapsed relish is jam and eats like it: the pop of the holdouts is the texture the dish is named for. The port variant brings a winey depth the water version can't. It keeps for weeks and improves for days, which is why it is always made too early and never too much.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cape%20Cod%20Cranberry%20Relish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cape Cod Cranberry Relish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cape Cod Cranberry Relish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cape%20Cod%20Cranberry%20Relish","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Orange","Dried fruit","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"yankee-pot-roast","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg beef chuck roast"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 potatoes, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 parsnips, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the beef well and dry it. Brown all sides hard in the oil, then set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions, add tomato paste and cook until brick red, then deglaze with the wine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the beef with stock, bay, and thyme. Cover and braise at 150C for 2.5 hours.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the carrots, celery, potatoes, and parsnips and cook 1 hour more until everything is tender.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the meat, skim the fat, reduce the liquid if thin, and serve the roast sliced with the vegetables and gravy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chuck is the cut: leaner roasts turn to string over four hours, while chuck's collagen melts into the gravy that makes the dish; choosing a 'better' cut is choosing a worse dinner. The vegetables go in late so they arrive tender rather than dissolved: the first hour belongs to the meat alone. Brown hard, braise low, and let the pot rest before carving. This is the Sunday engine of two centuries of New England kitchens, and it asks only patience and the right mistake-proof cut.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yankee%20Pot%20Roast%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yankee Pot Roast"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Yankee Pot Roast","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Yankee%20Pot%20Roast","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Parsnip","Thyme","Bay leaf","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["braising","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"rhode-island-clear-clam-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg quahogs, or 700g chopped clams with juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g bacon or salt pork, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g potatoes, in 2cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L clam broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steam the quahogs open in 250ml water, chop the meat, and strain the liquor carefully through cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the bacon; lift the bits out and sweat the onions in the fat until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes, strained clam broth, bay, and thyme. Simmer 15 min until the potatoes are tender.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the clams and cook 2 min only.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season with plenty of black pepper, scatter with parsley and bacon, and serve: no milk, no cream, no tomato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rhode Island's clear chowder is the oldest of the three and its argument is transparency: nothing hides the clam: no cream, no tomato, just broth that must be judged before serving, since it is the whole dish. Quahogs are the correct clam, their liquor the correct base, and restraint the correct seasoning. The clear style predates both the creamy and the red, a point Rhode Islanders make gently but will make. Salt pork and potato carry it; everything else stands aside.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","herb-fresh","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rhode%20Island%20Clear%20Clam%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rhode Island Clear Clam Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Rhode Island Clear Clam Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Rhode%20Island%20Clear%20Clam%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Mussels/Clams","Onion","Potato","Parsley","Thyme","Bay leaf","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"clam-cakes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml clam juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g chopped clams, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs with the clam juice and milk, then stir into the dry ingredients to a thick batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the chopped clams. Rest 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat oil to 180C and drop the batter in by heaped tablespoons, 5 or 6 at a time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry 4-5 min, turning, until deep gold and cooked through. Drain and salt immediately.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"They must be craggy, not smooth: drop the batter roughly and let it find its own shape, because the crags fry crisp while a smooth ball just breads itself. At any Rhode Island shore dinner they arrive with chowder, and dunking is expected; cake into clear broth is the state's own bread-and-soup ritual. Chopped quahogs and their liquor in the batter, never minced to invisibility. Sold by the dozen in paper bags at every seaside window from Galilee to Watch Hill.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Clam%20Cakes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Clam Cakes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Clam Cakes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Clam%20Cakes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Eggs","Milk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"stuffies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 large quahogs"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g chourico or linguica, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g stuffing crackers, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Old Bay or paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges and hot sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steam the quahogs open in a little water, reserving the liquor. Chop the meat and scrub 12 half shells for stuffing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, celery, and chourico in the butter until soft and the sausage has rendered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, mix in the cracker crumbs, chopped clams, parsley, Old Bay, and enough strained clam liquor to make a moist but not wet stuffing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mound into the shells and set on a tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 20-25 min until the tops are browned and crisp. Serve with lemon and hot sauce.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Portuguese sausage is the Rhode Island signature: Fall River and Providence brought chourico to the quahog and never looked back. Keep the stuffing moist; dry stuffies are the local scandal.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stuffies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Stuffies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Stuffies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Stuffies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Onion","Celery","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Hot sauce","Paprika","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"rhode-island-hot-wieners","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 small veal-and-pork wieners"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 small soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: 450g ground beef, 1 onion grated, 2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp allspice, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp paprika, 1/2 tsp celery salt, 1 tbsp paprika, 250ml water, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"To serve: yellow mustard, chopped raw onion, celery salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Break the raw ground beef into the water in a cold pan and mash it fine: it must stay loose, never browned in clumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the grated onion and all the spices and simmer 45 min, stirring, until thick and dark.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam the wieners and buns until soft and hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build in order: wiener in bun, then mustard, then meat sauce, then raw onion, then a shake of celery salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Eat immediately, standing up if possible.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Order them all the way and count them by the armload, as the counter men do: the New York System houses line them up the arm, dress them assembly-line, and take offense at the word 'hot dog'. The sauce is simmered from raw, never browned, which is why it stays fine-grained rather than crumbly: a cumin-allspice meat sauce that is seasoning, not chili. Meat sauce, mustard, onion, celery salt, in that order. Coffee milk alongside completes Rhode Island's strangest and best meal.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, warm spice humming through the middle, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rhode%20Island%20Hot%20Wieners%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rhode Island Hot Wieners"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Rhode Island Hot Wieners","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Rhode%20Island%20Hot%20Wieners","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Onion","Celery","Chiles","Bread","Mustard","Cumin","Paprika","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"johnnycakes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g white flint cornmeal, stone-ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the griddle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Maple syrup or butter to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the cornmeal, salt, and sugar in a bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the boiling water over and stir hard: it will thicken into a stiff mush. Rest 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Loosen with the milk to a thick, droppable batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle in generous butter over medium heat, 5-6 min a side, until a deep crust forms and the middles are creamy.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve hot with butter and syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rhode Island fights over these: the South County style is thin and lacy, the Newport style thick and creamy. Both demand white flint cornmeal, which the state protects by law and by argument.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Johnnycakes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Johnnycakes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Johnnycakes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Johnnycakes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"coffee-cabinet","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120ml coffee syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 large scoops vanilla ice cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ice, if you like it colder"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour the coffee syrup and milk into a blender.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the ice cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend on high 20-30 seconds: it should be thick enough to resist the straw but still drinkable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a tall chilled glass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately with a fat straw and a long spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coffee milk is the official state drink and a cabinet is its milkshake form, named for the wooden cabinet the blender sat in. Anywhere else this is a frappe, and Rhode Islanders will correct you.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Coffee%20Cabinet%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Coffee Cabinet"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Coffee Cabinet","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Coffee%20Cabinet","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Cream","Vanilla","Coffee/Espresso"]},{"slug":"rhode-island-grilled-pizza","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g pizza dough, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil for brushing"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml crushed tomatoes, seasoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g fresh mozzarella, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parmesan, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fresh basil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, chili flakes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat a grill to high on one side, medium on the other.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stretch the dough thin and free-form on an oiled tray: no rolling pin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay it directly on the hot grates, oiled side down. Grill 2-3 min until the underside is marked and it releases.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip onto the cooler side, brush with oil, then top quickly with tomato, cheeses, and seasoning.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and grill 4-5 min until the cheese melts and the base crisps. Finish with basil off the fire.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Al Forno in Providence started this in 1980: Johanne Killeen and George Germon dropping dough straight on the grate and inventing an American original. Top it after the flip, not before, and keep the toppings sparse: a wet pizza will never crisp on a grate, and grilled pizza is about the smoke-blistered crust, not the cargo. The dough wants oil in it and a hot zone and cool zone on the grill. It is the rare restaurant invention that works better at home than its imitators do in restaurants.","equipment":["Grill","Sheet pan","Rolling pin","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","rich","herb-fresh","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, richness that coats and comforts, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rhode%20Island%20Grilled%20Pizza%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rhode Island Grilled Pizza"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Rhode Island Grilled Pizza","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Rhode%20Island%20Grilled%20Pizza","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Chiles","Basil","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Mozzarella"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"doughboys","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"320ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Granulated sugar for dusting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, yeast, salt, and sugar; add the water and oil and knead 8 min to a soft dough.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rise, covered, 1 hour until doubled.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tear off pieces the size of an egg and stretch each into a rough flat round.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 180C for 2 min a side until puffed and deep gold.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain briefly and toss in sugar while still hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beach food, sold from stands along Narragansett Bay: fried dough by weight and sugar by avalanche, the Rhode Island shore's counterpart to the zeppole and the elephant ear. Stretch them thin in the middle so they blister and puff, leaving the rim thicker to hold; sugar them the instant they leave the oil so the coat sticks. Iggy's sells them by the dozen next to clam cakes, and the correct order is both. Eat immediately: a doughboy's half-life is measured in minutes.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Doughboys%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Doughboys"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Doughboys","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Doughboys","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"snail-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g cooked scungilli (whelk), sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 celery rib, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 red onion, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp chili flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black olives (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slice the cooked whelk as thin as you can manage: thickness is toughness here.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss with the celery, onion, garlic, and olives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress with olive oil, vinegar, chili flakes, and lemon; season well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Marinate at least 30 min in the fridge, tossing once or twice.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with parsley and serve cold with crusty bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"An Italian-American Rhode Island bar staple: scungilli sliced thin and dressed sharp, on every shore-dinner menu and almost no menus anywhere else. The slicing is the technique: thin, cold, and given half an hour in the dressing so the acid can do its softening work on the firm whelk meat; thick-cut snail salad chews like an argument. Lemon, oil, garlic, celery, olives: the dressing is a vinaigrette with opinions. Rhode Island's Italian coast in one cold plate.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Snail%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Snail Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Snail Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Snail%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Celery","Chiles","Olives","Lemon","Parsley","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"dynamite-sandwich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green peppers, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp chili flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 torpedo rolls"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef hard in a wide pan, breaking it up, then drain most of the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion, pepper, and celery and cook until they soften, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the tomato paste and cook 2 min, then the crushed tomatoes, chili flakes, paprika, and water.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 30-40 min until thick and glossy, seasoning as you go.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon into rolls, heaped, and eat with napkins to spare.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Woonsocket original that should carry real heat: the name is a promise, not a brand, and the mill-town French-Canadian families who invented it meant it. It is a sloppy-joe-adjacent pepper-onion-beef simmer served in a torpedo roll, and it improves overnight, so make it a day ahead if you can; the rest marries the peppers into the sauce. Made in big batches for crowds, which is its natural habitat: dynamites were always fundraiser and block-party food.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dynamite%20Sandwich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dynamite Sandwich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Dynamite Sandwich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Dynamite%20Sandwich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Celery","Bell pepper","Chiles","Bread","Tomato paste","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"new-haven-white-clam-apizza","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g bread flour, 340ml water, 10g salt, 2g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g shucked littleneck clams, chopped, with their juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pecorino romano, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chili flakes and black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the dough and knead briefly, then cold-ferment it 24-48 hours in the fridge: plan a day ahead; the active work below is 45 minutes.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":38700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat an oven with a stone or steel as hot as it will go, 280C or above, for a full hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stretch the dough thin and slightly uneven: apizza is oblong and rustic, never a perfect circle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush with olive oil, scatter garlic, clams with a little of their juice, oregano, chili, and pecorino. No mozzarella.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 6-8 min until the edges blister black in spots. Finish with more oil, pepper, and a squeeze of lemon.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true}],"note":"Frank Pepe put this on the map in 1964, though the coal ovens on Wooster Street had been charring apizza for decades before the white clam pie made them a pilgrimage. There is no tomato and no mozzarella: the clams, garlic, oil and pecorino are the whole argument, and the char is not a mistake: 'burnt' edges are the New Haven signature, and asking for a paler pie marks you. Freshly shucked littlenecks, never canned. 'Apizza' is pronounced ah-BEETZ, and yes, that matters there.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Shucking shellfish","Fermentation","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","umami-deep","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Shucking shellfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20shuck%20oysters%20clams%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Haven%20White%20Clam%20Apizza%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Haven White Clam Apizza"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Haven White Clam Apizza","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Haven%20White%20Clam%20Apizza","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pecorino-romano","term":"Pecorino Romano"}],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Bread","Flour","Clove"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"connecticut-hot-lobster-roll","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Meat from 2 lobsters (about 700g), in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"170g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 split-top buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter, softened, for the buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt and black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the 170g butter in a wide pan over low heat with the lemon juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the lobster meat and warm it through very gently, 3-4 min: the butter should never sizzle.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Meanwhile butter and griddle the buns until gold and crisp on both flat sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the lobster out with a slotted spoon, letting excess butter drain, and pile into the buns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon a little warm butter over, season with flaky salt and pepper, and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Connecticut roll came first: Perry's in Milford served it hot in the 1920s, a decade before Maine chilled it. Keep the butter under a simmer; boiling it makes the lobster squeak.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Connecticut%20Hot%20Lobster%20Roll%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Connecticut Hot Lobster Roll"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Connecticut Hot Lobster Roll","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Connecticut%20Hot%20Lobster%20Roll","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lobster","Lemon","Chives","Butter","Bread"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"steamed-cheeseburgers","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef (80/20), formed into 4 thick patties"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g extra-sharp cheddar, cut in thick chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 hard rolls or kaiser buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mustard and sliced tomato to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Set a steamer over simmering water. Season the patties well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Place the patties in the steamer trays and steam, covered, 8-10 min until cooked through and juicy.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Steam the cheddar chunks separately in a small dish for the last 5 min until molten and pooling.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toast the buns lightly if you like.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set a patty in each bun and pour the molten cheddar over generously. Top with onion, mustard, and tomato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Central Connecticut and nowhere else: Meriden claims the invention, and the steam cabinets survive in a dozen lunchrooms in one small radius. The burger stays remarkably juicy since nothing renders away, and the cheese must be poured, not laid: steamed cheddar becomes a molten sauce ladled from its own tray, which is the actual point of the apparatus. No crust, no char, and that is the confession: this burger trades the sear for juice and gets away with it.","equipment":["Steamer","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Steamed%20Cheeseburgers%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Steamed Cheeseburgers"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Steamed Cheeseburgers","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Steamed%20Cheeseburgers","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Bread","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"hartford-election-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the sponge: 200g flour, 7g instant yeast, 250ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 400g flour, 200g brown sugar, 170g butter, 2 eggs, 1 tsp cinnamon, 1/2 tsp nutmeg, 1/2 tsp cloves, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g raisins soaked in 120ml sherry or brandy"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g currants"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the glaze: 150g icing sugar, 2 tbsp milk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the sponge ingredients together and let stand, covered, 1 hour until bubbly.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream the butter and brown sugar, beat in the eggs, then add the sponge, remaining flour, spices, and salt. Knead to a soft, rich dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the drained fruit. Rise, covered, 2 hours until doubled.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape into a buttered bundt or tube pan and rise 1 hour more.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 45-55 min until deep brown and hollow-sounding. Cool, then drizzle with glaze.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Baked for town election days in the 1700s so voters would linger and celebrate. It is a yeasted cake, closer to a rich bread than to a modern batter cake, and it keeps for a week.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Creaming butter & sugar","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hartford%20Election%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hartford Election Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Hartford Election Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Hartford%20Election%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Flour","Wine","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"shad-roe-with-bacon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 pairs shad roe"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 rashers bacon"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flour, seasoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the roe gently and pat dry; do not separate the pairs or pierce the membrane.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the bacon until crisp in a heavy skillet; lift out and keep warm, leaving the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the roe lightly in seasoned flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook gently in the bacon fat with the butter, 4-5 min a side, spooning fat over. It should be firm at the edge and just set inside: high heat makes it burst.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with lemon and parsley, and serve on the bacon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A brief spring rite on the Connecticut River: the shad run gave the state a season and a festival, and the roe sacs are its prize. Cook it low: the membrane traps steam and a hot pan will pop it, spattering the kitchen with the evidence; some cooks prick it once with a pin as insurance. Bacon fat is the traditional medium and the bacon the traditional escort. Medium is done: pink at the center, never gray through. Weeks, not months, and then it's gone.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shad%20Roe%20with%20Bacon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shad Roe with Bacon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Shad Roe with Bacon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Shad%20Roe%20with%20Bacon","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"new-haven-tomato-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g bread flour, 340ml water, 10g salt, 2g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed San Marzano tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pecorino romano, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and chili flakes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix and cold-ferment the dough 24-48 hours ahead: the active work below is 45 minutes.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":38700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the oven with a stone or steel at maximum for an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the crushed tomatoes with garlic, oil, oregano, salt, and chili: they go on raw, never cooked first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stretch the dough thin and oblong, spread the tomato to the edges, and dust with pecorino only.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 6-8 min until charred at the rim. Finish with olive oil.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true}],"note":"The plain pie of New Haven: tomato, pecorino, oil, and char; mozzarella is an extra you must ask for by name, and the purists never do, because the plain pie is the test of the tomatoes and the oven and nothing else. Crushed tomatoes with body, a hard grating cheese for salt, and the coal-fired char doing the seasoning. This is the apizza baseline against which the clam pie is the celebrated exception. Thin, oblong, cut into unequal strips as tradition demands.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Haven%20Tomato%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Haven Tomato Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Haven Tomato Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Haven%20Tomato%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Bread","Flour","Clove"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"fried-oysters-with-tartar","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"24 shucked oysters, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the tartar: 200g mayonnaise, 2 tbsp cornichons minced, 1 tbsp capers, 1 tbsp lemon juice, 1 tbsp parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the tartar ingredients together and chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soak the oysters in buttermilk 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix cornmeal, flour, salt, and cayenne in a wide dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat oil to 190C. Dredge the oysters, shake off excess, and fry in batches 90 seconds until gold and crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt, and serve immediately with the tartar and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Norwalk built its fortune on oysters and still runs the beds: the oyster sloops and the September festival keep the ledger open. Fry hot and fast: an oyster held too long in the oil goes from plump to leathery in under a minute, so the crust must finish before the oyster notices it's cooking. Cornmeal in the dredge for crunch, and a tartar sharpened with real pickle brine. Plump, briny, barely cooked at the center: the fried oyster is a timing exam with a rich reward.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Shucking shellfish","Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","gently hot","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":37,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Shucking shellfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20shuck%20oysters%20clams%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fried%20Oysters%20with%20Tartar%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fried Oysters with Tartar"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Fried Oysters with Tartar","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Fried%20Oysters%20with%20Tartar","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Capers","Lemon","Parsley","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Mayonnaise","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"connecticut-grinder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 grinder rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g Genoa salami"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g capicola"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g mortadella or ham"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g provolone, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Shredded iceberg lettuce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sliced tomato and red onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sliced pepperoncini"},{"kind":"item","text":"Olive oil and red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oregano, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the rolls and hollow a little bread from the top half so the fillings sit steady.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the cheese first (it protects the bread from the dressing), then the meats in folded ribbons.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add lettuce, tomato, onion, and pepperoncini.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress with oil and vinegar, then oregano, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the sandwich closed, wrap tightly, and rest 10 min before cutting on the diagonal.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"New England calls it a grinder for the chew of the crust: the name is the sandwich's spec sheet, demanding bread with resistance. Cheese against the bread and dressing on top of the vegetables is the order that keeps it from going soggy: the cheese is a moisture barrier, the oil stays high where it seasons instead of soaking. Toasted, the grinder becomes its best self: the hot grinder is a Connecticut lunch-counter institution, and the crunch is the signature.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","briny","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Connecticut%20Grinder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Connecticut Grinder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Connecticut Grinder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Connecticut%20Grinder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Lettuce/Greens","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Bread","Vinegar","Wine"]},{"slug":"nutmeg-apple-crisp","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg apples, peeled and sliced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp fresh-grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the topping: 150g rolled oats, 150g flour, 165g brown sugar, 1/2 tsp salt, 170g cold butter, cubed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the apples with sugar, lemon, cinnamon, and nutmeg and pile into a buttered baking dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the topping ingredients together with your fingers to rough clumps, leaving some butter in pieces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter over the apples without pressing down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 45-50 min until the topping is deep gold and the juices bubble at the edges.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 20 min and serve with cream or vanilla ice cream.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Connecticut is the Nutmeg State, from an old joke about peddlers selling carved wooden fakes. Grate it fresh here: the pre-ground jar is exactly the counterfeit the joke describes.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nutmeg%20Apple%20Crisp%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nutmeg Apple Crisp"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nutmeg Apple Crisp","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nutmeg%20Apple%20Crisp","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Apple","Butter","Bread","Flour","Oats","Sugar","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"norwalk-oyster-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml shucked oysters with their liquor"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml heavy cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 shallot, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp celery salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"White pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paprika and chives to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oyster crackers"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the celery and shallot in the butter over low heat until soft without color, 8 min.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the milk and cream and heat until steaming: do not boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain the oyster liquor into the pot and heat through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the oysters and cook 2-3 min, only until their edges curl.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season with celery salt and white pepper, dust with paprika, and serve with crackers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Curled edges mean done: one minute past that and they shrink to buttons, so the oysters go in last and the pot comes off almost immediately. Let the finished stew stand off the heat five minutes before serving so the flavor comes forward; oyster stew is one of the rare dishes that improves in the pause. Milk and butter and the oysters' own liquor: the Christmas Eve stew of the whole Northeast coast, and Norwalk's beds supplied it for a century.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shucking shellfish","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":37,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Shucking shellfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20shuck%20oysters%20clams%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwalk%20Oyster%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Norwalk Oyster Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Norwalk Oyster Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Norwalk%20Oyster%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Celery","Chives","Butter","Milk","Cream","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"new-york-slice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"320ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g low-moisture mozzarella, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pecorino, chili flakes, and garlic powder to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the dough, knead 8 min, and rise 90 min, or cold-ferment overnight for better flavor.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat a stone or steel at maximum for a full hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stretch the dough to a thin 40cm round, leaving a modest rim; the center should be nearly translucent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the seasoned tomato thinly, then the mozzarella to the edge of the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 8-10 min until the cheese blisters and the underside is spotted. Slice into wide wedges: it should fold in the hand without cracking.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"The fold test is the whole New York standard: crisp enough to hold, pliable enough to bend: a slice that cracks when folded is overbaked, one that flops is underdone, and the city judges in silence. Low-moisture mozzarella, not fresh: fresh releases water and floods the slice, which is why the corner slice shop and the Neapolitan purist buy different cheese. High heat, thin center, puffed rim, oil running down your wrist: eaten walking, folded, on a paper plate that did nothing to deserve this.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","umami-deep","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20York%20Slice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New York Slice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New York Slice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20York%20Slice","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Mozzarella","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"bagels-with-lox-and-schmear","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 fresh bagels, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cream cheese, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cold-smoked salmon, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp capers"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fresh dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper and lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slice the bagels and toast only if they are a day old: a fresh bagel is never toasted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread cream cheese thickly on both halves, edge to edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drape the salmon in loose folds rather than flat sheets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add tomato, onion, capers, and dill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with cracked pepper and a squeeze of lemon; serve open or closed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lox is salt-cured, nova is cold-smoked, and New Yorkers argue about both: lox is the older, saltier tradition from the belly, nova the gentler modern default, and asking for one when you mean the other marks you. Slice the onion on a mandoline if you have one; thick onion bullies everything else on the bagel. The bagel must be boiled-then-baked and dense enough to matter. Appetizing stores, the dairy-and-fish counters like Russ & Daughters, are the institution this plate comes from.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bagels%20with%20Lox%20and%20Schmear%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bagels with Lox and Schmear"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Bagels with Lox and Schmear","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Bagels%20with%20Lox%20and%20Schmear","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Smoked fish","Onion","Tomato","Capers","Lemon","Dill","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese"]},{"slug":"pastrami-on-rye","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g hot pastrami, sliced by hand"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices seeded rye bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"Deli mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"Half-sour pickles"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coleslaw and Russian dressing (optional, for the debate)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steam the pastrami over simmering water 15-20 min until it is hot through and the fat turns soft and translucent.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slice it by hand, thick and slightly against the grain: machine-thin pastrami loses its texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the rye out and spread with mustard only. Nothing else touches the bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the pastrami high, at least 250g a sandwich, and press the top slice on gently.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut on the diagonal and serve with pickles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Steam it, never microwave it: the steam melts the fat that carries the spice crust, which is why deli pastrami arrives glistening and home reheats arrive gray. Hand-sliced, thick, against the grain. And mustard alone: mayonnaise on pastrami is a hill New Yorkers will die on, and the deli counter will say so out loud. The pastrami itself is Romanian-Jewish immigrant genius: brisket brined, spiced, smoked and steamed until a tough cut becomes the city's monument.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastrami%20on%20Rye%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pastrami on Rye"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Pastrami on Rye","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Pastrami%20on%20Rye","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"buffalo-wings","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg chicken wings, split at the joint"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder (not soda)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml Frank's-style cayenne hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Blue cheese dressing and celery sticks"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the wings thoroughly and toss with the baking powder and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest them uncovered on a rack in the fridge at least 4 hours: dry skin is crisp skin.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry at 190C for 10-12 min, or roast at 220C for 45 min, turning once, until deep gold and crackling.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Melt the butter with the hot sauce and vinegar; do not boil it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the wings in the sauce the moment they come out and serve at once with blue cheese and celery.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born at the Anchor Bar in Buffalo in 1964, Teressa Bellissimo frying wings for her son's friends after midnight, or so the founding legend runs. The sauce is only butter and cayenne pepper sauce; everything else is somebody else's wing, and 'Buffalo-style' with additives is fan fiction. Fry naked, no breading, until the skin crisps. Blue cheese, never ranch (in Buffalo this is not a preference but a citizenship requirement), and the celery exists to prove you tried.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deep frying","Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Buffalo%20Wings%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Buffalo Wings"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Buffalo Wings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Buffalo%20Wings","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Celery","Chiles","Butter","Cheese (any)","Blue cheese","Vinegar","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"beef-on-weck","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg top round roast"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp kosher salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the kummelweck rolls: 6 kaiser rolls, 1 egg white, 2 tbsp pretzel salt, 2 tbsp caraway seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fresh horseradish, grated"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the roast hard, rest overnight, then roast at 230C for 15 min, drop to 135C, and cook to 52C internal for rare. Rest 30 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush the rolls with egg white and press on the salt and caraway; bake 5 min at 200C to set the crust.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the beef as thin as you can, against the grain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the stock, dip the top half of each roll in the jus briefly, and pile in the beef.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with grated horseradish and extra jus for dipping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Western New York's own sandwich, and Buffalo's quieter masterpiece beside the wing. The kummelweck salt is the seasoning for the meat, coarse salt and caraway crusted on the roll, so do not salt the beef again at the table, and keep the horseradish fierce; the sandwich is calibrated around sinus-clearing doses. The top bun gets dipped in jus, the beef is sliced rare and thin, and the whole thing exists in a fifty-mile radius that considers exporting it unnecessary.","equipment":["Thermometer","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beef%20on%20Weck%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beef on Weck"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Beef on Weck","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Beef%20on%20Weck","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Bread","Stock/Broth","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"manhattan-clam-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900g chopped clams with their juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml clam broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon, lift out the bits, and sweat the onion, carrot, and celery in the fat until soft, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, clam broth, potatoes, bay, and thyme.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 25 min until the potatoes are tender and the broth has body.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the clams and cook 2 min only.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season with pepper, scatter with parsley and bacon, and serve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Maine once tried to outlaw tomatoes in chowder by statute. This is the version that offended them: brighter and leaner, and it wants a full 25 minutes of simmering before the clams arrive.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Manhattan%20Clam%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Manhattan Clam Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Manhattan Clam Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Manhattan%20Clam%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Mussels/Clams","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Parsley","Thyme","Bay leaf","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"black-and-white-cookies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp lemon extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the icing: 400g icing sugar, 60ml hot water, 1 tbsp corn syrup, 60g cocoa powder"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream butter and sugar, beat in the egg, vanilla, and lemon extract.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Alternate the dry ingredients with the buttermilk to make a thick batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scoop large mounds well apart onto lined trays and bake at 190C for 15-17 min until set but pale. Cool completely, flat side up.","durationSec":1020,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1020,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk icing sugar, hot water, and corn syrup to a thick fondant. Ice half of each cookie white.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the cocoa into the remaining icing with a splash more hot water and ice the other half, keeping the line clean.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"They are cakes pretending to be cookies, and they are iced on the flat bottom, not the domed top: the drop-cake batter spreads into a dome, gets flipped, and the flat side becomes the canvas. Work fast: the fondant skins over in minutes and stops flowing smooth, so ice all the vanilla halves first, then all the chocolate. The cake should be lemon-scented and soft. New York's deli-counter icon: Seinfeld preached its racial-harmony sermon, but the bakeries were already believers.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Black%20and%20White%20Cookies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Black and White Cookies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Black and White Cookies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Black%20and%20White%20Cookies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","creaming-butter-and-sugar"]},{"slug":"egg-cream","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60ml cold chocolate syrup (Fox's U-bet, traditionally)"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml very cold whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cold seltzer from a pressurized siphon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour the syrup into a tall chilled glass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cold milk and stir briefly to combine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold a spoon at an angle in the glass and spray the seltzer hard against it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The foam should rise into a white head an inch tall over dark liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir once and drink immediately: an egg cream will not wait.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No egg, no cream, and nobody can explain the name: the leading theories involve Yiddish echt or a lost egg-and-cream original, and Brooklyn has argued about it for a century without settling anything. The seltzer must be hard and cold, from a siphon if you can manage it; a gentle pour gives you chocolate milk with bubbles in it, not the white-capped, sinus-prickling fountain drink. Fox's U-Bet syrup is the canonical chocolate. Drink it immediately: an egg cream does not keep for even five minutes.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egg%20Cream%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Egg Cream"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Egg Cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Egg%20Cream","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Cream","Chocolate/Cocoa"]},{"slug":"rochester-garbage-plate","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g home fries, fried crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g macaroni salad"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cheeseburger patties or 4 red hots"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the hot sauce: 450g ground beef, 1 onion grated, 2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp allspice, 1 tsp paprika, 250ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yellow mustard, chopped onion, and white bread to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the raw ground beef in the water with the onion and spices 45 min until dark and loose; never brown it first.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the home fries until crisp and make or buy a proper mayonnaise macaroni salad.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover half a plate with home fries and the other half with macaroni salad.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the burgers or hot dogs across both.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle the meat sauce over everything, then mustard and raw onion. Two slices of white bread on the side, no argument.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nick Tahou Hots invented it and students perfected the timing, which is late; the Garbage Plate is Rochester's 2 a.m. institution, trademarked name and all, and every rival diner sells a 'trash plate' in tribute-by-legal-necessity. The architecture is fixed: home fries and macaroni salad as the foundation, meat on top, mustard and onions, and the meat hot sauce, a cumin-spiced fine-ground chili, over everything. Assembled hot, eaten immediately with a fork, mixing as you go. Regret is scheduled for morning.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","earthy","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rochester%20Garbage%20Plate%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rochester Garbage Plate"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Rochester Garbage Plate","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Rochester%20Garbage%20Plate","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Chiles","Cheese (any)","Pasta","Bread","Mustard","Hot sauce","Cumin","Paprika","Allspice"]},{"slug":"syracuse-salt-potatoes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg small new potatoes, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g kosher salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"170g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives or parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scrub the potatoes but leave them whole and unpeeled.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve the salt in the water: it should be a genuine brine, cloudy and heavy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the potatoes in the brine 20-25 min until tender to a knife.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain and let them sit a minute; a fine white salt crust will bloom on the skins as they dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve in a bowl of melted butter with herbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"An Erie Canal salt-worker's lunch from the 1800s. The brine raises the boiling point so the flesh cooks creamier, and the crust that dries on the skin is the entire point. Do not rinse them.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Syracuse%20Salt%20Potatoes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Syracuse Salt Potatoes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Syracuse Salt Potatoes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Syracuse%20Salt%20Potatoes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Parsley","Chives","Butter"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"pork-roll-egg-and-cheese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices pork roll (Taylor ham), 5mm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 kaiser rolls, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices American cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and ketchup if you must"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut three or four notches into the edge of each pork roll slice so it does not dome as it cooks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle the slices over medium-high heat 2-3 min a side until the edges char and curl.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the eggs in the same pan, breaking the yolk or not according to conscience.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay cheese on the hot pork roll to melt, then build: roll, pork roll and cheese, egg, salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap in foil for a minute so everything settles, then eat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"North Jersey says Taylor ham, South Jersey says pork roll, and the state legislature has debated it. Notching the slices is not optional: an un-notched slice cups into a bowl and cooks unevenly.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pork%20Roll%2C%20Egg%20and%20Cheese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pork Roll, Egg and Cheese"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Pork Roll, Egg and Cheese","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Pork%20Roll%2C%20Egg%20and%20Cheese","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"disco-fries","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg frozen or hand-cut fries"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mozzarella, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry or bake the potatoes until deeply crisp: soft fries collapse under gravy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a brown roux with the butter and flour, cooking it to the color of peanut butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the stock gradually and simmer to a pourable gravy. Season with Worcestershire, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the hot fries in an ovenproof dish, scatter the mozzarella over, and grill until melted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle the gravy over and serve immediately, at any hour after midnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"New Jersey's answer to poutine, born in 24-hour diners: mozzarella instead of curds, brown gravy over everything, and the state's diner density (highest in the nation) as the delivery system. Crispness is everything: undercook the fries and you get gravy soup with potatoes hiding in it, which is why the double-fry matters more here than anywhere. The gravy goes on at the last second, at the table if possible. Named for the post-club hours it was engineered to serve.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","nutty-toasted","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Disco%20Fries%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Disco Fries"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Disco Fries","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Disco%20Fries","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Butter","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux"]},{"slug":"trenton-tomato-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g pizza dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed San Marzano tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g low-moisture mozzarella, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pecorino, salt, and chili flakes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stretch the dough into a thin round or a rectangular pan and let it relax 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Scatter the mozzarella directly onto the dough: cheese first is the Trenton order.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the crushed tomatoes with garlic, oregano, salt, and oil and spoon them over the cheese in irregular pools, not a smooth layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust with pecorino and chili.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 260C for 12-14 min until the crust is crisp and the tomato has cooked down in patches.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":840,"estimated":false}],"note":"Cheese under sauce is the distinction, and it works: the cheese browns against the dough while the tomato stays bright on top rather than steaming under a blanket: the inversion is the whole invention. Trenton's tomato pie predates most of America's pizza vocabulary; Papa's Tomato Pies has served it since 1912, one of the country's oldest continuously running pizzerias. Crushed tomatoes, not sauce: the fruit should taste of itself. Thin, crisp, and fiercely local.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","umami-deep","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trenton%20Tomato%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Trenton Tomato Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Trenton Tomato Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Trenton%20Tomato%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Mozzarella","Clove"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"newark-italian-hot-dog","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 hot dogs, deep-fried until blistered"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 round pizza breads, quartered into pockets"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 potatoes, in thick rounds, fried"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green peppers, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and oregano"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the potato rounds until gold and crisp inside; drain and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onions and peppers in the same oil until soft and browned at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deep-fry the hot dogs until the skins blister and split.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Open a bread quarter into a deep pocket and line the bottom with potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add two hot dogs, then heap the peppers and onions on top. Season and eat over the wrapper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jimmy Buff's built this in 1932 and the architecture matters: potatoes go in first as the floor, or the grease soaks straight through the bread: the half-round of pizza bread is a vessel under real load. Deep-fried dogs, then peppers, onions and potatoes from the same oil, stacked in that order. It is a sandwich with a load-bearing wall, engineered by people who understood that structural failure means wearing your lunch. Newark's own, still made the same way at the original.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Newark%20Italian%20Hot%20Dog%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Newark Italian Hot Dog"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Newark Italian Hot Dog","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Newark%20Italian%20Hot%20Dog","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Potato","Bell pepper","Oregano","Bread"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"jersey-sloppy-joe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 slices rye bread, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g sliced turkey"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g sliced pastrami or corned beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g Swiss cheese, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g coleslaw, well drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the Russian dressing: 200g mayonnaise, 4 tbsp ketchup, 2 tbsp minced pickle, 1 tsp horseradish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the dressing ingredients and chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Trim the crusts from the rye and lay out three slices per sandwich.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build the first layer: turkey, Swiss, slaw, and dressing. Add the middle slice of bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build the second layer: pastrami, Swiss, slaw, and dressing. Cap with the third slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press gently, chill 20 min, then cut into four neat rectangles per sandwich.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing like the ground-beef sandwich of the same name: this is a triple-decker deli sandwich from a Maplewood tavern, built on thin rye with layered meats, Swiss, coleslaw and Russian dressing; the Town Hall Deli still claims the 1930s original. Drain the slaw hard or the whole tower goes to pieces: the dressing brings enough moisture, and wet slaw dissolves rye into pulp. Cut in quarters, held together by pressure and faith. North Jersey knows; everyone else gets corrected.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","smoky","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, wood-smoke running underneath, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jersey%20Sloppy%20Joe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jersey Sloppy Joe"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Jersey Sloppy Joe","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Jersey%20Sloppy%20Joe","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Turkey","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mayonnaise","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"jersey-tomato-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg ripe Jersey tomatoes, in thick slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fresh basil leaves, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp good olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt and coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crusty bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slice the tomatoes thick and lay them on a platter in a single overlapping layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt them and let them sit 10 min: the juices that come out are the dressing.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Scatter the onion and basil over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress with olive oil and just enough vinegar to sharpen; the tomatoes bring their own acid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind pepper over and serve with bread to mop the plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"August only, and never refrigerated: cold murders the aroma compounds that make a Jersey tomato worth the wait, and the fridge crime is unrecoverable. Salt first, dress second: the salt pulls the juices that become the dressing's base. The Jersey tomato's reputation is real: sandy soil, hot summers, and varieties bred for flavor over shipping. This salad is a monument to doing almost nothing at exactly the right moment of the year.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","briny","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jersey%20Tomato%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jersey Tomato Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Jersey Tomato Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Jersey%20Tomato%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Basil","Bread","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"salt-water-taffy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla or other flavoring"},{"kind":"item","text":"Food coloring (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a marble slab or sheet pan generously.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine sugar, syrup, water, cornstarch, butter, and salt and cook to 121C on a candy thermometer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat stir in flavoring and color, then pour onto the slab and let it cool until you can handle it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the taffy with buttered hands, stretching and folding, for 15-20 min until it turns pale, opaque, and satiny.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Roll into ropes, cut with scissors, and wrap each piece in wax paper immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Atlantic City boardwalk, 1880s. And there is no seawater in it. The pulling is the recipe: it works air into the candy, and stopping early leaves it hard and dark instead of chewy and pale.","equipment":["Thermometer","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salt%20Water%20Taffy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salt Water Taffy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Salt Water Taffy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Salt%20Water%20Taffy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"blueberry-crumb-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the crumb: 250g flour, 165g brown sugar, 1 tbsp cinnamon, 1/2 tsp salt, 170g melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the cake: 250g flour, 1 tsp baking powder, 1/2 tsp soda, 1/2 tsp salt, 115g butter, 200g sugar, 2 eggs, 1 tsp vanilla, 180ml sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g blueberries"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the crumb ingredients together until they hold in large clumps; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream butter and sugar, beat in the eggs and vanilla, then alternate the dry ingredients with the sour cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the blueberries and spread in a lined 23cm square pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Break the crumb mixture into big pieces over the top: pea-sized crumbs disappear, so keep them chunky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 50-55 min until a skewer comes clean. Cool, then dust heavily with icing sugar.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"New Jersey grows the cultivated blueberry: it was domesticated in Whitesbog in 1916, when Elizabeth White and Frederick Coville turned a wild pine-barrens crop into an industry, so the state's claim on this cake is botanical fact. Make the crumb clumps far larger than looks sensible; they settle and shrink as they bake, and timid crumbs vanish into the batter. Equal parts cake and crumb is the Jersey bakery ratio: the crumb is not a topping, it is the co-star.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Blueberry%20Crumb%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Blueberry Crumb Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Blueberry Crumb Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Blueberry%20Crumb%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Berries","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"rippers","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 natural-casing beef hot dogs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for deep frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 hot dog buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the relish: 200g yellow mustard, 1 onion finely chopped, 100g pickle relish, 1 tsp celery salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C in a deep pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the hot dogs 4-6 min until the casings split open and curl: that split is what names them.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain briefly on paper and toast the buns in the fryer basket for a moment if you like.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the mustard, onion, relish, and celery salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Load the dogs and spoon the relish over generously.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rutt's Hut in Clifton grades them by time in the fryer: in-and-outer, ripper, weller, cremator, a taxonomy of deep-fried hot dogs refined since 1928. A true ripper has burst its casing and no further: the tear lets the fat in and crisps the edges while the center stays juicy; the cremator is for people who want the dog to have a past. The house mustard-relish, yellow with chopped cabbage, is the mandatory finish. Eaten at the counter, standing, as designed.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rippers%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rippers"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Rippers","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Rippers","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Celery","Bread","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"jersey-shore-zeppole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar for dusting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the water, butter, sugar, and salt to a boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the flour all at once and beat over the heat until the dough pulls from the sides and films the pan, 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off the heat beat in the eggs one at a time until the dough is glossy and drops thickly from the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop rough spoonfuls into 175C oil and fry 4-5 min, turning, until deep gold and hollow-sounding.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and bury in icing sugar while hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boardwalk zeppole are choux, not yeasted: they must puff hollow, which is the entire textural point; fry a test one first, and if it stays dense the dough needs another egg beaten in. The paste should be glossy and drop reluctantly from the spoon. Fried to deep gold, buried in powdered sugar, served in a paper bag that becomes part of the experience: shake the bag, eat over it, wear some anyway. Saint's-festival food that the Shore adopted as a summer religion.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Choux pastry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=choux%20pastry%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jersey%20Shore%20Zeppole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jersey Shore Zeppole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Jersey Shore Zeppole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Jersey%20Shore%20Zeppole","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"philly-cheesesteak","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g ribeye, frozen 30 min then shaved paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 Amoroso-style hoagie rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices provolone, or 250ml cheese sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sweet peppers or hot cherry peppers (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Freeze the ribeye briefly so you can shave it as thin as paper: this is the whole technique.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onions on a flat griddle over medium heat until soft and browned; push to one side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the meat across the hot surface, season, and chop it with two spatulas as it cooks; 90 seconds is enough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the meat and onions, lay the cheese over the pile, and let it melt into the meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scoop the whole mass into the roll with the spatula in one movement.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Order it whiz wit or provolone wit: the argument is eternal. The roll must be soft with a light crust; a crusty artisan roll shreds the roof of your mouth and Philadelphia will know.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Philly%20Cheesesteak%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Philly Cheesesteak"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Philly Cheesesteak","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Philly%20Cheesesteak","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ribeye-prime-rib","term":"Ribeye / Prime Rib"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Stone fruit","Cheese (any)","Bread"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"philadelphia-soft-pretzel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the bath: 2L water, 60g baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse pretzel salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yellow mustard to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix and knead the dough 8 min until smooth; rise 1 hour.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into 8, roll each into a 60cm rope, and twist into the classic knot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the water and baking soda to a rolling boil: stand back, it foams.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil each pretzel 30 seconds, lift out, drain, brush with egg, and salt heavily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 14-16 min until deep mahogany.","durationSec":960,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":960,"estimated":false}],"note":"The soda bath is what makes a pretzel a pretzel: it raises the surface pH so the crust browns fast and tastes of that particular pretzel tang. Philadelphia eats them soft, in rows, with mustard.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The pretzel bath","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Philadelphia%20Soft%20Pretzel%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Philadelphia Soft Pretzel"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Philadelphia Soft Pretzel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Philadelphia%20Soft%20Pretzel","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Bread","Flour","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"scrapple","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg pork shoulder and 500g pork liver"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g buckwheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sage"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of cayenne and nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the pork and liver with the onion and bay 2 hours until falling apart. Strain, keeping 1.5L of the broth, and chop or grind the meat fine.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the broth back to a simmer and rain in the cornmeal and buckwheat, whisking hard to avoid lumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 30 min, stirring often, until very thick. Stir in the meat and all the seasonings: taste and season boldly.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into loaf pans, cool, and chill overnight until solid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice 1cm thick and fry in butter over medium heat 5-6 min a side without moving it, until a deep crust forms.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pennsylvania Dutch thrift at its most honest. The one rule at the stove: leave it alone in the pan. Scrapple needs an unbroken crust to hold together, and flipping early guarantees mush.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Scrapple%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Scrapple"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Scrapple","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Scrapple","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Liver & Offal","Onion","Chiles","Thyme","Sage","Bay leaf","Butter","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"shoofly-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the crumb: 250g flour, 165g brown sugar, 1/2 tsp salt, 115g cold butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the liquid: 250ml molasses, 250ml boiling water, 1 tsp baking soda, 1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the crumb ingredients together to fine crumbs and set aside a third for the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the baking soda into the boiling water, then whisk in the molasses and the egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir two-thirds of the crumbs into the molasses liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the shell and scatter the reserved crumbs over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 15 min, then 175C for 30-35 min until set with a slight wobble. Cool completely.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false}],"note":"Wet-bottom is the Lancaster County standard: a gooey molasses layer under the cake. The name is said to come from shooing flies off the cooling pie, which tells you how sweet it is.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shoofly%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shoofly Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Shoofly Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Shoofly%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"primanti-style-sandwich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 thick slices Italian bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g pastrami or capicola, griddled"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices provolone"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g hand-cut fries, fried crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the slaw: 400g shredded cabbage, 4 tbsp oil, 2 tbsp vinegar, 1 tsp sugar, salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, sliced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dress the cabbage with oil, vinegar, sugar, salt, and pepper: no mayonnaise; this slaw must stay crisp and tangy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the potatoes until deeply crisp and salt them at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle the meat and melt the provolone over it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build: bread, meat and cheese, then the fries, then the slaw, then tomato, then the top slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press once, cut in half, and eat immediately before the fries soften.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pittsburgh built this for mill workers who needed one hand free: the whole meal between two slices of Italian bread, eaten standing at the Strip District counter at 3 a.m. The fries go inside, not beside, and the vinegar slaw is doing real work: it cuts the starch and the fat at once, the acid keeping a sandwich this heavy from eating like a brick. Primanti Bros. has stacked it the same way since 1933. Grilled meat, cheese, slaw, fries, tomato: order is architecture.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","smoky","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Primanti-Style%20Sandwich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Primanti-Style Sandwich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Primanti-Style Sandwich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Primanti-Style%20Sandwich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"provolone-dolce-and-piccante","term":"Provolone (Dolce & Piccante)"}],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Cabbage","Cheese (any)","Bread","Vinegar","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"pennsylvania-dutch-chicken-corn-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole chicken, about 1.6kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"3L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 ears corn, kernels cut off"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g fine egg noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the rivels: 125g flour, 1 egg, pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 hard-boiled eggs, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chicken with the water, onion, celery, and bay 90 min. Lift out the bird, pull the meat, and strain the broth.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the broth to the pot, add the corn, and simmer 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rub the flour, egg, and salt between your fingers into small crumbly rivels and drop them into the simmering broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the noodles and cook 6 min more.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the chicken meat, season well, and finish with parsley and chopped hard-boiled egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rivels are the point: little hand-rubbed dumplings that thicken the broth as they cook, flour and egg pinched between fingers straight into the pot. Keep them small and irregular; rolled or uniform, they turn into dough balls and the soup goes leaden. Saffron is the old Pennsylvania Dutch touch: the Lancaster markets sold it when it was grown in county gardens. This is fire-hall and church-supper soup, made in kettles and judged by the rivels every single time.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pennsylvania%20Dutch%20Chicken%20Corn%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pennsylvania Dutch Chicken Corn Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Pennsylvania Dutch Chicken Corn Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Pennsylvania%20Dutch%20Chicken%20Corn%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Celery","Corn","Parsley","Bay leaf","Noodles","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"philadelphia-pepper-pot-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g honeycomb tripe, cleaned"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 veal or beef bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"3L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dumplings: 125g flour, 1 tsp baking powder, 1 egg, 60ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and cayenne"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the tripe with the bone and water 2.5 hours until tender, skimming. Lift the tripe out, cut into strips, and strain the broth.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, celery, and carrot, then add the broth, tripe, potatoes, cracked pepper, allspice, bay, and thyme.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 45 min until everything is tender and the broth is fragrant with pepper.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the dumpling ingredients to a soft batter and drop small spoonfuls into the simmering pot; cover and cook 12 min.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season hard with salt and cayenne: this soup is named for its pepper and should sting a little.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Legend gives it to Washington's cook at Valley Forge: tripe and pepper rallying a starving army, a story better documented as legend than as fact and beloved anyway; the soup itself was sold on Philadelphia streets by Black women vendors whose cry ('Pepper pot, smoking hot!') is the better-attested history. The pepper is the whole identity: crack it coarse and add it early so the heat infuses the broth rather than sitting on top. Tripe demands its long simmer; give it every minute.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","warm-spiced","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Philadelphia%20Pepper%20Pot%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Philadelphia Pepper Pot Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Philadelphia Pepper Pot Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Philadelphia%20Pepper%20Pot%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"allspice","term":"Allspice"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Liver & Offal","Onion","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Chiles","Thyme","Bay leaf","Milk","Flour","Honey","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"sticky-buns","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g flour, 7g instant yeast, 50g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 2 eggs, 85g butter softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the goo: 170g butter, 220g brown sugar, 120ml honey or corn syrup, 1/2 tsp salt, 200g pecan halves"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 85g butter softened, 165g brown sugar, 2 tbsp cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the dough ingredients 10 min to a soft, smooth dough; rise 90 min until doubled.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Melt the goo ingredients together and pour into a 23x33cm pan; scatter the pecans over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough to a 40x50cm rectangle, spread with the filling butter and cinnamon sugar, and roll up tightly from the long side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into 12 and set cut-side up on the goo. Rise 45 min until puffed.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C for 30-35 min until deep gold. Rest exactly 5 min, then invert onto a tray: any longer and the caramel sets in the pan.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Philadelphia's German bakers made these before Cinnabon existed: the schnecken tradition arrived with the Pennsylvania Dutch, and the caramel-glued spiral is theirs by right of precedence. The five-minute rule after baking is the whole trick: too soon and the goo runs off, too late and it welds to the pan; invert onto the tray in that window and everything releases glossy. Corn syrup or honey in the goo keeps the caramel from crystallizing. Nuts down in the pan means nuts up on the plate.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sticky%20Buns%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sticky Buns"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sticky Buns","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sticky%20Buns","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Honey","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"chow-chow","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g green beans, cut in pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g cauliflower florets"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g lima beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp mustard seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp celery seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch each vegetable separately until just tender: they cook at different rates and mixing them raw gives you mush and gravel in one jar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the vinegar, water, sugar, mustard seed, celery seed, turmeric, and salt to a boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add all the vegetables and simmer 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pack into sterilized jars with the hot brine to cover.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal and rest at least a week before eating; it keeps for months.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Pennsylvania Dutch answer to a full garden and a coming winter. Blanching separately is the tedious step everyone wants to skip and the one that separates a good chow chow from a jar of pickle mud.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chow%20Chow%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chow Chow"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Chow Chow","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Chow%20Chow","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Carrot","Celery","Bell pepper","Cauliflower","Corn","Green beans","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Sugar","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"philadelphia-water-ice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g fruit: lemon juice, cherries, or mango, pureed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the water and sugar together until the sugar dissolves, then cool completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the syrup with the fruit puree or juice and the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Freeze in a shallow pan, scraping and stirring every 30 min for 3-4 hours.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Once fully frozen, work it hard with a fork or pulse in a processor to break every crystal down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with a flat wooden spoon: it should be smooth and scoopable, never crunchy like a granita.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Not Italian ice and not sorbet: water ice has no dairy and is worked far smoother than granita; the repeated scraping is the labor that buys the texture, breaking every crystal before it can organize. Real fruit, real sugar, and a paddle: the South Philly stands have run on that formula for a century. Lemon and cherry are the orthodox flavors; a proper water ice is soft enough to drink at the bottom of the cup, and 'wooder ice' is the only correct pronunciation within city limits.","equipment":["Food processor"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Philadelphia%20Water%20Ice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Philadelphia Water Ice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Philadelphia Water Ice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Philadelphia%20Water%20Ice","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mango-the-monsoon-herald","term":"Mango: The Monsoon Herald"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Mango","Stone fruit","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"chicken-and-slippery-dumplings","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole chicken, about 1.6kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dumplings: 375g flour, 1 tsp salt, 55g lard or shortening, 180ml cold chicken broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chicken with the vegetables and bay 75 min until tender. Lift out, pull the meat, and strain the broth back into the pot.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the lard into the flour and salt, then bind with just enough cold broth to make a stiff dough. Rest 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough very thin on a floured board and cut into 5cm squares.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the broth to a strong simmer and drop the squares in one at a time so they do not stick. Cook 15 min: they stay flat and slippery, never puffed.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the chicken, season generously, and finish with parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Slippery dumplings are rolled and cut, not dropped: the flat noodle-dumpling of the Delmarva peninsula, closer to a thick pasta than to the puffy Southern kind, and the distinction is regional identity. Add them one at a time or they weld into a single raft; a gentle simmer, never a boil, keeps them intact. The broth thickens itself from the flour they shed, which is the design. Sunday-dinner food from Delaware's chicken country, which raises more broilers than nearly anywhere.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20and%20Slippery%20Dumplings%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken and Slippery Dumplings"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Chicken and Slippery Dumplings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Chicken%20and%20Slippery%20Dumplings","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Parsley","Bay leaf","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"boardwalk-vinegar-fries","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg russet potatoes, cut in thick sticks, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Apple cider vinegar in a shaker"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paper cups, ideally"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the cut potatoes in cold water 30 min, then dry them completely.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry at 150C for 6-7 min until soft but pale. Lift out and rest 10 min.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Raise the oil to 190C and fry again 3-4 minutes until gold and crisp.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain briefly, salt hard, and pile into paper cups.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Douse with cider vinegar at the table, enough that it pools at the bottom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Thrasher's on the Rehoboth boardwalk has sold these since 1929 and famously refuses to stock ketchup; the vinegar bottle chained to the counter is the entire condiment program, and asking for ketchup gets you a polite Delaware refusal. The double fry gives the structure that survives the vinegar; a single-fried chip drinks it and collapses. Peanut oil is the house medium. Served in a paper bucket meant to be carried into the wind and finished before the gulls organize.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boardwalk%20Vinegar%20Fries%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boardwalk Vinegar Fries"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Boardwalk Vinegar Fries","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Boardwalk%20Vinegar%20Fries","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Apple","Vinegar","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"delaware-peach-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry for a double-crust 23cm pie"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg ripe peaches, peeled and sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp almond extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drop the peaches in boiling water 30 seconds and into ice water; the skins slip off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss with sugar, cornstarch, lemon, cinnamon, almond extract, and salt. Rest 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the lined pie plate and dot with butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay a lattice, brush with egg, and dust with sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 20 min, then 190C for 40 min until the juices bubble thickly. Cool 4 hours.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":false}],"note":"The peach is Delaware's state flower for good reason: the orchards once fed the whole East Coast. A drop of almond extract makes a peach taste more like itself; more than a quarter teaspoon makes it taste like marzipan.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Delaware%20Peach%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Delaware Peach Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Delaware Peach Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Delaware%20Peach%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Stone fruit","Butter","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"the-bobbie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 long sub rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g roast turkey, sliced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g stuffing, warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cranberry sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the rolls and spread both faces with mayonnaise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the warm stuffing along the bottom half, pressing it into an even bed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the turkey on top and season with salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread cranberry sauce over the turkey rather than the bread, so it does not soak through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Close, press gently, cut on the diagonal, and serve while the stuffing is still warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Capriotti's in Wilmington built it in 1976 and it is essentially Thanksgiving in a roll. Warm stuffing against cold turkey is the correct temperature contrast. Do not heat the whole sandwich.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Bobbie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of The Bobbie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on The Bobbie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20The%20Bobbie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Turkey","Dried fruit","Bread","Mayonnaise"]},{"slug":"snapper-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg turtle meat or veal shank, in cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme, 2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 hard-boiled eggs, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml dry sherry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the meat hard in butter, then set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the flour in the remaining butter to a dark brown roux, 10 min, stirring constantly.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the vegetables and sweat until soft, then the stock, tomatoes, Worcestershire, and spices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the meat, cover, and simmer 2 hours until it falls apart. Season hard.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chopped egg and serve with a shot of sherry poured into each bowl at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Delaware Valley institution passed down from colonial taverns, when snapping turtle was cheap protein and the soup was every inn's pride. The dark roux and the sherry are non-negotiable, and the sherry goes in at the table, never in the pot, each spoonful baptized to the eater's taste from the cruet alongside. The hard-boiled egg garnish is the old-school tell. Rich, dark, faintly sweet: closer to a ragout than a broth, and one of the last living links to tavern cooking.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Snapper%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Snapper Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Snapper Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Snapper%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","White fish","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Lemon","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"sussex-county-chicken-and-corn","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs, bone-in"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 ears corn, kernels cut off, cobs reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season and brown the chicken thighs skin-side down until deeply gold; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the stripped cobs in the stock 15 min, then discard them.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sweat the onion and celery in the butter, sprinkle in the flour, and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the corn stock, return the chicken, cover, and simmer 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the corn kernels and cream and cook 5 min more. Season and finish with chives.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sussex County raises more broiler chickens than any county in America, and sweet corn grows in the next field. Simmering the stripped cobs in the stock is the free step that makes it taste local.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sussex%20County%20Chicken%20and%20Corn%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sussex County Chicken and Corn"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sussex County Chicken and Corn","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sussex%20County%20Chicken%20and%20Corn","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Celery","Corn","Thyme","Chives","Butter","Cream","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"delmarva-oyster-fritters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml shucked oysters, drained, liquor reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml oyster liquor and milk combined"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon and hot sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chop the oysters coarsely if they are large; leave small ones whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt, and cayenne.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the egg with the liquor and milk and stir into the dry ingredients to a thick batter. Fold in the oysters and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop heaped spoonfuls into 180C oil and fry 3-4 min, turning, until deep gold.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve immediately with lemon and hot sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The oyster liquor in the batter is what makes these taste of the bay rather than of fried dough: every drop from the shucking goes in, and the batter should be barely thick enough to hold. Do not overmix once the oysters go in; they break and weep, and a weeping fritter fries flat. The Delmarva fritter is a sandwich on the shore: served on white bread or saltines with mustard, at church suppers and fire-hall fundraisers where the recipe is guarded by committee.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Shucking shellfish","Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","gently hot","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":37,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Shucking shellfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20shuck%20oysters%20clams%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Delmarva%20Oyster%20Fritters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Delmarva Oyster Fritters"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Delmarva Oyster Fritters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Delmarva%20Oyster%20Fritters","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Chiles","Lemon","Parsley","Milk","Flour","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"delaware-succotash","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g fresh lima beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the limas in salted water 12-15 min until tender; drain.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sweat the onion and pepper in the butter until soft, 8 min.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the corn and cook 3 min over higher heat to catch a little color.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the limas, cream, and thyme and simmer 3 min until the cream thickens slightly.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season well and finish with parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Delaware grows limas by the ton and eats them all summer: the lima is the state vegetable of neighboring Delmarva pride, and fresh ones bear no resemblance to the frozen gray argument against them. Cook the corn briefly and hard: it should still snap; simmered corn goes tired and starchy, and the dish's whole point is the fresh sweetness of both crops at their August crossing. Butter and cream at the end, gently. Succotash is Narragansett by name and mid-Atlantic by devotion.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Delaware%20Succotash%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Delaware Succotash"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Delaware Succotash","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Delaware%20Succotash","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Bell pepper","Corn","Parsley","Thyme","Butter","Cream"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"eastern-shore-corn-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 ears corn, kernels and scraped milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grated nutmeg"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the kernels from the cobs, then scrape the cobs hard with the back of the knife to collect the starchy milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs with the milk, cream, melted butter, flour, sugar, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the corn and its scrapings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered dish and grate nutmeg over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 45-55 min until set at the edges with a slight wobble at the center.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":false}],"note":"Scraping the cobs is the difference between corn custard and corn soup: that milky starch is what sets the pudding, the cob's hidden contribution that canned corn can never make. Pull it while it still moves; it firms as it rests, and an extra five minutes in the oven trades silk for sponge. Sweet corn, eggs, cream, restraint. This is the Eastern Shore's covered-dish diplomat, present at every supper where somebody's grandmother is being judged kindly.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20Shore%20Corn%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Eastern Shore Corn Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Eastern Shore Corn Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Eastern%20Shore%20Corn%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Corn","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"beer-battered-rockfish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g rockfish or striped bass fillet, in strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour, plus extra for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"330ml cold beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges and tartar sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the fish dry and dust lightly with plain flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt, and Old Bay, then add the cold beer and stir just to combine: lumps are fine, overmixing is not.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip the fish in the batter, let the excess run off, and lower into the oil away from you. Fry 3-4 min until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt at once, serve with lemon and tartar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The beer must be cold and the batter must be mixed at the last moment: the carbonation is the leavening, and it starts dying the second it hits the flour; a rested beer batter is just wet flour with regrets. Rockfish is what Maryland and Delaware call striped bass, the mid-Atlantic's flagship fish, firm enough to hold in the fryer. Dust the fillets in dry flour first so the batter grips. Fry hot, salt immediately, and squeeze the lemon while the crust still crackles.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beer-Battered%20Rockfish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beer-Battered Rockfish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Beer-Battered Rockfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Beer-Battered%20Rockfish","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Lemon","Flour","Beer/Cider","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"steamed-blue-crabs-with-old-bay","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"24 live blue crabs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml cider vinegar or beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g Old Bay seasoning"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Melted butter and lemon to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"Newspaper for the table"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the water and vinegar to a boil in the base of a large steamer with a rack set above the liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the crabs in the pot, dusting each layer heavily with Old Bay and salt, heavier than feels reasonable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover tightly and steam 20-25 min until the shells turn bright orange-red all over.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tip onto a table spread with newspaper and let them cool just enough to handle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with melted butter, lemon, and mallets; the picking is the meal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Steamed, never boiled: boiling washes the seasoning off and waterlogs the meat, and in Maryland the distinction is close to constitutional. Season between every layer, vinegar and beer in the pot below, and reckon on six crabs per person and two hours at the table: the labor is the meal, mallets and brown paper and conversation included. Old Bay was invented in Baltimore in 1939 by a German refugee spice merchant, and the state has never seriously considered an alternative.","equipment":["Steamer"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Steamed%20Blue%20Crabs%20with%20Old%20Bay%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Steamed Blue Crabs with Old Bay"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Steamed Blue Crabs with Old Bay","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Steamed%20Blue%20Crabs%20with%20Old%20Bay","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Lemon","Butter","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"maryland-crab-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg crab shells and bodies for stock, or 1L seafood stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g lump crab meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g bacon or beef shank (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g green beans, cut"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g lima beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the shells in 2L water 45 min to make stock, then strain.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot, and celery until soft, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the stock, tomatoes, potatoes, Old Bay, and bay leaves. Simmer 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the green beans, corn, and limas and cook 12 min more.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold in the crab meat at the very end and heat 2 min only. Season and serve with more Old Bay at the table.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A vegetable soup that happens to have crab in it, and the crab goes in last so it stays in lumps; stirred in early, jumbo lump becomes expensive confetti. Making the stock from the shells costs 45 minutes and doubles the flavor; the bodies and claws you picked are the soup's foundation, not trash. Old Bay in the broth, tomatoes and summer vegetables carrying it. This is the crab feast's second act: the soup that justifies saving every shell from the first.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maryland%20Crab%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maryland Crab Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Maryland Crab Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Maryland%20Crab%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"crab-vocabulary","term":"Crab Vocabulary"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Bacon","Crab","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Corn","Green beans","Bay leaf","Stock/Broth","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"cream-of-crab-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450g lump crab meat, picked over"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 shallot, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml heavy cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml seafood stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dry sherry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper, and chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the shallot in the butter until soft without color.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the flour and cook 2 min to a blond roux.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the stock, then the milk, and simmer gently 10 min until lightly thickened and smooth.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cream, Old Bay, and sherry and heat through without boiling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the crab, warm 2 min, season with salt and white pepper, and finish with chives.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The two Maryland soups are cousins (one tomato and vegetables, one cream), and traditionalists order a cup of each, poured together, called half and half; most Maryland menus will do it unasked if you know to request it. The cream version is a gentle béchamel that exists to carry lump crab and a dusting of Old Bay, and it must never boil once the crab is in. Sherry at the table, Delaware-snapper-style. Rich enough that the cup, not the bowl, is the wise order.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Béchamel: the mother sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bechamel%20mother%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cream%20of%20Crab%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cream of Crab Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cream of Crab Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cream%20of%20Crab%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Shallot","Chives","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"crab-imperial","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g jumbo lump crab meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, minced fine (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paprika and butter for the top"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the mayonnaise, egg, mustard, Worcestershire, Old Bay, and lemon into a smooth imperial sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the crab meat with a rubber spatula in as few strokes as possible: the lumps are the luxury.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide among 4 ramekins or clean crab shells.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dot with butter and dust with paprika.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 20-25 min until puffed, set, and golden on top.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"Fold, never stir. The whole price of jumbo lump is in the size of the pieces, and an enthusiastic spoon turns a special-occasion dish into a crab salad casserole: the imperial's only job is to bind and gild the lumps without breaking them. The mayonnaise-egg binder should be barely enough; imperial is crab wearing a veil, not a coat. Broil to a blistered gold at the end. This is the Chesapeake's dress-up dish, the one the wedding menus and the old hotels built their names on.","equipment":["Ramekins"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","bright","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Crab%20Imperial%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Crab Imperial"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Crab Imperial","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Crab%20Imperial","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Eggs","Bell pepper","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Mustard","Mayonnaise","Paprika","Old Bay/Creole"]},{"slug":"baltimore-pit-beef","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg top round roast"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Kaiser rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the tiger sauce: 200g mayonnaise, 4 tbsp prepared horseradish, 1 tsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sliced raw onion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the roast hard with the salt, pepper, garlic powder, paprika, and cayenne and rest it an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill directly over hot charcoal, turning often, so the outside chars while the inside stays rare; 40-50 min to an internal 50C.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 15 min, then slice against the grain as thin as you can manage.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the horseradish into the mayonnaise for tiger sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the beef on a kaiser roll with raw onion and tiger sauce. The roll is a handle, not a sandwich.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Baltimore pit beef is charred outside and rare inside: it is grilled, not smoked, and it lives or dies on the slicing; thick slices eat like a steak nobody asked for, while paper-thin slices pile into the sandwich the pit stands invented. Top round, blasted over charcoal, sliced on a machine if you have one. Tiger sauce, horseradish cut with mayo, and raw onion on a kaiser roll is the full Pulaski Highway specification. Baltimore's answer to barbecue argues char beats smoke.","equipment":["Thermometer","Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","charred","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, char used as punctuation, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltimore%20Pit%20Beef%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baltimore Pit Beef"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Baltimore Pit Beef","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Baltimore%20Pit%20Beef","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Bread","Mayonnaise","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"smith-island-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE LAYERS"},{"kind":"item","text":"375g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"375ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE ICING"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"225g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g dark chocolate"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar, add the eggs one at a time, then alternate the dry ingredients with the evaporated milk and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide the batter into 8-10 thin layers, baking each in a greased 23cm pan at 175C for 8-10 min. They should be pale and thin, barely half a centimeter.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For the icing, boil the sugar, evaporated milk, and butter 5 min, then off the heat stir in the chocolate and vanilla until glossy.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stack the layers while the icing is still warm and pourable, spreading a thin coat between each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Coat the top and sides and let the icing set firm. Chill before slicing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Maryland's official dessert, from an island in the Chesapeake: eight to ten layers, and the icing is a cooked fudge that sets hard rather than a buttercream. Thin layers are the whole discipline.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Creaming butter & sugar","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Smith%20Island%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Smith Island Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Smith Island Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Smith%20Island%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","creaming-butter-and-sugar"]},{"slug":"berger-cookies","ingredients":[{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE COOKIES"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml milk"},{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE FUDGE"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g dark chocolate"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp corn syrup"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar, beat in the egg and vanilla, then alternate the dry ingredients with the milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop spoonfuls onto lined trays and bake at 190C for 10-12 min until set but pale and cakey. Cool completely.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the cream, pour over the chocolate, then beat in the butter, icing sugar, and corn syrup until thick and glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the fudge cool until it holds a mound on a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread a thick dome of fudge over each cookie: the icing should outweigh the cookie.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Baltimore institution since 1835. The proportions are deliberately absurd: the cookie is a soft plain vehicle and the fudge is the reason. Wait for the fudge to thicken or it slides off.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Berger%20Cookies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Berger Cookies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Berger Cookies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Berger%20Cookies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","creaming-butter-and-sugar"]},{"slug":"southern-maryland-stuffed-ham","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 corned ham, about 5kg, bone in"},{"kind":"item","text":"2kg kale and cabbage, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bunch watercress or mustard greens"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp crushed red pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp celery seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cheesecloth and kitchen string"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch the greens and onions briefly, drain very well, and mix with all the seasonings: the stuffing should be aggressively spicy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut deep pockets into the ham every few centimeters and pack them tight with the greens mixture. Rub any remainder over the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap the ham firmly in cheesecloth and tie it well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer in a large pot of water at a bare tremble for 4 hours, about 20 min per 500g.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":15600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely in the poaching liquid, then chill overnight. Slice cold, thin, showing the green swirl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"A St. Mary's County Easter tradition older than the state fair. It is always served cold and sliced thin so the pattern shows, and the stuffing must taste too spicy on its own: the ham tames it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Southern%20Maryland%20Stuffed%20Ham%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Southern Maryland Stuffed Ham"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Southern Maryland Stuffed Ham","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Southern%20Maryland%20Stuffed%20Ham","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Celery","Bell pepper","Kale/Collards","Cabbage","Lettuce/Greens","Arugula/Watercress","Cheese (any)","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"coddies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450g salt cod, soaked 24 hours with several water changes"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g potatoes, boiled and mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cracker meal"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Saltine crackers and yellow mustard to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the soaked cod 15 min, drain, cool, and flake, checking for bones.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mash into the potatoes with the egg, parsley, pepper, and Old Bay. The mixture should be stiff.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Form into small flat patties and chill 30 min so they hold.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust with cracker meal and shallow-fry 3 min a side until deep gold.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve the traditional way: pressed between two saltines with a smear of yellow mustard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Baltimore corner-store food from the Depression, sold two for a nickel beside the register: codfish cakes stretched with potato and cracker meal until the fish was nearly a rumor, which was the economics and became the taste of home. The saltine-and-mustard service is not a garnish; it is the dish, and Baltimore will tell you so: one coddie between two saltines, yellow mustard, standing up. The pharmacy lunch counters that sold them are gone; the coddie survives on stubbornness.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Mashing & puréeing","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Coddies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Coddies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Coddies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Coddies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Salt cod","Eggs","Potato","Parsley","Mustard","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"baltimore-snowball","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Finely shaved ice, packed into cups"},{"kind":"item","text":"For egg custard syrup: 250ml water, 300g sugar, 2 egg yolks, 1 tbsp vanilla, 1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marshmallow creme, warmed to pouring consistency"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the water and sugar until dissolved, then cool slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks in a bowl and temper the warm syrup in slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return to low heat and cook 3-4 min, stirring, without boiling. Add vanilla and salt and chill completely.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shave the ice as fine as snow; a snowball is not crushed ice, and the texture is the whole argument.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack a cup, douse with the egg custard syrup, and crown with warm marshmallow.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Egg custard is the flagship Baltimore flavor and marshmallow on top is the local signature: a snowball stand without both is under suspicion. Shaved fine enough, the ice drinks the syrup instead of letting it run to the bottom; the shave, not the syrup, is the craft. Baltimore's snowball culture goes back to the 1800s ice wagons, and the summer stands are neighborhood institutions passed between generations. It is not a snow cone, and the difference is texture and civic pride.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","floral","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltimore%20Snowball%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baltimore Snowball"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Baltimore Snowball","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Baltimore%20Snowball","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"smithfield-ham-with-redeye-gravy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices country ham, 5mm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp lard or butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml strong black coffee"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp brown sugar (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Biscuits or grits to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Trim a little fat from the ham and render it in a cast-iron skillet over medium heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the ham slices 2-3 min a side until the edges curl and brown; do not overcook or salt-cured ham turns to leather. Keep warm.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the coffee into the hot skillet and scrape up every bit of browned fond.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 3-4 min until it reduces slightly and turns the color of a red eye. Add the sugar only if your ham is very salty.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind pepper in and pour over the ham, biscuits, or grits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two ingredients and a hot pan. The gravy is thin by design: it is a seasoned pan liquor, not a sauce, and thickening it with flour makes it something else entirely; black coffee deglazing ham drippings is the whole recipe, bitter against salt-cure. Smithfield ham is Virginia's dry-cured, peanut-fed legend, protected by state statute since 1926: genuinely salty, sliced thin, and never in need of more. Grits or biscuits underneath to catch what the pan surrenders.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Smithfield%20Ham%20with%20Redeye%20Gravy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Smithfield Ham with Redeye Gravy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Smithfield Ham with Redeye Gravy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Smithfield%20Ham%20with%20Redeye%20Gravy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Butter","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar","Coffee/Espresso"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"virginia-ham-biscuits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the biscuits: 375g flour, 1 tbsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 1/2 tsp soda, 115g cold butter, 60g lard, 300ml cold buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g country ham, sliced very thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot mustard or fig preserves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the cold fats into the dry ingredients until pea-sized, then stir in the buttermilk just until it comes together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat to 2cm on a floured board, fold in thirds twice, and cut small rounds straight down without twisting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 12-14 min until risen and gold. Cool slightly.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":840,"estimated":false},{"text":"Split each biscuit, butter the faces, and fold in a ruffle of ham.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add a dab of mustard or preserves and serve warm or at room temperature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Small biscuits, thin ham, and something sweet or sharp to answer the salt. These are the currency of every Virginia gathering, and they are better an hour after baking than straight from the oven.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Letter-folding dough","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Letter-folding dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=letter%20fold%20dough%20lamination%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Virginia%20Ham%20Biscuits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Virginia Ham Biscuits"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Virginia Ham Biscuits","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Virginia%20Ham%20Biscuits","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"brunswick-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole chicken, about 1.6kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork shoulder, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g lima beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot sauce, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chicken and pork in the water 90 min until both fall apart. Lift out, shred the meat, and keep the broth.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the broth to the pot with the onion, tomatoes, and potatoes and simmer 30 min until the potatoes begin to break down.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the limas and cook 15 min, then the corn and the shredded meat.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season with Worcestershire, vinegar, sugar, hot sauce, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered another 30-45 min, stirring often, until the stew is thick enough for a spoon to stand in it.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true}],"note":"Virginia and Georgia both claim the invention and neither will yield. The finish is the test: it must be thick enough to stand a spoon, which means uncovered simmering and frequent stirring at the end.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brunswick%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Brunswick Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Brunswick Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Brunswick%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Corn","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"virginia-peanut-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g smooth peanut butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp celery salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper, and cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chopped peanuts to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion and celery in the butter until soft without color, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the flour and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the stock slowly, whisking, and simmer 15 min. Strain out the vegetables for the classic smooth texture.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the peanut butter into the hot broth until fully dissolved, then add the cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with celery salt, lemon, salt, white pepper, and a pinch of cayenne. Serve dusted with chopped peanuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Served at the King's Arms Tavern in Williamsburg since the colonial revival, and older than that in the kitchens where it started. The lemon at the end is what keeps it from tasting like a sauce.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","bright","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Virginia%20Peanut%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Virginia Peanut Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Virginia Peanut Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Virginia%20Peanut%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Celery","Chiles","Lemon","Butter","Cream","Flour","Peanut","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"spoonbread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"750ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g fine white cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scald the milk, then rain in the cornmeal, whisking hard, and cook 5 min until very thick.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off the heat beat in the butter, salt, and sugar, then cool 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the egg yolks one at a time, then the baking powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites to soft peaks and fold them through in two additions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered dish and bake at 190C for 35-40 min until puffed and browned. Serve immediately with a spoon and plenty of butter.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"It is closer to a savory souffle than to bread and it falls within minutes; carry it to the table straight from the oven. The name is literal: it is served with a spoon, never sliced.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Spoonbread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Spoonbread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Spoonbread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Spoonbread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"sally-lunn-bread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"240ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the milk and butter together until the butter melts; cool to lukewarm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs into the milk, then mix into the flour, yeast, sugar, and salt to a very soft, sticky batter-dough. Beat 5 min with a mixer.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and rise 90 min until doubled and bubbly.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat it down, turn into a buttered tube pan, and rise 45 min more.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 40-45 min until deep gold and hollow-sounding. Turn out and serve warm with butter.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true}],"note":"A rich English batter bread that Virginia adopted and kept: Williamsburg taverns baked it in the 1700s and the colonial capital never let it go. The dough is too soft to knead by hand. Beat it instead, and resist adding flour to make it behave; the batter's looseness is what bakes into the fine, cake-like crumb. A turk's-head or tube pan is traditional. Sliced warm with butter, or toasted the next day, when it makes the best toast in the commonwealth's repertoire.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sally%20Lunn%20Bread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sally Lunn Bread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sally Lunn Bread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sally%20Lunn%20Bread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"virginia-chess-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the sugar, cornmeal, flour, and salt together in a bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs one at a time, then the melted butter, buttermilk, vinegar, and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the shell and set on a low oven rack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 45-55 min until the top is golden and cracked at the edges with a slight wobble at the center.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely, at least 3 hours, before slicing.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false}],"note":"The spoon of cornmeal is the signature: it gives chess pie its faint grain and keeps the custard from being merely sweet; the vinegar does the same job from the other direction, a quiet acid against a filling that is mostly butter, sugar and eggs. Nobody agrees on the name: 'just chess pie' from 'jes' pie' is the favorite folk etymology and unprovable. Bake until the center barely trembles; chess pie sets as it cools and overbaking curdles it. Desperation pie, perfected.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Virginia%20Chess%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Virginia Chess Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Virginia Chess Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Virginia%20Chess%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"virginia-peanut-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g Virginia peanuts, roasted and salted"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml dark corn syrup or cane syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scatter the peanuts evenly in the pie shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, brown sugar, syrup, melted butter, vanilla, and salt until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the peanuts; they will float to form the top layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 50-60 min until the center is set with only a faint tremble.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely before slicing, ideally 4 hours.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Virginia peanuts are large and crisp, and salted ones are correct here: the salt is what keeps a syrup pie from being unbearable. Cover the crust edge with foil if it darkens before the filling sets.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Virginia%20Peanut%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Virginia Peanut Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Virginia Peanut Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Virginia%20Peanut%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Peanut","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"skillet-fried-apples","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg firm apples, cored and sliced thick, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of nutmeg and salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar or lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the butter in a wide cast-iron skillet over medium heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the apples in a single layer and cook 5 min without stirring so they take color.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn, sprinkle with the brown sugar, cinnamon, nutmeg, and salt, and cook 8-10 min more.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the vinegar and let it bubble away: this keeps the dish from becoming dessert.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve hot alongside pork, ham, or biscuits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Skin on: it holds the slices together over ten minutes of heat and carries most of the apple flavor. This is a side dish for the savory plate, which is what the splash of vinegar is defending.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","warm-spiced","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Skillet%20Fried%20Apples%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Skillet Fried Apples"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Skillet Fried Apples","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Skillet%20Fried%20Apples","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Apple","Butter","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"planked-shad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole shad, filleted, about 1.2kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"An untreated oak or hickory plank, soaked in water 2 hours"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Bacon rashers"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the plank thoroughly so it smolders rather than burns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the fillets with salt, pepper, and Old Bay and set them skin-side down on the plank.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay bacon over the flesh and brush with the melted butter and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook beside a hardwood fire, or in a 200C oven with a smoking chip tray, 35-50 min, basting with butter every 15 min.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"The fish is done when it flakes and the plank is charred at the edges. Serve straight from the board with lemon and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Potomac spring ritual older than the Republic: planked shad feasts were political institutions in Virginia, where campaigns were launched over split fish nailed to oak boards around a fire. The plank is the cooking vessel and the seasoning both: soak it well, and never use treated lumber or softwood; oak or hickory only, or the fish tastes of the hardware store. Shad is famously bony; the slow plank heat softens what filleting can't remove. Butter, salt, and the run's brief calendar.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","smoky","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Filleting fish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20fillet%20a%20fish%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Planked%20Shad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Planked Shad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Planked Shad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Planked%20Shad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Old Bay/Creole"]},{"slug":"pepperoni-rolls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g sliced or stick pepperoni"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g provolone or mozzarella (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Melted butter for brushing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix and knead the dough 8 min until smooth and soft; rise 1 hour until doubled.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into 12 pieces and flatten each into an oval.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay 6-8 slices of pepperoni down the middle, with cheese if using, and roll up tightly, sealing the seam and ends.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set seam-side down on a lined tray, cover, and rise 40 min.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 18-20 min until deep gold. Brush with melted butter as they come out: the orange grease streaks are the badge of honor.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented in Fairmont in 1927 for coal miners who needed a lunch that kept without refrigeration. The pepperoni grease soaking into the crumb is the entire point, so do not drain it or wrap it in foil.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pepperoni%20Rolls%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pepperoni Rolls"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Pepperoni Rolls","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Pepperoni%20Rolls","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"west-virginia-slaw-dog","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 hot dogs"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: 450g ground beef, 1 onion grated, 2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp paprika, 2 tbsp tomato paste, 250ml water, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the slaw: 400g cabbage shredded fine, 4 tbsp mayonnaise, 1 tbsp vinegar, 1 tbsp sugar, 1/2 tsp celery seed, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yellow mustard and chopped onion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the raw ground beef in the water with the onion, spices, and tomato paste 45 min until thick and fine-grained; never brown it first.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the cabbage very fine and dress with the mayonnaise, vinegar, sugar, celery seed, and salt. Chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam or griddle the hot dogs and warm the buns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build in the state-mandated order: dog, mustard, sauce, onion, then slaw on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately, before the slaw warms.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The slaw goes on top, the sauce goes underneath, and the cabbage must be chopped fine rather than shredded long. Cross the state line into Ohio and the order changes; West Virginia does not.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","fiery","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=West%20Virginia%20Slaw%20Dog%20recipe","sub":"Search films of West Virginia Slaw Dog"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on West Virginia Slaw Dog","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20West%20Virginia%20Slaw%20Dog","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Celery","Chiles","Cabbage","Bread","Vinegar","Tomato paste","Mustard","Mayonnaise","Sugar","Cumin","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"pinto-beans-and-cornbread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried pinto beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g salt pork or a ham hock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt to taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the cornbread: 250g cornmeal, 60g flour, 1 tbsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 1 egg, 400ml buttermilk, 3 tbsp bacon fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chopped raw onion and chow chow to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the beans, cover with fresh water by 5cm, and add the salt pork, onion, bay, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 2-3 hours, uncovered, adding water as needed, until the beans are creamy and the liquid has turned into a thick pot liquor.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt only at the end: salting early keeps the skins tough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For the cornbread, heat the bacon fat in a cast-iron skillet at 220C until smoking. Mix the batter quickly, pour it into the hot fat, and bake 20-25 min.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the beans in bowls with cornbread, raw onion, and chow chow alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pot liquor is the dish as much as the beans: do not drain it, and cook uncovered so it reduces to a broth with body; cornbread is for crumbling in, and Appalachian cornbread carries no sugar, a line the mountains hold firmly. A ham hock or fatback seasons the pot, and salt waits until the beans are tender. This was the supper that carried coal-camp families through winters: 'beans and cornbread' is shorthand for making do with dignity, and it still eats like it.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pinto%20Beans%20and%20Cornbread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pinto Beans and Cornbread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Pinto Beans and Cornbread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Pinto%20Beans%20and%20Cornbread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Bay leaf","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Kidney/Pinto beans"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"ramps-and-potatoes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g ramps, cleaned, whites and greens separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g potatoes, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp bacon fat or butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cider vinegar to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon in a cast-iron skillet until crisp; lift out and keep the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes in an even layer and cook over medium heat 12-15 min, turning occasionally, until browned and tender.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chopped ramp whites and cook 3 min until softened.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the ramp greens and cook 1 min only: they wilt instantly.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season, return the bacon, and finish with a splash of vinegar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ramp season is three weeks in April and half the state marks it with a supper. Dig responsibly: cut leaves and leave bulbs where you can, or there will be no ramps for the next generation.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ramps%20and%20Potatoes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ramps and Potatoes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ramps and Potatoes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ramps%20and%20Potatoes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Potato","Butter","Vinegar","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"buckwheat-cakes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g buckwheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted, plus more for the griddle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sausage, butter, and syrup to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the dry ingredients together in a wide bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs, buttermilk, and melted butter and stir into the dry ingredients with a light hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the batter 15 min: buckwheat needs the time to hydrate or the cakes taste raw.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Griddle in butter over medium heat until bubbles break across the surface, then flip once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve stacked with butter, syrup, and sausage.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Preston County holds a buckwheat festival every autumn. Buckwheat is not wheat at all and brings a dark, faintly bitter earthiness. The plain flour in the mix keeps the cakes tender rather than dense.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Buckwheat%20Cakes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Buckwheat Cakes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Buckwheat Cakes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Buckwheat%20Cakes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Eggs","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"apple-butter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4kg apples, quartered, cores in"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L apple cider"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cider vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the apples in the cider 45 min until completely soft, then pass through a food mill: the cores and skins give pectin and go in the mill, not the bin.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the puree to a heavy pot with the sugar, spices, salt, and vinegar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over the lowest heat 4-5 hours, stirring often as it thickens and darkens. A spatter screen saves your arms.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":true},{"text":"It is done when a spoonful mounded on a cold plate weeps no liquid at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Jar hot and seal, or refrigerate for a month.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The old way was a copper kettle over a fire and a wooden paddle worked all day by whoever was losing the argument. Low and slow is the only method: high heat scorches the sugar and the whole batch tastes burnt.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Apple%20Butter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Apple Butter"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Apple Butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Apple%20Butter","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sieves-chinois-tamis-and-food-mill","term":"Sieves: Chinois, Tamis & Food Mill"}],"pantryItems":["Apple","Butter","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"sausage-gravy-over-biscuits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g bulk breakfast sausage"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml whole milk, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper, plus more"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp sage"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt to taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 hot split biscuits"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the sausage in a cast-iron skillet over medium heat, breaking it into rough pieces and letting it stick and crust a little.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle the flour over the meat and its fat and cook 2 min, stirring, until the raw taste goes.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the warm milk in a slow stream, whisking constantly to keep it smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 8-10 min until it coats a spoon; it thickens further off the heat, so stop a little thin.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season hard with black pepper, sage, cayenne, and salt. Ladle over split biscuits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pepper past the point that seems polite: this gravy is properly speckled, and pale sausage gravy is the tell of a timid hand. Brown the sausage hard and build the roux in its own fat. If it thickens too far, more warm milk fixes it; a stiff sausage gravy is a paste with meat in it, and it keeps thickening off the heat, so stop a little loose. Biscuits split and waiting. This is the mountain breakfast that assumes a day of real work is coming, and portions accordingly.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sausage%20Gravy%20over%20Biscuits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sausage Gravy over Biscuits"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sausage Gravy over Biscuits","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sausage%20Gravy%20over%20Biscuits","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Chiles","Sage","Milk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"pawpaw-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g pawpaw pulp, seeds and skin removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Whipped cream to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Press the ripe pawpaw flesh through a sieve to remove the large seeds and any skin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the sugar, flour, baking powder, salt, and cinnamon together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs with the milk, melted butter, and vanilla, then stir in the pawpaw pulp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine wet and dry and pour into a buttered dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 50-55 min until set and browned. Serve warm with whipped cream.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"America's largest native fruit, tasting of mango and banana, and it ripens for two weeks in September before it goes. The pawpaw never commercialized because it bruises if you look at it, which is why the pudding stayed a backwoods secret. Never cook the seeds and never eat the skin; the custardy flesh is the only part invited. The pudding bakes like a dense spiced custard, pumpkin-pie-adjacent. Foragers guard their patches like mushroom hunters, and for the same reasons.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pawpaw%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pawpaw Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Pawpaw Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Pawpaw%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"cornmeal-crusted-trout","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole trout, cleaned, or 8 fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g bacon fat or butter for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the trout dry inside and out and season lightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the cornmeal, flour, salt, pepper, and cayenne in a wide dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip the fish in buttermilk, then dredge in the cornmeal, pressing it on well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in hot bacon fat 4-5 min a side for whole fish, 2-3 for fillets, until the crust is deep gold and the flesh flakes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain briefly and serve with lemon and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mountain stream trout, fried in the fat you rendered at breakfast: bacon grease is the traditional medium and the flavor bridge between the smokehouse and the stream. Cornmeal alone can go sandy: the spoonful of flour is what binds the crust to the fish, and a dry-then-wet-then-dredge sequence keeps it attached through the flip. Cook it the day it's caught if you possibly can. Lemon, and nothing else the creek didn't already season.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cornmeal-Crusted%20Trout%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cornmeal-Crusted Trout"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cornmeal-Crusted Trout","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cornmeal-Crusted%20Trout","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Chiles","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"salt-rising-bread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the starter: 60g cornmeal, 250ml scalded milk, 1 tbsp sugar, 1/2 tsp salt, 1/4 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sponge: 500ml warm water, 2 tbsp sugar, 1 tsp salt, 250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: about 700g flour, 3 tbsp butter softened, 2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the scalded milk into the cornmeal with the sugar, salt, and soda. Keep it at 40-45C overnight, 12 hours, until it foams and smells sharp and cheesy.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain the starter into the warm water with the sugar, salt, and 250g flour. Keep warm 2-3 hours until bubbly and risen.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the butter, salt, and enough flour to make a firm dough, and knead 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Shape into two loaves in buttered pans and rise in a warm place 3-4 hours: this bread is slow and needs steady warmth.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 35-40 min until deep brown and hollow-sounding.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"It uses no commercial yeast at all: the rise comes from wild bacteria in the cornmeal, which is why it needs constant warmth and why it smells so strongly while it works. Appalachia has baked it for two centuries and nobody has made it faster.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salt-Rising%20Bread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salt-Rising Bread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Salt-Rising Bread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Salt-Rising%20Bread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"eastern-nc-whole-hog-barbecue","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 pork shoulder, about 4kg, bone in (or a whole hog if you dare)"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the mop and sauce: 500ml cider vinegar, 250ml water, 2 tbsp crushed red pepper, 1 tbsp black pepper, 1 tbsp salt, 1 tbsp sugar, 1 tsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Soft buns or white bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coleslaw"}],"steps":[{"text":"Build a fire for indirect heat and hold the cooker at 110-120C with hickory or oak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the shoulder only with salt and pepper and set it fat-side up, away from the coals.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smoke 10-12 hours, mopping with the vinegar mixture every hour after the first three, until the meat pulls apart at 92C internal.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 30 min, then chop or pull the whole thing, skin and all, working the crisp bits through.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress with the vinegar sauce, not too wet, and pile onto buns with slaw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Eastern North Carolina uses the whole hog and a thin vinegar-pepper sauce with no tomato at all. The chopped bark worked back through the meat is the soul of it: keep the sauce sharp, and let the vinegar cut the fat.","equipment":["Thermometer","Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","smoky","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, wood-smoke running underneath, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20NC%20Whole%20Hog%20Barbecue%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Eastern NC Whole Hog Barbecue"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Eastern NC Whole Hog Barbecue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Eastern%20NC%20Whole%20Hog%20Barbecue","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bell pepper","Bread","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"lexington-style-pork-shoulder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 pork shoulder, about 4kg, bone in"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dip: 250ml cider vinegar, 120ml ketchup, 120ml water, 2 tbsp brown sugar, 1 tbsp red pepper flakes, 1 tsp black pepper, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"For red slaw: 400g cabbage, 120ml of the dip, extra sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Hold the smoker at 110C over hickory. Season the shoulder with salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smoke fat-side up 8-10 hours until it pulls at 92C internal, spritzing with vinegar in the last hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the dip ingredients together 5 min into a thin tomato-tinged sauce.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest, then chop the shoulder, working through the bark, and dress lightly with the dip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the cabbage with dip and extra sugar for red slaw and serve both on a bun or a plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lexington, in the Piedmont, uses the shoulder alone and a dip that admits a little ketchup, the great North Carolina schism. The red slaw dressed in the same dip is the tell that you are west of the coastal plain.","equipment":["Thermometer","Smoker"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","bright","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Low & slow smoking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lexington-Style%20Pork%20Shoulder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lexington-Style Pork Shoulder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Lexington-Style Pork Shoulder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Lexington-Style%20Pork%20Shoulder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bell pepper","Cabbage","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking"]},{"slug":"carolina-fish-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg firm white fish (rockfish or catfish), in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g streaky bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g potatoes, in thick slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L water or fish stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs (optional, for poaching)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp red pepper flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, and hot sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon in a heavy pot; lift out the bits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the onions and potatoes in the pot, add the tomatoes, water, and red pepper, and simmer 25 min until the potatoes soften.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the fish on top, cover, and poach gently 12 min without stirring; stirring breaks the fish.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack the eggs onto the surface if using and poach 4 min in the steam.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season hard, scatter the bacon over, and ladle out carefully so the fish stays whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"An Eastern Carolina river-camp dish, built in layers and never stirred once the fish goes in. The eggs poached on top are the old riverbank touch, and they are better than they sound.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carolina%20Fish%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carolina Fish Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Carolina Fish Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Carolina%20Fish%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","White fish","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Bell pepper","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"livermush","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork liver"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork head meat or shoulder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sage"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the liver and pork with the onion 1 hour until tender. Strain, keeping 1L of the broth, and grind or chop the meat fine.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the broth to a simmer and whisk in the cornmeal, cooking 20 min until very thick.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the meat, salt, pepper, sage, and cayenne. Season boldly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into a loaf pan, cool, and chill overnight until firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice 1cm thick and fry in butter over medium heat 4-5 min a side until a deep crust forms. Do not flip early.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Piedmont cousin of scrapple with more liver and more pepper. As with scrapple, the rule at the stove is patience: leave it alone until the crust sets or it falls to pieces in the pan.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Livermush%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Livermush"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Livermush","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Livermush","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Liver & Offal","Onion","Chiles","Sage","Butter","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"carolina-coleslaw","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 small cabbage, shredded fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dressing: 120ml mayonnaise, 2 tbsp cider vinegar, 1 tbsp yellow mustard, 1 tbsp sugar, 1 tsp celery seed, salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Shred the cabbage as fine as you can and grate the carrot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the cabbage lightly and let it drain in a colander 20 min, then press out the water.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the dressing ingredients together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the cabbage and carrot with just enough dressing to coat; it should not be swimming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill an hour before serving so the flavors marry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Draining the salted cabbage first is what keeps barbecue slaw from turning to soup by the second helping. On an Eastern Carolina plate the slaw goes right on top of the pork sandwich.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salting to draw the water out","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carolina%20Coleslaw%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carolina Coleslaw"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Carolina Coleslaw","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Carolina%20Coleslaw","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Carrot","Celery","Cabbage","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Mayonnaise","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"hush-puppies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the cornmeal, flour, baking powder, salt, and sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the grated onion, egg, and buttermilk to a thick batter. Rest 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the batter in by heaped spoonfuls, 5 or 6 at a time, and fry 3-4 min, turning, until deep gold and cooked through.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack and salt immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The name is said to come from frying scraps to quiet the dogs. Keep the batter thick so they hold their shape, and fry a test one first: if the middle stays raw, your oil is too hot.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hush%20Puppies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hush Puppies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Hush Puppies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Hush%20Puppies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"sweet-potato-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g sweet potatoes, roasted and mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Roast the sweet potatoes whole until collapsing, then scoop and mash the flesh smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the brown sugar, eggs, evaporated milk, melted butter, spices, vanilla, and salt until silky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 15 min, then 175C for 35-40 min until set at the edges with a slight wobble.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely before slicing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"North Carolina grows more sweet potatoes than any state, and roasting rather than boiling them is the difference: boiling waterlogs the flesh and dilutes the flavor you are baking the pie for.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Mashing & puréeing","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sweet%20Potato%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sweet Potato Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sweet Potato Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sweet%20Potato%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ginger","Potato","Sweet potato","Butter","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"calabash-fried-shrimp","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g shrimp, peeled and deveined"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon and cocktail sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the shrimp in buttermilk 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the flour, cornmeal, Old Bay, salt, and pepper in a wide dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the shrimp from the buttermilk, dredge in the flour, shake off the excess, and fry in small batches 90 seconds until pale gold: they cook fast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt, and serve at once with lemon and cocktail sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Named for the fishing town of Calabash, where the style is a light, thin, barely-there crust, never a heavy batter. Fry hot and fast and pull them the second they turn gold; a minute too long and shrimp go rubbery.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Calabash%20Fried%20Shrimp%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Calabash Fried Shrimp"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Calabash Fried Shrimp","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Calabash%20Fried%20Shrimp","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Lemon","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"moravian-sugar-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE DOUGH"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mashed potato"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml potato water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE TOPPING"},{"kind":"item","text":"170g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the potato water and dissolve the yeast in it with a spoon of the sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the mashed potato with the remaining sugar, butter, and eggs, then add the yeast liquid, flour, and salt to a soft dough. Knead briefly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rise 90 min, then press into a buttered sheet pan and rise 45 min more.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":8100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dimple the surface all over with a floured finger and fill the dimples with pieces of butter and brown sugar mixed with cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 20-25 min until the dimples bubble with cinnamon butter and the cake is gold.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"The Moravians of Winston-Salem made this for Easter and the potato in the dough is the reason it stays tender for days. Press the dimples deep: they are wells for the cinnamon butter, which is the whole reward.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moravian%20Sugar%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moravian Sugar Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Moravian Sugar Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Moravian%20Sugar%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Potato","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"carolina-brunswick-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g smoked pork or barbecue"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g cooked chicken, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g lima beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp barbecue sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot sauce, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onions in the butter until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, potatoes, and stock and simmer 30 min until the potatoes start to break down.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the limas and cook 15 min, then the corn, pork, and chicken.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the barbecue sauce and Worcestershire and simmer 45 min more, uncovered, stirring often.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with hot sauce, salt, and pepper. It should be thick, not soupy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"In North Carolina the stew rides shotgun to a barbecue plate and often starts with the smoked pork itself. Long uncovered simmering at the end is what turns a vegetable soup into a proper Brunswick stew.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carolina%20Brunswick%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carolina Brunswick Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Carolina Brunswick Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Carolina%20Brunswick%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Corn","Butter","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"she-crab-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g lump crab meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g crab roe if you can get it"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 shallot, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml heavy cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml seafood stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp mace"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp dry sherry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper, and cayenne"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the shallot in the butter until soft, then stir in the flour and cook 2 min to a blond roux.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the stock and milk and simmer 10 min until smooth and lightly thickened.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cream, mace, and Worcestershire and heat through without boiling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the crab and roe and warm 3 min.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off the heat stir in the sherry, season with salt, white pepper, and cayenne, and serve with an extra thread of sherry in each bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The roe is what makes it she-crab soup and gives the pale orange color; without it you have a fine crab bisque but not the Charleston classic. The sherry goes in off the heat so it does not cook away.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","umami-deep","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=She-Crab%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of She-Crab Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on She-Crab Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20She-Crab%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Shallot","Chiles","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"charleston-shrimp-and-grits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g stone-ground grits"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sharp cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g shrimp, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley, hot sauce, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the grits in the water and milk 40-50 min, stirring often, until creamy. Beat in the butter and cheddar and keep warm.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the bacon until crisp; lift out and reserve, leaving the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onion and garlic in the bacon fat until soft, add the shrimp, and cook 2 min a side until just pink.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the stock and lemon and simmer 2 min into a light pan sauce.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season, fold in the bacon and parsley, and spoon over the grits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lowcountry grits must be stone-ground and cooked slow: instant grits are a different, sadder dish. Pull the shrimp the moment they curl; carryover heat in the sauce finishes them.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Charleston%20Shrimp%20and%20Grits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Charleston Shrimp and Grits"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Charleston Shrimp and Grits","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Charleston%20Shrimp%20and%20Grits","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Bacon","Shrimp","Onion","Garlic","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Cornmeal/Polenta","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"frogmore-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg small new potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g smoked sausage, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 ears corn, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2kg shrimp, shell on"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 lemons, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter and cocktail sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring a very large pot of water to a boil with the Old Bay, onion, and lemons.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes and boil 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the sausage and corn and boil 8 min more.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the shrimp and cook 3 min only, until they just turn pink.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain the whole pot and pour it out onto a newspaper-covered table. Serve with butter and cocktail sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Also called Lowcountry boil or Beaufort stew, and the timing is the whole recipe: everything is staged so it all finishes together and nothing overcooks. The shrimp go in last and come out fast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The seafood boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Frogmore%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Frogmore Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Frogmore Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Frogmore%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Shrimp","Onion","Potato","Corn","Lemon","Butter","Old Bay/Creole"]},{"slug":"lowcountry-hoppin-john","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g dried field peas or black-eyed peas, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 smoked ham hock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g long-grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L water or stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, and hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chopped scallions"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained peas with the ham hock, onion, celery, bay, and thyme in the water 45 min until tender.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pull the hock, shred its meat, and return it to the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the rice, adding water if needed to cover by a finger, and season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and cook 18-20 min until the rice is tender and the liquid absorbed.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 10 min, fluff, and serve with scallions and hot sauce.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"The Lowcountry's New Year's dish, eaten for luck, with the peas standing for coins. Cook the rice in the pea liquor so it takes on the smoke; plain rice stirred in afterward misses the point.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lowcountry%20Hoppin'%20John%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lowcountry Hoppin' John"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Lowcountry Hoppin' John","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Lowcountry%20Hoppin'%20John","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Scallion","Celery","Peas","Thyme","Bay leaf","Rice","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"carolina-gold-rice-pilau","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g Carolina Gold or long-grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"600ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, and cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon until crisp; lift out and reserve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and celery in the bacon fat until soft, 8 min.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rinse the rice, add it to the pot, and stir 1 min to coat.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the stock, bay, and thyme, bring to a boil, then cover and cook on the lowest heat 18 min.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1080,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 10 min off the heat, fold in the bacon and scallions with a fork, and season.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pilau (say 'perlo') is the Lowcountry's one-pot rice, and Carolina Gold is the heirloom grain that built the plantation economy and nearly vanished. Do not stir it while it cooks: you want separate grains, not risotto.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carolina%20Gold%20Rice%20Pilau%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carolina Gold Rice Pilau"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Carolina Gold Rice Pilau","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Carolina%20Gold%20Rice%20Pilau","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Bacon","Onion","Scallion","Celery","Chiles","Thyme","Bay leaf","Rice","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"boiled-peanuts","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg raw green peanuts in the shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Old Bay or cayenne (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the raw peanuts well to remove field dirt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put them in a large pot, cover with the water, and add the salt and any spice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Weight them down with a plate so they stay submerged and bring to a boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 3-4 hours, tasting after two, until the peanuts are soft as a cooked bean. Add water as needed.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn off the heat and let them soak another hour in the brine for the salt to penetrate. Eat warm from the shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The official state snack, sold from roadside pots and gas stations all summer. Green peanuts (freshly dug, not dried) are essential; dried peanuts never soften right. They should be soft, not crunchy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boiled%20Peanuts%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boiled Peanuts"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Boiled Peanuts","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Boiled%20Peanuts","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Peanut","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"pimento-cheese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g sharp cheddar, grated by hand"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g cream cheese, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g jarred pimentos, drained and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp onion powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crackers, celery, or white bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the cheddar yourself on the large holes: pre-grated cheese will not bind properly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the cream cheese and mayonnaise smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the cheddar, pimentos, Worcestershire, onion powder, and cayenne.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with black pepper and mash lightly so it holds together but stays coarse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill an hour. Serve as a dip, a sandwich, or a burger topping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pâté of the South, and hand-grated cheese is the one rule that matters: the anti-caking dust on pre-grated cheddar keeps it from coming together and makes it grainy; grate sharp cheddar yourself and the spread turns silky in a way that surprises first-timers. Duke's mayonnaise is the regional orthodoxy. It began, oddly, as a Northern manufactured food before the South adopted, perfected and naturalized it. On celery, on white bread, on a burger: it improves everything it meets.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pimento%20Cheese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pimento Cheese"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Pimento Cheese","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Pimento%20Cheese","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Celery","Chiles","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Cream cheese","Bread","Mayonnaise","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"bog-style-chicken-and-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole chicken, about 1.6kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g smoked sausage, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g long-grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, and cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chicken in the stock 45 min until tender. Lift out, pull the meat, and keep the broth.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the sausage in the pot, then sweat the onion and celery in the rendered fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the rice and stir 1 min, then add the measured broth, bay, and thyme.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the chicken meat, cover, and cook on low 20 min until the rice is tender and wet.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 10 min. A bog is meant to be moist, not dry: season well and finish with scallions.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"A chicken bog is wetter than a pilau, which is the whole distinction: the rice should be soft and loose, almost soupy, and drying it out into distinct grains makes a different (and to Horry County, wrong) dish. Williamsburg County holds a festival for it every autumn, and the competition pots feed hundreds. Smoked sausage in with the chicken is standard practice. The name likely comes from the bird getting 'bogged down' in the rice: low-country humor, low-country supper.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","herb-fresh","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bog-Style%20Chicken%20and%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bog-Style Chicken and Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Bog-Style Chicken and Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Bog-Style%20Chicken%20and%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Onion","Scallion","Celery","Chiles","Thyme","Bay leaf","Rice","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"benne-wafers","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g benne (sesame) seeds, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"125g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the sesame seeds in a dry pan until golden and fragrant, then cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream the butter and brown sugar, beat in the egg and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the flour, baking powder, salt, and toasted seeds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop tiny half-teaspoons far apart on lined trays: they spread dramatically.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 8-10 min until deep gold at the edges. Cool on the tray until crisp.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Benne is the West African word for sesame, brought to Charleston by enslaved people, and the wafers are a Lowcountry emblem of good luck. Space them wide: they spread to twice their size and weld together if crowded.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Creaming butter & sugar","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Benne%20Wafers%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Benne Wafers"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Benne Wafers","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Benne%20Wafers","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sesame","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"charleston-red-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g long-grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g smoked sausage, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, and cayenne"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon and sausage in a heavy pot until browned; lift out and reserve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, pepper, and celery in the fat until soft, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the tomato paste and cook 2 min, then add the crushed tomatoes and cook 5 min more.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rinse the rice, stir it in, then add the stock, thyme, and the reserved meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and cook on the lowest heat 25 min. Rest 10 min, then fork through gently: the grains should be separate and stained deep red.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false}],"note":"A direct descendant of West African jollof, carried to the Lowcountry. The tomato paste cooked dark before the liquid goes in is what gives the color and depth; skip it and you get pink rice, not red.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","smoky","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, wood-smoke running underneath, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Jollof & party smoke","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=jollof%20rice%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Charleston%20Red%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Charleston Red Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Charleston Red Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Charleston%20Red%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Bacon","Sausage","Onion","Tomato","Celery","Bell pepper","Chiles","Thyme","Rice","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"georgia-peach-cobbler","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg ripe peaches, peeled and sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the batter: 115g butter, 190g flour, 200g sugar, 2 tsp baking powder, 1/4 tsp salt, 240ml milk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the peaches with the sugar, lemon, cinnamon, and cornstarch and let them macerate 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Melt the butter in a 23x33cm baking dish in the oven as it heats to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, sugar, baking powder, salt, and milk into a smooth batter and pour it over the melted butter. Do not stir.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the peaches and their juice evenly over the batter; again do not stir.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 45-55 min until the batter has risen up and around the fruit and browned. The cake rises through the peaches as it bakes.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"The magic is not stirring: the batter floats up through the fruit as it bakes, so you get cake on top and peaches suspended below. Georgia peaches in July are the reason the state is nicknamed for them.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgia%20Peach%20Cobbler%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Georgia Peach Cobbler"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Georgia Peach Cobbler","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Georgia%20Peach%20Cobbler","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Stone fruit","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"]},{"slug":"georgia-pecan-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g pecan halves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml dark corn syrup or cane syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp bourbon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scatter the pecans in the shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, brown sugar, syrup, melted butter, bourbon, vanilla, and salt until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the pecans; they rise to form the top crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 50-60 min until the center is set with only a slight jiggle.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely, at least 4 hours, before slicing.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Georgia grows more pecans than any state. Toast the pecans for eight minutes first if you have the time: it deepens the flavor past what raw nuts can give, and it is the one improvement worth making.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgia%20Pecan%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Georgia Pecan Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Georgia Pecan Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Georgia%20Pecan%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"fried-chicken-georgia-style","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut in 8 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Peanut oil or lard for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brine the chicken in the buttermilk and hot sauce at least 4 hours, ideally overnight.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the flour with all the seasonings in a wide dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift each piece from the buttermilk, letting the excess drip, and dredge heavily in the seasoned flour. Rest on a rack 15 min so the coating sets.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 165C in a cast-iron skillet. Fry the dark meat 14-16 min, the white 12-14, turning once, to a deep gold and 74C internal.","durationSec":960,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt at once, and rest 5 min before serving.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"Cast-iron and a modest 165°C are the Southern way: too hot and the crust burns before the inside cooks, and the skillet's steady heat is what lets a cook manage eight pieces by sound and color alone. Resting the dredged chicken before it fries is what glues the coating on; wet dredge slides off in the fat. A shallow fry with turning, lid on for the first half in the old Georgia manner: the steam helps the meat while the second half crisps the crust. Salt the moment it lands on the rack.","equipment":["Skillet","Thermometer"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","nutty-toasted","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fried%20Chicken%2C%20Georgia%20Style%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fried Chicken, Georgia Style"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Fried Chicken, Georgia Style","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Fried%20Chicken%2C%20Georgia%20Style","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Chiles","Buttermilk","Flour","Peanut","Hot sauce","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"vidalia-onion-casserole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 large Vidalia onions, sliced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g grated Parmesan"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g crushed crackers"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onions slowly in the butter until soft and sweet but not browned, 20 min; the sweetness of a Vidalia is the whole point.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the cream, eggs, half the Parmesan, nutmeg, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the onions in a buttered dish and pour the custard over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with the cracker crumbs and remaining Parmesan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 30-35 min until set and golden.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false}],"note":"Vidalias are sweet only because of the low-sulfur soil around Vidalia, Georgia; they are a legally protected crop. Cook them low and slow so their sugar comes forward without browning to bitterness.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","warm-spiced","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vidalia%20Onion%20Casserole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vidalia Onion Casserole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Vidalia Onion Casserole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Vidalia%20Onion%20Casserole","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Butter","Cream","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"georgia-brunswick-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g smoked pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g cooked chicken, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g lima beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp barbecue sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot sauce, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onions in the butter until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, potatoes, and stock and simmer 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the limas and cook 15 min, then the corn, pork, and chicken.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the barbecue sauce, Worcestershire, and vinegar and simmer 60 min uncovered, stirring often, until thick.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with hot sauce, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brunswick, Georgia, claims the invention on a plaque by an old iron pot; Virginia disagrees. Either way the Georgia version leans smoky and sweet with barbecue in the base, and it must cook down thick.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","bright","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgia%20Brunswick%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Georgia Brunswick Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Georgia Brunswick Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Georgia%20Brunswick%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Corn","Butter","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"country-captain","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs, bone in"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour, seasoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"75g currants or raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g slivered almonds, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dredge the chicken in seasoned flour and brown well in the oil; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, pepper, and garlic in the pan, then add the curry powder and cook 1 min until fragrant.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes and stock and scrape up the fond.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the chicken, add the currants, cover, and simmer 40 min until tender.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Scatter the toasted almonds and parsley over and serve on rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A curried chicken that came into Savannah and Charleston through the spice trade in the 1800s. Bloom the curry powder in the fat before the liquid goes in: raw curry powder tastes dusty and flat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","herb-fresh","aromatic","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Country%20Captain%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Country Captain"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Country Captain","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Country%20Captain","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Dried fruit","Parsley","Rice","Flour","Stock/Broth","Nuts","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"savannah-crab-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450g lump crab meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml seafood stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp mace"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp dry sherry"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 hard-boiled eggs, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper, and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion and celery in the butter until soft, then stir in the flour and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the stock and milk and simmer 10 min until lightly thickened.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cream and mace and heat through without boiling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the crab and the chopped egg and warm gently 3 min.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat add the sherry, season, and finish with parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coastal Georgia's answer to she-crab soup, thickened a little more and enriched with chopped egg. As always with a sherry-finished stew, the sherry goes in off the heat to keep its perfume.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Savannah%20Crab%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Savannah Crab Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Savannah Crab Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Savannah%20Crab%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Eggs","Onion","Celery","Parsley","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"georgia-peanut-brittle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g raw Georgia peanuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a large sheet pan and warm it slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar, corn syrup, and water to 120C, then add the butter and peanuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep cooking, stirring constantly, to 150C: the syrup will turn amber and the peanuts will smell toasted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat quickly stir in the salt, baking soda, and vanilla; it will foam up pale.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour onto the pan, spread thin with a buttered spatula, and cool completely before breaking into shards.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The baking soda is what makes brittle brittle: it foams the hot syrup full of tiny bubbles so it shatters instead of cracking teeth. Work fast once it goes in; the candy sets in minutes.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgia%20Peanut%20Brittle%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Georgia Peanut Brittle"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Georgia Peanut Brittle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Georgia%20Peanut%20Brittle","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Peanut","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"peach-and-bourbon-bread-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g day-old brioche or French bread, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 peaches, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp bourbon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: 115g butter, 150g brown sugar, 60ml bourbon, 120ml cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a baking dish and scatter in the bread and peaches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, milk, cream, sugar, bourbon, vanilla, and cinnamon and pour over. Press the bread down and soak 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dot with butter and bake at 175C for 45-50 min until puffed and set.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"For the sauce, simmer the butter, brown sugar, bourbon, and cream 5 min until glossy.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the warm sauce over the pudding and serve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Stale bread is essential: fresh bread turns to paste. Let it drink the custard for a full half hour before it bakes, and do not skimp on the bourbon sauce; it is the reason anyone remembers the dish.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peach%20and%20Bourbon%20Bread%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Peach and Bourbon Bread Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Peach and Bourbon Bread Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Peach%20and%20Bourbon%20Bread%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Stone fruit","Butter","Milk","Cream","Bread","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"]},{"slug":"key-lime-pie-florida-keys","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the crust: 200g graham crackers crushed, 100g sugar, 115g butter melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 4 egg yolks, 400g sweetened condensed milk, 150ml key lime juice, 1 tbsp lime zest"},{"kind":"item","text":"Whipped cream and lime zest to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the crumbs, sugar, and butter and press into a 23cm pie plate. Bake at 175C for 10 min and cool.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with the lime zest until pale, then beat in the condensed milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the lime juice and whisk until the mixture thickens visibly: the acid sets it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the crust and bake at 175C for 12-15 min, just until set with a faint wobble.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool, then chill 4 hours. Top with whipped cream and zest before serving.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Real key lime juice is tarter and more floral than regular lime, and the filling is genuinely cooked, not just chilled: the short bake sets the egg and keeps it safe. The color is pale yellow, never green; green means food coloring and a stranger's recipe.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Key%20Lime%20Pie%2C%20Florida%20Keys%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Key Lime Pie, Florida Keys"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Key Lime Pie, Florida Keys","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Key%20Lime%20Pie%2C%20Florida%20Keys","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lime","Butter","Milk","Cream","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"florida-stone-crab-with-mustard-sauce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg stone crab claws, cooked and chilled"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: 200g mayonnaise, 2 tbsp Colman's dry mustard, 1 tbsp Worcestershire, 1 tbsp cream, 1 tsp lemon juice, pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"A wooden mallet"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill the claws thoroughly; stone crab is always served cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the dry mustard into the mayonnaise and let it sit 10 min to bloom and sharpen.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the Worcestershire, cream, lemon, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack the claws with a mallet, cracking each segment so the meat pulls free clean.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the claws on ice with the mustard sauce and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Only the claws are taken; the crab is returned to the sea to regrow them, which makes stone crab one of the few truly sustainable luxuries. The mustard sauce is the traditional partner and it must be sharp: let the dry mustard bloom.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shucking shellfish"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Florida%20Stone%20Crab%20with%20Mustard%20Sauce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Florida Stone Crab with Mustard Sauce"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Florida Stone Crab with Mustard Sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Florida%20Stone%20Crab%20with%20Mustard%20Sauce","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Lemon","Cream","Mustard","Mayonnaise"]},{"slug":"cuban-sandwich-tampa-style","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 loaves Cuban bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g roast pork, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g glazed ham, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g Genoa salami, sliced (the Tampa addition)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g Swiss cheese, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dill pickle slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yellow mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter or lard for the press"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the bread lengthwise and spread mustard on both faces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer in order: ham, then roast pork, then salami, then Swiss, then pickles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Close the sandwich and butter the outside of the bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press in a plancha or a heavy skillet with a weight on top, 4-5 min, until the cheese melts and the bread is flat and crisp.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cut on a sharp diagonal and serve hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tampa's Cuban includes Genoa salami, a mark of the Italian immigrants in Ybor City, a point of pride that starts arguments with Miami. The bread must be flattened hard in the press until it crackles.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20Sandwich%2C%20Tampa%20Style%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cuban Sandwich, Tampa Style"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cuban Sandwich, Tampa Style","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cuban%20Sandwich%2C%20Tampa%20Style","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ham/Prosciutto","Dill","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"conch-fritters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g conch meat, ground or minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 celery rib, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Key lime and remoulade"}],"steps":[{"text":"Tenderize the conch by mincing it fine: it is naturally tough and needs it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, egg, and milk into a thick batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the conch, onion, pepper, celery, hot sauce, and Old Bay.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop heaped spoonfuls into 180C oil and fry 3-4 min, turning, until deep gold and cooked through.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve with key lime wedges and remoulade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Key West and the Bahamas share the conch, and the trick is mincing it fine enough that it never turns rubbery. Fry a test fritter to check the seasoning and that the center cooks through before the outside burns.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Conch%20Fritters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Conch Fritters"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Conch Fritters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Conch%20Fritters","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Celery","Bell pepper","Lime","Milk","Flour","Hot sauce","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"florida-grouper-sandwich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 grouper fillets, about 180g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, and red onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: 150g mayonnaise, 2 tbsp relish, 1 tbsp lemon, 1 tsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the grouper in buttermilk 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the flour, cornmeal, and Old Bay. Whisk the sauce ingredients and chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C. Dredge the fish, shake off the excess, and fry 3-4 min until gold and flaking.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the rolls and spread with the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build with lettuce, tomato, onion, and the hot fish, and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Gulf grouper sandwich is a Florida coast institution and it can be fried, grilled, or blackened. Fried is the classic; whatever you do, do not overcook grouper: it goes from succulent to cottony fast.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Florida%20Grouper%20Sandwich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Florida Grouper Sandwich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Florida Grouper Sandwich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Florida%20Grouper%20Sandwich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Tomato","Lettuce/Greens","Lemon","Buttermilk","Bread","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Mayonnaise","Hot sauce","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"cuban-black-beans-and-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g dried black beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved, plus 1 diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, halved, plus 1 diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g white rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained beans with the halved onion, halved pepper, and bay in fresh water 90 min until tender. Discard the halved vegetables.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a sofrito: cook the diced onion, diced pepper, and garlic in the olive oil until soft, then add the cumin and oregano and cook 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the sofrito into the beans, add the vinegar, and simmer 30 min uncovered until thick, mashing some beans against the pot for body.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the rice separately until fluffy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the beans well and serve over or alongside the rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Frijoles negros are the everyday soul of Cuban Miami. Mashing a ladle of the beans against the pot thickens the pot liquor into something silky, and the splash of vinegar at the end brightens the whole thing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Soaking dried goods back to life","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20Black%20Beans%20and%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cuban Black Beans and Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cuban Black Beans and Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cuban%20Black%20Beans%20and%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Bell pepper","Oregano","Bay leaf","Rice","Black beans","Vinegar","Wine","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"florida-orange-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"170g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Zest of 2 oranges"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml fresh orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the glaze: 150g icing sugar, 3 tbsp orange juice, 1 tsp zest"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter, sugar, and orange zest until light and fragrant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs one at a time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Alternate the dry ingredients with the orange juice and buttermilk in three additions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered bundt or loaf pan and bake at 175C for 45-55 min until a skewer comes clean.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool 15 min, then spoon the orange glaze over while still warm so it soaks in.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rubbing the zest into the sugar first releases the oils and perfumes the whole cake: juice alone gives sweetness but not aroma. Florida's citrus peaks in winter, when this cake tastes most of the state.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Florida%20Orange%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Florida Orange Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Florida Orange Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Florida%20Orange%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Orange","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"sofrito-shrimp-creole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g shrimp, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp capers"},{"kind":"item","text":"75g green olives, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Rice to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the onion, pepper, and garlic in the olive oil to a soft sofrito, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the cumin and oregano and cook 1 min, then the wine and let it reduce by half.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":60,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, bay, capers, and olives and simmer 15 min into a thick sauce.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the shrimp and cook 3-4 min only, until they curl and turn pink.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season with salt, pepper, and cayenne and serve over rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Spanish-Caribbean cousin of Louisiana shrimp creole, built on a sofrito with olives and capers. Add the shrimp at the very end: they need only minutes, and a shrimp simmered too long is a small tragedy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","gently hot","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sofrito%20Shrimp%20Creole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sofrito Shrimp Creole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sofrito Shrimp Creole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sofrito%20Shrimp%20Creole","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Olives","Capers","Oregano","Bay leaf","Rice","Wine","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"floridian-flan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar for the caramel"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"375ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Zest of 1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the sugar in a dry pan to a deep amber caramel and pour it into a round mold, swirling to coat the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, yolks, condensed milk, evaporated milk, vanilla, lime zest, and salt until smooth; do not froth it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain into the caramel-lined mold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the mold in a roasting pan, pour hot water halfway up the sides, and bake at 160C for 55-65 min until just set with a slight wobble.","durationSec":3900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool, then chill overnight. Run a knife around the edge and invert onto a plate so the caramel floods over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Cuban flan of Miami is denser than a French creme caramel because of the condensed milk. The water bath is essential (it keeps the custard from splitting), and the overnight chill lets the caramel liquefy into sauce.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Straining & passing through a sieve","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","bright","floral","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Floridian%20Flan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Floridian Flan"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Floridian Flan","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Floridian%20Flan","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lime","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"georgia-buttermilk-biscuits-with-sausage","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"375g self-rising flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g cold butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g cold lard or shortening"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml cold buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 breakfast sausage patties"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter and cane syrup to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the cold butter and lard into the flour, sugar, and salt until the pieces are pea-sized.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the buttermilk just until it comes together into a shaggy dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat to 3cm thick, fold in thirds twice for layers, and cut straight down with a floured cutter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 230C for 12-15 min until tall and golden, brushing with butter as they come out.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the sausage patties until browned and serve tucked in the split biscuits with cane syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Southern biscuits live and die on cold fat and a light hand: the moment the dough looks smooth you have overworked it. Cut straight down without twisting or the layers seal and the biscuit stays squat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Letter-folding dough","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Letter-folding dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=letter%20fold%20dough%20lamination%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgia%20Buttermilk%20Biscuits%20with%20Sausage%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Georgia Buttermilk Biscuits with Sausage"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Georgia Buttermilk Biscuits with Sausage","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Georgia%20Buttermilk%20Biscuits%20with%20Sausage","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"minorcan-datil-pepper-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g firm white fish or 400g minced clams"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g salt pork, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L fish stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2-3 datil peppers, minced (or habanero)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the salt pork until crisp; lift out and reserve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, pepper, celery, and garlic in the fat until soft, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, stock, potatoes, datil pepper, bay, and thyme and simmer 25 min.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the fish or clams and cook 8 min until the fish flakes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season and scatter the pork bits over: it should carry a real, fruity heat from the datil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"St. Augustine's Minorcan community built this around the datil pepper, a fiery fruit they have grown there for two centuries. The datil brings heat with a distinct sweetness; a habanero is the nearest substitute if you cannot find one.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","briny","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, a briny, maritime salinity, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minorcan%20Datil%20Pepper%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minorcan Datil Pepper Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minorcan Datil Pepper Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minorcan%20Datil%20Pepper%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","White fish","Mussels/Clams","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Celery","Bell pepper","Chiles","Thyme","Bay leaf","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"alabama-white-sauce-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole chicken, spatchcocked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the white sauce: 300ml mayonnaise, 120ml cider vinegar, 2 tbsp lemon juice, 1 tbsp horseradish, 1 tsp black pepper, 1 tsp sugar, 1/2 tsp cayenne, 1 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the spatchcocked chicken hard with salt and pepper and let it sit an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smoke or grill over indirect heat at 150C for about 75 min until 74C in the thigh.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk all the white sauce ingredients together: it should be thin, sharp, and peppery.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"When the chicken comes off the heat, dunk the whole bird in the white sauce or brush it on generously.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 10 min and serve with more sauce on the side.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Big Bob Gibson invented this in Decatur in 1925, and northern Alabama has dunked smoked chicken in it ever since. The sauce is mayonnaise and vinegar, not tomato: keep it thin and tangy, and sauce the bird at the end, never during the smoke.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Butchery basics","Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","bright","gently hot","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, acid holding everything up to the light, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Butchery basics","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i_ZkAHCR1D0","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alabama%20White%20Sauce%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alabama White Sauce Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alabama White Sauce Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alabama%20White%20Sauce%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Chiles","Lemon","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mayonnaise","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"fried-green-tomatoes-alabama","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 firm green tomatoes, sliced 1cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"75g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Remoulade to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the tomato slices and let them drain on paper 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Set up a dredge: buttermilk in one dish, and the cornmeal, flour, salt, pepper, and cayenne mixed in another.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip each slice in buttermilk, then cornmeal, pressing it on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 5mm of oil over medium-high heat 2-3 min a side until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve hot with remoulade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Under-ripe tomatoes are firm and tart enough to hold up to frying where a ripe one collapses. Cornmeal is the traditional Southern coat and it gives the crunch that plain flour cannot.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fried%20Green%20Tomatoes%2C%20Alabama%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fried Green Tomatoes, Alabama"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Fried Green Tomatoes, Alabama","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Fried%20Green%20Tomatoes%2C%20Alabama","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Chiles","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"alabama-banana-pudding-baked","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 ripe bananas, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g vanilla wafers"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the meringue: 4 egg whites, 100g sugar, 1/4 tsp cream of tartar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a custard: heat the milk and cream, whisk yolks with sugar and cornstarch, temper, return, and cook to a thick boil. Off heat beat in butter and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer wafers, banana slices, and custard in a dish, repeating and ending with custard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites with cream of tartar, adding the sugar slowly, to stiff glossy peaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the meringue over the pudding, sealing to the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 15-20 min until the meringue is golden. Cool, then chill 2 hours.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":8400,"estimated":false}],"note":"The baked, meringue-topped version is the Southern church-supper standard, distinct from the icebox kind. Seal the meringue right to the edge of the dish or it will shrink and weep as it bakes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, melting away silken."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alabama%20Banana%20Pudding%2C%20Baked%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alabama Banana Pudding, Baked"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alabama Banana Pudding, Baked","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alabama%20Banana%20Pudding%2C%20Baked","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Banana","Butter","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"alabama-fried-catfish-plate","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g catfish fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon and hot sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the catfish in buttermilk and hot sauce 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the cornmeal, flour, and seasonings in a wide dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C in a cast-iron skillet or deep pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the fillets in the cornmeal, pressing it on, and fry 4-5 min until deep gold and floating.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt at once, and serve with lemon, hot sauce, hush puppies, and slaw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"All-cornmeal coating is the Deep South standard for catfish: it fries up sandy-crisp and shatters, where flour blankets go soft; a little hot sauce in the soak is the quiet seasoning move. Get the oil properly hot so the crust sets before the fish overcooks: 175°C, small batches, no exceptions. Farm-raised catfish made Alabama a top producer and cleaned up the muddy reputation of the wild fish. Hushpuppies, slaw, white bread, pickle: the plate is a fixed liturgy.","equipment":["Skillet","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alabama%20Fried%20Catfish%20Plate%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alabama Fried Catfish Plate"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alabama Fried Catfish Plate","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alabama%20Fried%20Catfish%20Plate","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"alabama-lane-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the cake: 320g flour, 1 tbsp baking powder, 1/2 tsp salt, 225g butter, 400g sugar, 8 egg whites, 250ml milk, 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 8 egg yolks, 200g sugar, 115g butter, 150g raisins, 100g pecans, 100g coconut, 120ml bourbon"},{"kind":"item","text":"White frosting to cover"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar, alternate the dry ingredients with the milk, then fold in whipped egg whites. Bake in three 23cm pans at 175C for 22-25 min. Cool.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"For the filling, cook the yolks, sugar, and butter over low heat, stirring, until thick, 8 min.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat stir in the raisins, pecans, coconut, and bourbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the filling, then spread it thick between the cake layers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover the outside with a simple white frosting and let it set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Made famous by Harper Lee's Mockingbird, where it is described as loaded enough with bourbon to affect a neighbor. The boozy fruit-and-nut filling is the heart of it: the cake is just the vehicle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Folding: keeping the air in","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alabama%20Lane%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alabama Lane Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alabama Lane Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alabama%20Lane%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","creaming-butter-and-sugar"]},{"slug":"conecuh-sausage-and-white-beans","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g smoked sausage (Conecuh or similar), sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried great northern beans, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, and hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cornbread to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the sausage in a heavy pot until it renders and colors; lift out and reserve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, celery, and garlic in the fat until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the drained beans, bay, thyme, and stock and simmer 90 min until the beans are creamy.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the sausage and simmer 20 min more, mashing some beans against the pot to thicken.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season and serve with cornbread and hot sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Conecuh County sausage is Alabama's smoky pride, hickory-smoked in the same small town since 1947, and a pot of white beans is its natural home; the sausage seasons the whole pot the way a hock would, with more snap. Salt only near the end: early salt keeps the bean skins tough for hours, the oldest bean law there is. Sear a few slices to lay on top of the finished bowl. Cornbread alongside, because Alabama and West Virginia agree on at least that much.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Conecuh%20Sausage%20and%20White%20Beans%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Conecuh Sausage and White Beans"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Conecuh Sausage and White Beans","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Conecuh%20Sausage%20and%20White%20Beans","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Onion","Garlic","Celery","Thyme","Bay leaf","White beans","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"alabama-peach-and-pecan-cobbler","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg peaches, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pecans, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the topping: 190g flour, 100g sugar, 2 tsp baking powder, 1/2 tsp salt, 115g butter melted, 180ml milk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the peaches with sugar, cornstarch, and cinnamon and spread in a buttered dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the topping ingredients into a batter and spoon it over the fruit in patches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the pecans over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 45-50 min until the topping is gold and the fruit bubbles.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 20 min and serve with ice cream.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chilton County peaches are Alabama's summer glory: the water tower is shaped like one, and the June roadside stands are a pilgrimage. The pecans on top toast as the cobbler bakes and add a crunch the fruit and cake lack on their own, the state tree contributing to the state dessert argument. Peel the peaches if ripe, don't if barely; taste the fruit before sugaring, because a Chilton peach in season needs half what the recipe says. Serve warm, cream optional, ice cream traditional.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alabama%20Peach%20and%20Pecan%20Cobbler%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alabama Peach and Pecan Cobbler"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alabama Peach and Pecan Cobbler","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alabama%20Peach%20and%20Pecan%20Cobbler","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Stone fruit","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"]},{"slug":"alabama-brunswick-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g smoked pork or chicken, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g lima beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp barbecue sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot sauce, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onions in the butter until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, potatoes, and stock and simmer 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the limas and cook 15 min, then the corn and the smoked meat.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the barbecue sauce, Worcestershire, and vinegar and simmer 45 min uncovered.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season and cook down until thick enough to hold a spoon upright.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Served alongside barbecue across the state, and like all Brunswick stews it must be cooked down thick: a spoon should stand a moment before falling, and a runny Brunswick is a vegetable soup wearing the wrong name. The smoked meat in the base is what ties it to the pit it sits beside; leftover pulled pork is the traditional engine. Georgia and Virginia both claim the stew's origin, and Alabama wisely skips the argument and just serves it with white bread.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","umami-deep","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alabama%20Brunswick%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alabama Brunswick Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alabama Brunswick Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alabama%20Brunswick%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Corn","Butter","Vinegar","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"alabama-cornbread-dressing","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the cornbread: 250g cornmeal, 60g flour, 1 tbsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 2 eggs, 400ml buttermilk, 3 tbsp bacon fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices day-old white bread, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sage"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bake the cornbread in a hot cast-iron skillet and let it go stale overnight; crumble it with the torn white bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and celery in the butter until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the crumbs, vegetables, sage, and thyme, then stir in the beaten eggs and enough stock to make it wet and loose, wetter than seems right.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread in a buttered dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 40-45 min until set and browned on top.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true}],"note":"Southern dressing is baked in a pan, not stuffed in a bird, and it starts with real cornbread gone stale. Make the mixture wetter than you think: it firms as it bakes, and a dry dressing is the holiday's quiet disappointment.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alabama%20Cornbread%20Dressing%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alabama Cornbread Dressing"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alabama Cornbread Dressing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alabama%20Cornbread%20Dressing","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Bacon","Eggs","Onion","Celery","Thyme","Sage","Butter","Buttermilk","Bread","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"fried-okra","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g fresh okra, sliced 1cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the sliced okra in the buttermilk to coat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the cornmeal, flour, salt, pepper, and cayenne in a bag.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the okra out, letting the excess buttermilk drip, and toss in the cornmeal mix until coated.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 1cm of hot oil over medium-high heat, in batches, 4-5 min until deep gold and crisp, turning as it browns.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on paper, salt at once, and serve immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"High heat and a cornmeal coat are what defeat okra's famous slime: the mucilage sets and crisps instead of running. Do not crowd the pan or the temperature drops and the okra steams into the very thing you were avoiding.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fried%20Okra%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fried Okra"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Fried Okra","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Fried%20Okra","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Okra","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"mississippi-mud-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the crust: 250g chocolate cookies crushed, 115g butter melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the fudge layer: 200g dark chocolate, 115g butter, 3 eggs, 150g sugar, 50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the mousse: 300g dark chocolate, 500ml cream, 2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chocolate shavings to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the cookie crumbs and butter, press into a springform, and chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the chocolate and butter, whisk in the eggs, sugar, and flour, pour over the crust, and bake at 175C for 25 min until set with a slight wobble. Cool.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the mousse chocolate and cool slightly. Whip the cream with the sugar and fold it in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the mousse thick over the cooled fudge layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 4 hours, then top with chocolate shavings and unmould.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Named for the thick dark mud of the Mississippi River, and the point is the layers of increasing intensity, dense fudge under airy mousse. Let the fudge layer cool fully before the mousse goes on or it will melt into it.","equipment":["Springform"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mississippi%20Mud%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mississippi Mud Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mississippi Mud Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mississippi%20Mud%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"mississippi-comeback-sauce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml chili sauce or ketchup"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the mayonnaise, chili sauce, and oil together until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the Worcestershire, mustard, hot sauce, and grated garlic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the paprika, black pepper, and lemon juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season to taste: it should be tangy, garlicky, and a little spicy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill at least 2 hours so the garlic mellows and the flavors marry. Keeps a week.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jackson's all-purpose sauce, born in the Greek-owned restaurants of the mid-century, good on everything from fried catfish to crackers to salad. The rest in the fridge is essential; freshly made, the raw garlic dominates everything.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mississippi%20Comeback%20Sauce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mississippi Comeback Sauce"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mississippi Comeback Sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mississippi%20Comeback%20Sauce","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Mustard","Mayonnaise","Hot sauce","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"mississippi-delta-hot-tamales","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 700g ground beef or pork, 2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tbsp cumin, 1 tbsp paprika, 1 tsp cayenne, 3 garlic cloves minced, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the masa: 400g masa harina, 120ml lard, 700ml warm broth, 1 tbsp chili powder, 2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Corn husks, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"Simmering spiced broth for cooking"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the raw meat with all the filling spices and garlic; it cooks inside the tamale.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the lard until fluffy, then work in the masa harina, spiced broth, chili powder, and salt to a soft, spreadable dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread masa thinly on each soaked husk, add a line of filling, and roll tight, folding the bottom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand the tamales upright, packed close, in a deep pot and cover with spiced broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer covered 90 min until the masa pulls cleanly from the husk. Serve dripping in the broth.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Delta tamale is simmered in broth rather than steamed, which is what makes it wetter and spicier than its Mexican cousin, a Mississippi tradition since sharecropping days. Spread the masa thin; a thick-walled tamale is stodgy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Working with masa"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","aromatic","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mississippi%20Delta%20Hot%20Tamales%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mississippi Delta Hot Tamales"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mississippi Delta Hot Tamales","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mississippi%20Delta%20Hot%20Tamales","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Cornmeal/Polenta","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa"]},{"slug":"mississippi-fried-catfish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g farm-raised catfish fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g fine yellow cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Peanut oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon, hush puppies, and slaw"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the catfish in the buttermilk and hot sauce 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season the cornmeal with salt, pepper, cayenne, and garlic powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the fillets in seasoned cornmeal, pressing it on, and fry 4-5 min until they float and turn deep gold.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve with lemon, hush puppies, and slaw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mississippi is the catfish-farming capital of America, and the Delta fries it in pure cornmeal, no flour. A candy thermometer earns its keep here: below 180C the crust sponges up oil, above 200C it burns before the fish is done.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mississippi%20Fried%20Catfish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mississippi Fried Catfish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mississippi Fried Catfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mississippi%20Fried%20Catfish","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Buttermilk","Cornmeal/Polenta","Peanut","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"mississippi-roast","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.8kg chuck roast"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 packet ranch seasoning (or 2 tbsp dried herbs, buttermilk powder, garlic)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 packet au jus mix (or 2 tbsp beef base and cornstarch)"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 pepperoncini peppers, plus 60ml of their brine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dust the roast with flour and sear all sides hard in the oil in a Dutch oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the ranch and au jus seasonings over the meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the stick of butter on top and arrange the pepperoncini and their brine around.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and braise at 150C for 4 hours, or in a slow cooker on low 8 hours, until it shreds.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":43200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Shred the meat, stir it through the buttery pepper juices, and serve on rolls or over mashed potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A viral 2010s recipe from Ripley, Mississippi, and genuinely good despite the packet mixes. The pepperoncini brine is the secret: it cuts the butter and richness with just enough tang to keep the whole thing from being heavy.","equipment":["Dutch oven"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Braising","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mississippi%20Roast%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mississippi Roast"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mississippi Roast","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mississippi%20Roast","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["braising","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"mississippi-sweet-potato-pone","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g raw sweet potatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cane syrup or molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Zest of 1 orange"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the raw sweet potatoes on the coarse side of a box grater.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the cane syrup, brown sugar, eggs, milk, melted butter, spices, salt, and orange zest.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the grated sweet potato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered dish and bake at 175C for 55-65 min, stirring once at the halfway mark so it cooks evenly.","durationSec":3900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":true},{"text":"The finished pone is dense, dark, and set. Serve warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Older than sweet potato pie and made from raw grated potato rather than mashed cooked flesh, which gives it a chewy, pudding-dense texture. Stirring once partway through keeps the edges from setting hard before the middle cooks.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mississippi%20Sweet%20Potato%20Pone%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mississippi Sweet Potato Pone"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mississippi Sweet Potato Pone","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mississippi%20Sweet%20Potato%20Pone","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ginger","Potato","Sweet potato","Orange","Butter","Milk","Sugar","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"]},{"slug":"delta-fried-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut in 8"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lard or peanut oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brine the chicken in buttermilk and hot sauce overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the flour heavily with the salt, pepper, paprika, cayenne, and garlic powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge each piece, rest on a rack 15 min so the crust sets, then dredge a second time for extra crunch.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry in 165C fat in a cast-iron skillet, dark meat 15 min and white 13, turning once, to a deep mahogany and 74C internal.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain on a rack and rest 5 min before serving.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"The double dredge is the Delta trick for an armored crust: flour, dip, flour again, and the second coat fries into ridges the first alone can't build. Lard fries the finest chicken there is, and a cast-iron skillet holds heat steady where a thin pan swings and burns; the skillet's thermal mass is the real recipe. Shallow-fry and turn, the old way, rather than deep-fry and forget. Rest on a rack, never paper: the Delta knows a steamed crust is a wasted crust.","equipment":["Skillet","Thermometer","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","aromatic","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Delta%20Fried%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Delta Fried Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Delta Fried Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Delta%20Fried%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Chiles","Buttermilk","Flour","Peanut","Hot sauce","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"comeback-fried-shrimp","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g shrimp, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Cajun seasoning"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Comeback sauce to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the shrimp in buttermilk 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the flour, cornmeal, Cajun seasoning, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the shrimp, shake off the excess, and fry in small batches 90 seconds until gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve with a bowl of comeback sauce for dipping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Gulf shrimp and comeback sauce are a Mississippi marriage. Shrimp fry in barely a minute and a half: pull them the second they turn, because carryover heat finishes them on the plate.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Comeback%20Fried%20Shrimp%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Comeback Fried Shrimp"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Comeback Fried Shrimp","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Comeback%20Fried%20Shrimp","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"arkansas-fried-pies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the pastry: 375g flour, 1 tsp salt, 170g cold butter or shortening, 120ml cold buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 300g dried apricots or peaches, 250ml water, 100g sugar, 1/2 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the dried fruit with the water and sugar until soft and thick, 20 min, then mash and cool.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cut the fat into the flour and salt, bind with buttermilk, and chill 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the pastry thin and cut 12cm rounds. Spoon fruit onto one half, fold over, and crimp the edges hard with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 180C oil 2-3 min a side until deep gold and blistered.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain and dust with icing sugar while warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dried fruit, not fresh, is traditional: it makes a thick filling that will not burst the crust in the fryer. Crimp the edges firmly and fry a test pie first to make sure the seal holds.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Deep frying","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","warm-spiced","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arkansas%20Fried%20Pies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arkansas Fried Pies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arkansas Fried Pies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arkansas%20Fried%20Pies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"arkansas-chocolate-gravy-and-biscuits","ingredients":[{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE GRAVY"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g cocoa powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"section","label":"TO SERVE"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 hot split buttermilk biscuits"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the cocoa, sugar, flour, and salt together in a saucepan while it is still off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slowly whisk in the milk until smooth, then set over medium heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook, whisking constantly, 6-8 min until it thickens to a pourable gravy.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off the heat beat in the butter and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle hot over split biscuits and serve immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"An Ozark breakfast tradition, poured over biscuits like sausage gravy's sweet cousin. Whisk the dry ingredients before the milk goes in: cocoa and flour added to hot liquid clump instantly and never smooth out.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arkansas%20Chocolate%20Gravy%20and%20Biscuits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arkansas Chocolate Gravy and Biscuits"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arkansas Chocolate Gravy and Biscuits","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arkansas%20Chocolate%20Gravy%20and%20Biscuits","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"]},{"slug":"arkansas-rice-and-gravy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 pork chops or 700g cubed beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour, seasoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml beef or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g long-grain rice, cooked"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, and hot sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dredge the meat in seasoned flour and brown hard in the oil; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onions in the same pan until deep gold, scraping up the fond, then add the garlic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle in the remaining seasoned flour and cook to a brown roux.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the stock and thyme, return the meat, cover, and simmer 45 min until tender and the gravy is rich.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season and serve over the cooked rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Arkansas grows nearly half the nation's rice, so rice and gravy is a weeknight birthright. The browned onions and a proper brown roux are what give the gravy its color and depth: pale gravy tastes of flour.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arkansas%20Rice%20and%20Gravy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arkansas Rice and Gravy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arkansas Rice and Gravy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arkansas%20Rice%20and%20Gravy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Thyme","Rice","Flour","Stock/Broth","Hot sauce","Clove"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"arkansas-possum-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the crust: 150g flour, 115g butter, 75g chopped pecans"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the cream layer: 200g cream cheese, 100g icing sugar, 250ml whipped cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the chocolate layer: 500ml milk, 4 yolks, 100g sugar, 40g cornstarch, 100g dark chocolate"},{"kind":"item","text":"Whipped cream and pecans to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the flour, butter, and pecans together, press into a pie plate, and bake at 175C for 20 min. Cool.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the cream cheese and icing sugar smooth, fold in the whipped cream, and spread over the cooled crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a chocolate pudding: heat the milk, whisk yolks with sugar and cornstarch, temper, return, add the chocolate, and cook to a thick boil. Cool slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the chocolate pudding over the cream cheese layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 3 hours, then top with whipped cream and pecans.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true}],"note":"It is called possum pie because it plays possum: the plain-looking top hides the layers beneath. Let the chocolate pudding cool before it goes on the cream cheese or the two layers merge into one.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arkansas%20Possum%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arkansas Possum Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arkansas Possum Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arkansas%20Possum%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Cream","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"arkansas-cheese-dip","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g white American cheese, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 pickled jalapenos, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp chili powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tortilla chips"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the butter in a saucepan and cook the garlic 30 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cheese and milk and melt over low heat, stirring constantly, until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the tomato, jalapenos, cumin, and chili powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep warm over the lowest heat, loosening with a splash of milk if it thickens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve hot with tortilla chips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Arkansas claims to have invented cheese dip in the 1930s, before Texas queso, and holds a competition over it. Low heat and constant stirring keep it silky: a boil makes the cheese seize and turn oily.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arkansas%20Cheese%20Dip%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arkansas Cheese Dip"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arkansas Cheese Dip","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arkansas%20Cheese%20Dip","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Bread","Pickles","Cumin","Clove"]},{"slug":"ozark-blackberry-cobbler","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g blackberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the batter: 115g butter, 190g flour, 200g sugar, 2 tsp baking powder, 1/4 tsp salt, 240ml milk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the blackberries with the sugar, cornstarch, and lemon and let them stand 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter in a baking dish in the oven as it heats to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, sugar, baking powder, salt, and milk into a batter and pour it over the melted butter: do not stir.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the berries and juice over the batter; do not stir.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 45-55 min until the cake has risen around the fruit and browned deeply.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wild blackberries grow thick along Ozark fencerows in July. As with all batter cobblers, the trick is not stirring: the cake floats up through the berries as it bakes, and stirring ruins the effect.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ozark%20Blackberry%20Cobbler%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ozark Blackberry Cobbler"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ozark Blackberry Cobbler","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ozark%20Blackberry%20Cobbler","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Berries","Butter","Flour","Sugar"]},{"slug":"arkansas-fried-chicken-and-waffles","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt, 1 tbsp pepper, 1 tsp cayenne, 1 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the waffles: 250g flour, 2 tsp baking powder, 1 tbsp sugar, 1/2 tsp salt, 2 eggs, 400ml buttermilk, 85g butter melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Maple syrup and hot honey"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brine the chicken in buttermilk and hot sauce 4 hours. Dredge in seasoned flour, rest, and fry at 165C for 14-16 min until deep gold and 74C internal.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the waffle dry ingredients. Separately whisk eggs, buttermilk, and melted butter, then combine lightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the waffles in a hot iron until crisp and golden.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set a piece of fried chicken on each waffle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drizzle with maple syrup and hot honey and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sweet-savory-spicy combination is the whole joy: maple for sweet, hot honey for heat, salt from the crust. Keep the fried chicken on a rack in a warm oven so it stays crisp while the waffles cook.","equipment":["Thermometer","Deep-fry pot","Waffle iron"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arkansas%20Fried%20Chicken%20and%20Waffles%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arkansas Fried Chicken and Waffles"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arkansas Fried Chicken and Waffles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arkansas%20Fried%20Chicken%20and%20Waffles","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Chiles","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Honey","Sugar","Maple syrup","Hot sauce","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"arkansas-smoked-turkey-and-dumplings","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g smoked turkey, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L turkey or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dumplings: 250g flour, 2 tsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 3 tbsp cold butter, 180ml cold buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion, celery, and carrot in the butter, then stir in the flour and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the stock and simmer 15 min, then add the cream, thyme, and shredded turkey.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the cold butter into the flour, baking powder, and salt, then bind with buttermilk to a soft dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop spoonfuls of dough onto the simmering stew, cover tightly, and cook 15 min without lifting the lid.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season, scatter with parsley, and serve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Smoked turkey gives the stew a depth that plain poached bird cannot. Do not lift the lid while the dumplings cook: they steam-rise, and letting the steam out gives you dense, gummy dumplings.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arkansas%20Smoked%20Turkey%20and%20Dumplings%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arkansas Smoked Turkey and Dumplings"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arkansas Smoked Turkey and Dumplings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arkansas%20Smoked%20Turkey%20and%20Dumplings","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Turkey","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Parsley","Thyme","Butter","Cream","Buttermilk","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"kentucky-hot-brown","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 thick slices toasted white bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g roast turkey, sliced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 rashers bacon, cooked crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the Mornay: 55g butter, 55g flour, 500ml milk, 100g Pecorino or Parmesan, 60ml cream, pinch of nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paprika and parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the Mornay: melt the butter, cook in the flour 2 min, whisk in the milk, and simmer to a thick sauce. Off the heat stir in the cheese, cream, and nutmeg.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set two pieces of toast in each gratin dish and pile the turkey on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover the turkey completely with the Mornay: it must be sealed or it will dry out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill under a hot broiler until bubbling and browned in spots.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top each with tomato slices, crisp bacon, paprika, and parsley and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented at the Brown Hotel in Louisville in 1926 as a late-supper dish for dancers. The turkey must be fully blanketed in Mornay before it goes under the grill: any exposed meat dries to leather.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["The broiler: heat from above"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred","umami-deep","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kentucky%20Hot%20Brown%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kentucky Hot Brown"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kentucky Hot Brown","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kentucky%20Hot%20Brown","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Turkey","Tomato","Parsley","Butter","Milk","Cream","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Bread","Flour","Paprika","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above"]},{"slug":"kentucky-burgoo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g mutton or lamb shoulder, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g chicken thighs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork shoulder, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g lima beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cabbage, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cayenne, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer all three meats in the water 2 hours until falling apart. Lift out, shred, discarding bones, and keep the broth.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the broth to the pot with the onions, tomatoes, and potatoes and simmer 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the limas, corn, and cabbage and cook 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the shredded meat, add Worcestershire and vinegar, and simmer 90 min, stirring often, until thick and melded.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season hard with cayenne, salt, and pepper. It should be almost a stew, not a soup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Bluegrass burgoo is a communal pot for festivals and derby day, traditionally with three meats and whatever the garden gives. Long, slow, uncovered cooking is what marries it: a burgoo rushed is just vegetable soup with too much meat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kentucky%20Burgoo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kentucky Burgoo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kentucky Burgoo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kentucky%20Burgoo","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mutton-and-hogget","term":"Mutton & Hogget"},{"slug":"lamb-spring-hogget-and-mutton","term":"Lamb: Spring, Hogget & Mutton"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Lamb","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Chiles","Cabbage","Corn","Vinegar","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"kentucky-derby-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g pecans, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g chocolate chips"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp bourbon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scatter the pecans and chocolate chips in the shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, both sugars, melted butter, flour, bourbon, vanilla, and salt until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the nuts and chocolate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 45-50 min until set at the edges with a slight wobble at the center.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely before slicing so the chocolate re-sets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A chocolate-walnut-bourbon pie tied to the Derby (the trademarked name belongs to one bakery, so everyone else calls it something careful). The flour in the filling keeps it sliceable rather than gooey: do not leave it out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kentucky%20Derby%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kentucky Derby Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kentucky Derby Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kentucky%20Derby%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"beer-cheese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g sharp cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml flat beer, room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dry mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pretzels, celery, and crackers"}],"steps":[{"text":"Let the cheese come to room temperature and grate it fresh.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the cheese, garlic, mustard, Worcestershire, cayenne, and paprika in a food processor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With the motor running, pour in the flat beer a little at a time until it comes together into a smooth, spreadable paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste and adjust the heat and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill at least 2 hours; it firms and the flavors meld. Serve with pretzels and vegetables.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"A snack from the Ohio River towns near Winchester, spread on everything at a Kentucky gathering. Use flat, room-temperature beer: cold beer seizes the cheese, and the fizz makes the paste foamy.","equipment":["Food processor","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","umami-deep","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beer%20Cheese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beer Cheese"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Beer Cheese","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Beer%20Cheese","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"blenders-immersion-high-speed-and-food-processor","term":"Blenders: Immersion, High-Speed & Food Processor"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Celery","Chiles","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"kentucky-spoonbread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"750ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g white cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scald the milk, rain in the cornmeal whisking hard, and cook 5 min to a thick mush.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat in the butter, salt, and sugar, then cool 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the egg yolks one at a time, then the baking powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites to soft peaks and fold through in two additions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered dish and bake at 190C for 35-40 min until puffed and golden. Serve at once with a spoon.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kentucky claims spoonbread as hard as Virginia does, and Berea holds a festival for it. It is a savory cornmeal souffle and it falls fast: get it to the table straight from the oven.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kentucky%20Spoonbread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kentucky Spoonbread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kentucky Spoonbread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kentucky%20Spoonbread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"benedictine-spread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber, peeled, seeded, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cream cheese, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp grated onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"A drop of green food coloring (traditional, optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"White bread, crusts off"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the cucumber and onion, then squeeze both hard in a cloth to remove all their water: this is the crucial step.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the cream cheese smooth with the mayonnaise and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the drained cucumber and onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with salt and white pepper, and tint the palest green if you want the traditional look.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill an hour. Spread on crustless white bread for tea sandwiches, or serve as a dip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Created by Louisville caterer Jennie Benedict around 1900. Wringing every drop of water from the cucumber is the difference between a spread and a puddle: a watery benedictine soaks the bread to mush within minutes.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Benedictine%20Spread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Benedictine Spread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Benedictine Spread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Benedictine%20Spread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Cucumber","Lemon","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Bread","Mayonnaise"]},{"slug":"kentucky-bourbon-balls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g vanilla wafers or pecans, ground fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp bourbon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g chopped pecans"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g dark chocolate for dipping"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pecan halves to top"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the butter with the icing sugar, bourbon, and corn syrup until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the ground wafers and chopped pecans to a stiff, moldable paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into small balls and chill on a tray until firm, 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the chocolate and dip each ball, letting the excess drip off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set on parchment, press a pecan half on top, and let the chocolate firm up. They improve after a day.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Derby-season confection and a bourbon-country gift. Chill the centers hard before dipping or they fall apart in the warm chocolate, and let them rest a day so the raw bourbon mellows into the pecan.","equipment":["Parchment","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kentucky%20Bourbon%20Balls%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kentucky Bourbon Balls"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kentucky Bourbon Balls","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kentucky%20Bourbon%20Balls","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"fried-chicken-kentucky-skillet","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut in 8"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sage"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lard for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brine the chicken in buttermilk overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the flour with the salt, pepper, paprika, cayenne, and sage.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge each piece and rest on a rack 15 min so the coating sets.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt lard in a cast-iron skillet to a depth of 2cm and heat to 165C. Fry covered for the first half so the meat steams through, then uncovered to crisp, turning once, 15-18 min total.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack and rest 5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"Skillet-fried and lard-cooked is the old Kentucky way, older than any bucket. Covering the pan for the first half steams the meat tender before the crust locks; uncovering finishes the crackle.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fried%20Chicken%2C%20Kentucky%20Skillet%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fried Chicken, Kentucky Skillet"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Fried Chicken, Kentucky Skillet","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Fried%20Chicken%2C%20Kentucky%20Skillet","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Chiles","Sage","Buttermilk","Flour","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"mississippi-fried-green-tomatoes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 firm green tomatoes, sliced 1cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"75g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Comeback sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the tomato slices and drain on paper 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Set up buttermilk in one dish and the cornmeal, flour, salt, pepper, and cayenne in another.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip each slice in buttermilk, then dredge in the cornmeal mix.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 5mm oil over medium-high 2-3 min a side until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve hot with comeback sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Firm green tomatoes hold up to the fryer where a ripe one turns to mush: the greenness is structural, not just tart, and the fruit should be sliced thick enough to stay juicy inside the crust. Comeback sauce is the Mississippi partner: sharp and garlicky against the tart fruit, the state's own Thousand Island ancestor born in Jackson's Greek-immigrant restaurants. Cornmeal in the dredge for crunch, a preliminary salting to pull moisture, and a hot skillet so nothing steams.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mississippi%20Fried%20Green%20Tomatoes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mississippi Fried Green Tomatoes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mississippi Fried Green Tomatoes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mississippi%20Fried%20Green%20Tomatoes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Chiles","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"mississippi-pecan-pralines","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g pecan halves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a sheet of parchment on a tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine both sugars, the cream, butter, and salt and cook to 116C on a candy thermometer, stirring.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat stir in the vanilla and pecans and beat until the mixture starts to lose its shine and thicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop tablespoons quickly onto the parchment before it sets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool until firm. They should be creamy, not grainy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beating the candy off the heat is what turns it from a shiny syrup into a creamy praline. Stop dropping the moment it thickens: it seizes fast, and a praline poured too late is a lump.","equipment":["Thermometer","Parchment","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mississippi%20Pecan%20Pralines%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mississippi Pecan Pralines"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mississippi Pecan Pralines","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mississippi%20Pecan%20Pralines","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"arkansas-fried-chicken-livers","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g chicken livers, trimmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Comeback or hot sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the livers in buttermilk and hot sauce 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season the flour with the salt, pepper, cayenne, and garlic powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the livers, shake off the excess, and fry carefully 3-4 min until deep gold and just cooked through: they spit, so use a screen.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve with comeback sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Southern snack and diner staple: livers by the basket with hot sauce and white bread, the gas-station delicacy Arkansas never felt the need to apologize for. Do not overcook them: livers turn chalky in seconds past done, and they should stay just pink and creamy at the center, which means a hot fry and a short one. Dredge them wet from buttermilk and stand back: livers pop in the oil like they hold a grudge. A squeeze of lemon or a shake of hot sauce finishes the argument.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arkansas%20Fried%20Chicken%20Livers%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arkansas Fried Chicken Livers"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arkansas Fried Chicken Livers","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arkansas%20Fried%20Chicken%20Livers","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Chiles","Buttermilk","Flour","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"ozark-skillet-cornbread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g yellow cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp bacon fat or butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Put the bacon fat in a cast-iron skillet and heat it in a 220C oven until smoking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the cornmeal, flour, baking powder, soda, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs and buttermilk quickly to a loose batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the batter into the screaming-hot fat, it should sizzle hard, and return to the oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 20-25 min until the top is gold and the edges are crisp. Turn out and serve hot with butter.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"The smoking-hot skillet is the whole secret to the crackling crust. Ozark cornbread carries no sugar: sweet cornbread is a Northern habit that starts arguments below the Mason-Dixon line.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ozark%20Skillet%20Cornbread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ozark Skillet Cornbread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ozark Skillet Cornbread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ozark%20Skillet%20Cornbread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"]},{"slug":"kentucky-transparent-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the sugar and melted butter together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs one at a time, then the cream, flour, vanilla, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 40-50 min until the top is golden and just set with a slight wobble.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely; it firms as it cools into a translucent custard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Maysville specialty and cousin to chess pie, but simpler and clearer: no cornmeal, no vinegar, just a translucent butter custard. It should set to a shimmer, so pull it while the center still moves.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","silky","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kentucky%20Transparent%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kentucky Transparent Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kentucky Transparent Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kentucky%20Transparent%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"kentucky-beer-cheese-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml vegetable or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"330ml lager"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sharp cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cayenne, salt, and white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Popcorn or croutons to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot, and celery in the butter until soft, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the flour and cook 2 min to a blond roux.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the stock and beer and simmer 10 min, then add the milk.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, add the cheese in handfuls, stirring until each melts before adding more: never let it boil after the cheese goes in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with mustard, Worcestershire, cayenne, salt, and pepper. Top each bowl with popcorn, the Kentucky touch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The soup form of Kentucky's beloved beer cheese. As with any cheese soup, the pot comes off the heat before the cheese goes in: a boil turns it grainy and greasy. Popcorn on top is not a joke; it is tradition.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kentucky%20Beer%20Cheese%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kentucky Beer Cheese Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kentucky Beer Cheese Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kentucky%20Beer%20Cheese%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Chiles","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Bread","Flour","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"cincinnati-chili","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, 1 grated and 1 diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp chili powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cocoa powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g spaghetti"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g cheddar, grated fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oyster crackers and kidney beans"}],"steps":[{"text":"Break the raw beef into the water in a cold pot and mash it very fine: never brown it first; the boiling is what gives the smooth texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the grated onion, all the spices, cloves, bay, vinegar, and tomato paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered 1.5-2 hours, stirring, until dark and slightly thickened but still loose.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the spaghetti and lay it on plates.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle the chili over for a two-way; add beans and onions for a three or four-way, and always bury it under a mound of fine-grated cheddar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Greek-Macedonian immigrants built this in 1922 and it is a spiced meat sauce, not a Texas chili: the cocoa, cinnamon, and clove are the signature. Simmering raw beef in water is the trick to its fine, saucelike grind.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","gently hot","umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cincinnati%20Chili%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cincinnati Chili"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cincinnati Chili","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cincinnati%20Chili","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Liver & Offal","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Bay leaf","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Pasta","Kidney/Pinto beans","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Tomato paste","Chocolate/Cocoa","Cumin","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"ohio-goetta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g steel-cut oats (pinhead)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L water or stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sage"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the oats in the water with the bay, onions, and seasonings 1 hour until thick and creamy.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the raw ground meats, breaking them up, and cook 1 more hour, stirring often, until very thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season boldly: it dulls once cold. Pack into loaf pans and chill overnight until solid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice 1cm thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in butter over medium heat 5-6 min a side without moving it, until a deep crust forms.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cincinnati's German answer to scrapple, bulked with oats instead of cornmeal. As with scrapple, the one rule is patience in the pan: leave the slice alone until the crust sets or it crumbles.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ohio%20Goetta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ohio Goetta"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ohio Goetta","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ohio%20Goetta","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"carbon-steel-pans","term":"Carbon Steel Pans"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Onion","Thyme","Sage","Bay leaf","Butter","Oats","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"buckeye-candy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g peanut butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g dark chocolate"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp shortening"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the peanut butter, butter, vanilla, and salt together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the icing sugar until stiff enough to roll into balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into balls and chill on a tray 30 min until firm.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the chocolate with the shortening. Spear each ball on a toothpick and dip most of the way, leaving a circle of peanut butter showing at the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set on parchment and smooth the toothpick hole. Chill to firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"They are made to look like the nut of the Ohio buckeye tree, hence the ring of exposed peanut butter left at the top. Keep the centers cold before dipping or they slump in the warm chocolate.","equipment":["Parchment","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Buckeye%20Candy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Buckeye Candy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Buckeye Candy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Buckeye%20Candy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Peanut","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"ohio-sauerkraut-balls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g sauerkraut, drained and squeezed dry, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g bulk sausage"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g cream cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp caraway"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"For coating: 100g flour, 2 eggs beaten, 150g breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the sausage with the onion, then stir in the flour and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chopped kraut, cream cheese, mustard, and caraway and cook until it binds into a thick paste. Cool and chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into walnut-sized balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge in flour, then egg, then breadcrumbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 180C for 3-4 min until deep gold. Drain and serve hot with mustard.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"An Akron party staple with roots in the German communities. Squeeze the kraut bone-dry before it goes in: any water left in it makes the mixture too loose to roll and the balls burst in the oil.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Salting to draw the water out","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","tangy-ferment","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, ferment tang with real depth behind it, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ohio%20Sauerkraut%20Balls%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ohio Sauerkraut Balls"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ohio Sauerkraut Balls","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ohio%20Sauerkraut%20Balls","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Eggs","Onion","Cabbage","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Mustard","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"ohio-corn-and-bacon-fritters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 ears corn, kernels cut off"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g bacon, cooked and crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 scallions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt, and sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs and milk to a thick batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the corn, bacon, and scallions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop heaped spoonfuls into 180C oil and fry 3-4 min, turning, until deep gold.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and salt at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A late-summer supper from the corn belt. Keep the batter thick enough that a spoonful holds its shape when it hits the oil: a thin batter spreads and the fritters fry greasy and flat.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ohio%20Corn%20and%20Bacon%20Fritters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ohio Corn and Bacon Fritters"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ohio Corn and Bacon Fritters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ohio%20Corn%20and%20Bacon%20Fritters","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Scallion","Corn","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"ohio-apple-dumplings","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 small tart apples, peeled and cored"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry for a double batch, rolled thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 100g brown sugar, 55g butter, 1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the syrup: 400g sugar, 500ml water, 55g butter, 1 tsp cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the pastry into six squares large enough to wrap an apple.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set an apple on each square, pack the cavity with brown sugar, butter, and cinnamon, and draw the pastry up to seal at the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange in a buttered baking dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the syrup ingredients together 5 min and pour around the dumplings.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 40-45 min, basting once with the syrup, until the pastry is gold and the apples are tender. Serve warm with cream.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true}],"note":"An Amish-country dessert where the apple bakes whole inside its pastry shell. Baste once with the syrup partway through: it glazes the pastry and keeps the tops from drying before the apples soften.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ohio%20Apple%20Dumplings%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ohio Apple Dumplings"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ohio Apple Dumplings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ohio%20Apple%20Dumplings","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Apple","Butter","Bread","Sugar","Cinnamon"]},{"slug":"cleveland-polish-boy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 kielbasa sausages"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 long soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the fries: 500g cut fries, fried crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the slaw: 300g cabbage, 4 tbsp mayonnaise, 1 tbsp vinegar, 1 tsp sugar, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml barbecue sauce, warmed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grill or fry the kielbasa until browned and blistered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the potatoes until deeply crisp and salt them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress the cabbage with mayonnaise, vinegar, sugar, and salt for a quick slaw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set a sausage in each bun, then pile the fries directly on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Douse with warm barbecue sauce and crown with slaw. Eat with many napkins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Cleveland street-food monument: sausage, fries, slaw, and barbecue sauce all in one bun. The fries go inside, on top of the sausage, and the whole thing is meant to be structurally unsound.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cleveland%20Polish%20Boy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cleveland Polish Boy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cleveland Polish Boy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cleveland%20Polish%20Boy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Cabbage","Bread","Vinegar","Mayonnaise","Sugar"]},{"slug":"ohio-buckeye-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs, bone-in"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml apple cider"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season and brown the chicken thighs skin-side down until deeply gold; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic in the fat until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the stock, cider, vinegar, brown sugar, mustard, and thyme and scrape up the fond.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the chicken skin-side up, cover, and simmer 30 min. Uncover and reduce the sauce 10 min more until glossy.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season and serve with the pan sauce over mashed potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A cider-braised farmhouse chicken from Ohio's orchard country. Uncovering it at the end to reduce the sauce is what turns thin braising liquid into a glaze worth the potatoes underneath.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","bright","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ohio%20Buckeye%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ohio Buckeye Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ohio Buckeye Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ohio%20Buckeye%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Apple","Thyme","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"cincinnati-opera-cream-candy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g dark chocolate for dipping"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a marble slab or sheet pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar, cream, corn syrup, butter, and salt to 114C, stirring gently.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour onto the slab and let it cool to lukewarm without stirring.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work it with a spatula, folding and turning, until it turns creamy, opaque, and thick, 10-15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the vanilla, form into small logs or squares, let them dry an hour, then dip in melted chocolate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Cincinnati confectionery tradition, softer and creamier than fudge. The patience is in the cooling: stir it hot and it turns grainy; let it drop to lukewarm before you work it and it turns silk.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cincinnati%20Opera%20Cream%20Candy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cincinnati Opera Cream Candy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cincinnati Opera Cream Candy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cincinnati%20Opera%20Cream%20Candy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Cream","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"indiana-breaded-pork-tenderloin","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 pork loin cutlets, pounded to 5mm and wider than the bun"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs beaten with 60ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cracker crumbs or panko"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, 1/2 tsp pepper, 1/2 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mustard, pickle, onion, lettuce, tomato"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the cutlets between plastic to a thin, wide oval: they should overhang the bun by inches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set up flour, the egg wash, and the seasoned crumbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge each cutlet in flour, then egg, then crumbs, pressing them on firmly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 5mm of 180C oil 2-3 min a side until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Set on a bun that it dwarfs and dress lightly with mustard, pickle, and onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The tenderloin must be pounded thin and fried wide enough to embarrass the bun: the comic overhang is the whole Indiana identity. Pound it between plastic wrap so it stretches instead of tearing.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indiana%20Breaded%20Pork%20Tenderloin%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Indiana Breaded Pork Tenderloin"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Indiana Breaded Pork Tenderloin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Indiana%20Breaded%20Pork%20Tenderloin","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tenderloin-filet-mignon","term":"Tenderloin / Filet Mignon"},{"slug":"pork-loin-vs-tenderloin","term":"Pork Loin vs. Tenderloin"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Lettuce/Greens","Milk","Bread","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"indiana-sugar-cream-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 blind-baked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nutmeg and cinnamon to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the brown sugar, cornstarch, and salt in a saucepan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slowly whisk in the cream until smooth, then set over medium heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook, whisking constantly, until it thickens and bubbles, 8-10 min. Off the heat beat in the butter and vanilla.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the baked shell and dust the top with nutmeg and cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 10 min just to set the top, then cool and chill 3 hours.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":11400,"estimated":false}],"note":"The unofficial state pie, also called Hoosier pie: a custard with no eggs, thickened by cornstarch alone. Whisk without stopping while it cooks; the moment you pause, the bottom scorches and the whole pie tastes of it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indiana%20Sugar%20Cream%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Indiana Sugar Cream Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Indiana Sugar Cream Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Indiana%20Sugar%20Cream%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking"]},{"slug":"indiana-persimmon-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g wild persimmon pulp"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"125g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Whipped cream to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Push very ripe persimmons through a sieve for smooth pulp: under-ripe fruit is unbearably astringent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the pulp with the sugar and eggs, then the buttermilk and melted butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, soda, baking powder, cinnamon, and salt and stir in, then thin with the milk to a loose batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered dish and bake at 175C for 55-65 min until set and deeply browned, the edges pulling from the sides.","durationSec":3900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool and serve in squares with whipped cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Southern Indiana holds a persimmon festival in Mitchell every autumn. Only fully ripe, jelly-soft native persimmons will do; an under-ripe one turns the whole pudding mouth-puckeringly astringent.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indiana%20Persimmon%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Indiana Persimmon Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Indiana Persimmon Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Indiana%20Persimmon%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"hoosier-fried-biscuits-with-apple-butter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Apple butter to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, yeast, sugar, and salt, then add the milk and melted butter and knead to a soft dough. Rise 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 1cm thick and cut into squares or rounds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the biscuits 2-3 min, turning, until puffed and deep gold.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain and serve hot with apple butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Brown County tradition, served by the basket at family-style restaurants. Fry them the moment they are cut: a fried biscuit left to over-proof deflates in the oil instead of puffing.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hoosier%20Fried%20Biscuits%20with%20Apple%20Butter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hoosier Fried Biscuits with Apple Butter"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Hoosier Fried Biscuits with Apple Butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Hoosier%20Fried%20Biscuits%20with%20Apple%20Butter","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Apple","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"indiana-corn-and-sausage-skillet","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g smoked sausage, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 ears corn, kernels cut off"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 potatoes, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the sausage in a large skillet; lift out and reserve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the diced potato in the rendered fat until tender and browned, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion and pepper and cook 5 min, then the corn and cook 3 min over high heat.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the sausage, add the cream and thyme, and simmer 3 min until it thickens slightly.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season and finish with parsley. Serve straight from the skillet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A one-pan farm supper built on the corn belt's summer glut: the kind of dish that exists because the field is fifty feet from the kitchen and the sausage came from the neighbor. Cook the corn hard and briefly so it keeps its snap; simmered corn goes starchy and tired, and the skillet's whole argument is fresh sweetness against smoked pork. Brown the sausage first and let its fat do the frying. Salt at the end: the sausage usually got there first.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indiana%20Corn%20and%20Sausage%20Skillet%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Indiana Corn and Sausage Skillet"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Indiana Corn and Sausage Skillet","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Indiana%20Corn%20and%20Sausage%20Skillet","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Onion","Potato","Bell pepper","Corn","Parsley","Thyme","Butter","Cream"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"indiana-fried-chicken-and-noodles","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the noodles: 250g flour, 3 egg yolks, 1 egg, 1/2 tsp salt, 3 tbsp broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mashed potatoes to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the noodles: mound the flour, add the eggs and salt, and work to a stiff dough with a little broth. Rest 30 min, roll very thin, cut into ribbons, and dry 20 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the chicken in the oil, then set aside and sweat the vegetables in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the stock, return the chicken, and simmer 30 min. Pull the chicken, shred it, and return the meat.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the noodles into the simmering broth and cook 12-15 min until tender and the broth thickens.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season and serve: traditionally ladled over mashed potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chicken and noodles over mashed potatoes is a Hoosier comfort classic, carbohydrate on carbohydrate without apology. Homemade egg noodles thicken the broth as they cook: that starch is the point.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fresh pasta","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indiana%20Fried%20Chicken%20and%20Noodles%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Indiana Fried Chicken and Noodles"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Indiana Fried Chicken and Noodles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Indiana%20Fried%20Chicken%20and%20Noodles","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Parsley","Noodles","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"indiana-sugar-cream-bars","ingredients":[{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE CRUST"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"170g butter"},{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE FILLING"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nutmeg to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the flour, sugar, and butter to crumbs, press into a lined pan, and bake at 175C for 18 min until pale gold.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the brown sugar and cornstarch in a pan, slowly add the cream, and cook stirring until thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat beat in the butter and vanilla and pour over the crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust with nutmeg and chill until set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into bars.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sugar cream pie in a hand-held form, Indiana's unofficial state pie (Hoosier pie, the desperation pie made when the larder held only sugar, cream and flour) translated for the potluck table. As with the pie, whisk the filling without stopping: the cornstarch catches and scorches the instant you turn your back, and a scorched sugar cream cannot be rescued by anything. Nutmeg on top is the traditional finish. Chill fully before cutting or the bars surrender their shape.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indiana%20Sugar%20Cream%20Bars%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Indiana Sugar Cream Bars"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Indiana Sugar Cream Bars","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Indiana%20Sugar%20Cream%20Bars","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"chicago-deep-dish-pizza","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 400g flour, 60g cornmeal, 7g yeast, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm water, 60ml oil, 3 tbsp melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"450g low-moisture mozzarella, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed San Marzano tomatoes, seasoned with garlic, oregano, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g Parmesan"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the dough with the cornmeal and butter, knead briefly, and rise 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the dough up the sides of an oiled deep 23cm cake pan to form a tall crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the mozzarella slices directly on the dough first: this protects it from the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add any fillings, then top with the crushed tomatoes and a blanket of Parmesan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 30-35 min until the crust is deep gold and the sauce bubbles. Rest 10 min before cutting.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true}],"note":"The order is upside down for a reason: cheese on the bottom keeps the long bake from turning the crust soggy, and the chunky tomato on top does not overcook. Rest it before cutting or the whole thing lands in your lap.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicago%20Deep-Dish%20Pizza%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicago Deep-Dish Pizza"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Chicago Deep-Dish Pizza","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Chicago%20Deep-Dish%20Pizza","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Oregano","Butter","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Mozzarella","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"chicago-italian-beef","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg beef top round or sirloin roast"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Italian seasoning"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"For giardiniera: pickled peppers, celery, carrot, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Long Italian rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sweet peppers, sauteed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the roast with the seasonings and roast at 160C to 55C internal for rare, about 90 min. Chill completely: this is essential for thin slicing.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the cold beef as thin as humanly possible on a slicer or with a very sharp knife.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the stock with the roasting juices and any leftover seasoning to make the jus.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the beef slices in the jus just until heated through: never simmer them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile into rolls and top with giardiniera or sweet peppers. For 'wet', dip the whole roll in jus.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chilling the roast before slicing is non-negotiable: warm beef tears instead of shaving thin. Order it dipped, wet, or dry, hot with giardiniera or sweet with peppers, but the beef only warms in the jus; boiling it makes it grey and tough.","equipment":["Thermometer"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicago%20Italian%20Beef%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicago Italian Beef"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Chicago Italian Beef","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Chicago%20Italian%20Beef","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-essential-smallwares","term":"The Essential Smallwares"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Carrot","Celery","Bread","Stock/Broth","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"chicago-style-hot-dog","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 all-beef natural-casing hot dogs"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 poppy seed buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yellow mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sweet green pickle relish (neon, ideally)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 dill pickle spears"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 sport peppers"},{"kind":"item","text":"Celery salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steam or simmer the hot dogs until plump and hot, never grilled, by tradition.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam the poppy seed buns until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the dog in the bun and add, in order: mustard, relish, onion, tomato wedges along one side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tuck in a pickle spear and two sport peppers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with a dash of celery salt. And never, under any circumstances, ketchup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dragged through the garden, as they say: the whole point is the parade of toppings. The one iron law of Chicago is no ketchup on a hot dog; the celery salt at the end is what ties the garden together.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicago-Style%20Hot%20Dog%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicago-Style Hot Dog"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Chicago-Style Hot Dog","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Chicago-Style%20Hot%20Dog","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Celery","Dill","Bread","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"illinois-horseshoe-sandwich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick slices Texas toast"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 hamburger patties or fried pork cutlets"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g thick-cut fries, fried crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the cheese sauce: 55g butter, 55g flour, 500ml milk, 300g sharp cheddar, 1 tsp mustard, 1 tsp Worcestershire, dash of beer, cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Paprika"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the cheese sauce: cook a roux with the butter and flour, whisk in the milk, and simmer to a thick sauce. Off the heat stir in the cheddar, mustard, Worcestershire, beer, and cayenne.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the bread and lay it in a shallow dish or plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the cooked meat patties on the toast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the crisp fries on top of the meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smother the whole thing in the hot cheese sauce and dust with paprika. Eat with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Springfield open-faced monster: the name comes from the U-shape the meat once made on the plate. The cheese sauce must be smooth and pourable; keep it off a hard boil once the cheese goes in or it turns grainy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Illinois%20Horseshoe%20Sandwich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Illinois Horseshoe Sandwich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Illinois Horseshoe Sandwich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Illinois%20Horseshoe%20Sandwich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Chiles","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux"]},{"slug":"illinois-corn-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 ears corn, kernels and scraped milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nutmeg"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the kernels from the cobs, then scrape the cobs hard for the starchy milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, milk, cream, melted butter, flour, sugar, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the corn and scrapings and pour into a buttered dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate nutmeg over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 45-55 min until set at the edges with a slight wobble at the center.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":false}],"note":"Prairie-state corn at its most comforting. Scraping the cobs for their milk is what sets the pudding: that starchy liquid is nature's cornstarch, and skipping it leaves you with corn soup.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Illinois%20Corn%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Illinois Corn Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Illinois Corn Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Illinois%20Corn%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Corn","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"chicago-jibarito","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 large green plantains"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g skirt steak, thin-sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, mashed with 2 tbsp oil and salt (mojo)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices American cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the plantains and cut each lengthwise into two flat slabs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the slabs in 170C oil 3 min until soft but pale; flatten each between two boards, then fry again until crisp and golden. These are the 'bread'.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season and quickly sear the skirt steak with the garlic oil until browned.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush one plantain slab with the garlic mojo, then layer steak, cheese, lettuce, tomato, and mayonnaise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with a second plantain slab, press, and cut in half.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Puerto Rican Chicago invention using fried flattened plantains in place of bread. Fry the plantains twice (once to soften, once to crisp) and brush the mojo on while they are hot so it soaks in.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicago%20Jibarito%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicago Jibarito"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Chicago Jibarito","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Chicago%20Jibarito","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Tomato","Lettuce/Greens","Plantain","Cheese (any)","Mayonnaise","Clove"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"illinois-pumpkin-bars","ingredients":[{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE BARS"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"240ml oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g pumpkin puree"},{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE FROSTING"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cream cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, cinnamon, ginger, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs, sugar, oil, and pumpkin until smooth, then fold in the dry ingredients.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread in a lined sheet pan and bake at 175C for 25-30 min until a skewer comes clean. Cool completely.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the cream cheese, butter, icing sugar, and vanilla into a smooth frosting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread over the cooled bars and cut into squares.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Illinois grows more pumpkins than any state, nearly all of it for canning near Morton. These sheet-pan bars are the church-supper staple. Cool them completely before frosting or the cream cheese slides right off.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","floral","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Illinois%20Pumpkin%20Bars%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Illinois Pumpkin Bars"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Illinois Pumpkin Bars","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Illinois%20Pumpkin%20Bars","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ginger","Squash/Pumpkin","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"detroit-style-pizza","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 400g bread flour, 300ml warm water, 7g yeast, 1 tsp salt, 2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g brick cheese or Monterey Jack, cubed, spread to the edges"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g crushed tomatoes, seasoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pepperoni (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oregano and Parmesan"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the wet dough, knead briefly, and rise 1 hour. Press it into a well-oiled rectangular steel pan and rise 1 hour more until it fills the pan.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the pepperoni on the dough if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the cheese cubes right to the edges of the pan: the cheese caramelizing against the metal is the whole point.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the sauce over in three racing stripes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 260C for 15 min until the cheese edges are deep brown and lacy. Run a knife around and lift out.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born in Detroit auto-parts pans in the 1940s. The cheese must reach the pan wall to form the crunchy caramelized 'frico' edge, and the sauce goes on top in stripes, added last so it stays bright.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Detroit-Style%20Pizza%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Detroit-Style Pizza"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Detroit-Style Pizza","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Detroit-Style%20Pizza","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"michigan-pasty","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the pastry: 375g flour, 1 tsp salt, 170g lard or suet, 120ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g beef skirt or chuck, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 potatoes, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 rutabaga, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and plenty of black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the fat into the flour and salt, bind with cold water, and rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the raw beef, potato, rutabaga, and onion with lots of salt and pepper: the filling cooks inside the pastry, so season it hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the pastry into rounds, mound filling on one half, dot with butter, fold over, and crimp the edge into a rope seal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut a vent, brush with egg, and set on a tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 20 min, then 175C for 40 min until deep gold. Cool 10 min: the filling is molten.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cornish miners brought the pasty to the Upper Peninsula's copper mines and it became Yooper soul food. Everything goes in raw, so dice it small and season it hard; the crimped rope seal was once the miner's handle, held and discarded.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Michigan%20Pasty%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Michigan Pasty"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Michigan Pasty","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Michigan%20Pasty","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Butter","Flour"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine"]},{"slug":"michigan-cherry-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry for a double-crust 23cm pie"},{"kind":"item","text":"900g tart Montmorency cherries, pitted"},{"kind":"item","text":"180g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp almond extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the cherries with the sugar, cornstarch, lemon, almond extract, and salt and let stand 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line the pie plate with pastry and fill with the cherries. Dot with butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay a lattice top, brush with egg, and dust with sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 20 min, then 190C for 35-40 min until the juices bubble thickly through the lattice.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool at least 4 hours so the filling sets.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Michigan grows most of America's tart cherries around Traverse City. Tart Montmorencies, not sweet, make a real cherry pie, and the drop of almond extract amplifies the cherry flavor, a trick worth knowing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Michigan%20Cherry%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Michigan Cherry Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Michigan Cherry Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Michigan%20Cherry%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Stone fruit","Butter","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine"]},{"slug":"michigan-coney-dog","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 natural-casing beef hot dogs"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: 450g ground beef, 1 onion grated, 2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tbsp cumin, 1 tbsp paprika, 1 tbsp Worcestershire, 2 tbsp tomato paste, 250ml beef stock, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yellow mustard and diced onion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the raw ground beef in the stock with the grated onion, spices, Worcestershire, and tomato paste 45 min until fine-grained and loose: never brown it first.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill or steam the hot dogs and warm the buns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set a dog in each bun and stripe with mustard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle the coney sauce over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with diced raw onion and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Detroit and Flint argue endlessly over whose coney is right, but both agree the sauce is a loose, fine-ground meat sauce simmered from raw, not a chunky chili. The onion and mustard go on last.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","fiery","earthy","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, real fire that builds and stays, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Michigan%20Coney%20Dog%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Michigan Coney Dog"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Michigan Coney Dog","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Michigan%20Coney%20Dog","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Bread","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Mustard","Cumin","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"michigan-booyah","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut up"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef shank"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork shoulder, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corn"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g green beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chicken, beef, and pork in the water with the bay 2.5 hours until all fall apart. Lift out, shred, discard bones, and keep the broth.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the broth to the pot with the onions, carrots, celery, and potatoes and simmer 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, peas, corn, and green beans and cook 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the shredded meat and simmer 1 hour more until everything melds.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season well. It should be a thick, rich meal in a bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Belgian-American three-meat stew of the Upper Midwest, cooked in vast kettles for church and firehouse fundraisers. The name is thought to come from 'bouillon'. It is meant to cook all day: the long simmer is the flavor.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Michigan%20Booyah%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Michigan Booyah"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Michigan Booyah","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Michigan%20Booyah","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Pork","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Corn","Peas","Green beans","Bay leaf"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"mackinac-island-fudge","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g dark chocolate, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a marble slab or a shallow pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar, cream, milk, corn syrup, and butter to 114C, stirring gently.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat add the chocolate, vanilla, and salt but do not stir: let it cool to lukewarm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat hard with a wooden spoon until it thickens, loses its shine, and holds its shape.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour onto the slab, spread, and score into squares before it sets fully.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mackinac Island shops still pour and paddle fudge on marble slabs in the window. The rule is the same as any fudge: cool it undisturbed to lukewarm before beating, or you fold in sugar crystals and it turns grainy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mackinac%20Island%20Fudge%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mackinac Island Fudge"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mackinac Island Fudge","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mackinac%20Island%20Fudge","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"michigan-wild-blueberry-buckle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g wild blueberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the crumb: 100g sugar, 75g flour, 1 tsp cinnamon, 55g cold butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar, beat in the egg, then alternate the dry ingredients with the milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the blueberries and spread in a buttered 23cm pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the crumb ingredients to pebbles and scatter over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C for 45-50 min until a skewer comes clean.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool 20 min and cut in the pan.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Michigan grows more blueberries than any state. Wild or small berries beat the big cultivated ones here (less water, more flavor), and tossing them in a spoonful of the flour keeps them from sinking.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Rubbing fat into flour","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Michigan%20Wild%20Blueberry%20Buckle%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Michigan Wild Blueberry Buckle"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Michigan Wild Blueberry Buckle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Michigan%20Wild%20Blueberry%20Buckle","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Berries","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"michigan-baked-whitefish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 Great Lakes whitefish fillets, about 180g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cracker crumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the fillets dry, season, and lay skin-side down in a buttered dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the cracker crumbs with the melted butter, parsley, and paprika.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze lemon over the fish and pack the crumb topping on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 12-15 min until the crumbs are gold and the fish flakes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve at once with lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lake whitefish is the sweet, delicate signature of the Great Lakes. Buttered cracker crumbs are the traditional coat and they beat breadcrumbs. Do not overcook the fish, which flakes and dries within a minute of doneness.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Michigan%20Baked%20Whitefish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Michigan Baked Whitefish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Michigan Baked Whitefish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Michigan%20Baked%20Whitefish","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Paprika"]},{"slug":"ohio-city-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g pork shoulder and 300g veal, in 3cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"Wooden skewers"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, 1/2 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml chicken stock"}],"steps":[{"text":"Thread the pork and veal cubes onto short skewers to resemble a small drumstick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge each skewer in flour, then egg, then breadcrumbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown all sides in the oil in an ovenproof skillet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the stock, cover, and braise at 175C for 30 min until tender.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Uncover for the last 10 min to re-crisp the coating. Serve with the pan juices.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Depression-era dish from when chicken cost more than pork: the skewers were shaped to mimic drumsticks, breaded and baked until the disguise half-worked; there is no chicken in it at all, which is the whole charming joke, and Cleveland and Pittsburgh still buy pork pre-cubed for it under the name. Brown the skewers first, then finish covered so the pork stays juicy inside the crust. It survives as pure nostalgia food: proof that thrift, given enough decades, becomes heritage.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Braising","Searing: the hard crust","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ohio%20City-Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ohio City-Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Ohio City-Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Ohio%20City-Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Eggs","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["braising","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"indiana-fried-catfish-and-cornbread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g catfish fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt, 1 tsp pepper, 1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For cornbread: 250g cornmeal, 60g flour, 1 tbsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 2 eggs, 400ml buttermilk, 3 tbsp bacon fat"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the bacon fat in a cast-iron skillet at 220C, mix the cornbread batter, pour it in, and bake 20-25 min.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Soak the catfish in buttermilk 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season the cornmeal and flour with salt, pepper, and cayenne.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the fillets and fry in 190C oil 4-5 min until deep gold and floating.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve with the cornbread and hot sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"River catfish and skillet cornbread are Ohio Valley staples: the Friday fish fry is civic infrastructure across southern Indiana, church halls and firehouses running on this exact plate. Pure cornmeal on the fish fries up sandiest and crispest; the spoon of flour just helps it cling. Soak the fillets in buttermilk if the fish ran muddy. The cornbread bakes in the same skillet tradition, and the two share a plate because they always have.","equipment":["Skillet","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indiana%20Fried%20Catfish%20and%20Cornbread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Indiana Fried Catfish and Cornbread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Indiana Fried Catfish and Cornbread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Indiana%20Fried%20Catfish%20and%20Cornbread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","White fish","Eggs","Chiles","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"indiana-green-bean-casserole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g green beans, trimmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mushrooms, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"For topping: 2 onions sliced thin, 60g flour, oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the thin-sliced onions in flour until crisp and gold for the topping; drain and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanch the beans 4 min and drain.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sweat the mushrooms and diced onion in the butter, stir in the flour, and cook 2 min. Whisk in the milk and cream and simmer to a sauce. Add soy sauce, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the beans and turn into a baking dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 20 min, top with the crisp onions, and bake 5 min more.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"The from-scratch version of the mid-century casserole, invented for a 1955 recipe booklet in the Midwest. Fresh mushroom sauce and hand-fried onions turn a joke into a genuinely good dish.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":39,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Indiana%20Green%20Bean%20Casserole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Indiana Green Bean Casserole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Indiana Green Bean Casserole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Indiana%20Green%20Bean%20Casserole","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Mushrooms","Green beans","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Soy sauce"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"hoosier-sugar-cookies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"170g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sugar for rolling"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, soda, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar, beat in the egg, sour cream, and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the dry ingredients to a soft dough and chill 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll walnut-sized balls in sugar and set well apart on lined trays.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 10-12 min until set but pale: they should stay soft and cakey.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":false}],"note":"A soft, cakey sugar cookie leavened partly by sour cream, a Hoosier bakery tradition. Pull them while still pale; a browned sugar cookie has crossed from soft into crisp and lost the point.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Creaming butter & sugar","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hoosier%20Sugar%20Cookies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hoosier Sugar Cookies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Hoosier Sugar Cookies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Hoosier%20Sugar%20Cookies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"illinois-pork-chops-and-applesauce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick bone-in pork chops"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cider"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the applesauce: 4 apples peeled and chopped, 60ml water, 2 tbsp sugar, 1/2 tsp cinnamon, squeeze of lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the apples with the water, sugar, cinnamon, and lemon until soft, then mash to a chunky sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the chops and brown hard in the oil and butter, 3 min a side; set aside.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the onion in the pan, add the cider and thyme, and scrape up the fond.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the chops, cover, and simmer 15 min until cooked through.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the chops, reduce the pan sauce, and serve with the warm applesauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Corn-belt pork is Illinois's pride, and pork with apples is a marriage older than the state. Make the applesauce chunky and fresh: jarred sauce is sweeter and thinner and misses the tart contrast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Illinois%20Pork%20Chops%20and%20Applesauce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Illinois Pork Chops and Applesauce"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Illinois Pork Chops and Applesauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Illinois%20Pork%20Chops%20and%20Applesauce","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Lemon","Apple","Thyme","Butter","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"illinois-sweet-corn-ice-cream","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 ears sweet corn, kernels and cobs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the corn kernels and broken cobs in the milk and cream 20 min, then discard the cobs.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Blend the mixture and strain, pressing hard to extract the corn flavor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with the sugar and salt, temper in the hot corn cream, and cook to 82C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain, add the vanilla, and chill thoroughly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Churn and freeze until firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A prairie-state celebration of August corn in dessert form. Steeping the cobs as well as the kernels is what gives it real corn flavor rather than just sweetness: the cob holds as much as the kernel.","equipment":["Ice-cream churner"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Churning ice cream"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Churning ice cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ice%20cream%20churning%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Illinois%20Sweet%20Corn%20Ice%20Cream%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Illinois Sweet Corn Ice Cream"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Illinois Sweet Corn Ice Cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Illinois%20Sweet%20Corn%20Ice%20Cream","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Corn","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"chicago-rainbow-cone","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Chocolate ice cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Strawberry ice cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Palmer House ice cream (cherry-walnut vanilla)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pistachio ice cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Orange sherbet"},{"kind":"item","text":"Waffle cones"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soften each ice cream slightly so it can be layered without melting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press a layer of chocolate into the bottom of the cone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add strawberry, then Palmer House, then pistachio.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with a cap of orange sherbet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately: the layers are meant to show in a rainbow slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A South Side Chicago institution since 1926, served as flat slabs of five flavors, not scoops. The order of the layers is fixed by tradition, and the orange sherbet on top is the tangy finish that keeps it from being cloying.","equipment":["Waffle iron"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","earthy","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicago%20Rainbow%20Cone%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicago Rainbow Cone"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Chicago Rainbow Cone","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Chicago%20Rainbow%20Cone","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Orange","Berries","Stone fruit","Cream","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla","Nuts"]},{"slug":"michigan-bumpy-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the cake: 250g flour, 60g cocoa, 1 tsp soda, 1/2 tsp salt, 115g butter, 300g sugar, 2 eggs, 240ml buttermilk, 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the bumps: 115g butter, 300g icing sugar, 60ml cream, 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the ganache: 200g dark chocolate, 200ml cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a chocolate cake: cream butter and sugar, add eggs, alternate the dry ingredients with buttermilk. Bake in a rectangular pan at 175C for 30 min. Cool.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the buttercream ingredients stiff and pipe it in thick parallel ridges across the cake: these are the bumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill until the buttercream is firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the cream, pour over the chocolate, and stir to a glossy ganache. Cool slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the ganache over the whole cake so it flows around the buttercream ridges, showing the bumps beneath.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Detroit bakery classic from Sanders, defined by its ridges of buttercream under a chocolate glaze. Chill the buttercream bumps hard before glazing or they collapse under the warm ganache.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Michigan%20Bumpy%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Michigan Bumpy Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Michigan Bumpy Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Michigan%20Bumpy%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ganache-bloom-and-chocolate-craft","term":"Ganache, Bloom & Chocolate Craft"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","creaming-butter-and-sugar"]},{"slug":"michigan-dried-cherry-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g mixed greens"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g dried Michigan cherries"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g walnuts, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g blue cheese or goat cheese, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 apple, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dressing: 3 tbsp cider vinegar, 6 tbsp oil, 1 tbsp maple syrup, 1 tsp Dijon, salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the walnuts until fragrant and cool them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the dressing ingredients into an emulsion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the greens with just enough dressing to coat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the dried cherries, walnuts, cheese, and apple over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve at once, with extra dressing on the side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Traverse City's dried cherries are a year-round crop from the summer glut. The sweet-tart cherry against blue cheese and toasted walnut is a study in balance: dress the greens lightly so the toppings stay the stars.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","earthy","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Michigan%20Dried%20Cherry%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Michigan Dried Cherry Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Michigan Dried Cherry Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Michigan%20Dried%20Cherry%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lettuce/Greens","Apple","Stone fruit","Cheese (any)","Goat cheese","Blue cheese","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Nuts","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-beer-cheese-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml vegetable or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"330ml lager"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"450g sharp Wisconsin cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Dijon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cayenne, salt, white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Popcorn to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot, and celery in the butter until soft, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the flour and cook 2 min to a blond roux.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the stock and beer and simmer 10 min, then add the milk.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, add the cheese in handfuls, stirring until each melts before adding more; never boil after the cheese goes in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with mustard, Worcestershire, cayenne, salt, and pepper. Top with popcorn.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dairy state pride in a bowl. The pot comes off the heat before the cheese to keep it silky: a boil breaks it into grease and strings. Popcorn on top is a beloved Wisconsin garnish, not a gimmick.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Beer%20Cheese%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Beer Cheese Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Beer Cheese Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Beer%20Cheese%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Chiles","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-fried-cheese-curds","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g fresh cheese curds"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cold beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ranch or marinara to dip"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the curds dry and freeze them 15 min so they hold their shape in the oil.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, egg, cold beer, salt, and baking powder into a batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip the curds in batter a few at a time and fry 1-2 min until gold and puffed.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve immediately while the inside is molten and squeaky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fresh, squeaky curds are essential: day-old curds lose the squeak that makes them worth frying. Chill them first and keep the oil hot so the batter sets before the cheese leaks out. Eat them at once; they wait for no one.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Fried%20Cheese%20Curds%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Fried Cheese Curds"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Fried Cheese Curds","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Fried%20Cheese%20Curds","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Cheese (any)","Flour","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-bratwurst-in-beer","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 fresh bratwurst"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 bottles lager"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp caraway"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 hard rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"Stone-ground mustard and sauerkraut"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the brats gently in the beer with the sliced onions, butter, and caraway 20 min: do not boil, which splits the casings.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the brats out and grill them over medium coals until browned and blistered, 10 min, turning.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Keep the beer and onions simmering to reduce into a soft, sweet topping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set each brat in a roll.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with the beer onions, mustard, and kraut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Sheboygan method: poach in beer first, then grill for color. Poaching cooks them through gently so the grill only has to brown: grilling raw brats chars the outside while the middle stays raw and splits the casing.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","rich","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, richness that coats and comforts, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Bratwurst%20in%20Beer%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Bratwurst in Beer"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Bratwurst in Beer","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Bratwurst%20in%20Beer","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Onion","Cabbage","Butter","Bread","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-booyah","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut up"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef shank"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork shoulder"},{"kind":"item","text":"4L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corn"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g green beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chicken, beef, and pork in the water with bay 3 hours until all fall off the bone. Lift out, shred, discard bones, keep the broth.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onions, carrots, celery, and potatoes to the broth and simmer 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, peas, corn, and green beans and cook 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the shredded meat and simmer 90 min more until thick and melded.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season generously. It should be a spoon-standing stew.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Northeastern Wisconsin's Belgian church-and-tavern stew, cooked in enormous kettles overnight for fundraisers. The name comes from 'bouillon', and the all-day simmer is not optional: it is what marries three meats and a garden into one pot.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Booyah%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Booyah"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Booyah","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Booyah","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Pork","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Corn","Peas","Green beans","Bay leaf"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-kringle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the pastry: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 50g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 3 eggs, 225g cold butter for laminating"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 200g pecans, 150g brown sugar, 55g butter, 1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the icing: 200g icing sugar, 3 tbsp milk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make an enriched dough, chill it, then laminate with the cold butter through three fold-and-roll turns like a rough puff. Chill between turns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough into a long strip, fill with the pecan-sugar-butter mixture, and fold over lengthwise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape into a large oval ring and set on a tray. Rise 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 25-30 min until deep gold and flaky.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool and drizzle with the icing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Racine's Danish bakers made the flat oval kringle the official state pastry. The lamination is the work: keep the butter cold through every fold, or it melts into the dough and you lose the flake.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Lamination","Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Kringle%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Kringle"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Kringle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Kringle","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-butter-burger","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g ground beef (80/20)"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, plus more"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices American cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pickles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Form the beef into four patties and season well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle them in a generous knob of butter over medium-high heat, 3 min a side, basting with the melting butter.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lay cheese on to melt in the last minute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the cut buns in more butter until gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter the toasted buns again, add the patty, onion, and pickles, and serve: butter at every stage is the point.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Wisconsin obsession where butter appears at every step, including a pat inside the finished bun at some stands. Baste the patty with the browning butter as it cooks; that is where the flavor comes from.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Butter%20Burger%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Butter Burger"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Butter Burger","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Butter%20Burger","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-friday-fish-fry","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g cod or perch fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"330ml cold beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"Extra flour for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For sides: rye bread, coleslaw, potato pancakes, tartar sauce, lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the fish dry and dust with plain flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt, and cold beer into a batter: lumps are fine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip the fish in batter, let the excess drip, and fry 3-4 min until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain and serve the Wisconsin way: with rye bread, slaw, potato pancakes, tartar, and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Friday fish fry is a statewide institution born of Catholic and Lutheran fasting custom. Cold beer batter is the classic coat: the carbonation lifts it crisp, so mix it at the last minute before the fizz dies.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Friday%20Fish%20Fry%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Friday Fish Fry"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Friday Fish Fry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Friday%20Fish%20Fry","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Potato","Lemon","Bread","Flour","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-cranberry-bars","ingredients":[{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE BASE"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g rolled oats"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"170g melted butter"},{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE FILLING"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g fresh cranberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Zest and juice of 1 orange"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cornstarch"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the cranberries with the sugar, orange zest and juice, and cornstarch until they burst and thicken, 10 min. Cool.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the flour, sugar, oats, salt, and melted butter to crumbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press two-thirds into a lined pan and bake at 175C for 12 min.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the cranberry filling over and scatter the remaining crumb on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 25-30 min more until golden. Cool completely before cutting.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wisconsin grows more cranberries than any state, in the central marshes. Cook the berries until they truly burst and the cornstarch thickens, or the filling stays runny and the bars will not hold their cut.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Cranberry%20Bars%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Cranberry Bars"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Cranberry Bars","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Cranberry%20Bars","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Orange","Dried fruit","Butter","Bread","Flour","Oats","Sugar"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-cheese-and-potato-pierogi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 400g flour, 1 egg, 1 tsp salt, 250ml warm water, 2 tbsp sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 500g mashed potato, 200g sharp cheddar grated, 1 onion sauteed, salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter and more onions to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the dough ingredients smooth, cover, and rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the mashed potato, cheddar, sauteed onion, salt, and pepper for the filling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough thin and cut rounds. Spoon filling onto each, fold, and crimp the edges tightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil in batches 3-4 min until they float, then lift out.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the boiled pierogi in butter with more onions until golden. Serve with sour cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wisconsin's Polish communities brought pierogi and the cheese state made them cheesier. Boil first, then fry: boiling cooks the dough through, and the butter-fry gives the color and crisp that boiling alone cannot.","equipment":["Rolling pin","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Pierogi","Pie crust & blind baking","Kneading dough","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pierogi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20pierogi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Cheese%20and%20Potato%20Pierogi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Cheese and Potato Pierogi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Cheese and Potato Pierogi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Cheese%20and%20Potato%20Pierogi","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Potato","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"minnesota-tater-tot-hotdish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mushrooms, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g frozen peas and corn"},{"kind":"item","text":"1kg frozen tater tots"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion, celery, and mushrooms; drain and season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In the same pan make a roux with the butter and flour, whisk in the milk and stock, and simmer to a sauce with the Worcestershire: this is the from-scratch soup base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the beef and vegetables into the sauce with the peas and corn and spread in a baking dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange the tater tots in neat rows over the top and scatter with cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 35-40 min until the tots are crisp and gold and the filling bubbles.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"The signature hotdish of church basements and family suppers. Making the sauce from a roux instead of canned soup is the upgrade that turns it from convenience food into something genuinely good, and the tots must go on in tidy rows.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minnesota%20Tater%20Tot%20Hotdish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minnesota Tater Tot Hotdish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minnesota Tater Tot Hotdish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minnesota%20Tater%20Tot%20Hotdish","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Celery","Mushrooms","Corn","Peas","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"juicy-lucy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g ground beef (80/20)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g American cheese, cut in thick squares"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the griddle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pickles and mustard"}],"steps":[{"text":"Divide the beef into 8 thin patties. Season them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Place a folded square of cheese in the center of four patties, leaving a border.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top each with a second patty and pinch the edges tightly all around to seal the cheese inside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle over medium heat 4-5 min a side: medium, not high, so the cheese melts before the outside burns. Pierce is forbidden.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set on buttered buns with onion, pickle, and mustard. Warn everyone to wait: the cheese is molten.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two Minneapolis bars claim the invention and spell it differently. Seal the edges completely or the cheese escapes, and cook it over medium heat: the whole point is molten cheese inside, which needs time, not a hard sear.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Juicy%20Lucy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Juicy Lucy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Juicy Lucy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Juicy%20Lucy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"minnesota-wild-rice-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g wild rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mushrooms, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g cooked chicken, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sherry"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the wild rice in the water 45 min until the grains split open. Drain any excess.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot, celery, and mushrooms in the butter until soft, 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the flour and cook 2 min, then whisk in the stock and simmer 10 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cooked rice, chicken, and cream and heat through without boiling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with the sherry, season, and scatter with chives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"True wild rice is a Minnesota native grain, hand-harvested from lakes by the Ojibwe. Cook it until the grains burst open and curl: that is doneness, and undercooked wild rice stays tough and grassy no matter how long the soup sits.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minnesota%20Wild%20Rice%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minnesota Wild Rice Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minnesota Wild Rice Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minnesota%20Wild%20Rice%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"wild-mushrooms-the-forest-calendar","term":"Wild Mushrooms: The Forest Calendar"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Mushrooms","Chives","Butter","Cream","Rice","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"minnesota-walleye-shore-lunch","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 walleye fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g crushed saltine crackers"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter and oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the fillets dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set up flour, beaten egg, and the crushed saltines seasoned with salt, pepper, and paprika.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge each fillet in flour, then egg, then the cracker crumbs, pressing them on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in a mix of butter and oil over medium-high heat 3-4 min a side until the crust is deep gold and the fish flakes.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve immediately with lemon. Traditionally cooked on the lakeshore right after the catch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Walleye is the prized freshwater fish of the northern lakes, and the shore lunch is a ritual of frying the morning's catch on the spot. Saltine crumbs make the classic crust; do not overcook walleye, which is lean and flakes dry fast.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minnesota%20Walleye%20Shore%20Lunch%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minnesota Walleye Shore Lunch"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minnesota Walleye Shore Lunch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minnesota%20Walleye%20Shore%20Lunch","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"minnesota-lefse","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg russet potatoes, riced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter and sugar to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil, rice, and cool the potatoes completely: warm potato makes a gluey dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the riced potato with the melted butter, cream, salt, and sugar, then chill overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the flour just until it comes together; the less flour, the more tender the lefse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll each portion paper-thin on a floured board.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a dry hot griddle 30 seconds a side until spotted with brown. Fold with butter and sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Norwegian potato flatbread and a Minnesota Christmas tradition. Chilling the potato before adding flour, and using as little flour as possible, is what keeps lefse tender: too much flour and it turns to cardboard.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minnesota%20Lefse%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minnesota Lefse"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minnesota Lefse","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minnesota%20Lefse","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"minnesota-swedish-meatballs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g ground pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g breadcrumbs soaked in 120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the gravy: 55g butter, 55g flour, 750ml beef stock, 120ml cream, 1 tsp soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lingonberry jam and mashed potatoes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the meats, grated onion, soaked breadcrumbs, egg, allspice, nutmeg, salt, and pepper. Roll into small balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the meatballs all over in butter and set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a roux in the pan with butter and flour, whisk in the stock, and simmer to a gravy. Add the cream and soy sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the meatballs and simmer 15 min until cooked through and the gravy is silky.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve over mashed potatoes with lingonberry jam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Minnesota's Scandinavian heritage runs deep. Allspice and nutmeg are the spices that make them Swedish rather than Italian, and soaking the breadcrumbs in milk first (a panade) is what keeps the meatballs tender.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minnesota%20Swedish%20Meatballs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minnesota Swedish Meatballs"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minnesota Swedish Meatballs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minnesota%20Swedish%20Meatballs","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"allspice","term":"Allspice"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Soy sauce","Stock/Broth","Nutmeg","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"iowa-loose-meat-sandwich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp yellow mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft hamburger buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mustard, pickle, and onion to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef in a dry pan, breaking it into fine crumbles, the finer the better.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion and cook until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the stock, mustard, vinegar, sugar, salt, and pepper and simmer 20 min until most of the liquid cooks away but the meat stays moist and loose.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Spoon the loose meat onto buns: it will not hold together like a burger, and that is correct.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with mustard, pickle, and onion and eat with a spoon nearby for the fallout.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Also called a tavern or a Maid-Rite, this is a sloppy joe without the tomato: just seasoned loose ground beef. Keep it loose and moist, never a formed patty; the crumble spilling out is the whole character.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iowa%20Loose%20Meat%20Sandwich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Iowa Loose Meat Sandwich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Iowa Loose Meat Sandwich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Iowa%20Loose%20Meat%20Sandwich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Bread","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"iowa-breaded-pork-tenderloin","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 pork loin cutlets, pounded to 5mm and very wide"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs beaten with 60ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cracker crumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, 1/2 tsp pepper, 1/2 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mustard, pickle, onion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the cutlets between plastic until thin and wide: they should be twice the width of the bun.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set up flour, egg wash, and seasoned cracker crumbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge in flour, egg, then crumbs, pressing them on firmly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 5mm of 180C oil 2-3 min a side until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Set on the small bun it overwhelms, with mustard, pickle, and onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Iowa rivals Indiana for the tenderloin crown and both insist theirs is the original. Pound it thin between plastic wrap so it spreads wide without tearing; the comic overhang past the bun is mandatory.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iowa%20Breaded%20Pork%20Tenderloin%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Iowa Breaded Pork Tenderloin"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Iowa Breaded Pork Tenderloin","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Iowa%20Breaded%20Pork%20Tenderloin","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tenderloin-filet-mignon","term":"Tenderloin / Filet Mignon"},{"slug":"pork-loin-vs-tenderloin","term":"Pork Loin vs. Tenderloin"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Milk","Bread","Flour","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"iowa-corn-casserole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 ears corn, kernels and scraped milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the kernels from the cobs and scrape the cobs for their milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, cream, melted butter, cornmeal, flour, sugar, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the corn and half the cheddar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered dish and top with the remaining cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 40-50 min until set and golden.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false}],"note":"Iowa grows more corn than any state, and this custardy casserole is a potluck fixture. Scrape the cobs for their starchy milk: it is what sets the custard and carries the concentrated corn flavor.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iowa%20Corn%20Casserole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Iowa Corn Casserole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Iowa Corn Casserole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Iowa%20Corn%20Casserole","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Corn","Butter","Milk","Cream","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"]},{"slug":"maid-rite-style-tavern","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml beef broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp yellow mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yellow mustard, chopped onion, dill pickles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown and crumble the beef very fine in a dry skillet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the minced onion and cook until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the broth, mustard, Worcestershire, sugar, salt, and pepper and simmer gently until the liquid is nearly gone but the meat is still moist.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold it warm and loose, never pressed together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon onto buns and top with mustard, onion, and pickle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Maid-Rite chain, founded 1926 in Muscatine, made the loose-meat sandwich an Iowa institution. The steamed, never-pressed meat is the trademark; the moment you form it into a patty it becomes something else entirely.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maid-Rite%20Style%20Tavern%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maid-Rite Style Tavern"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Maid-Rite Style Tavern","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Maid-Rite%20Style%20Tavern","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Dill","Bread","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"iowa-scotcheroos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g peanut butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"180g crisped rice cereal"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g chocolate chips"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g butterscotch chips"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a 23x33cm pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the sugar and corn syrup until it just boils, then remove from the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the peanut butter until smooth, then fold in the crisped rice cereal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press into the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the chocolate and butterscotch chips together, spread over the top, and let set before cutting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A church-cookbook staple across Iowa, richer and chewier than a plain rice cereal bar. Do not overboil the syrup: take it off the heat the moment it bubbles, or the bars set rock-hard instead of chewy.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iowa%20Scotcheroos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Iowa Scotcheroos"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Iowa Scotcheroos","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Iowa%20Scotcheroos","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Rice","Peanut","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa"]},{"slug":"st-louis-toasted-ravioli","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"24 meat or cheese ravioli, fresh or thawed"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs beaten with 2 tbsp milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g Italian breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g Parmesan"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Marinara and grated Parmesan to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Set up flour, egg wash, and the breadcrumbs mixed with Parmesan and oregano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge each ravioli in flour, then egg, then the breadcrumb mixture, pressing it on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the ravioli in batches 2-3 min until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain, dust with more Parmesan, and serve hot with warm marinara for dipping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A St. Louis Hill neighborhood accident, said to be born when a cook dropped ravioli in oil instead of water. Toasted here means fried: pat the ravioli dry first so the coating grips, and serve them the instant they drain.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","nutty-toasted","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=St.%20Louis%20Toasted%20Ravioli%20recipe","sub":"Search films of St. Louis Toasted Ravioli"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on St. Louis Toasted Ravioli","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20St.%20Louis%20Toasted%20Ravioli","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Oregano","Milk","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"st-louis-gooey-butter-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the base: 250g flour, 7g yeast, 50g sugar, 1/2 tsp salt, 120ml warm milk, 1 egg, 85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the gooey top: 115g butter, 300g sugar, 1 egg, 60ml corn syrup, 1 tsp vanilla, 190g flour, 60ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the yeasted base: mix the ingredients to a soft dough and press into the bottom of a lined pan. Rise 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar for the topping, beat in the egg, corn syrup, and vanilla, then alternate the flour with the water to a thick batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the topping over the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 30-35 min until the edges are set and golden but the center still jiggles: underbaking is essential to the gooey middle.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool and dust heavily with icing sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A St. Louis bakery accident from the 1930s, when a baker reversed the proportions of butter and flour. The center must stay gooey: pull it while the middle still wobbles, because a fully set gooey butter cake has missed its own name.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven","Creaming butter & sugar","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=St.%20Louis%20Gooey%20Butter%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of St. Louis Gooey Butter Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on St. Louis Gooey Butter Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20St.%20Louis%20Gooey%20Butter%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-essential-smallwares","term":"The Essential Smallwares"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"missouri-st-paul-sandwich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the egg foo young patty: 3 eggs, 100g bean sprouts, 1 scallion sliced, 50g minced onion, 100g diced cooked pork or shrimp, 1 tsp soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 slices white bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, and dill pickle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the eggs and mix with the sprouts, scallion, onion, meat, and soy sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry as a thick patty in hot oil, 2-3 min a side, until set and golden.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Spread mayonnaise on both slices of plain white bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the hot egg foo young patty on the bread with lettuce, tomato, and pickle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Close, cut in half, and serve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Chinese-American St. Louis invention found only in the city's takeout shops: an egg foo young patty on white bread with mayonnaise and pickle. The contrast of the hot savory patty against cold mayonnaise and crisp lettuce is the whole strange charm.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Missouri%20St.%20Paul%20Sandwich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Missouri St. Paul Sandwich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Missouri St. Paul Sandwich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Missouri%20St.%20Paul%20Sandwich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Shrimp","Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Tomato","Lettuce/Greens","Bean sprouts","Dill","Bread","Soy sauce","Mayonnaise","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"missouri-ozark-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 apple, peeled and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pecans, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Whipped cream to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the egg and sugar until pale and thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the flour, baking powder, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the diced apple, pecans, and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread in a buttered dish; it will be a thin layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 30-35 min until set and crisp on top but soft beneath. Serve warm with whipped cream.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true}],"note":"A homely Ozark dessert that Bess Truman reportedly served at the White House. It bakes into a crisp meringue-like top over a soft, chewy apple-nut base: do not overbake, or you lose the pudding-soft middle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Missouri%20Ozark%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Missouri Ozark Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Missouri Ozark Pudding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Missouri%20Ozark%20Pudding","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Apple","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"missouri-fried-chicken-with-cream-gravy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut in 8"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt, 1 tbsp pepper, 1 tbsp paprika, 1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lard for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the gravy: 4 tbsp pan drippings, 4 tbsp flour, 750ml milk, salt and lots of black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brine the chicken in buttermilk overnight, then dredge in the seasoned flour and rest 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 165C lard in a cast-iron skillet, dark meat 15 min and white 13, turning once, until deep gold and 74C internal. Drain.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour off all but 4 tablespoons of the drippings, keeping the browned bits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the flour and cook 2 min, then add the milk gradually, whisking, and simmer to a thick gravy.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season hard with salt and pepper and serve the chicken smothered or with gravy alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cream gravy from the frying drippings is the Missouri and wider Ozark way. Keep the browned bits in the pan when you pour off the fat: those cracklings are the flavor of the gravy, and a clean pan makes a bland one.","equipment":["Skillet","Thermometer"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Missouri%20Fried%20Chicken%20with%20Cream%20Gravy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Missouri Fried Chicken with Cream Gravy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Missouri Fried Chicken with Cream Gravy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Missouri%20Fried%20Chicken%20with%20Cream%20Gravy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Chiles","Milk","Cream","Buttermilk","Flour","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"missouri-persimmon-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ripe wild persimmon pulp"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pecans, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g raisins"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sieve the very ripe persimmons for smooth pulp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the pulp with the sugar, eggs, and oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, soda, cinnamon, nutmeg, and salt and fold in, then the pecans and raisins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered bundt or loaf pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 55-65 min until a skewer comes clean. Cool before slicing.","durationSec":3900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wild persimmons drop across the Missouri Ozarks in autumn after the first frost softens them. Only fully ripe, jelly-soft fruit will do: an under-ripe persimmon is so astringent it will pucker the whole cake.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Missouri%20Persimmon%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Missouri Persimmon Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Missouri Persimmon Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Missouri%20Persimmon%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Flour","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"wisconsin-old-fashioned","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 oz brandy (Wisconsin uses brandy, not whiskey)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sugar cube"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dashes Angostura bitters"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 orange slice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 maraschino cherries"},{"kind":"item","text":"Splash of lemon-lime soda or sour mix"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Muddle the sugar cube with the bitters, orange slice, and one cherry in the bottom of a glass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add ice and the brandy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with a splash of lemon-lime soda for a 'sweet' or sour mix for a 'sour'.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Garnish with the second cherry and an orange slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wisconsin drinks its old fashioned with brandy, a quirk dating to the 1893 World's Fair, and orders it sweet or sour by the mixer on top. It is the unofficial state cocktail, poured in every supper club before the Friday fish fry.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wisconsin%20Old%20Fashioned%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wisconsin Old Fashioned"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wisconsin Old Fashioned","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wisconsin%20Old%20Fashioned","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-liqueur-shelf-orange-maraschino-chartreuse-and-friends","term":"The Liqueur Shelf: Orange, Maraschino, Chartreuse & Friends"},{"slug":"the-old-fashioned-template","term":"The Old Fashioned Template"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Lime","Orange","Stone fruit","Sugar"]},{"slug":"minnesota-hot-dish-wild-rice-and-beef","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g wild rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mushrooms, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the wild rice in salted water 45 min until the grains split; drain.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion and mushrooms; drain and season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a roux with the butter and flour, whisk in the stock and milk, and simmer to a sauce with the Worcestershire.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the cooked rice and beef into the sauce and turn into a baking dish. Top with cheddar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 30 min until bubbling and golden.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"A heartier cousin of the tater tot hotdish, built on Minnesota's native wild rice. Cook the rice until the grains burst; undercooked wild rice stays tough and no amount of baking will soften it.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minnesota%20Hot%20Dish%20Wild%20Rice%20and%20Beef%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minnesota Hot Dish Wild Rice and Beef"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minnesota Hot Dish Wild Rice and Beef","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minnesota%20Hot%20Dish%20Wild%20Rice%20and%20Beef","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"wild-mushrooms-the-forest-calendar","term":"Wild Mushrooms: The Forest Calendar"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Mushrooms","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Rice","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"minnesota-blueberry-buckle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g wild blueberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the crumb: 100g sugar, 75g flour, 1 tsp cinnamon, 55g cold butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar, beat in the egg, then alternate the dry ingredients with the milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the blueberries and spread in a buttered pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the crumb ingredients to pebbles and scatter over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C for 45-50 min until a skewer comes clean.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool 20 min and cut in the pan.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The northern lakes country fills with wild blueberries in late summer. Small wild berries beat the big cultivated ones (less water and more flavor), and a toss in the flour keeps them from sinking to the bottom.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Rubbing fat into flour","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minnesota%20Blueberry%20Buckle%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minnesota Blueberry Buckle"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minnesota Blueberry Buckle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minnesota%20Blueberry%20Buckle","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Berries","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"minnesota-wild-rice-pancakes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g cooked wild rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Maple syrup and butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, sugar, baking powder, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs, buttermilk, and melted butter, then combine with the dry ingredients.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the cooked wild rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle in butter over medium heat until bubbles break, then flip once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve stacked with butter and maple syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cooked wild rice folded into the batter gives the pancakes a nutty chew and a northwoods character. Make sure the rice is fully cooked and split before it goes in: a hard grain in a soft pancake is an unwelcome surprise.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minnesota%20Wild%20Rice%20Pancakes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minnesota Wild Rice Pancakes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minnesota Wild Rice Pancakes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minnesota%20Wild%20Rice%20Pancakes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Buttermilk","Rice","Flour","Sugar","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"minnesota-booya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut up"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef shank"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g pork shoulder"},{"kind":"item","text":"4L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g corn"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g rutabaga, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chicken, beef, and pork in the water with bay 3 hours until falling apart. Lift out, shred, discard bones, keep the broth.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onions, carrots, celery, potatoes, and rutabaga and simmer 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, peas, and corn and cook 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the meat and simmer 90 min more until thick.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season generously.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Minnesota spells it booya and makes it a communal event, cooked in huge kettles overnight for church fundraisers, especially in the St. Paul area. The rutabaga is the northern touch, and the all-day cook is the tradition and the flavor both.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Minnesota%20Booya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Minnesota Booya"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Minnesota Booya","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Minnesota%20Booya","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Pork","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Corn","Peas","Bay leaf"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"iowa-skillet-sweet-corn","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 ears fresh sweet corn, kernels cut off"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, black pepper, and chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the kernels from the cobs and scrape the cobs for their milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter in a skillet and add the corn, kernels and scrapings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over medium-high heat 5 min, stirring, until it takes a little color.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sprinkle the flour over, then add the cream and sugar and simmer 3 min until it thickens.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with salt and pepper and finish with chives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Iowa sweet corn in August needs almost nothing done to it. Cook it briefly and hot so it keeps its snap and its sugar: corn simmered soft turns starchy and loses the very freshness that makes Iowa corn famous.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iowa%20Skillet%20Sweet%20Corn%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Iowa Skillet Sweet Corn"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Iowa Skillet Sweet Corn","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Iowa%20Skillet%20Sweet%20Corn","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Corn","Chives","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"iowa-ham-balls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground ham"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g ground pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the glaze: 200g brown sugar, 120ml cider vinegar, 120ml water, 1 tbsp dry mustard"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the ground ham, pork, breadcrumbs, eggs, and milk and roll into balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange in a baking dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the brown sugar, vinegar, water, and mustard into a glaze and pour over the balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 45-55 min, basting twice with the glaze, until browned and cooked through.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with the thickened glaze spooned over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Midwestern potluck fixture built on the ham-and-pork farm economy. The sweet-and-sour mustard glaze is what makes them; baste as they bake so the sauce lacquers the balls instead of pooling in the pan.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iowa%20Ham%20Balls%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Iowa Ham Balls"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Iowa Ham Balls","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Iowa%20Ham%20Balls","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Milk","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"iowa-snickers-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 tart apples, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 Snickers bars, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml whipped cream or whipped topping"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g vanilla pudding, prepared"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp caramel sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dice the apples and chop the Snickers bars into bite-sized pieces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the prepared vanilla pudding into the whipped cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Gently fold in the apples and chopped candy bars.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drizzle with caramel and fold once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 30 min and serve: assemble close to serving so the apples stay crisp and the candy does not dissolve.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"A tongue-in-cheek Midwestern 'salad' that appears at every Iowa gathering. Assemble it near serving time; make it too early and the apples weep and the candy bars go soft, turning a crisp dish into pudding.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iowa%20Snickers%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Iowa Snickers Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Iowa Snickers Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Iowa%20Snickers%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Apple","Cream","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"iowa-maytag-blue-cheese-dip","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g Maytag blue cheese, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp chives"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crudites and crackers"}],"steps":[{"text":"Crumble the blue cheese, leaving some larger pieces for texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the sour cream, mayonnaise, lemon, and grated garlic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the blue cheese and chives, leaving it chunky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with black pepper: the cheese brings the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 1 hour and serve with vegetables and crackers.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Maytag Dairy Farms in Newton has made its famous blue cheese since 1941, from the family that also made washing machines. Fold it in rather than beating it smooth: the crumbles of sharp blue are the whole reason to make the dip.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iowa%20Maytag%20Blue%20Cheese%20Dip%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Iowa Maytag Blue Cheese Dip"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Iowa Maytag Blue Cheese Dip","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Iowa%20Maytag%20Blue%20Cheese%20Dip","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Lemon","Chives","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Blue cheese","Mayonnaise","Clove"]},{"slug":"iowa-rhubarb-custard-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g rhubarb, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the rhubarb with half the sugar and let it stand 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, remaining sugar, flour, cream, vanilla, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the rhubarb in the shell and pour the custard over. Dot with butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 15 min, then 175C for 35-40 min until the custard is set and the rhubarb tender.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely before slicing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rhubarb grows in every Iowa backyard and returns every spring without asking. The custard tames rhubarb's sourness into something mellow: pull the pie while the center holds a faint wobble, as it firms as it cools.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iowa%20Rhubarb%20Custard%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Iowa Rhubarb Custard Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Iowa Rhubarb Custard Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Iowa%20Rhubarb%20Custard%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"missouri-burnt-ends","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg beef brisket point"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml barbecue sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp honey"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the brisket point all over with the mixed sugar and spices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smoke at 110C over hickory 6-7 hours until the internal temperature reaches 90C and the bark is dark.","durationSec":25200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":25200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the point into 3cm cubes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the cubes with the barbecue sauce and honey and return them to the smoker in a pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smoke 1-2 hours more until the sauce caramelizes and the cubes turn sticky and tender.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false}],"note":"Kansas City made burnt ends famous: the fatty point of the brisket, cubed and re-smoked in sauce until candied. The second smoke is what separates true burnt ends from merely sauced brisket; do not skip it.","equipment":["Thermometer","Smoker"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","sweet-edged","charred","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, a sweet edge rounding the corners, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Low & slow smoking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"dish":{"label":"BBQ with Franklin: The Brisket","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Missouri%20Burnt%20Ends%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Missouri Burnt Ends"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Missouri Burnt Ends","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Missouri%20Burnt%20Ends","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"brisket-point-vs-flat","term":"Brisket (Point vs. Flat)"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Chiles","Honey","Sugar","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking"]},{"slug":"kansas-city-barbecue-ribs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 racks pork spare ribs"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the rub: 4 tbsp brown sugar, 2 tbsp paprika, 1 tbsp salt, 1 tbsp pepper, 1 tbsp garlic powder, 1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: 250ml ketchup, 120ml molasses, 60ml cider vinegar, 2 tbsp brown sugar, 1 tbsp mustard, 1 tsp smoked paprika"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the membrane from the back of the ribs and coat both racks in the rub. Rest 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smoke at 110C over hickory 5-6 hours until the meat pulls back from the bones and bends easily.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the sauce ingredients together 15 min into the thick, sweet, tomato-based Kansas City style.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brush the ribs with sauce in the last 30 min and let it set into a glaze.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 10 min and cut between the bones.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kansas City barbecue means a thick, sweet, molasses-and-tomato sauce, and pork ribs are its showcase. Sauce only at the very end: brush it on too early over the long smoke and the sugar burns black and bitter.","equipment":["Smoker"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","sweet-edged","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, a sweet edge rounding the corners, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Low & slow smoking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20City%20Barbecue%20Ribs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas City Barbecue Ribs"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas City Barbecue Ribs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20City%20Barbecue%20Ribs","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ribs-baby-back-vs-spare-vs-st-louis","term":"Ribs: Baby Back vs. Spare vs. St. Louis"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Chiles","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Sugar","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking"]},{"slug":"missouri-corn-and-bacon-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 ears corn, kernels and scraped cobs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the stripped cobs in the milk 20 min to draw out their sweetness, then discard them.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Render the bacon; lift out the bits and sweat the onion in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle in the flour, cook 1 min, add the potatoes and the corn milk, and simmer 15 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the corn kernels and cream and cook 5 min more.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season, finish with butter, and scatter bacon and chives over each bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A farm-country chowder that puts the state's corn front and center. Steeping the cobs in the milk first is the free step that doubles the corn flavor: the cob holds as much sweetness as the kernel.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Missouri%20Corn%20and%20Bacon%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Missouri Corn and Bacon Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Missouri Corn and Bacon Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Missouri%20Corn%20and%20Bacon%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Onion","Potato","Corn","Chives","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"missouri-blackberry-cobbler","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g blackberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the batter: 115g butter, 190g flour, 200g sugar, 2 tsp baking powder, 1/4 tsp salt, 240ml milk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the blackberries with the sugar, cornstarch, and lemon and let stand 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter in a baking dish in the oven as it heats to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, sugar, baking powder, salt, and milk into a batter and pour over the melted butter. Do not stir.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the berries and juice over the batter; do not stir.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 45-55 min until the cake rises around the fruit and browns.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wild blackberries grow thick along Ozark fencerows and roadsides in July. The magic is not stirring: the batter floats up through the berries as it bakes, giving cake on top and fruit suspended below.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Missouri%20Blackberry%20Cobbler%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Missouri Blackberry Cobbler"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Missouri Blackberry Cobbler","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Missouri%20Blackberry%20Cobbler","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Berries","Butter","Flour","Sugar"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-knoephla-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the knoephla: 300g flour, 1 egg, 1/2 tsp salt, 120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper, and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a stiff dough from the flour, egg, salt, and milk. Rest 20 min, then roll into ropes and snip small pea-sized dumplings.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and celery in the butter until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes and stock and simmer 15 min until nearly tender.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drop the knoephla into the simmering soup and cook 12-15 min until they float and are cooked through.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the cream and thyme, season, and finish with parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A German-Russian dumpling soup that defines North Dakota comfort food, brought by settlers from the Ukrainian steppe. Keep the knoephla small, pea-sized, so they cook through; large ones stay doughy at the center.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Knoephla%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Knoephla Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Knoephla Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Knoephla%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Celery","Parsley","Thyme","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-fleischkuekle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 400g flour, 1 egg, 1 tsp salt, 250ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 500g ground beef, 1 onion grated, 1 tsp salt, 1/2 tsp pepper, 1/2 tsp allspice, 60ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the dough smooth and rest it 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the raw ground beef with the grated onion, seasonings, and water so it stays moist.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough thin and cut large rounds. Spread raw filling over half, fold into a half-moon, and seal the edge firmly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the pockets 4-5 min a side until deep gold and the meat inside is cooked through.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"A German-Russian fried meat pocket, a fair and church-supper staple across the state. The filling goes in raw and cooks inside the dough, so keep the meat moist with a little water or it dries out during the fry.","equipment":["Rolling pin","Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Deep frying","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Fleischkuekle%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Fleischkuekle"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Fleischkuekle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Fleischkuekle","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Flour","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-kuchen","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 400g flour, 7g yeast, 50g sugar, 1/2 tsp salt, 180ml warm milk, 1 egg, 55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the custard: 4 eggs, 250ml cream, 100g sugar, 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fruit: sliced apples, prunes, or peaches"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cinnamon sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a soft yeasted dough and rise 1 hour. Press into round pans, building up a rim.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange the fruit over the dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the custard ingredients and pour over the fruit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust with cinnamon sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 30-35 min until the custard is set and the crust golden.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false}],"note":"The official state dessert, a German-Russian custard cake with fruit. The trick is a dough rim high enough to hold the custard: too shallow and it runs over the edge and burns onto the pan.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","rich","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Kuchen%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Kuchen"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Kuchen","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Kuchen","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Apple","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-lefse","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg russet potatoes, riced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter and sugar to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil, rice, and cool the potatoes completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the riced potato with the melted butter, cream, salt, and sugar, then chill overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the flour just until it comes together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll each portion paper-thin on a floured board.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a dry hot griddle 30 seconds a side until spotted. Fold with butter and sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Norwegian settlers made lefse a Dakota Christmas tradition too. Chill the potato before adding flour and use as little flour as possible: the less flour, the more tender the lefse; too much and it turns to cardboard.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Lefse%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Lefse"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Lefse","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Lefse","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-bison-pot-roast","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.8kg bison chuck roast"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 potatoes, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the bison well and brown all sides hard in the oil: bison is lean and needs a good crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions, add tomato paste and cook until brick red, then deglaze with the wine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the roast with stock, bay, and thyme. Cover and braise at 150C for 2.5 hours.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the carrots and potatoes and cook 1 hour more.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the meat, skim the fat, and reduce the sauce if needed. Slice against the grain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bison is North Dakota's native meat and far leaner than beef, so it wants low, slow, moist heat: a hot fast cook turns it to leather. Do not overcook it past fork-tender or the lack of fat shows immediately.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Bison%20Pot%20Roast%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Bison Pot Roast"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Bison Pot Roast","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Bison%20Pot%20Roast","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Thyme","Bay leaf","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["braising","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-caramel-rolls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 50g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 2 eggs, 85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the caramel: 170g butter, 220g brown sugar, 120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"For filling: 85g butter, 150g brown sugar, 2 tbsp cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the dough and rise 90 min until doubled.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the caramel ingredients together and pour into a baking pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough into a rectangle, spread with the filling, roll up, and cut into 12.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set cut-side up on the caramel and rise 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C for 30 min until golden. Invert onto a tray immediately so the caramel flows down over the rolls.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Caramel rolls are a Dakota cafe institution, served warm and gooey. Invert the pan within a couple of minutes of baking: wait too long and the caramel welds to the pan instead of flooding the rolls.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Caramel%20Rolls%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Caramel Rolls"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Caramel Rolls","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Caramel%20Rolls","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-wild-rice-hotdish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g wild rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cheddar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the wild rice 45 min until the grains split; drain.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion and mushrooms; drain and season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a roux, whisk in the stock and milk, and simmer to a sauce with the Worcestershire.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the rice and beef, turn into a dish, and top with cheddar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 30 min until bubbling.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"Wild rice grows in the northern lakes and rivers and gives the hotdish a nutty backbone. Cook the rice until the grains truly burst; undercooked wild rice stays tough no matter how long it bakes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Wild%20Rice%20Hotdish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Wild Rice Hotdish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Wild Rice Hotdish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Wild%20Rice%20Hotdish","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Mushrooms","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Rice","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-sour-cream-raisin-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 blind-baked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"For meringue: 3 egg whites, 75g sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the raisins in the water 10 min until plump; drain.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the sour cream, sugar, yolks, cornstarch, and spices in a pan and cook over medium heat, stirring, until thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the raisins and pour into the baked shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites with the sugar to stiff peaks and spread over, sealing to the crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 15 min until the meringue is golden. Cool and chill.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Great Plains diner classic beloved across the Dakotas. Cook the custard until it truly thickens before the raisins go in, and seal the meringue right to the crust edge or it shrinks and weeps.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Pie crust & blind baking"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Sour%20Cream%20Raisin%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Sour Cream Raisin Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Sour Cream Raisin Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Sour%20Cream%20Raisin%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-borscht","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef shank or short rib"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 beets, peeled and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g cabbage, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sour cream to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the beef in the water 90 min until tender. Lift out, dice the meat, and keep the broth.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the beets, onion, and carrots to the broth and simmer 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the cabbage, potatoes, and tomatoes and cook 25 min until everything is tender.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the meat, add vinegar, sugar, and dill, and simmer 10 min. Balance the sweet and sour to taste.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve topped with sour cream and fresh dill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The German-Russian settlers of the Dakotas brought their own borscht, heartier and beefier than the Ukrainian original. Balance the vinegar and sugar at the end: that sweet-sour tension is what makes a borscht sing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Borscht%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Borscht"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Borscht","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Borscht","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Cabbage","Beets","Dill","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"north-dakota-chokecherry-jelly-thumbprints","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"225g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chokecherry jelly (or other tart jelly)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar, beat in the yolks and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the flour and salt to a soft dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into balls and press a deep well into each with your thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill each well with chokecherry jelly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 12-15 min until set but pale. Cool on the tray.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"The chokecherry is North Dakota's state fruit, tart and wild along the prairie draws. Its jelly is the traditional filling: press the wells deep so they hold enough jelly, and refill any that puff closed as they bake.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand","Creaming butter & sugar","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=North%20Dakota%20Chokecherry%20Jelly%20Thumbprints%20recipe","sub":"Search films of North Dakota Chokecherry Jelly Thumbprints"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on North Dakota Chokecherry Jelly Thumbprints","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20North%20Dakota%20Chokecherry%20Jelly%20Thumbprints","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-chislic","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g lamb or beef sirloin, in 2cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garlic salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Saltine crackers and hot sauce to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the cubed meat with the garlic, oil, Worcestershire, and seasonings and marinate 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat oil in a deep skillet to 190C, or heat a grill very hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry or grill the cubes in batches 2-3 min, turning, until browned outside and still pink within.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain briefly and dust with more garlic salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve on toothpicks with saltine crackers and hot sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The official state nosh, brought by German-Russian immigrants from the Crimea and traditionally made with mutton. Cook it hot and fast so the outside sears while the inside stays pink; overcooked chislic is chewy and dull. Saltines are the mandatory partner.","equipment":["Skillet","Grill","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","charred","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Chislic%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Chislic"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Chislic","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Chislic","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Garlic","Hot sauce","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-indian-tacos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For fry bread: 400g flour, 2 tbsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 300ml warm milk, oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g pinto beans, cooked"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, cheese, salsa, sour cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, baking powder, salt, and milk into a soft dough. Rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide, pat into rounds, and fry in 180C oil until puffed and golden, about 1 min a side.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion and spices, then stir in the beans and simmer 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the meat and beans onto each fry bread round.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with lettuce, tomato, cheese, salsa, and sour cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fry bread carries a complicated history as a food of survival on the reservations, and the Indian taco built on it is now a powwow and state fair staple. Fry the bread the moment it is shaped so it puffs; over-rested dough fries flat and dense.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Searing: the hard crust","Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Indian%20Tacos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Indian Tacos"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Indian Tacos","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Indian%20Tacos","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Lettuce/Greens","Milk","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Bread","Flour","Kidney/Pinto beans","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-kuchen","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 400g flour, 7g yeast, 50g sugar, 1/2 tsp salt, 180ml warm milk, 1 egg, 55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the custard: 4 eggs, 250ml cream, 100g sugar, 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fruit: apples, peaches, or rhubarb"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cinnamon sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a soft yeasted dough and rise 1 hour. Press into round pans with a raised rim.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange the fruit over the dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the custard and pour over the fruit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust with cinnamon sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 30-35 min until the custard sets and the crust is golden.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false}],"note":"South Dakota named kuchen its official state dessert, a legacy of German-Russian settlement. Build the dough rim high enough to hold the custard: the whole dish fails if the custard escapes over a shallow edge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","rich","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Kuchen%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Kuchen"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Kuchen","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Kuchen","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Apple","Stone fruit","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-pheasant-with-cream-gravy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 pheasants, cut in pieces (or 8 pheasant breasts)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt, 1 tsp pepper, 1 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil and butter for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the gravy: 4 tbsp drippings, 4 tbsp flour, 750ml milk, salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the pheasant in buttermilk 1 hour: it is lean and dries easily, so the brine matters.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Dredge in seasoned flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown in oil and butter, then cover and cook gently 25-30 min until just done; do not overcook lean pheasant.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off all but 4 tbsp drippings, whisk in the flour, cook 2 min, and add the milk to make a gravy.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season the gravy hard and serve the pheasant smothered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"South Dakota is the pheasant-hunting capital of America, and this is how the birds are cooked come autumn. Pheasant is far leaner than chicken; the buttermilk soak and gentle cooking are what keep it from turning dry and stringy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Pheasant%20with%20Cream%20Gravy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Pheasant with Cream Gravy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Pheasant with Cream Gravy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Pheasant%20with%20Cream%20Gravy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Venison/Game","Butter","Milk","Cream","Buttermilk","Flour","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-tiger-meat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g very fresh lean beef, ground twice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, grated fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cocktail rye bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"Raw onion and more pepper to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Use only the freshest lean beef, ground twice by a trusted butcher.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the beef with the grated onion, salt, and plenty of pepper, and the yolk if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill thoroughly and keep cold until the moment of serving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread on slices of cocktail rye bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with raw onion and a crack of pepper and serve at once, cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A German-Russian raw beef spread, a holiday-table tradition in the Dakotas akin to steak tartare. Absolute freshness and constant cold are non-negotiable with raw beef: buy from a butcher you trust and serve it straight from the fridge.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Tiger%20Meat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Tiger Meat"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Tiger Meat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Tiger%20Meat","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Bread"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-bison-chili","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground bison"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp chili powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g kidney beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cocoa powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the bison hard, then add the onion, pepper, and garlic and cook until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chili powder, cumin, and oregano and cook 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, beans, stock, and cocoa and simmer 90 min, stirring, until thick.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with cornbread, cheese, and onions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bison is South Dakota's native meat and leaner than beef, so it takes well to the long moist cooking of a chili. The spoon of cocoa deepens the color and roundness without reading as chocolate.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","earthy","aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, an earthy, grounding base, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Bison%20Chili%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Bison Chili"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Bison Chili","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Bison%20Chili","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Oregano","Kidney/Pinto beans","Stock/Broth","Chocolate/Cocoa","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-corn-and-wild-rice-casserole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g wild rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 ears corn, kernels cut off"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mushrooms, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml vegetable stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g Swiss cheese, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and thyme"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the wild rice 45 min until the grains split; drain.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sweat the onion and mushrooms in the butter, stir in the flour, and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the stock and cream and simmer to a sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the wild rice, corn, and half the cheese, then turn into a dish and top with the rest.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 30 min until bubbling and golden.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"Wild rice and prairie corn together in one dish. Cook the wild rice fully, until the grains burst and curl, before it goes in, because it will not soften further inside the creamy casserole.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":39,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Corn%20and%20Wild%20Rice%20Casserole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Corn and Wild Rice Casserole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Corn and Wild Rice Casserole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Corn%20and%20Wild%20Rice%20Casserole","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Mushrooms","Corn","Thyme","Butter","Cream","Cheese (any)","Rice","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-sour-cream-kuchen-bars","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the crust: 250g flour, 100g sugar, 170g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the topping: 300ml sour cream, 2 eggs, 150g sugar, 1 tsp vanilla, 3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cinnamon sugar and fruit (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the flour, sugar, and butter to crumbs and press into a lined pan. Bake at 175C for 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the sour cream, eggs, sugar, vanilla, and flour into a custard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the warm crust and add fruit if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust with cinnamon sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 25-30 min until the custard is just set. Cool and cut into bars.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"A hand-held take on the state dessert: kuchen came to the Dakotas with Germans-from-Russia homesteaders, and South Dakota made it official in 2000; the sour-cream custard over sweet dough is the prairie's teatime standard. Do not overbake the custard: pull it while the center holds the faintest wobble, as it firms as it cools into the silky kuchen texture, and an overbaked bar is merely cheesecake's dull cousin. Fruit under the custard is regional preference; cinnamon on top is near law.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Sour%20Cream%20Kuchen%20Bars%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Sour Cream Kuchen Bars"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Sour Cream Kuchen Bars","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Sour%20Cream%20Kuchen%20Bars","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-walleye-and-chips","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g walleye fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"330ml cold beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"Extra flour for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"1kg potatoes, cut in chips"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tartar sauce and lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the chipped potatoes at 150C until soft, rest, then fry again at 190C until crisp; salt at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat the walleye dry and dust with flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt, and cold beer into a batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip the fish and fry at 190C for 3-4 min until deep gold and the fish flakes.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve with the chips, tartar, and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Walleye from the Missouri River reservoirs is South Dakota's prize fish. Mix the beer batter at the last moment so the carbonation lifts it crisp, and do not overcook the lean walleye: it flakes dry in seconds past done.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Walleye%20and%20Chips%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Walleye and Chips"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Walleye and Chips","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Walleye%20and%20Chips","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Lemon","Flour","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"south-dakota-kolaches","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 60g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 2 egg yolks, 85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For filling: poppy seed paste, prune butter, or sweetened cream cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"For posypka topping: 50g flour, 50g sugar, 25g butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a rich yeasted dough and rise 1 hour until doubled.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into balls, set on trays, and rise 30 min. Press a deep well into each.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the wells with poppy seed, prune, or cheese filling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the posypka ingredients to crumbs and sprinkle over the filling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rise 20 min, then bake at 190C for 12-15 min until golden.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false}],"note":"Czech immigrants brought kolaches to the eastern Dakotas and Nebraska. Press the wells deep and re-press them after the second rise: the dough wants to close over the filling, and a shallow kolache spills as it bakes.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20Dakota%20Kolaches%20recipe","sub":"Search films of South Dakota Kolaches"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on South Dakota Kolaches","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20South%20Dakota%20Kolaches","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"nebraska-runza","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 50g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 1 egg, 55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 500g ground beef, 1 onion diced, 400g cabbage shredded, 1 tsp salt, 1 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a soft yeasted dough and rise 1 hour until doubled.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion, add the cabbage, and cook until wilted and the liquid has gone. Season well and cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough into rectangles, spoon filling into the center, and fold into sealed rectangular pockets, seam-side down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rise 30 min, then brush with egg.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 20-25 min until deep gold.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"A German-Russian stuffed bread that became a Nebraska icon, sold at its own fast-food chain. Cook the cabbage filling until dry: any liquid left in it steams inside the dough and makes the bottom soggy and the seal burst.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Runza%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Runza"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Runza","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Runza","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Cabbage","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"nebraska-bierocks","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 50g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 1 egg, 55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 500g ground beef, 1 onion, 400g cabbage, 2 tbsp mustard, 1 tsp caraway, salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a soft yeasted dough and rise 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion, add the cabbage, mustard, and caraway, and cook until wilted and dry. Season and cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough into squares, fill, and pinch closed into round buns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set seam-down, rise 30 min, and brush with egg.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 20-25 min until golden.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"Bierocks and runzas are close cousins from the same German-Russian roots: bierocks tend to be round with caraway and mustard. As with runzas, cook the filling dry, or the moisture ruins the seal and the crumb.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Bierocks%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Bierocks"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Bierocks","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Bierocks","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Cabbage","Butter","Milk","Flour","Mustard","Sugar","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"nebraska-reuben-sandwich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices rye bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g corned beef, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g sauerkraut, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices Swiss cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"For Russian dressing: 150g mayonnaise, 3 tbsp ketchup, 2 tbsp minced pickle, 1 tsp horseradish"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the griddle"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the dressing ingredients.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter one side of each bread slice. On the unbuttered sides, spread dressing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build: Swiss, corned beef, drained kraut, more Swiss, and close with butter-side out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle over medium heat 4-5 min a side, pressing gently, until the bread is deep gold and the cheese melts.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cut on the diagonal and serve hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Omaha claims the Reuben was invented at the Blackstone Hotel in the 1920s. Drain the sauerkraut hard: wet kraut steams the bread limp and floods the griddle, and a soggy Reuben is a fallen one.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","tangy-ferment","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, ferment tang with real depth behind it, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Reuben%20Sandwich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Reuben Sandwich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Reuben Sandwich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Reuben%20Sandwich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Cabbage","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mayonnaise","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"nebraska-corn-fritters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 ears corn, kernels cut off"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Honey or maple syrup"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt, and sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs and milk to a thick batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the corn kernels.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop heaped spoonfuls into 180C oil and fry 3-4 min, turning, until deep gold.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve hot drizzled with honey.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nebraska is corn country to its core. Keep the batter thick so the fritters hold their shape, and fry a test one to check the center cooks before the outside burns: a runny batter fries flat and greasy.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Corn%20Fritters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Corn Fritters"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Corn Fritters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Corn%20Fritters","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Corn","Milk","Flour","Honey","Sugar","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"nebraska-chicken-fried-steak","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 cube steaks (tenderized round steak)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt, 1 tsp pepper, 1 tsp paprika, 1/2 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the gravy: 4 tbsp drippings, 4 tbsp flour, 750ml milk, lots of black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Set up buttermilk beaten with eggs, and seasoned flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge each steak in flour, then the egg wash, then flour again, pressing it on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 5mm of 180C oil 3-4 min a side until deep gold and crisp. Keep warm.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour off all but 4 tbsp drippings, whisk in the flour, cook 2 min, and add the milk to make a peppery cream gravy.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the steak smothered in gravy with mashed potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beef country makes chicken fried steak a birthright: breaded and fried like chicken, hence the name. The double flour dredge builds the crust; keep the browned bits in the pan for the gravy, as they are its whole flavor.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Chicken%20Fried%20Steak%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Chicken Fried Steak"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Chicken Fried Steak","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Chicken%20Fried%20Steak","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Eggs","Chiles","Milk","Buttermilk","Flour","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"nebraska-kool-aid-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 graham cracker crust"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 packet unsweetened Kool-Aid (lemonade or cherry)"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml whipped topping"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the Kool-Aid powder into the sweetened condensed milk until the color is even.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the lemon juice and stir: the acid thickens the filling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the whipped topping until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread into the graham crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill at least 3 hours until set. Top with more whipped topping.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kool-Aid was invented in Hastings, Nebraska, in 1927, and this no-bake pie is a nostalgic tribute. The acid in the drink powder and lemon juice sets the condensed milk: no baking needed, just time in the fridge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Kool-Aid%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Kool-Aid Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Kool-Aid Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Kool-Aid%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Stone fruit","Milk"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"nebraska-dorothy-lynch-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 head iceberg or romaine, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, wedged"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"For Dorothy Lynch-style dressing: 120ml tomato soup, 120ml oil, 80ml vinegar, 100g sugar, 1 tbsp grated onion, 1 tsp celery seed, 1 tsp paprika, salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend all the dressing ingredients until smooth and emulsified; it should be sweet, tangy, and tomato-red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill the dressing at least an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the greens and vegetables and combine in a bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss with the dressing just before serving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Dorothy Lynch is a sweet-and-sour tomato dressing invented in Nebraska in the 1940s and treated as a state condiment. It is good on far more than salad: Nebraskans put it on everything from fries to sandwiches.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Building an emulsion"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Dorothy%20Lynch%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Dorothy Lynch Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Dorothy Lynch Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Dorothy%20Lynch%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Celery","Cucumber","Lettuce/Greens","Vinegar","Sugar","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion"]},{"slug":"nebraska-popcorn-balls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 cups popped popcorn"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for your hands"}],"steps":[{"text":"Keep the popped corn warm in a large bowl, picking out unpopped kernels.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar, corn syrup, butter, and salt to 115C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat stir in the vanilla, then pour over the popcorn and fold quickly to coat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With buttered hands, press handfuls into firm balls while still warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set on parchment to cool and firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nebraska is a top popcorn-producing state. Work fast with buttered hands while the syrup is still hot and pliable; let it cool too far and it sets before you can shape the balls, and burnt fingers help no one.","equipment":["Parchment"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Popcorn%20Balls%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Popcorn Balls"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Popcorn Balls","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Popcorn%20Balls","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"nebraska-wilted-lettuce-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 large head leaf lettuce, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 rashers bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 scallions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp bacon drippings"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 hard-boiled egg, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon until crisp; lift out and reserve, keeping the drippings hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the torn lettuce and scallions in a bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the sugar, vinegar, salt, and pepper to the hot drippings and swirl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the hot dressing over the lettuce and toss immediately: it wilts the greens just so.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with the bacon and chopped egg and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A prairie farmhouse salad that turns spring lettuce into something warm and savory. The dressing must be poured boiling hot and tossed instantly: that flash of heat wilts the greens without cooking them, and it must be eaten right away.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Wilted%20Lettuce%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Wilted Lettuce Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Wilted Lettuce Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Wilted%20Lettuce%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Scallion","Lettuce/Greens","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"nebraska-cinnamon-rolls-with-chili","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the chili: 500g ground beef, 1 onion, 400g crushed tomatoes, 400g kidney beans, 2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin, 500ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 large cinnamon rolls, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cheddar and onion to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion, add the chili powder and cumin, and cook 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, beans, and stock and simmer 40 min until thick.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Warm the cinnamon rolls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve a bowl of chili with a cinnamon roll alongside, the traditional Nebraska school-lunch pairing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Eat by alternating bites, or dunk the roll in the chili.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chili and a cinnamon roll is a genuine Nebraska (and Iowa) school-lunch tradition that locals defend fiercely. The sweet roll against the savory, slightly sweet chili is the whole point: dunking is encouraged and not the least bit strange to a Nebraskan.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","warm-spiced","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, warm spice humming through the middle, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nebraska%20Cinnamon%20Rolls%20with%20Chili%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nebraska Cinnamon Rolls with Chili"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nebraska Cinnamon Rolls with Chili","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nebraska%20Cinnamon%20Rolls%20with%20Chili","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Liver & Offal","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Bread","Kidney/Pinto beans","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"kansas-cracklin-cornbread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g pork cracklings, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp bacon fat"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the bacon fat in a cast-iron skillet at 220C until smoking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the cornmeal, flour, baking powder, salt, and sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs and buttermilk to a loose batter, then fold in the cracklings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the batter into the screaming-hot fat and return to the oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 22-25 min until the top is gold and the edges crisp. Turn out and serve hot.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cracklin cornbread studs a plain skillet cornbread with crisp bits of rendered pork, a thrifty way to use every scrap of the hog. The smoking-hot skillet gives the shattering crust, and Kansas cornbread stays unsweet.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Cracklin%20Cornbread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Cracklin Cornbread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Cracklin Cornbread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Cracklin%20Cornbread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Eggs","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"]},{"slug":"kansas-bierocks","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 50g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 1 egg, 55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 500g ground beef, 1 onion, 400g cabbage, 1 tsp salt, 1 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a soft yeasted dough and rise 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion, add the cabbage, and cook until wilted and dry. Season and cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough into squares, fill, and pinch into sealed buns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set seam-down, rise 30 min, and brush with egg.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 20-25 min until golden.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"Volga German immigrants brought bierocks to the Kansas wheat plains, where they remain a Friday and church-supper staple. Cook the cabbage filling dry; leftover moisture bursts the seal and sogs the bottom.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Bierocks%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Bierocks"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Bierocks","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Bierocks","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Cabbage","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"kansas-fried-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut in 8"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt, 1 tbsp pepper, 1 tbsp paprika, 1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lard or oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brine the chicken in buttermilk overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the flour heavily and dredge each piece; rest on a rack 15 min so the crust sets.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt lard in a cast-iron skillet to 2cm depth and heat to 165C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry covered for the first half to steam the meat through, then uncovered to crisp, turning once, 15-18 min total.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack and rest 5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"The Brookville Hotel and Stroud's built Kansas fried-chicken fame on cast-iron, pan-fried birds. Covering the pan for the first half steams the meat tender before the crust locks in; uncovering finishes the crackle.","equipment":["Skillet","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Fried%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Fried Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Fried Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Fried%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Chiles","Buttermilk","Flour","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"kansas-wheat-bread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g whole wheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml warm water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flours, yeast, and salt. Add the honey, oil, and water and knead 10 min to a smooth, elastic dough.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rise, covered, 1 hour until doubled.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Knock back, shape into a loaf, and set in a buttered tin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rise 45 min until it crowns the tin.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 30-35 min until deep brown and hollow-sounding. Cool before slicing.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kansas is the wheat state, growing more than any other, and a good loaf honors the harvest. Knead until the dough is truly smooth and elastic: that gluten development is what gives wheat bread its rise and chew.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Wheat%20Bread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Wheat Bread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Wheat Bread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Wheat%20Bread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour","Honey"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"kansas-bran-muffins","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g wheat bran"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g raisins"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the bran in the buttermilk 15 min until softened.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat in the egg, brown sugar, oil, and molasses.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, soda, and salt and fold in, then the raisins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon into muffin cups.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 18-20 min until risen and set.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"A wheat-country breakfast that puts the bran to use: Kansas grows the wheat, and the muffin is the state's daily monument to milling's leftovers. Soaking the bran in buttermilk first is essential: it softens the coarse flakes so the muffins are moist rather than gritty, and the batter actually improves after a night in the fridge: the old refrigerator-muffin recipes kept a crock of it ready for a week of breakfasts. Molasses for depth, raisins by household vote.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Bran%20Muffins%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Bran Muffins"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Bran Muffins","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Bran%20Muffins","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar"]},{"slug":"kansas-beef-and-noodles","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg beef chuck, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the noodles: 250g flour, 3 egg yolks, 1 egg, 1/2 tsp salt, 3 tbsp broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mashed potatoes to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef hard in the oil, then add the onion and cook until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the stock and bay and simmer 90 min until the beef is tender.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Make the noodles: work the flour, eggs, and salt to a stiff dough, rest, roll thin, cut into ribbons, and dry 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the noodles into the simmering broth and cook 12-15 min until tender and the broth thickens.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season and serve over mashed potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beef and noodles over mashed potatoes is a Kansas plains comfort staple. Homemade egg noodles thicken the broth as they cook: that released starch is what turns thin stock into a rich gravy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Beef%20and%20Noodles%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Beef and Noodles"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Beef and Noodles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Beef%20and%20Noodles","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Bay leaf","Noodles","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"kansas-sunflower-seed-brittle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g raw shelled sunflower seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a large sheet pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar, corn syrup, and water to 120C, then add the butter and sunflower seeds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook, stirring, to 150C until amber and the seeds smell toasted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat quickly stir in the salt, baking soda, and vanilla; it foams pale.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour onto the pan, spread thin, and cool before breaking into shards.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sunflower is the state flower and Kansas grows them by the field. Sunflower seeds stand in for peanuts here; the baking soda foams the syrup so the brittle shatters crisp rather than cracking teeth.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Sunflower%20Seed%20Brittle%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Sunflower Seed Brittle"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Sunflower Seed Brittle","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Sunflower%20Seed%20Brittle","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"kansas-prairie-chicken-and-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs, bone in"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g long-grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mushrooms, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season and brown the chicken thighs skin-side down; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, celery, and mushrooms in the fat until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the rice to coat, then add the stock and thyme.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Nestle the chicken back in skin-side up, cover, and bake at 180C for 35 min until the rice is tender and the chicken cooked.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 10 min, season, and finish with parsley.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"A one-pan farmhouse supper. Brown the chicken skin well first: it renders fat to cook the rice in and gives the crisp skin that a covered bake alone cannot, so the browning is worth the extra pan.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Prairie%20Chicken%20and%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Prairie Chicken and Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Prairie Chicken and Rice","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Prairie%20Chicken%20and%20Rice","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Celery","Mushrooms","Parsley","Thyme","Rice","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"kansas-peppernuts","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml molasses or corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"375g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp anise"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar, beat in the egg and molasses.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, soda, and all the spices and work into a stiff dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into thin ropes and chill until firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the ropes into tiny pea-sized pieces and spread on trays.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 10-12 min until firm. They harden as they cool and keep for weeks.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pfeffernusse, or peppernuts, are a Mennonite Christmas tradition in Kansas, baked by the bowlful. Cut them tiny, the size of a hazelnut at most, and expect them to harden into a crunchy, spiced keeping-cookie as they cool.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Shaping by hand","Creaming butter & sugar"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Peppernuts%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Peppernuts"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Peppernuts","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Peppernuts","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"kansas-fried-pies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the pastry: 375g flour, 1 tsp salt, 170g shortening, 120ml cold buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 300g dried apricots or peaches, 250ml water, 100g sugar, 1/2 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the dried fruit with the water and sugar 20 min until soft and thick; mash and cool.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the fat into the flour and salt, bind with buttermilk, and chill 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll thin, cut rounds, fill one half, fold, and crimp hard with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 180C oil 2-3 min a side until deep gold and blistered.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain and dust with icing sugar while warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hand pies fried rather than baked, a Plains and border-state tradition. Dried fruit makes a thick filling that will not burst the crust in the fryer: crimp the edges firmly and fry a test pie to be sure the seal holds.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Deep frying","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","warm-spiced","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansas%20Fried%20Pies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansas Fried Pies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Kansas Fried Pies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Kansas%20Fried%20Pies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-onion-burger","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g ground beef (80/20)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices American cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mustard and pickles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Divide the beef into 4 loose balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"On a hot griddle, place each ball and pile a mound of thin-sliced onion on top. Smash the ball down hard through the onions into a thin patty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 3-4 min until the underside is crusted, salt, then flip so the onions hit the griddle and caramelize.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lay cheese on to melt, cook 2 min more.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Set on buttered buns with mustard and pickles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Born in El Reno during the Depression, when onions stretched scarce beef. Smashing the onions into the patty is the whole technique: they steam the meat, then caramelize when flipped, becoming half the burger.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Caramelizing onions","Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Onion%20Burger%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Onion Burger"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Onion Burger","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Onion%20Burger","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Butter","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-chicken-fried-steak","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 cube steaks"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt, 1 tsp pepper, 1 tsp paprika, 1/2 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the gravy: 4 tbsp drippings, 4 tbsp flour, 750ml milk, black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Set up buttermilk beaten with eggs and seasoned flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge each steak in flour, then egg, then flour again, pressing it on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 5mm of 180C oil 3-4 min a side until deep gold and crisp. Keep warm.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour off all but 4 tbsp drippings, whisk in the flour, cook 2 min, and add the milk for a peppery cream gravy.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the steak smothered with mashed potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oklahoma named chicken fried steak part of its official state meal. The double flour dredge armors the crust; keep the browned cracklings in the pan for the gravy, since a clean pan makes bland gravy.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Chicken%20Fried%20Steak%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Chicken Fried Steak"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Chicken Fried Steak","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Chicken%20Fried%20Steak","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Eggs","Chiles","Milk","Buttermilk","Flour","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-fried-okra","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g okra, sliced 1cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the sliced okra in buttermilk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the cornmeal, flour, salt, pepper, and cayenne in a bag.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the okra out and toss in the cornmeal mix to coat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 1cm of hot oil over medium-high 4-5 min until deep gold and crisp, turning.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Part of the official state meal. High heat and a cornmeal coat defeat okra's slime: the mucilage sets and crisps instead of running. Do not crowd the pan, or the temperature drops and the okra steams gummy.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Fried%20Okra%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Fried Okra"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Fried Okra","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Fried%20Okra","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Okra","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-indian-tacos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For fry bread: 400g flour, 2 tbsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 300ml warm milk, oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g pinto beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, cheese, salsa"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the fry bread dough and rest 30 min. Pat into rounds and fry in 180C oil until puffed and golden.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion and spices, stir in the beans, and simmer 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the meat and beans onto each fry bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with lettuce, tomato, cheese, and salsa.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve open-faced and eat with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oklahoma is home to 39 tribal nations, and the Indian taco on fry bread is a powwow and state fair icon. Fry the bread the instant it is shaped so it puffs; over-rested dough fries flat.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Searing: the hard crust","Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Indian%20Tacos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Indian Tacos"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Indian Tacos","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Indian%20Tacos","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Lettuce/Greens","Milk","Cheese (any)","Bread","Flour","Kidney/Pinto beans","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-fried-catfish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g catfish fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt, 1 tsp pepper, 1 tsp cayenne, 1 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon, hush puppies, and slaw"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the catfish in buttermilk and hot sauce 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season the cornmeal with the salt, pepper, cayenne, and garlic powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the fillets in cornmeal and fry 4-5 min until they float and turn deep gold.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve with lemon, hush puppies, and slaw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Catfish from Oklahoma's lakes and rivers, fried in pure cornmeal for the sandiest crust: the state's farm ponds and reservoirs made catfish the default fish fry from Tahlequah to Lawton. Get the oil properly hot so the crust sets before the lean fish overcooks and dries; 175°C and small batches is the whole discipline. A mustard smear under the dredge is the Okie variation worth trying once: it seasons and glues at the same time. Hushpuppies fry in the same oil, always last.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Fried%20Catfish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Fried Catfish"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Fried Catfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Fried%20Catfish","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Buttermilk","Cornmeal/Polenta","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-fried-pies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the pastry: 375g flour, 1 tsp salt, 170g shortening, 120ml cold buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 300g dried apricots or peaches, 250ml water, 100g sugar, 1/2 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the dried fruit with the water and sugar 20 min until thick; mash and cool.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the fat into the flour and salt, bind with buttermilk, and chill 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll thin, cut rounds, fill one half, fold, and crimp hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 180C oil 2-3 min a side until deep gold.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain and dust with icing sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oklahoma has a whole town, Davis, known for its roadside fried pies. Dried fruit makes the thick filling that will not burst the crust in the fryer: crimp firmly and test one first to be sure the seal holds.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Deep frying","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Fried%20Pies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Fried Pies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Fried Pies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Fried%20Pies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-grape-dumplings","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"750ml grape juice (traditionally wild possum grape)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dumplings: 250g flour, 2 tsp baking powder, 1/2 tsp salt, 2 tbsp sugar, 3 tbsp oil, 120ml grape juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the grape juice and sugar to a simmer in a wide pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the flour, baking powder, salt, sugar, oil, and grape juice into a soft dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough thin and cut into strips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the strips into the simmering grape juice and cook 12-15 min until the dumplings are tender and the juice thickens.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve warm in bowls with the purple syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A traditional dish of the Cherokee and other Oklahoma tribes, made from wild possum grapes. The dumplings thicken the juice into a purple sauce as they cook. Keep the simmer gentle so they cook through without falling apart.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Grape%20Dumplings%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Grape Dumplings"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Grape Dumplings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Grape%20Dumplings","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Grapes","Flour","Sugar"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-cornbread-and-beans","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried pinto beans, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ham hock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"For cornbread: 250g cornmeal, 60g flour, 1 tbsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 2 eggs, 400ml buttermilk, 3 tbsp bacon fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chopped onion to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained beans with the ham hock, onion, bay, and pepper 2-3 hours until creamy and the liquor is thick. Salt at the end.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the hock, shred the meat, and return it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the bacon fat in a cast-iron skillet at 220C, mix the cornbread batter, pour it in, and bake 20-25 min.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve the beans in bowls with cornbread and raw onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crumble the cornbread into the pot liquor to eat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beans and cornbread fed the Dust Bowl and never left the Oklahoma table. Salt the beans only at the end, early salt keeps the skins tough, and cook uncovered enough that the pot liquor thickens.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Cornbread%20and%20Beans%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Cornbread and Beans"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Cornbread and Beans","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Cornbread%20and%20Beans","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Bay leaf","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Kidney/Pinto beans"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-pecan-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g pecans"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml dark corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scatter the pecans in the shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, brown sugar, corn syrup, melted butter, vanilla, and salt until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the pecans; they rise to form the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 50-60 min until set with a slight jiggle.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely before slicing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pecan is Oklahoma's state tree and the pie a Thanksgiving fixture. Toast the pecans for eight minutes first if you have time: it deepens the flavor beyond what raw nuts give, the one improvement worth making.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Pecan%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Pecan Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Pecan Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Pecan%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"oklahoma-sausage-and-biscuits-with-gravy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For biscuits: 375g self-rising flour, 1 tsp sugar, 85g cold butter, 55g lard, 300ml cold buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g bulk sausage"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper, 1/4 tsp sage, pinch cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the cold fats into the flour and sugar, bind with buttermilk, pat out, fold, and cut. Bake at 230C for 12-15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the sausage in a cast-iron skillet, letting it crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle the flour over the meat and fat and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the warm milk in a stream, whisking, and simmer 8-10 min to a thick gravy.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season hard with pepper, sage, cayenne, and salt. Ladle over split biscuits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A cornerstone of the Oklahoma breakfast. Season the gravy with more pepper than seems polite (it should be visibly speckled), and if it thickens too far, more warm milk brings it back.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oklahoma%20Sausage%20and%20Biscuits%20with%20Gravy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oklahoma Sausage and Biscuits with Gravy"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oklahoma Sausage and Biscuits with Gravy","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oklahoma%20Sausage%20and%20Biscuits%20with%20Gravy","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Chiles","Sage","Butter","Milk","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"texas-chili-con-carne","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg beef chuck, in 1cm cubes (never ground)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 dried ancho chiles"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dried guajillo chiles"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 chipotle in adobo"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp lard or oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Mexican oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp masa harina"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the dried chiles in a dry pan, soak in hot water 20 min, then blend with the chipotle and a little soaking water into a smooth paste.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the beef cubes hard in the lard in batches; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic, add the cumin and oregano, then the chile paste and cook 5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the beef with the stock and simmer 2 hours until the meat is tender and the sauce is deep.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the masa harina with water and stir in to thicken; add vinegar and salt. Simmer 15 min more.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"A true Texas bowl of red has no beans and no tomatoes: just beef, chiles, and time. Cube the chuck by hand rather than grinding, and toast and soak whole dried chiles rather than reaching for powder; that is the difference between chili and stew.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Working with masa","Searing: the hard crust","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","smoky","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, wood-smoke running underneath, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Chili%20con%20Carne%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Chili con Carne"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Chili con Carne","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Chili%20con%20Carne","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Oregano","Cornmeal/Polenta","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"texas-breakfast-tacos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 flour tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g chorizo or crumbled bacon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 potatoes, diced small and fried crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cheddar or Monterey Jack, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salsa roja and pico de gallo to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the diced potato until crisp and golden; drain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the chorizo or bacon in the same pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs, pour into the pan, and scramble softly with the meat until just set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the tortillas on a dry comal until pliable and slightly charred.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill each with egg, potato, and cheese and serve with salsa and pico.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The breakfast taco is San Antonio and Austin's morning fuel, and the tortilla must be warmed on a hot comal, never microwaved. Keep the eggs soft: pull them while still glossy, because they carry cooking on the warm tortilla.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Breakfast%20Tacos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Breakfast Tacos"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Breakfast Tacos","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Breakfast%20Tacos","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"monterey-jack-and-pepper-jack","term":"Monterey Jack & Pepper Jack"}],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Sausage","Eggs","Potato","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"texas-frito-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 bag corn chips (Fritos)"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the chili: 500g ground beef, 1 onion, 2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tbsp cumin, 400g crushed tomatoes, 400g pinto beans, 500ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Diced onion, jalapenos, and sour cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion, add the chili powder and cumin, and cook 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, beans, and stock and simmer 40 min until thick.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Split a bag of corn chips down the side, or pile them in a bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle the hot chili over the chips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with cheddar, onion, jalapenos, and sour cream and eat straight from the bag.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Texas and New Mexico ballpark and gas-station classic, often served right in the split chip bag. Add the chili just before eating so the chips keep some crunch: a Frito pie built too early is a bag of mush.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Frito%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Frito Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Frito Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Frito%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Corn","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Kidney/Pinto beans","Stock/Broth","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"texas-beef-fajitas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g skirt steak"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the marinade: 120ml lime juice, 60ml oil, 4 garlic cloves, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp chili powder, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bell peppers, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"Guacamole, pico de gallo, sour cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the skirt steak in the lime, oil, garlic, and spices 2-4 hours.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill the steak over very high heat 3-4 min a side for medium-rare, then rest it.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill or sear the onions and peppers until charred and soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the steak thin against the grain: this is essential for tender fajitas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile onto warm tortillas with the vegetables and toppings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fajita means little belt, after the skirt steak cut ranch hands were given. High heat and slicing against the grain are the two rules: skirt steak is stringy along the grain and must be cut across it, or every bite is chewy.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","bright","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, acid holding everything up to the light, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Beef%20Fajitas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Beef Fajitas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Beef Fajitas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Beef%20Fajitas","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Bell pepper","Chiles","Lime","Sour cream/Crema","Bread","Flour","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"texas-kolaches-and-klobasniky","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 60g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 2 egg yolks, 85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For klobasniky: sausage links wrapped in dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"For kolaches: fruit or cream cheese filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"Posypka crumb topping"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make a rich yeasted dough and rise 1 hour until doubled.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"For klobasniky, wrap a sausage link in a piece of dough and seal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For kolaches, shape balls, press wells, and fill with fruit or cheese; top with posypka crumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set on trays and rise 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 12-15 min until golden.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false}],"note":"Czech immigrants settled central Texas and made West, Texas the kolache capital; the savory sausage-filled version is a klobasnik, not a kolache, a distinction Texans enforce. Seal the sausage dough well or it splits open in the oven.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Kolaches%20and%20Klobasniky%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Kolaches and Klobasniky"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Kolaches and Klobasniky","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Kolaches%20and%20Klobasniky","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"texas-pecan-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g Texas pecans"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml dark corn syrup or cane syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp bourbon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scatter the pecans in the shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, brown sugar, syrup, melted butter, bourbon, vanilla, and salt until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the pecans; they rise to form the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 50-60 min until set with a slight jiggle.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely before slicing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pecan is the Texas state tree and the pie a point of pride. Toast the pecans eight minutes first for deeper flavor, and cover the crust edge with foil if it browns before the filling sets.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Pecan%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Pecan Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Pecan Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Pecan%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"texas-smoked-sausage","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg coarse-ground pork and beef (70/30)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g curing salt number 1 (6.25 percent sodium nitrite), 0.25 percent of the meat, weighed on a scale reading to 0.01 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hog casings, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"White bread, pickles, onion, and mustard to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the coarse-ground meat with all the seasonings and the cold water until it just binds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stuff into the soaked hog casings and twist into links; do not pack too tight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest in the fridge overnight to develop flavor and dry the casing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smoke at 110C over post oak 3-4 hours until the internal temperature reaches 71C (160F).","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve Central Texas meat-market style: on butcher paper with white bread, pickles, onion, and mustard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The German and Czech meat markets of Central Texas built the barbecue tradition, and sausage was their signature. Coarse grind and post oak are the Texas markers, and the overnight rest is what lets the casing dry so it snaps.","equipment":["Thermometer","Smoker"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking","Brining & curing","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Low & slow smoking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Smoked%20Sausage%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Smoked Sausage"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Smoked Sausage","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Smoked%20Sausage","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"casings-molds-and-the-curing-chamber","term":"Casings, Molds & the Curing Chamber"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Sausage","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Bread","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking","the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"texas-sheet-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE CAKE"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"225g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g cocoa"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE ICING"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g cocoa"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 tbsp milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pecans, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, sugar, soda, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the butter, cocoa, and water, then pour over the dry ingredients and mix. Beat in the eggs, buttermilk, and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a large sheet pan and bake at 175C for 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"For the icing, boil the butter, cocoa, and milk, then beat in the icing sugar, vanilla, and pecans.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the warm icing over the warm cake and let it set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A thin, fast chocolate cake made in a big sheet pan for a crowd, church suppers and funerals alike. The icing goes on while both cake and icing are warm, so it soaks in and sets to a fudgy glaze rather than a frosting.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Sheet%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Sheet Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Sheet Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Sheet%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting"]},{"slug":"texas-migas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 corn tortillas, cut in strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 jalapeno, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g Monterey Jack, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Warm tortillas, salsa, and refried beans to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the tortilla strips in the oil until crisp and golden; leave them in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion and jalapeno and cook until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomato, then pour in the beaten eggs and scramble softly with the crisp tortilla strips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the cheese as the eggs set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with warm tortillas, salsa, and refried beans.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Migas means crumbs, and the dish rescues day-old tortillas by frying them crisp and folding them into eggs. Fry the strips until genuinely crisp before the eggs go in: they soften in the eggs but should keep some chew.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Migas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Migas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Migas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Migas","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Corn","Cheese (any)","Bread"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"texas-buttermilk-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unbaked 23cm pie shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Nutmeg to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the sugar and flour together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs, then the buttermilk, melted butter, lemon, vanilla, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the shell and dust with nutmeg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 45-55 min until the top is golden and just set with a slight wobble.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely before slicing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A thrifty custard pie made from pantry staples, a fixture of the Texas and Southern table. The lemon juice cuts the richness and keeps it from being merely sweet; pull it while the center still moves, as it sets on cooling.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Texas%20Buttermilk%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Texas Buttermilk Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Texas Buttermilk Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Texas%20Buttermilk%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-green-chile-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g pork shoulder, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6-8 roasted Hatch green chiles, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Mexican oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Warm flour tortillas to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the pork cubes hard in the oil; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic, add the cumin and oregano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the pork with the stock, chile, and potatoes and simmer 90 min until the pork is tender and the potatoes soft.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mash a few potatoes against the pot to thicken the stew.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season and serve with warm flour tortillas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hatch green chile is New Mexico's obsession and its official state question is red or green. Roasted, peeled Hatch chile is non-negotiable: its smoky heat is the soul of the stew, and no other pepper substitutes for it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Green%20Chile%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Green Chile Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Green Chile Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Green%20Chile%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Chiles","Oregano","Bread","Flour","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-carne-adovada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg pork shoulder, in large cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 dried New Mexico red chiles, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Mexican oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the dried chiles briefly, soak in hot water 20 min, then blend with the garlic, cumin, oregano, salt, and some soaking water into a smooth red sauce.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the pork cubes in the red chile sauce and marinate at least 4 hours, ideally overnight.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn everything into a Dutch oven with the stock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Braise at 160C for 2.5-3 hours until the pork is falling apart and the sauce is deep and thick.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the vinegar and season. Serve with rice, beans, or tortillas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pork marinated and braised in pure red chile: no tomatoes, just chile, garlic, and time. Use whole dried New Mexico chiles, not powder; the long marinade lets the chile penetrate the meat, which is the whole point of adovada.","equipment":["Dutch oven"],"techniques":["Braising","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Soaking dried goods back to life","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","earthy","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Carne%20Adovada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Carne Adovada"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Carne Adovada","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Carne%20Adovada","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Garlic","Chiles","Oregano","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-posole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried posole (hominy) or 2 cans hominy"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g pork shoulder, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 dried red chiles, or 6 green chiles for green posole"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Mexican oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Garnishes: shredded cabbage, radish, lime, oregano"}],"steps":[{"text":"If using dried posole, simmer it 2-3 hours until the kernels burst open like flowers; canned needs only warming.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the pork, then add the onion and garlic and cook until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the soaked red chiles into a paste (or chop the green chiles) and add with the oregano and bay.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the posole and stock and simmer 90 min until the pork is tender and the broth rich.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve with cabbage, radish, lime, and oregano to add at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Posole is the New Mexican celebration stew, made for Christmas and feast days. The dried hominy must simmer until each kernel bursts open: that blooming is doneness, and canned hominy, while faster, never quite matches the texture.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Posole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Posole"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Posole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Posole","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Cabbage","Corn","Radish/Turnip","Lime","Oregano","Bay leaf","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-sopaipillas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lard or shortening"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Honey to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, baking powder, and salt, cut in the lard, then add the water to make a soft dough. Rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough thin, 5mm, and cut into squares or triangles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the pieces one or two at a time, spooning hot oil over so they puff into hollow pillows, 1-2 min a side.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and serve hot with honey to pour into the hollow center.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The hollow puff is the whole magic: roll the dough thin and keep the oil hot so each piece balloons in seconds. In New Mexico they arrive with every meal, drizzled with honey to cool the chile heat.","equipment":["Rolling pin","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Sopaipillas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Sopaipillas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Sopaipillas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Sopaipillas","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour","Honey"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-red-chile-enchiladas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 corn tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the red sauce: 10 dried New Mexico red chiles, 4 garlic cloves, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp oregano, 500ml stock, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g cheese (longhorn cheddar), grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs (for stacked, fried on top)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Shredded lettuce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast and soak the dried chiles, then blend with the garlic, cumin, oregano, stock, and salt into a smooth sauce. Simmer 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm each tortilla in oil until pliable, then dip in the red sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"New Mexico serves them stacked, not rolled: layer tortilla, cheese, onion, sauce, and repeat into a stack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top the stack with more sauce and cheese and bake at 190C for 10 min until bubbling.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Crown each stack with a fried egg and shredded lettuce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"New Mexico stacks its enchiladas flat rather than rolling them, and crowns them with a fried egg. The red sauce is pure chile, no tomato, so toast and soak whole dried pods, and let the sauce simmer to lose its raw edge.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Red%20Chile%20Enchiladas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Red Chile Enchiladas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Red Chile Enchiladas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Red%20Chile%20Enchiladas","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Lettuce/Greens","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Bread","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-biscochitos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"225g lard (or shortening for a vegetarian version)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp anise seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp brandy or orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"375g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"For coating: 100g sugar, 1 tbsp cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the lard and sugar until fluffy, beat in the egg, anise seed, and brandy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the flour, baking powder, and salt to a firm dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll out and cut into shapes, traditionally fleur-de-lis.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 10-12 min until set but pale.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toss the warm cookies in cinnamon sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The official state cookie of New Mexico, a lard-shortbread scented with anise. Lard is traditional and gives the flaky, tender crumb butter cannot; the anise and brandy are the flavors that mark it as New Mexican.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Biscochitos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Biscochitos"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Biscochitos","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Biscochitos","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Orange","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-chile-rellenos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 roasted Hatch green chiles, peeled, slit, seeds removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g Monterey Jack or asadero cheese, in batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour, plus more for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: red or green chile sauce, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stuff each roasted chile with a baton of cheese and pinch the slit closed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust the stuffed chiles lightly with flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the egg whites to stiff peaks, then fold in the yolks and 3 tbsp flour for a light batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip each chile in the batter and fry in 180C oil, spooning oil over, until golden and puffed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and serve smothered in warm chile sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The whipped-egg batter is what makes a relleno light rather than heavy. Fold the yolks in gently to keep the air. Roast and peel real Hatch chiles; the smoky flesh is the whole reason to make the dish.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Chile%20Rellenos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Chile Rellenos"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Chile Rellenos","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Chile%20Rellenos","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Chiles","Cheese (any)","Flour"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-calabacitas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 zucchini or Mexican squash, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil or butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 roasted green chiles, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ears corn, kernels cut off"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream or milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cheese, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic in the oil until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the diced squash and cook 8 min until it begins to soften.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the corn and green chile and cook 5 min more.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the cream and cheese and cook until melted and bound.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season and serve as a side or a vegetarian main.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A squash, corn, and green chile side that turns up on every New Mexican plate in late summer. Do not overcook the squash: it should keep some bite, and green chile plus corn is the flavor that makes it local.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Calabacitas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Calabacitas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Calabacitas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Calabacitas","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Zucchini","Squash/Pumpkin","Corn","Butter","Milk","Cream","Cheese (any)","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-green-chile-cheeseburger","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 roasted green chiles, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Mustard and pickles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Form the beef into four patties and season well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle over medium-high heat 3-4 min a side.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pile chopped roasted green chile on each patty and lay cheese over to melt it into the chile.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the buns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build with the chile-cheese patty, onion, mustard, and pickles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"New Mexico claims the green chile cheeseburger as its own and holds a trail of shrines to it. The chile must be roasted Hatch, chopped and piled on hot so the cheese melts down into it: a raw pepper misses the smoky point entirely.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Green%20Chile%20Cheeseburger%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Green Chile Cheeseburger"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Green Chile Cheeseburger","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Green%20Chile%20Cheeseburger","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Chiles","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"new-mexico-natillas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"750ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg whites"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cinnamon to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk a little of the milk with the yolks, sugar, cornstarch, and salt into a smooth slurry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the rest of the milk, then temper in the slurry and cook, stirring, until thickened.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat add the vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the egg whites to soft peaks and fold them through the warm custard for lightness.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill and serve dusted with cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A soft stirred custard of Spanish New Mexico, lightened with folded egg white. Fold the whites in while the custard is still warm so they lighten it without cooking to lumps: the texture should be loose, almost pourable.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","warm-spiced","floral","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, warm spice humming through the middle, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=New%20Mexico%20Natillas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of New Mexico Natillas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on New Mexico Natillas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20New%20Mexico%20Natillas","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"sonoran-hot-dog","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 hot dogs, wrapped in bacon"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 bolillo or bakery-style buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g pinto beans, warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the crema: mayonnaise, mustard, and jalapeno salsa"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grilled scallions and jalapenos to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wrap each hot dog spirally in bacon and secure the ends.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle over medium heat, turning, until the bacon is crisp all around and the dog is hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the split-top bolillo buns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the bacon dog in the bun and top with pinto beans, tomato, and onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drizzle with mayonnaise, mustard, and salsa and serve with a grilled scallion and jalapeno.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Tucson and Sonora border creation: the bacon-wrapped dog in a fluffy bolillo, loaded like a taco. Wrap the bacon spirally and crisp it fully; a limp bacon wrap slides off and misses the smoky point of the whole thing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","herb-fresh","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sonoran%20Hot%20Dog%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sonoran Hot Dog"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Sonoran Hot Dog","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Sonoran%20Hot%20Dog","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Onion","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Sour cream/Crema","Bread","Kidney/Pinto beans","Mustard","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"arizona-chimichanga","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 large flour tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g shredded beef or chicken, cooked in red chile"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g refried beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cheese, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For topping: red or green chile sauce, sour cream, guacamole, lettuce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the tortillas so they fold without cracking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread refried beans, then pile shredded chile meat and cheese in the center of each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold into tight sealed burritos, tucking the ends in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry seam-side down in 180C oil, turning, until deep gold and crisp all over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and smother with chile sauce, sour cream, guacamole, and lettuce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tucson claims the chimichanga was born when a burrito was dropped in the fryer. Seal the ends tightly and fry seam-side down first so it holds together: an unsealed chimichanga bursts and spills its filling into the oil.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arizona%20Chimichanga%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arizona Chimichanga"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arizona Chimichanga","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arizona%20Chimichanga","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Chiles","Lettuce/Greens","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"arizona-cheese-crisp","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 large flour tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g Monterey Jack or longhorn cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chopped green chile (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salsa to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brush a large flour tortilla lightly with melted butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay it flat on a hot comal or in the oven and scatter cheese right to the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add green chile if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until the tortilla is crisp and browned and the cheese is bubbling and lacy at the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into wedges and serve flat with salsa.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"An Arizona invention: a flat, open-faced crisped tortilla under bubbling cheese, distinct from a quesadilla, which is folded. Take the cheese right to the edge so it caramelizes against the pan, and serve it crisp before it softens.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arizona%20Cheese%20Crisp%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arizona Cheese Crisp"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arizona Cheese Crisp","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arizona%20Cheese%20Crisp","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal"]},{"slug":"arizona-carne-seca","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg beef flank or round, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of 4 limes"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"For cooking: 3 tbsp oil, 1 onion sliced, 2 tomatoes, 3 green chiles, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour tortillas to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the thin beef slices in lime, garlic, and salt, then dry them, traditionally in the desert sun, or in a low oven or dehydrator until leathery.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shred or chop the dried beef finely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Saute the onion, tomato, and green chile in the oil until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the shredded carne seca and cook until it absorbs the flavors and heats through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve piled in warm flour tortillas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tucson's carne seca is beef dried in the Sonoran sun, then rehydrated by cooking with chile and tomato. El Charro Cafe still dries it in a cage on the roof. The drying concentrates the beef flavor into something jerky-deep.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arizona%20Carne%20Seca%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arizona Carne Seca"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arizona Carne Seca","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arizona%20Carne%20Seca","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Bread","Flour","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"arizona-green-corn-tamales","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 ears fresh sweet corn"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g masa harina"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml lard or vegetable shortening"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g Monterey Jack, in strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 roasted green chiles, in strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fresh corn husks"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the kernels from the corn and grind or blend them coarse; reserve the fresh husks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the lard, then work in the ground fresh corn, masa harina, salt, and sugar into a soft dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the corn masa on each husk, add a strip of cheese and green chile, and fold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand the tamales upright, packed close, in a steamer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam 60-75 min until the masa sets and pulls cleanly from the husk.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":false}],"note":"Made only in high summer when fresh sweet corn is in, these Sonoran-style tamales use ground fresh corn rather than dried masa alone, giving a sweeter, softer texture. The cheese and green chile inside are the Arizona signature.","equipment":["Steamer"],"techniques":["Working with masa","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arizona%20Green%20Corn%20Tamales%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arizona Green Corn Tamales"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arizona Green Corn Tamales","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arizona%20Green%20Corn%20Tamales","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Corn","Cheese (any)","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"arizona-prickly-pear-margarita","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60ml prickly pear syrup (from the fruit of the nopal cactus)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml tequila blanco"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml orange liqueur"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lime and salt for the rim"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub a lime around the glass rim and dip in salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine the tequila, lime juice, orange liqueur, and prickly pear syrup in a shaker with ice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shake hard until cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain over fresh ice in the salted glass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Garnish with a lime wheel; the drink glows a deep magenta from the cactus fruit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Prickly pear fruit, the tuna of the nopal cactus, grows across the Arizona desert and turns the margarita a vivid magenta. The fruit is more floral than sweet. Balance it with enough lime so the drink stays tart, not candied.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arizona%20Prickly%20Pear%20Margarita%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arizona Prickly Pear Margarita"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arizona Prickly Pear Margarita","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arizona%20Prickly%20Pear%20Margarita","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tequila-and-mezcal","term":"Tequila & Mezcal"}],"pantryItems":["Lime","Orange"]},{"slug":"arizona-fry-bread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml warm milk or water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Honey, powdered sugar, or savory toppings"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, baking powder, and salt, then add the liquid to make a soft dough. Rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide and pat each piece into a thin round, pressing a hole in the center to help it cook evenly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry each round 1-2 min a side until puffed and golden.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve hot with honey and powdered sugar, or as the base for a Navajo taco.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fry bread carries a difficult history as a food of survival on the Navajo reservation, born of government commodity rations. It is now central to gatherings across Arizona: fry it the moment it is shaped so it puffs.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arizona%20Fry%20Bread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arizona Fry Bread"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arizona Fry Bread","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arizona%20Fry%20Bread","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Bread","Flour","Honey","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"arizona-chiltepin-salsa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp dried chiltepin peppers (the wild desert chile)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fresh cilantro"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char the tomatoes, garlic, and onion on a comal until blackened in spots.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the tiny chiltepin peppers briefly; they are fierce, so use fewer if unsure.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend or crush everything in a molcajete with the lime and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in chopped cilantro.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 20 min for the flavors to meld before serving.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"The chiltepin is the wild mother of all chiles, growing on the Arizona-Sonora border and packing serious heat in a tiny package. Use them sparingly, they are far hotter than they look, and char the tomatoes for smoky depth.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The molcajete","Tortillas on the comal","Charring over open flame","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The molcajete","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=molcajete%20salsa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arizona%20Chiltepin%20Salsa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arizona Chiltepin Salsa"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arizona Chiltepin Salsa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arizona%20Chiltepin%20Salsa","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chile-fluency-fresh-and-dried","term":"Chile Fluency (Fresh & Dried)"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Cilantro","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"arizona-nopalitos-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 nopal cactus pads, spines removed, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 jalapenos, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g queso fresco, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro, salt, and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the diced nopales in salted water 10-15 min until tender; they release a sticky liquid, so rinse well after draining.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine the cooled nopales with the tomato, onion, and jalapeno.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress with olive oil, lime, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the cilantro.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with crumbled queso fresco and serve cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nopales, the pads of the prickly pear cactus, taste like a tart green bean and are a desert staple. Rinse them well after boiling to wash away the mucilage they release, just as you would defeat okra's slime.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arizona%20Nopalitos%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arizona Nopalitos Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arizona Nopalitos Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arizona%20Nopalitos%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"queso-fresco","term":"Queso Fresco"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Cilantro","Cheese (any)"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"arizona-sonoran-enchiladas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the masa cakes: 300g masa harina, 250ml warm water, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the red sauce: 8 dried red chiles, 3 garlic cloves, 1 tsp cumin, 500ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g longhorn cheese, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the masa harina, water, and salt into a dough and pat into thick round cakes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the masa cakes on a comal or in oil until set and lightly crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast and soak the dried chiles, then blend with the garlic, cumin, and stock into a red sauce; simmer 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip the masa cakes in the sauce and layer with cheese and onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 10 min until bubbling.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Sonoran-style enchiladas use thick fresh masa cakes rather than tortillas, a distinctive Arizona border form. Fry the masa cakes until they hold together before saucing, or they fall apart under the red chile.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Working with masa","Tortillas on the comal","Soaking dried goods back to life","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arizona%20Sonoran%20Enchiladas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arizona Sonoran Enchiladas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Arizona Sonoran Enchiladas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Arizona%20Sonoran%20Enchiladas","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Cheese (any)","Cornmeal/Polenta","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","tortillas-on-the-comal"]},{"slug":"nevada-basque-lamb-shoulder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg lamb shoulder"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head garlic, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the lamb well and brown all sides hard in the oil in a Dutch oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions, garlic, and pepper in the fat until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the wine and reduce, then the tomatoes, stock, bay, and thyme.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the lamb, cover, and braise at 150C for 3 hours until falling off the bone.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest, skim the fat, and reduce the sauce. Serve family style with beans and bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Basque sheepherders settled northern Nevada, and their boarding-house hotels still serve family-style feasts. Lamb is the centerpiece: brown it hard for the crust, then braise it slow, and serve it the Basque way: family style, passed around a long table.","equipment":["Dutch oven"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Basque%20Lamb%20Shoulder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Basque Lamb Shoulder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Basque Lamb Shoulder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Basque%20Lamb%20Shoulder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Thyme","Bay leaf","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["braising","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"nevada-shrimp-cocktail","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g small bay shrimp, cooked and chilled"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the cocktail sauce: 200ml ketchup, 3 tbsp horseradish, 1 tbsp lemon juice, 1 tsp Worcestershire, dash hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"Tall sundae glasses"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill the cooked bay shrimp thoroughly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the ketchup, horseradish, lemon, Worcestershire, and hot sauce into a sharp cocktail sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon a little sauce into the bottom of each tall glass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the shrimp in and top with more sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve ice-cold with a lemon wedge and a long spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Las Vegas shrimp cocktail, served in a sundae glass, was a casino loss-leader for decades, famously fifty cents at the Golden Gate. Use small sweet bay shrimp and keep everything ice cold: it is a dish about chill and sharpness, nothing more.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Shrimp%20Cocktail%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Shrimp Cocktail"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Shrimp Cocktail","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Shrimp%20Cocktail","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Lemon","Hot sauce"]},{"slug":"nevada-basque-chorizo-and-beans","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g Basque-style chorizo, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried pinto or red beans, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the chorizo in a heavy pot until it renders; lift out and reserve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, garlic, and pepper in the fat until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the paprika, tomatoes, drained beans, and stock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 90 min until the beans are creamy, then return the chorizo and cook 20 min more.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mash a few beans to thicken and season well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beans and chorizo are the everyday side of the Basque boarding-house table in Nevada. Salt the beans only near the end, as early salt keeps the skins tough, and let the chorizo fat flavor the whole pot from the start.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Basque%20Chorizo%20and%20Beans%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Basque Chorizo and Beans"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Basque Chorizo and Beans","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Basque%20Chorizo%20and%20Beans","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Kidney/Pinto beans","Stock/Broth","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"nevada-picon-punch","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"45ml Amer Picon (or Torani Amer)"},{"kind":"item","text":"15ml grenadine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Soda water"},{"kind":"item","text":"15ml brandy float"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon twist"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fill a stemmed glass with ice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the grenadine and Amer Picon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with soda water and stir gently.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Float the brandy on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Garnish with a lemon twist and serve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The unofficial cocktail of Basque Nevada, served in every sheepherder hotel bar before dinner. The bitter Amer Picon is the backbone; the brandy float on top is traditionally sipped through first, before the drink is stirred.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Picon%20Punch%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Picon Punch"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Picon Punch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Picon%20Punch","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon"]},{"slug":"nevada-awful-awful-burger","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 large buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices American cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, onion, pickle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Special sauce: mayonnaise, ketchup, relish, mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fries to pile on"}],"steps":[{"text":"Form the beef into large patties and season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle over medium-high heat 4 min a side, melting cheese in the last minute.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toast the buns and spread with special sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build with the patty, lettuce, tomato, onion, and pickle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with a pile of fries; the Reno diner tradition calls it awful big, awful good.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Awful Awful is a Reno casino-coffee-shop legend from the Nugget, named for being awful big and awful good. It is a diner burger writ large: pile it high and serve it with a mountain of fries, the way the graveyard-shift crowd expects.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Awful%20Awful%20Burger%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Awful Awful Burger"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Awful Awful Burger","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Awful%20Awful%20Burger","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Lettuce/Greens","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mustard","Mayonnaise","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"nevada-pine-nut-cookies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g pine nuts (from the single-leaf pinyon)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g almond paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg whites"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Break up the almond paste and beat with the sugar until crumbly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the egg whites and salt to a sticky dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll spoonfuls into balls and press each into a bowl of pine nuts to coat all over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set on lined trays and flatten slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 165C for 15-18 min until golden. Dust with icing sugar when cool.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pinyon pine nuts are a native Nevada harvest, gathered by the Great Basin tribes for millennia. Their rich, resinous flavor coats these soft almond cookies: press them on generously, as the nuts are the whole character.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Pine%20Nut%20Cookies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Pine Nut Cookies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Pine Nut Cookies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Pine%20Nut%20Cookies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"nevada-trout-almondine","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole trout, cleaned, or 8 fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g flour, seasoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sliced almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the trout dry and dredge lightly in seasoned flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in half the butter over medium-high heat 3-4 min a side until golden and the fish flakes. Keep warm.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Wipe the pan, add the rest of the butter, and toast the almonds until golden.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the lemon and parsley and swirl into a pan sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the almond butter over the trout and serve with lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Trout from the Sierra Nevada streams and Lake Tahoe, dressed in the classic almondine style. Toast the almonds until genuinely golden and watch the butter: brown butter is the goal, but a second too long turns it black and bitter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brown butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brown%20butter%20beurre%20noisette%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Trout%20Almondine%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Trout Almondine"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Trout Almondine","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Trout%20Almondine","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Flour","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"nevada-beans-and-linguica","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g linguica or Portuguese sausage, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g dried white beans, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 potato, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g kale, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the linguica in a pot; lift out and reserve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, garlic, and carrot in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the beans, potato, paprika, and stock and simmer 90 min until the beans are tender.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the kale and the linguica and cook 15 min more.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mash a few beans to thicken, and season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Portuguese and Basque immigrants both shaped Nevada's ranch tables, and this hearty bean and sausage soup reflects it. The smoky linguica flavors the whole pot: brown it first to render its paprika-rich fat into the base.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Beans%20and%20Linguica%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Beans and Linguica"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Beans and Linguica","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Beans%20and%20Linguica","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Carrot","Kale/Collards","White beans","Stock/Broth","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"nevada-chile-verde","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg pork shoulder, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g tomatillos, husked"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green chiles, roasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cilantro, lime, salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour tortillas"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the pork cubes hard in the oil; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Char the tomatillos and blend with the roasted green chiles, garlic, and cumin into a green sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, add the green sauce, and cook 10 min until it darkens.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the pork with the stock and simmer 90 min until tender and the sauce thick.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Finish with cilantro and lime and serve with tortillas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Green chile pork is a staple across the mining and ranching towns of Nevada. Char the tomatillos before blending: that roasting is what gives the sauce its depth instead of a raw, sour edge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Charring over open flame","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Chile%20Verde%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Chile Verde"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Chile Verde","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Chile%20Verde","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Tomatillo","Cilantro","Bread","Flour","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"nevada-date-shake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 Medjool dates, pitted"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cold milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 scoops vanilla ice cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the dates in a little of the milk 10 min to soften.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Blend the dates with the milk until smooth, scraping down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the ice cream, vanilla, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend just until thick and creamy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a tall glass and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dates thrive in the desert oases of the Southwest, and the date shake is a roadside institution across the Mojave into Nevada. Soak the dates first so they blend smooth: un-soaked dates leave chewy bits that clog the straw.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nevada%20Date%20Shake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nevada Date Shake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Nevada Date Shake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Nevada%20Date%20Shake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Milk","Cream","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"utah-funeral-potatoes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg frozen shredded hash browns, thawed"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"For topping: 100g crushed cornflakes, 3 tbsp melted butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion in the butter, stir in the flour, and cook 2 min. Whisk in the milk and simmer to a sauce.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat stir in the sour cream and two-thirds of the cheese, then season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the hash browns and turn into a buttered dish. Top with the rest of the cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the crushed cornflakes with melted butter and scatter over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C for 40-45 min until bubbling and the cornflake top is golden.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false}],"note":"A cornerstone of the Latter-day Saint potluck, so named because they appear at every funeral luncheon. The buttered cornflake topping is the signature crunch: do not skip it, and use real shredded cheese, not pre-grated, so the sauce stays smooth.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Funeral%20Potatoes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Funeral Potatoes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Funeral Potatoes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Funeral%20Potatoes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Potato","Butter","Milk","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"utah-fry-sauce-burger","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the fry sauce: 150g mayonnaise, 100g ketchup, 1 tbsp pickle brine, 1/2 tsp paprika, pinch of garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, onion, pickle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fries to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the mayonnaise, ketchup, pickle brine, paprika, and garlic powder into fry sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Form the beef into patties, season, and griddle 3-4 min a side, melting cheese in the last minute.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the buns and spread generously with fry sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build with the patty, lettuce, tomato, onion, and pickle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with fries and extra fry sauce for dipping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fry sauce is a Utah institution, popularized by the Arctic Circle chain in the 1950s and beloved across the state. The exact ratio is argued endlessly: the pickle brine and paprika are what lift it above plain mayo-ketchup.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Fry%20Sauce%20Burger%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Fry Sauce Burger"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Fry Sauce Burger","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Fry%20Sauce%20Burger","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Lettuce/Greens","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mayonnaise","Pickles","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"utah-scones-with-honey-butter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For honey butter: 115g butter softened, 60ml honey, pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, yeast, sugar, and salt, then add the milk and melted butter to a soft dough. Rise 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 1cm thick and cut into squares.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the squares 1-2 min a side until puffed and golden.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whip the honey butter and serve slathered on the hot scones.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Utah scone is not a British scone: it is fried yeast dough, closer to a sopaipilla or fry bread, served with honey butter. Fry them the moment they are cut so they puff into hollow pillows before the dough over-proofs.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Scones%20with%20Honey%20Butter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Scones with Honey Butter"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Scones with Honey Butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Scones%20with%20Honey%20Butter","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Flour","Honey","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"utah-green-jell-o-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 packets lime Jell-O"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g crushed pineapple, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cottage cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g whipped topping"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g grated carrot or chopped walnuts (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the Jell-O in the boiling water, then stir in the cold water. Chill until syrupy but not set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the drained pineapple and cottage cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the whipped topping for a creamy set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add grated carrot or walnuts if you like.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a mold and chill until firm, at least 4 hours.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Utah is the unofficial Jell-O capital of America, and lime green is the state's signature. Chill the Jell-O until syrupy before folding in the add-ins, or they sink to the bottom instead of suspending through the mold.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Green%20Jell-O%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Green Jell-O Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Green Jell-O Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Green%20Jell-O%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Carrot","Lime","Pineapple","Cheese (any)","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"utah-pastrami-burger","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g pastrami, sliced and warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices Swiss or American cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"For fry sauce: mayonnaise, ketchup, pickle brine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pickles"}],"steps":[{"text":"Form and season the beef patties and griddle 3-4 min a side.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the pastrami on the griddle until the edges crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt cheese over the patty in the last minute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the warm pastrami on the cheeseburger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set on a fry-sauced bun with lettuce, tomato, onion, and pickle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Greek-American Utah creation: a cheeseburger crowned with a pile of hot pastrami, found at Crown Burgers and its many imitators across Salt Lake. Warm the pastrami until the edges crisp; cold deli pastrami just sits there limp.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Pastrami%20Burger%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Pastrami Burger"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Pastrami Burger","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Pastrami%20Burger","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Lettuce/Greens","Cheese (any)","Bread","Mayonnaise","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"utah-raspberry-shake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g fresh raspberries (Bear Lake, ideally)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cold milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 scoops vanilla ice cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the raspberries with the milk and sugar until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain out the seeds if you prefer, or leave them for texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the ice cream and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend just until thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a tall glass and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bear Lake on the Utah-Idaho border is famous for its raspberries, and the raspberry shake is the summer tradition there. Use ripe berries and a touch of sugar to balance their tartness: great raspberries need very little help.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Raspberry%20Shake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Raspberry Shake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Raspberry Shake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Raspberry%20Shake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Berries","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"]},{"slug":"utah-dutch-oven-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, cut in pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 potatoes, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season and brown the chicken in a cast-iron Dutch oven over campfire coals or in the oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes, carrots, and onions around the chicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the stock and Worcestershire and season with paprika and thyme.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and set coals on the lid, or bake at 190C, for 60-75 min until the chicken and vegetables are tender.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest and serve straight from the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dutch-oven cooking is a Utah pioneer tradition, and the cast-iron pot is the official state cooking pot. For the authentic method, set coals on the lid as well as underneath so the heat surrounds the pot and browns the top.","equipment":["Dutch oven","Skillet","Grill"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Dutch%20Oven%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Dutch Oven Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Dutch Oven Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Dutch%20Oven%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Potato","Carrot","Thyme","Stock/Broth","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"utah-navajo-taco","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For fry bread: 400g flour, 2 tbsp baking powder, 1 tsp salt, 300ml warm milk, oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g ground beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g pinto beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 2 tbsp chili powder, 1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, cheese, salsa"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix and rest the fry bread dough, then pat into rounds and fry until puffed and golden.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the beef with the onion and spices, then add the beans and simmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the beef and beans onto each fry bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with lettuce, tomato, cheese, and salsa.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve open-faced.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Southern Utah is Navajo country, and the Navajo taco on fry bread is a fixture from Bluff to Monument Valley. Fry the bread the moment it is shaped so it puffs, and build the taco just before eating so the bread stays crisp underneath.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Navajo%20Taco%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Navajo Taco"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Navajo Taco","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Navajo%20Taco","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Lettuce/Greens","Milk","Cheese (any)","Bread","Flour","Kidney/Pinto beans","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"utah-honey-wheat-rolls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g whole wheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Melted butter and honey for brushing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flours, yeast, and salt. Add the honey, butter, milk, and egg and knead 10 min to a smooth dough.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rise, covered, 1 hour until doubled.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into rolls, shape, and set in a buttered pan. Rise 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 18-20 min until golden.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brush with melted butter and a little honey as they come out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Utah's honey and wheat both feature in this soft dinner roll, a Sunday-table staple. Local honey in the dough and brushed on top after baking gives a floral sweetness: do not rush the second rise, or the rolls bake dense.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Honey%20Wheat%20Rolls%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Honey Wheat Rolls"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Honey Wheat Rolls","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Honey%20Wheat%20Rolls","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Honey"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"utah-peach-cobbler-dutch-oven","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg fresh peaches, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the batter: 115g butter, 190g flour, 200g sugar, 2 tsp baking powder, 240ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ice cream to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the peaches with sugar, cornstarch, and cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter in a Dutch oven, then whisk the flour, sugar, baking powder, and milk into a batter and pour it over the butter. Do not stir.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the peaches over the batter; do not stir.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and bake with coals above and below, or at 190C, for 45-55 min until the cake rises around the fruit and browns.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve warm from the pot with ice cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Utah's Dixie region in the south grows fine peaches, and cobbler in the Dutch oven is a camp and reunion tradition. As with any batter cobbler, the trick is not stirring: the cake floats up through the fruit as it bakes.","equipment":["Dutch oven","Grill"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Utah%20Peach%20Cobbler%2C%20Dutch%20Oven%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Utah Peach Cobbler, Dutch Oven"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Utah Peach Cobbler, Dutch Oven","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Utah%20Peach%20Cobbler%2C%20Dutch%20Oven","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Stone fruit","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"]},{"slug":"colorado-green-chili","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg pork shoulder, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil or lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 roasted Pueblo or Hatch green chiles, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flour tortillas or smothered burritos"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the pork cubes hard in the oil; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic, sprinkle in the flour, and cook 2 min to a light roux.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the green chile, tomatoes, cumin, and oregano and cook 5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the pork with the stock and simmer 90 min until the pork is tender and the chili thickened.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season and serve as a stew or smothered over burritos and eggs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Colorado green chili is thicker and often pork-and-tomato based, distinct from New Mexico's version; a roux gives it body. Pueblo chiles are the Colorado pride; the chili is poured over everything from breakfast burritos to fries across the Front Range.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","fiery","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Green%20Chili%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Green Chili"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Green Chili","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Green%20Chili","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Oregano","Bread","Flour","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"colorado-rocky-mountain-trout","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole rainbow trout, cleaned"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, 1/2 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 rashers bacon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon in a cast-iron skillet until crisp; lift out and reserve the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the trout in the cornmeal, flour, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in the bacon fat and half the butter 3-4 min a side until the crust is gold and the fish flakes.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the rest of the butter, lemon, and parsley to make a pan sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the trout with the crumbled bacon and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rainbow trout from the cold Rocky Mountain streams is Colorado's classic catch, often cooked streamside. Cornmeal gives the crisp crust and bacon fat the flavor. Do not overcook trout, which is lean and flakes dry within a minute of done.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Rocky%20Mountain%20Trout%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Rocky Mountain Trout"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Rocky Mountain Trout","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Rocky%20Mountain%20Trout","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"colorado-denver-omelette","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g ham, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toast to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Saute the ham, green pepper, and onion in a little butter until softened and lightly browned.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs with salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt butter in an omelette pan, pour in the eggs, and let them set at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the ham and vegetable mixture and the cheese to one half.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold over, slide onto a plate, and serve with toast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Denver omelette (or Western omelette) has roots in the chuck-wagon and railroad cooks of the frontier, some say as a way to freshen aging eggs with ham and onion. Cook the filling first so it is soft and browned before it meets the delicate eggs.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"dish":{"label":"Pépin’s omelet lesson","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1XoCQm5JSQ","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Denver%20Omelette%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Denver Omelette"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Denver Omelette","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Denver%20Omelette","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Bell pepper","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cheddar"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"colorado-palisade-peach-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry for a double-crust 23cm pie"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg Palisade peaches, peeled and sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp almond extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch and peel the peaches, then toss with sugar, cornstarch, lemon, cinnamon, almond extract, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the lined pie plate and dot with butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay a lattice top, brush with egg, and dust with sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 20 min, then 190C for 40 min until the juices bubble thickly.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool 4 hours before slicing.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Palisade, on Colorado's Western Slope, grows some of the country's finest peaches in its high desert orchards. A drop of almond extract amplifies the peach flavor, but no more than a quarter teaspoon, or it tips into marzipan.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Palisade%20Peach%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Palisade Peach Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Palisade Peach Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Palisade%20Peach%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Stone fruit","Butter","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"colorado-bison-burger","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g ground bison"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 roasted green chiles, chopped (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for the griddle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lettuce, tomato, pickle"}],"steps":[{"text":"Form the bison into patties and season: handle it gently, as lean bison toughens if overworked.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle over medium-high heat 3 min a side, no more; bison is lean and dries out past medium.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt cheese over the patty and add green chile if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the buns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build with the patty, onion, lettuce, tomato, and pickle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bison ranching is big across Colorado's plains, and the leaner, sweeter meat makes a fine burger. The key is not to overcook it: bison has far less fat than beef, so pull it at medium or it turns dry and livery.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":33,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Bison%20Burger%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Bison Burger"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Bison Burger","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Bison%20Burger","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Lettuce/Greens","Cheese (any)","Bread","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"colorado-smothered-breakfast-burrito","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 large flour tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs, scrambled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 potatoes, diced and fried crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g chorizo or sausage"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml Colorado green chili (see separate recipe), warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sour cream and onion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the diced potatoes until crisp; brown the chorizo separately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scramble the eggs softly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill each warm tortilla with eggs, potato, chorizo, and cheese and roll into a burrito.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set on a plate and ladle warm green chili over the top to smother it completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add more cheese, sour cream, and onion and serve with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The smothered breakfast burrito, drowned in green chili, is a Colorado morning institution. The whole point is the green chili poured over the top; a burrito served dry on the side is not smothered, and a Coloradan will notice.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Smothered%20Breakfast%20Burrito%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Smothered Breakfast Burrito"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Smothered Breakfast Burrito","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Smothered%20Breakfast%20Burrito","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Chiles","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"colorado-lamb-chops","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 Colorado lamb chops"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp rosemary, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the chops with the garlic, rosemary, olive oil, and mustard and marinate 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season well with salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear over high heat or on a hot grill 3-4 min a side for medium-rare.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the chops 5 min.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve with lemon and a simple potato or green side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Colorado lamb is prized for its mild flavor, raised on the high mountain pastures. Chops want high heat and a short cook: sear them hard for the crust and pull at medium-rare, as lamb past that loses its delicacy.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","floral","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a floral thread lifting the aroma, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Lamb%20Chops%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Lamb Chops"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Lamb Chops","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Lamb%20Chops","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Garlic","Lemon","Rosemary","Mustard","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"colorado-cinnamon-roll","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 50g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 2 eggs, 85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For filling: 115g butter, 200g brown sugar, 3 tbsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"For icing: 200g cream cheese, 55g butter, 200g icing sugar, 1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the dough and rise 90 min until doubled.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into a rectangle, spread with the butter, brown sugar, and cinnamon, and roll up tightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into 12 and set in a buttered pan. Rise 45 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C for 25-30 min until golden.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat the cream cheese icing and spread over the warm rolls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Colorado's Johnson's Corner truck stop made its cinnamon rolls famous to generations of I-25 travelers. Roll the dough tight so the spiral holds, and ice them while still warm so the cream cheese frosting melts into the crevices.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Cinnamon%20Roll%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Cinnamon Roll"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Cinnamon Roll","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Cinnamon%20Roll","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"colorado-elk-chili","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g ground elk or venison"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp chili powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g kidney beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cocoa"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the elk hard, then add the onion, pepper, and garlic and cook until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chili powder and cumin and cook 1 min.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, beans, stock, and cocoa and simmer 90 min, stirring, until thick.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with cornbread, cheese, and onions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Elk is a Colorado hunting-season staple, leaner and gamier than beef. The long moist simmer of a chili suits it well, and the cocoa deepens the color and rounds the gaminess without reading as chocolate.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","earthy","aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, an earthy, grounding base, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Elk%20Chili%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Elk Chili"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Elk Chili","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Elk%20Chili","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Venison/Game","Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Kidney/Pinto beans","Stock/Broth","Chocolate/Cocoa","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"colorado-avalanche-bark","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g white chocolate"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g dark chocolate"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pretzels, broken"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g toasted almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g dried cranberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the white chocolate gently and spread on a lined tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the dark chocolate and drizzle over, swirling with a skewer for a marbled effect.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"While still wet, scatter the pretzels, almonds, and cranberries over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle with flaky salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill until firm, then break into shards.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A mountain-lodge treat that captures the sweet, salty, crunchy appeal of trail mix in bark form. Scatter the toppings while the chocolate is still wet so they set in, and the flaky salt is what keeps it from being merely sweet.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colorado%20Avalanche%20Bark%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colorado Avalanche Bark"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Colorado Avalanche Bark","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Colorado%20Avalanche%20Bark","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Chocolate/Cocoa","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"butte-pasty","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the pastry: 375g flour, 1 tsp salt, 170g lard, 120ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g beef skirt or sirloin, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 potatoes, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 rutabaga, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and plenty of black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"Brown gravy to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the lard into the flour and salt, bind with cold water, and rest 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the raw beef, potato, onion, and rutabaga and season hard: everything cooks inside the crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll rounds, mound filling on one half, dot with butter, fold, and crimp a rope seal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Vent, brush with egg, and bake at 200C for 20 min, then 175C for 40 min until deep gold.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 10 min and serve with brown gravy poured over, the Butte way.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cornish miners carried pasties into the copper mines of Butte, where they are still sold by the dozen. Butte serves them under brown gravy, which distinguishes them from the dry Michigan version. Season the raw filling boldly.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Butte%20Pasty%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Butte Pasty"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Butte Pasty","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Butte%20Pasty","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Butter","Flour"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine"]},{"slug":"butte-pork-chop-sandwich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 boneless pork loin chops, pounded thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cracker crumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, 1/2 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Yellow mustard, pickle, and raw onion"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the chops between plastic to 5mm thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge in flour, then egg, then cracker crumbs, pressing them on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 5mm of 180C oil 3 min a side until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Set on a plain soft bun.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress simply with mustard, pickle, and onion, nothing more.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Butte institution since 1924, sold from a walk-up window on Mercury Street. The dressing is deliberately spare (mustard, pickle, onion) because the crisp pork is the whole sandwich and anything else muddies it.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Butte%20Pork%20Chop%20Sandwich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Butte Pork Chop Sandwich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Butte Pork Chop Sandwich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Butte%20Pork%20Chop%20Sandwich","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Onion","Bread","Flour","Mustard","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"montana-huckleberry-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry for a double-crust 23cm pie"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g wild huckleberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"180g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of cinnamon and salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the huckleberries with the sugar, cornstarch, lemon, cinnamon, and salt and let stand 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the lined pie plate and dot with butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay a lattice, brush with egg, and dust with sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 20 min, then 190C for 35-40 min until the juices bubble thickly through the lattice.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool at least 4 hours: huckleberry juice runs freely when warm.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Huckleberries cannot be farmed; they are picked wild in the mountains, which is why a jar costs what it does. Their flavor is tarter and wilder than a blueberry, so do not over-sugar them: the point is that wild tartness.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Montana%20Huckleberry%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Montana Huckleberry Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Montana Huckleberry Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Montana%20Huckleberry%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine"]},{"slug":"montana-huckleberry-milkshake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g huckleberries (fresh or frozen)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cold milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 scoops vanilla ice cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the huckleberries with the milk and sugar until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the ice cream and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend briefly, just until thick: over-blending melts it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a chilled tall glass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve at once with a fat straw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Every roadside stand and lodge from Glacier to Yellowstone sells one all summer. Blend it short: a milkshake worked too long warms and thins, and the whole appeal is thick, cold, and vividly purple.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Montana%20Huckleberry%20Milkshake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Montana Huckleberry Milkshake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Montana Huckleberry Milkshake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Montana%20Huckleberry%20Milkshake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"]},{"slug":"flathead-cherry-crisp","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg Flathead or other sweet cherries, pitted"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp almond extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the topping: 150g oats, 150g flour, 165g brown sugar, 1/2 tsp salt, 1 tsp cinnamon, 170g cold butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the pitted cherries with the sugar, cornstarch, lemon, and almond extract and turn into a buttered dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the topping ingredients together with your fingers into rough clumps, leaving some butter in pieces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter over the cherries without pressing down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 40-45 min until the topping is deep gold and the juices bubble at the edges.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 20 min and serve with cream or ice cream.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Flathead Lake's cherry orchards ripen in late July, warmed by the lake and cooled by the mountains. Sweet cherries need the lemon and a whisper of almond extract to keep them from tasting flat: almond amplifies cherry like nothing else.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Flathead%20Cherry%20Crisp%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Flathead Cherry Crisp"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Flathead Cherry Crisp","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Flathead%20Cherry%20Crisp","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Stone fruit","Butter","Flour","Oats","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"montana-bison-ribeye-with-wild-mushrooms","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 bison ribeye steaks, 250g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g wild mushrooms (morels or chanterelles), cleaned"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and coarse pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the steaks to room temperature and season hard with salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear in a screaming-hot skillet 2-3 min a side for rare to medium-rare: bison is lean and must not go past medium.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the steaks on a warm plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the mushrooms in butter until browned, add the garlic and thyme, then deglaze with the wine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the mushrooms and pan juices over the rested steaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bison has perhaps a third the fat of beef, which means it cooks faster and turns to leather past medium-rare. Sear it hard and pull it early: the lean sweetness is the reward for watching the pan closely.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Montana%20Bison%20Ribeye%20with%20Wild%20Mushrooms%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Montana Bison Ribeye with Wild Mushrooms"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Montana Bison Ribeye with Wild Mushrooms","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Montana%20Bison%20Ribeye%20with%20Wild%20Mushrooms","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ribeye-prime-rib","term":"Ribeye / Prime Rib"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Mushrooms","Thyme","Butter","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"montana-elk-backstrap-with-huckleberry-sauce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g elk backstrap (loin), in medallions"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: 200g huckleberries, 120ml red wine, 120ml stock, 2 tbsp sugar, 1 tbsp balsamic, 1 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the elk medallions well and let them come to room temperature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear in the hot oil 2 min a side for rare, basting with butter and thyme. Rest: elk is very lean and must stay pink.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the huckleberries with the wine, stock, sugar, and balsamic 10 min until syrupy.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the butter into the sauce off the heat for gloss.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the rested backstrap and spoon the huckleberry sauce over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Backstrap is the prize cut of the elk and the leanest thing in the freezer: treat it like a fine steak and never cook it past rare. Huckleberry and game is a Montana marriage: the tart wild fruit answers the mineral depth of the meat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Montana%20Elk%20Backstrap%20with%20Huckleberry%20Sauce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Montana Elk Backstrap with Huckleberry Sauce"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Montana Elk Backstrap with Huckleberry Sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Montana%20Elk%20Backstrap%20with%20Huckleberry%20Sauce","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Venison/Game","Thyme","Butter","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"montana-beef-and-barley-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g beef shank or chuck, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g pearl barley"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g mushrooms, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef hard in the oil in batches; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot, and celery until soft, then add the mushrooms and cook until their water goes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the beef with the stock, bay, and thyme and simmer 90 min until tender.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the barley and cook 40 min more until plump and the soup has thickened.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season well and finish with parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Montana grows a great deal of the nation's barley, much of it for beer, and the rest goes into pots like this on a cold ranch night. Add the barley late: it drinks liquid greedily, and a soup left standing overnight will need thinning.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Montana%20Beef%20and%20Barley%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Montana Beef and Barley Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Montana Beef and Barley Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Montana%20Beef%20and%20Barley%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Mushrooms","Parsley","Thyme","Bay leaf","Couscous/Bulgur","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"montana-sourdough-biscuits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g active sourdough starter"},{"kind":"item","text":"375g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g cold butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml cold buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"Melted butter for brushing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt, soda, and sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut in the cold butter until the pieces are pea-sized.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the sourdough starter and buttermilk just until it comes together, shaggy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat to 3cm, fold in thirds twice, and cut straight down without twisting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 15-18 min until risen and gold. Brush with melted butter.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sourdough came west with prospectors who kept their starter warm inside their coats: the old-timers were called sourdoughs for it. The starter adds tang and tenderness; the baking powder still does the lifting.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sourdough starter","Letter-folding dough","Rubbing fat into flour","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sourdough starter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"dish":{"label":"Weissman’s starter guide","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Montana%20Sourdough%20Biscuits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Montana Sourdough Biscuits"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Montana Sourdough Biscuits","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Montana%20Sourdough%20Biscuits","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Buttermilk","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"montana-lentil-and-wheat-berry-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g green lentils"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g wheat berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, diced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g feta, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dressing: 4 tbsp olive oil, 2 tbsp red wine vinegar, 1 tsp Dijon, 1 garlic clove, salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley and mint, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the wheat berries in salted water 45 min until tender but chewy; drain.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the lentils separately 20 min until tender but holding shape; drain.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the dressing and toss it through the warm grains and lentils: warm absorbs better than cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the onion, carrot, and herbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with feta and serve at room temperature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Montana's northern plains grow lentils, peas, and wheat in rotation, and this salad puts all of them on one plate. Dress the grains while warm: cold grains repel dressing, and the difference in flavor is considerable.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","herb-fresh","briny","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Montana%20Lentil%20and%20Wheat%20Berry%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Montana Lentil and Wheat Berry Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Montana Lentil and Wheat Berry Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Montana%20Lentil%20and%20Wheat%20Berry%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Berries","Parsley","Mint","Cheese (any)","Feta","Lentils","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"wyoming-chuckwagon-beans","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried pinto beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g salt pork or bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp mustard powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp chili powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L water or stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the salt pork in a Dutch oven until crisp, then sweat the onion and garlic in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomato paste and cook 2 min, then the molasses, sugar, mustard, and chili powder.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the drained beans and water, cover, and simmer 2-3 hours until creamy, adding water as needed.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Uncover for the last 30 min so the liquid reduces to a thick sauce.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Salt only at the end and season with pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The chuckwagon fed cattle drives out of Cheyenne and Casper, and beans were the daily bread of the trail. Salt them at the end, early salt keeps the skins tough, and finish uncovered so the pot liquor thickens to a glaze.","equipment":["Dutch oven"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","gently hot","umami-deep","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wyoming%20Chuckwagon%20Beans%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wyoming Chuckwagon Beans"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wyoming Chuckwagon Beans","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wyoming%20Chuckwagon%20Beans","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Bacon","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Kidney/Pinto beans","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Mustard","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"rocky-mountain-oysters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g calf fries (bull testicles), membrane peeled, sliced 1cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Cocktail sauce and lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the tough outer membrane away and slice the meat into rounds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soak in buttermilk at least 1 hour to mellow the flavor.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the flour, cornmeal, salt, pepper, cayenne, and garlic powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the slices and fry at 190C for 2-3 min until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt at once, and serve with cocktail sauce and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A ranch-country delicacy born of branding season, when nothing on the animal was wasted. Peeling the membrane is the essential prep: leave it on and the pieces curl and toughen. Fried hot and fast, they eat like tender fried mushrooms.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rocky%20Mountain%20Oysters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rocky Mountain Oysters"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Rocky Mountain Oysters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Rocky%20Mountain%20Oysters","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Buttermilk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"wyoming-prime-rib","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4kg standing rib roast"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp rosemary and thyme, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the jus: pan drippings, 250ml red wine, 500ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Horseradish cream to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the roast all over with salt, pepper, garlic, herbs, and oil and rest it uncovered in the fridge overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring to room temperature, then roast at 230C for 20 min to build a crust.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop to 120C and roast slowly to an internal 52C for medium-rare, about 2.5 hours. Use a thermometer, not a clock.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 30 min under foil: this is not optional on a roast this size.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Deglaze the pan with wine, add stock, reduce, and serve the jus with horseradish cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cattle country eats beef at its plainest and best. The overnight uncovered salting dries the surface for a better crust and seasons deep; the long rest lets the juices redistribute, and carving early costs you the whole roast.","equipment":["Thermometer"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Roasting in a hot oven","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wyoming%20Prime%20Rib%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wyoming Prime Rib"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wyoming Prime Rib","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wyoming%20Prime%20Rib","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Thyme","Rosemary","Cream","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"wyoming-cowboy-coffee","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1L cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g coarsely ground coffee"},{"kind":"item","text":"A splash of cold water for settling"},{"kind":"item","text":"An enamel pot, ideally over a fire"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the water to a boil in the pot, then pull it off the heat for 30 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the coarse grounds and return to a bare simmer for 4 minutes.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pot off the heat and let it stand 2 minutes.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sprinkle a splash of cold water over the surface: this drops the grounds to the bottom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour slowly and steadily, leaving the last inch in the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No filter, no press, just a pot and patience. The splash of cold water at the end is the trail trick that settles the grounds, and pouring slowly without jostling the pot is what keeps them there.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wyoming%20Cowboy%20Coffee%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wyoming Cowboy Coffee"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wyoming Cowboy Coffee","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wyoming%20Cowboy%20Coffee","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coffee/Espresso"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"wyoming-elk-meatloaf","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g ground elk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g ground pork (elk is too lean alone)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g breadcrumbs soaked in 120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the glaze: 120ml ketchup, 2 tbsp brown sugar, 1 tbsp cider vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the elk and pork with the grated onion, soaked breadcrumbs, eggs, Worcestershire, thyme, salt, and pepper; handle it lightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape into a loaf on a lined tray rather than packing it into a tin, so the sides brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the glaze and brush half over the loaf.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 60-70 min, brushing with the rest of the glaze twice, until 70C internal.","durationSec":4200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 15 min before slicing.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false}],"note":"Elk is so lean it needs pork fat to make a meatloaf that is not dry and crumbly. Free-forming the loaf on a tray rather than pressing it into a tin gives you browned sides instead of steamed ones.","equipment":["Thermometer","Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wyoming%20Elk%20Meatloaf%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wyoming Elk Meatloaf"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wyoming Elk Meatloaf","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wyoming%20Elk%20Meatloaf","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Venison/Game","Onion","Thyme","Milk","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"wyoming-beef-jerky","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg beef eye of round, trimmed and sliced 5mm with the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp onion powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp smoked paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cayenne"}],"steps":[{"text":"Freeze the beef 1 hour to firm it, then slice thin with the grain for a chewy jerky (across the grain gives a tender one).","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Marinate the slices in all the remaining ingredients 6-24 hours in the fridge.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat the strips dry and lay them on racks without overlapping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dry at 70C in an oven with the door cracked, or in a dehydrator, 4-6 hours.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":false},{"text":"It is done when a strip cracks but does not snap when bent. Cool fully before storing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Drying meat is older than the state and still how ranch families handle a full freezer. The bend test is the only reliable doneness check: a strip should crack and show white fibers but not break clean, which means it has gone too far.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Marinating: acid, salt and time","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","smoky","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, wood-smoke running underneath, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wyoming%20Beef%20Jerky%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wyoming Beef Jerky"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wyoming Beef Jerky","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wyoming%20Beef%20Jerky","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Soy sauce","Sugar","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"wyoming-cowboy-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg beef chuck, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g crushed tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 potatoes, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Worcestershire"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef hard in the oil in batches; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions and garlic, sprinkle in the flour, and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the stock, tomatoes, Worcestershire, and bay and return the beef. Simmer 90 min.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes and carrots and cook 30 min until tender.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the corn, cook 5 min more, and season well.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Dutch-oven stew built from whatever the chuckwagon carried. Brown the beef in batches without crowding: a pan packed too full steams the meat grey, and that first hard crust is most of the flavor in the finished pot.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wyoming%20Cowboy%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wyoming Cowboy Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wyoming Cowboy Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wyoming%20Cowboy%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Corn","Bay leaf","Flour","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"wyoming-chokecherry-jelly","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg chokecherries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 packet pectin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chokecherries in the water 20 min until soft, mashing as they cook.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain through a jelly bag overnight without squeezing: squeezing clouds the jelly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Measure 1L of the clear juice into a pot with the lemon juice and pectin and bring to a hard boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the sugar all at once and boil hard 1 minute, stirring.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Skim the foam and jar immediately, sealing while hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chokecherries grow wild along every draw and creek bed in Wyoming and are far too astringent to eat raw: jelly is their destiny. Do not squeeze the bag: patience overnight buys a jewel-clear jelly instead of a cloudy one.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wyoming%20Chokecherry%20Jelly%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wyoming Chokecherry Jelly"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wyoming Chokecherry Jelly","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wyoming%20Chokecherry%20Jelly","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"wyoming-cowboy-cookies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"225g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g rolled oats"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g chocolate chips"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pecans, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g shredded coconut"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter with both sugars until light, then beat in the eggs and vanilla.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, soda, baking powder, and salt and fold in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir through the oats, chocolate chips, pecans, and coconut: the dough will be very full.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop large mounds well apart on lined trays.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 175C for 12-14 min until set at the edges and still soft in the middle.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":840,"estimated":false}],"note":"A cookie with everything in the pantry thrown in, meant to travel and keep. Make them large and pull them while the centers still look underdone: they finish setting on the hot tray and stay chewy for days.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wyoming%20Cowboy%20Cookies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wyoming Cowboy Cookies"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wyoming Cowboy Cookies","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wyoming%20Cowboy%20Cookies","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Butter","Bread","Flour","Oats","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar"]},{"slug":"wyoming-lamb-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg lamb shoulder, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2L stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 potatoes, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp rosemary"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the lamb hard in the oil in batches; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions and garlic, sprinkle in the flour, and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Deglaze with the wine, scraping the fond, then add the stock, rosemary, and bay.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the lamb, cover, and simmer 90 min until tender.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the potatoes and carrots and cook 30 min more. Season and serve.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wyoming ran vast sheep bands before and alongside the cattle, and lamb remains a ranch staple. Rosemary and lamb is a fixed pairing for good reason: the resinous herb answers the fat, so be generous with it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wyoming%20Lamb%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wyoming Lamb Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Wyoming Lamb Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Wyoming%20Lamb%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"deglaze-and-fond","term":"Deglaze & Fond"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Carrot","Rosemary","Bay leaf","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"idaho-baked-potato","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 large russet Burbank potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 scallions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g bacon, cooked and crumbled (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scrub the potatoes and dry them completely, then prick each several times with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub all over with oil and roll in coarse salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake directly on the oven rack at 200C for 60-70 min until the skin is crisp and a skewer slides through with no resistance. Never wrap in foil, which steams the skin soft.","durationSec":4200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split the top with a cross, squeeze the ends to fluff the interior, and season inside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Load with butter, sour cream, cheddar, scallion, and bacon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Idaho's volcanic soil and cool nights grow the russet Burbank, the definitive baking potato. Foil is the enemy: it traps steam and gives you a boiled potato in a crisp-looking jacket. Bake it naked on the rack and salt the skin.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idaho%20Baked%20Potato%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idaho Baked Potato"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Idaho Baked Potato","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Idaho%20Baked%20Potato","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Scallion","Potato","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Cheddar"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"idaho-finger-steaks","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g sirloin or tri-tip, cut in finger-sized strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, 1 tsp pepper, 1 tsp garlic powder, 1/2 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fry sauce or cocktail sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the beef strips in buttermilk 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the flour with the seasonings, then beat in the egg and beer to make a loose batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip each strip in the batter, let the excess drip, and fry 2-3 min until deep gold and crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt at once, and serve with fry sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Invented in Boise in the 1950s and found on nearly every Idaho menu since. Cut the strips no thicker than a finger so they cook through before the batter darkens, and serve immediately: a finger steak waiting on the pass goes soft.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idaho%20Finger%20Steaks%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idaho Finger Steaks"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Idaho Finger Steaks","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Idaho%20Finger%20Steaks","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Garlic","Buttermilk","Flour","Beer/Cider","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"idaho-ice-cream-potato","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 large scoops vanilla ice cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp cocoa powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml whipped cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp chocolate sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp crushed nuts (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Shape each scoop of ice cream into an oval and freeze hard, at least 2 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll each frozen oval in cocoa powder until completely coated so it looks like a dirt-dusted potato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set on a plate and cut a slit down the middle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe whipped cream into the slit so it looks like sour cream on a baked potato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with a drizzle of chocolate sauce for gravy and nuts for chives. Serve immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Boise dessert joke played entirely straight: it looks exactly like a loaded baked potato. Freeze the ovals rock-hard before rolling in cocoa, or they slump and the illusion collapses on the plate.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idaho%20Ice%20Cream%20Potato%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idaho Ice Cream Potato"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Idaho Ice Cream Potato","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Idaho%20Ice%20Cream%20Potato","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Cream","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"idaho-basque-croquetas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"600ml whole milk, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g jamon or cured ham, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"For coating: 100g flour, 2 eggs beaten, 200g fine breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"Oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the butter, stir in the flour, and cook 3 min without coloring.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the warm milk slowly, whisking hard, and cook 10-15 min until the bechamel is very thick and pulls from the pan.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the minced ham, nutmeg, salt, and pepper. Spread in a tray, cover, and chill overnight until firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape into small cylinders, then coat in flour, egg, and breadcrumbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 190C for 2 min until deep gold. They should be crisp outside and molten within.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boise holds one of the largest Basque communities outside Spain, and its restaurants keep croquetas on every menu. The overnight chill is not optional: a warm bechamel will burst in the fryer and leave you with breadcrumbs in oil.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Béchamel: the mother sauce","Deep frying","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Béchamel: the mother sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bechamel%20mother%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idaho%20Basque%20Croquetas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idaho Basque Croquetas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Idaho Basque Croquetas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Idaho%20Basque%20Croquetas","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"jamon-iberico-and-the-bellota-grades","term":"Jamón Ibérico (and the Bellota Grades)"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Butter","Milk","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"idaho-lentil-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g Palouse green lentils"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp smoked paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L vegetable stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot, and celery in the oil until soft, 10 min, then add the garlic.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the tomato paste, cumin, and paprika and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the lentils and stock and simmer 35-40 min until the lentils are tender but still holding shape.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash a ladleful against the pot to thicken the broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with the vinegar (this is what wakes the whole pot up) and season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Palouse region straddling Idaho and Washington grows most of America's lentils. The splash of vinegar at the end is the step people skip and the one that matters most; lentils are earthy and flat without acid to lift them.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","bright","umami-deep","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idaho%20Lentil%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idaho Lentil Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Idaho Lentil Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Idaho%20Lentil%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Parsley","Lentils","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"idaho-huckleberry-ice-cream","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g huckleberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook half the huckleberries with 2 tbsp of the sugar and the lemon juice 5 min into a loose jam; cool.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the milk and cream until steaming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with the remaining sugar and salt, temper in the hot dairy, and cook to 82C until it coats a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain, add the vanilla and the huckleberry jam, and chill thoroughly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Churn, folding in the remaining whole berries in the last minute, and freeze until firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Northern Idaho's huckleberry country runs from Coeur d'Alene into the panhandle. Cooking half the berries into jam gives the base its color and depth, while folding whole berries in at the end keeps bursts of fresh tartness.","equipment":["Ice-cream churner"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Reducing a sauce","Churning ice cream"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Churning ice cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ice%20cream%20churning%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idaho%20Huckleberry%20Ice%20Cream%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idaho Huckleberry Ice Cream"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Idaho Huckleberry Ice Cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Idaho%20Huckleberry%20Ice%20Cream","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"snake-river-trout-with-bacon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole rainbow trout, cleaned"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 rashers bacon"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, 1/2 tsp pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon in a large skillet until crisp; lift out and keep the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the trout in the cornmeal, flour, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in the bacon fat 3-4 min a side until the crust is gold and the flesh flakes at the thickest point.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the butter and lemon juice at the end and spoon over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve each trout with crumbled bacon and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Idaho raises more farmed rainbow trout than any state, in the cold springs of the Snake River canyon. Cornmeal and bacon fat are the traditional treatment. And pull the fish the moment it flakes, since trout is lean and dries in seconds.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Snake%20River%20Trout%20with%20Bacon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Snake River Trout with Bacon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Snake River Trout with Bacon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Snake%20River%20Trout%20with%20Bacon","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"idaho-scalloped-potatoes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg russet potatoes, sliced 3mm thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sharp cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic in the butter, stir in the flour, and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the milk and cream and simmer to a smooth sauce. Season with nutmeg, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the thin-sliced potatoes in a buttered dish, pouring sauce over every layer and scattering cheese between.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with foil and bake at 180C for 45 min, then uncover and bake 25 min more until browned and bubbling.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 15 min before serving so the layers set.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Slice the potatoes on a mandoline if you have one: uneven slices cook unevenly and leave you with crunchy pockets. Resting the dish before serving lets the starch set the layers so it cuts into squares instead of sliding apart.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idaho%20Scalloped%20Potatoes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idaho Scalloped Potatoes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Idaho Scalloped Potatoes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Idaho%20Scalloped%20Potatoes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Scallops","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Butter","Milk","Cream","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Flour","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"idaho-spudnuts","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g mashed potato, cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"240ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the glaze: 300g icing sugar, 60ml milk, 1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the cooled mashed potato with the milk, eggs, sugar, and melted butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the flour, yeast, and salt and knead to a soft, tacky dough. Rise 90 min until doubled.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 1cm and cut rings. Rise 30 min on floured trays until puffy.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 175C for 60-90 seconds a side until deep gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip in the glaze while still warm and set on a rack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Potato doughnuts are lighter and stay softer than plain flour ones because the potato starch holds moisture. Idaho, unsurprisingly, adopted them wholesale. Do not skip the second rise: an under-proofed spudnut fries dense.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idaho%20Spudnuts%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idaho Spudnuts"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Idaho Spudnuts","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Idaho%20Spudnuts","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Potato","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"idaho-morel-and-asparagus-saute","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g fresh morels, halved and rinsed carefully"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g asparagus, trimmed and cut in lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 shallots, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the morels quickly in cold water to flush the honeycomb, then drain and dry: never soak them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the morels in butter over medium-high heat 5 min until they release their water and it cooks away.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the shallots and garlic and cook 2 min, then the asparagus and cook 4 min until bright and just tender.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the cream and thyme and simmer 2 min to bind.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with lemon and season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Morels flush across Idaho's burned mountain slopes each spring and must always be cooked, never eaten raw. Rinse rather than soak: the honeycomb drinks water like a sponge and a soaked morel steams instead of browning.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idaho%20Morel%20and%20Asparagus%20Saute%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idaho Morel and Asparagus Saute"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Idaho Morel and Asparagus Saute","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Idaho%20Morel%20and%20Asparagus%20Saute","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Shallot","Mushrooms","Asparagus","Lemon","Thyme","Butter","Cream","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"california-french-dip","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg beef top round or sirloin roast"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 crusty French rolls"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Hot mustard and horseradish to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the roast hard and brown all over in the oil, then roast at 160C to 52C internal for rare, about 90 min. Rest, then chill for slicing.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the pan drippings with the stock, onions, and bay 30 min to make a rich jus, then strain.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the cold beef as thin as you possibly can.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the slices briefly in the hot jus. Never boil them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile into split rolls and either dip the whole sandwich in jus or serve the jus alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two Los Angeles restaurants, Philippe's and Cole's, have argued over the invention since 1908. Chill the roast before slicing (warm beef tears rather than shaving thin) and only warm the meat in the jus, since boiling turns it grey.","equipment":["Thermometer"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Roasting in a hot oven","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=California%20French%20Dip%20recipe","sub":"Search films of California French Dip"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on California French Dip","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20California%20French%20Dip","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Bay leaf","Bread","Stock/Broth","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"cobb-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 head romaine and 1 bunch watercress, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g cooked chicken breast, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 rashers bacon, crisp and crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 hard-boiled eggs, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 avocados, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g Roquefort, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the vinaigrette: 6 tbsp olive oil, 2 tbsp red wine vinegar, 1 tsp Dijon, 1 garlic clove, salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chop all the greens very fine and spread across a wide platter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange the chicken, bacon, egg, avocado, tomato, and Roquefort in neat parallel rows over the greens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the vinaigrette until emulsified.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Present the salad in its rows at the table first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress and toss thoroughly just before serving so every forkful carries everything.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bob Cobb built it at the Hollywood Brown Derby in 1937 from leftovers found in the icebox. The rows are for presentation, but the salad is meant to be tossed, and everything must be chopped fine enough to eat with a spoon.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cobb%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cobb Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cobb Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cobb%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"roquefort","term":"Roquefort"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Bacon","Eggs","Garlic","Tomato","Lettuce/Greens","Avocado","Arugula/Watercress","Chives","Blue cheese","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion"]},{"slug":"santa-maria-tri-tip","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg tri-tip roast, fat cap trimmed to 5mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp granulated garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"Red oak wood for the fire"},{"kind":"item","text":"To serve: pinquito beans, salsa, and grilled garlic bread"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the tri-tip with only salt, pepper, and garlic (the Santa Maria seasoning is that spare), and rest 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build a red oak fire and let it burn to hot coals.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear the roast over direct heat 5 min a side, then move it higher or to indirect heat.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook to 52C internal for medium-rare, about 30-40 min, turning occasionally.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 15 min, then slice thin against the grain: the grain changes direction mid-roast, so watch for the turn.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Santa Maria barbecue is a Central Coast ranching tradition, cooked over red oak with a three-ingredient rub. The tri-tip's grain shifts direction partway along the roast; find the turn and adjust your knife, or half the slices will be tough.","equipment":["Thermometer","Grill"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Grilling over live coals","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Santa%20Maria%20Tri-Tip%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Santa Maria Tri-Tip"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Santa Maria Tri-Tip","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Santa%20Maria%20Tri-Tip","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Bread"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"california-roll","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g sushi rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml rice vinegar, 2 tbsp sugar, 1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sheets nori"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cooked crab or surimi"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 avocado, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber, in batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp toasted sesame seeds or tobiko"},{"kind":"item","text":"Soy sauce, wasabi, pickled ginger"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the rice until the water runs clear, cook it, then fold through the warmed vinegar, sugar, and salt. Cool to body temperature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay a nori sheet on a rolling mat covered in cling film and spread rice over it, then flip so the nori faces up: this is the inside-out roll.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the crab, avocado, and cucumber along the center.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll tightly with the mat, pressing to compact.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the outside in sesame seeds and cut with a wet knife into eight pieces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Created in Los Angeles in the 1960s to introduce American diners to sushi by hiding the nori inside and swapping raw fish for crab and avocado. Wet the knife between every cut, or the rice drags and the roll loses its shape.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sushi rice & rolling","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","nutty-toasted","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sushi rice & rolling","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20sushi%20rice%20rolling%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=California%20Roll%20recipe","sub":"Search films of California Roll"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on California Roll","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20California%20Roll","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"roe-caviar-ikura-tobiko-bottarga","term":"Roe: Caviar, Ikura, Tobiko, Bottarga"}],"pantryItems":["Crab","Ginger","Cucumber","Avocado","Rice","Bread","Soy sauce","Sesame","Vinegar","Sugar","Pickles","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"mission-style-burrito","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 extra-large flour tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g carne asada or carnitas"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g Mexican rice, cooked"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g pinto or black beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cheese, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 avocado or guacamole"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salsa and sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Foil for wrapping"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steam each tortilla until soft and pliable: it must stretch without tearing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay a line of rice, then beans, then meat, then cheese, salsa, guacamole, and sour cream down the center. Do not overfill, however tempting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the sides, then roll tightly from the bottom, tucking as you go.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap tightly in foil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle the wrapped burrito briefly, or unwrap and sear the seam, to seal it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"San Francisco's Mission District built the oversized foil-wrapped burrito in the 1960s, rice and all. Steaming the tortilla is the technique that makes it possible: a cold tortilla splits the moment you roll it this full.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Confit","Searing: the hard crust","Griddle & plancha work","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mission-Style%20Burrito%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mission-Style Burrito"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Mission-Style Burrito","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Mission-Style%20Burrito","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Avocado","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Rice","Bread","Flour","Black beans","Kidney/Pinto beans"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"hangtown-fry","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 small oysters, shucked"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 rashers bacon"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cracker crumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten, for breading"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the bacon until crisp; set aside, leaving the fat in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the oysters in flour, then beaten egg, then cracker crumbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the oysters in the bacon fat 1 min a side until golden; do not overcook them.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the remaining eggs, pour into the pan with butter, and scramble softly around the oysters.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the bacon, season, finish with parsley, and serve as a loose open omelette.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Gold Rush dish from Placerville, once called Hangtown, said to be ordered by a miner who wanted the three most expensive things in the house: eggs, bacon, and oysters. Keep the eggs soft and the oysters barely cooked.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shucking shellfish","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Shucking shellfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20shuck%20oysters%20clams%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hangtown%20Fry%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hangtown Fry"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Hangtown Fry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Hangtown%20Fry","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Parsley","Butter","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"green-goddess-dressing","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 anchovy fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp tarragon, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp chives, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the anchovies, garlic, lemon juice, and vinegar into a paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the herbs and blend until the mixture turns bright green.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the mayonnaise and sour cream: blending them fully can loosen the emulsion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill at least an hour so the herbs perfume the base. Use within three days while the color holds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Created at the Palace Hotel in San Francisco in 1923 for an actor starring in a play called The Green Goddess. The anchovies are not optional: they provide the savory depth beneath the herbs, and nobody will identify them.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Green%20Goddess%20Dressing%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Green Goddess Dressing"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Green Goddess Dressing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Green%20Goddess%20Dressing","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Garlic","Lemon","Parsley","Tarragon","Chives","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Vinegar","Wine","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion"]},{"slug":"san-francisco-garlic-noodles","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g spaghetti or egg noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 garlic cloves, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oyster sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g Parmesan, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Scallions to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the noodles until just al dente and reserve a cup of the water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter over low heat and cook the minced garlic very gently 3-4 min until fragrant and pale gold, never browned, which turns it bitter.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the oyster sauce, fish sauce, soy, and sugar and swirl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the drained noodles through the garlic butter with a splash of pasta water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat fold in the Parmesan and finish with scallions and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A Vietnamese-American San Francisco invention from the Crustacean kitchen, now on menus all over the Bay. Cook the garlic gently and never let it brown: bitter garlic ruins the dish, and there is a great deal of it in here.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":40,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=San%20Francisco%20Garlic%20Noodles%20recipe","sub":"Search films of San Francisco Garlic Noodles"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on San Francisco Garlic Noodles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20San%20Francisco%20Garlic%20Noodles","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Garlic","Scallion","Butter","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Pasta","Noodles","Soy sauce","Fish sauce","Oyster sauce","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"castroville-artichokes-with-aioli","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 large globe artichokes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the aioli: 4 garlic cloves, 2 egg yolks, 1 tsp Dijon, 250ml olive oil, 1 tbsp lemon juice, salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Trim the artichoke stems and snip the thorny leaf tips. Rub the cut surfaces with lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer in salted water with the lemon halves, garlic, and bay 35-45 min until an outer leaf pulls away easily.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pound the garlic to a paste, whisk in the yolks and mustard, then add the oil drop by drop at first, then in a thin stream, until thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish the aioli with lemon and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain the artichokes upside down and serve warm with the aioli for dipping leaf by leaf.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Castroville calls itself the artichoke capital of the world and once crowned Marilyn Monroe its queen. The leaf test is the only reliable doneness check, and add the oil to the aioli slowly at the start or the emulsion will break.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Salted water & the float test","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","warm-spiced","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Castroville%20Artichokes%20with%20Aioli%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Castroville Artichokes with Aioli"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Castroville Artichokes with Aioli","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Castroville%20Artichokes%20with%20Aioli","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Garlic","Lemon","Artichoke","Bay leaf","Mayonnaise","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"meyer-lemon-bars","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the crust: 250g flour, 75g icing sugar, 1/4 tsp salt, 225g cold butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the filling: 6 eggs, 400g sugar, 180ml Meyer lemon juice, 2 tbsp zest, 60g flour, 1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the crust ingredients to crumbs, press firmly into a lined pan, and bake at 175C for 20-25 min until pale gold.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs and sugar, then the lemon juice, zest, flour, and salt until smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the filling over the hot crust: this is what fuses the two layers together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 25-30 min more until the center is just set and no longer wobbles when nudged.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely, chill, then cut and dust with icing sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Meyer lemon, a lemon-mandarin cross, grows in backyards all over California and is sweeter and more floral than a Eureka. Pour the filling onto the crust while the crust is still hot: that is the trick that stops the layers separating.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Meyer%20Lemon%20Bars%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Meyer Lemon Bars"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Meyer Lemon Bars","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Meyer%20Lemon%20Bars","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"oregon-marionberry-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry for a double-crust 23cm pie"},{"kind":"item","text":"900g marionberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the marionberries with the sugar, cornstarch, lemon, and salt and let stand 15 min.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the lined pie plate and dot with butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay a lattice top, brush with egg, and dust heavily with sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C for 20 min, then 190C for 40-45 min until the juices bubble thickly through the lattice.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool at least 4 hours: marionberry juice is copious and needs time to set.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"The marionberry was bred at Oregon State in 1956 and is the state's own blackberry: darker, tarter, and more complex than any other. Use the full measure of cornstarch: these berries run more juice than you expect.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oregon%20Marionberry%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oregon Marionberry Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oregon Marionberry Pie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oregon%20Marionberry%20Pie","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine"]},{"slug":"cracked-dungeness-crab-with-drawn-butter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole Dungeness crabs, about 1kg each, live"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g salt for the pot"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 lemons, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"225g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, smashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sourdough bread and a green salad to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring a very large pot of heavily salted water to a rolling boil with the lemon and bay.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the crabs 12-15 min until the shells turn bright orange-red; lift out and cool briefly.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Clean the crabs: lift the top shell, remove the gills and viscera, and rinse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack the legs and body with a mallet or the back of a heavy knife.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter gently with the garlic, skim it clear, and serve alongside with bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dungeness season opens in December and the Oregon coast marks it like a holiday. Do not over-boil: fifteen minutes is plenty, and crab held longer goes stringy. Crack it before serving so the meat pulls free clean.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cracked%20Dungeness%20Crab%20with%20Drawn%20Butter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cracked Dungeness Crab with Drawn Butter"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cracked Dungeness Crab with Drawn Butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cracked%20Dungeness%20Crab%20with%20Drawn%20Butter","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Garlic","Lemon","Bay leaf","Butter","Bread","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"oregon-hazelnut-torte","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g Oregon hazelnuts, toasted and skinned"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs, separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the cream: 300ml cream, 2 tbsp sugar, 1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"Toasted hazelnuts to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the hazelnuts, rub off the skins in a towel, and grind to a fine meal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the yolks with three-quarters of the sugar until pale and thick, then fold in the ground nuts, flour, baking powder, vanilla, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites with the remaining sugar to soft peaks and fold through in three additions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in two lined 23cm pans at 175C for 22-25 min until springy. Cool completely.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whip the cream and sandwich the layers, then top with more cream and chopped hazelnuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oregon grows nearly all of America's hazelnuts in the Willamette Valley. Toast and skin them yourself: the papery skins are bitter, and the toasting is what brings out the deep nuttiness the torte is built on.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oregon%20Hazelnut%20Torte%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oregon Hazelnut Torte"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oregon Hazelnut Torte","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oregon%20Hazelnut%20Torte","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"tillamook-cheddar-mac-and-cheese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g elbow macaroni"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"70g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L whole milk, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"450g sharp Tillamook cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dry mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dash of hot sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the top: 100g breadcrumbs, 3 tbsp melted butter, 50g cheddar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the macaroni two minutes shy of al dente and drain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter, whisk in the flour, and cook 2 min. Add the warm milk gradually, whisking, and simmer to a smooth sauce.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, add the cheddar in handfuls, stirring until each melts before the next. Season with mustard, nutmeg, hot sauce, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the pasta through the sauce and turn into a buttered dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the breadcrumbs with melted butter and cheese, scatter over, and bake at 190C for 25 min until bubbling and gold.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tillamook's coastal creamery has made cheddar since 1909. The pot must come off the heat before the cheese goes in: a sauce that boils after cheddar is added turns grainy and weeps oil, and no amount of stirring brings it back.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Making a roux"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tillamook%20Cheddar%20Mac%20and%20Cheese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tillamook Cheddar Mac and Cheese"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Tillamook Cheddar Mac and Cheese","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Tillamook%20Cheddar%20Mac%20and%20Cheese","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Pasta","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Mustard","Hot sauce","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux"]},{"slug":"oregon-pinot-noir-braised-short-ribs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg beef short ribs"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml Oregon Pinot Noir"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the short ribs hard and brown all sides in the oil; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot, and celery until soft, add the garlic and tomato paste, and cook until brick red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the wine and reduce by half: this cooks off the raw alcohol and concentrates the fruit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the ribs with the stock, bay, and thyme. Cover and braise at 150C for 3 hours until the meat pulls from the bone.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest, skim the fat, and reduce the sauce until it coats a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Willamette Valley makes some of the world's finest Pinot Noir, and braising in it is a local pleasure. Reduce the wine hard before the stock goes in: an under-reduced braise tastes sharp and boozy rather than deep.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oregon%20Pinot%20Noir%20Braised%20Short%20Ribs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oregon Pinot Noir Braised Short Ribs"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oregon Pinot Noir Braised Short Ribs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oregon%20Pinot%20Noir%20Braised%20Short%20Ribs","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"short-ribs-english-vs-flanken","term":"Short Ribs (English vs. Flanken)"},{"slug":"pinot-noir","term":"Pinot Noir"},{"slug":"pinot-noir-and-burgundy","term":"Pinot Noir & Burgundy"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Thyme","Bay leaf","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"portland-maple-bacon-doughnut","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For the dough: 500g flour, 7g yeast, 60g sugar, 1 tsp salt, 240ml warm milk, 2 eggs, 85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the glaze: 300g icing sugar, 60ml maple syrup, 3 tbsp milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 rashers bacon, cooked crisp and broken"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the dough until smooth and rise 90 min until doubled.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 1cm, cut into bars or rings, and rise 30 min until puffy.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 175C for 60-90 seconds a side until deep gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the maple glaze and dip each warm doughnut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay strips of crisp bacon across the wet glaze so they set in place.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Portland's Voodoo Doughnut made the maple bar with bacon a national curiosity in the 2000s. Lay the bacon on while the glaze is still tacky: set glaze will not hold it, and the salt against the maple is the entire argument for the thing.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portland%20Maple%20Bacon%20Doughnut%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Portland Maple Bacon Doughnut"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Portland Maple Bacon Doughnut","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Portland%20Maple%20Bacon%20Doughnut","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"oregon-chanterelle-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g fresh chanterelles, cleaned and torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 shallots, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1L vegetable or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp dry sherry"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, white pepper, and chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brush the chanterelles clean rather than washing them: they absorb water readily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook them in the butter over medium-high heat 8 min until they release their liquid and it cooks away and they begin to brown.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the shallots and garlic, then the flour, and cook 2 min.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the stock and simmer 20 min, then add the cream and thyme.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with the sherry off the heat, season, and scatter with chives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chanterelles flush through Oregon's coastal forests each autumn and are the state mushroom. Brush rather than wash them, and cook them hard enough that their water evaporates and they brown: a wet chanterelle just steams and tastes of nothing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","herb-fresh","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oregon%20Chanterelle%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oregon Chanterelle Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oregon Chanterelle Soup","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oregon%20Chanterelle%20Soup","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Shallot","Mushrooms","Thyme","Chives","Butter","Cream","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux"]},{"slug":"cedar-planked-chinook-salmon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 side of Chinook salmon, about 1kg, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"An untreated cedar plank, soaked 2 hours"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dill and thyme sprigs"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the cedar plank thoroughly so it smolders rather than catches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the salmon with the brown sugar, salt, and pepper and rest 20 min.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the salmon skin-side down on the plank, brush with oil, and lay lemon slices and herbs over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a covered grill over medium heat, or at 200C in the oven, 20-30 min until the flesh flakes at the thickest point and reads 52C for medium.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve straight from the plank.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Planking salmon on cedar comes from the Pacific Northwest tribes, who cooked it on stakes beside alder and cedar fires. Soak the plank well, use untreated wood only, and pull the fish at medium: Chinook is rich and dries fast past that.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","sweet-edged","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a sweet edge rounding the corners, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cedar-Planked%20Chinook%20Salmon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cedar-Planked Chinook Salmon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Cedar-Planked Chinook Salmon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Cedar-Planked%20Chinook%20Salmon","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Lemon","Dill","Thyme","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"oregon-albacore-tuna-melt","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g cooked Oregon albacore tuna, flaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp red onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp capers"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices sourdough bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices sharp cheddar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the griddle"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fold the flaked tuna with the mayonnaise, celery, onion, lemon, and capers and season well.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter one side of each bread slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build with cheese, the tuna salad, and more cheese: cheese on both sides glues the sandwich shut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Griddle over medium heat 4 min a side, pressing gently, until deep gold and the cheese has melted through.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cut on the diagonal and serve hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oregon's troll-caught albacore is line-caught young and far richer than canned skipjack. Cheese on both sides of the filling is the structural trick: it seals the tuna in so the sandwich holds together when cut.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oregon%20Albacore%20Tuna%20Melt%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oregon Albacore Tuna Melt"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Oregon Albacore Tuna Melt","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Oregon%20Albacore%20Tuna%20Melt","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tuna","Onion","Celery","Capers","Lemon","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Bread","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"hood-river-pear-and-blue-cheese-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 ripe Hood River pears, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g mixed bitter greens"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g Oregon blue cheese, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g hazelnuts, toasted and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the dressing: 3 tbsp hazelnut or olive oil, 2 tbsp cider vinegar, 1 tsp honey, 1 tsp Dijon, salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the hazelnuts and rub off their skins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the dressing until emulsified and taste for balance: it should be sharp enough to answer the pear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the greens lightly with just enough dressing to coat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange the pear slices over and scatter the blue cheese and hazelnuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind pepper over and serve at once, before the pears brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Hood River Valley grows pears in the shadow of Mount Hood, and Rogue Creamery makes the blue. Slice the pears at the last moment; they oxidize within minutes, and a browned pear looks tired next to the greens.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hood%20River%20Pear%20and%20Blue%20Cheese%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hood River Pear and Blue Cheese Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Hood River Pear and Blue Cheese Salad","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Hood%20River%20Pear%20and%20Blue%20Cheese%20Salad","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chicories-and-bitter-greens","term":"Chicories & Bitter Greens"}],"pantryItems":["Cheese (any)","Blue cheese","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Honey","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"seattle-style-teriyaki-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 chicken thighs, boneless, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the sauce: 250ml soy sauce, 120ml mirin, 100g brown sugar, 60ml sake, 4 garlic cloves grated, 2 tbsp ginger grated, 1 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"Steamed rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Iceberg lettuce, shredded, with a light vinegar dressing"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sesame seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the soy, mirin, sugar, sake, garlic, and ginger 10 min, then thicken with the cornstarch slurried in a little water.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Marinate the chicken thighs in a third of the sauce 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill or broil the thighs skin-side down until charred and cooked through, 12-15 min, basting with sauce as they cook.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slice the chicken across into strips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve over rice with the shredded lettuce salad on the side and more sauce spooned over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Seattle teriyaki is its own institution, sweeter and thicker than the Japanese original, served in hundreds of neighborhood shops since the 1970s with rice and a wedge of iceberg. Baste as it grills so the sugars lacquer rather than burn.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The broiler: heat from above","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","aromatic","sweet-edged","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, aromatics blooming from the first bite, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Seattle-Style%20Teriyaki%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Seattle-Style Teriyaki Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Seattle-Style Teriyaki Chicken","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Seattle-Style%20Teriyaki%20Chicken","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Ginger","Lettuce/Greens","Rice","Soy sauce","Sesame","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-broiler-heat-from-above","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"geoduck-crudo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 geoduck siphon, blanched, skinned, and sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp lemon or yuzu juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp good olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 shallot, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red chile, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chives or shiso"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch the geoduck 10 seconds in boiling water, then plunge into ice water; the tough outer skin peels away easily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the cleaned siphon as thin as you possibly can: thickness is toughness with geoduck.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange the slices in a single layer on chilled plates.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress with lemon, olive oil, shallot, and chile just before serving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with flaky salt and chives and serve immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The geoduck is Puget Sound's giant burrowing clam, prized in Asia and eaten raw at its best. Everything depends on the knife: sliced paper-thin it is sweet and crisp, and cut any thicker it is rubber. Dress it at the last second.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","briny","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a briny, maritime salinity, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Geoduck%20Crudo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Geoduck Crudo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Geoduck Crudo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Geoduck%20Crudo","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Chiles","Lemon","Chives","Shiso"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"rainier-cherry-galette","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 disc of all-butter pastry"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g Rainier cherries, pitted and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp almond extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ground almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"Demerara sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the cherries with the sugar, cornstarch, lemon, and almond extract.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the pastry into a rough 30cm round on a lined tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the ground almonds over the center (they absorb juice and keep the base crisp), then mound the cherries, leaving a 5cm border.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the border up over the fruit in loose pleats, brush with egg, and sprinkle with demerara.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 40-45 min until the pastry is deep gold and the juices bubble. Cool 20 min.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":false}],"note":"The Rainier cherry was bred at Washington State in 1952 and is the sweetest of them all, yellow blushed with red. The layer of ground almonds under the fruit is the galette-maker's insurance against a soggy bottom.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","bright","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rainier%20Cherry%20Galette%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rainier Cherry Galette"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Rainier Cherry Galette","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Rainier%20Cherry%20Galette","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Stone fruit","Butter","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine"]},{"slug":"washington-apple-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 apples, peeled and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g walnuts, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the glaze: 115g butter, 150g brown sugar, 60ml cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, soda, cinnamon, nutmeg, and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs, brown sugar, oil, and vanilla, then fold in the dry ingredients; the batter will be very thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the diced apple and walnuts; the apples release the moisture that makes the batter come together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in a lined pan at 175C for 50-60 min until a skewer comes clean.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the glaze ingredients 5 min and pour over the warm cake.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"Washington grows more apples than any state, and this dense, moist cake uses a great many of them. Do not add liquid when the batter looks too stiff: the diced apples will weep enough moisture as they bake.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","sweet-edged","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a sweet edge rounding the corners, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Washington%20Apple%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Washington Apple Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Washington Apple Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Washington%20Apple%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Apple","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"washington-razor-clam-fritters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g razor clams, cleaned and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 scallions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Old Bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon and tartar sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Clean the razor clams thoroughly, removing the dark parts, and chop them coarsely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, egg, and milk into a thick batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the clams, scallions, Old Bay, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop heaped spoonfuls into 180C oil and fry 3 min, turning, until deep gold.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, salt, and serve with lemon and tartar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Razor clam digs on the Washington coast are a licensed, tide-timed ritual followed by thousands. Chop rather than mince the clams so you keep some texture, and do not overmix once they go in: they toughen with handling.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Washington%20Razor%20Clam%20Fritters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Washington Razor Clam Fritters"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Washington Razor Clam Fritters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Washington%20Razor%20Clam%20Fritters","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Eggs","Scallion","Lemon","Milk","Flour","Old Bay/Creole"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"penn-cove-mussels-steamed-in-cider","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg Penn Cove mussels, scrubbed and debearded"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 shallots, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"330ml hard cider"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"Crusty bread and frites"}],"steps":[{"text":"Discard any mussels that stay open when tapped.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the shallots and garlic in the butter until soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cider and bring to a hard boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip in the mussels, cover, and steam 4-5 min, shaking the pot once, until they open.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the mussels out, whisk the cream and thyme into the liquor, pour it back over, and finish with parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Penn Cove on Whidbey Island grows some of the finest mussels in the country. Discard any that will not close before cooking and any that will not open after, and never overcook them, since a mussel goes from plump to shrunken in a minute.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Penn%20Cove%20Mussels%20Steamed%20in%20Cider%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Penn Cove Mussels Steamed in Cider"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Penn Cove Mussels Steamed in Cider","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Penn%20Cove%20Mussels%20Steamed%20in%20Cider","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Garlic","Shallot","Parsley","Thyme","Butter","Cream","Bread","Beer/Cider","Clove"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"seattle-dog","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 hot dogs"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 soft buns"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cream cheese, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced and griddled until soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"Sriracha or jalapenos to taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter for the griddle"}],"steps":[{"text":"Griddle the onions slowly in butter until soft, sweet, and lightly browned.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill the hot dogs until blistered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split the buns and griddle the cut faces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread a thick layer of softened cream cheese inside the warm bun so it melts slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the dog, pile on the griddled onions, and finish with sriracha or jalapenos.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A late-night Seattle street cart institution born outside the bars and music venues. The cream cheese sounds improbable and is entirely the point: spread it on the warm bun so it softens into a tangy sauce under the onions.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Seattle%20Dog%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Seattle Dog"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Seattle Dog","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Seattle%20Dog","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Chiles","Butter","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Bread","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"walla-walla-onion-rings","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 large Walla Walla sweet onions, sliced in 1cm rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cornstarch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cold beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Separate the onion rings and soak them in buttermilk 30 min.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the flour, cornstarch, baking powder, salt, and paprika, then add the cold beer to a batter just thicker than cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 190C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift each ring from the buttermilk, dip in the batter, let the excess run off, and fry 2-3 min until deep gold.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain on a rack and finish with flaky salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Walla Walla sweet is so low in sulfur it can be eaten like an apple, and it is the state vegetable. The cornstarch in the batter is what keeps the crust crisp rather than bready: do not substitute more flour for it.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Walla%20Walla%20Onion%20Rings%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Walla Walla Onion Rings"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Walla Walla Onion Rings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Walla%20Walla%20Onion%20Rings","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Buttermilk","Flour","Beer/Cider","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"alder-smoked-sockeye-salmon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 side of sockeye salmon, about 1.2kg, pin-boned"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the cure: 150g brown sugar, 100g coarse salt, 1 tbsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Alder wood chips, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp maple syrup or honey for glazing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the cure and pack it over the flesh side of the salmon. Cure in the fridge 6-8 hours.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the cure off thoroughly, pat dry, and rest the fish uncovered in the fridge 2 hours until the surface turns tacky: this pellicle is what smoke adheres to.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Smoke over alder at 80C for 3-4 hours until the flesh flakes and reads 60C.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brush with maple syrup in the last 30 min for a lacquered finish.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool and serve with rye bread, or flake into chowder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Alder is the Pacific Northwest's smoking wood, used by coastal tribes for millennia. Do not skip the drying step after rinsing: without a tacky pellicle the smoke slides off and the fish takes almost no flavor.","equipment":["Smoker"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking","Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, a sweet edge rounding the corners, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alder-Smoked%20Sockeye%20Salmon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alder-Smoked Sockeye Salmon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alder-Smoked Sockeye Salmon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alder-Smoked%20Sockeye%20Salmon","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Honey","Sugar","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"washington-smoked-salmon-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g hot-smoked salmon, flaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml fish or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon; lift out the bits and sweat the onion and celery in the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle in the flour and cook 2 min, then whisk in the stock.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes and simmer 15 min until tender.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the milk and cream and heat through without boiling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the flaked smoked salmon and dill at the very end, warm 2 min, and finish with lemon and pepper.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pike Place Market has sold versions of this for decades. Add the smoked salmon at the very end: it is already cooked, and simmering it breaks the flakes apart and turns the whole pot muddy and over-salted.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Washington%20Smoked%20Salmon%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Washington Smoked Salmon Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Washington Smoked Salmon Chowder","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Washington%20Smoked%20Salmon%20Chowder","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Bacon","Salmon","Smoked fish","Onion","Potato","Celery","Lemon","Dill","Milk","Cream","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"alaska-king-crab-legs-with-drawn-butter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2kg Alaskan king crab legs, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"2L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 lemons, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"225g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, smashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt and lemon wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"King crab is sold cooked and frozen, so the job is reheating, not cooking. Thaw it fully in the fridge first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the salted water and lemon to a boil in a wide pot with a steamer basket.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam the split legs 4-5 min, just until heated through. Any longer and the meat toughens and goes stringy.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter gently with the garlic, skim the foam, and pour off the clear butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the legs on a warm platter with drawn butter, lemon, and crackers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bering Sea king crab is the great luxury of the Alaskan table. Because it is cooked at sea and frozen immediately, over-steaming is the only real way to ruin it: four minutes to warm through is all it needs.","equipment":["Steamer"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alaska%20King%20Crab%20Legs%20with%20Drawn%20Butter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alaska King Crab Legs with Drawn Butter"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alaska King Crab Legs with Drawn Butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alaska%20King%20Crab%20Legs%20with%20Drawn%20Butter","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Garlic","Lemon","Butter","Clove"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"baked-alaska-halibut-with-mayonnaise-crust","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 halibut fillets, about 200g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g Parmesan, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 scallions, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g panko breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Lemon wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the halibut fillets dry and season lightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the mayonnaise, Parmesan, scallion, lemon, dill, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the mixture thickly over the top of each fillet, sealing it edge to edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the panko with melted butter and press onto the mayonnaise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C for 12-15 min until the crust is gold and the fish flakes at the thickest point.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false}],"note":"Halibut is extraordinarily lean and turns to chalk if overcooked; the mayonnaise blanket is an Alaskan cook's insurance, sealing in moisture as it bakes. Cover the fillet completely: any exposed fish dries out.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baked%20Alaska%20Halibut%20with%20Mayonnaise%20Crust%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baked Alaska Halibut with Mayonnaise Crust"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Baked Alaska Halibut with Mayonnaise Crust","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Baked%20Alaska%20Halibut%20with%20Mayonnaise%20Crust","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Scallion","Lemon","Dill","Butter","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Mayonnaise"]},{"slug":"akutaq","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g solid vegetable shortening or rendered fat, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml water, added slowly"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g wild berries (blueberries, salmonberries, crowberries)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whip the room-temperature fat with a wooden spoon or mixer until it is white, light, and fluffy; this takes real effort and is the whole technique.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the sugar gradually.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the water a spoonful at a time, whipping constantly, until the mixture is airy and holds soft peaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the berries gently by hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill until firm and serve cold in small portions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Akutaq is a traditional food of the Yup'ik, Inupiat, and other Alaska Native peoples, historically made with rendered seal or caribou fat and berries and carried as high-energy food. Whipping the fat until genuinely light is the skill; rushed, it stays greasy and heavy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akutaq%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Akutaq"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Akutaq","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Akutaq","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Berries","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"alaska-reindeer-sausage-skillet","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g reindeer sausage, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 potatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bell pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt, pepper, and hot sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the diced potatoes in the oil until crisp and golden, 12 min; season and push to one side.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the sliced reindeer sausage in the same pan until the edges crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion and pepper and cook until softened.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make four wells in the mixture and crack the eggs in. Cover and cook 4-5 min until the whites set and the yolks stay soft.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the cheese over, season, and serve with hot sauce straight from the skillet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Reindeer sausage is an Anchorage staple, sold from downtown carts and served at every diner breakfast. It is leaner and slightly sweeter than pork sausage, so brown it in the potato fat rather than dry: it has little of its own to give.","equipment":["Skillet","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alaska%20Reindeer%20Sausage%20Skillet%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alaska Reindeer Sausage Skillet"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alaska Reindeer Sausage Skillet","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alaska%20Reindeer%20Sausage%20Skillet","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Bell pepper","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Hot sauce"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"birch-glazed-king-salmon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 side of king salmon, about 1kg, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp birch syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp grated ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Dill to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the birch syrup, soy, mustard, lemon, garlic, and ginger into a glaze.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the salmon and set it skin-side down on a lined tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush a third of the glaze over the fish and roast at 220C for 8 min.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush again and roast 6-8 min more until the flesh flakes at the edge and reads 50-52C at the thickest point for medium.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush with the last of the glaze, scatter with dill, and rest 5 min before serving.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Birch syrup is tapped in the Alaskan interior and is darker, more mineral, and less sweet than maple, closer to molasses. Build the glaze in three thin coats rather than one thick one, or the sugars burn before the fish is done.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Birch-Glazed%20King%20Salmon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Birch-Glazed King Salmon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Birch-Glazed King Salmon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Birch-Glazed%20King%20Salmon","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Garlic","Ginger","Lemon","Dill","Soy sauce","Mustard","Clove"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"alaska-sourdough-pancakes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g active sourdough starter"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter and syrup to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"The night before, mix the starter with the flour and milk and leave covered at room temperature to ferment.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In the morning the sponge should be bubbly and smell tangy. Beat in the eggs, sugar, salt, and melted butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle the baking soda over and fold it through: it will foam as it meets the acid, and this is the lift.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook straight away on a buttered griddle over medium heat until bubbles break across the surface, then flip once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with butter and syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Alaskan prospectors were called sourdoughs because they slept with their starter to keep it alive through the winter. Add the soda at the last moment and cook immediately: the foam it makes against the sour sponge is what raises the pancake.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sourdough starter","Fermentation","Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sourdough starter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"dish":{"label":"Weissman’s starter guide","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alaska%20Sourdough%20Pancakes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alaska Sourdough Pancakes"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alaska Sourdough Pancakes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alaska%20Sourdough%20Pancakes","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","fermentation"]},{"slug":"alaska-salmon-pirok","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"Pastry for a double-crust pie, or 500g yeasted dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g salmon fillet, skinned and cut in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g rice, cooked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"55g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 hard-boiled eggs, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion in the butter until soft, then fold it through the cooked rice with the chopped egg, dill, lemon, salt, and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a baking dish or tray with the bottom pastry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer half the rice mixture, then the raw salmon chunks seasoned well, then the remaining rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the top crust over, seal the edges, cut steam vents, and brush with egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C for 45-55 min until deep gold. Rest 15 min before cutting.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pirok came to Kodiak and the Aleutians with Russian settlers and remains an Alaskan church-supper dish. The rice layers above and below the fish absorb its juices and protect the pastry: build it in that order or the base goes soggy.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alaska%20Salmon%20Pirok%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alaska Salmon Pirok"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alaska Salmon Pirok","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alaska%20Salmon%20Pirok","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Eggs","Onion","Lemon","Dill","Butter","Rice"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"alaska-moose-stew","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg moose shoulder or venison, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 potatoes, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, chunked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the moose well, season, and toss in the flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown hard in the oil in batches: crowding the pan steams the meat and costs you the crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions and garlic, add the tomato paste and cook it dark, then deglaze with the wine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the meat with the stock, juniper, bay, and thyme. Cover and braise at 150C for 2.5 hours.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes and carrots for the last 40 min and season again: game takes more salt than beef.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2400,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"A moose fills a family's freezer for a winter, and stew is where the tougher cuts go. Moose is even leaner than elk, so the browning, the juniper, and a long slow braise all work to supply what the animal itself does not.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Braising","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alaska%20Moose%20Stew%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alaska Moose Stew"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alaska Moose Stew","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alaska%20Moose%20Stew","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"juniper","term":"Juniper"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Venison/Game","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Berries","Thyme","Bay leaf","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"alaska-rhubarb-upside-down-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE TOPPING"},{"kind":"item","text":"85g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g rhubarb, cut in 3cm lengths"},{"kind":"section","label":"FOR THE CAKE"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"115g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml milk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the butter in a 23cm cake pan, stir in the brown sugar, and spread it over the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange the rhubarb tightly over the sugar in a single close layer: it shrinks considerably as it bakes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar, beat in the eggs and vanilla, then alternate the dry ingredients with the milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the batter over the rhubarb and bake at 175C for 45-55 min until a skewer comes clean.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest exactly 5 min, then invert onto a plate. Wait longer and the caramel welds to the pan.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rhubarb thrives in the long Alaskan summer daylight and grows to remarkable size in dooryard gardens. Pack the rhubarb tightly, as it collapses in the oven, and mind the five-minute rule on turning the cake out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Creaming butter & sugar","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alaska%20Rhubarb%20Upside-Down%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alaska Rhubarb Upside-Down Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alaska Rhubarb Upside-Down Cake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alaska%20Rhubarb%20Upside-Down%20Cake","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"alaska-wild-blueberry-fritters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g wild blueberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp melted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar or a lemon glaze"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, sugar, salt, and cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the egg, milk, and melted butter to a thick batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the wild blueberries with a light hand so they stay whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop heaped spoonfuls into 180C oil and fry 3-4 min, turning, until deep gold and cooked through.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and dust with icing sugar or drizzle with lemon glaze while warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wild Alaskan blueberries are small, dark, and far more intense than any cultivated berry, picked across the tundra in August. Keep the batter thick so the fritters hold their shape, and fold the berries gently: crushed berries bleed and weigh the batter down.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deep frying","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=deep%20frying%20technique%20oil%20temperature","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alaska%20Wild%20Blueberry%20Fritters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Alaska Wild Blueberry Fritters"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: J. Kenji López-Alt on Alaska Wild Blueberry Fritters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J.%20Kenji%20L%C3%B3pez-Alt%20Alaska%20Wild%20Blueberry%20Fritters","sub":"A YouTube search for J. Kenji López-Alt and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Berries","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"baechu-kimchi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 napa cabbage, about 1.2kg, quartered lengthwise"},{"kind":"item","text":"90g coarse sea salt, not iodised"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g gochugaru, coarse Korean chilli flake"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g garlic, about 8 cloves, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g salted shrimp (saeujeot), minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g glutinous rice flour cooked to a paste with 150ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g Korean radish, julienned"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 spring onions, in 4cm lengths"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the cabbage between every leaf, heaviest at the stem, and leave 2 hours turning once. It is ready when a stem folds double without cracking.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse three times and drain cut side down 30 min. Water left in the leaf dilutes the brine and slows the ferment.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the rice paste, then beat in gochugaru, garlic, ginger, shrimp and fish sauce. Fold through radish and spring onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wearing gloves, paint the paste over every leaf. Pack into a jar, press until brine rises over the top, and leave 2cm of headroom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ferment at 18 to 20C for 2 to 4 days until it tastes sour and fizzes against the tongue, then refrigerate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false}],"note":"The salting is the whole technique. It draws water out by osmosis until the cell walls go limp, which is why a ready stem folds instead of snapping, and it leaves the cabbage salty enough that lactobacillus can work while almost nothing else can. That selective pressure is what makes fermentation safe rather than lucky. Keep everything under the brine: the spoilage organisms that ruin a jar are aerobic, and a leaf floating in air is where they start. Bubbles and a clean sour smell mean it is working; fuzz on a dry surface means something was left exposed.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Fermentation","Salting to draw the water out","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","tangy-ferment","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, ferment tang with real depth behind it, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"dish":{"label":"Maangchi’s tongbaechu kimchi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eTucCw1w6Ak","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baechu%20Kimchi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baechu Kimchi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Chiles","Cabbage","Radish/Turnip","Rice","Flour","Fish sauce","Kimchi","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","fermentation"]},{"slug":"sauerkraut-the-two-ingredient-ferment","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg white cabbage, cored and shredded 2mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g fine sea salt, not iodised"}],"steps":[{"text":"Weigh the cabbage and use exactly 2% of that weight in salt. Precision here is not fussiness; it is the safety margin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss and then squeeze in handfuls for 10 min until the cabbage releases enough liquid to pool when pressed.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into a jar a fistful at a time, pressing hard, until the brine covers everything. Weight it down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ferment at 18 to 22C for 2 to 4 weeks, releasing pressure every few days. Taste weekly and refrigerate when it is sour enough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two percent salt by weight is the number to memorise, because it is the concentration at which lactobacillus is comfortable and most spoilage organisms are not. Below about 1.5% the kraut goes soft and can turn; above 3% the ferment stalls and stays harshly salty. Weigh the cabbage rather than the jar, and weigh the salt rather than measuring it by spoon, since flake salts differ in density by a factor of two. Cold slows everything: a cellar at 15C takes twice as long as a kitchen at 22C and produces a rounder, less sharp result.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauerkraut%2C%20the%20Two%20Ingredient%20Ferment%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauerkraut, the Two Ingredient Ferment"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ice-and-dilution-the-invisible-ingredient","term":"Ice & Dilution: The Invisible Ingredient"},{"slug":"ferment-funk-the-taste-of-time","term":"Ferment Funk: The Taste of Time"},{"slug":"sous-vide-hardware-circulators-sealers-and-safety-kit","term":"Sous Vide Hardware: Circulators, Sealers & Safety Kit"}],"pantryItems":["Cabbage"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"shio-koji","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g dried rice koji"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml water, filtered if yours is chlorinated"}],"steps":[{"text":"Break the koji grains apart with your fingers so none are clumped, then stir through the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add water to cover by 1cm and stir. Keep the surface submerged.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave at room temperature 7 to 10 days, stirring daily; it is ready when the grains are soft, the liquid is cloudy, and it smells of sweet chestnut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend smooth for a marinade, or leave whole. Refrigerate up to 6 months.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Koji is Aspergillus oryzae grown on rice, and what you are buying is its enzymes: proteases that cut protein into free amino acids, and amylases that cut starch into sugar. Rubbed on fish or chicken at about 10% of its weight for a few hours, shio koji does what a long dry age does, tenderising while building glutamate, which is why the result tastes savoury rather than merely salty. The limit is time: leave protein in it overnight and the proteases go too far, turning the surface soft and faintly bitter. Two to four hours is the working window.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shio%20Koji%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shio Koji"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Rice"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"preserved-lemons","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 unwaxed lemons, thin skinned"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of 4 further lemons, as needed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut each lemon in quarters from the top, stopping 1cm short of the base so it stays hinged.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack salt into every cut surface, close the fruit, and press into a sterilised jar, layering bay and peppercorns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press down hard. The lemons should release enough juice to cover; top up with fresh juice if not.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal and leave a month at room temperature, turning the jar occasionally. Refrigerate after opening.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a salt cure, not a fermentation in the lactic sense, and the salt is doing two jobs: drawing juice out to make the covering brine, and breaking down the pith so the bitterness leaves. That is why a month matters and a week does not. The rind is the ingredient; most cooks scrape the flesh away and use only the peel, sliced fine. A white bloom on the surface is usually harmless kahm yeast, and can be lifted off, but any fuzzy growth or a jar where the fruit has risen out of the brine should go in the bin.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Preserved%20Lemons%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Preserved Lemons"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Bay leaf","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"brown-veal-stock","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4kg veal bones, knuckle and marrow, cut 6cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g carrot, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g leek, white only"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head garlic, halved across"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, 6 parsley stalks, 1 tsp black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 litres cold water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Roast the bones at 220C (425F) for 45 min until deep brown, turning once. Pale bones make pale stock and there is no recovering it later.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the vegetables and roast 20 min more, then smear on the tomato paste and give it 10 min until it darkens and smells sweet rather than tinny.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Transfer to a tall pot, deglaze the roasting tray with water and scrape every brown speck in, then cover with cold water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring to 90C and hold there 8 to 12 hours, never boiling, skimming for the first hour. Add the aromatics in the final hour.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain through muslin without pressing, chill overnight, and lift the fat cap off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two rules carry this. Start cold, because proteins that will become scum are soluble in cold water and set into the liquid if you start hot, and a stock clouded at the beginning never clears. Then never let it boil: at a rolling boil the rendered fat emulsifies into the water and the stock turns greasy and permanently opaque, where at 90C it simply floats and can be lifted off cold. The gelatine comes from knuckle and connective tissue rather than marrow, so ask for joints. Properly made it sets to a firm jelly in the fridge, and that wobble is the only test worth running.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Roasting in a hot oven","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","fiery","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brown%20Veal%20Stock%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Brown Veal Stock"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"deglaze-and-fond","term":"Deglaze & Fond"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Leek","Tomato","Carrot","Parsley","Bay leaf","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"dashi-ichiban","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"20g kombu, wiped but not washed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre soft water"},{"kind":"item","text":"25g katsuobushi, thick shaved"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the kombu in the cold water 30 min, then bring slowly to 60C and hold 20 min. Do not let it boil.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the kombu out the moment small bubbles climb the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise to 80C, add the katsuobushi all at once, and take off the heat immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the flakes sink, about 2 min, then strain through muslin without pressing.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dashi is a lesson in restraint: almost everything that goes wrong is caused by more heat or more time. Kombu gives up glutamate slowly at low temperature, and boiling it drags out alginates that turn the broth slippery and faintly bitter. Katsuobushi gives inosinate in seconds, and left to steep it turns sour and fishy. The reason the two are used together is synergy rather than tradition: glutamate and inosinate together read as far more savoury than the sum of each alone, which is why a two ingredient broth can carry a whole meal. The spent kombu and flakes make niban dashi, a second, weaker stock for simmering vegetables.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Dashi","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dashi%2C%20Ichiban%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dashi, Ichiban"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"levain-boule","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g strong white bread flour, 12-13% protein"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g stoneground wholemeal flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"675 g water at 28C (82F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"18 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ripe sourdough starter"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g strong white bread flour, for the levain"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g water at 26C (79F), for the levain"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g rice flour, for the banneton"}],"steps":[{"text":"Build the levain: whisk the starter into its 50 g water, stir in the 50 g flour, and hold at 24C (75F) for 5-6 hours until it is domed, webbed inside and a spoonful floats.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the two flours with 640 g of the water and rest 45 minutes. At 76% this dough feels slack and tears easily; that is correct, the strength comes later.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze in the levain, then the salt with the last 35 g water. Bulk at 25C (77F) with four sets of stretch and fold at 30 minute intervals, and stop when the dough has risen by half, domes at the edges and shows bubbles against the wall of the tub, about 4-5 hours.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Preshape round, rest 25 minutes uncovered until the ball relaxes and spreads slightly, then shape with tension until the skin is drum tight and the seam grips itself. Into a rice floured banneton, seam up, and cold retard 12-16 hours at 4C (39F).","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":37500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in a cast iron pot preheated to 250C (480F): score deep, lid on for 20 minutes, then lid off at 230C (450F) for 20-25 minutes until the crust is dark chestnut and the centre reads 96C (205F).","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false}],"note":"A loaf climbs in the oven because trapped gas expands and water flashes to steam, but only while the crust is still plastic. Bake it dry from the start and the surface sets within three minutes: the score seals, the spring stalls, the loaf spreads instead of rising. The lid holds the dough's own moisture around it. Judge bulk by a 50% rise and bubbles at the tub wall, never by the clock, because 3C of room temperature moves it by an hour.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Sourdough starter"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sourdough starter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levain%20Boule%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Levain Boule"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour"]},{"slug":"focaccia-genovese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g water at 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g extra virgin olive oil, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g extra virgin olive oil, for the tray"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g water, 20 g extra virgin olive oil and 4 g fine salt, whisked, for the brine"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g flaky salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix flour, water and yeast to a shaggy mass and rest 20 minutes, then work in the salt and the 40 g oil until the dough pulls clean off the side of the bowl. At 80% it will be loose and sticky, not a ball.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bulk 2 hours at 22C (72F) with two folds in the tub, until doubled and wobbling like set custard when you shake it.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil a 30x40 cm tray, tip the dough in, and leave 45-60 minutes until it has crept to the corners on its own. Pull it there early and it will tear and shrink back.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dimple with flat fingers right down to the tray floor, whisk the brine again and pour it over so it pools in the holes, then scatter the flaky salt and prove 30 minutes until the surface is puffed and quivering.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake on the lower shelf at 240C (465F) for 18-20 minutes, until the top is amber and a spatula slides under a crisp, blistered base.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"The dimples are structural. Pressed to the tray floor they pin the crumb so it rises around them into ridges and pockets rather than doming into one slab. The brine does two jobs in sequence: the water keeps the top soft and stops the salt scorching while it evaporates, then the oil left behind fries the surface to amber. Dimple a cold, tight dough and the gluten tears and springs back, so let it slacken until a finger sinks without resistance.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Brining & curing","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","charred","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, char used as punctuation, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Focaccia%20Genovese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Focaccia Genovese"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"tawa-naan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g whole milk at 35C (95F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g whole milk yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ghee, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ghee, for brushing"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g nigella seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g coriander leaf, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the yeast and sugar into the warm milk and stand 10 minutes until it foams, then mix into the flour with the yoghurt, salt and the 30 g ghee to a soft, faintly tacky dough at about 64% hydration.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead 8-10 minutes until smooth, and until a pinched piece stretches to a translucent sheet before it tears. Prove covered 90 minutes at 26C (79F) until doubled.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into 8 pieces of about 110 g, ball them and rest 15 minutes, then roll each into a teardrop 5 mm thick and press in the nigella seeds and coriander.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a cast iron tawa over high heat to about 250C (480F): a drop of water should skitter and vanish in two seconds. Wet one face of the naan with your palm and slap it wet side down so it grips the iron.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 60-90 seconds until the top domes and the big bubbles set, then lift the tawa and invert it over the flame, holding the naan 20-30 seconds until it takes leopard spots. Brush with ghee and stack under a cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A tandoor cooks from two sides at once: the clay wall conducts into the base while radiant heat from above sets the top. A tawa gives you only the first half, so the wetted face glues the naan to the iron and you invert the whole pan over the flame for the second. The pan has to be genuinely hot, near 250C (480F). Too cool and the water boils away before the gluten grips, and the naan falls into the burner.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Tandoor breads","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tandoor breads","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=naan%20tandoor%20technique%20home%20oven","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tawa%20Naan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tawa Naan"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Yogurt","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"teff-injera","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g brown teff flour, stoneground"},{"kind":"item","text":"750 g water at 30C (86F), for the ferment"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g boiling water, for the absit"},{"kind":"item","text":"200-300 g water, for slackening"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the teff into the 750 g water until lump free and about the thickness of double cream. Cover loosely and leave at 22-25C (72-77F) for 3 days, each morning pouring off the grey liquid that has risen and stirring the batter down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"The ferment is ready when it smells sharply sour like buttermilk rather than yeasty, has stopped rising, and the surface carries a fine even stipple of bubbles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make the absit: whisk 150 g of the batter into the 300 g boiling water and cook 2-3 minutes, stirring, until it thickens to a glossy paste that coats the spoon. Cool it to blood heat, then beat it back into the batter with the salt and enough water that the batter pours off a ladle in an unbroken ribbon.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 2 hours until fine bubbles rise again, and heat a flat clay or non stick mitad to 200C (390F), hot enough that a bead of water dances.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour in one continuous spiral from the outside in, covering the plate in a single pass. Lid on the moment the surface loses its shine, and lift after 2-3 minutes once the eyes have set open and the edges curl free of the plate.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The eyes are burst gas bubbles frozen in setting starch. Three days of wild fermentation supplies the gas; the absit, a portion of batter cooked to a gel and stirred back in, thickens the batter just enough to hold a bubble while it climbs. Too thin and the gas escapes and the injera is blank; too thick and the eyes never reach the surface. The lid is not optional, since injera cooks on one side only and the trapped steam is what sets the top.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Injera","Sugar stages & caramel","Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Teff%20Injera%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Teff Injera"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","fermentation"]},{"slug":"pocket-pita","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"320 g water at 24C (75F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine semolina, for the peel"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix flour, water and yeast and rest 20 minutes, then add the salt and oil and knead 8 minutes until smooth. At 64% this is a firm dough: it should windowpane without tearing and leave the bench clean.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bulk 60-90 minutes at 24C (75F) until doubled and a floured finger leaves a dent that fills back slowly.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 8 pieces of about 105 g, ball them tightly, cover, and rest 25 minutes until they have slackened and spread a little.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Preheat a baking stone or 6 mm steel at 260C (500F) for a full 45 minutes. Roll each ball to an even disc 4-5 mm thick, working from the centre out and leaving no thin patches.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide onto the stone: it should balloon completely inside 90 seconds. Pull it at 2-3 minutes while still pale with only faint blisters, and stack under a cloth so it steams itself soft.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pocket is steam. On a 260C (500F) surface both faces set into gas tight skins within seconds, and the water in the crumb then flashes and forces them apart. Anything that leaks kills it: a nicked skin, a thin patch from uneven rolling, or a heavy dusting of flour that stops the surface sealing. The usual culprit is a stone given ten minutes of preheat instead of forty five, so it surrenders its heat to the cold disc and the skin sets slowly.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pocket%20Pita%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pocket Pita"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour","Semolina"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"hand-rolled-bagels","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1000 g high gluten bread flour, 13-14% protein"},{"kind":"item","text":"560 g water at 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g barley malt syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 litres water, for the boil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g barley malt syrup, for the boil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sesame or poppy seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g neutral oil, for the trays"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix everything to a stiff, dry mass and knead on low in a mixer for 10-12 minutes. At 56% it will never look silky; it is ready when it is dense and barely tacky and a torn edge shows fine strands rather than a ragged break.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 16 pieces of about 100 g, roll each to a 22 cm rope, wrap it around four knuckles and roll the overlap against the bench to weld the seam shut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove on oiled trays 20 minutes at room temperature, then cold retard 12-18 hours at 4C (39F). They are ready when a test bagel dropped into cold water floats within 10 seconds.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the pot to a rolling boil with the 40 g malt. Boil 30 seconds a side for a thin crisp skin, 60 for a thicker chew, drain on a rack for a moment, then press seed side down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 240C (465F) for 16-20 minutes, turning the tray at 10 minutes, until the crust is glossy mahogany and the base sounds hollow.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"The boil gelatinises the outer millimetre of starch into a skin that has already set before the oven can inflate the crumb. That restraint is the whole point: it is why a bagel chews and a bread roll does not. Much beyond a minute a side and the skin thickens to leather and wrinkles as it cools. The malt in the water is there as sugar for colour, since a 56% dough retarded overnight has little of its own left to brown with.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Boiling bagels","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hand-Rolled%20Bagels%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hand-Rolled Bagels"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour","Couscous/Bulgur","Sesame"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"nixtamal-corn-tortillas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg dried field corn, white or blue dent"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g food grade calcium hydroxide, cal"},{"kind":"item","text":"150-250 g warm water, for grinding"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 plastic sheets cut from a freezer bag, for the press"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the corn, then bring it to the boil with the 3 litres of water and the cal, stirring as it heats; the water will turn yellow and feel slippery.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold just below a simmer for 15-20 minutes, until the skin slips off a kernel rubbed between thumb and finger but the centre is still chalky and firm. Cut the heat and steep, covered, 8-14 hours.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":37200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and rub the corn hard through several changes of water until most of the hulls float away and the water runs almost clear. It should smell sweet and of corn, never soapy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind wet to a smooth masa in a mill or in batches in a food processor, adding warm water only as needed. It is right when a ball flattened between your palms does not crack at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press 30 g balls between the plastic sheets to 2 mm and cook on a comal at 220C (430F): 15 seconds, flip, 45 seconds, flip back, and it should dome and puff within 20 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack them in a cloth lined basket straight off the comal so they steam each other and stay foldable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Alkali does what plain water cannot: it dissolves the hull, frees the bound niacin, and lets the corn's starch and protein bond into a dough that holds together with no gluten at all. Untreated cornmeal and water will never do this. Cook past the point where the hull slips and the kernels go mushy, and the masa turns gluey and sticks to the press. Overdose the cal and it tastes of soap. One percent of the corn's weight, no more.","equipment":["Food processor"],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nixtamal%20Corn%20Tortillas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nixtamal Corn Tortillas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sieves-chinois-tamis-and-food-mill","term":"Sieves: Chinois, Tamis & Food Mill"}],"pantryItems":["Corn","Bread"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"brioche-nanterre","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"240 g cold eggs, about 4 large"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g unsalted butter at 18C (64F), in 20 g pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk beaten with 15 g milk, for the wash"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g soft butter, for the tin"}],"steps":[{"text":"In a mixer with a dough hook combine flour, sugar, salt, yeast, eggs and milk. Mix 4 minutes on low, then 8-10 minutes on medium until the dough climbs the hook, clears the bowl completely and windowpanes.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":1080,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the butter a piece at a time with the mixer running, each one only once the last has vanished. The finished dough should be glossy and elastic and no warmer than 24C (75F); if it hits 26C (79F), stop and chill the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bulk 1 hour at room temperature, fold once in the bowl, then chill 6 hours or overnight at 4C (39F) until it is firm enough to handle without flour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3840,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 8 pieces of about 115 g, ball each one tightly against the bench, and set them in two rows of four in a buttered 25x11 cm tin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove at 24-26C (75-79F), no hotter, for 2-3 hours, until the dough fills the tin and a light press springs back slowly leaving a shallow mark. Egg wash twice, ten minutes apart.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (355F) for 30-35 minutes to an internal 92C (198F), tenting with foil if it colours early, and turn out at once.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false}],"note":"Fat coats gluten strands and stops them linking, so the network has to be built first and the butter worked in afterwards, piece by piece, into a dough that already windowpanes. Temperature is the other half of it. Above roughly 26C (79F) the butter melts out of suspension, the dough goes greasy and slack, and it bakes heavy with a dense ring at the base. Cold eggs, a cool room, and stop the mixer the moment it warms.","equipment":["Stand mixer","Thermometer"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brioche%20Nanterre%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Brioche Nanterre"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"laminated-roti-canai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"260 g water at 24C (75F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, 55 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g ghee, melted, for coating and cooking"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the water, egg, condensed milk, salt and sugar together, stir in the flour, then knead 10-12 minutes until the dough is satiny and a pulled piece thins to a film before tearing.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 8 balls of about 105 g, roll each in melted ghee and set them well apart in a tray with more ghee poured over so they sit half submerged. Cover and rest 6-8 hours at 22C (72F) until they are slack and floppy and hold a fingerprint.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"On a lightly oiled bench flatten a ball with the heel of your hand, then flip and slap it outward until it is a sheet about 50 cm across that you can read newsprint through. Tears smaller than a coin do not matter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the far edge in repeatedly to make a long band, coil the band into a flat spiral and tuck the tail underneath. Rest the coils 10 minutes so the gluten lets go.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press a coil to a 20 cm disc, cook on a griddle at 200C (390F) in a little ghee for about 2 minutes a side until deeply blistered, then clap it between your palms so the layers shatter apart.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The layers are one sheet, not many. A long rest under ghee relaxes the gluten until the dough will pull to a translucent film, and the fat coating both faces stops those faces welding when the sheet is folded and coiled; steam in the gaps then drives them apart in the pan. Rush the rest and the dough snaps back and tears into holes. Stretch it dry, with the ghee wiped off, and the coil bakes into a single tough disc.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Lamination","Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Laminated%20Roti%20Canai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Laminated Roti Canai"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"vollkornbrot-with-rye-soaker","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"350 g wholegrain rye flour, 90 g for the sour and 260 g for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g cracked rye or coarse rye chops"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g boiling water, for the soaker"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g water at 28C (82F), for the sour"},{"kind":"item","text":"170 g water at 40C (105F), for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ripe rye sourdough starter"},{"kind":"item","text":"16 g fine salt, 8 g in the soaker and 8 g in the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g sunflower seeds, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g neutral oil, for the tin"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sixteen hours ahead, whisk the starter into the 90 g warm water, beat in the 90 g rye flour, and hold at 26C (79F) until it is domed, webbed inside when spooned open, and sharply acidic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"At the same time pour the boiling water over the cracked rye with 8 g of the salt, cover, and leave 12 hours until the grains are soft and no free water is left in the bowl.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat the sour, the soaker, the 260 g rye flour, the 170 g warm water, the remaining salt and the seeds together for 4-5 minutes. It stays a stiff, sticky paste: rye never forms a smooth ball, so do not chase one.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"With wet hands load it into an oiled 25x11 cm tin, press out the air pockets and smooth the top with a wet spatula. Prove at 28C (82F) for 60-90 minutes, until fine cracks open right across the surface. That crazing is the only reliable signal.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 250C (480F) for 15 minutes, drop to 180C (355F) and give it 60-70 minutes more, to an internal 98C (208F). Cool completely, wrap in a cloth, and do not cut it for 24 hours.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":41100,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rye builds no gluten. Pentosans hold the water instead, and the crumb is set by gelatinised starch, which is why you read a rye by its surface crazing rather than by springback. The acidity of the sour is structural: without it rye's own amylase carries on chopping starch into sugar as the loaf heats, and the crumb bakes sticky and sinks in the middle. The starch also needs a day to firm up, so a warm slice will always seem gummy.","equipment":["Thermometer"],"techniques":["Sourdough starter","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","nutty-toasted","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sourdough starter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vollkornbrot%20with%20Rye%20Soaker%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vollkornbrot with Rye Soaker"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bread%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bread Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"jiaozi-pork-and-napa-cabbage","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g plain flour, plus more for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"155g cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g fine salt, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g pork shoulder, 20% fat, hand chopped or coarse minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g napa cabbage, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g salt, for the cabbage"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g spring onion, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g toasted sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g cold chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chinkiang vinegar and chilli oil, to dip"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the 4g salt into the cold water, pour it into the flour and work to a stiff shaggy mass. Knead 6 minutes until smooth and slow to spring back, then cover and rest 40 minutes: it should give like an earlobe, not like putty.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the cabbage with its 6g salt, leave 15 minutes, then wring it in a cloth until no more water runs. Make it weep now or it weeps inside the wrapper.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the cold stock into the pork in three additions until the meat is sticky and holds a ridge, then fold in cabbage, spring onion, ginger, soy, wine, sesame oil and pepper. Chill 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough into 2cm ropes, cut 10g pieces, and roll each to an 8cm round thick at the centre and thin at the rim. Fill with 15g, pleat one side only against the flat far side, and press the seam shut with dry fingers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil in plenty of water, adding a cup of cold water each time it returns to a hard boil, three times over. They are ready when the skins go glossy and taut and a dumpling rides high and firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold water hydrates flour slowly and builds gluten, so the wrapper stays elastic and survives a rolling boil; hot water would gelatinise the starch and denature that gluten, giving a soft skin fit only for steaming. Salting the cabbage matters as much: unsalted it weeps inside the wrapper and splits the seam. To keep them, freeze apart on a floured tray until solid, then bag and boil straight from frozen. A thawed dumpling goes slack and tears.","equipment":["Rolling pin","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Pleating dumplings","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","gently hot","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiaozi%2C%20Pork%20and%20Napa%20Cabbage%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jiaozi, Pork and Napa Cabbage"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Chiles","Cabbage","Flour","Soy sauce","Sesame","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["pleating-dumplings","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"xiao-long-bao","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g pork skin, scraped clean of back fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g chicken wings, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.6L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g spring onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"125g water at 40C (104F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g pork belly and shoulder, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g toasted sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fine ginger julienne and Chinkiang vinegar, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch the pork skin and wings 3 minutes and rinse, then simmer with the 1.6L water, ginger and spring onion at a bare tremble for 3 hours. Strain and chill overnight: it must set firm enough to cut in clean cubes, with no wobble.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":39780,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dice 300g of the set aspic into 5mm cubes and keep it cold. Beat soy, wine, sugar, pepper and sesame oil into the pork until sticky, fold the aspic through at the last moment, and refrigerate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead flour, salt and the warm water 8 minutes to a smooth firm dough, rest 30 minutes, then roll 10g pieces into 8cm rounds, thin at the rim and noticeably thick at the centre.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill with 20g and gather 16 to 18 pleats around the rim with your thumb, twisting the last pleat closed. Keep the seam dry: a floured rim will not weld.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam over a hard boil on oiled parchment for 8 minutes, until the skin is translucent and the soup audibly swims when you nudge the basket.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false}],"note":"The soup is not poured in, it is set. Three hours of pork skin and chicken wings turns collagen into gelatin, solid at fridge temperature and liquid by about 35C (95F), so you fold a cold cube and steam it back to broth. Two failure modes: a thin, wet base blows out under pressure, and steaming past 9 minutes stretches the skin until it leaks. Keep the base thicker than the rim and pull them early.","equipment":["Parchment"],"techniques":["Pleating dumplings","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking","Straining & passing through a sieve","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Xiao%20Long%20Bao%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Xiao Long Bao"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Flour","Soy sauce","Sesame","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["pleating-dumplings","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"har-gow","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100g wheat starch, not wheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"25g tapioca starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g salt, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g water at a full rolling boil"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g raw prawns, peeled and deveined"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g tinned bamboo shoot, finely diced and blanched"},{"kind":"item","text":"25g pork back fat, finely minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g salt, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g toasted sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g potato starch"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chop two thirds of the prawns into 1cm pieces and mince the rest to a paste. Combine with bamboo, pork fat, salt, sugar, pepper, sesame oil and potato starch, and chill 1 hour until the mix is tacky and stiff enough to stand on a spoon.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the wheat starch, tapioca starch and salt in a metal bowl, pour the water on straight from the boil in one go, stir hard for 10 seconds, and cover for 2 minutes.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the lard and knead the hot dough on an oiled bench until it is satiny and smooth, like warm marzipan. Keep it under a cloth: it is workable only while warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press 12g pieces flat with an oiled cleaver blade into 8cm discs. Fill with 15g and pleat 8 to 10 folds on the near side only, pressing each onto the flat far side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam on oiled parchment over a hard boil for exactly 6 minutes, until the skins are white-clear and taut and the prawns have turned through.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":360,"estimated":false}],"note":"Wheat starch is flour with the gluten washed out, so nothing here is elastic: the structure comes from starch granules swelling and gelatinising, which is also why the cooked skin reads translucent rather than opaque. That needs water at a genuine boil, 100C (212F). Water at 90C leaves the dough chalky, cracking at the edges and grey once steamed. Work fast, keep it covered, and never steam past six minutes or the skins slump and go gummy.","equipment":["Parchment"],"techniques":["Pleating dumplings","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Har%20Gow%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Har Gow"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Shrimp","Potato","Bread","Flour","Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["pleating-dumplings","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"momo-kathmandu-style","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"165g cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g salt, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g minced buffalo or lamb, 20% fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g red onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g garlic and ginger paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g coriander leaf and stem, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g timur or Sichuan pepper, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g salt, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g sesame seeds, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 dried red chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cloves garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g lime juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the salted water into the flour, knead 8 minutes to a firm smooth dough, and rest it covered 30 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Char the tomatoes, chillies and garlic in a dry pan until blistered and collapsing, then blend with the toasted sesame, lime juice and salt to a loose, nutty achar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix meat, onion, garlic-ginger paste, coriander, cumin, timur, salt and mustard oil, working it 2 minutes until it turns pale and sticky and clings to the bowl. Fry a teaspoon to check the salt.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 10g pieces into 9cm rounds with a thick centre. Fill with 20g and either pleat all the way round into a closed purse or fold to a half moon and crimp the arc.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam in an oiled basket, none touching, for 10 to 12 minutes over a hard boil, until the skins go shiny and the filling reads 74C (165F). Serve with the achar.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true}],"note":"Momo carry no added stock: the juice is onion water plus rendered fat, which is why the onion goes in raw and finely diced and the meat is worked until sticky. That salted working dissolves myosin, which glues the filling into a mass that holds its own liquid instead of shedding it. The steamer is where beginners lose them. Surface starch gelatinises against bare metal and welds fast, so oil the basket or line it with cabbage leaf, and leave a finger between each.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pleating dumplings","Pie crust & blind baking","Kneading dough","Charring over open flame","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","fiery","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Momo%2C%20Kathmandu%20Style%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Momo, Kathmandu Style"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sichuan-peppercorn-and-mala","term":"Sichuan Peppercorn & Málà"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Flour","Sesame","Mustard","Cumin","Clove","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["pleating-dumplings","pie-crust-and-blind-baking"]},{"slug":"pierogi-z-kapusta-i-grzybami","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg (50g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"140g warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g soured cream, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g dried porcini"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g sauerkraut, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g butter, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g butter, to fry"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g soured cream, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the porcini with 200g boiling water and leave 20 minutes. Lift them out, chop them, and keep the strained liquor.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze the sauerkraut dry and chop it. Sweat the onion in the 80g butter until golden, add kraut, mushrooms, bay, pepper and the liquor, and cook down 25 minutes until a spoon drawn across the pan leaves a dry track. Cool completely.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub flour, salt, egg, soured cream and warm water into a soft dough, knead 5 minutes, and rest 30 minutes under a bowl. Unrested it shrinks straight back off the pin.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 2mm thin and cut 8cm discs. Wipe any floury rim with a wet finger, load 15g of cold filling, and crimp firmly: flour is a release agent, not a glue.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer in well salted water at a lazy bubble, never a hard boil, for 90 seconds after they rise. Drain, then fry in the 50g butter until blistered brown. Serve with soured cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two mechanisms decide this one. The dough is warm water and enriched, which relaxes gluten quickly and keeps the sheet extensible; skip the rest and it springs back, so you roll thinner and thinner chasing the shape. Then the filling must be dry and cold, because warm wet kraut steams inside the pierog and forces the seam apart in the pot. And floating is not doneness, it is buoyancy from trapped steam. Give them 90 seconds beyond it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pierogi","Pie crust & blind baking","Kneading dough","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","tangy-ferment","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":39,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pierogi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20pierogi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pierogi%20z%20Kapust%C4%85%20i%20Grzybami%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pierogi z Kapustą i Grzybami"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Mushrooms","Cabbage","Bay leaf","Butter","Cream","Flour","Dried mushrooms"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"kalakuri-khinkali","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cold water, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g beef chuck, hand chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g pork shoulder, hand chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g onion, minced to a pulp"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"180g cold water, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g ground caraway"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g coriander leaf, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse black pepper, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead flour, salt and cold water 10 minutes into a very stiff dough that resists the heel of your hand, rest it covered 1 hour, then knead 3 minutes more.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":780,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the meats with onion, garlic, salt, caraway, pepper and coriander, then beat in the cold water in four additions until the filling is loose and almost pourable with no free water standing in the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 45g pieces into 14cm rounds, thin at the edge. Cup one in your palm, load 45g of filling, and gather the rim into 18 or more pleats, twisting the topknot tight and pinching off any long tail.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt a wide pot of water heavily, stir it into a spinning vortex, and slide the khinkali in so they ride the current instead of settling on the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 7 to 8 minutes from the moment they surface, until the dough is glossy and the belly hangs heavy. Serve with coarse pepper and nothing else.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No broth is added: it is plain cold water beaten into raw meat, held there by salt-solubilised protein and released as the fat renders. That makes the dough a bag under pressure, so it is kneaded stiff and rested long to build real gluten, and the twisted knot is a thick plug of it, the one place that never leaks. A khinkali sitting on the pot base cooks a flat split spot, which is why the water is spun before they go in. Hold the knot, drink first, leave the knot.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pleating dumplings","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kalakuri%20Khinkali%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kalakuri Khinkali"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Flour","Clove","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["pleating-dumplings","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"kayseri-mantisi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg (50g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g salt, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g minced lamb, 20% fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g onion, grated and squeezed dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g salt, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g strained yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cloves garlic, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g dried mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g pul biber"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead flour, salt, egg and water to a firm dough, rest 40 minutes, then roll it to 1mm, thin enough to read the grain of the bench through it.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the sheet into 1.5cm squares. Put a lentil-sized 1g of the seasoned lamb on each, lift the four corners over the top and press them together into a tiny purse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the garlic into the yoghurt and leave it at room temperature. Cold yoghurt chills the dumplings and grains the moment hot butter hits it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter, stir in tomato paste, pul biber and mint off the heat, and hold it warm without letting it colour past the smell of toasted mint.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the mantı in salted water 8 to 10 minutes, until there is no chalk left in the dough at the pinch. Drain, bed them on the yoghurt, and spoon the spiced butter over.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kayseri counts them: forty to a spoon is the local boast, so the sheet goes to 1mm and the meat to a lentil. The dough needs the egg for strength, because at that thickness a water dough simply tears at the pinch. The sauce is the real trap. Yoghurt casein aggregates and turns grainy when heated fast, so the yoghurt is served warm from the room, the butter is poured over, and the mixture never sees the pot. A spoon of the cooking water tempers it.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Salting to draw the water out","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","umami-deep","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kayseri%20Mant%C4%B1s%C4%B1%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kayseri Mantısı"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Mint","Butter","Yogurt","Flour","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"hitokuchi-gyoza-hakata-style","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"110g water at 90C (194F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g salt, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g minced pork, 25% fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g cabbage, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g salt, for the cabbage"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g garlic chives, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 clove garlic, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g toasted sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g neutral oil, to fry"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g water, to steam"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ponzu and yuzu kosho, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour the near-boiling water into the salted flour, stir with chopsticks until it clumps, then knead 6 minutes once it is cool enough to handle. Rest 45 minutes: the dough should feel slack and pliable, not springy.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the cabbage, wait 15 minutes, wring it out hard, and mix it into the pork with the chives, ginger, garlic, soy, sesame oil and sugar until sticky. Chill.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Roll the dough into 8g balls and press each to a 6cm round. Fill with 8g, pleat three or four folds along one side, and stand them on a floured tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set a heavy pan over medium-high heat with the neutral oil, pack the gyoza in tight rows, and fry undisturbed about 2 minutes, until the bases are set and hazelnut brown.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the 80g water, cover at once, and steam 4 minutes. Uncover, let every drop of water go, then fry 1 minute more until the bases crackle under the spatula. Invert onto the plate.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Hakata version is one bite each, which is the point: a small skin crisps all over instead of turning leathery at the edges. The sequence is fry, steam, fry. That first fry sets and browns the base so the water that follows cannot dissolve it; add water to an unset base and the loose starch glues the row to the pan. The hot water dough is deliberate too, gelatinised starch and slack gluten, so it rolls thin and stays tender under the lid.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Rolling pin","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Pleating dumplings","Kneading dough","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","herb-fresh","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hitokuchi%20Gyoza%2C%20Hakata%20Style%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hitokuchi Gyoza, Hakata Style"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Garlic","Ginger","Cabbage","Chives","Flour","Soy sauce","Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["pleating-dumplings","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"ravioli-del-plin","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g 00 flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 egg yolks (150g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole egg (50g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g roast veal or pork shoulder, cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g roast rabbit or chicken thigh, cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cooked spinach, squeezed dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, to bind the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"1g grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 sage leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g roasting juices"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the yolks, whole egg and salt into the flour, knead 10 minutes until the surface turns silky, then wrap and rest 1 hour. A yolk dough is dry, and without that rest it cracks along the edges on the pin.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mince the roast meats fine with the spinach, then bind with the egg, Parmigiano, nutmeg, salt and pepper. It should hold its shape on a spoon rather than slump.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll one sheet to about 0.8mm, thin enough to read print through. Pipe hazelnut-sized mounds in a line 1cm from the long edge, then fold the near edge over them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pinch hard between each mound with thumb and forefinger, pushing the trapped air out sideways as you go. That pinch, the plin, is the entire seal. Cut through each pinch with a fluted wheel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 90 seconds in salted water, then finish in butter foamed with sage and the roasting juices until the sauce clings and the pasta darkens a shade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things seal a plin, and neither is water. The dough sits near 45 percent hydration, so it is tacky and welds to itself; flour the bench under the fold and nothing will hold. The pinch does the rest, driving out the trapped air that would otherwise expand in the pot and split the seam from inside. At 0.8mm they overcook in seconds, so count ninety and lift. Any that burst were dusted, not undercooked.","equipment":["Piping bag","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Roasting in a hot oven","Salted water & the float test","Salting to draw the water out","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ravioli%20del%20Plin%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ravioli del Plin"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"parmigiano-reggiano","term":"Parmigiano-Reggiano"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Eggs","Spinach","Sage","Butter","Parmesan","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"empanadas-de-pino","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g lard, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g beef chuck, cut into 5mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g onion, cut into 5mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g lard, for the pino"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g sweet paprika or merken"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"25g plain flour, to bind"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 black olives, stones in"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 hard-boiled eggs, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk beaten with 15g milk, to glaze"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion in the 40g lard with a good pinch of salt for 25 minutes, until translucent and sweet with no raw bite left, then add the beef and cook only until it loses its red.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in cumin, paprika, oregano and the 25g flour, cook 2 minutes, add the stock, and simmer until a spoon drawn through leaves a track that closes slowly. Cool, then chill overnight: the pino has to be cold and set.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the melted lard into the flour and salt, add the egg and warm milk, and knead 5 minutes to a smooth soft dough. Rest 30 minutes, then roll 3mm thick and cut 18cm discs.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Load each disc off centre with 60g of cold pino, an olive, a slice of egg and a few raisins. Fold the sides in and the top over into a rectangle, and press the edges shut with a wet finger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sit them folds down on the tray, glaze, and bake at 220C (425F) for 22 to 25 minutes, until deep brown and hollow when tapped.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"The rule is cold filling in a warm-fat dough. Hot pino melts the lard in the pastry before the oven can set it, and the empanada tears and bleeds; chilled overnight it is a solid block you can fold around cleanly. Cook the onion well past translucent first, because raw onion water is what floods the seam later. Bake hot at 220C (425F) so the crust sets before the filling boils and lifts the lid off it.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Empanada folding","Reducing a sauce","Kneading dough","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","earthy","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Empanada folding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=empanada%20repulgue%20folding%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Empanadas%20de%20Pino%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Empanadas de Pino"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dumpling%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dumpling Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Olives","Dried fruit","Oregano","Milk","Flour","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"fresh-soybean-tempeh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g dried soybeans"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 litres cold water, for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml white vinegar (5 percent acidity)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g tempeh starter (Rhizopus oligosporus)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the beans in the cold water for 12 hours, then rub handfuls between your palms under water until the hulls float free; skim and repeat until fewer than one bean in ten still wears a skin.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the dehulled beans in fresh water with the vinegar for 30 minutes, until a bean crushes to paste between finger and thumb but still holds its shape, then drain hard.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread on towels and dry under a fan until the surface is matte and the beans squeak against the cloth, and they have cooled below 35C (95F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the starter through, pack 2 cm deep into bags or a tray pricked every 2 cm, and incubate at 30C (86F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"At 24 to 36 hours the cake is bound solid, white as felt and warm to the hand. Grey or black patches at the holes are spores and harmless; a slimy surface or a smell of ammonia means bacteria won, and it goes out.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rhizopus knits the beans into one cake, but only on a substrate it has to itself. Vinegar in the cooking water holds the beans near pH 4.5, which the mould tolerates and Bacillus does not, and drying the surface takes away the free water bacteria need. Once the mycelium takes hold the block self-heats 3 to 5C above ambient, so keep the box at 30C (86F) or the core passes 40C and kills the culture that made it.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fresh%20Soybean%20Tempeh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fresh Soybean Tempeh"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"acidity-the-backbone","term":"Acidity (The Backbone)"}],"pantryItems":["Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"black-tea-kombucha","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3.5 litres filtered water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g loose black tea"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g white sugar (about 70 g per litre)"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml mature unflavoured kombucha, as starter (10 percent by volume)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kombucha pellicle"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml fruit juice, for the second ferment (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the tea in 1 litre of just-boiled water for 10 minutes, strain, and stir in the sugar until no grain is left on the bottom.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top up with cold water to 3.5 litres and let it fall below 30C (86F) before it goes near the culture; above 40C (104F) the heat kills it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the starter liquid and the pellicle, cover with tight-woven cloth under a band, and hold at 22 to 26C (72 to 79F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste through a straw from day 7. It is ready when it is sharply tart with only a trace of sweetness behind it, around pH 3.0, usually day 8 to 14.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For fizz, decant into pressure-rated swing-tops with 10 percent fruit juice, leave 2 to 3 days at room temperature, burping one bottle daily as a gauge, then refrigerate to stop it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two organisms working in sequence: yeasts split the sucrose and ferment it to ethanol, then acetic acid bacteria oxidise that ethanol to acid. The starter liquid matters more than the pellicle, because 10 percent by volume puts the jar at pH 4.5 on day one, under the line where mould can take hold. Brown strands and a new pale film are normal. Dry fuzzy blue or green spots standing proud of the surface are mould: bin the lot, liquid included.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking","Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Black%20Tea%20Kombucha%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Black Tea Kombucha"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking","fermentation"]},{"slug":"rye-sourdough-starter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g wholegrain rye flour, across the whole build"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g water at 28C (82F), across the whole build"}],"steps":[{"text":"Day 1: stir 50 g rye flour into 50 g water to a thick paste, scrape the sides down, cover loosely and hold at 24 to 26C (75 to 79F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Days 2 and 3: it balloons, smells sharp and cheesy, then collapses and goes silent. Do not restart it; stir it down and wait.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"From day 3, once daily, discard all but 25 g and feed 50 g flour with 50 g water. Once it is rising within 8 hours, move to two feeds a day.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"It is ready when it doubles in 4 to 6 hours at 25C (77F), domes at the top, and smells of yogurt and green apple rather than acetone. Expect day 7 to day 14.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"To hold it, feed and refrigerate; revive with two warm feeds 12 hours apart before you bake.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Days two and three lie to you. Leuconostoc and coliforms bloom first, gas the paste and smell cheesy, then die in the acid they make as the pH drops under 4. The culture you actually want, lactobacilli with wild yeast, arrives after that lull, so a stalled rise on day 3 is progress and not failure. Discarding is dilution, not waste: an unfed starter drifts to pH 3.3, where the yeast stops making gas. Grey liquid on top means underfed.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sourdough starter","Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","bright","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, acid holding everything up to the light, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sourdough starter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"dish":{"label":"Weissman’s starter guide","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rye%20Sourdough%20Starter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rye Sourdough Starter"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"lacto-fermented-red-chilli-sauce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg red chillies such as fresno or red jalapeno, stems removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g garlic cloves, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"32 g fine non-iodised sea salt (3 percent of the mash weight)"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml white wine vinegar, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blitz the chillies and garlic to a coarse mash, weigh it, then work in salt at 3 percent of that weight until the mash slackens and turns wet and glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into a jar, press down until liquid rises over the solids, weight them under, and leave 5 cm of headspace beneath an airlock or a lid you burp daily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ferment at 20 to 24C (68 to 75F): it bubbles hard by day 3, the colour dulls from scarlet to brick, and the raw green smell turns sour and fruity by day 14.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend smooth with the vinegar and check that a strip reads pH 3.6 or lower before it leaves the jar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bottle and refrigerate; pass it through a coarse sieve first if you want it pourable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt at 3 percent of mash weight is the whole safety argument: it slows every organism in the jar except lactobacilli, which keep working and take the mash under pH 4.0 inside two weeks, below the line where anything dangerous grows. Whatever sits above the brine will mould, so weight the mash down. A flat white skin is kahm yeast and skims off. Fuzzy blue, green or black growth is mould, and that jar goes out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","aromatic","warm-spiced","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lacto-Fermented%20Red%20Chilli%20Sauce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lacto-Fermented Red Chilli Sauce"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"natto","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g small dried soybeans"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres water, for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.1 g natto spore powder (Bacillus subtilis var. natto), or 20 g live natto"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml water, boiled and cooled to 70C (158F), to carry the spores"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the beans for 18 hours in cool water, until they have roughly tripled in weight and one crushes between finger and thumb with no hard core left.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and cook until a bean smears to paste under light thumb pressure: 40 minutes at full pressure, or about 4 hours steamed.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2640,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scald the tray, spoon and cover with boiling water, then stir the spore solution through the beans while they are still around 70C (158F), hot enough to kill rivals and harmless to spores.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread 2 cm deep, cover with foil pricked all over so air still reaches the surface, and hold at 40 to 42C (104 to 108F) for 22 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"It is done when a white bloom coats the beans and a lifted spoon pulls long threads. Refrigerate 24 hours before eating: the strings tighten and the ammonia settles back.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Natto is the odd one in this chapter: an alkaline ferment, not an acid one, run by a Bacillus that is aerobic and heat loving. Spores survive boiling, so adding them at about 70C (158F) sterilises the beans around them. Hold 40 to 42C (104 to 108F) with air reaching the surface; under 37C you get a weak bloom and no threads, and past 24 hours the ammonia turns from savoury to acrid and the beans go bitter.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Natto%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Natto"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":[],"judgedBy":["fermentation","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"full-sour-dill-pickles","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg small pickling cucumbers, 8-10 cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g non-iodised salt (a 3.5 percent brine)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head of flowering dill, or 15 g dill seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g dried chilli flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 fresh grape, oak or blackcurrant leaves, or 5 g black tea leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut 2 mm off the blossom end of every cucumber, the end opposite the stem: it carries the pectinase that turns pickles to mush.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve the salt in the water, then pack the cucumbers upright in a crock with the dill, garlic, spices and tannin leaves.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the brine over, weight everything under the surface, and leave 3 cm of headspace beneath a loose lid or airlock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold at 18 to 21C (64 to 70F). The brine clouds and fizzes by day 3; skim any white film as it appears.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"They are ready when a cut pickle is translucent to the centre with no pale opaque core and the skin has gone from bright green to olive, 7 to 14 days. Jar and refrigerate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brine strength is measured on the water, not on the jar: 70 g of salt in 2 litres is 3.5 percent, and once the cucumbers give up their own water the whole vessel settles near 2.5 percent, the window where lactobacilli work and pathogens do not. Mush has two causes, the blossom end and heat over 24C, so trim one and avoid the other. Cloudy brine is normal; a soft, hollow, foul pickle is not.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","fiery","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Full-Sour%20Dill%20Pickles%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Full-Sour Dill Pickles"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fermentation%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Fermentation Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fermentation-and-pickling","term":"Fermentation & Pickling"},{"slug":"tannin","term":"Tannin"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Cucumber","Grapes","Dill","Pickles","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"chicken-liver-parfait","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g chicken livers, trimmed of green spots and sinew"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g unsalted butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g shallots, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml ruby port"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml Madeira"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml brandy"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 whole eggs, about 165 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g clarified butter, for sealing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the shallots, garlic, thyme, port, Madeira and brandy until reduced to about 3 tablespoons of syrup, then strain and set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter and hold it at 55C (131F). Warm the livers, eggs, reduction, salt and pepper in a blender jug standing in hot water until the mix reads 50C (122F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend smooth, then pour in the warm butter in a slow steady stream until the mixture is glossy and thick, like pouring custard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pass through a fine chinois, pressing hard on the solids, and pour into a lined 1 litre terrine. Tap the mould to bring up the bubbles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in a bain-marie at 100C (212F) until the centre reads 63C (145F), about 45 minutes, then chill overnight and seal under a 5 mm layer of clarified butter.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true}],"note":"A parfait is butter emulsified into liver puree and held together by egg protein, so temperature is the whole recipe. Blend the livers at 50C (122F) and stream in butter at 55C (131F): below 40C the fat seizes into grains, above 70C the emulsion splits and weeps yellow. Bake only until the core reads 63C (145F), which is also the point at which the livers are safe to eat.","equipment":["Blender","Bain-marie","Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","The water bath","Straining & passing through a sieve","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","silky","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Liver%20Parfait%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Liver Parfait"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"port-madeira-and-the-fortified-sweets","term":"Port, Madeira & the Fortified Sweets"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Shallot","Thyme","Butter","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"grilled-veal-sweetbreads","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g veal sweetbreads, heart lobes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre cold water with 20 g salt, for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, 1 onion and 1 celery stick, roughly cut"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf and 6 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g capers, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fine salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the sweetbreads in the salted cold water in the fridge, changing it four times over 4 hours, until they are pale and no longer leaking pink.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cover with fresh water, the wine, vegetables, bay and peppercorns, bring slowly up to 85C (185F), hold 4 minutes, then lift straight into ice water.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"While they are still warm, peel away the outer membrane, fat and ducts with your fingers, keeping each lobe whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press between two trays under a 1 kg weight in the fridge for 2 hours, until every lobe is an even 2 cm thick.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season, then sear in the butter and oil over high heat until deep brown and springy to the finger, about 3 minutes a side, and finish with capers, lemon and parsley.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sweetbreads are glands wrapped in a membrane that shrinks hard in heat and buckles the lobe, so it comes off while the gland is warm and slack. The soak is not superstition: four changes of cold water pull the blood that would otherwise cook to grey. The 2 hour press matters just as much. Unpressed, the thick end is still raw when the thin end has gone to leather.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny","gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Grilled%20Veal%20Sweetbreads%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Grilled Veal Sweetbreads"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Capers","Lemon","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter","Wine"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"steak-and-kidney-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g beef chuck, cut in 3 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g ox kidney, cored and cut in 2 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour, seasoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml stout"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g beef dripping"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g self-raising flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g shredded beef suet"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"about 180 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Butter, for the basin"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the kidney in cold salted water for 30 minutes, then cut out every trace of the white core and its tubes and pat the pieces dry.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss beef and kidney in the seasoned flour, brown hard in the dripping in batches, then stir in the onion, stout and stock and cool the filling completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the suet into the self-raising flour and salt, add the water and bring it together into a soft dough with as little working as you can manage.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a buttered 1.2 litre basin with two-thirds of the dough, pack in the cold filling, cap with the rest and seal the edges with wet fingers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with pleated paper and foil, tie down, and steam over gently boiling water for 4 hours, topping up with boiling water so the pan never stops steaming.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 10 minutes and turn out: the crust should be pale gold and the gravy should run dark and clinging when you cut in.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Suet melts near 45C (113F), later than butter, so the crust sets around the fat before it runs and the trapped steam lifts it: that is why the pastry is light with no lamination. Two rules hold the dish together. Core the kidney ruthlessly, cutting away every white tube, or the pudding tastes of ammonia; and never let the pan go off the boil, because a drop in steam leaves the crust sodden.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pleating dumplings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20pleat%20dumplings%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Steak%20and%20Kidney%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Steak and Kidney Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Liver & Offal","Onion","Butter","Flour","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"trippa-alla-romana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg honeycomb tripe, cleaned and blanched by the butcher"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g carrot, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g celery, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g peeled plum tomatoes, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g mint leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g pecorino romano, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fine salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the tripe with cold water, add the lemon halves, boil hard for 10 minutes, then drain, rinse and cut it into strips 1 cm wide.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot, celery and garlic in the oil over low heat until soft and sweet with no colour, about 12 minutes.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour in the wine and reduce until the pan smells of wine rather than raw alcohol, then add the tomatoes and the tripe with water to just cover.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer covered at a bare tremble for 2.5 to 3 hours, until a strip gives with no spring when you press it and the sauce coats the tripe.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, stir through the torn mint and half the pecorino, correct the salt, and send the rest of the cheese to the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tripe is stomach muscle sheathed in collagen and it has one setting: long. At 2 hours it is rubber, at 3 it is silk, and there is no penalty for going further, which makes it far more forgiving than liver or kidney. The hard first boil with lemon carries off the barnyard volatiles. Cheese and mint go in off the heat: boiled pecorino turns stringy and boiled mint tastes of nothing.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trippa%20alla%20Romana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Trippa alla Romana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Lemon","Stone fruit","Mint","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Wine","Honey","Clove"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"boudin-noir-aux-pommes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre fresh pig's blood, strained"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pork back fat, diced 5 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g quatre epices"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 m hog casings, 32-34 mm, soaked and rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 firm apples, in 1 cm wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g unsalted butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render 100 g of the back fat gently, add the onions and cook over low heat until completely soft and translucent with no colour, about 25 minutes, then cool below 30C (86F).","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the blood with the cream, salt, quatre epices and pepper, then pass it through a fine sieve to catch any strands of clot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the cooled onions and the remaining diced fat, then fill the casings loosely to two-thirds and twist into 15 cm links.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach in water held steady at 80C (176F) for 25 to 30 minutes, until a needle pushed into a link comes out clean with no bead of blood, then chill at once in ice water.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"To serve, fry the apples in the butter until caramelised, lay in 2 cm slices of boudin and warm them through gently on both sides until glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Blood is about 15 percent protein, mostly albumin, and it sets between 70C and 80C (158F to 176F). Poach at a bare 80C (176F) and never let it boil: agitation splits the mix into grains of fat and coagulated blood, and the expanding filling bursts the casing, which is also why you fill only two-thirds. Buy blood the day it is drawn, hold it below 4C (39F) and cook it within 24 hours.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boudin%20Noir%20aux%20Pommes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boudin Noir aux Pommes"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"boudin-noir-and-boudin-blanc","term":"Boudin Noir & Boudin Blanc"},{"slug":"quatre-epices-and-the-european-baking-blends","term":"Quatre Épices & the European Baking Blends"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Apple","Butter","Cream"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"crispy-pig-ear-strips","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 pig ears, about 600 g, scrubbed and singed of bristle"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves and 10 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g smoked paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt and lemon, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the ears with cold water with the onion, bay, peppercorns, vinegar and salt, and simmer very gently for 2.5 to 3 hours, until a skewer meets no resistance at the thick base.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift them out, lay them flat between two paper-lined trays, weight with 2 kg and refrigerate overnight until firm and cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into strips 5 mm wide, pat them bone dry, and toss in the cornflour mixed with the paprika.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 180C (356F) in small batches for 2 to 3 minutes, until the spitting quietens and the strips are blistered and rigid.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt while hot, and squeeze lemon over at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"An ear is skin over cartilage: collagen that stays rubbery until two hours or more of moist heat turns it to gelatin. Simmer until a skewer meets nothing, then press and chill overnight, because gelatin holds water and warm strips will spit hard enough to burn you. Fry in a pan no more than a third full, stand back until the bubbling calms, and salt only after they leave the oil.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","smoky","fiery","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, wood-smoke running underneath, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Crispy%20Pig%20Ear%20Strips%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Crispy Pig Ear Strips"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Lemon","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Wine","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"roast-bone-marrow-with-parsley-salad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 beef marrow bones, centre cut, 7 cm long, split lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres cold water with 40 g salt, for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g flat parsley leaves, picked"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g shallot, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g capers, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick slices sourdough"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flaky salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the bones in the salted iced water in the fridge for 12 to 24 hours, changing the water twice, until the marrow is pale ivory rather than red.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain and pat dry, sit them cut side up on a tray and roast at 220C (425F) for 15 to 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull them the moment the marrow is glossy, loose and translucent but still holding its shape, when a skewer slides through with no cold core.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress the parsley, shallot and capers with the lemon, oil and a pinch of salt only as the bones come out, so the leaves stay crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the bread, scrape the marrow onto it while it is hot, and eat with the salad and flaky salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Marrow is close to 90 percent fat and starts flowing near 40C (104F), so the oven is a race rather than a rest. Fifteen to twenty minutes at 220C (425F) leaves it translucent and wobbling; five minutes more and it has run out of the bone into the tray, leaving an empty tube. The overnight salted soak draws out blood that would otherwise roast grey and taste of iron.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Soaking dried goods back to life","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Roast%20Bone%20Marrow%20with%20Parsley%20Salad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Roast Bone Marrow with Parsley Salad"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Shallot","Capers","Lemon","Parsley","Bread"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"grilled-lamb-heart-skewers","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g lamb hearts, about 4"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g sweet smoked paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split each heart, rinse out the chambers, then cut away the valves, the tough vessel tops, the fat cap and the silverskin inside and out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the walls into 3 cm cubes across the grain and marinate in the oil, vinegar, garlic, cumin, paprika, oregano and pepper for 2 hours in the fridge.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thread onto skewers with the pieces just touching, and salt them as they go over the fire.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot coals about 2 minutes a side, until crusted outside and 54C (129F) at the centre, which cuts deep rose all the way through.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 3 minutes off the heat and squeeze lemon over.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"Heart is lean working muscle: no marbling, heavy myoglobin, and sheets of connective tissue you have to strip or the cubes bow off the skewer as they tighten. Treat it like steak, not like offal. Hot coals, two minutes a side, pull at 54C (129F). Past 60C (140F) the fibres wring out the little water they hold and the meat turns dry and unmistakably livery.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","charred","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, char used as punctuation, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Grilled%20Lamb%20Heart%20Skewers%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Grilled Lamb Heart Skewers"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Garlic","Lemon","Oregano","Vinegar","Wine","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"lamb-brains-with-black-butter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 sets lamb brains, about 500 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre cold water with 10 g salt, for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml white wine vinegar, plus 30 ml to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf, 1 sliced carrot and 6 peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g plain flour, seasoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g capers, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fine salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the brains in the salted cold water in the fridge for 2 hours, changing it three times, until they are white and the water stays clear.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Peel off the fine outer membrane and the threads of vessel under a trickle of cold water, working gently so the lobes stay whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach in water with the 50 ml vinegar, bay, carrot and peppercorns held at 80C (176F) for 15 minutes without ever letting it bubble, then cool them in the liquid and chill.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Halve the lobes, pat dry, dust in the seasoned flour and fry in 30 g of the butter until crisp and gold on both sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe the pan, cook the rest of the butter to a dark hazelnut that smells nutty and stops foaming, add the capers, then take it off the heat, add the 30 ml vinegar and the parsley, and pour it over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brain is a fat and water emulsion held in fragile membrane, which is why a boiling poach turns it to porridge in minutes. Soak until the water runs clear, peel the membrane, then poach at 80C (176F) with vinegar: the acid firms the surface proteins just enough to slice and flour. Use lamb or pork. Bovine brain is restricted as specified risk material across much of Europe and beyond.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lamb%20Brains%20with%20Black%20Butter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lamb Brains with Black Butter"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Carrot","Capers","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter","Flour","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"calfs-liver-onions-and-sage","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g calf's liver, in slices 1 cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 sage leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour, seasoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fine salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the fine outer membrane from each slice and cut out any tubes, then keep the liver cold and dry until the pan is ready.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onions in half the butter and the oil over low heat for 30 minutes, stirring, until they collapse to soft gold with no browning at the edges.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the wine and vinegar to the onions and reduce until the pan is almost dry, then hold them warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Get a heavy pan smoking hot, flour the liver only as it goes in, and sear 60 to 90 seconds a side, until beads of pink juice rise on the upper face.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the liver 2 minutes, crisp the sage in the last of the butter, and serve the slices on the onions with the sage and pan butter spooned over.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true}],"note":"Liver has almost no connective tissue, so it moves from set to grainy inside a minute. Sear 1 cm slices 60 to 90 seconds a side and pull at rosy pink, near 60C (140F): past that the iron-rich myoglobin coagulates hard and the taste turns metallic. Peel the outer membrane or the slice curls off the pan, and flour it as it goes in, never before, or the coating goes to glue.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Calf's%20Liver%2C%20Onions%20and%20Sage%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Calf's Liver, Onions and Sage"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Offal%20%26%20the%20Whole%20Animal%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Offal & the Whole Animal kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Onion","Sage","Butter","Flour","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"quick-pickled-red-onions","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g red onion, 2 medium"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g white wine vinegar, 5 percent acidity"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g dried oregano"}],"steps":[{"text":"Halve the onions pole to pole and slice 2 mm across the grain; a thicker slice never softens through and a thinner one goes to thread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the slices into a clean jar with the peppercorns, bay and oregano, pressing down until they sit below the shoulder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring vinegar, water, sugar and salt to a boil, stirring only until the crystals vanish, then pour it over the onions to cover.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the onions under with a spoon and leave the jar open until it is cool to the back of the hand, about 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lid and refrigerate. Eat from 30 minutes, best on day two, gone by two weeks when they turn soft and grey at the edges.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Red onion goes fluorescent pink because its anthocyanins shift colour below roughly pH 3.5, so the colour is your reading of the acid, not decoration. The brine runs 4 percent salt and goes on hot, which collapses cell turgor fast enough to take brine in half an hour. The failure is lidding the jar while it steams: trapped heat cooks the rings to slime. Cool it open, then seal.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Quick%20Pickled%20Red%20Onions%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Quick Pickled Red Onions"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"acidity-the-backbone","term":"Acidity (The Backbone)"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Oregano","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"chicago-hot-giardiniera","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g celery, 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g carrot, 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cauliflower florets, 1 cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g red pepper, 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g serrano or green chilli, sliced 5 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g green olives, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g garlic, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g chilli flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g celery seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g cracked black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the salt into the water until it dissolves, pour it over the diced vegetables and chilli, and weight them under. Leave 12 hours at room temperature.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and rinse well, then taste one: it should read seasoned, not salty. Spread on a cloth and pat dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the vegetables with the vinegar and leave 30 minutes. Keep the vinegar: it goes into the jar with them.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold through the olives, oregano, garlic, chilli flakes, celery seed and pepper, pack into jars with the vinegar, and pour over both oils to cover by 1 cm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Refrigerate 48 hours before serving, and stand the jar out 20 minutes first so the olive oil clears from cloudy to loose. Refrigerate, and use within 4 days.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":73200,"estimated":false}],"note":"Two stages, and skipping the first is what ruins it. Twelve hours in 6 percent brine draws free water out osmotically; pack the vegetables wet instead and that water bleeds into the oil, thins the acid and sours the jar inside a week. The vinegar stays in the jar because it is the only acid the pack has, and raw garlic under oil with no acid is the textbook botulism vector; the fridge slows the organism but does not stop it, which is why the keep-by is four days and not a month.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicago%20Hot%20Giardiniera%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicago Hot Giardiniera"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Garlic","Carrot","Celery","Bell pepper","Chiles","Cauliflower","Olives","Oregano","Vinegar","Wine","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"half-sour-dills-in-salt-brine","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg small ridged pickling cucumbers"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre unchlorinated water"},{"kind":"item","text":"35 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dill, stalks and seed heads"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, cloves bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g yellow mustard seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 grape, oak or blackcurrant leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Trim 3 mm from the blossom end of every cucumber, the end without the stem scar. Scrub off any grit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the salt into the water until clear. This is a 3.5 percent brine and the ratio is the whole recipe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack cucumbers upright with the dill, garlic, spices and the tannin leaves, then pour on the brine and weight everything below the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover loosely and hold at 18-22C (64-72F). Bubbles start on day two and the brine clouds; this is correct.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste from day three. Pull them at half-sour, still bright green at the core, usually day four to six, and refrigerate to stall the ferment.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No vinegar goes in. At 3.5 percent salt, Lactobacillus outcompetes spoilage bacteria and makes the acid itself, reaching about pH 3.4 in a week at 20C (68F). Soft or hollow pickles come from pectinase in the blossom end, so trim it and add a tannin leaf to inhibit what is left. White film is kahm yeast, harmless, skim it; fuzzy or coloured mould means start over. Keep everything submerged.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Half-Sour%20Dills%20in%20Salt%20Brine%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Half-Sour Dills in Salt Brine"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tannin","term":"Tannin"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Cucumber","Grapes","Dill","Mustard","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"torshi-makhlut","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g cauliflower, 5 mm chop"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g carrot, 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g celery, 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g green beans, 5 mm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g mint leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g tarragon, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g nigella seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground golpar, Persian hogweed"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g white wine vinegar, 5 percent acidity"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chop every vegetable to a rough 5 mm so a spoonful carries all of them at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the chopped vegetables and herbs on clean cloths and leave an hour, turning once, until no surface water shows.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss with the salt, nigella and golpar, then pack into sterilised jars, pressing to knock out air pockets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in vinegar to cover by 2 cm, seal, and store in the dark at cool room temperature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wait 4 weeks minimum. It is ready when the raw vinegar sting has rounded and the cauliflower has gone translucent at the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Torshi is preserved by acetic acid, not fermentation, so nothing sours in the jar and the vinegar must arrive strong: 5 percent acidity, undiluted. Water clinging to washed vegetables is the entire failure mode, dragging the jar below the roughly 3.5 percent acidity that holds spoilage off, which shows as cloudy brine and slack vegetables. Dry the cut vegetables first, keep 2 cm of vinegar over the top, and give it a month.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Torshi%20Makhlut%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Torshi Makhlut"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"acidity-the-backbone","term":"Acidity (The Backbone)"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Carrot","Celery","Cauliflower","Green beans","Parsley","Mint","Tarragon","Vinegar","Wine","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"aam-ka-achar","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg unripe green mango, in 3 cm pieces with the shell of the stone"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g yellow mustard seed, coarsely cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fennel seed, coarsely cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fenugreek seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g Kashmiri chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g nigella seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g asafoetida"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g mustard oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wipe the mango pieces dry, toss with the salt and turmeric, and leave in a covered bowl 24 hours; drain off the liquor that collects and dry the fruit again.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the mustard oil until it smokes steadily, about 250C (480F), then take it off and let it fall to hand-warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the fenugreek in a dry pan until it darkens a shade and smells of burnt sugar, then grind it; leave the mustard and fennel cracked but raw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold all the spices and the asafoetida through the mango, pack into a dry jar, and pour on the oil to stand 1 cm above the fruit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with cloth and leave in sun or a warm window 7-10 days, shaking daily, until the mango is amber and yields to a fingernail. Seal and keep a year.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mustard oil goes in cooked, never raw: take it to smoking point, about 250C (480F), and cool it, which drives off the harsh allyl isothiocyanate that otherwise makes the pickle taste like a poultice. Preservation is the 10 percent salt cure plus a sealed cap of oil, not the spices. Mould means the oil line was broken by a wet spoon or a piece of fruit floating proud. Press it back under.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","smoky","fiery","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, wood-smoke running underneath, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aam%20ka%20Achar%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Aam ka Achar"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"asafoetida-hing","term":"Asafoetida (Hing)"},{"slug":"stone-fruit-in-high-summer","term":"Stone Fruit in High Summer"}],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Mango","Mustard","Turmeric","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"chimichurri-verde","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 g flat-leaf parsley leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, about 6 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g coarse chilli flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g flaky salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"160 g olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g cracked black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the salt in the warm water, then stir in the vinegar, oregano and chilli flakes and leave 10 minutes for the dried herb to swell.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the parsley by hand to a coarse mince, stopping while the pieces still read as leaf, and mince the garlic fine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir parsley, garlic and pepper into the vinegar mixture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the oil over and stir once. Do not whisk; it should sit in two layers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 2 hours at room temperature before serving, and stir up from the bottom each time you spoon it out.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a broken vinaigrette on purpose: there is no emulsion, the oil sits on top, and you stir before every spoonful. Dried oregano is correct here, and it needs its ten minutes in the vinegar or it stays like hay in the teeth. The processor is the failure: it ruptures the parsley cells, the acid then strips magnesium from the chlorophyll, and within the hour you have khaki sludge. Chop by hand, use within two days.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chimichurri%20Verde%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chimichurri Verde"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Parsley","Oregano","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"zhoug-akhdar","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g green chillies, serrano or jalapeno, stemmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g coriander, leaves and tender stem"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g green cardamom seed, from about 10 pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the cumin, coriander seed, cardamom and peppercorns in a dry pan over medium heat until the cumin darkens and the cardamom smells resinous, about 90 seconds, then grind.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split the chillies and scrape out the pith from half of them if you want it servable to everyone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound or pulse chillies, garlic and salt to a rough paste, then add the herbs and work them in just until chopped, never smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the ground spice and the oil, then stir in the lemon juice last.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest an hour, then keep in a jar under a film of oil. It is bright for two days and tired by ten.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cardamom is what separates zhoug from any other green chilli sauce, so grind it fresh from the pods. Work it coarse: taken to a smooth puree the coriander goes bitter and the colour dulls, because acid converts chlorophyll into olive-brown pheophytin. That is also why the lemon goes in last and why this is a two-day sauce, not a keeper. Heat sits in the pith, so seed half the chillies to dial it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright","earthy","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zhoug%20Akhdar%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zhoug Akhdar"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"coriander-seed","term":"Coriander Seed"},{"slug":"cardamom-green-and-black","term":"Cardamom: Green & Black"}],"pantryItems":["Ham/Prosciutto","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Parsley","Cumin","Coriander seed","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"harissa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"80 g dried mild red chillies, guajillo or New Mexico, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dried arbol chillies, stemmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g caraway seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the chillies in a dry pan 30-45 seconds a side, pressing them flat, until they smell sweet and go flexible. Stop the instant any smoke smells acrid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with hot water and soak 30 minutes until they are fully soft, then lift them out and squeeze; keep 50 g of the soaking water.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the caraway, coriander and cumin until fragrant and grind them with the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic to a paste, then work in the chillies and the soaking water, either in a mortar or a blender, to a thick puree.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the spice and then the oil, a third at a time, then the vinegar. Jar it under a 5 mm slick of oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Caraway is the signature; without it this is only chilli paste. Soak the chillies rather than boiling them, which leaches colour and pulls bitterness out of the skins, and toast only to flexible, never to smoke. Capsaicin is fat-soluble, so the heat does not read properly until the oil is worked in: judge the seasoning after that, not before. The vinegar takes it under pH 4, which buys three weeks refrigerated.","equipment":["Blender","Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Soaking dried goods back to life","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","smoky","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, wood-smoke running underneath, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Harissa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Harissa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Vinegar","Wine","Curry paste","Cumin","Coriander seed","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"xo-sauce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 g dried scallop, conpoy"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g dried shrimp"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g cured ham, Jinhua or prosciutto, in 3 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g shallot, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g garlic, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g dried red chillies, soaked and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine sea salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the conpoy with warm water and the Shaoxing and soak 2 hours until it pulls apart into threads; soak the dried shrimp 30 minutes, then drain both and chop the shrimp coarse.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the oil to 130C (265F) and fry the shallot until pale gold, about 8 minutes, then add the garlic and ginger and give them 2 minutes more.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the ham and fry until the fat renders clear, then the shrimp, scallop threads and chilli.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold everything at 120-140C (250-285F), stirring often, for 30-40 minutes. It is done when the furious bubbling has gone quiet and the solids are crisp in clear red oil.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2640,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in sugar and salt off the heat, cool completely, and jar with the solids submerged. Refrigerate; it keeps a month.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a confit, not a stir-fry. While water remains, the pot is pinned at 100C (212F) and nothing browns, so hold the oil at 120-140C (250-285F) until the bubbling falls silent: that silence is the endpoint, not the clock. Any moisture left behind is what turns a jar sour inside a week. Stage the additions, because shallot wants eight minutes and sliced garlic burns in two.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Soaking dried goods back to life","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=XO%20Sauce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of XO Sauce"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"duck-prosciutto-magret-seche","term":"Duck Prosciutto (Magret Séché)"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Scallops","Ham/Prosciutto","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"salsa-macha","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"50 g dried morita chillies, stemmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dried arbol chillies, stemmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dried guajillo chillies, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g raw peanuts, skinned"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g garlic cloves, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g sesame seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g cider vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Tear the chillies open and shake out most of the seeds; leave a few for texture but not the whole load, which only adds bitterness.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 120C (250F) and fry the garlic and peanuts until pale gold, 3-4 minutes, then lift them out with a slotted spoon.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat and let the oil drop to about 100C (210F), then add all the chillies and swirl 30-45 seconds until they smell toasty and darken a shade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the sesame to the residual heat for 20 seconds, then pour everything into a blender with the garlic, peanuts and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pulse to a coarse rubble that still pours, stir in the vinegar, and rest 24 hours before use.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dried chillies scorch in seconds and scorched chilli is permanently acrid, so the oil comes off the heat and down to about 100C (210F) before they go anywhere near it, and they stay under a minute. The oil is the point: capsaicin and the red carotenoids are both fat-soluble, so frying moves flavour and colour into the oil instead of leaving them locked in the solids. Blend coarse, and give it a day.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","bright","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salsa%20Macha%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salsa Macha"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickle%20%26%20Condiment%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pickle & Condiment Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Peanut","Sesame","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"fond-blanc-de-volaille","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg chicken wings and backs, chopped through the joint"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, peeled and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g carrot, in 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g leek white, split and washed"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g celery"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 parsley stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 white peppercorns, cracked"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the wings and backs under cold water until it runs clear, then set them in a tall narrow pot and cover with the 4 litres of cold water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring up slowly over about 40 minutes to 90C (195F), lifting off the grey foam as it collects. Never let it break into a boil.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold at 85-90C for 3 hours, the surface trembling with one bubble every few seconds, and add the vegetables and aromatics for the final hour only.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Ladle through a muslin lined chinois without pressing the bones, then cool fast over ice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill overnight and lift off the set fat cap. The stock beneath should wobble like a soft jelly; if it does not, you had too little collagen or too much water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold start is not tradition, it is chemistry: soluble albumin dissolves in cool water and coagulates only as the pot climbs, rising as scum you can lift off. Drop bones into hot water and that protein sets inside the stock and clouds it for good. Hold at 85-90C, where collagen unwinds into gelatine but the surface never churns. A boil shears rendered fat into droplets the stock cannot let go of, and you get greasy grey water instead of jelly.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fond%20Blanc%20de%20Volaille%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fond Blanc de Volaille"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Leek","Carrot","Celery","Parsley","Bay leaf"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"fumet-de-poisson","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg flat fish bones and heads (sole, turbot, whiting), gills removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g leek white, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g button mushroom trimmings, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 parsley stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 white peppercorns"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the bones 20 minutes in cold water, pulling out the gills and the dark blood line along the spine, then drain well.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sweat the onion, leek and mushroom in the butter under a lid over low heat for 8 minutes, until glassy and smelling sweet with no colour anywhere.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the bones and stir 5 minutes, until the scraps of flesh have turned opaque white, then pour in the wine and reduce by half.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cover with the cold water, bring to 85C (185F), skim once, and hold there exactly 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain through muslin without pressing and cool immediately. A pressed fumet turns cloudy and stays cloudy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fish collagen is fragile and gives up its gelatine inside twenty minutes. Past that the fine bones start yielding bitterness, the delicate aromatics leave with the steam, and no reduction later will put them back. Gills and blood are the other fault: both go bitter and cloud the pot, so pull them before the bones ever see the pan. Sweat, never colour, and keep the liquid at 85C (185F) where it barely trembles.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fumet%20de%20Poisson%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fumet de Poisson"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Leek","Mushrooms","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter","Wine"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"prawn-head-and-shell-stock","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g prawn heads and shells, or langoustine and crab shells"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fennel, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g carrot, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml brandy"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.8 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tarragon stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the shells on paper. Heat the oil in a wide pan until it shimmers, add the shells in one layer and press them flat, cooking 6-8 minutes until deep coral and smelling toasted rather than scorched.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion, fennel and carrot and sweat 6 minutes, then stir in the tomato paste and cook it until it darkens to brick and smells sweet instead of tinny.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Deglaze with the brandy, then the wine, scraping the fond off the base, and reduce until syrupy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with the cold water, add the bay, tarragon and salt, bring to 85C (185F), skim, and hold 45 minutes.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2700,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Crush the shells lightly with the end of a rolling pin, strain through a coarse sieve, then pass again through muslin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Shells turn red because heat denatures the crustacyanin protein and frees astaxanthin, which is fat soluble. Fry the shells in oil and the colour and most of the aroma go into the fat, where the stock can carry them; boil them raw in water and you get a pale, thin, faintly marine liquid. Cook the tomato paste to brick red first to lose its tinny edge, and stop at 45 minutes, after which the heads push a bitter metallic note into the pot.","equipment":["Rolling pin","Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Prawn%20Head%20and%20Shell%20Stock%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Prawn Head and Shell Stock"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Crab","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Fennel","Bay leaf","Tarragon","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"fond-de-legumes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g leek, white and pale green"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g fennel"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g celery"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g carrot"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g button mushrooms, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ripe tomato, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 parsley stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 white peppercorns"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut every vegetable to 5 mm dice or thin slices. Small pieces give up their flavour inside the short window this stock allows.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter in a wide pan, add the vegetables and the salt, set the lid ajar and sweat over medium-low heat for 18 minutes, until the onion is glassy and the pan smells sweet, with no browning at all.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1080,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomato and the cold water, bring to 85C (185F), and hold 30 minutes without ever letting it boil.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pull the pot off the heat, drop in the parsley, bay and peppercorns and steep covered for 15 minutes, as you would tea.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain through a fine sieve without pressing, and cool fast. Season it only when you know what it is for.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Vegetables carry no collagen, so time cannot give this body: it can only take flavour away. Past about forty minutes the sugars are spent, the volatile aromatics have gone up with the steam and the onion and fennel start pushing a dull sulphur drone. That is exactly how vegetable stock becomes brown water. Build the flavour instead in a slow salted sweat, hold the pot under 90C, and steep the herbs off the heat so they stay green.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Straining & passing through a sieve","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","rich","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fond%20de%20L%C3%A9gumes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fond de Légumes"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Leek","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Mushrooms","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"tonkotsu-broth","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg pork trotters, split lengthwise by the butcher"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg pork femur or neck bones, cut so the marrow is exposed"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pork back fat or fatty belly trim"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 litres water, plus more boiling water for topping up"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g garlic, whole head split"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g spring onion tops"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the trotters and bones in cold water for 1 hour, changing it twice, until the water pulls off pink rather than red.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cover with cold water, boil hard for 15 minutes as brown scum foams up the sides, then drain and scrub every bone under running water until the surface is white and no clotted blood is left in the joints.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the bones and back fat to a clean pot, cover with the 5 litres, and hold at a full rolling boil for 8 to 12 hours, topping up with boiling water to keep everything submerged.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"It is done when it is opaque as single cream and leaves a film on the back of a spoon that does not run off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion, garlic, ginger and spring onion for the final hour only, then strain through a coarse chinois, pushing the softened marrow and cartilage through with a ladle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The one stock where boiling is the method rather than the fault. A rolling boil shears rendered pork fat into fine droplets that gelatine and pulverised collagen coat and hold in suspension, and that emulsion is the whiteness, the body and the way it coats the mouth. Hold it at a gentle simmer and you get thin brown broth. The same violence will suspend blood proteins, so the hard blanch and the scrub are not optional: skip them and it sets grey and livery, and no extra hours will fix it.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Ramen broth","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Ramen broth","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tonkotsu%20ramen%20broth%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tonkotsu%20Broth%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tonkotsu Broth"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"pho-bo-broth","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg beef leg and knuckle bones, cut across"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g beef brisket in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 litres cold water, plus more for blanching"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g onion, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g ginger, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g star anise, about 6 whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g cassia bark"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 black cardamom pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fennel seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g yellow rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the bones and brisket with cold water, boil hard 10 minutes, then throw the water away and scrub both the bones and the pot until nothing grey clings to either.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Char the unpeeled onion and ginger straight on a gas flame or under a hot grill until blistered black on every face and smelling sweet, then rub off the loose ash.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the star anise, cassia, black cardamom, coriander, cloves and fennel in a dry pan for 2 minutes, until fragrant and just beginning to darken, and tie them in muslin.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover the clean bones and brisket with the 6 litres of cold water, bring to 90C (195F) and hold there, skimming every 20 minutes for the first hour. Pull the brisket at 90 minutes, when a skewer slides out with no resistance, and chill it in cold water.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the bones 5 hours more with the charred aromatics, adding the spice bag only for the last 90 minutes, then season off the heat with the fish sauce, rock sugar and salt.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":23400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two opposite heats. The first is a hard ten minute blanch that boils the blood and marrow scum out of the bones, and that water is thrown away with the foam. After the scrub the pot never boils again, because agitation drives the same fine particles back into suspension and pho is judged on being seen through. The spices go in late for the same reason they are toasted: their aroma is volatile, and five hours of simmering leaves a broth tasting of dust and medicine.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","charred","umami-deep","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, char used as punctuation, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pho%20Bo%20Broth%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pho Bo Broth"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"star-anise","term":"Star Anise"},{"slug":"fennel-anise-and-the-licorice-line","term":"Fennel, Anise & the Licorice Line"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Ginger","Fish sauce","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Coriander seed","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"chicken-consomme-raft-method","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 litres cold chicken stock, fully degreased and set to a jelly"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g lean chicken leg meat, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"170 g egg white, about 5 whites"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g onion, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g carrot, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g leek, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g celery, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g ripe tomato, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 parsley stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 white peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the minced chicken, egg whites, chopped vegetables, tomato and aromatics into a loose slurry and chill it hard for 30 minutes. Everything that goes into the pot must be cold.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the slurry into the cold jellied stock in a tall heavy pot, then stir constantly over medium heat until it reaches 55-60C and turns thick and cloudy. Stop stirring at that moment and do not touch it again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the raft rise and knit for 10 minutes, cut a 5 cm chimney in it with the back of a ladle, and drop the heat until a single bubble breaks through the chimney every second or two, around 82C (180F).","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold 55 minutes untouched, until the liquid seen down the chimney runs transparent amber and the raft above has gone grey and firm.","durationSec":3300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Ladle out through the chimney into a double layer of muslin, never pouring, then blot the surface with paper to take the last beads of fat. Season at the end.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The raft is a filter that builds itself. Egg white and the myosin in lean mince coagulate as the pot climbs through 55-60C, knitting into a mesh that floats up and takes every clouding particle with it. It only forms from cold stock, stirred until it thickens and then left completely alone. Boil it and the raft tears and drops its cargo back through; press or pour it and you push grey silt into clear liquid. Ladle through the chimney, always.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Consomm%C3%A9%2C%20Raft%20Method%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Consommé, Raft Method"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"consomme-and-clarification","term":"Consommé & Clarification"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Leek","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Parsley","Bay leaf","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"shojin-dashi-kombu-and-shiitake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dried kombu, two pieces about 10 cm square"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dried shiitake, whole caps"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres cold soft water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dried soybeans, for depth (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wipe the kombu with a dry cloth and leave the white bloom on it. That is mannitol and it is flavour, not mould, so do not rinse it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the soybeans in a dry pan over medium heat for about 4 minutes, until they smell nutty and one or two split.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true},{"text":"Submerge the kombu, shiitake and soybeans in the cold water, cover, and refrigerate 8 to 12 hours, until the water is pale gold and smells of sea and forest floor.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Set over the lowest heat and bring to 60C (140F), then hold between 60C and 70C (140F-158F) for 45 minutes, never letting a bubble rise anywhere in the pot.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift out the kombu and strain through a fine sieve without squeezing the mushrooms. A squeezed shiitake gives bitterness and cloud. Keep the caps for another dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two umami compounds that multiply rather than add: glutamate from the kombu, 5'-guanylate from the shiitake. The guanylate is not in the dry mushroom. An enzyme builds it during a slow warm soak, and that enzyme is dead above roughly 70C, so a fast pot destroys the flavour before it exists. Kombu has its own ceiling: past 80C it releases alginates that go slimy and a bitterness no straining removes. Built cold, held warm, and ruined by hurry.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Dashi","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shojin%20Dashi%2C%20Kombu%20and%20Shiitake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shojin Dashi, Kombu and Shiitake"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stock%20%26%20Fond%20Atlas%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Stock & Fond Atlas kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mushrooms","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori","Dried mushrooms"],"judgedBy":["dashi","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"dill-and-aquavit-cured-salmon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg salmon side, skin on, pin bones pulled"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g fine sea salt (2.5% of the trimmed fish weight)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g caster sugar (2%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g white peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g coriander seed, toasted and cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dill, stalks and all, roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml aquavit or vodka"}],"steps":[{"text":"Weigh the trimmed fish and scale the salt and sugar to it, 2.5% and 2%, rather than reaching for a fixed spoonful.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the spirit over the flesh, press on the salt, sugar and cracked spice, then pack the dill over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal in a vacuum bag, or wrap tight in two layers of film in a tray, and hold at 4C (39F) for 48 hours, turning the parcel every 12 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Open it when the flesh has gone deep translucent orange throughout and a fingertip pressed into the thick end springs back instead of denting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape off the dill, pat dry, and slice paper thin at a shallow angle down toward the skin, leaving the grey line behind.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt does not soak in. It pulls water out by osmosis, that water makes a brine in the bag, and the brine carries salt back into the muscle until both sides even out. Weigh the fish and the cure cannot overshoot the percentage you chose. Bury the same side in loose salt instead and it keeps giving up water, so a thin tail turns chalky and ham-like while the loin is still raw. Turn the parcel so both faces sit in brine.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Curing gravlax","Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dill%20and%20Aquavit%20Cured%20Salmon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dill and Aquavit Cured Salmon"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Dill","Sugar","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"cold-smoked-salmon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 kg salmon side, skin on, pin bones pulled, held at -20C (-4F) for 7 days first"},{"kind":"item","text":"42 g coarse sea salt (3% of the fish weight)"},{"kind":"item","text":"28 g demerara sugar (2%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g black peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 large handfuls oak or alder smoking dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the salt, sugar and pepper, rub it over the flesh side, and cure skin down in a tray at 4C (39F): 24 hours for a thin side, 36 for a thick one.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse briefly, pat dry, then leave uncovered on a rack in the fridge for 12 hours until the surface turns tacky and faintly glossy and you can tap it without wetting a finger.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cold smoke below 30C (86F) for about 6 hours, in two sessions if the box warms up, until the flesh is the colour of old brass.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Wrap and rest in the fridge for 48 hours so the smoke moves inward and stops smelling like a bonfire.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice long and thin off the skin with the knife held almost flat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Smoke sticks to a pellicle, the dry tacky protein film that forms in cold moving air. Smoke a wet fillet and the phenols dissolve in surface water and run off, leaving patchy colour and a sooty edge. Hold the box under 30C (86F): warmer and the proteins set and you have hot smoked fish instead. Lox is the other branch of the family, a salt brined belly cut, never smoked at all.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking","Brining & curing","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","gently hot","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, a warm current of chile, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cold-Smoked%20Salmon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cold-Smoked Salmon"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"smoked-fish-hot-vs-cold","term":"Smoked Fish: Hot vs. Cold"}],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Smoked fish","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"duck-prosciutto","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 duck breast, 350-400 g, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g black peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf, crumbled"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bury the breast in the salt in a non-reactive dish, covered above and below, and refrigerate 24 hours for a 350 g breast, 30 for a heavy magret.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rinse every grain of salt off, pat bone dry, and weigh it now: this is the number every later weighing is measured against.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub with the pepper, thyme and bay, wrap in a double layer of muslin, tie, and hang somewhere 12-15C (54-59F) with air moving over it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Weigh it every few days. It is finished at 30% loss from that post-cure weight, usually 10 to 21 days, when the thick end feels like the heel of your hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice across the grain, thin enough to see light through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a box cure: buried in loose salt the meat never reaches equilibrium, so time is the only control and thickness sets the clock. Run it an extra day and the outer centimetre goes hard and salty. Weight loss, not the calendar, says when the drying is done, because at 30% the water activity is low enough that nothing grows. That is also why a small muscle drying fast in moving air needs no nitrite. Go thicker or slower and it does.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Straining & passing through a sieve","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Duck%20Prosciutto%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Duck Prosciutto"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"duck-prosciutto-magret-seche","term":"Duck Prosciutto (Magret Séché)"}],"pantryItems":["Duck","Ham/Prosciutto","Thyme","Bay leaf"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"bresaola","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg beef eye of round, every scrap of silverskin and fat trimmed off"},{"kind":"item","text":"36 g fine sea salt (3%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g curing salt no. 2 (0.25%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g black pepper, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 beef bung, large collagen casing or drying net"}],"steps":[{"text":"Weigh the trimmed muscle and scale every cure ingredient to that weight, then rub the lot on and seal it in a vacuum bag with the wine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cure at 4C (39F) for 12 days, flipping the bag daily so the liquor washes both ends.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe the meat down, case it or truss it tight in netting, and hang at 12-14C (54-57F) and 70-75% humidity.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Weigh it weekly. It is ready at 38% weight loss, four to eight weeks, when the whole muscle feels evenly firm with no soft give at the core.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice across the grain as thin as the knife allows and let it warm on the plate before serving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nitrate earns its place here. A thick anaerobic muscle drying for weeks at cellar temperature is exactly where botulism grows, and cure no. 2 releases nitrite slowly across the whole run rather than all at once. The other failure is case hardening: dry air shrinks the outside into a shell that traps water in the middle, which then spoils. Hold humidity at 70-75%, and if a crust does form, vacuum bag the piece for a week to let the moisture even out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Trussing","Brining & curing","Keeping the cooking liquid","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Trussing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20truss%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bresaola%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bresaola"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"bresaola-della-valtellina","term":"Bresaola della Valtellina"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Berries","Thyme","Bay leaf","Bread","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"guanciale","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg pork jowl, skin on, glands and bruised meat cut away"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g fine sea salt (3%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g curing salt no. 2 (0.25%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g black pepper, coarsely cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Square off the jowl so it dries evenly, then weigh it and scale the cure to that weight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the cure into every face, seal in a bag or covered tray, and refrigerate 7 days at 4C (39F), turning daily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe off the wet cure, pierce one corner and thread string through, then hang at 12-15C (54-59F) at about 70% humidity.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Give it 3 to 5 weeks, until it has lost 30% of its weight and the lean seam at the thick end is firm under a thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Wipe any bloom off with a vinegar cloth, wrap in paper, and keep it in the fridge, cutting only what you need.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jowl is mostly fat, and fat holds almost no water, so the weight comes out of the lean seams and comes out slowly. That is why the cure runs a week and the hang runs a month: you are seasoning fat and drying a little meat, not dehydrating a muscle. Powdery white mould is Penicillium and belongs there. Green fuzz or black patches do not, so wipe with vinegar, raise the airflow, and check nothing is touching its neighbour.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a sweet edge rounding the corners, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Guanciale%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Guanciale"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"guanciale-jowl-and-lardo","term":"Guanciale, Jowl & Lardo"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Thyme","Bay leaf","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"salt-cod-home-cured","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg thick cod loin, skin on, pin boned"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg coarse sea salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay a 1 cm bed of salt in a perforated tray set over a dish, put the cod on it skin down, and bury it completely in more salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Refrigerate. Each day pour off the brine that has collected and replace the wet salt with dry, because the fish must never sit in its own liquor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"By day 7 the loin should be stiff, amber at the edge and about half its starting weight. Rack it another 5 days in a cold airy place if you want it board hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"To cook with it, rinse the surface salt away and soak in a large bowl of cold water in the fridge for 48 hours, changing the water four times.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Poach a sliver to test: it should taste seasoned, not salty. Thin tail pieces need only 24 hours, so soak those separately.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salting to saturation drags water activity under 0.85, below which spoilage bacteria simply stop, which is how a fish crossed oceans before refrigeration. Soaking runs that gradient backwards, and it stalls the moment the water around the fish gets salty, so volume and changes matter far more than hours. Soak in warm water, or too little of it, and the outside goes woolly while the middle stays a salt lick.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salt%20Cod%2C%20Home-Cured%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salt Cod, Home-Cured"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"building-the-home-and-starting-bar","term":"Building the Home & Starting Bar"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Salt cod"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"confit-duck-leg","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 duck legs, about 1.2 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"24 g coarse sea salt (2%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g black peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 thyme sprigs"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg duck fat"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the legs with the salt, pepper, thyme, bay and garlic, then cure uncovered on a rack in the fridge for 24 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe off the cure and aromatics and pat the legs dry, because salt crystals left clinging will make the fat too salty to reuse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the fat in a deep pan, settle the legs in fully submerged, and hold at 85C (185F), one lazy bubble every few seconds and no more, for 3 hours.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"They are done when a skewer meets no resistance at the thigh joint and the drumstick meat has pulled back from the bone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool in the fat, lift the legs into a jar, pour the strained fat over to cover by 1 cm, and refrigerate at least a week before eating.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Confit preserves twice: salt lowers water activity at the surface, and the set fat cap keeps oxygen out. What breaks it is the jelly. Meat juices sink below the fat in the pan, and if that layer goes into the jar you have sealed a growth medium under an airtight lid. Pour the fat off the jelly, or chill and lift the fat cake clean. Hold 85C (185F): above 95C (203F) the fibres squeeze shut and the meat goes stringy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Confit","Brining & curing","Straining & passing through a sieve","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Confit%20Duck%20Leg%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Confit Duck Leg"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"confit","term":"Confit"}],"pantryItems":["Duck","Garlic","Thyme","Bay leaf","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"pork-rillettes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg pork shoulder, boned and cut into 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pork back fat, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"18 g fine sea salt (1.8%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g white pepper, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g quatre epices"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 thyme sprigs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the pork and back fat with the salt and spices and leave in the fridge overnight, a short cure rather than a long one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the meat, aromatics, wine and water in a heavy casserole, cover, and cook at 130C (265F) for 4 hours.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"It is ready when a cube collapses under the back of a spoon with no push behind it and the fat has run clear above the liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the meat, pick out thyme stalks and bay, and shred with two forks or in a mixer on its lowest speed until it is strands, never paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in enough strained cooking fat and juice to make it spoonable, then chill a teaspoonful and taste that before you correct the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into jars, pressing out air pockets, seal with 5 mm of melted fat, and keep 3 days before eating.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things go wrong. A food processor shears the fibres into wallpaper paste, so pull the meat and keep the strands. And seasoning judged hot always lands flat, because fat coats the tongue and blunts salt more as it chills: season, chill a spoonful, taste the cold one. The fat seal is not decoration either. Any pocket of trapped air turns the meat beneath it grey within a week.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","bright","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pork%20Rillettes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pork Rillettes"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"quatre-epices-and-the-european-baking-blends","term":"Quatre Épices & the European Baking Blends"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Thyme","Bay leaf","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"biltong","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg beef silverside, cut with the grain into strips 25 mm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml brown malt or cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g coarse sea salt (2.5%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander seed, toasted and coarsely cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g black pepper, cracked"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dip each strip in the vinegar, let it drain, then toss it in the salt, coriander, sugar and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack in a non-reactive dish and refrigerate 6 hours, turning once, until the surface has gone dull and firm.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Wipe each strip with a vinegar-dampened cloth, hook it, and hang in a warm box at 25-30C (77-86F) with a fan pulling air past every face, nothing touching.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Weigh the batch daily. Medium biltong, still red in the middle, is 50% weight loss and takes 3 to 4 days. Dry sticks are 60%.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice across the grain and store in paper, not plastic, or it sweats and softens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Biltong is not jerky. Jerky is thin slices cooked dry in a warm oven; biltong is a thick strip cured raw and dried by moving air, so the vinegar does real work, dropping surface pH under 5 while the interior takes days to lose its water. Salt sits at only 2.5% because the drying, not the brine, preserves. Stalled air is the failure: a strip touching its neighbour sweats at the contact point and sours from the inside out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Biltong%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Biltong"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"boquerones-en-vinagre","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g fresh anchovies, held at -20C (-4F) for 48 hours first"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml mild olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pinch off each head and pull it downward to draw the guts with it, open the fish along the belly, and lift the backbone out from the head end, leaving the fillets joined at the tail.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the fillets in cold water, changing it until the water stays clear, because leftover blood turns the finished fish grey.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay them skin down in a shallow dish, pour over the vinegar, water and salt, and refrigerate 4 hours for small fish, 6 for fat ones.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cut one fillet across to check: the moment no translucent line is left at the centre, drain the lot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer in a container with the garlic and parsley, cover completely with the oil, and leave a day before eating. Use within five days.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is an acid cure, not a salt one. Acetic acid unfolds the muscle proteins much as heat does, which is why the flesh goes white and firm with no pan involved, and the change front travels in from both faces, so thickness sets the time and the clock only follows it. Leave them in past white and they go chalky and taste of nothing but vinegar. Oil is the stop: drained and covered, the acid has nothing left to work on.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Filleting fish","Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","aromatic","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boquerones%20en%20Vinagre%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boquerones en Vinagre"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Cure%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Cure kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Garlic","Parsley","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"milanese-saffron-risotto","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"320 g carnaroli rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L light beef or chicken stock, held at a bare simmer"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g beef marrow, scooped from the bone and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g onion, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.4 g saffron threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g cold unsalted butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the saffron in 60 ml of the hot stock and leave it while you start, until the liquid is deep orange and smells of hay rather than dust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the marrow with the onion in a wide pan over low heat until the onion is translucent and slippery with no colour at all, about 6 minutes.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the rice and toast it, stirring, until the grains rattle, feel hot to the back of the hand and turn glassy at the edges around an opaque white core. Pour in the wine and let it vanish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add simmering stock a ladle at a time, stirring often and keeping the pan at a lazy boil; stir in the saffron at minute 12 and stop when a grain bends without snapping but still shows a chalk line at its centre, 16 to 18 minutes.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat, count to sixty, then beat in the cold butter and the cheese hard until the rice is glossy and settles in a slow wave when you knock the pan. Season and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sauce is not cream: it is the rice's own surface starch, rubbed loose by the grains turning against the pan and suspended in hot stock. Add the stock in stages and keep it simmering, because cold stock drops the pan off the boil and the starch stops shedding. Mantecatura goes off the heat with cold butter; beaten into a boiling pan the butter splits and the risotto turns greasy instead of glossy.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Risotto method","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Risotto method","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=risotto%20technique%20mantecatura","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Milanese%20Saffron%20Risotto%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Milanese Saffron Risotto"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"saffron","term":"Saffron"},{"slug":"parmigiano-reggiano","term":"Parmigiano-Reggiano"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Onion","Butter","Parmesan","Rice","Wine","Stock/Broth","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"paella-valenciana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g bomba rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g chicken, jointed into small bone-in pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g rabbit, jointed into small bone-in pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g flat green beans, topped and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cooked garrofo or butter beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ripe tomato, grated to a pulp"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g sweet pimenton"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.3 g saffron threads, warmed and crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.8 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig rosemary"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a 42 cm pan over medium and brown the chicken and rabbit hard on every side until the meat is mahogany and no longer sticks, 12 to 15 minutes, then push it out to the rim.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the beans in the clear centre, add the grated tomato and cook it down until it darkens and the oil runs red at the edges, then stir in the pimenton and count to ten only, since it burns almost immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the water with the saffron, rosemary, garrofo and salt; boil 25 to 30 minutes until the level sits just below the pan rivets and the broth tastes a shade too salty.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rain the rice across the pan in a cross and shake it level, no deeper than 2 cm. Do not stir again. Boil hard for 8 minutes, then drop to a bare simmer for 8 to 10 more, until the surface liquid is gone and small craters open.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the heat for 60 to 90 seconds until you hear a fine even crackle and smell toasted grain; cut the heat the moment the smell sharpens, cover with a cloth and rest 5 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Socarrat is the last of the broth, its sugar and starch, caramelising against the steel in the final minute. It only forms if the rice sits still: stir after the grains go in and you strip starch into the liquid and get risotto in a paella pan. Keep the layer under 2 cm so the top cooks in steam while the base fries, and go by the nose. Toasted is done, acrid is a minute too late.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Paella & socarrat","Searing: the hard crust","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","nutty-toasted","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Paella & socarrat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=paella%20socarrat%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Risotto method","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=risotto%20technique%20mantecatura","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Paella%20Valenciana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Paella Valenciana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Tomato","Green beans","Rosemary","Butter","Rice","White beans","Saffron","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"hyderabadi-dum-biryani","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g goat shoulder on the bone, in 4 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g aged basmati rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g whole milk yogurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, sliced thin and fried brown in ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger and garlic paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g green chillies, slit"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g mint leaves, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coriander leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g red chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 g saffron steeped in 60 ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom, 1 black cardamom, 5 cm cassia bark, 4 cloves, 2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour worked to a stiff dough for sealing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the goat with the yogurt, ginger and garlic paste, chilli powder, garam masala, lime, chillies, mint, coriander, half the fried onion, 20 g of the ghee and 12 g of the salt. Leave it 4 hours at cool room temperature, or overnight in the fridge.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soak the rice 30 minutes and drain. Boil the water hard with the rest of the salt and the whole spices, drop in the rice and cook 3 to 4 minutes only, until a grain crushed between finger and thumb is soft outside with a firm white core. Drain at once.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2040,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the marinated meat in an even layer in a heavy pot, pile the drained rice over it without pressing, and scatter on the rest of the fried onion, the saffron milk and the remaining melted ghee.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough into a rope, press it round the rim and clamp the lid on. Cook 5 minutes on high to raise steam, then on the lowest flame, or on a tava, for 45 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest it sealed off the heat for 15 minutes, break the crust, and lift the biryani out in wedges with a flat spoon from the bottom of the pot rather than stirring.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dum finishes rice in trapped steam instead of water. The grain goes in at about 70 percent, soft outside and chalky in the middle, and the meat's rising juices carry it the rest of the way. Boil the rice one minute too long and the top layer turns to paste under the lid; leave the seal open and the steam escapes, giving chalky grains over wet meat. Use basmati aged a year: the drier grain elongates rather than splitting.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Biryani & dum","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Marinating: acid, salt and time","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Biryani & dum","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=biryani%20dum%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hyderabadi%20Dum%20Biryani%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hyderabadi Dum Biryani"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cardamom-green-and-black","term":"Cardamom: Green & Black"},{"slug":"garam-masala","term":"Garam Masala"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Lime","Cilantro","Mint","Bay leaf","Milk","Yogurt","Rice","Flour","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Saffron","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"party-jollof-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g long grain parboiled rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g ripe plum tomatoes, or 400 g tinned plum tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g red bell pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 or 2 scotch bonnet chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g dried thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml hot chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the tomatoes, bell pepper, scotch bonnet and half the onion smooth, then boil the puree hard in a wide pan for 15 to 20 minutes until it turns from scarlet to brick and stops smelling raw and green.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"In a heavy pot fry the remaining onion in the oil until soft, add the tomato paste and fry it 5 minutes until it darkens to rust and the oil floats free around it.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the reduced puree, curry powder, thyme, bay and salt and fry, stirring, for 10 to 15 minutes more, until the oil separates at the edges again and the base tastes sweet rather than sharp.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the rice and stir it through until every grain is coated red, then add the hot stock so the liquid sits exactly level with the rice. Cover with foil, then the lid, and cook on the lowest heat for 30 minutes without stirring or peeking.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the lid, raise the heat for 2 to 3 minutes until the base smells faintly of smoke, then kill the heat, fork the rice through from the sides and leave it covered 10 minutes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":780,"estimated":false}],"note":"Parboiled rice is steamed in the husk and dried before milling, so its starch is set before it ever meets the pot; it holds its shape through half an hour in acidic tomato where basmati would collapse. Acid also slows softening, which is why the pepper base has to be fried down until the oil breaks out before the rice goes in. Keep the stock level with the rice, never above, and do not stir it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Jollof & party smoke","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","umami-deep","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Jollof & party smoke","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=jollof%20rice%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Party%20Jollof%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Party Jollof Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Stone fruit","Thyme","Bay leaf","Rice","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"pork-and-century-egg-congee","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g jasmine or other long grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt, plus more to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g pork loin, rubbed with 6 g salt and chilled overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 century eggs, peeled and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g ginger, in fine matchsticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g spring onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fried shallots"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the rice until the water runs milky rather than white, drain it well, then toss it with the oil and salt and leave it 30 minutes; an hour in the freezer does the same job faster.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the water to a rolling boil in a tall pot, add the rice and ginger, return it to the boil, then hold a lively simmer with the lid ajar, scraping up from the base every few minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"After 50 to 70 minutes the grains will have burst and lost their edges, and the congee should fall off the spoon in a sheet rather than in drops. Slacken with boiling water if it gets ahead of you.","durationSec":4200,"handsOnSec":4440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the salted pork thin across the grain, stir it in with half the century egg and simmer 5 minutes, just until the pork turns opaque and firms.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with the white pepper and more salt, then ladle out and top with the remaining century egg, the spring onion and the fried shallots.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Congee is a starch suspension, not rice sitting in soup, and agitation is what builds it: stirring shears starch off the grains until the liquid thickens on its own. The oil and salt rub, or a spell in the freezer, damages the grain surface so it collapses sooner. Work at about 1:12 rice to water by weight and stop it looser than you want to eat it, because congee sets hard as it cools.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Congee","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Congee","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20congee%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pork%20and%20Century%20Egg%20Congee%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pork and Century Egg Congee"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Shallot","Rice"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"dolsot-bibimbap","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g cooked short grain rice, cooled to just warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g beef sirloin, sliced 3 mm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml toasted sesame oil, plus 40 ml for the bowls"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g spinach"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g mung bean sprouts"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g carrot, in fine matchsticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g courgette, in fine matchsticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g shiitake, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g gochujang, loosened with 20 ml water and 10 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g toasted sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook and dress each vegetable on its own: spinach blanched 30 seconds and wrung out dry, sprouts boiled 3 minutes, carrot, courgette and shiitake fried separately until just limp. Season each with a little garlic, sesame oil and salt and keep them apart.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Marinate the beef 20 minutes in the soy, sugar, garlic and sesame oil, then sear it in a dry hot pan in one layer until the edges catch and darken.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the stone bowls on a medium flame and heat them 5 minutes, until a flicked drop of water skitters and disappears on contact, then swirl 10 ml of sesame oil around the inside of each.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Press the warm rice firmly against the base and walls in an even layer and leave it completely alone for 3 to 4 minutes, listening for the sizzle to tighten into a fine crackle.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the vegetables and beef over the top in wedges, slide on a fried egg, and carry the bowl to the table still ticking. The diner mixes in the gochujang, which lifts the crust off the stone in shards.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The crust, nurungji, is dextrinised starch and Maillard browning against the stone, and it needs a dry surface to start. Use rice that has stood and given off its steam, not wet rice straight from the cooker, and once it is pressed in do not touch it. A bowl that is not properly hot glues the layer on instead of browning it and the crust tears. Oil the stone and load it at once, or the sesame oil smokes and the bowl tastes bitter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Blanching & shocking","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","gently hot","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dolsot%20Bibimbap%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dolsot Bibimbap"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Garlic","Carrot","Chiles","Mushrooms","Spinach","Zucchini","Bean sprouts","Rice","Soy sauce","Sesame","Sugar","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"chelow-ba-tahdig","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g aged basmati rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g whole milk yogurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.3 g saffron, ground with a pinch of sugar and steeped in 60 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ghee or butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml neutral oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the rice in several changes of cold water until the water runs clear, then soak it in salted water for 1 to 2 hours and drain.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil the 4 L of water with the salt, drop the rice in and cook it 4 to 6 minutes, until a grain is soft at the outside but still snaps at a firm needle in the centre. Drain and rinse briefly under tepid water.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the yogurt with the yolk, half the saffron water and two ladles of the parboiled rice. Pour the oil and half the melted ghee into the pot and spread this mixture over the base in an even layer about 1 cm deep.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mound the rest of the rice on top loosely in a pyramid, punch five holes down to the base with the handle of a spoon, and pour over the remaining ghee, the rest of the saffron water and 3 tablespoons of water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap the lid in a tea towel and clamp it on. Cook on medium 8 to 10 minutes until you hear a steady sizzle, then on the lowest heat for 45 to 50 minutes. Stand the pot on a wet cloth for 2 minutes to release the crust, then invert onto a plate.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":3120,"estimated":true}],"note":"Parboiling in heavily salted water and draining carries off the surface starch, which is why chelow steams into separate grains instead of clumping. The yogurt layer is the tahdig itself: its protein and milk sugar brown where plain rice would only harden, and it releases as one disc. Punch steam vents to the base or the mound cooks wet, and wrap the lid so condensation is absorbed rather than raining back down.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chelow%20ba%20Tahdig%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chelow ba Tahdig"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Yogurt","Rice","Sugar","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"arroz-con-pollo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg chicken thighs and drumsticks, bone in and skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g long grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml hot chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g green pepper, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g tomato, grated to a pulp"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground annatto, or 0.2 g saffron"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g green olives, with 30 ml of their brine"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the chicken and brown it skin down in the oil in a wide heavy pot until the skin lets go of the pan by itself and is deep brown, about 8 minutes, then 2 minutes on the other side. Lift it out.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onion, pepper and garlic in the same fat over medium until translucent and sweet smelling, then add the tomato, oregano, bay and annatto and cook until the mixture darkens and the oil runs orange around the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the rice and turn it in the fat for 2 minutes until every grain is coated and the edges go slightly translucent, then pour in the wine and let it boil away.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the hot stock, the olives and their brine and taste: it should be a shade saltier than you want. Settle the chicken back on top, bring it to a boil, cover, and cook on the lowest heat 18 to 20 minutes without lifting the lid.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the peas over, put the lid back and leave the pot off the heat for 10 minutes, then fork the rice up from the edges.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"This is the pilaf method: coating each grain in hot fat before any liquid arrives slows water into the starch and keeps the grains separate. Count every source of liquid, the wine, the tomato and the juice the chicken will give up, and err dry, because the covered ten minute rest moves moisture from the sodden base up to the drier top. Lift the lid mid cook and the pot falls out of steam, leaving the top layer chalky.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","tangy-ferment","aromatic","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, ferment tang with real depth behind it, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arroz%20con%20Pollo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arroz con Pollo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Peas","Olives","Oregano","Bay leaf","Rice","Wine","Stock/Broth","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"shari-seasoned-sushi-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g Japanese short grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 cm piece of kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"36 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash the rice in cold water, turning it with an open hand and changing the water four or five times, until it runs faintly cloudy rather than clear. Over-washing chips the grains. Drain in a sieve for 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook it with the measured water and the kombu laid on top; lift the kombu out as the water comes to the boil, cover, cook on the lowest heat 12 minutes, then leave the lid on off the heat for 10 minutes more.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":1560,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the vinegar, sugar and salt together just until the crystals dissolve, no further, or the acid boils off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip the hot rice into a wide wooden or plastic tub, pour the seasoning over the back of a paddle so it lands spread out, and cut through the rice at a slant, folding rather than stirring, while fanning it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stop when the rice is at body temperature, about 37C (99F), the grains gloss individually and a squeezed handful holds together. Cover with a damp cloth and use it within the hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Season the rice hot: the starch granules are still swollen and open, and they drink the vinegar in. Let it cool first and the seasoning sits on the outside and reads harsh. Cut with the edge of the paddle instead of stirring, since crushed grains give paste, and fan to drive off surface moisture so the rice glosses rather than sweats. Never refrigerate it. Near 4C (39F) the starch retrogrades and goes chalky for good.","equipment":["Stand mixer"],"techniques":["Sushi rice & rolling","Straining & passing through a sieve","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sushi rice & rolling","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20sushi%20rice%20rolling%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shari%2C%20Seasoned%20Sushi%20Rice%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shari, Seasoned Sushi Rice"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Rice","Vinegar","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"moong-dal-khichdi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g split skinned moong dal"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g cumin seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g asafoetida"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green chilli, slit"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry roast the moong dal in a pan over medium heat, shaking it, for 3 to 4 minutes until it smells nutty and a few grains freckle brown, then rinse it with the rice until the water runs clear.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat half the ghee in a heavy pot and crackle the cumin until the seeds darken a shade and stop spitting, then add the asafoetida, ginger and chilli and stir for 30 seconds only, since asafoetida scorches fast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the rice, dal, turmeric, salt and water, bring to a boil and simmer covered for 25 minutes, or pressure cook to 3 whistles, until the grains have lost their edges and the mass drops off a spoon in a soft ribbon.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the rest of the ghee off the heat and loosen with boiling water until it pours slowly, then serve straight away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Khichdi is the opposite of pilaf: at 1:4 grain to water the rice goes past absorption into full gelatinisation, and collapse is the point, so wash it but do not chase separate grains. Roasting the dal dry first sets its surface protein and stops it dissolving into slime. Add the second half of the ghee off the heat, where it stays aromatic instead of cooking flat, and serve it looser than looks right; it tightens within minutes of leaving the pot.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moong%20Dal%20Khichdi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moong Dal Khichdi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Rice%20Bowl%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Rice Bowl kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"asafoetida-hing","term":"Asafoetida (Hing)"}],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Chiles","Rice","Lentils","Cumin","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"brined-whole-roast-cauliflower","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole cauliflower, 900 g to 1.1 kg, leaves trimmed, stem left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g white miso"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g Aleppo pepper or mild chilli flakes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the water, salt, sugar, bay and coriander seed to the boil in a pot deep enough to submerge the head, then lower the cauliflower in stem down and weight it with a small plate so it stays under.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer gently 12 to 15 minutes, until a thin knife slides into the thickest part of the stem with only slight resistance and the florets have gone from chalk white to faintly translucent.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift it out onto a rack, stem up so the hollows drain, and leave 20 minutes until the surface is dry to the back of a finger; wet cauliflower will steam, not colour.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub all over with 40 ml of the olive oil, set on a tray and roast at 220C (425F) for 30 to 40 minutes, turning once, until the florets are mahogany with black tips.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the miso, lemon, remaining oil and chilli, brush over the head for the last 8 minutes only, and carve at the table in thick vertical slabs.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A whole head cannot be salted from the inside once it is in the oven, so the seasoning has to arrive by diffusion: a 5% brine, simmered, carries salt to the core and softens the pectin there. Dry heat alone blackens the outer florets while the stem stays squeaky and raw. Cook it through in the brine, then dry the surface properly. The miso goes on late because it is mostly sugar and salt and will burn.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Roasting in a hot oven","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","briny","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brined%20Whole%20Roast%20Cauliflower%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Brined Whole Roast Cauliflower"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"coriander-seed","term":"Coriander Seed"}],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Cauliflower","Lemon","Bay leaf","Miso","Sugar","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"celeriac-baked-in-salt-dough","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 celeriac, about 800 g, scrubbed hard, root hairs left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"260 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g hazelnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml cold pressed rapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml sherry vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the flour, salt and thyme with the water to a stiff, smooth dough, adding the last of the water only if it will not come together; rest it 15 minutes so it rolls without tearing.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 8 mm, wrap the celeriac completely with no thin patches, pinch the seams and patch any split with a scrap: every crack is a steam leak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (390F) for 2 to 2 hours 15 minutes, until a skewer pushed through the crust meets no resistance at the centre and comes out steaming.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":8100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the sealed parcel 20 minutes so the heat evens out, then crack the crust with the back of a knife and lift the celeriac clear, brushing off any salt that clings.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the hazelnuts until they smell of biscuit, crush them and whisk with oil, vinegar, mustard and parsley; peel the celeriac, cut into thick wedges and spoon the dressing over while hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The salt crust seasons almost nothing: it is a sealed ceramic shell that turns the celeriac into its own steamer, holding it just above 100C in its own moisture so the sugars concentrate instead of leaching into a pan of water. A cracked crust vents that steam and the outer centimetre dries to cork, so patch every split before it goes in and rest the parcel before you break it open.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","bright","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Celeriac%20Baked%20in%20Salt%20Dough%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Celeriac Baked in Salt Dough"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Celeriac","Parsley","Thyme","Flour","Vinegar","Wine","Mustard","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"mushroom-xo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"50 g dried shiitake"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml just-boiled water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g king oyster mushrooms, cut in 3 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g shallots, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g garlic, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g fresh ginger, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g dried red chillies, soaked and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g kombu, blitzed to powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fermented black beans, rinsed and roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g soft brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml rice vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the shiitake in the hot water 30 minutes, squeeze them dry over the bowl, chop to 3 mm dice and keep 100 ml of the strained soaking liquid.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the oil to about 140C (285F), fry the shallots until pale gold and lift them out, then the garlic and ginger for 60 seconds only, and set both aside; they will keep browning off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the two mushrooms in the same oil, 12 to 15 minutes, until the loud bubbling of escaping water quietens to a slow fizz and the pieces are shrunken and chewy at the edge.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the aromatics with the chillies, black beans and kombu powder, add the soy, sugar, vinegar and reserved soaking liquid and cook 10 minutes more, until the liquid has gone and the oil sits clear on top.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely and jar with the oil covering the solids by 5 mm; it keeps 3 weeks refrigerated and tastes better after two days.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"XO tastes bottomless because glutamate and nucleotides multiply each other rather than simply add: dried shiitake brings guanylate, kombu and soy bring glutamate, and the dried scallop of the original is not doing anything a mushroom cannot. The risk is the frying. Each addition dumps water, the oil temperature drops, then spikes as it dries, and garlic burns in the ten seconds after the bubbling goes quiet. Fry in stages, by moisture content.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":39,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mushroom%20XO%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mushroom XO"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Mushrooms","Black beans","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori","Dried mushrooms"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"twice-cooked-aubergine-with-miso-glaze","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 aubergines, about 300 g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g white miso"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml sake or water"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ginger, finely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g toasted sesame seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Halve the aubergines lengthways, salt the cut faces and leave 15 minutes, then blot dry; steam them cut side up for 10 to 12 minutes, until a skewer passes through the shoulder without pushing back.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1620,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool 5 minutes, then score the cut faces in a 1 cm diamond, cutting halfway into the flesh but never through the skin.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil in a heavy pan until it shimmers, lay the halves cut side down and press flat with a spatula for 4 to 5 minutes, until the whole face is lacquer brown and the flesh has collapsed to a custard.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the miso, mirin, sugar, sake, ginger and vinegar for 2 minutes to a glossy paste that falls from the spoon in a ribbon.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn the halves skin down, spoon the glaze over the scored faces and grill close to the element for 90 seconds, until it blisters and darkens at the ridges; scatter spring onion and sesame.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Raw aubergine is a sponge, much of its volume air, and it will drink a hot pan dry and then weep the oil back out when the cells finally collapse: greasy, beige and still squeaky. Collapse that structure first with moist heat, and the second cooking becomes a sear that browns on a spoonful of oil in four minutes. Glaze at the very end and watch it: the miso mix is roughly a quarter sugar and goes from blistered to acrid in under a minute.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Scoring","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","nutty-toasted","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Twice-Cooked%20Aubergine%20with%20Miso%20Glaze%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Twice-Cooked Aubergine with Miso Glaze"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Eggplant","Miso","Sesame","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["scoring","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"beetroot-roasted-in-the-embers","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 beetroot, 150 to 180 g each, scrubbed, unpeeled, tails left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg hardwood charcoal or a hardwood fire burnt down"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g horseradish, freshly grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g walnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g flaky salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Burn the fire down until the flames are gone and the coals are red under a grey ash skin; flame gives you a charcoal shell over a raw middle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Nestle the beetroot directly into the embers, heap more coals over the tops, and turn them every 15 minutes so no face sits still against the hottest spot.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 45 to 60 minutes, until a skewer runs to the centre with no resistance and the skins are blistered black and lifting away from the flesh.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 10 minutes, then rub the skins off in a dry cloth; they should come away in sheets with the charred layer.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Quarter while still hot, dress at once with vinegar, oil, horseradish, toasted walnuts, dill, salt and pepper, and turn them once in the bowl as they cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The skin is the cooking vessel. Sealed, the beet steams in its own water while radiant heat caramelises the sugar just beneath it, and beetroot carries more sugar than almost any other vegetable you will roast. Foil gives you a boiled flavour and no char; live flame gives a burnt shell over a raw core. Use embers with grey edges. Dress while hot: a warm beet pulls vinegar into the flesh, a cold one sheds it onto the plate.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Charring over open flame"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","charred","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, char used as punctuation, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beetroot%20Roasted%20in%20the%20Embers%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beetroot Roasted in the Embers"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beets","Dill","Vinegar","Wine","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"leeks-vinaigrette","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 slim leeks, about 1.2 kg untrimmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml sherry vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g shallot, very finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g capers, rinsed and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Trim the roots flat but leave the root plate attached, cut off the coarse dark green, then split each leek lengthways to within 2 cm of the root and fan the layers under cold running water until no grit remains.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer in the salted water 12 to 16 minutes, until a knife tip enters the thickest white and meets nothing; a leek that squeaks against the teeth is undercooked, not al dente.","durationSec":960,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":960,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift onto a tray lined with a folded cloth, cut side down, lay another cloth on top and press with a light tray for 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the mustard with the vinegar and a pinch of salt, then beat in the oil in a thin stream until it thickens, and stir through shallot, capers and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange the halves still warm, spoon the vinaigrette along the length and leave 20 minutes before serving so they drink it in.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two failures live here. Undercooked leek shreds into strings between the teeth, so take it well past tender-crisp until a knife meets nothing at the white. Then the water: a leek holds it between its layers, and a wet leek sheds vinaigrette straight into a puddle on the plate. Press the halves on a cloth, then dress them warm. As they cool they contract and pull the dressing into the layers instead of floating on it.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Leeks%20Vinaigrette%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Leeks Vinaigrette"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Leek","Capers","Parsley","Vinegar","Wine","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"king-oyster-scallops","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 large king oyster mushrooms with thick stems, about 450 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g kombu, one 10 cm piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 thyme sprigs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the kombu in the water at 60C (140F) for 20 minutes, lift it out before it turns slippery, add soy and mirin and reduce to 60 ml of syrupy glaze.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cut the stems into 3 cm cylinders, score a 2 mm crosshatch about 1 mm deep on both flat faces, salt them and rest 10 minutes, then blot the beads of moisture away.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Get a heavy pan properly hot, add the neutral oil, and lay the cylinders down spaced apart; leave them completely alone 3 to 4 minutes, until the underside is deep brown and lifts without sticking.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn, add olive oil, garlic and thyme, and baste for 2 minutes as the second face colours and the mushroom goes springy rather than soft.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Spoon on the kombu glaze off the heat, let it bubble to a shine, and finish with lemon zest and a squeeze of juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A mushroom is around 90% water held in walls of chitin, and chitin does not dissolve with heat, which is why this takes a sear that would reduce any other vegetable to a slick. It just cannot brown until the water has gone. Salting the cut faces and blotting means you start already partly dry. Crowd the pan or start it lukewarm and they release their water, stew grey and turn rubbery. The scoring is not decoration: it holds the glaze.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi","Reducing a sauce","Scoring","Searing: the hard crust","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=King%20Oyster%20Scallops%20recipe","sub":"Search films of King Oyster Scallops"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"scallops-dry-vs-wet-u-10","term":"Scallops: Dry vs. Wet, U-10"},{"slug":"baste-arroser-and-glaze","term":"Baste, Arroser & Glaze"}],"pantryItems":["Scallops","Garlic","Mushrooms","Lemon","Thyme","Soy sauce","Wine","Seaweed/Nori","Clove"],"judgedBy":["scoring","dashi"]},{"slug":"charred-hispi-cabbage-with-brown-butter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 hispi or pointed cabbage, about 800 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 ml rapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g capers, rinsed and drained dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coarse sourdough breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Quarter the cabbage through the root so each wedge holds together, salt the cut faces and leave 15 minutes, then blot.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat a heavy pan until a drop of water skitters, add the oil and lay the wedges on one cut face; do not move them for 4 to 5 minutes, until the face is mahogany with black frilled edges, then repeat on the second face.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Transfer the pan to a 200C (390F) oven for 8 to 10 minutes, until a knife slips into the root end without effort while the leaf edges stay crisp.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Melt the butter in a pale pan over medium heat and cook until it stops foaming, the solids on the base go the colour of hazelnut skin and it smells of toasted nuts, then drop in the capers and breadcrumbs and pull it off the heat immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the lemon juice to stop the butter cooking, stir in the parsley and pour the whole pan over the wedges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brassicas carry sulphur compounds that accumulate the longer they cook: slow gentle heat is exactly what makes cabbage smell like cabbage. Fast dry heat browns the sugars before that chemistry gets going, which is why a black-edged wedge tastes sweet and a simmered one does not. Then watch the butter. Once its water has boiled off the milk solids brown at around 150C, and the gap between hazelnut and burnt is about twenty seconds; the lemon is the brake.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brown butter"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brown butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brown%20butter%20beurre%20noisette%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Charred%20Hispi%20Cabbage%20with%20Brown%20Butter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Charred Hispi Cabbage with Brown Butter"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Cabbage","Capers","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Bread","Breadcrumbs/Panko"]},{"slug":"pressed-potato-terrine","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg floury potatoes such as Maris Piper or russet"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the butter without stirring, let it settle 5 minutes and pour off the clear fat, leaving the milky solids behind; you want about 230 g of clarified butter, and the solids would burn later.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peel the potatoes and slice 2 mm thick on a mandolin straight into a bowl with the butter, garlic, salt, thyme and pepper, and do not rinse them at any point.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a 900 g loaf tin with baking paper, then layer the slices flat and overlapping, pressing each layer down with your palm so no air is trapped, and level the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover tightly with foil and bake at 160C (320F) for 1 hour 45 minutes to 2 hours, until a skewer meets no resistance anywhere from top to base.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Press with a board and a 2 kg weight, cool, then refrigerate overnight; turn out, cut into bars and fry in the oil over medium heat until all four long faces are glassy and crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Do not rinse the slices. The starch you would wash away is the glue: amylose gelatinises between the layers as they cook and sets as the terrine cools, and that, not the butter, is what makes the block sliceable. Waxy potatoes never do it and slide apart on the knife. Press while warm to drive out the air, then chill overnight so the starch retrogrades and firms. Salt every layer, because there is no seasoning the middle afterwards.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pressed%20Potato%20Terrine%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pressed Potato Terrine"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Potato","Thyme","Butter","Flour","Clove"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"tomato-water-consomme","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg very ripe tomatoes, the softest you can find"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 basil sprig, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml sherry vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cherry tomatoes, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small cucumber, peeled, seeded, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 basil leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Core and roughly chop the tomatoes, blitz with the salt in short pulses to a coarse, still-lumpy purée; a smooth blend makes a fine suspension that will drive pulp through the cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the kombu and basil sprig, cover and leave at room temperature 30 minutes for the salt to draw the water out.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a sieve with a doubled muslin, set it over a deep bowl, tip in the purée and refrigerate 8 to 12 hours, letting it drip; never squeeze or twist the cloth.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Discard the pulp and taste the liquid, which will be pale gold: correct with the vinegar and more salt until it tastes distinctly seasoned, well past where you would stop with a soup that had body.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve cold at 8C, or warmed to no more than 60C (140F), poured at the table over cherry tomatoes, cucumber, torn basil and a thread of olive oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"It runs clear because it drips, not because of any raft, so never squeeze the bag: pressure forces pectin-bound pulp through the weave and cloudy tomato water tastes flat. Salt at 1% of the fruit weight seasons and pulls water out osmotically, taking the yield to around 60%. With the pulp goes all the body, so it needs more salt, more acid and the kombu glutamate to stand up. Do not boil it; the aroma of raw tomato leaves above about 70C.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","umami-deep","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tomato%20Water%20Consomm%C3%A9%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tomato Water Consommé"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Vegetable-Led%20Plate%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The Vegetable-Led Plate kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"consomme-and-clarification","term":"Consommé & Clarification"}],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Cucumber","Stone fruit","Basil","Vinegar","Wine","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"shorshe-ilish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g ilish (hilsa) steaks, cut 2cm thick, 6 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"45g black mustard seeds (kalo shorshe)"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g yellow mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 kancha lonka (green chillies), 2 kept whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, for the grinding jar"},{"kind":"item","text":"90ml cold-pressed mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the steaks with 1 tsp turmeric and 1 tsp salt and leave 15 minutes, until water beads stand on the cut faces and the flesh firms to the touch.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soak both mustards in warm water 20 minutes, drain, then grind with 4 green chillies, 1 tsp salt and 3 tbsp cold water in short pulses, stopping the moment the paste smells sharp and clean rather than dull. Push it through a sieve and discard the husk.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat 75ml mustard oil in a kadai until the raw pungency flattens and a thin white wisp lifts off the surface, then take the pan off the flame for 30 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return it to a low flame, pour in the sieved paste with the water, remaining turmeric and salt, and cook 4 minutes until it thickens to a loose custard and oil beads yellow around the rim. Never let it gallop.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the fish in one layer, spoon gravy over each piece, cover and cook 7 minutes until the flesh parts from the backbone under a fork. Trail the last 15ml raw mustard oil and the whole chillies over, cover, and rest 5 minutes off the heat.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":660,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mustard pungency is made, not stored: crushing puts myrosinase onto sinigrin and the reaction wants cold water and a minute. Grind long and the jar warms, the volatile isothiocyanate blows off, and the husk gives up bitter phenols in its place. So soak the seeds, pulse briefly with salt and a green chilli, sieve out the husk, and never boil the gravy hard once the paste is in.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","earthy","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, an earthy, grounding base, melting away silken."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shorshe%20Ilish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shorshe Ilish"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mustard-seeds-yellow-brown-and-black","term":"Mustard Seeds: Yellow, Brown & Black"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Chiles","Mustard","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"rui-macher-jhol","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g rui (rohu) steaks, cut 2cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"90ml mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp kalojeere (nigella seeds)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 kancha lonka (green chillies), slit"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g potato, cut into thick lengthwise wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g potol (pointed gourd), peeled in stripes and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ginger paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp Kashmiri chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"800ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of coriander leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the fish with 1.5 tsp turmeric and 1.5 tsp salt and leave 15 minutes until the surface goes tacky.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the mustard oil until the raw smell flattens and a haze lifts, then fry the steaks over a high flame, 2 minutes a side, and do not touch them until each piece releases itself from the pan and shows a set tan crust. Lift out.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the potato and potol in the same oil until the cut edges turn gold and translucent, then lift them out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the kalojeere and slit chillies into the oil and wait for the seeds to crackle and the pan to smell of toasted onion, about 20 seconds. Add the ginger paste and ground spices with 2 tbsp water and fry until the oil separates and runs orange.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the hot water with the vegetables and remaining salt, boil 10 minutes until a knife slides into the potato, then slide the fish back in and simmer 4 minutes until the jhol is thin, clear and faintly gold. Scatter coriander and rest 5 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1140,"estimated":false}],"note":"Carp is soft-fleshed and its connective tissue is slack, so raw steaks dropped into liquid shred and cloud the jhol. The turmeric and salt rub pulls surface moisture out, and hot oil then sets a thin denatured protein crust that holds the steak together through the simmer. The failure is impatience: move the fish before that crust forms and it tears and welds to the pan. Wait until it lets go by itself.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Searing: the hard crust","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","gently hot","aromatic","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, a warm current of chile, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rui%20Macher%20Jhol%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rui Macher Jhol"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ginger","Potato","Chiles","Cilantro","Mustard","Cumin","Coriander seed","Turmeric","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"kosha-mangsho","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg goat on the bone, shoulder and leg, in 4cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g full-fat yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g onion, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ginger paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp garlic paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp Kashmiri chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom, 4 cloves, 5cm cassia bark, 2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml hot water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the goat with the yoghurt, turmeric, ginger, garlic, salt and 1 tbsp of the mustard oil for at least 2 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the rest of the oil until the pungency flattens, add the sugar and let it foam to the colour of dark honey, then the whole spices, then the onions. Fry 20 minutes until they collapse into a jammy brown mass that no longer catches on the spoon.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip in the meat with its marinade over a high flame and stir until the pieces tighten, go grey-brown, and the yoghurt breaks into a rough curd around them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Now kosha it. Let the mass dry out and stick, splash in 60ml water, scrape the brown fond off the base and stir it back through. Repeat 5 or 6 times across 30 minutes, until the meat is dark mahogany and oil pools red at the rim of the pan.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the hot water, cover, and cook on the lowest flame 50 minutes until the meat gives at the bone. Stir in the ghee and garam masala and rest 10 minutes.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Koshano is a browning technique disguised as stirring. Each cycle of drying out and splashing lets onion sugars and yoghurt proteins scorch onto the pan, then lifts that fond back into the meat, so colour and depth build in layers. Add all the water at once and the pot never climbs past 100C (212F), nothing browns, and you get a pale thin jhol. Stop only when oil separates and runs red at the edge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","aromatic","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, aromatics blooming from the first bite, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kosha%20Mangsho%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kosha Mangsho"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Bay leaf","Yogurt","Mustard","Sugar","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"aloo-posto","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g waxy potato, cut in 1.5cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"70g white poppy seeds (posto)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 kancha lonka (green chillies)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp kalojeere (nigella seeds)"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.25 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the posto in hot water for 30 minutes, drain, then grind with 2 green chillies, a pinch of salt and 3 tbsp cold water until the paste ribbons off the spoon and feels smooth, not sandy, between finger and thumb.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat 45ml mustard oil until the raw bite flattens, drop the flame to medium and crackle the kalojeere for 10 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potato, turmeric and salt and fry over medium heat 8 minutes until the cut faces are gold and the edges have gone translucent.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the water, cover, and cook 8 minutes until a cube yields to a spoon but still holds its corners.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Take the pan off a hard boil, stir the posto paste through with the sugar, and cook gently 4 minutes until it tightens around the potato and oil beads on the surface. It must stay ivory. Finish off the heat with the last 15ml raw mustard oil and the remaining chillies, slit.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Poppy seed is around 45 per cent oil inside a hard shell, so it becomes a paste only if the shell is softened by soaking and then ground wet and cool. Grind it dry or hot and the oil cracks out: what you get is a gritty, greasy sludge that will not bind to anything. In the pan there is a second trap. Past pale ivory the paste scorches, and scorched posto tastes bitter and sandy. Low flame, short cook.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aloo%20Posto%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Aloo Posto"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Chiles","Mustard","Sugar","Turmeric","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"shukto","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g uchche (bitter gourd), sliced 5mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 shojne data (drumsticks), in 6cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g kanchkola (green banana), in 3cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g potato, in thick batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g mulo (white radish), in batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g begun (aubergine), in thick fingers"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g bori (sun-dried lentil dumplings)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp panch phoron"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp radhuni, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ginger paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp white poppy seeds ground to a paste with 1 tsp yellow mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar and 1.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the bori in the mustard oil until they puff and go biscuit-gold, then lift them out. Fry the uchche slices by themselves until the edges crisp and darken, and set them aside on their own plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the remaining vegetables in batches until the cut edges take colour but the centres are still raw, then lift them out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Temper the panch phoron and bay leaves in the same oil over a low flame, and judge the pan by the methi: pull it the second the fenugreek darkens one shade, because ten seconds later the whole tempering is acrid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return every vegetable except the gourd, add the ginger, the poppy and mustard paste, salt, sugar and water, cover, and simmer 15 minutes until a drumstick gives up its pulp to a fingernail.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drop the flame, stir the milk in below a simmer and cook 3 minutes until the gravy turns opaque white and just coats a spoon.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the fried gourd and bori back in, finish with the ghee and ground radhuni, and rest 5 minutes so the bori soften without going to mush.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Shukto opens the meal because bitterness sharpens the palate for the rice, dal and fish that follow, so that bitterness has to stay a note and not a wash. The cucurbitacins in uchche are water-soluble: simmer the gourd from the start and the whole white gravy turns bitter. Fry it alone, fold it back at the end, and hold the milk below a simmer, since heat plus ginger will curdle it into grains.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Reducing a sauce","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","earthy","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shukto%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shukto"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"panch-phoron","term":"Panch Phoron"}],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Potato","Eggplant","Radish/Turnip","Banana","Bay leaf","Milk","Lentils","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"cholar-dal","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g chana dal (Bengal gram)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"900ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g fresh coconut, cut into 5mm chips"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dried red chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"5cm cassia bark, 4 green cardamom, 4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ginger paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g nolen gur or jaggery, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp garam masala"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the dal 2 hours, drain, then boil with the turmeric, bay, salt and water, skimming the grey foam, 25 minutes, until a grain crushes between finger and thumb yet is still visibly a separate grain.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the coconut chips in the ghee until the edges go the colour of pale toast, then lift them out before they darken and turn bitter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In the same ghee temper the cumin, dried chillies, cassia, cardamom and cloves until the cumin darkens a shade and the cardamom smells sweet rather than green.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the tempering and the ginger into the dal, stir in the jaggery, and simmer 8 minutes until the dal holds a spoon trail for two full seconds.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the coconut, raisins and garam masala and rest off the heat 5 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is the dal you eat with luchi, so it has to stay grain-distinct: boiled to the point where a grain crushes on the palate but has not surrendered its skin. The trap is the gur. Sugar competes with the starch for water and firms the seed coat, so dal cooked sweet from the start never softens further, however long it sits. Soften and salt first, sweeten at the end.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","earthy","sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, an earthy, grounding base, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cholar%20Dal%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cholar Dal"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Chiles","Coconut","Dried fruit","Bay leaf","Lentils","Sugar","Cumin","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"begun-bhaja","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g large begun (aubergine), cut into 1.5cm rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp Kashmiri chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml mustard oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score a shallow lattice into both faces of each round, rub in the turmeric, chilli, salt and sugar, and leave 20 minutes until beads of water stand on the surface.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blot the rounds dry on a cloth, or that water will spit and drag the oil temperature down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the mustard oil in a flat pan until the sharp raw smell flattens and a white wisp lifts off, then drop the flame to medium and let it settle for 30 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the rounds in without crowding and leave them alone 3 minutes, until the underside is deep mahogany and the flesh at the rim has gone translucent. Turn once and give the second side 2 minutes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain the slices standing on edge on a rack rather than flat on paper, and salt them while they are still hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Raw mustard oil is a condiment, not a frying medium: it carries allyl isothiocyanate and a high free fatty acid load, and both taste raw and catch at the back of the throat. Take it to the smoke point, around 250C (480F), until the sharpness flattens and a wisp lifts, then drop to medium. Fry below that and aubergine, which is mostly air, drinks oil faster than its pectin can collapse.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Scoring","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","earthy","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, an earthy, grounding base, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Begun%20Bhaja%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Begun Bhaja"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Eggplant","Mustard","Sugar","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["scoring","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"chingri-malai-curry","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g bagda chingri (tiger prawns), shell and head on"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric and 1.5 tsp salt, for the prawns"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, 4 green cardamom, 5cm cassia bark, 4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g onion, ground to a smooth paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ginger paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Kashmiri chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml thin coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml thick coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 kancha lonka (green chillies), slit"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp garam masala"}],"steps":[{"text":"Devein the prawns through the back, leaving the shell and head on, and rub them with the first lot of turmeric and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the mustard oil until the raw pungency flattens, then sear the prawns 40 seconds a side, until they turn opaque coral and curl into a loose C. Lift them out while they are still slightly under.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Temper the whole spices until the cardamom smells sweet, add the onion paste, and fry 10 minutes until the raw smell goes and the oil separates and runs orange at the edge.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the ginger, turmeric, chilli powder and sugar with 2 tbsp water and fry 2 minutes, until the paste no longer smells of raw spice.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the thin coconut milk and simmer gently 6 minutes until it coats a spoon, then add the thick coconut milk, the prawns and the chillies and hold the pan at a lazy shiver, never a boil, for 3 minutes. Finish off the heat with the ghee and garam masala.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":540,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coconut milk is a protein-stabilised emulsion, not cream. Boil it hard and those proteins coagulate, the fat breaks out as an oily slick, and the gravy goes grainy. Prawns want exactly the same restraint: their muscle sets near 60C (140F) and starts wringing out water above 70C (158F). Sear for colour, pull them early, and return them only to a shivering pan. A prawn curled to a C is done; an O is a rubber band.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chingri%20Malai%20Curry%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chingri Malai Curry"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Ginger","Chiles","Coconut","Bay leaf","Milk","Coconut milk","Mustard","Sugar","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"luchi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g maida (refined white flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g ghee, soft, for the moyan"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.75 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml lukewarm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"700ml refined oil or ghee, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the ghee into the flour and salt with your fingertips until the mixture looks like damp sand and holds its shape when squeezed in a fist.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the water in stages and knead 5 minutes to a dough noticeably firmer than chapati dough, smooth and tight. Smear the surface with oil, cover, and rest 30 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into 16 balls and roll each into a 10cm disc of even thickness, using a smear of oil on the board rather than a dusting of flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the frying oil to 190C (375F), the point where a scrap of dough surfaces in two seconds. Slide a luchi in and press its centre gently under with the back of a slotted spoon until it balloons, about 10 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip it, give it 10 seconds more, and lift it out while it is still puffed and pale. A browned luchi is an overcooked one. Drain them standing on edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The puff is trapped steam, so everything you do protects the seal. Moyan, the ghee rubbed into the flour, shortens the gluten for tenderness, but past roughly one part fat to eight of maida the sheet can no longer hold pressure and the luchi lies flat and greasy. Roll evenly on an oiled board, never a floured one, since loose flour burns and darkens the oil. At 190C (375F) the skin sets before the steam finds a way out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Luchi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Luchi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"mishti-doi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g granulated sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g nolen gur (date palm jaggery), chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g live plain yoghurt, at room temperature"}],"steps":[{"text":"Reduce the milk over a medium flame, scraping the skin off the sides back in every few minutes, for 30 minutes, until it is down to about 1 litre and has turned the colour of ivory.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Melt the sugar with the water in a dry pan until it is the colour of strong tea and smells nutty, then stand back and kill it with a ladle of the hot milk. Stir the caramel and the gur into the pot until both dissolve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the sweetened milk to 45C (113F). Against the inside of the wrist it should read warm, not hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the starter yoghurt smooth with a little of the warm milk, fold it into the rest without beating air in, and pour into unglazed earthenware pots.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and hold undisturbed at 35C to 40C (95F to 104F) for 7 hours, until the surface wobbles as one piece and pulls cleanly from the side of the pot, then chill 4 hours before serving.","durationSec":25200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":39600,"estimated":false}],"note":"The set is casein flocculating once the culture drags the milk down to about pH 4.6, and that culture only works between 35C and 45C (95F to 113F). Stir the starter into milk above 50C (122F) and you kill it; below 30C (86F) it stalls and you are left with sweet soup. Reducing the milk by a third raises the solids so the gel is firm without any thickener. While it sets, do not move the pots: shearing a young gel makes it weep whey.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mishti%20Doi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mishti Doi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bengali%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Bengali kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Milk","Yogurt","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"suen-yung-jing-daai-ji-steamed-scallops-with-garlic-and-fun-see","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 scallops on the half shell, 400 g total"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fun see (mung bean glass noodles)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g garlic, half chopped raw and half chopped and fried golden"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 spring onions, green parts finely shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red chilli, deseeded and finely diced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the fun see in cold water for 15 minutes until the strands are pliable and dull white, cut into 5 cm lengths and coil a small nest into each shell under the scallop.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry half the garlic in the oil over low heat until it is pale straw and the fizzing stops, about 3 minutes, then pull it off the heat and stir in the raw garlic, soy, wine, sugar, pepper and water: the mix should end up warm, not sizzling.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the garlic oil over the scallops and hold the plate back until the steamer water is at a hard rolling boil that rattles the lid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam covered on the highest heat for 3 minutes 30 seconds for scallops 3 cm thick, and pull them the moment the outside has turned from glassy to chalk white while the centre still reads faintly translucent.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":false},{"text":"Shower with spring onion and chilli, pour 2 tablespoons of smoking oil over the greens so they hiss and collapse, and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Scallop adductor is nearly pure muscle with almost no collagen, so it crosses from set to squeaky in under a minute. The lid must already be rattling when the plate goes in: a slow climb through 50 to 60C (120 to 140F) contracts the fibres gradually and wrings the liquor out into the shell. Load into full steam, time from the moment the lid is back down, and pull while the centre still looks glassy.","equipment":["Steamer"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Suen%20Yung%20Jing%20Daai%20Ji%20(Steamed%20Scallops%20with%20Garlic%20and%20Fun%20See)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Suen Yung Jing Daai Ji (Steamed Scallops with Garlic and Fun See)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lobster-new-shell-vs-hard-shell","term":"Lobster: New Shell vs Hard Shell"},{"slug":"mussels-scallops-and-the-cold-water-winter","term":"Mussels, Scallops & the Cold-Water Winter"},{"slug":"scallops-dry-vs-wet-u-10","term":"Scallops: Dry vs. Wet, U-10"}],"pantryItems":["Scallops","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Chiles","Noodles","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"see-yau-gai-soy-sauce-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 free-range chicken, 1.4 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dark soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 spring onions, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 star anise"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 stick cassia bark"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 pieces chan pei (dried tangerine peel)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 slices liquorice root"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g sa geung (sand ginger), sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml sesame oil, to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres iced water, for the plunge"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the soys, water, rock sugar, wine, ginger, spring onion and whole spices to a boil in a pot just wide enough to hold the bird, then drop the heat until only a bubble or two breaks the surface every few seconds, around 85C (185F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lower the chicken in breast down, holding the legs so the cavity floods, and lift it clear twice in the first two minutes so the cold cavity liquid drains and is replaced: the brine must never climb back to a rolling boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach 25 minutes for a 1.4 kg bird, then kill the heat and leave it covered in the brine off the stove for 20 minutes, until the thickest part of the thigh reads 74C (165F) and runs clear amber juice when pierced.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the bird into iced water for 3 minutes, until the skin tightens and squeaks under a finger, then hang it to drip for 10 minutes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":780,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Chop through the bone into 2 cm strips, rebuild the bird on the plate, brush with sesame oil and spoon over 100 ml of the hot brine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The bird cooks on the brine's stored heat, not on boiling. Past about 90C (194F) the skin's collagen shrinks and splits and the breast muscle squeezes out its water and goes stringy. So hold the pot at a shiver, cut the heat early and let carryover finish the thigh. The iced plunge then contracts the skin so it sits tight and glossy instead of sagging away from the meat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=See%20Yau%20Gai%20(Soy%20Sauce%20Chicken)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of See Yau Gai (Soy Sauce Chicken)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Soy sauce","Sesame","Wine","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"siu-yuk-crackling-roast-pork-belly","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg pork belly in one piece, skin on, ribs removed, 4 cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine sea salt, for the flesh"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g five spice powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g naam yu (fermented red bean curd), mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml Chinese white rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g coarse rock salt, for the skin cap"},{"kind":"item","text":"flaky salt, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch the belly skin side up in boiling water for 4 minutes, until the skin turns from waxy to matte and firm, then dry it and prick the skin all over, 40 to 50 punctures per 10 cm square, through the skin but never down into the meat.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rub the salt, sugar, five spice, pepper, naam yu and wine into the flesh and cut sides only, keeping every trace off the skin, then sit the piece skin up on a rack and refrigerate uncovered for 24 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe the skin with the vinegar, wall the meat in a tight foil boat so only the skin is exposed, pack the rock salt over the skin in a 1 cm slab and roast at 200C (390F) for 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the salt cap off in one piece, scrape the skin clean, then raise the oven to 250C (480F) and cook 12 to 20 minutes more, turning the tray, until the whole skin has puffed into fine blisters and rings hollow when tapped with a spoon.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 15 minutes, then chop skin side down through the crackling with one heavy cleaver stroke per cut into 3 cm squares, and scatter with flaky salt.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Crackling is skin dried until its last water flashes to steam and inflates the collagen into a foam. Leave surface moisture and you get leather instead. So the skin dries uncovered overnight, takes a vinegar wipe that evaporates fast and carries water out with it, and wears a rock salt cap that wicks and holds it under 100C (212F) while the meat roasts. Pull the cap, then blast the dry skin at 250C (480F).","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Roasting in a hot oven","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","tangy-ferment","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, ferment tang with real depth behind it, melting away silken."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Siu%20Yuk%20(Crackling%20Roast%20Pork%20Belly)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Siu Yuk (Crackling Roast Pork Belly)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pork-belly","term":"Pork Belly"},{"slug":"whole-muscle-vs-fermented-salami-the-two-churches","term":"Whole-Muscle vs. Fermented Salami: The Two Churches"},{"slug":"chinese-five-spice","term":"Chinese Five-Spice"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Kidney/Pinto beans","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Five-spice"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"gon-chau-ngau-ho-dry-fried-beef-ho-fun","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450 g fresh flat ho fun rice noodles, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g beef flank, sliced 3 mm across the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml light soy sauce, for the beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml groundnut oil, for the beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml groundnut oil, for the wok"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced 5 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g bean sprouts, tails picked"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 spring onions, cut into 6 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml dark soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slap the bicarbonate and water into the beef until every slice has drunk it and the pile turns tacky, rest 20 minutes, rinse well and squeeze dry, then work in the soy, cornflour and 20 ml oil until the slices are slick and fall apart singly.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the ho fun into single strands with your fingers, rubbing a teaspoon of oil through as you go: they must be loose and at room temperature, so steam them for 1 minute first if they came straight from the fridge.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":60,"estimated":true},{"text":"Get the wok smoking with a thin film of oil, lay the beef out in one layer and leave it until the underside browns and it releases on its own, about 45 seconds, turn once and tip it out still pink inside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe the wok, heat the rest of the oil until it shimmers and the first wisp of smoke rises, give the onion 30 seconds, then add the noodles and press them flat against the metal in 20 second stretches until you smell scorched starch and see brown freckles on the strands.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the soys, sugar and wine down the hot side of the wok so they caramelise before they reach the noodles, return the beef, throw in the sprouts and spring onion and toss six or seven times: out of the wok while the sprouts still squeak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things make this dish. Bicarbonate raises the beef's pH so the myosin cannot contract tight and wring out water, which is what velveting means. And gon chau means dry: no stock, no cornflour slurry, because any added liquid parks the wok below the roughly 200C (390F) at which scorched starch and vaporised soy give you wok hei. Cold noodles have retrograded and will snap, so warm them first.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","smoky","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, wood-smoke running underneath, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Velveting","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=velveting%20meat%20chinese%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gon%20Chau%20Ngau%20Ho%20(Dry-Fried%20Beef%20Ho%20Fun)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gon Chau Ngau Ho (Dry-Fried Beef Ho Fun)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-wok","term":"The Wok"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Scallion","Bean sprouts","Noodles","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"cheung-fun-with-sweet-soy-rice-noodle-rolls","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g rice flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g wheat starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g tapioca starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml groundnut oil, plus more for the tray"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml dark soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml water, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 slices ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 spring onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"toasted sesame seeds, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the rice flour, wheat starch, tapioca starch, cornflour and salt into the water until there is not a lump left, stir in the oil and rest 30 minutes: the batter should run off the whisk as thin as single cream.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the soys, water, rock sugar, ginger and spring onion for 5 minutes until the sugar has gone and the ginger smells sweet, then strain and keep warm.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil a flat metal tray, sit it on a trivet in a wok of hard boiling water, stir the batter up from the bottom, ladle in 60 ml and tilt to a 2 mm film, then cover and steam 3 minutes until the sheet is translucent, blistered all over and no longer milky in the middle.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the tray into cold water for 5 seconds to shock the base, scrape the sheet up with an oiled scraper and roll it into a loose three-fold log: it should feel slippery and slack, never springy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into 6 cm lengths, pour the warm sauce over, finish with sesame seeds and eat within a few minutes while the rolls are still soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The slip comes from the starch blend, not the rice alone. Rice flour sets the sheet, wheat starch keeps it clear and tender, tapioca supplies the stretch. Too much rice flour and it tears as you roll; too much tapioca and it eats like chewing gum. The batter also separates within a minute of standing, so stir it up from the bottom before every single ladle: an unstirred pot pours a gluey sheet, then a sheet too thin to lift.","equipment":["Wok","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cheung%20Fun%20with%20Sweet%20Soy%20(Rice%20Noodle%20Rolls)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cheung Fun with Sweet Soy (Rice Noodle Rolls)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Noodles","Bread","Flour","Soy sauce","Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"lo-mai-gai-lotus-leaf-sticky-rice-parcels","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g glutinous rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 dried lotus leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g boneless chicken thigh, in 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 lap cheong (Chinese cured sausage), sliced on the angle"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 dried shiitake, soaked and quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dried shrimp, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 salted duck egg yolks, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml oyster sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml dark soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml shiitake soaking liquid"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the glutinous rice in cold water for 4 hours, until a grain crushes to powder between your nails, then drain; soak the lotus leaves in hot water until leathery and pliable and wipe both faces.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Marinate the chicken in the oyster sauce, both soys, sugar, cornflour, wine and pepper for 20 minutes, until the pieces look glazed and cling to each other.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the ginger, dried shrimp and shiitake in the oil until the shrimp smells sweet and the mushrooms have squeaked and gone slack, add the soaking liquid, boil 2 minutes, then fold the lot through the drained raw rice until every grain is stained brown.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cup 150 g of rice into each leaf, bury chicken, lap cheong and half an egg yolk in the middle, cover with more rice and fold the leaf into a tight brick with no gap at the seam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam over a hard boil for 60 minutes, then open one: the rice should be glossy and hold its shape under a press, with no chalky white core.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Glutinous rice is almost pure amylopectin. Boiled, it never takes on enough water to cook through: it goes to paste outside and stays chalky in the centre. It has to soak cold for hours so water enters the grain slowly, then steam so it gelatinises evenly. The leaf is not decoration either; it seals the parcel so the rice cooks in its own trapped steam and picks up that tea-like smell.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lo%20Mai%20Gai%20(Lotus%20Leaf%20Sticky%20Rice%20Parcels)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lo Mai Gai (Lotus Leaf Sticky Rice Parcels)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lap-cheong-and-the-chinese-cured-pantry","term":"Lap Cheong & the Chinese Cured Pantry"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Duck","Shrimp","Eggs","Ginger","Mushrooms","Rice","Soy sauce","Oyster sauce","Wine","Sugar","Dried mushrooms"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"jiu-yim-yau-yu-salt-and-pepper-squid","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g squid, cleaned, tubes opened flat and tentacles left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt, for the squid"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre groundnut oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves garlic, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 red chillies, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chillies, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 spring onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt, for the seasoning"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g five spice powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caster sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the inside of the tubes in a 5 mm crosshatch two thirds of the way through, cut into 4 cm pieces, toss with the wine and salt for 10 minutes, then dry every piece hard between cloths until the surface is matte and takes a fingerprint.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the seasoning salt, white pepper, five spice and sugar in a dry pan for 40 seconds, until the pepper turns sharply floral, then tip it straight out so it stops cooking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dredge the squid in the mixed potato starch and cornflour, then shake it in a sieve until only a chalk-thin coat is left clinging.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 190C (375F) in two batches for 60 to 75 seconds, until the coating goes from white to pale gold and the pieces have curled and float high in the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, then flash the garlic, chillies and spring onion in a nearly dry wok for 20 seconds until fragrant but uncoloured, return the squid, shower with the toasted seasoning and toss three times before it hits the plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Squid has two edible windows: under about 2 minutes, or over 30. In between, the collagen in the mantle shrinks and squeezes the water out and the flesh goes to rubber. Frying at 190C (375F) keeps you inside the first window, but only if the squid is bone dry. Wet squid drops the oil temperature, so it sits far longer and crosses into the rubber zone while the crust is still pale.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Scoring","Deep frying","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiu%20Yim%20Yau%20Yu%20(Salt%20and%20Pepper%20Squid)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jiu Yim Yau Yu (Salt and Pepper Squid)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Squid/Octopus","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Potato","Chiles","Wine","Sugar","Clove","Five-spice"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","scoring"]},{"slug":"bo-jai-fan-claypot-rice-with-lap-cheong","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g jasmine rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"360 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 lap cheong (Chinese cured sausage), sliced 3 mm on the angle"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g boneless chicken thigh, in 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml oyster sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml light soy sauce, for the chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ginger, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 heads choy sum, blanched"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml light soy sauce, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml dark soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml water, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, shredded"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the rice until the water runs almost clear and soak it 30 minutes; meanwhile marinate the chicken in the oyster sauce, light soy, cornflour, wine, ginger and pepper, and simmer the sauce ingredients until the rock sugar dissolves.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain the rice, bring it to a boil with the 360 ml water in an oiled clay pot, uncovered, until craters open across the surface and the free water has gone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the chicken and lap cheong over the rice, cover and cook on the lowest flame for 12 minutes: the sausage fat should render down through the grains and the pot should smell of caramel, never of smoke.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drizzle 30 ml of oil down the inside wall of the pot, raise to a medium flame and rotate the pot a quarter turn every 45 seconds for 3 minutes, listening for a steady crackle rather than a hiss.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest off the heat 5 minutes, lay the choy sum on top, pour the sauce over and cut straight down to the base with a spoon: the fan jiu crust should lift away in deep amber sheets you can hear.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"The crust is not burnt rice. It is the layer touching the clay that ran out of free water first, so it climbs past 100C (212F) and browns while the grains above are still only steaming. Oil down the wall carries heat into that layer and stops it welding to the pot. Rotate the pot as it forms, because a pot left still cooks one hot spot black while the rest of the base stays pale and soft.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Blanching & shocking","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bo%20Jai%20Fan%20(Claypot%20Rice%20with%20Lap%20Cheong)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bo Jai Fan (Claypot Rice with Lap Cheong)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lap-cheong-and-the-chinese-cured-pantry","term":"Lap Cheong & the Chinese Cured Pantry"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Sausage","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Rice","Soy sauce","Oyster sauce","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"baak-cheuk-gai-lan-blanched-gai-lan-with-ginger-oil","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g gai lan"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml groundnut oil, for the blanching water"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g ginger, finely shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml groundnut oil, for the finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml water, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caster sugar, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, shredded"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split any stem thicker than a pencil down its length so stem and leaf finish together, and trim the dry base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the water to a hard rolling boil with the salt, sugar and 30 ml oil: it has to stay boiling when the greens go in, so work in two batches if the pan is small.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanch 90 seconds for medium stems, until a stem bends without snapping and the leaves have flipped to a hard bright green, then lift them straight out and stand them upright to drain, with no ice bath, which only waterlogs them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the ginger in the remaining oil for about 90 seconds, until it fizzes gently and smells sweet rather than raw, and stir the soy, water and sugar together in a separate bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the greens in one direction on a warm dish, spoon the soy onto the plate rather than over the leaves, pour the ginger oil across the greens and eat while the stems still steam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Blanching drives the air out of the leaf so the green underneath reads through clearly. Keeping it short matters because the cells release acid as they break down, and that acid strips the magnesium out of chlorophyll and turns everything olive. A big, hard-boiling, lightly salted and oiled pot does all of this at once: in a small pot the water stalls off the boil, the greens stew, and the acid wins.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baak%20Cheuk%20Gai%20Lan%20(Blanched%20Gai%20Lan%20with%20Ginger%20Oil)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baak Cheuk Gai Lan (Blanched Gai Lan with Ginger Oil)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Soy sauce","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"geung-jup-jong-nai-ginger-milk-curd","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml whole milk, at least 3.5 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g old fibrous ginger, to yield 90 ml juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g caster sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the ginger finely and squeeze it through a cloth for 90 ml of juice, then divide 15 ml into each of six bowls and leave them to stand while you heat the milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the milk with the sugar, stirring, to 75C (167F): the surface should shiver and steam but never carry a bubble. Take it off and let it fall to 70C (158F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the settled white sediment back up through the juice in each bowl, then pour the milk in from about 20 cm above in one steady stream so it slams into the juice and mixes itself. Do not stir afterwards.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave the bowls undisturbed for 10 minutes: the curd is set when you can tilt one to 45 degrees and the surface holds, taking a spoon like a soft panna cotta.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"A protease in ginger clips the kappa casein off the milk's micelles exactly as rennet does, and the bared micelles then link into a gel. That enzyme is wrecked above roughly 80C (176F) and too sluggish below 65C (149F), so you pour between 70 and 75C (158 and 167F). Old fibrous ginger carries far more of it than young, and it settles out with the starch, so restir the juice. Stirring after the pour shears the gel and it will never set.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Geung%20Jup%20Jong%20Nai%20(Ginger%20Milk%20Curd)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Geung Jup Jong Nai (Ginger Milk Curd)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Milk","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"tortellini-in-brodo-di-cappone","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g farina 00, plus more for the board"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 whole eggs, 55 g to 60 g each, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 capon, about 1.8 kg, or a 1.5 kg boiling hen"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g beef shin or brisket"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 1 carrot, 1 celery stalk, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g pork loin, in 2 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g mortadella di Bologna, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g prosciutto di Parma, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g Parmigiano Reggiano, 24 month, grated fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole egg, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"quarter of a nutmeg, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt for the broth"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the capon, beef and vegetables with 4 litres of cold water and hold at 90C (194F), never a rolling boil, skimming until the surface is clear and the liquid is pale gold, about 3 hours; strain, chill, and lift off the set disc of fat.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the pork loin in the butter until the cubes are deep gold and stiff to the press, cool, then mince fine with the mortadella and prosciutto and work in the Parmigiano, egg and nutmeg until the paste holds a fingerprint without weeping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mound the flour, break in the eggs, knead 10 minutes until the ball is smooth and springs back slowly, then rest it under an upturned bowl for 30 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Roll with a long pin, turning and stretching outward, until you can read newsprint through the sheet; cut 3 cm squares and keep the rest under cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put a chickpea of filling on each square, fold to a triangle pressing the air out from the filling to the edge, then wrap it round your little finger and pinch the two points until the cuff stands up on its own.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop them into barely trembling broth and cook until they rise and the chalky core at the pinched knot is gone, 3 to 4 minutes.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":false}],"note":"A hand sheet is stretched outward in every direction under the pin, so it keeps a fine nap that holds broth; a roller compresses and polishes it, and polished pasta arrives at the table naked. The other failure is trapped air: it expands in the hot broth and splits the seam. Press the fold down onto the filling and out to the edge before you close the cuff.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Fresh pasta","Kneading dough","Searing: the hard crust","Salted water & the float test","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tortellini%20in%20Brodo%20di%20Cappone%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tortellini in Brodo di Cappone"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"prosciutto-di-parma-and-san-daniele","term":"Prosciutto di Parma & San Daniele"},{"slug":"mortadella-bologna","term":"Mortadella Bologna"},{"slug":"parmigiano-reggiano","term":"Parmigiano-Reggiano"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Butter","Parmesan","Stock/Broth","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"passatelli-in-brodo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g dry breadcrumb, ground fine from stale unsalted bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g Parmigiano Reggiano, 30 month, grated fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 whole eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a nutmeg, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 unwaxed lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g farina 00, only if the dough is slack"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres capon and beef broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the breadcrumb, Parmigiano, nutmeg and lemon zest together, then work the eggs in with your fingers until the mass is uniform and squeezes into a lump with no dry crumb left in the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap and rest 30 minutes so the crumb takes up the egg; the dough should now feel like firm putty and hold a ridge when pinched, not stick to the hand.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the broth to a bare tremble, press the dough through a passatelli iron or a ricer with 4 mm holes, and cut the strands off at 4 cm as they fall in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until they float and the surface turns from matte to satiny, about 2 minutes, then ladle broth and passatelli together into warm bowls.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"There is no gluten here. The strands hold together only because egg and Parmigiano protein set as they heat, so a rolling boil tears them into cloudy porridge before they ever firm up. Keep the broth at a bare tremble. Slack dough disintegrates the same way, and the usual cause is soft fresh breadcrumb: use dry crumb from stale bread, which absorbs rather than smears.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Passatelli%20in%20Brodo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Passatelli in Brodo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Lemon","Parmesan","Bread","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Stock/Broth","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"lasagne-verdi-alla-bolognese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g farina 00"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g cooked spinach, squeezed bone dry and pureed"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g pancetta, chopped to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g soffritto: onion, carrot and celery in equal weight, diced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g beef skirt or chuck, minced coarse"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g pork belly, minced coarse"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g double tomato concentrate"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre light beef broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre whole milk, for the besciamella"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g unsalted butter and 70 g farina 00, for the besciamella"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter, for the dish"},{"kind":"item","text":"nutmeg, fine salt, black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the pancetta slowly, add the soffritto and sweat it 20 minutes until it collapses to a sweet jammy base with no raw onion smell, then raise the heat and brown the beef and pork until the pan is dry and the meat catches brown on the bottom.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deglaze with the wine and cook until the sharpness is gone, stir in the tomato concentrate, then feed in the milk and broth a ladle at a time over 3 hours at a lazy blip, until the sauce is brick brown and a spoon leaves a clean track across the pan.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead the flour, eggs and spinach 10 minutes to a firm green dough, rest 30 minutes, roll by hand to 1 mm, then blanch the sheets 20 seconds, chill them in cold water and lay them flat on cloths until the surface is dull and dry.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the besciamella out until it coats the spoon and tastes only of milk and nutmeg, with no chalk of raw flour on the tongue.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build 6 thin layers, each barely veiled with ragu and besciamella and dusted with Parmigiano, dot the top with butter and bake at 180C (350F) until the edges are lacquered dark and the centre trembles when you shake the dish, about 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tomato is a seasoning here, not the sauce: 30 g of concentrate against 450 g of meat. Body comes from milk fed in over hours, whose sugars brown and whose casein slackens the meat fibres. Lead with tomato and the sauce stays thin and acid, and that acid will also curdle the milk into grains. Add milk only once the wine has cooked off, and never let the pan come above a blip.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Searing: the hard crust","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Mashing & puréeing","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lasagne%20Verdi%20alla%20Bolognese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lasagne Verdi alla Bolognese"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pancetta-tesa-and-arrotolata","term":"Pancetta (Tesa & Arrotolata)"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Spinach","Butter","Milk","Parmesan","Wine","Stock/Broth","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"garganelli-al-pettine-con-prosciutto-e-piselli","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g farina 00"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 whole eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"nutmeg, grated, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g prosciutto di Parma, an end piece, in 5 mm ribbons"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fresh shelled peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated, to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the flour, eggs, Parmigiano and nutmeg 10 minutes until the dough is tight and a cut face shows no marbling, then rest it covered 30 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll by hand to about 1 mm, cut 4 cm squares, and lay each square on the comb corner first; roll it up on a pencil-thick dowel, pressing so the ridges bite, and slide it off with the seam sealed and the surface deeply grooved.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter and warm the prosciutto just until the ribbons turn opaque and perfume the pan, no colour, then add the peas and a splash of pasta water and cook until they are bright green and just yield.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the garganelli 2 to 3 minutes until they float and the seam is tender, then finish them in the pan off the heat with the cream and Parmigiano until the sauce clings and the grooves are filled.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The comb exists to make surface. Ridges plus the nap left by a hand pin give the sauce something to key into, which is why an extruded tube of the same shape comes to the plate bare. The prosciutto is the other trap: held over heat its collagen tightens and it turns to salty leather, so it goes into warm butter and comes out before it takes any colour.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Keeping the cooking liquid","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garganelli%20al%20Pettine%20con%20Prosciutto%20e%20Piselli%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Garganelli al Pettine con Prosciutto e Piselli"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"prosciutto-di-parma-and-san-daniele","term":"Prosciutto di Parma & San Daniele"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Peas","Butter","Cream","Parmesan","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"gramigna-alla-salsiccia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g gramigna, fresh egg gramigna if you can get it"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g coarse Emilian pork sausage, casings removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, diced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g double tomato concentrate"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Crumble the sausage into cold butter and bring the heat up slowly so the fat runs before the meat sets, breaking it small until the pieces are gold and loose rather than welded into a patty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion and sweat until translucent and sweet, then pour in the wine and reduce until the pan smells of sausage and not of alcohol.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the tomato concentrate and the milk and hold at a lazy blip for 20 minutes, until the sauce is pale russet and the fat beads at the edge.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil the gramigna, drain it a minute early, and finish it in the pan with a ladle of pasta water and the Parmigiano until the curls are coated and no sauce pools on the plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sausage meat dropped into a hot pan seizes on contact: the proteins set before the fat renders and you get grey pellets rattling in a dry pan. Start it cold and let the fat come out to fry the meat in. Milk is the sauce here, not tomato. The 20 g of concentrate is for colour and depth only; more turns the dish sharp and buries the fennel and pepper in the sausage.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gramigna%20alla%20Salsiccia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gramigna alla Salsiccia"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Sausage","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Butter","Milk","Parmesan","Wine"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"tortelli-derbetta-di-parma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g farina 00"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 whole eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g Swiss chard leaves, stalks removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fresh ricotta, drained overnight in a sieve"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"quarter of a nutmeg, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wilt the chard in only the water clinging to the leaves, then wring it in a cloth until nothing more runs out and the ball is dense and dark; chop it fine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the chard, ricotta, Parmigiano, yolk and nutmeg together and season; the filling should sit on a spoon in a soft mound and hold its shape rather than slump.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead the flour and eggs 10 minutes to a firm dough, rest 30 minutes, then roll by hand to a sheet you can see your fingers through, working fast so it does not dry and crack.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Place walnut-sized mounds along the sheet, fold it over, press the air out from the fold outward, and cut with a fluted wheel so the crimped edge seals itself.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil 3 minutes until they float and the sealed edge turns translucent, then dress with butter melted to a nut-brown foam and a heavy hand of Parmigiano.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chard carries far more water than it looks. Leave it in and the filling steams inside the parcel, softening the seal from within until it splits in the pot, so wring the leaves until the cloth runs dry. Undrained ricotta does the same. Judge it on the spoon: a filling that slumps there will slump on the sheet, and no crimp will hold a seam it is pushing apart.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","warm-spiced","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tortelli%20d'Erbetta%20di%20Parma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tortelli d'Erbetta di Parma"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ricotta","term":"Ricotta"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Ricotta","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"erbazzone-reggiano","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g farina 00"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g strutto (rendered pork lard), cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"130 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg Swiss chard, leaves and tender stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g pancetta or lardo, in small dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, sliced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bunch flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the cold strutto into the flour and salt to a coarse crumb, add the water and work briefly to a smooth slack dough, then rest it 30 minutes so it will stretch thin without tearing.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanch the chard 2 minutes, refresh it, and wring it in a cloth to a tight dark ball; chop it and season hard with salt and pepper.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry half the pancetta with the onion, garlic and parsley until the fat runs clear and the garlic smells sweet, then fold it through the chard with the Parmigiano and egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll two thin sheets, line an oiled tray with one, spread the filling 2 cm deep, cover with the second, seal the rim and prick the lid all over; scatter the remaining raw pancetta on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (400F) on the oven floor until the lid is blistered and crackling brown and the base lifts crisp off the tray, 30 to 35 minutes.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":2100,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"This crust is short, not layered: cold lard coats the flour and blocks gluten, which is why it stays crisp instead of turning to leather. Steam is its enemy. Wet chard boils under the lid, lifts the top sheet off the filling and leaves the base grey and sodden. Wring the leaves until the cloth runs dry, prick the lid so steam can leave, and bake low in a hot oven.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","umami-deep","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Erbazzone%20Reggiano%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Erbazzone Reggiano"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pancetta-tesa-and-arrotolata","term":"Pancetta (Tesa & Arrotolata)"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Parsley","Parmesan","Clove"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"cotoletta-alla-petroniana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 veal loin cutlets, 120 g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g farina 00"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g fine dry breadcrumb"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter, clarified"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g prosciutto di Parma, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g Parmigiano Reggiano, shaved"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml light meat broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the cutlets between two sheets to an even 6 mm; thicker than that and the crumb burns long before the centre is hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flour, egg and crumb them, pressing the crumb on firmly, then rest them 15 minutes so the coating sets and will not slide off in the pan.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in clarified butter over moderate heat until both faces are an even hazelnut brown and the crust sounds hollow when tapped, about 3 minutes a side.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay a slice of prosciutto and a drift of Parmigiano on each, pour the broth around the cutlets and no more than a third of the way up, then cover and hold at the lowest heat until the cheese has slumped and the broth is syrupy, 5 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The whole point of the finish is that the crust survives it. Broth poured over the top dissolves the crumb in seconds, so it goes around the cutlets and stays low: the base softens into the sauce while steam alone melts the Parmigiano above. Clarified butter matters for the same reason. The milk solids in whole butter blacken well before the crumb is properly brown.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cotoletta%20alla%20Petroniana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cotoletta alla Petroniana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"prosciutto-di-parma-and-san-daniele","term":"Prosciutto di Parma & San Daniele"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Butter","Parmesan","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"piadina-romagnola","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g farina 00"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g strutto (rendered pork lard), soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml whole milk, lukewarm"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g bicarbonate of soda"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the strutto into the flour, salt and bicarbonate until it looks like damp sand, add the milk and knead 5 minutes to a soft dough that is smooth but still takes a fingerprint.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 6, roll into balls and rest them covered 30 minutes, until they no longer spring back when pressed.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll each into a 25 cm disc about 3 mm thick and prick it lightly all over with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a dry cast iron plate at 250C (480F), about 90 seconds a side, turning when the underside is freckled with brown blisters and the rim has stiffened.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack them under a cloth as they come off the iron so their own steam keeps them pliable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no yeast: the lift is bicarbonate and steam, so the window is short and hot. On a cool plate the water leaves before the disc can blister and you get a cracker that snaps instead of folding. Get the iron properly hot and keep each side under two minutes. Lard, not oil, is what keeps it supple as it cools, because it stays soft at room temperature.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Shaping by hand","Rubbing fat into flour","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Piadina%20Romagnola%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Piadina Romagnola"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Milk"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"zuppa-inglese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g farina 00"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip unwaxed lemon zest"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g unsweetened cocoa powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g savoiardi, or day-old pan di Spagna cut in fingers"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml alchermes"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Infuse the milk with the lemon zest at 80C (176F) for 10 minutes, then lift the zest out.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks, sugar and flour to a pale ribbon, temper in the hot milk, and cook it back over moderate heat, stirring into the corners of the pan, until it thickens and then blips once at 85C (185F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide the hot cream, beat the cocoa into one half until it is glossy and free of lumps, and press cling film onto the surface of both so no skin forms.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the alchermes with the water, dip each savoiardo in and out in a single second, and layer the biscuits with alternating pale and chocolate cream in a glass dish, finishing with cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill at least 6 hours, until a spoon lifts a clean edge and the biscuit is moist through with no dry white core.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things break this. A flour-thickened crema that never reaches a blip is still full of live amylase from the yolks, which digests the starch overnight and leaves you soup in the morning, so take it to 85C (185F) and let it bubble once. The second is the dip: savoiardi drink alarmingly fast, and more than a second in the alchermes gives you paste that will not hold a layer.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zuppa%20Inglese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zuppa Inglese"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emilia-Romagna%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emilia-Romagna kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Milk","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"hong-shao-rou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g skin-on pork belly, cut into 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g rock sugar (bing tang), crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml Shaoxing huadiao wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml dark soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 slices ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 spring onions, knotted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 star anise"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 short piece cassia bark"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml just-boiled water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the belly with cold water, bring it up slowly with a slice of ginger and a splash of the wine, and lift the cubes out once the grey scum stops rising; drain and pat every face dry or the sugar will spit at you.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the rock sugar in the oil over a low flame and watch it go from clear syrup to weak tea to the colour of dark honey, about 90 seconds from the first colour; the moment it smells nutty, tip in the pork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss hard until every cube is lacquered mahogany and the fat has started to run clear at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Splash the Shaoxing down the hot side of the pot, add both soys, the aromatics, the salt and just enough boiling water to cover, then hold at a bare tremble for 60 minutes until a chopstick passes through the skin with no resistance.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Take the lid off, raise the heat and reduce, spooning the liquid back over the cubes, until it hangs on them in a glossy sheet and the oil runs clear red round the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tang se is caramel, not soy sauce. Rock sugar melts near 185C (365F) and browns, and that browning gives the red plus a faint bitterness that keeps the dish off pure syrup. Past roughly 195C it goes acrid and nothing added later covers it. Work by colour and smell with the pork already at hand: tipping it in is what stops the sugar cooking.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sugar stages & caramel","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","sweet-edged","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hong%20Shao%20Rou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hong Shao Rou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar","Cinnamon","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"dongpo-rou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg skin-on pork belly in one slab"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g spring onions, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ginger, thickly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml Shaoxing huadiao wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml dark soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g rock sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch the slab from cold water for 5 minutes, lift it when the foam stops rising, cool it, then cut 5 cm squares and tie each one in a cross with string so the fat cannot slide off the skin.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build a close raft of whole spring onions and ginger across the base of a clay pot and sit the squares on it skin down, so no pork touches the pot itself.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the wine, both soys and the rock sugar, lid on, and hold at the barest tremble for 90 minutes until a skewer goes through the skin without catching.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Turn every square skin up, lift them into individual bowls with a ladle of the braising liquid, and steam hard for 30 minutes until the fat is translucent amber and the square shivers when you knock the bowl.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reduce what is left in the pot to a syrup that ribbons off the ladle and spoon it over each square.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pot holds wine and sugar and almost no water, so anything resting on bare metal scorches long before the pork is tender: the spring onion and ginger raft is a heat break first and a seasoning second. The finish is steam because a rolling braise batters the square until the fat parts from the skin. Steam renders that fat with nothing moving, so the block keeps its corners.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dongpo%20Rou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dongpo Rou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"braising-and-stewing","term":"Braising & Stewing"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"sheng-jian-bao","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pork shoulder, minced, about 30 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g pork skin aspic (pi dong), diced 5 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml cold ginger and spring onion water"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g spring onion, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water, for steaming"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g white sesame seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the flour, yeast, sugar and warm water into a firm dough, knead it smooth, and rest it 40 minutes only: you want it half risen, still springy, slow to fill a thumb dent.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the soy, wine, salt, pepper and cold ginger water into the pork a spoonful at a time, always turning one way, until the mince is sticky and drags at the spoon; fold in the spring onion and the diced aspic and chill it hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 25 g discs thicker in the middle than at the rim, pleat 25 g of filling shut and pinch the seam properly closed; the seam is a weld, not a decoration.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the buns seam down in a cold oiled pan a thumb apart and fry over medium heat for 3 minutes, until the bases are deep gold and sound hard under a chopstick.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the water, lid on at once, and steam 9 minutes until the pan runs dry and starts hissing again and the tops are matt and dry to the touch; scatter the sesame and spring onion and give the base 1 minute more.","durationSec":540,"handsOnSec":60,"unattendedSec":540,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest them 2 minutes before anyone bites, because the soup inside is at boiling point.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":false}],"note":"The soup is not poured in: it is diced pork skin aspic, which sets hard cold and slumps to liquid near 35C (95F), so it is solid while you pleat and broth by the time the bun is hot. Proof the dough halfway only. A fully risen bun is too weak and open to hold that liquid, so the seam blows, the soup boils off in the pan and you get a dry bun on a burnt base.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sheng%20Jian%20Bao%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sheng Jian Bao"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"doneness-by-touch-sight-and-sound","term":"Doneness by Touch, Sight & Sound"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Flour","Soy sauce","Sesame","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"yangzhou-shizi-tou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pork, two parts belly to one part shoulder"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g water chestnuts, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml cold ginger and spring onion water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg white"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head Chinese cabbage (huang ya cai)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L light pork stock"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chop the fat and the lean separately, then together, until the meat is loose grains the size of pomegranate seeds; a machine smears the fat through the lean and the ball comes out dense and bouncy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the wine, salt, pepper, egg white and starch, then beat in the cold ginger water 20 ml at a time, one direction only, until the meat is glossy and clings to an upturned palm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold through the water chestnuts and shape four balls of about 170 g, slapping each from palm to palm a dozen times until the surface is smooth and no air pockets are left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line the pot with the outer cabbage leaves, lower the balls onto them and pour in stock to cover; bring it to 85C (185F) and hold it there, covered, for 90 minutes, never letting the surface do more than shiver.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the cabbage hearts in for the last 20 minutes and serve everything in the broth, which should still be clear enough to read the pattern on the bowl.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"Salt plus beating in one direction pulls myosin out of the lean, and that protein sets into a net around 60C (140F) that holds both the water you beat in and the fat as it renders. So keep the pot at a shiver. At a true boil the fat leaves faster than the net can trap it and the ball turns from silky to crumbly and grey. Nothing here is browned; it is meant to stay pale.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yangzhou%20Shizi%20Tou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yangzhou Shizi Tou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Potato","Cabbage","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"songshu-guiyu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 mandarin fish (gui yu), about 700 g, scaled and gutted"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 L neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml Zhenjiang black vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g pine nuts, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g green peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g bamboo shoot, finely diced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Take the head off in one cut and keep it, then run the knife along the backbone to lift two fillets, leaving them joined at the tail with the skin on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay each fillet skin down and score it in 1 cm diamonds at 45 degrees, cutting through the flesh to the skin but never into it; salt, wine, and rest 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blot the fillets dry and rub starch down into every cut with your fingers, then shake them hard so the squares fall open and none stay glued shut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold the tail and lower a fillet into 180C (350F) oil so the squares spring open into spikes, let go and fry 4 minutes until rigid and pale gold, then lift and return it for 40 seconds at 200C (390F) until it rings when tapped.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar, vinegar, tomato paste, soy and water, thicken with a starch slurry until it ribbons off the ladle and coats the back of it, stir in the peas, bamboo and pine nuts and pour it over at the table for the hiss.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cut to the skin and never through it. Skin contracts hard in the oil while the scored blocks do not, and that difference is what stands the squares up. Cut through and the fillet shreds as it fries; stop short of the skin and it comes out flat. Starch has to reach the bottom of every cut, or the wet faces weld back together and the fish fries as a plank.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Scoring","Filleting fish","Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Songshu%20Guiyu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Songshu Guiyu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"peas-favas-and-the-green-spring","term":"Peas, Favas & the Green Spring"}],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Potato","Peas","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Wine","Tomato paste","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["scoring","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"zui-ji","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 free range chicken, about 1.3 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g salt, for the bird"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 spring onions, knotted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml Shaoxing huadiao wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g salt, for the brine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g goji berries"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the bird inside and out with the 25 g of salt and leave it uncovered in the fridge for 4 hours, until the skin feels dry and tacky rather than wet.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide it into water held at 85C (185F) with the ginger and spring onion, keep it there 25 minutes, then kill the heat and steep 25 more, until juice from the thigh joint runs clear and the meat at the bone is just set.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Plunge the bird into iced water for 10 minutes until the skin is taut and slippery, then joint it into large pieces, bone in.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool 500 ml of the poaching liquid to fridge cold, stir in the wine, brine salt, rock sugar and goji, and taste it: it should read a shade too salty on its own.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Submerge the pieces, weight them under the surface and refrigerate 24 hours at least; serve cold with the set jelly spooned over.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"The wine goes into cold stock and nothing is ever warmed again. Ethanol denatures surface protein on contact, so a warm brine turns the outside of the meat chalky and grainy, and heat drives off the esters that carry huadiao's smell. Cold, the alcohol travels inward slowly alongside the salt; give it a full day in the fridge and the flavour reaches the bone.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zui%20Ji%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zui Ji"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"berries-the-late-spring-sprint","term":"Berries: The Late-Spring Sprint"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Berries","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"longjing-xia-ren","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g freshwater river shrimp, shelled to about 250 g meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg white"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g Longjing tea leaves, pre-Qingming if you have them"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water at 80C (175F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml neutral oil, for velveting"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the shelled shrimp in salted water until it runs clear, then wring them in a cloth until the surface squeaks; a wet shrimp sheds its coating in the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix them with the salt until tacky, work in the egg white, then the starch, then a spoonful of the oil, and chill 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steep the tea in the 80C (175F) water for exactly 1 minute, keep the opened leaves and 60 ml of the pale liquor, and throw the rest away.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":60,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slip the shrimp into oil at 120C (250F), stirring them apart, and lift them the second they go opaque and curl into a loose C, around 30 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe the wok, splash in the wine, return the shrimp with the leaves and the liquor and toss 15 seconds until everything is glossy and barely wet. No soy, no colour, nothing else.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two clocks run here. The shrimp poach in barely warm oil at 120C (250F) because once they pass about 60C inside they tighten from crisp to rubber within seconds: pull them at a loose C, never a closed ring. The tea is a one minute infusion at 80C (175F); boiling water strips catechins out of the leaf and the whole dish turns brown and bitter instead of green and sweet.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Velveting","Salted water & the float test","Keeping the cooking liquid","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Velveting","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=velveting%20meat%20chinese%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Longjing%20Xia%20Ren%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Longjing Xia Ren"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"velveting-and-marinades","term":"Velveting & Marinades"}],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Eggs","Potato","Wine"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"yan-du-xian","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g salt cured pork belly (xian rou)"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g fresh pork belly or ribs"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g spring bamboo shoots"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g tofu knots (bai ye jie)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, knotted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 L water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the cured pork in cold water for 3 hours with two changes, then boil a slice for a minute and taste it; if it stings, soak it longer, because this is the only salt the pot will get.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanch both meats from cold water, skimming until the grey foam stops rising, then rinse the meat and the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the meats with the ginger, spring onion and wine at a bare tremble for 60 minutes, skimming as you go, then push it to a rolling boil for 20 minutes until the broth turns opaque and milky.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peel the shoots to the pale core, roll cut them and blanch 3 minutes to take the scratch out, then add them with the tofu knots and simmer 20 minutes until the shoots are translucent at the edge and the knots are heavy with broth.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste it before it goes to the table and add nothing but more of its own liquid if it falls short.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing in this pot gets seasoned. The cure in the xian rou is the only salt, and it concentrates as the broth reduces, so a three hour soak with two changes of water is most of the work; an unsoaked piece leaves broth no one can drink. The milky colour is not cream but rendered fat and collagen emulsified by a hard boil, so simmer throughout and it stays thin and clear.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, melting away silken."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yan%20Du%20Xian%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yan Du Xian"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Tofu","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Wine"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"you-men-chun-sun","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g spring bamboo shoots, still in the sheath"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml dark soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml sesame oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Strip the sheaths back to the pale core, cut away the woody base and roll cut into wedges, so every piece carries a slanted face for the glaze to catch on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanch 3 minutes in hard boiling water and drain them well.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry in the oil over a high flame for 5 minutes without crowding the pan, until the cut faces are blistered and gold at the edges.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the wine, both soys, the rock sugar and the water and braise uncovered for 12 minutes, tossing, until the liquid is a dark syrup hanging off the wedges and the pan hisses more than it bubbles.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, sesame oil, and leave it until warm rather than hot: the glaze tightens as it cools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Spring shoots carry oxalic acid, which is what scratches the throat and turns the pan grey, and three minutes in hard boiling water takes it out. The braise runs uncovered because the point is to lose the liquid, not to soften the shoots any further. Stop when the bubbles go slow and glassy: past that the sugar is alone in a dry pan and burns within seconds.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Braising","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","nutty-toasted","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=You%20Men%20Chun%20Sun%20recipe","sub":"Search films of You Men Chun Sun"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Soy sauce","Sesame","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["braising","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"gui-hua-nuomi-ou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 sections lotus root (lian ou), about 800 g, fat with short nodes"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g glutinous rice (nuo mi), soaked 2 hours and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g dark brown sugar slab (pian tang)"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 dried red dates"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dried osmanthus blossom (gui hua)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 L water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut a 2 cm lid off one end of each root and keep it, then rinse the open channels out under the tap.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand the root upright and push the rice into the channels with a chopstick, tapping the root down on the board to settle it, filling to about nine tenths and no further; replace the lids and pin them with cocktail sticks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the roots in a stainless or clay pot, cover with the water, add both sugars and the dates, and simmer covered for 2 hours until a skewer slides in easily and the cut faces have gone deep garnet.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave them to cool in the liquid overnight, then lift them out and slice 5 mm thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reduce the cooking liquid to a loose syrup, take it off the heat, stir in the osmanthus and spoon it over the slices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pack the channels loosely and only to the shoulder: glutinous rice takes up around half its own weight in water and will split the root open if it has nowhere to swell. Cook in stainless or clay, never iron or aluminium, or the root's tannins bind with the metal and it goes slate grey instead of garnet. Osmanthus goes in off the heat, at the end; its aroma boils away to nothing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gui%20Hua%20Nuomi%20Ou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gui Hua Nuomi Ou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jiangnan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jiangnan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"stainless-clad-tri-ply","term":"Stainless Clad (Tri-Ply)"}],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Rice","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"meen-moilee","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g seer fish (neymeen) steaks, 2 cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric powder, plus 1/2 tsp for the gravy"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g shallots (chuvanna ulli), sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, julienned"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green chillies, slit lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thin coconut milk (randam paal)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml thick coconut milk (onnam paal)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g tomato, in 1 cm rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp black pepper, coarsely cracked"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the steaks with 1 tsp turmeric and 1 tsp salt and leave them while you build the base; the surface should go tacky and the flesh firm slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pop the mustard seeds in coconut oil until they jump and turn grey, drop in the curry leaves until they crackle and go glassy, then sweat shallots, ginger, garlic and green chilli until translucent and slumped with no colour anywhere.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the remaining turmeric, pour in the thin coconut milk and simmer uncovered until it reduces by a quarter and lightly coats the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide in the fish in one layer and poach, tilting the pan rather than stirring, until the flesh turns opaque to the bone and a skewer meets no resistance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the tomato rounds on top, pull the pan off the heat, then stir in the thick coconut milk and swirl until the gravy is a uniform pale gold that steams without a single bubble breaking the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with lime juice and cracked pepper and leave to sit five minutes before serving with appam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Onnam paal is an emulsion held by coconut protein, and much above 80C (176F) that protein denatures, the droplets coalesce and the gravy splits into white grains under an oil slick. Randam paal is dilute enough to boil, so it does the reducing and the cooking. The thick milk goes in off the heat and is only warmed through.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","earthy","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Meen%20Moilee%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Meen Moilee"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Milk","Coconut milk","Mustard","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"kottayam-meen-curry-with-kudampuli","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g seer fish (neymeen) steaks, 2 cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 pieces kudampuli (Malabar tamarind), rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp fenugreek seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g shallots, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp Kashmiri chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the kudampuli in 100 ml warm water until the pieces are soft and the water runs amber.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the coconut oil in a manchatti over low heat, pop the mustard and fenugreek until the seeds crackle and the fenugreek turns one shade darker, then add curry leaves, shallots, ginger and garlic and cook until the shallots are soft and smell sweet, with no browning.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pot off the fire, stir in the chilli powder and turmeric so they bloom in the residual heat until the paste smells toasted rather than acrid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return to low heat, add the water plus the kudampuli and its soaking liquid, and simmer until the gravy darkens to brick red and the raw chilli smell has gone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide in the fish, cover and cook without stirring, swirling the pot by its rim, until the flesh flakes at a fork and clear red oil beads at the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take off the heat and leave the pot uncovered to settle for at least an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kudampuli is not tamarind: its acid firms fish protein and keeps the curry sound for days, but more than about six pieces turns it bitter and metallic. The unglazed manchatti holds a bare simmer through its own mass and does not react with that acid the way thin aluminium does. Once the fish is in, never stir; swirl the pot by the rim or the steaks shred into threads.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","earthy","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kottayam%20Meen%20Curry%20with%20Kudampuli%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kottayam Meen Curry with Kudampuli"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Mustard","Tamarind","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"palappam","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g raw white rice (pachari), soaked 4 hours and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cooked white rice, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fresh grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar, plus 1 tsp for the yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml thick coconut milk (onnam paal)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water, plus 60 ml warm water for the yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"coconut oil, for wiping the pan"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grind the soaked rice with the cooked rice, grated coconut and 200 ml water until the batter is smooth between finger and thumb with only a faint grit left, like wet silk over sand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yeast and 1 tsp sugar into the warm water until it foams and caps, then stir it through the batter, holding back the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and ferment at 28C to 30C (82F to 86F) until the batter has doubled, domed and smells of sour bread and toddy, six to eight hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knock it down and beat in the remaining sugar, the salt and the thick coconut milk to the consistency of single cream that ribbons off the ladle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour a ladleful into a hot, lightly oiled appachatti, lift the pan and rotate it once so the batter climbs and clings to the wall, then cover and cook until the rim is lace crisp and dry and the centre is a set white sponge. Do not flip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cooked rice is the whole trick: its gelatinised starch gives the batter enough body to cling in a thin film when you swirl the pan, and that film crisps to lace while the yeasted pool at the centre steams into a sponge. Salt and thick coconut milk go in after fermenting, because salt slows the yeast and the milk sours over eight hours. Never flip it; the top sets in trapped steam.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Palappam%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Palappam"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"puttu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g coarse roasted rice flour (puttu podi)"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml water, roughly"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fresh grated coconut"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the salt in the water, then sprinkle it over the flour in stages, raking with your fingertips, until the flour darkens evenly and a squeezed fistful holds its shape but shatters when you poke it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the damp flour between your palms and push it through a coarse sieve so every lump breaks back into loose crumb, then cover and rest it ten minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the puttu kutti with a spoonful of grated coconut, then a loose handful of flour, alternating and never pressing, and finish with coconut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the mould over hard boiling water and steam until a straight plume of steam blows from the top and holds for a full minute, six to eight minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the cylinder out with the handle of a wooden spoon; it should stand up on the plate, then break into hot loose crumbs under a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Puttu is a hydration problem: about 45 percent water to flour weight, added in stages so every grain wets evenly. Squeeze a fistful and it must hold, then shatter under a finger. Wetter than that and the steam cannot climb through, so you get a gluey plug; drier and it collapses to sand. Never pack the mould, because compressed layers block the steam that is meant to cook them.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puttu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Puttu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Flour"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"avial","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g elephant foot yam (chena), in 5 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g raw plantain, in 5 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g ash gourd, in 5 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g carrot, in 5 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g drumstick, in 5 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g yardlong beans, in 5 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fresh grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 green chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 shallots"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g thick sour curd, whisked smooth"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp raw coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs curry leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Layer the vegetables by density with the yam and plantain at the bottom, add turmeric, salt and the water, cover and cook until a fingernail sinks into the yam while the batons still hold square edges and matte colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound or pulse the coconut with green chilli, cumin and shallots to a damp, gritty crumb, stopping well short of a smooth paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the crumb through, shaking the pot by its handles rather than stirring, and cook two minutes more until the raw coconut smell turns sweet and the liquid clings to the batons instead of pooling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pot off the heat, let the steam die back, then beat in the curd so it disappears without a single grain forming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour on the raw coconut oil, tear in the curry leaves, cover and leave five minutes so the oil perfumes the whole pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Avial is bound by ground coconut, not milk, so keep the paste coarse: the fragments hold their own oil and coat the batons instead of dissolving into gravy. Curd goes in off the heat, since its proteins seize into grains above a simmer and the dish turns watery. The finishing coconut oil is never cooked, because its aroma compounds are volatile and heat drives them off in seconds.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","silky","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Avial%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Avial"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Sweet potato","Carrot","Chiles","Plantain","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Cumin","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"cabbage-thoran","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g cabbage, shredded fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fresh grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 green chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 shallots"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp cumin seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp turmeric powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dried red chillies, broken"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sprigs curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the coconut with green chilli, shallot, cumin and turmeric in a stone mortar to a dry, fluffy crumb that just clumps when pressed in the palm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the coconut oil until it shimmers, pop the mustard seeds until they jump and go grey, then add the dried chilli and curry leaves and let them crackle for five seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip in the cabbage and salt and toss over high heat, uncovered and with no water at all, until the shreds slump and turn glossy but still squeak against the teeth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the coconut crumb, spread everything flat and leave it undisturbed for two minutes until the coconut smells toasted and the pan floor is dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Thoran is a dry dish and salt works against that: it pulls water out of the cabbage within a minute, so the pan stays uncovered on high heat and the steam is allowed to leave. Put a lid on and the shreds stew grey and limp. The pounded coconut goes in only at the end, where residual heat toasts it; add it early and its moisture and released fat turn the whole pan into wet paste.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Pounding a curry paste"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","earthy","aromatic","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, an earthy, grounding base, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cabbage%20Thoran%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cabbage Thoran"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Chiles","Cabbage","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Mustard","Cumin","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","pounding-a-curry-paste"]},{"slug":"thalassery-kozhi-biryani","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g kaima rice (jeerakasala), rinsed and drained 20 minutes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg chicken on the bone, in 8 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g shallots, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger and 30 g garlic, ground to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 green chillies, ground with the paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g tomato, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coriander leaf and 15 g mint, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Malabar garam masala (cinnamon, clove, green cardamom, fennel)"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g cashews and 40 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"750 ml boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the cashews and raisins in half the ghee until the nuts are pale gold and the raisins swell, lift them out, then fry two thirds of the shallots in the same fat until crisp and mahogany, pulling them before they go past that.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In that fat with the coconut oil, cook the remaining shallots with the ginger, garlic and chilli paste until the raw smell goes and the oil pulls away at the edge, add tomato and half the herbs and cook to a thick masala, then the chicken, garam masala and salt until the chicken is done and the pan is almost dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In a second pan, fry the drained rice in the rest of the ghee until the grains turn chalky and rattle loose, pour on the boiling water and salt, cover and cook until the water is gone and craters open on the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the rice over the masala, scatter the fried shallots, nuts, raisins, remaining herbs and the lime juice across the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal the pot with a tight lid under a weight, set it on a tawa over the lowest flame and leave it on dum for twenty minutes, until the steam escaping at the rim smells of ghee and cardamom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest sealed for ten minutes, then cut down through the layers with the edge of a spoon rather than stirring.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kaima is a short, fat aromatic grain, not basmati: it drinks about one and a half times its volume and goes to paste if you soak it, so rinse, drain and fry it in ghee to set the surface starch before any water. On dum the heat must come from below through a tawa, or the masala scorches while the top rice is still raw. Seal the pot; the trapped steam is doing all the remaining cooking.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Biryani & dum","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Biryani & dum","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=biryani%20dum%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Thalassery%20Kozhi%20Biryani%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Thalassery Kozhi Biryani"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"toasting-nuts-seeds-grains-and-coconut","term":"Toasting: Nuts, Seeds, Grains & Coconut"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Dried fruit","Mint","Rice","Nuts","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"beef-ularthiyathu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g beef shin or chuck, in 2 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp Kashmiri chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp coriander powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp black pepper, coarsely crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp fennel powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fresh coconut, in thin slivers (thengakothu)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g shallots, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g ginger and 8 garlic cloves, crushed together"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 green chillies, slit"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp garam masala"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the beef with turmeric, chilli, coriander, half the pepper, fennel, salt and the water, covered, until a cube crushes between thumb and finger and only a syrupy liquor is left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the coconut slivers in the coconut oil until they are the colour of weak tea, then lift them out before they go any darker.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In the same oil fry the curry leaves until they blister, then the shallots, ginger, garlic and green chilli until the shallots collapse and brown at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip in the beef with its liquor and stir over medium heat until the liquid disappears, the cubes begin to stick and a dark crust builds on the pan floor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep going, scraping that crust back onto the meat every minute or so, until the cubes are nearly black, glossy and dry, and clear oil beads out at the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, fold back the coconut slivers with the garam masala and the reserved pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ularthu means to stir until dry, and the flavour is entirely fond: as the liquor goes, sugar and protein lacquer onto the pan, and every time you scrape that back onto the cubes they darken a shade further. Stop while it is still saucy and you have a curry instead. The coconut slivers come out before the browning starts, since they scorch bitter inside a minute, and half the pepper goes in off the heat because piperine's aroma boils away.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Searing: the hard crust","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","earthy","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beef%20Ularthiyathu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beef Ularthiyathu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Turmeric","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"nadan-vegetable-ishtu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g waxy potatoes, in 3 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g carrot, in 2 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g French beans, in 4 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g shallots, sliced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g ginger, julienned"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 green chillies, slit"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 cm cassia bark, 4 cloves, 4 green cardamom pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thin coconut milk (randam paal)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml thick coconut milk (onnam paal)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 1/2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the coconut oil over low heat and bruise in the peppercorns, cassia, cloves and cardamom until the pods split and the oil smells of them, letting nothing take colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the shallots, ginger, green chilli and curry leaves and sweat until the shallots are translucent and slumped but still ivory.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the thin coconut milk with the potatoes, carrot and salt and simmer uncovered until a knife tip slides into the potato and the liquid has thickened enough to coat it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the beans and cook until they turn bright green and lose their raw squeak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the pan off the heat, stir in the thick coconut milk and swirl until the ishtu is glossy and ivory and steaming, with no bubble breaking the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"An ishtu is white, so nothing is allowed to brown: the spices go in whole and bruised rather than ground, and the shallots are sweated only to translucent. The body comes from potato starch leaching into the thin milk as it reduces, which is why the pot stays uncovered and no flour is needed. Onnam paal goes in off the heat; boil it and the emulsion splits into oil above and white grains below.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nadan%20Vegetable%20Ishtu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nadan Vegetable Ishtu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Shallot","Potato","Carrot","Chiles","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Milk","Coconut milk","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"ada-pradhaman","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g rice ada"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g dark jaggery (sharkara), chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml thin coconut milk (second and third pressings)"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml thick coconut milk (first pressing)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fresh coconut, in thin chips"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g cashews"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp green cardamom, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp dried ginger (chukku), ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pinch cumin, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pinch salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour boiling water over the ada and leave it until the pieces are pliable and fall apart from each other, then drain and rinse under cold water so they stop sticking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the jaggery with the water, strain the syrup through a sieve to leave the grit behind, then reduce it until it just threads off the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the coconut chips, cashews and raisins in the ghee until the chips are amber and the raisins puff, and lift them out, leaving the ghee in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the drained ada in that ghee for two minutes, add the jaggery syrup and the thin coconut milk, and simmer, stirring often, until the ada turns translucent and the payasam coats a spoon and darkens to old bronze.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, stir in the thick coconut milk, cardamom, dried ginger, cumin and salt, then the fried coconut and nuts; it should still pour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coconut is milked three times: the first pressing is thick and fragile, the second and third are thin and take a hard boil. Jaggery carries acid, and acid plus heat curdles that first pressing on contact, so the ada is cooked and the syrup reduced entirely in thin milk. Onnam paal goes in with the pan off the fire. Serve it looser than you want it, because the ada starch keeps setting as it cools.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","earthy","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ada%20Pradhaman%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ada Pradhaman"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kerala%20%26%20the%20Malabar%20Coast%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kerala & the Malabar Coast kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Coconut","Dried fruit","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Sugar","Nuts","Cumin","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"lanzhou-lamian","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g high-gluten wheat flour, 12 to 13 percent protein"},{"kind":"item","text":"260 g water at 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g penghui alkaline water, or 3 g food-grade sodium carbonate dissolved in 15 g water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g neutral oil, for coating the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 g beef shin, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g beef leg bones, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3.5 L cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g white radish, in half moons"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g caoguo (black cardamom), cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g huajiao (Sichuan pepper)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fennel seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g cassia bark"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt, for the broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coarse dried chilli flakes, for the la you"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g beef tallow or neutral oil, for the la you"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of coriander and garlic greens, chopped, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the fine salt in the water, mix it into the flour until no dry flour is left, then knead 8 minutes until the dough is cohesive but still tears with a ragged edge rather than a clean one. Cover and rest 30 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Work the penghui solution in over three additions, slapping the dough flat on the bench between each, until it turns slack, faintly grey and glossy and will stretch to a translucent window without splitting. Oil it, cover, and rest 2 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile cover bones and shin with cold water, bring to the boil and skim until the scum runs thin and white, then add ginger, radish and spices and hold at a bare tremble for 3 hours, until the shin gives to a skewer with no spring back.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the tallow to 140C (285F) and pour it over the chilli flakes in three goes, stirring; it should foam and smell toasted, never acrid, then settle to a deep red oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll a piece of dough into a rope, hold both ends, pull it and slap the belly of it against the bench, then twist, fold in half and repeat for 6 to 8 rounds, dusting with flour, until the strands hang even and ring faintly against the wood.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the noodles into hard boiling water for 40 to 60 seconds, until they float and the chalky core is gone, then lift into bowls, ladle over broth strained clear, and spoon the la you on so it beads red across the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Alkali is what makes the pull possible: sodium carbonate raises the dough's pH, which loosens the gluten network so it yields under tension instead of snapping back. Overdo it and the dough goes yellow, soapy and slack, tearing on the third fold. Underdo it, or cut the two hour rest short, and the rope recoils and breaks. Slapping the bench is not theatre; the impact stretches the strands faster than they can recover.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Hand-pulled noodles","Kneading dough","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Hand-pulled noodles","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=hand%20pulled%20noodles%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lanzhou%20Lamian%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lanzhou Lamian"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Radish/Turnip","Flour","Stock/Broth","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"shanxi-dao-xiao-mian","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain wheat flour, about 11 percent protein"},{"kind":"item","text":"225 g water at 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g ripe tomatoes, scored, peeled and roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs (about 220 g), beaten with 2 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ginger, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 spring onions, whites sliced and greens reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g Shanxi laochencu (aged black vinegar)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g green bok choy, cut in lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g chilli oil, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the salt and water into the flour and knead hard for 10 minutes; this dough fights back. Stop when it is dense and smooth and barely takes a fingerprint, like modelling clay. Wrap and rest 40 minutes, then shape into a fat loaf and rest 20 minutes more.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the eggs into hot oil, let them puff and set at the edges, break them into large curds and lift them out while still glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry ginger, garlic and spring onion whites for 30 seconds, add the tomatoes, sugar and salt and cook down 10 minutes, until they collapse to a jam and the oil separates and runs orange at the edge. Return the egg, kill the heat and stir in the vinegar.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold the loaf on your forearm over a pan at a hard rolling boil and shave with a curved blade in long flicks, so each noodle flies in as a leaf, thick along the spine and thin at the wings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil 90 seconds, until the leaves float and the spine loses its chalky centre, adding the bok choy for the last 20 seconds, then drain and fold through the sauce with a spoonful of chilli oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This dough is deliberately underhydrated at about 45 percent, stiff enough to hold a cut edge under the blade. Wetter and the loaf slumps, the knife drags and you shave lumps rather than leaves. Each leaf is thick at the spine and thin at the wing, so one noodle gives two textures, but that only survives if the water is at a hard rolling boil when it lands. Add the aged vinegar off the heat: boiled, its aroma flattens and only sourness is left.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shanxi%20Dao%20Xiao%20Mian%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shanxi Dao Xiao Mian"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Cabbage","Flour","Vinegar","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"lao-beijing-zhajiangmian","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pork belly, hand chopped to 5 mm dice, about 30 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g ganhuangjiang (dry yellow soybean paste)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g tianmianjiang (sweet flour paste)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g water, to slake the pastes"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 spring onions, whites minced and greens sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 star anise"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g fresh thick wheat noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber, cut in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g mung bean sprouts, blanched 20 seconds"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g shelled young soybeans, boiled 8 minutes"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g white radish, cut in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, peeled, to eat alongside"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slake the pastes: stir the ganhuangjiang and tianmianjiang with the water until smooth and pourable, about the body of double cream, then leave them to sit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the pork belly in a cold wok over medium heat for 8 minutes, until the fat has run clear and the dice are browned and crisping at the edges.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add ginger, spring onion whites, star anise and wine, then pour in the slaked paste and the rest of the oil and fry over low heat, stirring almost constantly, for 20 to 25 minutes, until the paste darkens to the colour of wet earth and the oil breaks out and pools dark on top. Stir in the sugar.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the noodles until just past the chalk stage, then either serve them straight from the pot or, in summer, drop them into cold water and drain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the raw vegetables around each bowl, spoon the sauce into the middle and let each person toss their own, eating with a raw garlic clove bitten between mouthfuls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sauce is fried, not simmered. Raw huangjiang is harsh and aggressively salty; twenty minutes over low heat in generous oil drives off its water so the sugars and amino acids can brown, and the endpoint is visible when the oil breaks out and pools dark on the surface. Rush it over high heat and the paste scorches bitter before that water is gone. Slake the paste with water first so it never meets a dry hot pan, and keep the spoon moving.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Blanching & shocking","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lao%20Beijing%20Zhajiangmian%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lao Beijing Zhajiangmian"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"star-anise","term":"Star Anise"},{"slug":"pork-belly","term":"Pork Belly"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Cucumber","Radish/Turnip","Bean sprouts","Noodles","Flour","Wine","Sugar","Clove","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"ziran-yangrou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g lamb leg or shoulder, sliced 3 mm across the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g lamb fat, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g whole cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g coarse Xinjiang chilli flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, julienned"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 white onion, cut in 2 cm petals"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 spring onions, in 4 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g toasted white sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of coriander, roughly torn"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the lamb with wine, soy, salt and cornflour until every slice is slick and no liquid pools in the bottom of the bowl, then leave it 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the cumin in a dry wok over medium heat for about a minute, until it darkens a shade and smells resinous, then crack it coarsely in a mortar, leaving half the seeds whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the lamb fat in a scorching wok until the pieces are gold and the base is filmed with clear fat, then lay in the lamb in one layer and leave it absolutely still for 45 seconds, until the underside is brown and lifts cleanly off the metal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss in garlic, ginger and onion and stir hard for 90 seconds, until the onion petals are translucent with charred edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Kill the heat, then add cumin, chilli, sesame and spring onion and toss in the residual heat until the chilli turns deep red and fragrant but has not blackened. Finish with coriander off the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two rules, both about heat. The lamb goes in as one layer and stays still for 45 seconds: stir it early and the pan temperature crashes, so the meat weeps and greys instead of browning. The cumin and chilli go in with the heat off, because cuminaldehyde and the chilli's aromatic oils are volatile and scorch above about 180C (355F), turning from red and resinous to black and ashy within seconds. Residual pan heat is all they need.","equipment":["Wok","Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Mashing & puréeing","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","nutty-toasted","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ziran%20Yangrou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ziran Yangrou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Chiles","Soy sauce","Sesame","Wine","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"suan-cai-bai-rou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g pork belly, skin on, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 spring onions, in lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 star anise"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g suan cai (northeastern fermented napa cabbage)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g sweet potato glass noodles, soaked 30 minutes"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g pork blood sausage, sliced 5 mm (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g furu (fermented bean curd), mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g Chinese sesame paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g jiucai hua (fermented chive flower sauce)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g chilli oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of coriander, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the belly with the water, add ginger, spring onion, star anise and wine, bring it up and then drop the heat so the surface only trembles. Hold it there 60 minutes, until a skewer meets no resistance at the thickest point and the skin has gone translucent.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the belly out, press it under a weight and chill 2 hours, until it is firm enough to slice 2 mm thin without the layers sliding apart. Keep the broth.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the suan cai once, squeeze it hard in both hands and shred it very fine along the grain, then simmer it in the strained broth for 40 minutes, until it turns from squeaky to yielding and the broth smells clean and sour rather than sharp.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2640,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide in the sliced belly, the glass noodles and the blood sausage if using, and simmer 10 minutes more, until the noodles turn clear and the fat on the pork has gone glassy.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk furu, sesame paste, chive flower sauce, garlic and chilli oil into a dipping sauce, scatter coriander over the pot, and let each person dip slice by slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two long stages, neither of which can be hurried. The belly goes into water that only trembles: at a rolling boil the muscle fibres contract and squeeze out their water, so the slices turn dry and collapse on the board. Then the shredded suan cai needs its forty minutes in that broth, long enough for the lactic acid to soften the cabbage's pectin and spread through the fat. Cut it short and you get squeaky cabbage in a broth that is merely sour.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Suan%20Cai%20Bai%20Rou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Suan Cai Bai Rou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"star-anise","term":"Star Anise"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Sausage","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Potato","Sweet potato","Chiles","Cabbage","Chives","Noodles","Tahini","Sesame","Wine","Clove","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"guo-bao-rou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g pork loin, sliced 4 mm thick across the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g neutral oil, to loosen the batter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g clear rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g water, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 spring onions, in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small carrot, in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"a few coriander stems, cut in lengths"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the potato starch into the cold water and leave it 30 minutes, until it settles into a hard white cake under clear water. Pour the water off and work the cake with the 20 g of oil into a batter the texture of thick cream.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the pork with salt and wine, then fold it through the batter until every slice hangs heavy and fully cloaked.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 150C (300F), sliding the slices in one at a time so they cannot weld together, for about 2 minutes, until set and pale with no colour at all. Lift out and rest 3 minutes.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry again at 200C (390F) for 40 seconds, until the crust blisters, goes glassy and rattles against the spider. Drain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil sugar, vinegar, soy and water in a clean wok until it just turns syrupy and coats the back of a spoon, then kill the heat and throw in the ginger, garlic, spring onion, carrot and coriander stems.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the pork and toss for no more than ten seconds, until every piece is lacquered and still audibly crackling, then plate at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Potato starch, not cornflour: its large granules gelatinise into a glassy, brittle shell that shatters, where cornflour goes sandy. Let the slurry settle and use the wet cake, which carries far more starch than a pourable batter ever could. The dish then lives or dies in the last ten seconds, because that crust is porous and drinks sauce. Reduce the sauce to a syrup first, kill the heat, toss briefly and plate. Any longer in the pan and it is soft.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Guo%20Bao%20Rou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Guo Bao Rou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Potato","Carrot","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"rou-jia-mo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg pork belly, skin on, cut in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"spice bag: 2 star anise, 3 g cassia bark, 4 bay leaves, 5 g fennel seed, 3 g huajiao, 2 cracked caoguo, 4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain wheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"165 g water at 30C (86F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chillies, finely minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"a small bunch of coriander, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Start the belly in cold water, bring it to the boil and skim until the scum runs thin and white, then drain and rinse the pieces clean.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return them to fresh water with rock sugar, wine, soy, ginger, salt and the spice bag, and hold at a bare simmer for 2 hours, until a chopstick slides through the skin without pressure and the fat wobbles. Leave the meat to cool in its liquid.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix 100 g of the flour with 65 g of the water and all the yeast into a soft sponge and leave it 60 minutes, until domed and webbed underneath, then knead it into the remaining flour, water and salt to make a firm dough and rest it 20 minutes.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3600,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into six, roll each piece into a long rope, coil the rope flat like a snail and press it out to a 10 cm disc so the layers spiral inside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake dry in a heavy pan over medium heat, 4 minutes a side, until tiger stripe scorch marks appear and the bun sounds hollow, then finish 6 minutes at 200C (390F) so the centre sets.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Chop the hot belly on the board, skin, fat and lean together, with a spoonful of the braising liquid, fold in chilli and coriander, split each bun through the equator without cutting it in two, and pack it while both are still steaming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Baijimo is a half leavened dough: only a third of the flour is yeasted, so the crumb is tender but tight. That matters, because a fully risen, open bun is a sponge; the braising liquid soaks straight through and the bread slumps to wet pulp within a minute of filling. Dry pan first, oven second, sets a crisp skin that holds. Chop the meat rather than slice it, so skin, fat and lean land in every bite.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rou%20Jia%20Mo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rou Jia Mo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fennel-anise-and-the-licorice-line","term":"Fennel, Anise & the Licorice Line"},{"slug":"braising-and-stewing","term":"Braising & Stewing"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ginger","Chiles","Bay leaf","Flour","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"cong-shao-hai-shen","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 g dried sea cucumber (6 medium), rehydrated to about 600 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g dacong (large Shandong scallion), white parts only, in 6 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml clear unsalted chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g oyster sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g dark soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g potato starch slaked in 20 g water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the dried sea cucumbers in cold water for 3 days in a scrupulously clean bowl, changing the water twice daily and giving them a 40 minute simmer on the second day. They are ready when they have swollen to four times their dry size and a finger sinks into the flank with a slight spring.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":38400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the dacong batons slowly in the oil over low heat for about 12 minutes, turning them, until they collapse, go deep amber and the oil smells sweet and toasted. Lift the scallions out and keep both them and the oil.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanch the sea cucumbers 30 seconds in a little of the stock with the wine and ginger to drive off any raw sea smell, then drain them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the remaining stock, both soys, oyster sauce, sugar and pepper to a simmer, slide in the sea cucumbers and the fried scallions, and braise gently 8 minutes, until the flesh darkens, turns springy and the liquid has reduced by a third.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pull the pan back off the boil, swirl in the starch slurry until the sauce coats and lifts to a mirror gloss, then finish with sesame oil and the reserved scallion oil so it pools bright across the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The body wall is almost pure collagen, and it will only swell in clean cold water. A trace of oil, salt or acid in the soaking bowl halts uptake, and the cucumber stays shrunk and rubbery however long you leave it, so keep hands, bowl and knife free of grease. At the other end, boiling it hard breaks that collagen down to slime. The braise stays at a tremble, and the endpoint you want is springy, not soft.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Braising","Blanching & shocking","Soaking dried goods back to life","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cong%20Shao%20Hai%20Shen%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cong Shao Hai Shen"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Ginger","Scallion","Potato","Cucumber","Soy sauce","Oyster sauce","Sesame","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["braising","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"zhima-shaobing","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g plain wheat flour, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"240 g water at 35C (95F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g plain wheat flour, for the yousu"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g neutral oil, for the yousu"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g salt, for the yousu"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g ground huajiao (Sichuan pepper)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g five spice powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g maltose, loosened with 40 g hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g white sesame seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the dough ingredients and knead 8 minutes, until smooth and able to stretch to a rough window, then cover and prove 60 minutes until doubled.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":4080,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the 70 g of oil to 150C (300F) and pour it over the yousu flour, salt, huajiao and five spice, stirring to a loose paste with the texture of wet sand that holds a ribbon for a second before sinking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough into a large thin rectangle, spread the yousu edge to edge, roll it into a tight log and cut it into 8, then stand each piece on its cut end, coil it and press it flat so the layers spiral inside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush the tops with the loosened maltose and press them face down into the sesame; the maltose should be tacky rather than runny, or the seeds slide straight off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C (430F) for 15 to 18 minutes, until the sesame is gold, clear seams open along the sides, and the bun feels surprisingly light for its size.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":false}],"note":"The yousu is what makes leaves rather than one loaf. Oil at 150C (300F) poured onto flour cooks the starch and coats each granule in fat, so the dough sheets cannot weld together and steam prises them apart in the oven. Use cold oil, or a paste thin enough to run, and it soaks into the dough and bakes as one dense brick. Maltose for the wash, not honey: it stays tacky enough to grip sesame and browns without scorching.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","fiery","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, real fire that builds and stays, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zhima%20Shaobing%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zhima Shaobing"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chinese-five-spice","term":"Chinese Five-Spice"}],"pantryItems":["Flour","Sesame","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"bingtang-hulu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g fresh hawthorn (shanzha), stems removed and cores pushed out"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g maltose"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g toasted white sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 bamboo skewers"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry every hawthorn thoroughly on a cloth and thread 5 or 6 onto each skewer, all facing the same way. Any surface water left on the fruit will seize the syrup into a cloudy crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put sugar, water and maltose in a wide pan, bring it to the boil and then leave the spoon out of it entirely, washing stray crystals down the sides with a wet brush.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook to 150C (300F): the syrup goes still, the bubbles turn small and slow, and a drop into cold water sets to a shard that snaps cleanly rather than bending.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tilt the pan, roll each skewer through the syrup in a single unbroken pass and lay it on oiled parchment, scattering sesame while the coat is still wet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave 5 minutes, until the glass has gone clear and rings against a fingernail.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Sugar wants to crystallise, and one stray crystal or a stir of the spoon will seed the whole pan cloudy and grainy. So it goes on the heat and is left alone, with the maltose there to interrupt the crystal lattice. Take it to 150C (300F): below about 145C (295F) the coat stays tacky and pulls at the teeth instead of shattering. Dry the fruit properly, because surface water chills the syrup where it lands and leaves a dull, sticky patch.","equipment":["Parchment","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bingtang%20Hulu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bingtang Hulu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"mole-negro-oaxaqueno","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.6 kg turkey legs and thighs, jointed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 L turkey or chicken stock, hot"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g chilhuacle negro, stemmed and seeded, seeds reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g chile pasilla mexicano, stemmed and seeded, seeds reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g chile mulato, stemmed and seeded, seeds reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g chile guajillo, stemmed and seeded, seeds reserved"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g almonds, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g raw peanuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g sesame seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 very ripe plátano macho (200 g), peeled and sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g pan de yema and 1 stale corn tortilla, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe tomatoes and 250 g miltomate, husked"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g white onion in wedges, 40 g garlic cloves, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 stick canela, 5 cloves, 6 allspice berries, 10 black peppercorns, 3 g oregano de monte"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 dried hoja de aguacate"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g chocolate de metate, 40 g piloncillo, fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the four chillies on a dry comal, pressing them flat, until they blister and go almost black, about a minute a side; open a window first, they will smoke.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Burn the reserved seeds in the same dry pan, stirring, until they are true charcoal, then tip seeds and chillies into hot water, soak 30 minutes and drain, throwing away every drop of that black water.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the almonds, peanuts, sesame, raisins, plantain, bread and tortilla in the lard in that order, lifting each out as it turns gold, then char the onion, garlic, tomatoes and miltomate on the comal until blistered black in patches and slumping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the drained chillies with a little stock and sieve them until no grit stays in the mesh, then blend and sieve the fried solids and charred vegetables with the spices and avocado leaves.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reheat the lard until it shimmers, pour in both pastes and fry, stirring, 20-25 minutes, until the mole darkens, holds a channel behind the spoon and black fat runs at the edge.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thin with hot stock to the body of pouring cream, melt in the chocolate and piloncillo, salt it, and simmer the turkey in it for 30 minutes until the surface goes glossy and a slick of fat sits on top.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The black is burnt carbon, and carbon is bitter, so the seeds go all the way to charcoal and are then soaked and drained: the acrid phenols are water soluble and leave with the black water, while the colour and the smoke stay in the solids. Keep that soaking water to save time and the pot turns harsh, and no amount of chocolate pulls it back.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Mole","Tortillas on the comal","Charring over open flame","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","smoky","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, wood-smoke running underneath, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Mole","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20mole%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mole%20Negro%20Oaxaque%C3%B1o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mole Negro Oaxaqueño"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Turkey","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Corn","Berries","Dried fruit","Oregano","Bread","Peanut","Sesame","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Nuts","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"mole-coloradito","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120 g chile ancho, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g chile guajillo, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g chilcostle, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g white onion, in thick wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g garlic cloves, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ripe plátano macho (180 g), sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g sesame seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 stick canela"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g dried Mexican oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g chocolate de metate"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L chicken stock, hot"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g piloncillo and fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the chillies on a dry comal 15-20 seconds a side until they puff, smell like toasted bread and lighten a shade inside, then soak in hot water 20 minutes until limp enough to tear with a fingernail.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Char the tomatoes, onion and garlic on the same comal until the skins blister black in patches and the tomatoes collapse and weep.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the plantain, almonds and sesame in 40 g of the lard until deep gold, then blend the lot with the drained chillies, spices and half the stock and push it through a sieve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the rest of the lard until a drop of paste hisses hard, add all the paste at once and fry, stirring without stopping, 15-20 minutes: it darkens to brick red, tightens, and clear fat separates and pools at the edge.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Only now thin with the remaining hot stock, melt in the chocolate and piloncillo, season, and simmer 20 minutes until it coats a spoon and tastes round rather than raw.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"A mole is fried before it is thinned, and that is not ceremony. In hot fat the paste reaches temperatures water cannot: sugars and proteins brown, the raw starch of nut and plantain cooks out, and the fat soluble pigments bloom red. Thin it first and it can only stew, staying thin, sour and gritty however long you give it. Fry until the fat pools, then add liquid.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mole","Reducing a sauce","Tortillas on the comal","Charring over open flame","Straining & passing through a sieve","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mole%20Coloradito%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mole Coloradito"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Thyme","Oregano","Sesame","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Nuts","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"mole-amarillo-con-chochoyotes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg chicken thighs and drumsticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 L chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g chilcostle, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g chile costeño amarillo, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g chile guajillo, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g miltomate, husked"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g white onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves, 6 black peppercorns, 2 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 large hoja santa leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g chayote, in batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ejotes, topped and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g waxy potatoes, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fresh nixtamal masa, for thickening"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fresh nixtamal masa, for the chochoyotes"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the three chillies on a dry comal 15 seconds a side, until they smell of toast and no darker, soak them in hot water 20 minutes, then blend with the miltomate, onion, garlic and spices and sieve.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the sieved sauce in a hot dry pot for 8 minutes until it darkens, tightens and no longer smells raw, then add the stock and the chicken and simmer until the meat is just firm.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slake the 250 g of masa in 300 ml of cold stock, working it with your fingers until not one lump is left, and pour it into the pot through a sieve while stirring; it thickens in 3-4 minutes and then needs a gentle boil for 5 more to lose its chalky edge.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the lard and a good pinch of salt into the remaining masa, roll walnut sized balls, press a deep dimple into each with your little finger and drop them in; they are ready when they float and the dimple no longer looks wet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chayote, beans and potato, tuck in the torn hoja santa, and cook 12 minutes until a knife slides into the chayote and the pot smells of aniseed.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Amarillo is thickened with masa, not fat, so the masa is slaked in cold liquid first: tipped straight into hot broth the outside of every grain gelatinises at once and seals a raw core, which is why lumpy mole never smooths out again. Once in, it wants a gentle boil for five minutes. Pulled off early it tastes chalky and slackens as it stands.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mole","Working with masa","Tortillas on the comal","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mole%20Amarillo%20con%20Chochoyotes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mole Amarillo con Chochoyotes"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Chiles","Corn","Cornmeal/Polenta","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","tortillas-on-the-comal"]},{"slug":"tlayuda-con-asiento-y-tasajo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 tlayudas, 30 cm dried nixtamal tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g asiento, the unrefined lard from the bottom of the rendering pot"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g cooked frijoles negros, with a little of their liquor"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 dried hoja de aguacate"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g quesillo, pulled into threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g tasajo, beef leg cut in a 4 mm sheet and salted"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cabbage, shredded fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g tomatoes, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 avocado, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g white onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g salsa de chile pasilla oaxaqueño"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime, in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the avocado leaves on the comal until brittle and smelling of aniseed, crumble them into the beans, and fry the beans down in 20 g of the asiento for 8 minutes to a paste stiff enough to hold a line.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill the tasajo over hot charcoal: cut that thin it takes 40 seconds a side and is ruined at 90, so pull it while the centre is still juicy, then chop it coarse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm a tlayuda on the comal until it turns pliable, smear asiento right to the rim, spread the beans and scatter the quesillo.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold it over, lay it on the grill well back from the flame and turn it every 30 seconds for 3-4 minutes, until it is rigid, blistered and cracks under a press of the thumb, and the cheese pulls in threads.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Open it up, load in the cabbage, tomato, avocado, onion and tasajo, and eat with salsa and lime while the base is still crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A tlayuda is not just a big tortilla: it is cooked twice and left leathery and dry, and that low moisture is what lets charcoal turn it to glass instead of steaming it floppy. The asiento seals the surface against wet toppings. Keep it back from the flame, because hard direct heat tightens the aligned protein of the quesillo and weeps its fat out greasy instead of stringing.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","smoky","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, wood-smoke running underneath, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tlayuda%20con%20Asiento%20y%20Tasajo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tlayuda con Asiento y Tasajo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"schmaltz-and-rendering","term":"Schmaltz & Rendering"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Cabbage","Avocado","Lime","Bread"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"memelas-con-asiento","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g fresh nixtamal masa"},{"kind":"item","text":"warm water, to slacken"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g asiento"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g frijoles negros refritos, stiff"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dried hoja de aguacate"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g quesillo, pulled into threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g salsa de chile de árbol or salsa verde de miltomate"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g white onion, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the masa with a splash of warm water until a ball flattened between your palms holds a smooth edge with no cracks, then divide it into 60 g pieces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat each piece into a disc 10 cm across and 7 mm thick, twice the thickness of a tortilla, and lay it on a comal at about 200C (390F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 90 seconds a side until the surface goes dry and matt and pale brown freckles come up, then lift it and, while it is still too hot to hold comfortably, pinch a wall up around the rim with wet fingertips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smear the hot inside with asiento until it soaks in and the masa looks translucent, then spread the beans with the crumbled avocado leaf worked through them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Back on the comal, scatter the quesillo, cover for a minute until it strings, and finish with salsa and raw onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The rim is pinched inside a narrow window. Straight off the comal the masa starch has gelatinised enough to hold a shape but has not yet dried, so it folds like clay; wait two minutes and the same rim splits and shales away. Wet fingertips stop it sticking so you can work fast. Roll the base too thin and the wall tears through and the asiento runs out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Working with masa","Tortillas on the comal"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Memelas%20con%20Asiento%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Memelas con Asiento"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chile-fluency-fresh-and-dried","term":"Chile Fluency (Fresh & Dried)"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Chiles","Corn","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","tortillas-on-the-comal"]},{"slug":"chileajo-de-verduras","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"80 g chile guajillo, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g chile ancho, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g chile costeño rojo, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g garlic cloves, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g dried Mexican oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g carrots, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g chayote, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g green beans, in 2 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g cauliflower, in small florets"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"tostadas and crumbled queso fresco, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the chillies on a dry comal 10-15 seconds a side, pressing them down: they should darken half a shade and smell like toasted bread, and any that smoke or blacken go in the bin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover them with hot water, soak 20 minutes until floppy, then blend with the garlic, spices, vinegar and 150 ml of the soaking water until smooth and push through a sieve.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the sauce in the oil over medium heat 8-10 minutes, stirring, until it darkens, thickens and red oil rises at the edge; salt it hard, there is a lot of vegetable to season.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the vegetables separately in salted water, lifting each one the moment a knife tip meets slight resistance, and spread them on a tray to cool fast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the cold vegetables through the warm sauce, rest at least two hours, and pile onto tostadas with queso fresco.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Toasting is for aroma, not colour. A few seconds of dry heat volatilises the chilli aromatics and makes the brittle skin blend smooth, but the sugars scorch fast and scorched chilli is permanently acrid, so one blackened guajillo taints the batch. The soak then rehydrates the flesh so a blender can break it down, and the vinegar keeps the finished chileajo a week.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","warm-spiced","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, warm spice humming through the middle, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chileajo%20de%20Verduras%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chileajo de Verduras"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"queso-fresco","term":"Queso Fresco"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Carrot","Chiles","Cauliflower","Peas","Green beans","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"caldo-de-piedra","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 dense dry river stones, fist sized, granite or basalt"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g mojarra or other firm river fish, filleted and cut in 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g raw prawns, shelled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L cold water or light fish broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g ripe tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g white onion, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 chiles de agua, or 4 serranos, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 sprigs epazote"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small bunch coriander, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 limes"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Build a wood fire and bury the stones in the embers, bringing them up slowly over an hour or more until they glow dull red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"While they heat, divide the raw fish, prawns, tomato, onion, garlic, chilli, epazote and coriander between four deep jícaras or thick clay bowls and season them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour 300 ml of the cold water into each bowl so the solids are just covered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift a stone with tongs, knock the ash off and lower it in: the bowl erupts and boils hard for 30-40 seconds, then falls back to a rolling simmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the stone out when the boiling stops, check the fish has turned opaque and flakes under a fork, and serve with lime.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A kilo of stone at red heat carries enough stored energy to bring a bowl of cold broth to a violent boil in seconds, and because that heat enters at one point the liquid churns and cooks the fish through in under a minute, which is why it stays flaky instead of chalky. Use dense dry river stone and heat it slowly: porous or damp stone traps water that flashes to steam and spalls the rock apart.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Churning ice cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ice%20cream%20churning%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caldo%20de%20Piedra%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Caldo de Piedra"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"chapulines-al-ajo-y-limon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g chapulines, small grade, cleaned"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g garlic, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g lard or neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g chile de árbol or chile piquín, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"warm tortillas, guacamole and quesillo, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pick the chapulines over for stray legs and grit, and dry them in a low oven at 90C (195F) for 10 minutes if they feel at all soft.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Set a comal or heavy pan over medium heat with the lard, add the garlic and cook until it is pale gold and stops bubbling hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip in the chapulines and toast, shaking the pan constantly, 4-6 minutes: they turn from dull grey brown to brick red and begin to rattle against the metal like dry rice.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, dust with the ground chilli and salt, squeeze the lime over and toss twice, then eat them at once in a warm tortilla with guacamole and quesillo.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The colour reads the moisture: as the shell dries it shifts from grey brown to brick red and the sound changes to a dry rattle. That crispness is the whole texture and it is fragile. Lime juice is water, so it goes on off the heat and at the last second; add it while the pan is hot and the shells drink it straight back and go to leather within a minute.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chapulines%20al%20Ajo%20y%20Lim%C3%B3n%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chapulines al Ajo y Limón"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Bread"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal"]},{"slug":"tejate","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g fresh nixtamal masa, from white corn"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g fermented cacao beans, lightly toasted and husked"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g pixtle, the pits of mamey sapote, toasted and cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dried rosita de cacao flowers"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 L cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"sugar syrup, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the pixtle on the comal until the pieces are brittle and smell of bitter almond, and give the rosita only a few seconds, until it releases its perfume.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the pixtle, rosita and cacao together on a metate or in a mill, in stages, to a dark stiff paste that smears like oiled putty, then grind the masa into it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the paste in a wide bowl and work in cold water a ladle at a time with the flat of your hand, kneading and folding rather than stirring, until it slackens to a batter with no grain left in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the rest of the cold water the same way, lifting your hand clear on each stroke to drag air down; after 10-15 minutes a thick pale foam builds and rises to the top.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle the liquid into cups, spoon the foam over, and let each drinker sweeten with syrup at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cap is not froth. It is cocoa butter and pixtle fat, ground fine, kept solid by the cold water and coated in masa starch so it traps the air your hand drags down. That is why tejate is beaten cold and never warmed: warm water melts the fat, the bubbles collapse, and what rises is a grey slick instead of a cap you could stand a spoon in.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Working with masa","Tortillas on the comal","Fermentation","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tejate%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tejate"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Corn","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","tortillas-on-the-comal"]},{"slug":"nicuatole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fresh nixtamal masa, from white corn"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"220 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 stick canela"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the canela in 400 ml of the water for 10 minutes, until the liquid is fragrant and faintly amber, then lift the stick out.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slake the masa in the remaining 800 ml of cold water, rubbing it between your fingers until the liquid is uniformly milky, then press it through a fine sieve and discard the bran left in the mesh.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine both liquids with the sugar and salt in a heavy pan and bring to the boil over medium heat, dragging a wooden spoon across the bottom the whole time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold it at a lazy boil 8-10 minutes: it thickens abruptly, the spoon leaves a channel that holds for a second, and the surface turns glossy and stops tasting chalky.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour it hot into a wetted shallow mould and leave it uncovered at room temperature for about 2 hours, until it is set firm enough to cut into clean squares.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nicuatole sets on maize starch alone. The grains have to reach a full boil and stay there several minutes to swell and burst, or the gel is weak and the block weeps; it firms as it cools and the loose starch chains re-link. Slake the masa cold and sieve it, because tipped into hot water it lumps on contact. Do not chill it hard or freeze it: the gel goes grainy and pushes its water out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Working with masa","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nicuatole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nicuatole"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oaxacan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Oaxacan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Corn","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"dal-makhani","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g sabut urad (whole black gram), picked over for stones"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g rajma (red kidney beans)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 litres water, plus 2 litres for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, half pounded to a paste and half cut in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chillies, slit lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g ripe tomatoes, blended smooth and passed through a sieve"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g deghi mirch, for colour rather than heat"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g unsalted butter, cubed and kept cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g garam masala, ground within the week"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g kasoori methi, crushed between the palms"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the urad and rajma until the water runs clear, then soak both in 2 litres of cold water for at least 8 hours: the urad is ready when a bean crushes to a smooth paste between finger and thumb.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, cover with the fresh water, add the ginger paste, garlic, chillies and 8 g of the salt, boil hard for 10 minutes, then drop the flame until the surface only trembles and hold it there 4 to 5 hours, topping up, until the skins have collapsed and the liquid turns glossy and clings to the spoon.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":18600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the sieved tomato and the deghi mirch and cook uncovered another 45 minutes, dragging a spoon across the base every few minutes, until the raw tomato smell is gone and the colour has dulled from orange to a deep maroon.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pot off the heat and beat in the cold butter one cube at a time, waiting until each disappears before the next goes in so it never pools, then the cream: the dal should be the colour of milky coffee and fall from the spoon in one thick sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crush the kasoori methi over the surface, add the garam masala, the ginger julienne and the remaining salt to taste, and rest it 20 minutes off the heat before serving.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Urad starch has to leach out and gelatinise before the dal will body up, and that only happens under a simmer, over hours. Boil it hard and the beans burst before the starch disperses, leaving grit suspended in thin water. The butter goes in cold and off the heat: dropped into a boiling dal it melts faster than it can emulsify and the fat floats off as a slick.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dal%20Makhani%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dal Makhani"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Butter","Cream","Lentils","Kidney/Pinto beans","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"rajma-chawal","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g rajma, Jammu chitra if you can get them"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water, plus 1.5 litres for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g onions, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe tomatoes, blended smooth"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g ginger and 20 g garlic, pounded together to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cumin seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 black cardamom, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g deghi mirch"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g amchur (dried mango powder)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g aged basmati, rinsed until the water runs clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coriander leaves, chopped with their stems"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the rajma 8 hours in cold water, then pressure cook them in the fresh water with the black cardamom and 5 g salt for 25 minutes, until a bean smears against the pan side rather than splitting.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the mustard oil until it smokes and its sharpness burns off, drop the flame, and fry the cumin until the seeds darken one shade and stop sputtering.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onions and cook 18 minutes over medium heat, stirring only when they catch, until they collapse, release the pan and go the colour of an old penny.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1080,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the ginger garlic paste until the raw smell turns sweet, then pull the pan off the flame, add the coriander, deghi mirch and turmeric with a spoonful of water, and set it back on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomato and bhuna for 15 minutes without hurrying it, stirring, until the mass draws away from the sides in one piece and a rim of red oil stands clear at the edge: everything else depends on reaching this point.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tip in the beans with their liquor, mash a ladleful against the pan side, simmer 20 minutes until the gravy coats a spoon, then finish with the garam masala, amchur and coriander and serve over the drained basmati.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bhuna is dehydration, not browning. The water in the onion and tomato must leave before the pan can push past 100C (212F), and only then does the fat separate out carrying the spice with it. That rim of oil is the signal; the clock is not. Add the beans before it happens and the pan never gets hot enough again, so the masala stays raw and sour and the gravy thin and pink.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","warm-spiced","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, warm spice humming through the middle, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rajma%20Chawal%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rajma Chawal"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Mango","Cilantro","Rice","Mustard","Cumin","Coriander seed","Turmeric","Cardamom","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"amritsari-chole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g kabuli chana (chickpeas)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g loose Assam tea, tied in muslin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 black cardamom, a 5 cm piece of cassia, 4 cloves and 2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g ripe tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g ginger, half pounded to a paste and half in julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g anardana (dried pomegranate seeds), ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g amchur"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g deghi mirch"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g kala namak (black salt)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cumin seeds, dry roasted and coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 green chillies, slit, and 1 lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the chana 8 hours in 1.5 litres of cold water with the bicarbonate: they are ready when each has doubled and splits cleanly instead of snapping.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil them in fresh water with the muslin bag of tea, the whole spices and 6 g salt for 45 minutes, until a chana crushes to a smooth paste against the roof of the mouth and the skins have stained near black; lift out the bag and the whole spices.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the onions in the ghee over medium heat for 15 minutes, until they slump, lose their edges and go the colour of an old penny, then add the ginger and garlic paste and cook until the raw garlic smell turns sweet.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Take the pan off the flame before you add the ground coriander, deghi mirch, anardana and amchur, slacken them with a spoonful of the black cooking liquor, and set the pan back on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes and bhuna 12 minutes until the mass pulls from the sides in one piece and dark oil stands at the rim, then fold in the chana with 300 ml of their liquor and simmer 15 minutes until the gravy clings.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1620,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash a ladleful of chana against the side to thicken it, then finish with the kala namak, roasted cumin, ginger julienne, slit chillies and lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bicarbonate raises the water's pH, weakening the pectin between cell walls so the chana turns creamy rather than mealy; go much past 2 g per 300 g and it saponifies the skins into slime with a soapy taste. Whole spices can sit in the boil for 45 minutes and only sweeten. Ground spice has far more surface area and scorches bitter in hot fat, so it goes in off the flame, wetted.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Amritsari%20Chole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Amritsari Chole"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-souring-spices-amchur-loomi-anardana-and-tamarind","term":"The Souring Spices: Amchur, Loomi, Anardana & Tamarind"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Lemon","Pomegranate","Bay leaf","Chickpeas","Cumin","Coriander seed","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"sarson-da-saag","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g sarson (mustard greens), thick stems pulled off"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g palak (spinach)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g bathua (goosefoot greens), or more spinach"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ginger, half chopped and half julienned"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g makki da atta (maize flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g tomato, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g deghi mirch"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g white butter, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the sarson, palak and bathua uncovered in a heavy pot with the chopped ginger, chillies, salt and water for 45 minutes: they are done when a mustard stem crushes to nothing between thumb and finger and the steam smells sweet rather than sharp.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash the greens in the pot with a wooden churner, pounding and turning for 10 minutes, until the leaves have broken down but you can still feel fibre against the tongue.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rain in the makki da atta a little at a time, stirring hard so it cannot clump, and cook 20 minutes more until the saag pulls away from the side of the pot and holds the mark of the spoon.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In a separate pan brown the onion in the ghee, add the garlic, tomato and deghi mirch, and cook until the oil runs red and separates at the edge, then stir two thirds of the tadka through the saag.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the rest of the tadka over the top with the ginger julienne and let the white butter melt into it at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mustard leaf carries sinigrin, which is what makes it bitter and sharp, and only a long uncovered boil drives that off; there is no shortcut and no lid. A blender shears the cells instead of tearing them and whips in air, so the saag goes grey and slimy: use a wooden churner. The makki da atta thickens, but give it 20 minutes or it tastes of raw grain.","equipment":["Ice-cream churner"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tadka: blooming spices in fat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tadka%20tempering%20spices%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Churning ice cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ice%20cream%20churning%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sarson%20da%20Saag%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sarson da Saag"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Spinach","Butter","Flour","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"makki-di-roti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g makki da atta (fine maize flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"260 ml water at 70C (160F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g mooli (white radish), grated and squeezed dry, optional"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g white butter or ghee, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the salt through the flour, then pour in the hot water in three goes, working it with the back of a spoon until the dough is cool enough to touch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead 5 minutes with the heel of the palm, folding in the mooli if using, until the dough is smooth and warm and takes a thumbprint without cracking at the rim; if it cracks, wet the palm and keep going.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take a ball the size of a lime and pat it flat between wet palms, turning it a quarter after every pat, until it is 12 cm across and 5 mm thick with an unbroken edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay it on a tawa hot enough to make a water drop skitter, cook 2 minutes until the underside is freckled brown, turn it, then press the edges down with a folded cloth until it puffs.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish it straight on the flame or coals for 20 seconds a side, until it blisters and smells toasted, then tear it open and load it with white butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Maize has no gluten, so the only thing holding this roti together is starch swollen in water hot enough to gelatinise it, around 70C (160F). Mixed with cold water the dough crumbles at every edge and falls apart on the tawa. Shape one at a time and pat it between wet palms: a rolling pin shears the sheet, and the dough stiffens as it cools.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","nutty-toasted","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Makki%20di%20Roti%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Makki di Roti"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Radish/Turnip","Butter","Flour"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"aloo-paratha","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g atta (stoneground wholemeal chapati flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ghee for the dough, plus 60 g for cooking"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g floury potatoes, boiled in their skins and cooled completely"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chillies, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ajwain (carom seeds), crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g amchur"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cumin seeds, dry roasted and coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g deghi mirch"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coriander leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt for the filling"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the water into the atta with the salt and 15 g ghee for 6 minutes, until the dough takes a finger with no resistance and springs back slowly, then cover and rest it 30 minutes.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2160,"estimated":false},{"text":"Peel the cold potatoes and grate them on the coarse side of a box grater into a wide bowl: they should fall in loose threads, never in clods.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss through the ginger, chillies, ajwain, amchur, roasted cumin, deghi mirch and coriander with fingertips so the threads stay separate, and add the filling salt only when you are ready to stuff.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cup a ball of dough in the palm, load it with its own weight of filling, gather the edges over the top, pinch off the knot and press it flat; roll from the centre outward, turning often, to 18 cm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook it dry on a medium tawa for 1 minute a side until brown freckles appear, then smear ghee on both faces and press with a cloth until it swells and crisps in patches.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":60,"estimated":true}],"note":"The filling has to be dry and cold before it meets the dough. Boil the potatoes in their skins so they take on less water, cool them fully, then grate rather than mash: mashing ruptures the starch cells into a wet glue that steams the seam open. Salt pulls more water out, so season at the moment of stuffing. A tear means warm filling, not thin dough.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aloo%20Paratha%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Aloo Paratha"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Potato","Chiles","Cilantro","Flour","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"paneer-tikka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g fresh paneer, cut in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g full fat dahi, hung in muslin 4 hours to give about 250 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g besan (gram flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger and garlic, pounded together to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml mustard oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g deghi mirch"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g kasoori methi, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ajwain"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt, plus 20 g for the soaking water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green capsicum and 1 onion, cut into petals"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter, melted, for basting"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g chaat masala and 1 lemon, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the paneer 15 minutes in water held at 60C (140F) with the 20 g salt dissolved in it, then lift it out and pat dry: the cubes should now yield slightly under a finger.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Dry roast the besan over a low flame for 3 minutes until it smells of toasted biscuit and loses its raw chalkiness, then whisk it into the hung curd with the ginger garlic, mustard oil, deghi mirch, turmeric, garam masala, kasoori methi, ajwain and salt.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the paneer and the capsicum and onion petals through the marinade, which should hold a soft peak and cloak the cubes without sliding off, and leave it 1 hour and no longer.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Thread them on skewers with a petal between each cube and cook over hot coals, or under a grill at its highest setting, for 8 to 10 minutes, turning, until the marinade sets dry and the edges blacken in spots.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Baste with the melted butter, dust with chaat masala and squeeze the lemon over while they are still hissing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Paneer squeaks when its casein network tightens and wrings out whey, which begins above roughly 70C (160F) and does not reverse. Soak the cubes in salted water at 60C (140F) to relax and rehydrate them, then cook fast and fierce so the outside chars before the centre climbs there. The roasted besan thickens the curd so the marinade grips instead of sliding into the fire.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Paneer%20Tikka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Paneer Tikka"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"chaat-masala","term":"Chaat Masala"},{"slug":"paneer","term":"Paneer"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Bell pepper","Lemon","Butter","Cheese (any)","Flour","Chickpeas","Mustard","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"mutton-seekh-kebab","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g mutton shoulder, trimmed and passed twice through the coarse plate"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g mutton or lamb fat, minced with the second pass"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g onion, chopped very fine and squeezed dry in muslin"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g ginger and garlic, pounded to a paste and squeezed of excess water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 green chillies, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g roasted chana dal, ground to a flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cumin seeds, dry roasted and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g deghi mirch"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper, coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g kasoori methi, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coriander leaves, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ghee, melted, for basting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill the meat and fat to just above freezing, then pass both through the coarse plate a second time so the fat runs evenly through the mince instead of sitting in lumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the bowl over ice and knead the mince with the salt, onion, ginger garlic, chillies, chana dal flour and spices for 8 minutes, until the mass turns pale and tacky and lifts out in one piece that clings to an upturned palm.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest it covered in the fridge 1 hour, then take a handful, press it onto a flat skewer from the middle outward with a wet hand and run a thumb down its length to leave ridges.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hang the skewers over hot coals without letting the meat touch them and leave them still for 4 minutes, until the surface sets matt and the kebab releases when you turn it.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rotate and baste with ghee for 6 minutes more, until the fat drips and hisses in the coals and juices run clear from a ridge.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Salt plus hard kneading pulls myosin out of the meat, and that dissolved protein is the only glue holding a seekh to its skewer. It sets only while cold: much above 12C (54F) the fat smears and coats the protein, the gel never forms and the kebab slumps into the fire. Squeeze the onion dry, keep the bowl over ice, and rest the mix before moulding.","equipment":["Grill","Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Grilling over live coals","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Salting to draw the water out","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","charred","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mutton%20Seekh%20Kebab%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mutton Seekh Kebab"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mutton-and-hogget","term":"Mutton & Hogget"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Cilantro","Flour","Lentils","Cumin","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"kadhi-pakora","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g sour dahi, three days old, whisked smooth"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g besan for the kadhi"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ghee, split between the two tadkas"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g methi dana (fenugreek seeds)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cumin seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g hing (asafoetida)"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 sabut lal mirch (whole dried red chillies)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, sliced, for the first tadka"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger and 15 g garlic, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g besan for the pakoras"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onion, sliced paper thin, for the pakoras"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ajwain and a pinch of bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml mustard oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g deghi mirch for the second tadka"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the dahi, the 120 g besan, turmeric, salt and water together cold until no grit is left on the whisk and it pours like single cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For the first tadka heat 40 g of the ghee, add the methi dana and let them darken one shade only, about 15 seconds, then the cumin, hing, three of the chillies, the sliced onion, ginger and garlic, and cook until the onion is soft and gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the yogurt mixture and stir without once stopping until it reaches a full rolling boil, then leave it uncovered at a lazy simmer for 50 minutes, until it has reduced by a third, gone the colour of old ivory and coats the back of a spoon.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the pakora besan with just enough water to hold a peak, fold in the thin onion, ajwain and bicarbonate, and fry spoonfuls at 170C (340F) for 4 minutes until they are amber and sound hollow when tapped.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the pakoras into the kadhi 10 minutes before serving so they drink it in and go soft through the middle.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the remaining ghee, pull it off the flame, drop in the last chillies and the deghi mirch so they sizzle in the residual heat alone, and pour it over the surface at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yogurt curdles because heat makes its casein clump. Besan is the stabiliser, its starch swelling around the micelles to hold them apart, but only once hydrated, so whisk every lump out cold and stir without pause to the first boil: stop early and the base overheats into grains no whisking recovers. Two tadkas because methi needs the hour to mellow and the second must stay loud.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Salted water & the float test","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tadka: blooming spices in fat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tadka%20tempering%20spices%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kadhi%20Pakora%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kadhi Pakora"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-opening-soft-launch-and-the-first-90-days","term":"The Opening: Soft Launch & the First 90 Days"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Chickpeas","Mustard","Cumin","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"phirni","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 g basmati rice, soaked 30 minutes then drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre full fat milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 green cardamom pods, seeds ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.2 g saffron, bloomed in 1 tbsp warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml kewra water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g pistachios, slivered"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g blanched almonds, slivered"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the rice and pound or pulse it to the feel of fine sand: pinch some between the fingers and you should still make out separate grains, not flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reduce the milk over medium heat for 20 minutes, scraping the skin off the sides back in, until it has lost a quarter of its volume and faintly coats the spoon.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slacken the ground rice with 100 ml of the hot milk, rain it in while whisking, and cook on low for 18 minutes, dragging a spoon across the base without pause, until the grains are soft and a spoon drawn through leaves a clean line.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Only now stir in the sugar, then take the pan off the heat and add the cardamom, saffron milk and kewra water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour it hot into shallow earthenware bowls, scatter the nuts over, and chill at least 4 hours until the top sets firm enough to hold a spoon mark.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Grind the rice coarse, to the feel of sand: milled to flour it sets as paste with nothing to chew. Sugar goes in last because it competes with the starch for water and lifts the temperature at which the grains swell, so added early it leaves the rice gritty however long you cook it. Unglazed earthenware wicks moisture out as it chills and tightens the surface.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","floral","nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Phirni%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Phirni"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Punjabi%20%26%20North%20Indian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Punjabi & North Indian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"toasting-nuts-seeds-grains-and-coconut","term":"Toasting: Nuts, Seeds, Grains & Coconut"}],"pantryItems":["Milk","Rice","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"hongyou-sichuan-chilli-oil","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"70 g dried erjingtiao chillies, stems removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dried zidantou chillies, stems removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml rapeseed oil (caiziyou)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g huajiao, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g white sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, cut in lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g shallot, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 star anise, 4 cm cassia bark, 2 bay leaves and 1 cracked caoguo"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast both chillies in a dry wok over low heat, turning constantly, until they smell of tobacco and snap rather than bend, about 4 minutes; stop while they are still deep red, because past that the oil tastes of ash.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool, then pound coarse: flakes the size of coffee grounds with visible seed, not powder. Mix with the sesame, salt and hot water until the flakes look damp and clump slightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the rapeseed oil to 190C (375F), until its raw green smell burns off and the surface goes flat and shimmering, then fry the ginger, spring onion, shallot and whole spices until the ginger curls and turns biscuit brown; lift them all out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the oil fall to 150C (300F) and pour one third over the flakes: they should roar and foam up the bowl. At 130C (265F) pour the second third. At 110C (230F) drop the huajiao into the pan, swirl 20 seconds, and pour the last third through a strainer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and leave a full day before using; colour keeps bleeding out of the flakes overnight and the raw edge goes with it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chilli colour and chilli aroma live at different temperatures. The red pigment only bleeds out around 150C (300F), while the volatile aromatics scorch above roughly 170C (340F), and once they do the oil is bitter and resting will not fix it. Hence three pours down a temperature ladder. Huajiao goes in last and coolest: its numbing sanshools are fragile and one hot minute boils them off.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hongyou%20(Sichuan%20Chilli%20Oil)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hongyou (Sichuan Chilli Oil)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Shallot","Chiles","Bay leaf","Sesame","Cinnamon","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"shuizhu-niurou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g beef topside or flank, sliced 3 mm thick against the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg white"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml water, for the velveting"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml rapeseed oil, plus 120 ml for the final pour"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g Pixian doubanjiang, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g dried erjingtiao chillies, snipped in 2 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g huajiao, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ginger, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml unsalted beef or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g celery, cut in 6 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g soybean sprouts"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground roasted huajiao, to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves garlic, minced, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the beef with salt, wine and the 15 ml water until every drop is drawn in and the slices feel tacky, then work in egg white and starch and leave 15 minutes; they should look glossed, not gluey.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the erjingtiao and whole huajiao in the 60 ml oil over low heat until the chilli turns dark garnet and smells toasted, about 90 seconds; lift out, chop coarse and reserve for the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In that same oil fry the doubanjiang over medium low heat until the paste loosens, the oil separates and runs bright red at the edge of the pan, about 3 minutes. There is no shortcut past this point.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add ginger and garlic, then the stock and soy; simmer 5 minutes, blanch the celery and sprouts in it until just limp, and lift them into a deep bowl.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off any hard boil and slide the beef into barely trembling stock, poaching 60 to 90 seconds until the slices turn opaque at the edges and curl; pour beef and stock over the vegetables.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the chopped chilli, ground huajiao and raw garlic on top, heat the remaining 120 ml oil to 200C (390F) and pour it over so it crackles and lifts the garlic in one wave.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Shuizhu does not mean boiled. It means the beef finishes in liquid rather than oil, and above a lazy simmer the velveting starch sloughs off and the slices go grey and stringy, so the stock must stop bubbling before the meat goes in. The doubanjiang is fried until the oil separates and runs red because both the colour and the fermented depth sit in the paste's oil phase; stir it straight into liquid and you get muddy brown soup.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Velveting","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","umami-deep","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Velveting","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=velveting%20meat%20chinese%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shuizhu%20Niurou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shuizhu Niurou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"velveting-and-marinades","term":"Velveting & Marinades"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Eggs","Garlic","Ginger","Potato","Celery","Chiles","Soy sauce","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"hui-guo-rou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pork belly, skin on, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g huajiao, whole, for the poaching water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml rapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g Pixian doubanjiang, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g tianmianjiang"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g douchi, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g suan miao (green garlic shoots), cut on the bias in 4 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the belly whole with ginger, spring onion, wine and huajiao at a bare simmer until a chopstick enters the lean against slight resistance, about 30 minutes; cool it in its liquid, then chill hard.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the cold belly across into pieces 2 mm thick and about the size of a large stamp, skin, fat and lean in every slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the slices in the oil over medium heat without crowding until the fat runs out and each piece curls into a small cup with a browned rim: this is the deng zhan wo, the lantern shape, and it is the whole point.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the pork aside, drop the doubanjiang into the rendered fat and fry until the oil runs red, then add tianmianjiang and douchi for 30 seconds, until they smell toasted and sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add soy, sugar and the garlic shoots and toss about 40 seconds, only until the white stems turn translucent and the green tops slump; they must stay squeaky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Twice cooked means twice. The poach sets the meat firm enough to slice thin when cold; the fry renders it. Slice it warm and it tears, slice it thick and it never curls, and the curl is what matters: a flat slice sits in its own fat and stews, while a cupped slice presents a browned rim and gives up its fat for the doubanjiang to fry in. Garlic shoots go in at the very end or they turn to grey rag.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hui%20Guo%20Rou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hui Guo Rou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"yuxiang-qiezi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g Chinese aubergine, cut in batons 8 cm by 2 cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt, for drawing"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml rapeseed oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g pao la jiao (brine pickled Sichuan chillies), chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g Pixian doubanjiang, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g garlic, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g spring onion, white and green, sliced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml Baoning vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g salt, for the sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the aubergine and leave 15 minutes, then blot dry; the salt collapses the sponge so it drinks far less oil.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk sugar, vinegar, soy, the second salt, starch and water into one sauce and stand it beside the wok. Yuxiang is mixed before it is cooked, never corrected in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the aubergine at 180C (355F) for about 2 minutes, until the skin blisters and the flesh goes translucent and slumps; drain on a rack, not paper, so it stays loose.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off all but 30 ml of oil, add pao la jiao and doubanjiang over medium low heat and fry about 2 minutes, until the fat turns clear orange red and the sourness turns fragrant; add ginger and garlic for 20 seconds.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the aubergine, stir the sauce to lift the settled starch, pour it round the rim of the wok and toss 30 seconds until it goes glossy and clings in a thin coat; kill the heat and fold in the spring onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yuxiang has nothing to do with fish. It is the seasoning once used for fish, and its spine is pao la jiao, chillies fermented in brine, not chilli oil. The order is sour first, then sweet, both sharp enough to read against raw garlic. The sauce is premixed because the starch sets in seconds; add sugar and vinegar separately over live heat and the vinegar boils off, leaving a flat sweet gloss.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Brining & curing","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salting to draw the water out","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","umami-deep","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yuxiang%20Qiezi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yuxiang Qiezi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Potato","Chiles","Eggplant","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"gan-bian-si-ji-dou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g green beans, topped and tailed, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml rapeseed oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g coarsely minced pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g Yibin yacai, rinsed and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dried erjingtiao chillies, snipped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g huajiao, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g garlic, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ginger, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the beans absolutely; any surface water turns the oil violent and steams the bean instead of blistering it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 170C (340F) for 3 to 4 minutes, until the skins pucker into fine wrinkles all over and the bright green dulls to olive. A bean that still squeaks is not done: you want it limp and faintly leathery.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In 20 ml of the drained oil, fry the pork over medium heat about 3 minutes, until it stops clumping and the grains turn sandy and dry, then add the yacai and fry until it smells sweet and smoky.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add chilli, huajiao, garlic and ginger and stir just 20 seconds, only until the chilli darkens and the huajiao smells of citrus peel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the beans with wine, soy, sugar and salt and toss over high heat until the pan is dry and nothing pools at the bottom.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Gan bian means dry fried, and the goal is dehydration rather than browning. A green bean is water held inside a waxy skin, and until that water leaves, seasoning sits on the outside and slides straight off; the wrinkled bean has room to absorb. Cook it to bright crisp tender instead and it tastes of raw bean and oil. Huajiao goes in at the end because 20 seconds of heat is already the limit for its numbing compounds.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","umami-deep","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gan%20Bian%20Si%20Ji%20Dou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gan Bian Si Ji Dou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Green beans","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"fuqi-feipian","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g beef shin"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g beef tripe, cleaned"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g beef tongue"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g beef heart"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 spring onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g huajiao, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 star anise, 5 cm cassia bark, 2 bay leaves and 1 cracked caoguo"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml hongyou, with its sediment"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground roasted huajiao"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g roasted peanuts, crushed, and 20 g toasted sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g celery leaf, roughly chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring shin, tripe, tongue and heart up from cold water, skimming the grey scum until the surface runs clear, then rinse each piece and rub the skin off the tongue while it is still too hot to hold comfortably.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build a brine from fresh water, ginger, spring onion, whole huajiao, the spices and salt; simmer the offal 60 to 90 minutes and the shin about 90, pulling each piece the moment a skewer meets no resistance.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn off the heat and let everything cool in the brine to room temperature, then chill. This is the step people skip and the one that decides the texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice every piece cold and across the grain, thin enough to read light through: about 2 mm for shin and tongue, wider ribbons for tripe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress with hongyou and its sediment, soy, sugar and ground huajiao, turning until every slice is lacquered, and scatter peanuts, sesame and celery leaf only as it goes to the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no lung in it. The original fei meant discarded scraps, and the dish is shin, tripe, tongue and heart. Slice the meat warm and the fibres tear and weep, giving grey slices sitting in a pool of red oil. Cooling it in its own brine lets it draw liquid back and set firm, so it cuts clean and the dressing coats each slice instead of pooling underneath.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Resting meat & slicing against the grain","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fuqi%20Feipian%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fuqi Feipian"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Liver & Offal","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Celery","Bay leaf","Soy sauce","Peanut","Sesame","Sugar","Cinnamon","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"guaiwei-ji-si","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g free range chicken, leg and breast on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cucumber, cut in fine batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g zhima jiang (Chinese sesame paste)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml Baoning vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml hongyou, with its sediment"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml roasted sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground roasted huajiao"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g garlic, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g spring onion greens, sliced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g roasted peanuts, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the chicken with ginger, spring onion and wine at 80C (175F), never letting it boil, for about 20 minutes, then drop it straight into iced water until cold through; the skin tightens and the flesh stays juicy.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the sesame paste down with the soy and vinegar a spoonful at a time, stirring smooth after each addition. Add the liquid all at once and the paste seizes into grit that never comes back.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in sugar, salt, garlic, ground huajiao, sesame oil and hongyou, then taste for each flavour in turn, salty, sweet, sour, numbing, hot, fragrant and nutty, correcting until none of them arrives first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tear the cold chicken along the grain into shreds about the thickness of a pencil lead, discarding bone and keeping the skin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bed the cucumber, pile the shreds on it and spoon the sauce over only as it goes out; dressed early, the shreds go slack and the sauce splits.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Guaiwei means strange flavour, and the strangeness is that nothing wins. Sweet, sour, salty, hot, numbing, nutty and fragrant are set at roughly equal weight so the palate cannot settle on what it is eating. The failure mode is the sesame paste: it is oil suspended in solids, and it seizes when liquid hits it all at once. Thin it in stages with the soy and vinegar and it stays a smooth ribbon.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","bright","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Guaiwei%20Ji%20Si%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Guaiwei Ji Si"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cleavers-chinese-chef-vs-bone","term":"Cleavers: Chinese Chef vs. Bone"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Cucumber","Soy sauce","Tahini","Peanut","Sesame","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"zhong-shui-jiao","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g minced pork, about 30 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml ginger and spring onion water, cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml light soy sauce, for the fuzhi jiangyou"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g Chinese slab sugar or brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 star anise, 5 cm cassia bark, 2 bay leaves, 5 g huajiao and 1 cracked caoguo"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, sliced, for the soy"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml hongyou, with its sediment"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g garlic, crushed to a paste with 20 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g toasted sesame seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the soy with sugar, spices and sliced ginger at the lowest possible heat for 20 minutes, until it coats a spoon and reads sweet and liquorice rather than salty, then strain. This is fuzhi jiangyou and it keeps for weeks.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work flour, salt and water into a firm dough, knead 8 minutes until it gives like an earlobe, rest it covered 30 minutes, then roll and cut 8 cm rounds, thin at the rim and a shade thicker at the centre.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the ginger and spring onion water into the pork 20 ml at a time until each addition disappears and the mince turns pale and sticky, then season with wine, salt and white pepper; it should hold a peak on the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold each round into a plain half moon with no pleats, pressing every bubble out as you seal: trapped air bursts the skin in the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil in plenty of water, adding a cup of cold water each time it returns to a rolling boil, three times over, until the dumplings float and the skin goes translucent over the filling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon fuzhi jiangyou into warm bowls, add garlic water and hongyou, lift the drained dumplings in and scatter sesame. The sauce sits underneath, never poured over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"These are dressed, not souped, and the dressing carries no vinegar at all: that absence is what makes them Sichuanese rather than northern. Sweetness comes from fuzhi jiangyou, soy reduced with sugar and spice to a syrup, and it must reduce slowly or the sugar carries past caramel into bitter. Let the raw garlic down in cold water, because hot liquid cooks off the allicin and the sauce loses its edge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","sweet-edged","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zhong%20Shui%20Jiao%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zhong Shui Jiao"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Bay leaf","Flour","Soy sauce","Sesame","Wine","Sugar","Cinnamon","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"chuan-bei-liang-fen","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g wandou dianfen (pea starch)"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml hongyou, with its sediment"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g garlic, crushed to a paste with 30 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground roasted huajiao"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml Baoning vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g spring onion greens, sliced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g toasted sesame seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the starch and salt into 300 ml of the cold water until no lump survives and the slurry pours like single cream; a lump here becomes a hard nugget in the finished block.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the remaining 600 ml to a boil, then pour the slurry in a thin stream, stirring hard and always in one direction. It thickens almost at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop to the lowest heat and keep stirring 6 to 8 minutes, until the paste turns from milky to glassy and pulls away from the pan in a single sheet. Stop before that and the centre stays chalky and tastes of raw starch.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape into an oiled tray, level the top, cool uncovered to room temperature and then chill about 2 hours, until it cuts without sagging.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into thick ribbons and dress at the table with garlic water, soy, vinegar, sugar, ground huajiao and a generous flood of hongyou.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The block is a starch gel and it needs two things: enough starch to water, roughly one to six by weight, and long enough cooking to burst every granule. Undercook it and it never fully gelatinises, so it sets soft and weeps on the plate. Dress it only at the table, because salt and vinegar draw water straight back out of the gel; let it sit ten minutes and you get diluted sauce and a slippery, flavourless block.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","nutty-toasted","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chuan%20Bei%20Liang%20Fen%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chuan Bei Liang Fen"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Soy sauce","Sesame","Vinegar","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"yibin-ranmian","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g thin fresh alkaline wheat noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g minced pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g Yibin yacai, rinsed and chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml rapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml hongyou, with its sediment"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml roasted sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground roasted huajiao"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g roasted peanuts, crushed coarse"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g walnuts, toasted and crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g spring onion greens, sliced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, crushed to a paste with 20 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the pork in the rapeseed oil over medium heat until the grains separate and crisp at the edges, add the wine and yacai, and keep going until the pan is dry and the yacai smells sweet and smoky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the noodles in a lot of water for 60 to 90 seconds, until they bend without snapping but still show a thin white line at the core.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain hard, spread them on a tray and toss with the sesame oil until every strand is separate and no water beads on the surface. This step is what the name is about.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip into a warm bowl with soy, garlic water, hongyou, ground huajiao and salt, and turn from the bottom until the strands go glossy red and nothing pools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with the pork and yacai, peanuts, walnuts and spring onion, and eat at once, while the noodles are still hot enough to carry the aroma.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ran mian means burning noodles: burning because a strand this dry and this oily will take a flame, not because it is hot. Everything depends on drying the noodle. A wet strand dilutes the dressing, which then runs to the bottom of the bowl and leaves the top half naked. Drain, spread and coat with sesame oil before any seasoning goes near it.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","earthy","aromatic","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, an earthy, grounding base, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yibin%20Ranmian%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yibin Ranmian"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Noodles","Soy sauce","Peanut","Sesame","Wine","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"pasta-con-le-sarde","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g bucatini"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g fresh sardines, scaled and filleted (about 300g clean fillet)"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g wild fennel fronds (finocchietto selvatico)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g white onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g sultanas (uva passa), soaked 20 minutes in warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g pine nuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 salted anchovy fillets, rinsed and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.2g saffron threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"80ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g coarse dry breadcrumbs, for the muddica atturrata"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp olive oil, for the muddica"},{"kind":"item","text":"coarse salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the wild fennel in 3 litres of heavily salted water for 10 minutes, until the stems collapse under the back of a spoon; lift it out, squeeze it, chop it, and keep every drop of the green water.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion in the olive oil over low heat for 12 minutes until it is translucent and slumped with no colour at the edges, then melt in the anchovy until the oil smells of the sea.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the pine nuts, drained sultanas, chopped fennel and the saffron loosened in a ladle of the fennel water, lay the sardine fillets on top and cook 6 minutes without stirring, until they turn from glassy to opaque and fall apart when nudged.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the breadcrumbs in a dry pan with the spoon of oil, tossing until they are the colour of dark honey and rattle loose, then tip them straight onto a cold plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the bucatini in the fennel water, drain it two minutes short and finish it in the pan with a ladle of that water until the strands are lacquered and no liquid pools underneath; scatter the muddica at the table, not before.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wild fennel is blanched first and that green water becomes the pasta water: the anethole and sugars leach into it, so tipping it away throws out half the dish. Lay the sardines on the onion and leave them alone; stirring shreds them into grey paste. Muddica goes on at the table because toasted crumb wicks sauce and turns to sludge within a minute.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","umami-deep","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20con%20le%20Sarde%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pasta con le Sarde"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Sardines","Onion","Pasta","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Nuts","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"caponata-catanese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900g aubergine, cut in 3cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g fine salt, for degorging"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g inner celery stalks, sliced 1cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g white onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g ripe tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g Nocellara green olives, pitted and torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g salted capers, rinsed and soaked 10 minutes"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g pine nuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g sultanas"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml sunflower oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"a large handful of basil leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the aubergine in a colander for 45 minutes, until brown beads stand on the cut faces, then rinse and press dry in a cloth until the cubes squeak.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry them in 180C (355F) oil in small batches for about 4 minutes, until mahogany and floating, and drain on a rack rather than paper so they stay crisp.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"In the olive oil, cook the celery 5 minutes until it turns from raw green to olive but still snaps, add the onion until translucent, then the tomatoes, olives, capers, pine nuts and sultanas and simmer 10 minutes until no water runs at the pan edge.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Raise the heat until the pan hisses, pour the vinegar and sugar in together and boil hard for 60 seconds, until the sharp smell drops away and the surface turns glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the aubergine back in off the heat, tear in the basil, and leave it at room temperature for at least 4 hours; it is not finished until it is cool.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Agrodolce is a ratio, not a splash: 60ml white wine vinegar to 30g sugar, poured into a pan hot enough to hiss so the acetic acid flashes off and leaves sweetness with a spine. Added cold and off the heat, it stays raw and acrid for days. Then rest it four hours; warm caponata reads as muddle, cool it resolves into celery, olive and sugar.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salted water & the float test","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caponata%20Catanese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Caponata Catanese"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Celery","Eggplant","Olives","Capers","Basil","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"pasta-alla-norma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g maccheroni or rigatoni"},{"kind":"item","text":"700g aubergine, sliced 1cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g fine salt, for degorging"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g ripe plum tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, lightly crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml sunflower oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g ricotta salata"},{"kind":"item","text":"a large bunch of basil"},{"kind":"item","text":"coarse salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the aubergine slices in a colander for 40 minutes, until water beads on the surface, then rinse and press hard in a cloth until they feel dense and dry.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2640,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry them at 180C (355F) in batches, about 3 minutes a side, until deep gold and no longer squeaking against the spoon, and drain on a rack.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the garlic in the olive oil until it just turns blond, lift it out, add the tomatoes and simmer 20 minutes until the sauce pulls away from the pan and no water separates at the edge.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil the pasta in heavily salted water, drain it a minute short and toss it through the sauce with a splash of cooking water until every tube is coated and the pan is dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in half the aubergine and the torn basil, then grate the ricotta salata over each plate in a snowfall so it stays dry instead of melting into the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Modern aubergines are bred free of the old bitterness, so salting is about structure, not taste. Salt draws water out by osmosis and collapses the open cell walls so the flesh cannot wick oil like a sponge. Skip it and the slices drink the pan dry at 180C (355F), then weep the oil back out grey and greasy. Salted, rinsed and squeezed, they brown instead.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salted water & the float test","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","briny","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a briny, maritime salinity, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20alla%20Norma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pasta alla Norma"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Eggplant","Stone fruit","Basil","Cheese (any)","Ricotta","Pasta","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"sarde-a-beccafico-alla-palermitana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g fresh sardines, scaled, boned and butterflied, tails left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g coarse dry breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g sultanas, soaked and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g pine nuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 salted anchovy fillets, rinsed and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice and zest of 1 orange"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 fresh bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the breadcrumbs in 50ml of the oil over medium heat, stirring until they are the colour of toffee and smell nutty, then spread them out and cool them completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the cold crumb with the pine nuts, sultanas, anchovy, parsley, orange zest and pepper until it holds when squeezed in the fist but crumbles when dropped.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay each sardine skin down, set a heaped teaspoon of stuffing at the head end and roll towards the tail so the tail stands up, then pack the rolls into an oiled dish with a bay leaf standing between each one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the orange juice, lemon juice, sugar and remaining oil until cloudy and pour it around the rolls, not over them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (400F) for 12 minutes, until the skins are taut and bronzed and a roll gives no resistance at the centre, then serve just warm.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true}],"note":"Toast the crumb before it ever meets the fish. Raw breadcrumb soaks up the sardine fat as it renders and the roll ends up wet and pasty; toasted crumb has already lost its free water and stays granular. Bay leaves stand between the rolls to hold them apart so the sides colour. Twelve minutes at 200C (400F) is the ceiling: past about 63C the muscle squeezes out its moisture and goes chalky.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","herb-fresh","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sarde%20a%20Beccafico%20alla%20Palermitana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sarde a Beccafico alla Palermitana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Sardines","Lemon","Orange","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"involtini-di-pesce-spada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g swordfish loin in one piece, very cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g dry breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g caciocavallo, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g pine nuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g sultanas, soaked and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g salted capers, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 fresh bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 tbsp extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dried Sicilian oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the chilled loin into 3mm slices, lay each between two sheets of film and beat gently with a flat mallet until it is translucent at the edges and about 2mm thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the onion in 2 tbsp of the oil until it collapses without colouring, then stir in the breadcrumbs, caciocavallo, pine nuts, sultanas, capers and parsley until the mix clumps in the hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put a teaspoon of filling on each slice, roll it tight and thread the rolls onto skewers alternating with bay leaves so no two rolls touch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over fierce heat for about 90 seconds a side, until the surface is marked and opaque and the rolls still spring back under a finger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the lemon juice with the warm water, beat in the remaining oil and the oregano until it thickens and turns pale, and spoon this salmoriglio over the skewers while they are hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Swordfish is lean and almost free of collagen, so there is no gelatine to hide overcooking: it goes from silky to cotton wool inside a minute. Chill the loin hard, cut 3mm and beat it thinner, then grill fierce and brief. Whisk the salmoriglio with warm water first; without that emulsion the lemon and oil separate and slide straight off the fish.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Soaking dried goods back to life","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny","herb-fresh","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Involtini%20di%20Pesce%20Spada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Involtini di Pesce Spada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Capers","Lemon","Parsley","Oregano","Bay leaf","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"sfincione-palermitano","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g strong bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g semola rimacinata di grano duro"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml water at 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"7g fresh yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g white onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g peeled plum tomatoes, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 salted anchovy fillets, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g primosale or young caciocavallo, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g coarse dry breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp olive oil, for the topping"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flours with 350ml of the water and rest 30 minutes until no dry flour remains, then work in the yeast, salt, oil and the last of the water until the dough is slack, sticky and peels off the bowl in sheets.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold it in the bowl four times at 30 minute intervals, until it holds a low dome, then refrigerate overnight.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onions in the topping oil over low heat for 40 minutes until jammy, pale gold and sweet with no bite left, melt in the anchovies, then add the tomato and reduce until the mix mounds on the spoon.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the dough into an oiled 30x40cm tray and prove 2 hours at room temperature, until it reaches the corners and a poked dent fills back only halfway.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the cheese, lay the onion on in spoonfuls without pressing, then the breadcrumbs and oregano, and bake on the lowest shelf at 220C (430F) for 25 minutes, until the base is crisp when lifted with a palette knife and the crumb topping is dark gold.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sfincione means sponge, and that crumb comes from 80 percent hydration plus a slow cold proof: the wetter the dough, the larger and thinner walled the gas cells. Dress it only after the pan proof, laying the topping on rather than pressing, or you crush the structure you waited overnight for. Breadcrumb goes over the onion last so it drinks the onion water and the top roasts instead of steaming.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Fresh pasta","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sfincione%20Palermitano%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sfincione Palermitano"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Onion","Tomato","Stone fruit","Oregano","Bread","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"cuscusu-di-pesce-alla-trapanese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g coarse semolina (semola grossa)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml salted water at room temperature, for the incocciatura"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp extra virgin olive oil, for the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg mixed rock fish, scaled and gutted (scorfano, gallinella, tracina)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g onion, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g ripe tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g blanched almonds, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.2g saffron threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small dried peperoncino"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"a large bunch of flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g plain flour mixed to a paste with water, for the sealing rope"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Spread the semolina in a wide bowl, flick on the salted water a few drops at a time and work it in one direction with spread fingertips for 20 minutes, until every grain wears a skin and nothing is larger than a peppercorn.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the 3 tbsp of oil through the grain, then rake it with your fingers until it feels dry and separate again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the fish heads and bones with half the rock fish, the onion, garlic, tomato, almonds, saffron, bay, peperoncino and parsley in 2 litres of water for 40 minutes, until the broth runs orange and coats a spoon, then press it through a sieve.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Load the grain loosely into the couscoussier basket, set it over the simmering broth, seal the joint with a rope of flour paste and steam 60 minutes, until steam breaks through the top of the grain.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Poach the remaining fish in the strained broth for 8 minutes, until the flesh parts from the bone at the touch of a fork.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tip the grain into a warm bowl and add hot broth a ladle at a time until it drinks its fill and stands in soft peaks, cover with a cloth and leave 30 minutes before serving the fish alongside.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Incocciatura is the whole technique: salted water is flicked onto the semolina a few drops at a time and worked with fingertips until each grain wears a skin. Pour the water in and the grains cement into lumps that steam through as paste. Seal the pot rim with a flour rope so steam has one exit, then rest the cooked grain under hot broth so it swells evenly.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Reducing a sauce","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","floral","briny","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a floral thread lifting the aroma, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuscusu%20di%20Pesce%20alla%20Trapanese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cuscusu di Pesce alla Trapanese"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Parsley","Bay leaf","Flour","Semolina","Nuts","Clove","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"panelle-palermitane","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g chickpea flour (farina di ceci)"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of flat parsley, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre sunflower oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"sesame rolls and lemon wedges, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the chickpea flour into the cold water with the salt until there is not one lump left, rest it 10 minutes and whisk again.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set it over medium heat and stir without stopping for about 12 minutes, until it thickens past custard and comes away from the pan wall as a single mass that holds the mark of the spoon.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the parsley and pepper, then spread the paste 3mm thin on oiled trays while it is still hot, working fast before it sets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool 20 minutes until firm and matt, then cut into rectangles or triangles.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 180C (355F) in small batches for about 2 minutes, until the surfaces blister into blond bubbles and the panelle float, drain, salt, and eat within five minutes in a sesame roll with lemon.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chickpea starch gelatinises around 75C (165F), so whisk the flour into cold water and bring it up slowly; hot water sets the outside of each lump and seals raw flour inside. Stir until the mass pulls cleanly from the pan wall, then spread it 3mm while hot. Fry at 180C (355F): cooler and the panella turns leathery, hotter and the crust sets before trapped water can blister it.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, melting away silken."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panelle%20Palermitane%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Panelle Palermitane"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Parsley","Bread","Flour","Chickpeas","Sesame"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"cassata-siciliana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg fresh sheep's milk ricotta"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g dark chocolate, 70 percent, chopped small"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g marzipan (pasta reale), tinted green with pistachio"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g pan di Spagna, cut in 1cm sheets"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g candied pumpkin (zuccata), diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"50ml maraschino, let down with 50ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml water, for the glaze"},{"kind":"item","text":"candied orange, citron and cherries, to decorate"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the ricotta in a cloth in the fridge for 12 hours, then push it through a fine sieve twice until it is smooth with no grain to it.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat in the caster sugar and leave it 1 hour, then rub a little between finger and thumb: if you feel any grit, wait longer.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the chocolate and zuccata, keeping the cream stiff enough to hold a soft peak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a sloping 24cm tin with film, press cold marzipan strips 8mm thick around the sides and line the base with pan di Spagna brushed with the diluted maraschino.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill with the ricotta cream, level it, cap with more sponge and chill at least 6 hours, until the surface is firm under a fingertip.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn it out, warm the icing sugar with the water to 40C (105F) until it pours like thick cream, coat the cassata in one unbroken pass and decorate before the glaze skins over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Drain the ricotta 12 hours, sieve it, then beat with the sugar and rest it an hour so the crystals fully dissolve. Undissolved sugar keeps pulling whey by osmosis and the finished cassata weeps inside its marzipan wall. Work the pasta reale cold: almond oil sweats out of warm marzipan and the glaze will not key to a greasy surface. Pour the fondant at 40C (105F) for a glossy set.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Folding: keeping the air in","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cassata%20Siciliana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cassata Siciliana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Squash/Pumpkin","Orange","Stone fruit","Milk","Cheese (any)","Ricotta","Pasta","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"granita-di-mandorla-con-brioche-col-tuzzu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g raw almonds, Avola if you can get them"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre cold water, for the almond milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"160g caster sugar, for the granita"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pinch fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml whole milk, warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g caster sugar, for the brioche"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g fresh yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g soft butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 orange"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk beaten with 1 tbsp milk, to glaze"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the almonds 8 hours, slip off the skins, blitz them with the litre of cold water and press through a cloth until the pulp is dry; never warm it.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the sugar and salt into the cold almond milk until dissolved, pour it into a shallow metal tray and freeze.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape the whole surface with a fork every 30 minutes for about 3 hours, until it is a bank of loose crystals that collapse under a spoon.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"For the brioche, mix the flour, warm milk, sugar, yeast, eggs, salt and orange zest to a smooth dough, then work in the butter a piece at a time until the dough is glossy and stretches to a windowpane.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove until doubled, divide into 100g balls, pinch a small ball on top of each as the tuzzu, prove again until pillowy and slow to spring back, glaze and bake at 180C (355F) for 15 minutes, until deep amber.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Split a warm brioche, load it with granita until it overflows and eat it standing up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sugar is what makes granita scrapeable: about 160g per litre, near 15 percent, keeps enough water unfrozen to shear into crystals. Less and you get a solid block, more and it never sets at all. Scrape every 30 minutes across three hours; leave it alone and the ice grows into shards. Never heat the almond milk, because the volatile benzaldehyde that reads as almond goes off with the steam.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Mashing & puréeing","Proofing: the rise before the oven","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Granita%20di%20Mandorla%20con%20Brioche%20col%20Tuzzu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Granita di Mandorla con Brioche col Tuzzu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sicilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sicilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Orange","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"idli","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g idli rice (parboiled short grain puzhungal arisi)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g whole skinless urad dal (ulundhu)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fenugreek seeds (vendhayam)"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g non-iodised rock salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ice water for grinding, roughly 350 ml in total"},{"kind":"item","text":"Gingelly (raw sesame) oil, for the moulds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the idli rice until the water runs clear and soak it in cold water; soak the urad dal and fenugreek in a separate bowl for 5 hours, until a grain crushes to paste between finger and thumb.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the urad dal first, feeding in ice water 30 ml at a time, until the batter has trebled, is bright white and glossy, and a spoonful dropped into a glass of water floats instead of sinking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the drained rice to the grit of fine semolina, then fold it into the urad batter with your bare hand for two minutes, lifting and turning so you do not knock the foam out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and hold at 26 to 30C (79 to 86F) for 8 to 12 hours, until the batter has domed, is honeycombed against the side of the bowl and smells sharply sour.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold in the salt with a light hand, ladle into oiled moulds and steam 10 to 12 minutes, until a skewer comes out clean and the surface has lost its wet shine.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true}],"note":"Urad dal brings the two things rice cannot: globulin protein and a slippery arabinogalactan mucilage that together spin a film strong enough to hold the CO2 the Leuconostoc gives off. That film shears apart if the grinder heats the batter past about 30C (86F), so grind the urad with ice water and stop when it holds a peak. Past roughly 14 hours the foam collapses and the idli steams flat and sour.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Soaking dried goods back to life","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idli%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idli"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Rice","Lentils","Sesame"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"kal-dosai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g parboiled idli rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g raw rice (pacharisi)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g whole skinless urad dal"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fenugreek seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g thick poha (aval)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g rock salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Ice water for grinding, about 350 ml"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml gingelly oil, for the griddle"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved, for wiping the iron"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak both rices with the poha in one bowl and the urad dal with the fenugreek in another, 5 hours, until the dal crushes clean.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the urad with ice water until it is white and aerated and floats in water; grind the rice finer than you would for idli, until no grit catches on a fingertip. Fold the two together by hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ferment at 26 to 30C (79 to 86F) for 8 to 12 hours, until doubled and ringed with bubbles at the edge of the bowl.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir the batter down, salt it, and thin with water until it falls off the ladle in a ribbon like thin cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a cast iron griddle until a flicked drop of water skitters and vanishes in two seconds. Splash a palmful of water on, wipe it dry with the cut onion, and pour a ladle at the centre, spreading outward in one tightening spiral.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drizzle gingelly oil around the rim and cook until the edges lift free on their own and the underside is lacy and red gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The griddle owns this. Below about 180C (355F) the batter's proteins bond to the iron before the water under them flashes to steam, and the dosai welds itself down; above about 230C (445F) it sets under the ladle and will not spread. The water splash and the onion wipe drop the surface back into the window, which is why cooks do it between every dosai. Thin the batter only after fermentation, never before.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Griddle & plancha work","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kal%20Dosai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kal Dosai"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Rice","Lentils"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","fermentation"]},{"slug":"arachuvitta-sambar","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g toor dal (thuvaram paruppu)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tamarind, soaked in 300 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g small onions (chinna vengayam), peeled whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 drumsticks (murungakkai), cut in 6 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g brinjal and ash gourd, in large chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g chana dal"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 dried red chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp fenugreek seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fresh grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp asafoetida"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g jaggery"},{"kind":"item","text":"35 ml gingelly oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sprigs curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the toor dal with the turmeric until it collapses under the back of a spoon into a smooth puree, then whisk it loose with a little hot water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In 2 tsp of the gingelly oil, roast the coriander seeds and chana dal until deep gold, then the chillies, pepper and fenugreek for 30 seconds; kill the heat and stir the coconut through the residual warmth until it is fawn and smells toasted. Grind to a fine paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the small onions, drumstick and vegetables in 300 ml plain salted water until the drumstick gives to a fingernail, about 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour in the strained tamarind extract with the jaggery and boil hard for 5 minutes, until the raw metallic smell has gone and the liquid smells rounded.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the dal and the ground paste and hold at a bare tremble for 5 minutes only, until the surface turns glossy and the sambar coats a spoon.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the remaining oil until it shimmers, crackle the mustard until it pops and greys, throw in the curry leaves and asafoetida for three seconds until the leaves go translucent, and pour it over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two rules the dal cannot save you from. Tamarind's acid keeps the vegetables' pectin cross-linked, so anything simmered in the sour liquid from the start stays hard: soften the drumstick and the small onions in plain water first, then pour the tamarind over. And the ground coconut masala is aroma, not structure. Give it five minutes at a tremble; boiled hard, the coconut fat breaks out grainy and the roasted coriander goes flat.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Reducing a sauce","Roasting in a hot oven","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arachuvitta%20Sambar%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arachuvitta Sambar"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"asafoetida-hing","term":"Asafoetida (Hing)"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Chiles","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Lentils","Mustard","Sugar","Tamarind","Coriander seed","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"milagu-jeera-rasam","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"25 g tamarind, squeezed in 400 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ripe tomatoes (about 150 g), crushed by hand"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thin water skimmed from the top of cooked toor dal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black peppercorns (milagu)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cumin seeds (jeeragam)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves garlic, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried red chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp asafoetida"},{"kind":"item","text":"A handful of coriander leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the pepper, cumin, garlic and one sprig of curry leaves coarsely in a stone mortar, until the mix smells sharp and you can still see broken pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the tamarind water with the turmeric, salt and crushed tomatoes for 6 to 8 minutes, until the raw sourness is gone and the tomato skins curl away from the flesh.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the pounded masala and the dal water, and bring it only to the point where a pale froth climbs the sides of the pot. Pull it off there. It must not roll.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the ghee, pop the mustard, add the dried chilli and the remaining curry leaves until they crackle stiff, pour it over the rasam and cover for two minutes before scattering the coriander.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rasam is a decoction, not a soup you reduce. Pull it the moment the surface lifts into a pale froth, around 95C (203F). The heat of pepper is piperine and stays where it is, but the smell is volatile oil, cuminaldehyde from the jeeragam and pinene from the milagu, and a rolling boil carries it out of the pot in under a minute. Boiled rasam is hot and hollow. Pound coarse and add it late.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Pounding a curry paste","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Milagu%20Jeera%20Rasam%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Milagu Jeera Rasam"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Cilantro","Curry/Makrut leaves","Lentils","Mustard","Tamarind","Cumin","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","pounding-a-curry-paste"]},{"slug":"vatha-kuzhambu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml gingelly oil, the unrefined dark sesame oil, not the toasted Chinese kind"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp urad dal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp fenugreek seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp asafoetida"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g sundakkai vathal or manathakkali vathal, the sun-dried salted berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 dried red chillies, broken"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g shallots, peeled and left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g tamarind, soaked in 400 ml hot water and squeezed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp sambar powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g jaggery"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp rice flour slaked in 2 tbsp water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the gingelly oil until a mustard seed sent in cracks at once, then splutter the mustard, urad dal and fenugreek in that order, 20 seconds apart, so the fenugreek goes gold and not brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the curry leaves, asafoetida, chillies and vathal and fry 2 min: the dried berries should darken and puff slightly, which is the only chance they get to take on fat.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the shallots whole and fry 3 min until their edges catch, then pour in the strained tamarind water with the sambar powder, turmeric and salt.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered 20 to 25 min. It is done when the raw green edge of the tamarind is gone, the volume has dropped by a third, and a clear layer of oil has risen and stays risen.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the jaggery and the slaked rice flour and give it 2 min more to thicken to a pouring gravy.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"The oil is not a cooking medium here, it is the preservative, which is why the quantity looks alarming and why the dish is finished only when it floats clear on top. That layer plus the tamarind's acid and a firm hand with salt is what let this keep a week on a Tamil counter with no refrigeration, and it is the reason vathal exists at all: vegetables salted and dried in the sun so a household has something to cook in the months when nothing grows. Two failures to watch. Fenugreek taken past gold turns the whole pot bitter and cannot be corrected. And tamarind pulled off early tastes raw and green rather than sour, so judge the reduction by the oil separating, not by the clock.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vatha%20Kuzhambu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vatha Kuzhambu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Chiles","Berries","Curry/Makrut leaves","Flour","Lentils","Sesame","Mustard","Sugar","Tamarind","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"muttaikose-poriyal","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g white cabbage, shredded fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml gingelly oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp whole urad dal"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp chana dal"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dried red chillies, broken"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"A pinch of asafoetida"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g small onions (chinna vengayam), chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fresh grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the gingelly oil, add the mustard and wait until it pops and greys, then the urad and chana dal, stirring until they are the colour of clear honey and smell like toasted nuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chillies, curry leaves and asafoetida for 5 seconds until the leaves blister and go translucent, then the small onions until soft and sweet at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip the cabbage in over high heat, uncovered, and toss 5 to 6 minutes until it loses its raw squeak, wilts by half and picks up a few brown edges. No lid and no water at any point.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, salt it and stir the fresh coconut through so it stays white and sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Here the tadka starts the dish instead of finishing it, because the urad and chana dal need real time in oil to toast nutty and then stay crunchy inside the cabbage. Everything after is a race against water: salt at the start pulls it out by osmosis and a lid traps it, and either one steams the cabbage limp and grey. Cook uncovered on high, salt at the end, coconut off the heat.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","aromatic","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, aromatics blooming from the first bite, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tadka: blooming spices in fat","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=tadka%20tempering%20spices%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Muttaikose%20Poriyal%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Muttaikose Poriyal"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"toasting-nuts-seeds-grains-and-coconut","term":"Toasting: Nuts, Seeds, Grains & Coconut"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Chiles","Cabbage","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Lentils","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat"]},{"slug":"kozhi-chettinad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg chicken on the bone, cut into small pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 dried gundu chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fennel seeds (sombu)"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g cumin seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g kalpasi (stone flower)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 star anise, 3 cm cassia, 4 cloves, 3 green cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fresh grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g small onions (chinna vengayam), sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g ginger and 25 g garlic, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml gingelly oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry roast on low heat: coriander seeds until they crack and darken a shade, then fennel, pepper, cumin, cassia, cloves, cardamom and star anise for a minute until fragrant. Kill the heat and drop the kalpasi into the residual warmth for 10 seconds only, until it smells of woodsmoke.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast the coconut separately, stirring without pause, until it is the colour of dark toast and smells nutty. Grind everything with a splash of water to a fine paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the gingelly oil and brown the small onions until collapsed and sweet, add the ginger garlic until the raw edge lifts, then the tomatoes until they break to a jam that pulls away from the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the ground masala in that oil for 6 to 8 minutes, stirring, until the paste darkens and a rim of red oil pulls away at the sides of the pan.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chicken and turmeric, coat and sear 5 minutes until every piece is lacquered and no pink shows, then 100 ml water, lid on, 20 minutes until the meat pulls from the bone.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tear in the curry leaves and simmer uncovered until the gravy clings to the chicken instead of pooling under it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kalpasi is the signature and the trap. It is a lichen, so its aroma sits in a resin that dissolves into fat but scorches to flat bitterness within seconds of dry heat: roast the coriander first, the fennel and pepper after, and give the kalpasi only the last few seconds off the flame. Roast the coconut to the colour of dark toast as well, because pale coconut grinds into a sweet thin gravy that never thickens.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Searing: the hard crust","Roasting in a hot oven","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","aromatic","earthy","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kozhi%20Chettinad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kozhi Chettinad"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fennel-anise-and-the-licorice-line","term":"Fennel, Anise & the Licorice Line"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Cumin","Coriander seed","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Star anise"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"thayir-sadam","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g raw rice (sona masoori)"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml water, for cooking the rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml whole milk, warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g thick fresh curd, not sour, beaten smooth"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp whole urad dal"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chillies, slit"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ginger, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried red chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"A pinch of asafoetida"},{"kind":"item","text":"Seeds of half a pomegranate"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the rice in the full 700 ml until the grains have burst and collapsed, well past al dente. There should be no bite left at all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash it hot with the back of a ladle into a coarse sticky paste, then stir the warm milk in while it is still hot so the starch drinks it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let it cool until the bowl is only just warm against the back of your hand, below 40C (105F), then fold in the beaten curd and the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the ghee, pop the mustard, add the urad dal until golden, then the ginger, green chilli, dried chilli, curry leaves and asafoetida for 10 seconds until the leaves go crisp and translucent. Pour it over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the pomegranate and rest 20 minutes before serving, so the rice and curd come together.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"Cook the rice past done and mash it hot: fully burst starch granules cannot retrograde, which is why proper thayir sadam stays creamy in the fridge while al dente rice turns chalky and weeps. Then let it fall below 40C (105F) before the curd goes in, or the casein curdles into grains and the warmed lactic bacteria sour the whole bowl by evening. The milk goes in first because the rice keeps drinking.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","gently hot","aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, a warm current of chile, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Thayir%20Sadam%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Thayir Sadam"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Chiles","Pomegranate","Curry/Makrut leaves","Milk","Rice","Lentils","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"medhu-vadai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g whole skinless urad dal (ulundhu)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml ice water, and not a drop more"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chillies, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 black peppercorns, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig curry leaves, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre groundnut or gingelly oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the urad dal 3 hours until a grain crushes clean, then drain it bone dry in a colander for 10 minutes.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":11400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind it, feeding the ice water in 15 ml at a time, until the batter is white, fluffy and stiff enough to stand in a peak, and a pea of it dropped into water rises to the surface within seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the oil to 170 to 175C (340 to 350F) first, and only then fold the ginger, chilli, pepper, curry leaves and salt through the batter by hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wet your palm, flatten a lime sized ball on it, punch a hole through the middle with a wet thumb, and slide the ring off your fingers into the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry, turning once, for 5 to 6 minutes until the hissing dies down and they are an even deep gold; a finished vadai feels light and sounds hollow when tapped with the slotted spoon.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The whole dish is air ground into urad dal, so add the ice water by the spoon and stop the moment a pea of batter floats. Slack batter will not hold a hole and drinks oil. Fry at 170 to 175C (340 to 350F): under 160C (320F) the crust never sets and the vadai comes out grease logged, over 190C (375F) it browns while the centre is still raw batter. Salt only just before frying, because it slackens the batter within minutes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deep frying","Salted water & the float test","Soaking dried goods back to life","Shaping by hand","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Medhu%20Vadai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Medhu Vadai"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Chiles","Curry/Makrut leaves","Lentils"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"paasi-paruppu-payasam","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g moong dal (paasi paruppu)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml water, for cooking the dal"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g dark jaggery (vellam)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water, for the jaggery"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thin coconut milk (second extract)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml thick coconut milk (first extract)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp green cardamom, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp dry ginger powder (sukku)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cashews"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g fresh coconut, in slivers"},{"kind":"item","text":"A pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry roast the moong dal in 10 g of the ghee over low heat, stirring, until it turns a shade darker and smells like roasted nuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook it in the 400 ml water until soft and about a third of the grains have collapsed, so the payasam carries both body and texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the jaggery in its own 100 ml water and strain out the grit, then stir it into the dal off the boil and cook 3 minutes until it smells caramelly.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the thin coconut milk and bring it only to a tremble, 3 to 4 minutes, until it thickens enough to coat the back of a ladle.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Kill the heat, wait until the pot stops steaming, then stir in the thick coconut milk, cardamom, dry ginger and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the cashews and coconut slivers in the last of the ghee until golden and pour them over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coconut milk is an emulsion held by coconut protein, and jaggery's acid and minerals are exactly what breaks it. Melt the jaggery separately and strain it, for grit and so it never meets a hard boil; stir the thin milk in at a tremble and the thick first extract in off the heat entirely. Boil after that and you get sweet water with an oil slick riding on top.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Paasi%20Paruppu%20Payasam%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Paasi Paruppu Payasam"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tamil%20%26%20the%20South%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Tamil & the South kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Lentils","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"recado-rojo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 g whole annatto seed (achiote), brick red and not faded brown"},{"kind":"item","text":"14 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g allspice berries (pimienta gorda)"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g Mexican oregano, the large brittle leaf kind"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cm piece true cinnamon bark (canela)"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g garlic cloves, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"18 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml sour orange juice (naranja agria), or 40 ml orange with 20 ml grapefruit and 20 ml lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml white vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char the unpeeled garlic on a dry comal over medium heat, turning, until the skins are blackened in patches and the cloves collapse under a thumb, about 12 minutes, then peel.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the oregano, allspice, cumin, cloves and cinnamon in the dry pan for about 40 seconds, only until the cumin smells nutty and the first wisp of smoke lifts, then tip them straight out of the hot pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the annatto seed on its own in a spice mill until it is a fine rust powder with nothing rattling, grind the toasted spices with the peppercorns, and sift both through a fine sieve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash in the garlic and salt, then work in the sour orange juice and vinegar a spoonful at a time until you have a stiff clay brick that holds a clean thumbprint.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap tightly and rest at least 12 hours before use so the colour bleeds through evenly. It keeps a month refrigerated.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"The colour is bixin, a resinous carotenoid sitting on the outside of a seed whose kernel is as hard as gravel. A blender jar just polishes the seeds and hands you orange water, so grind them dry in a spice mill or the paste stays gritty forever. Keep the annatto off the heat as well: toasted past warm, the red goes brown and the flavour goes bitter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Charring over open flame","Straining & passing through a sieve","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","earthy","tangy-ferment","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Recado%20Rojo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Recado Rojo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"annatto-achiote","term":"Annatto (Achiote)"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Lime","Orange","Berries","Oregano","Vinegar","Curry paste","Cumin","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"cochinita-pibil","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.8 kg boneless pork shoulder, cut into 6 cm chunks with the fat left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"130 g recado rojo"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml sour orange juice (naranja agria), or 150 ml orange with 75 ml grapefruit and 75 ml lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"22 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, charred on a comal and peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g Mexican oregano, toasted and crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g pork lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 large banana leaves, thick spine trimmed away"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pickled red onion, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 habaneros, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slake the recado rojo into the sour orange juice with the salt, garlic and oregano until it is a smooth loose paint with no gritty specks, turn the pork through it and refrigerate 6 to 12 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pass each banana leaf over a flame or hot comal for a few seconds a side, until it flips from matte olive to glossy deep green and hangs limp; unwilted leaf cracks along its ribs and the parcel leaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a deep dish with crossed leaves, pile in the pork with every drop of marinade and the lard, fold the leaves over into a sealed parcel, then cover the whole dish with foil and crimp it hard to the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook at 150 C (300 F) for 4 hours without opening it once. It is ready when a chunk collapses under sideways pressure from a spoon and the juices run clear orange under a finger of red fat.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shred coarsely in its own juices, never squeezed dry, and serve with pickled red onion and habanero.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pib is a steam oven, not a roaster. Wet leaves and buried heat hold the meat near 100 C (212 F) for hours, so collagen melts to gelatin while the surface never dries out. A domestic oven will roast instead unless you rebuild that seal. Let steam escape and the marinade boils away, the pork surface reaches oven temperature and it shreds into dry string, so crimp the foil over the leaf and do not peek.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Pie crust & blind baking","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","fiery","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, real fire that builds and stays, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cochinita%20Pibil%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cochinita Pibil"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Orange","Banana","Oregano","Curry paste","Pickles","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","pie-crust-and-blind-baking"]},{"slug":"panuchos-yucatecos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g fresh nixtamal masa, or 300 g masa harina hydrated with 380 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g black beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 white onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs epazote"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 habanero, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g pork lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml neutral oil, for shallow frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g shredded cochinita pibil, or poached turkey breast"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g white cabbage, shaved fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g pickled red onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 avocados, sliced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the beans with the onion, epazote and whole habanero until they collapse, about 90 minutes, then lift out the aromatics and blend the beans with just enough of their own liquor to pour.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the puree through a sieve, then dry it in a pan with the lard over medium heat, stirring, until it pulls away from the base in one mass and a spoon track holds open for three seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press 30 g balls of masa into 12 cm rounds and cook on a hot comal, 30 seconds, flip, 45 seconds, flip again and press the centre gently with a cloth until the tortilla balloons.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"While it is still hot, slit the puffed upper skin along a third of the rim with a knife tip, spoon in a tablespoon of bean paste, and press the pocket flat and shut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shallow fry at 180 C (355 F) for about 40 seconds a side, until the surface blisters and stiffens to biscuit colour, drain, then top with the meat, cabbage, pickled onion and avocado.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pocket is not cut into the tortilla, it is steam. On a hot comal both faces set into skins before the interior water flashes, and the trapped steam lifts them apart. Dry masa never seals, so it never puffs: the dough should feel like fresh putty and show no cracked edges when pressed. Fry hot and brief, because a slow fry heats the bean paste through, it swells, and the seam splits.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Working with masa","Tortillas on the comal","Deep frying","Sautéing & the shallow fry","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panuchos%20Yucatecos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Panuchos Yucatecos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Turkey","Onion","Chiles","Cabbage","Corn","Avocado","Cornmeal/Polenta","Black beans","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","tortillas-on-the-comal"]},{"slug":"sopa-de-lima","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg turkey thigh, or chicken legs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 white onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head garlic, charred whole on a comal"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g Mexican oregano, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g ripe tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green bell pepper, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 habanero, pricked once with a knife tip"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 lima agria (Yucatecan sour lime), or 3 limes plus a wide strip of grapefruit peel"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 corn tortillas, cut into 5 mm strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the turkey in the water with half the onion, the charred garlic, oregano, allspice and cloves at a bare tremble for 1 hour, skimming, until the meat pulls away from the bone. Strain and keep 2 litres of broth.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the remaining onion with the bell pepper and tomato in a spoonful of oil until the tomato has gone jammy and the pan smells sweet rather than green, then add the broth, the shredded meat and the pricked habanero and simmer 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the tortilla strips at 180 C (355 F) until they stop bubbling and turn pale gold, then drain on paper. Baked strips dissolve in the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pot off the heat, drop in a thin coin of lima peel with no white pith attached, cover and steep 3 minutes, then fish the peel out and stir the juice in.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle over the strips and carry it to the table at once, before they soften.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lima agria is grown for its rind, not its juice: the perfume is peel oil and the acid is mild. Hold the peel in simmering broth and you extract bitter limonin from the pith instead, which sours the whole pot within minutes, so the coin of zest goes in off the heat and comes straight back out. Add the juice off the heat too, since its aroma is volatile and a simmer strips it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Braising","Charring over open flame","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Sautéing & the shallow fry","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","fiery","bright","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, real fire that builds and stays, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sopa%20de%20Lima%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sopa de Lima"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Turkey","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Corn","Lime","Berries","Oregano","Bread","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"papadzules","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g hulled green pumpkin seeds (pepita menuda)"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs epazote"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"16 corn tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a white onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 habanero"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the pumpkin seeds in a dry pan over medium heat, shaking without pause, until they swell, pop and smell of green tea but stay pale. Any browning turns the sauce khaki and bitter. Cool fully, then grind to a fine flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the water with the epazote and salt, take it off the heat and let it fall to about 75 C (167 F), then pour it slowly onto the seed flour, kneading with your hand to a thick green cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tilt the bowl and press the paste with your palm until a slick of dark green oil pools at the low edge. Spoon that oil off and keep it for the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Char the tomatoes, onion, garlic and habanero on a comal until blistered black in patches, blend, then fry the sauce in the oil until it darkens and holds a line behind a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the eggs 9 minutes and chop them, dip each warm tortilla into the seed sauce, roll it around the egg, then flood the plate with the remaining seed sauce, the tomato sauce and the reserved green oil.","durationSec":540,"handsOnSec":780,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The seed sauce is an emulsion held together by ground pepita solids, and it breaks like any other. Take it much above 80 C (176 F) and the solids clump, the oil floods out and the sauce turns gritty and greasy. So the epazote water is cooled before it meets the flour, the bowl never goes back over a flame, and the green oil you press out deliberately becomes the garnish instead of the failure.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Charring over open flame","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Papadzules%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Papadzules"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Squash/Pumpkin","Corn","Bread","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"poc-chuc","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g pork leg, cut across the grain into 6 mm steaks"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml sour orange juice, or 90 ml orange with 45 ml grapefruit and 45 ml lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, charred and mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g coarsely cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 white onions, cut into thick rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 habanero"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g xnipec, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"corn tortillas, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Turn the pork through the sour orange, garlic, salt and pepper and leave it exactly 20 minutes, no longer.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Char the tomatoes and habanero on the comal until the skins are black and slipping, then crush them with salt into a coarse chiltomate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the pork out and blot both faces bone dry on cloth, because a wet surface steams itself grey instead of browning.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over a hot charcoal bed for about 90 seconds a side, until the edges curl up and the surface is mottled deep brown with a few black freckles, and lay the onion rounds alongside until limp and singed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 3 minutes, chop coarsely on the board, and serve with the chiltomate, the grilled onion and xnipec.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sour orange does two jobs and only the first is wanted. In twenty minutes its acid opens the surface to flavour; past that it denatures protein right through a 6 mm steak, which then wrings out its water on the fire and eats like chalk. Dry the meat before it meets the coals: the juice carries sugar and browns fast, but only once the surface water has gone.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Grilling over live coals","Charring over open flame","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","fiery","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, real fire that builds and stays, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Poc%20Chuc%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Poc Chuc"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Corn","Lime","Orange","Bread","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"pavo-en-escabeche-oriental","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.6 kg turkey legs and thighs, jointed"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g white onion, sliced 5 mm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head garlic, charred whole on a comal"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 chile xcatic (pale yellow chilli), or banana peppers"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g Mexican oregano, toasted until brittle"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cm true cinnamon bark"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml sour orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grind the toasted oregano, peppercorns, allspice, cloves, cinnamon and cumin with the peeled charred garlic and a splash of the vinegar into a stiff pale recado.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the turkey in the water with half the recado and a heavy hand of salt until the meat is loose on the bone, about 70 minutes, then lift the pieces out and keep the broth.","durationSec":4200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Blister the xcatic chillies over a flame until the skin lifts away in blackened sheets, then peel, seed and slit them lengthways.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the onion into the broth for 2 minutes only, then pour in the vinegar and sour orange with the chillies and the rest of the recado and take the pot off the heat; the onion should still snap.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Char the turkey over coals or under a fierce grill until the skin crisps and blackens at the edges, then return it to the hot escabeche and serve in deep bowls with the broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Onion stays crunchy in acid for a reason. The reaction that softens vegetable pectin only runs above roughly pH 5, and vinegar shuts it down, so onion added after the acid is still raw-crisp an hour later. That is why the slices get their two minutes in plain broth first and the vinegar goes in last, and why the pot is never boiled once soured.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Grilling over live coals","Charring over open flame","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright","charred","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pavo%20en%20Escabeche%20Oriental%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pavo en Escabeche Oriental"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Turkey","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Orange","Banana","Berries","Oregano","Vinegar","Cumin","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"xnipec","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 habaneros, ideally the orange Yucatecan kind"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, about 180 g, diced 3 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g firm ripe tomato, seeded and diced 5 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coriander leaf, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml sour orange juice, or 45 ml orange with 25 ml grapefruit and 20 ml lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the diced onion with the salt and half the juice and leave 10 minutes, until the pieces go translucent pink at the edges and lose their raw bite.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slit the habaneros and scrape out the pale ribs to bring the heat down, or mince them whole for the full thing; the seeds themselves carry almost nothing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the chilli, tomato and coriander through the onion with the rest of the juice, then let it stand 15 minutes, until the liquid turns rosy and lifts a sharp chilli smell as you lean over the bowl.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve within the hour, while the tomato still has edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Capsaicin is made in the pale placental ribs, not the seeds, so seeding a habanero barely moves the heat; strip the ribs instead. Salt is the second dial. It pulls water out of onion and tomato by osmosis, which is what you want briefly, to cure the onion's raw sulfur bite. Leave the whole salsa salted an hour and it slumps into pink soup with no crunch left in it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Xnipec%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Xnipec"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Orange"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"huevos-motulenos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 corn tortillas"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g refried black beans, thick enough to hold a line"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"a quarter white onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 habanero"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g cooked green peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g cooked ham, diced 5 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 plantain, skin blackening, sliced on the bias"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g aged Edam or queso de bola, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char the tomato, onion, garlic and habanero on a comal until blackened in patches, blend to a rough sauce, then fry it in a spoonful of oil until it darkens and stops smelling raw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the plantain in shallow oil until the cut faces are deep amber and the flesh gives like custard, then drain on paper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the tortillas one at a time at 180 C (355 F), about 30 seconds a side, until they stiffen and stop bubbling but before they take much colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread each hot tortilla with warm beans, slide a fried egg on top with the white set and the yolk still liquid, then spoon the tomato sauce over and scatter with peas, ham and cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ring the plate with plantain and eat immediately, before the beans wet the tortilla through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Everything stacked here is wet, so the tortilla has to be waterproofed first. Frying at 180 C (355 F) flashes off surface moisture and sets a rigid starch shell faster than oil can soak in. Below about 160 C (320 F) the oil wins: the disc turns limp and greasy, and the bean layer then softens it to paste under the egg. Build and serve in one movement, not down a queue of plates.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Tortillas on the comal","Charring over open flame","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Huevos%20Motule%C3%B1os%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Huevos Motuleños"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Corn","Peas","Plantain","Cheese (any)","Bread","Black beans"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","charring-over-open-flame"]},{"slug":"marquesitas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"260 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g aged Edam or queso de bola, grated fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cajeta or thick dulce de leche"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, sugar and salt into the eggs, let down with the milk, melted butter and vanilla to the thickness of single cream, and rest 20 minutes so the flour hydrates.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a heavy nonstick pan or a wafer iron over medium and spread 60 ml of batter as thin as it will go, roughly 20 cm across.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook without moving it until the steam coming off the surface stops and the whole disc goes evenly pale gold and stiff at the rim, 2 to 3 minutes, then flip for 30 seconds more.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the grated cheese and a ribbon of cajeta across the hot wafer and roll it tight into a tube within about ten seconds, while it still bends.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand the roll upright for a minute; it should crack audibly at the first bite.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Crispness here is a glass, not a bake. The wafer bends only while hot, with its last water still mobile; falling through about 60 C (140 F) the sugar and starch set rigid, so you have seconds to roll. Roll it while it is still visibly steaming and the trapped moisture softens it from inside. Use a dry aged cheese too: a young melting cheese weeps water and the tube goes limp before it reaches the table.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Marquesitas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Marquesitas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yucatecan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yucatecan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"]},{"slug":"beiju-de-tapioca","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g polvilho doce (sweet cassava starch)"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fresh or frozen grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g unsalted butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the water and salt into the polvilho doce with your fingertips until the starch feels like damp sand: no dry pockets, no wet lumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the damp starch through a fine sieve onto a tray. What falls through should be loose and powdery; discard the hard grains left behind.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter a 4 mm even layer of sieved starch across a dry non-stick pan over medium heat and press it flat. The grains turn glassy and knit into one sheet in about 90 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip when the disc lifts free in a single piece and the top has lost its chalky look; give it 30 seconds more, with no colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter the hot disc, scatter the coconut over half, fold and eat at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing binds this but the starch itself: cassava granules gelatinise on dry contact heat and fuse into a sheet, so water content is the whole recipe. Too wet and the layer cements into a rubbery pancake; too dry and it stays powder and never knits. Sieving is the calibration step, and the disc must come off pale, because browning makes it brittle.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beiju%20de%20Tapioca%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beiju de Tapioca"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Butter"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"acaraje","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g dried black-eyed peas (feijão fradinho), soaked 8 hours"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre dendê (unrefined red palm oil) for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g whole dried shrimp"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g vatapá (bread, cashew and coconut milk purée)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g ripe tomato, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g onion, sliced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 pimenta malagueta chillies, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the soaked peas hard between your palms under water until the skins float off; the peas should look bare and pale yellow with no grey flecks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the drained peas with the grated onion and salt to a thick paste with no whole pieces left in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the paste with a wooden spoon until it lightens in colour and a test spoonful floats in cold water, about 5 minutes of hard work.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the dendê to 170C (340F), slide in oval spoonfuls and fry until the crust is set and deep orange and the fritter rolls over on its own, about 4 minutes.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split each fritter while hot, spread with vatapá and top with dried shrimp, tomato, sliced onion and malagueta.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Soak the peas eight hours first. The skins carry the fibre that stops the paste holding air, so the beating that makes acarajé light only works once they are rubbed off. Beat until a spoonful floats: unaerated paste sinks and fries into a dense, greasy lump. Dendê is not interchangeable with neutral oil here; its carotenes give the colour and the flavour, and they degrade past about 180C (355F) into a dull, harsh fat.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","rich","nutty-toasted","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Acaraj%C3%A9%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Acarajé"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Peas","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Bread","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"bobo-de-camarao","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g mandioca mansa (aipim or macaxeira), peeled, in 5 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g raw prawns, shelled"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g tomato, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g red pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml dendê (unrefined red palm oil)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coriander, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g spring onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the cassava in salted water until a knife slides through with no resistance and the pieces begin to split, 20 to 25 minutes; lift out and pull the woody cord from the centre of each piece.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the hot cassava with 200 ml of the coconut milk to a purée thick enough to fall from the spoon in a sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat onion, garlic, pepper and tomato in the olive oil until the tomato has collapsed and no raw garlic smell is left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the purée and the remaining coconut milk, loosen with cassava cooking water to a soft dropping consistency, then add the prawns and cook only until they turn opaque and curl into a loose C.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, stir in the dendê, coriander, spring onion and lime juice, and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sweet cassava is the only kind you can simply boil and eat; bitter cassava carries far more cyanogenic glycoside and has to be grated, pressed and toasted into flour before it is safe. The purée thickens by starch alone and sets to a paste as it cools, so keep it looser than you want it. Dendê goes in off the heat: simmered, it turns from fruity and orange to flat and greasy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","silky","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bob%C3%B3%20de%20Camar%C3%A3o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bobó de Camarão"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Lime","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"vaca-atolada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg beef short rib, cut into single bones"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg mandioca mansa, peeled, in 6 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g tomato, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pimenta malagueta, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cheiro-verde (spring onion and parsley), chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the ribs in the oil in batches until every face is crusted and the pan base is dark brown; a crowded pan steams and the meat stays grey.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic in the same fat, scraping until the crust dissolves into the onions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the ribs with tomato, bay, chilli, salt and water and simmer covered at a bare tremble until the meat pulls off the bone with no spring, 2 to 3 hours unattended.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the cassava, cutting the woody cord out of each piece as you go, and cook uncovered until half the pieces have collapsed and the liquid has thickened around the rest.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the cheiro-verde and check the salt; the sauce should coat the bones rather than pool around them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The name means the cow got stuck in the mud, and the mud is cassava starch. Half the root should disintegrate to thicken the braise while half stays in pieces, which only happens if the cassava goes in after the meat is already tender. Add it early and it dissolves to glue while the ribs are still tough. Old roots never soften at all, so buy cassava that snaps white and wet.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vaca%20Atolada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vaca Atolada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Parsley","Bay leaf","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"feijao-tropeiro","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g dried carioca beans, simmered until tender and drained hard"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g smoked bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g linguiça calabresa, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g farinha de mandioca torrada (toasted cassava meal)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g couve, shredded fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g spring onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon from a cold pan until the fat runs clear and the pieces are crisp; lift it out and brown the linguiça in the same fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion and garlic in that fat until the garlic is pale gold, then scramble the eggs straight into the pan in loose curds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the drained beans and the meats and warm through, stirring gently enough to leave the bean skins intact.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Kill the heat, rain in the farinha while tossing, and stop the moment every grain is coated and the mixture is loose and sandy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold through the couve and spring onion off the heat so they stay bright green, then season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Farinha de mandioca is already grated, pressed and toasted, which is exactly how bitter cassava is made safe to eat. Here it is a dry ingredient soaking up rendered fat, not a thickener to be simmered. Add it to a wet pan or over live heat and it swells into a paste that sets like plaster on the plate. Drain the beans hard for the same reason: tropeiro is loose and sandy, never saucy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","smoky","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Feij%C3%A3o%20Tropeiro%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Feijão Tropeiro"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"farofa-de-dende","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g coarse farinha de mandioca torrada"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml dendê (unrefined red palm oil)"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the butter with the dendê over low heat until the fat is uniformly orange and just fluid; never let it smoke.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic in it until translucent and sweet, with no colour on the garlic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs and scramble them into small curds, then rain in the farinha from a height while stirring.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast, stirring constantly, until every grain is orange and dry and the pan smells nutty; it should pour rather than clump.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season, fold in the parsley and take it off the heat while still loose, since it tightens as it cools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Farofa is a fat delivery system: toasted cassava meal is dry and thirsty, and its job is to soak up whatever the rest of the plate leaves behind. The ratio is the whole trick, roughly one part fat to three parts meal by volume. Wetter and it clumps into pellets; drier and it is dusty and catches in the throat. Dendê gives a savoury, almost sweet weight no other fat has, but it turns bitter if it smokes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","smoky","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, wood-smoke running underneath, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Farofa%20de%20Dend%C3%AA%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Farofa de Dendê"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Parsley","Butter","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"couve-a-mineira","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g couve manteiga or collard greens, thick stems stripped out"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stack the leaves ten at a time, roll them into a tight cigar and slice across into 2 mm ribbons; shake them loose and dry them completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the oil, butter and garlic together from cold over medium heat until the garlic is pale gold and smells sweet, then lift it out if it threatens to brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the heat to maximum, add the couve all at once and toss constantly for 60 to 90 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stop while the ribbons are glossy, bright green and still springy, return the garlic and salt off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things ruin couve: water and time. Wet leaves drop the pan temperature and steam instead of frying, which turns the chlorophyll olive and washes out the pepperiness; long cooking does the same. So cut the ribbons fine enough to be done in a minute and dry them before they meet the fat. Garlic starts in cold oil and comes out gold, because at full heat it burns before the greens even wilt.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Couve%20%C3%A0%20Mineira%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Couve à Mineira"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Kale/Collards","Butter","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"coxinha-de-frango","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g chicken thigh on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml of the chicken poaching broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g soft fresh cream cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fine dry breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres sunflower oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the chicken with the onion and bay until it shreds under a fork; keep the broth and pull the meat into fine threads.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring 500 ml of that broth, the milk and the butter to a rolling boil, tip in all the flour at once and beat over the heat until the dough leaves the sides clean and a film sets on the pan base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn the dough out and knead it while it is almost too hot to hold, until it is smooth and elastic with no cracks when you fold it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flatten walnut-sized pieces, fill with the shredded chicken bound with cream cheese, garlic and parsley, and pinch each into a teardrop with no open seam and no trapped air.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll through beaten egg then breadcrumbs and fry at 175C (350F) until deep gold and audibly crisp, about 4 minutes; drain standing point upwards.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a scalded dough: boiling broth gelatinises the flour starch before any frying happens, so the shell holds its teardrop and stays tender rather than reading as raw paste. The failures are seams and heat. An open pinch or a trapped air pocket bursts in the fryer and the filling escapes; below 170C (340F) the crumb drinks oil, above 185C (365F) it colours before the middle is hot.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Coxinha%20de%20Frango%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Coxinha de Frango"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"molho-de-pimenta-e-limao","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"40 g pimenta malagueta chillies, stems removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g onion, very finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml dendê or olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander leaf, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the malaguetas lengthways and scrape out as much pith as you want heat removed; the pith holds more of it than the seeds do.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the chillies with the garlic and salt to a rough paste, coarse enough that you can still see flecks of skin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the onion, lime juice, vinegar and oil until the salt has dissolved.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the coriander last and taste: it should be aggressively sour on its own, because it will be met by beans, pork fat and farofa.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is not heat for its own sake; it is the acid that cuts dendê, pork fat and bean starch, which is why the lime is measured and the chilli is not. Malagueta is thin walled and fierce, and most of that lives in the pith, so scrape it if you want the fruit without the burn. Rest it 30 minutes so the raw onion loses its edge, and use it within a day before the coriander blackens.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Molho%20de%20Pimenta%20e%20Lim%C3%A3o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Molho de Pimenta e Limão"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste"]},{"slug":"quindim","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fresh or frozen grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g unsalted butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"butter and sugar for the moulds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the yolks into the sugar with a spatula until the sugar has dissolved and the mix is smooth; do not whisk, and let no foam build on the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the melted butter, coconut milk, salt and grated coconut, then rest the mixture until the coconut has hydrated and any bubbles have risen so you can skim them off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter and sugar the moulds heavily, fill to 5 mm below the rim and stand them in a roasting tin; pour boiling water halfway up their sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 160C (320F) until the surface is set and glossy amber and a skewer comes out clean while the centre still trembles, 35 to 45 minutes.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely in the moulds, run a knife around each one and invert in a single movement.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Quindim builds itself in layers as it bakes: the coconut floats, the yolk custard sinks, and the sweet comes out of the mould upside down with a clear golden top over a coconut base. That only happens in a water bath held below about 165C (330F). Direct heat curdles the yolk protein into grains and pushes bubbles through the surface, and any air whisked in on purpose stays as holes in the glass.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Quindim%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Quindim"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Butter","Milk","Coconut milk","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"kyet-thun-ni-kyaw","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g red shallots, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml peanut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slice the shallots across the grain at an even 2 mm; a thick slice is still wet when a thin one has burnt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put shallots and cold oil into a wok together and set it over medium heat; bring it only to a lazy, even fizz, never a roar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir every minute or two for 20 to 25 minutes; the loud bubbling of escaping water quietens to a whisper as the shallots go from white to straw.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift them out with a spider while they are still pale gold and spread them on a rack in one layer; they darken a full shade off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the shallots only once they are cold and crisp, and bottle the cooled oil on its own.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is the base of the whole table, and it yields two things that must be kept apart. The oil holds the shallot's soluble aroma; the slices hold the crunch, and crunch only survives dry storage. Left sitting in their own oil they go limp overnight. The other failure is colour: sugars keep browning on residual heat, so shallots pulled at brown finish bitter.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyet%20Thun%20Ni%20Kyaw%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kyet Thun Ni Kyaw"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Peanut"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"wet-tha-see-byan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g pork belly, skin on, in 4 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g red shallots"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g fresh ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml peanut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g mild dried red chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ngapi, fermented fish paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend shallots, garlic and ginger with 50 ml water to a smooth paste with no visible shreds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil until a drop of paste sizzles at once, take the pot off the flame, stir in turmeric and chilli for five seconds so they bloom without scorching, then add the paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry on medium for 20 to 25 minutes, scraping the base: the paste goes from pale and loose to brick red and thick, pulls away in one mass, and oil weeps out at the edges.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add pork, ngapi, fish sauce and salt, turn to coat every piece, then pour in water to sit just below the meat and simmer covered until a skewer meets no resistance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the lid off, raise the heat and cook down until the water has gone and a clear red oil floats a finger deep over the meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"See byan means the oil returns, and that is the doneness test, not a clock. While water remains the fat stays dispersed through the sauce; once it boils away the fat separates and rises, carrying the chilli and turmeric with it. Stopping while the sauce is still creamy leaves raw shallot in the mouth, and splashing water back in at the end simply re-emulsifies it and undoes the work. The covered simmer runs about 70 minutes and is not active time.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","earthy","tangy-ferment","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, an earthy, grounding base, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wet%20Tha%20See%20Byan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wet Tha See Byan"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Fish sauce","Peanut","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"mohinga","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g catfish, whole or thick fillet"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 stalks lemongrass, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fresh ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g red shallots"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ngapi, fermented fish paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g chickpea flour, toasted in a dry pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g raw rice, toasted dark and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g banana stem, outer sheaths removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml peanut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g fresh round rice noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, hard boiled and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bunch coriander, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 limes, in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g crushed dried chilli"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the catfish with lemongrass, half the ginger and the water for 15 minutes until the flesh flakes off the bone; lift it out, flake it, and keep every drop of the broth.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the banana stem 3 mm thick straight into water sharpened with lime, winding the long threads off your knife as you go, then rub with salt and rinse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend shallots, garlic and the remaining ginger, fry in the oil with turmeric until the paste is brick red and the oil separates, then stir in the ngapi until it smells toasted rather than sharp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the chickpea flour and rice powder into 400 ml of cool broth until slack and lump free, then pour it into the simmering pot with the paste, fish and banana stem.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir at the base and simmer 25 minutes, until the soup coats a spoon and the chalky taste of raw flour is gone; season with fish sauce.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle over rinsed rice noodles and hand round egg, coriander, lime and chilli at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The body is chickpea flour, and it disperses only in cool liquid: tipped into a hot pot the outer starch gelatinises on contact and seals lumps that never break down. Toasted ground rice adds grip without the gluey pull of more flour. Banana stem goes into acidulated water because its cut faces brown within a minute, and the threads must be wound off or they hang together in the bowl. The soup keeps thickening as it stands, so leave it looser than you want it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Fermentation","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","tangy-ferment","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mohinga%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mohinga"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Eggs","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Lime","Banana","Lemongrass","Rice","Noodles","Flour","Chickpeas","Fish sauce","Peanut","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"ohn-no-khao-swe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g chicken thigh, diced 2 cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g red shallots"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fresh ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml peanut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g mild dried red chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g chickpea flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml chicken stock, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g fresh wheat noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs, hard boiled and quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fried shallots"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml shallot oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime, in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g crushed dried chilli"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend shallots, garlic and ginger, then fry in the peanut oil with turmeric and chilli until the paste is brick coloured and oil pools at the sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chicken and turn it in the paste until the outsides tighten and go opaque.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the chickpea flour into the cold stock, pour it in with the fish sauce, and simmer 15 minutes, stirring, until it lightly coats a spoon.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the coconut milk and bring it back only to a tremble, where a bubble breaks the surface every few seconds; hold it there five minutes and no hotter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the noodles, drain them hard, ladle the soup over, and finish each bowl with egg, lime, chilli, then the fried shallots and a spoon of their oil last.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The thickener is chickpea flour, not the coconut: the starch is what holds the fat in suspension. Boiling after the coconut goes in breaks that suspension and gives a greasy slick over grainy curds, so the pot never rises past a tremble again. Fried shallots and their oil arrive at the table separately for the reason they are made separately: the oil perfumes the broth, the shallots give crunch, and crunch is gone a minute after it meets hot liquid.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ohn%20No%20Khao%20Swe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ohn No Khao Swe"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Garlic","Ginger","Shallot","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Noodles","Flour","Chickpeas","Fish sauce","Peanut","Stock/Broth","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"laphet-thoke","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120 g laphet, fermented tea leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml garlic oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, sliced paper thin and fried crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g yellow split peas, soaked and fried crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dried broad beans, fried crisp"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g roasted peanuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g toasted white sesame"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dried shrimp, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g firm tomato, in thin wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g white cabbage, finely shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chillies, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime"}],"steps":[{"text":"Taste the laphet first; rinse it briefly in cold water and squeeze it dry only if it is harshly astringent, because rinsing takes the ferment with it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Loosen the leaves with half the garlic oil and a squeeze of lime until they sit as a glossy dark mound rather than a dry clump.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heap the crisp peas, beans, peanuts, sesame, fried garlic, dried shrimp, tomato, cabbage and chilli in separate piles around the leaves on a wide plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss it at the table with the fish sauce and the rest of the oil and lime, and eat it straight away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Laphet is steamed tea leaf pressed under weight and left to sour, and it is eaten as food, caffeine and all, not brewed. Its own acid and tannin are most of the dressing, which is why so little else goes on. Everything around it is texture, and all of it is thirsty: mixed in the kitchen, the fried peas and beans pull moisture out of the leaves and tomato and are soft before the plate lands. Build it in piles, mix it in front of the eater.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","tangy-ferment","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Laphet%20Thoke%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Laphet Thoke"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Cabbage","Peas","Lime","Fish sauce","Peanut","Sesame"],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"chin-baung-kyaw","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g roselle leaves, stripped from the stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml peanut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g red shallots, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 green chillies, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g bamboo shoot, in fine strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pull every leaf off its stem; the stems stay woody however long you cook them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the shallots and garlic in the oil over medium high heat until the edges are gold, then add the chillies and turmeric.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the leaves in handfuls with the pan uncovered, tossing as each lot collapses; they drop to a fifth of their volume and turn from red green to deep olive.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the bamboo shoot, two tablespoons of water and the salt, and cook five minutes more until the liquid has gone and the oil runs clear at the edge of the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Roselle brings its own sourness, sharp and clean, straight out of the acid in the leaf, so the dish needs no lime or tamarind; either one makes it taste tinny. Keep the pan uncovered, because a lid traps acidic steam over the leaves and turns them grey brown and slippery inside a minute. Salt goes in at the end for the same reason: early salt draws water out and the leaves stew in it instead of frying.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chin%20Baung%20Kyaw%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chin Baung Kyaw"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Shallot","Chiles","Peanut","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"balachaung-kyaw","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g dried shrimp"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g garlic, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red shallots, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml peanut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g ngapi, fermented shrimp paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g mild dried red chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml thick tamarind water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grind the dried shrimp in a dry blender to a light floss; stop while it is still fluffy, before it packs into oily pellets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the garlic in the oil until pale gold and lift it out, then do the shallots the same way; keep the oil in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slacken the ngapi in the tamarind water and fry it in that oil for two minutes, until the ammoniac edge is gone and it smells like toasted anchovy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the shrimp floss, chilli and salt and fry, stirring, for 10 to 15 minutes until the floss is rust coloured, dry, and rustles against the spoon instead of clinging to it.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the garlic and shallots back in off the heat, cool it completely, and jar it with the oil standing over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"It keeps for weeks only because the water has been driven out and the oil seals the surface; leave any damp in the shrimp floss and it moulds within the week. Garlic and shrimp are fried apart and reunited because garlic browns in three minutes and the floss needs fifteen, so cooked together the garlic is bitter carbon long before the shrimp is dry. Frying the ngapi is not optional: raw it is harsh and volatile, fried it turns round and savoury.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":["Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","umami-deep","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Balachaung%20Kyaw%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Balachaung Kyaw"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Garlic","Shallot","Chiles","Fish sauce","Peanut","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"bu-thee-kyaw","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g bottle gourd, peeled and cut in 5 mm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g rice flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g chickpea flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml ice cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 L peanut oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml thick tamarind water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g palm sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g crushed dried chilli"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the gourd batons, leave them 10 minutes, then squeeze them firmly; the water they give up would otherwise thin the batter on contact.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk both flours, the turmeric and salt into the ice cold water to the thickness of pouring cream; it should sheet off a spoon leaving a thin, even film.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 175 C (350 F), the point where a drop of batter sinks, rises within two seconds and fizzes hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Coat the gourd and lift out loose tangled clusters rather than single sticks; fry about four minutes, until the loud hiss falls away and they are deep gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, never on paper on a plate, and serve with the tamarind, palm sugar, garlic and chilli stirred together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rice flour has no gluten, so the crust sets brittle and stays brittle; a wheat batter goes leathery within minutes as trapped steam softens the gluten network. Ice cold batter into 175 C oil sets the shell before the gourd's water can escape into it. The two usual failures are batter left standing until it warms and slackens, and oil below 165 C, which lets the coating drink oil instead of sealing against it.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","sweet-edged","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a sweet edge rounding the corners, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bu%20Thee%20Kyaw%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bu Thee Kyaw"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Flour","Chickpeas","Peanut","Sugar","Tamarind","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"nan-bya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g plain yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"230 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml peanut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ghee, melted"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, yeast, sugar and salt, add the yoghurt, water and oil, and knead 8 minutes until the dough is slack and tacky yet leaves the bowl wall clean.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Prove it covered until doubled and domed, then divide into eight balls of about 110 g and rest them until a pressed dent no longer springs back.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a heavy iron pan or an upturned wok, dry, until a flick of water skitters and vanishes rather than sitting and steaming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press each ball out to 20 cm with oiled palms, working from the middle outwards and leaving the rim thick; do not reach for a rolling pin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slap it onto the dry pan; when the top domes up in big blisters and the underside is spotted brown, turn it for 40 seconds, brush with ghee and stack under a cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The blister is steam caught between two skins of gluten, and a rolling pin presses out the gas pockets that let it form, which is why teashop bread is hand pressed and a rolled one bakes flat and hard. The pan has to be hot enough to set the outside before the crumb dries, or you get a pale cracker. Proving is waiting, roughly 90 minutes plus 20 to rest. Eat it with boiled yellow peas, pe byouk, and a scrape of ghee.","equipment":["Wok","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nan%20Bya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nan Bya"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Yogurt","Flour","Peanut","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"sanwin-makin","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g coarse semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g palm sugar, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g white poppy seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry toast the semolina in a wide pan over medium heat for about 8 minutes, until it smells of biscuit and has gone straw coloured; do not let it brown.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the palm sugar into the coconut milk, water and salt, then pour that onto the semolina off the heat, whisking as you go so no lumps set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return it to medium heat and stir without stopping for about 15 minutes, until the mass pulls away from the pan in one lump and a spoon stands up in it.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the pan off the heat, beat in the ghee, and wait until the mixture has stopped steaming before working in the eggs a third at a time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread it 3 cm deep in a greased tin, scatter the poppy seeds, and bake at 190 C (375 F) until the top is mahogany, blistered and firm to a fingertip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Toasting the semolina first breaks its starch down enough that the grains swell less, which is the difference between a cake you can cut in squares and wallpaper paste. The eggs are the risk: beaten into a mass still above about 70 C they set on contact into visible yellow threads, so take the pan off and let it stop steaming first. It firms a great deal as it cools, so cut it only when cold.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sanwin%20Makin%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sanwin Makin"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Burmese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Burmese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Semolina","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"ersho","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g whole brown teff flour, stone milled and unbleached"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g unchlorinated water at 30C (86 F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g whole brown teff flour per daily feed"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unchlorinated water per daily feed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the teff flour into the warm water in a glass jar until no dry pockets remain; the batter should pour like single cream. Cover loosely and hold at 22 to 26C (72 to 79 F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave it undisturbed until a thin grey liquid stands on the surface and the smell has turned from wet grass to sharp sour, roughly three days.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off the grey liquid and keep it; discard half the batter, stir in 50 g flour and 100 g water, and repeat this every day.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Judge it ready when a fine even foam covers the surface within eight hours of a feed and the sourness has a green apple edge rather than a vinegar sting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"From then on, the clear liquid poured off a finished injera batter is the ersho for the next batch; keep it cold and it stays live for two weeks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Teff bran carries its own lactobacilli and wild yeast, so the culture builds itself; ersho is only the sour liquid saved off a finished batter. Below 20C (68 F) the yeast stalls while acid bacteria keep working and the starter turns vinegary and dead, with no gas left to open the eyes in injera. Hold it at 22 to 26C (72 to 79 F) and feed the day it separates, not later.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Injera"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","tangy-ferment","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, ferment tang with real depth behind it, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ersho%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ersho"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour"]},{"slug":"chechebsa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain wheat flour, or barley flour for the highland version"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g niter kibbeh"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g berbere"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g honey, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ayib, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the salt into the flour, add the water in a stream, and knead until the dough stops sticking to the bowl and springs back slowly when pressed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into two rounds 3 mm thick and cook on a dry ungreased pan over medium heat until the surface dulls and blisters and the underside shows brown freckles, about two minutes a side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tear the hot kita into thumbnail pieces while it is still soft; cold bread shatters instead of tearing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe the pan, melt the niter kibbeh over low heat, and stir in the berbere until it darkens by a shade and smells toasted rather than raw and dusty, around 40 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the torn bread through the spiced butter until every piece is coated and glossy, then serve at once with honey spooned over and cold ayib alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The kita is cooked on a dry pan because fat on the surface stops the crumb drying, and damp bread turns to paste when it hits the butter later. Berbere is mostly chilli flesh, heavy in sugar, and it scorches to bitterness in seconds over a high flame. Bloom it in warm niter kibbeh on the lowest heat: the fat pulls out capsaicin and colour, and the moment it darkens you stop.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","sweet-edged","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chechebsa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chechebsa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"berbere-and-mitmita","term":"Berbere & Mitmita"}],"pantryItems":["Flour","Couscous/Bulgur","Honey","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"berbere","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120 g dried mild red chillies, stems pulled and most seeds shaken out"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dried hot red chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fenugreek seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g korarima seeds, husks discarded"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ajwain"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g nigella seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black peppercorn"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cassia bark"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g dried ginger, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dried garlic, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried besobela leaf, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the chillies further in an oven at 60C (140 F) with the door ajar until they snap cleanly rather than bend, about 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toast the whole spices separately in a dry pan over low heat until each smells nutty and the coriander begins to pop; pull the fenugreek at the first waft of burnt sugar, because it turns bitter two seconds after it turns fragrant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the toasted spices to a fine powder, then grind the chillies alone and sieve out the coarse skin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the ginger, garlic, besobela and salt, then pass the whole blend through the sieve twice so no pocket of one spice survives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal in a jar away from light and leave it to settle before use; it tastes flat on day one and rounded by day three.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Berbere is a bulk ingredient, not a seasoning: eight parts chilli flesh to one part aromatic, which is why a wat takes it by the spoonful. That flesh is hygroscopic, so any chilli that still bends holds enough moisture to cake the blend and grow mould within a month. Dry them to a snap first. Fenugreek is the other trap; toast it a shade too far and the bitterness never leaves.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","fiery","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Berbere%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Berbere"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fenugreek-seed-leaf-and-blue","term":"Fenugreek: Seed, Leaf & Blue"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Curry paste","Coriander seed","Cinnamon","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"niter-kibbeh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g red onion, coarsely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g garlic, crushed flat"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g korarima pods, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fenugreek seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried koseret, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g dried besobela leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g nigella seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cassia bark"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g clove"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the butter over the lowest heat without stirring until it is fully liquid under a raft of white foam, then skim the foam away with a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide in the onion, ginger, garlic and all the spices; they should sink and sit, not sizzle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold the pan at a bare shiver with no bubbles breaking the surface until the milk solids on the base go the colour of hazelnut skin and the fat above turns clear enough to read print through, roughly an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain while hot through doubled muslin and do not press the solids; squeezing forces water and sediment back into the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave uncovered until set to a grainy pale gold; it keeps three months cold and a month in a cupboard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Butter is roughly 16 percent water and milk protein, and both cap it: it spits, it browns unevenly, and it burns near 150C (300 F). Clarified and spiced, niter kibbeh runs to about 250C (480 F), so it survives the long dry onion cook and a berbere bloom that would blacken butter. It also does work seasoning cannot: korarima, koseret and besobela are fat soluble, so the fat itself carries them into every dish. Let it boil and the solids scorch; the fat goes acrid and no straining fixes it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Niter%20Kibbeh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Niter Kibbeh"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Butter","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"key-sega-wat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900 g red onion, chopped fine and even"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g beef chuck, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g niter kibbeh"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g berbere"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g garlic, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water, plus more as needed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml tej or dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g korarima, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"9 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Put the onions alone in a heavy dry pot over medium low heat, no fat and no water, and stir every few minutes as they weep and the liquid steams off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep going until the pile has sunk to a third of its volume, gone tan, and a spoon dragged across the base leaves a dry track that does not fill in; expect 45 minutes and do not rush it.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Only now stir in half the niter kibbeh, then the garlic and ginger, and cook until the raw garlic smell turns sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the berbere and the remaining kibbeh and stir constantly over low heat until the paste darkens to brick red and beads of clear red oil rise at the edge, about eight minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the beef, the salt and 300 ml water, cover, and hold at the barest simmer, adding water in splashes whenever the surface tightens, until the meat parts under a spoon and the sauce holds a furrow.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, stir through the tej and korarima and let it sit 15 minutes before it goes to the mesob.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"The dry onion cook is the whole technique. With no fat in the pot the onion's own water boils off and its sugars caramelise against the metal instead of frying in oil, and the cell walls collapse into a pulp that becomes the body of the sauce. A wat is thickened by onion, nothing else. Add butter at the start and the onion fries: the shreds stay intact, they never break down, and you finish with thin greasy gravy and floating strings. Total braise runs about 90 minutes beyond the active work.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Reducing a sauce","Braising","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Key%20Sega%20Wat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Key Sega Wat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Wine","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","braising"]},{"slug":"kik-alicha","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g yellow split peas, rinsed until the water runs clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g red onion, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g niter kibbeh, or 80 ml sunflower oil on fasting days"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g garlic, pounded"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, pounded"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g korarima, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chillies, slit lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"9 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the split peas in the water, skimming the grey foam for the first ten minutes, until they slump into mush under the back of a spoon, about 45 minutes.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile cook the onion dry in a wide pot over low heat, stirring, until it is limp, translucent and halved in volume with no colour anywhere; the moment an edge goes gold you have gone too far.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the niter kibbeh, garlic, ginger and turmeric and cook until the fat runs clear gold and the turmeric loses its chalky raw smell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip in the peas with their liquid and simmer, stirring up from the base, until the wat mounds on a spoon and then slumps slowly, around 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt now, drop in the slit chillies and korarima, and pull it off the heat while it is still loose enough to pour, since it sets firm as it cools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Alicha is the wat with the berbere taken out, so there is no chilli to hide behind. The onions are still cooked dry, but here you are only driving off water: take them past pale gold and the caramel reads as scorch in a dish meant to taste of ginger and turmeric. Curcumin is fat soluble and almost insoluble in water, so the turmeric must go into the hot niter kibbeh, never straight into the pot liquid, or it stays dusty and chalky on the tongue.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kik%20Alicha%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kik Alicha"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Peas","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"atkilt-wat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g white cabbage, cut into 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g waxy potato, in 3 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g carrot, cut on a steep angle"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g red onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g niter kibbeh, or 60 ml sunflower oil on fasting days"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ginger, julienned"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green chilli, slit"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the onion dry in a wide pot over medium low heat until limp and translucent with no browning at all, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the niter kibbeh, garlic, ginger and turmeric and stir until the fat runs bright yellow and smells of ginger rather than raw spice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn the carrot and potato through the fat, add the water, cover, and cook until a knife enters the potato with slight resistance still at the centre.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the cabbage on top without stirring, cover again, and let it steam down until it has collapsed and gone translucent at the cut edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Only now fold everything together, salt, add the chilli, and cook uncovered until the base is nearly dry and the vegetables wear a yellow glaze instead of sitting in liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cabbage is over 90 percent water and gives all of it up at once. Put it in with the roots and the pot floods, the potato boils and breaks, and you serve grey soup. Stacking it on top instead turns it into the steam source for the carrot and potato beneath, and the delayed fold keeps the potato edges square. The uncovered finish is not optional: as the water leaves, the turmeric fat comes out of suspension and coats the vegetables.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","earthy","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, an earthy, grounding base, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Atkilt%20Wat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Atkilt Wat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Potato","Carrot","Chiles","Cabbage","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"zilzil-tibs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g beef rump or sirloin, cut with the grain into strips 1 cm thick and a finger long"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g niter kibbeh"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g red onion, in thick wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green chillies, quartered lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig rosemary, 10 cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato, 100 g, in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml tej or dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g mitmita, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pat the strips bone dry and spread them on a rack, uncovered, while the pan heats; surface water is the whole difference between searing and stewing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a heavy pan or clay dish until a flicked drop of water skitters and vanishes, add half the niter kibbeh, then lay in the beef in one uncrowded layer, working in two batches if you must.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave it untouched until the underside releases from the metal by itself and is crusted deep brown, about 90 seconds, turn once, then lift the meat out to a warm plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the onion, chillies, garlic, rosemary and tomato into the hot fat and toss until the onion edges char and the wedges soften at the surface but still snap at the core.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the beef with the rest of the kibbeh and the tej, toss for 30 seconds off the heat until the fat glosses every strip, then salt and dust with mitmita.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cutting with the grain, not across it, is what makes zilzil: thick strips whose fibres run the length, so the outside can crust while the middle stays pink. Browning needs the surface above about 150C (300 F), and a crowded pan drops below 100C the moment the meat sweats. It then boils in its own juice and comes out grey and tough. Two batches, dry meat, and a pan hot enough to hurt.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zilzil%20Tibs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zilzil Tibs"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Rosemary","Wine"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"ayib","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres whole milk, not ultra pasteurised"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g live plain yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried koseret, crumbled, optional"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g salt, optional"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the milk to 40C (104 F), whisk in the yoghurt, cover, and hold somewhere warm until it sets into a thick sour clabber that shears cleanly off a spoon, about 24 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the soured milk into a wide pan and bring it up over the lowest heat without stirring; at 50 to 55C (122 to 131 F) soft white flakes will lift away and the whey will go clear and yellow green.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold it there, nudging gently, until the curds gather into a single loose mass, then ladle them into a muslin lined sieve and drain until dripping stops.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse briefly under cold water to halt the acid, press lightly, and crumble; fork through the koseret and salt if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ayib is heat set, not rennet set. Souring has already pulled the milk down near casein's isoelectric point around pH 4.6, where the protein loses its charge and stops repelling itself; warmth then only nudges those destabilised micelles together. Push past 60C (140 F) or stir hard and the curd contracts, squeezes out water and turns to rubbery grains with half the yield. Kept low and slow it stays in moist flakes, which is what cools a wat loaded with berbere.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ayib%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ayib"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Yogurt"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"dabo-kolo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g plain wheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g niter kibbeh, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g sesame seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g berbere, for the savoury version"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml warm water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the melted niter kibbeh into the flour, salt, sesame and berbere until the mixture looks like damp sand and holds a shape when squeezed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the honey into the warm water, pour it in, and knead to a dough far stiffer than bread; it should feel like modelling clay and barely tack to the hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest it covered for 20 minutes, then roll walnut sized lumps into ropes the thickness of a pencil and cut across into pieces the size of a lentil.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast in a dry heavy pan over medium low heat, shaking every minute, until they rattle like gravel, are freckled dark brown, and no soft give remains when you crush one, 20 to 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip onto a cold tray in a single layer and leave them alone; they finish hardening as they cool, not in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a biscuit baked in a frying pan, and the dough is deliberately dry so the pieces dehydrate through rather than steam. Honey is mostly fructose, which browns at a far lower temperature than the flour needs to dry out, so a hot pan lacquers them black outside while the centres stay raw and bendy. Keep it under medium and let colour arrive late. They always feel soft while hot; judge them cold.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","earthy","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dabo%20Kolo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dabo Kolo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour","Sesame","Honey","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"imeruli-khachapuri","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain white wheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g matsoni or full fat natural yoghurt, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g soft butter, plus 30 g for finishing"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g imeruli cheese, or 400 g low moisture mozzarella with 200 g feta"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the yeast and sugar into the warm water until it loosens and foams, then work it into the flour with the matsoni, egg, salt and soft butter and knead 8 minutes, to a smooth ball that is still slightly tacky at the palm.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and leave until doubled; meanwhile grate the cheese, taste it, and if it stings with salt cover it in cold water for 20 minutes, then wring it in a cloth until no more liquid runs.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide dough and cheese in three, flatten each piece of dough to a 22 cm disc, mound the cheese in the centre, gather the edges over it like a purse and pinch the seam shut with no gaps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn seam down, press out with your palms, then roll gently from the middle to a 25 cm round about 1 cm thick, stopping the moment the dough starts to go translucent over the seam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake on a stone or steel preheated to 250C (480F) for 12 to 14 minutes, until the top is blistered brown and the base sounds hollow and dry, then brush with butter and rest 5 minutes before cutting.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1140,"estimated":true}],"note":"The dough wants an hour at 24C (75F) to double. Imeruli is a young brined curd, close to half water and aggressively salted, so it weeps and oversalts at the same time. Grate and wring it, or the steam it throws lifts the top crust clear of a raw grey base. Bake on a preheated stone so the base sets before the filling goes fully liquid and finds the seam.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Khachapuri shaping","Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny","charred","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Khachapuri shaping","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=khachapuri%20adjaruli%20shaping%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Imeruli%20Khachapuri%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Imeruli Khachapuri"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Yogurt","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Feta","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"elarji","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g coarse white maize grits (ghomi)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fine white maize flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g sulguni cheese, torn into small pieces"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the salted water to the boil, rain in the coarse grits while stirring, and cook at a bare bubble for 25 minutes, stirring often, until no grain crunches between your teeth.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rain in the fine maize flour and beat hard for 5 minutes, until the porridge pulls away from the sides of the pan as one mass.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drop the heat to its lowest setting and add the sulguni in three lots, beating with a flat wooden spoon and folding the mass over on itself.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep beating until no lumps of curd remain and the mass stretches into thick ropes when you lift the spoon 30 cm clear of the pan; serve straight away, from the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sulguni is a pasta filata curd, its protein already stretched into aligned strands, so around 60C (140F) it flows into ropes rather than pooling as fat. The maize must be fully cooked before the cheese goes in, because once it is in you cannot raise the heat again: push it and the curd tightens into rubbery knots and weeps oil. Beat without stopping. Elarji sets hard within minutes of leaving the stove.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Elarji%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Elarji"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sulguni","term":"Sulguni"}],"pantryItems":["Cheese (any)","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"satsivi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, about 1.6 kg, or the same weight of turkey legs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g shelled walnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g onions, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground blue fenugreek (utskho suneli)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground dried marigold (kviteli kvavili)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g dried red chilli flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"18 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the chicken with the bay in water just to cover, the surface barely trembling, until a leg joint moves freely, about 50 minutes; lift the bird out and keep 1 litre of the broth.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions in the butter over low heat with a little salt until translucent and completely soft with no bite left, then add half the broth and simmer 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grind the walnuts to a damp paste that beads oil when you squeeze a handful, pound in the garlic, spices and salt, and let it down with the rest of the broth, cool, to the thickness of pouring cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the onion pan off the heat, wait until it stops bubbling, stir in the walnut cream, then set it over the lowest flame at 80C (175F) for 10 minutes, stirring, never letting a bubble break the surface.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the vinegar, joint the bird, submerge the pieces in the sauce and cool to room temperature before chilling; serve cold, with the sauce thick enough to sit on the meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Walnuts are about two thirds oil, locked in cell walls; grinding frees it and the nut's own protein and fibre hold it in the broth as an emulsion. That emulsion carries half this cuisine. It breaks at a boil: the oil rises as a yellow slick and the sauce goes grainy underneath. Hold it below 85C (185F). If it does split, whisk in a ladle of cold broth off the heat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","nutty-toasted","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Satsivi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Satsivi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Turkey","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Bay leaf","Butter","Vinegar","Wine","Nuts","Coriander seed","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"chakhokhbili","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 kg chicken, jointed into 10 pieces, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g ripe tomatoes, skinned and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red chilli, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground blue fenugreek (utskho suneli)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground dried marigold (kviteli kvavili)"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g coriander leaf, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g summer savory or basil"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay the chicken skin down in a dry, wide, heavy pan over medium heat and leave it alone until it sits in a pool of its own fat and the skin is deep brown, about 12 minutes, then turn and colour the second side.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the onions and salt to that rendered fat, lower the heat, cover, and cook until they collapse to pale gold with no crunch anywhere, about 15 minutes, scraping the brown film off the base as they give up water.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Only now add the tomatoes, and simmer uncovered until they no longer taste raw and a spoon dragged across the base leaves a track that fills back slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the garlic, chilli and dried spices, cook 2 minutes, then kill the heat, fold in every herb and cover for 5 minutes so they wilt without going khaki.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true}],"note":"No oil goes in: the skin renders enough fat to cook 600 g of onion, and that fat is the flavour. The order is the entire dish. Tomato acid locks onion pectin in place, so onions that are still firm when the tomatoes land will stay firm for an hour. Collapse them first. Blue fenugreek and marigold go in near the end; both are heat labile and long boiling reduces them to hay.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chakhokhbili%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chakhokhbili"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Parsley","Basil","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"mtsvadi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg pork neck, in 5 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced into rings, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml tkemali, or the seeds of 1 pomegranate"},{"kind":"item","text":"dried grapevine prunings, or lump hardwood charcoal"}],"steps":[{"text":"Squeeze the sliced onions hard in your fists until they run with juice, then toss them through the pork with the salt and pepper and leave it cold and covered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Burn the vine wood right down until no flame is left, the embers are coated in grey ash and glow orange when you fan them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe every scrap of onion off the meat, thread it onto flat skewers with the fat edges all facing the same way, and set them 12 cm above the coals.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn every 2 minutes for about 15 minutes, until the outside is mahogany and crusted and juice beading at a cut runs clear rather than pink.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":1380,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pile the hot skewers into a covered dish with the raw onion rings for 5 minutes, then strip the meat off and serve with tkemali or pomegranate.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Pork neck is the cut because its fat runs through the muscle and bastes from inside; loin dries out long before a crust forms. Onion juice and salt only, no oil and no vinegar: acid denatures the surface protein and the meat cooks mealy. Marinate 3 hours in the cold. Wait for grey embers, because any live flame catches the dripping fat and lacquers the meat in soot.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","bright","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, acid holding everything up to the light, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mtsvadi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mtsvadi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Pomegranate"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"badrijani-nigvzit","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 large aubergines, about 900 g, sliced lengthways 8 mm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt, for drawing the slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml sunflower oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g shelled walnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground blue fenugreek (utskho suneli)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground dried marigold (kviteli kvavili)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g cayenne or dried red chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 to 90 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander leaf, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"seeds of half a pomegranate"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the slices on both sides, stand them in a colander for 30 minutes, then press each one between cloths until it feels limp and the surface is dry.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in 1 cm of oil held at 180C (355F), a few at a time, until deep gold on both faces and slack enough to roll, and drain them on a rack rather than paper so they do not steam soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the walnuts with the garlic, salt and dried spices to a coarse damp paste, or pulse them cold in short bursts, then beat in the vinegar and just enough cold water to make it spreadable and glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the chopped coriander through, spread a thick finger of paste down each slice, roll from the narrow end and stand the rolls seam down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave 30 minutes at room temperature for the paste to firm, then scatter with pomegranate seeds.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Aubergine flesh is a foam: its air pockets drink oil until the cell walls collapse, and only then does it stop. Fry hot at 180C (355F) and the collapse is quick and the slice takes little; fry cool and it empties the pan and turns to grease. Grind the walnuts cold and briefly. A hot blade shears the oil straight out and leaves a stiff dull putty that water and vinegar will not bring back.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","nutty-toasted","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Badrijani%20Nigvzit%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Badrijani Nigvzit"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fenugreek-seed-leaf-and-blue","term":"Fenugreek: Seed, Leaf & Blue"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Eggplant","Pomegranate","Vinegar","Wine","Nuts","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"tkemali","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg hard unripe tkemali plums, or underripe greengages or damsons"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander leaf and stalk"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried ombalo (pennyroyal), or dried mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 to 4 g dried red chilli flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the whole plums with the water, covered, until the skins split and the flesh slides off the stones, about 20 minutes; add no sugar at any point.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push everything through a sieve or food mill, leaving stones and skins behind, and return the puree to the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reduce over low heat, stirring, until a spoon drawn across the base leaves a channel that closes slowly and the puree mounds rather than runs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic, herbs, ombalo, ground coriander, chilli and salt to a wet green paste, stir it into the hot puree and take the pan straight off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool completely before judging it: the first taste should make you wince, then settle. Bottle and keep cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Everything rests on the fruit being hard and green. Unripe plums carry both the malic acid and the pectin; ripening converts them to sugar, so ripe fruit gives a thin sauce that tastes like jam. Never sweeten it. That acidity is also what preserves it, with ombalo, a pennyroyal, as the second preservative. Herbs go in off the heat, or the green turns grey and the scent flattens.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tkemali%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tkemali"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Stone fruit","Mint","Dill","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"kharcho","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g beef shin, in 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g long grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g tklapi (sour plum leather), or 80 ml tkemali"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g shelled walnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground blue fenugreek (utskho suneli)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground dried marigold (kviteli kvavili)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g dried red chilli flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g coriander leaf, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the beef with cold water, bring it up slowly to a bare simmer and skim the grey foam until the surface runs clear, then add the bay and cook until a piece crushes against the side of the pan, about 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onions and rice and simmer 15 minutes, until the grains are soft and starting to blur at the edges.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tear the tklapi into a ladle of hot broth on its own and whisk until it has completely dissolved, then stir it back into the pan and add more until the soup makes you blink.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the walnuts with the garlic, salt and dried spices to a paste and let it down with another ladle of hot broth to a smooth cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the heat until the surface is still, stir the walnut cream in and hold it there 10 minutes without a single bubble, then kill the heat, add the whole handful of coriander and cover for 5 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Kharcho is sour first and thick second. The sour is tklapi, plum puree dried in sheets on a board; whisk it into a ladle of broth by itself, because dropped into the pan it sets into rubbery lumps that never come apart. Add it after the rice has softened, since acid slows starch swelling and rice cooked in a sour broth stays chalky. Walnut cream last, off the boil, or it splits.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","nutty-toasted","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kharcho%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kharcho"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fenugreek-seed-leaf-and-blue","term":"Fenugreek: Seed, Leaf & Blue"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Stone fruit","Bay leaf","Rice","Nuts","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"ajapsandali","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g aubergine, in 3 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g waxy potatoes, in 3 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g red and green peppers, in wide strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g ripe tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red chilli, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground blue fenugreek (utskho suneli)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground dried marigold (kviteli kvavili)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coriander leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g summer savory or basil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the potato in the oil over medium heat until the edges are gold and a knife still meets slight resistance in the middle, then lift it out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the aubergine in the same oil, hot and in one layer, until browned and slumped, then the peppers until their skins blister and go slack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return everything to the pan with the onions, add the tomatoes and salt, cover and cook until the tomatoes have collapsed into a sauce and the potato gives completely, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pound the garlic, chilli, blue fenugreek and marigold with a pinch of salt, stir it in with the vinegar and lift the pan off the heat at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the herbs, put the lid back on for 10 minutes, and serve warm or at room temperature rather than straight from the stove.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Blue fenugreek and dried marigold are the two spices that make this cuisine sound like itself, and heat wrecks both. Their aroma sits in volatile compounds, and marigold shifts from saffron gold to brown within minutes of boiling, its scent going to old hay. Pound them raw with the garlic and stir them in as the pan leaves the flame. The rule holds across the table: fenugreek, marigold and coriander leaf go in last.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","bright","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ajapsandali%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ajapsandali"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"peppers-and-eggplant-the-nightshade-late-summer","term":"Peppers & Eggplant: The Nightshade Late Summer"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Eggplant","Parsley","Basil","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"churchkhela","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres fresh unfermented red grape must, from Saperavi or another dark grape"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g plain white wheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g walnut halves, thoroughly dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"strong linen thread and a long needle"}],"steps":[{"text":"Thread the walnut halves flat face to flat face into strings 25 cm long, knotted at the bottom and with 10 cm of bare thread left at the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the must down by about a third, skimming, until it tastes concentrated rather than fresh; this is the badagi.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slake the flour into 500 ml of cooled badagi until there is not a single lump, then pour it back into the hot pan through a sieve, whisking hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook this tatara at a low bubble, stirring constantly, for 15 minutes, until it turns glossy, coats the spoon like thin custard and no longer tastes of raw flour.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip each string, hang it to drip, and once the surface is dry to a light touch dip again; three or four coats build the sheath.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hang the strings in a dry, shaded, airy place until the outside is leathery and no longer tacky anywhere.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tatara is grape must thickened with wheat flour, and it fails two ways. Lumps: slake the flour into cool must before it meets the hot pan, never the reverse. Raw flour: it needs a full 15 minutes past thickening or the coating tastes of paste and will not keep. Each coat must dry to the touch before the next, or trapped moisture sours between the layers. Hanging takes two to four weeks, and the white bloom on a finished string is sugar, not mould.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","nutty-toasted","tangy-ferment","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Churchkhela%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Churchkhela"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Georgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Georgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Grapes","Flour","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"langos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g floury potato, boiled, riced and cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml lukewarm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre sunflower oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed into 100 ml warm salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g tejföl (20 percent sour cream)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g mild semi-hard cheese, coarsely grated"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the yeast and sugar into the lukewarm milk and leave until the surface domes and foams.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the riced potato, flour and salt to a soft tacky dough that slaps the bowl but pulls away clean, then cover and leave until doubled and slack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into eight, flatten each on an oiled palm and stretch from the centre outward until the middle is almost translucent and the rim stays thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide into 180C (355F) oil and fry until the underside is blistered deep gold, then turn once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush the hot surface with the garlic water, spread with sour cream and cover with cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fried dough drinks oil below about 170C (340F): the crust sets too slowly and the crumb soaks, giving a heavy grey lángos. Above 190C (375F) the outside colours before the middle cooks. The riced potato holds water that flashes to steam and lifts the dough, but only where it is stretched thin; a thick centre steams into a raw core. The rise takes ninety minutes.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=L%C3%A1ngos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lángos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Potato","Milk","Sour cream/Crema","Cheese (any)","Flour","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"marhaporkolt","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg beef shin or chuck, in 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g onions, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"35 g édesnemes paprika (noble sweet, ground)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ripe tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 TV paprika (Hungarian white wax pepper), sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 hot green peppers, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caraway seed, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the lard and cook the onion over a low flame, stirring, until it collapses to a soft gold mass with no white edges left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat, wait until the fat stops sizzling, then stir in the paprika and caraway until the fat runs deep red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return it to the heat with the water at once, add the beef, tomato, sliced wax pepper, whole hot peppers and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and hold at a bare shiver, adding water only when the pan threatens to catch, until a cube gives to a fork but still holds its shape.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Uncover and reduce until the sauce is glossy and clinging, thickened by onion alone, then stir in the garlic off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pörkölt, gulyás and paprikás are three dishes that the rest of the world runs together. Pörkölt is meat braised in onion with almost no added liquid; gulyás is a soup with potato and pinched pasta; paprikás is finished with soured cream. Paprika is about a tenth sugar, and past roughly 140C (285F) those sugars scorch bitter in seconds, so the pan leaves the heat first and the liquid follows at once. Two unhurried hours of simmering follow.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Reducing a sauce","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Marhap%C3%B6rk%C3%B6lt%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Marhapörkölt"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Paprika","Clove","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"gombapaprikas-nokedlival","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g brown mushrooms, thickly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onions, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g édesnemes paprika (noble sweet, ground)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 TV paprika (Hungarian white wax pepper), diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g tejföl (20 percent sour cream)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g plain flour, for the tempering"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain flour, for the nokedli"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water, for the nokedli"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g butter, for tossing"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the onion in the butter over a low flame until slack and pale gold, add the mushrooms and diced wax pepper, and cook hard until their water has boiled away and the pan squeaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat and off the boil, stir the paprika into the fat, pour in the water at once, season and simmer for ten minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the nokedli flour, eggs, water and salt to a thick sticky batter, rest it, then scrape thin ribbons off a wet board into rolling water and lift them out as they bob up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the tempering flour into the sour cream, then beat in three ladles of the hot mushroom liquid one at a time until the mixture is loose and hot to the finger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the tempered cream back into the pan off the heat, bring it only to a tremble to cook out the flour, and never let it boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the drained nokedli in butter and spoon the paprikás over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sour cream at 20 percent fat curdles when acid and heat reach its casein together: tipped cold into a boiling pan it seizes into white grains floating in thin orange liquid. Two defences work together. Flour whisked into the cream swells and shields the protein, and warming it ladle by ladle means the cream never faces more than a few degrees of change at once. After that, keep the pan below a boil.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, melting away silken."},"pairingId":39,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gombapaprik%C3%A1s%20nokedlival%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gombapaprikás nokedlival"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"tempering-eggs-and-chocolate","term":"Tempering (Eggs & Chocolate)"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Mushrooms","Butter","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"halaszle","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg whole carp, scaled and gutted, filleted with head, bones and fins kept"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g onions, roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g édesnemes paprika (noble sweet, ground)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g hot ground paprika (erős)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"carp roe and milt, if present"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 hot cherry peppers, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the fillets into thick pieces, salt them and leave them in the cold while the broth is built.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the head, bones and fins with the onion and water, uncovered, until the onion has gone to pulp and the liquid tastes strongly of fish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the whole lot through a sieve, pressing hard on the solids, then return the red brown broth to the pot and take it just off the boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir both paprikas and the cherry peppers into the still broth, salt it, and bring it back only to a lazy simmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lower in the fish and the roe and cook without a spoon touching the pot, swirling it by the handles, until the flesh parts from the bone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two rules govern this kettle. Paprika stirred into liquid at a rolling boil goes dull and bitter, because the pigment oxidises and the sugars catch on the pot floor, so it always goes in off the boil. And once the fish is in, a spoon destroys it: the collagen has only just let go and the flakes hold together by almost nothing, so the pot is swirled by its handles instead of stirred.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hal%C3%A1szl%C3%A9%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Halászlé"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Stone fruit","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"lecso","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g TV paprika (Hungarian white wax peppers), seeded and cut in wide strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g ripe tomatoes, peeled and roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g édesnemes paprika (noble sweet, ground)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 hot green pepper, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the onion in the oil over a low flame until glassy and yielding but not coloured.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat, stir in the ground paprika until the oil runs red, then set it back and tip in the pepper strips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and let the peppers sweat in their own moisture, stirring now and then, until they are limp and their skins have gone slack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes and salt and cook uncovered until the liquid has reduced to a thick red syrup that clings to the strips rather than pooling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"TV paprika is a pale thin-walled wax pepper with little heat and a great deal of water, and it collapses rather than roasts, which is the entire point. Salt it at the start and that water leaves early, so the strips boil to mush long before the tomato reduces; salt goes in with the tomatoes instead. Bell peppers give a flabby sweeter dish and skins that curl off in sheets.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lecs%C3%B3%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lecsó"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"tokfozelek","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg pale marrow squash, peeled, seeded and cut in long shreds"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g tejföl (20 percent sour cream)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dill, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g édesnemes paprika (noble sweet, ground)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the shredded marrow with the salt and leave it in a colander until it weeps and slumps to half its bulk, then squeeze it dry by the handful.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the butter and flour together, whisking, until the paste smells of biscuit and is the colour of straw, then take it off the heat and stir the paprika into the hot fat before any liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the cold water into the off-heat roux, then the sour cream, until the sauce is smooth and free of lumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the squeezed marrow and the dill and simmer until the strands turn translucent and the sauce holds a spoon track.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with the vinegar and sugar, tasting until the sourness sits clearly ahead of the sweetness.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rántás is the Hungarian roux: fat and flour cooked to blond, then paprika stirred in off the heat, while the fat is still hot enough to dissolve its pigment but no longer hot enough to burn its sugars. Pour liquid into a roux still over the flame and the starch gelatinises on contact and seizes into lumps. Off the heat, with cold liquid, the granules scatter before they swell.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=T%C3%B6kf%C5%91zel%C3%A9k%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tökfőzelék"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Squash/Pumpkin","Dill","Butter","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux"]},{"slug":"korozott","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g juhtúró (soft sheep's milk curd), at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter, soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g onion, grated to a pulp"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g édesnemes paprika (noble sweet, ground)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caraway seed, toasted and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g chives, finely cut"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the butter on its own until it is pale, light and holds a soft peak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the paprika and ground caraway into the butter and let it stand until the butter has gone apricot and smells of spice rather than of dust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the curd a spoonful at a time, then the grated onion, mustard and chives, and salt harder than feels right.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press into a bowl, cover and hold in the cold for an hour, then bring it back to cool room temperature before serving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Paprika's colour and most of its aroma sit in a fat-soluble oleoresin. Stirred into cheese alone it stays gritty and pale and the spread tastes of raw dust; creamed into soft butter first, the pigment dissolves and the whole bowl turns apricot. Butter below about 18C (65F) will not accept the curd, and the mixture breaks into wet grains that no amount of beating brings back.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=K%C3%B6r%C3%B6z%C3%B6tt%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Körözött"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Chives","Butter","Milk","Mustard","Paprika","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"kovaszos-uborka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg small ridge cucumbers, firm and unwaxed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g fine salt, not iodised"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 thick slices of sourdough rye bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 dill crowns with their stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, bruised"}],"steps":[{"text":"Trim both ends of each cucumber and split them lengthways from tip to almost the base, so brine can reach the seed core.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve the salt in the water, warming it if it will not go, and let it fall to blood heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the jar with dill, garlic and cucumbers standing upright, tuck the bread down the sides below the surface and pour the brine over so nothing floats.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover the mouth with a plate rather than a lid and stand the jar in full sun until the brine turns cloudy and the cucumbers go from opaque green to olive and translucent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the bread, strain the brine and store cucumbers and brine together in the cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The bread is a starter culture, not a flavouring: its lactobacilli seed the brine and outrun the spoilage yeasts, so two to four days in high summer sun taste clean rather than fizzy and slack. Salt at 3 percent selects for those bacteria. Anything sitting above the brine, bread included, grows white kahm yeast and softens the whole jar, so weight everything under the surface.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","earthy","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kov%C3%A1szos%20uborka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kovászos uborka"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Cucumber","Dill","Bread","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"toltott-kaposzta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg whole soured cabbage head, leaves separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g savanyú káposzta (drained sauerkraut)"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g coarsely minced pork shoulder, about a quarter fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g long grain rice, raw"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onions, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g édesnemes paprika (noble sweet, ground)"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g smoked pork ribs"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caraway seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g tejföl (20 percent sour cream), to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat half the onion in half the lard until soft, take it off the heat, stir in a third of the paprika and let it go completely cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix that cold paprika onion into the pork with the raw rice, egg, garlic, caraway, salt and pepper and beat until the mass turns tacky and holds together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pare the thick rib from each soured leaf, roll a heaped spoonful of filling into a loose parcel and tuck the ends in so the rice has room to swell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread half the sauerkraut in a heavy pot, lay the parcels and the smoked ribs on it, cover with the rest of the kraut and pour in water until it just shows at the sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the remaining onion in the rest of the lard, stir the last paprika in off the heat, pour it over the pot and hold everything at a whisper for two hours without stirring.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon cold sour cream over each portion at the table rather than into the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Raw rice is the point: it drinks the sour cabbage liquor as it swells, which is why the parcels are rolled loose. Packed tight they split, and pre-cooked rice gives a pasty filling in a watery pot. Acid slows starch gelatinisation, so kraut-braised rice needs the full two hours at a whisper. The sour cream goes on cold at the table because the pot's acidity would break it on contact.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky","tangy-ferment","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=T%C3%B6lt%C3%B6tt%20k%C3%A1poszta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Töltött káposzta"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Cabbage","Sour cream/Crema","Rice","Paprika","Clove","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"dobos-torta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs, separated, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g caster sugar, for the sponge"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour, sifted"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g unsalted butter, melted and cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 egg yolks, for the buttercream"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g caster sugar, for the buttercream"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g unsalted butter, soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g dark chocolate at 60 to 70 percent, melted and cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar, for the caramel"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g walnuts, finely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"sunflower oil, for the knife and paper"},{"kind":"item","text":"a pinch of fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whip the whites to a firm meringue with half the sponge sugar, beat the yolks with the rest until pale and ribboning, then fold the two together with the sifted flour and melted butter without knocking the air out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the batter as six thin 22 cm discs on lined trays and bake at 200C (390F) until the edges are dry and just tan, then peel each one off hot and cool it flat under a board.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the buttercream sugar and water to 118C (245F), pour it in a thread onto the beating yolks and keep beating until the bowl is no warmer than your hand before working in the soft butter, the chocolate and the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the flattest layer on oiled paper, cook the caramel sugar with the lemon juice to a clear mid amber, and pour it over, tilting to a thin even sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The moment the caramel stops running, mark it into twelve wedges with an oiled knife and cut clean through while it still yields.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack and fill the other five layers with buttercream, mask the sides and press on the ground walnuts, then prop each caramel wedge on a ridge of buttercream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The caramel lid is the whole difficulty. Sugar much past 180C (355F) turns acrid, and once it cools below roughly 100C (210F) it is glass: a knife then shatters it into shards. So it is scored and cut in the few seconds while it is still plastic, on oiled paper with an oiled blade. The lemon juice inverts some of the sugar and stops the sheet crystallising to a dull opaque slab.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Sugar stages & caramel","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dobos%20torta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dobos torta"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"kaak-al-quds","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml warm whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g white sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml dibs enab (grape molasses)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"za'atar and boiled eggs, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, yeast, sugar and salt, pour in the warm milk and oil, then knead 10 minutes until the dough pulls clean off the bowl and a stretched piece goes translucent before it tears.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into six, roll each to a rope about 40 cm long, join the ends and stretch the ring into a long oval with a hole you can put a fist through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the dibs into the warm water, dip each ring so both faces are wet, then press it into the sesame until no dough shows through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove the rings on lined trays until a fingertip dent springs back only halfway and the hole has closed to a slot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 230C (450F) for 12 to 14 minutes, until the sesame is toasted brown and the base sounds hollow when tapped.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":840,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sesame sticks because dibs is a sugar syrup, not because the dough is wet: the sugar sets tacky at the surface and glues the seeds while they toast. Dip in plain water and the seeds slide off in the oven and scorch on the tray. Open the ring wider than looks right, because proving closes it, and a sealed ka'ak steams from the inside instead of crisping.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Shaping by hand","Proofing: the rise before the oven","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","sweet-edged","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, a sweet edge rounding the corners, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ka'ak%20al-Quds%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ka'ak al-Quds"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"zaatar-the-blend","term":"Za'atar (The Blend)"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Grapes","Milk","Flour","Sesame","Sugar","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"labneh-bi-zeit-wa-zaatar","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg full-fat live yoghurt, with no added gum or starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dried wild thyme leaf (za'atar baladi)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g white sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ground sumac"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt, for the blend"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml green extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"warm flatbread, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the sea salt through the yoghurt, tip it into doubled muslin, tie the corners and hang it over a bowl in the fridge until it stops dripping and holds a peak, 12 to 24 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the sesame in a dry pan over medium heat, shaking, until the seeds turn straw gold and begin to skip, then tip them out to cool completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the dried thyme between your palms to break the leaf, then stir through the cooled sesame, the sumac and the blend salt; it should smell resinous and sour, not dusty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the labneh in a shallow bowl, hollow the surface with the back of a spoon, flood the hollow with olive oil and scatter the za'atar over only as it goes to the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt drives the drain: it collapses the casein gel so whey runs, and about 1.2 percent sets labneh firm without tasting seasoned. Yoghurt thickened with gum or starch holds its water and will never drain, so read the tub. Dress at the last moment, because sumac is powdered malic acid; once it meets the oil it wets, dulls and stops tasting sour within minutes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","nutty-toasted","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Labneh%20bi%20Zeit%20wa%20Za'atar%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Labneh bi Zeit wa Za'atar"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"labneh","term":"Labneh"},{"slug":"zaatar-the-blend","term":"Za'atar (The Blend)"}],"pantryItems":["Thyme","Yogurt","Sesame","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"msabbaha","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g dried chickpeas, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g tahini, pale and pourable"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml iced water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g Aleppo pepper (pul biber)"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of flat leaf parsley, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the soaked chickpeas, rub them with the bicarbonate in a dry pan over medium heat for 3 minutes until they squeak and smell toasted, then cover well with water and bring to a simmer.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook, skimming off the loose skins and grey foam, until a chickpea crushes to paste between finger and thumb with no chalky core, 45 to 70 minutes; keep 200 ml of the cooking liquor.","durationSec":4200,"handsOnSec":4440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic to a paste with the salt, stir in the lemon juice and leave it 10 minutes, then whisk in the tahini: it will tighten into stiff putty.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the iced water a spoonful at a time, whisking hard, until the paste loosens, pales to ivory and falls from the whisk in ribbons; season with the cumin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold two thirds of the warm chickpeas through the sauce, slacken with cooking liquor to a spoonable cream, then spread it out and top with the rest, the olive oil, Aleppo pepper and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tahini is a fat continuous paste. Adding acid or a trickle of water flips it to a water continuous emulsion, and at the crossover it seizes to putty. That stage is correct, not a failure, so keep whisking and keep adding water until it thins and pales; stop early and you serve cement. The bicarbonate raises the cooking pH so the skins dissolve and the pulses collapse; without it they stay chalky and the dish reads gritty.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Soaking dried goods back to life","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","gently hot","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Msabbaha%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Msabbaha"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Parsley","Chickpeas","Tahini","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"mujaddara-hamra","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g small brown lentils"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g coarse bulgur, grade 3"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the lentils in the water until tender but still holding their shape, about 25 minutes, and leave them sitting in their liquid.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the onions in the olive oil over medium heat, stirring often, through translucent, through gold, and on for another 10 to 15 minutes until they are dark mahogany and smell of caramel rather than raw sugar.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift a third of the onions onto paper to drain and crisp, then scrape the rest into the lentils, oil and all, with the salt, cumin and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the bulgur, bring back to a simmer, cover and cook on the lowest heat until the grain has drunk the liquid and a spoon dragged across the base leaves a clean channel, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest off the heat, still covered, for 10 minutes, then fork it loose and pile the reserved onions on top; serve warm or at room temperature.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Colour and flavour here are onion, not spice, so the pan has to run past golden into mahogany. Golden onions are only sweet and thin; the darker stage builds the faintly bitter edge that stops the lentils tasting flat. Push on to black and it turns acrid with no way back, so pull the pan the moment the smell shifts from caramel towards coffee. Coarse bulgur, not rice, and it goes in after the lentils are soft because it only needs to swell.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","sweet-edged","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, a sweet edge rounding the corners, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mujaddara%20Hamra%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mujaddara Hamra"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Couscous/Bulgur","Lentils","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"musakhan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, about 1.6 kg, jointed into six"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ground sumac"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 loaves khubz taboon or other thick flatbread"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g pine nuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the chicken with half the sumac, the allspice, cardamom, salt and 60 ml of the oil, and leave it on the side while the oven comes up to 200C (400F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions in the remaining oil over low heat with a pinch of salt, covered, until they collapse into a soft pale sludge with no bite left, about 25 minutes; do not let them take colour.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat and stir in the rest of the sumac, so the onions turn purple brown and smell sharp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast the chicken skin side up on a rack for 35 to 40 minutes, until the skin is crisp and the juices at the thigh joint run clear; fry the pine nuts in a little oil to pale gold and lift them out at once.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the sumac onions thickly over the bread, sit the chicken and its roasting juices on top, scatter the pine nuts and squeeze over the lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sumac is the acid in this dish, not a dusting: 40 g sours a kilo of onions the way a lemon would, and the bread underneath exists to drink the oil and the juice. Stir it in off the heat, because prolonged frying turns its tannins bitter and drives off the fruit. The onions are sweated pale and never browned; caramel sweetness fights the sourness instead of carrying it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","warm-spiced","sweet-edged","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, warm spice humming through the middle, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Musakhan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Musakhan"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Lemon","Nuts","Cardamom","Allspice","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"kousa-mahshi-bi-laban","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 small pale courgettes (kousa), about 1.2 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g minced lamb shoulder, fatty"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g short grain rice, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg full-fat yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg white"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dried mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g samneh or butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the stalk ends flat and core each courgette with a narrow corer, turning and drawing out flesh until the wall is about 5 mm thick and the tube runs almost to the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the lamb, rice, allspice, cinnamon and half the salt, then fill each shell two thirds full and no more, because the rice swells and a packed courgette splits along its side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand them upright and snug in a wide pan, cover with water, weight with a plate and simmer 25 minutes until a skewer meets no resistance; save 200 ml of the liquid and drain the rest.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yoghurt with the cornflour, egg white and remaining salt in a cold pan, then bring it to a full boil over medium heat, stirring without pause so the surface never stops moving, until it thickens and turns glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide in the courgettes with the reserved liquid, simmer 10 minutes uncovered, then pound the garlic with salt, fry it in the samneh with the dried mint until it smells sweet, and pour it over.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yoghurt splits on the stove because its casein has already been acid set and has nothing left holding it in suspension. Cornflour and egg white rebuild that scaffold, but only if the sauce reaches a genuine boil once, stirred without stopping; short of that it grains the moment the cold courgettes go in. Keep the pan uncovered, because condensation dripping back chills the surface locally and curdles it in patches.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Keeping the cooking liquid","Mashing & puréeing","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","herb-fresh","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kousa%20Mahshi%20bi%20Laban%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kousa Mahshi bi Laban"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Eggs","Garlic","Zucchini","Mint","Butter","Yogurt","Rice","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"kibbeh-maqliyeh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g fine bulgur, grade 1"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g lean lamb leg, well chilled and twice minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g ground clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml iced water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g minced lamb shoulder, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g pine nuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g samneh"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground allspice, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g pomegranate molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres sunflower oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the fine bulgur, squeeze it dry in your fist and leave it to swell for 10 minutes while you make the filling.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the diced onion in the samneh until soft, add the shoulder mince and cook until it loses its raw colour and the fat runs, then stir in the pine nuts, allspice and pomegranate molasses and cool it completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the lean lamb, grated onion and spices to a smooth paste, blend in the bulgur, then knead with iced water a splash at a time until the dough is tacky, cold and takes a fingerprint without cracking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With wet hands, open a walnut of dough around one finger into a shell no thicker than a coin, fill it, seal the mouth and taper both ends to a torpedo, smoothing the seam under a wet thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry a few at a time at 180C (355F) until deep brown and floating, 4 to 5 minutes, then drain them standing on end so the seams stay dry.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fine bulgur is the binder, not a filler: it hydrates on the meat's own moisture and its starch glues the shell together. That is why the shell meat must be lean and twice minced. Fat in the dough renders in the fryer, steams, and blows the seam open. Iced water keeps the paste below the temperature at which fat smears and turns it greasy and short. Any shell thicker than a coin burns outside before the middle is hot.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","sweet-edged","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kibbeh%20Maqliyeh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kibbeh Maqliyeh"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Pomegranate","Couscous/Bulgur","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"loubieh-bi-zeit","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g flat romano green beans, topped and tailed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe tomatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onions in the olive oil over low heat until soft and just colouring at the edges, then add the garlic and cook one minute more, until it smells sweet rather than raw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the beans and turn them in the oil for about 8 minutes, until they lose their squeak and the raw green dulls to olive.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Only now stir in the grated tomato, the paste, the salt, pepper and water, and simmer uncovered until the beans slump when lifted on a spoon and the oil pools clear and red at the edge of the pan, 25 to 35 minutes.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":2340,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take off the heat, sharpen with lemon to taste, and let it come down to room temperature before serving; fridge cold flattens both the oil and the tomato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beans first, tomato second. Acid keeps pectin cross linked, so beans dropped into tomato at the start can simmer an hour and still chew like leather. Soften them in oil until they are already yielding, then acidify. The oil is the cooking medium rather than a dressing, and the dish is done when it splits and the beans sit in a clear pool. Squeaky and bright green is the failure here, not the goal.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","aromatic","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Loubieh%20bi%20Zeit%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Loubieh bi Zeit"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Green beans","Lemon","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"muhammara","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g red romano peppers"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g shelled walnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g coarse dried breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g pomegranate molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g Aleppo pepper (pul biber)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lemon juice, if needed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blacken the peppers whole over a flame or under a hot grill until the skin is loose and papery all over, close them in a bowl for 10 minutes, then rub the skin away without rinsing, or the smoke goes down the sink.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toast the walnuts in a low oven for about 8 minutes, until they smell of butter and the skins craze, then cool them fully; warm walnuts grind to a bitter oily sludge.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound or briefly pulse the walnuts, breadcrumbs, garlic, Aleppo pepper, cumin and salt to a coarse rubble, stopping well short of powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the peppers and pomegranate molasses, work to a paste that still has grain in it, then beat in the olive oil in a slow stream until it turns glossy and holds a ridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest an hour and taste: it should read sweet, then sour, then a slow warm heat. Correct with molasses or lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The breadcrumbs are structural. Peppers are mostly water, and without a starch to hold it the walnut oil and pepper juice separate into a puddle inside the hour. Aleppo pepper is sun dried and salted, so it brings raisin sweetness and a slow heat; cayenne in its place makes the dish sharp and one dimensional. Pomegranate molasses is the acid and also the sugar, so add it last and in stages or it slides into jam.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, a sweet edge rounding the corners, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Muhammara%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Muhammara"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Pomegranate","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Sugar","Nuts","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"halawet-el-jibn","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g akkawi cheese, or low-moisture mozzarella"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g fine semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml orange blossom water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g ashta or thick clotted cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g pistachios, finely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml rose water"}],"steps":[{"text":"If you are using akkawi, cube it and soak in several changes of cold water over about 8 hours, tasting until only a whisper of salt is left, then drain and pat it dry.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil the sugar, water and lemon juice until the syrup runs off a spoon in a thread rather than drops, take it off the heat and stir in the orange blossom water; set half aside for serving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the other half of the syrup in a wide pan over low heat, add the cheese and stir until it melts into long strings, then rain in the semolina and keep stirring until the mass leaves the sides in one glossy sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip it onto a syrup wetted surface and knead with a wet palm until smooth and elastic, then press or roll it out to about 3 mm and cut into strips a hand wide.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe a line of ashta down each strip, roll it up, chill briefly, then dress with the reserved syrup, the ground pistachio and the rose water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cheese is the dough here: heat melts casein into stretchable strands and the semolina starch fixes them into a pliable sheet. Both failures are temperature. Too hot and the curd tightens and squeezes out its fat, leaving greasy rubber that tears instead of rolling; too cool and the semolina stays raw and grits the teeth. Hold the pan low and stop the second it comes together. Akkawi is brined, so desalt it or the sweet turns savoury.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Kneading dough"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Halawet%20el%20Jibn%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Halawet el Jibn"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-floral-pantry-rose-orange-blossom-and-mahleb","term":"The Floral Pantry: Rose, Orange Blossom & Mahleb"},{"slug":"akkawi-and-nabulsi","term":"Akkawi & Nabulsi"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Orange","Cream","Cheese (any)","Mozzarella","Semolina","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"nan-e-barbari","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g water at 30C (86F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g plain flour, for the roomal glaze"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g water, for the roomal glaze"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g white sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g nigella seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the bread flour, 350 g water, yeast, salt and oil to a shaggy mass, rest 20 minutes, then stretch and fold in the bowl every 20 minutes until the dough peels off the sides in one smooth sheet.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the 15 g plain flour, 200 g water, bicarbonate of soda and sugar in a small pan and simmer, stirring, to the thickness of single cream; cool it completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bulk ferment until the dough is domed and doubled, then tip it out and shape one long oval on parchment without knocking the gas from the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush the cooled roomal on thickly, then drag four fingers the length of the loaf to plough deep furrows, and scatter the sesame and nigella into them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake on a stone at 260C (500F) until the furrow ridges are dark amber and the base sounds hollow, 12 to 14 minutes.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":840,"estimated":false}],"note":"The roomal is the whole trick. Its gelatinised starch seals the surface so the crust stays glossy and pliable rather than crisping shut, and the bicarbonate raises surface pH so browning runs hard and fast before the crumb dries. Skip it, or brush it on thin and watery, and you get a pale papery loaf whose furrows close up in the oven instead of holding their shape.","equipment":["Parchment"],"techniques":["Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nan-e%20Barbari%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nan-e Barbari"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour","Sesame","Sugar","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"zereshk-polo-ba-morgh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g aged basmati rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 L water, for the par-boil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fine salt, for the par-boil water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g saffron threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caster sugar, for grinding with the saffron"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg bone-in skinless chicken thighs"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt, for the chicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g thick plain yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g dried barberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g caster sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grind the saffron with the 5 g sugar to a powder and steep it in 60 ml water just off the boil until the liquid is deep orange, about 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the onions in 45 ml of the oil until deep gold, add the turmeric, pepper, chicken, 8 g salt and half the saffron water, cover and cook low until the thigh meat slides off the bone under a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the rice until the water runs clear, then boil it in the 3 L of water with the 60 g salt until a grain bends without snapping and a bitten one still shows a chalky core, 5 to 7 minutes; drain at once.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":660,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold two ladles of drained rice with the yoghurt and a spoonful of saffron water, spread it over the remaining 45 ml oil and half the butter in the pot and press it flat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mound the rest of the rice in a cone, drive five holes to the base with a spoon handle, drizzle over 60 ml water, wrap the lid in a tea towel and steam on the lowest heat until the pot smells of toasted crust, 45 to 50 minutes.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":3240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Swirl the rinsed barberries with the 20 g sugar in the last of the butter off the heat for under a minute, just until they plump and go glossy red, then scatter them over the rice with the rest of the saffron.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rice takes salt only while it boils, so that water is brined like pasta water; once drained it steams in its own moisture and absorbs nothing further. Drain while the core is still chalky. A grain boiled through has no dry starch left to set into a separate strand and the 45 minute steam turns it to porridge. Bloom the saffron ground, warm and wet: the colour is water soluble and needs twenty minutes to leave the thread.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","rich","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, richness that coats and comforts, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zereshk%20Polo%20ba%20Morgh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zereshk Polo ba Morgh"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Butter","Yogurt","Rice","Sugar","Turmeric","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"ghormeh-sabzi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g lamb shoulder, cut in 3 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g flat-leaf parsley leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g coriander leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g garlic chives or leek greens"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dried fenugreek leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g dried red kidney beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 dried limes (about 30 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the onions in 45 ml of the oil until deep gold, add the turmeric and the lamb and brown the meat on all faces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the parsley, coriander and chives very fine and fry them in the remaining oil over medium heat, stirring, until the volume collapses by two thirds and the smell turns dark and nutty, 15 to 20 minutes; stir the fenugreek in only for the final 2 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1560,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip the herbs onto the meat, add the drained beans and the water, and pierce each dried lime twice with a skewer before dropping it in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer with the lid ajar until the lamb yields to a spoon, the beans are creamy and a slick of dark green oil stands on the surface, about 2.5 hours.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the limes against the side of the pot to release their juice, then taste and correct salt and sourness.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Frying the herbs down is what makes the stew dark and oily rather than grassy; raw herbs give a soup the colour of pond water. Fenugreek is the exception and about a tenth of the herb weight, added at the very end: the dry leaf has no moisture to protect it, scorches in seconds, and scorched fenugreek turns the whole pot bitter. Pierce the dried limes rather than splitting them, since the harshest bitterness sits in the seeds.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Soaking dried goods back to life","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","earthy","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghormeh%20Sabzi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ghormeh Sabzi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Leek","Lime","Cilantro","Parsley","Chives","Kidney/Pinto beans","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"khoresh-e-fesenjan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g shelled walnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg bone-in skinless chicken thighs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g pomegranate molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 g saffron threads, ground and steeped in 30 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the walnuts in a dry pan over medium heat until they smell of nut oil but are still pale, then grind them to a fine, oily paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the onion in the oil, colour the chicken on both sides in the same pan, season with the salt and pepper and lift the meat out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the walnut paste into the water in a heavy pot and cook on the lowest heat, scraping the base every few minutes, until it goes from grey-beige to milk chocolate and a slick of oil rises, 45 to 60 minutes.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the pomegranate molasses, sugar and saffron, return the chicken, and simmer uncovered until the sauce is mahogany and coats the back of a spoon, about 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2640,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste and settle the balance with more molasses or sugar; it should read sour first and sweet second.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Colour is the doneness gauge. Ground walnut held at a bare simmer long enough releases its oil, and the solids then toast in it, which both darkens the sauce and thickens it with no flour anywhere. The paste sinks and catches, so it wants a heavy pan, the lowest flame and a spoon across the base every few minutes; burnt walnut is acrid and no quantity of molasses or sugar will bury it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","nutty-toasted","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Khoresh-e%20Fesenjan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Khoresh-e Fesenjan"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Pomegranate","Sugar","Nuts","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"kabab-e-koobideh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g lamb shoulder, twice minced, about 20 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.3 g saffron threads, ground and steeped in 20 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground sumac, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the onion on the coarse side of a box grater, then wring it in a cloth until no more juice runs and discard every drop of that juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead the mince with the onion pulp, salt, pepper and turmeric for a full 8 to 10 minutes, until the mass turns tacky and hangs from an upturned palm without falling.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill the mixture for an hour, then with wet hands press it onto flat 2.5 cm skewers in an even 2 cm ribbon, pinching ridges every 3 cm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot coals with the skewers raised off the bars, turning every 30 seconds and never letting the kebab hang still, until juice beads on the surface and the ridges are browned, 5 to 6 minutes.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Baste with the saffron melted into the butter in the last turn, grill the tomatoes alongside until their skins split, and dust with sumac.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt plus hard kneading pulls myosin out of the mince into a sticky sol, and that sol is the only thing gluing meat to metal. Knead until it clings to an upturned palm. Onion juice wrecks the bond, hence the wringing, and below roughly 20 percent fat the ribbon cracks as it sets. Turn every half minute: the protein must firm all the way round before the underside softens and drops the lot into the coals.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","rich","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kabab-e%20Koobideh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kabab-e Koobideh"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Tomato","Butter","Turmeric","Saffron","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"ash-e-reshteh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g dried chickpeas, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g dried red kidney beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g green lentils"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"450 g onions, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g flat-leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g spinach, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g coriander, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g garlic chives or spring onion greens, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g reshteh noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g kashk"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dried mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained chickpeas and kidney beans in the water until they crush easily between finger and thumb, adding the lentils for the last 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the onions in half the oil until deep brown and reserve half of them; soften the garlic in the same pan, stir in the turmeric and tip pan and all into the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the parsley, spinach, coriander and chives by the bowlful, salt, and simmer until the ash is dark green and a spoon dragged across leaves a track that closes slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Break in the reshteh and cook until soft, stirring often so it cannot settle and catch; its starch is what gives the soup its body.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the remaining oil off the heat, wait fifteen seconds, then stir in the dried mint until it darkens and smells of mint toffee, no longer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pot off the boil, stir in three quarters of the kashk, then swirl the rest over each bowl with the mint oil and the reserved fried onions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things go in off the boil. Kashk is soured, dried whey whose proteins already sit near the point where acid drops them out of suspension, so a rolling boil curdles it into chalky grains and greys the soup. Dried mint has no water in it to buy you time and blackens to something acrid in about five seconds over direct heat, so pull the oil off the flame and let residual heat do the work.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ash-e%20Reshteh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ash-e Reshteh"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Spinach","Parsley","Mint","Chives","Noodles","Lentils","Chickpeas","Kidney/Pinto beans","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"mirza-ghasemi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg aubergines"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 garlic cloves, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe tomatoes, skinned and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char the whole aubergines directly on a gas flame or over coals, turning, until the skin is black and blistered everywhere and the body collapses when pressed, 15 to 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Peel them while hot, drain the flesh in a sieve for 10 minutes, then chop it coarsely with a knife.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the garlic in the oil over medium-low heat until pale gold and sweet smelling, never brown, then stir in the turmeric for 20 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the aubergine and fry, pressing and turning, until the water has gone and it sizzles rather than steams; add the tomatoes, salt and pepper and cook until no liquid pools when you draw the spoon through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the mixture aside, break the eggs into the bare pan, let them just set, then fold them through in streaks rather than beating them in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The smoke is the seasoning: charring over a live flame lays phenols onto the flesh through the split skin, which is why an oven-baked aubergine gives you a sweet, flat, entirely different dish. Drain the hot flesh before it hits the pan or its water holds the mixture at boiling point and it stews grey instead of frying. Chop by hand; a blender shears the pectin-rich flesh into glue.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Charring over open flame","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","charred","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, char used as punctuation, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mirza%20Ghasemi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mirza Ghasemi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Garlic","Tomato","Eggplant","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"torshi-liteh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg aubergines"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g mint leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g tarragon leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g coriander leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dried summer savory"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g garlic, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g nigella seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground golpar"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char the aubergines whole over a flame until collapsed, peel them, chop the flesh fine and press it in a colander under a weight until it stops dripping, about an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the vinegar to a boil, then cool it completely before it goes near the herbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the mint, tarragon and coriander very fine and mix them with the pressed aubergine, savory, garlic, nigella, golpar, salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in enough of the cold vinegar to make a loose, spoonable mash, pack it into a sterilised jar and tap out the air pockets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Top with a finger of vinegar as a seal, cap, and keep in the dark until the raw vinegar edge has gone and the mash smells of herbs rather than acid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The aubergine must be pressed dry before it meets the vinegar, because its water dilutes the acid and a pickle that drifts above roughly pH 4 grows mould instead of souring. Keep a centimetre of vinegar standing over the surface at all times and push the solids back under after every spoonful; whatever sits proud of the liquid is the part that spoils. It needs six weeks in the dark before it is worth opening.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Torshi%20Liteh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Torshi Liteh"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Eggplant","Cilantro","Mint","Tarragon","Vinegar","Wine","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"salad-e-shirazi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g small ridged cucumbers"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g firm ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g red onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"75 ml lime juice or verjuice"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Quarter the tomatoes and scrape out the seed jelly, keeping only the firm walls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the tomato walls, cucumber and onion into an even 5 mm dice; the uniformity is the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the diced onion under cold water and squeeze it dry in a cloth to pull the sulphurous heat out of it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crush the dried mint between your palms into the lime juice and leave it 5 minutes to swell and go fragrant.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Dress with the mint juice, oil, salt and pepper only as the bowl goes to the table, and toss just twice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt draws water out of cut cucumber and tomato within minutes, so a salad dressed in the kitchen arrives as pink brine with limp dice adrift in it. Salt and acid go on at the table, and the tomato seed jelly is scraped out for the same reason. Use dried mint rather than fresh, and give it five minutes in the lime juice first, or it stays as dust on the tongue instead of perfuming the dressing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salad-e%20Shirazi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salad-e Shirazi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Cucumber","Lime","Mint"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"sholeh-zard","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g short-grain pudding rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g saffron threads, ground and steeped in 60 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml rosewater"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g slivered almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g pistachios, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the rice, then simmer it uncovered in the water with the salt, stirring now and then, until the grains have burst and there is no bite left at all, about 50 minutes.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":3000,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Only now stir in the sugar and butter; the pot will slacken and go soupy before it thickens again over the next 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the saffron and its water and cook on until a spoon dragged across the base leaves a track that closes slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, stir through the rosewater and the almonds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into shallow dishes while still loose and leave to cool to room temperature, then pattern the tops with cinnamon and pistachio.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sugar goes in only once the grain has collapsed. In syrup it competes with the starch for water and lifts the temperature at which that starch gelatinises, so rice cooked with sugar from the start stays chalky however long you give it. Rosewater goes in off the heat, its aroma being volatile. Pull the pot while it still looks too loose: amylose retrogrades as it cools and sets it firm in the dish.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","nutty-toasted","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sholeh%20Zard%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sholeh Zard"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Rice","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"tiradito-nikkei","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g sole or corvina loin, sashimi grade, skinned"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml juice of small key limes (limón sutil)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ají amarillo, deveined and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ají limo, deveined and cut in fine strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml sillao (light soy sauce)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml cold dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g kion (fresh ginger), grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 ice cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g coriander leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Firm the loin in the freezer for 15 minutes, then read the direction of the muscle fibres and set the knife across them at 45 degrees.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Take each slice in one long pull, 3 mm thick, and lay them flat and just overlapping on a chilled plate; a sawing stroke shreds the fibres and the fish weeps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the ají amarillo, garlic, kion, salt, ice and half the lime to a loose sauce, then stir in the sillao, the dashi and the rest of the lime; it should sting first and read salty second.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the cold sauce over the fish at the moment of serving, scatter the ají limo and coriander, and carry it out while the slices are still translucent at the centre.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lime does not marinate, it denatures. Acid unwinds the surface proteins from the outside in, so a 3 mm slice cut across the grain goes opaque in under a minute and stays juicy. Cut with the grain, or let the fish sit in the sauce, and the fibres contract, wring out their water and turn chalky. Sauce it at the pass, never before.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi","Ceviche"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","umami-deep","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Ceviche","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ceviche%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tiradito%20Nikkei%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tiradito Nikkei"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sashimi-grade-and-ikejime","term":"Sashimi-Grade & Ikejime"}],"pantryItems":["White fish","Garlic","Ginger","Lime","Cilantro","Soy sauce","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["dashi"]},{"slug":"papa-a-la-huancaina","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g yellow waxy potatoes (papa amarilla), unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 fresh ají amarillo, halved and deveined"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g queso fresco"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 soda crackers"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a red onion, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, hard boiled"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g black botija olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 lettuce leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the deveined ají for 2 minutes, drain, and repeat twice with fresh water; the flesh should slump and the raw green smell give way to apricot.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic in the oil over medium heat until translucent with no colour, add the drained ají and cook 2 minutes more, until the oil stains orange. Cool to room temperature.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the potatoes whole in well salted water until a skewer meets no resistance, about 20 minutes, peel them warm, then cool and slice 1 cm thick.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the cooled ají base with the queso fresco, crackers, evaporated milk and salt in short bursts, to a sauce that ribbons off the spatula and holds its line for a second.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the potatoes on lettuce, top with egg and olives, and serve at room temperature; fridge cold, the sauce sets stiff and reads only of raw onion.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ají amarillo keeps its heat in the pale placenta and its bitterness in a waxy skin. Devein it and boil it through three changes of water and you keep the fruit and the apricot smell without the burn. The sauce is an oil in water emulsion held by cracker starch and cheese protein: blend it long and friction heat melts the fat out of the cheese, leaving it grainy and greasy. Short bursts, cool ingredients.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Salted water & the float test","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Papa%20a%20la%20Huanca%C3%ADna%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Papa a la Huancaína"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"queso-fresco","term":"Queso Fresco"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Lettuce/Greens","Olives","Milk","Cheese (any)","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"salsa-criolla","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 large red onions, about 300 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ají limo, deveined and cut in fine strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g coriander leaves, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g black pepper, coarsely cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Halve the onions through root and tip, then slice each half lengthwise, following the lines on the skin, into 2 mm feathers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soak the feathers in salted ice water for 10 minutes, until the fumes stop catching at the back of the throat, then drain and pat them properly dry.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toss with the ají limo, lime, salt, pepper and oil no more than 10 minutes before serving; the feathers should still snap when you bite one.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the coriander through at the last second and check the salt; it should taste sharp, saline and barely oily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Slice the onion pluma, root to tip, so the blade runs parallel to the cells rather than bursting them; that alone halves the sulphur burn. Ten minutes in salted ice water leaches out the rest, then drain hard, because carried water dilutes the lime. Dress it within ten minutes of the table: salt pulls water from the shreds and they slump grey and limp.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salsa%20Criolla%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salsa Criolla"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Lime","Cilantro"]},{"slug":"solterito-arequipeno","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 cobs choclo, the big kernelled Andean corn"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fresh broad beans (habas), podded"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g queso fresco, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ripe tomato, seeded and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a red onion, in fine feathers"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 rocoto, deveined and finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g black botija olives, torn off the stone"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g dried oregano, crumbled between the palms"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the choclo in unsalted water until a kernel is chalky tender but still snaps between the teeth, about 25 minutes; salt in the water toughens the skins. Cool, then cut the kernels off.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanch the habas 3 minutes, chill them in ice water and pinch each one out of its grey second skin; they should come out bright green and yield with no chalk.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soak the onion feathers in ice water for 10 minutes, then drain and dry them hard.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toss everything except the cheese with the vinegar, oil, oregano and salt, then fold the queso fresco through last so the dice stay square.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 5 minutes and taste: the vinegar should sit behind the vegetables, and the rocoto should arrive as heat only after you swallow.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"Rocoto is Capsicum pubescens, a different species from the ajíes: thick fleshed, apple crisp, with its heat locked in the pale placenta and the black seeds. Strip both and it eats like a sweet pepper. Habas must lose their second skin after blanching or they stay leathery and taste of chalk. Dress it late, because vinegar standing on queso fresco draws it wet and crumbly.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Solterito%20Arequipe%C3%B1o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Solterito Arequipeño"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Corn","Olives","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"carapulcra-limena","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g papa seca, dried potato"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g pork shoulder, in 3 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ají panca paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ají amarillo paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g roasted peanuts, ground fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre light stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the papa seca in a dry heavy pan over medium heat, shaking constantly, until it smells of popcorn and has gone a shade darker; tip it out at once, cover with warm water and leave it to swell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the pork hard in the oil in the same pan until the cubes release from the metal on their own and the base is mahogany, then lift them out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the heat, sweat the onion and garlic in the fat until soft, then add both ají pastes and cook, stirring, until the oil splits out of the paste and runs red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the pork with the cumin, cloves, bay and stock, cover, and hold at a bare tremble until the meat gives to the edge of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain the papa seca, stir it in with the ground peanuts and cook uncovered until the potato is tender and the pot holds the mark of the spoon; loosen with stock if it seizes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Papa seca is potato boiled, peeled and sun dried. Toast it in a bare dry pan until it smells of popcorn before soaking, or it stays gritty and tastes of dust; give it an hour in warm water. It drinks three times its weight, so hold back stock to loosen the pot at the end. Fry the ají panca in fat over medium heat until the oil runs red: a dry hot pan scorches its sugars and the whole dish turns bitter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","earthy","nutty-toasted","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carapulcra%20Lime%C3%B1a%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carapulcra Limeña"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Lime","Bay leaf","Peanut","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"seco-de-cordero-a-la-nortena","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg lamb shoulder on the bone, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g coriander, leaves and tender stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ají amarillo paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ají panca paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml chicha de jora, or a dark beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large red onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g zapallo loche or butternut squash, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g cooked canario beans, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the lamb and brown it in the oil in a heavy pot, in batches, until every face is deep brown and the base of the pot is sticky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the meat out, sweat the onion and garlic until translucent, then add the ají pastes and cumin and fry until the oil separates and stains red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deglaze with the chicha, scraping the base clean, return the lamb with the grated squash and water to come halfway up, cover, and cook at a shiver for about 90 minutes, until the meat pulls off the bone under a spoon.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the coriander with 150 ml cold water to a bright green puree and keep it cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the puree in uncovered for the last 10 minutes only, and pull the pot off the heat while the sauce is still grass green and smells raw and citric. Serve with the beans and rice.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The coriander goes in raw, blended, and only for the last ten minutes. Its aroma lives in volatile aldehydes that boil off, and its chlorophyll turns olive drab under long heat and acid, so coriander added at the start gives a grey braise that tastes of hay. Chicha de jora supplies the mild acid that loosens collagen; a dark beer will stand in, plain water will not.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Seco%20de%20Cordero%20a%20la%20Norte%C3%B1a%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Seco de Cordero a la Norteña"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Squash/Pumpkin","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"arroz-chaufa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g cooked long grain rice, a day old and fridge cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g chicken thigh, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs, beaten with a pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 spring onions, whites and greens kept separate"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml sillao, light and dark soy mixed"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml toasted sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g kion (fresh ginger), minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml neutral oil with a high smoke point"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, in strips"}],"steps":[{"text":"Break the cold rice apart with wet hands until no grain clings to another; if it smears, it is still too warm to fry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the wok to smoking, oil it and set a thin omelette from the beaten eggs; slide it out, roll it and cut it into ribbons.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Re-oil, lay the chicken in a single layer and leave it until it releases from the metal and is browned, then add the kion, garlic, red pepper and onion whites for 20 seconds, until fragrant and just short of colouring.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the rice and toss it against the hot metal until the grains dance loose and a faint scorched smell rises.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the sillao down the hot side of the wok, toss once, then kill the heat and fold in the egg ribbons, the onion greens, the sesame oil and the white pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wok hei is a heat problem. The rice must be cold and a day old so its surface starch has retrograded and the grains stay separate; warm rice steams and welds into clumps. Cook in two batches, because a full wok falls below the browning threshold and you braise instead of fry. Pour the sillao down the hot side so it hits bare metal and caramelises rather than pooling into a puddle.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","smoky","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arroz%20Chaufa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arroz Chaufa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Bell pepper","Rice","Sesame","Clove"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"pan-con-chicharron","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg pork belly, skin on, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g orange sweet potato (camote), in 1 cm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 crusty white rolls (pan francés)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 quantity salsa criolla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Put the belly, water, salt, garlic, cumin and pepper in a wide heavy pan and simmer uncovered, the meat barely covered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until the water has gone and the sound changes from soft bubbling to a sharp crackle, roughly 45 minutes, then stop stirring and let the cubes fry in their own rendered fat.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn them once the underside is amber and the skin has blistered glassy, then lift onto a rack; they should sound hollow when knocked together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the camote slices in a little of the rendered fat until the edges caramelise and the centre gives to a knife.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split the warm rolls, lay in camote, then chicharrón sliced thick, then a fistful of salsa criolla, and eat it standing up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The water is the technique. Belly dropped straight into hot fat sets its collagen before it can melt, so it fries hard outside and stays rubbery inside. Simmered in water first, the collagen turns to gelatin and the fat renders, and the pan cannot pass 100C (212F) until the water has gone, so nothing burns early. Listen: bubbling means water, crackling means fat, and the frying has begun.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","aromatic","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pan%20con%20Chicharr%C3%B3n%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pan con Chicharrón"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Potato","Sweet potato","Orange","Bread","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"chicha-morada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g maíz morado, whole purple corn cobs"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"peel and core of 1 pineapple"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 quinces or green apples, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cinnamon sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green apple, in fine dice, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Snap the cobs in half and put them in the pot with the water, pineapple peel, quince, cinnamon and cloves.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring to the boil and hold at a lazy simmer for 45 minutes, until the liquid is an opaque ink purple and the cobs have gone pale and spent.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain through a fine sieve without pressing the solids, then cool completely; pressing pushes starch through and the drink turns cloudy and thin tasting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the sugar into the cold liquid until it dissolves, then the lime: the colour should jump from slate to ruby as it goes in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve over ice with the diced apple stirred through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The colour lives in anthocyanins in the pericarp of the corn, so the whole cob goes in, kernels and core together, and the boil is 45 unattended minutes. Anthocyanins are pH indicators: in neutral water the brew reads a dull slate purple, and lime juice tips it to ruby. Add the lime and sugar only once it is cold, because lime aromatics boil off and a long hot steep flattens the colour.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicha%20Morada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicha Morada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Corn","Lime","Apple","Pineapple","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"suspiro-a-la-limena","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg whites"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml port"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the two milks together in a heavy pan over medium heat, scraping the whole base with a flat spatula, until the mixture is the colour of wet sand and a track drawn across the pan stays open for a beat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, whisk a ladle of the hot manjar into the yolks with the vanilla, return it all to the pan and stir over low heat for 2 minutes until it coats the spatula thickly; never let it boil or the yolks curdle into threads.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into glasses and chill until cold and set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the port and sugar without stirring to 118C (244F), the point where a drop pressed in cold water rolls into a soft ball.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites with the salt to soft peaks, pour the syrup in a thin thread down the side of the bowl and beat until the meringue is glossy, holds a stiff hook and the bowl is only blood warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon it over the cold manjar and dust with cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Manjar blanco is lactose and milk protein browning in a shrinking amount of water, so the pan base runs far hotter than the bulk and scorches the moment you stop scraping. Use a flat spatula, edge to edge. The port syrup must reach 118C (244F): that concentration cooks the whites and sets the foam. Below 115C (239F) the meringue weeps and slides off the manjar in the glass.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","floral","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a floral thread lifting the aroma, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Suspiro%20a%20la%20Lime%C3%B1a%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Suspiro a la Limeña"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lime","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"kiri-bath","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g short grain white rice, washed until the water runs clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thick coconut milk, first extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the rice and water to a boil in a heavy pan, cover, and cook on the lowest heat until no free water remains and the grains have burst open.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the thick coconut milk with the salt and stir steadily over low heat; the mass loosens first, then begins to thicken again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep stirring until a spoon dragged across the base leaves a track that closes slowly, and the rice tastes creamy rather than milky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip onto a tray, flatten to 3 cm with a wet spatula, and leave until completely cold and firm before cutting into diamonds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coconut fat coats the starch granules and stops them swelling, so rice cooked in coconut milk from the start stays chalky while the milk scorches on the pan floor. Cook the grains in plain water first, then stir the thick milk in at the end and drive it off until the mass holds a track. Cut only when stone cold; warm kiri bath smears.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kiri%20Bath%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kiri Bath"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"appa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g rice flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cooked short grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thin coconut milk, warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml thick coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"coconut oil for the pan"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the cooked rice with 150 ml of the warm thin coconut milk to a smooth cream, then whisk it into the rice flour with the sugar, yeast and remaining thin milk until lump free and the consistency of single cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and leave in a warm place until the surface domes, carries fine bubbles all over, and smells faintly of beer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the thick coconut milk and salt; the batter should now coat a spoon thinly and run off it in a sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a hopper pan or small deep wok over medium heat, wipe with coconut oil, pour in a small ladleful and immediately swirl so batter climbs the walls in a thin film.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and cook without moving until the rim is dry, lacy and lifting away from the metal while the centre is set but still soft, then run a thin spatula round and lift it out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yeast gas gives the lace; the swirl gives the shape. Batter at single cream consistency climbs the hot bowl as a film that dries to a crisp rim, while the pool at the base steams soft under the lid. Too thick and it slides back into a pancake. Ferment 8 to 10 hours at warm room temperature; past about twelve it sours and the walls slump.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Appa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Appa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique"]},{"slug":"parippu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g masoor dal, split red lentils, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thin coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml thick coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small red onion, 80 g, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp unroasted Sri Lankan curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 fenugreek seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 cm piece pandan leaf, rampe"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dried red chillies, broken"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green chilli, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 cm Ceylon cinnamon quill"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a lime"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the lentils uncovered with the thin coconut milk, turmeric, curry powder, green chilli, cinnamon and half the salt until the lentils have lost their shape and the liquid holds a line drawn through it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile heat the coconut oil in a small pan and fry the fenugreek, onion, garlic, curry leaves, pandan and dried chillies until the onion is deep gold and the curry leaves crackle and turn glassy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape the whole tempering into the lentils, oil included, and stir it through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat, stir in the thick coconut milk and the rest of the salt, then warm it back only until steam rises; it must not bubble.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze in the lime off the heat and check the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Unroasted curry powder is the point here: the coriander, cumin and fennel go in pale, so the dish stays yellow and tastes green and fresh rather than toasted. Thick coconut milk goes in off the boil, because that emulsion splits at a simmer and beads oil across the top. Fenugreek thickens but turns bitter fast, so count the seeds; ten suits 200 g of lentils.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","rich","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Parippu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Parippu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Milk","Coconut milk","Lentils","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"kukul-mas-curry","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg bone in chicken thighs and drumsticks, skin removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large red onion, 150 g, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thin coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml thick coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp roasted Sri Lankan curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves and 3 cm ginger, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 cm pandan leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 cm Ceylon cinnamon quill"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cardamom pods, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a lime"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the chicken with the roasted curry powder, chilli, turmeric, pepper, garlic ginger paste and salt, and leave it while you slice the aromatics.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the coconut oil and fry the onion, curry leaves, pandan, cinnamon, cardamom and cloves until the onion edges brown and the leaves smell of toasted spice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chicken and turn it until the rub darkens, stops looking dusty and clings to the meat, then pour in the thin coconut milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered, turning the pieces occasionally, until the meat pulls back from the drumstick bone and the gravy has reduced by about a third and gone glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, stir in the thick coconut milk and the lime, then return it to the stove only until steam rises.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Roasted curry powder is already at the far edge of toasting: coriander, cumin and fennel taken to the colour of dark chocolate before grinding. Bloom it in hot oil the way you would a raw masala and it goes past toast into ash, acrid and unrecoverable. Rub it onto the wet chicken instead so it meets liquid, not bare fat. Thick milk last, below a simmer.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","aromatic","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, aromatics blooming from the first bite, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kukul%20Mas%20Curry%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kukul Mas Curry"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Milk","Coconut milk","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"ambul-thiyal","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g tuna or kingfish, cut into 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g goraka, about 8 dried pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cm ginger, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 cm Ceylon cinnamon quill"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 cm pandan leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the goraka with 100 ml boiling water and leave until soft, then pound it with the garlic, ginger and 1 tsp of the salt to a black paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn the fish cubes in the paste with the pepper, chilli, turmeric and remaining salt until every face is coated dark.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the fish in one layer in a clay pot or heavy pan with the cinnamon, curry leaves, pandan, coconut oil and the 150 ml water, and bring to a bare simmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and cook, shaking the pan rather than stirring, until the cubes are firm to a fingertip and the liquid has gone to a thick black glaze that clings to them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave it off the heat for at least an hour before eating; the sourness settles into the fish as it cools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Goraka sours with hydroxycitric acid and a resinous, smoke cured edge; tamarind's tartaric acid is fruitier and arrives faster. That difference is the dish. Goraka's acid with the salt firms the fish protein so the cubes hold their corners, and the near dry finish is preservation, not reduction for looks. Never stir it: shake the pan, or you serve flakes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","earthy","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ambul%20Thiyal%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ambul Thiyal"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Tuna","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"pol-sambola","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g freshly scraped coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g Maldive fish, umbalakada"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small red onion, 60 g, roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 dried red chillies, or 2 tbsp Sri Lankan chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.25 tsp ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice of 1 lime"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the dried chillies with the salt in a mortar to a coarse flake, then add the Maldive fish and work it until no hard splinters remain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion and pound to a rough wet pulp; the mixture turns bright red as the onion water releases the chilli.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the coconut a handful at a time, pressing rather than striking, until it is stained an even brick red and beads of oil show on the pestle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze in the lime, add the pepper, and press the sambola into a fist a few times; it should hold its shape when released.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pound it, never blend it. The fat in freshly scraped coconut carries the chilli and lime; a blade shears the flesh, drives that oil out and leaves grey paste. Maldive fish is rock hard cured tuna, so break it down before the coconut goes in or it stays as splinters. With desiccated coconut, wet it first with 3 tbsp warm water and rest 10 minutes, or the sambola drinks the lime and eats dusty.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pol%20Sambola%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pol Sambola"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mortar-and-pestle-molcajete-suribachi-granite","term":"Mortar & Pestle: Molcajete, Suribachi, Granite"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Chiles","Lime","Coconut"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"wambatu-moju","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g aubergine, quartered lengthways and cut into 6 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, 120 g, cut into thin wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml coconut oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green chillies, halved lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp brown mustard seeds, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the aubergine batons with the turmeric and 1 tsp of the salt, leave 15 minutes, then blot them dry.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry them in hot coconut oil in batches until they are collapsed and deep brown at the edges, well past golden, and lift onto paper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off all but 2 tbsp of the oil and fry the onion, chillies and garlic until the onion is limp and browning at the tips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the ground mustard, vinegar, sugar, chilli powder, pepper and remaining salt to a loose paste, pour it into the pan and let it hiss and thicken to a glaze.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the hot aubergine through, then cool completely; the colour deepens and the sharpness rounds after a night in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The aubergine has to be fried past golden to a collapsed deep brown. Vinegar and sugar only penetrate cells whose water has already been driven off; underfried batons stay spongy, weep, and turn the dressing watery within the hour. Turmeric and salt rubbed on first pull moisture out and cut oil uptake. Dress it hot, then keep it cold and it holds a week.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wambatu%20Moju%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wambatu Moju"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Eggplant","Coconut","Vinegar","Mustard","Sugar","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"kottu-roti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 godamba roti, about 320 g, a day old"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g cooked chicken curry meat, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml chicken curry gravy, hot"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, 100 g, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, 100 g, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, 80 g, julienned"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 green chillies, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp roasted Sri Lankan curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Shred the roti into strips the width of a finger and leave them uncovered while you prep the rest; they should feel dry and papery, not pliable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Get a flat steel plate or heavy pan very hot, add the coconut oil and fry the onion, leek, carrot, chillies and curry leaves until the leek is limp and the carrot still snaps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push it all to one side, pour the eggs onto the bare metal, let them set, then chop them into the vegetables with two blunt metal spatulas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chicken, curry powder and pepper and keep chopping and turning until the meat is heated through and the pan smells toasted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Throw in the roti and chop hard for a minute so the edges catch and colour, then pour the hot gravy round the rim, toss twice and serve; it should be damp, never wet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Day old roti is the whole trick. Fresh roti is still humid and turns to paste the instant gravy touches it, while dried shreds toast on the plate and then take up sauce without collapsing. Keep the plate hot enough to sear, add the gravy only in the last thirty seconds, and chop rather than stir so cut edges keep meeting hot metal and browning.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","aromatic","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, aromatics blooming from the first bite, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kottu%20Roti%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kottu Roti"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Leek","Carrot","Chiles","Coconut","Curry/Makrut leaves","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"gotu-kola-sambola","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120 g gotu kola leaves, stalks pulled off"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g freshly scraped coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small red onion, 50 g, very finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tomato, 80 g, seeded and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green chilli, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice of 1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.25 tsp ground black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stack the gotu kola leaves a handful at a time, roll them into a tight cigar and shred across the roll with a sharp knife until the shreds are thread thin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the shreds with the coconut, onion, tomato and chilli using fingertips only, keeping the pile loose and dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"At the table, add the lime, salt and pepper, turn it over twice and eat within five minutes, before the leaves darken and slump.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt and lime draw water out of gotu kola by osmosis, so a sambola dressed in the kitchen reaches the table as a dark wet heap. Shred the leaves as fine as tobacco with a knife, hold them dry with the coconut and onion, and add the acid and salt at the last second. Freshly scraped coconut only: desiccated stays hard, will not moisten, and grits the mouth.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gotu%20Kola%20Sambola%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gotu Kola Sambola"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Coconut"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"wattalappam","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g kithul jaggery, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thick coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g cashew nuts, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp ground cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.25 tsp freshly grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"an eighth tsp ground cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.25 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the jaggery with the water over low heat until it is a smooth dark syrup with no grains left on the spoon, then take it off and let it cool to blood heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs with the salt and spices just until no strings of white remain; do not aerate them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the coconut milk into the cooled jaggery, pour that onto the eggs in a thin stream while whisking, then strain into a heatproof dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover tightly with foil and steam over water that is barely shivering until the edge is set and the centre wobbles as one piece when nudged.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter over the cashews, cool, then chill until firm enough to slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a coconut milk custard: the eggs set near 80C (175F) and curdle just above it, so steam over water that is barely shivering, 40 to 45 minutes, and pull it while the middle still wobbles as one piece. Jaggery must be melted and cooled to blood heat before it meets the eggs, or it scrambles them into threads. Cover the dish or condensation pits the surface.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Wattalappam%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Wattalappam"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"hollandaise-and-the-two-ways-it-splits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 egg yolks, about 54 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g clarified butter, warm at 50C (122F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml water, plus 15 ml held back to slacken"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.2 g cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g white pepper, ground"}],"steps":[{"text":"Set a stainless bowl over a pan of water held at 70C (158F), a bare shiver with no bubbles breaking the surface, and check that the bowl base sits clear of the water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with 30 ml water off the heat until frothy, then set the bowl over the bath and whisk steadily until the sabayon triples in volume, holds a ribbon on the surface for two seconds and reads 65C (149F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the bowl off the heat and add the clarified butter in a thread no thicker than a pencil lead for the first 50 g, whisking until each addition disappears and the sauce climbs the wires.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the rest faster once the emulsion is set, stopping to slacken with the held 15 ml of water whenever the sauce stiffens to mayonnaise thickness instead of falling off the whisk in a ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with lemon, salt, cayenne and white pepper, then hold covered at 55C (131F) and no hotter; a metal pan left on a warm hob will take it past 70C (158F) unnoticed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yolk proteins begin to thicken near 65C (149F) and set solid by 72C (162F), so the sabayon lives in a seven degree window. Split one is heat: the protein coagulates into grains and drops the fat it was holding. Split two is crowding: butter poured faster than the water phase can carry it, past roughly four parts fat to one of water, and the droplets merge into a slick. Water is the cure for both.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Hollandaise","Deglazing & pan sauces","Building an emulsion","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Hollandaise","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=hollandaise%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hollandaise%2C%20and%20the%20Two%20Ways%20It%20Splits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hollandaise, and the Two Ways It Splits"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"emulsions","term":"Emulsions"},{"slug":"stainless-clad-tri-ply","term":"Stainless Clad (Tri-Ply)"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Chiles","Lemon","Butter"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"sauce-bearnaise","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g shallot, finely minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g tarragon stalks, plus 8 g leaves chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g chervil, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 black peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 egg yolks, about 54 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g clarified butter, warm at 50C (122F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the vinegar, wine, shallot, tarragon stalks and cracked peppercorns until reduced to 45 ml, thick enough that a spoon drawn across the pan leaves a track that fills back slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain the reduction, pressing the solids hard, and cool it to blood heat; you want about 45 ml of sharp, shallot sweet liquid and no more.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks into the cooled reduction over water held at 70C (158F) until the sabayon holds a figure of eight on its own surface and reads 65C (149F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, thread in the clarified butter, drop by drop for the first 50 g and then in a steady stream, until the sauce holds the shape a spoon cuts into it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the chopped tarragon and chervil off the heat and hold at 55C (131F); herbs added any earlier lose their anise to the steam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The reduction is not only flavour, it is the water phase. Diluting each yolk with 15 ml of liquid lifts its setting point several degrees, and that margin is what keeps a béarnaise from scrambling where neat yolk would. Reduce past 45 ml and the buffer goes with it; the sauce then grains first at the bowl edge, where the metal runs hottest and the film is thinnest.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Building an emulsion","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauce%20B%C3%A9arnaise%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauce Béarnaise"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"emulsions","term":"Emulsions"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Shallot","Tarragon","Butter","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"beurre-blanc-the-cold-butter-mount","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g unsalted butter at 82% fat, cut into 20 g cubes and kept refrigerator cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g shallot, finely minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml double cream at 48% fat, optional insurance"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g white pepper, ground"}],"steps":[{"text":"Reduce the wine, vinegar and shallot in a heavy pan until only 30 ml of syrupy liquid is left and the shallot sits in it like wet jam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cream if you are using it and bring to a bare simmer; casein tolerates more heat than butter's own emulsifiers and buys you a wider working band.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the heat to its lowest setting and whisk in the cold butter two cubes at a time, adding the next pair only when the last has vanished and the sauce reads opaque and glossy rather than yellow and clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep a probe in the pan and hold the sauce between 55C and 65C (131F and 149F), lifting it off the heat whenever it climbs; by 80C (176F) it will have broken to oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season, strain if you want it smooth, and hold in a water bath at 52C (126F); it cannot be reboiled, so make it inside the hour you plan to serve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Butter is already an emulsion: roughly 82% fat, 16% water, 2% milk solids, and it is those solids and their phospholipids that hold the sauce together. Cold butter enters as part crystalline fat and is sheared into fine droplets before it melts. Warm butter arrives as free oil and coalesces on contact. Boil it and the water phase evaporates, leaving more fat than the solids can coat.","equipment":["Bain-marie"],"techniques":["Beurre blanc","Deglazing & pan sauces","Building an emulsion","The water bath","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","aromatic","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Beurre blanc","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=beurre%20blanc%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beurre%20Blanc%2C%20the%20Cold%20Butter%20Mount%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beurre Blanc, the Cold Butter Mount"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"strainers-hawthorne-julep-and-the-double-strain","term":"Strainers: Hawthorne, Julep & the Double Strain"},{"slug":"emulsions","term":"Emulsions"}],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Butter","Cream","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath","building-an-emulsion"]},{"slug":"beurre-monte-butter-held-liquid","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450 g unsalted butter, cut into 25 g cubes and kept cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the water to a rolling boil in a saucepan just wide enough to get a whisk into; you want a shallow, energetic base rather than a deep pool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in two cubes of cold butter at once and keep the water moving until the mixture turns opaque and pale, like thin cream, with no clear yellow layer anywhere.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the rest cube by cube, never letting the pan exceed 88C (190F) on a probe; past 90C (194F) the water boils away and the fat separates within seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season and hold between 82C and 88C (180F and 190F) for poaching or for finishing vegetables, whisking once if the edge starts to look clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The 60 ml of water is the seed of a continuous water phase; without it butter simply melts, its own 16% water flashes off, and fat separates from browning solids. Held between 82C and 88C (180F and 190F) the emulsion is stable enough to poach in. Above 90C (194F) it breaks. Chilled it sets solid, and comes back only if rewarmed slowly with a whisk, never over a fierce flame.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Building an emulsion","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beurre%20Mont%C3%A9%2C%20Butter%20Held%20Liquid%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beurre Monté, Butter Held Liquid"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"the-deglazed-pan-sauce-white-wine-and-shallot","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g chicken thighs or breasts, to build the fond"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g shallot, finely minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml brown chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g unsalted butter, cold, in four cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sear the chicken in the oil in a stainless or carbon steel pan, never non-stick, until the pan base carries a continuous mahogany film and the meat releases without tearing; set the meat aside to rest.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off all but 15 ml of the fat, add the shallot and sweat for a minute until translucent, scraping nothing yet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the wine and scrape hard with a flat wooden spatula; the fond lifts in sheets and the liquid browns within seconds. Reduce until the pan floor stays dry for a beat behind the spatula, about 30 ml left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the stock and reduce by half to roughly 90 ml, until it coats the spatula and a drip hangs for a moment before falling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat, wait until it drops below 80C (176F), then swirl in the cold butter a cube at a time until the sauce turns glossy and thickens by a shade; finish with lemon, parsley and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fond is not burnt residue, it is water soluble Maillard product welded to hot metal: peptides, melanoidins, caramelised sugars. Water lifts them, but the ethanol in wine also dissolves aroma compounds water cannot touch, which is why deglazing with stock alone tastes flat. Pour the fat off first, since fat is insoluble and only slicks the sauce. Mount off the heat or the butter breaks.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Building an emulsion","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Deglazed%20Pan%20Sauce%2C%20White%20Wine%20and%20Shallot%20recipe","sub":"Search films of The Deglazed Pan Sauce, White Wine and Shallot"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sweat-vs-saute-vs-sear","term":"Sweat vs. Sauté vs. Sear"},{"slug":"steel-science-carbon-stainless-and-hrc","term":"Steel Science: Carbon, Stainless & HRC"},{"slug":"carbon-steel-pans","term":"Carbon Steel Pans"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Shallot","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"cream-reduction-the-sauce-that-may-boil","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml double cream at 48% fat, or heavy cream at not less than 36%"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g shallot, finely minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g tarragon leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the shallot in the butter without letting it colour, about three minutes, until it is translucent and smells sweet rather than raw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the wine and reduce until the pan is almost dry and the shallot glistens; harshness left in the wine will concentrate here, not cook away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the cream and boil it openly and hard, which no butter emulsion would survive; the agitation is what reduces it evenly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take it to about 200 ml, until the sauce coats the back of a spoon and a finger drawn through leaves a channel that holds its edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, and only then, whisk in the mustard, lemon and tarragon, tasting for salt as the acid sharpens the reduction.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cream boils without breaking because its fat is already dispersed as membrane coated globules in a casein rich water phase, with no free fat waiting to be released. That protection needs fat: below about 30% the casein flocculates under heat and acid. Reduction thickens by evaporation alone, so judge nappe, not minutes. Add lemon or mustard off the heat; acid near pH 4.6 drops the casein out as grains.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Building an emulsion","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cream%20Reduction%2C%20the%20Sauce%20That%20May%20Boil%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cream Reduction, the Sauce That May Boil"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Lemon","Tarragon","Butter","Cream","Wine","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"red-wine-jus-monter-au-beurre","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"375 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g shallot, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml brown veal stock, gelatine rich"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 thyme sprigs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 black peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g unsalted butter, cold, in 10 g cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Reduce the wine with the shallot, thyme, bay and peppercorns to about 90 ml, until the purple foam has gone and what is left pours like thin syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the veal stock and simmer off centre, skimming the grey foam that gathers on the cool side, until about 150 ml remains, clinging to a spoon and tasting concentrated but not salty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain through a fine sieve without pressing, and chill a spoonful on a cold plate: it should set to a soft jelly, which tells you the gelatine is there to stabilise the mount.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat, let it fall to 70C (158F), then whisk in the cold butter a cube at a time until the jus turns from matt to a mirror and thickens by a shade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve within twenty minutes and never let it reach 80C (176F) again; a mounted jus that boils goes dull and floats a ring of fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mounting is not enrichment for its own sake. Butter fat is dispersed into the stock's water phase and the gelatine there does the stabilising, crowding the droplet surfaces and thickening what surrounds them. A gelatine poor stock will not mount; it slicks instead, and no amount of whisking fixes that. Add the butter cold and off the heat, because above 80C (176F) the droplets merge and the shine dies.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Building an emulsion","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Red%20Wine%20Jus%2C%20Monter%20au%20Beurre%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Red Wine Jus, Monter au Beurre"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"warm-aioli-garlic-in-olive-oil","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"16 g garlic, about four cloves, green germ removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks, about 36 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml mild extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml neutral oil such as grapeseed"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the garlic with the coarse salt in a mortar until it is a smooth ivory paste with no fibre left; the salt crystals are the abrasive, so do not shortcut them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the yolks and 10 ml of the warm water and beat until the mixture pales and thickens slightly; this is the water phase, and it must exist before any oil goes near it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the oils drop by drop for the first 30 ml, then in a thin thread, keeping the sauce stiff and stopping to slacken with the last 5 ml of water whenever it turns dull and claggy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the bowl over water at 50C (122F) and warm the aioli, whisking constantly, until it reads 52C (126F) and loosens to a pourable cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the lemon off the heat and serve at once; held on the bath it will keep for ten minutes, but at 65C (149F) the yolk sets and the sauce drops its oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yolk lecithin coats each oil droplet and stops it merging with the next. The sauce holds up to about 75% oil by weight; past that the droplets crowd, touch and coalesce, so allow each yolk 100 ml of oil and a spoon of water, no more. Warming is the risk: over 65C (149F) the yolk coagulates and the emulsion fails. Blend extra virgin at speed and you shear out its bitterness, so work by hand.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Building an emulsion","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Warm%20Aioli%2C%20Garlic%20in%20Olive%20Oil%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Warm Aioli, Garlic in Olive Oil"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Garlic","Lemon","Mayonnaise","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"flambe-pan-sauce-brandy-and-green-peppercorn","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml brandy at 40% ABV or higher"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g steaks, seared to leave a fond"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g shallot, finely minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g green peppercorns in brine, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml brown beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml double cream at 48% fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g unsalted butter, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Measure the brandy into a small jug before you light anything; pouring from the bottle over a live pan can carry the flame back up the neck.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear and rest the steaks, pour off all but 15 ml of fat, sweat the shallot and peppercorns for a minute, then take the pan right off the heat and wait until it stops spitting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand back, pour the brandy in from the jug, then tilt the pan to the flame or touch a long match to the vapour above it; it lights, burns for 20 to 40 seconds and goes out on its own. Keep a lid in your other hand to smother it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the stock and reduce by half, then the cream, and simmer to a coating consistency that stripes when you draw a spoon through it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat and below 80C (176F), swirl in the cold butter until the sauce is glossy, then season.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The flame burns vapour above the pan, not the liquid in it, so it needs a spirit near 40% ABV; wine at 12% will never light. It also removes far less alcohol than cooks assume, roughly a quarter, so keep reducing afterwards. The safety rule is the pan comes off the heat before the spirit goes in: on a hot pan the whole 60 ml vapourises at once and the flame goes up rather than across.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Building an emulsion","Flambé","Brining & curing","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Flamb%C3%A9%20Pan%20Sauce%2C%20Brandy%20and%20Green%20Peppercorn%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Flambé Pan Sauce, Brandy and Green Peppercorn"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sweat-vs-saute-vs-sear","term":"Sweat vs. Sauté vs. Sear"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Shallot","Bell pepper","Butter","Cream","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"the-broken-emulsion-three-rescues","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"240 g broken sauce: hollandaise, beurre blanc or mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk, about 18 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml cold water, plus 15 ml boiling"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml double cream at 48% fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g unsalted butter, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"ice and cold water for a bath"}],"steps":[{"text":"Diagnose before you touch it: grainy specks in a thin liquid means the yolk cooked, a clear oil layer over sludge means the emulsion was overloaded, a stiff dull paste means it only got cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For a cold seize, stand the pan in water at 45C (113F) and whisk until it flows again; adding anything here just dilutes a sauce that was never broken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For an overloaded emulsion, put 15 ml of boiling water in a clean warm bowl and whisk the broken sauce into it a teaspoon at a time until it takes, then in a thread; the fresh water phase gives the droplets somewhere to sit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For a cooked yolk, strain out the specks, beat a fresh yolk with 15 ml of cold water off the heat, and whisk the strained sauce into that drop by drop; the grains themselves never come back.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For a broken butter sauce, drop the pan into the ice bath until it is below 60C (140F), then whisk 30 ml of cream or a 20 g cube of cold butter in a clean pan and rebuild the broken sauce into it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Every rescue is the same move: build a fresh continuous water phase and feed the broken sauce into it slowly enough that the fat redisperses as droplets rather than pooling. Heat damage is the exception, because coagulated protein cannot be uncoagulated; strain the grains out and accept a thinner sauce. Work below 60C (140F) throughout or you will break the rescue too.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Hollandaise","Beurre blanc","Deglazing & pan sauces","Building an emulsion","Blanching & shocking","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Hollandaise","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=hollandaise%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Beurre blanc","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=beurre%20blanc%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20Broken%20Emulsion%2C%20Three%20Rescues%20recipe","sub":"Search films of The Broken Emulsion, Three Rescues"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Emulsions%20%26%20Pan%20Sauces%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Emulsions & Pan Sauces kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"vinaigrette-the-three-to-one","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 g red wine vinegar, 6 to 7 percent acidity"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g extra virgin olive oil (3 parts oil to 1 part acid by weight)"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g shallot, minced to 2 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g fine salt, about 1.4 percent of the finished sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g black pepper, cracked"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the shallot in the vinegar with the salt for 10 minutes, until every crystal has gone and the shallot has turned translucent at the cut edges; salt will not dissolve once the oil is in.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the mustard until the acid phase is smooth and opaque, the colour of thin cream, with no seed flecks sitting apart from the liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the oil in a thread no thicker than a pencil lead, whisking hard, until the sauce turns pale, holds the whisk marks for a beat and coats the back of a spoon without running clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste on a leaf rather than a spoon: dip a lettuce leaf and adjust, until the vinegar reads as brightness across the tongue and not a sting at the back of the throat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Three to one is calibrated to a 6 to 7 percent wine vinegar, so the ratio moves with the acid: an 8 percent sherry vinegar wants 4 to 1, lemon juice at roughly 5 percent acid wants 2.5 to 1. Taste the acid before you set the ratio. Mustard is the stabiliser; its mucilage coats the oil droplets and keeps them from touching. Pour the oil in a thread, because oil dumped in cannot be sheared fine enough and the sauce slicks instead of thickening.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","rich","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vinaigrette%2C%20the%20Three%20to%20One%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vinaigrette, the Three to One"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Vinegar","Wine","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"mayonnaise-by-hand-and-the-rescue","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks, about 36 g, at 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g water, plus more to let the sauce down"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g neutral oil such as grapeseed, 150 g per yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g lemon juice, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the yolks, mustard, salt and vinegar in a bowl seated on a damp cloth for 30 seconds, until the base is smooth, glossy and pale enough to show clean whisk tracks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the oil drop by drop for the first 50 g, until the base thickens and turns matte; the emulsion has to be seeded before it can be fed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the rest in a slow thread, whisking each addition out before the next, until the mayonnaise stands in a soft peak and a spoon drawn through leaves a channel that does not close.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whenever it climbs the whisk and starts to look stiff and greasy, let it down with 5 g of water at a time until it slackens back to a glossy ribbon, then carry on with the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"To rescue a split batch, put 15 g of water or a fresh yolk in a clean bowl, whisk in one spoonful of the broken sauce until it thickens and grips, then feed the remainder back in a thread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yolk lecithin and lipoproteins crowd the oil and water boundary; one yolk will hold about 200 g of oil before the surfactant runs out. Splitting is rarely too much oil, it is too much at once: the droplets pack past roughly 74 percent by volume and coalesce back into a slick. That is also why water is the cure. Adding it rebuilds the continuous phase the droplets are suspended in.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mayonnaise%20by%20Hand%2C%20and%20the%20Rescue%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mayonnaise by Hand, and the Rescue"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Vinegar","Wine","Mustard","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion"]},{"slug":"green-salad-dressed-at-the-pass","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g tender leaves: butterhead, lamb's lettuce, a little frisee for structure"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g vinaigrette, about 12 g per 100 g of leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g chervil and chive, torn rather than cut"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g flaky salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper from the mill"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash the leaves in a deep sink of cold water, lifting them out of the grit rather than pouring the water off them, then spin dry in three light loads until a leaf pressed to your palm leaves no damp print.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold the dry leaves in a covered box at 4C (39F) until service; a cold turgid leaf stays crisp several minutes longer once the dressing is on it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"At the moment of serving, tip the leaves into a bowl twice their volume, spoon the vinaigrette down the inside wall, and turn with open hands 8 to 10 times until every leaf carries a shine and nothing pools at the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with flaky salt and pepper on the plate, scatter the herbs and send it; from here you have about three minutes before the leaves slump.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt and acid pull water out of the leaf cells by osmosis, so a dressed salad loses turgor within about three minutes and ends up sitting in a puddle of its own sap. Dress at the pass, 12 to 15 g of vinaigrette per 100 g of leaves, and no earlier. Dry the leaves first: oil will not wet a film of water, so on damp leaves the dressing beads, slides off and collects in the bottom of the bowl.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Green%20Salad%2C%20Dressed%20at%20the%20Pass%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Green Salad, Dressed at the Pass"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Lettuce/Greens","Chives"]},{"slug":"salade-composee-built-in-layers","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g new potatoes, matched for size"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fine green beans, topped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, straight from the fridge at 4C (39F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g tender leaves, butterhead or little gem"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g vinaigrette"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g shallot, sliced into fine rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g capers, rinsed and dried"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g tarragon and chervil leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt per litre for the potato water, 1 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt per litre for the bean water, 2 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"flaky salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Start the potatoes in cold 1 percent salted water, bring to a bare tremble and cook 18 to 22 minutes, until a knife slides to the centre and withdraws with no drag.","durationSec":1320,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1320,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain, halve, and dress with a third of the vinaigrette while they read about 55C (131F) to the finger; warm swollen starch drinks the dressing, and a cold potato sheds it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the beans in 2 percent water for 3 minutes, until they bend without snapping, then plunge into iced water and lift them out the instant they are cold, before the green slides toward olive.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lower the fridge-cold eggs into rolling water for 6 minutes 30 seconds, chill 3 minutes in ice, peel and halve; the yolk should be set at the rim and jammy at the centre.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season and dress each element on its own, then build from the base up: potatoes, beans, shallot and capers, the leaves turned last in the remaining vinaigrette, eggs on top with flaky salt on the cut yolk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Composed means every element is cooked, seasoned and dressed alone, then assembled, so nothing is diluted or wilted by its neighbour. The one time-critical step is the potato: dress it at about 55C (131F), because the swollen starch matrix is still open and absorbs vinaigrette, while a cooled potato has retrograded and simply sheds it onto the plate.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","aromatic","fiery","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salade%20Compos%C3%A9e%2C%20Built%20in%20Layers%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salade Composée, Built in Layers"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Shallot","Potato","Green beans","Capers","Tarragon"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"terrine-de-campagne","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g pork shoulder, diced and chilled to 2C (36F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g pork back fat, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g pork liver, sinew removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"18 g fine salt, 1.8 percent of the 1 kg forcemeat"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 g curing salt at 6.25 percent sodium nitrite, 0.25 percent, optional for colour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper, coarsely cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g quatre epices"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g garlic, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g shallot, sweated in butter and cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g brandy"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, 50 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caul fat, soaked and rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the diced shoulder, back fat and liver with the salts, spices, garlic, shallot, brandy and wine, and cure covered at 2C (36F) for 12 hours, until the surfaces have gone matte and tacky and the meat feels sticky between the fingers.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the shoulder through an 8 mm plate and the fat and liver through 4 mm, holding everything under 4C (39F); if the fat begins to look shiny and smeared on the plate, stop and return the lot to the freezer for 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the egg by hand for 45 seconds, until the forcemeat pulls together and a spoonful pressed to the bowl holds a standing ridge, then fry a teaspoon and taste for salt before it is committed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line the mould with caul fat, pack in three layers, knocking the mould hard on the bench after each so the air pockets close, lay on bay and thyme and fold the caul over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 150C (302F) in a bain-marie held at 80C (176F) until the core reads 68C (154F), around 75 minutes, when the juices at the edge run clear amber rather than pink.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Press at once under a board and about 1 kg of weight, cool to room temperature, then chill 48 hours; serve at 12C (54F), never fridge-cold, or the fat eats like wax.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fat is the whole argument. Below 25 percent the forcemeat eats dry and grainy; above 40 percent it renders out and the slice is greasy. This mix runs near 35 percent. Keep every part under 4C (39F) through grinding, because warm fat smears and then breaks out as a cap in the oven. Press under about 1 kg while it cools, to close the voids left by shrinkage; heavier than that and you squeeze the fat straight back out.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle","Bain-marie"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","The water bath","Brining & curing","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Terrine%20de%20Campagne%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Terrine de Campagne"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pink-salt-nitrite-and-nitrate-the-science-straight","term":"Pink Salt, Nitrite & Nitrate (The Science, Straight)"},{"slug":"quatre-epices-and-the-european-baking-blends","term":"Quatre Épices & the European Baking Blends"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Shallot","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Wine"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath","pounding-a-curry-paste"]},{"slug":"chicken-liver-parfait-butter-emulsion-method","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g chicken livers, trimmed of sinew and any green stain"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g whole egg, about 4 medium"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g ruby port"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g madeira"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g shallot, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 clove garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"13 g fine salt, 1.2 percent of the total mix"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 g curing salt at 6.25 percent sodium nitrite, optional for a pink set"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g milk, for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g clarified butter, for the seal"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the trimmed livers in the milk at 4C (39F) for 6 hours, then drain and pat dry; the milk draws out blood that otherwise reads metallic and sets a dull grey.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reduce the port, madeira, shallot, garlic and thyme to 60 g, a loose syrup that leaves a clear trail on the pan base, then strain and cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the livers, eggs, reduction and salts to a smooth liquid, pass through a fine chinois pressing with a ladle, and warm the puree over a bain-marie to 50C (122F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With the blender running, pour in butter melted to 55C (131F) in a steady thread, until the mix is glossy and pours like double cream; stay inside 45C (113F) to 65C (149F) throughout.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a terrine, cover, and bake at 100C (212F) in a bain-marie of 80C (176F) water to a core of 63C (145F), about 45 minutes, when a tap on the mould no longer sends a ripple across the surface.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Chill overnight, then flood the top with clarified butter at 40C (104F) to a depth of 4 mm and set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a butterfat in water emulsion and it lives in a narrow band. Below 45C (113F) the butterfat crystallises and the mix goes grainy; above 65C (149F) the egg sets in the blender jug and the emulsion splits. Bake to a core of 63C (145F): past 70C (158F) the egg proteins contract and squeeze out water, and the parfait weeps in the tray. The butter seal is not decoration; without it the surface oxidises grey inside a day.","equipment":["Blender","Bain-marie","Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","The water bath","Brining & curing","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Liver%20Parfait%2C%20Butter-Emulsion%20Method%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Liver Parfait, Butter-Emulsion Method"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"port-madeira-and-the-fortified-sweets","term":"Port, Madeira & the Fortified Sweets"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Shallot","Thyme","Butter","Milk"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath","building-an-emulsion"]},{"slug":"gravlax-the-cure-by-weight","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg salmon fillet, skin on and pin-boned, previously frozen at -20C (-4F) for 7 days"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g fine sea salt, 3 percent of the fillet weight"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g caster sugar, 2 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g white peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g coriander seed, toasted and cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g dill, stalks included, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Weigh the pin-boned fillet, then weigh the cure to it: salt at 3 percent, sugar at 2 percent, mixed with the pepper and coriander.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the cure over the flesh side, working the thin tail end last and lightest, then press the dill on in a thick blanket.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal tight in a bag or double wrap with no air pocket, sit it in a tray under a 500 g weight, and cure at 3C (37F) for 36 to 48 hours, turning it every 12 hours in the brine it throws.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Unwrap, scrape off the dill and pat dry; the flesh should have darkened to deep coral, feel springy under the thumb and no longer hold a dent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice on a shallow angle almost parallel to the board, in sheets thin enough to read a knife blade through, leaving the grey line against the skin behind.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dosing the cure as a percentage of the fish removes the guesswork. Sealed with 3 percent salt and 2 percent sugar there is nowhere for the salt to go but into the flesh, so it equilibrates at 3 percent and cannot over-salt however long it sits. Burying a side in loose salt is a timing gamble by comparison. The sugar is not there to sweeten; it offsets the salt and holds water, so the slices stay supple rather than leathery.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Curing gravlax","Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Curing gravlax","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=gravlax%20curing%20salmon%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gravlax%2C%20the%20Cure%20by%20Weight%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gravlax, the Cure by Weight"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Dill","Sugar","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"sea-trout-filleted-and-cured","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole sea trout, 1.8 kg, gutted and scaled"},{"kind":"item","text":"28 g fine sea salt, 3.5 percent of the roughly 800 g of trimmed fillet"},{"kind":"item","text":"14 g caster sugar, 1.75 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g white peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fennel seed, toasted and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"finely grated zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"iced water, for rinsing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wipe the scaled fish dry, cut down behind the pectoral fin to the backbone, then turn the blade flat and run it head to tail along the spine in long strokes, letting the flex of the blade ride the bones, until the fillet lifts away and the frame is left clean and pearl white.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Repeat on the second side, trim the belly line and the thin tail, and weigh what you have: a round fish yields about 45 percent of its whole weight, so expect around 800 g.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Run a fingertip down the centre line to find the pin bones and pull each one toward the head with pliers, following the angle it sits at, so the flesh closes behind it rather than tearing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the cure, weigh it to the fillet at 3.5 percent salt, and spread it evenly on the flesh side only; leave 40 minutes at 4C (39F), by which time beads of liquid stand on the surface.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse in iced water, pat dry, and rest uncovered on a rack at 4C (39F) for 4 hours, until the surface is tacky to a fingertip and dulled to a matte sheen.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Weigh the fillet, never the fish: a round fish gives up only about 45 percent of its whole weight, so cure dosed to the live weight lands roughly twice as salty as intended. At 3.5 percent for 40 minutes the salt only reaches the outer few millimetres, firming surface protein without curing through. The 4 hour dry forms a pellicle of dissolved protein, and that skin is what lets the flesh slice cleanly instead of tearing.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot","rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sea%20Trout%2C%20Filleted%20and%20Cured%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sea Trout, Filleted and Cured"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"round-fish-vs-flat-fish","term":"Round Fish vs. Flat Fish"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"pickled-vegetables-for-the-plate","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g rice or white wine vinegar, 5 percent acidity"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cucumber, deseeded and cut into 5 mm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g breakfast radish, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fennel, sliced 2 mm on a mandoline"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g carrot, sliced 2 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g dill seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 strips lemon peel, pith scraped away"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the vinegar, water, sugar, salt, seeds, bay and lemon peel to 90C (194F) and hold 2 minutes, until the brine runs clear and no grit remains on the pan base, then draw it off the heat.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pack the carrot and fennel into a jar and pour the brine over at 80C (176F); the heat opens the firmer roots and drives diffusion, and the edges will go translucent within a minute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the remaining brine to 55C (131F) before pouring it over the cucumber and radish, and hold them there for 20 minutes; at that temperature their pectin methylesterase cross-links the cell walls and sets the crunch.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool everything uncovered to room temperature, then hold at 4C (39F) for at least 4 hours before service; a baton is ready when it snaps cleanly and tastes seasoned rather than raw.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Plate pickles season a dish, they do not preserve it. This brine settles near 1.7 percent acetic acid, well short of the level a shelf-stable jar needs, so it lives in the fridge and is finished within ten days. Texture is decided by pouring temperature: hold cucumber and radish at 55C (131F) and their pectin firms, but pour brine on at a boil and the cell walls collapse to slush in the jar.","equipment":["Mandoline"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pickled%20Vegetables%20for%20the%20Plate%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pickled Vegetables for the Plate"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Carrot","Cucumber","Fennel","Radish/Turnip","Lemon","Dill","Bay leaf","Rice","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Pickles","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"sauce-verte","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g flat parsley leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g spinach leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g watercress leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g chervil"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g tarragon leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g chives"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt per litre of blanching water, 2 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"iced water, for refreshing"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blanch all the greens together in the 2 percent water at a hard rolling boil for 20 seconds, until they go slack and flare to a bright emerald.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift them straight into iced water and pull them out the moment they are cold; left to soak, the flavour washes into the bath.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze the greens in a cloth to a tight dry ball of about 25 g, then blend to a fine puree with the lemon juice, scraping down twice, until no fibre shows on the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the puree into the mayonnaise a third at a time, checking the body as you go; it should still hold a soft peak. Season with white pepper and serve within two hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Twenty seconds in boiling water denatures the chlorophyllase that would brown the herbs, and the ice bath stops the clock. Even so, the acid in the mayonnaise slowly strips magnesium from the chlorophyll and turns it olive, so build this within two hours of service. Squeeze the greens hard: more than about 20 g of free water per 250 g of mayonnaise thins a spoonable sauce into a pouring dressing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauce%20Verte%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauce Verte"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garde%20Manger%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Garde Manger kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Spinach","Lettuce/Greens","Lemon","Arugula/Watercress","Parsley","Tarragon","Chives","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"sfoglia-alluovo-the-hand-rolled-sheet","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g 00 flour, soft wheat, W180 to W220 (100%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole eggs, 110 g shelled"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 egg yolks, 108 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine sea salt (1.7%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"semola rimacinata for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt per litre of cooking water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mound the flour, crater it, and break the whole eggs and yolks into the well with the salt; draw flour in from the inner wall with a fork until the paste is too stiff to stir and drags on the tines.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead 10 minutes on an unfloured board, pushing with the heel and folding back, until the surface turns from ragged to satin and a pressed thumbprint fills back slowly instead of snapping shut.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap airtight and rest 45 minutes at room temperature; it is ready when a pinched corner pulls to twice its length before it parts.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll from the centre outward with a long pin, giving the sheet a quarter turn each pass and wrapping the far edge around the pin to stretch rather than press, until it is 0.6 mm and the grain of the board reads through it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut, then boil 90 seconds in water at 100C (212F) salted at 10 g per litre; this is the only seasoning the pasta itself will ever take.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yolk is roughly half water, half fat and protein, and the fat coats gluten strands and shortens them, which is why a yolk-rich sheet rolls thin and stays tender. But yolks bring little water: 218 g egg to 300 g flour sits near 45% hydration, and below that the sheet cracks at the edges as you roll. Cracking means dry, not underkneaded. Rest the full 45 minutes so water reaches the starch.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fresh pasta","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sfoglia%20all'Uovo%2C%20the%20Hand-Rolled%20Sheet%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sfoglia all'Uovo, the Hand-Rolled Sheet"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"semola-e-acqua-the-eggless-durum-dough","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g semola rimacinata di grano duro (100%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g water at 45C (113F) (50%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine sea salt (1.6%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"extra semola for the board and tray"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the salt in the warm water, pour it over the semola while turning with one hand, and keep turning until no dry grain is left and the mass holds as a coarse, pebbled rubble.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead 10 minutes, driving the heel forward and folding back, until the surface stops tearing and takes a dull sheen; the ball should feel as firm as stiff modelling clay.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap and rest 30 minutes at room temperature, then cut the ball in half and read the cut face: one even yellow, with no chalky white core.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll ropes and drag or press them into cavatelli, trofie or busiate on a board dusted with semola only, dry 30 minutes on a semola tray, and boil 6 to 8 minutes at 100C (212F) salted at 10 g per litre.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2280,"estimated":true},{"text":"For a bronze die extruder drop the water to 150 g (30%), so the mix stays a damp sand that clumps only when squeezed in the fist; anything wetter binds the auger and the strands weld together as they leave the die.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Durum is milled hard and coarse, so its particles drink slowly. The dough can look smooth while the granule cores are still dry, and that chalky core dissolves in the pot and the shape falls apart. Warm water at 45C (113F) and a 30 minute rest are the fix, and the test is a cut face of one even colour. No egg here, so all the chew comes from durum protein.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Fresh pasta","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Semola%20e%20Acqua%2C%20the%20Eggless%20Durum%20Dough%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Semola e Acqua, the Eggless Durum Dough"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"ravioli-di-ricotta-the-seal","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g 00 flour (100%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole eggs and 6 yolks, 218 g total"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine sea salt for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g whole milk ricotta, drained 12 hours in a cloth to about 380 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g hard aged grating cheese, finely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk, 18 g, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine sea salt for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.2 g grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"semola rimacinata for the tray"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make and rest the egg dough as for sfoglia, then beat the drained ricotta with the cheese, yolk, salt and nutmeg until it is stiff enough to hold a piped ridge without slumping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll one sheet to 0.8 mm and work fast; a sheet dried past tacky will not weld, and the parcels open in the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe 8 g mounds at 4 cm centres, drape the second sheet over, and press outward from each mound with the sides of both hands, chasing every pocket of air to the edge before the border goes shut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut with a wheel, pinch each border once more between finger and thumb, and set the parcels on semola, never on flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook at a bare simmer, 90C to 95C (194F to 203F), for 3 minutes; a rolling boil batters the seams open.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things burst a raviolo. Trapped air expands as it heats and splits the weakest seam, and a wet filling steams and does the same. So the ricotta is drained overnight and bound with a yolk that sets near 65C (149F), and the air is chased outward before the border closes. Seal with dry hands on a still tacky sheet: brushed water makes a slippery layer, not a weld.","equipment":["Piping bag","Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Fresh pasta","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ravioli%20di%20Ricotta%2C%20the%20Seal%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ravioli di Ricotta, the Seal"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ricotta","term":"Ricotta"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Cheese (any)","Ricotta","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"gnocchi-di-patate-from-baked-potato","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 kg floury baking potatoes, matched in size, unpeeled and unpricked"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g 00 flour, 20% of the riced flesh"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks, 36 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.2 g grated nutmeg"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bake the potatoes on the bare oven rack at 200C (392F) for 60 to 75 minutes, until a skewer meets no resistance at the centre and the skins have gone papery.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Split them the moment they come out, rice the steaming flesh in a thin layer onto a cool board, weigh it, and leave 5 minutes until the surface looks dry and floury rather than glossy.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the flour, salt and nutmeg over, add the yolks, cut everything together with a scraper, then press only until there are no dry patches left; stop there.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll finger thick ropes, cut 2 cm pillows, and press each one down a gnocchi board or fork tines with a single light thumb stroke to leave ridges on one face and a hollow on the other.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Test one in water at a bare simmer, 90C (194F), salted at 10 g per litre: it should rise in 60 to 90 seconds and hold its edges. Give the rest 20 seconds past the rise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A boiled potato takes on water, and every extra gram of water demands another gram of flour, and flour is exactly what turns gnocchi rubbery. Baking drives moisture off, so 20% flour is enough. Rice while steaming hot: as potato cools its amylose retrogrades into crystals that will not bind, you compensate with more flour, and you get bullets. Work it only until it coheres.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Gnocchi","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Gnocchi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20gnocchi%20light%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gnocchi%20di%20Patate%20from%20Baked%20Potato%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gnocchi di Patate from Baked Potato"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Potato","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"spatzle-the-scraped-batter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain wheat flour, 10 to 11% protein (100%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 whole eggs, 165 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.2 g grated nutmeg"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the eggs, milk, salt and nutmeg, beat in the flour with a wooden spoon, and keep beating hard for 4 to 5 minutes until the batter blisters and falls from the lifted spoon in one slow unbroken ribbon.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and rest 20 minutes so the gluten relaxes and the batter drops off the board cleanly instead of clinging in tails.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bring a wide pan to 100C (212F) salted at 10 g per litre, hold it at a lazy boil, and press or scrape the batter through in three batches so the water never falls off temperature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift each batch 30 seconds after it surfaces, about 90 seconds in all, into iced water if you are frying them later, or straight into foaming butter if you are not.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Spätzle is the one pasta you want at roughly 90% hydration with the gluten fully built, and you cannot knead a batter, so you beat it. The strands have to be strong enough to survive the fall through the press. Underbeaten batter shreds into ragged crumbs. If the ribbon breaks as it leaves the spoon, beat two minutes more; if it will not drop at all, thin with 10 g milk and no more.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sp%C3%A4tzle%2C%20the%20Scraped%20Batter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Spätzle, the Scraped Batter"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"knife-cut-wheat-noodles","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong bread flour, 12 to 13% protein (100%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"240 g water at 20C (68F) (48%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g fine sea salt (1.4%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"semola rimacinata for dusting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the salt into the water, rub it through the flour with your fingertips until the bowl holds only damp crumbs, squeeze those crumbs into one rough lump, and cover 20 minutes; do not try to force it smooth yet.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead 6 minutes. The dough will fight back and stay lumpy, which is correct. Cover and rest 60 minutes, after which the same dough spreads under the heel like putty and its torn edge is smooth rather than shaggy.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 2 mm on a semola dusted board, dust the sheet itself, fold it loosely in four, and cut 3 mm ribbons with single downward strokes of a long blade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift and shake the ribbons apart at once, toss with more semola, and boil 3 minutes at 100C (212F) salted at 10 g per litre.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"At 48% hydration there is barely enough water to wet the gluten, so the dough resists from the first minute and will not go smooth by force. Time does the work: two rests let water migrate and the protein network build itself. Dust with semola, not flour. Flour drinks the surface moisture and glues the folds shut, while coarse durum granules sit between the layers and keep them apart.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fresh pasta","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Knife-Cut%20Wheat%20Noodles%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Knife-Cut Wheat Noodles"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Noodles","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"orecchiette-dragged-with-a-knife","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g semola rimacinata di grano duro (100%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g water at 45C (113F) (50%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine sea salt (1.5%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"extra semola for the board and tray"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix, knead 10 minutes to a firm smooth ball, and rest 30 minutes wrapped; the dough must be stiff enough that a rolled rope holds its shape without sagging on the board.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll one piece into a rope 1.5 cm thick and cut 8 mm discs, keeping the rest covered, since a dried surface skids instead of dragging.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the flat of a round tipped table knife on a disc and drag it 3 cm across unvarnished wood, so the disc smears forward into a curled shell with a torn, matt face.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip that shell inside out over the pad of your thumb; the finished ear should be about 1 mm at the dished centre and 3 mm at the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dry 30 minutes on semola, then boil 8 to 10 minutes at 100C (212F) salted at 10 g per litre, timing it by the thick rim and not the thin centre.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false}],"note":"The drag is not decoration. It tears the semola surface open and exposes free starch, and that rough matt face is what holds sauce where a smooth cut shape sheds it. Two things stop the drag: a varnished board, or dough gone wet or crusted, and both make the disc skid. Dust the board with semola, never flour, and keep unworked dough covered while you shape.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Fresh pasta","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Orecchiette%20Dragged%20with%20a%20Knife%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Orecchiette Dragged with a Knife"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pasta"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"lasagne-sheets-laminated-egg-dough","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g 00 flour (100%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole eggs, 220 g (55%)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"semola rimacinata for dusting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the dough together, knead 8 minutes to a firm ball that dents slowly under a thumb, and rest it wrapped for 45 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":3180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Flatten a quarter, pass it through the widest roller setting, fold it in three like a letter, give it a quarter turn and pass again; repeat seven times, until the sheet leaves the rollers with square edges and an even matt face instead of ragged sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Now step down one setting at a time without folding, to 1 mm; lift the sheet by one end and it should carry its own weight without thinning at the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut to the tin, blanch 40 seconds at 100C (212F) salted at 10 g per litre, chill in iced water, and lay the sheets flat on damp cloths.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build the dish, then bake at 180C (356F) for 40 minutes, until the edges bubble and a skewer slides to the base with no resistance.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Lamination here means folding and re-passing at the widest setting: it aligns the gluten on one axis so the sheet stops snapping back and comes out square. Thickness then decides the dish. Rolled to tagliatelle thinness the sheet dissolves into the sauce; left above 1.5 mm it stays chalky at the core, because it can only hydrate from the sauce around it. Hold 1 mm and blanch.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Lamination","Fresh pasta","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lasagne%20Sheets%2C%20Laminated%20Egg%20Dough%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lasagne Sheets, Laminated Egg Dough"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Flour"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"spaghetti-alla-chitarra-the-square-strand","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g semola rimacinata di grano duro (75% of the flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g 00 flour (25% of the flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole eggs, 220 g (55% of total flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"semola rimacinata for dusting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead 10 minutes to a firm dough that is smooth and springs straight back under a thumb, then rest it wrapped for 60 minutes so it will roll without recoiling.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":4440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Measure the gap between the wires of the chitarra, then roll the sheet to exactly that thickness, 2 to 3 mm, and trim it to the width of the frame.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the sheet on the wires and run a rolling pin over it in two or three firm unhurried passes, pressing straight down; do not saw, which frays the cut faces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pluck the wires so the strands drop through, toss them with semola, and coil them into loose nests.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil 3 minutes at 100C (212F) salted at 10 g per litre, then finish in the pan with the sauce so the flat faces pick it up.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"The point of the chitarra is a square section: four flat faces grip a sauce that a round drawn strand would shed. That squareness rests on one number, the sheet thickness matching the wire spacing at 2 to 3 mm. Roll thinner and the wires cut flat ribbons; roll thicker and they cannot reach the board, so the strands stay joined by a web along the base. Press straight down, firmly, once.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Fresh pasta","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Spaghetti%20alla%20Chitarra%2C%20the%20Square%20Strand%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Spaghetti alla Chitarra, the Square Strand"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Pasta","Flour"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"gluten-free-egg-pasta-with-psyllium","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fine white rice flour, 50% of the blend"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g tapioca starch, 30% of the blend"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g potato starch, 20% of the blend"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g psyllium husk powder, 3% of the blend"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 whole eggs, 165 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks, 36 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g water at 40C (104F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the three flours, the psyllium and the salt together until the colour is uniform; psyllium that goes in as a lump hydrates as a lump and shows up later as a slimy streak in the sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs, yolks, water and oil, pour them into the dry mix, and work to a dough; it stays sticky for the first minute and then visibly tightens as the psyllium gels.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap and rest 30 minutes at room temperature, then test: a pinch pulled between finger and thumb should stretch 2 cm and part cleanly, with no cracking along the edge.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll between two sheets of baking paper to 1 mm, peel the top sheet back and cut; with no gluten the dough will not spring back, but it will not tolerate being stretched by hand either.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook at a bare simmer, 90C (194F), for 3 to 4 minutes, salted at 10 g per litre; a rolling boil knocks the strands apart.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true}],"note":"With no gluten there is no elastic network, so a hydrocolloid stands in: psyllium husk at 3% of the flour blend, or xanthan at 1.5%. It must be fully hydrated before rolling, which takes 30 minutes, and a sheet rolled early cracks along every edge. The starch choice matters too: rice flour alone is sandy and brittle, and it is the tapioca and potato starch that supply chew.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gluten-Free%20Egg%20Pasta%20with%20Psyllium%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gluten-Free Egg Pasta with Psyllium"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasta%20%26%20Fresh%20Dough%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pasta & Fresh Dough kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Potato","Pasta","Flour"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"pate-a-choux-the-base-dough","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"125 g water"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"110 g unsalted butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"140 g plain flour, T55 (flour 100 percent, liquid 179 percent, butter 79 percent, egg 161 percent)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"225 g whole egg, beaten (about 4 medium)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring water, milk, butter, sugar and salt to a full rolling boil in one movement, with the butter fully melted before the surface breaks, so you do not boil away liquid before the flour goes in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, beat in all the flour at once until no dry patch remains, then return to medium heat and stir 2 to 3 minutes until the paste pulls from the sides and a thin film forms on the pan base.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the paste on a cold tray and let it fall to 60C (140F), warm but not hot to a fingertip, then beat the egg in over four additions.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stop adding egg when the paste falls from the spatula in a slow V that closes in about three seconds, and a furrow drawn with a finger fills back halfway.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe on to a lined tray and bake at 200C (390F) for 20 minutes without opening the door, then drop to 170C (340F) for 15 to 20 minutes until deep gold and light in the hand.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pierce each shell at the base and return it to the switched off oven for 10 minutes to dry the inside.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"The paste is a cooked starch gel carrying a great deal of egg. Water in it flashes to steam and inflates the shell while gelatinised starch and coagulating egg hold the wall until it sets. Add egg above 60C (140F) and it scrambles into lumps. Underbake and the wall is still soft, so the steam condenses and the shell folds.","equipment":["Piping bag","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Choux pastry","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Choux pastry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=choux%20pastry%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pate%20a%20Choux%2C%20the%20Base%20Dough%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pate a Choux, the Base Dough"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"creme-patissiere-the-boiled-starch-custard","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, split and scraped"},{"kind":"item","text":"110 g egg yolk (about 6)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cornflour (8 percent of the milk)"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g unsalted butter, cold and cubed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the milk with the vanilla to 80C (176F) and infuse off the heat for 20 minutes, then bring it back to the boil.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk yolks, sugar and cornflour to a smooth pale paste, then temper with a third of the hot milk, whisking without stopping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return everything to the pan over medium heat and whisk hard through the thickening, keeping the whisk moving into the corners.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Once it thickens, hold it at a full boil for a further 60 seconds, until large slow bubbles break the surface and the cream slackens slightly and turns glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat beat in the cold butter, spread on to a cold tray, film flat against the surface and chill to 4C (39F) within 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk it smooth before use; the chilled gel breaks back to a pipeable cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cornflour does two jobs: it gelatinises near 85C (185F) and it shields the yolk protein, which is why this custard can boil where anglaise cannot. Boil it hard for a full minute. Anything less leaves the alpha amylase in the yolks alive, and that enzyme cuts the starch overnight, turning a firm cream into soup by morning.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","silky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Creme%20Patissiere%2C%20the%20Boiled%20Starch%20Custard%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Creme Patissiere, the Boiled Starch Custard"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"creme-anglaise-stirred-to-the-nappe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g double cream, 48 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"110 g egg yolk (about 6)"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, split and scraped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g cornflour, optional insurance at 1 percent of the dairy"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat milk, cream and vanilla to 80C (176F) and infuse off the heat for 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks and sugar only until combined; whipping in air builds a foam that hides the thickening from you.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Temper with a third of the hot dairy, return everything to the pan and stir over low heat with a spatula in a figure of eight, reaching into the corners.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook to 82C (180F), the point where the foam on top disappears and the sauce coats the spatula so a finger drawn through leaves a clean channel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour it at once through a fine sieve into a bowl set on ice and stir until it drops below 30C (86F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No starch here, so nothing protects the yolk. Diluted and sweetened, its protein thickens from 78C (172F) and curdles above 85C (185F): a working window of seven degrees. Stop at 82C (180F) and strain straight into a bowl on ice, because carryover heat in a hot pan carries it past the ceiling after you stop stirring.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Creme%20Anglaise%2C%20Stirred%20to%20the%20Nappe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Creme Anglaise, Stirred to the Nappe"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"cooked-meringue-italian-and-swiss","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"For Italian: 100 g egg white at 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"For Italian: 200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"For Italian: 60 g water, 30 percent of the sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"For Italian: 1 g cream of tartar"},{"kind":"item","text":"For Swiss: 100 g egg white"},{"kind":"item","text":"For Swiss: 180 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"For Swiss: 1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"White wine vinegar, for wiping out the bowl"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wipe bowl and whisk with vinegar and dry them; a trace of fat or yolk stops the foam climbing at all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For Italian, start the whites and cream of tartar at medium speed while the sugar and water cook to 118C (244F), soft ball, brushing crystals off the pan sides with a wet brush.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With the whites at soft peak, pour the syrup in a thin steady thread down the inside of the bowl, never on to the moving whisk, then whip until the bowl is barely warm at 30C (86F) and the peak holds a straight beak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For Swiss, whisk whites and sugar together in a bowl over barely simmering water, the base clear of the water, to 65C (150F), until no grit is left between finger and thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Move it to the mixer and whip 8 to 10 minutes on high until the bowl is cool and the mass is dense, glossy and holds a sharp ridge.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Use Italian where it must stay light and stable, in mousse, buttercream and torched tops; use Swiss where you want density, for buttercream and for shapes piped to dry at 90C (194F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Both cook the white so the foam holds for days. Italian sets it with 118C (244F) syrup, which brings the whipping mass to about 72C (162F); Swiss sets it in the bowl at 65C (150F). Whip the whites past soft peak before the syrup lands and the bubble walls have no stretch left, so the meringue turns grainy and weeps its syrup out.","equipment":["Torch"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooked%20Meringue%2C%20Italian%20and%20Swiss%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cooked Meringue, Italian and Swiss"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"meringues-french-swiss-italian","term":"Meringues: French, Swiss, Italian"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Cream","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"tempered-dark-chocolate-by-seeding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"350 g dark couverture, 64 to 70 percent cocoa solids, in callets or finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g of the same couverture, held back as seed, 30 percent of the total"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cocoa butter, optional, for thinning a stiff mass"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the 350 g over a bain marie or in short bursts to 50C (122F), stirring, keeping every drop of water and every wisp of steam off the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, add the seed in three lots and stir steadily down to 28C (82F); let the seed dissolve rather than forcing the temperature with cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm back to 31C (88F) for dark chocolate and no higher, then lift out any seed still unmelted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Test on the tip of a palette knife: at 20C (68F) it should set glossy and firm within 3 to 5 minutes, snapping cleanly with no grey streaks.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Work between 31 and 32C (88 and 90F); if it thickens, thin it with a little melted cocoa butter, never with heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Milk chocolate runs the same three points at 45C (113F), 27C (81F) and 29C (84F); white runs 45C (113F), 26C (79F) and 28C (82F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cocoa butter sets in six crystal forms and only form V is glossy and snaps; it melts at 34C (93F). The three point curve exists to kill the others: 50C (122F) wipes the slate, 28C (82F) with seed grows form V, 31C (88F) melts off the soft form IV without touching V. Hold it above 33C (91F) and temper is lost. One drop of water seizes the mass for good.","equipment":["Bain-marie"],"techniques":["Tempering chocolate"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tempered%20Dark%20Chocolate%20by%20Seeding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tempered Dark Chocolate by Seeding"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Chocolate/Cocoa"]},{"slug":"sugar-syrup-through-the-named-stages","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g water, 30 percent of the sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g liquid glucose, 10 percent of the sugar, or 1 g cream of tartar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre iced water, for the cold water test and for shocking the pan"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir sugar, water and glucose over low heat until every grain has dissolved and the syrup runs clear, then stop stirring for good.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover for 2 minutes so the trapped steam washes the pan walls, then uncover, raise the heat and brush any stray crystals down with a wet brush.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Read the stages as water leaves: thread at 110C (230F), soft ball at 115C (239F), firm ball at 120C (248F), hard ball at 125C (257F), soft crack at 140C (284F), hard crack at 150C (302F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Confirm by hand: a spoonful dropped into iced water rolls into a ball you can flatten between finger and thumb at 115C (239F), and shatters into brittle threads at 150C (302F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Past 160C (320F) the sugar starts to colour and no thermometer keeps up; pull at pale amber around 170C (338F) and dip the pan base in cold water to stop the run.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The thermometer reads water, not sugar: as water boils off the concentration climbs and the boiling point climbs with it, so 115C (239F) means soft ball and 150C (302F) means hard crack. A single stray crystal on the pan wall seeds the whole syrup back to grit, so wash the sides down and never stir once it boils. Glucose or acid blocks the lattice.","equipment":["Thermometer","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sugar%20Syrup%20Through%20the%20Named%20Stages%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sugar Syrup Through the Named Stages"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Cream","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"vanilla-souffle-on-a-patissiere-base","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"20 g soft butter, for the moulds"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g caster sugar, for the moulds"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g creme patissiere, thick, at 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g egg white (about 6)"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g caster sugar, for the whites"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g cream of tartar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Seeds of 1 vanilla pod"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter six 200 ml ramekins in upward strokes only, chill them, butter again, then coat with caster sugar and knock out the excess.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Loosen the pastry cream with the vanilla seeds until it drops from the spatula in a soft ribbon; a cold stiff base will not take the whites.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites and cream of tartar to medium peak, adding the 45 g sugar in three lots from the moment they turn opaque; the peak should nod over at its tip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat a third of the whites into the base to slacken it, then fold in the rest in two additions, stopping the instant the last white streak disappears.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill to the rim, level with a palette knife and run a thumb round the inside edge so the batter is free of the lip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake on a preheated tray at 190C (375F) for 12 to 14 minutes, until risen 4 to 5 cm with a set collar and a centre that still trembles; serve within two minutes.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":840,"estimated":true}],"note":"The rise is gas expanding, not leavening: trapped air grows about a third by volume on the way to 100C (212F) and steam does the rest, while egg and starch set the wall near 80C (176F). Whip the whites only to medium, because stiff whites cannot stretch and tear instead. Butter upward and wipe the rim, or the batter has nothing to climb.","equipment":["Ramekins","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The soufflé"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vanilla%20Souffle%20on%20a%20Patissiere%20Base%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vanilla Souffle on a Patissiere Base"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"vanilla","term":"Vanilla"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"rough-puff-pastry-in-four-turns","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour, 100 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g cold unsalted butter in 2 cm cubes, 100 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g iced water, 50 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt, 2 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the butter cubes through the flour and salt, pressing each cube flat once between finger and thumb, and stop while the pieces are still the size of a thumbnail.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the iced water and lemon juice and bring it together with a scraper into a shaggy marbled block that looks dry in patches; it should not look like a dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to a rectangle three times as long as it is wide, fold in three like a letter, turn 90 degrees, repeat once, then rest at 4C (39F) for 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Give it two more letter folds the same way and rest again; each fold trebles the count, so four folds leave 81 sheets of butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Check the dough sits at 12 to 15C (54 to 59F) before every roll, and if butter starts smearing through the surface, chill 20 minutes before going on.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the shaped pastry 30 minutes cold, then bake at 200C (390F) until the layers are set and a cut edge shows open leaves rather than a seam.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lift comes from the water in butter, 16 to 18 percent of it, flashing to steam between the sheets. Butter is only plastic from 15 to 19C (59 to 66F): colder and it shatters, so layers break and leak fat; warmer and it merges into the flour, which gives shortcrust. Rest cold between turns and roll straight, never past the folded edges.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rough%20Puff%20Pastry%20in%20Four%20Turns%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rough Puff Pastry in Four Turns"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Butter","Flour","Phyllo/Puff pastry"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"pate-sucree-by-the-sanding-method","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour, 100 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cold unsalted butter in 1 cm cubes, 60 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"95 g icing sugar, 38 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ground almonds, 12 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g whole egg, beaten, about 1 medium"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the cold butter into the flour, salt and ground almonds with fingertips until it looks like damp sand with no lump bigger than a grain of rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir through the icing sugar, add the egg and cut it together with a scraper only until it clumps; the moment it forms a ball, stop working it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flatten to a 2 cm slab, wrap and rest at 4C (39F) for 2 hours at least, overnight for a shell that will not move.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 3 mm between two sheets, line the ring without stretching, press into the corner with a ball of offcut, and freeze the lined ring for 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blind bake at 160C (320F) for 18 minutes with paper and beans, then 8 to 10 minutes uncovered until it is sandy gold right into the corner.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1680,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brush the hot shell with beaten yolk and return it for 2 minutes to seal the inside against a wet filling.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sanding coats the flour in fat before any water arrives, so the grains cannot link into gluten and the baked shell breaks with a clean snap instead of chewing. What shrinks in the oven is gluten you built by kneading and rolling; two hours at 4C (39F) lets it relax, and freezing the lined ring sets the fat so the walls hold their height.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pate%20Sucree%20by%20the%20Sanding%20Method%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pate Sucree by the Sanding Method"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"creme-caramel-the-set-custard","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar, for the caramel"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g water, for the caramel"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g double cream, 48 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g whole egg, about 3 medium"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g egg yolk, about 2"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g caster sugar, for the custard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, split and scraped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the 150 g sugar and the water without stirring to a dark amber at 175C (347F), then pour at once into six 150 ml moulds and tilt to floor them evenly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat milk, cream and vanilla to 80C (176F), infuse 20 minutes, and whisk the eggs, yolks and 80 g sugar together without raising any foam.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the warm dairy on to the eggs, stir, strain, then skim every bubble from the surface; bubbles left there bake into holes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sit the moulds in a deep tray, pour water at 60C (140F) two thirds up their sides and bake at 150C (300F) for 35 to 45 minutes.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pull them when the centre reads 80C (176F) and the whole custard shivers as one piece; a ripple that travels independently means it is still liquid inside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill at 4C (39F) for at least 4 hours, then run a knife round the rim and invert.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Egg alone sets this, with no starch: whole egg coagulates from 62C (144F), but milk and sugar hold it off to between 78 and 82C (172 and 180F). Pull it at 80C (176F) in the centre. Past 85C (185F) the protein network tightens and squeezes water out, so the custard weeps and turns honeycombed. The water bath is the brake; it cannot pass 100C (212F).","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","silky","rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a silken, spoon-slow texture, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Creme%20Caramel%2C%20the%20Set%20Custard%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Creme Caramel, the Set Custard"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastry%20Foundations%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pastry Foundations kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"rewena-paraoa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 floury potato, about 150 g, peeled and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water, for the bug"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour, for the bug"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g high grade (strong) flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml warm water, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"butter, for the tin"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the potato in the 250 ml water until it collapses, mash it into its own cooking water and let it cool to blood heat; above 40C (104F) you kill the wild yeast you are trying to catch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the bug flour and the sugar, cover loosely and leave in a warm corner for two to four days, stirring once daily, until it domes with bubbles and smells sharply of cider rather than of paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the whole bug into the high grade flour, salt and warm water, then knead about ten minutes until the dough is slack, tacky and smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sprinkle over the baking soda and knead it through until you cannot see a streak of it; streaks bake out as grey soapy patches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove in a buttered tin until a fingertip dent fills back slowly, then bake at 200C (390F) until deep brown and hollow when knuckled underneath, about 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2640,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The bug is a wild culture: yeast off the potato skin and the flour, fed on sugars the gelatinised potato starch gives up. Mix it into anything above 40C (104F) and it dies. Starved, it turns grey and smells of nail polish; feed it weekly with flour, water and a slice of raw potato. Baking soda is not the lift, it corrects the acid the bug makes, and too much gives a green-grey, soapy crumb.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=R%C4%93wena%20Paraoa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rēwena Paraoa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"boil-up","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg pork neck bones or hock, cut through"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 L cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g kūmara, in large chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g potatoes, in large chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pūhā, or watercress"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour, for the doughboys"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water, for the doughboys"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the bones and onions with the cold water, bring them to a bare tremble and skim the grey scum off until the surface runs clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold that tremble, never a rolling boil, for about two hours, until the meat slides off the bone at a push.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the pūhā between your palms in a sink of cold water until the water clouds and the leaves go limp, then wring them out; that is where the bitterness goes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the kūmara and potato to the pot and cook until a knife slides out of them clean.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the flour, baking powder and a pinch of salt to a soft dough with the water, drop it in spoonfuls onto the surface, put the lid on and steam until the doughboys are dry at the centre, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the greens under the broth, give them four minutes until they collapse and darken, and serve straight from the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pork neck bones are collagen and little else; held at a bare tremble for two hours that collagen turns to gelatin and gives the broth its slip. Boil it hard instead and the fat emulsifies into the liquid, leaving it cloudy and greasy. Pūhā carries a bitter white latex, so rub the leaves under water until it clouds before they go near the pot, and give the greens four minutes at the most.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boil-Up%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boil-Up"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Potato","Lettuce/Greens","Arugula/Watercress","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"hangi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 kg pork shoulder, on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg lamb or mutton shoulder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, about 1.8 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg kūmara, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg potatoes, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg crown pumpkin, in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green cabbages, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 dense volcanic stones, fist sized"},{"kind":"item","text":"dry mānuka or other hardwood, enough for a four hour fire"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 hessian sacks, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cotton sheets, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dig a pit about a metre square and 60 cm deep, stack the stones through a criss cross of hardwood and burn it down for three to four hours, until the stones glow dull red and knock together with a dead thud.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rake out the embers and level the stones, then lay wet cabbage leaves and a soaked sack over them until the steam coming off is white rather than smoky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the salted meat baskets straight onto the sacking and the vegetable baskets above them, so fat and juice run down through nothing you want dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Throw a bucket of water onto the stones at the edges, cover the lot with the soaked sheets and sacks and shovel the earth back fast, until not one wisp of steam escapes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Patch any leak the moment you see it and leave the mound shut for three and a half hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Open from the downwind side, peel the sacking back away from yourself, and lift the baskets straight up so no soil falls in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The earth is the lid. Hot stones and wet sacking make the steam, and packed soil holds it against the food at a little over 100C (212F) for hours; nothing roasts, everything steams. Two things ruin it. A porous stone still holds water and will burst, so use dense volcanic rock and reject any that rings rather than thuds. And every wisp of steam leaving the mound is heat leaving: shovel more soil on it at once, or the centre comes out raw.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=H%C4%81ng%C4%AB%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hāngī"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Lamb","Potato","Cabbage","Squash/Pumpkin"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"oven-hangi-vegetables","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g kūmara, in 4 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g floury potatoes, in 4 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g crown pumpkin, in 6 cm wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a green cabbage, plus its outer leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large square of muslin, wetted"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blacken the outer cabbage leaves directly over a gas flame or on a dry pan until they blister and smell of smoke; this is the only honest smoke a kitchen can give you.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a deep roasting tin with the charred leaves, pack in the kūmara, potato, pumpkin, onion and cabbage quarters, salt through the layers and dot with the butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the water down the side of the tin, lay the wet muslin over the vegetables and clamp on a lid or doubled foil so nothing can vent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 160C (320F) until a skewer meets no resistance in the kūmara and the cabbage beneath has gone translucent and sweet, about 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Leave it sealed off the heat for ten minutes, then lift the vegetables out on their leaves and spoon the tin juices over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Call this what it is: a steam bake, not a hāngī. No oven makes smoke and hot stone, and charred cabbage leaves are the nearest honest substitute. What an oven can copy is the long wet ramp, which keeps the kūmara below the point where its surface dries and lets its amylase go on turning starch into sugar. Lid off, or above 180C (355F), and you have roast vegetables: a good dish, a different one.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","charred","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oven%20H%C4%81ng%C4%AB%20Vegetables%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oven Hāngī Vegetables"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Potato","Cabbage","Squash/Pumpkin","Butter","Flour"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"whitebait-fritters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g fresh whitebait (inanga)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"white bread and butter, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Tip the whitebait into a sieve and let it drain until no more water runs; do not rinse it again, the fish bruise and go milky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs only until yolk and white are one and no further; whipped air collapses in the pan and leaves a hollow.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the fish through with the salt and pepper until it looks like fish held in a film of egg, not batter with fish in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Get the butter foaming in a heavy pan over high heat, ladle in a thin round and cook until the edges turn opaque and the underside is pale gold, about 90 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip once, count thirty seconds and lift it while the centre is only just set; lemon at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing binds this but egg, which sets between 62 and 70C (144 to 158F); there is no gluten network, so the pan must be hot and the fritter must leave early. Drain the inanga properly first, because water in the mix steams the egg apart. Past about two minutes total the tiny fish wring out their own water, the fritter weeps and the texture goes rubbery. Flour is the usual cheat and it makes a pancake.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Whitebait%20Fritters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Whitebait Fritters"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Bread"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"paua-fritters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 pāua, about 500 g of foot once shucked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, very finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"coarse salt, for scrubbing"},{"kind":"item","text":"neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Run a blunt knife under the foot at the wide end to lift it off the shell, cut away the gut and the hard mouth, then scrub the foot with coarse salt until the slime is gone and the black skirt is clean.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat each foot with a mallet until it goes from rubber firm to slack and floppy and the edges frill; you are tearing the sheets of connective tissue that run through the muscle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mince the beaten foot coarsely by knife or mincer, stopping well short of paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir through the egg, flour, milk, onion, salt and pepper to a batter that only just coats the meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry spoonfuls in a very hot thin film of oil for about 45 seconds a side, until the edges are set and lacquered brown, and lift them out on the early side; lemon over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pāua foot is dense muscle laced with sheets of connective tissue, and heat gives two outcomes only: seconds, or two hours. Pounding tears those sheets mechanically, which is what makes the seconds route possible at all. In a very hot pan a fritter sets in about 45 seconds a side; past a minute the collagen contracts, wrings out the water and leaves you chewing an eraser. Get the pan hotter than feels right and pull them early.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shucking shellfish","Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Shucking shellfish","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20shuck%20oysters%20clams%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=P%C4%81ua%20Fritters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pāua Fritters"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Lemon","Milk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"kina-on-buttered-bread","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 live kina"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml seawater, or water with 18 g salt dissolved in it"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices rēwena paraoa or soft white bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Hold each kina mouth side up on a folded cloth and cut a circle out around the mouth with a knife point; the shell is brittle and lifts away as a cap.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip the dark gut liquor out and away from the shell before it can run over the roe, then find the five orange tongues lying against the inside wall.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift each tongue out whole with a teaspoon and swill it only in seawater or the salted water; fresh water bursts the cells.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay them on buttered bread at sea temperature, and squeeze the lemon at the last second rather than in advance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kina roe sits in seawater at roughly 35 grams of salt per litre and its cells are in balance with that. Rinse them under the tap and water floods in by osmosis: the cells swell, burst, and firm sweet roe goes slack and metallic. Wash only in seawater or water salted to match, never in ice melt. Tip the dark gut liquor away first, or the whole lot tastes of iodine.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kina%20on%20Buttered%20Bread%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kina on Buttered Bread"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Butter","Bread"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"kutai-and-kumara-chowder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg live green lipped mussels (kūtai)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g golden kūmara, in 1.5 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery stalks, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml full cream milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of parsley, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scrub the mussels and pull each beard down towards the hinge; throw out any that stays open when you tap it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam them in the water under a lid over high heat and lift each one out the moment it gapes, three to four minutes for the lot; pick the meat and strain the liquor through muslin to drop the grit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and celery in the butter until translucent with no colour, stir in the flour and cook it two minutes, until it smells of biscuit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the mussel liquor, then the milk and bay, add the kūmara and simmer until the dice is tender but still holds its edge, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, stir in the cream, the mussel meat, white pepper and parsley and warm it through without letting it boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A gaping shell means the mussel is already cooked, and a minute later the muscle contracts and squeaks against the teeth; lift each one as it opens instead of waiting for the pan. The liquor they give up is close to seawater in salt, so taste before you reach for the salt and never reduce the soup once dairy is in. The body comes from the roux and from starch the golden kūmara sheds as it softens.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=K%C5%ABtai%20and%20K%C5%ABmara%20Chowder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kūtai and Kūmara Chowder"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Onion","Celery","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"tomato-relish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml malt vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp mustard powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cayenne"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g cornflour"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour boiling water over the tomatoes, count thirty seconds and slip the skins off, then chop them and the onions coarsely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer them with the salt in a bowl and leave overnight, then drain off the brine that has collected and throw it away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the drained tomato and onion with the sugar and 300 ml of the vinegar, uncovered and stirred often, until the pieces slump and the syrup sheets off a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slake the cornflour, mustard, curry powder, cayenne and pepper in the last 50 ml of cold vinegar, stir it in and cook five minutes more, until dragging a spoon across the pan leaves a channel that closes slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle into hot sterilised jars while the relish is still above 85C (185F) and seal at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The overnight salt does the work: it pulls water out of tomato and onion by osmosis so the relish thickens by concentration rather than by an hour of hard boiling, which would carry the fresh tomato off in the steam. Cornflour is insurance at the end, and it must be slaked in cold vinegar or it lumps. Jar it above 85C (185F) and seal immediately; potted cool, it grows mould at the surface inside a fortnight.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tomato%20Relish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tomato Relish"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Vinegar","Mustard","Sugar","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"hokey-pokey","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g golden syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g baking soda, sifted"},{"kind":"item","text":"butter, for the tin"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a 20 cm tin and line it with paper before you start; once the soda goes in there is no time for anything else.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the sugar and golden syrup together in a deep heavy pan, stirring only until the sugar has dissolved, then stop stirring altogether.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil to 150C (300F), or until a drop in cold water sets hard and snaps between the fingers; the colour will be deep amber.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat, tip in the sifted baking soda and beat hard for three seconds only, while it quadruples and climbs the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour it into the tin without spreading it, leave it until cold and hard, then break it into shards.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Baking soda breaks down in the hot syrup into sodium carbonate, water and carbon dioxide, and that gas is trapped as the sugar cools through its glass transition. That is the honeycomb. The syrup must reach 150C (300F) hard crack; under it, the candy stays chewy and goes sticky within the hour. Beat three seconds, then pour without spreading, because every stir and scrape knocks the bubbles back out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hokey%20Pokey%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hokey Pokey"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"damper","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450 g self-raising flour, plus extra for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g cold butter, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml full cream milk, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp milk, for brushing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oven to 220C (430F) with a heavy tray inside. The leavening starts the second liquid meets the flour, so the oven must be at temperature before you mix.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the cold butter into the flour and salt until it looks like coarse breadcrumbs with a few flatter flakes left; stop while the bowl still feels cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the milk and water and cut it together with a butter knife until no dry flour remains, then bring it into a round with two or three folds only. It should feel tacky, not smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sit the round on the hot tray, slash a cross 2 cm deep across the top, brush with milk and dust with flour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 25 to 30 minutes until the crust is deep gold and the base sounds hollow on a knuckle rap; a skewer through the cross comes out dry.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Self-raising flour carries bicarbonate and an acid phosphate that start gassing on contact with cold milk and are largely spent inside twenty minutes, so a cold oven means a flat, tight loaf. Knead it like bread and the gluten you build traps nothing and chews like leather: cut it together, fold twice, bake. The deep cross lets the middle set before the crust seals it shut.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Damper%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Damper"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"aussie-meat-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g beef chuck, cut into 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp beef dripping"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large brown onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp dark yeast extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp Worcestershire sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp cornflour slaked in 50 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g shortcrust pastry"},{"kind":"item","text":"375 g all butter puff pastry"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten with a pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the diced beef hard in the dripping in two batches until each piece is crusted and the pan base is dark; a crowded pan greys the meat and the gravy tastes of nothing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the onion in the same pan, stir in the flour and cook it out for two minutes, then whisk in the stock, yeast extract, Worcestershire, tomato sauce and pepper and return the beef.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer covered on the lowest heat for about 2 hours until a cube crushes between finger and thumb, then stir in the slaked cornflour and cook until a spoon dragged through leaves a parting that closes slowly.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the filling completely, ideally overnight in the fridge, until it can be spooned out in a solid block.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line the tins with shortcrust, blind bake at 200C (390F) for 15 minutes until the base looks dry and sandy, fill cold, lid with puff, seal the rim, egg wash and cut a steam vent.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (390F) for 25 to 30 minutes until the lid is risen and mahogany and gravy just blips at the vent.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"The base is the whole problem. Hot, loose gravy steams the pastry from inside and you serve raw dough. Two defences, both needed: blind bake the shortcrust so its starch is set and its fat has sealed the crumb, and load the filling fridge cold and thick enough to stand in a spoon so the base bakes before the gravy loosens. Puff goes on top only; it will not carry weight.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aussie%20Meat%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Aussie Meat Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Butter","Flour","Phyllo/Puff pastry","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry"]},{"slug":"slow-roast-saltbush-lamb-shoulder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 lamb shoulder on the bone, about 2.2 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dried old man saltbush leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp sea salt flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ground coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head garlic, halved across the equator"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs rosemary"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the fat cap in a 2 cm lattice, down to the fat line but not into the meat, so the rub reaches it and the fat has somewhere to run.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the saltbush, salt, coriander and pepper together, work the mix into the scores with the oil, and leave the shoulder uncovered in the fridge overnight to dry the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sit it on the garlic and rosemary in a deep tray, pour the wine and water around it rather than over it, cover tightly with foil and cook at 150C (300F) for 4 hours.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pull the foil, raise the oven to 220C (430F) and roast 25 minutes more until the cap is blistered and a skewer slides to the bone with no resistance at all.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 20 minutes, fork the meat into the tray juices, skim the fat, then sharpen with the vinegar until the pan sauce tastes bright rather than only rich.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Old man saltbush is a halophyte: it parks sodium in its leaves, so the ground leaf is already seasoning and brings a savoury, faintly mineral edge where you might otherwise reach for anchovy. Taste it, then salt as if half the salt is in. The other rule is temperature, not time. Collagen only melts to gelatine held well above 70C for hours, so a shoulder pulled at 65C is simply tough. Cook to the skewer.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Scoring","Roasting in a hot oven","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","floral","aromatic","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a floral thread lifting the aroma, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Slow-Roast%20Saltbush%20Lamb%20Shoulder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Slow-Roast Saltbush Lamb Shoulder"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Garlic","Rosemary","Vinegar","Wine","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["scoring","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"barramundi-with-lemon-myrtle-butter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 barramundi fillets, skin on, about 180 g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp grapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g cold unsalted butter, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dried lemon myrtle leaf, finely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 finger lime, pearls scooped out"},{"kind":"item","text":"sea salt flakes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the skin hard with paper, salt it lightly ten minutes ahead, then blot again; any visible moisture steams instead of crisping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the fillets skin down in a hot oiled pan and press each one flat with a spatula for the first 20 seconds, until it stops trying to dome, then leave them alone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 4 to 5 minutes over medium heat until the flesh has turned opaque two thirds of the way up the side and the skin releases cleanly, then turn and give the flesh 45 seconds only.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"In a small pan bring the lemon juice and water to a bare simmer, take it off the heat and whisk in the cold butter piece by piece to a glossy emulsion, then stir the lemon myrtle in with the pan off the flame.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the butter over the fish, scatter the finger lime pearls and finish with sea salt flakes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lemon myrtle is close to pure citral, several times the concentration in lemongrass, and citral is volatile and heat labile. Boil it and the lemon lifts off; hold it hot and what stays behind reads eucalyptus, medicinal and bitter at the back of the throat. So it goes into the butter off the heat, at about a quarter the dose you would use of a dried garden herb. Barramundi skin is thick and gelatinous, so press it flat early or only the edges will crisp.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Sautéing & the shallow fry","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Barramundi%20with%20Lemon%20Myrtle%20Butter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Barramundi with Lemon Myrtle Butter"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Lemon","Lime","Butter"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"kangaroo-loin-with-mountain-pepperberry","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g kangaroo loin fillet, silverskin trimmed off"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp dried mountain pepperberry, coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sea salt flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp grapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp riberry or redcurrant jelly"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the loin to room temperature, dry it thoroughly and season with the salt and half the pepperberry; a cold wet fillet needs longer in the pan and there is no fat inside to buy you that time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear in smoking oil for 60 to 90 seconds a side, turning to build an even crust, and lift it out the moment a probe in the thickest part reads 50C (122F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest on a warm plate for 8 minutes, uncovered; it will climb to about 55C (131F), which is rare and correct.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deglaze the pan with the wine and reduce to a syrup, add the stock, jelly and remaining pepperberry, reduce again, then swirl in the butter and thyme off the heat until glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Carve across the grain in 1 cm slices and read the cut face: even deep ruby throughout, with no grey band under the crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kangaroo runs under two percent fat and very high in myoglobin. There is no intramuscular fat to buffer the heat and nothing to lubricate a fibre once it has tightened, so between about 55 and 65C the muscle contracts, wrings out its water and goes from ruby and tender to grey, dry and livery in well under a minute. Probe it, do not guess. Mountain pepperberry is fruity first, then numbing, and it builds for a minute after the mouthful, so season before the rest and taste again.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Searing: the hard crust","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kangaroo%20Loin%20with%20Mountain%20Pepperberry%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kangaroo Loin with Mountain Pepperberry"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"fillet-steak-loin-and-collar","term":"Fillet, Steak, Loin & Collar"},{"slug":"reverse-sear-and-the-gray-band","term":"Reverse Sear & the Gray Band"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Thyme","Butter","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"moreton-bay-bugs-split-and-grilled","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 raw Moreton Bay bugs, about 250 g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g soft unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 long red chilli, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp flat leaf parsley, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"sea salt flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, cut into cheeks"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill the bugs in the freezer for 20 minutes until they are still, then split each one lengthways through the head with one stroke of a heavy knife and lift out the dark gut thread.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat the butter with the garlic, chilli, lemon zest, parsley and a good pinch of salt, and press it into the split tails.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the halves shell down on a fiercely hot barbecue plate or grill and leave them there. Do not turn them at any point.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill 5 to 6 minutes until the butter is bubbling and the flesh has gone from glassy to chalk white right through the thickest part of the tail, then serve with the lemon cheeks.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"The shell is the cooking vessel. Bug tail is lean and sweet and sets hard: the proteins are done around 60C, and past that the muscle expels water and turns cottony with a faint ammoniac edge. Cooked shell down, the shell takes the fierce heat and the flesh poaches gently in its own butter from below, so there is no second side to overshoot. Flip them flesh down for even a minute and you grill the sweetness straight out.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","charred","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moreton%20Bay%20Bugs%2C%20Split%20and%20Grilled%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moreton Bay Bugs, Split and Grilled"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Clove"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"roast-kent-pumpkin-warrigal-greens-and-macadamia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg Kent pumpkin, unpeeled, in 3 cm wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sea salt flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g warrigal greens, thick stems pulled off"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g macadamias, toasted and roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fresh ricotta"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small red chilli, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"cracked black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the pumpkin with 2 tbsp of the oil and the salt and spread it in one layer with space around every wedge; a crowded tray traps steam and nothing will colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast at 220C (430F) for 30 to 35 minutes, turning once, until the cut faces are burnished and a knife tip meets no resistance at the thickest point.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile drop the warrigal greens into a large pot of boiling unsalted water for 45 seconds, lift them into iced water, squeeze dry, and tip the blanching water down the sink.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the lemon juice, honey and remaining oil with a pinch of salt and dress the squeezed greens in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the warm pumpkin and the greens together, dot with ricotta, scatter the macadamias and chilli and crack pepper over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Warrigal greens are not spinach with an accent. The leaves carry soluble oxalates that bind calcium in the mouth and leave a chalky, tongue numbing film, and raw in quantity they are genuinely unpleasant. Forty five seconds in plenty of boiling water leaches most of it out, which is why the water is discarded and why you do not salt it. Macadamia is around 75 percent fat and slides from gold to bitter in seconds, so toast it low and pull it early.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","sweet-edged","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Roast%20Kent%20Pumpkin%2C%20Warrigal%20Greens%20and%20Macadamia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Roast Kent Pumpkin, Warrigal Greens and Macadamia"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chiles","Squash/Pumpkin","Lemon","Cheese (any)","Ricotta","Honey","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"bush-tomato-and-chilli-relish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg ripe roma tomatoes, roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 brown onions, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ground bush tomato (akudjura)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g raw sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml apple cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 long red chillies, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp yellow mustard seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cornflour slaked in 2 tbsp cold water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion with the salt in a wide heavy pan until translucent and sweet, with no raw bite left when you chew a piece.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, chilli, mustard seed, coriander, sugar and vinegar, bring to a simmer and cook uncovered at a lazy bubble for about 90 minutes, stirring more often as it thickens and the pan starts to catch.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the ground bush tomato only for the last 10 minutes; it is ready when a spoon dragged across the base leaves a channel that fills back slowly rather than at once.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cornflour paste only if it is still watery, cook two minutes more, then ladle boiling hot into sterilised jars and seal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Akudjura is the dried desert raisin, ground: caramel and tamarillo at a low dose, harsh and tannic much above two percent of the batch, so weigh it rather than eyeball it. It goes in late because its aromatics are fragile and a long boil flattens them to dust. The relish sets on cooling, from pectin and sugar, so judge it a shade looser than you want in the jar. Boiled to the right texture while hot, it will be a brick cold.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bush%20Tomato%20and%20Chilli%20Relish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bush Tomato and Chilli Relish"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Apple","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Sugar","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"anzac-biscuits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g rolled oats"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g desiccated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g raw sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g golden syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp boiling water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the oats, flour, coconut, sugar and salt in a wide bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter with the golden syrup over low heat until just fluid and pourable, not bubbling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve the bicarbonate in the boiling water and stir it into the warm butter; it will foam up pale and roughly double, and that foam is the entire leavening, so use it at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the hot mixture into the dry, stir to a stiff, faintly greasy dough, roll walnut sized balls and flatten them lightly on lined trays with 5 cm between each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 160C (320F), 12 minutes for chewy or 18 for snap, and pull them while the edges are deep gold and the centres still look underdone; they set on the tray.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true}],"note":"No egg here, so texture is decided by sugar, syrup and minutes alone. Golden syrup is largely invert sugar, which holds onto water and keeps the middle chewy, while the bicarbonate does two jobs: it gasses against the syrup's acidity and it lifts the pH, which speeds Maillard browning to that deep amber. Bake until the centre firms in the oven and you have made hard tack. Pull them soft and let the tray finish them.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Anzac%20Biscuits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Anzac Biscuits"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Butter","Bread","Flour","Oats","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"macadamia-and-wattleseed-tart","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g sweet shortcrust pastry"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g macadamias, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g roasted wattleseed, coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml pouring cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g golden syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g unsalted butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Line a 23 cm tart tin with the pastry, chill it hard for 30 minutes, then blind bake at 180C (350F) for 20 minutes with beans and 10 minutes without, until the base is dry and sandy under a fingertip.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bring the cream to just below a simmer, stir in the ground wattleseed, cover and steep 20 minutes, then strain and press the grounds firmly to recover the liquid.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the golden syrup, brown sugar, melted butter, salt and vanilla into the wattleseed cream, then whisk in the eggs one at a time, keeping the whisk under the surface so you beat in no air.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the macadamias across the blind baked shell and pour the filling over; the nuts will float and set as a single layer on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 160C (320F) for 35 to 40 minutes until the edge is set and the centre wobbles as one piece, like a set custard, then cool completely before cutting.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wattleseed is roasted acacia seed: coffee, dark chocolate and hazelnut, with a bitter edge that wants fat and sugar around it. It is a hard seed, so ground and stirred in raw it stays gritty and tastes of dust. Steep it in hot cream and strain, exactly as you would coffee. Then bake gently. Push a syrup and egg filling much past 85C and the proteins tighten, weep and crack, and the tart goes rubbery under the nuts.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Pie crust & blind baking","Straining & passing through a sieve","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Macadamia%20and%20Wattleseed%20Tart%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Macadamia and Wattleseed Tart"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"bannock","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g all-purpose flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cold butter, cut small"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp sunflower oil for the pan"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt and sugar, then rub in the cold butter with your fingertips until the flour looks like coarse meal and no lump is bigger than a pea.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the water all at once and turn it with a fork only until a shaggy sticky dough gathers; stop while a few dry streaks still show at the side of the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip onto a floured board, fold it over on itself four times and no more, press to a disc 2 cm thick and cut into eight wedges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil in a heavy pan over medium-low until a scrap of dough sizzles lazily rather than fiercely, then fry the wedges 5 to 6 minutes a side until deep gold and hollow-sounding when tapped.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain on a rack rather than paper so the underside stays crisp, and split them while still warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bannock is chemically leavened, so it rises once: the baking powder starts giving off gas the moment water hits it, and a dough kneaded like bread turns rubbery instead. The other failure is heat. This dough is thick and conducts slowly, so a hot pan gives you a dark crust over a raw wet middle. Keep the oil at a lazy sizzle and give it the full five minutes a side.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bannock%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bannock"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"poutine","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg russet potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L sunflower oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g fresh cheese curds, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g all-purpose flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the potatoes into 1 cm batons, rinse until the water runs clear and hold them in cold water while you make the gravy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the butter and flour together over medium heat, stirring, until the roux smells of toasted biscuit and is the colour of peanut butter, about 6 minutes; stir in the tomato paste, then whisk in both stocks in three additions so no lumps set.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer until a finger drawn through the gravy on the back of a spoon leaves a clean line, season with the vinegar, pepper and salt, and hold it at 80 to 85C (175 to 185F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dry the potatoes hard, fry at 150C (300F) for 6 minutes until pale and floppy, lift them out and rest 10 minutes, then fry again at 190C (375F) until they rattle in the basket and are deep gold.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the fries, pile them hot into bowls, scatter the room-temperature curds over them and ladle the gravy on last so it runs down through the pile.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The squeak is an intact casein network dragging on tooth enamel. It belongs to curds a day or two old and it goes for two reasons: cold sets the butterfat rigid, and the cheese's own enzymes cut the protein into shorter pieces that no longer grip. So bring them out of the fridge an hour ahead, and buy them fresh. Gravy at 80 to 85C (175 to 185F) softens the curds; boiling gravy melts them into a puddle.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Making a roux","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Poutine%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Poutine"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Tomato","Potato","Butter","Cheese (any)","Flour","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"ragout-de-boulettes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g ground pork, not lean"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fresh breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp ground clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp dry mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L pork or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g all-purpose flour for toasting"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml cold water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the flour dry in a heavy pan over medium heat, stirring without stopping, until it is the colour of milk chocolate and smells of hazelnut, 10 to 12 minutes; tip it straight onto a cold plate so it stops there.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the pork with the onion, breadcrumbs, egg, cinnamon, clove, allspice, mustard, salt and pepper, and roll walnut-sized balls with wet hands.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the boulettes in the butter in batches until they are crusted on two sides and release from the pan on their own, then return them all to the pot with the stock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold at a bare tremble for 45 minutes, until a boulette gives way under light pressure from a spoon.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the toasted flour into the cold water until there is no lump left, stir it into the pot and simmer 10 minutes more, until the sauce is glossy and clings to the boulettes; taste for salt.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Farine grillée is the backbone here. Dry toasting breaks the starch down into dextrins, which is where the nutty flavour comes from, but a dextrinised starch swells far less, so you need roughly twice what you would use raw. Push it past dark chocolate and it turns bitter and stops thickening altogether. Slurry it with cold water; hot liquid gelatinises the outside of every lump and seals the dry flour inside.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","nutty-toasted","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rago%C3%BBt%20de%20Boulettes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ragoût de Boulettes"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"clove","term":"Clove"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Butter","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Cinnamon","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"montreal-smoked-meat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 kg beef brisket, point end, fat cap trimmed to 8 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g coarse pickling salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"7.5 g curing salt, 6.25 percent sodium nitrite"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cracked coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g yellow mustard seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried summer savory"},{"kind":"item","text":"maple or oak wood for smoking"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the pickling salt, curing salt, sugar, savory, bay, garlic, mustard seed and half the pepper and coriander; rub it into every surface and into the seam between the muscles, then pack the brisket in a non-reactive tub, weight it and refrigerate 8 to 10 days, turning it daily, until a cut into the thickest part shows the same even pink right through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse hard under cold water and soak 2 hours in fresh cold water, changing it once; fry a coin-sized slice and taste it, and soak longer if it sends you to the tap.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pat dry, press on the remaining pepper and coriander in a thick even crust, and leave the meat uncovered on a rack in the fridge overnight until the surface is dry and tacky to the finger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smoke at 105 to 110C (220 to 230F) over maple until the internal temperature reaches 74C (165F), about 6 hours; the crust will be near black and that is right.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam the whole piece over rolling water for about 3 hours, until a skewer slides into the point with no resistance at all and the fat between the muscles has gone translucent; slice by hand across the grain at 5 mm.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Smoked meat and pastrami part company at the cure and at the finish. Pastrami is usually wet-brined and carries real sugar; this cure is dry and salt-forward with only a token of sugar, and leans hard on coriander, garlic and cracked pepper, so it reads peppery and sour rather than sweet. The steam is not a reheating step. Collagen in the point only melts under wet heat, and a brisket sliced straight off the smoker at 74C (165F) is tight and dry however good the smoke was.","equipment":["Thermometer","Smoker"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking","Brining & curing","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","aromatic","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Low & slow smoking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Montreal%20Smoked%20Meat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Montreal Smoked Meat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Bay leaf","Mustard","Sugar","Maple syrup","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"soupe-aux-pois-jaunes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g whole yellow peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 smoked ham hock, about 800 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried summer savory"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the peas with cold water by 8 cm and leave them overnight, then drain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot and celery in the butter until the onion is translucent and smells sweet, with no colour on it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the drained peas, the hock, savory, bay and the water; bring it up and skim the grey foam for the first ten minutes, then drop it to a bare tremble and leave the lid off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 2.5 to 3 hours, until a pea crushes to nothing against the side of the pot and the hock meat pulls off the bone in strings.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the hock, shred the meat back into the pot and discard the bone, skin and bay; season with plenty of pepper and only now with salt, and thin with water if it will not hold the track of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Whole yellow peas, not split: split peas dissolve to a puree inside an hour and habitant soup wants some of them still whole. Two things keep the skins tough, and neither is salt. Acid is one, so no vinegar or tomato goes in until the peas are soft. A hard boil is the other; it batters the peas apart at the top while the starch on the bottom scorches. Salt at the end, because the hock keeps giving up salt for three hours.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Soupe%20aux%20Pois%20Jaunes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Soupe aux Pois Jaunes"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Peas","Bay leaf","Butter"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"hodge-podge","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g new potatoes, walnut-sized, skins on"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g baby carrots, halved lengthwise"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g yellow wax beans, topped"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g green beans, topped"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g shelled green peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g butter, in pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml 35 percent cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp chopped chives"}],"steps":[{"text":"Put the potatoes and carrots in a wide pot with the water and salt, cover, and cook at a low boil until a knife enters a potato against slight resistance, about 12 minutes.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lay the wax and green beans on top, cover again and steam 5 minutes until they have lost their raw snap but still squeak against the tooth, adding the peas for the last 2 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":420,"estimated":false},{"text":"Do not drain. Drop in the butter and swirl the pot until it disappears into the starchy liquor and the whole thing turns cloudy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the cream, bring it only to the point where the surface shivers, and take it straight off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with white pepper, check the salt and scatter the chives over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sauce is not cream poured over vegetables, it is the cooking liquor: new potatoes shed enough starch into 300 ml of water to hold butter and cream in suspension. So do not drain the pot, and use waxy new potatoes, because a floury baker sloughs its outside into the water and gives you glue. Bring the cream just to a shiver. At a rolling boil that emulsion splits and you get butterfat floating on grey water.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hodge%20Podge%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hodge Podge"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Carrot","Peas","Green beans","Chives","Butter","Cream","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"ketchup-aux-fruits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg ripe tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp pickling salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g peaches, peeled and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pears, peeled and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 apples, peeled and diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery ribs, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp mixed pickling spice tied in muslin"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp ground clove"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the tomatoes with the salt and leave them in a colander 30 minutes; what drains out is water you would otherwise boil off for an hour.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the drained tomatoes in a wide preserving pan with the peaches, pears, apples, onion, celery, sugar and the spice bag, and bring to a simmer, stirring until you cannot feel sugar on the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook uncovered at a lazy blip for about 90 minutes, stirring in from the corners as it thickens, until no free liquid pools around the fruit and a spoon drawn across the base leaves a track that closes slowly.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the vinegar, cinnamon and clove and simmer 10 minutes more, then lift out the spice bag and taste for salt and sharpness.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle hot into sterilised jars and seal, or cool and keep it in the fridge for a month.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Use a wide pan: evaporation is the entire job and a deep pot doubles the time. Vinegar goes in at the end because acetic acid boils off, and an hour of simmering leaves the ketchup tasting flat and only sweet. Once the sugar concentrates it will catch and scorch in seconds, so keep the spoon moving in the corners. It thickens again as it cools, so pull it one shade looser than you want it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ketchup%20aux%20Fruits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ketchup aux Fruits"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"apples-and-pears-the-autumn-keepers","term":"Apples & Pears: The Autumn Keepers"},{"slug":"clove","term":"Clove"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Celery","Apple","Stone fruit","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"butter-tarts","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g all-purpose flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"170 g cold butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g soft brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"170 g golden corn syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g raisins, soaked 10 minutes in hot water and drained"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the cold butter into the flour and salt until the biggest pieces are the size of split almonds, add the ice water, press the dough together without kneading and chill 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 3 mm thick, cut 11 cm rounds, line a 12-cup muffin tin and chill again until the pastry is firm to the fingertip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the brown sugar, corn syrup, melted butter, eggs, vinegar, vanilla and salt just until smooth, stopping before it goes pale; beaten-in air makes the filling dome and then crater.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the plumped raisins into the shells and fill each two-thirds only, since the filling climbs and any overflow glues the tarts into the tin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C (375F) for 5 minutes to set the bases, drop to 175C (350F) and bake 15 to 18 minutes, until the filling is puffed at the rim and still shivers like loose jelly in the middle.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1380,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool 10 minutes in the tin, then twist each tart free while the caramel at the edge is still soft.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"The filling is a custard, not a caramel: egg sets it, and egg curdles. Pull them while the centre shivers and let carryover finish the job; leave them in until the middle is firm and you get a grainy tart that weeps syrup. Runny or firm is two minutes apart. On the raisin question Ontario has argued for a century and will not stop, but the technical point is that dry raisins pull moisture out of the filling around them, so plump them first.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Butter%20Tarts%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Butter Tarts"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Butter","Flour","Vinegar","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"tire-derable-sur-la-neige","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml amber maple syrup, rich taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"a tray of clean packed snow, or crushed ice frozen hard"},{"kind":"item","text":"wooden sticks"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pack the snow into a tray, press it flat and hard, and put it back outside or in the freezer until you need it; loose snow melts under the syrup and gives you sweet slush.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the syrup and butter to a boil in a pan at least four times as deep as the syrup, because it climbs violently near the finish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook to 113 to 115C (235 to 240F), or until a drop into cold water forms a ball that flattens between finger and thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the syrup in long ribbons over the cold snow and count ten seconds, until the surface goes from shiny to dull.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Catch one end with a stick and roll the ribbon up onto it, and eat it before it stiffens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Grades tell you when the sap ran, not how good it is. Golden, delicate, is early and belongs on a plate uncooked; amber, rich, is the middle of the run and the one to boil, since its flavour survives concentration; dark and very dark come late and suit baking and glazes. Then it is all temperature: 113C (235F) ropes onto the stick, two degrees more shatters like glass, two less slides off as syrup. Subtract from the target however far your boiling point sits under 100C.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tire%20d'%C3%89rable%20sur%20la%20Neige%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tire d'Érable sur la Neige"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Sugar","Maple syrup"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"seared-digby-scallops","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 large dry-pack sea scallops, about 600 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice of half a lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp chopped parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"flaky salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pull the small tough side muscle off each scallop, lay them on a towel and leave them uncovered in the fridge 30 minutes, turning once, until the surface reads matte and tacky instead of wet and glossy.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the oil in a heavy pan until it shimmers and a flicked drop of water skitters; salt the scallops as they go in, not before, and lay them down with a finger's gap between each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear undisturbed for about 90 seconds, until a deep even crust has built and each one releases from the pan by itself, then turn them once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the butter, let it foam and go nut-brown, and baste for 30 seconds; lift them at 50 to 52C (122 to 126F), when the sides have just gone opaque and the centre still looks translucent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, sharpen the brown butter with lemon, stir in the parsley and spoon it over the scallops with a grind of pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ask for dry scallops. Wet-pack ones are soaked in phosphate, carry up to a third more water and weep it into the pan on contact, so they steam grey instead of browning and then shrink to rubber. Even dry ones need their surface water gone before they take colour. After that it is a race: the protein sets around 50C and starts squeezing out its own moisture past 55C, which is why the pan has to be hot enough to build the crust inside ninety seconds.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brown butter","Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brown butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brown%20butter%20beurre%20noisette%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Seared%20Digby%20Scallops%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Seared Digby Scallops"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Canadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Scallops","Lemon","Parsley","Butter"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"sofrito-cubano","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g white onion, in 5mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g green cubanelle peppers, seeded and in 5mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g garlic (about 12 cloves)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g fine salt, plus more to taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g dried oregano, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g ripe tomato, grated on the coarse side and skins discarded"},{"kind":"item","text":"80ml vino seco (Cuban dry white cooking wine)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1g cracked black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the oil in a wide pan over low heat, add the onion and pepper with a pinch of the salt and sweat until they slump and turn glassy with no trace of browning, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mash the garlic to a smooth paste in a mortar with a little salt, stir it in with the cumin and oregano and cook only until the raw sting turns nutty and the oil stains gold, roughly 90 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the grated tomato and bay and cook down, stirring less and less often, until the mass pulls back from the pan and a clear rim of red oil pools at the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the vino seco, scrape the base clean and cook until the alcohol smell has gone and the pan is thick again; season with the remaining salt and the pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool completely, pack into a jar and cover the surface with a film of oil; it keeps ten days cold and freezes in ice cube trays.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The signal is the oil breaking out. Sofrito is done when the water has gone and the fat re-separates, because only then can the pan get above 100C and the sugars brown. Push the heat early and the onion colours while it is still wet: you get scorched bitterness and no sweetness underneath. Cumin goes into the hot oil, never the watery stage, since its aroma is fat soluble.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Mortar & pestle","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sofrito%20Cubano%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sofrito Cubano"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Oregano","Bay leaf","Wine","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"frijoles-negros-dormidos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g dried black beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green cubanelle pepper, halved and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g white onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g green cubanelle pepper, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g garlic (about 9 cloves)"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml vino seco"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the beans overnight in the cold water with the halved pepper; do not drain them, that black liquor is the body of the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring to a boil, skim the grey foam, then hold at a bare tremble, topping up so the beans stay just covered, until a bean crushes to smooth paste with no chalky point in the middle, about 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile sweat the diced onion, pepper and garlic in the olive oil until slumped, stir in the cumin and oregano until fragrant, then mash in a ladle of cooked beans to thicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape the sofrito into the pot with the bay and the salt and simmer uncovered until the liquor slides off a spoon in a glossy sheet rather than running clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take off the heat and stir in the vinegar, the vino seco and the sugar; cool, refrigerate overnight and reheat gently to serve over white rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Acid is what keeps a black bean leathery: vinegar, wine or tomato added early stops the pectin in the cell walls from dissolving, and no amount of boiling will fix it. Hold all three until a bean crushes clean between finger and thumb. The overnight sleep is not superstition either; leached starch sets as the pot cools and thickens the liquor without a spoonful of flour.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Keeping the cooking liquid","Soaking dried goods back to life","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":36,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Frijoles%20Negros%20Dormidos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Frijoles Negros Dormidos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Oregano","Bay leaf","Black beans","Vinegar","Sugar","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"moros-y-cristianos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g dried black beans, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.8 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g long grain white rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g tocino or slab bacon, in 5mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"180g white onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g green cubanelle pepper, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g garlic (about 9 cloves), mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml vino seco"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the soaked beans in the water with one bay leaf until tender but still whole, then measure out 800ml of the black cooking liquor and keep the beans separately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the tocino in the olive oil over medium heat until the fat runs and the dice are amber, then sweat the onion, pepper and garlic in that fat until slumped and sweet; stir in the cumin and oregano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the rice under cold water until the water runs almost clear, drain it well, then stir it through the sofrito until every grain is filmed in fat and has turned chalky opaque.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the 800ml of liquor with the vino seco, beans, bay, salt and sugar; taste the liquid and pull it a shade saltier than you want the finished rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil hard and uncovered until craters open on the surface and the liquid drops level with the rice, then clamp on a lid, drop to the lowest heat for 18 minutes, and rest 10 minutes off the heat before forking it up from the edges.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1680,"estimated":false}],"note":"The colour comes from the bean liquor, so throwing it away leaves you grey rice and a lost dish. Rinsing strips loose surface amylose and the coat of fat seals what remains, which is what keeps the grains separate rather than gluing into a cake. Lift the lid during the 18 minutes and the steam that should be cooking the top layer escapes: chalky grains above, a wet slick below.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","earthy","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moros%20y%20Cristianos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moros y Cristianos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Onion","Garlic","Oregano","Bay leaf","Rice","Black beans","Sugar","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"lechon-asado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4kg bone-in pork shoulder, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"70g garlic (about 20 cloves)"},{"kind":"item","text":"35g coarse salt, plus 10g for the skin"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml naranja agria juice, or 350ml orange juice with 150ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 white onions, sliced into rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the skin in a close diamond lattice, cutting through rind and fat but stopping before the meat, then drive a thin knife deep into the flesh in twenty places to make pockets.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic, coarse salt, cumin, oregano and peppercorns to a wet paste in a mortar, push it into every pocket, whisk the remainder into the sour orange with the oil, onions and bay and pour it over; marinate cold for 24 hours, turning twice.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the meat out, wipe the skin bone dry and rub it with the reserved salt; roast fat side up at 150C (300F), basting the meat but never the rind, until a probe slides in with no resistance and the centre reads 92C (198F), about 5 hours.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the oven to 230C (450F) and roast until the rind has blistered into crackling all over and taps hollow, 20 to 30 minutes; rest 30 minutes, then pull the meat into strands.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain the marinade, boil it hard for 3 minutes with a spoonful of the pan fat and pour it over the shredded pork as mojo.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two temperatures doing two opposite jobs. The long 150C stretch is for collagen, which needs hours above 70C to melt into gelatin; the 92C reading tells you it has, not the clock. Crackling needs the reverse, a bone dry surface and fierce heat, which is why the rind is never basted. Any liquid on it steams the skin and it stays leathery however hot you finish. Boil the used marinade before it touches cooked meat.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Asado fire","Scoring","Roasting in a hot oven","Straining & passing through a sieve","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","earthy","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, an earthy, grounding base, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Asado fire","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=argentine%20asado%20fire%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lech%C3%B3n%20Asado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lechón Asado"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Lime","Orange","Oregano","Bay leaf","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["scoring","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"yuca-con-mojo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg yuca, fresh or frozen"},{"kind":"item","text":"25g salt for the cooking water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g garlic (about 12 cloves)"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g flaky salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml naranja agria juice, or 80ml orange juice with 40ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g white onion, in thin rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"1g cracked black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the yuca into 8cm lengths, pare away the brown bark and the pink underskin beneath it, split each length and pull out the woody central cord, which never softens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil hard in heavily salted water until a knife meets no grip and the pieces begin to split along their seams, 20 to 30 minutes; drain and keep them hot in the covered pan.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash the garlic with the flaky salt to a loose paste in a mortar and scrape it into a heatproof bowl with the cumin, the onion rings and the pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the olive oil until it shimmers and a scrap of garlic fizzes the instant it lands, around 130C (265F), and pour it straight over the paste; it should hiss and foam up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the sour orange juice in off the heat, taste for salt, and pour the mojo over the hot yuca so it drinks while the starch is still open.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mojo is scalded garlic, not fried garlic. Oil at roughly 130C hits the raw paste hard enough to knock out the harsh allicin and set its sweetness, but not hot enough to brown it. Cooler and it stays raw and acrid; hotter and it turns bitter in seconds. The juice goes in off the heat, because boiling the sour orange kills the sharp edge that cuts through all that starch.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","earthy","briny","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, an earthy, grounding base, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yuca%20con%20Mojo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yuca con Mojo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Lime","Orange","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"tostones-rellenos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 green plantains, hard and dark skinned"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water with 20g salt and 4 mashed garlic cloves, for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g raw shrimp, peeled and roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g white onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g green cubanelle pepper, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g garlic, mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g ripe tomato, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml vino seco"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g hot pimentón"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the plantain skin along its ridges and lever it off in strips, cut the flesh into 4cm barrels and drop them into the garlic salt water while the frying oil comes up to heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and dry the barrels, then fry at 150C (300F) until pale straw, floating, and yielding all the way to the centre when you push in a skewer, 5 to 6 minutes; lift out onto paper.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"While they are still hot, press each barrel into a tostonera cup or over the base of a small glass to open it into a walled cup; if one cracks apart, the first fry was short.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For the filling, sweat the onion, pepper and garlic in the olive oil, add tomato, cumin, oregano and pimentón and cook until the oil separates, then stir in the shrimp and vino seco and pull the pan off the heat the moment they curl and go opaque.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the frying oil to 190C (375F) and fry the cups until deep gold and audibly rattling, about 2 minutes; drain, salt at once, fill hot and serve with lime.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two fries, two jobs. The first at 150C is really a poach in oil: it gelatinises the plantain's dense starch right through to the middle, which is the only reason you can press the barrel flat instead of shattering it. The second at 190C drives water out of that crushed, opened surface and browns it. One hot fry gives a burnt shell over a raw soapy core, and a cooled barrel breaks under the press every time.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Scoring","Deep frying","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","smoky","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, wood-smoke running underneath, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tostones%20Rellenos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tostones Rellenos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Plantain","Lime","Oregano","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["scoring","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"picadillo-a-la-habanera","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g coarsely ground beef chuck, about 20 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g waxy potato, in 1cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g white onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g green cubanelle pepper, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"25g garlic, mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g ripe tomato, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml vino seco"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g pitted green Spanish olives, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"25g capers, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"15ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the diced potato in the olive oil until the edges are gold and the centres give, lift onto paper and salt lightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the heat, spread the beef in one flat layer in the same pan and leave it completely still until the underside has crusted and releases on its own; only then break it up and salt it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the meat aside, sweat the onion, pepper and garlic in the rendered fat until slumped, then stir the cumin and oregano into the hot fat until they smell toasted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomato, vino seco and bay and simmer until the fat rises and shines on the surface, then fold in the olives, capers and raisins and cook 10 minutes more.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off the heat, stir in the vinegar and return the potatoes; check the salt, aiming for a balance that lands savoury, then sweet, then sour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt the beef in the pan, not in the bowl. Salt sitting on raw mince dissolves myosin, the strands bind to each other and it cooks into springy pellets instead of loose crumb. Brown it as one motionless layer: stirring or crowding drops the pan under 100C and the meat greys and steams in its own juice. The raisins are structural, not decorative, and they are the sweet leg braced against the olives, capers and vinegar.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Picadillo%20a%20la%20Habanera%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Picadillo a la Habanera"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Olives","Capers","Dried fruit","Oregano","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"arroz-con-pollo-a-la-chorrera","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4kg chicken, jointed into 8 pieces, bone in and skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g medium grain white rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"80ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"180g white onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g green cubanelle pepper, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"25g garlic, mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g ground annatto seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g ripe tomato, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml vino seco"},{"kind":"item","text":"330ml pale lager"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.1 litres chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g petit pois"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g jarred pimientos morrones, in strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the chicken and brown it hard in the oil, skin down and unmoved, until the skin is deep gold and lifts away from the pan by itself; set it aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, pepper and garlic in the same fat until slumped, stir in the cumin, oregano and annatto, then the tomato, and cook until an orange rim of oil separates.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the chicken with the vino seco, lager, stock and bay and simmer until the thigh reads 74C (165F) against the bone, about 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rain in the rice, stir once to settle it, then simmer uncovered at a lazy bubble without touching it again until the grains are tender but the pot is still loose and soupy, 18 to 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the peas and morrones over the surface, cover, cut the heat and let it stand 5 minutes; ladle into bowls with lime.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"A la chorrera means running wet, and that is the whole point: about three parts liquid to one of rice by volume, where a dry arroz takes two. Medium grain leaks amylose and thickens the broth into sauce around the grains. Stir once the rice is in and you scrub out far more starch than you want, and the pot sets to paste. Peas go on at the very end or the acid in the broth turns them olive.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","briny","tangy-ferment","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, a briny, maritime salinity, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arroz%20con%20Pollo%20a%20la%20Chorrera%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arroz con Pollo a la Chorrera"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Lime","Oregano","Bay leaf","Rice","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"vaca-frita","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg beef flank or skirt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 white onion, halved, plus 2 more sliced thin for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"35g garlic (about 10 cloves), mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml lime juice, or naranja agria"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the flank with the halved onion, carrot, bay and peppercorns in barely trembling water until a strand pulls free with no spring left in it, about 90 minutes; let it cool in the broth.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shred the meat along the grain into thin strands, then squeeze each handful dry over the sink so no broth is left in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the strands with the mashed garlic, lime juice, cumin and salt and leave exactly 30 minutes, no longer.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Get a heavy pan or plancha very hot with a film of olive oil, lay in half the beef in a thin layer, press it down flat and do not touch it until the underside is mahogany and crisping at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn once, throw in half the sliced onion and let it char at the edges while staying juicy at the heart, squeeze lime into the pan so it hisses, scrape up and serve at once; repeat with the second batch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boiled tender then fried dry, and the second half only works if the strands are squeezed, spread thin and left alone. Wet heaped meat steams: its surface never climbs past 100C and no crust can form. Work in two batches even when the pan looks big enough. Keep the lime marinade to half an hour as well, because acid on already cooked protein goes on tightening it and the strands turn to wire.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","gently hot","earthy","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a warm current of chile, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vaca%20Frita%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vaca Frita"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"flank-skirt-and-hanger","term":"Flank, Skirt & Hanger"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Lime","Bay leaf","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"flan-cubano","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"397g tin sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"354ml tin evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 whole eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"10ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the sugar with the water and lime over medium heat without stirring, swirling the pan only, until it is the colour of dark honey and smells faintly bitter, then pour it at once into a warmed 20cm mould and tilt to coat the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs and yolks just until the ropes of white have broken, stopping well short of foam, then whisk in the condensed milk, evaporated milk, vanilla and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain the custard through a fine sieve onto the set caramel and lift off any bubbles from the surface with the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with foil, set in a roasting tin, pour boiling water halfway up the mould and bake at 160C (325F) until the edge is set and the middle wobbles as one piece, or the centre reads 78C (172F), 55 to 65 minutes.","durationSec":3900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Chill at least 6 hours, run a thin knife around the rim and invert onto a lipped dish so the loosened caramel floods out with it.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Egg proteins set from about 75C and curdle by 85C, so the water bath is structural, not fussy: it caps the mould at 100C and heats evenly, while a dry oven drives the outer custard past 85C and leaves it weeping and pitted with holes. Bake to a single wobble, never to firm, because it keeps setting as it cools. The caramel only turns liquid after a night of drawing water out of the custard; unmould early and half stays in the tin.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Sugar stages & caramel","Straining & passing through a sieve","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","silky","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a silken, spoon-slow texture, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Flan%20Cubano%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Flan Cubano"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuban%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cuban kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lime","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"aish-baladi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g stoneground wholemeal flour, full extraction"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"390 g warm water at 32C (90F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g coarse wheat bran, for the boards and the coating"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix both flours, the yeast and all the water to a shaggy mass and leave it 20 minutes, then work in the salt and knead until the dough stops tearing and pulls off the bowl wall in one sheet.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cover and ferment warm until doubled and domed, roughly 90 minutes, then divide into six pieces of about 165 g and roll each ball through bran until dry to the hand.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flatten each into an 18 cm disc a good 8 mm thick on a bran dusted board, cover, and prove until a pressed fingerprint fills back slowly, about 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a baking stone or steel at 300C (570F) for a full 45 minutes, then slide two discs straight on.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bake 2 to 3 minutes: the disc rises like a pillow, the crown pales to gold and the bran freckles. Stack them under a cloth as they come out so they collapse soft rather than crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pocket is steam. At 300C the skin sets in seconds while the interior water flashes and lifts it, so a cool oven is fatal: below about 260C the disc dries out before pressure builds and you get a flat cracker. Bran, not flour, on the boards; flour hydrates into a paste that glues the two faces together and pins the loaf shut.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aish%20Baladi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Aish Baladi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"taameya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g dried split skinless fava beans, soaked 12 hours"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g leek, white and pale green only"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g coriander leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres sunflower oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the soaked fava hard and spread it on a cloth until the beans look matt and feel dry to the fingertips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pass the fava through the coarse plate of a mincer with the onion, leek, garlic and all three herbs, then send it through a second time: you want a damp green rubble that takes a fingerprint, never a smooth puree.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill the paste covered for an hour, then fold in the salt, spices and bicarbonate only when the oil is already hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape 45 g patties 2 cm thick, press one face hard into the sesame and slide them sesame down into oil at 175C (350F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry 3 to 4 minutes, turning once, until the crust is dark and crackles under a spoon; drain on a rack, not paper, and salt while steaming.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Torn bean cell walls and their own starch are the only binder here; no egg, no flour. Salt and bicarbonate pull water out fast, so a paste seasoned early goes slack and the patty steams itself hollow and bursts in the oil. A blender does the same damage by another route: a smooth paste has no structure, drinks fat and fries grey instead of green.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Falafel","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","nutty-toasted","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ta'ameya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ta'ameya"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Leek","Parsley","Dill","White beans","Sesame","Cumin","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"besara","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g dried split skinless fava beans, soaked 8 hours"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g leek, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g coriander leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 hot green chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml sunflower oil, for the onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained fava with the leek and garlic, skimming the grey scum off the top, until a bean crushes to cream between finger and thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop in the parsley, dill, coriander and chilli and give them 2 minutes only, just until the leaves go limp and still smell raw and green.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":false},{"text":"Blitz hot with the cumin, salt and olive oil to a thick pouring green, slackening with cooking water; judge it looser than you want it, because it sets firm as it cools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Start the sliced onion in cold sunflower oil and fry, stirring, until it is evenly amber; lift it out while a shade paler than you want, it keeps browning off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the besara in a shallow dish, flood the surface with the hot onion oil, heap on the crisp onion and squeeze lemon at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The herbs go in at the end because chlorophyll turns from green to olive brown within a few minutes of heat, and once the puree is acidified with lemon it browns faster still. Blitz while the beans are hot so the starch is gelatinised and blends silky; reheated or reboiled, besara splits into a watery ring around a stiff green paste.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","briny","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Besara%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Besara"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Leek","Chiles","Lemon","Parsley","Dill","White beans","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"molokhia-bi-taqliya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g frozen finely minced molokhia leaf, or 100 g dried molokhia"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres strong chicken or rabbit stock, skimmed clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g garlic, about 8 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g samna"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried red chilli, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the stock to a bare shiver and season it properly now; it carries the whole dish, the leaf brings almost no salt of its own.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the molokhia, breaking up the frozen block, and hold the pot at about 85C (185F) with only the odd bubble at the rim until the leaf swells and hangs suspended through the broth, 8 to 10 minutes. It must never roll.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic to a paste with a pinch of salt and fry it in the samna over medium heat with the ground coriander and chilli, 60 to 90 seconds, until the garlic is pale gold and the smell turns sharp and toasted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crash the taqliya into the pot so it hisses, clap on the lid for a minute, stir once and kill the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve at once over rice, with lemon, bread and raw onion in vinegar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The body of molokhia is a soluble mucilage that holds the shredded leaf in suspension. A rolling boil shears it, the leaf sinks and you are left with green sludge under grey broth that no amount of stirring re-marries. Hold it under a simmer, add the taqliya at the last second, and do not reheat past a shiver. Take the garlic off at pale gold; brown garlic turns the pot bitter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Molokhia%20bi%20Taqliya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Molokhia bi Taqliya"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Stock/Broth","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"fatta-bil-lahma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg lamb neck on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods, 2 bay leaves, 1 g mastic, 6 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g Egyptian short grain rice, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 rounds aish baladi, about 200 g, split and torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g tomato passata"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g samna"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the lamb with cold water, bring it up slowly and skim until the surface runs clear, then add the onion, cardamom, bay, mastic and pepper and hold at a bare simmer until a bone pulls free clean. Strain and keep the broth hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the torn bread in a little samna in a wide pan, or on a tray at 200C (390F), until it is dry to the centre, brittle and biscuit brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the rice in 1.5 parts strained broth to 1 part rice with samna and salt until craters open on the surface, then cover and rest it off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the crushed garlic in samna until it just takes colour, stand back and pour in the vinegar, let it boil hard for 30 seconds, then add the passata and cook it down until it darkens and tastes sharp rather than raw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer in a wide dish: bread first, a ladle of hot broth to wet it, then rice, most of the sauce, the meat, and the last of the garlic sauce over the top. Carry it straight to the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The bread is a sponge on a clock. Toasted dry it drinks broth and still holds its shape for a few minutes; left soft, or wetted early, its starch saturates and the whole dish collapses into one grey paste. The vinegar is structural rather than decorative: it cuts the lamb fat so the layers stay legible. Toast hard and assemble at the last moment.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","warm-spiced","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, warm spice humming through the middle, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fatta%20bil%20Lahma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fatta bil Lahma"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bay leaf","Rice","Vinegar","Cardamom","Clove"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"hawawshi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g minced beef, about 20 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g green pepper, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 hot green chilli, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 rounds aish baladi"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g samna, melted"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wring the grated onion in a cloth until no more juice runs; that water is what steams the bread limp from the inside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the mince with the onion, pepper, parsley, chilli, tomato paste, spices and salt until the mixture turns tacky and clings to the back of the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split each bread at its seam and spread about 150 g of filling right to the rim in a layer no thicker than a finger, then press the round flat with your palm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush both faces with samna and bake on a preheated tray at 230C (450F), turning once, until the bread is blistered, patched dark brown and fat is bubbling at the edge, 12 to 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 3 minutes on a rack so the base drains and firms, then cut into wedges.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"The bread is both pan and lid, and it can only take so much time in the oven. A thick filling needs long enough to cook through that the base sits boiling in rendered fat and turns grey and floppy. Keep the layer finger thin, squeeze the onion dry, use a heat high enough to set the crust before the fat escapes, and rest it on a rack so the underside is not steamed by its own drippings.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","fiery","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hawawshi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hawawshi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Parsley","Tomato paste","Cumin","Coriander seed"]},{"slug":"mahshi-kromb","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 white cabbage, about 1.5 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g Egyptian short grain rice, rinsed until the water runs clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe tomatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g coriander leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g samna"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried mint"}],"steps":[{"text":"Core the cabbage, lower the head into boiling salted water and peel the leaves away as they slacken, lifting each one while it is still bright and bends without cracking; shave the thick rib flat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the raw rice with the grated tomato, onion, herbs, oil, cumin, coriander and salt. It should taste a shade too salty in the bowl; the rice will take it up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll pencil thick lines of filling in the leaves, seam down, packed loose, leaving a third of the leaf empty at each end for the rice to grow into.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line the pot with the shaved ribs and the outer leaves, pack the rolls in tight layers, set a plate on top as a weight, then pour in the water slaked with tomato paste and lemon juice to just cover.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring to a boil, drop to the faintest simmer for 45 minutes, then pour over the garlic and dried mint fried in the samna, cover and leave off the heat for 5 minutes.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false}],"note":"Rice roughly triples as it cooks. Fill a leaf tight and the swelling grains have nowhere to go, so they split the wrapper and empty into the broth, which then thickens to porridge and scorches on the base. Pack loose, leave headroom at both ends, and set the plate on before the liquid goes in so the rolls cannot float and unwind. A hard boil undoes all of it.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","umami-deep","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mahshi%20Kromb%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mahshi Kromb"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Cabbage","Lemon","Parsley","Mint","Dill","Rice","Tomato paste","Cumin","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"dukkah","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100 g hazelnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g roasted chickpeas"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g flaky salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried mint"}],"steps":[{"text":"Roast the hazelnuts at 170C (340F) until the skins split and a cut nut is straw coloured to the centre, then rub them in a cloth while hot to shed the skins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the sesame in a dry pan, moving it without pause, until the seeds hiss, jump and smell oily; tip them out of the pan at once, they keep colouring in the residual heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the coriander seed until it smells of orange peel, then the cumin on its own until it darkens one shade; they reach their point minutes apart, so never toast them together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool everything to room temperature, then pound or pulse the nuts, chickpeas and seeds in short bursts to a dry rubble that still rattles; stir in the salt, pepper and mint.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dukkah is a dry crumble, and the thing that ruins it is hazelnut oil. Pounded while warm, or ground a few seconds too long, the nuts weep and the mix packs into a paste that smears on oiled bread instead of clinging in separate grains. Cool completely, work in bursts, and keep it airtight and dark; free oil also turns rancid within a fortnight.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","herb-fresh","earthy","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dukkah%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dukkah"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"dukkah","term":"Dukkah"},{"slug":"coriander-seed","term":"Coriander Seed"}],"pantryItems":["Mint","Chickpeas","Sesame","Nuts","Cumin","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"feteer-meshaltet","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g samna, melted and kept warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"honey, thick cream or salty white cheese, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the flour, salt and water to a soft slack dough and knead just 5 minutes, to smoothness and no further; this dough needs to stretch, not to fight back.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into eight, roll each ball through the melted samna until slick, cover and rest 45 minutes, until a poked ball dents with no resistance and does not spring back.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"On a heavily greased worktop, push and coax one ball outwards from the centre until it is a sheet you can read newsprint through; holes at the thin edge are of no consequence.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush the sheet with samna, fold it in to a small square parcel, and set it aside; repeat with the rest, then stack the parcels, brushing samna between them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press and stretch the stack into a greased tray, brush the top, and bake at 220C (430F) until the top leaves lift, blister and go mahogany and the base is crisp, 25 to 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Samna is clarified, so it is pure fat: it coats each sheet and keeps it separate, which is why the finished feteer shatters into leaves. Whole butter carries water, and water hydrates the flour where the sheets touch, gluing them into bread. Flour on the bench does the same. If the dough snaps back and tears, it is underrested, not underworked; give it warmth and another 20 minutes.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Feteer%20Meshaltet%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Feteer Meshaltet"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Cream","Cheese (any)","Flour","Honey"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"basbousa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g coarse semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g full fat yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g samna, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g desiccated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g tahini, for the tin"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 blanched almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g sugar, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Make the syrup first: boil the sugar, water and lemon juice to 105C (220F), about 8 minutes, until it runs from the spoon in a thin thread, then cool it completely.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir the semolina with the caster sugar, coconut and baking powder, then beat in the yoghurt and warm samna to a thick dropping batter and leave it 30 minutes for the grain to drink.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Smear the tin with tahini, level the batter to 2 cm, score it into diamonds cutting right down to the base and press an almond into each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C (375F) until the top is an even chestnut brown and the edges have shrunk from the tin, 30 to 35 minutes.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":2100,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour the cold syrup over the cake the second it comes out of the oven, aiming into the score lines; it should hiss and vanish within a couple of minutes. Leave it 2 hours before lifting the pieces out.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The soak works on a temperature gap: steam condensing inside a hot crumb pulls cold syrup in fast and evenly. Hot syrup on hot cake sits on the surface and sogs the top while the middle stays dry; cold on cold barely penetrates at all. Score to the base so every diamond can drink from its sides, and never pour more syrup on a cake that has already stopped hissing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Scoring","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basbousa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Basbousa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Coconut","Yogurt","Semolina","Tahini","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["scoring","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"banku","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g fermented white maize dough, three days old"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g grated cassava dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Break both doughs into the cold water and squeeze them through your fingers until no lump larger than a grain survives; the slurry should pour like thin cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set over medium heat and stir without pause with a flat wooden spatula, scraping base and corners, until the mixture turns from chalk white to glossy and starts to drag on the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the heat and keep beating, pressing the paste up the pot wall and folding it back, for 20 minutes; it is ready when it pulls away in one mass and tastes of sour bread rather than raw flour.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the salt, then wet your hands in cold water and mould the hot paste into smooth balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap each ball while it is still hot and eat warm, before the cooling starch sets it rubbery.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sour is lactic acid from three days of wild fermentation in the maize dough; the cassava dough brings the amylopectin that lets banku stretch instead of crumble. Slake both cold: dropped into hot water they skin over and lump for good. Stir without stopping or the base scorches bitter. Then pinch, dip, swallow whole; chewing floods it with salivary amylase and the mouthful goes slack and sweet.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Banku%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Banku"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":[],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"tuo-zaafi-ne-ayoyo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g white maize flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.9 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ayoyo (jute mallow) leaves, stripped from the stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp kanwa (potash), crushed fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp dawadawa (fermented locust bean), crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ground dried okra"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ground dried chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the kanwa in 800 ml of the water, bring to the boil with the onion, then drop in the ayoyo and boil uncovered until the leaves collapse and the liquid strings off the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the dawadawa, ground okra, chilli and salt and simmer 10 minutes, until the soup draws in an unbroken thread and smells of fermented bean rather than raw leaf.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"In a second pot, whisk 100 g of the maize flour into the remaining cold water and bring to a simmer, whisking, until it thickens to a porridge that coats the whisk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the rest of the flour a handful at a time with a flat wooden spatula, crushing each addition against the side of the pot before the next goes in, until the mass is smooth, glossy and just holds a shape.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and leave on the lowest heat 5 minutes to cook out the raw flour, beat once more, then mould with a wet ladle and serve with the ayoyo alongside.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kanwa is the lesson. The alkali lifts the pH, which swells the jute's mucilage into the draw that carries the swallow, and it holds the leaf green rather than olive. Overdo it and the soup turns slippery and soapy and the leaf tastes of ash, so one level teaspoon for the pot. The tuo is cooked in two stages because flour beaten into an already gelatinised porridge cannot lump.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","tangy-ferment","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, ferment tang with real depth behind it, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tuo%20Zaafi%20ne%20Ayoyo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tuo Zaafi ne Ayoyo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Chiles","Okra","Flour"],"judgedBy":["fermentation"]},{"slug":"waakye","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g dried black-eyed peas, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g long grain rice, rinsed until the water runs clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 dried sorghum leaf sheaths (waakye leaves)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp gari, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the sorghum leaves with the bicarbonate in the water for 10 minutes, until the liquid runs the colour of dark mahogany, then lift the leaves out and throw them away.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the drained peas and simmer with the lid ajar until a bean crushes to paste between finger and thumb while its skin stays whole, topping up with boiling water to keep them covered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the rice and salt so the liquid sits two fingers above the grain, then boil hard and uncovered until craters open right across the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover, drop to the lowest heat and leave until steam holes have tunnelled through to the base and no water pools when you tilt the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest covered off the heat, then fork through so the beans lift and the grains separate, stained rust red. Serve with gari.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The colour is anthocyanin leached out of the dried sorghum leaf, and it reads red-brown only away from acid, which is why the pot takes a pinch of bicarbonate; the same alkali softens the bean skins by loosening their pectin. Go past a quarter teaspoon and the rice turns slimy and soapy and the peas slump to mush. Rinse the rice hard first or its surface starch will clag the grains into a block.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Waakye%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Waakye"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Peas","Rice"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"nkatenkwan-groundnut-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg chicken, jointed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g unsweetened groundnut paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ginger, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 scotch bonnet chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garden eggs, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the chicken with salt, half the ginger and one grated onion, then set it over low heat with a splash of water, covered, until it gives up its own juices and the flesh stiffens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the tomatoes with the second onion, the garlic, the rest of the ginger and the chillies, pour into a wide pot and boil hard, stirring, until it darkens and the raw smell is gone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slake the groundnut paste with 500 ml warm water, whisking to a smooth cream with no grain left in it, then stir it into the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chicken with its juices, the garden eggs and the remaining water; simmer uncovered, stirring the base every few minutes, until the soup coats a spoon and a slick of red gold oil rises and holds on the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the garden eggs, crush them and stir them back for body, then season and serve with rice balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Groundnut paste is oil suspended in peanut protein. Tip it straight into a hot pot and the protein tightens, the oil floods out and the soup goes grainy, so slake it to a cream in warm water first. After that it has to simmer uncovered and be stirred, because the paste sinks and catches within seconds and a scorched base turns the whole pot bitter. The oil that finally rises is the doneness cue, not a split.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nkatenkwan%20(Groundnut%20Soup)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nkatenkwan (Groundnut Soup)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"nkrakra-tilapia-light-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole tilapia, about 400 g each, scaled, gutted and cut into thick steaks"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garden eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 scotch bonnet chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 pods hwentia (grains of selim), bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.3 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the tomatoes, one onion, the garden eggs, chillies and ginger in the water until the tomato skins slip and the garden eggs are collapsing soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the solids into a blender with a ladle of the liquid, blend smooth and return them to the pot with the hwentia and the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered 20 minutes, until the raw tomato smell has gone and the soup tastes round and seasoned but stays thin enough to drink from a spoon.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the second onion into the pot, slide in the fish and do not stir; hold the surface at a bare tremble until the flesh at the backbone turns opaque and lifts cleanly from the bone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest off the heat 5 minutes, then lift the fish out with a slotted spoon and serve with fufu or banku.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Light soup carries no fat and no thickener. All its body is pulp from blended garden egg and tomato, so it must be simmered down before the fish goes in and never boiled after. A rolling boil shreds tilapia, clouds the broth and drags collagen out of the skin. That thin, sharp, aromatic liquid is the whole point, and the exact opposite of nkatenkwan, where rendered fat is the body.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nkrakra%20(Tilapia%20Light%20Soup)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nkrakra (Tilapia Light Soup)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","White fish","Eggs","Onion","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"red-red","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g dried black-eyed peas, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml red palm oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ground dried chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ripe plantains, skins heavily black spotted"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml groundnut oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp gari, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained peas with half an onion in unsalted water until the skins loosen and a bean crushes easily while still holding its shape; drain and keep the liquor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the palm oil over medium heat only until it thins and runs bright orange, well short of any smoke, then add the sliced onion and cook it to translucent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the ginger, chilli and tomato paste and fry, stirring, until the paste darkens to brick and oil pools red at the edge of the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the peas with enough of their liquor to loosen, salt, and simmer until the stew thickens and every bean is stained red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry thick diagonal slices of plantain in hot groundnut oil until the edges go deep amber and the centres yield to a spoon; serve alongside with gari.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The red is carotene and carotene is heat fragile. Push palm oil to its smoke point and it pales to straw, the fruity smell turns acrid, and no amount of tomato paste puts the colour back; warm it only until it runs fluid and bright. The plantain has to be black spotted. A yellow one is still starch rather than sugar, so it fries pale and dry instead of caramelising at the edge.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Keeping the cooking liquid","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","smoky","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, wood-smoke running underneath, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Red%20Red%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Red Red"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Peas","Plantain","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"chichinga","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g beef topside, cut across the grain into 3 mm ribbons"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g kulikuli (pressed groundnut cake), ground to a powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ground dried chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp onion powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp ground cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion and 2 tomatoes, sliced, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Thread the beef ribbons onto soaked skewers in a loose concertina so heat reaches both faces of every fold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the kulikuli with the chilli, ginger, garlic, onion powder, cloves and salt to a dry sandy dust; keep it dry, with no oil worked in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush the meat with the groundnut oil, press half the dust into it and leave 15 minutes while the coals burn down to grey ash with no flame licking up.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill close to the coals, turning every 30 seconds, until the edges frill and char in spots and the meat is only just firm; 4 to 6 minutes.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the fire, shower on the remaining dust and serve with raw onion and tomato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kulikuli is groundnut cake with the oil pressed out, and that is exactly the point: free peanut oil over coals at 250C (480F) goes acrid and bitter within seconds. Half the dust before the fire, so it toasts and grips, half after, so it stays raw and aromatic. Cut the beef thin and turn it constantly; a thick cube only cooks through long after its coating has burnt.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","aromatic","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chichinga%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chichinga"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Clove"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"kontomire-abom","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g kontomire (cocoyam leaves), coarse stalks stripped out"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 kpakpo shito chillies, or 2 scotch bonnet"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml red palm oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Shred the stripped leaves and wash them through several changes of water until no grit settles in the bottom of the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil hard in salted water, uncovered, for at least 15 minutes, until the leaves are drab army green and completely limp; still bright green means not yet.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and press the leaves against the sieve with the back of a ladle to force out every drop of water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the onion, chillies and tomato to a rough paste in an asanka, then work the leaves in a handful at a time to a coarse dark mash.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt it, pour the palm oil over off the heat and fold it through; eat warm or at room temperature with boiled yam or plantain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cocoyam leaf is loaded with needle shaped calcium oxalate crystals and a quick blanch leaves them intact, so the dish scratches and itches at the back of the throat for an hour afterwards. They break down only in a long, hard, uncovered boil: judge by colour and give it a full 15 minutes. The palm oil goes in off the heat, where it stays fruity and red instead of frying flat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kontomire%20Abom%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kontomire Abom"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"shito","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g onions, blended to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ginger, blended"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g garlic, blended"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g dried smoked herring, boned and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g dried shrimp, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g ground dried chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ground hwentia (grains of selim)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the onion, ginger and garlic paste in the oil over medium heat, stirring often, until the loud crackle of boiling water dies to a quiet sizzle and the mass is brown and grainy; 35 to 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the tomato paste and fry until it darkens to rust and clear oil separates at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the ground herring and shrimp and fry until they smell toasted rather than raw and the paste tightens around the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pot to the lowest heat, stir in the chilli, cloves, hwentia and salt, and cook, stirring constantly, until the whole mass is near black and the oil floats clear on top; about 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool completely, then pack into dry jars with the oil covering the solids by a finger's width.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Shito keeps because it is a confit: everything in it is fried until its water is gone, then sealed under oil that nothing can grow through. There are two ways to lose a batch. Rush the onion and the moisture left behind blooms mould inside a week. Add the chilli over a lively heat and it scorches in under a minute, leaving the pot grey and bitter instead of black and sweet.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","umami-deep","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shito%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shito"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Chiles","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"bofrot","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp nutmeg, freshly grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres groundnut oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, sugar, yeast, nutmeg and salt together, then beat in the egg and warm water to a thick sticky batter that falls from the spoon in a slow ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and leave somewhere warm until it has doubled and domed, the surface pocked with bubbles that pop when you knock the side of the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 170C (340F); a scrap of batter should surface within three seconds and take a full minute to take colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze the batter up between thumb and forefinger into a ball, scoop it off with a wet spoon straight into the oil, and fry until each one rolls over by itself and is an even deep amber all round; 5 to 6 minutes.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift onto a rack rather than paper so the crust stays crisp, and eat within the hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a batter, not a dough: nothing is kneaded, there is no gluten network, and the lift is all yeast gas held in a loose starch matrix. Give it the full rise or the centres stay wet and heavy. Hold the oil at 170C (340F). Hotter and the crust browns while the middle is raw batter; cooler and the ball sits still, drinks oil and comes out greasy. A pale belt round the equator says it turned at the right moment.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bofrot%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bofrot"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghanaian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ghanaian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Flour","Sugar","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"obe-ata-din-din","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g plum tomatoes, cored"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g red tatashe peppers, deseeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g red scotch bonnet, about six, stems pulled"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g red onion, half for blending and half sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 vegetable seasoning cubes, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dried thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the tomatoes, tatashe, scotch bonnet and half the onion coarse rather than smooth, so the finished sauce keeps some grain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the blend in a wide uncovered pot until it has lost half its volume and a spoon dragged across the base leaves a track that fills back slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil in a second pot and fry the sliced onion until the edges brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip in the reduced puree and fry over medium heat, stirring every couple of minutes, until it darkens from orange scarlet to deep brick and clear oil pools at the sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the seasoning cubes, thyme and salt, cook two minutes more, then leave off the heat to settle so the oil rises in a sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Raw blended pepper is mostly water, and oil cannot fry what is still boiling. Reduce the puree hard first, then fry it in the fat: the oil strips the carotenoids out of the pulp and the colour deepens to brick. Oil rising clear at the edges is the only reliable signal it is done. Stop before that and every stew you build on this base tastes thin and raw.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Obe%20Ata%20Din%20Din%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Obe Ata Din Din"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Stone fruit","Thyme"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"amala","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g elubo, dark yam flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring 500 ml of the water to a rolling boil in a heavy pot and keep the rest hot in a jug.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rain the elubo in through your fingers in a thin veil, stirring hard with a flat wooden turner the whole time so nothing settles in a heap.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the paste against the side of the pot, crushing every lump you find, until it pulls away from the base in one mass and turns from beige to dark chocolate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Splash in a little of the reserved hot water, cover, and stand it off the heat for two minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat again until smooth and elastic, then turn it into a bowl and swirl it round to give a domed, glossy surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Elubo is already cooked yam, so the job is only rehydrating starch. Dumped in, the outside of each clump gelatinises on contact and seals dry flour inside; that is a lump, and no amount of beating opens it later. Rain it in as a veil, crush anything that forms against the pot wall, then cover off the heat so the middle steams through. It darkens as it hydrates, so pale amala is underworked.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Amala%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Amala"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sweet potato","Flour"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"efo-riro","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g efo shoko, washed, shredded and wrung dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml obe ata din din, or blended tatashe and scotch bonnet reduced thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml red palm oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ponmo, boiled soft and cut into strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g beef, boiled with the stock kept"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g smoked panla, flaked and boned"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g crayfish, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp iru, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 seasoning cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the palm oil until it thins and loosens but well short of smoking, then soften the onion in it with the iru.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the pepper base and fry until it tightens and the oil separates back out, about eight minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the crayfish, panla, ponmo and beef with a ladle of the beef stock, and simmer until the meats take the colour of the stew.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the wrung greens through in two additions on the barest flame, with the pot left uncovered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Check the salt and serve while the leaves are still bright and separate rather than collapsed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Shoko carries an enormous amount of water and will dump every drop into the pot. Wash it, shred it, then wring it out in your hands before it goes anywhere near the stew. Fold it in at the very end and keep the lid off: covered greens steam in their own acid, chlorophyll converts to pheophytin, and the efo turns olive and flat inside five minutes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","fiery","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, real fire that builds and stays, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Efo%20Riro%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Efo Riro"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Crawfish","Onion","Chiles","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"ayamase","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g green tatashe peppers, deseeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g green scotch bonnet"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion for blending, plus 150 g sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml red palm oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp iru"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g shaki, boiled tender"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g ponmo, boiled"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g beef, boiled with the stock kept"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, hard boiled and peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 seasoning cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the green peppers, scotch bonnet and the 200 g onion coarse, then boil the blend down in a wide pot until it is almost dry and jammy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the palm oil in an open pot over a strong flame with the window wide open until the red fades to pale straw and the heavy smoke thins; never cover it, never walk away from it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the heat, let the oil settle for a minute, then fry the sliced onion and iru in it until fragrant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the reduced pepper and fry over medium heat until it darkens and the oil rises and pools on top, around twenty minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the shaki, ponmo, beef and a splash of stock, season, simmer until glossy, then bury the eggs in the stew to warm through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bleaching drives the raw palm out of the oil: heat breaks down the carotene that makes it red and boils off the volatile free fatty acids that taste grassy. That is why ayamase reads dark and clean while efo riro, made with oil only warmed, reads sweet and orange. Kill the flame the moment the oil goes pale straw; a minute past that and the whole pot tastes of soot. Boil the shaki and ponmo an hour ahead.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, wood-smoke running underneath."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ayamase%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ayamase"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Chiles","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"ofe-ogbono","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ogbono, ground fine that day"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml red palm oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml stock from the boiled meats"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g assorted beef and ponmo, boiled tender"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g stockfish, soaked soft and picked over"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g crayfish, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ground dried chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ogiri okpei"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g ugu leaves, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 seasoning cube"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the palm oil off the heat and stir the ground ogbono into it until you have a loose, smooth paste with no dry specks left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the stock to a simmer and thread the ogbono paste in slowly while stirring, so it disperses instead of seizing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold it at a low uncovered simmer, stirring now and then, until a spoon lifted from the pot pulls a thread that stretches without snapping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the meats, stockfish, crayfish, chilli, ogiri and seasoning, and bring it back to the barest simmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the ugu over the surface, stir once, and take the pot off the heat while the leaves are still bright.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The draw is polysaccharide from the ground seed, and it needs fat to disperse: stirred straight into water the powder seizes into dry pellets that never hydrate. Slake it into warm palm oil first, then thread that paste into the pot. Once it draws, keep the simmer bare and the lid off. Hard boiling shears the long chains and the soup collapses from stretching threads to gritty sludge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ofe%20Ogbono%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ofe Ogbono"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Crawfish","Chiles","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"ofe-nsala","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g fresh catfish, cut into thick steaks"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g puna yam, peeled and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 ehuru seeds, toasted and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp uziza seeds, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g uziza leaves, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g utazi leaves, shredded very fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 scotch bonnet, blended"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g onion, blended"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g crayfish, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ogiri okpei"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 seasoning cube"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the catfish in very hot salted water, rubbing each steak until the skin squeaks and no slime is left, then drain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the yam in a little water until it collapses under a fork, and pound or blend it to a stiff smooth paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the blended onion, scotch bonnet, crayfish, ogiri and seasoning in the water for five minutes to build the broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the yam paste in teaspoon pellets so each one dissolves on its own, stirring until the broth thickens to the weight of pouring cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the catfish in and swirl the pot rather than stirring so the steaks stay whole, then add the ehuru, uziza seed, uziza leaf and utazi in the last five minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no palm oil here, so the body is pure yam starch and the fish has nowhere to hide. Rinse the catfish in hot salted water until the skin squeaks or the slime will cloud the broth. Drop the pounded yam in small pellets so each gelatinises separately; one big lump skins over and stays raw inside. Ehuru is resinous and volatile, so toast and grind it fresh and add it at the end.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","bright","rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ofe%20Nsala%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ofe Nsala"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","White fish","Crawfish","Onion","Sweet potato","Chiles"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"asun","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg goat leg with the skin on, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, half quartered and half sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp dried thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 seasoning cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g red scotch bonnet, deseeded and slivered"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g red bell pepper, cut small"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp of the goat fat skimmed from the pot, or groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the goat with the quartered onion, thyme, bay, seasoning and salt in water barely to cover, skimming, until a skewer slides through with no resistance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the meat out, dry it well on a rack, and keep the stock and the fat that rises on it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Char the pieces over hot coals or under the fiercest grill, turning, until the skin blisters and the edges go almost black.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop into rough 2 cm cubes while still hot and tip them into a warmed bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss off the fire with the hot fat, sliced onion, scotch bonnet, bell pepper, a spoon of stock and a squeeze of lime, and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Goat leg is collagen, and collagen wants wet heat and time before dry heat will do anything but leather it. Simmer until the skewer slides, then char fast and hard so the outside crusts before the inside dries. The peppers and onion go in off the fire so their heat stays raw and sharp against the smoke; cook them into the meat and you have made a stew, not asun. The simmer runs about an hour.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Charring over open flame","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Keeping the cooking liquid","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","bright","charred","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, acid holding everything up to the light, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Asun%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Asun"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Bell pepper","Chiles","Lime","Thyme","Bay leaf"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"akara","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g dried black-eyed beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 red scotch bonnet"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 ml groundnut oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the beans in warm water, then rub them between your palms and float the skins off, changing the water until what is left is clean and white.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the beans with the onion and scotch bonnet using as little water as you dare, stopping at a thick paste that holds a ridge from the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the paste hard with a wooden spoon until it lightens in colour and a small spoonful floats when dropped into cold water; salt it only now.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 175C (350F) and drop in spoonfuls well apart, frying until the undersides are deep gold before turning.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift them out once both faces are crisp and the middle springs back, and drain standing on a rack so the bottoms stay dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no raising agent. The lift is air you beat into the paste, so blend with barely any water, keep it stiff enough to hold a ridge, and whip until a spoonful floats in cold water. Salt late: salt early slackens the paste and the foam falls. Loose batter spreads flat in the oil and drinks it, giving heavy grey akara instead of a crisp shell over an open crumb. Soak the beans two hours first.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salted water & the float test","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akara%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Akara"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Chiles"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"yaji","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g kuli kuli, broken up"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ground dried red chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ground ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g garlic powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g onion powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ehuru seeds, toasted and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground uda"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the kuli kuli in a low oven or a dry pan until a piece snaps cleanly instead of bending.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind it in short bursts so friction does not warm it and press the oil back out, then blot the powder between sheets of paper if any oil shows.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sieve it, regrinding the coarse bits, until it runs light and dusty through the mesh.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the chilli, ginger, garlic, onion, cloves, ehuru, uda and salt, then sieve the whole blend once more so the cloves spread evenly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Store it airtight in the dark and use it while the groundnut still smells sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Groundnuts are half oil, which is exactly why yaji is built on kuli kuli, the cake left behind after the oil has been pressed out. Whatever oil remains will cake the mix into paste within a week and turn it rancid soon after. Dry the kuli kuli until it snaps, grind, sieve, and only then bring in the dry spices. Cloves carry the blend: three grams is already loud, six is medicine.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","aromatic","fiery","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, aromatics blooming from the first bite, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yaji%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yaji"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Clove"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"chin-chin","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g cold butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole nutmeg, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre groundnut oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the butter into the flour, sugar, baking powder, nutmeg and salt until the mixture looks like damp sand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs with the milk and bring the dough together, adding the liquid a spoon at a time and stopping while it is still firm and dry to the touch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the covered dough, then roll it to 3 mm and cut it into 1 cm squares with a knife or a pastry wheel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 160C (320F) in batches, stirring so they colour evenly, until the loud fizzing drops to a whisper and the pieces are pale gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack and let them cool completely before they go into a tin, since they harden as they cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Crispness here comes from drying, not browning, so fry cool. At 160C (320F) the water has time to leave before the surface sets; at 180C the outside is gold in two minutes and the centre stays soft, then goes leathery in the tin overnight. Listen instead of watching: loud fizzing means water is still boiling out, and the piece is dry only when it quiets. Rest the dough twenty minutes.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chin%20Chin%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chin Chin"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"kokoda","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g walu (Spanish mackerel) or mahi mahi fillet, skinned and pin boned"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml fresh lime juice, from about 8 limes"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml first pressing coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion, 120 g, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ripe tomatoes, 200 g, seeded and cut into 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small cucumber, 150 g, seeded and cut into 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bird's eye chillies, deseeded and minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"a small handful of coriander leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the fish across the grain into 15 mm cubes so the pieces stay square instead of shredding, then toss with the salt and lime until every face is wet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cure in the coldest part of the fridge until the cubes have gone opaque, then cut one in half and stop while the very centre is still faintly translucent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain the fish in a sieve and press it gently with the back of a spoon; throw away the milky lime, which is now watery and bitter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold through the coconut cream, onion, tomato, cucumber, chilli and spring onion, then taste for salt; it will take more than you expect.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the coriander over and serve cold, in half coconut shells if you have them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lime does not cook the fish; the acid unfolds its proteins so they knit and go opaque, and it never stops. Twenty to thirty minutes is the window, and past about forty the cubes tighten to chalk. Drain hard before the cream goes in: leftover acid thins coconut cream and curdles it into oil and grit, so cure and dressing must never share a bowl.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","fiery","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kokoda%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kokoda"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Cucumber","Lime","Coconut","Cilantro","Cream","Coconut milk"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"lolo-first-and-second-pressing","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 mature brown coconuts, about 1.4 kg together, heavy in the hand and sloshing when shaken"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water at 60C (140F), hand hot and no hotter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pierce the two soft eyes with a clean nail and drain the water into a glass; that is the drink, not the cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tap the shell around its equator with the back of a heavy knife, turning the nut as you go, until it cracks open into two clean domes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the white meat straight out of the shell on a coconut scraper or the fine side of a box grater; you want a damp fluff, not shards.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze the bare gratings hard in both fists over a bowl until nothing more runs: that is the first pressing, about 250 ml, thick as single cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the warm water into the spent meat, leave it ten minutes, then wring it out through muslin into a second bowl: that is the second pressing, thin and blue white.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep the two apart and cold; within the hour the first pressing will set a firm cap of white fat on top, which you stir back in before use.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"First pressing is a true emulsion, roughly a quarter fat, and it is a finishing cream: boil it and it breaks. Second pressing is mostly water carrying the fat the first squeeze could not reach, so it is the cooking liquid, thin enough to simmer fish or root crops without splitting. Keep the soaking water under about 70C (158F); hotter sets the coconut protein in the gratings and the yield falls.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lolo%20(First%20and%20Second%20Pressing)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lolo (First and Second Pressing)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"miti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml first pressing coconut cream, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml fresh lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small red onion, 80 g, cut into 3 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bird's eye chillies, finely chopped with their seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 spring onion, thinly sliced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the diced onion in the serving bowl and leave it to weep while you juice the limes; the salt takes the raw bite out of it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the lime juice into the cold coconut cream a spoonful at a time until the sauce is smooth and the colour of milk, with no thin streaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the chilli and spring onion, then taste: it should read salty first, sour second, and the heat should arrive last.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a shallow bowl and serve within the hour, for dipping fish, taro and cassava.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lime is what makes miti taste of the sea and also what kills it. Acid strips the charge holding the coconut emulsion together, so the cream thins and then curdles to oil and flakes. Adding the juice slowly into cold cream, whisking, keeps it whole long enough to reach the table. Capsaicin is fat soluble, so the heat climbs the longer the bowl stands. Never warm it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","fiery","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, melting away silken."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Miti%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Miti"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Scallion","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"palusami","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"24 young taro leaves (lu), about 500 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml first pressing coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 100 g, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 squares of banana leaf, passed over a flame until glossy and pliable"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 sheets of foil, if no breadfruit leaf is to hand"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash the leaves and cut out the stem and the thick central rib, which hold the most oxalate and never soften.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack four leaves in your cupped palm, shiny side outward, and hold the edges up like a purse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the salt and onion into the coconut cream, ladle about 120 ml into each leaf cup, and gather the edges over the top to close it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap each parcel in banana leaf and then foil, seam upward so nothing runs out, and set them in a roasting tin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (350F) until the parcels smell nutty and a leaf pulled from the middle is limp, black green and falling apart under a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest ten minutes before opening; inside, the cream will have parted into a spoonable curd and clear oil, and it is scalding.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Taro leaf carries needle crystals of calcium oxalate held in cells that only long moist heat destroys, and undercooked leaf prickles and itches in the throat for hours; no amount of salt or lime hides it. Two hours in the oven, minimum, and the test is textural rather than clock based: the leaf must be black green and collapsing, never tender crisp. The coconut cream is not garnish, it is the medium that carries the heat in.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Palusami%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Palusami"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Banana","Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"lu-sipi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg mutton flap or lamb belly, cut into 4 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 young taro leaves (lu), ribs removed, about 800 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml first pressing coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, 250 g, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"banana leaves for lining, or a double layer of foil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Trim only the hardest of the fat cap off the flap and leave a good 5 mm on; that fat is the cooking medium and, later, the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a deep pot with a double layer of banana leaf, then build alternating layers of taro leaf, mutton, onion and salt, finishing with a thick lid of taro leaf.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the coconut cream over so it soaks down through every layer, fold the leaves across the top and put the lid on tight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook at 160C (320F), or on the lowest flame the hob will hold, until the mutton pulls apart between finger and thumb and the leaves have gone to a dark sludge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Open the parcel and stir once: the clear rendered oil sitting on top is stirred back through, never poured off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Here the coconut cream is meant to break. Held for three hours it separates, and the freed coconut oil plus the mutton's own rendered fat is what fries the leaf and makes lu taste of Tonga rather than of milk. What cannot be short is the leaf: taro needs the same long heat as the meat to destroy the oxalate itch, so tough mutton and a burning mouth arrive together. Use flap, not lean leg, or the dish comes out dry and thin.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lu%20Sipi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lu Sipi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Lamb","Onion","Banana","Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"lovo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, 1.8 kg, jointed into eight and rubbed with salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg dalo (taro corms), peeled and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg cassava, peeled, cored and cut into 10 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 mature breadfruit, 2 kg, quartered and cored"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml first pressing coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 parcels of taro leaf and coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 banana leaves, passed over a flame until glossy"},{"kind":"item","text":"35 fist sized stones of dense volcanic or hard river rock, bone dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"an armful of hardwood"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 hessian sacks, soaked in water"},{"kind":"item","text":"earth or clean sand for sealing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dig a pit 60 cm across and 40 cm deep, build the hardwood fire in it with the stones sitting in the flames, and burn until the stones glow dull red and the wood has fallen to white ash.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rake out the embers, level the hot stones with a green stick and lay a close bed of banana leaves straight onto them; they will hiss and scorch, and that is the seal forming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peel the dalo under running water to keep the sap off your hands, salt everything, and pack the pit densest first: dalo and cassava on the stones, breadfruit above, chicken and leaf parcels on top where the heat is gentler.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the coconut cream over the chicken, cover the whole load with a thick blanket of banana leaves, then the wet sacks, then shovel earth over the lot until not one wisp of steam escapes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Open only when the pit has stopped breathing and the earth is cool to the palm; the chicken should be pulling from the bone and a knife should go through the dalo like soft butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A lovo is a steam oven, not a barbecue. Heat stored in the stones turns the wet leaves and the food's own water to steam, and the earth cap is the lid; one leaking vent bleeds the temperature and gives you smoke taint and raw dalo at the centre, so chase every wisp with more soil and then leave it alone. Reckon two hours to bring the stones up and three hours buried. Use dense dry stone: layered or porous rock holds water that flashes and bursts it across the pit.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lovo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lovo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Banana","Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk","Lentils"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"ulu-tao","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 mature breadfruit, about 2 kg, matt skinned, firm all over and with no soft patches"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml first pressing coconut cream, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the stem out of the crown with the point of a knife and stand the fruit stem down for a few minutes to let the white latex bleed out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bury the whole fruit in the glowing embers of a burnt down wood fire and give it a quarter turn every ten minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until the skin is black and crazed all over and a skewer slides to the centre with no resistance; the fruit hisses steadily and then falls quiet as the steam finds its way out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll it out of the ashes, scrape the charred skin away with the back of a knife, halve the fruit and cut out the fibrous core.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice thick, salt while hot and pour coconut cream over; breadfruit left to go cold turns hard and waxy and will not come back.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Breadfruit cooks in its own steam behind a skin that chars into a sealed crust, so the black layer is the cooking vessel: peel it first and the flesh dries to cork. Allow about forty five minutes in the embers. Pick the fruit mature but hard, since an unripe one stays rubbery and bleeds latex that gums the knife, while a fruit already yellowing collapses into sweet paste on the coals.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query='Ulu%20Tao%20recipe","sub":"Search films of 'Ulu Tao"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"ika-vakalolo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g walu or snapper, cut into thick steaks on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml second pressing coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml first pressing coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, 200 g, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fresh ginger, cut into fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tomatoes, 300 g, roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bird's eye chillies, split lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime, juiced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, sliced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the fish steaks and leave them while you sweat the onion and ginger in a spoonful of the first pressing until the onion is soft and clear but has taken no colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomato and split chillies and cook until the tomato has slumped and turned glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the second pressing, bring it to a bare shiver with no rolling bubbles, and lay the fish in in a single layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach uncovered, spooning liquid over the steaks, until the flesh parts from the bone at the thickest point and not a moment longer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat, count to sixty, then stir in the first pressing and the lime; the sauce should thicken to a coating without a single bead of oil showing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the spring onion over and serve with dalo or cassava to mop the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This dish is the argument for two pressings. The thin second pressing holds a simmer because it has too little fat to break, so it poaches the fish and takes on the ginger and tomato. The thick first pressing goes in off the heat, where it thickens the sauce and stays whole; add it while the pan is still bubbling and it splits into oil and white grains inside a minute.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ika%20Vakalolo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ika Vakalolo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","White fish","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"faikakai-topai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml water, for the caramel"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml first pressing coconut cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the sugar with the 80 ml of water in a wide heavy pan and boil it without stirring until it darkens to the colour of strong tea and smells of toast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand back and pour in the coconut cream; it will boil up hard, then settle to a caramel the colour of wet sand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the sauce, stirring, until it coats the back of a spoon and a slick of clear oil begins to show at the edge, then take it off the heat to cool to just warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the flour, baking powder, salt and the 200 ml of water to a soft sticky dough and drop rough walnut sized lumps into a wide pan of boiling water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil until the dumplings bob to the surface, then give them two minutes more so the centres are not raw; a split one should be white and open, not grey and dense.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the dumplings straight out with a slotted spoon into the warm sauce and turn them over until every one is glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sauce is a caramel emulsified with coconut fat and it fails at both ends. Stop the sugar pale and it stays flatly sweet, with no bitterness to carry the cream; take it past dark mahogany and it turns acrid, which no cream will lift. Once the cream is in, keep the spoon moving, because coconut protein catches on the base and the whole pan goes grainy and scorched within seconds.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Faikakai%20Topai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Faikakai Topai"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Coconut milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"otai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg watermelon flesh, seeds picked out"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe mango flesh, or pineapple"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g fresh coconut meat, coarsely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml first pressing coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sugar, or to taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"ice, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the watermelon down the coarse holes of a box grater over a wide bowl so it comes away in threads and juice; do not put it near a blender.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the mango the same way and tip it in with every drop of juice it gives up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir through the grated coconut, the coconut cream, the cold water and the sugar, then taste; it should be barely sweet, because the fruit keeps releasing sugar as it stands.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill for an hour until it is properly cold, stir once more to bring the coconut back up from the bottom, and serve over ice with a spoon in the glass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Otai is a texture drink, which is why the fruit is grated and never blended: blades rupture the cells and give a foaming puree that falls apart into pink water and scum within minutes, while shredded flesh keeps its shape and its juice. Coconut cream and fruit acid also part company as they sit, so mix it cold, keep it cold and drink it the same day; by tomorrow it is fizzing.","equipment":["Blender","Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Otai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Otai"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mango","Pineapple","Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk","Sugar"]},{"slug":"thiebou-yapp","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900 g lamb shoulder on the bone, in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g broken rice (riz brisé)"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g onions, half sliced thin, half chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g double concentrate tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp netetou (fermented locust bean), crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole scotch bonnet, left unbroken"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head garlic, cloves peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g cassava, peeled, in thick batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g green cabbage, in wedges through the core"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots (200 g), halved lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 jaxatu (bitter garden eggs), pierced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.6 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound peppercorns, garlic, netetou, parsley and the chopped onion to a coarse wet nokoss; work half of it into slits cut in the lamb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the lamb in the oil in a wide pot, turning only when each face releases itself and the pot smells toasted rather than steamy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the sliced onion and the rest of the nokoss, then the tomato paste, and fry until the paste darkens from red to brick and oil beads out around it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the water, drop in the whole chilli and the vegetables, and simmer until cassava gives to a knife with no resistance at the centre; lift out meat, chilli and vegetables and cover them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste the broth: it should be a shade too strong and leave a film on the spoon. Reduce if it is thin, then stir in the rinsed broken rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook uncovered until the liquid drops to the level of the grains, then cover, drop the heat to the barest thread and steam until the surface is pitted with holes and a grain squashes clean. Pile the rice, crown with meat and vegetables.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Broken rice is milled fragments, all cut surface, so it sheds starch fast and drinks its liquid in about twenty minutes. Leave the lamb and vegetables in for that and they turn grey and collapse, so they come out first and the rice takes the pot to itself: gelatine, rendered onion, netetou, pepper. Judge the broth before the rice goes in. Thin broth steams the grains pale instead of feeding them.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Thi%C3%A9bou%20Yapp%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Thiébou Yapp"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Chiles","Cabbage","Parsley","Rice","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"yassa-poisson","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 sea bream or grouper steaks (about 1.2 kg), skin on and scored"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg onions, sliced thin with the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml lime juice, from about 8 limes"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g strong Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black peppercorns, coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp netetou, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix onions, lime juice, mustard, garlic, pepper and salt in a bowl, bury the fish in it and leave it cold for one to two hours, no longer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the fish out, wipe the onion off and pat the skin dry, then grill over hot charcoal or in a heavy pan until the skin blisters and lifts away from the grate on its own; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze the onions dry, keeping every drop of marinade, and fry them in the oil over medium heat until they slump and the edges take a toffee colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the reserved marinade, the water, the bay and the whole chilli, and simmer until the raw lime smell is gone and the sauce falls from the spoon in a thick sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the fish back on top, spoon sauce over it and warm it through just until the flesh parts at the score marks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lime does two jobs here and they fight. In the marinade its acid denatures the surface of the fish: flavour at thirty minutes, chalk at three hours, so the fish comes out of the bowl long before the onions do. In the pan the same acid blocks browning, so the onions are squeezed dry and fried first; the marinade goes back only once their edges have gone toffee coloured.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","smoky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yassa%20Poisson%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yassa Poisson"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","White fish","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Bay leaf","Mustard","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"domoda","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g beef chuck, in 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g unsweetened roasted groundnut paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g double concentrate tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g tamarind pulp, soaked in 150 ml hot water and sieved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions (300 g), chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g pumpkin or butternut, in large chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g sweet potato, in large chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp netetou, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper, coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef hard in the oil, then lift it out and soften the onion in the fat until translucent and sweet smelling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the tomato paste and netetou and fry until the paste goes brick dark and the oil runs clear at the edge of the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the beef with the water and the whole chilli. Slacken the groundnut paste in a ladle of the hot broth until it pours like cream, then stir it back in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer uncovered, stirring the base every few minutes, until the meat gives to a spoon and orange oil beads across the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the pumpkin and sweet potato and cook until a knife slides through the squash, then stir in the tamarind liquor and salt and simmer five minutes more.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Groundnut paste is an emulsion of oil in roasted protein. Spooned straight into a hot pot it seizes into pellets, the oil breaks out and scorches on the base, and the stew tastes burnt for the rest of its life; slacken it with hot broth first. Hold the tamarind back too. Acid sets pectin, so squash added to an already sour pot stays firm however long it simmers.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","umami-deep","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Domoda%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Domoda"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Sweet potato","Chiles","Squash/Pumpkin","Tomato paste","Tamarind"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"soupou-kandia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g okra, topped and grated on the coarse side of a box grater"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml red palm oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g dried smoked fish, soaked and flaked off the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g yeet (fermented sea snail), rinsed and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g fresh grouper or hake steaks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions (300 g), finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp netetou, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres fish broth or water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper, coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"white rice, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the palm oil over medium heat until it turns from opaque to clear orange and smells nutty; do not let it smoke.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and garlic in the oil until soft, then add the smoked fish, yeet, netetou, pepper and the whole chilli and pour in the broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer twenty minutes, until the broth tastes deeply of the smoked fish, then taste for salt: the yeet and netetou have already salted it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the grated okra in and hold the pot at a lazy blip with only occasional lifting strokes of the spoon, until the sauce turns glossy and ropes off the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the fresh fish in and poach without stirring until it flakes at the bone. Serve over white rice with lime on the side, not in the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Okra thickens through long soluble polysaccharide chains and they are fragile. A hard boil, or beating with the spoon, shears them and the sauce goes watery and never recovers. Acid does the same, which is why the lime belongs on the rice. Grate the okra rather than slice it for more cut surface, keep the pot barely moving, and stir by lifting. Palm oil must warm through but never smoke: past that it turns acrid.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","aromatic","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Soupou%20Kandia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Soupou Kandia"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","White fish","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Okra","Rice","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"dibi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 kg lamb ribs and shoulder, cut into thick slabs on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black peppercorns, coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g strong Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions (450 g), sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, deseeded and slivered"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"tapalapa or a plain baguette, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the lamb all over and leave it uncovered in the fridge for at least four hours so the surface dries and the salt works inward.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the onions with the vinegar, mustard, chilli and a pinch of salt and leave them to slacken while the fire burns down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bank the charcoal to one side and wait until the coals are grey and you can hold a hand a palm's width above them for three seconds only.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the lamb with oil and pepper and lay it fat side down over the coals; the moment flames chase the meat, slide it to the bare grate and let it go on rendering there.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill until the fat is crisp and translucent and the meat springs back at the thickest point, then rest it five minutes, cut across the grain and bury it under the vinegared onions while it is still hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rib fat is the whole point and the whole danger. It renders from around 60C (140F) and drips, and dripping fat on live coals flares and lays soot on the meat that tastes of paraffin. Build the fire to one side, start fat down to get the render going, then move the slabs to the bare grate as soon as flames reach them. The vinegared onions go on hot meat so the acid cuts the fat before it sets.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","charred","smoky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, char used as punctuation, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dibi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dibi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Chiles","Lime","Bread","Vinegar","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"accara","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"350 g dried black eyed peas (ñebbe), soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion (80 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, deseeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml ice cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre groundnut oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g ripe tomatoes, grated, for the kaani"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced thin, for the kaani"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato paste, for the kaani"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, chopped, for the kaani"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml groundnut oil, for the kaani"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the soaked peas hard between your palms in a bowl of water and pour off the floating skins; repeat until the peas are bare and chalk white.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the peas with the onion and chilli to a smooth stiff paste, as dry as the machine will allow, then salt it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the paste with a wooden spoon, adding the ice water a spoonful at a time, until it is pale, sticky and a half teaspoon dropped into a glass of cold water floats.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the kaani base: soften the onion in oil, add the tomato paste and chilli until brick dark, then the grated tomato, and cook until it holds a furrow drawn with the spoon. Sharpen with lime.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop rough spoonfuls of batter into oil at 175C (350F) and fry until deep gold and hollow when tapped, turning once. Drain and serve hot with the kaani.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things make accara hollow rather than heavy. Every skin must come off: they carry the bitterness and, being fibre, they cut the batter's ability to hold air. Then the paste is beaten, not stirred, until a half teaspoon floats; that trapped air expands in the oil and lifts the fritter. Underbeaten batter sinks, and below about 170C (340F) it drinks oil before the crust can set.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Accara%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Accara"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Peas","Lime","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"ndambe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g dried black eyed peas (ñebbe), soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml red palm oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions (300 g), finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g double concentrate tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp netetou, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black pepper, coarsely ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"tapalapa or a plain baguette, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the drained peas in plenty of unsalted water with the bay until they crush easily between finger and thumb but still hold their shape; keep the cooking liquor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the palm oil until it clears to bright orange, then soften the onion and garlic in it until slumped and sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomato paste, netetou and pepper and fry until the paste goes brick dark and oil separates around the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the peas with a ladle of their liquor and the whole chilli, then mash about a quarter of them against the side of the pan and simmer until the sauce clings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt now, sharpen with lime, and spoon into split bread hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cook the ñebbe in plain water. Salt is survivable, but tomato or lime in the pot puts acid on the skins, and acid keeps pectin insoluble, so the peas stay squeaky however long they boil. Season once they crush between finger and thumb. There is no flour in the sauce: mash about a quarter of the cooked peas back in and their own starch thickens it, which is why ndambé holds in bread instead of soaking through.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Soaking dried goods back to life","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","umami-deep","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ndamb%C3%A9%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ndambé"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Peas","Lime","Bay leaf","Bread","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"nokoss","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions (450 g), roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole head of garlic, cloves peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g flat parsley, thick stalks included"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 scotch bonnets, stalks removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g netetou (fermented locust bean)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml groundnut oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the netetou in a dry pan over low heat, shaking, until the sharp ammoniacal smell gives way to something malty and the beans darken a shade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In a mortar, crush the peppercorns to a coarse grit first, then work in the netetou, then the garlic and chillies with the salt as an abrasive.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the parsley and pound until it is bruised into the paste, then the onion last, working only until it breaks down and no further.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into a jar, level the surface and pour the oil over so it seals the paste from the air. Keep cold and spoon out with a dry spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pound in order of hardness: peppercorns, netetou, garlic and chilli against the salt, parsley, onion last and briefly. A blender reverses that order and shreds the onion cells first, flooding everything with juice, so the paste separates and sours within days. Toast the netetou before it goes in; dry heat drives off the ammoniacal top note and leaves the malty glutamate that is the point of it.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","fiery","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nokoss%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nokoss"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Parsley","Clove"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"thiakry","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fine millet couscous (thiéré)"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml water, for moistening"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g thick soured milk (lait caillé) or plain whole yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g crème fraîche or soured cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, seeds scraped"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp orange blossom water"},{"kind":"item","text":"grated nutmeg"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sprinkle the water and oil over the millet a little at a time, raking it through with spread fingers until every grain is damp and separate with no clumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam uncovered in a couscoussier over boiling water for twenty minutes, tip onto a tray, break up every lump with a fork and sprinkle again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam a second time until a grain crushed on the tooth is tender all the way through with no chalky centre, then spread it out and cool it completely, fluffing as it goes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the soured milk, crème fraîche, sugar, vanilla seeds, orange blossom water and salt until smooth and pourable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the cold millet and the raisins into the cold cream, chill an hour so the raisins swell, and grate nutmeg over at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Steam the millet twice. One pass leaves a chalky core that no amount of sitting in milk will soften, because the grain has to hydrate and gelatinise in steam, not in cold acid. Then cool it completely. Fold warm thiéré into soured milk and the casein splits while the grains keep drinking, so a bowl that looked right turns dry and grainy within the hour. Cold into cold, then rest it cold.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Thiakry%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Thiakry"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Orange","Dried fruit","Milk","Cream","Yogurt","Sour cream/Crema","Couscous/Bulgur","Sugar","Vanilla","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"bissap","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"70 g dried hibiscus calyces (bissap)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fresh mint leaves (nana)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp orange blossom water"},{"kind":"item","text":"pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the calyces briefly in cold water to shift dust and grit, then drain; do not let them soak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the water to about 90C (195F), when the base of the pan is chattering but no rolling bubbles break the surface, and take it off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the water over the calyces with the vanilla pod and steep until the liquid is a deep garnet and tastes bracingly sour, around twenty minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain through a sieve without pressing or wringing the calyces, then stir the sugar, salt and orange blossom water into the warm liquid until fully dissolved.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bruise the mint between your palms, drop it in, and chill hard. Taste again cold and adjust the sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Colour and tartness are anthocyanins and organic acids, and they leave the calyces fast in water around 90C (195F). Boiling gains nothing and costs plenty: the perfume goes off with the steam and the seed fragments start giving up tannin, which lands as a dry, dusty finish. Steep off the heat and strain without wringing. Sweeten a shade past correct, because cold flattens sugar on the tongue.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","herb-fresh","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bissap%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bissap"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Senegalese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Senegalese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Orange","Mint","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"bobotie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g coarsely minced lamb or beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 thick slices white bread, about 80 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml full cream milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fresh ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"14 g mild Cape curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g dried apricots, chopped small"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g sultanas"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml brown grape vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g smooth apricot jam"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 large eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 fresh lemon leaves, or 4 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g flaked almonds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the bread in 150 ml of the milk until it is saturated and falling apart, then squeeze it out over the bowl; keep both the pulp and the milk it gives back.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions in butter over medium heat until slack, translucent and sweet at the edges; add garlic, ginger, curry powder and turmeric and fry only until the spices smell toasted and the pan looks dry, about a minute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the heat, add the mince and break it up until it has just lost its raw colour without browning hard, then stir in apricots, sultanas, vinegar, jam, salt and the bread pulp until the mixture is glossy and tastes sweet, then sour, then salty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press it into a buttered 25 cm dish, loosely rather than packed tight, and stand the lemon leaves upright in the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs into the remaining milk plus the milk squeezed from the bread, pour it over so it floods the surface evenly, and scatter the almonds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 160C (320F) until the custard is set and lightly freckled at the edges but still trembles in the middle when the dish is knocked, 35 to 40 minutes; rest 10 minutes before cutting.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true}],"note":"What separates this from a shepherd's pie is acid and sugar carried into the meat itself: vinegar, jam and dried fruit season the mince the way salt does, and the curry powder is bloomed in fat first so it tastes toasted rather than dusty. The topping is a custard, not a sauce. Egg sets around 82C (180F) and curdles just above, so keep the oven at 160C (320F) and pull it while the centre still trembles, or it splits and weeps water into the meat.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","nutty-toasted","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bobotie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bobotie"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Lemon","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Grapes","Bay leaf","Butter","Milk","Cream","Bread","Vinegar","Nuts","Turmeric","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"umngqusho","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g dried samp, coarsely stamped white maize"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g dried sugar beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g floury potatoes, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarsely cracked black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse samp and beans separately through several changes of cold water until it runs clear, then soak each overnight in plenty of cold water; the samp will swell to roughly three times its dry volume.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain the samp, cover with 1.5 litres of fresh water and bring to a bare shiver with the lid ajar; cook until every kernel has split open and there is no chalk left at the centre when you crush one, about 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the drained beans and carry on at the same shiver until they crush to a paste between finger and thumb while their skins stay whole, 45 to 60 minutes more, topping up with boiling water whenever the surface goes dry.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the onion, potato and salt and cook until the potato collapses and thickens the pot into a loose, soft porridge that just holds a spoon mark.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the butter off the heat until it disappears, add the pepper and check the salt; it should taste of sweet corn first and beans second.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Samp is maize with the hull knocked off and it wants a good ninety minutes longer than the beans, so start it alone or you end up with collapsed beans sitting in chalky corn. Salt and any acid firm up bean pectin, so both go in only once the beans crush easily. Keep the water at a shiver rather than a boil: rolling water strips the skins off the beans and turns the whole pot into grey glue.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Umngqusho%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Umngqusho"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Potato","Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"lam-potjiekos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg lamb neck and shank on the bone, in thick pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, thickly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cracked black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g baby potatoes, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g carrots, in thumb lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g butternut, in large chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g green beans, topped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Set the three legged pot over a bed of coals and brown the lamb in the oil in batches until every face is properly dark; crowding steams it, so give each piece room, then lift it all out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the onions and garlic in the same fat until slack and browning at the edges, return the meat, and add wine, water, tomato paste, bay, thyme, pepper and half the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lid on, rake the coals down to a low glow you can hold a hand over for six seconds, and braise untouched until the neck meat gives when pushed with a spoon, about 2 hours; the lid should mutter, never rattle.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Now layer, and do not stir: potatoes straight onto the meat, then carrots, then butternut, then the green beans on top. Lid back on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until a skewer slides through the butternut without resistance and the beans are tender but still dark green, 45 to 60 minutes, then lift the pot off, tilt it and spoon gravy from the bottom over the top; serve in layers rather than mixed.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A potjie is a stacked steamer, not a stew. Only the meat sits in liquid; everything above it cooks in the steam rising off that liquid, which is why the vegetables go in by density and why lifting the lid to stir is the single thing that ruins the pot. Stir and they drop into the gravy, boil, shed their starch and go to mash. Keep the coals at a mutter; a hard boil does exactly the same damage from below.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Braising","Grilling over live coals","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred","umami-deep","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lam%20Potjiekos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lam Potjiekos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Green beans","Thyme","Bay leaf","Wine","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"snoek-op-die-kole","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole snoek of about 2 kg, gutted and split flat down the back"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g smooth apricot jam"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml brown grape vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, juiced, plus wedges to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarsely cracked black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the flesh side of the split fish heavily, leave it 30 minutes, then rinse and pat it absolutely dry; a wet surface is what welds a fish to the grid.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter with the apricot jam, garlic, vinegar, lemon juice and pepper into a loose, pourable smeer and keep it warm at the side of the fire.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Burn the fire right down until the coals are grey coated with no flame licking through, then oil a hinged grid and clamp the fish in it skin side down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill skin side down, a good hand span above the coals, for 15 to 20 minutes, brushing the flesh with smeer every few minutes once the surface has turned opaque; the skin should blister and char in patches.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip for only 2 to 3 minutes to set the glaze. It is done when the thick shoulder flesh lifts away from the two rows of pin bones in one clean sheet; serve with lemon and roosterkoek.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Snoek carries its oil in the skin and belly and dries out fast through the middle, so nearly all the time is spent skin side down: the skin is the pan. Flip it early and the flake tears straight through the grid. The smeer is not just sweetness, the jam sugars caramelise while the vinegar and lemon cut the oil, but brush it on only after the flesh has gone opaque or the sugar blackens long before the fish is cooked.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","charred","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Snoek%20op%20die%20Kole%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Snoek op die Kole"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Lemon","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Grapes","Butter","Vinegar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"mutton-bunny-chow","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 unsliced white loaf, about 800 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 g mutton on the bone, in 4 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 stick cassia or cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 fresh curry leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves and 20 g ginger, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g hot Durban masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g ripe tomatoes, coarsely grated and skins discarded"},{"kind":"item","text":"450 g potatoes, in quarters"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 handful coriander leaves, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil and crack the cassia and cardamom in it until they swell and the oil smells of them, then drop in the curry leaves and stand back while they spit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onions and fry with real patience until they are brown and jammy rather than merely golden; this browning, not the masala, is the body of the gravy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the garlic and ginger paste until the raw smell goes, then the masala and turmeric with a splash of water so nothing scorches, and fry until oil beads red at the edge of the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the mutton and salt and turn it until sealed all over, then the grated tomato and the water; simmer covered until the meat pulls away from the bone, about 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes and cook uncovered until they are soft and their starch has thickened the gravy enough that a spoon dragged through leaves a trail, 25 to 30 minutes; stir in the coriander.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Only now cut the loaf into quarters, pull the crumb out of each leaving 1.5 cm walls, ladle the curry in and cap it with the pulled crumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The loaf is the bowl and there is no plate underneath, so the gravy has to be thick enough to sit still. That comes from potato starch released in the last twenty minutes with the lid off, never from flour. Cut the bread at the last moment or the walls go damp and slump. And fry the masala until oil beads red at the pan edge; masala stirred into a wet pan tastes raw and dusty however long it simmers afterwards.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Pounding a curry paste","Reducing a sauce","Braising","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mutton%20Bunny%20Chow%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mutton Bunny Chow"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"cinnamon-vs-cassia","term":"Cinnamon vs. Cassia"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Potato","Cilantro","Curry/Makrut leaves","Bread","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","pounding-a-curry-paste"]},{"slug":"droewors","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.7 kg lean beef silverside, well trimmed and cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g hard beef or sheep tail fat, never pork fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g whole coriander seed, dry roasted and coarsely cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml brown grape vinegar, chilled"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 m narrow sheep casings, rinsed and soaked"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill the meat and fat until they are firm to the edge of frozen, then mince both once through a 6 to 8 mm plate; one coarse pass is what gives wors its open texture, a second fine pass gives you a hot dog.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the mince with the cracked coriander, pepper, nutmeg, cloves, allspice, salt and chilled vinegar using your hands, only until it is evenly speckled; do not knead it, the protein must not turn sticky and bind.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and rest in the fridge overnight so the salt and vinegar work through, then check the seasoning by frying a teaspoon of it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the sheep casings loosely and do not twist into links; a slack fill lets the sausage shrink inward as it dries instead of splitting its skin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hang the coils in a cool, dry place at 15C to 20C (59F to 68F) with a fan moving air over them, until the wors is hard at the surface and still gives faintly at the core, 2 to 4 days depending on thickness.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false}],"note":"This is not jerky. Jerky is cooked dry with heat and usually smoke; wors and biltong are raw, and their safety comes from salt pulling water out while brown grape vinegar drops the surface pH below what spoilage organisms tolerate. So the drying stays cool with air always moving; warm still air grows mould in the folds first. Use beef or sheep fat only. Pork fat goes rancid within days of hanging and there is no rescuing it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright","smoky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dro%C3%ABwors%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Droëwors"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Grapes","Vinegar","Coriander seed","Clove","Nutmeg","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"roosterkoek","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g white bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"320 ml lukewarm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter, softened, plus more to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"sunflower oil, for the grid"},{"kind":"item","text":"apricot jam, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, yeast, salt and sugar, add the water and softened butter, and work to a slack, faintly tacky dough; knead until you can stretch a piece thin enough to see light through it without tearing, 8 to 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove covered until doubled and the dough springs back slowly and incompletely when pressed with a finger, 60 to 90 minutes in a warm room.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 12 pieces of about 70 g, roll each into a ball then flatten to a disc 2 cm thick, and prove again under a cloth until visibly puffed and light to lift.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Burn the fire down until the coals are grey and you can hold a hand at grid height for five or six seconds, then oil the grid and lay the discs on well spaced.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill, turning every 3 to 4 minutes and shifting them to cooler patches as they colour, until both faces are branded and mottled brown and the underside sounds hollow when knocked, 15 to 20 minutes; tear open and butter while hot.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bread over coals bakes by heat conducting slowly inward, so the fire has to be well past flame. Over a hot bed the crust sets and blackens in three minutes while the middle is still raw dough, and no amount of extra time fixes a sealed crust. Hold your hand at grid height and count: five or six seconds is right. Turn often so neither face runs ahead, and knock the base; hollow means the crumb has set.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Roosterkoek%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Roosterkoek"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Butter","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"blatjang","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g dried apricots, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onions, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml brown grape vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g soft brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g fresh ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dried red chillies, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g coriander seed, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sultanas"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g flaked almonds, toasted"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the chopped apricots in the vinegar and water until they plump and go soft at the edges, about 2 hours; they should squash easily against the side of the bowl.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the soaked fruit and its liquid with the onion, garlic, ginger, chilli and ground coriander over low heat until the apricots collapse into a rough puree, about 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the sugar and salt and stir until every grain has dissolved before you let the pot come back to a simmer; undissolved sugar sinks and catches on the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook uncovered, stirring more and more often as it thickens, until the surface turns from matt to glossy and a spoon dragged across the base leaves a channel that fills back slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the sultanas and almonds off the heat, then jar into hot dry jars while the blatjang is still above 85C (185F) and seal at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Blatjang sets on apricot pectin held by sugar and acid, not on a boiling point, so judge it with the channel test rather than a thermometer. Dissolve the sugar completely before the pot returns to a simmer: crystals sink, scorch, and scorch cannot be stirred back out. Then leave it three weeks before opening a jar. The vinegar is harsh and one dimensional for the first fortnight and only rounds out after that.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Blatjang%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Blatjang"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Grapes","Vinegar","Sugar","Nuts","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"morogo-wa-thepe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g amaranth leaves, thick stalks stripped out"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g ripe tomatoes, coarsely grated and skins discarded"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green chilli, split lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g raw peanuts, roasted and coarsely pounded"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Strip the leaves off any woody stalk and wash them through three changes of water; grit sits in the leaf crowns and cooking will not shift it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion in the oil over medium heat until slack and just colouring, then add the chilli and grated tomato and cook until the tomato has gone from bright and watery to dark, thick and beading oil at the edges, about 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the wet leaves in on top, put the lid on and let them collapse in their own steam, turning once when the top layer has wilted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Uncover, add the water and simmer until the leaf stalks are tender and the pan liquid has reduced to a dark sauce that clings rather than pools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt now, fold in the pounded peanuts off the heat, and serve against stiff maize pap.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Amaranth leaves are almost entirely water, so the tomato base has to be cooked down to a dark paste before they go in; a wet base plus wet leaves gives grey soup you cannot reduce back without overcooking the greens. Salt at the end for the same reason. The peanuts are not garnish: their fat carries the leaf bitterness and their protein thickens the sauce enough to cling to pap. Stop while the leaves are still dark green, khaki means the flavour has gone with the chlorophyll.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Morogo%20wa%20Thepe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Morogo wa Thepe"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Peanut"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"koeksisters","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg white sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml cream of tartar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 stick cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 wide strips lemon peel"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml ground ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g cake flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cold butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml full cream milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres sunflower oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the sugar, water, cream of tartar, cinnamon, lemon peel, lemon juice and ginger for 5 to 8 minutes to a thin syrup that coats a spoon but still runs off freely, then cool and chill it overnight until it is ice cold; split it between two bowls and put one in the freezer.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the cold butter into the flour, baking powder and salt to fine crumbs, beat the egg into the milk and bring it together to a soft dough, knead just 2 minutes until smooth, then rest it covered in the fridge for an hour.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 6 to 8 mm, cut strips 7 cm by 4 cm, slit each into three legs leaving the top joined, and plait tightly, pinching the tail hard so it cannot unravel in the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry a few at a time at 175C (350F), turning, until deep golden right through the plait, 3 to 4 minutes; cooler oil soaks them and hotter oil browns the outside over raw dough at the crossings.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift each one straight from the oil, shake off the drips and plunge it immediately into the ice cold syrup, holding it under for 30 seconds before lifting onto a rack; swap to the frozen bowl the moment the first loses its chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The whole thing is a temperature gap. The koeksister leaves the oil at frying heat, its trapped steam condenses the instant it meets ice cold syrup, and the small vacuum that follows draws syrup deep into the crumb while the crust is chilled too fast to soften. Warm syrup soaks in slowly and evenly instead and you get a limp sweet doughnut with no crack to it. Keep the second bowl in the freezer and swap often. Cream of tartar inverts some sugar so the syrup never crystallises.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Koeksisters%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Koeksisters"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ginger","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"choucroute-garnie-a-lalsacienne","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg raw fermented sauerkraut"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g goose fat, or lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml dry Riesling or Sylvaner"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 juniper berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g smoked pork shoulder (palette fumée), in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g smoked pork belly, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 Strasbourg sausages"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 Montbéliard sausages"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg waxy potatoes, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Strong mustard and grated horseradish, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the sauerkraut once under cold water and squeeze it dry in handfuls. Taste a strand; it should still make you wince.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the goose fat in a deep pot and sweat the onions until slack and translucent with no colour, then pile in the kraut with the juniper, pepper, bay and cloves tied in muslin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bed the smoked shoulder and belly down in the kraut, pour in wine and water to two thirds depth, cover and cook at 150C (300F) until a strand crushes to nothing between finger and thumb, about two and a half hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach the Strasbourg and Montbéliard sausages in a separate pan of water held at 80C (176F) for 10 minutes, until plump and firm to a squeeze. Do not let it boil.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the potatoes in well salted water until a knife enters with no drag, then pile kraut, sliced meats, sausages and potatoes on a hot dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sauerkraut arrives already cooked and already sour; the oven only softens the strand and lets goose fat coat it. Rinse once, not three times: wash the acid out and the dish reads flat, and Riesling puts none of it back. Poach the fine sausages apart at 80C (176F), because at a rolling boil the casings split and all their fat leaves them.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Fermentation","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment","smoky","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it, wood-smoke running underneath, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Choucroute%20Garnie%20%C3%A0%20l'Alsacienne%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Choucroute Garnie à l'Alsacienne"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"riesling","term":"Riesling"},{"slug":"juniper","term":"Juniper"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Sausage","Onion","Potato","Cabbage","Berries","Bay leaf","Mustard","Clove"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"flammekueche-tarte-flambee","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"140 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml rapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fromage blanc, drained 2 hours in a sieve"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g thick crème fraîche"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work flour, water, oil and salt to a smooth dough that no longer tacks to the board, cover it, and heat a baking stone at the top of the oven at its maximum, 270C (520F) or hotter, for a full hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the drained fromage blanc with the crème fraîche, nutmeg and pepper until it holds a ribbon on the whisk; a loose cream floods the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough to 2 mm on a floured peel, thin enough to see your hand through, and spread the cream to within a finger of the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the raw lardons and the onion in a single sparse layer, never a heap, and slide the tart onto the stone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 6 to 8 minutes, until the rim is blistered black in patches, the lardons have thrown their fat and the base lifts stiff and dry off the stone.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false}],"note":"No yeast means no gas and no puff: this base exists to dry into a cracker in the time the cream takes to set, and only a fierce oven wins that race. Roll to 2 mm, slice the onion paper thin and drain the fromage blanc first. Every gram of surface water steams the base instead of baking it, and you get grey suede in the middle.","equipment":["Rolling pin","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Charring over open flame","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","smoky","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Flambé","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=flambe%20technique%20safely","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Flammekueche%20(Tarte%20Flamb%C3%A9e)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Flammekueche (Tarte Flambée)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"baeckeoffe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g pork shoulder, in 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g lamb shoulder, in 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g beef chuck, in 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"750 ml dry Sylvaner or Riesling"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, in rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 juniper berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg waxy potatoes, in 5 mm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"14 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour, for the seal"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml water, for the seal"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the three meats overnight in the cold with the wine, onion, leek, garlic, juniper, bay and thyme; by morning the meat looks grey and the wine has gone cloudy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the clay terrine potato, meat, potato, salting each layer, and pour in the strained marinade until it just glints between the top slices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead the flour and water to a stiff rope, press it round the rim and set the lid into it, working the paste up over the join until no gap will whistle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 160C (320F) for three hours without once lifting the lid. The seal is done when it has gone hard and pale gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack the seal at the table with a knife point. The escaping steam should smell of wine and juniper, and the top potatoes should still be whole and glossy, not dried at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The flour and water rope is not seasoning, it turns the pot into a low pressure oven: trapped steam pins the inside near 100C (212F), so nothing browns, nothing reduces, and the meat cooks in its own vapour. Flour and water only; add any fat and the paste slumps and vents. Reckon a night in the wine and three hours sealed, with no peeking.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baeckeoffe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Baeckeoffe"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"riesling","term":"Riesling"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Leek","Potato","Carrot","Berries","Thyme","Bay leaf","Flour","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"coq-au-riesling","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 free range chicken, 1.8 kg, jointed into 8"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g button mushrooms, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 shallots, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml dry Riesling"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g crème fraîche, 30 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Colour the chicken skin side down in the butter and oil over moderate heat until pale gold only, lifting it out before the butter smells nutty; a dark crust muddies a white braise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the shallots and lardons in the same fat until the shallot is glassy, add the mushrooms and cook until they squeak and have given up their water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the Riesling, scrape the pan clean and reduce by a third, until the smell loses its raw alcohol edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the legs, then the breasts twelve minutes later, and hold the pan at a bare tremble until the thigh joint runs clear and reads 74C (165F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the chicken out, stir in the crème fraîche and reduce until the sauce coats a spoon, then pull the pan off the heat and whisk in the yolk slaked with a spoonful of the hot sauce. Sharpen with lemon, scatter parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Riesling is the acid in this dish, and acid plus dairy is where a white braise curdles. Crème fraîche at 30 percent fat holds because the fat shields the casein; single cream grains on contact. Reduce the wine before any dairy goes near it, and once the yolk is in keep the pan under 82C (180F) or the liaison scrambles into threads.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Braising","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","umami-deep","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Coq%20au%20Riesling%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Coq au Riesling"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"riesling","term":"Riesling"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Eggs","Shallot","Mushrooms","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Sour cream/Crema"],"judgedBy":["braising","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"kouglof-alsacien","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fresh yeast, or 7 g dried"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml whole milk, at 20C"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g soft butter, plus 30 g for the mould"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml kirsch"},{"kind":"item","text":"24 whole blanched almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"Icing sugar, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the raisins in the kirsch until plump and the liquid is all but gone, then butter a cold earthenware mould heavily and drop one almond into each flute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix flour, sugar, salt, yeast, milk and eggs, then work the dough until it slaps clean off the sides of the bowl and stretches to a window without tearing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the soft butter in four additions, taking each fully in before the next, until the dough is glossy and slack. If it climbs past 24C (75F), chill it fifteen minutes and carry on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the drained raisins, fill the mould one third full and prove until the crown sits a centimetre below the rim and shivers as one piece when nudged.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (350F) to a deep chestnut crust reading 94C (200F) at the centre, turn out at once, and dust only when cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Forty percent butter is more than a raw dough can absorb, so build the gluten on milk and egg alone, then feed the butter in four goes. Hold the dough under 24C (75F): warmer and the butter melts out, greasing the crumb short instead of shreddable. Butter the mould cold, since a warm one sheds its film and the flutes tear on unmoulding. Allow two hours to prove and 45 minutes in the oven.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kouglof%20Alsacien%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kouglof Alsacien"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"spaetzle-a-lalsacienne","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse salt for the water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat flour, eggs, milk, salt and nutmeg hard with a wooden spoon until the batter blisters and falls from the spoon in a sheet rather than in drops, then rest it 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring a wide pan of well salted water to a shiver, not a rolling boil, and hold it there.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the batter through a spaetzle plate or a wide holed colander straight onto the water, in batches, so the surface is never crowded.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift them out 30 seconds after they float and drain hard; they should be firm at the edge and still tender at the core.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion in the butter until deep brown at the frill, add the spaetzle and toss over high heat until they take a lace of gold on one side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beat the batter until it blisters and hangs in a sheet, then rest it. Slack, unrested gluten frays into rags the moment it meets water. Keep the water at a bare shiver, because a rolling boil tears the strands before they set. They are cooked 30 seconds after they surface; longer and you carry waterlogged dumplings to the butter pan, where they steam instead of browning.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Spaetzle%20%C3%A0%20l'Alsacienne%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Spaetzle à l'Alsacienne"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Butter","Milk","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"potjevleesch","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g rabbit, boned, in 3 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g chicken thigh, boned, in 3 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g veal shoulder, in 3 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pork belly, in 3 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pig trotter, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml white beer or dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 juniper berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 white peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 thin slices unsmoked streaky bacon, to line"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 leaves gelatine, as insurance"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the four meats overnight in the beer, vinegar, onion, juniper, bay and pepper, turning once; the surfaces firm up and pale.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a terrine with the bacon, stand the batons upright and packed tight with the four meats alternating, and tuck the split trotter down one side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the strained marinade to just below the top of the meat, cover with paper and a lid, and cook at 150C (300F) for three hours, until a baton parts under a fork with no spring left in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon a little hot cooking liquid onto a cold saucer and wait five minutes: it must set to a firm wobble. If it slumps, dissolve the soaked gelatine into the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Weight the terrine lightly, chill overnight until fully set, then turn out and slice thick, serving at cellar temperature with frites and mustard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The jelly is collagen from the trotter and nothing else, and the vinegar earns its keep twice: it speeds collagen into gelatine and it stops cold fat tasting waxy. Always test a spoonful on a cold saucer before chilling, because a weak set collapses the moment you slice. Serve at about 14C (57F); fridge cold mutes the jelly and hardens the fat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","smoky","fiery","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, wood-smoke running underneath, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Potjevleesch%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Potjevleesch"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Bacon","Onion","Berries","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Wine","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"carbonade-flamande","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg beef chuck or paleron, in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g lard or beef dripping"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 large onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml Flemish brown ale"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g vergeoise or dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 thick slices pain d'épices"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g strong mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the beef on cloth and brown it hard in the lard in uncrowded batches, until every face is mahogany and the pan floor is dark but not black.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onions in the same fat until they collapse to a deep gold jam, using the water they give up to lift the fond off the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deglaze with the ale, add the stock, vergeoise, vinegar, bay and thyme, and bring to a bare simmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the pain d'épices thickly with mustard, lay the slices mustard side down on the surface, cover, and cook at 150C (300F) for three hours, until the beef gives at the push of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the softened bread through the sauce until it is glossy and coats, then taste and pull the bitterness back with a little more vergeoise or a few drops of vinegar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Beer reduces, the alcohol leaves and the bitterness stays, so a hoppy ale turns acrid over three hours: use a brown Flemish ale and keep the lid on so the volume barely falls. There is no roux in this pot. The pain d'épices spread with mustard melts down and its starch does the thickening, while vergeoise and vinegar at the end are the brake on the bitter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carbonade%20Flamande%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carbonade Flamande"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Thyme","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"waterzooi-de-poulet","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, 1.6 kg, jointed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres light chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 leeks, white only, in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 carrots, in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery sticks, in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g celeriac, in fine julienne"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 waxy potatoes, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"Chervil and parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the leek, carrot, celery and celeriac in the butter with a pinch of salt until the leek smells sweet and nothing has taken any colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the chicken on the vegetables, pour in the stock, add the potatoes and hold at a bare shiver until the thigh reads 74C (165F) and a knife slides through the potato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the chicken and the vegetables and keep them warm, then skim the fat off the broth and taste it for strength.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks and cream together and slacken them with two ladles of hot broth poured in a thread, then return the mixture to the pan off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir over the lowest possible heat until the broth thickens enough to coat the back of a spoon, 78 to 82C (172 to 180F), then finish with lemon, chervil and parsley and ladle it over the chicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a broth thickened with egg rather than flour, which makes the whole pot a custard held just below the point where yolk protein sets hard. Temper the yolks with hot broth poured in a thread, and never take the pan past 82C (180F). One second of boiling and the liaison breaks into strands of sweet scrambled egg in thin soup.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Waterzooi%20de%20Poulet%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Waterzooi de Poulet"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Leek","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Lemon","Celeriac","Parsley","Butter","Cream","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"munster-au-cumin-et-pommes-de-terre-en-robe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole farmhouse munster, about 400 g, in its box"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg small waxy potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cumin seed, or caraway"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 shallot, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml rapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head of frisée or a bowl of lamb's lettuce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Take the cheese out of the cold a full hour ahead and leave it in its box; at fridge temperature the paste is rigid and it tastes of nothing but salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the potatoes in their skins in heavily salted water until a knife slides in and out with no drag, then drain and keep them covered and hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the seed in a dry pan over low heat, shaking, until it darkens a shade and smells of aniseed rather than dust, then crush it coarsely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the whole cheese in its box at 90C (195F) for 10 to 12 minutes, until the centre wobbles under a fingertip while the rind still stands up.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":false},{"text":"Dress the leaves with shallot, vinegar and oil, split the hot potatoes open and spoon the running cheese over them, finishing with the crushed seed and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A washed rind cheese has already been broken down by its own enzymes: it flows at 30 to 35C (86 to 95F) and wants no more heat than that. Push it past 60C (140F) and the fat splits out while the rind's ammonia concentrates into something acrid. The seed, caraway as often as true cumin in Alsace, is not decoration; its carvone reads straight across those ammoniacal notes and settles them.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Munster%20au%20Cumin%20et%20Pommes%20de%20Terre%20en%20Robe%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Munster au Cumin et Pommes de Terre en Robe"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Alsace%20%26%20the%20North%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Alsace & the North kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"munster","term":"Munster"},{"slug":"cumin","term":"Cumin"},{"slug":"head-cheese-and-the-aspic-arts","term":"Head Cheese & the Aspic Arts"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Shallot","Potato","Lettuce/Greens","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"salmorejo-cordobes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg very ripe tomatoes, chilled"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g crumb from a day-old dense white loaf, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml extra virgin olive oil, picual if you have it"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small garlic clove, green germ removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml sherry vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, hard boiled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g jamón serrano, torn into strips"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blitz the chilled tomatoes on their own to a purée, then tear in the bread and leave it 10 minutes, until the crumb collapses when you press it.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the garlic and salt and blend in short bursts until no flecks of skin remain, stopping if the jug starts to feel warm under your hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop to the lowest speed and pour the oil in a thin thread until the colour turns from red to pale coral and the surface reads matt rather than shiny.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with the vinegar and chill for two hours; it is right when a spoon drawn through leaves a track that closes slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve in shallow bowls with the egg, the jamón and a last thread of oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no water in salmorejo: the body is tomato pulp thickened with bread starch, carrying about a fifth of its weight in oil as droplets. Two failures. Warm tomatoes give a thin, slack cream, so start cold. And a long blast of the blender shears the oil droplets small enough to release their phenolics, which turns the bowl bitter, so thread the oil in on the lowest speed and stop the moment it goes coral.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","fiery","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salmorejo%20Cordob%C3%A9s%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salmorejo Cordobés"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"jamon-serrano","term":"Jamón Serrano"},{"slug":"jamon-iberico-and-the-bellota-grades","term":"Jamón Ibérico (and the Bellota Grades)"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"ajoblanco-con-uvas-moscatel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g blanched Marcona almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g crumb from a day-old white loaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small garlic clove, green germ removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml extra virgin olive oil, a mild hojiblanca or arbequina"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml sherry vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"750 ml iced water, plus more to loosen"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g Moscatel grapes, halved and seeded"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the bread crumb in 100 ml of the iced water until sodden, then squeeze it lightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the almonds with the garlic and salt to a paste, adding water a spoonful at a time; it is ready when a smear rubbed between finger and thumb feels smooth with no grain at all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the soaked bread, then pour the oil in a slow thread on a low speed until the paste turns chalk white and glossy and thickens like loose mayonnaise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the rest of the iced water down in stages to the thickness of single cream, stir the vinegar in by hand off the machine, and chill hard for two hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve very cold with the grapes scattered over and a thread of oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Almonds carry no starch, so this emulsion rests on almond protein with a little bread starch behind it. Grit means under milling: take the almonds to a smooth paste before any liquid joins them. Splitting means acid too early; vinegar and salt together drive the almond protein to clump and shed its oil, so the vinegar goes in last, by hand, into a cold finished base.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","bright","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ajoblanco%20con%20Uvas%20Moscatel%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ajoblanco con Uvas Moscatel"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Grapes","Vinegar","Wine","Nuts","Clove"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"gazpachuelo-malagueno","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g waxy potatoes, peeled and cut in 1 cm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres light fish stock or salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g hake fillet, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g clams, purged in salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml mild olive oil or sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lemon juice, plus more to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the potatoes in the stock until a knife slides out without lifting the slice, then slide in the hake and clams and poach until the clams gape and the hake flakes at a light push.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the fish, clams and potatoes and keep them warm; take the pan off the heat and let the broth go still.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a mayonnaise with the egg, a pinch of salt and the oil, working the oil in a thread until it holds the whisk marks, then slacken it with the lemon juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk one ladle of the hot broth into the mayonnaise, then a second, then a third, before pouring the lot back into the pan off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir until the broth turns opaque ivory and thickens to single cream, return the fish and potatoes, scatter parsley and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a mayonnaise stirred into hot broth, and yolk proteins begin coagulating around 65C (150F). Ladle broth into the mayonnaise a little at a time to bring it up gently, then return it to the pan off the heat; one trip back to the boil curdles it into greasy shreds in cloudy water. If it looks like breaking, whisk in a spoon of cold water. It will not survive reheating.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","herb-fresh","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gazpachuelo%20Malague%C3%B1o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gazpachuelo Malagueño"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Eggs","Potato","Lemon","Parsley","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"pescaito-frito-gaditano","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g mixed small fish: whole boquerones, small red mullet, hake tails, squid rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g fine soft wheat frying flour, or 250 g plain flour sifted with 50 g chickpea flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres olive oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 lemons, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the fish lightly, leave it 10 minutes, then blot every piece dry; a wet surface will not take a thin coat.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the oil to 180C (355F); a pinch of flour dropped in should fizz and rise at once rather than sink and sit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss a handful of fish through the flour, shake it in a sieve until only a dusty veil clings, and lower it straight into the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in small batches so the oil never falls below 170C (340F); each piece is done when it floats and the loud bubbling drops to a whisper, 60 to 90 seconds for boquerones.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift onto a wire rack, salt while still glistening and eat within the minute, with lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The coat is a veil, not a batter: finely milled soft wheat hydrates on the fish's own damp surface and dehydrates into a shell before the flesh can push steam out. That needs 180C (355F). Below about 165C the shell forms too slowly, water escapes through it and oil moves in to take its place, which is exactly why bad fried fish is limp and heavy. Small batches, and a rack, never stacked paper.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pesca%C3%ADto%20Frito%20Gaditano%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pescaíto Frito Gaditano"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Squid/Octopus","Lemon","Flour","Chickpeas"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"tortillitas-de-camarones","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g tiny raw shrimp, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g chickpea flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml iced water, or half water and half clean sea water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g spring onion, very finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g flat leaf parsley, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml olive oil for shallow frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the two flours with the iced water and salt to a batter that runs off the ladle in an unbroken stream, thinner than instinct says, then rest it cold for an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the shrimp, spring onion and parsley, keeping the bowl sitting on ice so the batter stays cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat 1 cm of oil to 185C (365F); a drop of batter should spit and lace immediately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour a small ladle in close to the surface and spread it thin at once with the back of the ladle, so it opens into a ragged disc pierced with holes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry until the edges are lace brown and the underside lifts free without tearing, turn once, then drain on a rack and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The batter is meant to be as thin as crepe batter. Water flashing to steam is what blows the lace holes; a thick batter has nothing to blow and fries into a pancake. Chickpea flour brings no gluten, so nothing turns elastic and the starch sets brittle. Hold the oil at 185C (365F) and spread each ladleful thin on contact, or the tortillita puffs, drinks oil and stays raw in the middle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Crêpes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=crepe%20technique%20how%20to","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tortillitas%20de%20Camarones%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tortillitas de Camarones"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Onion","Scallion","Lemon","Parsley","Flour","Chickpeas"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"rabo-de-toro-al-vino-de-montilla","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.6 kg oxtail, cut through the joints"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml dry oloroso or amontillado"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml beef or veal stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the oxtail an hour ahead, dry it, dust it thinly with flour and brown it hard in the oil until every face is deep mahogany with no pale patches, then lift it out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the onion, carrot, pepper and garlic in the same fat until the onion is tawny and the pan base is sticky brown, then add the grated tomato and cook until it darkens and the oil runs clear at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the sherry and scrape the base clean, reducing until the sharp alcohol smell has gone and what rises is nutty and raisined.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the tail with the bay, peppercorns and clove, add stock to two thirds up the meat, and hold it at a bare tremble with the lid ajar for three hours, until a skewer meets no resistance and the meat has pulled back from the bone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool overnight, lift off the set fat cap, then reduce the sauce until it coats a spoon and warm the tail through in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oxtail is collagen, and collagen needs hours near 85 to 90C (185 to 195F) to turn to gelatine. At a rolling boil the muscle fibres wring themselves dry long before that happens, leaving shreds in thin liquid, so keep the surface barely trembling. Dry oloroso is the right wine because it carries oxidised, nutty depth with no residual sugar; reduce it hard before the stock goes in or a raw solvent edge stays in the sauce.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","gently hot","aromatic","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a warm current of chile, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rabo%20de%20Toro%20al%20Vino%20de%20Montilla%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rabo de Toro al Vino de Montilla"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Bell pepper","Bay leaf","Flour","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"espinacas-con-garbanzos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g cooked chickpeas, plus 200 ml of their cooking liquid"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g spinach, washed"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g day-old bread, in two thin slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cumin seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g sweet pimentón"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml sherry vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the bread and garlic in the oil until the bread is gold on both sides and the garlic smells nutty, pulling both out before either darkens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the bread, garlic, cumin and a good pinch of salt in a mortar, working in the vinegar, until the paste is stiff enough to hold a ridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wilt the spinach in the same pan in batches, tip it into a sieve and press hard; it should give up a surprising amount of green water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat, stir the pimentón through the warm oil for a few seconds only, then add the chickpeas, the pounded paste and the chickpea liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return to a low heat with the spinach and simmer until the sauce clings to the chickpeas instead of running around them, then check the salt and vinegar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The mortar does the thickening: fried bread pounded with garlic, cumin and vinegar spreads gelatinised starch and oil through the chickpea liquor so the sauce clings rather than pools. Two failures. Pimentón scorches in hot oil within seconds and turns acrid, so it goes in off the heat. And spinach holds enormous water; squeeze it hard or the pan floods and nothing ever binds.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","smoky","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Espinacas%20con%20Garbanzos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Espinacas con Garbanzos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Spinach","Bread","Chickpeas","Vinegar","Wine","Cumin","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"rinones-al-jerez","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g veal kidneys"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml fino or amontillado sherry"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml veal or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml sherry vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the kidneys and cut out every scrap of white core and hard fat, then soak the pieces 20 minutes in cold water with the vinegar, rinse, and dry them until the surface feels tacky rather than wet.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear them in very hot oil one layer at a time, browned outside but rosy at the centre, lifting each batch out the moment it colours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lower the heat and soften the onion and garlic with the bay leaf until translucent and sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the sherry and reduce by half, until the smell turns from sharp to nutty, then add the stock and reduce again to a glaze that coats the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the kidneys and their resting juices back for 30 seconds only, just to warm through, then take the pan off the heat, add parsley and serve with bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kidney is a sprint. Past about 60C (140F) the tissue contracts and squeezes out its residual urea in the juices, which is the ammonia note people blame on the offal itself, and it goes rubbery at the same moment. Sear hot in batches so they never steam, take them out rosy, and let them rejoin the sauce only to warm. Cut out every scrap of white core first, because that is where the taint lives.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ri%C3%B1ones%20al%20Jerez%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Riñones al Jerez"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"bay-leaf","term":"Bay Leaf"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Parsley","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"huevos-a-la-flamenca","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g ripe tomatoes, grated, or passata"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g jamón serrano, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g chorizo, in thin slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g green beans, cut short and blanched"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 green asparagus spears, blanched"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g sweet pimentón"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the jamón and chorizo in the oil until the fat runs orange, lift them out, and soften the onion, pepper and garlic in that fat until slack and sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat stir in the pimentón, then the tomato, and cook it down until the sauce darkens and a spoon drawn across the pan leaves a track that stays open.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat four earthenware cazuelas, divide the sofrito between them while it is still hot, and press two hollows into each with the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack an egg into each hollow, ring them with the peas, beans, asparagus, jamón and chorizo, and bake at 200C (390F) for 6 to 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pull them out while the whites are just opaque and the yolks still shiver; the earthenware finishes the cooking on the way to the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"White sets around 62 to 65C, yolk around 65 to 70C, and that narrow gap is the whole dish. The sofrito must be properly hot when the eggs go in, so the underside of the white cooks from below while the oven works from above; on a cold base the whites are still slimy underneath by the time the yolks go chalky. Earthenware keeps cooking off the heat, so pull the dish early.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","aromatic","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Huevos%20a%20la%20Flamenca%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Huevos a la Flamenca"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"jamon-serrano","term":"Jamón Serrano"}],"pantryItems":["Sausage","Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Peas","Green beans","Asparagus","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"tocino-de-cielo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip of lemon peel, no pith"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar for the caramel"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml water for the caramel"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the 60 g of sugar with its water to a light amber caramel and swirl it around a warm mould to coat the base and a finger's depth of the sides, then leave it to set hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the remaining sugar and water with the lemon peel to 105 to 110C (220 to 230F), the point where a cooled drop pulled between finger and thumb draws a short thread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the yolks smooth without whisking air into them, trickle the hot syrup in slowly while stirring, then pass the mixture through a fine sieve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the caramelled mould, cover with foil and bake in a bain marie at 150C (300F) for about 40 minutes, until the centre wobbles as one piece and a skewer comes out with only a film on it.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool, chill for four hours, then turn out so the caramel runs down the sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No whites, no cream, no gelatine: the only structure is yolk protein, which sets between 65 and 70C. So the syrup, at 105 to 110C (220 to 230F), goes onto the yolks in a slow thread with constant stirring, or it scrambles them on contact. The bain marie holds the custard near 85C; direct oven heat drives it past 90C and it curdles and weeps syrup. Stir without raising foam or the set turns spongy instead of dense.","equipment":["Bain-marie"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Straining & passing through a sieve","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","bright","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tocino%20de%20Cielo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tocino de Cielo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Andalusia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Andalusia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"bacalao-al-pil-pil","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 pieces salt cod loin, 160 g each, skin on, desalted"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml mild olive oil, arbequina or a soft picual"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried guindilla chilli, seeds shaken out"},{"kind":"item","text":"flaky salt, only if the cod needs it"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blot the desalted loins bone dry on a cloth, skin down; any surface water will thin the gelatine and stall the sauce later.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the oil with the garlic and chilli in a wide shallow cazuela over low heat until the slices turn pale straw and rise, then lift them out before they colour past gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the oil settle to 60 to 65C (140 to 150F), where a bead breaks the surface only every few seconds. Slide the cod in skin down and poach 6 to 8 minutes, until a white curd weeps from under the skin and the flakes shift when nudged.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the cod to a warm plate. Pour the oil into a jug and leave the pan until it is hand warm; a milky layer of gelatine will have collected on the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, add two tablespoons of oil to that gelatine and swirl the pan flat on the worktop in a slow circle. As it goes opaque, dribble in the rest of the oil in a thread, swirling without pause, until the sauce is ivory, glossy and coats the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the cod skin up, tilt to flood it with sauce, and scatter the reserved garlic and chilli over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No egg and no flour: the sauce is cod gelatine and olive oil alone. Skin and collagen release that gelatine only in a narrow band around 60 to 65C (140 to 150F); let the oil bubble and the protein tightens, the cod weeps water instead of gel, and nothing will emulsify. Build it off the heat and swirl the pan flat rather than stirring, because a spoon shears the oil droplets the swirl keeps whole. Desalting is 48 hours in cold water, changed four times.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","briny","silky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a briny, maritime salinity, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bacalao%20al%20Pil%20Pil%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bacalao al Pil Pil"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Salt cod","Garlic","Chiles","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"kokotxas-de-merluza-en-salsa-verde","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g hake kokotxas, the throats, grey membrane trimmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried guindilla chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 small clams, purged in salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml txakoli or another sharp dry white"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g flat parsley leaves, very finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the kokotxas lightly, leave 10 minutes, then blot both faces on a cloth until the surface is matt rather than slippery.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Warm the oil with garlic and chilli in a wide cazuela over the lowest heat until the garlic is pale gold, then fish it out and set it aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat until the oil is hand warm, lay the kokotxas in skin side down in a single layer, and set it back on the barest heat for 3 minutes, until white beads of gel gather round each edge.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off the heat again, swirl the pan flat and slow for 3 to 4 minutes; the oil and the seeping gel will bind and turn pale and cloudy. Add the wine and the clams, swirl over low heat until the shells gape and the sauce falls from the spoon in a ribbon.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Kill the heat, shower in the parsley, swirl once more so it stays bright green, and serve in the cazuela.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kokotxas are almost pure collagen, so salsa verde is pil pil with parsley: the gel that seeps from the throats emulsifies the garlic oil. Above a bare tremble they clench, squeeze out water instead of gel, and the pan becomes a puddle of oil floating on fish juice. Keep the cazuela hand warm, swirl it flat on the worktop, and add the wine only once the sauce has already gone pale and thick.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","briny","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kokotxas%20de%20Merluza%20en%20Salsa%20Verde%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kokotxas de Merluza en Salsa Verde"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Garlic","Chiles","Parsley","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"txangurro-a-la-donostiarra","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 spider crabs, about 1 kg each, or 500 g picked white and brown crab meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onion, in a very fine dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, white part only, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp choricero pepper pulp, scraped from 2 soaked dried peppers"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml brandy"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml txakoli or dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g tomato, grated on the coarse side and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g coarse dry breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"cayenne and salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the crabs in water salted to 35 g per litre at a bare shiver, 15 minutes per kilo, then cool. Pick every scrap of white meat and scrape the brown meat, coral and juices from the shell into a second bowl. Scrub the top shells and keep them.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion and leek in the oil with a pinch of salt over low heat for 25 minutes, until they slump into a pale jam that tastes sweet with no raw edge.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the garlic and choricero pulp for 2 minutes, add the brandy and cook it out until the fumes no longer catch the back of your throat, then the wine and tomato. Reduce until a spoon drawn across the pan leaves a track that stays open.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, fold in the white meat and all of the brown, which is both the flavour and the binder. Correct with salt and a knife tip of cayenne; it should taste half a step too seasoned while hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into the shells, cover with crumbs and dots of butter, and grill close to the element for 4 to 5 minutes, until the crust is dark gold and the edges bubble.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"The brown meat is the sauce. Its fat and protein bind the sofrito and carry the iodine flavour, so picking only white meat leaves a dry, sweet, characterless filling. Fold everything in off the heat: crab protein is already fully cooked and a second hard simmer turns the strands to cotton wool. The grill is there for the crumb, not the crab, so keep it fierce and brief.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","rich","charred","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Txangurro%20a%20la%20Donostiarra%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Txangurro a la Donostiarra"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Onion","Garlic","Leek","Tomato","Chiles","Butter","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"marmitako-de-bonito","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g bonito del norte loin, skinned and boned, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g waxy potatoes, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, 300 g, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp choricero pepper pulp, from 3 soaked dried peppers"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres light fish stock or water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried guindilla chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion, green pepper and garlic in the oil with a good pinch of salt over low heat for 20 minutes, until everything is translucent and smells sweet rather than sharp.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the choricero pulp and cook 2 minutes, until it darkens a shade and the raw paste smell gives way to dried pepper.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tear the potatoes rather than slicing them: push the knife tip in 2 cm and lever until the chunk snaps away, leaving a ragged face. Turn them through the sofrito, add the guindilla and cover with stock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer at a lazy blip for 20 to 25 minutes, until a potato crushes against the side of the pot with no resistance and the broth has thickened enough to cling to the spoon.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the bonito, take the pot off the heat entirely, submerge the cubes and clamp on the lid. After 5 minutes they should be opaque at the edge and still faintly rose at the centre; serve straight away.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things thicken this pot and neither is flour. Tearing the potato rips cells open so starch leaks into the broth, and choricero pulp adds body with its sweet dried pepper flavour. Bonito is very lean; its muscle proteins wring out moisture above roughly 55C (130F) and it goes chalky in seconds. Add it with the heat off and the lid on, and let residual warmth set it only to rosy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Marmitako%20de%20Bonito%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Marmitako de Bonito"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Potato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"gilda-de-getaria","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 piparra peppers in vinegar, the pale thin skinned ones, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 Cantabrian anchovy fillets in olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 manzanilla olives, pitted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp of the anchovy oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 cocktail sticks"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay the piparras on a cloth, pinch off the stalks and leave them until the cloth stops darkening; blot the anchovies on the same cloth. Wet components let the vinegar bleed and the pintxo slides apart on the stick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold each anchovy into a concertina of three, then thread piparra, anchovy, olive and a second piparra so the fish is trapped in the middle and nothing can spin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand them in a shallow dish, spoon over the anchovy oil, and leave 10 minutes in a warm room until the oil runs clear and loose before eating.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Three tastes on one stick, each with a job: anchovy for salt and glutamate, piparra for acid, olive for fat and bitterness. It fails on temperature and on water. Fridge cold, the anchovy oil is stiff and only salt registers; straight from the jar, vinegar bleeds out and blurs everything. Blot dry, build, then let them stand ten minutes at room temperature.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gilda%20de%20Getaria%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gilda de Getaria"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Olives","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"piquillos-rellenos-de-bacalao","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"16 whole piquillo peppers from Lodosa, drained but never rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g salt cod, desalted, in pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 150 g, in a fine dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"nutmeg and salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 broken or torn piquillos, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml single cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml light fish stock or the poaching milk"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the cod in the milk with the garlic clove at 70C (160F), just under a shiver, for 6 minutes, until the flakes part under a fork. Lift it out, keep the milk hot, and flake the fish, discarding skin and bone.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion in the oil until soft and blond, about 12 minutes. Scatter in the flour and cook 3 minutes until it smells of biscuit, then beat in the hot milk a ladle at a time until the bechamel pulls away from the base in one mass.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold in the flaked cod, season with nutmeg and salt, spread on a cold tray, press cling film onto the surface and chill until it is firm enough to hold a piped ridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe the filling into each pepper through its open end, stopping just short of the rim so there is room to expand, and set them seam down in a wide pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the broken peppers with the cream and stock, pour around the parcels, and warm at a bare tremble for 6 to 8 minutes, until the filling is hot through and the sauce has reduced to the colour of terracotta.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Piquillos from Lodosa are roasted over wood and peeled dry, never washed, so their smoke sits on the surface; rinsing them throws away the thing you paid for. The filling has to be a stiff bechamel, cooked until it leaves the pan in one mass and then chilled hard, because starch pastes loosen as they warm. Pipe a soft one and the pepper splits along its seam and empties into the sauce.","equipment":["Piping bag","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Béchamel: the mother sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Béchamel: the mother sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bechamel%20mother%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Piquillos%20Rellenos%20de%20Bacalao%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Piquillos Rellenos de Bacalao"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Salt cod","Onion","Garlic","Bell pepper","Milk","Cream","Flour","Stock/Broth","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"porrusalda","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g leeks, white and pale green only, in 2 cm rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g floury potatoes, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, 150 g, in rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water or a light vegetable broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"extra virgin olive oil, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drop the leek rings into a bowl of cold water and swish, then lift them out with your hands so the grit stays behind. Drain them hard and shake dry; wet leeks steam instead of sweating.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the leeks and garlic in the oil with a good pinch of salt over low heat for 15 minutes, lid ajar, until they slump and turn silky without taking any colour at all.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tear the potatoes with a knife tip so every face is ragged, add them with the carrot, cover with the water and simmer at a lazy blip for 25 minutes, until a potato collapses under the back of a spoon.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crush a third of the potato against the side of the pot and stir it through; the broth should thicken to the weight of single cream. Rest 10 minutes off the heat, season, and finish each bowl with raw oil.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"A soup of two ingredients has to be built out of texture. Tearing the potatoes gives ragged faces that shed starch into the water, and crushing a third of them at the end thickens the broth to the weight of single cream with no flour anywhere. The one real failure is colour: brown the leeks even slightly and the soup turns grey and faintly bitter. Sweat them salted and covered, and never let them sizzle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":42,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Porrusalda%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Porrusalda"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Leek","Potato","Carrot","Flour","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"txuleta-a-la-brasa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 aged rib chop on the bone, 1.2 to 1.4 kg, 5 cm thick, fat cap deep yellow"},{"kind":"item","text":"coarse sea salt, a generous handful"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 kg oak or holm oak charcoal, or dry vine cuttings"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green frying peppers, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stand the chop uncovered on a rack until the centre is 18 to 20C (64 to 68F) and the surface is dry to the touch. A fridge cold core forces you to burn the outside to warm the inside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Burn the charcoal down past the flames until the bed glows dull orange under a coat of grey ash and you can hold a hand 10 cm above the grill for three seconds, no longer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill unsalted, 6 to 8 minutes a side for 5 cm, turning once, until the fat crackles and the crust is mahogany and dry. Hold the chop on its fat edge with tongs for a final 2 minutes to render it.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest on a warm rack, never a plate, for 12 to 15 minutes; it is ready when the surface stops steaming and the meat gives like the heel of your palm.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Carve the eye off the bone, slice across the grain in 1 cm strips, and salt the cut faces heavily with coarse salt at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt goes on after carving, not before. On the raw surface it draws out water that has to boil off before a crust can form, so the meat greys; on the cut face it dissolves on contact and reads cleanly as seasoning. Grill over embers with no flame, because dripping fat that ignites lays down soot rather than smoke. Rest twelve minutes on a rack so the core, around 48C (118F), and the scorched outside meet in the middle.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Txuleta%20a%20la%20Brasa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Txuleta a la Brasa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":[],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"goxua","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml double cream, at least 35 percent fat, very cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip lemon peel and 1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs, 90 g caster sugar and 90 g plain flour, for the sponge"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water, 80 g sugar and 30 ml rum, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar, for the caramel"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whip the cream with the icing sugar only to soft peaks that hold their shape but fold over as the whisk lifts, then spoon it into the base of six straight glasses and chill; this layer has to carry the sponge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Infuse the milk with lemon peel and cinnamon. Whisk yolks, sugar and cornflour, temper with the hot milk, then cook it back in the pan, whisking hard, until it thickens and throws one heavy bubble; keep it there a full minute, then cool with film on the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs and sugar to the ribbon, fold in the sifted flour in three goes, bake in a shallow tin at 180C (350F) for 12 minutes until the top springs back, and cool.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut discs of sponge to fit the glasses, lay one on the cream and spoon over the rum syrup until the crumb is soaked through but no liquid pools at the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the cold pastry cream over the sponge and level it, chill an hour, then pour on thin caramel or dust with sugar and blowtorch to an amber sheet that cracks under a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pastry cream is a starch gel and it has to boil. Cornflour granules only burst above about 95C (203F), and the same heat destroys the amylase in the yolks that would otherwise digest the set overnight. Pull it early and it looks thick in the pan, then thins in the fridge and floods the sponge. Whisk through that first heavy bubble and hold it there a full minute.","equipment":["Torch"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The brûlée torch","Sugar stages & caramel","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The brûlée torch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=creme%20brulee%20torch%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Goxua%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Goxua"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"idiazabal-con-membrillo-casero","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg ripe quinces, hard and fragrant, grey down rubbed off"},{"kind":"item","text":"caster sugar, the same weight as the finished pulp, roughly 900 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g Idiazabal, a well smoked wheel, in thin wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g walnuts, cracked"}],"steps":[{"text":"Quarter the quinces without peeling or coring and cook them in barely enough water to cover, 30 to 40 minutes, until a knife slides through the flesh with no resistance. The cores and skins hold most of the pectin.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2640,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift them out, pull away cores and peel, and pass the flesh through a mouli. Weigh the puree and add an equal weight of sugar plus the lemon juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over low heat in a heavy pan, stirring and standing clear of the spits, for 45 to 60 minutes, until the paste has turned from blush pink to deep brick and a spoon leaves a track that closes only slowly.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a lined tin to a depth of 3 cm, level the top, and leave uncovered at room temperature until it has skinned over and is firm enough to cut cleanly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice 5 mm thick and serve with wedges of Idiazabal left out of the fridge for an hour, and the cracked walnuts alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Quince sets on its own pectin, which needs acid, sugar and a long enough boil to link into a network. Cook the fruit with cores and skins on, because that is where the pectin sits, and add lemon with the sugar. Underdone, the paste stays tacky and slumps on the board; pushed well past deep brick it goes grainy. Stop at the slow closing track, and let the cheese warm up so its fat softens.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","nutty-toasted","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Idiazabal%20con%20Membrillo%20Casero%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Idiazabal con Membrillo Casero"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basque%20%26%20Navarre%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Basque & Navarre kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"idiazabal","term":"Idiazábal"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"sofregit-de-ceba-i-tomaquet","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg white onions, halved and sliced thin along the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g very ripe tomatoes, halved and grated on the coarse side, skins discarded"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml Arbequina olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the oil in a wide low pan over the smallest flame, add the onions and the salt, and turn them through the oil. The salt pulls water out so they stew rather than fry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook uncovered, stirring every ten minutes and scraping the base, until the onions have collapsed to a third of their bulk, lost every trace of white and gone the colour of weak tea. No browned frilly edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"If a film sticks to the pan, splash in a spoon of water and scrape it back into the mass; that film is flavour, not damage.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the grated tomato and the bay leaf, lift the heat a little, and cook until the whole thing darkens to brick red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stop when the oil separates and pools clear around the edge and a spoon dragged through the middle leaves a track that fills back slowly. Discard the bay.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sofregit is a water removal exercise, not a browning one. While the onions still hold water they sit at 100C (212F) and cannot caramelise, so pushing the heat only scorches the outsides bitter before the insides give up their liquid. Wait for the oil to separate and run clear at the edge: that split is the signal the water has gone. Allow 90 minutes in the pan.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sofregit%20de%20Ceba%20i%20Tom%C3%A0quet%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sofregit de Ceba i Tomàquet"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"bay-leaf","term":"Bay Leaf"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Bay leaf"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"picada-de-fruits-secs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"30 g blanched almonds, toasted until they smell of biscuit"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g skinned hazelnuts, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g day-old country bread, fried in olive oil until deep gold"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, green germ removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g flat parsley leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"a good pinch of saffron threads, dried in a warm pan and crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ladle of the hot cooking liquid from the dish it will thicken"},{"kind":"item","text":"flaky sea salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the garlic with a pinch of flaky salt in a stone mortar until it is a smooth white paste with no fibre. The salt crystals give the pestle grip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the nuts a few at a time and grind, pressing and turning rather than striking down, until the paste is fine sand with no whole pieces left to catch on a tooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the fried bread and the saffron until the mass takes a dull matte sheen and holds together in one lump on the pestle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bruise in the parsley last, only until the paste is streaked green, then slacken with the hot cooking liquid to the thickness of double cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir into the simmering dish in its final ten minutes and cook no longer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A picada thickens by suspension, not by starch gelling: the nut and bread particles swell with liquid and the nut oils hold everything in a loose cloud. Put it in early and those oils cook out, the bread dissolves and the perfume of raw garlic, parsley and saffron boils away, leaving a muddy body and no lift. Ten gentle minutes binds it; longer undoes it.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Blanching & shocking","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","herb-fresh","floral","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Picada%20de%20Fruits%20Secs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Picada de Fruits Secs"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mortar-and-pestle-molcajete-suribachi-granite","term":"Mortar & Pestle: Molcajete, Suribachi, Granite"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Parsley","Bread","Nuts","Clove","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"allioli-de-morter","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"25 g garlic, about 4 fat cloves, green germ removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp flaky sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml mild olive oil, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"a few drops of lemon juice or warm water, only if needed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the garlic and salt in a heavy mortar, turning the pestle always in the same direction, until you have a slack ivory paste with no fibre left in it, three or four minutes of real work.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Start the oil literally drop by drop down the side of the pestle, never breaking the circular stroke and never stirring back and forth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Watch for the paste to go pale, glossy and thick enough to climb the pestle. That is the emulsion taking hold; only now move to a thin unbroken thread of oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stop the moment the allioli stands in a peak that does not slump. If it slackens, weeps a bead of oil or looks grainy, add no more oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"To rescue a split batch, pound one fresh clove with salt in a clean mortar and beat the broken sauce into it a teaspoon at a time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Garlic brings no egg lecithin, so the only emulsifiers are its own mucilage and pulverised protein, and every drop of oil has to be sheared into droplets before the next arrives. Pour fast, use fridge-cold oil, or reverse the pestle stroke and the droplets merge and the sauce weeps. Bitterness is a separate fault: hard shearing of a peppery oil shatters its polyphenols, so choose a mild one.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Allioli%20de%20Morter%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Allioli de Morter"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mortar-and-pestle-molcajete-suribachi-granite","term":"Mortar & Pestle: Molcajete, Suribachi, Granite"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Lemon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"salvitxada-de-calcotada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 nyora peppers, stems and seeds shaken out"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole head of garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g blanched almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g skinned hazelnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g country bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g flat parsley leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 mint leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp flaky sea salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the nyoras with warm water and leave them until the flesh is soft and slippery, then open each one flat and scrape the pulp off the skin with a teaspoon. Keep the pulp, bin the skin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil the tomatoes and the garlic head and roast at 200C (390F) until the tomato skins blister and split and a clove squeezed from the head comes out like paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the bread and the nuts in a little of the oil until deep gold and smelling toasted, then drain them on paper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the nuts and bread to fine sand in the mortar, then work in the nyora pulp, the roast garlic, the peeled tomato flesh, the herbs and the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the rest of the oil in a slow thread, then the vinegar, until the sauce is orange-red and falls from the spoon like loose cream. Rest it an hour before serving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nyora is a dried sweet pepper, not a chilli, and its whole flavour sits in a thin flesh glued to a leathery skin that no blender will ever break down. Blitz the pepper whole and the sauce is gritty and tastes of cardboard: soak it for 30 minutes, scrape the pulp off with a spoon, discard the skin. The nut and bread sand is what keeps the oil from splitting back out.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","nutty-toasted","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salvitxada%20de%20Cal%C3%A7otada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salvitxada de Calçotada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Parsley","Mint","Bread","Vinegar","Wine","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"calcots-a-la-brasa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"24 calçots, or 24 slim spring onions with at least 3 cm of white bulb"},{"kind":"item","text":"vine cuttings or dry hardwood for the fire"},{"kind":"item","text":"flaky sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 quantity salvitxada, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"newspaper or clean brown paper, for wrapping"}],"steps":[{"text":"Burn the wood down to leaping flame over glowing cuttings. You want live fire here, not the settled embers you would bank down for a slow roast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Trim the roots and cut the green tops back to a hand's length, then lay the calçots straight across the grate, touching each other.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Char hard, turning once, until the outer two layers are black the whole way round and the point of a knife slides into the bulb with no resistance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack them hot into newspaper, fold the parcel shut and leave it 20 minutes. The trapped steam lifts the burnt sheaths off the sweet core.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"To eat, hold the green top in one hand, pinch the black sheath at the base with the other and pull down. Salt the pale stem, drag it through salvitxada and lower it in from above.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The black is not a mistake, it is a sacrificial layer. A calçot holds enough water to steam its own middle while the outside burns, so the fire must be fierce and the cooking short. Grill them gently instead and the sugars set into a scorched crust welded to the flesh, and nothing peels cleanly. The paper wrap is the real second cooking stage; skip it and the skins tear off in shreds.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cal%C3%A7ots%20a%20la%20Brasa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Calçots a la Brasa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Scallion"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"escalivada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g red peppers, about 3"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g aubergines, about 2"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 large onions, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp Arbequina olive oil, plus more to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, sliced paper thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"flaky sea salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Set the vegetables whole, dry and unoiled straight over coals or a gas flame. Do not prick them: the skin is the cooking vessel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn them every few minutes until the pepper skin is blistered black and lifting away in sheets and the aubergine has slumped, hissing steam and feeling light for its size.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull each one as it is ready. The aubergine is done when a finger sinks in with no bounce back; the onions take longest, until a knife slides to the centre.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile everything hot into a bowl, cover it, and let the steam work on the skins for 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Peel with your fingers, never under a tap. Tear the flesh into long strips along the grain, salt it, and dress with the oil, the raw garlic and every drop of smoky juice left in the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Escalivar means to cook in the ashes. The skin is a sealed bag: inside it the flesh steams in its own water at 100C (212F) while the outside burns away, which is exactly why you must not pierce or oil it first. Rinsing the char off afterwards is the usual ruin, because water carries away the smoke and the concentrated juices with it. Wipe your fingers on a cloth instead.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Charring over open flame","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","aromatic","smoky","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Escalivada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Escalivada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Bell pepper","Eggplant","Clove"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"esqueixada-de-bacalla","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g salt cod loin, thick cut"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g ripe tomatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g sweet onion, sliced as thin as paper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 green pepper, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g small black olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml Arbequina olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the crust of salt off the cod, then soak it submerged in cold water in the fridge, changing the water four times, until a raw shred tastes seasoned rather than salty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and press the loin dry in a cloth, then pull it apart with your fingers, following the grain, into rough splinters. Do not touch it with a knife.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the diced tomato lightly, let it sit ten minutes and pour off the water it gives up, so the salad cannot go slack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the cod with the oil first, coating every shred, then add the tomato, onion, green pepper and olives and only then the vinegar. Grind over black pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 15 minutes, then taste before adding any salt at all. The cod almost always brings enough.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Esqueixar means to tear. A knife cuts across the muscle blocks and leaves wet chips that shed liquid into the bowl, while tearing follows the natural seams so each splinter keeps its collagen sheath and stays firm. Oil before vinegar for the same reason: the fat coats the fibres so the acid cannot draw more water out. Desalting takes 36 to 48 hours in the fridge and has no shortcut.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Esqueixada%20de%20Bacall%C3%A0%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Esqueixada de Bacallà"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Salt cod","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Olives","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"fideua-rossejada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g fideus number 2, the short hollow noodles"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L rockfish and prawn head stock, held at a bare simmer"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g monkfish tail, in 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 head-on red prawns"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g cuttlefish, cut in squares and lightly scored"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g sofregit"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g picada"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp nyora pulp, scraped from a soaked pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"a pinch of saffron, dried and crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"allioli, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"In a wide shallow pan, fry the prawns in the oil for 30 seconds a side, until the shells turn scarlet, and lift them out. Do the same with the cuttlefish until its edges curl, and set that aside too.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the heat and toast the dry fideus in that same oil, stirring without pause, until they are the colour of amber and smell like baked biscuit. This is the rossejat and it takes about five minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the sofregit, the nyora pulp and the saffron, then pour in all the hot stock at once and season. It should sit just level with the noodles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil hard for 4 minutes, then drop to a simmer for 5 more, tucking in the monkfish and cuttlefish. From the moment the stock goes in, do not stir.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the picada over the surface, shake the pan to settle it, and cook 2 minutes more, until the liquid is gone and the noodles at the surface stand up on end like bristles.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lay the prawns on top, rest the pan off the heat for 5 minutes and serve with allioli.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"Toasting the fideus is what separates a fideuà from a wet noodle stew: dry heat sets a shell of dextrins on each tube so it drinks the stock slowly and stays a separate strand. Stir once the liquid is in and you rub that shell off into the pan, and the dish turns to paste. Noodles standing on end mean the stock has gone; leave them any longer and the base burns rather than crisps.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fideu%C3%A0%20Rossejada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fideuà Rossejada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shrimp","Squid/Octopus","Noodles","Stock/Broth","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"pollastre-amb-escamarlans","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 free range chicken, 1.6 kg, jointed into 8"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 langoustines, or 8 large head-on prawns"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g sofregit"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml vi ranci, or a dry oxidised white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf and 1 sprig of thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g toasted almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g toasted hazelnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dark chocolate, 70 percent cocoa"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 slice of bread fried in olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"a pinch of saffron, dried and crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the chicken and brown it hard in the oil, skin down and unmoved, until the skin releases itself from the pan and is the colour of mahogany. Set it aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear the langoustines in the same fat for 40 seconds a side, just until the shells redden, and lift them straight out. They finish in the sauce later.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off half the fat, add the sofregit and the herbs, then the vi ranci, scraping the base and reducing until the pan no longer smells of alcohol.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the chicken with the stock, cover, and simmer gently until the thigh meat pulls away from the bone at a fork, about 35 minutes.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pound the almonds, hazelnuts, garlic, fried bread, saffron and chocolate to a paste with a ladle of the sauce, stir it in and simmer 10 minutes, until the sauce coats the back of a spoon.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the langoustines back in, cover, and leave them off the heat 3 minutes, until the tail meat is just opaque to the middle.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Mar i muntanya works because prawn shells and browned chicken skin throw off the same families of roasted amino acids, so the two palettes stack instead of fighting. The shellfish is the fragile half: langoustine tail sets at about 55C (130F) and shreds into cotton wool by 65C (150F), so it goes in twice and briefly, and it never simmers alongside the bird.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","floral","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, a floral thread lifting the aroma, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pollastre%20amb%20Escamarlans%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pollastre amb Escamarlans"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Shrimp","Garlic","Thyme","Bay leaf","Bread","Wine","Stock/Broth","Chocolate/Cocoa","Nuts","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"panellets-de-pinyons","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g ground blanched almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g sweet potato, boiled in its skin, peeled and riced"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg white"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten, for glazing"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g pine nuts"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rice the sweet potato while hot and spread it out to cool completely, then knead it with the almonds, sugar, lemon zest and enough egg white to make a stiff, barely tacky marzipan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap the paste and rest it overnight in the fridge so the almond oil and the sugar syrup even out and it stops sticking to your hands.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into 20 g balls. Rinse the pine nuts and drain them damp, then roll each ball through them, pressing so the nuts bed in halfway and stand upright.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush with beaten egg and bake on the top shelf at 200C (390F) for 8 to 10 minutes, until the pine nut tips are gold but the base is still pale.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool them on the tray without moving them; they are fragile hot and set firm as they come down to room temperature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Panellets fail on the bench or in the oven, and both faults are water. Too much sweet potato and the paste slumps and sheds its pine nuts; too little and it cracks as you roll. Bake hot, high and fast: the aim is to colour the nuts before the heat reaches the centre, because almond paste that dries through goes sandy and hard instead of staying fudgy. Rest the paste overnight.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panellets%20de%20Pinyons%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Panellets de Pinyons"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Catalonia%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Catalonia kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Potato","Sweet potato","Lemon","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"butatama-osaka-okonomiyaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g nagaimo or yamaimo, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml cold kombu and katsuobushi dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g white cabbage"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g tenkasu (tempura crumbs)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g beni shoga (red pickled ginger)"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g spring onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g thinly sliced pork belly"},{"kind":"item","text":"neutral oil for the griddle"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml okonomiyaki sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"Japanese mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"aonori"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g katsuobushi flakes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the nagaimo to a slippery paste, whisk it into the flour, cold dashi and one egg until only just smooth, then chill the batter for an hour; it should ribbon thickly off the whisk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the cabbage into 1 cm squares rather than shreds and blot it dry; fine shreds weep and turn the middle to soup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the cabbage, tenkasu, beni shoga, spring onion and remaining eggs in with a lifting motion, stopping while the mixture still looks loose and airy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle a 2 cm mound onto a griddle at 180C (355F), drape the pork belly over the top, and cook until the edge sets matt and the underside is deep gold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip once and never press down; it is ready when a skewer pushed into the centre comes out clean and hot against your lip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauce, then mayonnaise, then aonori, and the katsuobushi last so the flakes lift and curl in the steam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Grated nagaimo is a mucilage: long chain glycoproteins that hold water and trap air, so the middle sets as custard rather than pancake. Two habits kill it. Beat the batter and you build gluten; press with the spatula while it cooks and you squeeze out the steam that is doing the lifting. Flip once, then leave it alone.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","Griddle & plancha work","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","briny","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Butatama%20(Osaka%20Okonomiyaki)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Butatama (Osaka Okonomiyaki)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Sweet potato","Cabbage","Flour","Stock/Broth","Mayonnaise","Pickles","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","griddle-and-plancha-work"]},{"slug":"negiyaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml cold kombu and katsuobushi dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g Japanese negi or thin spring onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g squid, cut small"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tenkasu"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g beni shoga"},{"kind":"item","text":"neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml usukuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml sudachi or lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"shichimi togarashi"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, cold dashi and egg to a thin pourable batter, barely thicker than single cream, then slice the negi 5 mm thick, greens and all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil a medium hot plate, spread a thin disc of batter, and bury it under a deep drift of negi, squid, tenkasu and beni shoga; the onion should outweigh everything else in the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon a little more batter over the pile to glue it down and cook until the underside is set and freckled and the onion smells sweet instead of raw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Flip once, tidy only the edges flat, and cook until the second side is patched brown and the squid has turned opaque.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide onto a board, fold in half, cut into fingers and dress with usukuchi soy sharpened with citrus juice; shichimi at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Negiyaki is not okonomiyaki with onion in it. Cabbage holds its shape, so its batter can be thick; negi collapses and leaks sugary water, so the batter here is only glue, roughly one part flour to two and a half parts dashi. Thicker and you get a doughy pancake. Those same onion sugars scorch, so keep the iron moderate and finish with soy and citrus, never sweet sauce.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Negiyaki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Negiyaki"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"shichimi-togarashi","term":"Shichimi Tōgarashi"}],"pantryItems":["Squid/Octopus","Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Lemon","Flour","Soy sauce","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi"]},{"slug":"kushikatsu-shinsekai-rules","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g beef sirloin or top round"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g lotus root"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 shiitake mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g panko"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 L neutral frying oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml thin Japanese Worcestershire style sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 wedge white cabbage, raw"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the beef into 25 g cubes and the onion into layered wedges, keeping each ingredient on its own skewer with the last 3 cm left bare as a handle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crush the panko under a rolling pin to the texture of coarse sand, and whisk the flour, egg and cold water to a batter that runs off a skewer in a thin sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dip, let the excess drain back into the bowl, then roll in the crumb and pat it on; the coat should be about 2 mm and you should still read the shape underneath.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 170C to 180C (340F to 355F) in small batches until the bubbling quietens and the crust is pale gold, about 90 seconds for beef and a little longer for the vegetables.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand them upright to drain and serve with one communal pot of sauce and the raw cabbage alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two rules, both physical. Crush the panko fine: coarse crumb builds armour that soaks oil and shatters into the shared sauce. And the batter is a thin cream, not a paste; paste steams the food and the crust lifts away as a hollow shell. Never dip twice, because the pot is communal, which is exactly what the raw cabbage is for: use a leaf to scoop your second helping.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kushikatsu%20(Shinsekai%20Rules)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kushikatsu (Shinsekai Rules)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Eggs","Onion","Mushrooms","Cabbage","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"doteyaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g beef sinew (suji) or shin with tendon"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml sake"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml kombu and katsuobushi dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g Kansai white miso"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g red miso"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g konnyaku"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g spring onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"shichimi togarashi"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the sinew with cold water, bring to the boil and drain the moment grey scum rises, then rinse each piece; that first water carries the smell that would otherwise settle into the miso.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return it with the ginger, sake and dashi and hold at a bare tremble around 90C (195F) for two to three hours, until a skewer slides through and the tendon has gone glassy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tear the konnyaku into rough lumps rather than cutting it, so the sauce grips the torn faces, and simmer 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slacken both misos with a ladle of the hot liquid, stir them back in with the mirin and sugar, and cook at a whisper until the sauce clings to the meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest off the heat to thicken, then serve with spring onion and shichimi.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two clocks run here. Collagen in sinew only becomes gelatine after two hours or more at a bare tremble near 90C (195F); rush it and the suji chews like elastic. Kansai white miso is the opposite: low in salt, high in sugar and volatile aroma, so it goes dull and grainy at a boil and its sugars catch on the base. Simmer the meat first, stir the miso in last, never let it bubble.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","sweet-edged","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Doteyaki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Doteyaki"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"shichimi-togarashi","term":"Shichimi Tōgarashi"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Miso","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"ikayaki-sennichimae-style","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml kombu and katsuobushi dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g squid body"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml okonomiyaki sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"beni shoga"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, dashi and salt to a batter the thickness of pouring cream and chop the squid into 1 cm pieces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring a heavy pan to 220C (430F), oil it, pour a thin round of batter and scatter the squid over straight away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with a second heavy pan or a foil wrapped weight and lean on it for 60 seconds; steam should hiss from the edges and the disc should compact to about 3 mm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the weight, crack the egg on top and spread it thin, then fold the sheet in half as soon as the egg has just set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush with sauce, scatter beni shoga and eat immediately, while the rim is still lace crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pressure and speed are the whole dish. Squeezing the batter between hot iron drives out steam and forces contact with the metal, so it sets as a chewy crisp sheet instead of a fluffy pancake. Squid sets the clock: its collagen contracts between roughly 60C (140F) and 100C (212F) and only relaxes again after a long braise, so cook it under two minutes or for half an hour. Nothing in between works.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ikayaki%2C%20Sennichimae%20Style%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ikayaki, Sennichimae Style"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Squid/Octopus","Eggs","Flour","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"battera-pressed-saba-oshizushi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 mackerel fillets, about 300 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g seasoned sushi rice (shari), at body temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sheet shiro-ita kombu (white pressed kombu)"},{"kind":"item","text":"wasabi"},{"kind":"item","text":"gari (pickled ginger)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Use mackerel that has been frozen at -20C (-4F) for at least 24 hours. Bury the fillets in salt, skin and flesh, and leave 45 minutes, until the surface firms and beads liquid.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":2700,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rinse off the salt, pat dry, then steep in the rice vinegar with the sugar and kombu for 20 minutes, until the outer 2 mm has turned opaque white.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Peel the fine translucent membrane off the skin, working from tail to head; it should lift in one sheet. Slice the fillet to an even 8 mm, keeping the trimmings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wet a wooden oshibako, lay the fish skin side down with trimmings filling the gaps, smear a whisper of wasabi, and pack the shari on top to just over the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fit the lid and press with steady weight until it stops sinking, rest 20 minutes, then unmould and drape with the softened white kombu sheet.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut with a wet blade, one clean pull per slice, wiping the steel between cuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt before vinegar, always. Salt pulls water out by osmosis and firms the muscle, so the vinegar then soaks in evenly and whitens only the outer few millimetres. Go straight to vinegar on a wet fillet and you get a chalky rim over a raw, slack middle that weeps in the box. Press once with steady weight until the lid stops giving; pumping it mashes the shari to paste.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sushi rice & rolling"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","umami-deep","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sushi rice & rolling","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20sushi%20rice%20rolling%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Battera%20(Pressed%20Saba%20Oshizushi)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Battera (Pressed Saba Oshizushi)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Rice","Vinegar","Sugar","Pickles","Seaweed/Nori"]},{"slug":"kansai-kake-jiru-usukuchi-noodle-broth","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L soft water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g thinly shaved katsuobushi"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g shaved sababushi (dried mackerel)"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml usukuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wipe the kombu with a dry cloth and leave the white bloom on it; that is glutamate and mannitol, not dirt. Steep it in the cold water for 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring it slowly to 60C (140F), hold there 45 minutes, then lift the kombu out before the pot reaches 80C (175F).","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Take the water to 85C (185F), kill the heat, scatter in both shavings and leave until they sink, about 2 minutes.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain through damp muslin without pressing; squeezing pushes bitter fines through and clouds a broth whose whole point is clarity.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the mirin alone for 30 seconds to drive off the alcohol, add the usukuchi and salt, stir into the dashi and bring to 80C (175F) without boiling it again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste from a spoon: it should read one notch too salty, because the noodles it will dress are boiled in plain unsalted water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kansai broth is pale, not weak. Usukuchi runs around 18 percent salt against koikuchi's 16, and its lighter colour comes from that extra salt restraining fermentation and browning, not from dilution. Season this by eye and you will oversalt it. Measure. Keep the kombu under 80C (175F) as well; hotter and it sheds alginates, turning the dashi slippery and faintly bitter.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Dashi","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20Kake-jiru%20(Usukuchi%20Noodle%20Broth)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kansai Kake-jiru (Usukuchi Noodle Broth)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"umami-and-the-glutamate-pantry","term":"Umami & The Glutamate Pantry"}],"pantryItems":["Noodles","Soy sauce","Wine","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"kizami-udon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"20 g kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g dried shiitake"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g abura-age (2 sheets)"},{"kind":"item","text":"65 ml usukuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"65 ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g fresh udon"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g thin green onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"shichimi togarashi"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour boiling water over both sides of the abura-age, press it flat between your palms, and cut it into 5 mm strips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the kombu and dried shiitake in the water to 65C (150F), hold 45 minutes, lift the kombu out before the pot reaches 80C (175F), then strain.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2700,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the mirin 30 seconds, add the usukuchi and salt, stir into the dashi and hold at 80C (175F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the udon in unsalted water until the white core has just gone, then rinse under cold water until the strands stop feeling slippery and dunk them back in hot water for 10 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bowl the udon, pour the broth down the side, and pile on the abura-age, green onion and ginger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Abura-age is fried in oil that keeps oxidising on the shelf; skip the boiling water rinse and that oil surfaces as a film which flattens the dashi aroma and coats the tongue. Kizami is the unsweetened cousin of kitsune: no sugar simmered pouch to hide behind, so the fat must be clean and the ginger sharp. Rinse the boiled udon too, or its surface starch clouds and thickens the broth.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kizami%20Udon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kizami Udon"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Mushrooms","Noodles","Soy sauce","Wine","Seaweed/Nori","Dried mushrooms"],"judgedBy":["dashi"]},{"slug":"kasujiru-nada-style","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120 g sake kasu (sake lees)"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 ml kombu and katsuobushi dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g daikon"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g carrot"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g burdock (gobo)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g salted salmon fillet"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g abura-age"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g Kansai white miso"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml usukuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g spring onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"shichimi togarashi"}],"steps":[{"text":"Tear the sake lees into a bowl, ladle over 200 ml of the hot dashi, and leave until it mashes smooth against the side with no grains left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the daikon and carrot into 3 mm quarter rounds and shave the burdock straight into water so it does not brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the roots in the remaining dashi until the daikon edge goes translucent and a skewer meets no resistance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the salted salmon in 3 cm pieces and the abura-age in strips, and cook until the fish flakes at a nudge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the lees slurry and the white miso in through a ladle, add the usukuchi, and hold at 80C to 85C (175F to 185F) for 5 minutes; the surface should shiver, never bubble.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Finish with spring onion and shichimi.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sake lees is unpressed rice, koji and yeast: raw starch, protein and roughly 8 percent alcohol. Stirred in dry it stays gritty and tastes of raw spirit. Soften it in hot dashi and mash it smooth first, then hold the pot at 80C to 85C (175F to 185F), hot enough for the alcohol to leave, cool enough that the sweet koji smell and the white miso do not boil away.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":15,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kasujiru%2C%20Nada%20Style%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kasujiru, Nada Style"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"oak-malolactic-and-lees","term":"Oak, Malolactic & Lees"}],"pantryItems":["Salmon","Onion","Scallion","Carrot","Radish/Turnip","Soy sauce","Miso","Wine","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"mitarashi-dango","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100 g joshinko (non-glutinous rice flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g shiratamako (glutinous rice flour)"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml koikuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g katakuriko or kudzu starch slaked in 20 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the two rice flours together, work in the hot water a little at a time, and knead until the dough is as soft as an earlobe and stops cracking at the edges when rolled.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 15 g balls, boil until they float, give them two minutes more, then drop them into iced water so the surface firms.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thread four to a skewer and dry them thoroughly on a cloth; a wet dango steams instead of blistering.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill dry over a naked flame or on a bare pan, turning, until brown blisters patch the surface and the skin squeaks under the tongs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the water, sugar, mirin and koikuchi soy, used here for colour and roast where the rest of this chapter uses usukuchi, then rain in the starch slurry while whisking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the glaze until it turns from cloudy to glossy and coats a spoon, then spoon it over the hot skewers and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two flours, two jobs. Joshinko is milled from ordinary rice and gives bite plus a skin that can take a scorch; shiratamako alone would slump on the grill and pull into strings. Dry the boiled dango before it meets the flame or it steams rather than blisters. The glaze needs a full simmer after the starch goes in: pull it while still cloudy and it will thin out and slide off.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Roasting in a hot oven","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","charred","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, char used as punctuation, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mitarashi%20Dango%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mitarashi Dango"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kansai%20%26%20Osaka%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kansai & Osaka kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Rice","Flour","Soy sauce","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"yudofu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g kinugoshi tofu, the softest you can buy"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 L soft water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g dried shiitake"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml usukuchi shoyu"},{"kind":"item","text":"50ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g daikon, grated and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 kujo negi, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 yuzu, for zest"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the kombu and shiitake in the cold water for an hour. Lift the shiitake out and keep their soaking water; the kombu stays in the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For the tare, warm 200ml of the shiitake water with the shoyu and mirin until it steams and the raw alcohol smell has gone, then hold it warm in a jug.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the kombu water to 70 to 75C (158 to 167F): the surface should shiver and throw a thin plume, and never break into bubbles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the tofu into 5cm blocks and slide them in. They sink, then rise and sway as they warm through, three to four minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift each block out the moment it floats and wobbles, before any pitting shows on the surface, and serve in the warm tare with daikon, negi, ginger and grated yuzu.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tofu is a protein gel holding water, and above about 80C (176F) the gel contracts and squeezes that water back out: the block honeycombs, goes rubbery and tastes of nothing. Hold the kombu water at 70 to 75C (158 to 167F), a surface that trembles but never bubbles, and lift each block as soon as it floats.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":39,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yudofu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yudofu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tofu","Ginger","Mushrooms","Radish/Turnip","Wine","Seaweed/Nori","Dried mushrooms"],"judgedBy":["dashi","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"kumiage-yuba","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L unsweetened soymilk, at least 10 percent solids and nothing added"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g wasabi, freshly grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml usukuchi shoyu"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g flaky salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay the kombu in a wide shallow pan and pour the soymilk over it to a depth of 3 to 4cm. Surface area makes yuba, not volume.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold the milk at 75 to 80C (167 to 176F). The surface dulls, then wrinkles at the edge when you breathe across it: that is the film, five to eight minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For kumiage yuba, slide a flat ladle under the film and lift it away loose, with a little milk beneath it, into a chilled bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For hikiage yuba, run a bamboo skewer along the far edge, lift the whole sheet in one unbroken movement and hang it a minute to drain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep going for as long as the pot allows; each sheet comes thinner and sweeter than the last as the protein is used up. Serve with wasabi, a few drops of shoyu, or salt alone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The skin is soy protein and fat setting at the air interface as surface water evaporates, so it needs a still surface, 75 to 80C (167 to 176F) and milk of at least 10 percent solids. Boil it and the churn stops any film forming: you get grey scum and a scorched base. Thin milk with added emulsifiers never skins at all.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","fiery","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, real fire that builds and stays, melting away silken."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Churning ice cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ice%20cream%20churning%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kumiage%20Yuba%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kumiage Yuba"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"yuba-maki-no-ankake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"40g dried yuba sheets, about 6"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml kombu dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g hon-kuzu"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml usukuchi shoyu"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g fresh shiitake, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g kintoki carrot, in fine strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g mitsuba, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 yuzu, for zest"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soften the yuba: wipe each sheet with a damp cloth, or dip it for ten seconds in water at about 40C, until it bends without cracking. Hot water turns it to shreds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the shiitake, carrot and mitsuba in 100ml of the dashi with the salt until the carrot bends but still grips the tooth; drain and cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the filling in the yuba, seal each seam with a smear of kuzu slurry and cut the rolls into 4cm lengths.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the rest of the dashi with shoyu and mirin, bring it to a bare tremble and poach the rolls three minutes; lift them into warm bowls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slake the kuzu in the cold water, stir it into the dashi off the heat, then bring it just to a boil until it turns from cloudy to clear and coats the back of a spoon. Pour over, add ginger and grate yuzu on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kuzu has to be slaked cold, then taken just to a boil: the sauce turns from cloudy white to clear as the granules swell, and that is the signal the raw starch taste has gone. Hold it at a rolling boil and the chains shear apart and it thins back towards water. Dried yuba is the other trap; soak it tepid, never hot, or it dissolves.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yuba%20Maki%20no%20Ankake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Yuba Maki no Ankake"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Carrot","Mushrooms","Wine","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"kyo-yasai-no-takiawase","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 L soft water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g ebi-imo, or other taro"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kamo nasu, about 300g, or 2 small round aubergines"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 shogoin kabu, about 400g, or 2 small turnips"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g kinusaya mangetout"},{"kind":"item","text":"40ml usukuchi shoyu"},{"kind":"item","text":"40ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml sake"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml rice rinsing water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 yuzu, for zest"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the kombu in the cold water for an hour, bring it to 60C (140F) and hold it there half an hour, then lift the kombu out before it reaches 80C (176F). Divide the dashi between three pans.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peel the taro into clean flat facets, salt and rinse off the slime, and parboil it in the rice rinsing water until a skewer meets slight resistance. Finish it in the first pan with mirin, sake and half the shoyu under a drop lid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Score the cut faces of the aubergine and fry them in the sesame oil until the skin is glossy and blackish purple, then simmer in the second pan with the rest of the shoyu, so the oil never reaches the other pots.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peel the turnip thickly, past the fibrous ring under the skin, and simmer it in the third pan seasoned with salt alone, so it stays white and tastes of turnip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blanch the mangetout in salted water and cool them at once. Leave every element sitting in its own liquid until service.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set each vegetable in its own quarter of the bowl and pour a little of the palest broth around them, not over them. Grate yuzu at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Takiawase means cooked apart, served together. Aubergine leaks oil and dye, taro leaks starch and slime, turnip leaks sulphur: one pot and everything tastes of everything, brown. Give each its own dashi. Draw the kombu at 60C (140F) and lift it before 80C (176F), past which it gives up alginates and the broth goes slippery and smells of the shore.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi","Scoring","Salted water & the float test","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyo-yasai%20no%20Takiawase%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kyo-yasai no Takiawase"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"sake-service-and-pairing","term":"Sake Service & Pairing"}],"pantryItems":["Eggplant","Radish/Turnip","Rice","Sesame","Wine","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["scoring","dashi"]},{"slug":"manganji-togarashi-to-oage-san-no-taitan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g Manganji togarashi, or long thick-walled sweet green peppers"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g abura-age, 2 sheets"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml kombu dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml usukuchi shoyu"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"15ml sake"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15ml sesame oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g toasted sesame seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour a kettle of boiling water over both sides of the abura-age and press it between your palms until it stops dripping, then cut it into 1cm strips.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pierce each pepper once behind the stem so steam has a way out and it cannot burst; leave them whole.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blister the peppers in the sesame oil over a high heat until the skin bubbles and goes khaki in patches, two to three minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the dashi, sake, mirin and sugar, then the shoyu, lay a drop lid on and simmer until the peppers slump and the abura-age is heavy with broth, eight to ten minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat and leave it until it is no warmer than the room, then scatter the sesame and serve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things make this obanzai and not a stir fry. Pour boiling water over the abura-age first: the frying oil on it is old and oxidised, and it will slick the broth and taste stale. Then let the pot cool. Seasoning travels into the peppers as they contract on cooling, which is why this is cooked in the morning and eaten at noon.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","umami-deep","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Manganji%20Togarashi%20to%20Oage-san%20no%20Taitan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Manganji Togarashi to Oage-san no Taitan"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bell pepper","Sesame","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"sawara-no-saikyo-yaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 sawara fillets, 120g each, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g saikyo miso, the sweet pale one"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"40ml sake"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 hajikami pickled ginger shoots"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sudachi, halved"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the fillets lightly and leave them half an hour, then blot away the beads of moisture that rise; the fishy note leaves with them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the miso with the mirin and sake to the thickness of soft butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread half of it in a tray, lay muslin over it, the fish on that, muslin again and the rest of the miso; cover and refrigerate two to three days.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe the fillets clean with the back of a knife and a dry cloth. Do not rinse them, or you undo the cure with water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill skin side first, set well back from a moderate heat, until the surface is mahogany in patches and the flesh springs back, about four minutes a side. Rest two minutes and serve with hajikami and sudachi.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cure runs two to three days and it is gentle: saikyo miso holds only about 5 percent salt against a great deal of rice koji, so osmosis firms the flesh while koji enzymes turn protein into savoury amino acids. Its sugars are also why fish burns here. Wipe the miso off, never rinse, and grill well back from the heat, or the surface blackens before the centre is warm.","equipment":["Grill","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","sweet-edged","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sawara%20no%20Saikyo-yaki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sawara no Saikyo-yaki"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Miso","Wine","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"hamo-no-otoshi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 hamo, about 700g, filleted skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg ice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g umeboshi flesh, pounded smooth"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml mirin, boiled to drive off the alcohol"},{"kind":"item","text":"15ml kombu dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"5ml usukuchi shoyu"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green shiso leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sudachi"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay a fillet skin down and cut straight down through flesh and bone every 3mm, feeling for the moment the edge touches skin and stopping there. Free a piece every 3cm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the water and salt to 90C (194F): a surface that shivers, with no rolling bubbles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop in four or five pieces at a time. In twenty to thirty seconds the flesh turns white and the cuts fan open into a flower.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift them straight into ice water for ten seconds only, then drain skin down on a cloth; any longer and the flesh drinks and goes flabby.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the umeboshi with the mirin, dashi and shoyu, and serve the hamo cold on ice with shiso and sudachi.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hamo carries thousands of fine forked bones that cannot be pulled, so honekiri cuts them instead: the blade goes down every 3mm and stops at the skin, which holds the slice together. Cut through the skin and the piece falls to bits in the pot. Drop it into water at 90C (194F), not boiling; collagen between the cuts contracts and the slice opens like a peony.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hamo%20no%20Otoshi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hamo no Otoshi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shiso","Wine","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"nishin-soba","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 migaki nishin, dried herring, about 150g"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 L rice rinsing water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 L weak bancha"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml sake"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml koikuchi shoyu"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml mirin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L dashi of kombu and katsuobushi"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml usukuchi shoyu"},{"kind":"item","text":"40ml mirin, for the broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g dried soba"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 kujo negi, sliced on the angle"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the herring in the rice rinsing water in a cool place, changing it once, until a fingernail dents the flesh; scrape off any scales and rinse.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer it in the bancha for twenty minutes to strip the old surface fat, then throw the tea away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the herring in the sake, sugar and ginger under a drop lid at a bare tremble for an hour, then add the koikuchi shoyu and mirin and carry on until the liquid is syrupy and a chopstick pushes through the backbone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the dashi with the usukuchi shoyu, mirin and salt and hold it just below a simmer. Taste it pale and frankly salty; it has plain noodles to meet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the soba in a large pot of unsalted water until the core is no longer chalky, drain, rinse under cold water to strip the surface starch, then dip it back into hot water to reheat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle the broth over the noodles, lay a piece of herring on top and finish with kujo negi.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The herring soaks a day or two in rice rinsing water to soften, then loses its rancid surface fat in weak bancha. After that, simmer it in sake and sugar for an hour before any soy goes near it: salt is a small molecule, it reaches the flesh first and tightens it, and a tightened fish never softens and its bones never yield.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","sweet-edged","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a sweet edge rounding the corners, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nishin%20Soba%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nishin Soba"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Rice","Noodles","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"shibazuke","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg small aubergines, or kamo nasu in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g red shiso leaves, stripped from the stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g myoga, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g cucumber, in batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"56g fine sea salt, 4 percent of the total weight"}],"steps":[{"text":"Quarter the aubergines lengthwise, skin on, and weigh everything together; the salt is 4 percent of the combined weight of vegetables and shiso.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the shiso hard with a third of the salt until it gives up a dark, bitter liquid. Squeeze that out and throw it away, then rub again with a second third until what runs is clear purple.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the aubergine, myoga and cucumber with the shiso in a crock, salting each layer with what is left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Weight it with twice the vegetables' weight. Brine must cover everything within two days; if it does not, add more weight rather than water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep it at 15 to 20C (59 to 68F) until the liquid smells sour like yoghurt and the flesh has gone rose right through, three to six weeks. Squeeze, slice and serve cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kyoto pickles are salt and time, three to six weeks of it, and no vinegar. Salt pulls the water out and lactobacilli in the brine make the acid; that acid is what flips red shiso from murky violet to crimson and what keeps the crock. Vinegar gets the colour in an afternoon and tastes of it. Keep the weight heavy or the tops sit dry and go slimy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shibazuke%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shibazuke"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggplant","Cucumber","Shiso"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"warabimochi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100g hon-warabiko, true bracken starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"450ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g kinako"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g Uji matcha"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g kurozato black sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml water, for the kuromitsu"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the kurozato in its water over a low heat to a loose syrup and leave it to cool. Sift the kinako with the matcha and icing sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the warabiko, sugar and water together cold until no grit drags on the whisk, then pass it through a sieve; true bracken starch comes in hard lumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over a medium heat, stirring without stopping. It goes from milk white to grey and lumpy in patches, then pulls together into one mass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Once it is translucent all through, keep working it over the heat another three to five minutes until it is glossy and heavy on the spatula. This is the step people cut short.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop spoonfuls into ice water for half a minute, or pour into a wetted tray and cut with a wet scraper. Roll in the kinako and matcha, and pour the kuromitsu at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bracken starch sets into a soft gel that retrogrades fast: chill it and the chains re-associate, so it whitens and turns grainy within hours. Make it the day you eat it and keep it at room temperature. Stopping at first translucency is the other error; it leaves a raw starch taste and the mochi weeps water on the plate.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Warabimochi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Warabimochi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyoto%20%26%20Kaiseki%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Kyoto & Kaiseki kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sugar"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"quenelles-de-brochet-sauce-nantua","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pike fillet, skinned, pin boned and well chilled"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter, for the panade"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g cold butter, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs plus 2 yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g nutmeg, freshly grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g whole freshwater crayfish, live or frozen"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot and 1 shallot, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato purée"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml marc de Bourgogne"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml Bourgogne Aligoté"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter, for mounting the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig tarragon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Panade: boil the milk with the 60 g butter and half the salt, draw off the heat and rain in the flour, then dry the paste over low heat, beating hard, until it balls up and films the base of the pan. Spread it thin on a tray and chill until stone cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the pike to a smooth paste with the rest of the salt, work in the cold panade, then the eggs and yolks one at a time, then the diced cold butter. Keep the bowl on ice throughout; the mixture must stay below 12C (54F) and hold a soft peak on the spatula. Rest it two hours in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sauce: roast the crayfish until scarlet, pick the tails, crush shells and heads and sweat them with the carrot, shallot and tomato purée, flame with the marc, add wine and cream and simmer 20 min. Press hard through a fine sieve, add the tarragon, then mount with the 60 g butter off the heat.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape quenelles between two wet spoons and slide them into salted water held at 78 to 80C (172 to 176F), so the surface only shivers. Turn once. They are ready when they have doubled, roll over on their own and feel springy under a finger, about 12 min.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a cloth, set in a buttered dish, nap with the Nantua and scatter over the crayfish tails.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (390F) until the sauce blisters at the edges and the quenelles swell again, about 8 min.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false}],"note":"The panade is flour already gelatinised in hot milk: the granules have drunk their water, so in the poach they swell instead of dissolving while the egg sets a mesh around them. That swelling is the lift, not any raising agent. Hold the water at 78 to 80C (172 to 176F); at a boil the trapped steam escapes faster than the egg can set and the quenelles tear open.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","aromatic","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, melting away silken."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Quenelles%20de%20Brochet%2C%20Sauce%20Nantua%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Quenelles de Brochet, Sauce Nantua"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Crawfish","Shallot","Tomato","Carrot","Tarragon","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Tomato paste","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"saucisson-de-lyon-en-brioche","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 coarse pork cooking sausage with pistachios, about 400 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g strong plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fresh yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cold eggs, about 150 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cold butter, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk beaten with 10 ml milk, to glaze"},{"kind":"item","text":"flour, for dusting the sausage"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix flour, salt, sugar, yeast and the cold eggs to a shaggy dough and work it 10 min until it slaps clean off the bowl. Add the butter piece by piece and keep working until the dough is glossy and stretches to a windowpane. Chill overnight.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach the sausage in water held at 80C (176F), never bubbling, until a skewer meets no resistance, about 30 min. Cool it in its liquid, peel off the skin, pat dry and chill until firm.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the cold dough to a rectangle 1 cm thick, paint the sausage with the mustard, dust it with flour and roll it up, sealing the seam and pinching the ends closed. Set it seam down in a lined loaf tin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove at around 24C (75F) until the dough springs back slowly from a fingertip and has filled the tin by half again, about 90 min. Glaze twice.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (355F) until deep chestnut and the core reads 65C (150F), about 35 min. Rest 20 min before cutting or the crumb drags.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Poach the sausage first and chill it hard. Wrapped raw it shrinks as it bakes and leaves a cavity between meat and crumb, and the fat it renders soaks the base to paste. Keep the poaching water at 80C (176F) so the forcemeat's fat stays emulsified, dry the peeled sausage, then paint it with mustard: dough grips a tacky surface and slides off a wet one.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","fiery","nutty-toasted","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, real fire that builds and stays, melting away silken."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Kneading dough","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20knead%20dough%20windowpane%20test","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Saucisson%20de%20Lyon%20en%20Brioche%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Saucisson de Lyon en Brioche"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"saucisson-sec","term":"Saucisson Sec"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Sausage","Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Mustard","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"salade-lyonnaise","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 heads frisée, pale hearts only, about 250 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g smoked poitrine, cut into thick lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 very fresh eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g stale pain de campagne, in cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 shallot, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml red wine vinegar, plus 20 ml for the poaching water"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt and coarse black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash the frisée hearts and dry them hard in a cloth; water left on the leaves dilutes the hot dressing to nothing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the lardons from cold in a dry pan until the fat runs clear and the edges crisp but the centres still yield. Lift them out, fry the bread cubes in the fat until gold, then rub them with the cut garlic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach the eggs one at a time in water at 82C (180F) with the 20 ml vinegar, until the white is opaque and set but the yolk still swings loose inside it, about 3 min. Slip them into cold water.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, whisk the mustard, shallot, the 30 ml vinegar and the oil into the hot pan fat; it should turn cloudy and slightly thick rather than separating into pools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the hot dressing over the frisée and toss until the leaves slump but still crackle, add lardons and croutons, sit an egg on each plate and grind the pepper at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The dressing is rendered lardon fat deglazed with vinegar and poured on hot, so the leaf has to have rib: frisée hearts wilt without collapsing, soft lettuce turns to slime. Poach the eggs at 82C (180F) in barely moving water; a boil shreds the white before the yolk thickens. The yolk must stay liquid, because it is the second half of the dressing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Poaching eggs","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Poaching eggs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20poach%20eggs%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salade%20Lyonnaise%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salade Lyonnaise"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Garlic","Shallot","Lettuce/Greens","Vinegar","Wine","Mustard","Clove"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"gratin-dauphinois","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg firm yellow potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 fat garlic clove, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g nutmeg, freshly grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slice the potatoes 3 mm thick on a mandoline and do not rinse or soak them; the starch clinging to the cut faces is the only binder in the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the gratin dish hard with the cut garlic until the surface smells of it, then butter it heavily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the milk, salt, nutmeg, pepper and the garlic halves to a simmer, add the slices and cook, moving them gently, until the milk has visibly thickened and coats the back of a spoon, about 10 min.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the slices flat and overlapping into the dish, pour over the thickened milk, then the cream, and press the top down until the liquid rises just level with the potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 150C (300F) until a knife slides through with no resistance and the top is bronzed and set, about 1 h 15 min. Rest 20 min so it will cut.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5700,"estimated":true}],"note":"No cheese and no egg in the Dauphiné, so potato starch is the only thickener. Never rinse the slices, and simmer them in the milk first so the amylose leaches out and the liquid thickens before it meets the oven; rinsed potatoes bake into a puddle with the cream floating on top. Hold the oven at 150C (300F), because hotter curdles the milk proteins into grains.","equipment":["Mandoline","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","aromatic","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gratin%20Dauphinois%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gratin Dauphinois"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Potato","Butter","Milk","Cream","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"oeufs-en-meurette","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"750 ml red Burgundy, a young Pinot Noir"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml brown veal stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"16 small onions, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g button mushrooms, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter for the pan, plus 40 g softened and 30 g cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 shallot, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf and 2 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 very fresh eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick slices pain de campagne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, for the toast"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the lardons in the 40 g pan butter and lift them out, glaze the small onions with the sugar until burnished, then fry the mushrooms until they squeak and colour. Reserve all three together; this is the garniture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In the same pan sweat the shallot and crushed garlic, add the wine, thyme and bay and boil hard to reduce by half, until it smells of fruit rather than alcohol. Add the stock and reduce again to about 400 ml.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach the eggs one at a time in a small pan of that wine held at 82C (180F), spinning the liquid first, until the whites are firm and stained purple, about 3 min. Lift them out, trim the ragged skirts, and tip the wine back into the sauce.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead the 40 g softened butter with the flour and whisk it into the simmering sauce in small knobs; hold it at a simmer two minutes, until it coats a spoon and no longer tastes raw of flour, then strain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the garniture to warm through, take the pan off the heat and swirl in the 30 g cold butter until the sauce turns glossy and clings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the bread, rub it with the garlic clove, sit an egg on each and spoon the meurette over so it pools around rather than soaks in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wine is acid, tannin and no body at all. Reduce it alone by half to drive off the raw alcohol, then build weight with brown stock and a beurre manié simmered two minutes so it loses its floury taste. Push the reduction too far and the tannins turn inky and astringent. Poach the eggs in the wine itself: its acidity sets the white fast, so no vinegar is wanted.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Poaching eggs","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":39,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Poaching eggs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20poach%20eggs%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Oeufs%20en%20Meurette%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Oeufs en Meurette"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"poach-simmer-boil","term":"Poach, Simmer, Boil"},{"slug":"pinot-noir-and-burgundy","term":"Pinot Noir & Burgundy"},{"slug":"pinot-noir","term":"Pinot Noir"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Shallot","Mushrooms","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Flour","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"jambon-persille-de-bourgogne","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg unsmoked salted ham hock, on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pig's trotter, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml Bourgogne Aligoté"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion stuck with 2 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot"},{"kind":"item","text":"green of 1 leek"},{"kind":"item","text":"bouquet garni of thyme, bay and parsley stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 white peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g flat parsley leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed to a paste with salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 shallots, chopped very fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 leaves gelatine, only if the test spoon will not set"},{"kind":"item","text":"coarse black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the hock in cold water overnight, changing it twice, to pull the cure back out; the last water should taste barely salty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover hock and trotter with the wine and enough water to submerge, add vegetables, bouquet and peppercorns, and simmer at a bare tremble, skimming, until the meat pulls from the bone under a fork, about 3 h.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the meat. Strain the stock, boil it down to about 600 ml and chill a spoonful on a cold plate: it must set firm enough to hold a fingerprint. Only if it stays slack, soften the gelatine and whisk it in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tear the warm ham into coarse shreds with some of its fat, discarding rind and bone. Let the stock cool to a thick syrup, then stir in the parsley chopped at the last moment, the garlic, shallots, vinegar and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack meat and green jelly into a terrine in alternating spoonfuls so the parsley marbles rather than layers, press lightly, and chill overnight until firm. Cut in thick slices and serve with pickles and bread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The set comes from the trotter's collagen, not a packet: simmer it alongside the hock and test a spoonful on a cold plate before you commit the mould. Fold the parsley in only once the jelly has cooled to syrup; stirred into hot stock the chlorophyll goes khaki and tastes of boiled grass. Tear the ham into shreds rather than dicing it, so the fibres interlock and the slice holds together.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jambon%20Persill%C3%A9%20de%20Bourgogne%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jambon Persillé de Bourgogne"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Shallot","Leek","Carrot","Parsley","Thyme","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"cervelle-de-canut","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g fromage blanc, at least 20 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g crème fraîche"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml grapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml dry white wine, Aligoté or Mâcon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 shallots, chopped as fine as gravel"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small garlic clove, crushed with salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g chives"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g chervil"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g tarragon"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g small waxy potatoes, boiled and hot, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Line a sieve with muslin, tip in the fromage blanc and drain it in the fridge until it holds a ridge from the spoon and the whey has stopped dripping, at least 4 h.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the drained curd with the crème fraîche and salt, then trickle in the oil while whisking until the mixture is glossy and thick enough to keep the whisk marks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let it down with the vinegar and then the wine, a little at a time, until it drops slowly from a spoon; stop before it pours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the shallot, garlic and all the herbs, chopped only now, grind on plenty of pepper and serve within the hour with the hot potatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fromage blanc is mostly water held loosely by casein. Drain it in muslin for several hours, then beat the oil in first, to a body like thick mayonnaise, and only after that let it down with vinegar and wine; in the other order the acid thins the curd to a grey slop that weeps in the dish. Herbs go in last, because salt and acid draw their water out and they go limp within the hour.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cervelle%20de%20Canut%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cervelle de Canut"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Shallot","Potato","Parsley","Tarragon","Chives","Sour cream/Crema","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"tablier-de-sapeur","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g cooked honeycomb ox tripe, gras-double"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry white Mâcon"},{"kind":"item","text":"55 g Dijon mustard, 40 g for the marinade and 15 g for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 lemons"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g fine dried breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g clarified butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 hard boiled eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 raw egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml groundnut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cornichons, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g capers, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g tarragon, chervil and parsley, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the tripe into 8 palm sized pieces and marinate overnight in the wine, the 40 g mustard, the juice of one lemon and the pepper, turning once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and pat every piece bone dry on cloth, then pass it through flour, beaten egg and breadcrumbs, pressing the crumbs on firmly. Chill uncovered an hour so the coat sets and dries.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Gribiche: mash the cooked yolks with the raw yolk and the 15 g mustard to a smooth paste, then drive in the oil drop by drop until it grips and in a thin stream once it thickens. Slacken with the vinegar, then fold in the chopped whites, cornichons, capers and herbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in the clarified butter at 170C (340F), a few pieces at a time, until the crust is deep gold and crackles under the back of a spoon, about 3 min a side. Drain on a rack, never stacked on paper.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt at once and serve with the gribiche and the second lemon in wedges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tripe reaches you already cooked and saturated, so pat it bone dry before it meets the flour: wet tripe sheds its crust into the pan as the gelatine underneath melts. Fry in clarified butter at 170C (340F), since whole butter's milk solids blacken well before the crumb colours. The gribiche emulsifies on the raw yolk alone, the cooked yolks only give body, so start the oil drop by drop.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salted water & the float test","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","briny","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tablier%20de%20Sapeur%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tablier de Sapeur"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Liver & Offal","Capers","Lemon","Parsley","Tarragon","Butter","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Vinegar","Wine","Mustard","Honey","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"la-pochouse-verdunoise","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg mixed river fish: pike, perch, tench and a piece of eel, cleaned and cut into thick sections"},{"kind":"item","text":"750 ml Bourgogne Aligoté"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"16 small onions, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g button mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter, for the pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g softened butter, for the beurre manié"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"bouquet garni of thyme, bay and parsley stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml marc de Bourgogne"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick slices pain de campagne"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, for rubbing the croutons"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the lardons, then brown the small onions and the mushrooms in the same fat until glazed and squeaking. Lift the garniture out and hold it aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the sliced onion and crushed garlic in the 50 g butter without letting them colour, add the wine, bouquet and 250 ml water and simmer 20 min, until the broth no longer smells sharp.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the fish in by firmness: eel first, pike and tench four minutes later, perch last. Keep the pan at a shiver and lift each piece out the moment the flesh parts from the bone at a fork's nudge. Keep them warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk a beurre manié of the softened butter and flour into the broth in small knobs, simmer two minutes until it coats a spoon, then add the cream and reduce to a light nappé.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the marc, flame it into the sauce, return the garniture to heat through and correct the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the bread in butter, rub it with the garlic clove, and serve the fish in warm bowls under the sauce with the croutons alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A garniture bourguignonne is lardons, button mushrooms and small glazed onions, and that trio is what makes a dish Burgundian; here the wine is white, from the Saône valley, not red. Cook the garnish apart and add it at the end or the mushrooms bleed grey through the broth. Add the fish by firmness, eel first and perch last: they are five minutes apart, and river fish poached past that goes to cotton.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=La%20Pochouse%20Verdunoise%20recipe","sub":"Search films of La Pochouse Verdunoise"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Mushrooms","Parsley","Thyme","Butter","Cream","Bread","Flour","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"poires-a-la-beaujolaise","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 firm pears, about 1.1 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"750 ml Beaujolais, a young Gamay"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip orange zest"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml crème de cassis"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, halved"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the pears, leaving the stalks on, and rub them all over with the cut lemon so they do not brown before they go into the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the wine, sugar, cinnamon, zest, peppercorns and clove to a simmer and stir until no grain of sugar is left on the base; undissolved sugar scorches there and turns the syrup bitter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lower the pears in on their sides, cover with a paper cartouche and a small plate to keep them under the surface, and poach at a bare shiver, turning once, until a skewer meets only slight resistance at the core, 20 to 35 min according to ripeness.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":2100,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool the pears in the syrup, then chill them in it overnight; the colour keeps travelling inward as they sit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the pears out, boil the syrup with the cassis until glossy and just coating a spoon, cool it and pour it back over them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hold the syrup at a shiver. At a boil the pears knock about and the anthocyanins that are colouring them leach straight back into the pan. Sugar matters as much as wine: below roughly a fifth by weight the flesh loses water to the syrup and goes woolly. Lift the pears out before you reduce, or the ten minutes of hard boiling that glazes the syrup will cook them to mush.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","gently hot","bright","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a warm current of chile, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Poires%20%C3%A0%20la%20Beaujolaise%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Poires à la Beaujolaise"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyon%20%26%20Burgundy%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Lyon & Burgundy kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"gamay-beaujolais","term":"Gamay (Beaujolais)"},{"slug":"poach-simmer-boil","term":"Poach, Simmer, Boil"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Orange","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"goya-chanpuru","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 goya (bitter melon), about 250g"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g fine salt, for drawing the goya"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g shima dofu, or the firmest tofu you can buy"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g pork belly, rind off, sliced 3mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, about 110g, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"15ml awamori"},{"kind":"item","text":"10ml usukuchi (light) soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"1g fine salt, to season"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g katsuobushi, to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Halve the goya lengthwise and scrape out the seeds and every trace of the cottony white pith with a spoon, then slice 3mm thick across.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the slices with the 6g salt and leave 10 minutes, until water beads on them and a slice bends instead of snapping. Squeeze once, hard, in your fist and do not rinse. Taste a piece: it should still be plainly bitter.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tear the tofu into 3cm lumps and fry them undisturbed in a hot oiled pan until the torn faces are gold and squeak against the spatula. Lift out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the pork in the same pan until its fat runs clear and the edges crisp, then add the goya and toss over the highest heat for 90 seconds, until the green brightens and the cut edges blister.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the tofu, splash in the awamori and soy, season with salt and white pepper, then pour the egg round the edge of the pan and fold twice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Kill the heat while the egg is still glossy and barely set, pile it up, and scatter the katsuobushi so it curls in the steam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salting works osmotically: it pulls water out of the goya and the bitter momordicins leave dissolved in that water. Ten minutes and one squeeze takes out roughly a third, which is all you want. Blanch it, or salt it for an hour, and you have a limp green courgette and no reason to have cooked it. The other failure is a wet pan: tofu that was not pressed and browned first floods the heat, and champuru becomes a stew.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=G%C5%8Dy%C4%81%20Chanpur%C5%AB%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gōyā Chanpurū"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Tofu","Eggs","Soy sauce"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"rafute","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg skin-on pork belly, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 leek greens"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml awamori"},{"kind":"item","text":"600ml katsuo and kombu dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g kokuto (Okinawan black sugar), broken up"},{"kind":"item","text":"80ml koikuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml usukuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"karashi mustard, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the belly whole with the ginger and leek greens in water to cover for 40 minutes, skimming the grey scum until the surface runs clear. Lift out, rinse under hot water and cool enough to handle.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2640,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into 4cm squares, skin left on every piece: the skin is the dish, not a trimming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the awamori hard in a wide pan for 2 minutes, until the raw spirit smell has burned off and what rises smells of rice. Add the dashi and the pork, lay on a drop lid, and hold at a bare shiver, about 85C (185F), for 90 minutes, until a skewer slides through the fat with no resistance at all.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5520,"estimated":false},{"text":"Only now stir in the kokuto, and 20 minutes later both soy sauces.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer uncovered another 30 minutes, spooning the liquid over the pieces, until it darkens to a gloss on the skin and falls off the spoon in a slow ribbon.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool in the pan, lift off the set fat cap, and rewarm to serve with a dab of karashi.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Awamori, not sake, is what separates this from kakuni. It is distilled from long grain rice with black koji and lands at 30 percent or more, so it unwinds collagen faster than any brewed alcohol, and it carries no sugar of its own. That is why the kokuto goes in late. Sugar and salt raise the osmotic pressure of the braise and draw water out of the muscle fibres, tightening the meat before its collagen has had time to melt. Sweeten early and it stays stringy for hours.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rafut%C4%93%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rafutē"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ginger","Leek","Soy sauce","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"soki-soba","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5kg pork neck bones"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g kombu"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g katsuobushi"},{"kind":"item","text":"800g pork spare ribs, cut into 5cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml awamori"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g kokuto"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml koikuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g Okinawa soba noodles (wheat, alkaline, no buckwheat)"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g salt, or to taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices kamaboko"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"beni shoga"},{"kind":"item","text":"koregusu (island chillies steeped in awamori), to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the neck bones with cold water, bring to the boil, then throw the water away and scrub both bones and pot clean of grey scum. Refill with 3 litres cold water and the kombu, pull the kombu out just before it boils, and hold at a lazy simmer for 3 hours.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile simmer the ribs in the awamori with water to cover for 1 hour, then add the kokuto and soy and cook down 30 minutes more, turning them, until they are sticky and the meat has drawn back from the bone.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain the pork broth, bring it to 80C (176F), cut the heat, drop in the katsuobushi and leave 3 minutes. Strain again through a cloth without pressing, and salt it to just past what tastes right.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the noodles in unsalted water until they are springy rather than soft, drain, and rinse briefly in warm water to wash the surface alkali off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Load the bowls with noodles, hot broth, ribs, kamaboko, spring onion and a pinch of beni shoga. Koregusu goes on at the table, drop by drop.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Okinawa soba holds no buckwheat: it is wheat raised with alkaline water, and the high pH tightens the gluten and yellows the flour, which is why the strand stays springy sitting in hot broth. The broth is the other local marker, half pork and half katsuobushi rather than one or the other. Boil the bonito and you pull out bitterness and cloud; hold the pot near 80C (176F) for three minutes and you get the savour and nothing sour.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","earthy","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, an earthy, grounding base, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=S%C5%8Dki%20Soba%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sōki Soba"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"ribs-baby-back-vs-spare-vs-st-louis","term":"Ribs: Baby Back vs. Spare vs. St. Louis"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Scallion","Chiles","Noodles","Soy sauce","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"jimami-dofu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g raw skinless peanuts, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"700ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g sweet potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml kombu dashi, kombu only"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g ginger, finely grated"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the peanuts, rub off any clinging skins, and blend with the water in two batches until the liquid is chalk white and no grit rolls between your fingers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wring the puree through a doubled cloth, hard, until the pulp left in your hand is dry and crumbly. You want about 550ml of milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the sweet potato starch and salt into the cold milk until nothing settles out, then set the pan over low heat and stir with a spatula, corners included, without stopping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"It thickens all at once around 70C (158F) into a pale paste. Keep it moving a full 15 minutes past that, until it turns from matt to glossy, gathers away from the sides in one mass, and drags heavily on the spatula.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape into a wetted mould, tap out the air, cover the surface and set at room temperature for an hour. Turn out, cut, and spoon over the kombu dashi simmered with the soy, sugar and ginger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing here is coagulated. No nigari, no soy beans: this is a starch gel, and the peanut protein and fat are passengers in it. Sweet potato starch swells near 70C (158F), but the granules must be cooked well past that and worked constantly before the paste goes glossy and will hold a cut edge. Stop at the first thickening and it sets soft and weeps overnight. Eat it the day you make it; the starch retrogrades in the cold and turns chalky.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","nutty-toasted","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=J%C4%ABm%C4%81mi%20D%C5%8Dfu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jīmāmi Dōfu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Potato","Sweet potato","Soy sauce","Peanut","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"mimiga-no-pinatsu-miso-ae","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 pig's ears, about 400g"},{"kind":"item","text":"50ml awamori"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g roasted peanuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g white miso"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10ml awamori, for the dressing"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cucumber, salted and squeezed"},{"kind":"item","text":"shima rakkyo or spring onion, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scrape the ears with the back of a knife under running water and singe off any bristles over a flame, wiping them away as they char.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer in water with the 50ml awamori and the ginger, lid ajar, for 90 minutes, until a skewer passes easily through the fleshy base while the cartilage at the rim still resists and clicks under the point.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift out, press flat between two plates and chill until cold and firm, at least 2 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the peanuts to a coarse rubble, then work in the miso, sugar, vinegar and dressing awamori to a thick, spoonable paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the cold ear across into 2mm ribbons, toss with the cucumber and just enough peanut miso to coat, and scatter with shima rakkyo. Dress at the last moment so it clings rather than soaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A pig's ear is skin wrapped round elastic cartilage, and the two parts want different times. Ninety minutes at a simmer melts the skin collagen to gelatine and turns it silky, while the cartilage, which is elastin, keeps its crunch. Run it to two hours and that crunch slackens into rubber. The chilling is not for convenience either: cold sets the gelatine hard enough to take a 2mm slice, and a warm ear only shreds.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salting to draw the water out","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","tangy-ferment","nutty-toasted","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ferment tang with real depth behind it, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":40,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mimig%C4%81%20no%20P%C4%ABnatsu%20Miso%20Ae%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mimigā no Pīnatsu Miso Ae"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Cucumber","Miso","Peanut","Vinegar","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"nakami-jiru","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700g pork small intestine, split and rough cleaned"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g plain flour, for scrubbing"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g salt, for scrubbing"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.6 litres katsuo and kombu dashi"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 dried shiitake, soaked and sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g konnyaku, torn into pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml awamori"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10ml usukuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g ginger, grated, to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"toasted white sesame"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the flour and salt into the intestine in a big bowl, kneading it like dough for two minutes, until your hands are gloved in grey paste. Rinse and repeat three times, until the flour comes away white and the rinse water runs clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with cold water, boil hard 10 minutes, drain and wash each piece under the tap. Do that twice more with fresh water.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into 3cm strips and simmer in the dashi with the shiitake, konnyaku and awamori for 90 minutes, until a piece gives under the tooth with no chew left but still holds its tube.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season with the salt and only a trickle of light soy. The broth should stay pale and read clean, not brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle out, grate the ginger over each bowl at the table, and finish with sesame.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Flour is an abrasive here, not a flavouring. Salt and water slide over the mucus and fat that carry the smell, while a stiff flour paste grips and lifts them, and the starch adsorbs the indole and skatole as it goes. Three scrubs, then three changes of hard boiling water. Skip a round and the soup tastes of the animal the whole way through, and no amount of ginger at the end will cover it. Keep the broth clear: this is a salt soup, never a miso one.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","nutty-toasted","aromatic","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nakami%20Jiru%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nakami Jiru"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ginger","Mushrooms","Flour","Soy sauce","Sesame","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori","Dried mushrooms"],"judgedBy":["dashi","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"hirayachi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"180ml cold kombu dashi, kombu only"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, about 110g"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g nira (garlic chives), cut in 4cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g carrot, fine matchsticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"soy sauce, rice vinegar and shikuwasa juice, to dip"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, kombu dashi, eggs and salt to a batter the body of single cream, thin enough to run off the whisk in an unbroken thread, and rest it 10 minutes while you cut the nira.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the nira and carrot. It should look like greens held loosely in liquid, not a thick dough with things buried in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set a 24cm pan over medium heat with a slick of oil, pour in half the batter and tilt it out to a 3mm layer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook about 3 minutes, until the top loses its wet shine and the edge lifts away dry, then flip and give it 2 minutes more, until both faces are freckled tan and the chives inside have gone soft and dark instead of squeaking.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slide out, cut into six wedges and eat at once with the dip, the second one cooking while you do.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is not okonomiyaki. No cabbage, no yam, no thickener, and that absence is the whole discipline: a slack batter with almost no gluten worked into it stays floppy and folds, where a stiff one bakes into a chewy pad. Keep the pan at medium. Hot enough to blister the outside fast and the chives inside are still raw and squeaking, and that raw allium bite is the one flavour a pancake this thin cannot carry.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dashi","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hiray%C4%81ch%C4%AB%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hirayāchī"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Garlic","Carrot","Chives","Flour","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Stock/Broth","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"sata-andagi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"240g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"110g kokuto (Okinawan black sugar), finely crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, about 110g"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml neutral oil, for the batter"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml coconut milk or whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres neutral oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the eggs with the kokuto until the sugar has dissolved and the mix ribbons off the whisk, then beat in the oil and milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sift the flour, baking powder and salt over the top and fold with a spatula, stopping the moment no dry flour is left. The dough should be stiff, sticky and a little shaggy, never smooth and elastic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest it 20 minutes covered while the oil comes to 160C (320F). Hold it there; that number is the recipe.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil your hands, squeeze the dough up between thumb and forefinger into 4cm balls and slide them in, four or five at a time. They sink, then rise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry 7 to 8 minutes, turning, until each has split across the crown into three or four seams and the dough exposed in the cracks has browned. Still smooth at 5 minutes means the oil is too hot: pull the pan off the heat and let it fall.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":780,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain on a rack rather than paper, and eat warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The split is the doneness gauge, and it only happens slowly. At 160C (320F) the crust stays pliable long enough for steam and the baking powder to swell the ball and tear it open from inside, and by the time it has torn the centre is cooked. At 180C (356F) the surface sets first, the ball stays round and smooth, and you bite into raw batter. Below 150C (302F) it simply drinks oil.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=S%C4%81t%C4%81%20And%C4%81g%C4%AB%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sātā Andāgī"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"umibudo-with-shikuwasa-sanbaizu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g fresh umibudo (sea grapes)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml kombu dashi, kombu only"},{"kind":"item","text":"30ml rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml shikuwasa juice, or lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml usukuchi soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g daikon, finely grated and drained"}],"steps":[{"text":"Keep the umibudo out of the fridge from the moment you buy it, on the counter, loosely covered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the kombu dashi, vinegar, shikuwasa, soy and sugar only until the sugar dissolves, then cool the sanbaizu to room temperature and stir in the grated daikon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the umibudo in a bowl of room temperature water for 20 seconds, moving it with your fingers, until the beads plump and go from dull olive to translucent green. Lift it out and drain on a cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile it on a plate and set the sanbaizu beside it in a small dish. Dip at the table, a forkful at a time, and listen for the beads going off like small shot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sea grapes are single algal cells holding water under pressure, and that pressure is the pop. Two things burst it. Cold shrivels the beads and they never recover, so this is the one thing in the kitchen that must never see the fridge or iced water. And salt or vinegar draws the water straight out osmotically: leave them sitting in the dressing two minutes and you serve limp string. Dip, do not dress.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dashi"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":14,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Dashi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20dashi%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Umibud%C5%8D%20with%20Shikuwasa%20Sanbaizu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Umibudō with Shikuwasa Sanbaizu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Radish/Turnip","Lime","Grapes","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Seaweed/Nori"],"judgedBy":["dashi"]},{"slug":"takoraisu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g minced beef, 15 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g tomato puree"},{"kind":"item","text":"20ml soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"10ml Worcestershire sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"600g hot cooked Japanese short grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"120g mature cheddar, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g crisp lettuce, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g tomatoes, diced and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"koregusu and salsa, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the beef in a dry pan without stirring until one face has a crust and the fat has run, then break it up and add the onion and garlic, cooking until the onion turns translucent.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the spices to the hot fat for 30 seconds, until they smell toasted rather than dusty, then the tomato puree, soy, Worcestershire and water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer and stir until the pan is nearly dry and a spoon dragged across the base leaves a track that fills back slowly. Keep going past the point it looks ready.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build in order: hot rice, cheese straight onto it so it slumps, then the hot mince over the cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Only now the cold lettuce and tomato, with salsa and koregusu at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Post-war food, built round American mince and island rice, and the rice is exactly why the mince has to be reduced almost dry. A tortilla carries sauce; short grain rice does not. Its surface starch takes up free liquid on contact and the bowl goes to porridge under the topping. Build hot to cold as well: cheese onto rice hot enough to slump it, salad last, or the lettuce wilts into the meat before it reaches the table.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Takoraisu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Takoraisu"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Okinawa%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Okinawa kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lettuce/Greens","Oregano","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Rice","Soy sauce","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"soupe-de-poissons-de-roche","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg small rock fish (rascasse, weever, small wrasse, gurnard, a green crab or two), gutted"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml fruity olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 fennel bulb, sliced, plus 1 tsp fennel seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g very ripe tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 wide strips dried orange peel, white pith scraped away"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g saffron threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp fine sea salt, plus more to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the rouille: 4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 waxy potato (150 g), boiled soft in the soup and lifted out"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small hot dried red chilli, soaked and skinned"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml olive oil for the rouille"},{"kind":"item","text":"A pinch of saffron for the rouille"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 thin baguette, sliced and dried out in the oven"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onions, fennel and garlic in the olive oil over low heat until translucent and quite without colour, then add the fennel seed and wait until the pan smells of aniseed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the heat, turn the whole fish in the hot oil until the flesh whitens and begins to fall off the frames, then add tomatoes, orange peel and saffron and stir until the tomatoes slump.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour on the water, bring it to a bare shiver and hold it there for 30 minutes; the surface should tremble, never roll.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pass everything, bones and all, through the coarse plate of a food mill, pressing until only dry pulp remains, then push that liquid through a conical sieve and salt it harder than feels polite.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"For the rouille, pound the garlic and chilli to a smooth paste with salt, work in the boiled potato and saffron, then the yolk, then dribble in the oil until it stands up around the pestle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reheat the soup to just under a boil and serve it with the dried bread, letting each person melt rouille off a croûton into the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Saffron, fennel and dried orange are the spine of this soup, not its garnish; without them you have made fish broth. The body is gelatine milled out of small bones, held in suspension by the oil, which is why a hard boil ruins it: it shears the fat out and the soup turns grey and greasy. Keep it shivering, and use a food mill, never a blender, which grinds bone to grit.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","aromatic","briny","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":17,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Soupe%20de%20Poissons%20de%20Roche%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Soupe de Poissons de Roche"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"crab-seasons-around-the-world","term":"Crab Seasons Around the World"}],"pantryItems":["Crab","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Chiles","Fennel","Orange","Bread","Clove","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"le-grand-aioli","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g salt cod fillet, soaked 24 hours in cold water changed four times"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 fat garlic cloves, green germ removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml olive oil at room temperature, half fruity and half mild"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g small waxy potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g green beans, topped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 carrots, halved lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small cauliflower, in florets"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 fennel bulb, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"24 cooked petits gris snails (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the soaked cod in barely moving water with the bay until it flakes at a fork, about 8 minutes, and lift it out before the flakes tighten and go stringy.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil the eggs 9 minutes, and cook each vegetable separately in salted water: potatoes and carrots to a knife tip, beans and cauliflower still squeaking against the tooth.","durationSec":540,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pound the garlic with the coarse salt in a mortar until it is a wet sticky cream with no fibre left in it, then work in the yolks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dribble in the oil in a thread, the pestle always turning the same way, until the sauce climbs the sides of the mortar and holds the pestle upright; loosen with the lemon juice and a teaspoon of warm water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring everything to the table warm or at room temperature on one platter with the mortar in the middle, and eat it slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Garlic is as much the emulsifier here as the yolk: crushing it releases a mucilage that keeps oil droplets apart. Two things break the sauce. Oil poured faster than it can be taken up pools and splits, and the fix is to start a fresh yolk and beat the broken sauce into it a spoon at a time. And a blender shears the oil so hard that bitter phenolics flood out, so pound by hand.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Le%20Grand%20A%C3%AFoli%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Le Grand Aïoli"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Salt cod","Eggs","Garlic","Potato","Carrot","Cauliflower","Green beans","Fennel","Lemon","Bay leaf","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"daube-provencale","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.6 kg beef cheek or shin, in 6 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"750 ml young red wine from the Rhône"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g unsmoked salt pork belly, in lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 carrots, in thick coins"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 wide strips dried orange peel, white pith scraped away"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bouquet of thyme, bay, parsley stalks and a piece of dried fennel stalk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g black olives from Nyons, stoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the beef overnight in the wine with the onions, carrots, garlic, cloves and bouquet; next day lift the meat out and dry every face of every piece, or it will steam rather than brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the lardons in the olive oil in a heavy casserole, then the beef in unhurried batches until each side is properly dark, and set it aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the drained marinade vegetables in that fat, return the meat, pour on the strained wine with the tomatoes, tuck in the orange peel and bouquet and bring it to a bare tremble.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and hold at 85C (185F) in the oven for 4 hours; look in once an hour and confirm the surface is still only trembling.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the olives for the last 20 minutes, then cool the daube overnight, lift off the set fat, reheat it gently and sharpen it with the vinegar at the last moment.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"Collagen becomes gelatine from around 70C, but slowly, and four hours at 85C (185F) buys meat that slumps into its own melted connective tissue. Let it boil and the muscle fibres contract and wring themselves dry: stringy meat in a thin sauce. Scrape the orange peel too. The coloured zest perfumes the wine; the white pith turns the whole pot bitter over a cook this long.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","rich","warm-spiced","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Daube%20Proven%C3%A7ale%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Daube Provençale"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Olives","Orange","Parsley","Thyme","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"soupe-au-pistou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fresh white coco beans, shelled, or soaked dried white beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g fresh red borlotti beans, shelled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 courgettes, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g green beans, in 2 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 potatoes, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ripe tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g small pasta, coquillettes or broken vermicelli"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"For the pistou: 80 g basil leaves, from three large bunches"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g hard sheep's cheese made with vegetarian rennet, finely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml fruity olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ripe tomato, peeled and seeded, Nice style (optional)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the shelled beans in the salted water until their skins are tender and the insides no longer chalky, 30 to 40 minutes depending on how freshly they were picked.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add potato, carrot and leek, then ten minutes later the green beans, courgette and tomato, so that everything arrives at tender together instead of the soft ones dissolving.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the pasta in the soup itself; the starch it sheds is what gives the broth its cling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic to a paste with a pinch of salt, add the basil a handful at a time and work it to a dark green cream, then the cheese, then the oil in a thread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pot off the heat, let it stop bubbling, and only then stir in half the pistou; the rest goes on the table in the mortar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Basil's scent lives in volatile oils that boil away, and its phenols oxidise to a blackish grey the moment they meet sustained heat. So the pistou goes in off the flame, never simmers, and reheated leftovers want a fresh spoonful. Note also that there are no pine nuts: that is Genoa. Here it is garlic, basil, cheese and oil, pounded in that order so the salt and garlic can grind the leaves.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Soupe%20au%20Pistou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Soupe au Pistou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Leek","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Zucchini","Green beans","Basil","Cheese (any)","Pasta","Noodles","White beans","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"bohemienne-daubergines","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg aubergines, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg very ripe tomatoes, peeled, seeded and roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse salt and black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp chopped flat parsley"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the aubergine cubes, leave them 30 minutes and press them dry in a towel; once their cells have collapsed they drink far less oil.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reduce the tomatoes in a wide pan with the thyme, bay and a spoonful of oil until they hold their shape on a spoon and no water weeps out around them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In a second pan, very hot, fry the aubergine in batches in the rest of the oil until the cubes are bronzed and floppy, adding onion and garlic to the last batch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the aubergine into the tomato, cook five minutes more so they trade flavour, then take it off the heat and let it fall to warm before adding the parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is the ratatouille lesson taught in two pans. Aubergine only browns above about 140C (285F), and a pan full of tomato water cannot pass 100C (212F), so cooked together nothing browns: the aubergine simply boils in juice until its cell walls give way and the whole thing is one grey purée. Reduce the tomato to a paste, fry the aubergine hot and separate, marry them at the end.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boh%C3%A9mienne%20d'Aubergines%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bohémienne d'Aubergines"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Eggplant","Parsley","Thyme","Bay leaf","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"tian-de-courgettes-au-riz","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg courgettes, coarsely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g short grain rice, uncooked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml olive oil, plus more for the top"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g hard sheep's cheese made with vegetarian rennet, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp chopped basil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the grated courgette, leave it 20 minutes, then wring it out hard in a cloth over a bowl and keep the green juice.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Soften the onion and garlic in the olive oil without letting them colour, add the courgette and cook until it stops steaming and begins to catch on the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the raw rice, the reserved juice topped up with water to 200 ml, then the eggs, cheese, basil and plenty of pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread it no deeper than 4 cm in an oiled earthenware tian, trickle oil over the surface and bake at 180C (350F) until the top is crusted brown and a knife comes out clean, about 50 minutes.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":3240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The rice is not filler, it is there to drink. Courgette is over ninety percent water, and a tian is a wide shallow dish precisely so that water can leave as steam. Pile it deep, or leave the rice out, and you get green soup under a brown lid. Salt and wring the courgette first, keep the squeezed juice for the rice to swell in, and hold the layer under 4 cm.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tian%20de%20Courgettes%20au%20Riz%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tian de Courgettes au Riz"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Zucchini","Basil","Cheese (any)","Rice","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"pissaladiere-nicoise","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil, plus more for the tin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg onions, halved and finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"24 salted anchovy fillets, rinsed and split lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"24 small black olives from Nice, left whole with their stones"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the flour, salt, yeast, water and oil into a slack dough, knead it smooth and leave it to double.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onions with thyme, bay, oil and a pinch of salt under a lid on the lowest heat for at least an hour, stirring now and then, until they are a pale gold jam with no liquid left in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the dough into an oiled shallow tin of about 30 by 40 cm, no thicker than a finger, and let it puff for 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Spread the cooled onions right to the edges, lay the anchovies in a lattice, drop an olive into each diamond and grind over pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C (430F) until the underside is crisp and the anchovies have darkened, 20 to 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false}],"note":"The onions are dried rather than fried: an hour under a lid drives their water off and concentrates the sugar, and the pale gold comes from that, not from a hot pan. Hurry them and they scorch at the rim while staying wet in the middle, and that water soaks the base into a sog. Spread them cold, and salt nothing else on the tart; the anchovies carry all the salt it needs.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pissaladi%C3%A8re%20Ni%C3%A7oise%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pissaladière Niçoise"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Onion","Olives","Thyme","Bay leaf","Flour"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"tapenade-noire-au-mortier","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g black Tanche olives from Nyons, stoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g capers packed in salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 salted anchovy fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"Juice of half a lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp marc or brandy (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the capers and anchovies, soak them ten minutes and taste one of each; if either is still fierce, soak and taste again before anything else happens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic and thyme first, then the anchovies, then the capers, then the olives, adding each only once the one before has broken down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the oil a spoonful at a time so it is absorbed rather than pooling, then the lemon and the marc, and finish with pepper alone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave it an hour before serving; it should stay rough enough that you can still see the olive skins in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The name comes from tapeno, the caper, so capers are half the flavour and not a garnish. They and the anchovies both arrive packed in salt, and the only way to know how much salt is already in the bowl is to rinse and taste before pounding; add none of your own. Pound in order of hardness so each thing breaks down, and skip the blender, which whips the lot into a flat, greasy black emulsion.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","umami-deep","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tapenade%20Noire%20au%20Mortier%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tapenade Noire au Mortier"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Anchovy","Garlic","Olives","Capers","Lemon","Thyme"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"panisses-de-marseille","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g chickpea flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp olive oil, plus 800 ml for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"Coarse salt and black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the chickpea flour into the cold water off the heat until not one lump remains; added to hot water the flour sets a skin around each lump and locks it in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring it to the boil stirring constantly with a wooden spoon, add the salt and the 3 tbsp oil, and cook about 10 minutes, until the spoon leaves a clean trail across the pan floor and the paste pulls away from the sides.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour it at once into oiled saucers or a shallow tray to a depth of 1.5 cm, smooth the top and leave it to set cold and firm, at least two hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into thick chips and fry in olive oil at 175C (350F) until crisp and pale gold, then drain, salt coarsely, pepper heavily and serve with lemon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chickpea starch must gelatinise fully before it will set, which is why the paste is cooked until it leaves the pan sides; underdone, it sets soft and the water still trapped inside flashes to steam in the fryer and bursts the panisse open. Fry in olive oil rather than beside it: sound low acidity oil smokes near 200C (390F), so 175C (350F) sits comfortably below trouble.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","smoky","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panisses%20de%20Marseille%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Panisses de Marseille"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Flour","Chickpeas"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"navettes-marseillaises-a-la-fleur-doranger","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml mild olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml orange flower water"},{"kind":"item","text":"Finely grated zest of 1 orange"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pinch fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk beaten with 1 tsp water, to glaze"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work everything into a firm, barely tacky dough; it should feel dry against any biscuit dough you know, and should stop being worked the moment it is smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest it wrapped and cool for an hour so the gluten slackens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 30 g pieces into little spindles, pinch the ends to points, and cut one deep slit down the length of each, well into the dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Glaze them and bake at 180C (350F) for 18 to 20 minutes, until each slit has opened into a boat and the undersides are the colour of pale straw.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool them completely on a rack; they firm as they cool and keep for weeks in a tin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no raising agent and almost no water here, so the only lift comes from steam and the slit is the vent that decides where it escapes. Cut it shallow and the dough seals over in the oven, then splits along the flank instead. Skip the rest and the spindles retract into lumps as the gluten pulls back. Orange flower water is volatile, so be generous; half its perfume leaves in the heat.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Scoring","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Navettes%20Marseillaises%20%C3%A0%20la%20Fleur%20d'Oranger%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Navettes Marseillaises à la Fleur d'Oranger"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Provence%20%26%20the%20Midi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Provence & the Midi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Orange","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["scoring","egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine"]},{"slug":"piperade","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g piments doux verts (long sweet green peppers), deseeded and cut in 1 cm strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g ripe tomatoes, peeled, deseeded and roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp piment d'Espelette, plus a pinch to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onions and garlic in the olive oil over low heat with a pinch of salt until they are slack and glassy with no colour at all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the green peppers and cook on until they slump and lose their squeak; a strip should bend double without snapping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes and the Espelette and cook uncovered until a spoon dragged across the base leaves a clean channel and no watery juice runs back into it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan to the lowest possible heat, pour in the beaten salted eggs and stir slowly and constantly, scraping the base, until the mass thickens and turns glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take it off while it still looks slightly too loose, since it carries on setting in the hot pan, and dust with Espelette.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Espelette is not paprika under a French name: the pods are dried whole and milled coarse, so it carries fruit and a slow warmth near 4,000 SHU rather than sweet red dust. The eggs are the fragile part. They set from 62C (144F) and weep water past 75C (167F), so the vegetables must be reduced dry and the heat right down before the eggs go in, or you get scrambled egg sitting in a puddle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Piperade%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Piperade"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"poulet-basquaise","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken of 1.6 kg, jointed into 8"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g duck fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g jambon de Bayonne, cut in 5 mm lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"450 g piments doux verts, in 1 cm strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g red peppers, in 1 cm strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry white Irouléguy or another crisp white"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp piment d'Espelette"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf and 2 thyme sprigs"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the chicken and lay it skin down in the duck fat over medium heat; leave it until the skin is deep gold and lifts from the pan by itself, then set it aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the ham lardons in the same fat, then add the onions and both peppers and cook until they have collapsed and the edges are catching brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the garlic and Espelette for thirty seconds, until the fat stains red and smells sweet, then deglaze with the wine and reduce until the pan smells of wine and not of alcohol.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes and herbs, return the legs and thighs so the skin stands proud of the sauce, and simmer bare and gentle for 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tuck in the breast pieces for the last 12 minutes, until the juices at the thigh joint run clear and the flesh pulls from the bone with a little coaxing. Rest off the heat for 10 minutes.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Capsaicin and the carotenoids that carry Espelette's flavour are fat soluble, so the powder goes into the hot fat for half a minute, never straight into the tomato water, where it stays dusty and tastes raw. Keep the chicken skin standing above the sauce as it simmers. Submerged skin rehydrates into slack rubber, and no amount of later reduction will crisp it again.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Reducing a sauce","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Poulet%20Basquaise%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Poulet Basquaise"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"jambon-de-bayonne-and-french-whole-muscle-hams","term":"Jambon de Bayonne & French Whole-Muscle Hams"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Duck","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Thyme","Bay leaf","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"axoa-de-veau-despelette","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg veal shoulder, trimmed and cut by knife into 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g duck fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g onions, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g piments doux verts, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried Espelette pod, crumbled, or 1 small green chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp piment d'Espelette"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml light veal or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the shoulder into dice with a knife, never a machine, and keep every piece about a centimetre so they cook at one rate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Colour the veal in the duck fat in a wide pan in two or three batches, gold on two faces only; if the pan hisses quietly instead of cracking, it is too crowded.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onions, diced peppers and garlic in the same fat until they are melting and sweet and the brown fond has dissolved off the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the meat with both Espelettes and stock barely to cover, then hold it covered at a lazy blip for about an hour and a quarter, until a dice crushes between finger and thumb with no spring left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Uncover and reduce until the sauce pools around the meat rather than running off it, and salt only now.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Shoulder is collagen rich, and collagen only unwinds into gelatine above about 70C (158F) held for an hour or more. That gelatine is what makes axoa glossy with no flour in it. Mince the meat instead of dicing it and you rupture the fibres, so the juices run out in the first minutes and the finished pan is grainy and dry. Keep the dice at a centimetre and the pot at a blip, never a rolling boil, which wrings the fibres tight.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Axoa%20de%20Veau%20d'Espelette%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Axoa de Veau d'Espelette"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Duck","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"ttoro-de-saint-jean-de-luz","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg hake and monkfish heads and bones, gills cut out, rinsed until the water runs clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 langoustines, heads on"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g hake, in 4 thick pieces on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g monkfish tail, in 4 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g mussels, scrubbed and debearded"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onions and 1 leek, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp piment d'Espelette"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 slices of baguette, toasted and rubbed with raw garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the heads and bones with cold water, bring them to a bare tremble, skim the grey scum until the surface runs clear, hold twenty minutes only, then strain without pressing the bones.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the langoustines hard in the olive oil until the shells turn brick and the pan smells toasted, lift them out, and sweat the onion, leek and garlic in that same fat until slack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes and wine and reduce until the pan smells of fruit rather than alcohol, stir in the Espelette, pour in the strained fumet and simmer ten minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the heat until the surface only shivers, around 85C (185F), and stage the fish: monkfish first, hake three minutes behind it, mussels and langoustines last.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"It is done when the hake parts into white curd along its flakes and the mussels have just gaped. Ladle over the garlic toasts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fish bones give up their gelatine in about twenty minutes; past that the heads leach bitterness and cloud the pot, so a long fish stock is a worse one. In the broth, fish protein sets near 50C (122F) and starts squeezing out moisture by 60C (140F), and a rolling boil batters the flakes apart before the centre is even warm. Hold it at a shiver and stage by density: monkfish is dense and slow, hake is fragile and fast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","aromatic","nutty-toasted","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ttoro%20de%20Saint-Jean-de-Luz%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ttoro de Saint-Jean-de-Luz"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"mussels-scallops-and-the-cold-water-winter","term":"Mussels, Scallops & the Cold-Water Winter"}],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Onion","Garlic","Leek","Tomato","Bread","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"magret-de-canard-a-la-poele-froide","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 magrets de canard of 350 to 400 g each, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"piment d'Espelette"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the fat in a 1 cm lattice, cutting down to the meat but not into it, salt the skin, leave it fifteen minutes and blot away the beads of moisture that rise.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the magrets skin down in a cold dry heavy pan and set it over low heat; listen for a whisper, not a sizzle, and press the breasts flat with a spatula for the first minute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon or pour the rendered fat into a bowl every few minutes, keeping it, and continue for 12 to 15 minutes until the skin is thin, amber and stiff and only a few millimetres of fat remain under it.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Raise the heat, turn the breasts and give the flesh two to three minutes, to 52C to 54C (126F to 129F) at the core for rosé.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest them skin up on a rack for eight minutes, then carve across the grain in 5 mm slices and finish with flaky salt and Espelette.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A magret carries a centimetre of fat under thick skin, and that fat has to melt from around 35C (95F) and then escape through the skin before the skin can dry out and brown. A hot pan browns and sets the outer skin first, trapping the fat so it steams the meat grey. Cold and slow renders in stages: melt, drain, dehydrate, brown. Pour the liquid fat off as it comes, or the breast begins to confit itself and the skin stays flabby.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Scoring","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Salting to draw the water out","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Magret%20de%20Canard%20%C3%A0%20la%20Po%C3%AAle%20Froide%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Magret de Canard à la Poêle Froide"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":[],"judgedBy":["scoring","salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"foie-gras-de-canard-poele-au-verjus","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices of raw duck foie gras, 2 cm thick, about 70 g each, cut from a chilled deveined lobe"},{"kind":"item","text":"fine salt and coarsely cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml verjus"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml Armagnac"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml duck or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices of brioche, toasted"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the slices with a knife dipped in hot water and wiped, then lay them on a cold plate and return them to the fridge until the pan is ready.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a dry heavy pan until a flicked drop of water skitters and vanishes, and season the slices only as they leave the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear 45 to 60 seconds a side without moving them, until a lacquered brown crust has formed and the slice yields under a fingertip but is still cool at its centre; lift onto warm plates.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off all but a spoonful of the fat, deglaze off the heat with the verjus and Armagnac, add the stock and reduce over heat to a light syrup, then spoon it over and serve on the brioche.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Foie gras is roughly half fat, held in cells that rupture from about 45C (113F); once they let go the slice pours into the pan and leaves you a wafer. The defence is contrast: liver straight from the fridge, a pan hot enough to brown in under a minute, and salt applied at the very last second, since salt draws moisture that cools the surface and stalls the crust. If the slice slumps and the fat floods, the pan was too cool.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Searing: the hard crust","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Foie%20Gras%20de%20Canard%20Po%C3%AAl%C3%A9%20au%20Verjus%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Foie Gras de Canard Poêlé au Verjus"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"foie-gras-torchon-mi-cuit-and-ethics-of-service","term":"Foie Gras: Torchon, Mi-Cuit & Ethics of Service"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Duck","Liver & Offal","Bread","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"gesiers-de-canard-confits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg duck gizzards, silverskin and gristle trimmed away"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 thyme sprigs and 2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg duck fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"walnut oil and bitter leaves, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the gizzards with the salt, pepper, garlic and herbs, cover and cure them in the fridge overnight, until they feel firm and faintly tacky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the cure off completely and dry the gizzards very well; any water left on them will spit in the fat and shorten their keeping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the fat, submerge the gizzards and hold the pot at 85C (185F), the surface trembling with only an occasional lazy bubble, for two and a half to three hours, until a skewer slides in with no resistance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool them in their fat, then pack into a jar under at least 2 cm of fat with nothing breaking the surface, and pour off the meat juices that settle to the bottom as jelly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"To serve, lift out, wipe, and crisp in a dry hot pan for two minutes a side until the edges brown, then slice over leaves dressed with walnut oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Confit was storage long before it was a texture. Salt pulls out free water and lowers water activity, the long bath at 85C (185F) pasteurises and drives off more, and the fat cap then locks out air. Two rules keep it keeping: no meat may break the surface of the fat, and the juices that settle into jelly at the bottom of the jar are water, not fat, so pour them off before you store. Cold and sealed they hold for months.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Confit","Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","nutty-toasted","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=G%C3%A9siers%20de%20Canard%20Confits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gésiers de Canard Confits"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Duck","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Thyme","Bay leaf","Nuts","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"garbure-bearnaise","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 ham hock of about 700 g, or a jambon de Bayonne bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 confit duck legs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g haricots tarbais, soaked overnight in cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g green cabbage, cored and cut in wide ribbons"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g potatoes, in 1 cm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g carrots and 200 g turnips, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, split and washed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion studded with 2 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bouquet of thyme, bay and parsley stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"piment d'Espelette"},{"kind":"item","text":"a glass of dry red wine, to finish at the table"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the hock and the drained beans with 3 litres of cold water, bring up slowly and skim the grey foam until the surface runs clear, then add the clove onion, garlic and bouquet and hold at a murmur for an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the carrots, turnips and leek and cook until a knife enters a turnip with just a little resistance left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potatoes and cabbage and cook on until the cabbage is dark and silky rather than squeaky, about 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Warm the confit legs, pull the meat into large shreds and stir them in for the last ten minutes so their fat glosses the broth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crush some of the potato against the side of the pot until a spoon stood upright in the middle stays standing, then season with Espelette and taste for salt only now.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve, and let each person pour red wine into the last inches of broth in the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Garbure is measured by the spoon: it should stand up in the bowl. That body comes from potato starch and long cooked cabbage, not flour, which is why the potatoes go in as thick slices and are crushed against the pot at the end. Salt right at the finish. A ham hock leaches salt into the broth for the whole cook, and a pot seasoned early will be inedible by the time it has reduced.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Confit","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","aromatic","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, aromatics blooming from the first bite, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Garbure%20B%C3%A9arnaise%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Garbure Béarnaise"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"confit","term":"Confit"},{"slug":"jambon-de-bayonne-and-french-whole-muscle-hams","term":"Jambon de Bayonne & French Whole-Muscle Hams"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Duck","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Leek","Potato","Carrot","Cabbage","Green beans","Radish/Turnip","Parsley","Thyme","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"talo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g fine white maize flour, neither coarse polenta nor nixtamalised masa"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"330 ml water at 80C (176F)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour and salt, pour the hot water in all at once and stir with a spoon until it gathers into a mass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead it in the bowl as soon as you can bear the heat, a minute or two, until it is smooth and takes a fingerprint without cracking at the rim; rest it covered for fifteen minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into six, roll each into a ball, and press between two sheets of baking paper into discs 2 mm thick and about 18 cm across, patching any split edge with a wet thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a dry iron griddle over medium high heat for one to two minutes a side, until pale gold freckles rise and the disc puffs in patches when you press the centre with a folded cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack them under a cloth as they come off so their own steam keeps them pliable.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Maize has no gluten, so nothing holds a talo together but gelatinised starch, and starch only gelatinises in hot water. That is why the water goes in near 80C (176F). Mixed cold, the dough is sandy, cracks at the rim and falls to pieces on the griddle. Work it while it is hot, keep the disc thin and even, and eat them within the hour: as they cool the starch retrogrades, and reheating never fully undoes the chalkiness.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Griddle & plancha work","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Talo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Talo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Corn","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"gateau-basque-a-la-cerise-noire-ditxassou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g ground almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g soft butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole egg and 1 yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml dark rum"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g thick black cherry jam from Itxassou"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 yolk beaten with 1 tsp water, to glaze"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cream the butter and sugar to a pale soft paste, beat in the egg, yolk, rum and salt, then fold in the flour, almonds and baking powder just until no dry flour shows.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scrape the dough into two flat discs between sheets of paper and chill at least two hours, until it is firm enough to pipe but not hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread or pipe half into a buttered 22 cm ring, taking it 1 cm up the sides, then spread the jam inside a clean 1.5 cm border of bare dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe the second half over in a tight spiral, smooth it, and seal the rim by pressing all round with a wet fingertip until you cannot see the join.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Glaze, chill 20 minutes, glaze again and score a crosshatch with a fork, then bake at 175C (350F) for 40 to 45 minutes, until it is deep chestnut brown and firm in the centre.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool completely in the tin before cutting, since the crumb sets only as it cools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a cake batter behaving as a pastry: nearly three quarters butter to flour and no added water, so almost no gluten develops and it can be piped but never rolled. Two faults spoil it. A loose jam boils, finds the seam and bursts out, so use a properly set jam and seal a bare border. And an underbaked gâteau is pasty at the middle; take the colour to deep chestnut, not gold.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":["Scoring","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Creaming butter & sugar","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=G%C3%A2teau%20Basque%20%C3%A0%20la%20Cerise%20Noire%20d'Itxassou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gâteau Basque à la Cerise Noire d'Itxassou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=The%20French%20Basque%20%26%20Gascony%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the The French Basque & Gascony kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Stone fruit","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["scoring","creaming-butter-and-sugar"]},{"slug":"lavash","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"320 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"flour for the bench"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix flour, water, salt and yeast to a shaggy mass, then knead about 8 minutes, until the dough pulls off the bench in one clean piece and a pinched edge stretches thin without tearing.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cover and leave at room temperature until it feels slack and no longer springs back when pressed, at least 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 100 g pieces, ball them, rest 20 minutes, and meanwhile heat a dry cast iron pan or steel plate over the highest flame until a flick of water skitters and vanishes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll one ball out to 1 mm, as wide as the pan, lifting and turning the sheet often; if it fights back and shrinks, set it aside and roll the next one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the sheet on the dry pan. It should blister within 20 seconds; turn once the blisters brown in patches and lift it off while still flexible, about 40 seconds in all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack the finished sheets under a damp cloth so their own steam keeps them soft enough to fold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tonir lavash bakes in under a minute slapped against a clay wall at 300C (570F), which is why the dough carries no fat at all: nothing tenderises it, so all the extensibility has to come from a slack dough and a long rest. Roll it before the gluten relaxes and it snaps back and tears. Sheets dried hard are revived by spritzing with water and stacking under a cloth for ten minutes.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lavash%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lavash"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"spas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg matsun or full-fat plain yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g coarse bulgur (dzavar)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, about 150 g, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander or parsley, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the bulgur in the water until the grains are tender and the water has gone cloudy with released starch, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the matsun with the egg and flour until completely smooth, with no lumps clinging to the whisk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Temper it: stir in several ladles of the hot bulgur liquid one at a time, then pour the loosened yoghurt back into the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on low heat, stirring constantly and scraping the base, until the soup thickens enough to coat the spoon and the surface only trembles. Do not let it boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion in the butter until golden at the edges, pull the pan off the heat, stir the dried mint into the hot butter and pour it over. Salt now, and scatter the herbs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yoghurt curdles because heat drives the casein together; the egg and flour beaten into the matsun coat those proteins and hold them apart. So warm it slowly, stir without stopping so the pan floor never runs ahead of the rest, and take it only to a tremble. Once it grains it will not come back. Salt at the end, since salt added cold speeds the same aggregation.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","rich","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Spas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Spas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Parsley","Mint","Butter","Yogurt","Flour","Couscous/Bulgur"]},{"slug":"pasuts-tolma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"16 large sour cabbage leaves, from a brined head"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g coarse bulgur (dzavar)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g short grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g cooked red kidney beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cooked chickpeas"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g cooked green lentils"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, about 300 g, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g red pepper paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the onions in the sunflower oil over medium heat until soft and pale gold, then stir in both pastes and keep going until the colour deepens to brick and the raw tomato smell has gone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, fold in the bulgur, rice, beans, chickpeas, lentils, mint, allspice and pepper. The grains stay raw. Season carefully: the leaves are already salty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shave the thick rib flat on each leaf, lay a heaped spoonful of filling along the base and roll tight, tucking the sides in, filling each leaf only two thirds full.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the rolls seam down in a heavy pot in tight layers and set an inverted plate on top to hold them under.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add water to just cover, bring to a bare simmer and cook covered until a roll cuts cleanly and the bulgur has swelled through, about 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let them cool completely in the pot before lifting them out, and serve cold with a thread of raw oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sour cabbage rolls because lactic fermentation has already softened the pectin in the leaf; fresh cabbage cracks at the rib unless blanched, and it makes a flat tolma, because with no meat and no fat here the acid is the seasoning. Fill with the grain still raw and the leaf only two thirds full: bulgur and rice keep swelling in the pot and a tight roll splits. Weight them or they unwind.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rolling dolmas","Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","warm-spiced","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasuts%20Tolma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pasuts Tolma"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Cabbage","Mint","Rice","Couscous/Bulgur","Lentils","Chickpeas","Kidney/Pinto beans","Tomato paste","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"basturma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg beef eye of round, trimmed of all silverskin"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g coarse non-iodised sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g ground fenugreek"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g garlic, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sweet paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g hot paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bury the beef in the coarse salt in a non-reactive tray, refrigerate and turn it daily until it is stiff to the thumb all through, 4 to 5 days.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rinse the salt away, soak 2 hours in cold water, pat dry, then press the meat flat between boards under a 3 kg weight in the fridge for 24 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":43200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hang it in a cool draught, around 12C (54F), until dark, firm and about 40 percent lighter than its trimmed weight, 10 to 14 days.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the ground fenugreek into the water and leave it overnight: it should swell into a tacky paste that holds a ridge. Beat in the garlic, both paprikas, cumin, allspice and fine salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Jacket the meat all over in a layer 3 mm thick and hang it again until the crust is dry to the knuckle and taps hard, about 1 week.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice paper thin across the grain with a long knife held at a slant.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chemen is not a dry rub but a hydrated gel: ground fenugreek at roughly one part to four of water swells overnight into a mucilaginous paste that shrinks along with the meat and seals it while it finishes drying. Mixed stiff and dry it cracks off in plates and the beef case hardens. The muscle must lose about 40 percent of its trimmed weight before the jacket goes on, or it stays wet inside the crust and sours.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Basturma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Basturma"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Cumin","Paprika","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"harisa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 chicken, about 1.6 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g korkot, hulled and cracked wheat"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"coarse black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the korkot and soak it in cold water for at least 2 hours, then drain.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the whole chicken in the salted water, skimming the grey foam, until a leg pulls free of the carcass, about 1 hour 15 minutes.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":4500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the bird out, strip every scrap of meat back into the broth and discard the skin and bones.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the drained wheat and cook uncovered on the lowest heat, stirring up from the base with a flat wooden spoon every few minutes, until the grains have burst and the pot is thick and glossy, 2 to 3 hours.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Now beat it: press and drag the mass against the side of the pot until the chicken has gone to threads and a spoonful lifted out falls back as a sheet rather than in lumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the butter until it smells of hazelnuts, ladle the harisa into bowls, pull a well in each and pour the butter in. Pepper at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is not the North African chilli paste that shares the name: there is no chilli in it anywhere. Hulled wheat gives up amylose over hours while the bird's collagen goes to gelatin, and the beating shears the muscle into threads that hang suspended in that gel. Starch settles, so keep the heat low and stir up from the base with a flat spoon; once it catches, the whole pot tastes scorched.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":3,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Harisa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Harisa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Butter"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"sini-manti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g minced lamb, about 20 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, about 150 g, grated and squeezed dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat parsley, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sweet paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre beef or chicken broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g matsun or thick yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed with salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"sumac, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the flour, egg, water and salt into a firm dough, knead until smooth and elastic, and rest it covered for 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the lamb with the onion, parsley, paprika, pepper and salt, and keep it cold while you roll.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough to 1 mm, cut 3 cm squares, drop a chickpea of filling on each and pinch two opposite corners together so each one stands open like a little boat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand the boats shoulder to shoulder in a buttered tray, brush with the melted butter and bake at 200C (400F) until the exposed tips are deep brown and the tray smells toasted, 20 to 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour boiling broth down the side of the tray until it reaches halfway up the manti, and return it to the oven until the liquid has gone and the tips are still crisp, 8 to 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat the garlic into the matsun, spoon it over at the table and dust with sumac.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The dry bake is the whole point: toasting the open boats browns and dextrinises the surface starch, which is where the nutty flavour comes from and what stops them slumping, and only then does liquid go in to soften their insides. Pour the broth boiling and only halfway up, or the tray collapses into porridge; too little and the bases stay hard. Pack them touching so the sides steam while the tips brown.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Rolling pin","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Salting to draw the water out","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sini%20Manti%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sini Manti"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Beef","Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Parsley","Butter","Yogurt","Flour","Stock/Broth","Paprika","Clove","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"khorovats","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg pork neck, in 5 cm cubes with the fat left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, about 450 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp sumac"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bunch flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"lavash, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate one onion and squeeze its juice over the pork, add the salt and pepper, turn it by hand and leave it somewhere cool for 2 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Burn hardwood or vine cuttings down until no flame is left and the coals are grey over an orange core: a hand held a palm above the grill should be uncomfortable within 3 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wipe every trace of onion off the cubes so it cannot burn, then thread them onto flat skewers with a rim of fat facing outward, a finger of space between pieces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over the embers, turning a quarter turn each time the fat begins to drip and hiss, until the crust is mahogany and the centre reads 70C (158F), about 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slice the remaining onions thin, toss them with the sumac and torn parsley, and slide the meat straight off the skewers onto them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover the bowl for 5 minutes, then serve from it, wrapped in lavash that has caught the juices.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"No acid goes near this: pork neck is tender already, and vinegar or wine only firms the surface protein, so it is salt, pepper and onion juice alone. The fat that makes the dish is also the risk, since drips flare and coat the meat in soot. Burn the wood down to grey embers and turn a quarter turn at the first drip. Resting the cubes covered over raw onion softens the crust enough to wrap.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","charred","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, char used as punctuation, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Khorovats%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Khorovats"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Parsley","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"ghapama","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 thick walled pumpkin, 2.5 to 3 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g medium grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g dried apricots, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g pitted prunes"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g walnuts, roughly broken"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut a lid from the pumpkin with the knife slanting inward so the lid will drop back into its own seat, then scrape out seeds and strings, leaving the wall at least 2 cm thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the rice in salted water for 5 minutes only, until the grains are still chalky at the centre, and drain well.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toss the rice with the fruit, walnuts, honey, cinnamon, salt and most of the butter, fill the pumpkin to two fingers below the rim and pour in the water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seat the lid, brush the outside with the last of the butter and bake at 180C (350F) until a skewer slides through the wall with no resistance and the skin has slumped and browned, 1 hour 30 minutes to 2 hours.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 15 minutes, then cut straight down through the whole pumpkin so every wedge carries wall, rice and fruit.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pumpkin is the pot and most of the water. Rice goes in half cooked because the wall gives up moisture as it bakes: raw rice either stays chalky or needs so much added water that the walls boil through and collapse. Fill to two fingers below the rim, since the grain keeps swelling. Use a dense, thick walled pumpkin, never a hollow carving one, and slant the lid cut so it cannot fall in.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","earthy","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ghapama%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ghapama"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Squash/Pumpkin","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Butter","Rice","Honey","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"gata","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g butter, soft, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g matsun or sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g plain flour, for the khoriz"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cold butter, diced, for the khoriz"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk, for the wash"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the khoriz flour, cold butter and sugar between your fingertips into a crumb that clumps when squeezed and falls apart again when nudged, then chill it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the soda into the matsun until it foams, then work it with the soft butter, egg, salt and flour into a dough. Stop the moment it comes together: it should feel short, not springy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the dough 30 minutes, roll it to a rectangle 5 mm thick, scatter the cold crumb over two thirds of it and fold in three like a letter.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn the parcel a quarter turn, roll or press it gently to a round about 2 cm thick and lift it onto a lined tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Score a lattice across the top with a fork, cutting right through to the filling, and brush with the yolk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (350F) until deep amber and the scored lines stand proud of the surface, 35 to 40 minutes. Cool before cutting or the filling runs.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Khoriz is a dry crumb of flour, butter and sugar, never a paste. Wet it with egg or milk and the sugar dissolves, the filling boils out of the seam and welds the gata to the tray. Keep it cold and the layer thin so it only half melts into the dough and sets grainy and sweet. Handle the dough as little as you can, and score right through: those cuts vent steam, so the top stays flat instead of doming and splitting.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Scoring","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gata%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gata"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["scoring","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"tan-matsun-and-ice-water","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g matsun or full-fat set yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml ice cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dried mint, optional"},{"kind":"item","text":"ice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the matsun on its own, with nothing added, until it is glossy and pours off the whisk in a smooth ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve the salt in a splash of the water, whisk that in, then add the rest of the water in two or three goes, whisking smooth between each.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the dried mint between your palms over the jug if you are using it, and chill until the jug beads, or pour straight over ice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk again just before pouring: tan separates on standing and should reach the glass cloudy and even.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Break the matsun before you dilute it. Whisked alone, its weak casein gel shears into a smooth sol; water poured onto set yoghurt instead surrounds the curd in fragments and they stay as flecks no matter how long you stir. Salt at about half a percent seasons it and helps the protein hold water. Serve it close to freezing, since the acid and the fat both read harsh at room temperature.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tan%20(Matsun%20and%20Ice%20Water)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tan (Matsun and Ice Water)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Armenian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Armenian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mint","Yogurt"]},{"slug":"saldskabmaize","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g wholegrain rye flour, for the scald"},{"kind":"item","text":"450 ml boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ground rye malt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caraway seeds, lightly crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g wholegrain rye flour, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g active rye sourdough starter at 100 percent hydration"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dark honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"up to 50 ml warm water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the boiling water into the 300 g of rye flour with the caraway to a thick paste, let it fall to 65C (150F), then stir in the rye malt; cover and hold it warm for 3 hours, until it smells of malt loaf and tastes distinctly sweet.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the scald to blood heat, beat in the starter, and leave it covered 8 to 12 hours until the surface is domed, bubbled and sharply sour.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the remaining flour, honey and salt with a wet wooden spoon to a dough like thick mortar, adding the extra water only if it will not come together; it should never look like wheat dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wet your hands and shape one high oval loaf on a lined tray, smoothing the surface with wet palms; prove 2 to 3 hours until the smooth top is covered in fine cracks.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 250C (480F) for 15 minutes, drop to 190C (375F) and give it 50 minutes more, until the base sounds hollow and the centre reads 97C (207F).","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brush the hot crust with water for gloss, cool it completely, then wait a further 12 hours before the first cut.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rye has almost no useful gluten; the crumb is held by pentosan gums and by acid. The scald wakes the grain's own amylase, which turns starch into maltose: that, not sugar, is where the sweetness comes from. The sour starter then drops the pH far enough to stop amylase before it digests the crumb to paste. Cut this loaf warm and it gums the knife, so give it a full day.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Sourdough starter"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","sweet-edged","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, a sweet edge rounding the corners, with a lingering ferment tang."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sourdough starter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Saldsk%C4%81bmaize%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Saldskābmaize"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour","Honey","Caraway"]},{"slug":"cepelinai-su-spirguciais","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg floury potatoes, peeled, for grating"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g floury potatoes, peeled, for boiling"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g coarsely minced pork shoulder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp dried marjoram"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt, plus a heavy hand for the pot"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp potato starch, if needed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked streaky bacon, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, finely chopped, for the spirgučiai"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dill, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the 300 g of potatoes until a knife slides out clean, drain them very dry over the heat, rice them and leave to cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the raw kilo on the finest drum, wring it hard in a cloth over a bowl, then let the liquid stand 20 minutes; pour off the grey water and scrape the chalky white starch from the bottom back into the pulp.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix pulp, riced potato and salt to a dough that holds a fingerprint, adding the starch only if it sags; work the pork with grated onion, marjoram and pepper and roll it into 8 ovals.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With wet hands flatten a palmful of dough, seat a meat oval in it and close it into a zeppelin, then roll it between your palms to find any seam or thin patch before it reaches the water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide them into heavily salted water held at a bare tremble around 90C (195F), never a rolling boil, and poach 25 to 30 minutes until they float high and feel springy rather than soft.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Render the bacon slowly until the fat runs and the pieces crisp, soften the onion in it, then spoon it hot over the drained dumplings with cold sour cream and dill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The ratio is the dish: roughly three parts pressed raw potato to one part boiled and riced. Raw potato carries the flavour and ungelatinised starch, while the cooked potato carries gelatinised starch, which is the only real glue. Pour away the starch that settles out of the pressed liquid and they dissolve in the pot. Let the water boil rather than tremble and they split along the seam.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cepelinai%20su%20Spirgu%C4%8Diais%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cepelinai su Spirgučiais"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Bacon","Onion","Potato","Dill","Oregano","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"saltibarsciai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g beetroot, boiled, peeled and coarsely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 ml cold kefir"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml cold water or beet pickling liquid"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber, about 200 g, diced small"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 spring onions, thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g dill, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g small potatoes, to serve hot"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the eggs 9 minutes, cool them in iced water and peel; boil the potatoes separately in salted water and keep them hot.","durationSec":540,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":540,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the kefir smooth with the cold water and salt, then fold in the grated beetroot; the colour should turn instantly to a flat shocking pink.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in cucumber, spring onion and most of the dill, taste for salt and acid, then chill at least 2 hours until it is properly cold rather than merely cool.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle it out, halve the eggs onto the surface, scatter the rest of the dill and send the hot potatoes to the table in their own dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Betanin, the beet pigment, is the whole point and it is fragile: it browns above about 60C (140F) and dulls in anything alkaline. So nothing here is ever warmed, and the acid in kefir holds the colour bright. The hot potatoes stay in a separate dish for the same reason; drop them in and the soup goes muddy brown while the kefir curdles into grains.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","herb-fresh","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, melting away silken."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=%C5%A0altibar%C5%A1%C4%8Diai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Šaltibarščiai"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Potato","Beets","Cucumber","Dill"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"kugelis","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg floury potatoes, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g smoked streaky bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g sour cream, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon in a heavy pan until crisp, add the onion and cook to deep gold, and keep it all hot; scald the milk in a small pan alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the potatoes on the finest drum straight into a warm bowl and immediately pour over the boiling milk and the hot bacon fat with its onion, stirring hard; the mass should thicken slightly and stay pale.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs, salt and pepper, then pour into a buttered dish so the mixture stands about 4 cm deep.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (390F) for 20 minutes, drop to 175C (350F) and give it 70 minutes more, until the top is deep mahogany and a skewer comes out clean and dry.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 20 minutes before cutting into squares, and serve with cold sour cream against the hot crust.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Opposite rule to cepelinai: here the potato liquid stays in. Boiling milk and hot bacon fat poured straight onto the gratings gelatinise the surface starch, which binds the loaf, and the heat kills the enzyme that turns raw potato slate grey within minutes. Grate into a cold bowl and dawdle and you get a grey kugelis weeping water in the tin. Rest it before cutting or it slumps.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kugelis%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kugelis"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Onion","Potato","Milk","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"mulgipuder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g pearl barley"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg floury potatoes, peeled and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked pork belly or streaky bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarsely ground black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the barley in cold water overnight and drain; this is not optional, it is the difference between creamy and crunchy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the barley in the salted water, covered, for about 45 minutes, until the grains have burst open and the liquid has gone slippery.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sit the potatoes on top, adding water only if the pan looks dry, and cook covered until a knife passes through them with no resistance at all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain any free liquid into a jug, add the butter and mash coarsely with a wooden masher, loosening with the reserved liquid until it holds a soft ridge; stop while you can still see whole barley grains.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the pork until the fat runs and the edges crisp, add the onion and take it to deep gold, then pour it fat and all over the puree and grind on the pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Barley is what makes this Estonian rather than a plate of mash. Its starch is amylose rich and stays inside the burst grain, so it bulks the puree without gluing it. Potato starch does the opposite: take a blender to it, or beat it too long, and the cells rupture, free starch floods out and the pot turns to elastic wallpaper paste. A wooden masher, and few strokes.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Keeping the cooking liquid","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mulgipuder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mulgipuder"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Onion","Potato","Butter","Flour","Couscous/Bulgur"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"rosolje","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g beetroot, boiled, peeled and diced 8 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g waxy potatoes, boiled, peeled and diced 8 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g salted herring fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 pickled cucumbers, diced 8 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tart apple, peeled and diced 8 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a small red onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs, hard boiled"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g strong mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the herring in cold milk or water for 2 hours if it is heavily salted, then drain, dry and dice it; taste a piece first, it should read savoury rather than harsh.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the beetroot and potatoes separately and cool them completely, because warm potato drinks the dressing and turns pasty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the sour cream with mustard, vinegar, sugar and pepper, then stir in a spoonful of the diced beetroot so the dressing itself goes pink.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold everything together gently, keeping two eggs back and the dice intact, then chill 4 hours so the colour bleeds evenly through.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the reserved eggs over the top just before it goes to the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salted herring is the seasoning here, so almost no salt goes into the dressing; taste the fish first and soak it if it bites. Everything must be cold before it is dressed, because warm potato starch absorbs sour cream and collapses into paste. Cut every element to the same 8 mm so one spoon carries beet, potato, fish, pickle and apple at once, which is the balance of the dish.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","rich","bright","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rosolje%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rosolje"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Potato","Beets","Cucumber","Apple","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"marineeritud-korvits","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg firm pumpkin flesh, peeled and cut in 2 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml white wine vinegar at 5 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fresh ginger, sliced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring water, vinegar, sugar, salt and all the spices to a boil, then simmer 5 minutes until clove and cinnamon are clearly readable in the syrup.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slide in the pumpkin and hold it at a bare simmer for 3 to 6 minutes, depending on the variety, until the outer few millimetres go glassy and a knife tip still meets resistance at the centre.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":360,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the cubes into clean warm jars with a slotted spoon, packing them loosely, then pour the boiling syrup and its spices over to cover.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal, cool to room temperature and leave at least 3 days before opening; the cubes should be translucent right through and still snap under a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"The syrup cooks the pumpkin twice, once in the pan and again as the sealed jar cools, so pull it while the centre still resists or it arrives as jam. Sugar and acid together draw water out and firm the pectin, which is why the cubes shrink and turn glassy rather than soft. Clove and cinnamon instead of dill is what separates this from every other pickle on the coast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Marineeritud%20K%C3%B5rvits%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Marineeritud Kõrvits"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Squash/Pumpkin","Berries","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"sklandrausis","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g wholegrain rye flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml very hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g floury potatoes, boiled and riced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sour cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp caraway seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g carrots, boiled soft and pureed"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g honey, plus a little to glaze"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml double cream, plus 2 tbsp to glaze"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the melted butter into the hot water, pour it onto the rye flour with the salt and work fast to a warm, plastic dough; cover and rest it 30 minutes while it is still warm.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pinch off eight pieces, press each into a disc on the bench and draw a 2 cm wall up from the rim with your fingers to make a shallow open case, then prick the bases.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the riced potato with sour cream, yolk, caraway and salt to a stiff paste and spread it 1 cm deep in each case.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the carrot puree with honey, whole egg and cream until smooth and just pourable, then spoon it over the potato to sit just below the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (390F) for 25 to 30 minutes, until the carrot has set with no wobble left and the rye wall is dry and dark at the edge.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brush the hot tops with the cream loosened with honey and eat them warm or cold, the same day.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rye builds no gluten net, so the case is shaped by starch rather than stretch: near boiling water gelatinises enough of it to make the dough plastic under your fingers, and melted butter keeps it short. Mix rye with cold water and the wall cracks as you pinch it up, then leaks carrot custard across the tray. Work the dough warm and shape it the moment it has rested.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","sweet-edged","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sklandrausis%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sklandrausis"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Potato","Carrot","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Honey","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"rupjmaizes-kartojums","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g dense dark rye bread, at least a day old"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml double cream, very cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g cranberry or lingonberry preserve"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp dark rum, optional"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blitz the rye to coarse crumbs the size of grit, not to powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast them dry in a wide pan over medium heat, then add the caster sugar and cinnamon and keep everything moving until the sugar has melted, caught the crumbs and they rattle loose in the pan instead of clumping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip onto a cold tray, break up any lumps and cool completely, because a warm crumb will steam the cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the cream with the icing sugar to soft peaks that only just hold, and slacken the preserve with the rum.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer crumb, preserve and cream twice over in glasses or a bowl, finish with cream and a scatter of crumb, then chill 3 hours.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sugar toasted into the crumbs melts and sets again as a thin shell, waterproofing them against the cream; that shell is why the layers still read separately the next day. Skip the toasting, or build while the crumbs are warm, and within an hour the whole thing is one brown paste. Whip only to soft peaks, since the crumbs go on stiffening it as it sits.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rupjmaizes%20K%C4%81rtojums%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rupjmaizes Kārtojums"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Cream","Bread","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"kama-kefiiriga","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"80 g rye flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g barley flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g fine oat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g pea flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g cold kefir"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"a pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g blackcurrants, lingonberries or blueberries"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast each flour separately in a dry pan over medium heat, stirring without pause, until it is one shade darker and smells of biscuit rather than raw grain; the pea flour takes longest and turns bitter fastest.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the flours completely, then whisk them together; this is your kama and it keeps in a sealed jar for months.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rain 100 g of the kama over the kefir in a thin stream while whisking, add the honey and salt, and stop the moment it is smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave it 20 minutes for the flour to hydrate, so mix it looser than you want to eat it; it should end up a thick pourable cream.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into bowls and spoon the berries over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kama is already cooked, so nothing here needs heat, only time. The roasted flour hydrates from the outside in and skins over instantly, which is why tipping it into kefir gives lumps with dry hearts: rain it in, whisk, then leave it alone. It keeps drinking liquid for a quarter of an hour, so a mixture that looks right straight away will be stiff by the time it reaches the table.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kama%20Kefiiriga%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kama Kefiiriga"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Baltic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Baltic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Berries","Flour","Couscous/Bulgur","Honey"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"pino-de-carne-a-cuchillo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g beef rump or chuck, cut by knife into 8 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g white onion, cut into 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ají de color (Chilean sweet paprika)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp merkén"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the beef by hand into 8 mm dice and season it with the salt and pepper; minced beef crushes the fibre and weeps grey water instead of browning.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear it in the hot oil in two batches until the outsides are brown and the centres still pink, then lift it out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the onion into the same fat over low heat and stir until it has collapsed to half its volume, gone glassy and lost every white core, around 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the cumin, ají de color, oregano and merkén for one minute, until the fat runs orange and smells toasted rather than dusty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter over the flour, return the beef with the stock and simmer until a spoon dragged across the base leaves a track that fills back slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread it on a tray and refrigerate overnight; it is used only when cold and set.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pino is beef and onion in roughly equal weight, and the onion is the liquid, not the stock, so it has to collapse completely or the filling stays wet and sharp. Flour plus that liquid sets to a soft gel once cold, which is why pino is always made the day before. Used warm it steams, tears the dough and runs out in the oven.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","fiery","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, real fire that builds and stays, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pino%20de%20Carne%20a%20Cuchillo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pino de Carne a Cuchillo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Chiles","Oregano","Flour","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"pastel-de-choclo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg fresh corn kernels, cut from about 8 ears"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 fresh basil leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g cold pino de carne"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 chicken thighs, poached and boned"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 hard boiled eggs, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 black olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp caster sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mill or pulse the corn with the basil to a coarse porridge that still feels grainy between the fingers, never a smooth cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the onion in the butter, add the corn, milk and salt, and stir over medium heat until it holds the mark of the spoon and smells of cooked corn rather than green sap, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide the cold pino between clay dishes or one baking dish and lay the chicken, egg, olives and raisins over it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the corn on in a level 3 cm layer and bake at 200 C (392 F) until the edges bubble and the surface has firmed to a skin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust with the sugar and slide under a hot grill until it blisters dark brown in patches, then rest 10 minutes before serving.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"The topping is a starch paste and it has to be cooked before it goes on: raw milled corn weeps water down into the pino and tastes of green sap. Cook it until the spoon leaves a track. The sugar is not sweetening, it is a caramel crust, and sprinkled on a raw wet surface it simply dissolves and scorches, so it goes on only after the top has set in the oven.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastel%20de%20Choclo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pastel de Choclo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Eggs","Onion","Corn","Olives","Dried fruit","Basil","Butter","Milk","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"porotos-granados","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g fresh cranberry beans, shelled"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g winter squash, peeled and cut in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g fresh corn kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ají de color"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 fresh basil leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the beans in the water with a little salt until the skins slip and the centres crush to a floury paste, 30 to 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2400,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Meanwhile soften the onion and garlic in the oil, pull the pan off the heat and stir in the ají de color and cumin so the paprika colours the oil without scorching, then tip it all into the beans.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the squash and cook until the cubes slump at the edges and the broth turns opaque and orange.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the corn and give it 8 minutes, just until the kernels lose their raw milky taste.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off the heat, tear in the basil and season; the broth should coat the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no flour in this: the squash is the thickener, so it goes in only once the beans are soft and is cooked until it falls apart. Corn goes in last because its starch settles and catches on the base over a long simmer, turning the pot gluey and scorched. The basil is added off the heat; boiled, it blackens and turns bitter and the dish loses the smell that identifies it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Porotos%20Granados%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Porotos Granados"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"squash-summers-water-winters-starch","term":"Squash: Summer's Water, Winter's Starch"}],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Squash/Pumpkin","Corn","Dried fruit","Basil","Parmesan","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"pulmay-curanto-en-olla","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg mussels in the shell, scrubbed"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g clams, purged in salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 chicken thighs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pork ribs, cut into single bones"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 longaniza sausages"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g small potatoes, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 outer cabbage leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp merkén"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bed the base of a heavy pot with the onion and garlic, lay the ribs, chicken and sausage on top and season with pepper and merkén.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the wine and no water at all, cover, and steam over medium heat until the pork loosens from the bone, about 45 minutes.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pile the potatoes over the meat, then the mussels and clams on top of those, and cap the whole pot with the cabbage leaves to trap the steam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and cook until every shell has opened and a knife slides into the potatoes, around 15 minutes; discard any shell still shut.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve in bowls with a ladle of the liquor from the bottom of the pot poured over each one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"In the pit version, hot stones and nalca leaves do this work. In a pot the shellfish are the water supply: they sit on top so they open in the steam and drip their liquor down through the potatoes into the meat. Add water and you dilute that to nothing and boil the shellfish tough. Layer strictly by cooking time, meat nearest the heat, shells last, and keep the lid down.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","aromatic","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pulmay%20(Curanto%20en%20Olla)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pulmay (Curanto en Olla)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"bivalves-oysters-clams-mussels-scallops","term":"Bivalves (Oysters, Clams, Mussels, Scallops)"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Sausage","Mussels/Clams","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Cabbage","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"caldillo-de-congrio","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg conger eel, cut into 4 thick steaks, head and bones kept"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion halved, plus 2 onions sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g potatoes, cut in 1 cm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ají de color"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of coriander leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the head and bones with the halved onion, carrot, bay, wine and water for 25 minutes, skimming the grey foam, then strain; the broth should be clear and faintly sticky on the lips.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the sliced onion and pepper in the oil, stir in the ají de color and the tomato and cook until the oil separates out red at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potato slices and the strained broth and simmer until they are just yielding but not breaking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide in the fish steaks, drop the heat until the surface only shivers at about 80 C (176 F), and poach 8 minutes, until the flesh parts cleanly from the central bone.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir the cream into the broth at the side of the pot rather than over the fish, season, and scatter the coriander.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Congrio is a collagen fish: head and bones give a broth that sets in the fridge, which is why they are never thrown out. The flesh is the opposite, lean and fine grained, and a rolling boil shreds it to cotton and clouds the broth within a minute. Poach at a shiver. The cream goes in at the end and off the boil, or the tomato acid splits it into grains.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caldillo%20de%20Congrio%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Caldillo de Congrio"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Bell pepper","Cilantro","Bay leaf","Cream","Wine"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"machas-a-la-parmesana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"24 razor clams or surf clams, on the half shell"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g semi hard cow's milk cheese, coarsely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Open the clams, leave each one on its flat half shell, and flatten the foot with the back of a knife so it sits level.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the grill to its highest setting with a shelf close under the element, and let it come fully hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Arrange the shells on a tray, dot each with butter and a trace of garlic, spoon in a little wine and grind on pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover them with the cheese and grill 4 to 6 minutes, until the cheese blisters and bubbles at the rim while the clam beneath is still plump.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve straight from the tray with lemon; a plate left standing goes rubbery in its own shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Machas are a lean swimming muscle that seizes within a minute of overcooking, so the heat must come from above, hot and brief: the cheese has to brown before the flesh climbs past about 65 C (149 F). A moderate oven takes fifteen minutes and hands back erasers. Frozen machas must be thawed and blotted dry, or the water they shed boils them and thins the butter to a puddle.","equipment":["Grill","Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny","bright","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Machas%20a%20la%20Parmesana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Machas a la Parmesana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Mussels/Clams","Garlic","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Parmesan","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"pebre-chileno","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 large bunch coriander, leaves and fine stems, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 white onion, in 3 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green ají or green chillies, deseeded and minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp merkén"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed to a paste with salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the diced onion in cold salted water for 10 minutes, then drain and squeeze it dry in a cloth.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the coriander with a sharp knife in a few decisive strokes, never in a blender.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine the onion, coriander, chilli, garlic and merkén, then beat in the oil, vinegar and water until the sauce falls loosely from a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt it and leave it 20 minutes, until the merkén has swollen and stained the oil brick red; before that it tastes of nothing.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Merkén is ají cacho de cabra smoked over hardwood then ground with toasted coriander seed and salt: smoke, real heat and a seed spice, where paprika is sweet and unsmoked. It needs oil and twenty minutes to bloom. Soaking the onion leaches the sulphur compounds that would otherwise shout over everything, and knife chopping keeps pebre a relish; blended, the coriander oxidises grey and bitter within the hour.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","bright","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pebre%20Chileno%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pebre Chileno"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"sopaipillas-de-zapallo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g winter squash, peeled and cut in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"about 60 ml of the hot squash cooking water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres sunflower oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the squash until a knife slides through without resistance, drain it but keep the water, and mash it smooth while it is still steaming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the melted butter and salt into the hot puree, work in the flour, and add hot cooking water only until a soft, faintly tacky dough comes together; rest it 20 minutes under a cloth.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 4 mm, cut 8 cm discs and prick each one once through the centre with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry a few at a time at 180 C (356 F), turning once, until they balloon and colour deep gold, about 90 seconds a side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain them standing on a rack rather than flat on paper, so the trapped steam escapes instead of softening the underside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The puree must be hot when the flour meets it: it gelatinises the squash starch and lets the flour take up more liquid, which is what keeps a sopaipilla pliable rather than biscuit hard. There is no raising agent, so the puff is pure steam and the oil has to hold 180 C (356 F). Under about 165 C (329 F) they never inflate, they drink fat and come out pale. The centre prick stops them doming into a ball.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sopaipillas%20de%20Zapallo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sopaipillas de Zapallo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"squash-summers-water-winters-starch","term":"Squash: Summer's Water, Winter's Starch"}],"pantryItems":["Squash/Pumpkin","Butter","Flour"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"mote-con-huesillo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"12 dried peaches (huesillos)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water for soaking"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g chancaca or unrefined cane sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip of orange peel"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g husked wheat (mote)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water for the wheat"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the dried peaches overnight in the 1.5 litres of water and keep that water; it is the body of the syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer them in their soaking water with the chancaca, cinnamon and orange peel at a bare bubble until they are plump and yielding and the liquid has darkened to the colour of weak tea, about 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":2400,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cook the wheat in a separate pot of fresh water until each grain has swollen and is chewy but not chalky at the centre, then drain and rinse under cold water until it runs clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill both separately, then spoon wheat into the base of each glass and pour the cold syrup and two peaches over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two pots, always. Wheat cooked in the syrup sheds starch into it, turning the drink cloudy and gluey, and it goes on drinking the sugar until the syrup tastes of nothing; rinsing it cold strips the loose starch off the outside of each grain. Chancaca rather than white sugar gives the smoky mineral edge. Skip the overnight soak and the peach dissolves outside before its middle softens.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a warm current of chile."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mote%20con%20Huesillo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mote con Huesillo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Orange","Stone fruit","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"kuchen-de-frambuesa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cold butter, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pinch salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"450 g raspberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g sugar for the custard"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g flour for the streusel"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cold butter for the streusel"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g demerara sugar for the streusel"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the cold butter into the flour, caster sugar and salt to coarse crumbs, bind with the yolk and a spoon of cold water, press into a 24 cm tin and chill 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blind bake at 180 C (356 F) until the base is dry, sandy and pale gold, about 20 minutes; a soft shell will not survive the fruit.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the streusel flour, butter, demerara and cinnamon together with cold fingertips to pea sized lumps and keep them in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the raspberries with the semolina and tip them into the shell, then whisk the egg, cream and custard sugar and pour it around them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the streusel over and bake at 180 C (356 F) until the custard is set with only a slight wobble at the centre and the crumbs are gold, 30 to 35 minutes.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false}],"note":"Raspberries are mostly water and they let it go exactly when the pastry is at its most absorbent. Two defences: blind bake so the base starch is set and dry before the fruit lands, and toss the berries in semolina, which gelatinises in the escaping juice and holds it as a gel instead of a leak. Streusel butter must stay cold; warm, it melts into a sheet rather than crumbs.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Pie crust & blind baking","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Rubbing fat into flour","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","warm-spiced","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kuchen%20de%20Frambuesa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kuchen de Frambuesa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Berries","Butter","Cream","Flour","Semolina","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"arepa-de-choclo-con-cuajada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g fresh yellow corn kernels, from about 5 ears"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g masarepa, precooked yellow corn flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter, soft, plus more for the griddle"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g panela, finely grated, or dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g cuajada or other fresh curd cheese, in thick slabs"}],"steps":[{"text":"Strip the kernels and blitz them with the salt and panela to a coarse, spoonable batter; stop while you can still feel whole grains between finger and thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the masarepa and the soft butter, then leave it 10 minutes; it should mound on the spoon and settle slowly rather than run off it.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter a heavy pan over medium low heat and spread a heaped ladle to a disc about 12 cm across; the top loses its shine and the underside freckles dark gold in around 6 minutes.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn once and cook until the second side is spotted and the centre springs back under a fingertip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold each hot cake around a slab of cuajada so the cheese slumps and softens without running out of the seam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fresh choclo is mostly water and sugar, so this is a batter, not a dough. It sets by starch swelling while those sugars brown, and the spoon of masarepa is there only to stop it spreading. Too hot a griddle scorches the sugars before the middle sets, which is why the pan stays at medium low. This is also the line against Venezuela: there an arepa is a thick masarepa dough cooked hard, split and stuffed. Here it is a soft sweet cake, folded around the cheese.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Arepas","Sugar stages & caramel","Griddle & plancha work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Arepas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20arepas%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arepa%20de%20Choclo%20con%20Cuajada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arepa de Choclo con Cuajada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Corn","Butter","Cheese (any)","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["griddle-and-plancha-work","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"changua","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cebolla larga (spring onions), sliced thin, white and green kept apart"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cilantro, leaves and fine stems, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 thick slices of stale white bread, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g cuajada or fresh curd cheese, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the water, milk and the white of the spring onion to the point where the surface shivers and a ring of small bubbles clings to the edge of the pan; do not let it roll.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the eggs in one at a time, spaced apart, and leave them untouched until the whites are opaque and the yolks still wobble under the back of a spoon, about 3 minutes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift each egg into a bowl already holding torn bread and crumbled cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt the broth off the heat, stir in the cilantro and the green of the onion, and pour it over so the bread softens but keeps its edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Milk climbs the pan and skins because its proteins set at the surface as water leaves. The third of water here raises the volume it takes to boil over and keeps the broth thin enough to drink. Hold it at a shiver, near 85 C (185 F): a rolling boil scorches milk sugars on the pan floor and hardens the yolks in the same minute. The bread goes in the bowl, never in the pot, or it dissolves to paste.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, melting away silken."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Changua%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Changua"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Cilantro","Milk","Cheese (any)","Bread"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"sancocho-de-gallina","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.6 kg stewing hen, jointed, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cebolla larga (spring onions), bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml achiote oil, or a good pinch of ground annatto"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 green plantains, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g yuca (cassava), peeled, woody core pulled out"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g papa sabanera or other waxy potato, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ears of corn, in thick rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 leaves cilantro cimarrón (culantro), or a large handful of cilantro stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cilantro, chopped, to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 limes, in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the hen with the water and bring it up slowly, skimming the grey foam until the surface runs clear, then add the onions, garlic, cumin, achiote and culantro.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold at a bare simmer, one bubble breaking every second or so, until a skewer enters the thigh with only slight resistance; an old hen takes close to two hours and will not be hurried.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Snap the green plantains into thumb lengths with your hands rather than cutting them, drop them in and give them 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the yuca, then 10 minutes later the corn and the potato, and cook until the potato yields to a knife and the yuca is soft but still in recognisable pieces.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Season, cut the heat, scatter the chopped cilantro over and serve with lime and white rice alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Three starches on three clocks, and the broth is thickened by what they shed. Torn plantain sheds most, which is why it goes in by hand and not by knife. Yuca has a narrow window, firm one minute and ropey mush the next, so it follows rather than leads. Use a waxy potato that holds its shape; papa criolla would dissolve completely, which is exactly what you want in a Bogotá soup and exactly what you do not want here.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":36,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sancocho%20de%20Gallina%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sancocho de Gallina"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Potato","Corn","Plantain","Lime","Cilantro","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"sobrebarriga-a-la-criolla","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 kg sobrebarriga (beef flank or navel end), in one piece with the fat cap on"},{"kind":"item","text":"330 ml pale lager"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cebolla larga (spring onions), chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g ripe tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fine dry breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the meat and brown the fat cap hard in a dry heavy pan until it is mahogany and the pan is loud, then turn it once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the beer, onion, garlic, carrot, bay and cumin, top up with water to just cover, and hold below a simmer with the surface barely trembling until a skewer slides in and out without lifting the meat, close to three hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let it cool in its own liquid, then lift it out and blot the surface completely dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile cook the tomato and spring onion in the oil over low heat until the tomato has collapsed and a slick of red oil pools at the rim of the pan, then season it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread that sauce over the fat cap, dust with breadcrumbs and grill close to the element until the top blisters and darkens in patches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sobrebarriga is a sheet of muscle wrapped in collagen and a tough elastin membrane, and only a long wet hold near 85 C (185 F) turns that collagen to gelatin. Let it boil and the fibres contract and wring themselves dry, so the meat shreds instead of slicing. Cooling in the liquid draws moisture back in; the surface must then be blotted bone dry or the grill steams the sauce grey rather than blistering it.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","charred","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, char used as punctuation, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sobrebarriga%20a%20la%20Criolla%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sobrebarriga a la Criolla"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Carrot","Bay leaf","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"empanadas-vallunas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g masarepa amarilla, precooked yellow corn flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"520 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml achiote oil, plus a little more if the dough looks pale"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g pork shoulder, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g waxy yellow potatoes, peeled and cut small"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cebolla larga (spring onions), chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g ripe tomatoes, peeled and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 L neutral oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the pork in salted water until it pulls apart under a fork, then shred it and keep 200 ml of the cooking liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the spring onion and tomato in the oil over low heat until the tomato is a jammy red mass with oil separating at the rim, then fold in the potato, the pork and enough liquid to keep it loose; crush it roughly with a spoon and cool it completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the masarepa, salt and achiote oil into the hot water and rest the dough 10 minutes; it should feel like soft putty, and a pinched edge should smooth over instead of cracking.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll walnut sized balls flat between two sheets of plastic to about 2 mm, spoon on the cold filling, fold and press the seam shut with a wet finger, chasing out every pocket of air.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 180 C (355 F) in two batches until the shell is deep gold and covered in fine blisters, about 4 minutes, and drain them standing on end.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Precooked corn flour has no gluten, so the shell is held together by hydrated starch alone; it must rest until the flour has finished drinking or it splits at the fold. Roll it between plastic, never on a floured board. The filling goes in cold and the air goes out, because trapped steam bursts the seam in the fryer. Hold the oil at 180 C (355 F): cooler and the corn drinks fat, hotter and the shell colours before it has set.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Empanada folding","Deep frying","Salted water & the float test","Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Empanada folding","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=empanada%20repulgue%20folding%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Empanadas%20Vallunas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Empanadas Vallunas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Scallion","Tomato","Potato","Corn","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"aborrajados-de-platano-maduro","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 very ripe plantains, skins fully black, about 750 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fresh melting cheese, in fingers"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g bocadillo (guava paste), in thin slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 L neutral oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the plantains and cut them into 3 cm lengths, then fry at 160 C (320 F) until they are the colour of honey and give easily under the back of a spoon, about 4 minutes.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press each piece flat between two boards to roughly 1 cm; it should spread without splitting at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sandwich a finger of cheese and a slice of bocadillo between two flattened pieces and pinch the edges closed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, egg, milk, sugar, baking powder and salt to the thickness of double cream; it should coat a spoon and fall from it in a slow ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the oil to 180 C (355 F), dip each parcel and fry until the batter is set and pale gold, about 2 minutes, so the cheese is soft but still inside.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Ripeness is the whole recipe. At black skin the plantain's starch has turned to sugar, which is why it mashes flat instead of cracking and why it browns in minutes. So the first fry is gentle, at 160 C (320 F), only to soften it, and the second is hot and short, to set the batter before those sugars burn. An under ripe plantain will never flatten, and no amount of extra time in the oil will fix it.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aborrajados%20de%20Pl%C3%A1tano%20Maduro%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Aborrajados de Plátano Maduro"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Plantain","Milk","Cheese (any)","Flour","Sugar"]},{"slug":"papas-chorreadas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900 g small waxy potatoes, scrubbed, skins on"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cebolla larga (spring onions), sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe tomatoes, peeled, seeded and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml single cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fresh melting cheese, coarsely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the potatoes in well salted water until a knife meets no resistance at the centre, then drain them and hold them warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the spring onion in the butter over low heat until it is limp and sweet with no raw squeak left in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomato and cumin and cook until the pieces have slumped and the pan no longer runs watery, then pour in the cream and warm it through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat, stir in the cheese in three handfuls until it hangs in soft threads, and pour it over the potatoes at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fresh curd cheese has had none of the ripening that lets an aged cheese melt smoothly, so it keeps its shape and then collapses all at once; above roughly 70 C (160 F) it squeezes out its fat and turns rubbery in a greasy pool. Melt it off the heat, in handfuls, and let residual warmth do the work. Waxy potatoes only, since a floury one drinks the sauce and falls to mash in the bowl.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, melting away silken."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Papas%20Chorreadas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Papas Chorreadas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Scallion","Tomato","Potato","Butter","Cream","Cheese (any)","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"arroz-con-coco-titote","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thick first press coconut milk, with no gums or stabilisers"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml thin second press coconut milk, or water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g long grain white rice, rinsed until the water runs clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"35 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the thick coconut milk in a wide heavy pan over medium heat, stirring, until it breaks: the water goes, clear oil pools out and pale solids are left frying in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep going, stirring constantly now, until those solids are the colour of dark chestnut and smell toasted; they turn from brown to bitter in under a minute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the thin milk, sugar, salt and raisins all at once to stop the browning, and bring it back to a boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the rice and boil uncovered until the liquid has dropped level with the grains and craters open across the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover, drop to the lowest heat for 20 minutes until the grains stand separate and a crust has set on the base, then fork it through with the crust broken up.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"Coconut milk is an emulsion, and here you break it deliberately: hold it at a boil until the water leaves, the oil renders out and the milk solids fry in that oil and caramelise. Those dark solids, the titoté, are the entire colour and flavour of the dish. Stop at pale gold and you have oily white rice; go past dark chestnut and the whole pot is bitter. Canned milk thickened with gums will not break, so read the tin.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arroz%20con%20Coco%20Titot%C3%A9%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arroz con Coco Titoté"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Dried fruit","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Sugar"]},{"slug":"aji-picante-casero","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 cebolla larga (spring onions), white and pale green only, cut very fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cilantro, leaves and fine stems, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 small hot green chillies, seeds in, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ripe tomato, seeded and cut into tiny dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut everything by hand and as fine as your patience allows; the pieces must stay separate and identifiable on the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the solids with the salt and leave them 10 minutes, until beads of liquid gather in the bottom of the bowl.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the lime juice, vinegar and water; it should pour off a spoon rather than cling to it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest it 30 minutes before serving, and make it on the day you intend to eat it.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"This is a suspension, not a paste, and that is the whole difference from a chilli sauce: knife cut pieces sitting crisp in a thin sour liquid. Salt the solids first so they give up their own juice into it. The blender is the failure mode; it shears the onion into sulphur, tears the cilantro cells so the liquid oxidises brown within the hour, and leaves green sludge. Knife only, and eat it fresh.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aj%C3%AD%20Picante%20Casero%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ají Picante Casero"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Cilantro","Vinegar"]},{"slug":"natilla-colombiana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 L whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g panela, finely grated, or 200 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g cornflour (cornstarch)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cinnamon sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"ground cinnamon, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm 700 ml of the milk with the cinnamon sticks, cloves and panela until the panela has dissolved, then leave it to infuse off the heat and strain it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the cornflour into the remaining cold milk until there is not a single lump, then pour it into the warm milk with the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over medium heat, stirring flat across the base and into the corners without stopping, until it thickens suddenly and heaves rather than ripples.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Keep it at that heavy boil for three more minutes, until a spoon drawn across the base leaves a track that holds and the chalky taste has gone, then beat in the butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a wetted shallow dish, smooth the top and leave at room temperature until it is firm enough to cut into squares, then dust with cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There are no eggs in this. The set is pure starch, and starch only works if you take it to a full boil, near 90 C (195 F), so the granules swell and burst. Pull it early and it stays loose and tastes chalky. The danger is the base, where it thickens first and scorches in seconds, so stir flat and never stop. And slurry the cornflour in cold milk, because dry starch hitting hot milk seizes into lumps no whisk will break.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Natilla%20Colombiana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Natilla Colombiana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Milk","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove"]},{"slug":"houskovy-knedlik","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour, coarse ground if you can get it"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml whole milk at 35C (95F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fresh yeast, or 4 g dried"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g stale white rolls or crustless white bread, in 1 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt for the cooking water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the yeast and sugar into the warm milk and leave it until a foam cap forms and starts to crack, about ten minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the egg, then the flour and salt, working the dough until it slaps clean off your hand and hangs in strands from the spoon; it stays softer and wetter than a bread dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the bread cubes through without crushing them, then prove covered until the mass is domed and doubled.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll into two logs about 6 cm across, sitting the seam underneath, and rest them fifteen minutes on a floured cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lower into a wide pan of salted water held at a lazy simmer, turn each log at ten minutes, and cook until a skewer comes out dry, about twenty two minutes in total.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out and prick all over at once with a skewer, then cut into 1 cm slices by looping a length of sewing thread round the loaf and pulling the ends past each other.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The crumb is barely set gluten holding steam, so a blade compresses it into a gluey smear before it ever cuts; a thread parts the crumb instead of pressing it. Prick the loaf the second it leaves the water, because trapped steam condenses as it cools and drags the centre into a dense wet band. Keep the water at a lazy simmer: a rolling boil sloughs the surface off.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Shaping by hand","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Houskov%C3%BD%20Knedl%C3%ADk%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Houskový Knedlík"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"svickova-na-smetane","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg beef rump or topside in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g smoked streaky bacon, cut into long 5 mm strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g carrot, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g celeriac, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g parsley root or parsnip, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, sliced, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g whole cranberry sauce, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lard the beef: drive a thin knife along the grain at intervals and push a cold strip of bacon into each channel, then salt the joint all over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown every face in the butter until dark bronze and lift it out; sweat the carrot, celeriac, parsley root and onion in the same pan until the edges caramelise and the pan smells sweet rather than green.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the beef with the peppercorns, allspice, bay, thyme and enough stock to come a third of the way up, cover, and cook at 150C (300F) until a skewer slides in with no resistance, around two and a half hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the beef to rest, fish out the bay leaves, then blend the roots with the pan liquid and push the puree through a fine sieve, pressing until only dry fibre is left in the mesh.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the sauce to a bare simmer and sharpen it with the vinegar and sugar until it tastes bright rather than sweet, then pull the pan off the heat and stir in the cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Carve across the grain, warm the slices through in the sauce, and plate with dumpling slices, a spoon of cranberry and a lemon slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The body is sieved root vegetable, not roux: carrot and celeriac cook to a pulp whose pectin and starch hold the sauce together. Push it through a fine sieve or the fibres stay and the sauce reads gritty on the tongue. Cream goes in off the boil and only after the vinegar has simmered in; poured into a boiling acidic pan it curdles into flecks that will not blend back. Two and a half hours of the time is unattended oven.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","earthy","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sv%C3%AD%C4%8Dkov%C3%A1%20na%20Smetan%C4%9B%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Svíčková na Smetaně"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"smoked-fish-hot-vs-cold","term":"Smoked Fish: Hot vs. Cold"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Bacon","Onion","Carrot","Lemon","Berries","Dried fruit","Parsnip","Celeriac","Parsley","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Cream","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"vepro-knedlo-zelo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg pork shoulder with the skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g caraway seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, thickly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g sauerkraut, drained, brine kept"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, finely sliced, for the cabbage"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caraway seed, for the cabbage"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 floury potato, grated raw"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the skin in a 1 cm lattice, cutting through the fat but stopping short of the meat, and rub the salt, garlic and caraway into the flesh and into every cut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the joint on the sliced onion with the water in the tin and roast at 160C (325F), basting with the pan liquid, until a skewer meets no resistance in the thickest part, about three hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile soften the finely sliced onion in the lard until deep gold, then add the drained sauerkraut, sugar, caraway, bay and a splash of the reserved brine, cover and stew until the shreds turn translucent and slack, about forty minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the grated potato through the cabbage for the last ten minutes; it should cling to the shreds, not sit in a pool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the oven to 220C (425F) for the final twenty minutes, uncovered and unbasted, until the skin blisters and cracks under the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the pork fifteen minutes, carve thick, and spoon the pan juices over the dumpling slices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two failures share this plate. Skin only crackles when it is dry, so the basting that keeps the meat moist has to stop for the last twenty minutes under high heat. And sauerkraut left in all its own brine stays squeaky and harshly sour; drain most of it off, then cook the shreds slack in onion and fat with a little sugar so the acid reads as lift rather than sting.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Scoring","Brining & curing","Roasting in a hot oven","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment","rich","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vep%C5%99o%20Knedlo%20Zelo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vepřo Knedlo Zelo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Cabbage","Bay leaf","Flour","Sugar","Clove","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["scoring","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"hovezi-gulas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg beef shin or chuck, in 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g sweet paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g hot paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caraway seed, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato puree"},{"kind":"item","text":"330 ml dark lager"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dried marjoram"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt and 3 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, in raw rings, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the lard and brown the beef in batches to a hard dark crust, lifting each batch out before it steams.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the sliced onions in the same fat over low heat, stirring now and then, until they collapse into a brown jam and no longer read as slices, about forty minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan right off the heat, stir in both paprikas and the caraway so nothing sizzles, and wet it immediately with the beer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the beef with the stock, tomato puree and bay, and hold at a bare simmer with the lid ajar until the meat yields to a spoon but still holds its cube, about two and a half hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour into a ladle of the hot liquid until smooth, stir it back and simmer ten minutes, until the gravy coats the back of a spoon and holds a drawn line.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, crush the garlic with the marjoram and a pinch of salt and stir it through; rest ten minutes and serve with raw onion rings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is not Hungarian. Pörkölt is onion, lard and paprika alone, and gulyás is a soup with potatoes in it. Czech guláš is a gravy dish, and the differences are dark lager, a flour finish and marjoram stirred in raw at the end. The onions must cook to a brown jam, since that collapse is most of the body. Paprika goes in off the heat: its sugars scorch in seconds in hot fat and turn the whole pot bitter for good.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hov%C4%9Bz%C3%AD%20Gul%C3%A1%C5%A1%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hovězí Guláš"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Oregano","Bay leaf","Flour","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Paprika","Clove","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"bramborak","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g floury potatoes, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g dried marjoram"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caraway seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g lard, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the potatoes on the fine side of a box grater straight into a bowl and leave every drop of the liquid in with them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crush the garlic to a paste with the salt, then beat it into the potato with the marjoram, caraway, eggs, milk, flour and pepper; the batter should fall from the spoon in a thick ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at once in 5 mm of lard at 180C (350F), spreading each one as thin as a coin, until the edges go to lace and the underside is dark amber.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn once, give it half as long on the second side, then drain on a rack rather than paper so the base stays crisp, and salt while hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Grated raw potato starts leaking the moment it is cut, and that liquid is where the binding starch is, so never wring it out. Nor let the bowl stand: within fifteen minutes the batter has gone grey and slack and spreads into a soggy disc. Spread each one thin. A thick bramborak sets its crust long before the middle cooks, and the centre stays raw, wet and oddly sweet.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brambor%C3%A1k%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bramborák"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Garlic","Potato","Oregano","Milk","Flour","Clove","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"kulajda","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g waxy potatoes, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fresh mushrooms, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water or mushroom stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caraway seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml soured cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dill fronds, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt and 2 g white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the potato with the bay, allspice and caraway in the stock until the dice are tender but still hold square corners.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the mushrooms and cook five minutes more, until they darken and sink.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour into the cold soured cream until smooth, loosen it with a ladle of the hot broth, then stir it back and simmer five minutes so the flour loses its raw chalkiness.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach the eggs separately in vinegared water and keep them warm; a soft yolk is the whole point of them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the boil, season with the vinegar, sugar, salt and most of the dill, tasting until it lands sour first and sweet second.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide an egg into each bowl, ladle the soup around it and scatter the last of the dill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Soured cream is the whole soup and it curdles twice over, once from heat and once from the vinegar. Whisking flour into the cold cream first is what saves it: the swelling starch gets between the casein micelles and stops them clumping. Loosen it with hot broth before it goes back, and add the vinegar only once the pot is off the boil. Dill goes in last, since its aroma is volatile and five minutes of simmering leaves you hay.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Poaching eggs","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","herb-fresh","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":39,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Poaching eggs","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20poach%20eggs%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kulajda%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kulajda"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"poach-simmer-boil","term":"Poach, Simmer, Boil"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Potato","Mushrooms","Berries","Dill","Bay leaf","Cream","Flour","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Caraway","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"smazeny-syr-s-tatarskou-omackou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 slabs of semi hard cheese such as Edam or young Gouda, 1.5 cm thick, about 120 g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fine dry breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g gherkins, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g capers, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g smooth mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice of half a lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt and 2 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the cheese into even 1.5 cm slabs and chill them hard; a cold block buys the crust the time it needs.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Coat in flour, then egg, then crumb, and repeat the egg and crumb, pressing the crumbs on and sealing every cut edge and corner.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill the crumbed slabs thirty minutes so the coating dries into a shell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the gherkin, onion, capers, mustard and lemon into the mayonnaise and season it; it should taste sharper than you expect, because the cheese is bland and fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at a full 180C (350F) until deep gold, about two minutes a side; the slab will bulge slightly and give under a finger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack and eat straight away, with the tatarska alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The race is between the crust colouring and the cheese turning liquid, and the cheese is faster. Two coats of egg and crumb, a hard chill and oil at a full 180C (350F) tilt it back your way. Any bare patch at a corner is where it bursts: the melt finds the gap, runs into the oil and the block deflates into an empty shell. Fry it and eat it. Reheated, it is rubber in a soft jacket.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sma%C5%BEen%C3%BD%20S%C3%BDr%20s%20Tatarskou%20Om%C3%A1%C4%8Dkou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Smažený Sýr s Tatarskou Omáčkou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Capers","Lemon","Cheese (any)","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Mustard","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"bryndzove-halusky","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg floury potatoes, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g bryndza, or another soft salted sheep cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml soured cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g smoked streaky bacon, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the raw potatoes finely and keep all the liquid, then beat in the flour, egg and salt to a sticky batter that just holds its shape on the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the dough through a halusky board, or the coarse holes of a colander, into a wide pan of simmering salted water, so the pieces land about thumbnail size.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until they rise and float, then one minute more, and lift them into a warm bowl with a slotted spoon, keeping a cup of the cooking water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the bacon slowly from cold until the fat runs clear and the dice are crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash the bryndza with the soured cream and enough hot cooking water to make it pourable, then fold it through the hot halusky off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the bacon and its hot fat over the top, grind on pepper and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bryndza is an acid set sheep cheese with no melting stretch in it: put the pan back over the flame and it turns grainy and weeps. Slacken it off the heat with soured cream and a little of the starchy cooking water instead, and that starch carries it into a coating. The dough needs the potato liquid too, so never wring the gratings out; without the starch the halusky dissolve in the pot.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, wood-smoke running underneath."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bryndzov%C3%A9%20Halu%C5%A1ky%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bryndzové Halušky"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Potato","Cream","Cheese (any)","Flour"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"trdelnik","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml whole milk at 35C (95F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fresh yeast, or 4 g dried"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter, soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g butter, melted, for brushing"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar, for the coating"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g walnuts, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the yeast and a spoon of the sugar into the warm milk and wait for a foam cap.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the flour, yolks, remaining sugar, salt and zest, then the soft butter, kneading until the dough is smooth and pulls a thin translucent window.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove until doubled, roll out to 5 mm and cut ribbons 2 cm wide; wind them round greased foil wrapped wooden rollers with the edges just touching, and press the two ends down onto the roller.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush with melted butter, roll in the mixed sugar, walnut and cinnamon, and prove again on the roller until visibly puffed and the seams have started to close.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (400F), turning the rollers every few minutes, until the sugar has run into a shining caramel and the seams have knitted, about eighteen minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Twist each one free while it is still hot, then stand them upright to cool so the caramel sets rather than pools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Labelled honestly: this is sold in Prague as an ancient Czech tradition and it is not one. The spit cake is Transylvanian kurtoskalacs, which reached the tourist streets in the 1990s; the older Slovak claim is the Skalica trdelnik, a denser walnut cake baked another way. Mechanically the sugar has to caramelise before the thin ribbon dries out, so keep the heat high and the roller turning, and twist it off while hot or it welds to the form.","equipment":["Rolling pin","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trdeln%C3%ADk%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Trdelník"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"moravske-kolace","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml whole milk at 35C (95F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fresh yeast, or 7 g dried"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g butter, soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g tvaroh or dry curd cheese, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g caster sugar, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g povidla, thick plum butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g plain flour, for the crumb"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g caster sugar, for the crumb"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cold butter, for the crumb"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter, melted, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the yeast, a spoon of sugar and a spoon of flour into the warm milk and leave it until the surface caps and cracks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead in the flour, yolks, sugar, salt and zest, working the soft butter in last, until the dough is satin and slow to spring back from a finger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove until doubled, divide into 60 g balls, flatten each one and prove again on the tray until they feel like an earlobe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Only now press a wide well into each with the floured base of a glass, leaving a 1 cm rim, and fill with the tvaroh beaten with its yolk, sugar and raisins, then drop a spot of povidla in the centre.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the crumb ingredients to coarse sand, scatter it over, and bake at 190C (375F) until the rims are gold and the curd has just set with no wobble, about fifteen minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush with melted butter the moment they come out of the oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two rules keep the well open. Press it after the second prove, never before, or you drive out the gas you just made and the kolac bakes flat. And drain the tvaroh hard, in a cloth under a weight, because curd water turns to steam and lifts the filling out over the rim. Pull them while the curd still looks matt and only just set: a wobble means underdone, cracks mean it went too far.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven","Rubbing fat into flour","Egg wash & the baker’s shine","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","sweet-edged","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moravsk%C3%A9%20Kol%C3%A1%C4%8De%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moravské Koláče"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Czech%20%26%20Slovak%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Czech & Slovak kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"proofing-oven-spring-and-the-crumb","term":"Proofing, Oven Spring & the Crumb"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"rugbrod","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g cracked rye kernels"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g flaxseed"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g sunflower seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water, for the soaker"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g active rye sourdough starter"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml water, lukewarm"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g wholemeal rye flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g strong wheat flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dark malt syrup"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the cracked rye, flaxseed and sunflower seeds into the 500 ml water, cover, and leave overnight, until the kernels are plump and chew soft rather than gritty and no loose water is left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the starter with the lukewarm water, then the rye flour, wheat flour, salt and malt syrup, to a thick batter that falls from the spoon in sheets; this is not a dough you can knead.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the soaker, scrape into a greased 2 litre tin, and level the top with a wet spatula, because a dry hand tears the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove at room temperature until the batter has risen by half and the top is pocked all over with open bubbles, 4 to 6 hours depending on the starter.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (355F) for 1 hour 20 minutes, until the centre reads 98C (208F) and the turned out loaf sounds hollow on the base.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely, then wrap and leave a full day before the first slice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rye has almost no gluten. The crumb is held by a pentosan gel, and rye's own amylase chews that starch to gum unless the dough is acid, which is the real work of the surdej. Raise this on yeast alone and it bakes sticky. Cutting it warm does the same damage, because the gel is still setting, so wrap the cold loaf and wait a day.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sourdough starter"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sourdough starter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sTAiDki7AQA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rugbr%C3%B8d%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rugbrød"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour"]},{"slug":"dyrlaegens-natmad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices day old rugbrød, 1 cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g butter, at 18C (64F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g coarse liver pate"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g salt cured beef brisket, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g meat aspic, cold and firm"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small white onion, in fine rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cress"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g flaky salt and coarsely ground black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Spread the butter thin, unbroken and right into the crust on every side; any bare patch is where the bread will go soggy first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the liver pate over the whole slice in one even sheet, level to the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drape the salt beef in loose folds so it stands proud of the pate rather than lying flat against it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the cold aspic into 1 cm cubes and set them on the beef, then finish with onion rings, a tuft of cress, salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve at once, with a knife and fork; built even a few minutes ahead the base softens under the load.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rugbrød is a wet acid sponge and everything stacked on it leaks: pate, aspic, onion. Butter is the barrier, so it goes edge to edge and into the crust with no gaps, at 18C to 20C (64F to 68F) where it spreads instead of dragging holes. The order follows the same logic: fat on the bread, meat next, anything wet or sharp last and never touching the crumb.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dyrl%C3%A6gens%20Natmad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dyrlægens Natmad"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Liver & Offal","Onion","Butter"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"stegt-flaesk-med-persillesovs","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g pork belly, rind off, in 6 mm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg waxy potatoes, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g flat leaf parsley, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay the slices in a single layer on a cold rack over a cold tray, salt them lightly, and slide them into a cold oven set to 200C (390F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast 25 to 35 minutes, turning once, until the fat is glassy and a slice snaps when you bend it instead of folding over.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the potatoes in salted water until a knife meets no resistance, then drain and hold them covered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter, stir in the flour and cook 2 minutes until it smells of biscuit but has taken no colour, then add the milk in three lots, whisking smooth between each.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the sauce 10 minutes, stirring, until it coats a spoon and tastes of milk rather than flour; season, then stir the parsley in off the heat.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"The slices start in a cold pan or a cold oven. Fat renders slowly and has to leave before the lean sets, or the muscle contracts around soft fat and the slice bends rather than snapping. High heat from the start seals the outside and traps it. The sauce needs its ten minutes to lose the raw flour, but parsley goes in off the heat; held hot it turns olive and bitter.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Roasting in a hot oven","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Stegt%20Fl%C3%A6sk%20med%20Persillesovs%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Stegt Flæsk med Persillesovs"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Potato","Parsley","Butter","Milk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"flaeskesteg-med-sprod-svaer","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg boned pork loin, rind on"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the rind at 5 mm intervals, down through the fat but not into the meat, then leave the joint uncovered in the fridge overnight until the rind feels papery and dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the coarse salt down into every cut and push a bay leaf into every second one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the joint on the onions and carrots in a roasting tin, prop it with foil until the rind sits dead level, and pour the water into the tin around it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast at 160C (320F) for about 1 hour 30 minutes, until the centre reads 65C (150F).","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Raise the oven to 230C (445F) for 15 to 20 minutes, until the rind is blistered right across and shatters under a knuckle; put any leathery patch under the grill on its own.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 20 minutes, lift the crackling off in one sheet and break it up, then carve the meat.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Crackling puffs in two stages. Low heat with water in the tin melts the rind's collagen to gelatine; the final blast flashes the water still held in that layer to steam and blows the sheet into blisters. A damp rind never gets there, because the surface heat is spent boiling off free water and the rind leathers instead. Dry it overnight, salt into the cuts, and keep the joint level so nothing pools.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Scoring","Roasting in a hot oven","Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fl%C3%A6skesteg%20med%20Spr%C3%B8d%20Sv%C3%A6r%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Flæskesteg med Sprød Svær"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Carrot","Bay leaf"],"judgedBy":["scoring","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"boller-i-karry","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g minced pork"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, finely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp mild curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g long grain rice, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the mince with the salt and grated onion alone, beating hard for 2 minutes, until it turns sticky and glossy and hangs off the spoon.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the egg, then the flour, then the milk a little at a time, until the farce is soft but still holds a rounded shape on a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the water and bay to the boil, then drop it to a bare shiver at 85C (185F); shape the boller with two wet spoons, lower them in and poach 8 to 10 minutes, until they float and feel firm at the centre. Lift out and keep warm.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter, fry the curry powder in it for 30 seconds until it smells sweet rather than dusty, stir in the flour, then whisk in 600 ml of the strained poaching stock.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 10 minutes to a sauce that coats a spoon, stir in the cream, and return the boller to warm through without boiling. Serve over rice.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt goes into the mince first and is worked in alone until the farce turns sticky and glossy. That stickiness is dissolved myosin, and it is what holds the milk and egg; add them first and the boller fall apart in the pot. Then keep the water at a bare shiver, around 85C (185F). At a rolling boil they swell, burst, and cloud the stock the sauce is built on.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Boller%20i%20Karry%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Boller i Karry"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Bay leaf","Butter","Milk","Cream","Rice","Flour","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"gammeldags-oksesteg","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg beef topside, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g salt pork back fat, in 5 mm strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g soft butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp redcurrant jelly"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Freeze the fat strips 15 minutes so they stay stiff, then thread them through the joint with the grain at roughly 2 cm spacing.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brown the joint in the butter on every face until it is deeply coloured rather than grey; the colour built here is the colour of the finished sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onions, bay, thyme and enough stock to come a third of the way up, cover, and cook at 150C (300F), turning every 40 minutes, until a skewer slides in with only slight resistance and the centre reads 75C (167F), about 2 hours 30 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the meat 20 minutes under foil while you strain the liquor and reduce it by a third.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the flour and soft butter to a paste and whisk it in a little at a time, then the cream and the jelly; simmer to a coating sauce and carve the meat across the grain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Topside is lean, and lean meat comes out dry even from a pot of liquid: as collagen shrinks it wrings water out of the fibres, and nothing outside the joint goes back in. Larding answers that from inside, the fat melting along the seams so slices carve rather than crumble. Hold the oven at 150C (300F) and the liquid at a shiver; a boil contracts the fibres hard and no resting repairs it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gammeldags%20Oksesteg%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gammeldags Oksesteg"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Onion","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Cream","Flour","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"brunede-kartofler","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g small waxy potatoes, walnut sized"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt, for the water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the potatoes in salted water until just tender, peel them while warm, then leave them uncovered in the fridge overnight so the surfaces dry out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the sugar alone in a wide cold frying pan over medium heat, without stirring, tilting the pan, until it runs pale amber and smells of toast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat and whisk in the butter; it will hiss and thicken to a loose caramel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cold dry potatoes and roll them over the heat continuously for 5 to 8 minutes, until each wears an even glossy coat that turns tacky as it cools.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dry caramel is molten sugar near 170C (340F) and it clings only to a dry surface. A wet or freshly boiled potato drops its temperature and dilutes it to a syrup that slides straight off, or shocks it into grainy lumps. So boil the potatoes a day ahead, peel them warm and dry them cold in the fridge. Pull the caramel at pale amber; past dark it turns bitter within seconds.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brunede%20Kartofler%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Brunede Kartofler"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Butter","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"dansk-remoulade","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp mild yellow mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml rapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g pickled cauliflower and cucumber, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp mild curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm 1 tsp of the oil, stir in the curry powder and turmeric for 20 seconds until they smell fragrant rather than dusty, and set aside to cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with the mustard and the salt until sticky and pale, then add oil drop by drop until the whisk leaves clear tracks, and only then in a thin steady stream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Loosen with the vinegar and work in the last of the oil to a sauce that holds a soft peak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the pickles to 2 mm and wring them in a cloth until no more brine runs, then fold them in with the cooled spice oil and the sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill at least 2 hours so the colour and sweetness spread through, and taste again cold before serving.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Mayonnaise is oil droplets crowded into a small water phase, and the yolk's lecithin can only coat so many. Chopped pickles are mostly brine, so folded in wet they add water the emulsion has no room for; it thins on the spot and weeps overnight. Wring them dry first. Bloom the curry and turmeric in warm oil too, or the spice stays chalky and raw on the tongue.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dansk%20Remoulade%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dansk Remoulade"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Cauliflower","Cucumber","Vinegar","Wine","Mustard","Sugar","Pickles","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"risalamande-med-kirsebaersauce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g short grain pudding rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g blanched almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g stoned morello cherries"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar, for the sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml cherry juice or water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp potato starch"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the rice in the water for 2 minutes to swell the grains, add the milk, vanilla and salt, and cook uncovered on the lowest heat, stirring often, about 40 minutes, until the grains are soft and the porridge only just holds its shape.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":2520,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the sugar and chill the porridge until it is cold to the finger, not merely cooled.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the almonds coarsely, leaving one whole for the prize, and whip the cream to soft floppy peaks that fold over on the whisk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the cream into the cold porridge in three additions along with the almonds, keeping the mixture loose and airy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the cherries with the sugar and juice, take the pan off the heat, stir in the potato starch slaked in a little cold water, then return it only until it clears and thickens. Serve the sauce cold over the risalamande.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The porridge must be fridge cold before the cream goes in. Whipped cream is a foam braced by solid fat crystals; against warm rice that fat melts, the bubbles collapse and the dessert slumps into a soup that will not come back. The sauce has the opposite rule: potato starch sets at a lower temperature than cornflour and breaks down if boiled, so thicken it off the heat and bring it back only until it turns clear.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Risalamande%20med%20Kirseb%C3%A6rsauce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Risalamande med Kirsebærsauce"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Mushrooms","Stone fruit","Milk","Cream","Rice","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"drommekage-fra-brovst","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"75 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g butter, for the topping"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g desiccated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml whole milk, for the topping"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the eggs and sugar to a thick pale ribbon that holds its trail on the surface for three seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the milk with the butter until melted, then fold it into the eggs alternately with the flour, baking powder, vanilla sugar and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in a lined 20 by 30 cm tin at 200C (390F) for 18 to 20 minutes, until the top springs back and a skewer comes out with a few moist crumbs. Stop there.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Melt the topping butter with the brown sugar, coconut and milk to a thick spreadable slurry, without letting it boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread it gently over the hot cake and return it to 200C (390F) for 8 to 10 minutes, until the whole surface bubbles and the coconut has darkened. Cool in the tin.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cake is baked twice and the second bake is the point of it. Pull the first one at just set, a skewer with moist crumbs, because a fully baked crumb dries out under the topping while an underbaked one cannot carry the weight and lets it sink into a wet seam. Spread the topping warm and soft onto a hot cake so it flows. It is done when it bubbles right across, which is water leaving and coconut toasting.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dr%C3%B8mmekage%20fra%20Brovst%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Drømmekage fra Brovst"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"mangu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900 g green plantains (about 4), skins hard and matte"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt for the cooking water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water, ice cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter, cold and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g red onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the skin along each ridge, peel the plantains, cut into 5 cm lengths and drop them straight into the salted water so the cut faces do not go grey.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil hard until a knife slides to the core with no resistance and a piece breaks under its own weight, 20 to 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Meanwhile soften the onion in the oil over low heat until it slumps and turns translucent, add the vinegar and 3 g salt, and pull the pan the moment the sharpness in the steam softens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain the plantains and mash them at once with the butter, working in the ice cold water a splash at a time, until the mash is smooth, heavy and holds a furrow.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat once more with a wooden spoon until no fibres remain, spread on warm plates and crown with the onions and their vinegar; at breakfast this is the bed for los tres golpes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boiled plantain is gelatinised starch and it firms as it cools, so a mash that looks perfect over the flame sets into a brick on the plate. Cold water brings the paste down to serving temperature while you work, so what you see in the pot is what you get. Add it a splash at a time and beat by hand; a blender ruptures the granules and turns it to glue.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Scoring","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mang%C3%BA%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mangú"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Plantain","Butter","Vinegar"],"judgedBy":["scoring","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"sazon-dominicano","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g ají cubanela (Cubanelle frying peppers), seeded and roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ají gustoso or ají dulce, stems pulled out"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red onion, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g garlic cloves, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g cilantro ancho (culantro) leaves, or 40 g coriander with stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml sour orange juice, or 20 ml lime juice and 10 ml orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml neutral oil, to seal the jar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the dried oregano hard between your palms over the blender jar until it powders and the smell turns resinous rather than dusty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the peppers, onion, garlic and culantro with the vinegar and sour orange, and pulse to a coarse paste with the flecks still visible; taken to a smooth puree it aerates and browns within days.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold through the salt, pack into a clean jar, press out the air pockets and float the oil over the surface. It holds three weeks in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"To use, fry two tablespoons per pot in hot oil over medium heat until the water has gone, the hissing quietens and the raw green smell turns sweet, about 3 minutes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"This is where Dominican cooking parts from its neighbours: no achiote, no cured pork, and dried oregano rather than fresh. Vinegar and sour orange hold the jar raw for weeks by dropping the pH below where spoilage works. The flavour is fat soluble, so the sazón has to be fried until it stops hissing; stirred into a wet pan at the end it tastes of raw pepper and grass.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Saz%C3%B3n%20Dominicano%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sazón Dominicano"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Orange","Cilantro","Oregano","Vinegar","Clove"]},{"slug":"pollo-guisado-dominicano","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 kg bone-in chicken, chopped through the bone into small pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sour orange juice, or lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sazón dominicano"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g dried oregano, rubbed"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g white sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g ají cubanela, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g pitted green olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 leaves cilantro ancho"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the chicken with the sour orange, sazón, oregano and salt and leave it an hour in the fridge; the acid firms the surface so it browns instead of steaming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil in a heavy caldero over medium high heat, scatter in the sugar and leave it alone: it melts clear, then bronzes, then goes the colour of dark rum at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"The instant the first wisp of smoke lifts, put the chicken in all at once and turn the pieces until every one wears a mahogany coat, about 5 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the onion, ají and tomato paste and cook until the paste darkens from red to brick and the oil around it runs orange.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in water to come halfway up the meat, add the olives and cilantro ancho, cover and hold at a lazy blip until a thigh gives at the bone, then uncover and reduce until the sauce coats the spoon and leaves a trail.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Colour here comes from burnt sugar, not annatto, and that is the Dominican signature under la bandera. Sugar starts colouring near 170C (340F) in the hot oil; pull it at pale amber and the stew tastes thin and sweet, push past dark mahogany and the bitterness is permanent. The wet chicken is the brake: it drops the pan temperature and stops the browning where you left it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","briny","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pollo%20Guisado%20Dominicano%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pollo Guisado Dominicano"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Olives","Lime","Orange","Cilantro","Oregano","Tomato paste","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"habichuelas-guisadas-con-auyama","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g dried small red beans, soaked 12 hours"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g auyama (West Indian pumpkin), peeled and cut in 2 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g sazón dominicano"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried oregano, rubbed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 leaves cilantro ancho"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 L water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the drained beans in the water with the auyama until a bean crushes to cream between finger and thumb and the pumpkin has fallen apart, about an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scoop out most of the pumpkin and mash it smooth with a ladle of the cooking liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"In a second pot fry the sazón and tomato paste in the oil until the paste turns brick coloured and the oil separates and runs orange at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the beans with 700 ml of their liquid, the pumpkin puree, the oregano and the cilantro ancho, and simmer until the sauce is opaque and falls from the spoon in a thick ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt, then slacken with more bean liquid until it pours easily; it will tighten again in the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Auyama is the thickener, not flour. Its pectin and starch disperse as the flesh collapses and carry the sauce as a glossy, pourable suspension. Judge it one notch looser than you want, because the beans keep drinking as they stand; a sauce that ribbons in the pot is paste by the time it reaches the rice. Loosen with bean liquid only, since water dilutes the seasoning with it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","herb-fresh","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Habichuelas%20Guisadas%20con%20Auyama%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Habichuelas Guisadas con Auyama"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Chiles","Squash/Pumpkin","Cilantro","Oregano","Kidney/Pinto beans","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"moro-de-guandules-con-coco","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g long grain rice, rinsed until the water runs clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g guandules (pigeon peas), fresh or drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml neutral oil, plus a spoonful for the concón"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g sazón dominicano"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried oregano, rubbed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 leaves cilantro ancho"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g pitted green olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the sazón and tomato paste in the oil in a heavy caldero until the oil runs orange, then add the guandules and turn them until they are coated.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the coconut milk and water with the oregano, cilantro ancho, olives and salt, bring to the boil and taste the liquid: it should read a shade saltier than you want the finished rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the rice, level it once, and boil uncovered without touching it until the surface goes from soup to a pocked, glossy porridge and craters open, about 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold the rice over on itself a single time, drop the heat to its lowest, cover tightly and leave it 20 minutes with the lid down.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Uncover, trickle the extra oil around the rim, raise the heat and listen; pull the pot 3 minutes after a fine steady crackle starts.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coconut milk is the southern tell and it rewrites the timing: its sugars and fat brown well below the temperature plain starch needs, so the concón forms in about three minutes rather than ten, and a minute past the first fine crackle it is burnt rather than toasted. Listen instead of looking. Once the lid is down do not stir; broken grains leak amylose and the moro goes gummy.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Moro%20de%20Guandules%20con%20Coco%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Moro de Guandules con Coco"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Tomato","Chiles","Peas","Olives","Coconut","Cilantro","Oregano","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Tomato paste"]},{"slug":"chicharron-de-pollo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg bone-in chicken thighs and wings, chopped through the bone into 4 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml sour orange juice, or lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g dried oregano, rubbed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g black pepper, coarsely cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the chicken with the sour orange, soy, garlic, oregano, pepper and salt and leave it an hour in the fridge, no longer, or the citrus turns the surface chalky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip it into a colander and let it drain until the pieces look tacky rather than wet, about 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Toss through the flour and cornflour, pressing the ragged edges so they take a shaggy coat, then rest on a rack until the dredge turns from chalky white to damp grey.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 165C (330F) until the loud bubbling drops to a murmur and the pieces float, about 7 minutes, then lift onto a rack and rest 5 minutes.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bring the oil to 190C (375F) and fry again for 90 seconds, until the crust is dark honey and rattles when the pieces are tipped into a bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Soy sauce in the marinade is the Chinese Dominican fingerprint that separates this from every other fried chicken on the island, and the small bone-in cut gives ragged edges for crust while seasoning from the bone out. Dredge the chicken wet and the coat slides off in sheets. Drain until tacky, let the flour hydrate on a rack, then fry twice so the crust dries after it has cooked.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Deep frying","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","aromatic","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicharr%C3%B3n%20de%20Pollo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicharrón de Pollo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Lime","Orange","Oregano","Flour","Soy sauce"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"pastelon-de-platano-maduro","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 kg very ripe plantains (about 6), skins fully black"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter, plus more for the dish"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g minced beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sazón dominicano"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g green olives, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g capers, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried oregano, rubbed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g mild semi-hard white cheese, grated"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the plantains, cut them into lengths and boil until a knife meets no resistance, then drain and leave them in the colander to steam dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash hot with the butter, evaporated milk and half the salt to a smooth, sticky paste that holds a furrow, cool to warm, then beat in one egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the beef hard in the oil without moving it much, add the sazón, tomato paste, oregano and the rest of the salt, and cook until the pan is dry and the fat runs red, then fold in the olives and capers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press half the plantain into a buttered 22 cm dish with wet hands, spread the meat and the cheese over it, cover with the remaining plantain and brush the top with the second egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 190C (375F) until the top is burnished and the edges have pulled away from the dish, about 30 minutes, then rest 20 minutes before cutting.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true}],"note":"The layer only works with black-skinned plantains. As they ripen the starch turns to sugar and the flesh softens; a yellow one mashes dry and sandy and the slice crumbles on the plate. Black fruit mashes to a sticky paste that the egg sets into something you can actually cut. Cut it hot and the layers slump, so give the dish twenty minutes off the heat first.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Mashing & puréeing","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","briny","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a briny, maritime salinity, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pastel%C3%B3n%20de%20Pl%C3%A1tano%20Maduro%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pastelón de Plátano Maduro"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Eggs","Tomato","Plantain","Olives","Capers","Oregano","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"molondrones-guisados","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g molondrones (okra), young pods under 8 cm"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g sazón dominicano"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ripe tomato, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried oregano, rubbed"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wipe the pods with a dry cloth rather than washing them, trim only the stem cap so the seed cavity stays sealed, and halve lengthwise only those longer than a finger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear them in the hot oil in one uncrowded layer, without stirring, until the edges blister and the pods stop squeaking against the pan, about 4 minutes, then lift them out.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the onion in the same pan, add the sazón and tomato paste and fry until the paste is brick coloured and the oil runs orange.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the okra with the tomato, vinegar, oregano and water, shake the pan rather than stirring, and simmer until the pods are tender but still hold their shape and the sauce is glossy rather than stringy, about 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt, and let it settle off the heat for 5 minutes before serving.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Molondrones is the Dominican word for okra and the handling is its own too. The mucilage is a soluble polysaccharide sitting in the seed cavity: every cut opens it and water lets it string. Two controls do the work. Dry high heat sets the outside before the pod is breached, and the acid in vinegar and tomato breaks the gel. Wash, ring, and boil in water and you get rope.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Molondrones%20Guisados%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Molondrones Guisados"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Okra","Oregano","Vinegar","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"majarete","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fine yellow cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"450 ml whole milk, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"450 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"a quarter nutmeg, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"ground cinnamon, to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the cornmeal into the cold milk until no dry pockets remain and leave it 10 minutes to hydrate.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the coconut milk with the sugar, cinnamon stick, nutmeg and salt to a bare tremble, never a rolling boil, then fish out the stick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the slurry in through a sieve, stirring, and cook over medium low heat with a flat spoon working the base constantly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"It thickens at about 6 minutes; keep going another 8, until it no longer tastes chalky on the tongue and the surface holds the mark of the spoon for a beat.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the vanilla off the heat, pour into bowls, cool uncovered so a skin forms, chill until set, and dust heavily with ground cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cornmeal thickens by about 85C (185F), but the grains keep hydrating well past that, so a pot that has just gone thick still tastes chalky. Cook several minutes beyond the thickening point. Dry meal tipped into hot liquid gelatinises on the outside of each speck and locks the water out, which is why it goes in slurried cold and through a sieve.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","floral","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Majarete%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Majarete"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"morir-sonando","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml whole milk, chilled to 2C (36F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml evaporated milk, chilled"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml orange juice, freshly squeezed, strained and chilled hard"},{"kind":"item","text":"ice cubes"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the sugar into the two milks until it has completely dissolved, then hold the jug, the juice and the glasses in the coldest part of the fridge until everything is properly cold to the hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the glasses with ice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the orange juice into the milk in a slow thread, stirring steadily all the while; never tip the milk into the juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the ice and drink at once, while the colour is still even and opaque.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Casein aggregates when the pH reaches about 4.6, and orange juice sits well below that, so this drink lives one degree from curdling. Acid coagulation is temperature dependent: at 2 to 4C (36 to 39F) it is slow enough to beat, at room temperature it seizes in the glass. Pour juice into milk in a thread so no drop of milk meets undiluted acid, and drink it straight away.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Morir%20So%C3%B1ando%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Morir Soñando"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Orange","Milk","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"epis","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120g flat-leaf parsley, thick stems removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g spring onions, roughly cut"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g green bell pepper, seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g white onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g celery with its leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g garlic cloves, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g thyme sprigs, leaves stripped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, stem removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml sour orange juice, or 60ml lime juice plus 40ml orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash the greens and dry them properly in a cloth or spinner; every drop of rinse water thins the paste and shortens the life of the jar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the processor with parsley, spring onion, bell pepper, onion, celery, garlic, thyme, cloves and chilli. Pulse in short bursts, scraping down twice, until it reads as a coarse wet mince with green and white flecks still separate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the sour orange juice and salt and pulse twice more. Taste: it should be sharp and salty enough to make you blink, because a spoonful has to season a whole pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon into a clean jar, press it flat to drive out air pockets, then float the olive oil over the top until the green is completely sealed under it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Epis is a raw cure, not a sofrito: salt and sour orange pull juice out of the aromatics and hold them there. Blitz it smooth and you shear the parsley cells open, oxidise the lot and watch the jar go khaki within days. Pulse to a coarse mince, seal under oil, and use half at the marinade stage and half in the last five minutes so the fresh top notes survive.","equipment":["Food processor"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","warm-spiced","briny","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Epis%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Epis"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Celery","Bell pepper","Chiles","Lime","Orange","Parsley","Thyme","Clove"]},{"slug":"griot","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg pork shoulder, cut into 4cm cubes with the fat left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"150ml sour orange juice, or 90ml lime juice plus 60ml orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g epis"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, left whole and unpierced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"250ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash the pork in cold water acidulated with half the sour orange juice, rubbing each cube between your hands, then drain and pat dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss with the epis, the rest of the juice, onion, thyme, salt and pepper and leave covered in the fridge; overnight is better than four hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip the lot into a heavy pan with the water, lay the whole chilli on top, cover and hold at a bare tremble until a cube crushes under the back of a spoon with no resistance, 60 to 75 minutes.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":4740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the pork, discard the chilli, and boil the pan liquid down to a dark syrup that just coats a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the cubes on a rack and leave until the surface looks matte and dull rather than wet, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry at 180C (355F) in batches that do not touch, until deep mahogany and the crust crackles when tapped with tongs, 3 to 4 minutes. Toss the hot griot with a spoonful of the reduced glaze.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two cooks, two jobs. The covered simmer holds the pork near 85C (185F) long enough for the collagen to melt into gelatine; the fry only dries and browns two millimetres of surface. Skip the simmer and you get a good crust over rubber. Go straight from pot to oil and the surface water boils instead of browning, so the crust comes out pale and greasy. Rack it until matte first.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Deep frying","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Griot%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Griot"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Chiles","Lime","Orange","Thyme"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"sos-ti-malis","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200g onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g shallots, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g green bell pepper, cut in fine strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 scotch bonnets, seeded and slivered"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"45ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml sour orange juice, or 60ml lime juice plus 40ml orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"6g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sweat the onion, shallot and bell pepper in the oil over medium heat until slack and translucent with no colour at all, about 8 minutes; browning here makes the sauce sweet and muddy.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the garlic, chilli, thyme and cloves and cook only until the garlic smells toasted while still pale.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the tomato paste and keep it moving until it darkens from red to brick and leaves a dry film on the pan floor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Deglaze with the water and half the sour orange juice, then simmer until the onions are silky and the sauce falls off a spoon in a thin sheet, 8 to 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, stir in the remaining juice and the salt. Taste it warm: it should read sharper than you think you want, because it is going onto fried pork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Acid and onion are at war. Citrus added at the start locks the onion's pectin so the slivers stay crunchy however long they cook, and a long boil drives off the volatiles that make sour orange smell like sour orange. Sweat the onions soft first, add half the juice to simmer for body, then the rest off the heat for the raised resinous edge that cuts through griot fat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","umami-deep","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sos%20Ti%20Malis%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sos Ti Malis"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Shallot","Tomato","Bell pepper","Chiles","Lime","Orange","Thyme","Tomato paste","Clove"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"diri-ak-djon-djon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"25g dried djon djon mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"900ml boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g long grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g cooked lima beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g shallots, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g epis"},{"kind":"item","text":"45ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, whole and unpierced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour the boiling water over the mushrooms and leave until the liquid is opaque and inky and the caps have gone pale and spent, about 20 minutes. Strain through damp muslin, squeezing the caps hard, then discard them and measure out 750ml of the black liquor.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the rice through several changes of cold water until the water runs clear, then drain it well. Surface starch left on the grain is what glues the rice into grey clumps instead of separate black ones.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the shallots in the oil until translucent, stir in the epis and cook until the raw parsley smell turns sweet, then add the drained rice and turn it until every grain is slicked and just going translucent at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the measured mushroom liquor with the lima beans, thyme, cloves and salt, lay the whole chilli on the surface and bring to a hard boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"When the liquid has dropped level with the rice and craters open across the top, clamp on a tight lid, drop to the lowest heat and leave 18 minutes untouched. Lift out the chilli and thyme and fork the grains apart.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The black is water soluble and lives in the soaking liquor, not in the flesh. Djon djon caps are woody and stay woody however long you cook them, so the cook squeezes them dry, throws them away and cooks the rice in the ink. Skip the muslin and you get grit, since these mushrooms arrive with the soil they grew in. Keep the scotch bonnet whole and unpierced: perfume without heat.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","fiery","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, real fire that builds and stays, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Diri%20ak%20Djon%20Djon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Diri ak Djon Djon"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Chiles","Mushrooms","Thyme","Rice","Clove"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"legim","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g beef shank or chuck, in 4cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g epis"},{"kind":"item","text":"45ml sour orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g aubergine, peeled and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g chayote, peeled and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300g white cabbage, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g carrot, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g spinach or watercress, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, whole and unpierced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"700ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the beef with half the epis and the sour orange juice and leave it while you peel and cut the vegetables.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the beef hard in the oil in a heavy pot until crusted on two faces, then add the onion and cook until it slumps and lifts the fond off the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the water, thyme, salt and the whole chilli, cover and simmer until the beef pulls apart under a fork, about 75 minutes.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the chayote and carrot, then ten minutes later the aubergine and cabbage, and cook covered until every piece collapses when pressed against the side of the pot. Stir the spinach through at the end.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the beef and the chilli. Crush the vegetables against the pot wall with a wooden spoon until nothing left is bigger than a pea, keeping some texture.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the beef, stir in the remaining epis and the tomato paste and cook uncovered, stirring often, until the mass pulls away from the base in a clean sheet and oil beads back out on the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Legim is judged by what has left the pot, not by what went in. The vegetables are cooked to collapse, crushed by hand, then reduced uncovered until the water is gone and the oil beads back out on top; that separation is the endpoint, not a timing. The classic mistake is a blender, which shears chayote and aubergine into a slick gluey paste that will never dry down.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Legim%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Legim"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Chiles","Spinach","Cabbage","Eggplant","Lettuce/Greens","Orange","Arugula/Watercress","Thyme","Tomato paste"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"soup-joumou","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800g beef shank, in 5cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g epis"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml sour orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2kg giraumon or other dense winter squash, peeled, seeded and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g waxy potatoes, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g carrot, in batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g turnip, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g white cabbage, shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g celery, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g rigatoni or other short pasta"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, whole and unpierced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"15g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the beef with the epis and sour orange juice and leave it while you peel; overnight in the fridge is better.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the beef in the water with the thyme and cloves, skimming the grey foam off in the first ten minutes, until a piece shreds under a fork, about 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the squash and cook until it falls apart at the touch of a spoon. Lift it out, work it to a smooth cream with a ladle of the broth, and stir it back in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the potato, carrot, turnip and celery and lay the whole chilli on top; simmer until a knife meets only slight resistance at the centre of a potato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cabbage and pasta and cook to a shade under done. Lift the chilli out before it can split.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the butter and the salt, then sharpen with lime to taste. The soup should coat the back of a spoon, not cling to it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The body comes from the squash and nothing else: no roux, no flour. Giraumon is dense and low in water, so it breaks to a puree that holds everything else in suspension. Two traps. Pasta keeps drinking in the pot and overnight, so add it late and slightly under. And the scotch bonnet must come out whole; the moment it splits it dumps its seeds and the soup is finished.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","fiery","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, real fire that builds and stays, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Soup%20Joumou%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Soup Joumou"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"squash-summers-water-winters-starch","term":"Squash: Summer's Water, Winter's Starch"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Potato","Carrot","Celery","Chiles","Cabbage","Squash/Pumpkin","Radish/Turnip","Lime","Orange","Thyme","Butter","Pasta","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"pikliz","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g white cabbage, shredded fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g carrot, coarsely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g shallots, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 scotch bonnets, sliced across with the seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"12g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500ml cane or white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"}],"steps":[{"text":"Shred the cabbage no thicker than a coin; thicker than that and it stays raw tasting and softens unevenly in the jar. Toss it with the carrot, shallot, chilli and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the salt in with your hands and leave 20 minutes, until a handful can be squeezed without the shreds snapping. Drain off and discard the liquid that has pooled.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack tight into a clean jar with the thyme, cloves and peppercorns, pressing down hard so nothing sits above the shoulder of the jar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the vinegar and lime until everything is submerged, seal, leave a day at room temperature and then refrigerate. It is sharp at three days and properly itself at a week.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pikliz is a vinegar pickle, not a ferment, so the acid has to stay strong to do the preserving. Cabbage is over ninety percent water: pack it in unsalted and it dilutes the vinegar within a day, the crunch goes and the top of the jar spoils. Salt and drain first, then keep everything under the liquid line. Slice the scotch bonnets across, do not crush them.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","fiery","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, real fire that builds and stays, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pikliz%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pikliz"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Shallot","Carrot","Chiles","Cabbage","Lime","Thyme","Vinegar","Clove"]},{"slug":"tasso-kabrit","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1kg goat leg or shoulder, boned and cut in 3cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml sour orange juice, or 70ml lime juice plus 50ml orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g epis"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, whole and unpierced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"800ml neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the goat with half the sour orange juice, rinse and drain, then marinate cold with the epis, remaining juice, onion, cloves, thyme, salt and pepper for at least four hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip into a heavy pot with the water, lay the chilli on top, cover and simmer until a piece gives to a fork but still holds together as one piece, 60 to 80 minutes.","durationSec":4800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Uncover, remove the chilli and boil hard, turning the meat, until the liquid has gone and the pieces are sticky and coated rather than sitting in a puddle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread on a rack and leave until the surface is dry and tacky to the touch, about 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 175C (350F) in small batches until the edges are dark and brittle and the pieces rattle against the spider, 4 to 5 minutes. Drain and salt while hot.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Goat is lean where pork is not, so tassot is not simply griot with another animal. Take the simmer to fork tender and stop; carry on to falling apart and the fibres separate and the pieces shred into the oil. The dry off on the rack matters more here too, because there is little fat to render out: this crust has to be built by surface dehydration alone.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salted water & the float test","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","fiery","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, real fire that builds and stays, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tasso%20Kabrit%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tasso Kabrit"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Chiles","Lime","Orange","Thyme","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"akra-malanga","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600g malanga, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g epis"},{"kind":"item","text":"40g spring onion, minced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, seeded and minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"8g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"750ml neutral oil for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the peeled malanga on the fine side of a box grater straight into a bowl. Work fast, because the cut flesh greys as it stands, and stop while there is still visible shred rather than a puree.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Do not drain or squeeze the liquid it throws off. Stir in the egg, epis, spring onion, chilli, salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sift in the flour and baking powder and beat hard with a spoon for a full minute, until the batter lightens in colour and holds a soft peak on the back of the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 170C (340F) and drop in heaped dessertspoons, five at a time at most. They should hiss on contact and float within seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry, turning once, until deep gold and craggy and firm when pressed, 3 to 4 minutes. Drain on a rack, salt, and squeeze lime over while they are still hissing.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Malanga's own starch does the binding here; the spoon of flour is insurance, not structure. Grate on the fine side and keep every drop of liquid it throws, because wringing it out the way you would for a potato rosti leaves a batter that disintegrates on contact with the oil. A processor is the other failure: it wrecks the shred and the fritter fries dense instead of lacy.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akra%20Malanga%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Akra Malanga"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Scallion","Chiles","Lime","Flour"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"pen-patat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900g white-fleshed sweet potato, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"220g very ripe banana, mashed"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g butter, melted, plus more for the dish"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml dark rum"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2g grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 star anise"},{"kind":"item","text":"15ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"5g salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the raisins in the rum. Warm the coconut milk with the star anise until it steams, then take it off and leave it to infuse while you grate; fish the anise out before using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the sweet potato on the fine side of a box grater straight into a large bowl, keeping all the liquid. A processor gives a stringy pulp that bakes coarse and fibrous.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the banana, both milks, sugar, melted butter, cinnamon, nutmeg, vanilla, lime zest, salt and the rum with its raisins. The batter should pour like double cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered 25cm dish no more than 5cm deep and bake at 175C (350F) for 60 to 75 minutes, stirring the skin back in twice during the first half hour.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":true},{"text":"It is done when the centre no longer moves as a liquid, the top is deep bronze and a knife comes out coated but clean. Cool it completely before cutting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There are no eggs in this: the set comes entirely from sweet potato starch gelatinising and trapping the milk, which is slow and needs the full hour. Bake it deeper than 5cm and the top scorches while the middle stays a wet slurry. Stir the skin back in twice early on, or the solids sink and leave a custard layer under a crust. It will only slice once it is cold.","equipment":["Food processor","Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","sweet-edged","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pen%20Patat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pen Patat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Haitian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Haitian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Sweet potato","Lime","Banana","Coconut","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Coconut milk","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon","Star anise","Nutmeg"]},{"slug":"masgouf","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole common carp about 2 kg, scaled and gutted through the back"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g tamarind paste, loosened in 60 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, sliced into rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tomatoes, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g amba, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 flatbreads, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"fruitwood or other hardwood logs for the fire"}],"steps":[{"text":"Split the carp down the back, cutting either side of the spine from head to tail but leaving the belly skin joined, and open it flat like a book so the whole inner face lies in one plane.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the oil, tamarind water, turmeric, salt and pepper and brush it hard into the flesh side, then leave the fish open while the fire burns down to glowing embers with only a low flame licking above them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Skewer the fish through the skin on two stakes and stand it upright a hand's width from the fire, skin toward the heat, turning the stakes now and then until the skin is dark, blistered and starting to lift from the flesh, about an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the fish flat, skin down, straight onto the embers and let it steam through until the flesh behind the head flakes cleanly under a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide it onto flatbread, scatter the raw onion and tomato over the top and put the amba on the table beside it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Upright beside the fire, not over it: the fish takes only radiant heat, so the fat that renders drips onto the ground instead of the coals, and nothing flares up to soot the skin. Set it over the flame and the belly fat catches, the skin chars black in a minute and the shoulder is still raw. The skin is the pan here; keep it whole and it holds the flesh together for the final flat rest on the embers.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","earthy","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Masgouf%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Masgouf"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Tamarind","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"dolma-iraqiyya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g medium grain rice, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g minced lamb"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tomatoes, 2 chopped and 2 sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g flat leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dill, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baharat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g tamarind paste, loosened in 500 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 large onions, boiled whole and separated into layers"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 small aubergines, hollowed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 small courgettes, hollowed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 green peppers, seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g vine leaves in brine, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g lamb ribs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the rice, lamb, chopped onion, chopped tomato, tomato paste, parsley, dill, baharat, turmeric, oil and salt with your hands until every grain is stained orange and the mass moves as one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill each vegetable shell and each vine leaf two thirds full and no more, rolling the leaves tight but leaving the parcels slack enough to dent under a thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the lamb ribs and the sliced tomato across the base of a heavy pot, pack the parcels in upright layers so they brace each other, and set a heatproof plate on top to hold them down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the tamarind water down the side of the pot until it reaches the top layer, bring it to a bare simmer, cover and hold it on the lowest heat until a test parcel has no chalk left in the rice, about 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the covered pot off the heat for 10 minutes, then invert it onto a wide tray in one movement so the ribs and tomato come out on top.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"The sour goes in the pot, not over the plate, and it does structural work: acid keeps the pectin in the aubergine and courgette walls from breaking down, so the shells survive ninety minutes of simmering while raw rice swells inside them. Fill past two thirds and the swelling grains split every parcel and cloud the broth with loose starch.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Rolling dolmas","Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","umami-deep","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Rolling dolmas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20roll%20dolmas%20grape%20leaves","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dolma%20Iraqiyya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dolma Iraqiyya"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Zucchini","Eggplant","Parsley","Dill","Rice","Tomato paste","Tamarind","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"quzi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 lamb shoulder on the bone, about 2 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tsp baharat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 dried limes, pierced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cinnamon sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 green cardamom pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g basmati rice, soaked 30 minutes and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g blanched almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g flaked pistachios"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 large flatbreads, to line the platter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the shoulder all over with the baharat, turmeric and salt, set it on a rack in a deep tin and put the dried limes, cinnamon, cardamom, bay and 500 ml water in the tin beneath it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal the tin tightly with foil and cook at 150C (300F) until a skewer slides in with no resistance and the blade bone turns freely in the joint, about 4 hours.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain the tin juices, skim the fat, then cook the drained rice in the measured stock with a spoon of ghee until the surface is pitted with steam holes and no liquid pools at the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion in the remaining ghee until deep brown, then add the almonds and raisins and cook until the nuts are gold and the raisins swollen and glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Uncover the lamb and roast at 220C (425F) until the fat cap is crisp and blistered, then set it on the rice over the flatbread and shower it with the onion, nuts and pistachios.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Four hours under foil is a steam oven, not a roast. The surface stays wet, so the muscle fibres never tighten and wring themselves out before collagen gets its long hold above 70C (160F) and turns to gelatin. Roast it uncovered from the start and you get a grey crust over stringy meat. The blast at the end is for colour only; keep it short and watch it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Blanching & shocking","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","nutty-toasted","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Quzi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Quzi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Lime","Dried fruit","Bay leaf","Rice","Nuts","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"tepsi-baytinijan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 large aubergines, about 1 kg, sliced 1 cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g minced lamb"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated and squeezed dry"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baharat"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 potatoes, sliced 5 mm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tomatoes, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced into rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g tamarind paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"sunflower oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the aubergine slices and leave them 20 minutes until beads of water stand on the cut faces, then blot both sides completely dry.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry them in oil held at 180C (350F), in batches that do not crowd, until deep gold and slack, lifting each slice as soon as the hissing drops off, and drain on paper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the lamb with the grated onion, parsley, baharat and salt until it turns tacky and clings to the bowl, flatten it into discs the width of the aubergine slices and brown them on both sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Colour the potato slices in the same oil, then build the tray: aubergine first, then the kufta discs, potato, tomato, onion rings and garlic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the tomato paste and tamarind into the water with a pinch of salt, pour it down the side of the tray and bake at 190C (375F) until the top slices are blistered and the liquid has drawn down to a glaze, about 45 minutes.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Aubergine flesh is mostly air held in a sponge of thin cells. Frying at a full 180C (350F) collapses that sponge fast and sets the flesh, so the slices go creamy and take up sauce without drinking the tray dry. Slide raw or gently sweated slices in and they act as a wick: the liquid vanishes, the potato scorches and the aubergine stays squeaky.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","umami-deep","bright","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tepsi%20Baytinijan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tepsi Baytinijan"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Eggplant","Parsley","Tomato paste","Tamarind","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out"]},{"slug":"kubba-halab","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g short grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g minced lamb"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g blanched almonds, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baharat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sunflower oil, plus 1.5 litres for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the rice hard with the turmeric and a teaspoon of salt in plenty of water until the grains are soft well past pleasant eating and beginning to break apart, then drain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound or blitz the rice while it is still hot with the cornflour until it becomes a smooth yellow paste that leaves the side of the bowl in one mass, then cover it and keep it warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the onion in the 60 ml oil, add the lamb, baharat, cinnamon and remaining salt and cook until no pink shows and the pan runs dry, then fold through the almonds, raisins and parsley and cool the filling completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With wet hands, hollow a golf ball of paste into a cup, load a spoonful of cold filling, close the seam and roll it between your palms into a torpedo until the surface is unbroken and glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry a few at a time at 175C (350F), turning, until the shells are deep amber and sound hard to a tap, then drain them standing on end.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The shell holds together on gelatinised starch alone, so the rice has to be cooked past soft and worked while hot; once it cools it will not knit and the seams reopen. Any crack is a fault line. Oil rushes in, the steam trapped inside finds a way out, and the kubba splits in the fryer and empties. Roll until the skin is smooth, and keep your hands wet so it never tears.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Blanching & shocking","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","earthy","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, an earthy, grounding base, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kubba%20Halab%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kubba Halab"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Dried fruit","Parsley","Rice","Nuts","Turmeric","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"amba","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg hard green mangoes, peeled and cut into 2 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ground fenugreek"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp yellow mustard seeds, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp mild chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml cider vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the mango with the salt until every cut face is gritty with it, pack it into a clean jar and press down until the fruit's own liquid rises over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Weight the pieces under that brine and leave the jar at room temperature until the liquid turns cloudy, fine bubbles rise when you tap the glass and the smell goes sharp and yoghurt sour, three to five days.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the brine off into a jug, then warm the oil, take it off the heat and stir in the fenugreek, turmeric, ground mustard, chilli and garlic until the paste smells toasted rather than raw and dusty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the spiced oil and the vinegar through the drained mango, add back enough brine to cover the fruit and seal the jar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Amba is a pickle, not a poured sauce. Salt at four percent of the fruit's weight holds off spoilage organisms while lactobacilli work on the mango's sugars, and their acid is what preserves it. Salt lighter and you get white kahm yeast and slumped, mushy fruit; salt much heavier and the ferment never starts, leaving raw salty mango. Keep every piece under the brine. It sharpens over a week in the fridge and keeps a month.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","bright","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Amba%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Amba"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Mango","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Mustard","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"timman-bagilla","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g basmati rice, rinsed until the water runs clear then soaked 1 hour"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g podded broad beans, grey skins slipped off"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dill, thick stalks removed, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ghee, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g salt, for the boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, for the rice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring 3 litres of water to a rolling boil with the 30 g salt, tip in the drained rice and cook uncovered until a grain crushed between finger and thumb is soft at both ends and still chalky at the core, four to six minutes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain it at once and rinse briefly with warm water so the grains stop cooking and stay loose.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour half the melted ghee into a heavy pot, spread a thin even layer of rice across the base, then build the rest into a loose mound, folding in the beans, dill and remaining salt as you go.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Trickle the rest of the ghee over the mound, poke three holes right down to the base with a spoon handle, wrap the lid in a tea towel and clamp it on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam on the lowest heat until the grains stand separate and dry and the base has set into a crisp golden sheet, about 40 minutes, then lift the crust out in pieces with the rice.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Parboiling in a large volume of salted water carries away the loose surface amylose, which is why the finished grains stand apart as needles instead of gluing into a cake. The towel is not fussiness: condensate dripping back off a bare lid soaks the top layer and the base crust never dries out. Pull the rice at chalky, not tender, since it finishes in its own steam.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Timman%20Bagilla%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Timman Bagilla"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dill","Rice"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"margat-bamia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g lamb shoulder, cut into 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g small okra pods, none longer than a finger"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 cloves garlic, peeled and left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g tamarind paste, loosened in 200 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 dried limes, pierced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baharat"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pare the stem cone off each okra pod at a slant, taking the cap without cutting into the seed chamber, and keep the pods whole and bone dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the lamb hard in the oil in a wide pot, lift it out, then soften the onion and whole garlic in the fat until the cloves are gold on every side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the lamb with the tomato paste, baharat, chopped tomato, dried limes, salt and the water, and simmer covered until the meat gives under the edge of a spoon, about 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile spread the okra on a tray and roast at 200C (400F) until the pods look dull, feel dry and no longer squeak against each other, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the okra and the tamarind water into the pot and simmer uncovered until the sauce clings to the pods and the okra is tender but still holds its shape, 20 minutes more.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Okra's mucilage sits inside the seed chamber, so whole pods keep it there: pare the cone off at a slant and never slice into the body. Drying the pods in a hot oven sets the outer cells before they meet liquid, and the tamarind's acid breaks up the long sugar chains that make the slime rope. Cut them into rounds and stew them from the start and you are serving lamb in glue.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","warm-spiced","umami-deep","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Margat%20Bamia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Margat Bamia"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Okra","Lime","Tomato paste","Tamarind","Clove"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"kleicha-bil-tamur","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ghee, melted and cooled to hand warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g fast action dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp ground cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground fennel seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"half tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g soft date paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ghee, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cardamom, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"quarter tsp grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk beaten with 1 tbsp milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp nigella seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the melted ghee into the flour, sugar, cardamom, fennel and salt until the mixture looks like damp sand and holds its shape when squeezed in a fist.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the yeast into the warm milk, work it in to a soft dough that stops sticking to the bowl, and leave it covered until it has puffed and smells faintly of bread, about an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead the date paste with the 40 g ghee, cardamom and nutmeg over a low heat until it is hand warm and drops off the back of a spoon like putty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough into two rectangles 3 mm thick, spread the paste right to the edges, roll each into a tight log and cut into 1.5 cm slices laid cut face up on a lined tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush with the yolk wash, scatter the nigella and bake at 180C (350F) until the dough is set and just straw coloured at the rim, 15 to 18 minutes, then cool on the tray until firm.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":true}],"note":"Loosen the date paste with fat, never water. Ghee plasticises the sugar mass so it spreads over thin dough without dragging and tearing it, whereas water simply turns to steam in the oven, splitting the spirals and weeping syrup onto the tray. Pull them at pale straw as well: the filling is close to pure sugar and it scorches long before the dough looks brown.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Rolling pin","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Rubbing fat into flour","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kleicha%20bil%20Tamur%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kleicha bil Tamur"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Cardamom","Nutmeg","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"shalgham-bil-dibis","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg white turnips, about 6 cm across, trimmed and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 raw beetroot, peeled and quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g date syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"flatbread, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Put the turnips and beetroot into the cold water with the salt and bring them up slowly to a bare tremble, never letting it reach a rolling boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer covered until a skewer passes through a turnip with no catch and the liquid has stained deep pink, about an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the beetroot, stir in the date syrup and simmer uncovered until the liquid thickens enough to coat the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve the turnips hot in shallow bowls with a ladle of the syrup poured over and bread for mopping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Turnip pungency comes from glucosinolates, which only turn hot and sulphurous when the enzyme myrosinase reaches them across a cut surface. Halve them no smaller and start in cold water: the slow climb deactivates the enzyme before it can do much, and the date syrup masks whatever is left. Boil hard instead and the outer layers fluff away long before the centres soften.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shalgham%20bil%20Dibis%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shalgham bil Dibis"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Iraqi%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Iraqi kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beets","Radish/Turnip","Dried fruit"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"escovitch-fish","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole red snapper, about 500 g each, scaled and gutted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml coconut oil or vegetable oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml cane vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, 150 g, sliced into rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, 100 g, cut into matchsticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cho-cho (chayote), peeled and cut into matchsticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 scotch bonnet, sliced into rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g pimento berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, for the pickle"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score each fish to the bone three times a side, rub salt and pepper inside and out, then leave 15 minutes and pat the skin back to a matte, bone dry finish.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C (355F) and fry the fish 5 to 6 minutes a side, until the skin is deep gold and the dorsal fin pulls out clean. Drain on a wire rack, never on paper.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the vinegar with pimento, sugar, salt and thyme for 2 minutes, until the raw vinegar smell softens into something rounder.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drop in onion, carrot, cho-cho and scotch bonnet and count 90 seconds: they should lose their raw squeak and still snap.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the pickle boiling hot over the hot fish, cover loosely and leave to sit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The crust has to survive vinegar, so build it dry: fry fish patted matte, and drain it on a rack. On paper, trapped steam softens the skin before the acid ever reaches it. Pour the pickle boiling over hot fish so it penetrates, but hold the vegetables to 90 seconds in it; longer and they go limp and stay limp through the days the fish keeps. Best after a night in the fridge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deep frying","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Escovitch%20Fish%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Escovitch Fish"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Carrot","Chiles","Berries","Coconut","Thyme","Vinegar","Sugar","Pickles","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"brown-stew-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 kg chicken, jointed into 10 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 scallions, 60 g, bruised and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g pimento berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g dark cane sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 150 g, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red sweet pepper, 120 g, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, 100 g, in coins"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato puree"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole scotch bonnet, stem on and unpunctured"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the chicken with salt, pepper, thyme, scallion, garlic and crushed pimento and leave it to season in the cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the coconut oil until it shimmers, tip in the sugar all at once and watch: it melts, foams amber, then darkens to mahogany and throws its first thin wisp of smoke. At that instant lay in the chicken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn the pieces in the dark oil until every one is lacquered chestnut, then lift them out onto a plate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry onion, sweet pepper, carrot and tomato puree in the same pot until the onion goes slack and the pot smells sweet rather than sharp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the chicken with the water and the whole scotch bonnet, cover and simmer until the gravy coats a spoon and the meat pulls from the bone, about 35 minutes. Lift the pepper out unbroken before it splits.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":2340,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Browning is sugar taken past caramel. Sucrose melts near 160C (320F) and runs amber by 170C; push on to about 190C (375F) and it stops being sweet, polymerising into dark bitter pigment that colours the gravy without sugaring it. Stop at amber and the stew is pale and cloying. Go past the first wisp of smoke and it turns acrid. The chicken going in drops the pan temperature and halts the reaction.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","fiery","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, real fire that builds and stays, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brown%20Stew%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Brown Stew Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Carrot","Chiles","Berries","Coconut","Thyme","Sugar","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"jerk-pork","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg pork shoulder, cut into slabs 3 cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 scotch bonnets, 60 g, stems removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 scallions, 120 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g fresh ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"16 g pimento berries, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml cane vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 good handfuls pimento wood chunks, or pimento berries and bay leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend scotch bonnet, scallion, garlic, ginger, ground pimento, thyme, nutmeg, cinnamon, sugar, soy, vinegar and salt to a coarse dark green paste thick enough to sit on a spoon, then work it hard into the scored pork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build a small fire, let it burn down to grey coals and bank them to one side. Lay soaked pimento wood over the coals and wait until the smoke stops being white and acrid and turns thin and sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the pork on the grate away from the coals, cover, and hold the pit between 110C and 130C (230F to 265F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn the meat every half hour, feeding the fire a little wood each time, until a skewer meets no resistance and the surface has gone black and lacquered.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop across the grain with a cleaver so every piece carries both bark and interior, and serve with the pan juices scraped up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jerk is smoke, not marinade. Pimento wood and its leaves carry the same eugenol as the berries, and over a low fire that arrives on the meat as phenol and tar, which no paste can do. Hold the pit at 110C to 130C (230F to 265F): hotter and the sugar in the rub carbonises to soot long before the shoulder's collagen lets go. Without pimento wood, bury berries and bay leaves in the coals. Season overnight; cook about 3 hours.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Jerk","Grilling over live coals","Resting meat & slicing against the grain","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","fiery","charred","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, real fire that builds and stays, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Jerk","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=jamaican%20jerk%20technique%20pimento","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jerk%20Pork%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jerk Pork"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Chiles","Berries","Thyme","Bay leaf","Soy sauce","Vinegar","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Nutmeg","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"rundown","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g salted mackerel"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 ml coconut milk, first pressing if you have it"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 150 g, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 scallions, 45 g, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cloves garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g pimento berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, 200 g, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole scotch bonnet, stem on and unpunctured"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 green bananas, boiled in their skins, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the mackerel to draw out the salt, then drain, flake into large pieces and pull out the pin bones with your fingers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the coconut milk into a wide uncovered pan and simmer, stirring often, until it thickens and then breaks: the smooth white surface goes curdled and clear oil rises and pools. That break is the run down.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion, scallion, garlic, thyme and pimento in the separated oil until slack and fragrant, then add the tomatoes and cook until they collapse into the custard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the fish and lay the whole scotch bonnet on top; hold at a bare simmer 5 minutes so the flakes warm through without shredding.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the pepper, grind over black pepper, and spoon it over the hot green bananas.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coconut milk is an oil-in-water emulsion held together by protein. Simmered uncovered and stirred, that protein denatures, the emulsion breaks, and clear coconut oil rises out of a curdled custard. The break is the dish; everything after fries in the oil it released. Cover the pan, or add the fish early, and the milk never breaks. You are left with a thin white sauce that tastes of nothing. Soak the mackerel overnight, changing the water twice.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","rich","fiery","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rundown%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rundown"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Banana","Berries","Coconut","Thyme","Milk","Coconut milk","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"ital-ackee-and-callaloo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"540 g tinned ackee, drained, or 30 fresh arils cleaned and parboiled"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g callaloo (amaranth) leaves and stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 120 g, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 scallions, 60 g, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, 180 g, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g pimento berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole scotch bonnet, stem on and unpunctured"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cracked black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Strip the callaloo leaves from the stems, peel the fibrous skin from the thick stems with the back of a knife, and chop the stems small. They cook first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the coconut oil, sweat the onion, scallion, thyme and pimento until translucent, then add the stems, cover, and give them 3 minutes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the leaves and tomatoes with a splash of water and cover; in about 4 minutes they collapse into their own liquid and turn dark green. Sit the whole scotch bonnet on top.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip in the ackee, cover, and shake the pan rather than stir it; 3 minutes, only long enough to heat through.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ital cooking takes no salt, so finish with black pepper only, lift out the pepper whole, and serve with roast breadfruit or boiled food.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Ackee is food only after the pod yawns open by itself on the tree. Unripe arils carry hypoglycin A and a pod forced open early keeps it, so discard the black seed and every scrap of the pink membrane. After that, treat the aril like set custard rather than vegetable: it is curd held in a thin skin, and a stirring spoon turns the whole pan to yellow paste. Shake it instead.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","herb-fresh","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, melting away silken."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ital%20Ackee%20and%20Callaloo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ital Ackee and Callaloo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Berries","Coconut","Thyme","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"festival","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fine yellow cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g granulated sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml water, roughly"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre coconut oil or vegetable oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the cornmeal, flour, sugar, baking powder, salt and nutmeg together, then work in the vanilla and just enough water to make a firm dough that holds a thumbprint without sticking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and rest the dough until it feels smoother and slightly stiffer under the hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 10, and roll each piece between your palms into a tapered cigar about 12 cm long.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 165C (330F) and fry four at a time, turning, for 6 to 8 minutes, until they float high, are deep gold all over and sound hollow when tapped.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain on a rack and serve hot alongside fried fish or jerk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Sugar in the dough colours the crust long before the middle cooks, so the oil sits at 165C (330F) rather than the 180C you would use for chips. Too hot gives a dark shell over wet raw cornmeal. The rest matters as much: cornmeal hydrates slowly, and dough fried straight from the bowl swells and splits in the oil, letting fat into the crack. Give it 30 minutes.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salted water & the float test","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Festival%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Festival"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar","Vanilla","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"bammy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg sweet cassava (yuca), peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp salt, for the soak"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml coconut oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the cassava right down past the pink underskin, split each root and pull out the woody central cord, then grate it finely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap the gratings in a strong cloth and wring hard over a bowl until the juice stops running and what is left feels crumbly and almost dry. Throw the juice away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub in the salt and push the meal through a coarse sieve so it falls as loose crumbs rather than lumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press a fifth of the meal into a 15 cm ring in a dry pan over medium heat and cook about 5 minutes a side, until it knits into a firm pale disc that lifts in one piece and feels dry to the knuckle.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soak the finished bammies in the salted coconut milk for 10 minutes, then fry in coconut oil until spotted gold.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Grated cassava is wrung until the juice stops running, and that pressing is the recipe. The juice carries the bitterness, and in the bitter varieties the cyanogenic glycosides as well; the meal left behind binds on its own starch. Wet meal steams into a gummy disc that never knits. Bammy therefore comes off the pan bone dry by design, which is exactly why it is soaked in coconut milk before it is fried.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bammy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bammy"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"jamaican-curry-powder","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"40 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g fenugreek seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g yellow mustard seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g pimento berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried scotch bonnet or bird pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the coriander, cumin, fenugreek, mustard and pimento in a dry pan over medium heat, shaking constantly, until the coriander is one shade darker and the mustard seeds begin to hop. Pull the pan while it still smells of maple, not of burnt sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip everything onto a cold plate and leave until completely cool; warm seed grinds to a smear rather than a powder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind with the peppercorns and dried pepper to a fine dust, then sift and regrind the tailings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the turmeric and ground ginger without toasting them, and store airtight in the dark.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Turmeric-led and carrying pimento, this is why Jamaican curry tastes of the island and not of Madras. It is also built to be burnt: the powder goes into hot oil on its own and fries until it darkens and stops smelling dusty, about a minute, before any meat touches the pot. Skip that and the finished curry stays chalky, with raw turmeric sitting on top of everything. Use within three months.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","charred","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, char used as punctuation, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20Curry%20Powder%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jamaican Curry Powder"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Chiles","Berries","Mustard","Cumin","Coriander seed","Turmeric","Curry powder","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"sorrel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g dried sorrel sepals, or 500 g fresh"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fresh ginger, sliced and bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g pimento berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 stick cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 whole cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"pared peel of 1 orange"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g sugar, or to taste"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml overproof white rum, optional"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the sepals and pack them into a heatproof jar with the ginger, pimento, cinnamon, cloves and orange peel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the water to a boil, pull it off the heat and let it stop bubbling, around 90C (195F), then pour it over. Cover and leave it alone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain through a cloth once the liquor is deep ruby and tastes tart and spiced, pressing only lightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let it cool, then stir in sugar to taste and the rum if using.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill hard and serve over plenty of ice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Colour and sharpness both come from anthocyanins, which degrade under sustained heat: a rolling boil turns the liquor brown-purple and stewed. Pour water just off the boil over the sepals and walk away instead, 12 to 24 hours at room temperature. Sweeten only after it has cooled, because sugar in hot liquor reads flat and you will always add too much. Press the cloth hard and you get cloud and grit.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sorrel%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sorrel"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Orange","Berries","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"gizzada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g cold butter, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 ml ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g freshly grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fresh ginger, finely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the cold butter into the flour, sugar and salt until it looks like coarse crumbs with flakes of butter still visible, bind with ice water, then wrap and chill until firm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 3 mm, cut 10 cm rounds, and pinch each rim upward at eight points into a crimped wall about 1.5 cm high that stands on its own.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the coconut, brown sugar, water, ginger and nutmeg together, stirring, until the syrup runs off the spoon in a thick thread and a spatula drawn across the pan leaves a track that closes slowly. Stir in the butter and vanilla off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the filling to warm, not hot, then spoon it into the shells to just below the crimp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (355F) for about 25 minutes, until the rim is sandy gold and the filling has darkened and set at the edges. Cool in the tins.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"The tart lives on how far the filling is reduced. Take the syrup to a thick thread, where a spatula track across the pan closes slowly, and it sets around the coconut as it cools. Stop short and the syrup weeps into the pastry within the hour and the base turns to pulp; go further and it crystallises to sand in the shell. Use freshly grated coconut, not desiccated, for the moisture that keeps it chewy.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Pie crust & blind baking","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gizzada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gizzada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Coconut","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"raspeballer","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg floury potatoes, peeled and grated raw on the fine side of a box grater"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g floury potatoes, boiled the day before and mashed cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g barley flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour, plus more if the dough will not hold"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg salted lamb ribs or salted pork belly, soaked in cold water overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g swede, peeled and cut in large chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g salted butter, melted, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wring the grated raw potato hard in a cloth over a bowl until the pulp is barely damp; stand the juice 15 minutes, pour off the grey water and scrape the white starch that has settled underneath back into the pulp.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the cold mash, both flours and the salt until a handful squeezed in the fist holds its shape and does not slump; the dough goes grey as you work, which is only oxidation.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain the soaked meat, cover with the fresh cold water and hold at a shiver, skimming, until it pulls willingly from the bone; boil the swede separately and mash it with a knob of the butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shape balls the size of a fist with wet hands and lower them into the meat broth; hold the pot at 85 to 90C (185 to 195F) until they have risen and sat just under the surface for a further 40 minutes, and a skewer comes out clean.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out with a slotted spoon, serve with the meat and swede, and flood each plate with melted butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The binder is potato starch, not flour. The squeezed juice carries most of it, so let it settle and scrape it back; skip that and you compensate with more flour, which turns the balls to rubber. They hold together only once the surface starch gelatinises, so keep the pot at 85 to 90C (185 to 195F). A rolling boil tears them apart before that skin can form.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Mashing & puréeing","Shaping by hand","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Raspeballer%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Raspeballer"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Lamb","Potato","Butter","Flour","Couscous/Bulgur"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"farikal","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg lamb shoulder and neck on the bone, cut in 4 cm pieces with the fat left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg white cabbage, cut through the root into 3 cm wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g whole black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"14 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg small waxy potatoes, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay the fattiest pieces of lamb fat side down to cover the base of a heavy pot, then a layer of cabbage wedges, and scatter flour, salt and peppercorns over; repeat the layers and finish with cabbage.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the water down the side, cover, and bring up slowly until you hear it ticking rather than knocking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold at about 90C (195F) for 2 to 2 and a half hours, without stirring, until a piece of meat gives under the back of a spoon and the cabbage is translucent but the wedges are still wedges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tilt the pot and spoon off the clear fat, taste for salt, and serve with boiled potatoes and plenty of the liquor.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Four ingredients and a long hold. The cabbage gives up most of the liquid, so barely any water goes in, and the flour dusted between layers binds that liquor instead of thickening it. Keep the pot near 90C (195F): a boil emulsifies the rendered lamb fat into the broth so it turns greasy and grey, and the wedges collapse to string. Peppercorns stay whole because ground pepper goes bitter over two hours.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=F%C3%A5rik%C3%A5l%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fårikål"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Potato","Cabbage","Flour"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"lutefisk","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg dried stockfish, cod, sawn into 15 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 litres cold water, for the soaks and rinses"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 litres cold water, for the lye bath"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g food grade sodium hydroxide, for a 1 percent bath"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coarse salt, for drawing water before baking"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g smoked bacon, cut in lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg almond or other waxy potatoes, boiled"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g coarse mustard, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the stockfish in a plastic or stainless tub of cold water at 4 to 8C, changing the water daily, for 5 days, until it has swollen to about three times its dry thickness and a corner gives under a fingernail.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Wearing gloves and eye protection, add the sodium hydroxide to the 10 litres of cold water and never the reverse, since it heats sharply; submerge the fish and hold at cool room temperature for 2 days, until the flesh is translucent, slippery and wobbles like set jelly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the fish out with tongs into fresh cold water and soak a further 4 to 6 days, changing twice a day, until it smells of nothing, feels firm rather than soapy to the fingers, and a poached scrap tastes clean with no burn at the back of the throat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Salt the drained fish and leave 25 minutes; water beads on the surface and the flesh tightens.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse, lay skin down in a covered dish and bake at 200C (390F) for 10 to 12 minutes, until the flakes part under a fork and are white and glossy; a minute past that and it slumps.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the bacon until crisp and pour it over with its fat; serve with potatoes and mustard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lye takes the fish apart on purpose. At around pH 12 it hydrolyses protein and saponifies fat, so rehydrated stockfish swells past 90 percent water and sets as a gel. Two failures: left too long in the bath it dissolves to slime, and given too short a freshwater soak afterwards it holds residual alkali, which is caustic, soapy and unsafe. Soak until it is neutral, then bake briefly, because the protein is already half undone.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Soaking dried goods back to life","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The pretzel bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pretzel%20lye%20baking%20soda%20bath%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lutefisk%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lutefisk"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"smoked-fish-hot-vs-cold","term":"Smoked Fish: Hot vs. Cold"},{"slug":"stainless-clad-tri-ply","term":"Stainless Clad (Tri-Ply)"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","White fish","Potato","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"plokkfiskur","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g haddock or cod fillet, skinned and pin boned"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g floury potatoes, boiled in their skins the day before and left cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml whole milk, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"dark rye bread and cold salted butter, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the fish in barely trembling salted water until the flakes separate under light pressure, about 5 minutes; lift out and leave to drain.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion in the butter over low heat until translucent and sweet with no colour, stir in the flour and cook it out until it smells of biscuit rather than raw paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the warm milk in three additions, whisking each one smooth, until the sauce coats the back of a spoon; season heavily with salt and white pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peel the cold potatoes and break them into rough 2 cm lumps by hand, then fold them and the flaked fish through the sauce off the heat, in as few turns as you can manage.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm through gently until steaming but not bubbling, and serve piled on dark rye with cold butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Everything here is already cooked; the work is binding it without turning it to paste. Use potatoes boiled the day before, since their starch has retrograded and the lumps hold when folded. Hot fresh potato smears, releases amylose and the pan goes glue-like. Fold off the heat and count the turns, because each one breaks more flakes. Season hard with white pepper: this is milk, potato and lean fish, with nothing else to lean on.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Plokkfiskur%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Plokkfiskur"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Onion","Potato","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"kjotsupa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg lamb shoulder and neck on the bone, cut in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g swede, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g carrot, in 2 cm rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g potato, in 3 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g rolled oats"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g white cabbage, in wide strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g dried wild thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"16 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the lamb with the cold water and bring it up slowly without stirring, skimming the grey foam from the surface until what rises is clean and white.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Salt, add the onion and swede, and hold at about 90C (195F) for an hour, until the meat is loose on the bone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the carrot, potato, oats and thyme and simmer 20 minutes, until a knife slides into a potato with no resistance and the broth has thickened enough to cling to a spoon.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop in the cabbage for the last 7 minutes so it keeps its sweetness and a little bite.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest off the heat 10 minutes, lift the fat that pools, correct the salt, and ladle broth, meat and roots into the same bowl.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Start the lamb in cold water. Soluble albumin coagulates slowly as the pot warms and rises as a scum you can lift off; drop the meat into hot water and the same protein disperses instead, so the broth stays cloudy and tastes muddy however long it cooks. The oats are the thickener, leaching amylose to give body without flour. Stage the roots by density and put the cabbage in last, or everything under it goes to pulp.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kj%C3%B6ts%C3%BApa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kjötsúpa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Potato","Carrot","Cabbage","Thyme","Bread","Oats"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"rugbraud","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g wholemeal rye flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g dark cane syrup or molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"9 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g butter, for the tin"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the two flours, sugar, bicarbonate of soda and salt together thoroughly; any lump of soda left whole will taste soapy in the slice it lands in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the syrup and buttermilk to a thick pourable batter the texture of slack porridge, but do not beat it, since no gluten is wanted here.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered 1.5 litre tin, filling it two thirds, and seal it tightly with a buttered lid or a double layer of foil so the loaf steams rather than crusts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand the tin in a roasting tray of hot water and bake at 100C (210F) for 9 to 12 hours, until the loaf has pulled from the sides and the crumb at a skewer is near black and springs back.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool completely in the tin, wrapped, before slicing thin; cut warm it drags and tears.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is a steam, not a bake. Sealed at 100C (210F) for nine hours or more, the rye's own amylase keeps converting starch to sugar, and those sugars brown slowly, so the near black colour and the malty sweetness are made in the tin rather than poured into it. Bake it uncovered or hot and fast and you get a dry crust over a gummy middle: rye has no gluten net, and its pentosans need hours of wet heat to set.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=R%C3%BAgbrau%C3%B0%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rúgbrauð"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar"]},{"slug":"skyr","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 litres skimmed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g live skyr from a previous batch, or shop bought with live cultures"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 drops vegetarian rennet, stirred into 30 ml cool water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml double cream, to loosen (optional)"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g blueberries or crowberries, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the milk to 88C (190F) in a heavy pan, stirring so it does not catch, hold it there 10 minutes, then cool to 38C (100F) in a sink of cold water.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the live skyr until no streaks remain, add the diluted rennet and stir for no more than 30 seconds; too much movement now and the curd will never knit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and hold at 36 to 38C (97 to 100F) for 4 to 5 hours, until it has set to a clean custard that shears off a spoon in a sharp edge and beads a little clear whey.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill the set curd, then ladle it into a muslin lined sieve and drain in the fridge for 8 to 12 hours, until it has lost about three quarters of its volume and holds the ridge of a spoon.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat smooth with a splash of milk or the cream to the thickness you want, and keep the drained whey for baking; serve cold with berries.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Skyr is a strained fresh cheese, not a yoghurt: it is set with a trace of rennet as well as culture, then drained until four litres of skimmed milk have become about a kilo. The 88C (190F) hold before culturing denatures the whey proteins so they trap water in the curd; skip it and the skyr weeps and eats chalky. Incubate at 37C (99F) and no hotter, or the acid overshoots and the curd turns grainy and sour.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Skyr%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Skyr"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Berries","Milk","Cream"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"brunost","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"5 litres fresh sweet whey, drained from a cheese set with vegetarian rennet"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"neutral oil, for the mould"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the whey hard in a wide pan, skimming the pale foam of albumin that rises in the first 20 minutes, until it has reduced to about a fifth and the colour has gone from water to straw.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cream and milk and carry on reducing, now stirring often and scraping the corners, until it is a thick fudge that holds an open channel when you draw the spoon across the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat, stand it in a sink of cold water, and beat hard as it cools to about 60C (140F); it will lighten in colour, stiffen and lose its shine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack while still warm into an oiled mould, pressing out air pockets, then cool and chill overnight until firm enough to take a wire.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shave paper thin over dark bread, or melt two or three slices into a pan sauce for game.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brunost is not cheese; the curd was taken out long before. What is left in whey is lactose, whey protein and minerals, and boiling it down browns that sugar and drives it into the protein, so the colour and the caramel tang are Maillard rather than anything added. Two failures: unstirred at the end it scorches bitter in seconds, and cooled without beating the lactose sets in large crystals and the block eats gritty.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, melting away silken."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brunost%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Brunost"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"brunost-gjetost","term":"Brunost (Gjetost)"}],"pantryItems":["Milk","Cream","Cheese (any)"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"rommegrot","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre cultured sour cream, at least 35 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml whole milk, warmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar mixed with 5 g ground cinnamon, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the sour cream to a bare simmer in a heavy pan and hold it there, stirring, for 10 minutes, until it loosens and the surface begins to gleam.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sift in half the flour and beat hard, keeping the pan at a simmer, until clear yellow fat rises and pools; spoon it into a warm jug as it appears and keep going until no more comes, which should give you 150 to 250 ml.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the rest of the flour, cook it 3 minutes, then let the porridge down with warm milk a ladle at a time until it just falls from the spoon; salt it.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 5 minutes more, until the raw flour taste has gone and the gloss comes back.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve in warm bowls with a well of the reserved butter in each and cinnamon sugar scattered over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The dish is a controlled break. Flour starch gelatinises and takes the water, forcing butterfat out of the cultured cream so you can skim it, and that skimmed butter poured back on top is the whole point of eating it. It needs real cultured cream at 35 percent fat or more; low fat soured cream carries gums that hold the emulsion together, so no butter renders and you get wallpaper paste. Hold a bare simmer, since a boil curdles it grainy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=R%C3%B8mmegr%C3%B8t%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rømmegrøt"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Flour","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"kleinur","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g hjartarsalt (ammonium bicarbonate), crushed to a powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g butter, melted and cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, about 100 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres neutral oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the crushed hjartarsalt into the milk and leave 5 minutes; the smell of ammonia is correct and it will cook away.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, sugar, salt, cardamom and zest, work in the butter and eggs, then the milk, to a soft dough just short of sticky; knead 30 seconds only and rest it cold for half an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 4 mm thick, cut diamonds about 10 by 5 cm, slit each one down the middle and pull one long point through the slit so it twists back on itself; press the point flat so it cannot open in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry a few at a time at 180C (355F), turning once, until they have puffed and gone deep gold, about 90 seconds a side.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack rather than paper, and eat the same day; they go leathery as soon as they take up humidity.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hjartarsalt is ammonium bicarbonate, and above about 60C (140F) it decomposes completely into gas and water, leaving no salts behind. That is why kleinur eat dry and crisp where baking powder would give a soft, faintly soapy cake crumb. The catch is that the ammonia has to escape, so this works only rolled thin; in a thick piece it stays trapped and the middle reeks. Fry at 180C (355F), cooler and they drink oil.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kleinur%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kleinur"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"ocopa-arequipena","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 dried ají mirasol (about 30 g), stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g huacatay leaves (about 8 sprigs), or 2 tbsp huacatay paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red onion, in thick slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g roasted unsalted peanuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g plain soda crackers (about 3)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g queso fresco"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g yellow potatoes, boiled and peeled, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 hard-boiled eggs and 8 black olives, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the mirasol with boiling water and leave until the skins swell and go leathery-soft, about 20 minutes; drain and tear into strips.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion, garlic and drained chilli in the oil over medium heat until the onion edges brown and the chilli smells sweet and toasted rather than dusty, about 8 minutes. Cool to warm.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the fried base with peanuts, crackers, queso fresco and half the milk in short bursts, stopping as soon as it is smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop in the raw huacatay and pulse two or three times only, until the sauce is pale green and just coats a spoon; loosen with the rest of the milk and salt it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon over warm potatoes at room temperature and finish with egg and olives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Huacatay is not mint but a marigold, and its resin is volatile: cook it and the sauce turns bitter and tarry. So the onion, garlic and mirasol are fried first, and the huacatay goes in raw at the blender with the cheese, peanuts and cracker. Blend in short bursts; a long spin warms the sauce and the fat weeps out of the cheese.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","aromatic","rich","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, aromatics blooming from the first bite, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ocopa%20Arequipe%C3%B1a%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ocopa Arequipeña"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Olives","Milk","Cheese (any)","Peanut","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"chuno-phuti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g chuño negro"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g red onion, fine dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ají amarillo paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g queso fresco, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp chopped huacatay, or flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the chuño in plenty of cold water overnight, changing the water twice, until a piece squeezed between finger and thumb gives all the way through with no hard core.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub handfuls under the water so the black skins slip off, then wring each piece dry like a sponge until no more water runs; chop into rough 1 cm pieces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, garlic and ají in the oil until the onion is translucent and the paste no longer smells raw.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chuño and toss until every piece is coated and steaming, then pour the eggs over and stir slowly until just set and still glossy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat fold through the cheese and herb, season, and serve at once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Chuño is potato freeze-dried on the altiplano: frozen at night, trodden by day, sun-dried until it rattles. The cells are already collapsed, so it rehydrates spongy and never floury. Twelve hours in cold water with two changes is not optional; short-soaked chuño stays chalky and acrid at the centre. Wring each piece out, or the water it holds will boil the eggs instead of scrambling them.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Soaking dried goods back to life","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chu%C3%B1o%20Phuti%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chuño Phuti"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Parsley","Cheese (any)","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"chairo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g lamb neck on the bone, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g chalona (dried salted lamb), or 150 g more fresh lamb"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g peeled wheat (trigo pelado), or pearl barley"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g chuño negro"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole dry chuño, kept back for grating"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red onion, fine dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ají panca paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ají amarillo paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g lard or oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g yellow potatoes, 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot (100 g), diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g podded habas"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g mint or huacatay, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the chalona and the 150 g of chuño in separate bowls of cold water overnight; next day rinse the chalona and tear it into strips, and rub the skins off the chuño, squeeze it dry and chop it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the lamb in the lard until the edges are properly dark, then add onion, garlic and both ají pastes and fry until the paste turns brick red and oil beads at the side of the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the water, add the wheat and chalona, and hold at a bare tremble, lid ajar, for 1 to 2 hours, until the grains have split open and the lamb slides off the bone.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add potato, carrot, habas and the chopped soaked chuño and cook until a knife meets no resistance in the potato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the two dry chuño straight into the pot over the last five minutes, stirring, until the broth thickens enough to cling to the back of a spoon; finish with oregano and mint off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"At 3,800 m water boils near 88C (190F), so collagen unwinds slowly and highland pots run for hours. Raising the heat does not fix that, it only wrings the lamb dry. The grated dry chuño is the thickener: its collapsed starch swells well below a rolling boil, where flour would stay raw. Sprinkle it in over the last five minutes; dumped in early it sets into grey lumps and the soup turns to paste.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","aromatic","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chairo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chairo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Carrot","Mint","Oregano","Couscous/Bulgur","Clove"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"olluquito-con-charqui","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g ollucos"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g charqui (dried salted beef or llama)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red onion, fine dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp ají amarillo paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ají panca paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml oil or lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g white rice, cooked, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the charqui in cold water for 2 hours, changing it twice, then pound it flat with a mallet or a stone and pull it apart into fine threads along the grain.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the ollucos into 5 mm matchsticks and leave them dry; do not rinse them once cut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry onion, garlic and both ají pastes in the oil until the paste darkens to brick and the oil separates at the edge of the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the charqui threads to coat, then the ollucos, cover, and cook over low heat, stirring only twice, for 12 to 15 minutes, until they slump slightly but still snap when bitten.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add oregano, taste for salt at this point and not before, and serve over rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Olluco is not a potato: its starch sits in a mucilage that heat releases as free water. At fifteen minutes it still snaps; much past that it slumps into a slippery pool. So no stock goes into the pan, the lid stays on only at the start, and you stir twice rather than constantly. The charqui brings its own salt into the pot, so season at the end or you are stuck with it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Olluquito%20con%20Charqui%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Olluquito con Charqui"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Oregano","Rice","Clove"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"rocoto-relleno-arequipeno","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 rocoto peppers (about 800 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sugar and 100 ml red wine vinegar, for the blanching water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g fatty beef mince"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red onion, fine dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ají panca paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g roasted peanuts, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 hard-boiled eggs, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 black olives, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g queso fresco, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g yellow potatoes, sliced 3 mm"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g lard or oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut a lid from each rocoto and scrape out every seed and all the pale ribs with a teaspoon; that is where the heat lives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil a pan of water with a third of the sugar, vinegar and a little salt, give the rocotos and lids 3 minutes, then drain and repeat twice more with fresh water each time, until the flesh is pliable but still stands up and the burn has dropped to a warm hum.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry onion, garlic, panca, cumin and oregano in the lard, add the mince and cook until the water has gone and the pan looks glossy rather than wet; off the heat fold in peanuts and raisins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill each rocoto, press in a slice of egg and an olive, cap with cheese and set the lid back on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the potato slices with the rest of the cheese in a buttered dish, whisk the eggs into the milk with salt and pour it over, sit the rocotos on top and bake at 180C (350F) for about 45 minutes, until the custard is set and the cheese caps are blistered.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rocoto's heat is concentrated in the white placenta and seeds, so scraping those out does most of the work. Capsaicin barely dissolves in water and no amount of boiling will chase what remains in the wall. Three short blanches in fresh sweetened, soured water draw a little more out each time and season the flesh. One long boil instead leaves the pepper just as hot and collapses it into a rag.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Blanching & shocking","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","sweet-edged","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rocoto%20Relleno%20Arequipe%C3%B1o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rocoto Relleno Arequipeño"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Olives","Dried fruit","Oregano","Milk","Cheese (any)","Peanut","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"adobo-arequipeno","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg pork shoulder, in 4 cm chunks, fat left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"750 ml chicha de jora, or 500 ml dark beer with 3 tbsp red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp ají panca paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 red onions (300 g), in thick wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 rocoto, seeded and sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the pork with the chicha, panca, garlic, cumin, oregano, salt and pepper and leave it covered in the cold overnight; the meat should come out stained red through the outer centimetre.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the pork out, pat it dry, and brown it hard in the lard in batches until each face is crusted; a wet piece will only grey.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return everything with the marinade, add the onion wedges and rocoto, and bring it to the point where one bubble breaks at a time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold it there with the lid ajar for about 2 hours, until a spoon parts the meat without pressure and the onion has gone to threads.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest off the heat for 10 minutes, taste for salt and serve in bowls with the red fat still floating and bread to drag through it.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Chicha de jora is maize beer: sugars for colour and a lactic tang near pH 4 that swells collagen and shortens the braise. The failure here is heat, not time. Above a lazy tremble the pork fibres contract and wring out their water faster than the collagen turns to gelatine, so the meat ends dry inside a wet pot. Dark beer with a splash of vinegar stands in, thinner but honest.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"dish":{"label":"Panlasang Pinoy’s killer adobo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aoc1_LwKaUg","sub":"★ A canon film: verified"},"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Adobo%20Arequipe%C3%B1o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Adobo Arequipeño"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Garlic","Oregano","Vinegar","Wine","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"pachamanca-a-la-olla","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g pork ribs, cut into pairs"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 bone-in chicken thighs (about 600 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g huacatay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g chincho leaves, or a further 20 g huacatay"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g mint leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp ají panca paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp ají amarillo paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml chicha de jora, or dark beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg yellow potatoes, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g sweet potatoes, halved lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g habas in the pod"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 ears of corn, husks kept"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 banana leaves, or the reserved corn husks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the huacatay, chincho, mint, garlic, both ají pastes, cumin, oregano, salt and half the chicha to a coarse green paste, rub it into the pork and chicken and leave them in the cold for 4 hours.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a heavy pot with softened banana leaves so they hang over the rim, then stack in order: ribs on the base, chicken on top of them, potatoes and sweet potatoes above, corn and habas last.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the remaining chicha down the side, fold the leaves over the top, lay a damp cloth across the rim and clamp the lid on so no steam escapes at the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over the lowest flame for about 90 minutes without lifting the lid; the pot should hiss faintly and smell of huacatay, not of scorching.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 10 minutes, then unstack in reverse onto a board and serve with ocopa or a rocoto salsa.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"This is an earth oven turned inside out: almost no liquid, so everything cooks in herb steam that condenses back onto the food instead of escaping up a chimney. The seal does the work. Too much liquid and the marinade washes off into a grey broth; a loose lid and the pot runs dry and scorches through the leaves. A finger of chicha, a damp cloth under the lid, the lowest flame, and no peeking.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pachamanca%20a%20la%20Olla%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pachamanca a la Olla"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Garlic","Potato","Sweet potato","Corn","Banana","Mint","Oregano","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"cuy-chactado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 cuy, dressed and butterflied (about 700 g), or 1 rabbit (1.2 kg), jointed"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g fine maize flour, or chuño flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml lard or neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g yellow potatoes, boiled, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g mote (cooked hominy), to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 rocoto, 1 red onion and the juice of 2 limes, for the salsa"}],"steps":[{"text":"Open the cuy out flat and press down on the ribcage with the heel of your hand until it cracks and the whole animal lies at an even thickness.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub it inside and out with garlic, cumin and salt and leave it an hour; wipe off the surface moisture before it goes near the fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the lard in a wide pan to 170C (340F), dredge the cuy in maize flour and press the flour on so it clings in a thin, even coat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay it skin down and set a foil-wrapped flat stone or a heavy pan on top; fry until the skin is amber and audibly crisp at the edges, then turn, weight again and fry until a skewer slides into the thigh without resistance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack rather than paper, and serve with potatoes, mote and a sharp rocoto and onion salsa.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The stone is the recipe. Cuy is thin and bony and its skin shrinks and curls as it sets, so an unweighted animal arches: shoulders burn while thighs stay raw. Pressed flat, every part of the skin stays in contact with the lard and dehydrates evenly into crackling. Keep the fat between 165 and 175C (330 to 350F); below that the maize crust drinks it and turns to leather. A jointed rabbit takes the same treatment.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cuy%20Chactado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cuy Chactado"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Potato","Corn","Lime","Butter","Flour","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"quinua-atamalada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g white quinoa"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red onion, fine dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp ají amarillo paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g yellow potatoes, 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g queso fresco, in cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp chopped huacatay, or mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rinse the quinoa in a fine sieve, rubbing the grains between your palms under cold water, until the water runs clear and stops foaming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion, garlic and ají amarillo in the oil until the onion is translucent and the paste has darkened and lost its raw edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the quinoa, potato and water, season, cover and simmer until each grain has uncoiled its white germ into a ring and the potato crushes against the side of the pan, about 18 minutes.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Uncover and stir steadily with a wooden spoon until it thickens to the point where the spoon leaves a trail that closes slowly behind it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat, fold in the milk, cheese and huacatay, and serve as soon as the cheese has softened without stringing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Quinoa's seed coat carries saponins, bitter glycosides that foam like soap and do not cook away; rinse and rub until the water runs clear or the whole pot tastes of washing up. Atamalada means thickened like tamal batter, and that body comes from working the starch out of the grain at the end, not from flour. Add the milk and cheese off the heat: on it, the cheese ropes and the milk catches on the base.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Quinua%20Atamalada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Quinua Atamalada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Potato","Mint","Milk","Cheese (any)","Clove"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"queso-helado-arequipeno","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 L whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g finely grated coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cinnamon sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"a pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"ground cinnamon, to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm both milks with the coconut, cinnamon sticks and cloves to the point where the surface shivers, then take the pan off and leave it 20 minutes, until it smells strongly of cinnamon and the coconut has swelled.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with the sugar and salt, temper in a ladle of the hot milk, then return everything to low heat and stir until it coats the back of the spoon and a finger leaves a clean line, about 82C (180F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fish out the cinnamon and cloves, leaving the coconut in, cool the custard over ice and chill it thoroughly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the shallowest metal tray you own and freeze, scraping the set edges into the middle with a fork every 30 minutes, four or five times, until it is stiff and finely crystalline.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scoop into cold glasses and dust heavily with ground cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no cheese in it; it is named for the pale round it used to be frozen into. Yolk and milk fat get in the way of ice crystals, but only while the mixture keeps moving, which is why it is scraped rather than churned. Left to freeze undisturbed it sets into a solid block with the coconut settled at the bottom. A shallow tray freezes fast and keeps the crystals fine.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Queso%20Helado%20Arequipe%C3%B1o%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Queso Helado Arequipeño"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20Highlands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian Highlands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Milk","Cheese (any)","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"zakwas-zytni-na-zurek","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g wholemeal rye flour, coarse if you can get it"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water, boiled and cooled to lukewarm"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, peeled and lightly crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 crust of stale rye bread, optional"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarse salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the rye flour into the lukewarm water in a scalded 1 litre jar until no dry lumps remain; it should pour like thin cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bury the garlic, bay, allspice and salt in the batter and float the bread crust on top if you are using one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover the jar with a cloth and a rubber band, never a sealed lid, and stand it at around 22C (72F) out of draughts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir once a day with a clean wooden spoon. By day three or four it should smell of sour apples and buttermilk and taste sharply acid, never bitter or solventy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Refrigerate once the sourness bites. It keeps six weeks, settling into cloudy sediment under a clear liquor that you shake back together before use.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rye carries its own lactobacilli and enough pentosan gum to hold the starch in suspension; wheat will not sour the same way. Cover loosely so gas escapes but the surface stays wet. Fuzzy pink or blue growth means it went aerobic and warm: discard it, do not stir it in. Below 18C (64F) it stalls and turns bitter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","warm-spiced","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zakwas%20%C5%BBytni%20na%20%C5%BBurek%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zakwas Żytni na Żurek"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Berries","Bay leaf","Bread","Flour","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"zakwas-buraczany","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg raw beetroot, firm and unblemished"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water, boiled and cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"24 g fine sea salt, about 2 percent of the water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 slice of rye bread, optional starter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scrub the beetroot hard but do not peel it; the skin carries the lactobacilli that start the ferment. Trim the tops and any soft patches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into 1 cm slabs and pack into a scalded 2 litre jar with the garlic, bay and allspice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve the salt in the cooled water, pour it over until the beetroot sits 2 cm under, and weight it down with a saucer or a brine filled bag.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with a cloth at 20 to 22C (68 to 72F). By day four or five it should foam gently, smell earthy and sour, and taste like a savoury drink rather than raw beet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain into bottles and refrigerate. The colour holds about three weeks, then slides towards brown.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two ferments, two mechanisms. The rye sour runs on flour starch; this one runs on the beet's own sugars, and salt near 2 percent is what keeps it lactic instead of putrid. Betanin is the fragile part: it browns with heat and above pH 5, so never boil zakwas, and stir it into hot soup off the heat at the very end.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Fermentation","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zakwas%20Buraczany%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zakwas Buraczany"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Beets","Berries","Bay leaf","Bread","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"zurek-na-zakwasie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml rye zakwas, shaken to lift the sediment"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g biała kiełbasa, raw white sausage"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked boczek or streaky bacon, in lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g waxy potatoes, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 1 carrot and 1 parsley root, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp dried marjoram, crumbled between the palms"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 allspice berries and 2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml soured cream, 18 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, boiled 8 minutes and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the bacon in a dry pot until the fat runs clear and the pieces are amber, then add the onion, carrot and parsley root and let them take colour at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the water, allspice, bay and the whole sausage and hold at a bare shiver for 25 minutes, until the sausage is firm and the broth tastes of more than water.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the sausage out, cut it into coins, and cook the potatoes in the broth until a knife slides through without resistance.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pot off the heat. Shake the zakwas until the white sediment is suspended, stir it in, then return to the barest simmer for 8 minutes so the raw rye taste cooks out.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true},{"text":"Temper the soured cream with a ladle of hot soup, stir it back with the garlic and marjoram, and serve over the halved eggs and sausage without letting it boil again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sour is lactic acid near pH 3.5, and that is exactly what curdles soured cream dropped straight into a hot pot, so temper it. The zakwas also carries raw rye starch, the body of the soup: it needs a few minutes at a simmer to gelatinise, but a hard boil blows off the volatile acids and leaves it flatly sharp. Garlic and marjoram go in last for the same reason.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","warm-spiced","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=%C5%BBurek%20na%20Zakwasie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Żurek na Zakwasie"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Sausage","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Carrot","Berries","Parsley","Oregano","Bay leaf","Cream","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"zrazy-zawijane","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g beef topside, in 8 slices about 1 cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp sharp brown mustard, musztarda sarepska for preference"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked bacon, in thin strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ogórki kiszone, salt fermented cucumbers, in batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, halved and thinly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour for dusting"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g lard or clarified butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves and 4 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dried marjoram"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml soured cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat each slice between sheets of plastic out to 5 mm, working from the centre outward so the fibres stretch instead of tearing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread with mustard, season, lay on bacon, cucumber batons and a little raw onion, then roll along the grain and tie or pin closed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust the rolls in flour and brown them hard in the lard on every side, lift them out, and soften the rest of the onion in the same fat until sweet and tan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the rolls, pour in stock to come two thirds up, add bay and allspice, cover, and hold at 90C (194F) in the oven for 2 hours until a skewer meets no resistance.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the rolls out, reduce the liquor by a third, whisk in the soured cream and marjoram off the boil, and slide the rolls back to warm through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Topside is lean and full of collagen, so only a long hold just under a simmer turns that collagen to gelatin without wringing the muscle fibres dry. The fermented cucumber is structural, not garnish: its acid loosens collagen from the inside and seasons the sauce. Make them the day before. Cooled and gently reheated, the gelatin sets, the sour spreads through the meat and the sauce thickens on its own.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","Keeping the cooking liquid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","tangy-ferment","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zrazy%20Zawijane%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zrazy Zawijane"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Bacon","Onion","Cucumber","Berries","Oregano","Bay leaf","Butter","Cream","Flour","Stock/Broth","Mustard","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"kotlet-schabowy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 boneless pork loin chops, 150 g each, cut across the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, beaten with 1 tbsp water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g fine dry white breadcrumbs, bułka tarta"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g lard, or clarified butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Fine salt and white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Nick through the rim of fat and silverskin at 2 cm intervals all round each chop.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the chops between plastic to an even 5 mm, thinner at the edges than the middle, then salt both sides and leave 10 minutes until the surface looks damp.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pass through flour, then egg, then crumbs, pressing only lightly; crumbs packed down glue themselves to the meat and turn to sand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the lard 1 cm deep to 170C (338F). Slide in one cutlet and tilt the pan so hot fat washes over the top; it should sizzle steadily, not roar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry about 3 minutes a side to deep gold, then rest on a wire rack, never on paper, so the underside stays crisp.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Pork loin is lean and at 5 mm it is cooked in roughly six minutes, so the crumb sets the schedule, not the meat. Below 160C (320F) the coating drinks fat and goes greasy; above 185C (365F) it darkens before the centre is warm. Nicking the fat rim matters more than it sounds: the connective tissue there shrinks first and cups the cutlet off the pan, leaving a pale ring of raw crumb.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kotlet%20Schabowy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kotlet Schabowy"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Breadcrumbs/Panko"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"golabki-w-sosie-pomidorowym","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 head white cabbage, about 1.6 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g minced pork, or half pork and half beef"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g short grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp dried marjoram"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml passata or sieved tinned tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml light stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"Salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut a cone out of the cabbage core and lower the whole head into boiling salted water, peeling leaves away as they slacken, roughly one a minute, until you have sixteen.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the rice for 8 minutes only, until the grains are still chalky at the centre, and drain.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sweat the onion in the lard until translucent, cool it, then work it into the mince with the rice, egg, marjoram and plenty of salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shave the thick rib of each leaf flat with a knife, sit a spoonful of filling at the stem end, fold the sides in and roll tight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the rolls seam down in a heavy pot, add stock and bay to come halfway up, weight them with a plate, cover and cook at 160C (320F) for 75 minutes until a leaf gives to a spoon.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":4740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Make a pale roux with the butter and flour, whisk in the pot juices and the passata, season with the sugar and salt, and pour it back over the rolls to glaze.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is not the Hungarian version: sweet cabbage rather than soured leaves, and no paprika anywhere. The rice goes in half cooked because a parcel is a closed system with almost no free water; rice that still needs to hydrate takes it from the meat and splits the leaf. Shaving the rib is the other half of the job, since an unshaved rib springs back and unrolls the parcel in the pot.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Making a roux","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Go%C5%82%C4%85bki%20w%20Sosie%20Pomidorowym%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gołąbki w Sosie Pomidorowym"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Ground meat","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Cabbage","Oregano","Bay leaf","Butter","Rice","Flour","Stock/Broth","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["making-a-roux","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"placki-ziemniaczane","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg floury potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour or potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"Black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml rapeseed oil, or clarified butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"Soured cream, or sugar, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the potatoes on the fine side of a box grater, then grate the onion straight into them; its sulfur compounds stall the enzyme that greys raw potato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze the mass hard in a cloth over a bowl. Let the liquid stand two minutes, pour off the water and scrape the chalky starch from the bottom back into the potato.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix in the egg, flour, salt and pepper only once the pan is hot; salt added early pulls out more water and slackens the batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry heaped spoonfuls in 5 mm of oil at 175C (347F), pressing them thin, until the edges are lace brown and the centre is set, about 3 minutes a side.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain on a rack, salt again, and serve with soured cream, or with sugar if you grew up with them sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"That reclaimed sediment is the binder. Throw the squeezed liquid out entirely and the pancakes collapse; leave the liquid in and they steam grey instead of frying. Grating fine rather than coarse is what makes them Polish and not a rösti: the potato breaks down into a batter. If the mix greys while you work, that is polyphenol oxidase, and more grated onion or a squeeze of lemon halts it.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Placki%20Ziemniaczane%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Placki Ziemniaczane"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Potato","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"ogorki-kiszone","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg small ridge cucumbers, firm and freshly picked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g coarse salt without iodine, 30 g per litre"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head of garlic, cloves peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 flowering dill heads with stalk and seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fresh horseradish root, plus a leaf if you have one"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 blackcurrant or oak leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the cucumbers in cold water for two hours and rub off the blossom end of each one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the water with the salt to dissolve it, then cool it right down to room temperature.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the cucumbers upright in a scalded jar with the garlic, dill, horseradish and leaves distributed through them rather than piled on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour over the brine to cover by 3 cm, weight everything under the surface and cover loosely; whatever sits above the brine will mould.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold at 20C (68F) until the brine goes cloudy and the fizzing slows, three days for lightly soured małosolne and about ten for fully sour, then move to a cold cellar or the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Three percent salt is the window: strong enough to hold back putrefactive bacteria, weak enough for lactobacilli to work. Vinegar has no part in this jar. Softness is the usual failure and it is enzymatic, not bacterial: pectinase from the cucumber's own blossom end and from surface moulds dissolves the flesh, so rub that end off and add tannin from oak, blackcurrant or horseradish leaf to inhibit it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Og%C3%B3rki%20Kiszone%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ogórki Kiszone"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Cucumber","Dill","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"makowiec","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml whole milk, lukewarm"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks and 1 whole egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g soft butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g blue poppy seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g caster sugar for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg whites"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g raisins, soaked in hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g candied orange peel, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g flaked almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g icing sugar and 2 tbsp lemon juice, for the glaze"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour boiling water over the poppy seeds and leave them until a seed crushes to paste between finger and thumb, then drain hard and grind twice through the fine plate of a mincer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the flour, yeast, sugar, milk, eggs and salt to a smooth soft dough, adding the butter last in pieces, and knead until it clears the bowl; prove until doubled.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the honey, filling sugar and butter, stir in the ground seeds and cook until the mass pulls away from the pan and gleams with released oil; cool, then fold in the raisins, peel, almonds and the whites whipped to soft peaks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough to a 30 by 40 cm rectangle, spread the filling to within 2 cm of every edge, and roll up from the long side, tightening against the worktop at each turn.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tuck the ends under, prove until puffy, brush with beaten egg and bake at 180C (356F) for about 45 minutes, until a skewer into the end comes out clean.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Glaze with the lemon icing while the roll is still warm so it sets thin, and slice only when completely cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Whole poppy seeds are gritty and inert. The hull has to swell in near boiling water and then be broken open, twice through a mincer or once through a poppy mill, before the oil comes out and binds the filling. The classic fault is a hollow spiral: filling too wet, or a roll not tightened at each turn, so the rising dough lifts away from it. Whipped whites lighten the filling; they do not stand in for the grinding.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Makowiec%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Makowiec"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Orange","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Flour","Honey","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"sernik-krakowski","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg twaróg, full fat dry curd cheese, well pressed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 eggs, separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g soft butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"Seeds of 1 vanilla pod"},{"kind":"item","text":"Zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g raisins, optional"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour for the pastry"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g cold butter for the pastry"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks for the pastry"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"Pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the pastry ingredients to crumbs, bring them together without kneading and rest cold for 30 minutes; press two thirds into a 24 cm tin and keep the rest for the lattice.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pass the twaróg three times through a mincer, or twice through a drum sieve, until it is a smooth paste with no visible curd.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the butter with the sugar until pale, add the yolks one at a time, then work in the twaróg in additions with the potato starch, vanilla and lemon zest.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites only to soft drooping peaks and fold them in with the raisins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread over the base, lay the lattice on top, and bake at 160C (320F) for about 70 minutes, until the centre wobbles as one piece rather than in ripples.","durationSec":4200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn the oven off, wedge the door ajar and leave the cake inside for an hour, then chill overnight before cutting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Twaróg is a drained acid curd, not cream cheese: far less fat and no gums, so butter and yolks carry the richness while potato starch, which gelatinises lower and holds water better than flour, stops the whey weeping out. Both failures are structural. Unmilled curd stays grainy however long you beat it, and a hot oven sets the rim first so the middle climbs, then sinks and cracks as it cools.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sernik%20Krakowski%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sernik Krakowski"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Potato","Lemon","Dried fruit","Butter","Cheese (any)","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"sofrito","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g yellow onions, peeled and quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g cubanelle peppers, seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g red bell pepper, seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g ajíes dulces (cachucha peppers), stems pulled off"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g garlic cloves, peeled, about 2 heads"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g recao leaves (culantro), about 20 leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cilantro, leaves and stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pull the stems off the ajíes dulces. They are a sweet pepper, so there is nothing to deseed for heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the garlic, the ajíes and half the onion in a processor first and pulse until coarsely chopped, stopping well short of liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the remaining onion, both peppers, the recao and the cilantro with its stems, and pulse in short bursts to a wet relish; you should still see separate flecks of green and white.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pulse in the olive oil, which coats the cut surfaces and holds off browning.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Freeze in an ice cube tray, then bag the cubes. Two cubes is about 60 g, enough for one pot of rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Recao is the point. Its leaf is thick and fibrous and gives up aroma slowly, so it carries through an hour of braising where cilantro alone fades in ten minutes. Pulse to a coarse relish, never a juice; over-blended sofrito weeps water and scorches in patches. It goes into warm oil, not smoking oil, and cooks only until it stops smelling raw. Browned sofrito turns bitter and no amount of stock takes it back.","equipment":["Food processor","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","briny","aromatic","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a briny, maritime salinity, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sofrito%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sofrito"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Bell pepper","Cilantro","Clove"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"arroz-con-gandules","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g medium-grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g sofrito"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g salt pork or cured cooking ham, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml achiote oil, made by warming 2 tbsp annatto seeds in 60 ml olive oil and straining"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g cooked gandules (pigeon peas), drained, their liquid kept"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml tomato sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g alcaparrado (green olives with capers)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp dried oregano, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml chicken stock combined with the reserved gandules liquid"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp fine salt, plus more to taste"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the salt pork in a caldero over medium heat until the fat runs clear and the dice are gold at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the achiote oil and the sofrito and cook, stirring, until the pan goes quiet and the smell turns sweet rather than raw, about 5 minutes; the water has to leave before the rice goes in.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in tomato sauce, alcaparrado, bay, oregano and gandules, then the dry rice, turning it in the fat until every grain is orange.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the liquid, taste it for salt, and boil hard uncovered without stirring until the surface drops to the level of the rice and craters open across the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir once from the bottom, level the surface, cover tightly and cook on the lowest heat for 20 minutes, then lift the lid; the grains should be tender and separate.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the heat to medium for 3 minutes without stirring, listening for a steady crackle, then rest off the heat 10 minutes and lift the pegao out in sheets.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Pegao is engineered, not accidental. A caldero is thin spun aluminium with a flat heavy floor, so the bottom layer dries and toasts across its whole width instead of burning in one ring the way a thin stainless pan does; nonstick gives you no crust at all. Once the lid goes on, do not stir. Stirring past that point drags gelatinised starch through the grains and you get risotto.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","tangy-ferment"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, ferment tang with real depth behind it."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Risotto method","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=risotto%20technique%20mantecatura","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arroz%20con%20Gandules%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arroz con Gandules"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Ham/Prosciutto","Tomato","Peas","Olives","Capers","Oregano","Bay leaf","Rice","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"pernil-al-horno","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"3 kg bone-in pork shoulder with the skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g garlic, about 12 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml white vinegar or sour orange juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground annatto"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slide a knife between the skin and the fat along one long edge and work it back into a flap, leaving it hinged at the far side. Do not cut it off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash the garlic with the salt, oregano and pepper in the pilón to a paste, then loosen it with the oil, vinegar and annatto.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stab the meat all over to the depth of the blade and push the paste into every cut, coat the exposed flesh, then lay the skin flap back loose and leave the skin itself bare. Refrigerate uncovered overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast with the flap folded back at 150C (300F) until the meat twists off the bone with a fork, 5 to 6 hours; the fat under the flap should have rendered to a shallow pool.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the skin flat over the meat, blot it dry, and blast at 230C (450F) until it blisters, lifts and goes hard, 20 to 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 30 minutes, crack the cuerito off in sheets, and pull the meat through the pan juices.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false}],"note":"The cuerito is a drying problem, not a roasting one. Loosen the skin before it goes in so the fat beneath renders out and drains away; foil over the top, or pan juices touching the skin, steams it into leather that no final blast will rescue. Salt the flesh under the skin rather than the skin itself, and leave it uncovered in the fridge overnight. The 230C (450F) blast only puffs skin that is already dry.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["tangy-ferment","aromatic","warm-spiced","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with ferment tang with real depth behind it, aromatics blooming from the first bite, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pernil%20al%20Horno%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pernil al Horno"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Orange","Oregano","Vinegar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"trifongo-de-platano-y-yuca","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g green plantains, about 2, peeled and cut in 3 cm rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g ripe plantain with mostly black skin, peeled and cut in 3 cm rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g yuca (cassava), peeled, woody core removed, in 4 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g garlic, about 8 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml hot vegetable stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre neutral oil for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the green plantain skins end to end and peel them under running water; the latex slides off wet hands and stains dry ones.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the yuca in well salted water until a knife slides through with no resistance, about 20 minutes, then drain and dry it on a cloth.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the green plantain in 165C (330F) oil until pale gold and a fork enters with slight give, 6 to 7 minutes; then the ripe plantain until the edges caramelise, then the yuca until it blisters.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":660,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic with the salt and the olive oil in the pilón to a loose paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the hot pieces a handful at a time, pounding and turning them into the paste until they collapse into a coarse mash, splashing in hot stock until it holds together without going wet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into a small bowl, invert onto the plate and serve at once; it stiffens as it cools.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Green plantain is close to a quarter starch and almost dry. Boiling floods those granules and mashing them gives wallpaper paste, while frying at a moderate 165C (330F) cooks the starch through and drives water off, so the mash stays granular and drinks the garlic oil. Fry too hot and you set a crust over a chalky raw centre that shows up as grit. Pound while the pieces are still hot; cooled plantain will not come together.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot"],"techniques":["Scoring","Salted water & the float test","Mashing & puréeing","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","briny","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a briny, maritime salinity, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trifongo%20de%20Pl%C3%A1tano%20y%20Yuca%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Trifongo de Plátano y Yuca"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Plantain","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["scoring","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"bacalaitos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g salt cod, soaked in 3 changes of cold water for 12 hours"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml of the final soaking water, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sofrito"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 recao leaves (culantro), finely shredded"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"neutral oil for frying, 3 cm deep"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the cod, keeping 400 ml of the last soaking water. Simmer the fish in fresh water for 10 minutes, cool it, then flake it fine, hunting out skin and bones.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder and pepper together, pour in the reserved water and stir only until the last dry streak vanishes; the batter should run off the spoon in a thin thread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the cod, sofrito, recao and garlic, then fry a teaspoon of it and taste before you add any salt at all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C (355F) and slide in ladles of batter, three at a time, spreading each into a thin disc with the back of the ladle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry until the edges are brown and lacy and the centre has stopped bubbling hard, about 3 minutes a side, then drain upright on a rack rather than flat on paper.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The batter should look wrong: thinner than pancake batter, barely coating the spoon. That is what spreads into a lace-edged disc instead of a doughy puck. Stir only until the flour disappears, because worked batter builds gluten and fries up bready. Keep the oil at 180C (355F) and fry three at a time; a crowded pan drops the temperature and the fritters drink oil instead of setting.","equipment":["Deep-fry pot","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bacala%C3%ADtos%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bacalaítos"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["White fish","Salt cod","Garlic","Flour","Clove"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"asopao-de-pollo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg bone-in chicken thighs, skinned and cut through the bone in halves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp adobo seasoning of salt, garlic powder, oregano, pepper and turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g sofrito"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml achiote oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml tomato sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g alcaparrado (green olives with capers)"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g medium-grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.8 litres chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g green peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 roasted red pepper, in strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"a handful of recao or cilantro leaves, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the chicken with the adobo and leave it while you build the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the achiote oil in a caldero, add the sofrito and cook until it stops spitting water and smells sweet, then add the tomato sauce and alcaparrado and let the colour darken a shade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chicken and bay, turn the pieces in the sauce for 5 minutes, pour in the stock and simmer until the meat is loose on the bone, about 25 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rain in the rice and cook at a lazy simmer, stirring every couple of minutes, until the grains are just tender but the pot still pours like thick soup, 18 to 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1200,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the peas, scatter the pepper strips and recao on top, and carry it to the table at once with a jug of hot stock alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Asopao is the opposite pot to arroz con gandules. Here you stir on purpose, to strip starch off the grains for body, and you finish it looser than you want to eat it. Rice keeps drinking after the pot leaves the heat, so a perfect consistency in the pan is sludge by the time it reaches the table. Hold back a ladle of hot stock to slacken each bowl, and serve the minute the rice turns tender.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Asopao%20de%20Pollo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Asopao de Pollo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Peas","Olives","Capers","Cilantro","Oregano","Bay leaf","Rice","Stock/Broth","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"pasteles-de-masa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g pork shoulder, in 1.5 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g sofrito"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml tomato sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g alcaparrado (green olives with capers)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g cooked chickpeas"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg green bananas (guineos verdes), peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g green plantains, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g yautía (malanga), peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g calabaza (West Indian pumpkin), peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml achiote oil, plus more for the leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg banana leaves, cut in 30 cm squares"},{"kind":"item","text":"parchment paper, cut in 30 cm squares"},{"kind":"item","text":"kitchen string"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stew the pork with the sofrito, tomato sauce, alcaparrado, chickpeas and raisins in a covered pot until the meat gives under a spoon and the sauce clings rather than runs, about 1 hour. Cool it completely or it melts the masa.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the bananas, plantains, yautía and calabaza on the fine side of a box grater, then beat in the achiote oil, milk and salt until the masa is marigold orange and drops from the spoon in a soft ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pass each banana leaf over a flame or across a dry hot pan until it darkens, turns glossy and folds without cracking, then brush it with achiote oil and lay it on a parchment square.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread 100 g masa into a 12 cm oval on the leaf, spoon filling down the centre, fold the leaf over itself lengthwise twice, then fold both ends underneath and back the parcel with the parchment.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tie pairs face to face with string and boil in heavily salted water for 50 minutes, turning them once.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift out, rest 5 minutes, then cut the string; the masa should come away from the leaf in one clean slab.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pass every leaf over a flame until it darkens and goes glossy. Heat melts the waxy cuticle and softens the ribs so the leaf folds instead of splitting, and a split leaf lets the masa leach out until you are boiling a bag of grey water. The yautía is the binder; without it the banana masa stays slack and never sets. Brush both leaf and masa with achiote oil or the pastel welds to its wrapper and tears as you open it.","equipment":["Parchment","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a briny, maritime salinity."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasteles%20de%20Masa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pasteles de Masa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Tomato","Squash/Pumpkin","Corn","Plantain","Olives","Capers","Banana","Dried fruit","Milk","Cornmeal/Polenta","Chickpeas"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"guineitos-en-escabeche","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg green bananas (guineos verdes), about 12"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp white vinegar for the cooking water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, in thin rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g pitted green olives"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the ends off the bananas and score the skin the length of each one, cutting through the peel only.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil them in their skins in salted water with the 2 tbsp vinegar until a knife meets slight resistance at the centre, 15 to 18 minutes; cooked soft, they will fall apart later.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile warm the olive oil with the onion, garlic, bay and peppercorns over low heat until the onion is limp and translucent but not coloured, then add the cider vinegar, olives and salt and bring it to a bare simmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peel the hot bananas holding them in a cloth, cut them into 2 cm lengths, and pour the warm escabeche over while both are still hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn once, cool to room temperature, then refrigerate at least 4 hours. Serve at room temperature, never fridge cold, or the oil is waxy on the tongue.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boil the green bananas in their slit skins. The skin keeps the flesh from waterlogging and shields the cut surface from air, while a splash of vinegar in the water blocks the enzyme that turns green banana grey. Dress them warm: warm starch takes up the oil and vinegar, cold banana repels both and stays bland in the middle. Pull them the moment a knife meets slight resistance or they collapse in the marinade.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Scoring","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","briny","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a briny, maritime salinity, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Guine%C3%ADtos%20en%20Escabeche%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Guineítos en Escabeche"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Olives","Banana","Bay leaf","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Clove"],"judgedBy":["scoring","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"tembleque","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 ml unsweetened full fat coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g cornflour (cornstarch)"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/2 tsp fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"ground cinnamon, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the cornflour and salt into 200 ml of the cold coconut milk until no grain is left on the bottom of the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the remaining coconut milk with the sugar and the cinnamon stick until it steams, then fish the stick out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the slurry and stir without stopping, right into the corners of the pan, until it thickens, then keep going until slow bubbles burst through the surface and a furrow holds behind the spoon for a full minute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a wetted mould, press cling film onto the surface and chill 4 hours, until it wobbles as one piece.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn out and dust heavily with ground cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cornstarch thickens from around 80C, but the gel only sets firm once the pan has genuinely boiled. Pull it at the first thickening and the tembleque tastes chalky and slumps as soon as it leaves the mould. Take it to slow bursting bubbles and hold it there a minute, stirring into the corners throughout, because coconut fat settles and scorches fast. Wet the mould first so it releases in one piece.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tembleque%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tembleque"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"coquito","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g cream of coconut"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml white rum"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cinnamon sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"1/4 tsp freshly grated nutmeg"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the cinnamon sticks and cloves in the water for 5 minutes, then take it off the heat and leave it to cool; steeping beats stirring in ground spice, which stays gritty on the tongue.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the coconut milk, cream of coconut, evaporated milk and condensed milk with the strained spice water until uniform and lightly foamy, about 30 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With the blender on its lowest speed, pour the rum in a thin steady stream, then the vanilla and nutmeg, and stop the moment it comes together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bottle and refrigerate at least 24 hours for the spice to spread, then shake hard and pour over ice into small glasses.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is an emulsion, not a mixture: coconut fat and milk protein held in suspension by sugar. Pour the rum in a slow stream with the blender running, because dumped in cold and fast it shocks the protein and the coquito curdles into grains. Coconut fat sets solid below about 24C, so it will separate into a plug in the fridge whatever you do. That is not spoilage. Take it out fifteen minutes ahead and shake it hard.","equipment":["Blender","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, melting away silken."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Coquito%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Coquito"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Puerto%20Rican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Puerto Rican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Cream","Coconut milk","Vanilla","Cinnamon","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"mamaliga-cu-branza-si-smantana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g coarse maize meal (malai)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g branza de burduf or telemea, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smantana"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the water and salt to a rolling boil in a heavy pot, then rain in a fistful of the maize meal and whisk once; the water should never stop boiling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the rest in a thin steady stream, whisking hard, until no dry meal floats on top, then swap the whisk for a wooden spoon as it thickens.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook at a lazy blup, stirring and scraping the corners every couple of minutes for 25 to 30 minutes, until the mass pulls away from the pot side in one piece and a spoon stands upright in it.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste for chalk; the grains should be soft and sweet. Beat in the butter, then leave the pot covered off the heat for 5 minutes so a crust sets against the metal.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Invert onto a wooden board, cut with a taut thread rather than a knife, and serve with the crumbled cheese and cold smantana.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coarse maize meal needs both time and free water for the starch granules to swell and burst; pull it off at ten minutes and it tastes chalky and slumps on the board. Rain the meal in rather than dumping it, or the outsides gelatinise into lumps around dry cores. The crust comes from the pot wall drying the surface starch, so rest it covered off the heat before you turn it out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=M%C4%83m%C4%83lig%C4%83%20cu%20Br%C3%A2nz%C4%83%20%C8%99i%20Sm%C3%A2nt%C3%A2n%C4%83%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mămăligă cu Brânză și Smântână"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"sarmale-in-foi-de-varza-murata","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole soured cabbage, about 1.5 kg (varza murata)"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g pork shoulder, coarsely minced, about a quarter fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g short grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sweet paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp dried thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp chopped dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g smoked pork ribs"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml tomato passata"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water or cabbage brine"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the cabbage into whole leaves and taste one; if it is fiercely salty, soak the leaves in cold water for 20 minutes. Shave the thick spine off each leaf so it will roll.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion in the lard until translucent and sweet, pull the pan off the heat, stir the paprika through for 20 seconds, and let it cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the pork, rice, cooled onion, thyme, dill and pepper by hand until just combined; the rice should read as a scatter through the meat, not a mass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll walnut sized amounts in the leaves, snug but never compressed, tucking the ends in. Line the pot with shredded cabbage and the smoked ribs, then pack the rolls seam down in tight layers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the passata, brine, bay and peppercorns to just cover, weight with an upturned plate, and hold at the barest tremble on the hob or at 150C (300F) for about 3 hours, until a leaf yields to the side of a spoon with no resistance.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool them in the pot and reheat the next day; the liquid should taste rounded rather than sharp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rice takes up around two and a half times its weight in liquid, so a heavy hand bursts the rolls and still leaves a dry pellet inside. The soured leaf sets the other limit: lactic acid slows pectin from breaking down, so soured cabbage wants a far longer, gentler cook than a fresh leaf. Let it boil and the rolls split and the fat breaks out greasy. Barest tremble, a weight on top, a night to settle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","gently hot","rich","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, a warm current of chile, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sarmale%20%C3%AEn%20Foi%20de%20Varz%C4%83%20Murat%C4%83%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sarmale în Foi de Varză Murată"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Tomato","Cabbage","Dill","Thyme","Bay leaf","Rice","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"ciorba-de-burta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg cleaned veal tripe"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 veal knuckle, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 parsnip"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g celeriac"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g smantana"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml white wine vinegar, plus more at the table"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"hot green peppers, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the tripe with cold water, bring it to the boil, then drain and rinse; this first water carries off the barnyard smell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the tripe to the pot with the knuckle, roots, onions and peppercorns, cover with fresh water and simmer, skimming, for about 3 hours, until the tripe cuts under a spoon with no spring left.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the tripe and cut it into ribbons 5 mm wide, strain the broth, and bring it back with the tripe in it to a bare simmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic with a good pinch of salt to a smooth paste, loosen it with a ladle of hot broth, and stir it into the pot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with the smantana, then trickle in two ladles of hot broth while whisking. Take the pot off the heat, stir the liaison back in, and set it on the lowest flame only until it coats the back of a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sharpen with the vinegar off the boil, taste, and serve with more vinegar and hot peppers alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The liaison is the dish. Yolk and smantana thicken from about 65C (150F) and curdle by 80C (176F), so temper them with hot broth in a thin stream and never let the pot boil again. Acid lowers that curdle point, which is why the vinegar goes in last. Romania sours two ways: fermented bran bors for ciorba de perisoare and bean ciorbe, clean vinegar or lemon here, where it has to cut fat without fighting raw garlic.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","earthy","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, melting away silken."},"pairingId":36,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ciorb%C4%83%20de%20Burt%C4%83%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ciorbă de Burtă"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Bell pepper","Parsnip","Celeriac","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"mititei","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g beef chuck, coarsely minced and very cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pork belly, coarsely minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"16 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 garlic cloves, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml cold beef bone stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dried thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"half tsp ground caraway"},{"kind":"item","text":"sunflower oil, for the grill bars"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead the cold minced meats with the salt and bicarbonate of soda alone for 4 to 5 minutes, until the mass turns shiny and tacky and pulls away from the bowl in strings.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the garlic and ground spices, then beat in the cold stock a third at a time, waiting until each addition has vanished into the paste before adding the next.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn your hand over to test it: the paste should cling upside down. Cover and rest it in the fridge overnight, since there is no casing to hold these together and the bicarbonate needs the time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe the paste through a wide nozzle into cigars about 8 cm long and 3 cm thick, wetting your hands to square off the ends.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot bare coals with no flame licking up, turning every minute and brushing with water beaten with a little garlic, until the surfaces are dark and lacquered and one springs back when pressed, about 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bicarbonate of soda pushes the meat's pH away from the point where its proteins hold least water, so they swell and grip the stock instead of squeezing it onto the coals. The bounce and the juice are borrowed water. Past roughly 8 g per kilo it tastes of soap, and skipping the overnight rest leaves a metallic edge and slack grey sausages that sag through the bars.","equipment":["Grill","Piping bag","Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","aromatic","warm-spiced","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mititei%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mititei"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Garlic","Thyme","Stock/Broth","Coriander seed","Clove","Caraway","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"papanasi-cu-smantana-si-dulceata-de-afine","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g branza de vaci, fresh cow's curd cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour, plus more for shaping"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fine semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre sunflower oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g smantana"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g dulceata de afine, blueberry preserve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Hang the curd cheese in a cloth for an hour if it is at all wet, then press it through a sieve; it should take a thumbprint without weeping.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs, sugar, zest and salt, then fold in the flour, semolina and baking powder, adding only enough extra flour to give a soft dough that stays faintly sticky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"With floured hands shape eight rings about 8 cm across with a 2 cm hole, plus eight small balls from the trimmings.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 165C (330F) and fry in batches, turning once, for 6 to 7 minutes, until deep bronze and light in the hand; a skewer through the thickest part comes out clean.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":660,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain briefly, sit a ball in each ring, spoon over cold smantana and then the preserve, and eat at once while the crust is still crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Everything hangs on how wet the cheese is. Loose curd forces in more flour and the papanas turns bready; drained curd carries a batter that puffs. Fry at 165C (330F) and no hotter: baking powder needs time to lift a dense wet dough, and at 180C and above the crust locks before the middle cooks, so they colour beautifully, then sink and weep oil from a raw centre.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Papana%C8%99i%20cu%20Sm%C3%A2nt%C3%A2n%C4%83%20%C8%99i%20Dulcea%C8%9B%C4%83%20de%20Afine%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Papanași cu Smântână și Dulceață de Afine"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Berries","Cheese (any)","Flour","Semolina","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"banitsa-sas-sirene","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"450 g thin filo pastry, about 12 sheets"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g sirene, Bulgarian brined white cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g kiselo mlyako, Bulgarian yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"half tsp bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Crumble the sirene by hand into rubble the size of peas; mash it to a paste and it will melt into the custard and set rubbery.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs with the yoghurt and bicarbonate of soda until the mixture foams and lifts, then fold in the cheese with a good grind of pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay out a sheet of filo, brush it lightly with the mixed oil and butter, spread a line of filling along one long edge and roll it into a loose rope without tightening it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Coil the first rope in the centre of an oiled round tin and spiral the rest around it, leaving a finger's gap between the turns.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush the top, bake at 190C (375F) for 40 to 45 minutes, until deep bronze and hollow when tapped, then rest it 10 minutes under a cloth before cutting.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Bicarbonate meets the yoghurt's lactic acid and the custard puffs, holding the coils apart so steam can leave; rolled tight and packed edge to edge, the middle stews to paste while the rim burns. Know the two cheeses as well: sirene is a brined curd that crumbles and weeps salt and never melts to a thread, while kashkaval is scalded and stretched, so it strings and browns. Swap them and the banitsa turns greasy and chewy.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Handling phyllo","Brining & curing","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Handling phyllo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=working%20with%20phyllo%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Banitsa%20sas%20Sirene%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Banitsa sas Sirene"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Yogurt","Cheese (any)","Phyllo/Puff pastry"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"tarator","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g kiselo mlyako, Bulgarian yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml very cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g cucumbers"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g walnuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large handful dill, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"ice cubes, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pound the garlic with a good pinch of salt in a mortar until it is a smooth ivory paste with no visible pieces left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yoghurt on its own until it is completely smooth and glossy, then let it down with the cold water a splash at a time to the thickness of single cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dice the cucumber into 5 mm cubes, crush half the walnuts coarsely, and stir both into the yoghurt with the garlic, dill and oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season, chill for 30 minutes, then taste again; cold blunts salt and it will want more than it did warm.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve in bowls with an ice cube and the rest of the walnuts scattered over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yoghurt is a fragile casein gel. Whisk it smooth before a drop of water goes in, because water first leaves it grainy and it then separates into pools of whey in the bowl. Keep everything cold, since warmth splits it faster. Pound the garlic rather than chopping it: chopped cloves keep releasing pungency as the soup stands, and by the second bowl the burn has swallowed the dill.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","herb-fresh","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tarator%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tarator"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Cucumber","Dill","Yogurt","Nuts","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"shopska-salata","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 red peppers"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g cucumbers"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, or 4 spring onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g sirene, cold from the fridge"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"small handful flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blacken the peppers all over directly on a gas flame or on the coals, then shut them in a covered bowl for 10 minutes to steam loose.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rub the skins off dry with your fingers and a cloth, never under the tap, and tear the flesh into strips, flicking out the seeds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the tomatoes and cucumber into 2 cm chunks, slice the onion thinly, salt the tomatoes lightly and leave them 5 minutes to bead.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the vegetables with the parsley, oil and vinegar, check the salt, and spread them flat in a shallow dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the cold sirene over the top on the coarse side of a box grater so it falls as a white blanket, and carry it to the table unstirred.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The blanket is not decoration. Grated cold, sirene falls in dry flakes that sit on top and salt every forkful; stirred through, it dissolves into the tomato juice and the salad becomes a salty slurry. Skin the peppers dry too, because rinsing washes off the smoke that separates this from any other tomato salad. Sunflower oil stays neutral where olive oil would drag the plate toward Greece.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salting to draw the water out"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","smoky","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shopska%20Salata%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shopska Salata"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Scallion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Cucumber","Parsley","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["salting-to-draw-the-water-out","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"kavarma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g pork shoulder, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 red peppers, in strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g mushrooms, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g tomatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sweet paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp dried summer savory (chubritsa)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the pork hard in the lard in two batches, leaving each piece alone until it releases from the pan with a crust, then set it aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onions in the same fat until deep gold and jammy, add the peppers and mushrooms and keep going until their water has gone and the pan sizzles again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat, stir the paprika through for no more than 20 seconds, and pour in the wine at once to stop it cooking any further.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the pork with the tomatoes, savory, pepper and salt, divide between four clay pots or one lidded casserole, cover and bake at 170C (340F) for about 75 minutes, until the pork gives to the side of a spoon.","durationSec":4500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Break an egg over each pot and return them uncovered until the whites are just set and the yolks still run, about 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Paprika is the timer here. Its sugars and carotenoids scorch within seconds on a hot dry pan and turn bitter and brick brown, and no amount of stewing lifts that back out, so the pan comes off the heat and the wine follows immediately. The other marker is chubritsa, summer savory, resinous and sharper than thyme; it goes in at the start because it needs the long heat to soften.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kavarma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kavarma"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"peppers-and-eggplant-the-nightshade-late-summer","term":"Peppers & Eggplant: The Nightshade Late Summer"}],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Onion","Tomato","Bell pepper","Mushrooms","Wine","Paprika"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"lyutenitsa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg red kapia peppers"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g aubergine"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 or 2 hot red chillies, optional"}],"steps":[{"text":"Char the peppers and aubergine over a flame or on the grill until the skins are black and blistered all over, then sweat them covered for 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Skin and seed them, then leave the flesh in a colander for 20 minutes and throw away everything that drains out; that liquid is what makes thin lyutenitsa.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Halve the tomatoes and grate them on a box grater, discarding the skins, then boil the pulp hard in a wide pan until it has halved and mounds on a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop or pulse the drained peppers and aubergine coarsely, keeping some texture, and add them with the oil, sugar, salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook uncovered over a low flame, stirring into the corners every few minutes for 45 to 60 minutes, until a spoon drawn across the base leaves a trail that holds and oil beads at the edges.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the garlic and vinegar, cook 5 minutes more, and pot it hot into sterilised jars.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Two things go wrong. Water: peppers carry a great deal of it, so drain them and reduce the tomato separately, or you simmer for hours and it still slides off the bread. Scorching: as the paste thickens it stops convecting and the base catches, which tastes of nothing but burn, so drop the heat and keep the spoon in the corners. Garlic and vinegar go in last, since an hour of cooking would boil both away.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Charring over open flame","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Frying the paste until the oil splits"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","charred","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lyutenitsa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lyutenitsa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Eggplant","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["charring-over-open-flame","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"knackebrod","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g coarse wholemeal rye flour, plus more for rolling"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"9 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caraway or fennel seed, lightly crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water at 40C (104F)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the yeast into the warm water, then work in both flours, the salt and the seed to a stiff, slightly tacky dough; cover and leave until visibly puffed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into eight, and roll each piece on a rye dusted bench until you can see the grain of the wood through it, about 2 mm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dock the whole surface hard with a fork or a knobbly pin, then cut a coin sized hole out of the centre of each round.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 250C (480F) for 6 to 8 minutes, until the sheets are dry, blistered and freckled dark at the high points.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slide onto a rack at once and leave until stone cold; they snap rather than bend when they are done.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rye brings the water holding pentosans but not the gluten to trap gas, so this is not a raised bread; it is a sheet dried through. Roll to 2 mm or the middle stays leathery and goes limp inside a day. Dock it all over: undocked, steam pockets balloon the sheet into pillows that scorch on the domes and stay raw between them. Cool on a rack, never on the tray, or trapped steam softens the snap.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kn%C3%A4ckebr%C3%B6d%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Knäckebröd"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bread","Flour","Caraway"]},{"slug":"artsoppa-med-punsch","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g whole dried yellow peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres cold water for cooking"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g lightly salted pork shoulder or a smoked hock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, whole, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp dried marjoram"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp dried thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"Swedish hot mustard, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"Swedish punsch, warmed, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the peas overnight in plenty of cold water, then drain and lift off any hulls and shrivelled peas floating on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover with the fresh 2 litres, bring to the boil and skim the grey scum and loose skins until the surface stays clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop in the pork, the whole onion and the marjoram; set the lid ajar and hold at a lazy shiver until a pea crushes to nothing between finger and thumb, roughly 90 minutes.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the pork, discard the onion, dice the meat and return it; season with salt and white pepper only now.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve with mustard stirred in at the table and a small glass of warm punsch alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Acid is what keeps peas hard: it holds the pectin in the skins cross linked, so mustard and vinegar belong at the table, never in the pot. Salt does the opposite, sodium displacing the calcium that stiffens those skins, which is why salted pork helps rather than hinders the collapse. Skim the loose hulls off the first boil or they hang through the finished soup like fingernails.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=%C3%84rtsoppa%20med%20Punsch%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ärtsoppa med Punsch"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Peas","Thyme","Oregano","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"kalops","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg beef chuck, in 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, thickly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 whole allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 ml beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"pickled beetroot and boiled potatoes, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blot the beef bone dry on paper, then brown it in the butter in uncrowded batches until each face carries a hard mahogany crust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the onions in the same fat until slumped and catching gold at the edges, scatter over the flour and stir for two minutes to a sandy paste that smells of biscuit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the beef with the whole allspice and bay, pour in stock to barely cover and bring up until the surface only trembles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lid on, into the oven at 150C (300F) until a fork pushed into a cube slides back out with no resistance, about two and a half hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Skim the fat off the top, fish out the allspice and bay, correct the salt and serve with pickled beetroot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Whole allspice, always. Ground berries release their eugenol all at once and turn a long braise medicinal, and the powder clouds the sauce grey; whole ones leach slowly and can be picked out. The body here comes from flour cooked into the onion fat rather than from reduction, so the pot must stay at a bare shiver. At a rolling boil that flour sauce thins and separates and the beef fibres squeeze themselves dry.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kalops%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kalops"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Potato","Beets","Berries","Bay leaf","Butter","Flour","Stock/Broth","Pickles","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"raggmunk-med-stekt-flask","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g floury potatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g lightly salted pork belly, in 5 mm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"raw stirred lingonberries, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour into a third of the milk until there is not a lump left, then loosen with the rest of the milk and the egg; set aside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the pork slices in a dry pan over medium heat until the fat runs clear and the edges buckle and crisp; keep them warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grate the potatoes coarsely straight into the batter, working fast and pushing them under the liquid as you go; stir in the salt and pepper at the very last.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Ladle into foaming butter and spread to 1 cm thick, frying until the underside is lace brown and the edge lifts away clean, then flip once.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve immediately with the pork and cold lingonberries.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Do not rinse or wring the grated potato. The free starch clinging to the shreds is what binds the pancake, and the milk and flour batter is only its carrier; that batter is also the whole difference between raggmunk and bare rårakor. Salt pulls water out of raw potato within minutes, so it goes in last and the batter hits the pan at once. Delay, and you are frying grey shreds in a puddle.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Raggmunk%20med%20Stekt%20Fl%C3%A4sk%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Raggmunk med Stekt Fläsk"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Eggs","Potato","Butter","Milk","Flour"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"sillsallad","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g pickled herring fillets, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g cooked beetroot"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g cooked waxy potato, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tart apple"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g pickled gherkins"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml double cream, whipped soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 hard boiled eggs"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the herring, potato, apple, gherkin and onion into even 8 mm dice, and dice the beetroot separately into its own bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dress the pale dice with the vinegar, sugar and white pepper and taste; while it is still at room temperature it should read distinctly too sharp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the beetroot through only at the end, turning just enough to marble the salad pink rather than stain it through.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and chill for at least four hours so the dice trade flavour, then taste again and adjust.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spoon the whipped cream over the top and finish with the eggs pushed through a coarse grater.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold blunts both acid and sweetness, so season this while it is warm to a point that seems too sharp and it will land correctly at fridge temperature. Betanin bleeds on contact, which is why the beetroot goes in last unless you want uniform magenta paste. The cream sits on top rather than being stirred through; folded in, it slackens under the vinegar into a pink slurry within the hour.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","earthy","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, an earthy, grounding base, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sillsallad%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sillsallad"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Potato","Beets","Apple","Cream","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"gubbrora","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 Swedish ansjovis fillets, spice cured sprat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp of the ansjovis brine from the tin"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small red onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dill"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g chives"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp crème fraîche"},{"kind":"item","text":"white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 slices dark rye bread and butter, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the eggs for eight minutes for a yolk that is just set and still bright, then chill them in ice water and peel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the sprat fillets, the onion, the dill and the chives all fine, keeping them in separate piles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the eggs coarsely and fold everything together with a fork, loosening with the crème fraîche and the spoonful of brine; stop while the pieces are still distinct.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste before you reach for salt; the fillets and their brine carry almost all of it. Pile onto buttered rye and serve cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Swedish ansjovis is not anchovy. It is sprat cured in a sweet spiced brine carrying allspice, cloves, ginger and sandalwood, so it tastes aromatic and faintly of gingerbread where Mediterranean anchovy is salt and ferment. Swap one for the other and the mix turns savage. If salted anchovy is all you have, use half the weight, rinse it, and add a pinch of sugar with a crushed allspice berry to bring it back toward the right note.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","umami-deep","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gubbr%C3%B6ra%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gubbröra"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Dill","Chives","Butter","Sour cream/Crema","Bread"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"hovmastarsas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 g Swedish sweet mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml neutral rapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g dill, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the two mustards with the sugar, vinegar, salt and pepper until no grains of sugar grate on the bowl and the base is glossy and slack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the oil in a thread no thicker than string, whisking without pause, until the sauce climbs to the body of loose mayonnaise and holds a lazy ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the dill and leave it half an hour for the vinegar and sugar to settle into the mustard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Taste for the correct order: sweet first, then mustard heat, then vinegar last. Correct with sugar or vinegar, never with more oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no egg here. The emulsifier is the mucilage in the mustard seed coat, which is a weaker stabiliser than yolk lecithin, so oil delivered faster than it can be broken up simply pools and the sauce goes greasy and grained. If it splits, put a fresh teaspoon of mustard in a clean bowl and beat the broken sauce into it a few drops at a time. Mustard heat is volatile, so make this the day you serve it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, melting away silken."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hovm%C3%A4stars%C3%A5s%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hovmästarsås"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dill","Vinegar","Wine","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion"]},{"slug":"rarorda-lingon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g lingonberries, fresh or thawed from frozen"},{"kind":"item","text":"225 g caster sugar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pick the berries over for leaves, stalks and any that have gone soft, then drain them thoroughly and tip them out on a cloth until the surfaces look matt rather than wet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put them in a scalded bowl with the sugar and stir with a wooden spoon, deliberately crushing about a fifth of the berries against the side to start the juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Come back and stir every ten minutes until no sugar grates against the bowl and the syrup falls off the spoon in a slow rope rather than in drops.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into sterilised jars, press out any air pockets, cap and refrigerate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Lingonberries carry their own benzoic acid at a pH low enough to preserve them, which is why the Swedish preparation is stirred raw rather than boiled: no heat, no added pectin, no lost sharpness or faded colour. The sugar must dissolve completely, because undissolved crystals leave the syrup too dilute to protect itself and it ferments. Wet berries do the same thing. This lives in the refrigerator, not the cupboard.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=R%C3%A5r%C3%B6rda%20Lingon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rårörda Lingon"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sugar"]},{"slug":"semla","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"75 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, plus 1 beaten egg for washing"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g green cardamom pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"75 g soft butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g almond paste, half almond by weight"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml whole milk for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml double cream, very cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"icing sugar, for dusting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Crack the cardamom pods open, throw the papery husks away and grind only the black seeds to a fine powder in a mortar, at the bench, minutes before you mix.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the milk to 37C (99F), then mix and knead it with the flour, yeast, sugar, salt, egg and cardamom until the dough pulls clean off the bowl and a stretched piece goes translucent; work the soft butter in last.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove until doubled, divide into twelve 80 g balls, roll each tight against the bench and prove again until a poked finger leaves a dent that fills back slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Egg wash and bake at 220C (430F) for 8 to 10 minutes to a deep gold that sounds hollow underneath; cool completely or the cream will slide off.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut an angled lid from each bun, scoop a walnut of crumb from inside and mash it with the almond paste and enough milk to make a thick spoonable filling; spoon it back in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the cream to firm peaks, pipe it high, cap with the lid and dust heavily with icing sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The aroma of cardamom lives in volatile oils that begin leaving the ground seed within hours, which is why jarred pre ground powder is mostly the woody husk note and why copies of Swedish buns taste of nothing in particular. Crush the pods, discard the husks, grind the black seeds alone and do it at the bench. Grind pods whole and the husk fibre stays in the crumb, reading as bitter chaff between your teeth.","equipment":["Piping bag","Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Semla%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Semla"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"prinsesstarta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g potato starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar for the custard"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml double cream, very cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g raspberry jam"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g marzipan, 60 percent almond"},{"kind":"item","text":"green food colouring"},{"kind":"item","text":"icing sugar, for rolling and dusting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the eggs and sugar until pale and thick enough that a drizzled figure of eight sits on the surface for three seconds before sinking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sift the flour, potato starch and baking powder over in three additions and fold by cutting down and turning, stopping the moment the streaks vanish; bake in a 22 cm tin at 175C (350F) for 30 minutes, until the centre springs back.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the milk, yolks, custard sugar, cornflour and vanilla over medium heat to a boil and hold it bubbling a full minute, until it thickens and loses any floury taste; spread thin and chill under film.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split the cooled sponge into three; jam on the first layer, the beaten cold custard on the second, then whip the cream firm and build a tall smooth dome over the top layer, and chill until it is hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the coloured marzipan to 3 mm between icing sugared sheets, wide enough to reach the board with 5 cm spare, then drape it from the crown and smooth downward, driving the air out ahead of your palms.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Trim flush at the base, dust with icing sugar and finish with a marzipan rose.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Marzipan does not stretch, it flows. A flat top with a square shoulder splits at that corner every time, so the cream is built into a tall dome and chilled hard before draping; on a soft dome the sheet sinks and wrinkles instead. Roll to 3 mm: thinner tears over the crown, thicker eats like sweet putty. The potato starch in the sponge is not optional either, since it keeps the crumb short enough to cut without dragging.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","silky","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Prinsesst%C3%A5rta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Prinsesstårta"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Swedish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Swedish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Potato","Berries","Milk","Cream","Bread","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"green-seasoning","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"80g chadon beni (culantro) leaves, stems trimmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g fresh thyme, leaves picked from the woody stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 stalks chive or scallion, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head garlic, about 60g, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, 180g, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 pimento seasoning peppers, seeds in"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 stick celery with its leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"10g flat parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml white vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash the herbs and spin or pat them bone dry; surface water dilutes the paste and shortens the life of the jar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the chadon beni, thyme, chive, celery, parsley and pimento peppers into the blender first, lay the onion and garlic on top, then pour the vinegar and ice water down the side of the jug.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pulse in short bursts, scraping down between them, until the paste is coarse and flecked rather than smooth; stop while you can still see pieces of leaf, about 20 pulses.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the salt in by hand, fill ice cube trays and freeze, and keep one small jar in the fridge for the week's cooking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Blending is friction and friction is heat: run the motor continuously and the paste climbs past 40C (104F), the chlorophyll goes olive and the volatile oils condense on the lid. Salt compounds it by pulling cell water out, so it goes in after. Pulse cold with vinegar and ice water, freeze in cubes, and the green stays green.","equipment":["Blender","Sheet pan"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","bright","aromatic","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, acid holding everything up to the light, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Green%20Seasoning%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Green Seasoning"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Celery","Parsley","Thyme","Chives","Vinegar","Allspice"]},{"slug":"buss-up-shut-paratha-roti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g all-purpose flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"320ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g soft butter or ghee, for layering"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp neutral oil, for the tawa"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp extra flour, for dusting the spiral"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, baking powder, salt and sugar, add the water in one go and knead 3 minutes to a soft, slightly tacky dough; cover and rest 30 minutes, until a pressed dent no longer springs back.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 4 loyas. Roll each to a 25cm disc, spread it thinly with soft butter, dust with flour, then cut one straight line from the centre to the edge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the disc up from that cut into a tight cone, stand the cone on its point and press it down into a squat puck; rest the pucks 30 minutes under a cloth.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll each puck out to about 30cm, working gently from the centre outward; if fat squeezes through the surface you are pressing too hard and the spiral is being crushed shut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a medium tawa, oiling both faces, turning every 30 seconds until brown freckles rise and the roti balloons in patches, roughly 2 minutes a side.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take it straight off the heat into a clean cloth and clap it hard between both hands, or beat it with two wooden spatulas, until it collapses into rags.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cut and the cone build a spiral of dough sheets held apart by fat, and steam inflates them on the tawa. Clap it while it is still hot and pliable and those layers fracture into shreds. Let it cool first and the fat sets solid, the sheets weld together, and you end up tearing a stiff disc instead of bussing it up.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Buss%20Up%20Shut%20(Paratha%20Roti)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Buss Up Shut (Paratha Roti)"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Flour","Sugar"]},{"slug":"dhalpuri-roti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250g yellow split peas (dhal), picked over"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves garlic, 2 left whole and 2 crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp roasted ground geera (cumin)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp fine salt, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"500g all-purpose flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp fine salt, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"320ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml neutral oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the split peas with the whole garlic and the turmeric until a pea crushes between finger and thumb but still holds its shape, about 20 minutes; take them further and the filling turns to paste.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain hard, spread on a tray and let them steam dry for 10 minutes, then grind with the crushed garlic, geera and salt to a dry, sandy powder that will not hold a shape when squeezed in the fist.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile knead the flour, baking powder, salt and water to a soft dough, work the oil through it, cover and rest 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Divide into 5. Cup each piece in your palm, spoon in 3 heaped tablespoons of the ground dhal, gather the edges over the top and pinch them shut; rest the filled balls seam down for 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll out to about 28cm with light even pressure, giving the roti a quarter turn each stroke so the filling spreads with the skin rather than punching through it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a medium tawa for 90 seconds a side, oiling each face, until pale gold with brown freckles and the roti puffs; stack them in a cloth so they steam soft.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wet filling is what bursts a dhalpuri. Peas boiled to mush stay full of water, the grind comes out as a paste, and that paste tears the skin from inside and welds into a gummy grey patch. Boil the dhal only to the point where it crushes but keeps its shape, dry it on a tray, grind it to sand, and it spreads with the dough instead of through it.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dhalpuri%20Roti%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dhalpuri Roti"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Peas","Flour","Cumin","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"chicken-pelau","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900g bone-in chicken thighs, cut into pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 tbsp green seasoning"},{"kind":"item","text":"45ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"35g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 pimento seasoning peppers, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"400g parboiled long grain rice, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g cooked pigeon peas, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, 100g, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp ketchup"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"450ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole scotch bonnet, left unbroken"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20g butter"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the chicken through with the green seasoning and leave it while you cut and measure everything else; once the sugar goes in there is no time to prepare.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil in a heavy pot over medium-high, add the sugar and let it foam. Watch it run gold, then copper, then the deep red brown of molasses as the first thread of smoke lifts, about 90 seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip the chicken in at that instant and stir to coat; it will hiss and seize. Cook 6 to 8 minutes until every piece is lacquered mahogany, then add the onion, pimento and thyme.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the rice, pigeon peas, carrot and ketchup, then the coconut milk, water and salt; float the whole scotch bonnet on top and keep it intact.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover, drop the heat to low and cook undisturbed 22 minutes, until the liquid is gone and a crust has formed at the bottom; lift out the pepper, fork the butter through and rest 5 minutes.","durationSec":1320,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"Everything rides on the sugar. Gold is too early and the pelau comes out pale and sweet. Black is too late: caramel taken past roughly 190C (375F) turns acrid and no amount of coconut milk covers it. The window is the few seconds between copper and dark molasses, as the first smoke rises. Have the chicken in your hand before you start.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","smoky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicken%20Pelau%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicken Pelau"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Carrot","Chiles","Peas","Coconut","Thyme","Butter","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Sugar","Allspice"]},{"slug":"crab-and-callaloo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500g young dasheen (taro) leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 okra, topped and sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 blue crabs, about 700g, cleaned and quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"600ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 pimento seasoning peppers, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole scotch bonnet, left unbroken"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 chadon beni leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Strip out the thick leaf ribs and peel the stringy skin from the stems, wash the leaves through several changes of water and shred them; wear gloves if your hands are sensitive to the sap.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the leaves, okra, onion, garlic, thyme, pimento and water in a heavy pot, sit the whole scotch bonnet on top and simmer covered for 35 minutes, until the leaves have collapsed to a dull olive sludge.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the crab and the coconut milk and simmer 15 minutes more, until the shells have turned orange and the pot smells sweet.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift out the pepper and the crab pieces, then swizzle the pot hard with a lele, or blend it, until the callaloo is smooth, glossy and pours in a thick ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the crab, stir in the butter, chadon beni and salt, simmer 2 minutes and taste only now.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dasheen leaf is armed with needle crystals of calcium oxalate; raw or half cooked it scratches the throat for hours afterwards. They break down only under long wet heat, so the leaves must go slack and dull before anything else happens, and you do not taste the pot early. The okra does the other half: its mucilage is what lets the swizzle stick pull the soup smooth and thick.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","fiery","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, real fire that builds and stays, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Crab%20and%20Callaloo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Crab and Callaloo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Okra","Coconut","Thyme","Butter","Milk","Coconut milk","Clove","Allspice"]},{"slug":"curry-crab-and-dumpling","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 blue crabs, about 1.4kg, cleaned and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp green seasoning"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g all-purpose flour, for the dumplings"},{"kind":"item","text":"200ml water, for the dumplings"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt, for the dumplings"},{"kind":"item","text":"60ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp Trinidad curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml water, for slaking the curry"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 pimento seasoning peppers, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole scotch bonnet, left unbroken"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp amchar masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 chadon beni leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wash the crab with the lime and cold water, drain it well and rub the green seasoning right into the shells and body cavities.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead the flour, dumpling salt and water into a stiff dough, roll it into ropes, cut thumb-length pieces and flatten each once; hold them under a cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slake the curry powder with the 120ml water to a loose paste. Fry the onion and garlic in the oil until fragrant, pour the paste in and cook, stirring, until the water has gone, the paste darkens and oil breaks out at the rim, 4 to 5 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the crab, pimento and salt and turn everything through the dry curry for 3 minutes, then pour in the coconut milk and float the whole scotch bonnet on top.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drop the dumplings in, cover and hold at a bare simmer for 20 to 25 minutes; they are ready when they float and a cut one shows no chalky core.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the amchar masala and chadon beni through off the heat and let the pot stand 5 minutes before serving.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Burning the curry is the whole dish. The slaked paste hits hot oil, the water boils off, and only once the pan is dry does the powder get hot enough to toast: it deepens in colour and the oil separates at the edge. Add liquid before that point and the curry stays raw, dusty and bitter. Amchar masala is already roasted, so it goes in off the heat or its aroma is spent.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","rich","bright","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Curry%20Crab%20and%20Dumpling%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Curry Crab and Dumpling"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crab","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Flour","Clove","Curry powder","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"pholourie-with-tamarind-sauce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150g split pea flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150g all-purpose flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp roasted ground geera (cumin)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves garlic, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 chadon beni leaves, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"350ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"100g seedless tamarind pulp"},{"kind":"item","text":"300ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"80g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 clove garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a scotch bonnet, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp salt, for the sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the two flours with the baking powder, turmeric, geera, garlic, chadon beni and salt, then beat in the warm water to a thick batter that falls from the spoon in a slow ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and rest 30 minutes, until the surface looks aerated and the batter has lifted slightly in the bowl.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Meanwhile soften the tamarind in the hot water, work it through a sieve, then simmer with the brown sugar, garlic, scotch bonnet and salt until it coats the back of a spoon, about 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 170C (340F) and drop in teaspoons of batter; they sink, then rise and roll themselves over as they puff.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry 3 to 4 minutes to a deep gold that sounds hollow when tapped, drain on a rack and serve hot with the sauce.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"A pholourie should be hollow and light, not a doughboy. Two things get it there: the rest, which hydrates the flours and starts the baking powder, and oil at 170C (340F), hot enough to set a shell and flash the interior water to steam. Below about 160C (320F) they drink oil and sit heavy; above 190C (375F) the crust browns before the middle sets and the centre stays raw batter.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deep frying","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Straining & passing through a sieve","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","earthy","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pholourie%20with%20Tamarind%20Sauce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pholourie with Tamarind Sauce"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Flour","Sugar","Tamarind","Cumin","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"macaroni-pie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400g elbow macaroni"},{"kind":"item","text":"400ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"350g mature cheddar, coarsely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"50g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp yellow mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 pimento seasoning peppers, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the macaroni in well salted water two minutes short of the packet time; it must still be chalky at the core, because it goes on drinking custard in the oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain and stir the butter through while it steams, then the onion, pimento, mustard, salt, pepper and two thirds of the cheese.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs into the evaporated milk and fold that through; in the dish the mixture should look wet and loose rather than creamy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Level it into a buttered 30cm dish, scatter the remaining cheese over and bake at 180C (350F) for 35 to 40 minutes, until the top is blistered brown and the centre keeps only a faint wobble.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 15 minutes before cutting, so the custard finishes setting and the squares hold their edges.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is not a creamy mac; it is a baked custard that has to slice. Egg and evaporated milk set around the pasta near 82C (180F), and a few degrees beyond that the proteins tighten and wring water out, leaving a grainy pie sitting in a puddle. Pull it while the centre still wobbles and let the standing heat finish the set during the rest.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Salted water & the float test","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Macaroni%20Pie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Macaroni Pie"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cheddar","Pasta","Mustard","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"mango-chow","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 firm green mangoes, about 700g"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 chadon beni leaves, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves garlic, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"half a scotch bonnet, seeded and minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice of 1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.25 tsp black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pick mangoes that are hard under the thumb and still white-green at the cut; ripe fruit has already softened its pectin and will slump to pulp in the salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Peel and slice thin wedges straight off the seed into a bowl or a jar with a tight lid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the salt, garlic, scotch bonnet, chadon beni, black pepper and lime, then shake or turn until every piece is coated.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand 15 minutes at room temperature, turning once, until a thin syrupy brine has gathered at the bottom and the slices have lost their squeak but not their snap.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt is doing the cooking. It draws water out of the cut faces by osmosis and that liquid becomes the dressing, carrying raw garlic and pepper back into the fruit. The window is short: at fifteen minutes the mango is seasoned and still crisp, at an hour the cells have collapsed, the brine is a watery puddle and the chow is limp. Make it to eat, not to keep.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","fiery","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, real fire that builds and stays, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mango%20Chow%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mango Chow"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Chiles","Lime","Mango","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"kurma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300g all-purpose flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60g cold butter, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp finely grated fresh ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 tsp ground cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.25 tsp fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"120ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"800ml neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"250g granulated sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 thick slices fresh ginger, for the syrup"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the butter into the flour with the grated ginger, cardamom and salt until it looks like coarse meal, then bring it together with the evaporated milk into a stiff, smooth dough; rest 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll 5mm thick and cut strips about the size of a little finger, keeping them even so they dry through at the same rate.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry in batches at 160C (320F) for 6 to 8 minutes, until pale gold and the bubbling around them has almost stopped; drain and cool completely.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar, water and ginger slices to 115C (239F), then take the pan off the heat and fish out the ginger.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip in the kurma and stir without stopping; within a minute the clear syrup clouds, grains and sets as a dry white shell. Spread them out to cool separately.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The coating is crystallised sugar, not glaze, and you make it happen deliberately. Syrup boiled to 115C (239F) is supersaturated as it cools, and constant stirring gives it the seed points to grain into a dry white crust. Stop stirring and it sets sticky; take it past about 125C (257F) and it seizes to rock before the sticks are coated. Fry low and slow first, or the middles stay soft under the shell.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kurma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kurma"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Ginger","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"obi-non","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"190 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml whole milk, warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"9 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g butter, soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk beaten with 1 tbsp milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp nigella seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp white sesame seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix flour, salt, yeast and sugar, work in the milk, water and butter, then knead 10 minutes to a firm dough that is smooth and springs back slowly; it should feel tighter than pizza dough, not slack.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and prove until doubled and domed, about 1 hour 30 minutes in a warm kitchen, then divide in two, ball tightly and rest 20 minutes until the balls flatten under their own weight.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":6600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Press each ball out to a 22 cm disc, working from the middle so a 3 cm rim stays thick and puffy while the centre thins to about 5 mm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stamp the centre all over with a chekich or the tines of a fork, pushing right through the dough until you feel the tray, then brush with the yolk wash and scatter nigella and sesame over the docked area.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake on a steel or heavy tray preheated to 250C (480F) for 12 to 15 minutes, until the rim is deep chestnut and hollow when tapped and the stamped centre is dry, pale gold and crisp.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false}],"note":"The stamped centre is not decoration. Docking pins the middle to the tray so gas can lift only the rim; skip it and the loaf inflates into a hollow dome that tears away from its base and will not stack or dry properly. Press the chekich right through to the tray, and mist the discs before baking so the holes do not seal shut as the gluten relaxes.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout."},"pairingId":2,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Obi%20Non%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Obi Non"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sesame","Sugar","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"qovurma-palov","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g devzira or medium grain rice, rinsed until the water runs clear then soaked 1 hour in warm salted water"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g lamb shoulder, in 4 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g lamb tail fat, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml sunflower or cottonseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g carrots, cut into 5 mm batons by hand"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole head of garlic, unpeeled, root trimmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cumin seed, crushed between the palms"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried red chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g barberries or raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml boiling water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the tail fat in a kazan or heavy casserole over high heat until the cubes shrink to golden crackling and the fat runs clear; lift them out, add the oil and take it to a shimmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onions to deep gold, then brown the lamb on every face, then add the carrots and fold gently until they slump and glaze but still hold their edges; grated or stirred hard, they will dissolve.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the cumin, coriander, chilli, barberries, salt and boiling water, bury the garlic head, and simmer uncovered about 40 minutes until the zirvak is dark and the fat floats clear and separate on the surface.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drain the rice and level it over the zirvak without stirring, then pour boiling water over the back of a skimmer to sit 2 cm above the rice and boil hard, uncovered, until the surface water sinks to rice level and craters open across the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heap the rice into a dome, drive holes to the bottom with a chopstick, cover with a cloth wrapped lid and hold on the lowest heat 25 minutes, until a grain from the top is tender through with no chalk in the centre.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the garlic, turn the rice through the zirvak with a skimmer for the first time, and invert onto a platter with the meat on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The rice cooks in steam rising through the zirvak, never in it. Stir before service and the grains break, starch leaches into the fat and the plov collapses into porridge. The open boil must drop the water to rice level before the lid goes on: cover early and the top layer stays chalky while the base scorches onto the kazan.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","gently hot","earthy","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, a warm current of chile, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Qovurma%20Palov%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Qovurma Palov"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Chiles","Dried fruit","Rice","Cumin","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"qaynatma-shurpa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg lamb neck and shoulder on the bone, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, peeled and left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced thin"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g small waxy potatoes, peeled and left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g carrots, in thick chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g turnip, in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tomatoes, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sweet pepper, in wide strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 dried red chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"large handful of dill, coriander and purple basil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the meat with the cold water and bring it up so slowly that the surface only trembles, skimming the grey raft of scum off whole before it breaks up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the whole onion, cumin, chilli and salt and hold at a bare shiver for 1 hour 30 minutes, never letting a bubble break; the broth should stay clear enough to read the bottom of the pot.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide in the potatoes, carrots and turnip whole and cook until a skewer meets no resistance but the pieces still hold their shape, about 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, pepper and sliced onion for the last 10 minutes, then kill the heat, throw in the herbs and let it stand covered 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve the broth alone in bowls and the meat and vegetables on a platter, with more torn basil over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A rolling boil emulsifies rendered lamb fat into the water and the broth goes cloudy and greasy for good. Held at a bare tremble the fat stays as a clear film you can skim or leave. Take the scum off early, while it still holds together as a raft; once a boil has beaten it back into the liquid, no amount of straining recovers the clarity.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","earthy","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, melting away silken."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Qaynatma%20Shurpa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Qaynatma Shurpa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Chiles","Radish/Turnip","Basil","Dill","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"dimlama","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g lamb or beef shoulder, in 4 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g lamb tail fat, diced, or 60 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g onions, in thick slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g carrots, in thick rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g small waxy potatoes, whole or halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tomatoes, thickly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sweet peppers, in wide strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, unpeeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"quarter of a white cabbage, leaves separated whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"handful of dill and coriander, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Render the fat in a heavy lidded pot, then sear the meat hard until every face is brown and the base of the pot is sticky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Build the layers in order without stirring: onion, carrot, tomato, pepper, garlic, then potatoes on top, seasoning each layer with salt, cumin, coriander and pepper as you go, and add no liquid at all.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cap the pot with overlapping cabbage leaves, tucking their edges down the sides so they seal the food in like a lid inside the lid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover, run high heat 10 minutes until you hear a steady hiss and steam escapes at the rim, then drop to the lowest setting and leave 1 hour 30 minutes without lifting the lid once.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":6000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Test a potato with a skewer, then lift the layers out in order onto a platter, or invert the pot, and scatter the herbs over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No water goes in. Onion and tomato give up enough to steam the pot, and the cabbage cap is a gasket that returns the condensate to the food. Add liquid and the layers boil instead: the potatoes fall apart and you have soup. Lifting the lid mid cook vents the steam that is cooking the upper layers, so they stay chalky. Weight the lid if it rattles.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dimlama%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dimlama"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Potato","Carrot","Cabbage","Dill","Cumin","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"qovurma-lagmon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"260 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sunflower oil for coating the ropes"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g lamb leg, sliced thin across the grain"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sweet peppers, in strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g celery or green beans, cut on the angle"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tomatoes, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g Chinese chives or spring onion, in 4 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cumin seed, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp chilli flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tbsp black or rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g salt for the sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the salt and soda in the water, mix into the flour to a shaggy mass, knead until smooth, then rest covered 30 minutes and knead again for 2 minutes; the dough should now stretch a hand's width before tearing.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into 12 ropes, roll each pencil thick, coat heavily in oil and coil them in a tray under cling film for at least 1 hour, until a rope pulled between the fingers thins without any spring back.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull each rope out between your hands, doubling it back and slapping the loop on the board, until the strands run about 3 mm; boil in heavily salted water 2 to 3 minutes until they float and squeak against the tooth, then rinse cold and toss with oil.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear the lamb in a screaming wok until crusted, add the onion, peppers, celery and garlic and fry until the edges char, then the cumin, chilli, tomato paste and tomatoes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook until the paste darkens and red oil separates at the edge, then add the noodles, vinegar, salt and chives and toss hard until every strand is coated and some are catching brown on the metal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pulling works on relaxed gluten, so the long oiled rest is the recipe. Salt tightens the strand so it thins without snapping, and a pinch of soda raises the pH and keeps it springy in the water. If a rope fights back, return it to the oil for fifteen minutes rather than forcing it; forcing bruises the dough and the bruised spot cooks up thick and gummy.","equipment":["Wok","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Hand-pulled noodles","Searing: the hard crust","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Resting meat & slicing against the grain","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","bright","herb-fresh","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Qovurma%20Lagmon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Qovurma Lagmon"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Celery","Chiles","Green beans","Chives","Flour","Vinegar","Tomato paste","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"qovoq-manti","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g pumpkin or winter squash, peeled and cut into 5 mm dice by hand"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g cold butter, in small cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cumin seed, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"oil for the steamer trays and 30 g melted butter to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead flour, salt and water to a firm dough, wrap it and rest 40 minutes, until it rolls out without pulling back on itself.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dice the pumpkin small by hand and mix with the onion, cumin and pepper, but hold the salt back until you are ready to fill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough to 1.5 mm and cut 12 cm squares; salt the filling, then put a heaped spoonful and a cube of cold butter on each square, bring the four corners to the centre and pinch the seams shut, then join the two opposite ears underneath.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Oil the steamer trays well and set the parcels a finger apart, then steam over hard boiling water 40 minutes, until the wrappers are translucent and drum tight and no floury bloom is left on top.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift them off with an oiled spatula, brush with melted butter and serve at once with cold suzma.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Dice the pumpkin, never grate it. Cubes release their juice slowly and it stays in the parcel, while grated flesh floods the wrapper and blows the base out on the tray, which is also why the salt goes in only as you fill. Oil the trays and leave a finger between parcels: steam condensing in the gap between two touching manti glues them, and they tear at the seam.","equipment":["Steamer","Sheet pan","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Egg wash & the baker’s shine"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Qovoq%20Manti%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Qovoq Manti"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Squash/Pumpkin","Butter","Flour","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["egg-wash-and-the-bakers-shine","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"qiymali-somsa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"350 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g salt for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g lamb tail fat or butter, soft but not melted, for laminating"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g lamb shoulder, hand chopped into 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g onions, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g lamb tail fat, in 5 mm dice, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp cumin seed, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 tsp coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"9 g salt for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk beaten with 1 tsp water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp sesame or nigella seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Knead a firm dough from the flour, salt and water, rest it 30 minutes, then roll it as thin as newsprint, smear the soft fat right to the edges, roll into a tight log and chill 20 minutes until the fat is set but not hard.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the meat by hand and mix with an equal weight of onion, the diced fat, cumin, pepper and salt; it should look wet and loose, not like a paste.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the log into 12, stand each piece on its cut face and roll out to a 12 cm round so the spiral of layers shows on the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill each round, fold up into a triangle and seal the seams hard, then set them seam side down, brush with yolk and scatter the seeds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake on a steel or heavy tray preheated to 250C (480F) for 25 to 30 minutes, until the layers have visibly lifted apart and the base is deep brown and rigid enough to knock on.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"The onion is not seasoning, it is the liquid: equal weights of hand chopped meat and onion steam each other inside a sealed shell. Minced meat packs tight, weeps early and boils, which is why the knife matters. The fat smeared through the rolled log is what splits the layers, so it must be soft and cool rather than melted, or it soaks into the dough and bakes solid.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Qiymali%20Somsa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Qiymali Somsa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Eggs","Onion","Butter","Flour","Sesame","Cumin","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"achichuk","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g white onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g salt for the onion, plus a pinch to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green chilli, sliced into thin rings"},{"kind":"item","text":"small handful of purple basil leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"small handful of coriander leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"half tsp coarse black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slice the onion as close to translucent as your knife allows, toss it with the salt and leave 10 minutes until it weeps and goes limp.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the onion under cold water and wring it out in a cloth, then taste: it should read sweet and cool with no burn left at the back of the throat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice the tomatoes 3 mm thick over a bowl so none of the juice is lost.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Combine tomatoes, onion, chilli, pepper and the caught juice, tear the herbs in at the last second and serve cold, with no oil, beside plov or somsa.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No oil goes near it. The dish exists to cut the fat of the plov beside it, and the only dressing is the juice the tomatoes give up. Salting the sliced onion and then rinsing and wringing it leaches out the sulphur compounds that make raw onion burn, so it reads sweet instead of harsh. Dress it early and the tomatoes go pale and flood the bowl, so slice it at the table.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Achichuk%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Achichuk"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Tomato","Chiles","Cilantro","Basil"]},{"slug":"suzma","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g live natural yoghurt at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small garlic clove, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp dill, finely chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the milk to 85C (185F) and hold it there 10 minutes, stirring so it does not catch, then cool to 43C (110F), warm to a fingertip but not hot.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk in the yoghurt, cover and keep at blood heat 6 to 8 hours, until the surface is set firm enough to hold the mark of a spoon and smells cleanly sour.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the salt, pour into a doubled muslin, tie it and hang in the fridge 8 to 12 hours, until the curd holds its shape like soft cheese and the whey has stopped dripping.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat smooth with the garlic and dill and serve cold, spooned over manti, stirred into shurpa or eaten with non.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Holding the milk at 85C denatures the whey proteins so they knit into the casein net; skip that step and the curd is weak and drains down to a grainy, chalky paste. Salt before hanging rather than after, because salt pulls whey out faster and firms what is left behind. Hang it somewhere cold: draining warm lets the culture keep working and the suzma turns bitter.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","silky","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Suzma%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Suzma"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Dill","Milk","Yogurt","Clove"]},{"slug":"un-holvasi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g walnuts, roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"pinch of fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the sugar and water to a clear syrup at 110C (230F), then keep it hot at the side of the stove.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter in a heavy pan over medium low heat, stir in the flour and salt and cook, stirring without stopping, 20 to 25 minutes; it will go from pale sand to hazelnut brown, loosen from a paste to a pourable cream and smell of toasted nuts.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1740,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat, then pour the hot syrup in a thin stream while beating hard; the mixture hisses, seizes and then comes back glossy and thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the walnuts, press 2 cm deep into an oiled tray and leave until firm and just warm, then cut into diamonds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Everything rests on cooking the flour past the raw paste stage: below hazelnut colour it tastes of uncooked roux and sugar will not hide it. Syrup and flour must both be hot when they meet, or the starch sets in lumps that never smooth out again. Pull the pan off the heat before the syrup goes in, or the sugar catches on the base and turns the whole batch bitter.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Making a roux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20a%20roux%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Un%20Holvasi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Un Holvasi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uzbek%20%26%20the%20Silk%20Road%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uzbek & the Silk Road kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Flour","Sugar","Nuts"]},{"slug":"arepa-reina-pepiada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g masarepa, precooked white corn flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml warm water, about 40C (105F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp neutral oil, for the pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g chicken breast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf and half an onion, for poaching"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large ripe avocado, about 200 g flesh"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g mayonnaise"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coriander leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Poach the chicken in barely trembling salted water with the bay and onion until a skewer meets no resistance at the thickest point, about 18 minutes, then cool it in its liquid and pull the meat into long threads with two forks.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the salt into the warm water, rain in the masarepa with one hand while stirring with the other, and leave it 5 minutes: it swells from a loose batter into a soft dough that holds a fingerprint.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knead 2 minutes with wet hands and pat into 5 discs 1.5 cm thick; if the rim cracks the dough is dry, so work in another spoonful of water until the edge stays smooth.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook on a lightly oiled cast iron pan over medium heat until each face is matte, freckled and lifts cleanly, about 6 minutes a side, then finish in a 200C (400F) oven for 12 minutes, until the arepa sounds hollow when tapped.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":1080,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold the shredded chicken through the mashed avocado, mayonnaise, coriander, lime and spring onion, seasoning until it tastes faintly over salted, because it will be eaten inside bland corn.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split each hot arepa around the edge with a serrated knife leaving a hinge, scrape out any doughy crumb from the centre, and fill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Masarepa is corn cooked, dried and milled, so it hydrates cold and needs no further cooking; masa harina is nixtamalised with lime, tastes of it, and will not swap in. The flour drinks well over twice its weight in water, and the 5 minute rest is when it does so. Rush it and the dough cracks, the crust seals early, and no steam pocket forms to split.","equipment":["Skillet"],"techniques":["Arepas","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Shaping by hand","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":23,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Arepas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20arepas%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arepa%20Reina%20Pepiada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arepa Reina Pepiada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"serrated-slicers-and-the-long-blades","term":"Serrated, Slicers & the Long Blades"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Scallion","Corn","Avocado","Lime","Cilantro","Bay leaf","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Mayonnaise"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"cachapa-con-queso-de-mano","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g sweetcorn kernels, from about 6 ears"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g masarepa, precooked white corn flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g sugar, or 30 g grated papelón"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter, for the pan"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g queso de mano or another soft fresh curd cheese, thickly sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"butter, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blitz the kernels with the egg, milk, sugar and salt in short bursts and stop while the mixture is still coarse and speckled; a smooth purée loses the shreds of skin that keep the pancake from turning gluey.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the masarepa and rest the batter 10 minutes, until a spoonful mounds briefly before settling; if it still runs like cream, add masarepa 10 g at a time.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter a heavy pan over medium low heat and ladle in 150 ml, spreading it to a round about 15 cm across and 8 mm thick.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wait until the surface has gone from glossy to matte and the rim lifts without tearing, about 5 minutes, then flip once and give it 3 minutes more.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Off the heat, lay the cheese over one half, fold, and press for a moment so the residual warmth softens it rather than melting it out; finish with a knob of butter on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no gluten here. The only structure is corn starch swelling and setting, and that happens from the hot underside upward, so the top is still liquid long after the base has coloured. Flip on the matte surface, not the clock. Too early and it tears into scrambled batter, and no extra masarepa will glue the pieces back together.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":8,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cachapa%20con%20Queso%20de%20Mano%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cachapa con Queso de Mano"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Corn","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"pabellon-criollo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg beef flank or skirt, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion halved, plus 1 large onion sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 ají dulce peppers, seeded and chopped, or 1 extra red pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g ripe tomatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp Worcestershire sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g long grain white rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g cooked caraotas negras, warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 very ripe plantains, skins blackened"},{"kind":"item","text":"oil, for frying the tajadas"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the beef with cold water, add the halved onion and a spoonful of salt, and hold it at a bare tremble until a fork twists a strand away without force, about 2 hours; cool it in the broth and keep 300 ml of that broth.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tear the cooled beef by hand along the grain into long threads, never across it, since the fibres in flank all run one way and that is where the dish takes its name.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the sliced onion, red pepper, ají dulce and garlic in the oil, then add the grated tomato and cumin and cook until the pan floor stays dry for a second behind the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the shredded beef with the reserved broth and Worcestershire and simmer uncovered until the liquid has gone and the threads are glossy and separate rather than swimming.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the plantains into 5 mm diagonal slices and fry in 1 cm of oil over gentle heat until deep amber and still bendy, not crisp, then drain and salt lightly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam the rice plainly and plate the four elements in touching stripes, beef, rice, beans and tajadas, so every forkful can take two of them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Boil the beef first, sauce it second. Collagen needs long wet heat to melt, and salted acidic tomato around the meat from the start pulls water from the fibres and welds them, so it shreds short and chews tough. The finished mechada should be dry and glossy, nearer a seasoned floss than a stew; liquid left in the pan is the usual reason it tastes flat.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep","earthy","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pabell%C3%B3n%20Criollo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pabellón Criollo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"flank-skirt-and-hanger","term":"Flank, Skirt & Hanger"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Bell pepper","Plantain","Rice","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"asado-negro","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg beef eye of round, in one piece, trimmed"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g papelón, grated, or dark muscovado sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, white part only, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 ají dulce peppers, chopped, or 1 red pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g ripe tomatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml beef broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp Worcestershire sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"salt and black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the beef heavily the night before with salt, pepper, garlic and Worcestershire, then wipe it bone dry before cooking, because any wetness makes molten caramel spit and seize.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the papelón in the oil in a heavy pot over medium heat and keep going past golden, past mahogany, until it smokes lightly and is the colour of strong black coffee, roughly 5 minutes; the smell turns from sweet to bitter and that is the signal.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lower the dry beef straight into the caramel, which halts it, and turn it every minute until every face is lacquered black, about 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the meat out, soften the onions, leek, ají dulce, garlic and carrot in the same pot, then add the tomato, wine, bay and broth and return the beef.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and hold at the barest simmer, turning the meat every 40 minutes, until a skewer slides through the centre with no grip, about 3 hours.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest until cool enough to carve into 5 mm sheets without shredding, blend the sauce smooth, and reheat the slices in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The black is burnt sugar, not browning. Caramel darkens as sucrose fragments into bitter compounds, and this dish wants it taken to the edge of that, where the sweetness has nearly gone. Pull it early and you have a sweet brown pot roast; thirty seconds late and the sauce is acrid past rescue. Have the beef dry and standing beside the pot before you start.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Asado fire","Sugar stages & caramel","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","aromatic","umami-deep","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Asado fire","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=argentine%20asado%20fire%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Asado%20Negro%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Asado Negro"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Leek","Tomato","Carrot","Bell pepper","Bay leaf","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"tequenos","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cold butter, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, plus 1 beaten egg for sealing"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"110 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g firm salty white cheese, queso llanero or queso blanco duro"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres neutral oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the butter into the flour, sugar and salt to a sandy crumb, work in the egg and just enough water for a firm dough that is not sticky, then rest it wrapped for 30 minutes so it rolls without springing back.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut the cheese into batons 7 cm long and 1.5 cm square; it should squeak and stay square under the knife, since a wet cheese liquefies in the oil and blows the seam open.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the dough to 2 mm, cut ribbons 2 cm wide, and wind one around each baton in a spiral, overlapping every turn by a third and pinching both ends shut with beaten egg so no cheese shows.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Freeze the wrapped tequeños on a tray for at least 30 minutes, which lets the pastry set before the cheese has time to run.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry from frozen at 170C (340F), six at a time, turning until evenly deep gold and blistered, about 4 minutes, then drain standing on end.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Two failures share one cause: the cheese going loose before the pastry has cooked. Use a low moisture salty white cheese that holds its shape, wrap with a third of overlap and closed ends, and fry from frozen at 170C (340F). Hotter and the crust colours over raw dough; cooler and the pastry drinks oil and turns heavy before the seam sets.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting dough: the pause that does the work","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":0,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Teque%C3%B1os%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tequeños"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cheese (any)","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work"]},{"slug":"caraotas-negras","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g dried black beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water, plus more to top up"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red pepper, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 ají dulce peppers, chopped, or 1 extra red pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g papelón, grated, or dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g crumbly white cheese, grated, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the beans overnight in cold salted water; salt in the soak helps the skins soften, so it is acid, not salt, that you hold back.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, cover with fresh water and the bay, and simmer with the lid ajar until a bean crushes to paste between finger and thumb with no chalky centre, 60 to 90 minutes, topping up so they stay just covered.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile fry the onion, red pepper, ají dulce and garlic in the oil until soft and starting to catch, then add the cumin and stir it in the hot oil until it smells toasted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the sofrito into the tender beans with the salt and papelón and cook 15 minutes more, until the liquid thickens to a loose gravy that coats the back of a spoon.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve under a drift of grated white cheese, or spoon them into a split arepa with the same cheese, which makes a dominó.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hold the sugar and every acid back until the beans are already soft. Pectin in the skins only breaks down in a near neutral pot, so tomato, vinegar or an early acidic sofrito locks the skins and leaves beans that stay chalky however long they boil. Salted soaking water does the opposite and helps. The papelón at the end is what makes these Venezuelan rather than Cuban.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Arepas","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Arepas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20arepas%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caraotas%20Negras%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Caraotas Negras"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Bell pepper","Bay leaf","Cheese (any)","Black beans","Sugar","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits"]},{"slug":"guasacaca","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 ripe but firm avocados, about 300 g flesh"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g flat leaf parsley, leaves and fine stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander, leaves and fine stems"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green pepper, deseeded and chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small white onion, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 ml white wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blitz the parsley, coriander, green pepper, onion, garlic, vinegar and salt to a rough green slush before the avocado goes anywhere near the jug.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the avocado and pulse in short bursts only until the sauce is loose enough to pour, stopping while flecks of herb are still visible.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Trickle in the oil with the motor barely turning so it disperses through the purée instead of sitting on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Push the vinegar and salt further than feels comfortable; this goes over grilled beef and fried food, not onto a crisp, and it has to be sharp enough to cut fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is not guacamole. Avocado is only the body; herbs and vinegar carry the flavour, and the ratio runs the other way round. Long blending warms the mixture and shears the cells, releasing the enzymes that turn it bitter and khaki within an hour. Short pulses, cold ingredients and enough vinegar to hold the pH down keep it green and sharp for two days.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Building an emulsion","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","herb-fresh","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Guasacaca%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Guasacaca"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Bell pepper","Avocado","Parsley","Vinegar","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["building-an-emulsion","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"pan-de-jamon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white bread flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml warm whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, plus 1 for glazing"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g soft butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g thinly sliced smoked cooked ham"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g streaky bacon rashers"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g green olives stuffed with pepper, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g papelón, dissolved in 30 ml hot water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, yeast, sugar, salt, milk and egg to a shaggy mass, knead 10 minutes, then work in the butter piece by piece until the dough pulls a thin translucent window; prove until doubled.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to a rectangle roughly 35 cm by 45 cm and 5 mm thick, shingle the ham over it right to the edges, lay the bacon in stripes, and scatter the olives and raisins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll up tightly from the long side, keeping the ham taut and pressing air out ahead of the roll, then pinch the seam and tuck the ends underneath.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill the log 20 minutes so the fat firms, prick it to the centre all over with a skewer, and prove it seam down only until half again in size, not doubled.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bake at 180C (350F) for 40 minutes, glazed with beaten egg at the start and with the papelón syrup only in the last 5 minutes, since that sugar would blacken over a full bake.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool at least an hour before cutting, or the spiral drags apart under the knife.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The filling is wet, salty and fatty, and all three fight the crumb. Rendered fat and steam trapped between the layers push the spiral apart into a hollow ring, so roll it tight, prick the log to give steam an exit, and stop the second prove at half again. A fully proved dough has no strength left to carry that weight and sinks damp and dense at the base.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","briny","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pan%20de%20Jam%C3%B3n%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pan de Jamón"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Bacon","Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Olives","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Bread","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"quesillo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g sugar, for the caramel"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 large eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tin sweetened condensed milk, 397 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml dark rum"},{"kind":"item","text":"a pinch of salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the sugar and water without stirring to a clear amber that smells of toffee, pour it into the mould and swirl it up the sides at once; it stops flowing within seconds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the whole eggs, condensed milk, milk, vanilla, rum and salt hard for a full minute and do not strain, because the froth you are raising is the point of the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the caramelised mould, cover with its lid or foil, and stand it in a roasting tin with hot water reaching halfway up the sides.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (350F) until the outer 5 cm is set and the centre still shivers as one mass when nudged, 55 to 65 minutes.","durationSec":3900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Chill overnight, run a knife round the rim, and invert onto a lipped plate; the caramel will have drawn water out of the custard and gone back to sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Here quesillo and flan part company. Flan is strained to kill bubbles; quesillo is whisked to make them, and whole eggs rather than yolks alone supply the white protein that traps them as the honeycomb it is named for. It still sets by coagulation, so use a water bath and pull it wobbling: past about 85C the proteins tighten and the slice weeps.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a floral thread lifting the aroma, melting away silken."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Quesillo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Quesillo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"chicha-andina","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g long grain white rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml whole milk, plus more to loosen"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g sweetened condensed milk, plus more to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"a pinch of salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"ground cinnamon, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the rice with the water and cinnamon stick, stirring now and then, well past done, until the grains have burst and the liquid is cloudy and thick, about 35 minutes.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":2100,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift out the cinnamon stick and let the rice cool to room temperature before it goes near the blender; hot starch reads thinner than it is and you will overshoot the milk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend with the milk, condensed milk, vanilla and salt until completely smooth, then pass it through a sieve, pressing hard, to leave the grit behind.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill at least 4 hours, over which it thickens noticeably, then let it down with cold milk to the consistency of pouring cream.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve over ice with a spiral of condensed milk and a heavy dusting of ground cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The thickener is gelatinised rice starch, and it has not finished working when the pot leaves the heat. As it cools the starch chains reassociate and the drink sets, so a chicha blended to the right body while warm is a paste by morning. Blend it looser than you want it, chill it, then adjust with cold milk at the glass rather than at the blender.","equipment":["Blender"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","floral","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":32,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chicha%20Andina%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chicha Andina"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Rice","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"hawaij-lil-shorba","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g green cardamom pods, husks discarded"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g ground turmeric"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry toast the cumin, coriander, peppercorns and cloves in an unoiled pan over medium heat, shaking constantly, until the cumin darkens one shade and the smell turns nutty rather than green, 2 to 3 minutes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Tip them straight onto a cold plate; left sitting in the hot pan they carry on cooking and go acrid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Crack the cardamom pods, discard the husks, and grind the seeds with the cooled spices to a fine powder, sieving and regrinding the tailings so no pepper grit survives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the turmeric through the cold ground spice and store airtight, in the dark.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two blends share this name and they are not interchangeable. This one, for soup and rice, is built on cumin, black pepper and coriander with turmeric for colour; hawaij for coffee is ginger, cardamom, cinnamon and clove, with no cumin and no turmeric at all. Turmeric is a dried rhizome, not a seed: in the toasting pan it scorches to dust and bitterness long before the cumin is ready, so it goes in cold, after grinding.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","warm-spiced","gently hot","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hawaij%20lil%20Shorba%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hawaij lil Shorba"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Cumin","Coriander seed","Turmeric","Cardamom","Clove"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"sahawiq-hamra","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"25 g dried red chillies, stems removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"half tsp coriander seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g ripe tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the dried chillies with just boiled water and leave them until they are limp and the water has stained deep red, about 20 minutes; drain, keeping one spoonful of the soaking water.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic, salt, cumin and coriander in a mortar until the garlic no longer shows as white flecks and the paste is wet and smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tear in the drained chillies and pound until the skins break down and the paste turns glossy brick red, adding the reserved water only if the pestle starts to drag.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Halve the tomatoes and grate them down to the skin on the coarse side of a box grater, discarding the skins.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the pulp into the chilli paste a spoonful at a time so it stays thick and clinging, then sharpen with the lemon juice and more salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The red sahawiq takes its heat and its colour from rehydrated dried chilli rather than fresh, which is why it tastes deeper and rounder than the green. Pound it, do not blend: a blade whips in air, turns the paste pale orange and frothy, and shreds the seeds so bitterness comes forward. The tomato goes in last and slowly, or its water thins the sauce to a soup by morning.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","aromatic","bright","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, aromatics blooming from the first bite, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sahawiq%20Hamra%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sahawiq Hamra"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Tomato","Chiles","Lemon","Cumin","Coriander seed","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"saltah-bil-hulba","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"30 g fenugreek seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g lamb shoulder or neck, in 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp samn (clarified butter)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g tomatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g waxy potatoes, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp hawaij lil shorba"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp sahawiq hamra"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander leaf, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the fenugreek with cold water by 5 cm; after 8 hours pour off the yellow water, rinse the seeds and cover them again with fresh cold water for another 8 hours, until they are swollen and slippery.","durationSec":28800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":57600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, grind to a smooth paste with 3 tbsp cold water, then whip, adding cold water a spoonful at a time, until the paste has tripled and stands in a pale foam like whipped cream; fold in 1 tbsp of the sahawiq and chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the lamb in the samn in a heavy pot without crowding, until the pieces release from the base of their own accord, then add the onion and cook to soft gold, followed by the hawaij and grated tomato until the fat pools red at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the water and salt and simmer covered until the meat pulls apart under a fork, dropping in the potato for the last 20 minutes, then boil hard and uncovered until the stew coats a spoon.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat a stone or unglazed clay madra in a 250C (480F) oven until it stings the air above it, ladle in the boiling stew so it keeps a rolling boil, spoon the hulba and the remaining sahawiq over the top with the coriander, and carry it to the table still erupting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hulba is fenugreek whipped to a foam. It works because the seed carries saponins, natural surfactants, held in a galactomannan gum that traps the bubbles. Those same saponins are bitter and water soluble, so the seeds soak long and the yellow water is thrown away and replaced; skip that and the hulba is grey and acrid. Whip with cold water only, since warmth slackens the gum, and spoon it on at the last second: it collapses if stirred into the boiling madra.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sautéing & the shallow fry","Salted water & the float test","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Saltah%20bil%20Hulba%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Saltah bil Hulba"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Tomato","Potato","Butter"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"mandi-laham","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg lamb shoulder on the bone, in 4 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tbsp hawaij lil shorba"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp salt, plus 2 tsp for the rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g basmati rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml water or light lamb stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 loomi (dried lime), pierced twice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"large pinch saffron, steeped in 2 tbsp hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lump of hardwood charcoal and 1 tsp oil, for smoking"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the lamb with the salt and hawaij, working the mixture into every cut face, and leave it out while the oven heats to 160C (320F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the basmati until the water runs clear, soak it for 30 minutes and drain it well.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Spread the rice in a deep roasting tray with the water, onion, loomi, cardamom, cinnamon and salt, set a rack over the tray and the lamb on the rack, seal the whole thing under two tight layers of foil and roast about 3 hours, until the meat gives at the bone and the rice has drunk the stock and the drippings.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift off the rack; the meat is pale, not browned. Slide it under a hot grill for 5 minutes if you want the fat cap coloured, and fork the saffron water through the rice.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand a small metal dish on the rice, drop in a coal heated to red, pour on the oil and cover the tray at once; after 5 minutes lift the dish out and serve the meat on the rice.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"In a mandi pit the meat hangs above the rice inside a sealed clay oven, so it never browns: it cooks in trapped, fat saturated air and everything it renders falls into the grain below. The oven version only holds up if the tray is sealed airtight, or the fat evaporates and the rice dries to a crust. The coal stands in for the pit's ember bed; five minutes under cover reads as smoke, fifteen reads as tar.","equipment":["Grill","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","smoky","floral","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, wood-smoke running underneath, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mandi%20Laham%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mandi Laham"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Lime","Rice","Stock/Broth","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"machboos-dajaj","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg chicken, jointed into 8"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tbsp ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves and 20 g ginger, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g tomatoes, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp baharat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 loomi (dried limes), pierced twice each"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 green cardamom pods, 1 cinnamon stick, 3 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g basmati rice, rinsed and soaked 30 minutes"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"pinch of saffron, steeped in 2 tbsp hot water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Fry the onions in the ghee over medium heat, stirring, until they are deep gold and shrunk to a quarter of their bulk, then add the garlic and ginger paste and cook until the raw smell goes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the baharat, turmeric and whole spices for 30 seconds until they bloom, add the grated tomato and cook until the fat pools red at the edges.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay in the chicken with the water, salt and pierced loomi, cover, and simmer until the thigh joint loosens, about 35 minutes; lift out the chicken and the loomi.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the broth to a hard boil, make it up to 800 ml, stir in the drained rice and boil uncovered until craters open on the surface and the liquid sits level with the grains, then clamp on a lid and steam on the lowest heat for 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blister the chicken skin under a hot grill while the rice rests off the heat, fork the saffron water through, and set the chicken on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Loomi is a lime boiled in brine and dried in the sun until it is hollow, dark inside and smells of fermentation, and it is what separates machboos from a biryani. Pierce it twice and simmer it whole so it leaks slowly. Split or crushed, it dumps pith and seeds into the pot and the broth turns soapy and medicinal, a bitterness no sugar, salt or acid will pull back out.","equipment":["Grill","Mortar & pestle","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","aromatic","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, aromatics blooming from the first bite, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Biryani & dum","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=biryani%20dum%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Machboos%20Dajaj%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Machboos Dajaj"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Tomato","Lime","Rice","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"zurbian-hadrami","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg lamb shoulder on the bone, in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g full fat yogurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves and 20 g ginger, pounded to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp hawaij lil shorba"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp baharat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g samn (clarified butter)"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g waxy potatoes, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g basmati rice, soaked 30 minutes"},{"kind":"item","text":"large pinch saffron, steeped in 3 tbsp warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt, plus salt for the rice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coriander leaf, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the yogurt with the garlic and ginger paste, hawaij, baharat, cinnamon and salt, turn the lamb through it until every face is coated, and leave it to marinate for at least 4 hours.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onions in the samn until mahogany and crisp at the edges, lift out half for the top, then tip in the lamb with all its marinade.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook covered on a low flame until the yogurt has broken and reduced to a thick oily masala clinging to the meat and the lamb gives at the bone; fry the potatoes in the same fat until a crust sets on the cut faces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the drained rice in heavily salted water for about 5 minutes, until the grain still snaps at the core, and drain it.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Layer the meat and potatoes, then the rice, then the reserved onions, saffron milk and coriander, seal with a tight lid and cook on the lowest flame or at 160C (320F) until the top grains are dry and separate and the steam smells of saffron, not raw rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yogurt is both the marinade and the braising liquid. Its acid swells the surface proteins so they hold water, and its fat and solids reduce into the masala that coats the meat. It is meant to split here, unlike a Levantine laban sauce, but it must split slowly: on high heat the whey floods out at once, the lamb boils grey in it and the masala never comes together. Low flame, lid on.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Reducing a sauce","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright","aromatic","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zurbian%20Hadrami%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zurbian Hadrami"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Potato","Butter","Milk","Yogurt","Rice","Cinnamon","Clove","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"harees","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g whole wheat berries, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g lamb shoulder on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.8 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g samn (clarified butter), melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"ground cinnamon and sugar, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the soaked wheat and put it in a heavy pot with the lamb, salt, cinnamon and water; bring to the boil and skim off the grey foam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold it at the barest simmer with the lid ajar for about 3 hours, until a grain smears between your fingers with no chalk left in the middle and the meat collapses into threads.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fish out the bones, gristle and cinnamon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the pot over low heat with a wooden mallet or the back of a heavy spoon, working against the side, until wheat and meat lose their separate identities and the mass pulls away from the pot in one glossy sheet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread it in a shallow dish, hollow the centre and pour in the melted samn until it pools; dust with cinnamon and sugar at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The beating is not mashing. Three hours of wet heat gelatinises the wheat starch and melts the collagen to gelatin; beating then shears the swollen grains so the leached amylose binds the meat threads into one body. Start beating while any grain still has a chalky core and the harees stays gritty for good, because no amount of further work will cook a dry centre. Keep the heat low or it catches and scorches.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Harees%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Harees"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Berries","Butter","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"shafout","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 lahoh, the holed pancake bread, about 24 cm across"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g laban or thin natural yogurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coriander, leaves and stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g mint leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 spring onions"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"juice of half a lemon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the coriander, mint, spring onion, chilli, garlic and salt with 100 ml of the laban until the sauce is a uniform grass green with no leaf shreds left, then stir it back through the rest of the laban with the lemon juice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack or tear the lahoh into a wide shallow dish, holed side up.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the green laban over so every sheet is flooded, pressing gently until the bread stops floating.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill until the bread has drunk the sauce and gone silky but still holds its layers, 20 to 30 minutes, and serve cold.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Lahoh is a fermented batter bread riddled with open holes, and shafout is really a timed soak. Those holes drink the laban and go silky in twenty minutes; past an hour the crumb dissolves and the dish is paste, which is why pita cannot stand in for it. Blend only a cupful of the laban with the herbs. A full blender load bruises the coriander warm and the sauce turns bitter and grey green.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","gently hot","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, a warm current of chile, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":19,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Shafout%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Shafout"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Chiles","Lemon","Mint","Yogurt","Bread","Clove"]},{"slug":"bint-al-sahn","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"about 150 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g samn (clarified butter), melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g honey, sidr honey if you have it"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tsp nigella seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, yeast, sugar and salt, work in the eggs and enough warm water to give a soft, slightly tacky dough, and knead until it stretches to a translucent window without tearing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prove covered until doubled, about 1 hour, then divide into 10 equal balls, coat each one in melted samn and rest them 20 minutes so the gluten slackens.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":3600,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stretch each ball on an oiled worktop with flat palms, working from the middle outward, until the sheet is thin enough to read through and wider than the tin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay a sheet in a buttered 26 cm round tin, brush it with samn and repeat, folding the overhang inward, until all ten are stacked; give the top sheet a heavier coat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (390F) for 25 to 30 minutes, until the top is deep gold and the edges have crisped and pulled from the tin, then brush with more samn while hot, pour over the honey so it sinks between the layers, and scatter the nigella.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no butter block and no folding here. Each sheet is stretched paper thin, coated in samn and stacked, and in the oven the water in the dough flashes to steam and lifts one sheet off the next while the fat stops them welding together. Roll the dough with a pin instead of stretching it by hand and you drive that air out: the sheets fuse and it bakes as one bready slab. Honey goes on hot, so it thins and travels.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Proofing: the rise before the oven","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":6,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bint%20al%20Sahn%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bint al Sahn"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Honey","Sugar","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","proofing-the-rise-before-the-oven"]},{"slug":"luqaimat","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"half tsp salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"half tsp ground cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"large pinch saffron, crumbled into 2 tbsp warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain yogurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"about 300 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre neutral oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g date syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tbsp sesame seeds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the flour, cornflour, yeast, sugar, salt and cardamom together, then beat in the yogurt, saffron water and enough warm water to give a thick, sticky batter that falls from the spoon in a ribbon and slowly levels itself.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and leave until domed, full of bubbles and faintly beery, about 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Heat the oil to 170C (340F), squeeze the batter up through your fist and scrape each ball off with a wet spoon straight into the oil, frying in batches so the temperature does not crash.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry, stirring so they roll and colour evenly, until they are deep mahogany and rattle hard against the spoon, 4 to 5 minutes, then drain briefly.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss them in the date syrup while still hot, scatter with sesame and eat within the hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cornflour carries no gluten, so the shell stays brittle instead of bready, and the batter has to be slack enough to fall in a ribbon or the centres come out cakey. Hold the oil at 170C (340F): hotter and the crust sets before the middle cooks, leaving raw dough; cooler and they drink fat and go heavy. Fry them a shade darker than looks right, because the syrup softens the crust the moment it touches.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deep frying"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","warm-spiced","floral","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, warm spice humming through the middle, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Luqaimat%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Luqaimat"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Yogurt","Flour","Sesame","Sugar","Cardamom","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["deep-frying"]},{"slug":"makroud","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g coarse semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g fine semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml warm water, approximately"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pitted Deglet Nour dates"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml orange blossom water, 15 ml for the dates and 15 ml for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre sunflower oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml water, for the syrup"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the olive oil and melted butter through both semolinas with your fingertips for 3 minutes, until every grain is slick and a squeezed handful holds its shape, then cover and leave it to rest for at least 2 hours and preferably overnight.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve the salt in the warm water and add it a little at a time, gathering the dough with a flat hand rather than kneading, and stop the moment it coheres.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the dates with the cinnamon and 15 ml of the orange blossom water to a smooth paste, roll it into ropes the thickness of a finger, and case each rope in a flattened log of dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press a comb along the top, cut on the diagonal into 4 cm diamonds, and fry at 160C (320F) until pale gold and the bubbling has slowed to a whisper, about 7 minutes.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":660,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the honey with the 80 ml water and the remaining orange blossom water to 40C (104F) in a small deep pan, drop the pieces in straight from the fryer for 2 minutes so they stay submerged, then lift them onto a rack.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Semolina has no gluten worth developing, so this dough is held together by fat and water alone. Coat the grains in oil and rest them for hours, the same slow hydration the Maamoul dough gets, and they stay separate and sandy; let water reach them first and they glue into something hard. Honey is let down with water and held at 40C (104F) because neat honey is too viscous to enter the crumb, and it loses aroma the hotter and longer it is held. Hot pastry, cool syrup: condensing steam pulls it in.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","floral","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a floral thread lifting the aroma, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Makroud%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Makroud"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Makroud","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Makroud","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Orange","Dried fruit","Butter","Semolina","Honey","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"chebakia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g sesame seeds, toasted and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g blanched almonds, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground anise seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.3 g saffron threads, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml orange blossom water, 45 ml for the dough and 15 ml for the honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml warm water, approximately"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres sunflower oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g sesame seeds, toasted, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the crushed saffron in 45 ml of the orange blossom water for 10 minutes, until the liquid is deep orange.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the flour, ground sesame, almonds, yeast, anise, cinnamon and salt, work in the egg, butter, olive oil and saffron liquid, then add warm water until you have a firm dough and knead 10 minutes until it springs back slowly; rest 1 hour.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Roll to 2 mm thick, cut rectangles 8 cm by 5 cm, slit each four times down the middle, thread alternate strips through and pinch the ends into a rosette.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 165C (330F), turning once, until deep amber and the fierce crackling has died down, about 6 minutes.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift them straight into honey warmed to 50C (122F) with the remaining orange blossom water, hold them under for 7 minutes until they stop hissing and sink, then drain and shower with the toasted sesame.","durationSec":420,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"Yeast and a long rest look pointless in a pastry that ends up crisp, but they are what make the dough workable: fermentation relaxes the gluten so a 2 mm sheet will take four slits and a threaded rosette without tearing or springing back. The ground toasted sesame and almond are largely fat, and fat shortens, so the strips fry brittle rather than bready. Honey loses aroma the hotter and longer it is held, so 50C (122F) is a safe working temperature for the dip.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Kneading dough","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","floral","sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chebakia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chebakia"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Chebakia","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Chebakia","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Orange","Butter","Flour","Sesame","Honey","Nuts","Cinnamon","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["kneading-dough","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"balah-el-sham","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml rose water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water, for the choux"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g caster sugar, for the choux"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 large eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre sunflower oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the 400 g sugar, 250 ml water and lemon juice to 105C (220F), about 8 minutes, stir in the rose water off the heat, and chill until stone cold.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the second 250 ml water with the butter, 10 g sugar and salt to a rolling boil, beat in the flour all at once, and keep beating over low heat until the paste balls up and films the base of the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool it to 60C (140F), then beat in the eggs a little at a time until the paste falls from the spoon in a slow, reluctant ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe 6 cm fingers through a large star nozzle directly into oil at 150C (300F) and let the oil climb to 170C (340F) as they swell and colour, about 10 minutes, so the centres dry out before the outsides set.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift them hot into the cold syrup for 60 seconds, turning once, then drain on a rack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Syrup boiled to 105C (220F) is roughly 65 percent sugar: thin enough to run into the hollow centre. Take it to 112C (235F) and it is too dense to move, so it glazes the outside and weeps back off. The lemon juice inverts a little sucrose so the syrup will not crystallise in the fridge. Hot pastry into cold syrup keeps the dip short and the piped ridges crisp; hot into hot and the fingers keep drinking until they slump.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":["Choux pastry","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","floral","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Choux pastry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=choux%20pastry%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Balah%20El%20Sham%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Balah El Sham"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"coconut-candy","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g fresh coconut, coarsely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground nutmeg"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the sugar, water, salt and nutmeg to a boil in a wide, heavy pan, then stir in the grated coconut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over medium heat, stirring often, until the syrup has reduced to a heavy, slow glaze and a dragged spoon leaves a clean track across the pan base, about 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir constantly from here: the syrup will go cloudy, then dry, then break into sandy white grains that cling to the coconut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat while the grains still look faintly moist, spread the candy in a single layer on a tray, and leave it to cool and crisp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is the exact opposite of a brittle. A brittle wants sugar to cool as an amorphous glass, so you never stir it. Here you boil off water until the syrup is supersaturated and then stir hard, because the agitation seeds crystals and the sugar grains out as a dry white coat; that coat is what stops the coconut going sticky in humid air. Stop while it is barely moist. Cooked further the sugar starts to caramelise and the grains fuse back into toffee.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Coconut%20Candy%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Coconut Candy"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Sugar","Nutmeg"]},{"slug":"mkate-wa-sinia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain medium grain white rice, soaked 6 hours in cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml coconut milk, 400 ml for the batter and 100 ml after the ferment"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g rice flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ghee, for the tin"}],"steps":[{"text":"Drain the rice and blend it with 400 ml of the coconut milk and all the water until the batter is as smooth as double cream and you feel no grit when you rub a drop between finger and thumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the sugar, yeast and rice flour, cover, and leave at room temperature until the surface domes and breaks into bubbles and smells faintly sour, 3 hours to 4 hours.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir it down and fold in the last 100 ml of coconut milk, the cardamom and the salt, to a batter that pours like thin cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a ghee-greased 25 cm round tin to a depth of 3 cm and bake at 180C (350F) for 40 minutes to 45 minutes, until the top is bronzed and a skewer comes out clean.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool it completely in the tin before cutting.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no gluten here, so the crumb is nothing but gelatinised rice starch, and that has two consequences. The batter must be ground genuinely smooth, because any grain that does not burst reads as sand on the tongue. And the cake must cool right down in the tin, because rice starch only firms as it retrogrades on cooling, so a warm cut tears it into wet crumbs. The coconut fat coats the starch and keeps the set tender rather than rubbery.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Fermentation","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mkate%20wa%20Sinia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mkate wa Sinia"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Rice","Flour","Sugar","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"melktert","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g cold unsalted butter, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 large eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g unsalted butter, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cinnamon, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the cold butter into the 250 g flour, icing sugar and salt to coarse crumbs, bind with the yolk and cold water, line a 24 cm tin and chill it 30 minutes, then prick the base, line it with paper and baking beans and bake at 190C (375F) for 15 minutes, lift out the paper and beans and give it 5 minutes to 8 minutes more until the base is dry and sandy.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the milk to a bare simmer with the cinnamon stick, take it off the heat, and let it stand 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, caster sugar, cornflour and 40 g flour to a smooth slurry, temper it with a ladle of the hot milk, then return everything to the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook over medium heat, stirring into the corners, until it thickens and then bubbles lazily for a full 2 minutes.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Beat in the butter and vanilla, pour into the shell, dust heavily with ground cinnamon, and chill 4 hours until it cuts clean.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"This filling is set by starch, not by egg, which is why it has to reach a genuine boil. Egg yolk carries alpha-amylase, an enzyme that snips starch chains; below boiling it survives the pan and will quietly thin your set tart to a puddle overnight. Two minutes of lazy bubbling denatures it for good. The cornflour gives the clean firm set and the small measure of plain flour keeps the bite short rather than slippery.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","floral","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Melktert%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Melktert"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"cape-brandy-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g pitted dates, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g unsalted butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 large eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g pecans, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml brandy"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour the boiling water over the dates with the bicarbonate of soda and leave 10 minutes, until the fruit slumps and the liquid thickens.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cream the softened butter with the 200 g sugar until pale, beat in the eggs one at a time, then fold in the flour, baking powder, salt, pecans and the dates with all their liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in a buttered 23 cm dish at least 6 cm deep at 180C (350F) for 40 minutes to 45 minutes, until the centre springs back under a finger.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil the syrup sugar, water and butter for 5 minutes, then take the pan off the heat before stirring in the brandy and vanilla.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stand the dish on a tray, prick the cake all over with a skewer, and ladle the hot syrup over the hot cake in three goes, waiting each time for the surface to clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fried pastry wants one element hot and the other cold. A cake wants both hot, and the difference is the crust. A sponge has an open, unprotected crumb, so thin hot syrup runs down the skewer holes while the cooling crumb draws it inward. Let either side go cold and the syrup gels on the surface, leaving a wet top over a dry pudding. The brandy goes in off the heat because alcohol boils at 78C (172F) and the point of the dish is that it stays.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cape%20Brandy%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cape Brandy Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Dried fruit","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"qatayef-asafiri","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g fine semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g instant yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml warm water, approximately"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g caster sugar, for the ashta"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml orange blossom water"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g caster sugar, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml rose water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g pistachios, finely chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the 300 g sugar, 150 ml water and lemon juice to 105C (220F), stir in the rose water off the heat, and chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, semolina, yeast, 20 g sugar and salt with the warm water to a batter the consistency of single cream, and leave 45 minutes until the surface is a raft of fine bubbles.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the milk, cornflour and 40 g sugar to a boil and cook 2 minutes to a stiff paste, beat in the cream and orange blossom water, and chill; this is the ashta.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the baking powder into the rested batter, then cook 8 cm rounds on a dry medium pan on one side only, until the top has dried to a matt open honeycomb with no wet gloss left, about 90 seconds, stacking them under a damp cloth as they come off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe ashta into each, pinch half the seam shut, press the open cream edge into the pistachios, and pour the cold syrup over at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cooking on one side is the whole trick. Bubbles rise through the batter and burst at the top, leaving a dry honeycomb that will grip cream and syrup; flip the pancake and that surface seals, and nothing sticks or soaks. Wait until no gloss remains, but lift it before the edges curl, because a dried edge cracks instead of pinching shut. Here both the pastry and the syrup go on cold, because this one is meant to stay soft rather than crisp.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Qatayef%20Asafiri%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Qatayef Asafiri"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-floral-pantry-rose-orange-blossom-and-mahleb","term":"The Floral Pantry: Rose, Orange Blossom & Mahleb"}],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Orange","Milk","Cream","Flour","Semolina","Sugar","Nuts"]},{"slug":"maamoul","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g coarse semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g fine semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"280 g ghee, melted and warm"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground mahlab"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml warm milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml orange blossom water"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml rose water"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pitted dates"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g icing sugar, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pour the warm ghee over both semolinas and rub it through with your hands until every grain is slick, then cover and leave 12 hours at room temperature.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the sugar, mahlab, salt, milk, orange blossom water and rose water only until the dough holds together in your fist, and stop there.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the dates with the butter and cinnamon, mash them to a pliable paste, and roll into 12 g balls.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap 25 g of dough around each ball, press it firmly into a maamoul mould, and knock it out onto a lined tray.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 170C (340F) for 18 minutes to 20 minutes, until the bases are pale gold and the tops still blond, and dust with icing sugar only once they are cold.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"The overnight rest is the recipe. Coarse semolina is hard endosperm milled into sharp particles that hydrate very slowly, and twelve hours in warm ghee lets each grain take fat into itself. That is what gives the sandy, short crumb; skip the rest and you bake gravel. Bake pale on purpose, because colour on top means sugar caramelising and moisture gone, and maamoul that browns is maamoul that is dry.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing","Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maamoul%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Maamoul"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"the-floral-pantry-rose-orange-blossom-and-mahleb","term":"The Floral Pantry: Rose, Orange Blossom & Mahleb"}],"pantryItems":["Orange","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Semolina","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand","mashing-and-pureeing"]},{"slug":"tahini-halva","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g light tahini, well stirred"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g shelled pistachios"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the tahini to 40C (104F) in a bowl over hot water and stir it smooth, because cold tahini seizes the instant hot syrup hits it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar, water and lemon juice undisturbed to 121C (250F) on a thermometer, brushing stray crystals off the pan sides with a wet brush.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the syrup into the tahini in a thin stream, stirring with a spatula for no more than 2 minutes to 3 minutes, until the mass thickens, loses its shine and begins to pull into threads behind the blade.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":180,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold in the pistachios and salt, press firmly into a lined 20 cm tin, and leave 24 hours at room temperature before cutting.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Halva is a controlled crystallisation, not a syrup soak. At 121C (250F) the syrup is close to 90 percent sugar, and as it cools in the tahini the sucrose drops out of solution as microscopic crystals in sesame fat. The lemon juice keeps those crystals small; stir past the point where the mass dulls and they grow coarse, the texture turns gritty and the oil weeps out. Expect a short crumbly break. The flaky layered halva sold in shops is aerated with soapwort extract; this home version is not.","equipment":["Thermometer","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tahini%20Halva%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tahini Halva"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Tahini Halva","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Tahini%20Halva","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Tahini","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"muhallabia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ground rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 small mastic tears, about 0.5 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml rose water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g pistachios, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Freeze the mastic tears for 15 minutes, then grind them cold in a mortar with 10 g of the sugar, which stops the resin smearing and gumming into a ball.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the cornflour and ground rice into 200 ml of the cold milk until not one lump is left.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the remaining milk and sugar to a bare simmer, pour in the slurry while whisking hard, and cook, stirring constantly, until it coats the back of a spoon and one or two slow bubbles heave to the surface, about 6 minutes.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take it off the heat, stir in the mastic sugar and the rose water, and pour into bowls at once while it still flows.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chill 3 hours, then scatter with the pistachios.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false}],"note":"Cornflour granules do not finish swelling until about 90C (195F), so the pan has to reach a bubble. Stop at a simmer and the pudding thickens on the stove then slackens in the fridge. Pour while it is still fluid, because once the gel has set, stirring shears it and it will never knit again. Rose water and mastic go in off the heat since both are volatile. Weigh the mastic: a little more than this and the bowl tastes of pine resin.","equipment":["Mortar & pestle"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Muhallabia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Muhallabia"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Middle Eats on Muhallabia","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Middle%20Eats%20Muhallabia","sub":"A YouTube search for Middle Eats and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Rice","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"omani-halwa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"350 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g jaggery, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water, 1 litre for the slurry, 450 ml for the sugar and 50 ml for the saffron"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g ghee, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 g saffron threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cardamom"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g ground nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml rose water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g cashews, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Steep the saffron in 50 ml of warm water, and whisk the cornflour into 1 litre of the cold water until perfectly smooth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve the sugar and jaggery in the last 450 ml of water over gentle heat without letting it boil down, take the pan off the heat, and whisk the cold slurry in while the sugar solution is still thin and warm; never pour a cold liquid into a hot concentrated syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the pan to a low heat and stir without stopping until the mixture turns from milky to translucent and heavy and a dragged spoon holds an open trench, about 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the ghee in six additions, letting each one be fully absorbed and the mass come back together before the next, with the saffron water, cardamom, nutmeg, rose water and salt, until it pulls from the pan in one glossy amber sheet, about 45 minutes more.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the cashews, press into an oiled dish 3 cm deep, and cool 4 hours before cutting into squares.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is starch cooked far past the point where a sauce would be finished. Held at a bare simmer for the better part of an hour, the cornflour granules do not merely swell, they burst and disperse, and the freed amylose links into the chewy translucent gel that defines halwa. Cloudy means undercooked. The amber comes from the jaggery, not from the pan, so do not chase colour with heat. The ghee goes in a little at a time because a starch gel takes up only so much fat at once.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","warm-spiced","rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omani%20Halwa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Omani Halwa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Yemeni%20%26%20the%20Gulf%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Yemeni & the Gulf kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom","Nutmeg","Saffron"]},{"slug":"cajeta-de-celaya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2000 ml goat's milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 g bicarbonate of soda, dissolved in 15 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the milk with the sugar and cinnamon in your widest heavy pan over medium heat, stirring until no grit of sugar drags under the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat and stir in the dissolved bicarbonate of soda. It will foam up the sides at once, and it must fall back before the pan returns to the flame.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer at a lazy bubble, stirring every few minutes and scraping the corners, until the milk has gone from white through cream to the colour of weak tea, about an hour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Once it is the colour of wet sand, stand over it and stir without stopping. It is ready when a spoon dragged across the base leaves a channel that closes slowly, or at 105C to 107C (221F to 225F) on a probe.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the vanilla and pour into warm jars while it still runs; it thickens by half again as it cools. Cool the jars, then keep them in the fridge and use within a month. This is not a shelf stable preserve, whatever the jars suggest.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cajeta and dulce de leche are the same reaction in different milk: lactose browning against milk protein, not sugar caramelising. Goat's milk carries caproic and capric acids, which is the tang cow's milk cannot give. The bicarbonate raises pH, and Maillard browning runs faster in alkaline conditions, so colour arrives before the sugar at the base scorches. Add it off the heat: it throws carbon dioxide into a hot foam that climbs fast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","floral","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cajeta%20de%20Celaya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cajeta de Celaya"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Cajeta de Celaya","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Cajeta%20de%20Celaya","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Milk","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"pudim-de-leite-condensado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar, for the caramel"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tin (395 g) sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"395 ml whole milk, measured in the empty tin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 large eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the sugar and water in a small pan without stirring until it is the colour of an old copper coin and smells faintly bitter, then pour it into a 20 cm ring tin and tilt to coat the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the condensed milk, milk, eggs, yolk and salt for 30 seconds only, pour through a fine sieve into a jug, and let it stand until the foam has thinned to a rim of bubbles.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into the caramelled tin, stand it in a roasting tray, and pour boiling water into the tray to come halfway up the outside of the tin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 160C (325F) for 60 minutes to 70 minutes, until the edge is set and the centre moves in one piece, like a shrug, when you nudge the tray.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool, then chill at least 6 hours before running a knife round the edges and inverting onto a lipped plate.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Egg proteins unfold and link into a mesh at 80C to 85C (176F to 185F). Push past that and the mesh tightens and wrings out the water it was holding: that is the weeping, and the steam it leaves behind is the holes. The water bath is not about humidity; it is a thermal brake, keeping the tin walls near 100C (212F) so the outside cannot race the middle. Blend briefly and strain, because air whipped in now is holes later.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pudim%20de%20Leite%20Condensado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pudim de Leite Condensado"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"chocoflan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"120 g cajeta or thick dulce de leche"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g natural (non alkalised) cocoa powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g bicarbonate of soda"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large egg, for the cake"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml buttermilk"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml neutral oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml hot black coffee"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tin (395 g) sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"340 g evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 large eggs, for the flan"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml vanilla extract"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a 24 cm bundt tin heavily into every crease, warm the cajeta until it just loosens, and spoon it over the base in one even layer rather than in lumps.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the flour, cocoa, sugar, bicarbonate and salt, beat in the egg, buttermilk and oil, then the hot coffee, and pour the thin batter over the cajeta.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the condensed milk, evaporated milk, eggs and vanilla, strain, and pour it slowly over the back of a spoon onto the cake batter. It will sit on top and look wrong.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover tightly with foil, stand the tin in a roasting tray of boiling water reaching halfway up, and bake at 180C (350F) for 70 minutes to 85 minutes, until a skewer pushed into the cake band comes out clean.","durationSec":4200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool 30 minutes, chill 4 hours, then invert onto a plate: the flan is now on top and the cajeta has run down it.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":16200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The layers change places in the oven. The flan mix is around 1.1 g per millilitre and the aerating batter is nearer 0.9, so as the batter loosens with heat the denser custard sinks through it and the cake floats up. It only works if the batter is thin and the custard is laid on rather than stirred in. Natural cocoa and buttermilk are what the small dose of bicarbonate has to feed on; alkalised cocoa brings no acid and leaves it soapy.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","silky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chocoflan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chocoflan"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Chocoflan","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Chocoflan","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Milk","Buttermilk","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla","Coffee/Espresso"]},{"slug":"leche-asada","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1000 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 large eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip lemon peel, pith scraped away"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g ground cinnamon"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the milk with the lemon peel and cinnamon until it steams, then take it off and leave it 10 minutes for the peel to give up its oil.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, sugar and vanilla only until the sugar has dissolved, keeping the whisk under the surface so you raise no foam, then pour in the warm milk in a thin stream, whisking.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain into a 22 cm baking dish and lift off any froth with a spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the dish straight on the oven rack and bake at 180C (350F) for 45 minutes to 55 minutes, until the top is blistered dark brown in patches and the centre still trembles.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve warm or cold from the dish, cutting down through the browned skin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is flan's opposite, and worth making the same week as one. Sitting in dry heat, the dish walls run at oven temperature, so the custard against them climbs past 85C (185F), tightens and gives off steam: that is the honeycomb of bubbles you find when you cut in. On top, milk protein and sugar brown together into a bitter skin. Neither is a fault here; both are the dish. Nothing is turned out, so a loose set costs you nothing.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","warm-spiced","floral","gently hot"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, warm spice humming through the middle, with warmth in the tail."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Leche%20Asada%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Leche Asada"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chilean%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Chilean kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Milk","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"torta-rogel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 large egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter, soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml brandy"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g dulce de leche repostero"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar, for the meringue"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g pasteurised egg white, about 3 whites"}],"steps":[{"text":"Work the flour, yolks, butter, milk, brandy and salt into a smooth firm dough and rest it wrapped for 30 minutes, until it stops pulling back from the pin.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Divide into 8, roll each piece into a 20 cm disc about 1.5 mm thick, the depth of two coins stacked, prick all over, and bake at 190C (375F) for 5 minutes to 7 minutes, until pale gold and dry. They crisp as they cool.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":420,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack the cold discs with about 100 g of dulce de leche in each of the seven gaps, pressing gently so the caramel reaches the edge all round.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar and water to 118C (244F), then pour it in a thin thread down the side of the bowl into whites whipped to soft peak, beating until the bowl is only just warm and the meringue holds a hooked peak.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pile the meringue over the top, pull it up into points with the back of a spoon, and scorch the tips with a blowtorch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Repostero dulce de leche is cooked further and holds less water than the spreading kind, which is why eight layers do not slump into a puddle. Even so the discs are meant to go soft: over about four hours water migrates out of the caramel into the biscuit until it cuts like shortbread instead of shattering. Build it the morning you eat it. Syrup at 118C (244F) only carries the whites to about 60C (140F), well short of pasteurising them, so buy them pasteurised.","equipment":["Torch"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The brûlée torch","Sugar stages & caramel","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The brûlée torch","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=creme%20brulee%20torch%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Torta%20Rogel%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Torta Rogel"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Locos X el Asado on Torta Rogel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Locos%20X%20el%20Asado%20Torta%20Rogel","sub":"A YouTube search for Locos X el Asado and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"picarones","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g squash (zapallo macre or crown prince), peeled and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g sweet potato, peeled and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g aniseed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cinnamon sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g chancaca or panela, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip orange peel"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1500 ml neutral oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Tie the aniseed and one cinnamon stick in a square of muslin and boil them with the squash and sweet potato until collapsing. Lift the bag out, drain while keeping 200 ml of the cooking water, mash to a purée with no lumps left, and leave it to cool to blood heat, about 35C (95F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the salt through the flour. Beat the yeast into the cooled purée, then work in the flour to a thick sticky batter-dough, loosening it with the reserved water until it falls from a spoon in a heavy ribbon, then cover and leave 90 minutes, until domed and full of bubbles.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the chancaca, water, orange peel, remaining cinnamon and cloves until it coats a spoon, then strain and keep warm.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the oil to 180C (350F). With a wet hand take a lump of dough, punch a hole through it with your thumb, spin it open in the air and lower it away from you into the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry 2 minutes to 3 minutes a side until deep gold and hollow sounding on the spoon, drain, and pour the warm syrup over.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This dough is nearly half squash purée, so its water is held by starch rather than by gluten. That is why it pours instead of kneading, and why the ring is opened by hand in the air: rolling it would need flour, and flour turns picarones into bread rings. The purée must be at blood heat before the yeast goes in, since hot mash kills it outright. Oil at 180C (350F) cuts both ways. Cooler and the dough drinks fat before its crust sets; hotter and the crust hardens before the inside can puff.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Deep frying","Straining & passing through a sieve","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, a silken, spoon-slow texture."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Picarones%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Picarones"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Sweet potato","Squash/Pumpkin","Orange","Flour","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","deep-frying"]},{"slug":"trinidadian-black-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g mixed dried fruit: prunes, raisins, currants, glacé cherries"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dark rum, plus 50 ml for feeding"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml cherry brandy"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar, for the browning"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g unsalted butter, soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g dark muscovado sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 large eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g mixed spice"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lime"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml Angostura bitters"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blitz the dried fruit coarse, cover it with the rum and cherry brandy in a jar, and leave it in the dark: two weeks is the minimum, a year is normal.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the caster sugar alone in a heavy pan over medium heat, past amber to a smoking near black that no longer smells sweet, then take it off the heat and pour in the boiling water at arm's length. It will erupt. Swirl to a loose syrup and cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream the butter and muscovado until light, beat in the eggs one at a time, then fold in the flour, baking powder, spice, zest, vanilla, bitters, the drained fruit with 100 ml to 150 ml of its liquor, and enough browning to bring the batter to the colour of wet earth. Only fruit soaked for months takes up all of its liquor, so bottle what is left for feeding.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake in a double lined 23 cm tin at 150C (300F) for 2 hours to 2.5 hours, until a skewer comes out with crumbs on it but no wet batter.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Spoon the remaining rum over the cake while it is hot and let it cool in its tin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Browning is caramel taken past the point of pleasure. Sucrose melts near 186C (367F) and darkens as it goes; hold it beyond roughly 200C (392F) and it polymerises into caramelans and caramelens, which are near black, bitter and barely sweet. That bitterness is the entire job: it keeps a cake this rich from cloying, and colouring alone will not do it. Add the water off the heat, because it flashes to steam the instant it lands.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar","Folding: keeping the air in","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","rich","floral","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20Black%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Trinidadian Black Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Trinidadian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Trinidadian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"bitters-the-bars-spice-rack","term":"Bitters: The Bar's Spice Rack"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lime","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar","folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"jamaican-sweet-potato-pudding","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900 g Jamaican sweet potato (batata), peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"650 ml coconut milk: 400 ml for the batter, 250 ml for the top"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fine cornmeal"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml dark rum"}],"steps":[{"text":"Grate the sweet potato on the fine side of a box grater: you are after a wet pulp, not shreds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in 400 ml of the coconut milk with the sugar, flour, cornmeal, butter, raisins, cinnamon, nutmeg, salt, vanilla and rum until it is a loose batter that pours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a buttered 22 cm dish at least 7 cm deep, one holding 2.2 litres, leaving 1.5 cm of freeboard for the late pour, and bake at 180C (350F) for about 60 minutes, until the surface has gone dull and will hold a spoonful of liquid.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the last 250 ml of coconut milk gently over the top and return it for 25 minutes to 30 minutes, until that layer has thickened to a custard skin and the pudding no longer ripples when you move the dish.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool it completely in the dish before cutting; it firms as it goes cold.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no egg doing the work here. The set comes from the sweet potato's own starch, which swells and gels once the batter passes about 70C (158F) and then keeps stiffening as it cools, so a pudding that looks slack coming out will cut clean when cold. Grate fine: coarse shreds keep their starch locked inside and leave you a watery pudding. The late pour of coconut milk is the custard top of the old coal pot version, cooked from above.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","sweet-edged","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20Sweet%20Potato%20Pudding%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jamaican Sweet Potato Pudding"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jamaican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Jamaican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Potato","Sweet potato","Coconut","Dried fruit","Butter","Coconut milk","Flour","Cornmeal/Polenta","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"chaja","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 large eggs, for the sponge"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g caster sugar, for the sponge"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g egg white, about 3 whites"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g caster sugar, for the meringue"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml double cream, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g dulce de leche"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 tin (400 g) peaches in syrup, drained and diced, the syrup kept"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml peach syrup from the tin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml brandy"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the whole eggs and sugar over a pan of hot water to blood heat, then off the heat to the ribbon stage, fold in the sifted flour in three goes, and bake in a lined 20 cm tin at 180C (350F) for 25 minutes to 30 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites to soft peak, rain in the meringue sugar, beat to a stiff glossy peak, spread it 5 mm thick on a lined tray and dry at 90C (194F) for 3 hours, until a broken edge snaps dry right through with no chew at the centre, then leave it in the switched off oven to cool.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Split the cooled sponge into three and brush each cut face with the peach syrup mixed with the brandy.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the cream to soft peak only, then layer sponge, cream, diced peach and spoonfuls of dulce de leche twice over, finishing with sponge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mask the whole cake in the last of the cream and press broken meringue over every surface until none of it shows. Build it the day you eat it, chill it as soon as it is masked, and keep it in the fridge until 30 minutes before serving.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing raises this sponge but beaten egg. Warming the whole eggs and sugar to blood heat thins them enough to whip to three times their volume, and the flour goes in three goes because every stroke after that knocks the air back out. With no butter and no raising agent the crumb is dry by design, which is what lets it drink syrup rather than turn to paste. Whip the cream to soft peak only: the dulce de leche and the chilling stiffen it further.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chaj%C3%A1%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Chajá"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Uruguayan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Uruguayan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Stone fruit","Cream","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"dulce-de-lechosa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1500 g green unripe papaya, whole weight, about 1100 g once peeled and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g papelón or panela, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cinnamon sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip lime peel"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the papaya, halve it, scrape out the white seeds, and cut the flesh into strips 5 mm thick and about the length of a finger. Rinse the strips under cold water until the water runs clear and no more milky sap beads on the cut faces, since that latex irritates skin and eyes and carries a soapy bitterness into the syrup.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring 1000 ml of the water to the boil with a third of the papelón, the cloves, cinnamon and lime peel, slide in the papaya, and cook at a bare tremble for 45 minutes, until the edges turn from chalky to glassy.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2700,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the rest of the papelón in two goes over the next hour, topping up with the remaining water whenever the pan looks dry, and keep the heat low enough that nothing catches at the base.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook 1 hour to 2 hours more, until the strips are amber right through and the syrup falls from the spoon in thick slow drops.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the fruit in its syrup and keep it there; it is better after a week in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Candying is a race. Sugar has to travel into the fruit faster than water is drawn out of it, or the strips shrivel to leather. That is why the syrup starts thin and is strengthened in stages: green papaya dropped into heavy syrup loses water through its cell walls quicker than sugar can replace it, and collapses. Unripe fruit is the point, since its pectin has not yet been unpicked by ripening and the strips can stand three hours of heat.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dulce%20de%20Lechosa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dulce de Lechosa"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Venezuelan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Venezuelan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lime","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"habichuelas-con-dulce","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g cooked red kidney beans, drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"340 g evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g sweet potato (batata), in 2 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cinnamon sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 casabe or milk biscuits, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Blend the beans with the whole milk until completely smooth, then push the purée through a sieve and discard the skins caught in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the strained purée into a heavy pan with the coconut milk, evaporated milk, sugar, cinnamon, cloves, salt and butter, and bring it to a bare simmer, dragging a spoon across the base every minute or two.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 30 minutes, until it coats the back of the spoon and tastes of sweet milk rather than of bean.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the sweet potato and raisins and cook 20 minutes to 25 minutes more, until a cube gives to a knife but still has square corners, then stir in the vanilla off the heat.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1500,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Serve warm or cold in bowls with the biscuits laid on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A bean purée thickens the way any starch does, but its granules are still shut inside fragments of cell wall and skin, which is why the sieve is not optional: skin left in reads as grit however long you cook it. Sugar and milk fat both slow starch swelling, so the pot genuinely needs its 30 minutes to lose the raw bean taste. Put the sweet potato in late; started with the milk it dissolves and takes the body of the dish with it.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","silky","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Habichuelas%20con%20Dulce%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Habichuelas con Dulce"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dominican%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Dominican kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Potato","Sweet potato","Coconut","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Coconut milk","Kidney/Pinto beans","Sugar","Vanilla","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"postre-de-natas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2000 ml whole milk, unhomogenised if you can get it"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cinnamon sticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip lemon peel"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g raisins"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml brandy"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the raisins in the brandy while you work.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the milk, sugar, cinnamon, lemon peel and salt to the barest simmer in your widest uncovered pan, then stop stirring and leave the surface completely alone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Each time a wrinkled skin sets, lift it off whole with a fork into a bowl. Keep at it for about 90 minutes, until the milk beneath has reduced by half and gone the colour of straw.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the skins back in with the raisins and their brandy, breaking them up roughly with the spoon, and cook 10 minutes more, until a spoon drawn through it leaves a track that fills back slowly and the pan holds about 900 ml. It is still pourable hot.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a dish, cool, and chill 4 hours, over which it sets to a spoonable pudding.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"A milk skin is not scum. Water leaving the surface concentrates the whey proteins there, and above about 70C (158F) they unfold and knit into a film that traps fat and casein underneath. Stir and you never get one; leave the pan alone and you get one every few minutes, each taking protein and fat with it. Collecting them and folding them back is what gives this pudding its ropy, chewy body instead of a smooth custard, and why a wide pan beats a tall one.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","bright","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, acid holding everything up to the light, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Postre%20de%20Natas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Postre de Natas"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Dried fruit","Milk","Sugar","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"mizu-yokan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g agar powder (kanten)"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g koshian, ready-sweetened smooth red bean paste"}],"steps":[{"text":"Whisk the agar into the cold water in a saucepan and leave it to swell for 10 minutes, then bring it to a full rolling boil and hold it there for 2 minutes, stirring: agar will not dissolve below boiling, and any grain left undissolved shows later as a weeping, gritty gel.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the sugar and salt, then whisk in the bean paste a spoonful at a time so it disperses instead of forming lumps that never break up. The paste is already sweetened, which is why only 45 g of sugar goes in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer 3 minutes to 4 minutes, until a drop on a cold saucer skins over and holds a thumbprint within a minute.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sit the pan in a bowl of iced water and stir slowly until the mixture thickens to the body of double cream; this is what keeps the bean solids in suspension rather than letting them sink and leave a clear layer on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into a wetted 15 cm tray, pop any surface bubbles, and chill 2 hours until firm. Cut into blocks with a wet knife.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true}],"note":"Agar dissolves only at a rolling boil and sets near 40C (104F), and once set it holds its shape up to about 85C (185F): a yokan slice stands up in a warm room where a gelatine jelly would slump, and it breaks cleanly instead of melting on the tongue. At 4 g to 400 ml you get a slice that keeps an edge; drop to 2 g and it is a spoon sweet. Long boiling with acid hydrolyses agar and it will then never set, so keep fruit juice out of the pan.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mizu%20Yokan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mizu Yokan"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Mizu Yokan","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Mizu%20Yokan","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Kidney/Pinto beans","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"sakuramochi-domyoji-style","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g domyojiko, coarse cracked glutinous rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"a pinch of red food colouring, not cochineal, for a pale pink"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g koshian, smooth sweet red bean paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 salt-pickled cherry leaves (sakura no ha no shiozuke, from a Japanese grocer). Never fresh cherry leaves, which are not the same thing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the cherry leaves in cold water for 20 minutes to draw off the brine, then pat them dry; they should taste of salt and almond rather than of the pickling barrel.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the water, sugar and salt to the boil, stir in just enough colouring to tint the syrup a pale rose, pour it over the domyojiko in a heatproof bowl, stir once, cover, and stand 20 minutes until the grain has drunk the syrup and looks swollen and matt.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam the covered bowl over a full boil for 20 minutes, then rest it covered for 10 minutes and stir with a wet spatula, crushing about a quarter of the grains so they glue the rest together.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll the bean paste into 8 balls of 25 g. With wet hands, flatten 45 g of the warm rice in your palm, seat a ball in the middle, and close it into a barrel with no paste showing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap each one in a cherry leaf, vein side against the rice.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Domyojiko is glutinous rice that has been steamed, dried and cracked, so every grain is already gelatinised starch waiting for water. Glutinous rice is almost entirely amylopectin, and those branched chains cannot line up and recrystallise as they cool, which is why this stays soft and sticky on the plate while an ordinary rice cake turns chalky overnight. Steep the grain in hot syrup before it goes near the steamer: cold water only wets the outside and leaves a gritty core.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Shaping by hand","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","floral","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a floral thread lifting the aroma, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sakuramochi%2C%20Domyoji%20Style%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sakuramochi, Domyoji Style"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Sakuramochi, Domyoji Style","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Sakuramochi%2C%20Domyoji%20Style","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Stone fruit","Rice","Kidney/Pinto beans","Sugar","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["resting-dough-the-pause-that-does-the-work","shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"japanese-cotton-cheesecake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g full fat cream cheese"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 eggs, separated (about 300 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"140 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the cream cheese, butter and milk together in a bowl set over barely simmering water, whisking until it is smooth and pourable, then take it off the heat and whisk in the yolks and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sift the flour and cornflour over the top and whisk until not one lump remains; the batter should fall from the whisk in a thick ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites with the lemon juice, adding the sugar in three lots, only until a lifted peak bends over on itself; whites taken stiff tear the batter as you fold and split the top in the oven.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold a third of the whites in to slacken the batter, then fold in the rest in two lots, stopping the moment the streaks disappear. Pour into a lined 18 cm round tin at least 7 cm deep, or an 18 cm tin lined with a baking paper collar standing 4 cm proud of the rim, wrapped outside in foil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stand the tin in a roasting tray of hot water and bake at 160C (325F) for 20 minutes, then at 130C (265F) for a further 50 minutes, until the centre wobbles as one mass. Turn the oven off and leave the door ajar for 15 minutes before lifting it out.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":false}],"note":"This is a souffle held together by starch. The cornflour granules swell and hold water, so the egg proteins set into a wet, flexible net rather than a dry crumb, and the cake can stretch as it rises. The water bath keeps the tin near 100C (212F) so the outside cannot set into a crust before the middle has finished lifting, which is what tears the surface. The slow cool matters just as much: pulled straight into cold air, the trapped steam contracts and the cake folds in half.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Japanese%20Cotton%20Cheesecake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Japanese Cotton Cheesecake"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Just One Cookbook on Japanese Cotton Cheesecake","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Just%20One%20Cookbook%20Japanese%20Cotton%20Cheesecake","sub":"A YouTube search for Just One Cookbook and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"roasting-baking-and-carryover","term":"Roasting, Baking & Carryover"}],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Cream cheese","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"dan-tat-hong-kong-egg-tart","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"85 g unsalted butter, soft"},{"kind":"item","text":"37 g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g beaten egg, for the pastry"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"110 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"225 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs (150 g), for the custard"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml evaporated milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 ml vanilla extract"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the butter and icing sugar until pale but not aerated, beat in the egg, then fold in the flour and salt just until the dough gathers. Wrap and chill 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press 25 g of dough into each of 12 fluted tart tins of 7 cm, working from the base outwards with your thumb so the wall is an even 3 mm and stands a hair proud of the rim. Chill the shells while you make the filling.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dissolve the caster sugar in the hot water and let it cool to lukewarm, then whisk in the eggs, evaporated milk and vanilla without raising foam. Strain twice through a fine sieve and skim the surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill each shell to 3 mm below the rim and bake at 200C (400F) for 15 minutes, then drop to 180C (350F) for 10 minutes to 12 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1620,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the tray when a nudge sends one slow wave across the centre and the surface is still flat and glossy; carryover heat finishes the set as they cool.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Egg custard sets at 80C to 85C (176F to 185F). Past that the protein network contracts and wrings out the water it was holding, which is the pitted, weeping curd of an overbaked tart, and the puffed dome that collapses into a crater. Sugar raises the setting temperature and buys margin, so the oven starts hot for the pastry and drops for the filling. Strain twice and skim: every bubble left in the mix bakes into a pockmark.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","rich","floral","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dan%20Tat%20(Hong%20Kong%20Egg%20Tart)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dan Tat (Hong Kong Egg Tart)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Dan Tat (Hong Kong Egg Tart)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Dan%20Tat%20(Hong%20Kong%20Egg%20Tart)","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder"]},{"slug":"ma-tai-gou-water-chestnut-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g water chestnut flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g Chinese brown slab sugar, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g fresh water chestnuts, peeled and roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml groundnut oil, for the tin"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml groundnut oil, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Start this the day before: the cake has to cool for at least 3 hours before it can be cut. Whisk the water chestnut flour into 500 ml of the water until no lump remains, then pass it through a sieve and stir in the chopped water chestnuts.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the sugar and salt in the remaining 500 ml water and bring it to a boil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, pour a third of the raw slurry into the hot syrup while whisking hard: it thickens on contact. Set the pan on low heat and stir until it looks like single cream, then whisk that cooked paste back into the raw slurry still in the bowl.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour into an oiled 20 cm tin, where the batter sits about 4 cm deep, and steam over a full boil for 45 minutes to 50 minutes, until the surface has turned from milky to translucent and springs back under a finger. A layer no more than 3 cm deep, as in a 23 cm tin, is set in 30 minutes. Cool completely, at least 3 hours, before cutting into 1 cm slabs.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":13800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the slices in a little oil over medium heat until blistered and crisp on both sides and the inside has gone soft again.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Water chestnut starch gelatinises into a clear, short, brittle gel, the opposite of the long chew glutinous rice gives. Cooking a third of the slurry first and whisking it back is the whole trick: that thickened base is viscous enough to hold the raw starch and the chopped chestnut in suspension, so the cake steams as one body instead of settling into a starch floor under a layer of water. Cooling sets it hard by retrogradation, and that is what lets a slice hold together in the pan.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, char used as punctuation."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ma%20Tai%20Gou%20(Water%20Chestnut%20Cake)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ma Tai Gou (Water Chestnut Cake)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Ma Tai Gou (Water Chestnut Cake)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Ma%20Tai%20Gou%20(Water%20Chestnut%20Cake)","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"guihua-nuomi-ou-osmanthus-lotus-root-with-glutinous-rice","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 lotus root segments, about 700 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g glutinous rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g Chinese brown slab sugar, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 dried jujubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g dried osmanthus blossom"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Start this in the morning: the rice soaks for 4 hours, the roots simmer for 2, and they must cool before slicing. Soak the rice in cold water for 4 hours and drain it well. Scrub the lotus root and cut a lid off each fat end, 2 cm down, keeping the lids.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the rice into the open channels with a chopstick, tapping the root upright on the board to settle it, and stop at about nine tenths full: the grain swells and will split the root if it is rammed to the top. Replace each lid and pin it with cocktail sticks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the roots in a deep pot with both sugars, the jujubes and the water, bring to a boil, skim, then cover and hold it at a bare simmer for 2 hours, until a skewer slides through the flesh with no resistance.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the roots out, pull out the cocktail sticks, and lift out the jujubes to serve alongside. Boil the liquid down to a syrup that coats the back of a spoon, then return the roots and turn them in it to glaze.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool and slice 5 mm thick with a heavy knife. Stir the osmanthus into the warm syrup off the heat, since its scent boils away, and spoon syrup and blossom together over the slices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two starches, two behaviours, one pot. The rice is nearly all amylopectin, so it swells inside a sealed channel and sets sticky enough to hold a 5 mm slice once cold, where ordinary rice would go chalky and crumble away from the cut. The lotus root stays firm because sugar diffuses into the tissue and slows its pectin from dissolving, and because the pot is held at a bare simmer rather than a boil: two hours of that tenderises it without breaking it up.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["clean","simple"],"sentence":"A quiet dish that lets its main ingredient speak: season well and stand aside."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Guihua%20Nuomi%20Ou%20(Osmanthus%20Lotus%20Root%20with%20Glutinous%20Rice)%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Guihua Nuomi Ou (Osmanthus Lotus Root with Glutinous Rice)"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chinese Cooking Demystified on Guihua Nuomi Ou (Osmanthus Lotus Root with Glutinous Rice)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chinese%20Cooking%20Demystified%20Guihua%20Nuomi%20Ou%20(Osmanthus%20Lotus%20Root%20with%20Glutinous%20Rice)","sub":"A YouTube search for Chinese Cooking Demystified and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Rice","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"songpyeon","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g fine dry-milled non-glutinous rice flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml to 220 ml boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g toasted sesame seeds, coarsely ground with 1 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g red pine needles (Pinus densiflora) from an unsprayed tree, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml toasted sesame oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Sift the rice flour with the salt, then pour on the boiling water in three lots, stirring with a wooden spoon between each and holding back the last 20 ml, to be added only if the dough cracks. Knead for 5 minutes once it is cool enough to touch.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work the dough until it takes a fingerprint without cracking at the edge, then keep it under a damp cloth at all times; an exposed surface skins over in about two minutes and those dry flakes never rejoin.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the ground sesame with the brown sugar. Roll 20 g of dough into a ball, press a well with your thumb, load 6 g of filling, and seal the seam completely before pinching it into a half moon, squeezing out any trapped air that would burst the cake in the steamer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a steamer with the pine needles, set the cakes on them without touching, and steam over a full boil for 20 minutes, until they turn from chalk white to translucent. The needles perfume the cakes and are not eaten; lift them off before serving. Never substitute yew or an ornamental conifer, which are toxic.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop them into cold water for 10 seconds, drain, and toss with the sesame oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rice flour has no gluten, so nothing binds this dough but its own starch. Boiling water gelatinises a fraction of the flour and that paste is the glue that lets you pleat and seal; mixed with cold water the same flour simply crumbles. Amylose then recrystallises as the cakes cool, fastest just above 0C (32F), which is why songpyeon hardens in a refrigerator and is eaten the day it is steamed. The oil coat slows the surface drying, nothing more.","equipment":["Steamer"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Songpyeon%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Songpyeon"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Maangchi on Songpyeon","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Maangchi%20Songpyeon","sub":"A YouTube search for Maangchi and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Rice","Flour","Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"jalebi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g plain yoghurt"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml warm water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.2 g saffron threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml rose water"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g ghee, for frying"}],"steps":[{"text":"Start this the day before. Whisk the flour, yoghurt and warm water to a batter the thickness of double cream, beating hard for 3 minutes to work air in, then cover and leave at 25C to 30C (77F to 86F) for 12 hours to 24 hours, until it smells sour and the surface is domed with bubbles.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar, water and cardamom to one thread, 106C to 108C (223F to 226F), then stir in the saffron, lemon juice and rose water and hold the syrup at about 60C (140F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Knock the risen batter back with 30 seconds of whisking and no more, loosening it with a spoon of water if it no longer falls in an unbroken ribbon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heat the ghee to 180C (350F) and pipe coils from a squeeze bottle, three rings worked from the outside in. Fry about 45 seconds a side, until the hiss quietens and the coil is rigid and pale gold; browning means it is already dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shake off the fat, drop them straight into the warm syrup for 30 seconds, then lift them onto a rack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The lace comes from fermentation: twelve hours of yeast and bacteria fill the batter with gas, so the coil fries hollow and faintly sour instead of solid and sweet. The soak is a pressure trick. A jalebi out of the fat is full of steam, and dropped into syrup at about 60C (140F) that steam condenses and pulls syrup in through the shell. Syrup any hotter keeps softening it into a sponge; cold syrup sits on the outside and crystallises. Thirty seconds, then out.","equipment":["Piping bag"],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jalebi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jalebi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Jalebi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Jalebi","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Yogurt","Flour","Sugar","Cardamom","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"rasmalai","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres whole milk, for the chhena"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml lemon juice, mixed with 60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres whole milk, for the reduced milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar, for the poaching syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g caster sugar, for the milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.3 g saffron threads"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g pistachios, slivered"}],"steps":[{"text":"Start this the day before: the discs need at least 4 hours in the milk. Bring the 2 litres of milk to a boil, pull it off the heat, wait a minute, then add the diluted lemon juice a spoonful at a time, stirring slowly, and stop the instant the whey runs clear yellow-green and the curd draws away in soft clouds.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain through muslin, run cold water over the curd for 30 seconds to wash the acid out, then hang it for 30 minutes: chhena left too wet dissolves in the pot, too dry and the discs crack open.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Knead the chhena on the worktop with the heel of your hand for 6 minutes to 8 minutes, until the grain has gone and a smear on your palm feels smooth and slightly greasy. Divide into 12, roll, and press into 1 cm discs with no cracks at the edge.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the 200 g sugar with the 1 litre of water in a wide pan of at least 26 cm, slide the discs in, cover, and keep it at a hard rolling boil for 12 minutes; they must swim freely and roughly double.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":960,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Meanwhile reduce the 1.2 litres of milk with the cardamom by a third, sweeten with the 70 g sugar and add the saffron. Squeeze each disc gently to shed its syrup, settle them into the warm milk, and chill at least 4 hours. Scatter with pistachios.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Acid drops casein out of milk at its isoelectric point near pH 4.6, so boil first to carry the whey proteins into the curd, then add only enough juice to clear the whey; more gives a squeaky, rubbery chhena. Kneading is the real work, rupturing the curd granules and smearing the fat through them, and an under-kneaded disc disintegrates in the pot. Boil hard in a thin syrup so steam inflates them from within; a heavy syrup pulls water out and you get dense sinkers.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["silky","bright","warm-spiced","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a silken, spoon-slow texture, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rasmalai%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rasmalai"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Rasmalai","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Rasmalai","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Milk","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"watalappan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g kithul jaggery or dark palm sugar, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thick coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 eggs (250 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"0.5 g nutmeg, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cloves, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cashew nuts, toasted"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the jaggery with 60 ml of the coconut milk over low heat until it is a smooth dark syrup, then cool it to blood heat; poured in hot it will scramble the eggs on contact.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Break the eggs up with a whisk just until the whites and yolks are one colour, working slowly so you do not beat in air that will set as foam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the remaining coconut milk, the jaggery syrup, the spices and the salt, then strain the lot into a bowl or into ramekins and skim off any froth. Cover tightly with foil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam over gently simmering water, the lid set ajar: ramekins take 25 minutes to 30 minutes, a single 1 litre bowl 50 minutes to 60 minutes. The probe is the only endpoint that works for both vessels, so use it: the centre should read 80C to 82C (176F to 180F), with the edge set and the middle moving as one piece.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool, chill 3 hours, and scatter with the toasted cashews.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true}],"note":"A dairy custard gets help from casein, which buffers the set. Here the egg does all of it, in an emulsion of coconut fat and water, which is why the ratio runs near one egg to every 80 ml of liquid. Keep the steam lazy and the lid ajar. Above about 85C (185F) the egg network tightens and squeezes out the water and coconut fat it was holding, and you get a firm rubbery disc sitting in a puddle. Pull it while the middle still wobbles; it firms as it chills.","equipment":["Ramekins","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","rich","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Watalappan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Watalappan"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sri%20Lankan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sri Lankan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"kuih-talam-pandan","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"20 pandan leaves, snipped"},{"kind":"item","text":"550 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g rice flour, for the green layer"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g tapioca starch"},{"kind":"item","text":"160 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g rice flour, for the white layer"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g mung bean flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml groundnut oil, for the tin"}],"steps":[{"text":"Start this the day before: the kuih needs at least 4 hours to cool before it can be cut. Blend the pandan leaves with only 200 ml of the water and strain twice through muslin, squeezing hard, then make the juice back up to 550 ml with the rest of the water. Blending in the small volume is what gives a deep green rather than a wash.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the pandan juice into the 120 g rice flour, the tapioca starch and the sugar, strain, and pour into an oiled 20 cm tin. Steam over a full boil for 25 minutes with a cloth under the lid, until the surface is glossy and firm under a fingertip.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the 50 g rice flour, mung bean flour and salt into the coconut milk, strain, then warm it over low heat, stirring without stopping, until it just thickens to the body of single cream. Take it off before it boils.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scratch the set green layer lightly all over with a fork, pour the white layer on, and steam 15 minutes more.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool completely, at least 4 hours, then cut with an oiled knife, wiping the blade between cuts.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"The two starches are the recipe. Rice flour sets short and slices clean, tapioca gives stretch and shine, and much past a quarter tapioca the kuih turns gummy and drags on the knife. Warm the coconut layer with its flour before it goes on the base: poured raw onto a hot set layer, coconut cream splits and weeps oil, while starch granules already swollen around the fat hold the emulsion together. The cloth under the lid catches condensation that would pit the top.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Straining & passing through a sieve","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kuih%20Talam%20Pandan%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kuih Talam Pandan"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Malaysian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Malaysian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"che-chuoi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g ripe bananas, peeled and cut into 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g small tapioca pearls"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 pandan leaf, knotted"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g white sesame seeds, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g roasted peanuts, crushed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the banana with 30 g of the sugar and leave it 20 minutes; it weeps liquid and firms up, so the pieces hold their shape instead of melting into the pot.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the tapioca pearls in plenty of water for 8 minutes to 10 minutes, until only a white dot is left in each centre, then cover the pan off the heat for 5 minutes until the dot clears and rinse them in cold water.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Bring the coconut milk, water, pandan leaf, salt and remaining sugar to a bare simmer, slide in the banana, and poach 5 minutes to 7 minutes until the edges go translucent and a skewer meets no resistance.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":420,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the drained pearls and the coconut cream and warm it through without letting it bubble, then take the pan off the heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Serve warm or cold under the sesame and peanuts.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coconut cream is oil suspended in water behind a thin skin of coconut protein. Boil it and that protein denatures, the droplets merge, and the fat breaks out as a slick, so the cream goes in last and never simmers. A tapioca pearl is pure starch with no protein or fibre to hold it together, so the gelatinised outside sloughs into slime long before the chalky core is done. Stop at the white dot and let the covered pan carry the heat inward.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","floral","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Che%20Chuoi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Che Chuoi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Che Chuoi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20Che%20Chuoi","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Banana","Coconut","Milk","Cream","Coconut milk","Peanut","Sesame","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"bibingka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g rice flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 eggs (150 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cooked salted duck egg (itlog na maalat), peeled and sliced into rounds"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g kesong puti or another mild fresh white cheese set with vegetarian rennet, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 banana leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g unsalted butter, for brushing"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g fresh coconut, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g caster sugar, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pass the banana leaves over a gas flame until they turn glossy and deep green and go limp, then wipe them and line two 15 cm tins, letting the leaf stand above the rim.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the rice flour, baking powder, sugar and salt together. Beat the eggs with the coconut milk and melted butter, pour that into the dry mix, whisk smooth, and rest it 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the lined tins two thirds full and bake at 200C (400F) for 15 minutes, until risen and just set on top.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay on the salted egg and cheese and return them for 10 minutes to 15 minutes, until the top is patched brown and a skewer comes out clean.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brush with butter and serve hot, with the grated coconut and sugar alongside.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Heat is what makes a banana leaf usable: the flame collapses its rigid cells so it folds into a tin without splitting, and it releases most of the grassy perfume that ends up in the cake. The crumb is rice flour, held by starch and egg alone, which makes it tender and faintly sandy and also means it stales within the day as the starch recrystallises. Hold the egg and cheese back until the batter has set at 15 minutes, or they sink to the bottom.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bibingka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bibingka"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Panlasang Pinoy on Bibingka","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Panlasang%20Pinoy%20Bibingka","sub":"A YouTube search for Panlasang Pinoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Duck","Eggs","Banana","Coconut","Butter","Milk","Cheese (any)","Coconut milk","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"faloodeh-shirazi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"60 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml rose water"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 litres iced water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml sour cherry syrup, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the sugar in the 250 ml water, boil it 2 minutes, then cool it completely and stir in the rose water; added to a hot pan its scent goes up the extractor.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the cornflour into the 400 ml cold water, then cook it over medium heat, stirring without stopping, until it turns from milk white to a translucent glossy paste that holds the trail of the spoon, about 4 minutes. Keep it moving a minute longer to cook out the raw taste.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the hot paste through a potato ricer, or a piping bag fitted with a 3 mm nozzle, into the bowl of iced water; the threads set as they fall. Leave them 5 minutes, then drain.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the threads through the cold syrup in a shallow metal tray and freeze, dragging a fork through the whole tray every 30 minutes for about 3 hours, until it is a loose slush of separate icy threads.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Spoon into chilled glasses and pour the lime juice and sour cherry syrup over at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing in this traps air, so it can never be ice cream: it is a granita, and the fork is the entire technique, shearing the crystals every half hour so they stay small and separate. Sugar sets the freezing point, and the window is narrow. Less and you get one solid block, more and the tray stays syrup. The starch threads stay tender at freezer temperature because gelatinised starch grips water it cannot surrender to the ice around it.","equipment":["Piping bag","Sheet pan"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","floral","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Faloodeh%20Shirazi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Faloodeh Shirazi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Persian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Persian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lime","Stone fruit","Sugar"]},{"slug":"rillettes-de-canard","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 duck legs (about 1.4 kg)"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g duck fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"22 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g white pepper, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g ground mace"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves garlic, peeled and crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the legs with the salt, white pepper and mace, tuck the thyme, bay and garlic around them, and leave them covered in the fridge overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brush the herbs off and set the legs in a heavy pot with the duck fat, wine and water. Bring to the point where one bubble breaks every few seconds, cover, and cook at 130C (265F) for about 3 hours, until a leg bone pulls clean with no resistance at all.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the meat out warm, discard the bones, and pull the flesh apart with two forks into long threads running with the grain. Chop the skin small and add it back.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the strained cooking liquid into the meat a spoonful at a time until the threads are coated and glossy but still separate. Chill a teaspoonful and taste that for salt before you commit.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack into jars, pressing out every air pocket, and seal each with a 5 mm cap of the clear fat lifted off the top of the cooking liquid. Give them a week in the fridge before you open one. Keep them below 4C (39F), eat within a month, and once a jar is opened finish it within a week. Never store them out of the fridge, whatever the cap looks like.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cap is not what preserves rillettes. Salt, the long cook and the fridge do that; the fat only keeps air off, so it cannot go rancid and mould cannot land. Pour it from the clear top of the pot, because jelly trapped underneath is where spoilage starts. Shred with two forks along the grain, since a processor bursts the fibres and gives you paste. Season by tasting a chilled spoonful: fat and cold both blunt salt, and a rillette judged warm tastes flat on toast.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","herb-fresh","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rillettes%20de%20Canard%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rillettes de Canard"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Rillettes de Canard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Rillettes%20de%20Canard","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Duck","Garlic","Thyme","Bay leaf","Wine","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"pommes-de-terre-sarladaises","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900 g waxy potatoes, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g duck fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves garlic, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g flat leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g black pepper, ground"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slice the potatoes 4 mm thick and do not rinse them. Pat the slices dry in a cloth: the starch clinging to the cut faces is the glue that binds them into a cake.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the duck fat in a wide heavy pan over medium heat until it shimmers and a dropped slice sizzles the moment it lands. Add the potatoes and press them flat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Leave them completely alone for 8 minutes, then turn them over in sections with a fish slice. Repeat until the edges are amber and a knife slides into a slice with no crunch at the centre, 20 minutes to 25 minutes in all.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":1980,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat, scatter over the garlic, parsley, salt and pepper, and toss twice. The heat left in the pan takes the raw edge off the garlic without letting it colour.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Duck fat smokes at around 190C (375F), well above butter, which has milk solids that blacken long before a potato browns. That headroom is the whole point. Two things break this dish. Cold fat: the slices drink it instead of sealing, and you get grease rather than crust. And early garlic: over 25 minutes it burns to a bitter brown grit, so it goes in off the heat, where residual warmth is enough to take its raw edge away.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","herb-fresh","smoky","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pommes%20de%20Terre%20Sarladaises%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pommes de Terre Sarladaises"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Pommes de Terre Sarladaises","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Pommes%20de%20Terre%20Sarladaises","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Duck","Garlic","Potato","Parsley","Clove"]},{"slug":"libamaj-zsirjaban-sutve","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole goose liver (about 700 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g goose fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head garlic, cloves peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g sweet Hungarian paprika, to finish"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the liver in the milk in the fridge for 4 hours, turning it once. The milk draws out held blood and slackens the lobes so they can be lifted without tearing.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drain, pat dry, salt all over, and leave the liver uncovered in the fridge for 1 hour, until the surface looks dry and feels tacky rather than wet.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Melt the goose fat in a deep ovenproof dish the liver only just fits, add the garlic, bay and pepper, and lower the liver in so the fat covers it completely.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 120C (250F), letting the fat do no more than blip lazily once or twice a minute, until the centre reads 65C (150F) and holds there for at least 3 minutes with the probe left in, about 1 hour for a 700 g liver.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3780,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stand the dish 30 minutes on the bench, then refrigerate the liver in its fat for a full day. Slice it cold, dust with paprika, and let the slices stand 15 minutes before eating. Keep it under its fat below 4C (39F) and eat it within a week; once cut, re-melt enough fat to cover the face. It is not a room temperature preserve.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true}],"note":"A goose liver is close to half fat by weight, and that fat starts to run at about 35C (95F). Lose it and you are left with a grey, grainy lobe sitting in a pool of grease. Cooking it submerged in already melted fat conducts heat slowly and evenly, with no hot spot and no evaporation, so the proteins set before much fat has escaped. The moment the fat bubbles properly you are boiling the surface and the outer centimetre renders away. Watch the fat, not the clock.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, warm spice humming through the middle."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Libam%C3%A1j%20Zs%C3%ADrj%C3%A1ban%20S%C3%BCtve%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Libamáj Zsírjában Sütve"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hungarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Hungarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Garlic","Bay leaf","Milk","Paprika","Clove"]},{"slug":"martinsgans","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole goose, about 4.5 kg, with giblets"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g black pepper, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g dried marjoram"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried mugwort"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 tart apples (about 450 g), quartered and cored"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions (about 250 g), quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g pitted prunes"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dark beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g cornflour, slaked in 30 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml cold water with a pinch of salt, for the skin"}],"steps":[{"text":"Two days ahead, pull the loose fat pads from the cavity and neck and set them aside in the fridge, or freeze them, to render into schmaltz. Rub the bird inside and out with the salt, pepper and marjoram and leave it uncovered on a rack in the fridge until the skin feels dry and papery.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Prick the skin all over the breast, flanks and thighs at a shallow angle with a fine skewer, travelling through the fat layer and stopping before the meat. Fill the cavity with the apples, onions, prunes and mugwort and close it with skewers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the goose breast down on a rack over a deep tin holding the water and the giblets and roast at 150C (300F). Every 40 minutes ladle the rendered fat out into a bowl and top the tin up with hot water. Turn the bird breast up after 90 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast on until the leg swings loose in its socket and the thigh joint reads 82C (180F), about 3 hours 30 minutes in all. Raise the oven to 220C (425F), brush the breast with the salted water and give it 12 minutes more, until the skin blisters and cracks under a fingernail.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":12600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the goose 25 minutes. Strain the tin juices into a jug and discard the giblets, then lift off the last of the fat. Add the beer and boil hard until the jug is down to about 400 ml, then thicken with the slaked cornflour and sharpen with the vinegar.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"A goose carries its fat in a slab between skin and meat, and it can only leave through holes you make. Prick at a shallow angle so the point travels through fat and stops: score it and you cut muscle, the juices bleed out and the breast dries. Draw the fat off every 40 minutes, because drippings under it scorch and taint the gravy, and because the fat is worth more than the bird. Breast down for the first half keeps lean meat in wet heat while the legs climb to 82C (180F).","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Martinsgans%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Martinsgans"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Martinsgans","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Martinsgans","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Apple","Dried fruit","Oregano","Vinegar","Beer/Cider"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","rendering-fat-and-cooking-in-what-runs-out"]},{"slug":"goose-schmaltz-and-gribenes","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g goose skin and fat trimmings"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion (about 150 g), sliced 3 mm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine sea salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Chill the skin and fat until firm, then cut it into 1 cm pieces. Warm fat drags under the blade and you tear the skin instead of cutting it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the pieces in a heavy pan with the water over the lowest heat the hob will hold. Leave it, stirring now and then, while the water boils away and the pieces slowly come to sit in their own melted fat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Carry on until the bubbling changes from big and lazy to fine and quiet and the pieces have shrunk to pale gold curls, 45 minutes to 60 minutes in all.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the onion and cook until onion and skin are both deep brown and crisp, 10 minutes to 15 minutes. The onion runs ahead, so fish it out early if it darkens first.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain through a fine sieve into a clean jar. Salt the gribenes while they are hot and eat them the same day; the fat keeps for months in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rendering is drying. The fat is locked in cells that have to open, and the water in the skin has to leave before anything can brown. The splash of water at the start pins the pan at 100C (212F) while that happens, so the skin never sets into a shell with fat trapped inside. The bubbling is your moisture gauge: coarse loud bubbles are water, fine quiet ones are fat. Stop while the strained fat is clear straw. Amber means you browned the fat itself, and it will taste of it in everything you fry.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":10,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Goose%20Schmaltz%20and%20Gribenes%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Goose Schmaltz and Gribenes"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Eastern%20European%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Eastern European kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion"],"judgedBy":["straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"polgesek","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 goose breasts, skin on (about 900 g together)"},{"kind":"item","text":"23 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2.3 g curing salt number 2 (nitrite and nitrate cure, sold as Prague powder number 2 or peklosol). Never Himalayan pink salt or any plain salt sold as pink"},{"kind":"item","text":"9 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g black peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g alder or fruitwood chips"}],"steps":[{"text":"Weigh the breasts and scale the cure to them: 2.5 percent sea salt, 0.25 percent curing salt number 2, 1 percent sugar. Weigh the curing salt on a scale that reads 0.01 g, because a kitchen scale reading in whole grams cannot weigh this and must not be used. Rub it all in, then add the juniper, pepper, bay and garlic.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Box or bag them, refrigerate at 3C (37F), and turn them once a day for 7 days so the brine that forms bathes both faces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse briefly, pat dry, and hang them on hooks in the fridge for 24 hours, until the surface is matte and dry to the back of a finger. Smoke will not stick to wet skin, it will only steam it.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cold smoke over alder at under 25C (77F) in three sessions of about 3 hours, resting the breasts uncovered in the fridge overnight between sessions, until they are the colour of strong tea.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Hang at 12C to 14C (54F to 57F) at 70 percent to 80 percent relative humidity until each breast has lost 30 percent of the weight it went into the smoker at, 2 weeks to 3 weeks. If the surface hardens and the loss stalls, vacuum bag the breast, leave it a week in the fridge to equalise, then hang it again. Slice paper thin across the grain. Once it has lost 30 percent, wrap it and keep it below 4C (39F): it holds three months whole, and two weeks once cut. A cellar only counts if it truly holds 12C to 14C (54F to 57F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold smoke holds meat for hours in exactly the range bacteria like, so the nitrite in curing salt number 2 is doing the protecting, not the smoke, which only reaches the surface. Do not leave it out and do not round the salt down. Doneness here is a weight, not a date: 30 percent loss drops the water low enough for the breast to be stable at cellar temperature, while one that has shed 20 percent is still raw meat inside a dry shell. Seven days curing, three smoking, three weeks hanging.","equipment":["Smoker"],"techniques":["Low & slow smoking","Brining & curing","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","aromatic","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Low & slow smoking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VmTzdMHu5KU","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=P%C3%B3%C5%82g%C4%99sek%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Półgęsek"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pink-salt-nitrite-and-nitrate-the-science-straight","term":"Pink Salt, Nitrite & Nitrate (The Science, Straight)"}],"pantryItems":["Garlic","Berries","Bay leaf","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["low-and-slow-smoking","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"beijing-kaoya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole duck, about 2.4 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g maltose"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"16 Mandarin pancakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g sweet bean sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 spring onions, in 7 cm batons"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cucumber (about 250 g), in batons"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the duck inside and out and salt the cavity. Working through the neck opening, ease your fingers, or the nozzle of a bicycle pump, between the skin and the breast until the skin lifts away in a balloon, then tie the neck skin shut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold the duck over the sink and ladle the boiling water over every part of the skin, turning as you go, until it tightens, turns opaque and the pores close.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the maltose with the vinegar and wine until it pours, brush the whole skin twice, and stand the duck on a rack in the fridge, uncovered, for 24 hours. It is ready when the skin is taut and papery and taps like a drum.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast breast up on a rack above a tray of water at 200C (400F) for 30 minutes, then drop to 170C (340F) for 45 minutes to 55 minutes, until the skin is mahogany and hard to the tap and the thigh reads 78C (172F).","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 10 minutes, steaming the pancakes in their stack for 5 minutes while it rests. Carve the skin off first in coin sized pieces while it is at its crispest, then the meat, and serve with the pancakes kept covered at the table, the sweet bean sauce, spring onion and cucumber.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Crisp skin is dry skin. The scald shrinks and tightens the surface collagen, and the maltose wash gives it something to caramelise while pulling very little moisture back out of the air, so the skin keeps drying overnight instead of going tacky. The air pocket is the real trick: fat rendering out of the skin drips down into the gap and away rather than soaking the skin from underneath, so the outside dehydrates and lacquers while the meat below stays wet.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Beijing%20Kaoya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Beijing Kaoya"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Northern%20Chinese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Northern Chinese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Duck","Onion","Scallion","Cucumber","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"siu-ngo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 young goose, about 3.5 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ground bean sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g five spice powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, finely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 spring onions, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 L boiling water"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g maltose"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml rice vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml hot water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the bean sauce, sugar, five spice, ginger, garlic, spring onions, wine and salt to a thick paste, push it all into the cavity, and sew or skewer the opening tightly shut so that not a drop can run out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Separate the skin from the breast: work a pump nozzle in at the neck and inflate until the skin stands clear of the meat, then knot the neck flap. Sit the bird in a colander in the sink and ladle the boiling water over it in slow passes, front, back and both flanks, until the surface blanches and draws tight. Blot it dry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the maltose into the vinegar and hot water until it runs off the spoon in a thread, paint the bird all over, let that coat go tacky, then paint it again. Stand it uncovered on a rack in the fridge for 12 hours. It is ready when the skin has gone stiff and slightly see through, like parchment stretched on a frame.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sit the goose breast up on a rack with a tray of water beneath to quench the drips. Give it 30 minutes at 200C (400F), then turn the oven down to 170C (340F) and carry on for 1 hour 30 minutes to 2 hours, until the thickest part of the thigh reads 82C (180F).","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest 15 minutes, then snip the stitches over a bowl and let the cavity liquid pour out. Taste it, and if it has reduced to a glaze let it down with a splash of hot water or stock until it pours. Chop the goose through the bone and spoon the liquid over.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"The bird cooks in two climates at once. Sewn shut, the paste in the cavity boils and steams the meat from the inside, which is how a breast survives two hours or so of dry heat; leave the seam open and that liquid runs into the tray while the flesh roasts out. What pours from the cavity at the end is the sauce, bean paste and sugar and the bird's own juices reduced together, so it needs no seasoning added, only thinning if it has gone past pouring.","equipment":["Parchment","Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Siu%20Ngo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Siu Ngo"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantonese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Cantonese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Clove","Five-spice"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"zhangcha-ya","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole duck, about 2.2 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g Sichuan peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 spring onions, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g jasmine tea leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g raw long grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g soft brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g fresh camphor tree leaves, or dried cypress sprigs. Never camphor blocks, camphor oil or medicinal camphor of any kind"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L groundnut oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the Sichuan peppercorns and salt in a dry pan until the pepper smells of citrus, grind them coarsely, and rub the mix over and inside the duck with the wine. Tuck the ginger and spring onions into the cavity and cure it uncovered in the fridge for 24 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a wok with foil, tip in the tea, rice, sugar and camphor leaves, sit the duck on a rack above and clamp the lid on. Open a window and run the extractor hard, because 15 minutes of wok smoking will fill a kitchen. Smoke over medium heat for about 15 minutes, until the skin is the colour of weak coffee, and cut the heat the moment the smoke turns sharp instead of sweet.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Steam the duck on a plate over hard boiling water for 1 hour 30 minutes, until a skewer meets no resistance in the thigh and the leg is loose in its socket.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":5400,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool the duck uncovered on a rack in the fridge until it is cold through and the skin feels like parchment, at least 4 hours and better overnight. This is not optional: a warm, damp bird will throw hot oil out of the pan.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the duck through at 200C (400F) until the thickest part of the thigh reads 74C (165F), 35 minutes to 45 minutes from cold, while the oil comes to 190C (375F). Lower it in and spoon oil over any part that stands proud, about 4 minutes, until the skin is dark, blistered and rigid. Drain, rest 5 minutes, and chop through the bone into 3 cm pieces.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3240,"estimated":true}],"note":"Four processes, each doing one job, and the order is the recipe. The cure seasons and dries. Smoke only lands on a cool, dry surface, so it comes before any moisture. The long steam renders the fat under the skin and turns the leg tender, which frying never would. The fry is four minutes of colour on a bird that is already cooked through. Skip the cold drying and the water still in the skin flashes to steam the instant it meets 190C (375F) oil.","equipment":["Wok","Parchment"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","fiery","aromatic","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, real fire that builds and stays, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Zhangcha%20Ya%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Zhangcha Ya"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Duck","Onion","Ginger","Scallion","Rice","Wine","Sugar","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["wok-technique","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"pappardelle-al-ragu-danatra","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 duck legs (about 1 kg)"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion (about 150 g), finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot (about 100 g), finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 celery sticks (about 100 g), finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 cloves garlic, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sprigs rosemary"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g tinned peeled tomatoes, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g fresh pappardelle"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the legs and lay them skin down in a cold, dry, heavy pan. Set it over low heat and leave them 15 minutes to 20 minutes, until a pool of clear fat has run out and the skin is deep brown and stiff. Lift them out.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off all but 40 ml of the fat and keep the rest. Sweat the onion, carrot, celery and garlic in the pan with a pinch of salt until soft and sweet with no colour, about 15 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the rosemary and juniper, pour in the wine and boil until the pan smells of wine rather than of alcohol. Add the tomatoes, stock and the legs, skin up and half submerged, cover, and cook at 150C (300F) for 2 hours 30 minutes.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the legs out, pull the meat into threads, chop the skin small and return both to the pan. Discard the bones. Reduce until a spoon drawn across the base leaves a track that fills back slowly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the pappardelle in well salted water and drain it a minute early, keeping a ladle of the water. Toss it with the sauce and that water off the heat, then the cheese, until every ribbon is coated.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A duck leg is a hard working muscle wrapped in a fat blanket and the two want opposite treatments. Render the fat out first from a cold pan, then braise the lean leg long enough for its collagen to become gelatine. Tip all that fat back in and the sauce simply greases the pasta; return 40 ml and the gelatine emulsifies it with the starchy water into a coat that clings. That is why the sauce needs nothing else enriching it, and why the last toss happens off the heat.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["floral","aromatic","umami-deep","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with a floral thread lifting the aroma, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pappardelle%20al%20Rag%C3%B9%20d'Anatra%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pappardelle al Ragù d'Anatra"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Pappardelle al Ragù d'Anatra","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Pappardelle%20al%20Rag%C3%B9%20d'Anatra","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Duck","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Berries","Rosemary","Parmesan","Pasta","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["reducing-a-sauce","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"bun-mang-vit","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole duck, about 2 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine sea salt, for scrubbing"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml rice wine, for scrubbing"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ginger, 40 g halved and charred for the broth and 20 g finely grated for the dip"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions (about 250 g), halved and charred"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g dried bamboo shoots, soaked overnight, then boiled uncovered in three changes of fresh water, 20 minutes each, and rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml fish sauce, 60 ml for the broth and 30 ml for the dip"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g rock sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g fresh rice vermicelli"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cloves garlic"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bird's eye chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml lime juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g Vietnamese coriander leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g white cabbage, finely shredded"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scrub the duck in a bowl, not under a running tap, with the sea salt and rice wine, working into the skin, then rinse it and wash the bowl, the sink, the taps and your hands before touching anything else. Cut the cabbage and pick the herbs before the duck, never after. This strips the film that carries the loudest of the duck smell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lower the duck into the water with the charred ginger and onions, bring it to a bare shiver and hold it exactly there, skimming, for 50 minutes to 60 minutes, until the thigh reads 74C (165F). Lift the duck into iced water for 5 minutes, then drain it.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":3900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the prepared bamboo shoots in the broth for 30 minutes, then season with the 60 ml of fish sauce and the rock sugar until the broth tastes a shade too savoury on its own.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound the garlic, chillies and grated ginger with the caster sugar to a rough paste, then let it down with the lime juice and the 30 ml of fish sauce for the dip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop the duck through the bone into 2 cm pieces. Pile vermicelli, cabbage, bamboo and Vietnamese coriander into bowls, ladle the broth over, and set the dipping sauce beside each one.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two rules hold this together. Never let the pot boil: a rolling boil emulsifies the duck fat into the water and turns the broth grey and greasy, and it contracts the breast so it wrings itself out. A bare shiver leaves the fat in clear beads you can lift off. Second, bamboo is not a shortcut vegetable. Dried shoots want an overnight soak and boiling in three changes of water, 20 minutes each, both for the bitterness and because bamboo carries compounds that only sustained boiling drives off.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Soaking dried goods back to life","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","umami-deep","tangy-ferment","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=B%C3%BAn%20M%C4%83ng%20V%E1%BB%8Bt%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bún Măng Vịt"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Helen’s Recipes on Bún Măng Vịt","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Helen%E2%80%99s%20Recipes%20B%C3%BAn%20M%C4%83ng%20V%E1%BB%8Bt","sub":"A YouTube search for Helen’s Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Duck","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Cabbage","Lime","Cilantro","Noodles","Fish sauce","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"arroz-con-pato-a-la-chiclayana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 duck legs (about 1 kg)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ají amarillo paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 red onion (about 180 g), finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves garlic, minced"},{"kind":"item","text":"330 ml dark beer"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g coriander, leaves and stems, blended with 150 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g long grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g frozen peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g red pepper, in 1 cm strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper, ground"}],"steps":[{"text":"Pick a casserole wide enough to take the rice later, because the legs and the rice cook in the same pan. Sit the salted legs in it skin down while it is still cold and dry, set it over low heat, and give them 15 minutes to shed their fat and colour before you turn them to seal the flesh side. Lift them out.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour off all but 40 ml of the fat. Cook the onion, garlic, cumin and ají amarillo paste in it, stirring, until the paste darkens and the oil around it turns orange, about 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the legs, pour in the beer, scrape the base clean, cover and hold at a low simmer for 1 hour 15 minutes, until the meat gives at the bone. Lift the legs out and keep them warm.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the rice until the water runs clear. Add the coriander purée to the braising liquid and make the total up to 760 ml with as much of the stock as it takes. Bring it to a hard boil, rain in the rice with the pepper strips and black pepper, cover, and cook 18 minutes without once lifting the lid.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":1320,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fork the peas through, sit the legs back on top, and let it stand covered off the heat for 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Loose surface starch is the enemy here. Washed off, the grains steam separate; left on, it swells in all that rendered duck fat and cements them into paste, which is why this rice is rinsed and a risotto never is. The coriander is blended raw and stirred in at the boil because heat and acid dull chlorophyll steadily, so one cooking rather than two is what keeps any green at all. Expect olive, not emerald. Lift the lid early and the escaping steam is exactly the water the top layer needed.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["earthy","aromatic","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with an earthy, grounding base, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Risotto method","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=risotto%20technique%20mantecatura","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arroz%20con%20Pato%20a%20la%20Chiclayana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arroz con Pato a la Chiclayana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Duck","Onion","Garlic","Bell pepper","Peas","Rice","Beer/Cider","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["frying-the-paste-until-the-oil-splits","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"gaeng-phet-ped-yang","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g Chinese roast duck, boned and sliced 1 cm thick, skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g red curry paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml fish sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g palm sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g cherry tomatoes, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g pineapple, in 2 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g lychees, peeled and stoned"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 makrut lime leaves, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 red chillies, sliced on the angle"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g Thai basil leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lime juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Put the coconut cream alone in a wide pan over medium heat and stir it until the emulsion breaks and clear oil pools at the edges, 5 minutes to 8 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir the curry paste into that split oil and fry it until the raw garlic and shrimp smell has gone and the paste smells sweet and toasted, 3 minutes to 4 minutes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Loosen with the coconut milk, season with the fish sauce and palm sugar, bring to a bare simmer, then add the tomatoes, pineapple, lychees and lime leaves and cook 4 minutes.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reheat the duck apart from the curry: spread the slices skin up on a tray and give them 8 minutes to 10 minutes at 180C (350F), until they read 74C (165F) and the skin has crisped again.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat, stir in the basil, chillies and lime juice, then lay the hot duck on top with the skin proud of the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Coconut cream is oil suspended in water. Heat and stirring break that suspension, the oil pools out, and it is the only thing in the pan that can actually fry the paste. Frying is a different reaction from boiling: it browns the aromatics and drives off the raw allium and shrimp notes that simmering leaves sitting there. The duck is reheated on a tray because two minutes over a bare simmer will not carry cold slices to 74C (165F), and skin left in the sauce slackens within a minute anyway.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Pounding a curry paste","Building an emulsion","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","umami-deep","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":7,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pounding a curry paste","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=thai%20curry%20paste%20mortar%20pestle%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gaeng%20Phet%20Ped%20Yang%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gaeng Phet Ped Yang"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Pailin’s Kitchen on Gaeng Phet Ped Yang","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pailin%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Gaeng%20Phet%20Ped%20Yang","sub":"A YouTube search for Pailin’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Duck","Tomato","Chiles","Lime","Pineapple","Coconut","Stone fruit","Basil","Curry/Makrut leaves","Milk","Cream","Coconut milk","Fish sauce","Sugar","Curry paste"],"judgedBy":["pounding-a-curry-paste","building-an-emulsion"]},{"slug":"paris-brest","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"125 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"110 g unsalted butter, cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"140 g plain flour, sifted"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g eggs, beaten (about 5)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g flaked almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk, for the mousseline"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar, for the mousseline"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g unsalted butter, softened to 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g hazelnut praline paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g icing sugar, to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the water, 125 ml milk, 110 g butter, salt and sugar to a full boil, tip in the flour off the heat and beat to a smooth ball, then dry it over medium heat for 2 minutes until a film forms on the pan base.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":120,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the eggs a little at a time and stop when the dough falls from the spatula in a slow heavy V and a piped ridge holds its shape: the last 30 g may not be needed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pipe two 20 cm rings touching on a lined tray and a third on the seam, scatter the almonds, and bake at 180C (350F) for 45 minutes with the door shut, until deep gold and rigid.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks, 100 g sugar and cornflour, pour on the scalded 500 ml milk, return to the pan and boil hard for 1 minute while whisking, then spread thin, cover the surface and chill in the refrigerator until cold; bring it back to 20C (68F) over about 30 minutes just before you need it.","durationSec":60,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the softened butter into the smooth tempered pastry cream a spoonful at a time, beat in the praline, then pipe into the split ring and dust with icing sugar.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Choux is raised by steam alone: the water you boil into the flour turns to vapour and inflates a paste that egg and gelatinised starch set around. Drying the panade on the stove controls how much water is left, and opening the door before the shell is rigid drops it. The mousseline is butter emulsified into set pastry cream, and it splits when the two are at different temperatures. Hold both at 20C (68F).","equipment":["Piping bag","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","fiery","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Choux pastry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=choux%20pastry%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Paris-Brest%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Paris-Brest"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Paris-Brest","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Paris-Brest","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"caneles-de-bordeaux","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, split and scraped"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml dark rum"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g food grade beeswax"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g clarified butter, for the moulds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Warm the milk with the 50 g butter and the vanilla to 60C (140F), then leave it to infuse off the heat for 10 minutes.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Whisk the sugar, flour and salt, add the eggs and yolks and stir to a smooth paste, then pour in the warm milk in a thin stream and stir in the rum, stopping the moment it is even and as loose as pouring cream.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain, cover and refrigerate 24 hours to 48 hours, then bring the batter back to room temperature and stir gently to redistribute the fat.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the beeswax and the clarified butter together to 70C to 75C (158F to 167F), pour it into hot copper moulds so the film runs thin, swirl, invert to drain, then chill the moulds until the film is hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill the chilled moulds with the room temperature batter to 5 mm below the rim, bake at 240C (475F) for 15 minutes then at 180C (350F) for 45 minutes to 55 minutes until nearly black, and turn out onto a rack at once.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":4200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The cold rest is chemistry, not tradition. It hydrates the starch fully and lets the gluten relax, so the batter sets from the outside inwards instead of puffing like a Yorkshire pudding and hollowing out. The wax film drives heat into the sugar and releases the crust cleanly, which is how the outside reaches near black while the middle stays a slack custard. Moulds still warm at filling, or batter straight from the refrigerator, give a pale, empty shell.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","fiery","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Canel%C3%A9s%20de%20Bordeaux%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Canelés de Bordeaux"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Canelés de Bordeaux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Canel%C3%A9s%20de%20Bordeaux","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"]},{"slug":"kouign-amann","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"260 ml water, at 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g unsalted butter, at 14C (57F), in a flat slab"},{"kind":"item","text":"280 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g demerara sugar, for the tins"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g salted butter, for the tins"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, water, salt and yeast to a shaggy dough, knead 5 minutes to a smooth slack ball, then rest it covered in the refrigerator for 1 hour.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Roll to a 40 cm square, lay the butter slab across the middle, fold the dough over it and seal the edges, roll out and give one fold in three; chill 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll out again, scatter 140 g of the caster sugar over the surface, fold in three and chill 30 minutes, then repeat with the remaining 140 g sugar and chill a final 30 minutes before shaping.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut into 8 squares, fold the corners of each into the centre, drop them into tins buttered and lined with demerara, and prove 45 minutes until visibly puffed but still cold to the touch.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 200C (400F) for 35 minutes to 40 minutes until the sugar beneath each has gone to dark caramel, then lift them out immediately or the caramel welds them in.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false}],"note":"Sugar folded into a laminated dough behaves as a second liquid. It dissolves in the water the butter and dough already carry, runs to the base of the tin and caramelises there, and that is where the lacquer comes from. Everything hangs on the butter staying between 12C (54F) and 16C (61F): warmer and it absorbs into the dough, the sugar goes with it, and you bake a sweet brioche instead of leaves.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Kneading dough","Chilling to firm: the fridge as binder","Resting dough: the pause that does the work"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Lamination","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vpwY3nmLLaA","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kouign-amann%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kouign-amann"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Kouign-amann","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Kouign-amann","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["chilling-to-firm-the-fridge-as-binder","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"madeleines-au-beurre-noisette","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs (about 110 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g runny honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g baking powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g unsalted butter, softened, for the moulds"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g plain flour, for the moulds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the 100 g butter over medium heat until it stops foaming and smells of hazelnut, then pour it off the browned solids and cool it to 40C (105F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the eggs, sugar, honey, salt and zest for 3 minutes to a pale foam, fold in the sifted flour and baking powder, then fold in the brown butter until glossy.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and refrigerate the batter for at least 12 hours: it has to go into the oven properly cold.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Butter and flour the moulds and chill them, then fill each shell two thirds full with cold batter straight from the refrigerator.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 220C (425F) for 4 minutes, drop the oven to 180C (350F) for 6 minutes to 7 minutes, and turn them out the moment the edges brown and the hump springs back.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":660,"estimated":true}],"note":"The hump is thermal shock. Cold batter meets a hot mould, the outside sets while the middle is still liquid, and the steam and carbon dioxide can only escape through that soft centre, so it domes. Let the batter warm up first and it bakes flat. The long cold rest also hydrates the flour and relaxes the gluten, which is what keeps the crumb tender rather than rubbery.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Brown butter","Folding: keeping the air in"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brown butter","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brown%20butter%20beurre%20noisette%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Madeleines%20au%20Beurre%20Noisette%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Madeleines au Beurre Noisette"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Madeleines au Beurre Noisette","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Madeleines%20au%20Beurre%20Noisette","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Honey","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in"]},{"slug":"salzburger-nockerl","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 egg whites, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour, sifted"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g redcurrant preserve"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g icing sugar, to dust"}],"steps":[{"text":"Butter a shallow oval dish, pour in the cream, dot it with the preserve and set it in the oven at 220C (425F) to heat while you whisk.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the whites with the salt to soft peaks, then rain the sugar in over a minute and beat only until the foam is glossy and holds a peak that bends at the tip.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with the vanilla and zest, then fold them and the sifted flour into the meringue in three additions, stopping the second the streaks vanish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Heap three tall peaks into the hot dish and bake 10 minutes to 12 minutes, until the ridges are browned and the insides still creamy, then dust with icing sugar and carry it straight to the table.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true}],"note":"Meringue is air trapped in unfolded egg white protein, and sugar is what keeps those protein films flexible. Beat past glossy soft peak and the proteins bond too tightly, squeeze out the water they were holding and weep a puddle under the crust. Sugar added early slows the proteins unfolding and you never reach full volume, which is why it goes in over a minute once soft peaks are there. The flour is a token: the structure is protein, so it falls three minutes out of the oven.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Salzburger%20Nockerl%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Salzburger Nockerl"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"linzer-torte","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g ground hazelnuts, lightly toasted and cooled"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g unsalted butter, cold and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g ground cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml dark rum"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g redcurrant jam, firmly set"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten, to glaze"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g flaked almonds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the cold butter into the flour, ground hazelnuts, sugar, spices, salt and zest until it looks like coarse damp sand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the yolks and rum and press the dough together in as few movements as you can, then wrap it and chill 2 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press two thirds into a 24 cm tin and 2 cm up the side, spread the jam evenly, and lay over a lattice of strips rolled between two sheets of paper, patching every break with your fingers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Glaze the lattice, press flaked almonds around the rim and bake at 180C (350F) for 45 minutes to 50 minutes, until the jam bubbles slowly and the pastry is a dark biscuit brown.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool fully in the tin, then keep it a day before cutting so the crumb takes moisture from the jam and the spice comes forward.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nearly half the dry weight here is nut, and nuts bring fat and protein but nothing that can build gluten, so the dough has almost no elasticity. It will tear as you lay the lattice and you simply press it back together. That shortness is the entire texture. Use a firmly set redcurrant jam: a loose one thins as it warms and floods the lattice instead of sitting under it.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich","warm-spiced","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Linzer%20Torte%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Linzer Torte"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Nuts","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"bienenstich","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"375 g strong white flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"7 g instant dried yeast"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml whole milk, at 35C (95F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter, for the topping"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar, for the topping"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g runny honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml double cream, for the topping"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g flaked almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml whole milk, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g caster sugar, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 g cornflour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml double cream, for the filling"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, yeast, 45 g sugar, salt, warm milk and egg to a shaggy dough, knead 8 minutes, work in the 60 g soft butter and knead until the dough is soft and slightly tacky and a stretched piece goes translucent; prove until doubled.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the 100 g butter, 100 g sugar, honey and 60 ml cream to 112C (234F), stir in the almonds and cool until the mixture is thick enough to spread.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the dough into a lined 26 cm tin, spread the almond mixture right to the edge, prove 30 minutes, then bake at 180C (350F) for 30 minutes to 35 minutes until the top is amber and the crumb reads 96C (205F).","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks, 90 g sugar and cornflour, pour on the scalded 500 ml milk, return to the pan and boil hard for a full minute, then spread thin, cover the surface and chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the cold pastry cream smooth, fold in the 250 ml cream whipped to soft peaks, split the cooled cake, cut the top into wedges before you replace it, and fill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two thresholds decide this cake. The topping is a caramel taken to 112C (234F): under that it runs off the sides in the oven, and past about 120C (250F) it sets firm enough to tear the crumb under the knife, glass being hard crack at 150C (300F) and above. The filling has to boil hard for a full minute, because egg yolk carries an amylase that cuts starch chains; leave it unboiled and the set cream turns to liquid overnight.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Kneading dough","Salted water & the float test","Proofing: the rise before the oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bienenstich%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bienenstich"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Bienenstich","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Bienenstich","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Honey","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"turron-de-alicante","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g Marcona almonds, skinned"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g orange blossom honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg white (about 35 g)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sheets edible wafer paper (obleas)"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the almonds at 160C (320F) for 12 minutes to 15 minutes until pale gold right through, then hold them somewhere warm.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the honey in a wide pan, stirring, until it reads 125C (257F) and the foam settles: you are driving off its water.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the egg white with the salt to soft peaks in the smallest bowl your mixer takes, or with a hand mixer in a deep bowl, and have it at soft peak before either syrup is ready.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the sugar and water to 145C (293F), pour it into the whites in a thin thread with the mixer running, follow with the hot honey, then set the bowl over simmering water and stir with a spatula for 25 minutes to 35 minutes, until a teaspoon of the mass dropped into cold water sets hard enough to snap rather than bend.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":2100,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fold in the warm almonds and the zest, press into a wafer lined 20 cm frame, cap with wafer, weight it flat and cure 24 hours before cutting.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Hardness here is a moisture reading rather than a step you can time. Honey is roughly a fifth water, and the long stir over the bain-marie is the only way to drive that water out of an egg white foam without collapsing it. Test in cold water: bends means wet, snaps means done. Fold the almonds in warm, because cold nuts chill the mass and it seizes before it reaches the frame.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","nutty-toasted","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Turr%C3%B3n%20de%20Alicante%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Turrón de Alicante"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Turrón de Alicante","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Turr%C3%B3n%20de%20Alicante","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Orange","Honey","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","sugar-stages-and-caramel"]},{"slug":"pestinos-de-semana-santa","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 wide strip orange peel"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g aniseed"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sesame seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml olive oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g runny honey"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the 150 ml oil with the orange peel and aniseed until the peel darkens at the edges, then strain it and cool the oil to blood heat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the sesame seeds dry, then mix the flour, salt, cinnamon, seeds, warm oil and wine to a soft dough, knead 3 minutes until supple and rest it 30 minutes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 2 mm, cut 8 cm squares, and pinch two opposite corners together over your finger so each square folds into a loose loop.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry at 180C (350F) in small batches for about 90 seconds, turning once, until blistered and biscuit gold, then drain on a rack.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Warm the honey with the water to a thin syrup at 100C (212F), dip each pestiño for a few seconds and drain on the rack until the coat turns tacky.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Holy Week fritter of Andalusia, and the olive oil is why it works. It goes in hot and infused, coating the flour before the wine arrives, so gluten forms only weakly and the dough fries into blistered flakes instead of bread. Alcohol in the wine boils off faster than water and lifts those layers apart from inside. Thin the honey and use it at 100C (212F): a cold dip sits on the pastry as a heavy sticky sheet and never sets to a thin glaze.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deep frying","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Draining fried food: a rack, never paper"],"flavor":{"tags":["sweet-edged","briny","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with a sweet edge rounding the corners, a briny, maritime salinity, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pesti%C3%B1os%20de%20Semana%20Santa%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pestiños de Semana Santa"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Pestiños de Semana Santa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Pesti%C3%B1os%20de%20Semana%20Santa","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Orange","Flour","Sesame","Wine","Honey","Cinnamon"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","draining-fried-food-a-rack-never-paper"]},{"slug":"pao-de-lo-de-ovar","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"10 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 whole eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g plain flour, sifted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g unsalted butter, for the paper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the yolks, whole eggs, sugar and salt on high for 15 minutes, until the mass triples, goes ivory pale and falls in a thick unbroken ribbon that sits on the surface.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Sift the flour over in three lots and fold from the base of the bowl with a spatula, stopping the instant the last streak disappears.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a deep 20 cm clay bowl or cake tin with buttered paper pleated 5 cm above the rim, and pour the batter in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (350F) for 30 minutes to 35 minutes, then judge it by probe alone, pushed into the geometric centre of the bowl: pull it out when that reads 80C (176F), which is the reading that makes the loose centre safe to eat, and ignore the skewer, which comes away wet.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool it in its paper and serve straight from the bowl, spooning the soft centre over the outer crumb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Fourteen eggs, 250 g of sugar and only 90 g of flour: this is a foam held by egg protein, and the fifteen minutes of beating is most of the recipe. The wet middle is cooked, not raw. Sugar binds water and pushes the temperature at which egg protein coagulates upward, so at 80C (176F) that centre is safe and still pours. Bake past it and you have an ordinary dry sponge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pleating dumplings"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pleating dumplings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20pleat%20dumplings%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=P%C3%A3o%20de%20L%C3%B3%20de%20Ovar%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pão de Ló de Ovar"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["pleating-dumplings"]},{"slug":"lemon-posset-with-shortbread-fingers","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml double cream, 48 percent fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 2 unwaxed lemons"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml lemon juice, strained"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g rice flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"110 g unsalted butter, cold and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"55 g caster sugar, for the shortbread"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the cream, 150 g sugar and the zest to a rolling boil and hold it there for 3 minutes, stirring, so it thickens slightly.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat, stir in the lemon juice, stand 10 minutes, then strain into six glasses and chill 4 hours until set.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":15000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the cold butter into both flours, the 55 g sugar and the salt to fine crumbs, press firmly into a 20 cm tin and mark into fingers.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake the shortbread at 150C (300F) for 35 minutes to 40 minutes until an even pale sand colour, cut through the marks while hot and cool it in the tin.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things set this together. The lemon juice partly destabilises the casein micelles so they clump into a gel, and the 3 minute boil drives off water and concentrates the fat and solids enough for that gel to hold. Below about 40 percent fat there is too little structure and you get sweet lemon soup. Adding the juice off the heat is the safer habit, since protein meeting acid at a hard boil is the one way to grain it.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lemon%20Posset%20with%20Shortbread%20Fingers%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lemon Posset with Shortbread Fingers"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Lemon Posset with Shortbread Fingers","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Lemon%20Posset%20with%20Shortbread%20Fingers","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Butter","Cream","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"bakewell-tart","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter, cold and cubed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 ml ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g raspberry jam"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g unsalted butter, softened to 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"125 g ground almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g plain flour, for the frangipane"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 ml almond extract"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g flaked almonds"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the cold butter into the 200 g flour, icing sugar and salt to coarse crumbs, bind with the yolk and ice water, flatten to a disc and chill 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll to 3 mm, line a 23 cm tin, chill until hard, then blind bake at 190C (375F) for 20 minutes with beans and 8 minutes without, until the base is dry and biscuit coloured.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1680,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the softened butter and caster sugar pale, add the room temperature eggs one at a time, then fold in the ground almonds, 25 g flour and almond extract.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the jam over the base, level the frangipane on top, scatter the flaked almonds, and bake at 170C (340F) for 35 minutes to 40 minutes until risen, golden and springy at the centre.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two doughs with opposite rules share one tin. The pastry wants cold fat and almost no working, so the butter coats the flour and gluten never forms a sheet. The frangipane wants soft butter beaten until it holds air, and eggs at room temperature so they emulsify into it; cold eggs split the batter, and a split frangipane bakes as a greasy layer above the jam. Blind bake to a dry base or the jam steams it soft.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, melting away silken."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bakewell%20Tart%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bakewell Tart"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Bakewell Tart","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Bakewell%20Tart","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Berries","Butter","Flour","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking","rubbing-fat-into-flour"]},{"slug":"semifreddo-al-caffe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"6 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml double cream, cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 ml espresso, strong and cold"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml dark rum"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g amaretti biscuits, roughly crushed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the sugar and water without stirring to 118C (245F), the soft ball stage.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the syrup into the yolks and salt in a heatproof bowl, set it over simmering water and cook to 82C (180F), whisking constantly, then take it off and whip 8 minutes to 10 minutes until pale, thick and cool to the touch.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the cold espresso and rum, whip the cream to soft peaks, and fold the cream through in two additions, keeping as much air as you can.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in half the amaretti, scrape into a lined 2 litre terrine, cover the surface and freeze at least 6 hours.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn out, slice with a warmed knife and scatter the remaining amaretti over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing churns this, so the only defences against ice crystals are the air you beat into the yolks and the sugar you dissolve. Syrup poured into a cold bowl often leaves the yolks well under 70C (158F), which is why they go over simmering water to 82C (180F): that cooks them, and the dessert is never heated again. The sugar then depresses the freezing point, which is why the block stays sliceable at minus 18C (0F).","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Semifreddo%20al%20Caff%C3%A8%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Semifreddo al Caffè"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Semifreddo al Caffè","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Semifreddo%20al%20Caff%C3%A8","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Cream","Sugar","Coffee/Espresso"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"cantucci-di-prato","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"300 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g whole almonds, skin on and untoasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 whole eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 orange"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, beaten, to glaze"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the flour, sugar, salt and zest, then work in the eggs and yolk with a spatula to a stiff sticky dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the almonds whole, then with wet hands shape two logs 5 cm wide on a lined tray, flatten them slightly and brush with beaten egg.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 170C (340F) for 25 minutes to 30 minutes until firm and pale gold, then cool them on the tray for 15 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cut on a 45 degree angle into 1.5 cm slices with a serrated knife, lay them cut side down and bake again at 150C (300F) for 15 minutes to 20 minutes until dry all the way through.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true}],"note":"There is no chemical leavening here, as in the Prato original: the eggs alone lift, and the logs stay dense and crack free. These keep for months, and the reason is water. The second bake is a drying bake, not a colouring one, so take them further than looks right and judge by the snap once cool. Cut the logs warm, neither hot nor cold. Hot they squash under the knife, cold they shatter along the almonds.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Shaping by hand"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cantucci%20di%20Prato%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cantucci di Prato"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Cantucci di Prato","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Cantucci%20di%20Prato","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Orange","Flour","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["shaping-by-hand"]},{"slug":"torta-di-riso-bolognese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g originario or arborio rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 wide strip lemon peel"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g blanched almonds, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g candied citron, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml almond liqueur"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g unsalted butter, for the tin"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g dry breadcrumbs, for the tin"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the rice in the milk with the lemon peel, sugar and salt, stirring often, for 30 minutes to 35 minutes until the grains are soft and the mixture is still loose and creamy.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":2340,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread it out to cool below 40C (105F), then lift out the lemon peel.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the eggs and stir them in with the almonds, citron and liqueur: the mixture should pour thickly rather than hold a shape.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter a 24 cm tin, dust it with breadcrumbs, pour the mixture in and bake at 170C (340F) for 50 minutes to 55 minutes until set with a faint wobble and browned in patches.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool completely, cut into diamonds, and leave it a day before eating.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two things set this cake: the rice starch that leached into the milk, and the egg. Egg scrambles above about 60C (140F), so cooling the rice below 40C (105F) is the safe margin; stir eggs into hotter rice and they set into threads and the crumb turns grainy rather than dense. The breadcrumb lining is structural, not decorative; the mixture is wet and sugary, and the crumbs give it something to grip and release from instead of welding to the tin.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Torta%20di%20Riso%20Bolognese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Torta di Riso Bolognese"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Torta di Riso Bolognese","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Torta%20di%20Riso%20Bolognese","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Rice","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Sugar","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["blanching-and-shocking","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"galaktoboureko","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g fine semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"450 g phyllo pastry"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g unsalted butter, melted, for the sheets"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g caster sugar, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 wide strip lemon peel"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the 400 g sugar, water and lemon peel for 5 minutes, stir in the lemon juice and leave the syrup to go completely cold.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the milk with the vanilla and zest to a simmer, rain in the semolina while whisking, cook 5 minutes to a thick trail, then beat in the 100 g butter and 200 g sugar.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool the semolina to 60C (140F), then whisk in the eggs one at a time so they thicken it instead of setting into threads.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Butter seven phyllo sheets into a 30 cm by 22 cm tin at least 5 cm deep, overhang hanging out, pour in the custard, fold the overhang back, layer seven more buttered sheets on top and score the top layers into diamonds.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (350F) for 50 minutes to 55 minutes until deep gold and puffed, then pour the cold syrup over the pastry straight from the oven and leave it 4 hours.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":17700,"estimated":false}],"note":"Two temperatures decide this. The eggs go into semolina below 65C (150F), because above that they set on contact and you get threads running through the custard. And the syrup must be cold onto hot pastry: steam is still leaving the phyllo and pushing outward while the cool syrup soaks in, so the leaves stay crisp. Hot syrup on hot pastry, or cold on cold, gives you paste.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Tempering a custard","Handling phyllo","Scoring"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, melting away silken."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Handling phyllo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=working%20with%20phyllo%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Galaktoboureko%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Galaktoboureko"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Galaktoboureko","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Galaktoboureko","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Semolina","Phyllo/Puff pastry","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["scoring","tempering-a-custard"]},{"slug":"kyivskyi-tort","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"200 g egg whites (about 6), aged 24 hours in the refrigerator"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g roasted hazelnuts, roughly chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar, for the buttercream"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g unsalted butter, softened to 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g cocoa powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml cognac"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the aged whites to 20C (68F), whip them with the salt to soft peaks, then add the 250 g sugar in a slow stream and beat to a stiff glossy meringue.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the hazelnuts and sifted flour by hand, spread into two 22 cm circles on lined trays, and dry at 140C (285F) for 2 hours until they lift cleanly and sound hollow; leave them overnight.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks with the milk and the 200 g sugar, bring to a boil and simmer 4 minutes to 5 minutes, stirring, until it thickens to the consistency of condensed milk, then cover, chill in the refrigerator, and bring it back to 20C (68F) shortly before you need it.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the softened butter until white, beat the tempered custard in a spoonful at a time, then take out a third and beat the cocoa into it; flavour the pale cream with vanilla and cognac.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sandwich the discs with pale cream, coat the top and sides, pipe the cocoa cream over and refrigerate 12 hours so the meringue softens into cake.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Two waits do the work. Ageing the whites thins them, so they whip to a looser, wetter foam that spreads flat and dries without cracking. The discs leave the oven as hard nut brittle and only become cake by drawing moisture out of the buttercream overnight, so cutting the same day gives you shards. Custard and butter must both sit at 20C (68F) or the cream splits as you combine them.","equipment":["Piping bag","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Tempering a custard","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake","Icing & frosting"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","nutty-toasted","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, melting away silken."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tempering a custard","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20temper%20eggs%20custard%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kyivskyi%20Tort%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kyivskyi Tort"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["icing-and-frosting","whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"pate-en-croute","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g pork shoulder, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g pork back fat, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g chicken livers, trimmed of green and sinew"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked ham, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"18 g fine salt, 1.5 percent of the 1.2 kg of meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g curing salt number 1, 0.25 percent of the meat, weighed on a scale reading to 0.01 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml Madeira"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g quatre epices"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g peeled pistachios"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g cold butter, in 1 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt and 120 ml iced water, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg yolk beaten with 20 ml water, for the wash"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml clear chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g leaf gelatine"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the pork, back fat, livers and ham with the 18 g salt, the curing salt, Madeira, white pepper and quatre epices, cover, and cure in the fridge for 12 hours; the cure has worked when the cubes feel tacky and cling to one another.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the butter into the flour and dough salt to the texture of gravel, bring it together with the iced water into a firm dough with no dry patches, and rest it cold for 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind the cured meat through a 6 mm plate into a chilled bowl, beat in the eggs and pistachios, then fry a teaspoon of the mixture and taste it for salt before it goes anywhere near the pastry.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a 30 cm hinged mould with pastry, leaving 2 cm proud, pack the forcemeat in against the walls so no air is trapped, lay on a pastry lid, seal the edges with the wash, and cut two 15 mm chimneys. Bake at 200C (400F) for 20 minutes, then at 160C (325F) until the centre reads 71C (160F), about 1 hour 15 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool it 1 hour at room temperature, then chill it overnight. Soften the gelatine, dissolve it in the warm stock, let it cool to the thickness of egg white, and pour it through the chimneys in three goes as the gap drinks it, then chill a further 24 hours before the first slice.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":39600,"estimated":true}],"note":"The forcemeat shrinks as its proteins tighten and shed water; the baked crust cannot follow it, so a gap opens between them. Aspic fills that gap and seals the meat from air, which is why a pate en croute keeps a week and a bare one browns in two days. Pour the gelee cool and syrupy: hot, it melts the fat at the seams and runs straight out. Twelve hours of cure before it bakes and a day of chilling after are what make it sliceable.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Kneading dough","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","nutty-toasted","smoky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=P%C3%A2t%C3%A9%20en%20Cro%C3%BBte%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pâté en Croûte"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Pâté en Croûte","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20P%C3%A2t%C3%A9%20en%20Cro%C3%BBte","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"pate-en-croute","term":"Pâté en Croûte"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Eggs","Ham/Prosciutto","Liver & Offal","Butter","Flour","Stock/Broth","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","kneading-dough"]},{"slug":"rillons-de-tours","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg pork belly, rind on, in 5 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"24 g coarse salt, 2 percent of the meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g cracked black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml dry Vouvray or other dry white wine"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the belly cubes with the salt, pepper, thyme and bay and cure them covered in the fridge for 12 hours, then pour off the brine that has collected and pat every face of every cube dry.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the lard in a narrow, deep pot over low heat and lower the cubes in rind side down, adding more lard if you need it so the fat stands above them; they should whisper, not sizzle.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold the fat at a lazy blip, around 90C (195F), uncovered, for 2 hours, until a skewer meets no resistance and the rind gives under a thumbnail.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the cubes into a wide hot pan, scatter the sugar over them and let it darken to the colour of an old penny, then deglaze with the wine and roll the rillons in the syrup for a minute. Eat warm, or cold with mustard and a sharp salad.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt first, then fat, and the order is the lesson. The overnight cure pulls free water out of the belly, and dry meat entering warm fat colours instead of stewing. Confit is not frying: held near 90C (195F) the cubes poach in fat while their own collagen melts. Push the fat past about 130C (265F) and the outside sets hard long before the rind has turned to gelatine, leaving leather around a raw seam.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rillons%20de%20Tours%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rillons de Tours"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Rillons de Tours","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Rillons%20de%20Tours","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Thyme","Bay leaf","Wine","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"pot-au-feu","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg beef shin, on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g beef short rib"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g oxtail, in sections"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 marrow bones, 8 cm each"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved and charred black on the cut face"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves, pressed into the onion"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 carrots, halved lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 turnips, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 leeks, tied into a bundle"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 celery stalk"},{"kind":"item","text":"a bouquet of parsley stalks, thyme and bay"},{"kind":"item","text":"cornichons, Dijon mustard and coarse salt, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Put the shin, short rib and oxtail in a tall pot, cover with the cold water, and bring it to 90C (195F) over a slow 40 minutes, lifting off the grey foam every few minutes until no more rises.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the charred onion with its cloves, the bouquet, celery and salt, and hold the pot at a shiver, one bubble every second or two, for 3 hours. It must never reach a boil.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the carrots and turnips and give them 45 minutes, adding the leeks and standing the marrow bones upright in the broth for the last 20 minutes so the marrow softens without sliding out.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3900,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain a ladleful of broth per person and serve it alone as a first course, then bring the meat, vegetables and bones to the table on a warm platter with cornichons, mustard and coarse salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold water is the whole trick. Soluble proteins leave the meat while the water is still cool and coagulate into one raft you can lift off; drop the same meat into hot water and they set inside it, leaving nothing to skim and a broth that never clears. Boiling does the other damage, shearing rendered fat into droplets small enough to stay suspended for good. No amount of straining brings that broth back.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","briny","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pot-au-Feu%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pot-au-Feu"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Pot-au-Feu","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Pot-au-Feu","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Leek","Carrot","Celery","Radish/Turnip","Parsley","Thyme","Bread","Mustard","Pickles","Clove"]},{"slug":"blanquette-de-veau-a-lancienne","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.4 kg veal shoulder, in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, stuck with 2 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 carrots, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 leek, white part only"},{"kind":"item","text":"a bouquet of thyme, bay and parsley stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g button mushrooms"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g pearl onions, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, juiced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground white pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the veal with the cold water, bring it slowly to a shiver and skim until the foam stops rising; the liquid must stay pale and the meat must not take any colour at any point in this dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the clouted onion, carrots, leek, bouquet and salt and poach at 85C (185F) for 90 minutes, until a piece parts under spoon pressure. Lift the meat out and strain the broth.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the butter and flour together for 2 minutes without letting them blond, whisk in 1 litre of the hot broth, and simmer 15 minutes, skimming the skin that gathers where the pan is coolest.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Glaze the mushrooms and pearl onions in a little butter and water with the lid ajar and no browning, then slide them and the veal into the sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, whisk the yolks into the cream, temper with two ladles of hot sauce, return it all to the pan and stir over the lowest possible flame until it coats the back of a spoon. Finish with lemon and white pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A blanquette is defined by what never happens: nothing browns, not the meat, not the roux, not the garnish. The liaison is where it is lost. Yolk proteins set at 65C to 70C (149F to 158F), and the starch and cream only buy a few degrees, so above roughly 83C (181F) the sauce turns grainy. Temper it, keep it under a simmer, and add the lemon at the very end, since acid early curdles the same yolks.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sautéing & the shallow fry","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","umami-deep","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":39,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Blanquette%20de%20Veau%20%C3%A0%20l'Ancienne%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Blanquette de Veau à l'Ancienne"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Blanquette de Veau à l'Ancienne","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Blanquette%20de%20Veau%20%C3%A0%20l'Ancienne","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Onion","Leek","Carrot","Mushrooms","Lemon","Parsley","Thyme","Butter","Cream","Flour","Clove"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","sauteing-and-the-shallow-fry"]},{"slug":"cassoulet-de-toulouse","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g dried lingot or cannellini beans"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g fresh pork rind, in wide strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 ham hock, about 800 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pork shoulder, in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g unsmoked pork belly, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 Toulouse sausages, about 400 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 confit duck legs"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g duck fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, stuck with 2 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"a bouquet of thyme, bay and parsley"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre light pork or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the beans overnight in cold water, drain, then simmer them 15 minutes in fresh water with the rind, hock and clouted onion. Keep that liquid.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the shoulder, sausages and diced belly in the duck fat in unhurried batches, taking each piece out at the colour of a chestnut. The confit legs stay pale. Refrigerate the browned sausages and the confit legs while the pot bakes: they do not go in until the last stage, and warm browned mince left on the bench for two hours is the worst hold in the dish.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the beans, rind, hock, shoulder and belly in a wide earthenware pot with the garlic, bouquet, salt and pepper, pour in the reserved bean liquid and enough stock to cover by 2 cm, cover, and cook at 150C (300F) for 2 hours.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the sausages and duck legs down into the beans, wipe the rim clean, and cook uncovered for 90 minutes more, pushing the skin under the surface each time it firms and topping up with the reserved bean liquid, then with hot stock once that is gone, whenever the beans look dry.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true}],"note":"The rind is why this eats like cream and not like soup. Its collagen dissolves into the bean liquid as gelatine and holds the starch the beans shed in suspension, so the pot thickens itself. Keep acid out until the beans are tender, because wine or tomato early on locks the pectin in the skins while the insides blow to mush. Break the crust as it sets: each time it goes under it carries fat and gelatine back down.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Confit","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Confit","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=confit%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cassoulet%20de%20Toulouse%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cassoulet de Toulouse"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Cassoulet de Toulouse","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Cassoulet%20de%20Toulouse","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"confit","term":"Confit"}],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Sausage","Duck","Ham/Prosciutto","Onion","Garlic","Parsley","Thyme","White beans","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"carbonnade-flamande","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.3 kg beef chuck, in 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml dark Belgian abbey ale"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 thick slices of country bread"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g strong Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml red wine vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the beef on cloth, salt it, and brown it hard in half the butter in two batches; a crowded pan steams and you lose the fond the whole sauce is built on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop the heat, add the rest of the butter and the onions with a pinch of salt, and cook 25 minutes until they slump and go tawny, scraping the brown film off the base as their water releases it.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dust in the flour, return the beef, pour in the ale, and add the sugar, bay, thyme and pepper. Bring it to a bare simmer.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the bread thickly with mustard, float it mustard side down on the surface, cover, and braise at 150C (300F) for 3 hours. Stir the collapsed bread through the sauce and finish with the vinegar off the heat.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Brown ale is bitter and three hours of reduction concentrates that, so sugar goes in at the start and vinegar at the end: sweetness suppresses the perception of bitterness, acid lifts what long heat has flattened. The bread is a thickener dressed up as folklore. Its starch swells and the crumb dissolves into the sauce, while the mustard loses its raw volatile heat in minutes and leaves behind the toasted bitterness of the seed.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Braising","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","sweet-edged","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carbonnade%20Flamande%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carbonnade Flamande"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Belgian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Belgian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Bread","Flour","Vinegar","Wine","Mustard","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["braising","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"thuringer-rostbratwurst","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g pork shoulder, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g pork belly, rind off, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g veal shoulder, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g fine salt, 1.8 percent of the 1.4 kg of meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g dried marjoram, rubbed between the palms"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground caraway"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml iced water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 m hog casing, 26 mm to 28 mm, soaked and flushed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Spread the diced meat on a tray and freeze it 30 minutes, until the edges are stiff and the fat is hard to the fingernail. The grinder plate, blade and bowl go in with it.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grind everything through a 4.5 mm plate into the chilled bowl, then add the salt, marjoram, caraway, white pepper, nutmeg, garlic and iced water and work it for 3 minutes, until the mass turns sticky and a handful clings to an upturned palm.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry a small patty, taste it for salt and marjoram, and correct the whole batch before a single link is stuffed.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stuff the casing without stretching it, twist into 20 cm lengths, prick any air pockets, and rest the sausages uncovered in the fridge overnight so the salt completes the bind and the surface dries.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over medium coals for 12 minutes to 15 minutes, turning often, to an internal 71C (160F). The skin should blister and stay whole; a split link has been cooked too fast.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt at 1.8 percent does the binding, not the mixing. It dissolves myosin out of the muscle fibres into a sticky sol that sets on cooking and traps fat and water inside the link. That only happens cold. Above about 12C (54F) the fat smears into a paste that coats the protein and blocks the bind, and the sausage cooks out greasy, crumbling and hollow. Keep meat, bowl and blades below 4C (39F) throughout.","equipment":["Thermometer","Grill","Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","charred","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, char used as punctuation, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Th%C3%BCringer%20Rostbratwurst%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Thüringer Rostbratwurst"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Thüringer Rostbratwurst","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Th%C3%BCringer%20Rostbratwurst","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Oregano","Clove","Nutmeg","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"lancashire-hotpot","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg lamb middle neck chops, on the bone"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g lamb kidneys, cored and halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g onions, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 g floury potatoes, in 3 mm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml lamb or beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter, melted"},{"kind":"item","text":"pickled red cabbage, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Season the flour with the salt and pepper and turn the chops and kidneys in it, then layer them into a deep dish with the onions, thyme and bay, seasoning as you build.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Overlap the potato slices across the top in tight rows so no gaps show, pour the stock down the inside of the dish until it comes about halfway up the potato layer, and brush the top with the butter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover and bake at 160C (325F) for 2 hours, until a skewer slides through meat and onion without resistance.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Take the lid off, raise the oven to 200C (400F), and give it 30 minutes more, until the potato edges are brown and brittle. Rest 10 minutes before serving with the pickled cabbage.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":false}],"note":"Nothing is browned here and that is deliberate. Middle neck is fat, bone and collagen, and two hours of moist heat at 160C (325F) turns all three into a sauce that needs no fond to start it. The potato lid does two jobs at once: the slices below the waterline swell with stock and thicken it, the ones above stay dry enough to crisp when the lid comes off. Stop the stock halfway up the potatoes, because any higher and nothing browns.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lancashire%20Hotpot%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lancashire Hotpot"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Lancashire Hotpot","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Lancashire%20Hotpot","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Liver & Offal","Onion","Potato","Cabbage","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Flour","Stock/Broth","Pickles"],"judgedBy":["the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"osso-buco-alla-milanese","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 veal hind shin slices, 4 cm thick, about 1.4 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 celery stalk, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml veal or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, zest finely grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove, minced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Tie a length of string around the circumference of each shin slice, snug but not cutting in, then dry the slices, salt and pepper them, and dredge them in flour, knocking the loose dust off.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brown the slices in the oil until both cut faces carry a deep crust, lift them out, add the butter, and soften the onion, carrot and celery in the same fat until translucent and sweet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the wine and reduce until the pan smells of wine rather than alcohol, return the shins in one layer, add stock to come two thirds up them, and cover.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Braise at 150C (300F) for 2 hours, turning them once at the halfway mark, until the meat is loose around the bone but still standing inside its ring of string.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Mix the lemon zest, parsley and garlic and scatter this gremolata over the shins off the heat, seconds before they go to the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The string is structural, not decorative. As the shin's collagen becomes gelatine the muscle swells and slumps off the bone, and untied it arrives in the dish as rubble. Gremolata goes on last because lemon zest carries its smell in volatile oils that boil off in seconds; stirred into hot sauce it leaves only bitterness behind. Serve the bones upright: the marrow is the body of the sauce, and it will slide out of a tilted bone.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Braising","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The coat before the fry: dredge, crumb, batter","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","herb-fresh","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":4,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Osso%20Buco%20alla%20Milanese%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Osso Buco alla Milanese"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Osso Buco alla Milanese","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Osso%20Buco%20alla%20Milanese","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Celery","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","the-coat-before-the-fry-dredge-crumb-batter"]},{"slug":"porchetta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 kg pork belly, rind on, boned, in one rectangle"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g pork loin, trimmed to the length of the belly"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g fine salt, 1.5 percent of the 3.3 kg total"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g fennel seed, toasted and cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g black peppercorns, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 garlic cloves, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g rosemary leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g dried chilli flakes"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Score the rind in 1 cm lines with a blade held almost flat, cutting the skin and no deeper, then turn the piece over and score the meat side shallowly in a diamond so the seasoning has somewhere to go.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the salt into the meat side and wipe the rind bone dry, then spread the fennel, pepper, garlic, rosemary, chilli, lemon zest and oil across the meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the loin along one long edge, roll the belly tightly around it with the rind outside, and tie at 3 cm intervals. Sit it uncovered on a rack in the fridge for 24 hours, until the rind feels like parchment.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roast at 150C (300F) for 3 hours to 3.5 hours, until the centre of the loin reads 63C (145F) and a skewer slips through the belly wrapped around it.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":12600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the oven to 240C (475F) for 20 minutes to 30 minutes until the rind blisters and shatters under a knuckle, then rest the whole roll 20 minutes before cutting 2 cm slices.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Crackling is a drying problem before it is a heat problem. The water in the rind has to leave before the collagen under it can puff, and that is what the uncovered day in the fridge buys. Score only the skin, never into the fat, or the fat renders out through the cut and the rind boils instead of puffing. The rest matters as much as the roast, since juice driven toward the centre by heat needs 20 minutes to move back out into the slice.","equipment":["Parchment","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Scoring","Roasting in a hot oven"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":28,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Scoring","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=scoring%20bread%20dough%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Porchetta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Porchetta"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Porchetta","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Porchetta","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Garlic","Chiles","Lemon","Rosemary","Clove"],"judgedBy":["scoring","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"cotechino-con-lenticchie","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g pork rind, scraped clean of bristle"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g pork shoulder, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g pork back fat, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"31 g fine salt, 2.2 percent of the 1.4 kg of meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"3.5 g curing salt number 1, 0.25 percent of the meat, weighed on a scale reading to 0.01 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 m beef middle casing, 55 mm to 60 mm, soaked"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g Castelluccio or Puy lentils"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 carrot, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 celery stalk, in 5 mm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the rind 45 minutes, until a fingernail sinks in, cool it hard in the fridge, then grind it with the shoulder and back fat through a 4 mm plate into a chilled bowl.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Work in the salt, curing salt, pepper, cloves, nutmeg, cinnamon, garlic and wine for 2 minutes until the mass is tacky, stuff it into the beef casing with no air, tie both ends, and rest it in the fridge 24 hours.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Prick the sausage in six places, lower it into water held at 80C (175F), and poach 3 hours. The water must never boil.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion, carrot and celery in the oil, add the lentils, bay and water to cover by 3 cm, and simmer 25 minutes until tender but still whole, salting them only once they are soft.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the cotechino 10 minutes out of the water, cut it into 1 cm rounds, and lay them over the lentils with a spoonful of the poaching liquid.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cotechino is a lesson in collagen. Nearly a third of it is pork rind, which is almost pure collagen, and three hours at 80C (175F) turns that into gelatine: the reason a slice is sticky and yielding where a coarse salame is crumbly. Boil it and two things fail together, the casing splits and the rendered fat escapes, leaving dry meat inside and a greasy pot outside.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Keeping the cooking liquid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","earthy","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, an earthy, grounding base, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cotechino%20con%20Lenticchie%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cotechino con Lenticchie"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Cotechino con Lenticchie","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Cotechino%20con%20Lenticchie","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Celery","Bay leaf","Lentils","Wine","Cinnamon","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"bollito-misto-con-salsa-verde","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg beef brisket or cappello del prete"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 veal tongue, about 900 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 boiling hen, about 1.5 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 raw cotechino, about 500 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 carrots, whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 celery stalks"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g flat parsley leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g white bread crumb"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 anchovy fillets"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g capers, rinsed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 garlic clove"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 hard boiled egg yolk"},{"kind":"item","text":"180 ml olive oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring a large pot of water to a rolling boil with the carrots, celery, onions and salt, lower the brisket straight into it, and hold it at a bare simmer for 3 hours.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook the tongue in a second pot of boiling water for 2.5 hours and peel the skin away while it is still too hot to hold comfortably; once cold it will not come off at all.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the hen to the brisket pot for its last 2 hours, until the thigh reads 74C (165F) at the joint, and poach the cotechino in its own water at 80C (175F) for 3 hours so its fat does not cloud the broth.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Soak the bread crumb in the vinegar, then chop it together with the parsley, anchovy, capers, garlic and egg yolk and beat in the oil to a loose, spoonable green sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Carve everything across the grain onto a warm platter, ladle a little hot broth over to keep it wet, and pass the salsa verde.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"This is the mirror image of pot au feu. Meat lowered into boiling water coagulates at the surface at once, which slows the exchange with the water, so more flavour stays in the slice. That is what you want when the meat is the dish and the broth is the by-product. Tongue and cotechino get their own pots because one sheds skin and the other sheds fat. The salsa verde is the acid that boiled meat has none of.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","umami-deep","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":13,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bollito%20Misto%20con%20Salsa%20Verde%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bollito Misto con Salsa Verde"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Bollito Misto con Salsa Verde","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Bollito%20Misto%20con%20Salsa%20Verde","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Anchovy","Eggs","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Celery","Capers","Parsley","Bread","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Vinegar","Wine"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"carne-de-porco-a-alentejana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"900 g pork loin or leg, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g massa de pimentão, red pepper paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg live clams, the smallest you can find"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g waxy potatoes, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml sunflower oil, for frying"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g lard"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coriander leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the pork with the pepper paste, garlic, wine, bay and pepper and marinate it in the fridge for 12 hours, turning it once.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sit the clams in cold water salted to 3 percent for an hour so they spit their sand, and throw out any that stay open when tapped.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the diced potato in the sunflower oil at 170C (340F) in two batches, letting the oil come back to temperature between them, until golden, then drain it on paper and salt it while it is hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the pork out and pat it dry, keeping every drop of the marinade, then sear it hard in the olive oil and lard in two batches until browned and the centre reads 63C (145F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the marinade into the hot pan and let it bubble 2 minutes, tip in the clams, cover, and shake the pan until they gape, about 4 minutes. Return the pork and potatoes, throw in the coriander, and serve with lemon.","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Massa de pimentão is a cure as much as a flavour: roughly a tenth of it is salt, and twelve hours of it draws water out of the pork and then back in seasoned, so the cubes brown fast and stay juicy. The clams are the timer. Their adductor muscle gives up and the shell gapes near 70C (158F), and half a minute past that the meat is rubber, so they go in last and come off the heat as they open.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Marinating: acid, salt and time","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","bright","rich","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carne%20de%20Porco%20%C3%A0%20Alentejana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carne de Porco à Alentejana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Portuguese%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Portuguese kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Mussels/Clams","Garlic","Potato","Bell pepper","Lemon","Cilantro","Bay leaf","Wine","Clove"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"lomo-embuchado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg pork loin, in one piece, silverskin removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"41 g fine salt, 2.75 percent of the meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"3.7 g curing salt number 2, 0.25 percent of the meat, weighed on a scale reading to 0.01 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g sweet pimentón de la Vera"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g hot pimentón"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g dried oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 beef middle casing, 60 mm, soaked and flushed"}],"steps":[{"text":"Weigh the loin and write the number on a label; every decision from here is a percentage of it, and the curing salt is weighed on a scale that reads to 0.01 g, not a kitchen scale. Rub the salt and curing salt over every surface, seal it in a bag, and cure it at 3C (37F) for 10 days, turning it daily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the loin, dry it, and paste it all over with the pimentón, oregano, pepper, garlic and wine. Leave it another 24 hours in the fridge.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slide the loin into the soaked casing, press the air out from one end, tie it at 4 cm intervals with a loop to hang by, and prick any bubbles you can still see.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hang it in a purpose built curing chamber, or a fridge fitted with a temperature controller and a humidifier, at 12C to 15C (54F to 59F) and 75 percent to 80 percent humidity until it has lost 35 percent of the weight on the label, 8 weeks to 12 weeks, weighing it once a week. A soft white bloom is normal and wipes off with a cloth dipped in vinegar; black, green or fuzzy growth, an ammoniacal or sour smell, or a soft spongy patch means the loin goes in the bin. If the loss stalls short of 35 percent, the humidity is too high.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice it as thin as a coin across the grain and let the slices sit at room temperature for 10 minutes before eating, so the fat softens and releases its smell.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Everything here is arithmetic on the starting weight. Salt at 2.75 percent seasons and draws water; curing salt number 2 at 0.25 percent gives nitrite now and nitrate that converts slowly across months, which is what keeps botulism out of an airless interior. The 35 percent loss is the target because it drops water activity below 0.90. Dry it too fast and the outside sets into a shell that traps water in: that is case hardening, and it rots from the middle out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","bright","aromatic","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, acid holding everything up to the light, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lomo%20Embuchado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lomo Embuchado"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Omar Allibhoy on Lomo Embuchado","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Omar%20Allibhoy%20Lomo%20Embuchado","sub":"A YouTube search for Omar Allibhoy and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"lomo-embuchado","term":"Lomo Embuchado"},{"slug":"casings-molds-and-the-curing-chamber","term":"Casings, Molds & the Curing Chamber"}],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Garlic","Oregano","Wine","Paprika","Clove"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"pinnekjott","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 kg lamb rib racks, breast on the bone, in 2 slabs"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 peeled birch sticks, about 20 cm each"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water, for the pot"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g swede, peeled and in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g floury potatoes, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g grated nutmeg"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the racks all over with the coarse salt, pack them into a non metal tub with the rest of the salt between the layers, and leave them at 4C (39F) for 3 days, pouring off the brine that gathers each morning.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the salt off, pat the racks dry, and hang them in a cold, dry, airy place at 0C to 4C (32F to 39F) for 3 weeks, until they are stiff, dark and about a third lighter than they started. The surface must be dry to the touch within 48 hours; a sour or ammoniacal smell, or slime under the ribs, and the batch goes in the bin. Saw them down into single ribs.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":72000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soak the ribs in plenty of cold water in the fridge or a cold room below 5C (41F) for 30 hours, changing the water three times; boil a scrap in fresh water and taste it to judge when the salt has come back down to seasoning.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the birch sticks in a criss cross raft in the base of a heavy pot, pour the water in beneath them, pile the ribs on top, cover tightly and steam over low heat for 2.5 hours to 3 hours, adding hot water whenever the pot runs dry, until the meat slips off the bone.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Boil the swede and potatoes soft, mash them with the butter, pepper, nutmeg and a ladle of the salty pot liquid, and serve the ribs on top with the rest of that liquid poured over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt and time run forwards, then backwards. Three days in coarse salt and three weeks of cold dry air take about a third of the weight out of the ribs as water. The 30 hour soak reverses the gradient and draws salt back out, and you change the water because diffusion stalls once one bath reaches equilibrium with the meat. Steam rather than boil, or a month of concentrating flavour ends up in the pot.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Soaking dried goods back to life","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pinnekj%C3%B8tt%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pinnekjøtt"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Potato","Butter","Flour","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"rullepolse","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg pork belly, rind off, boned and butterflied flat"},{"kind":"item","text":"37 g fine salt, 2.5 percent of the meat"},{"kind":"item","text":"3.7 g curing salt number 1, 0.25 percent of the meat, weighed on a scale reading to 0.01 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground white pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, chopped as fine as you can"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Lay the belly flat, trim it into a neat rectangle, and rub the salt, curing salt, sugar, allspice and white pepper over both faces. Stack the trimmings back on top, cover, and cure it flat in the fridge for 3 days, turning it daily and pouring off the brine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Scatter the onion and parsley over the cured face, roll the rectangle tightly along its long side, and tie it every 3 cm, then run one length end to end so the spiral cannot telescope out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach it with the bay in water held at 80C (175F) until the centre reads at least 71C (160F) and a skewer meets no resistance, about 2 hours. Do not let the water boil.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the roll out, wrap it tightly, and cool it in cold water for 15 minutes to about 30C (86F) while the gelatine is still liquid, then press it under 3 kg of weight between two boards in the fridge overnight. Slice it as thin as you can manage and eat it on rye with mustard.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pressing is what turns a cooked roll into a cold cut you can slice. It goes under weight warm, near 30C (86F), while the gelatine between the layers is still liquid: the weight squeezes out loose fat and water and the gelatine sets in the gaps, gluing the spiral into one mass. Press it cold and the gelatine has already set where the gaps are, and every slice falls apart into ribbons. Three days at 2.5 percent salt firms the muscle first so the roll holds its shape.","equipment":["Rolling pin"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","warm-spiced","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rullep%C3%B8lse%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rullepølse"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Onion","Parsley","Bay leaf","Butter","Sugar","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"gespickte-rehkeule","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.8 kg venison haunch, boned, sinew stripped and tied"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g pork back fat, frozen 20 minutes, cut into 5 mm strips"},{"kind":"item","text":"16 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 allspice berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g coarsely ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g clarified butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g carrot, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g celeriac, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml game or brown veal stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g sour cream, at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g lingonberry preserve"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the haunch with the salt, juniper, allspice and pepper and leave it uncovered on a rack in the fridge overnight, until the surface is tacky rather than wet.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Thread the frozen fat strips through the muscle with a larding needle every 3 cm, following the grain, leaving 1 cm of fat standing proud at each end.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sear every face in the clarified butter until the crust is the colour of dark toast, add the carrot, celeriac, onion, bay and thyme, and roast at 140C (275F) until the centre reads 58C (136F), about 85 minutes.","durationSec":5100,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5100,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the meat under loose foil for 20 minutes while it climbs to 63C (145F), which is where a larded joint has to finish, and meanwhile deglaze the pan with the wine, reduce it to a glaze and add the stock.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Strain the sauce, whisk in the sour cream and lingonberry off the heat, and carve the haunch across the grain in 5 mm slices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A roe deer leg has next to no marbling, and barding, a sheet of fat tied over the top, only bastes the outside. Larding threads the fat along the grain where the muscle is driest, so it melts into the fibres as they contract. The price is that the needle carries surface bacteria to the centre, so this is no longer an intact cut and it goes to 63C (145F), not the 54C (129F) a seared loin may be served at. The overnight salt is a dry brine: the fibres hold water through that higher finish.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Searing: the hard crust","Roasting in a hot oven","Straining & passing through a sieve","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gespickte%20Rehkeule%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Gespickte Rehkeule"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Austrian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Austrian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Venison/Game","Onion","Carrot","Berries","Celeriac","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Cream","Sour cream/Crema","Wine","Stock/Broth","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["deglazing-and-pan-sauces","roasting-in-a-hot-oven"]},{"slug":"rehrucken-baden-baden","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"700 g venison loin, in 2 even pieces, silverskin removed"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g clarified butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g cold butter, in cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 firm pears, about 320 g, halved and cored"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip pared lemon zest"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g lingonberry preserve"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml game or brown veal stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 sprig rosemary"}],"steps":[{"text":"Slide a thin knife under the silverskin and strip it off in sheets, since it never renders and it bows the loin as it shrinks.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Poach the pear halves in the white wine with the sugar and lemon zest until a knife enters with slight resistance, about 12 minutes, then leave them to cool in their syrup.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Salt and pepper the loin, sear it hard in the clarified butter on every face, then roast at 160C (325F) until the centre reads 52C (126F), about 14 minutes.","durationSec":840,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":840,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the loin on a warm plate for 10 minutes while it climbs to 54C (129F), and meanwhile reduce the red wine with the juniper and rosemary to a glaze, add the stock and reduce by half.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain the sauce and swirl in the cold butter off the heat, fill the pear halves with the lingonberry, and carve the loin across the grain in 1.5 cm slices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Loin is the leanest muscle on the animal and the whole dish is a temperature problem. A 350 g piece gains only about 2C (4F) off the heat, so it leaves the oven at 52C (126F) and settles at 54C (129F); carryover scales with mass, and the haunch climbs further. Pink is safe here because the muscle is whole and unpierced and the sear kills what lives on its surface, while anything larded, rolled or minced carries that surface inward and goes to 63C (145F) or 71C (160F).","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Searing: the hard crust","Roasting in a hot oven","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rehr%C3%BCcken%20Baden-Baden%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rehrücken Baden-Baden"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: My German Recipes on Rehrücken Baden-Baden","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=My%20German%20Recipes%20Rehr%C3%BCcken%20Baden-Baden","sub":"A YouTube search for My German Recipes and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Venison/Game","Lemon","Berries","Rosemary","Butter","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain"]},{"slug":"jugged-hare","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 hare, about 2.5 kg, jointed into 8 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml hare's blood, reserved from the butcher"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g beef dripping"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g smoked streaky bacon, in 1 cm lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onion, half of it studded with 6 cloves, the rest sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml full bodied red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml beef or game stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml port"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip pared lemon zest"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g redcurrant jelly"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Stir the vinegar into the blood and refrigerate it, so it stays fluid instead of setting into a clot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Dry and salt the joints, dust them with the flour and brown them hard in the dripping with the bacon, in batches, until the pan floor is mahogany.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onion, wine, stock, port, juniper, bay, thyme and lemon zest, cover, and braise at 150C (300F) for 3 hours, until the meat slides from the bone, though with a young hare lift the saddle pieces out after 2 hours and return them at the end.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the meat, strain the liquor, skim the fat and reduce it to about 600 ml, then melt in the redcurrant jelly.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, slacken the blood with a ladle of the hot liquor, stir it back into the pan and hold it at 70C (158F) until it thickens and turns glossy, then return the hare.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Jugging meant the hare sealed in a tall stoneware jug standing in a pan of simmering water, and a covered pot at 150C (300F) stands in for it: gentle enveloping heat with nothing evaporating. Blood is a liaison like egg yolk. Its proteins set at 65C to 70C (149F to 158F) and pull the sauce into a dark gloss, then seize into grit above 80C (176F), which is why the pan comes off the heat first. The vinegar interferes with clotting so the blood pours rather than lumps.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Braising","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Keeping the cooking liquid"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","rich","smoky","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jugged%20Hare%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Jugged Hare"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Gordon Ramsay on Jugged Hare","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Gordon%20Ramsay%20Jugged%20Hare","sub":"A YouTube search for Gordon Ramsay and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Bacon","Onion","Lemon","Berries","Thyme","Bay leaf","Flour","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["braising","keeping-the-cooking-liquid"]},{"slug":"lapin-a-la-moutarde","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 rabbit, about 1.4 kg, jointed into 6 with the saddle cut in 2"},{"kind":"item","text":"90 g Dijon mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml sunflower oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g smoked bacon lardons"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g shallots, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g wholegrain mustard"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g tarragon leaves, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the joints and smear 50 g of the Dijon over every surface.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Colour them gently in the butter and oil with the lardons and shallots over medium heat, turning often, because mustard scorches in a fierce pan, then lift the two saddle pieces out onto a plate and leave the legs in the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the wine and stock, add the thyme and bay, cover and hold at a bare simmer for 35 minutes.","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2100,"estimated":false},{"text":"Return the saddle pieces and simmer 12 minutes more, until the loin reads 65C (149F) and the leg meat yields at the bone, then lift everything out.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":false},{"text":"Reduce the liquid by a third, take the pan off the heat, whisk in the cream, the remaining Dijon and the wholegrain mustard, then return the rabbit and scatter the tarragon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A rabbit is two different meats on one carcass. The hind legs are collagen rich and want 45 minutes at a bare simmer; the saddle is a fast lean muscle that is wrung out by 71C (160F), so it leaves the pan at 65C (149F), clear of the 63C (145F) whole cut standard and short of the ruin. Cook them together throughout and one of them is always wrong. Mustard's pungency is a volatile oil that boils off and leaves bitterness, so half goes in early for body and half off the heat for the edge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","herb-fresh","aromatic","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, fresh herbs arguing happily on top, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Lapin%20%C3%A0%20la%20Moutarde%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Lapin à la Moutarde"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Lapin à la Moutarde","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Lapin%20%C3%A0%20la%20Moutarde","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Bacon","Shallot","Thyme","Bay leaf","Tarragon","Butter","Cream","Wine","Stock/Broth","Mustard"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"faisan-aux-choux","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 pheasant, about 1.1 kg, trussed"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 Savoy cabbage, about 900 g, cored and cut into 8 wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g smoked streaky bacon, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 Morteau or other coarse smoked pork sausage, about 200 g"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g carrot, halved lengthways"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g onion, studded with 3 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g duck fat"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml chicken or game stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt and pepper the bird inside and out and leave it uncovered in the fridge overnight.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the cabbage wedges 5 minutes in salted water, drain them and press the water out, which takes the sulphurous edge off a vegetable that is about to cook for an hour.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Brown the pheasant in the duck fat, breast first, lift it out, and bed the pot with the cabbage, bacon, sausage, carrot, clove onion, juniper, bay and thyme.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sit the bird breast down on the cabbage, pour in the wine and stock, cover, and cook at 150C (300F) for 1 hour 15 minutes, until the thigh reads 74C (165F).","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":900,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest the bird 15 minutes, reduce the pot juices and swirl in the butter, then carve the legs and breasts back onto the cabbage.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Pheasant breast runs 1% to 2% fat and its skin has little to give, so a dry oven strips it in minutes. Breast down in a covered pot, the thin muscle sits in 100C (212F) steam under the fat rendering out of the bacon while the legs, which need time as well as 74C (165F), catch up. The overnight salt is a dry brine: the muscle holds water it would otherwise shed. An older bird, one with a rigid breastbone tip, belongs in this pot and not in a roasting tin.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Trussing","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["smoky","rich","warm-spiced","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with wood-smoke running underneath, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":41,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Choux pastry","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=choux%20pastry%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Trussing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20truss%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Faisan%20aux%20Choux%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Faisan aux Choux"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Faisan aux Choux","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Faisan%20aux%20Choux","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Pork","Bacon","Sausage","Duck","Venison/Game","Onion","Carrot","Cabbage","Berries","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Wine","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"coniglio-alla-ligure","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 rabbit, about 1.4 kg, jointed into 8"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml extra virgin olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sprigs rosemary"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml Vermentino or other dry white wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 ml light chicken stock, kept hot"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g Taggiasca olives in brine, stones in"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g pine nuts"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the joints and brown them in the olive oil with the garlic, rosemary and bay in a wide uncovered pan until they are gold and the pan smells of resin rather than scorch.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the wine and let it boil away completely, turning the joints, until the pan is dry again and the meat is frying in oil rather than sitting in liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the pepper and a ladle of the hot stock and cook uncovered, turning the joints and adding another ladle each time the pan runs dry, for about 45 minutes, until the hind leg meat gives at the bone; loosen with a splash of hot water if the stock is gone before it does.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the olives and pine nuts for the last 10 minutes, leaving the stones in so the olives hold their shape, and tell the table the stones are there.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Let the last of the liquid go so the joints fry once more in the oil, then take the pan off the heat and swirl it until the oil and pan juices cling to the pieces.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing thickens this sauce. The stock goes in a ladle at a time and is allowed to cook away, so the joints spend their last minutes frying in oil again and what clings to them is a reduction of oil and pan juices. Olives put in early leak their brine into the pan and give their fruit to nothing, so they arrive in the last ten minutes with the stones still in, which holds them whole against the heat. The pine nuts want that same short heat, long enough to toast and not to burn.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","floral","aromatic","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, a floral thread lifting the aroma, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":20,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Coniglio%20alla%20Ligure%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Coniglio alla Ligure"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Coniglio alla Ligure","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Coniglio%20alla%20Ligure","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Garlic","Olives","Rosemary","Bay leaf","Wine","Stock/Broth","Nuts","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"pappardelle-al-ragu-di-cinghiale","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg wild boar shoulder, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"750 ml Chianti or other dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g pancetta, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g carrot, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g celery, finely diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 sprigs rosemary"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g tinned peeled tomatoes, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml beef or game stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pappardelle"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g Parmigiano Reggiano, grated"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the boar overnight IN THE FRIDGE in the wine with the juniper, bay and peppercorns, then drain it, keep 300 ml of the marinade and dry the meat on cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Render the pancetta in the olive oil and brown the boar hard in three batches, so the pan stays dry enough to colour rather than steam.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the onion, carrot and celery in the same fat until sweet and just catching, then return the meat with the reserved marinade and boil off the raw alcohol.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tomatoes, stock, rosemary and salt and hold at 90C to 95C (194F to 203F), partly covered, for 3 hours, until the dice collapse under a spoon.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Boil the pappardelle a minute short of al dente, drag it into the ragù with a ladle of its water, toss until the sauce clings, and finish with the Parmigiano.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Wild boar shoulder runs 4% to 6% fat where farmed pork runs 15% to 20%, so the pancetta and the oil are structure and not seasoning. The marinade does not tenderise: it seasons the surface, tints the meat and carries the juniper and bay a few millimetres in. Three hours at 90C to 95C (194F to 203F) does that work, converting collagen to gelatine, which is what makes the sauce cling to a wide noodle. A hard boil tightens the fibres instead. Boar carries trichinella, so it must pass 71C (160F).","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Rendering fat: and cooking in what runs out","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Marinating: acid, salt and time","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["gently hot","umami-deep","floral","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with a warm current of chile, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pappardelle%20al%20Rag%C3%B9%20di%20Cinghiale%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pappardelle al Ragù di Cinghiale"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Italia Squisita on Pappardelle al Ragù di Cinghiale","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Italia%20Squisita%20Pappardelle%20al%20Rag%C3%B9%20di%20Cinghiale","sub":"A YouTube search for Italia Squisita and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Venison/Game","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Berries","Rosemary","Bay leaf","Parmesan","Pasta","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"pasztet-z-dziczyzny","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"600 g venison or wild boar shoulder, in 4 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g pork belly, skinned, in 4 cm chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g pig's liver, in 2 cm slices"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g carrot, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g celeriac, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 juniper berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1200 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g stale white bread roll, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"14 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground allspice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g grated nutmeg"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g smoked streaky bacon, in rashers"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the venison, pork belly, onion, carrot, celeriac, juniper, allspice berries and bay in the water, covered, for 2 hours, until the meat gives to a fork, topping up with boiling water whenever the level drops below the meat.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Slip the liver in for the last 10 minutes only, then lift everything out, skim the fat off the broth and keep both, and soak the torn roll in a ladle of the hot broth.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mince the meat, vegetables, skimmed fat and soaked bread twice through a 3 mm plate, then beat in the eggs, salt, pepper, ground allspice and nutmeg until the mixture is sticky, loosening it with a little broth if it is stiff.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a 1.5 kg loaf tin of about 2 litres with the bacon, press the mixture in without air pockets, fold the bacon over and bake at 150C to 160C (300F to 325F) in a water bath until the centre reads 71C (160F), 1 hour 15 minutes to 1 hour 45 minutes.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":6300,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool it completely in the tin and chill it overnight before slicing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true}],"note":"A pâté much under a fifth fat by weight crumbles on the knife and tastes chalky, which is why half a kilo of belly goes in and why the fat that renders into the broth is skimmed and minced back rather than poured away. Liver is the emulsifier, being rich in phospholipid, but it turns metallic and floury when cooked hard, hence the last ten minutes only. Egg and soaked bread bind the free water. This is minced meat, so the centre goes to 71C (160F), and it slices only once cold.","equipment":["Bain-marie","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["The water bath","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","earthy","smoky"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, on a drift of smoke."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: The water bath","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=bain%20marie%20water%20bath%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pasztet%20z%20Dziczyzny%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pasztet z Dziczyzny"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Polish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Polish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Pork","Bacon","Eggs","Venison/Game","Liver & Offal","Onion","Carrot","Berries","Celeriac","Bay leaf","Bread","Nutmeg","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["the-water-bath","skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth"]},{"slug":"summan-mashwi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 quail, about 180 g each"},{"kind":"item","text":"1500 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"75 g fine salt, for the brine"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed to a paste with 4 g salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g baharat"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml pomegranate molasses"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g sumac"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cut out each backbone with shears and press the breastbone flat, so the bird cooks in one plane.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Brine the quail 30 minutes in the water and salt, then dry the skin hard with cloth.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rub them with the olive oil, garlic paste, baharat, lemon juice and pepper and leave them 30 minutes.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":1800,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Grill over hot charcoal, skin side down first, 3 minutes to 4 minutes a side, brushing on the pomegranate molasses only in the last minute, until the thigh reads 74C (165F).","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 5 minutes, then dust with the sumac and parsley and serve with the lemon wedges.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":false}],"note":"A quail weighs 180 g and its breast is 15 mm thick, so it crosses from raw to ruined inside a minute. Two things buy margin: flattening the bird so every part meets the fire at once, and the 5% brine, which lets the muscle hold roughly a tenth more water through the heat. Pomegranate molasses is close to half sugar and turns to black carbon in ninety seconds over coals, so it is a glaze for the final minute, never a marinade. Thigh at 74C (165F).","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Brining & curing","Grilling over live coals"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","sweet-edged","warm-spiced","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, a sweet edge rounding the corners, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Summan%20Mashwi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Summan Mashwi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Venison/Game","Garlic","Lemon","Pomegranate","Parsley","Sugar","Clove","Sumac/Za'atar"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"hamam-mahshi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 pigeons, about 350 g each, cleaned"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g freekeh, rinsed and drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g samna or clarified butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1800 ml chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, halved, for the broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lemon, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the pigeons inside and out with 7 g of the salt an hour ahead and leave them in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the chopped onion in 60 g of the samna until golden, stir in the freekeh, the ground spices and the remaining 3 g salt, toast until the pan smells of green wheat smoke, then add 125 ml of the stock and cook 12 minutes, so the grain is half done and still chalky at the centre, and spread it out to cool.","durationSec":720,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":720,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fill each bird two thirds full, since the freekeh keeps swelling, close the vent with a cocktail stick and tie the legs together.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lower the pigeons into the remaining stock with the halved onion, cardamom and bay, in a pot narrow enough that they sit just covered, and poach at 85C (185F), covered, for 35 minutes, until both the thigh and the stuffing read 74C (165F).","durationSec":2100,"handsOnSec":2100,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift them out, dry them, brush with the last of the samna and roast at 220C (425F) for 8 minutes, until the skin blisters and browns, then serve with the lemon wedges.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":480,"estimated":true}],"note":"A stuffed bird is two cooking problems sharing one clock, and the stuffing is always the coldest point. So the freekeh goes in hot and half cooked, and the cavity is only two thirds filled because the grain is still swelling. The gentle 85C (185F) poach is the other half of it: at a rolling boil the outer muscle contracts and wrings itself dry long before the centre reaches 74C (165F). The oven at the end is for colour and crackle, nothing else.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright","smoky","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":9,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Hamam%20Mahshi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Hamam Mahshi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Egyptian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Egyptian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Lemon","Bay leaf","Butter","Couscous/Bulgur","Stock/Broth","Cumin","Coriander seed","Cinnamon","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"leng-chi-tu-cold-eaten-rabbit","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 rabbit, about 1.2 kg, chopped through the bone into 2 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml Shaoxing wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml light soy sauce"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml rapeseed oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g Pixian doubanjiang"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dried erjingtiao chillies, snipped, seeds shaken out"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g Sichuan pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g white sesame seeds, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 spring onions, sliced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Marinate the rabbit 20 minutes in the Shaoxing wine, soy and salt, then drain the pieces and pat them dry.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry them in two batches in a wok, where the 400 ml of oil gives real depth, at 180C (350F) for about 6 minutes a batch, until the loud bubbling quietens and the surfaces are firm and dry, lifting each batch out and bringing the oil back to 180C (350F) before the next.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":360,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Pour off all but 60 ml of the oil, drop the heat low and fry the ginger, garlic and doubanjiang until the oil runs red.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the chillies and Sichuan pepper and stir 30 seconds only, until fragrant and still bright, then return the rabbit with the sugar and toss 5 minutes until every piece is glossy.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":540,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread it out so it falls to room temperature within 2 hours, fold through the sesame seeds and spring onions, and if it is not eaten the same day cover and refrigerate it, where it keeps 4 days under its oil, then bring it back to cool room temperature to serve.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":43200,"estimated":true}],"note":"The first fry is dehydration, not colour. Rabbit is lean enough that a wet stew shreds it into string, so hot oil drives the surface water off and firms the protein, and the meat then finishes in fat rather than in liquid. Dried chillies scorch in about forty seconds at frying heat and burnt capsaicin is simply bitter, so the heat comes down before they go in. Sichuan pepper's tingle is volatile and fat soluble, which is why it is last in and why the dish is eaten cool.","equipment":["Wok"],"techniques":["Wok technique","Tadka: blooming spices in fat","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","fiery","umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, finishing long and savory."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Wok technique","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n10xBmqehik","sub":"★ A canon film, verified"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Leng%20Chi%20Tu%2C%20Cold-Eaten%20Rabbit%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Leng Chi Tu, Cold-Eaten Rabbit"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sichuan%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Sichuan kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Scallion","Chiles","Soy sauce","Sesame","Wine","Sugar","Clove","Five-spice","Sichuan peppercorn"],"judgedBy":["tadka-blooming-spices-in-fat","wok-technique"]},{"slug":"sauce-grand-veneur","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g venison bones and trimmings, chopped small"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g clarified butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g carrot, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g onion, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g celery, in 1 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 ml brown veal or game stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 juniper berries, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 sprigs thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g black peppercorns, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g redcurrant jelly"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 ml double cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g cold butter, in cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Brown the bones and trimmings in the clarified butter until the fond on the pan floor is mahogany, and stop it there.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the carrot, onion and celery, colour them, then pour in the vinegar and reduce it for about 3 minutes, until the pan is nearly dry and glazed and the smell has lost its sting.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the wine, reduce by two thirds, then add the stock, juniper, bay and thyme and hold at 90C (194F) for 45 minutes, skimming the grey foam.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Throw in the crushed peppercorns for the final 10 minutes only, then strain through a fine sieve, pressing the solids lightly.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the redcurrant jelly and the cream, salt it, and swirl in the cold butter off the heat, keeping the pan below 90C (194F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The vinegar reduction is what separates a poivrade from a plain red wine sauce. Boiled down to a glaze, the harshness leaves with the steam, since acetic acid is volatile, and what stays is fruit and a low pH that keeps iron rich meat tasting bright rather than livery. Peppercorns crack in for the last ten minutes because piperine leaches fast and a long boil turns it to dust and bitterness. The jelly rounds the acid and its pectin adds body.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","gently hot","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a warm current of chile, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":1,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Sauce%20Grand%20Veneur%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Sauce Grand Veneur"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Jacques Pépin on Sauce Grand Veneur","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Jacques%20P%C3%A9pin%20Sauce%20Grand%20Veneur","sub":"A YouTube search for Jacques Pépin and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Venison/Game","Onion","Carrot","Celery","Berries","Thyme","Bay leaf","Butter","Cream","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"mansaf","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.6 kg lamb shoulder on the bone, in 8 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g jameed (dried fermented sheep's yoghurt), broken up"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres cold water, for soaking the jameed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 short cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods, cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g long-grain rice, rinsed until the water runs clear"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 shrak or markook flatbreads"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g blanched almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g pine nuts"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g flat-leaf parsley, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the jameed with the cold water and leave it in the fridge overnight, until it gives like wet clay; blend it and pass it through a sieve to a thin, pourable, chalk-white liquid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cover the lamb with cold water, bring it to a bare tremble and skim until the surface stays clear, then add the onion, cinnamon and cardamom and hold it just under a simmer for about 2 hours, until a piece drops off the bone under its own weight.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift out the lamb and strain the broth; cook the rice in 700 ml of that broth with 20 g of the ghee and the turmeric, lid on, until the grains stand separate and the pan smells toasted.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the jameed into a wide pan and bring it up over low heat, stirring in one direction and never stopping, until it thickens a little and the raw chalk smell goes; slide in the lamb with the pepper, hold it there for 20 minutes, then taste and stir in the salt only if it needs it.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the almonds and pine nuts in the rest of the ghee to the colour of straw; lay the bread on a wide tray and ladle over enough hot jameed to soak it soft, then spread the rice, set the lamb on top, pour over the rest of the sauce and scatter the nuts and parsley.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Yoghurt breaks because heat makes acid-loosened casein clump and the whey weep out. Jameed is sheep's yoghurt salted and sun dried, so its protein is concentrated and more forgiving, but it still wants a slow climb and unbroken stirring to keep the clumps too small to see. Walk away, or let it boil, and you get grit in a thin grey liquid that will not come back. Its salt and acid would carry a counter soak, but the fridge costs nothing. Salt at the very end: jameed brings plenty of its own.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Fermentation","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","nutty-toasted","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Fermentation","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=fermentation%20basics%20vegetables%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mansaf%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mansaf"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Levantine%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Levantine kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Parsley","Yogurt","Rice","Nuts","Turmeric","Cinnamon","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["fermentation","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"birria-de-chivo","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg goat shoulder and rib on the bone, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 guajillo chillies, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 ancho chillies, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 chiles de árbol"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 white onion, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g Mexican oregano"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cumin seed"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 short stick Mexican cinnamon (canela)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 dried avocado leaves, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g masa harina, worked with water to a stiff rope, for sealing"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the goat all over with the salt, cover it and leave it in the fridge overnight so the salt travels to the bone.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toast the chillies on a dry comal, pressing them flat, until they smell of raisins and tobacco and go matte; pull them off before any wisp of acrid smoke, then soak them in hot water until limp.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Blend the chillies with the garlic, onion, vinegar, oregano, cumin, cloves and cinnamon until smooth, push the adobo through a sieve and coat the goat in it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set a rack in a heavy pot, pour the water underneath, lay the goat and avocado leaves on the rack, put the lid on and press the masa rope around the rim to seal it shut.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook at 150C (300F) for 4 hours, then break the seal: the shoulder blade should slide out clean. Shred the meat and skim the fat off the consommé below.","durationSec":14400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":14400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Goat carries far less fat inside the muscle than lamb, so it dries out long before a working shoulder gives way. The masa seal is the answer. It traps steam and holds the meat in saturated air near 95C (203F), so the pot puts back moisture as fast as the meat loses it, and four hours in air like that is not padding. Take the chillies off the comal while they still bend; scorch is bitter and never cooks out.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Working with masa","Tortillas on the comal","Straining & passing through a sieve","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright","earthy","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Working with masa","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tamales%20masa%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Birria%20de%20Chivo%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Birria de Chivo"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Birria de Chivo","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Birria%20de%20Chivo","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Corn","Avocado","Oregano","Cornmeal/Polenta","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["working-with-masa","tortillas-on-the-comal"]},{"slug":"barbacoa-de-borrego","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 kg lamb shoulder and leg on the bone, in 4 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 maguey leaves (pencas), spines trimmed, or 4 banana leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 avocado leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g dried chickpeas, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g long-grain rice, for body in the consommé; it dissolves over the cook and does not stay as grains"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 white onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 guajillo chillies, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g dried mint"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt, for the broth"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 litres water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Hold the maguey leaves over a flame or lay them on a hot comal until they darken, weep and go bendy; a raw penca cracks along its fibres and will not wrap.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the lamb with the 25 g of salt and leave it uncovered in the fridge overnight, so the surface dries and the salt goes in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the chickpeas, rice, onion, guajillos, garlic, mint, broth salt and water in a deep roasting tin and set a rack over it, high enough that nothing touches the liquid; over five hours the rice will blow apart completely and thicken the broth, which is the job it is there to do.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line the rack with the maguey, lay the lamb and avocado leaves on it, fold the leaves over the meat and seal the whole tin tightly with foil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook at 150C (300F) for 5 hours, until the meat slides off the bone; lift out the lamb, then skim and season the consommé that has caught every drop below.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":true}],"note":"The pot underneath is the point. Everything the lamb sheds, fat, gelatin and salt, lands in the chickpeas and rice and becomes the consommé, so nothing is lost to the oven. The leaf earns its place too: roasting a penca collapses its fibres and frees a sweet, faintly grassy sap that perfumes the fat. Use a yearling or older animal. Milk-fed lamb has too little connective tissue to stand five hours and shreds into dry floss.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Tortillas on the comal","Soaking dried goods back to life"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","aromatic","gently hot","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, aromatics blooming from the first bite, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":21,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Tortillas on the comal","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20tortillas%20comal%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Barbacoa%20de%20Borrego%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Barbacoa de Borrego"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Barbacoa de Borrego","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Barbacoa%20de%20Borrego","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Avocado","Banana","Mint","Rice","Chickpeas","Stock/Broth","Clove"],"judgedBy":["tortillas-on-the-comal","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"arni-giouvetsi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"4 lamb shanks, about 1.6 kg"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml olive oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 large onion, finely chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g tinned chopped tomatoes"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g tomato paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 short cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 allspice berries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 bay leaf"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml light lamb or chicken stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"350 g kritharaki (orzo)"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g kefalotyri, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper, ground"}],"steps":[{"text":"Salt the shanks and brown them hard in the oil in a wide casserole, until the surface is the colour of dark toast and the pan smells nutty rather than scorched.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift them out, soften the onion and garlic in the same fat, stir in the tomato paste and cook it until it turns from red to brick, then deglaze with the wine.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the shanks with the tomatoes, cinnamon, allspice, bay, pepper and 600 ml of the stock, cover, and braise at 160C (320F) for about 2 hours 30 minutes, until a fork turns in the meat with no grip.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Raise the oven to 180C (350F). Lift the shanks out, stir the raw orzo and the last 300 ml of stock, boiling hot, into the sauce, bring the pot back to a simmer on the hob, sit the shanks on top and return it uncovered to the oven for 15 minutes to 18 minutes.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull it while the orzo still has bite and the sauce looks too loose, scatter the kefalotyri and let it stand 10 minutes; it tightens to exactly right as it rests.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Orzo is pasta: it drinks about twice its weight in liquid and gives back starch. Add it early and you get paste; add it to a pot that has dropped off the boil and the outside gums before the middle hydrates, which is why the stock goes in hot and the simmer is re-established on the hob. Take the dish out looking wet, because the grains keep absorbing off the heat. Gelatin from the shanks plus that starch is what makes a giouvetsi cling to the spoon instead of pooling under it.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Deglazing & pan sauces","Braising","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","umami-deep","aromatic","briny"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes, ending saline and clean."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Deglazing & pan sauces","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pan%20sauce%20deglazing%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Arni%20Giouvetsi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Arni Giouvetsi"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Akis Petretzikis on Arni Giouvetsi","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Akis%20Petretzikis%20Arni%20Giouvetsi","sub":"A YouTube search for Akis Petretzikis and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Tomato","Berries","Bay leaf","Pasta","Wine","Stock/Broth","Tomato paste","Cinnamon","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["braising","deglazing-and-pan-sauces"]},{"slug":"kuzu-kapama","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg shoulder of spring lamb, in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g spring onions, in 4 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 heads romaine lettuce, leaves separated"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g dill, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat-leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g podded peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g black pepper, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml lemon juice"}],"steps":[{"text":"Melt the butter in a wide pan over low heat and turn the lamb in it for a few minutes without letting it take any colour; you are warming the fat, not browning the meat.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay half the lettuce over the lamb, then the spring onions, peas, dill and parsley, and finish with the rest of the lettuce so the pot is packed tight to the lid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the water, salt, pepper and sugar, cover, and cook over the lowest heat without stirring for 1 hour 15 minutes to 1 hour 45 minutes, until the lettuce has collapsed into a green broth.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":6300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Test a piece of lamb: it should give to a spoon but still hold its shape. If it resists, put the lid back and keep going in 15 minute stretches; the lettuce means it cannot dry out.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the lemon juice off the heat and let it settle for 10 minutes before serving.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false}],"note":"This is the one lamb dish here with no browning at all, and that is the lesson. A spring animal under six months has mild fat and little connective tissue, so Maillard would bury the thing you came for. The lettuce is the liquid: it is around 95 percent water and lets it go slowly, so the pot bastes itself and cannot boil dry. Add the lemon off the heat, because acid held at a simmer strips the chlorophyll and the greens go the colour of old khaki.","equipment":[],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","rich","bright"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, richness that coats and comforts, with a clean, bright snap."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kuzu%20Kapama%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kuzu Kapama"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Refika’s Kitchen on Kuzu Kapama","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Refika%E2%80%99s%20Kitchen%20Kuzu%20Kapama","sub":"A YouTube search for Refika’s Kitchen and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"peas-favas-and-the-green-spring","term":"Peas, Favas & the Green Spring"}],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Scallion","Peas","Lettuce/Greens","Lemon","Parsley","Dill","Butter","Sugar"]},{"slug":"khorkhog","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg mutton on the bone (leg and rib), in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g fat-tail or kidney fat, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 dense, dry basalt or granite stones, about fist sized"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 onions, quartered"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 g waxy potatoes, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 carrots, in thick lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 head of garlic, halved across"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"18 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper, ground"}],"steps":[{"text":"Build a hard wood fire and bury the stones in the embers for at least 45 minutes, until they glow dull red; use dense dry stones only, because porous or damp ones burst.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":2700,"estimated":true},{"text":"Season the mutton and pack a heavy lidded metal pot in layers: a stone, then meat, fat and vegetables, then another stone, keeping the stones off the pot wall.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the water and set the lid on loosely enough that steam can escape freely; a clamped vessel full of steam and glowing stone is a bomb.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Set the pot on the coals for 1 hour 30 minutes to 2 hours 30 minutes depending on the age of the animal, turning it now and then, until the meat slips off the rib and the water has gone dark and fatty; look in at the hour and add a cup of boiling water if the pot has gone quiet.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the stones out with tongs onto a metal tray and leave them there until they are warm rather than searing. A stone straight from the pot is at boiling point and greasy and will burn skin in seconds, so hand them round only once they have cooled, and in a folded cloth.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Khorkhog is Mongolian, cooked out on the steppe in a milk churn or a lidded pot set in the fire. Mutton is a sheep over two years old, its collagen cross linked with age, so it wants both real heat and real time, and its fat carries branched chain fatty acids that read as gamey to anyone raised on lamb. Do not trim it: that fat is the seasoning here, and there is little else. The stones heat the load from the inside as well as the outside. Vent the lid, always.","equipment":["Grill","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust","Grilling over live coals","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["briny","charred","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with a briny, maritime salinity, char used as punctuation, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Churning ice cream","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=ice%20cream%20churning%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Khorkhog%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Khorkhog"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Caucasus%20%26%20Central%20Asian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Caucasus & Central Asian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Carrot"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"rogan-josh","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg lamb shoulder on the bone, in 4 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 ml mustard oil, food grade (kachi ghani). Not the mustard oil sold for massage and marked for external use only"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g asafoetida"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 black cardamom pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 short cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g Kashmiri chilli powder; this quantity assumes true Kashmiri, mild and mostly pigment, so taste yours and start at 15 g if it bites"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground fennel"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g ground dry ginger (sonth)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g full-fat yoghurt, whisked smooth"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g dried cockscomb flowers (mawal), steeped in 60 ml hot water, for colour"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the mustard oil until it stops smelling raw and the first smoke rises, then let it cool a little; unheated mustard oil is harsh and this is the only way to soften it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop in the asafoetida, cloves, both cardamoms, cinnamon and bay, and the moment they swell add the lamb, browning it in the spiced oil until the pieces are coloured all over and the fat around them runs clear and smells of the spices.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat, stir the Kashmiri chilli, fennel and dry ginger into the fat for 20 seconds so they bloom without catching and the fat turns red, then whisk in the yoghurt a spoonful at a time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the water and salt, cover, and hold at a bare simmer for about 1 hour 30 minutes, until the meat is soft and a slick of red oil has risen and stayed.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Strain in the mawal water, stir through the garam masala and simmer uncovered for 5 minutes.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"The Kashmiri Pandit version builds its savour on asafoetida, which is why the resin hits hot fat first: heat turns its sulphur compounds from raw and sharp to something deep and rounded. The red is not heat. Kashmiri chilli is mild and heavy with pigment, and mawal, dried cockscomb, carries the rest as a water steeping. The old dye was ratan jot, left out here because alkanet root is hepatotoxic and permitted as food colour in neither the EU nor the US. Yoghurt off the heat, as in the mansaf.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","aromatic","smoky","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, aromatics blooming from the first bite, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":22,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rogan%20Josh%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Rogan Josh"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Rogan Josh","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Rogan%20Josh","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Ginger","Chiles","Bay leaf","Yogurt","Mustard","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"laal-maas","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg goat on the bone, in 4 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 Mathania or Kashmiri dried red chillies, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g garlic, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g full-fat yoghurt, whisked smooth"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 green cardamom pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 black cardamom pods"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 short cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml hot water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 piece of lump hardwood charcoal, for the dhungar, never a briquette or self-lighting fuel"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 ml ghee, for the dhungar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the chillies in hot water for 20 minutes, then grind them with the garlic and a little of the soaking water to a smooth brick-red paste.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false},{"text":"Fry the whole spices in the ghee until they crackle, add the onions and cook them only to soft and pale gold, not brown, then add the goat and sear it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pan off the heat and stir in the yoghurt in three additions, then the chilli paste, coriander and salt, and set it back over a low flame.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the hot water, cover and simmer for about 1 hour 45 minutes, until the meat is tender and a wide slick of red ghee stands clear on the surface.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":6300,"estimated":false},{"text":"Burn the lump charcoal until it is fully ashed over and glowing, never a briquette or self-lighting fuel, which puts fuel residue straight into the food. Open a window or run the extractor, sit a small steel bowl in the pot, drop the coal in, pour the 10 ml of ghee over it, clamp the lid down for 4 minutes, then take the bowl out.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Rajasthani, out of the Mewar hunting kitchens. The colour is chilli skin alone: Mathania chillies are mostly pigment and moderate heat, and twenty five of them carry it. The onions are held pale so they melt into the sauce instead of colouring it, and the onion and the chilli paste are all the body this has; no flour or nut goes in. The dhungar is a fat trick. Ghee hitting a live coal throws aromatic smoke that dissolves only into fat. Four minutes is plenty; longer tastes of ashtray.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Searing: the hard crust"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","warm-spiced","smoky","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, warm spice humming through the middle, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":34,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Laal%20Maas%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Laal Maas"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Laal Maas","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Laal%20Maas","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Chiles","Yogurt","Coriander seed","Cinnamon","Cardamom","Clove"],"judgedBy":["searing-the-hard-crust"]},{"slug":"bakri-ke-paye","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"8 goat trotters, hair singed off, split at the joint"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ghee"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ginger paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g garlic paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g Kashmiri chilli powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g garam masala"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g full-fat yoghurt, whisked smooth"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 litres water, plus more boiling water for topping up"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g plain flour, slaked in 100 ml cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, in fine matchsticks"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g coriander leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 limes, in wedges"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scrub the trotters in a filled basin rather than under a running tap, with a stiff brush, scraping off every trace of singed hair and hoof grime, then blanch them 10 minutes in boiling water and rinse; skip this and the broth tastes of the yard.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onions in the ghee until deep brown, stir in the ginger, garlic and ground spices, then take the pan off the heat and beat in the yoghurt a spoonful at a time.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the trotters, salt and water, bring to a bare tremble and cook with the lid ajar for 6 hours to 7 hours, skimming through the first hour. Keep the trotters covered the whole way, topping up with boiling water, never cold, as the level falls; expect to add a litre or more before the skin turns translucent and slides off the bone.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":25200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk in the flour slurry and simmer 15 minutes; the broth stays thin while hot, which is correct, and sets when cold.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir through the garam masala and serve with the ginger matchsticks, coriander and lime.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"A trotter is skin, tendon and bone with almost no muscle, so there is nothing to overcook and everything to convert. Collagen unwinds into gelatin above roughly 70C (158F), but the reaction is slow and tendon this dense wants six hours or more; that clock sits behind every long braise here. Boil it hard instead and the fat emulsifies into a cloudy grey broth that never clears. Judge it cold: a spoonful that sets to a wobble in the fridge is done. The flour is insurance, not the thickener.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Blanching & shocking","Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","rich","bright","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Braising","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=braising%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bakri%20ke%20Paye%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bakri ke Paye"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Ranveer Brar on Bakri ke Paye","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ranveer%20Brar%20Bakri%20ke%20Paye","sub":"A YouTube search for Ranveer Brar and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Lime","Cilantro","Yogurt","Flour","Coriander seed","Turmeric","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["skimming-foam-fat-and-a-clear-broth","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"goat-water","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg goat on the bone (neck, shoulder and shank), in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml coconut oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 onions, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g thyme sprigs"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 allspice berries (pimento), crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 Scotch bonnet, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 spring onions, bruised"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g breadfruit, peeled and in wedges"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g tomato ketchup"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml dark rum"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour, slaked in 100 ml cold water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil and melt the sugar in it until it foams and goes the colour of dark treacle, then tip in all the goat at once and turn it to coat; the cold meat drops the pan temperature and stops the caramel exactly where you want it, which browning in batches cannot do.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the onions, garlic, thyme, cloves, allspice, spring onions and the whole Scotch bonnet, then the water and salt.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer with the lid ajar for about 2 hours 30 minutes, fishing out the chilli as soon as the broth smells of it, until the neck meat parts from the bone.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the breadfruit, cook 20 minutes more, then whisk in the flour slurry and simmer 10 minutes, until the broth is glossy and just coats a spoon.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the ketchup and rum off the heat and let it stand 10 minutes before serving.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"The sugar browns on its own, before any meat is in the pan and with no protein there to slow it down, which is why the window between dark treacle and acrid is seconds wide. Watch it, do not time it, and have the goat within reach: the meat going in is what arrests the caramel. An older animal is worth having, since neck and shank stand up to two and a half hours and give the broth its weight. Lift the Scotch bonnet out whole before it splits, or the heat gets away and will not come back down.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Reducing a sauce","Sugar stages & caramel"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","fiery","sweet-edged","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, real fire that builds and stays, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Reducing a sauce","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=reducing%20sauce%20nappe%20consistency%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"},{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Goat%20Water%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Goat Water"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Goat Water","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Goat%20Water","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Scallion","Tomato","Chiles","Berries","Coconut","Thyme","Flour","Sugar","Clove","Allspice"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","reducing-a-sauce"]},{"slug":"goat-meat-pepper-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg goat on the bone, in 4 cm pieces, fat left on"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 uda pods (grains of Selim), toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 ehuru (calabash nutmeg) seeds, toasted and cracked"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g uziza seeds"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 Scotch bonnets, left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g ground crayfish"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 litres water"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g scent leaf (efirin) or basil, torn"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g utazi leaves, finely shredded"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the uda, ehuru and uziza in a dry pan until they smell of woodsmoke and camphor, then grind them to a coarse powder; bought ready ground, they taste of nothing.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the goat in a pot with the onion, ginger, garlic, salt and just enough water to cover, and simmer with the lid on for 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Add the ground spice, the crayfish, the Scotch bonnets and the rest of the water, and simmer uncovered for 1 hour more, until the meat gives easily at the bone and the broth smells of the toasted spice rather than raw goat.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Unlike the goat water, the bonnets stay in whole to the end here and the heat is meant to build; lift them out early only if you are cooking for someone who wants it mild.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take the pot off the heat, stir in the scent leaf and utazi and cover it for 3 minutes; the leaves should wilt, not cook.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":420,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The body of this broth is the goat's own bone and skin, which is why bony cuts beat a boneless leg and why the fat stays on the meat. Nothing else is doing that work, and the broth stays cloudy and brown to the end, which is right. The spices are the whole dish and they are volatile: uda and ehuru lose their camphor within weeks of grinding, so toast and grind in the same hour you cook. The leaves go in off the heat because scent leaf is a basil, and its aroma boils off in under a minute.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","nutty-toasted","fiery","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, toasted, nutty warmth throughout, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Goat%20Meat%20Pepper%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Goat Meat Pepper Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crawfish","Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Basil","Clove","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"mbuzi-wa-kupaka","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg goat leg or shoulder on the bone, in serving pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 red chillies"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper, ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml vegetable oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml thick coconut milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g tamarind pulp, soaked in 80 ml hot water and strained"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 lime, juiced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the goat in salted water with half the garlic and ginger for about 1 hour 15 minutes, until a skewer meets no resistance, and keep back 200 ml of the liquor.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":4500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Blend the rest of the garlic and ginger with the chillies and onion, then fry the paste in the oil with the turmeric, cumin, coriander and pepper until it darkens and loses its raw smell.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour in the coconut milk, tamarind water and reserved liquor and reduce over low heat, stirring, until it holds a line drawn across the pan with a spoon; never let it boil hard.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour a third of the sauce into a jug for the table before any brush touches it, and keep the rest as the basting sauce. Grill the goat over charcoal or under a hot grill, brushing on thin coats from the basting bowl and turning between them, until the outside is lacquered dark red in patches.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Squeeze over the lime and serve with the sauce from the jug. Anything left in the basting bowl has been on a brush that touched part-cooked meat, so boil it for a full minute before it goes to the table, or throw it out.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kupaka means to smear, and it names the method: thin coats over dry heat, each one set before the next goes on, because a thick layer steams and slides off. Coconut milk splits if it boils, since heat drives its proteins and phospholipids off the fat droplets they were holding apart, so the reduction stays under a simmer and is stirred. The goat is simmered first because charcoal alone would char a working leg long before its tough tissue let go.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Frying the paste until the oil splits","Salted water & the float test","Straining & passing through a sieve","Keeping the cooking liquid","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","earthy","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mbuzi%20wa%20Kupaka%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mbuzi wa Kupaka"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Onion","Garlic","Ginger","Chiles","Lime","Coconut","Milk","Coconut milk","Tamarind","Cumin","Coriander seed","Turmeric","Clove"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"drob-de-miel","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g lamb pluck (liver, heart, lungs and kidneys), trimmed; where lung cannot be bought, make the weight up with more heart and liver, keeping liver under half the total or the loaf turns pasty"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 piece caul fat, about 200 g, soaked in cold water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g spring onions, finely sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g dill, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g flat-leaf parsley, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g lovage leaves, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 eggs, hard boiled and peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g stale white bread, soaked in 100 ml milk and squeezed out"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g black pepper, ground"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the heart and lungs in salted water for 30 minutes, then drain and cool them; leave the liver and kidneys raw, since boiling sets them chalky.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Chop all the offal to the coarseness of gravel rather than a paste, and mix it with the spring onions softened in the butter, the herbs, bread, beaten egg, salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a 900 g (2 lb) loaf tin with the unfolded caul and stand it on a baking tray, since the caul drips as it renders; spread in half the mixture, lay the boiled eggs down the centre end to end, cover with the rest and fold the caul over the top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake at 180C (350F) for 50 minutes to 60 minutes, until the centre reads 71C (160F) and the caul has melted down to a thin brown skin.","durationSec":3000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool it in the tin for 1 hour to 1 hour 30 minutes, no longer, then refrigerate until cold right through and slice it straight from the fridge; a warm loaf falls apart on the knife and a dense offal loaf left to cool on the counter sits for hours at the temperature bacteria like best.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true}],"note":"Caul is the lace of fat around the stomach and it does two jobs in sequence: it holds the loaf together while the egg and muscle proteins set, then it renders away and bastes the crust on its way out. Unfold it cold and wet, because warm caul tears. Take the centre to 71C (160F), the rule for anything minced. Liver goes in raw: its proteins set low, and liver boiled first ends up grainy in the finished slice.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Drob%20de%20Miel%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Drob de Miel"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Romanian%20%26%20Bulgarian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Romanian & Bulgarian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Eggs","Liver & Offal","Onion","Scallion","Parsley","Dill","Butter","Milk","Bread"],"judgedBy":["salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"merguez","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g lamb shoulder, well chilled, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g lamb tail or kidney fat, well chilled, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"18 g fine sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g harissa"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g sweet paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g hot paprika"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g ground coriander"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground fennel"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground caraway"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground cinnamon"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed to a paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml ice water"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 metres sheep casing, 22 mm, rinsed and soaked"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the diced meat and fat with the salt, garlic and all the ground spices, spread it on a tray and freeze it for 30 minutes, until the fat is hard and the meat crunches at the edges.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mince through a 4.5 mm plate into a cold bowl; if the fat smears instead of cutting cleanly, stop and re-chill everything, mincer parts included.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the ice water and harissa for about a minute, until the mix turns tacky and clings to an upturned hand.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stuff into the casing without packing it tight, twist into 15 cm links and hang them in the fridge overnight so the colour deepens and the casing dries. There is no cure in these, and the hang has already spent a day of their life: grill within two days of stuffing, or freeze the links once the hang is done.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over medium coals for 8 minutes to 10 minutes, turning often, until the centre reads 71C (160F); fierce heat splits the casing and the fat you worked to keep runs into the fire.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Everything turns on temperature. Lamb fat is firm while it is cold, but past about 12C (54F) it smears, and smeared fat coats the protein so the sausage cooks out dry and crumbly. Keep meat, fat, bowl and blade under 4C (39F) throughout. The ice water and the minute of beating do the opposite job: salt draws myosin out of the muscle, and that sticky protein binds fat and water. Merguez is North African at large; the cinnamon and the weight of harissa here are the Moroccan turn.","equipment":["Grill","Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","charred","aromatic","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, char used as punctuation, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Merguez%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Merguez"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Merguez","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Merguez","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Sausage","Liver & Offal","Garlic","Curry paste","Cumin","Coriander seed","Paprika","Cinnamon","Clove","Caraway"],"judgedBy":["grilling-over-live-coals","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"pavlova-roulade-with-passionfruit-curd","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"140 g egg white (about 4 large), at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"220 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g cornflour (2 tsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml white wine vinegar (1 tsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract (1 tsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g flaked almonds"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 whole egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g caster sugar, for the curd"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g passionfruit pulp (about 6 fruit)"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g unsalted butter, cold, in small cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml double cream, at 4C (39F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g icing sugar, for dusting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oven to 180C (350F) and line a 33 by 23 cm tin with baking paper. Whisk the whites to soft peaks, then add the 220 g caster sugar a tablespoon at a time until the meringue is glossy and a pinch rubbed between finger and thumb feels smooth rather than gritty.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold in the cornflour, vinegar and vanilla with a few strokes, spread level in the tin, scatter the almonds and bake 18 minutes until the top is pale gold and dry to a fingertip while the centre still gives under it. Cool in the tin.","durationSec":1080,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1080,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the yolks, the whole egg, 50 g sugar and passionfruit pulp in a bowl set over barely simmering water until the curd coats the back of a spoon at 80C (176F), then take it off the heat and beat in the cold butter a cube at a time until glossy. Chill.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Turn the cooled meringue out onto paper dusted with icing sugar and peel off the lining. Whip the cream, still at 4C (39F) from the fridge, to soft peaks that flop when the whisk lifts, spread it over the meringue and spoon the cold curd across it in stripes.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll from a short side using the paper to lift, letting the top crack as it turns, and chill seam down for 30 minutes before cutting with a serrated knife.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cornflour and vinegar fight the same enemy: egg protein bonding too tightly. Acid slows the cross linking, and starch soaks up free water and gets between the proteins, so the inside stays marshmallow instead of drying to sugar glass. A round pavlova cracks because its thick rigid shell contracts away from a humid centre as it cools. Baked thin, the same meringue holds enough water in its marshmallow interior to stay pliable when cold, so this one is filled first and rolled once.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","silky","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, leaving a faint perfume."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pavlova%20Roulade%20with%20Passionfruit%20Curd%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Pavlova Roulade with Passionfruit Curd"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Cream","Vinegar","Wine","Sugar","Vanilla","Nuts"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"golden-syrup-dumplings","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g baking powder (2 tsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g cold unsalted butter, in small dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg, cold, lightly beaten"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml cold whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g golden syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g unsalted butter, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lemon juice (1 tbsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml pouring cream, cold, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Rub the cold diced butter into the flour, baking powder and salt with your fingertips until it looks like coarse sand with a few flat flakes of butter still showing. Stop there.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cut in the egg and milk with a table knife to a soft, shaggy dough that just holds together, and do not knead it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bring the water, golden syrup, brown sugar, 30 g butter and lemon juice to a simmer in a wide pan, stirring until the sugar has gone and the syrup smells faintly of toffee.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Drop in 8 spoonfuls of dough with a thumb of space between them, cover, and hold at a bare simmer for 20 minutes without lifting the lid, until a skewer pushed into the largest comes out clean.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the dumplings into bowls, then boil the syrup hard for 8 minutes to 10 minutes, until it has reduced by about half and coats the back of a spoon, pour it back over and serve with the cold cream.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cold butter is the entire structure. Rubbed in while firm it stays in flakes, and those flakes turn to steam in the syrup and prop the crumb open. Warm butter smears across the flour instead, waterproofs it and gives you a rubber ball. Keep the pan at a bare simmer, because a rolling boil batters a soft, barely bound scone dough to rags long before the middle has set. The lid matters too, since the exposed tops cook in steam rather than liquid.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias","Rubbing fat into flour"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Golden%20Syrup%20Dumplings%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Golden Syrup Dumplings"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Butter","Milk","Cream","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["rubbing-fat-into-flour","knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias"]},{"slug":"louise-cake","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"125 g unsalted butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"75 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 egg yolks"},{"kind":"item","text":"175 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g baking powder (1 tsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g raspberry jam"},{"kind":"item","text":"70 g egg white (about 2), at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar, for the meringue"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g desiccated coconut"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oven to 180C (350F). Beat the butter and 75 g sugar until pale, beat in the yolks, then work in the flour and baking powder to a soft dough.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Press the dough into a lined 20 cm square tin in an even layer, right into the corners, and bake 15 minutes until set and just colouring at the edges.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true},{"text":"While the base is in the oven, whisk the whites and salt to soft peaks, then add the 100 g sugar a spoonful at a time until the meringue is thick and glossy and a pinch feels smooth, not gritty, between your fingers. Fold in the coconut and stand it ready.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lower the oven to 160C (325F). The moment the base comes out, spread the jam over it right to the edges, spoon the coconut meringue over the hot jam in one movement and rough the surface up with a fork.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 20 minutes to 25 minutes until the meringue is set, dry on top and the colour of a plain biscuit, then cool completely in the tin before cutting into squares.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":true}],"note":"Meringue weeps for two reasons: sugar that never dissolved, which draws water back out as syrup, and cold wet filling underneath, which condenses against the raw foam. So the sugar goes in slowly until a pinch feels smooth, and the jam goes on while the base is still hot so the meringue's underside sets on contact. The coconut is not only flavour: it is dry, and it takes up the free water that would otherwise bead out and make the slice slippery.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Louise%20Cake%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Louise Cake"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Berries","Coconut","Butter","Flour","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"poe","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg very ripe bananas, peeled (about 8)"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g tapioca starch or arrowroot"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, seeds scraped"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coconut oil, for the dish"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 banana leaves, to line the dish"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml coconut cream, chilled"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g caster sugar, for the cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oven to 180C (350F). Mash the bananas with a fork to a coarse puree, leaving some lumps, then beat in the tapioca starch, 80 g sugar, vanilla seeds and salt until no dry pockets remain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold the banana leaves over a flame until they turn glossy and pliable, grease a 20 cm dish with the coconut oil, line it with the leaves and pour the mixture in about 3 cm deep.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Bake 45 minutes to 50 minutes, until the top is set and matt, the edges pull away from the leaf and a skewer comes out carrying a thick translucent smear rather than raw white paste.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":3000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cool to warm or to room temperature so the starch gel can firm; it will not cut while hot.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whisk the chilled coconut cream with the 12 g sugar until it loosens into a pouring sauce, cut the po'e into squares and spoon the cream over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Root starches such as tapioca and arrowroot carry less amylose than cereal starches, and their granules swell far more once they pass about 65C (149F), so they set clear and elastic where cornflour sets cloudy and short. That is why po'e is glossy and pulls in threads when you spoon it. Almost all the firming happens as it cools, not in the oven, so hot po'e looks like soup and a cook who bakes on until it seems sliceable will pull out rubber.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Mashing & puréeing","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma."},"pairingId":25,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Po'e%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Po'e"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Banana","Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","the-wobble-and-the-skewer-testing-a-bake"]},{"slug":"faausi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg taro corm, peeled and cut in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml coconut cream, for the taro"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml coconut cream, for the caramel"}],"steps":[{"text":"Peel the taro wearing gloves, since the raw sap irritates skin, and boil it in well salted water for 25 minutes to 30 minutes until a knife meets no resistance at the centre of the largest piece. Drain it, pour the cooking water away and let the steam blow off.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pound or coarsely grate the taro while it is still hot, working in the 200 ml coconut cream and the salt until you have a stiff, faintly stringy mass, then roll it up in baking paper into two 6 cm logs and chill for 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the sugar with the water in a heavy pan over a medium heat without stirring, swirling the pan only, until it is the colour of dark honey and smells of toast, about 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":720,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pull the pan off the heat and pour in the 250 ml coconut cream at arm's length: it will boil up hard and the caramel will seize. Return it to a low heat and stir until the lumps dissolve back into a smooth sauce.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Slice both logs 2 cm thick, lay the rounds in a warm dish and pour the hot coconut caramel over them.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Taro's starch granules are unusually small, which is why it pounds to something silky and slightly stringy rather than fluffy like potato. Work it hot: as it cools the starch retrogrades and the mass turns crumbly. The raw corm carries needle crystals of calcium oxalate alongside an irritant enzyme; heat destroys the enzyme and the cooking water carries off the soluble oxalate, so boil it right through and pour that water away. Seizing when the cream lands is normal, and low heat melts it back.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Sugar stages & caramel","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a sweet edge rounding the corners."},"pairingId":30,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Sugar stages & caramel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=sugar%20stages%20caramel%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Fa'ausi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Fa'ausi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["sugar-stages-and-caramel","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"riskrem","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"150 g short grain pudding rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"900 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 vanilla pod, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml double cream, at 4C (39F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g icing sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g raspberries or cloudberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lemon juice (1 tbsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g cornflour (1 tsp), slaked in 15 ml cold water"}],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the rice and water to a boil, cover, and cook 10 minutes until the water is absorbed and the grains have begun to swell. Add the milk, salt and vanilla pod and cook uncovered at the barest simmer for about 40 minutes, stirring often, until it is thick and the grains have burst.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":600,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift out the vanilla pod, spread the porridge in a shallow tray, stir it once or twice so no skin forms, and chill it to 4C (39F), which takes about 2 hours. It must be properly cold before the cream goes anywhere near it.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Simmer the berries with the caster sugar and lemon juice for 4 minutes, stir in the slaked cornflour and boil 30 seconds until the sauce turns glossy, then cool it.","durationSec":240,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":240,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the cream, straight from the fridge at 4C (39F), with the icing sugar only to soft peaks that flop over when the whisk is lifted, and no further.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold a third of the cream into the cold rice to loosen it, fold in the rest in two more additions keeping the mixture light, and serve cold with the red sauce spooned over.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cream whips because solid fat crystals inside each globule puncture their neighbours as they collide, and the half melted fat links up into a mesh that traps air. At 4C (39F) enough of the butterfat is crystalline to build that mesh. At 10C (50F) it is not: the globules slide past each other, you get slack foam, and pushing harder takes it straight to grain and buttermilk. The rice must be cold for the same reason: warm porridge melts the crystals on contact and the cream collapses.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","bright","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Riskrem%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Riskrem"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Berries","Milk","Cream","Rice","Sugar","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil"]},{"slug":"vinarterta","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g pitted prunes"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar, for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ground cardamom (1 tsp), for the filling"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml lemon juice (1 tbsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g unsalted butter, softened"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g caster sugar, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 egg"},{"kind":"item","text":"45 ml whole milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml almond extract (1 tsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g ground cardamom (2 tsp), freshly ground, for the dough"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g baking powder (1 tsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the prunes in the water for 20 minutes until collapsing, then blend with the 100 g sugar, 1 tsp cardamom and lemon juice to a paste thick enough to hold a clean line when a spoon is drawn through it. If it is loose, return it to the pan and cook it down over a low heat, stirring, until the spoon leaves a clean line. Cool completely.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cream the butter and 200 g sugar, beat in the egg, milk, almond extract and 2 tsp cardamom, then work in the flour, baking powder and salt to a stiff dough. Divide into 6, wrap and chill 1 hour.","durationSec":3600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":3600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Roll each piece on baking paper to a 20 cm round about 4 mm thick, trimming against an upturned plate, and bake at 180C (350F) for 9 minutes to 11 minutes until the edges are just tan and the middle is dry. Cool the rounds flat.","durationSec":540,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":660,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stack the layers with about 110 g of prune paste spread thinly and right to the edge of each, pressing gently so nothing bulges out, and leave the top layer bare.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Wrap the whole torte tightly in baking paper and then foil and leave it somewhere cool for at least 3 days before slicing it thinly with a serrated knife.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"The layers leave the oven hard as shortbread, and that is correct: this cake is made in the wrapping, not the baking. Water migrates out of the wet prune paste into the dry biscuit until both settle at the same humidity, which takes about three days and turns the stack tender all the way through. Wrap it airtight or the water leaves the cake instead of moving around inside it. Cook the filling until a spoon leaves a clean line, because a loose paste floods the layers to mush.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Creaming butter & sugar"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","rich","bright","nutty-toasted"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, richness that coats and comforts, closing toasted and warm."},"pairingId":11,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=V%C3%ADnarterta%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vínarterta"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Norwegian%20%26%20Icelandic%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Norwegian & Icelandic kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Lemon","Dried fruit","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Nuts","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["creaming-butter-and-sugar"]},{"slug":"vispipuuro","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"400 g lingonberries or cranberries"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 litre water"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g fine semolina"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml cold whole milk, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the berries in the water for 10 minutes until they have all burst, then strain through a sieve, pressing hard on the solids, and return the juice to the pan. Discard the skins.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rain the semolina into the simmering juice in a thin stream while whisking, add the sugar and salt, and cook at a bare simmer for 10 minutes, stirring, until the porridge holds the track of the whisk.","durationSec":600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour it into the cold bowl of a mixer, stand the bowl in a sink of iced water and stir now and then for about 20 minutes, until it is below 20C (68F). A fridge does the same job in about an hour. Whipping it warm does not work.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip on high for 6 minutes to 8 minutes: it will roughly double, turn from wine dark to pale pink and begin to hold soft peaks.","durationSec":360,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Spoon into bowls and pour the cold milk around it rather than over it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"No egg, no cream, no gelatine: the foam here is held up by cold starch alone. Semolina swells and leaks amylose, which sets to a weak elastic gel as it cools, and whipping tears that gel into bubble walls stiff enough not to drain. Warm, the same gel is fluid and the air simply walks out, which is why it must go below 20C (68F) first. Berry acid slowly cuts starch chains, so cook it briefly and whip it long, not the other way round.","equipment":["Fine sieve"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Straining & passing through a sieve"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts."},"pairingId":12,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Vispipuuro%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Vispipuuro"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Finnish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Finnish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[{"slug":"stand-mixer-and-attachments","term":"Stand Mixer & Attachments"}],"pantryItems":["Dried fruit","Milk","Semolina","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","straining-and-passing-through-a-sieve"]},{"slug":"bondepige-med-slor","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g cooking apples, peeled, cored and sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g caster sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip lemon zest"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract (1 tsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g stale dense rye bread, in coarse crumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g unsalted butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g dark brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml double cream, at 4C (39F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g dark chocolate, 70 percent, grated"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the apples with the caster sugar, water and lemon zest, covered, for about 15 minutes until they collapse, lift out the strip of zest, beat them to a coarse sauce, stir in the vanilla and cool completely.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":1140,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Melt the butter in a wide frying pan, add the rye crumbs and brown sugar and fry over a medium heat, stirring constantly, for 8 minutes to 10 minutes until they smell of toast and toffee.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":840,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Tip the crumbs onto a cold tray and spread them out to cool. They crisp on the tray, not in the pan, and warm crumbs will steam in the glass.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the cream, still at 4C (39F) from the fridge, to soft peaks that just hold their shape.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Layer the crumbs, cold apple and cream in glasses, finish with cream and the grated chocolate, and serve within 20 minutes.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":1440,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"The rye is doing structural work. Sugar melts around each crumb in the pan and sets to a brittle shell as it cools, and that shell is the only thing standing between the crumb and the apple. Rye is heavy in pentosans, gums that pull water fast, so a glass built an hour ahead is a bowl of wet bread. Everything goes in cold, apple and cream both, because steam softens the crumb from the inside just as quickly as juice does from the outside.","equipment":["Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Steaming: basket, leaf and lid"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","earthy","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, an earthy, grounding base, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Bondepige%20med%20Sl%C3%B8r%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Bondepige med Slør"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Danish%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Danish kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lemon","Apple","Butter","Cream","Bread","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["whipping-a-meringue","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"ptichye-moloko","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"100 g unsalted butter, softened, for the sponge"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g caster sugar, for the sponge"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 eggs"},{"kind":"item","text":"140 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g agar agar powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"140 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"460 g caster sugar, for the syrup"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g egg white (about 2), at room temperature"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 g citric acid, dissolved in 5 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g unsalted butter, softened to 20C (68F)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g sweetened condensed milk"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 ml vanilla extract (1 tsp)"},{"kind":"item","text":"100 g dark chocolate, 60 percent"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g unsalted butter, for the glaze"}],"steps":[{"text":"Beat the 100 g butter with the 100 g sugar, beat in the eggs one at a time and fold in the flour, then spread the batter into two 20 cm circles marked on lined trays and bake at 200C (400F) for 8 minutes to 10 minutes until springy. Cool and trim them to sit inside a 20 cm ring at least 8 cm deep, lined with a collar of baking paper or acetate.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat the 200 g butter with the condensed milk and vanilla until pale and fluffy and leave it at room temperature; it must not be fridge cold when the meringue arrives.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soak the agar in the 140 ml water for 30 minutes, bring it to a boil and boil 1 minute, then add the 460 g sugar and cook to 110C (230F), when the syrup falls from a spoon in a thin thread.","durationSec":1800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1860,"estimated":true},{"text":"Whip the whites with the citric acid to firm peaks, pour the hot syrup down the inside of the bowl in a thin steady stream, and keep whipping until the meringue is thick, glossy and down to about 55C (131F). Beat in the butter cream in three additions, working fast.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour half the mixture over the first sponge in the lined ring, lay the second sponge on, pour the rest, chill 3 hours until firm, then lift off the ring and collar and coat with the chocolate and 50 g butter melted together.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true}],"note":"Agar is not gelatine. It only hydrates above about 90C (194F), which is why the syrup is boiled, and it sets at about 40C (104F), which is why the meringue must still be hotter than that when the butter goes in: cooler and it sets in threads and the cream turns lumpy. The payoff is that agar does not melt again until roughly 85C (185F), so this holds a clean cut edge in a warm room. Acid weakens the gel, so the citric acid goes into the whites, never the syrup.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["Whipping a meringue","Folding: keeping the air in","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","floral","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, a floral thread lifting the aroma, on a slow, honest burn."},"pairingId":5,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Whipping a meringue","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20whip%20meringue%20stiff%20peaks%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ptichye%20Moloko%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Ptichye Moloko"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Russian%20%26%20Ukrainian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Russian & Ukrainian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Eggs","Butter","Milk","Flour","Sugar","Chocolate/Cocoa","Vanilla"],"judgedBy":["folding-keeping-the-air-in","whipping-a-meringue"]},{"slug":"nkwobi","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg cow foot, sawn into 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 150 g, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 stock cube, crumbled"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g food-grade edible potash (akanwu or kaun), stirred into 60 ml warm water and left to settle"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml red palm oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ground dried crayfish"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g ground dried chilli"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 g ehu seed (calabash nutmeg), toasted and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g utazi leaves, shredded as fine as tea"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 small onion, 80 g, in rings, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Simmer the cow foot with the halved onion, stock cube and 5 g of the salt in water to cover until a skewer slides through the skin with no resistance and the liquor is sticky on the lips, about 2.5 hours. Lift the meat out and keep 100 ml of the liquor.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the settled potash water carefully off its grit into a jug and throw the sediment away.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the palm oil in a wide bowl and beat the potash water into it a spoonful at a time: it turns from loose red to opaque yellow and thickens enough to hold the line a spoon draws through it. Stop at the turn, whether or not all the potash water has gone in.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the crayfish, chilli, ehu and remaining salt, then slacken with the reserved liquor until the paste drops heavily from the spoon.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the warm cow foot through until every piece is coated, and pile the utazi and onion rings on top.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Palm oil is thin and red; the yellow paste is an emulsion. Potash is alkaline, and it turns a small share of the oil's free fatty acids into soaps, and those soaps are the emulsifiers that let the oil take up water. Add the potash water in stages and stop at the colour change: past it the paste tastes metallic and slackens again. The gelatine cooked out of the cow foot is what gives the body, so the long simmer is not optional.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Keeping the cooking liquid","The wobble & the skewer: testing a bake"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","warm-spiced","nutty-toasted","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, warm spice humming through the middle, melting away silken."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nkwobi%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Nkwobi"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Nigerian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Nigerian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Crawfish","Onion","Chiles","Stock/Broth","Nutmeg"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts"]},{"slug":"skilpadjies","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"500 g lamb liver, in 3 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g caul fat (netvet), soaked in cool salted water for 10 minutes to 15 minutes, in the fridge if the kitchen is warm, then drained"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g lamb fat or butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g coriander seed, toasted and ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml white wine vinegar"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cook the onion and garlic in the fat over low heat until translucent and sweet, then cool them completely: warm onion starts cooking the liver before it reaches the fire.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Toss the liver with the cooled onion, coriander, salt, pepper and vinegar, and leave it 20 minutes in the fridge.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Spread the caul into a sheet, cut it into 12 cm squares, put a spoonful of the mixture on each and roll them into tight walnut-sized parcels, seam underneath.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill seam down over medium coals, turning once, until the caul has melted away into a brown lace and the centre of a parcel reads 71C (160F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Take them straight off at that reading and rest them 3 minutes, because they keep cooking.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":false}],"note":"Caul is the lace of fat from around the stomach, and it does the work here: it melts at grill heat and bastes the liver, which carries almost no fat of its own and wrings out water fast. Liver is offal, so the centre has to reach 71C (160F), and the window there is narrow: hit it and come straight off the fire, because a minute past it is chalk. Keep the coals medium, or the melting caul flares and the outside blackens long before the middle has set.","equipment":["Grill"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Dry-pan toasting: spices, seeds and nuts","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","bright","nutty-toasted","aromatic"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, acid holding everything up to the light, opening aromatic and staying there."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Skilpadjies%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Skilpadjies"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=South%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the South African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Butter","Vinegar","Wine","Coriander seed"],"judgedBy":["dry-pan-toasting-spices-seeds-and-nuts","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"tangia-marrakchia","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg beef shin on the bone, in 4 pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g smen, or 40 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 garlic cloves, peeled and left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 preserved lemon, quartered and pipped"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cumin, plus 3 g to finish"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 g saffron threads, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground ginger"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g flat parsley, chopped"}],"steps":[{"text":"Put everything except the finishing cumin and the parsley into a tall narrow pot or a clay tangia, and brown nothing first.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Seal it: a round of baking paper pressed to the rim, foil crimped tight over that, then the lid.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook at 120C (250F) for 6 hours without opening it. It is ready when a spoon pushed against the meat parts the fibres with no force.","durationSec":21600,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":21600,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the meat out, and if the juices are thin boil them hard for 5 minutes before pouring them back over.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Scatter the finishing cumin and the parsley at the table.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Only 150 ml of water goes in; the rest of the liquid is the shin's own. Sealed at 120C (250F) the pot holds the meat in its own saturated steam a little under boiling, and collagen turns to gelatine steadily from about 70C (158F) upward. Because nothing agitates it, the fibres stay whole instead of shredding into strings. A leaking seal is the one failure: the pot dries and the base scorches, so check the foil before it goes in.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Pie crust & blind baking"],"flavor":{"tags":["herb-fresh","earthy","aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with fresh herbs arguing happily on top, an earthy, grounding base, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Pie crust & blind baking","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=pie%20crust%20blind%20baking%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Tangia%20Marrakchia%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Tangia Marrakchia"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Cooking with Alia on Tangia Marrakchia","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cooking%20with%20Alia%20Tangia%20Marrakchia","sub":"A YouTube search for Cooking with Alia and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Lemon","Parsley","Butter","Pickles","Cumin","Clove","Saffron"],"judgedBy":["pie-crust-and-blind-baking"]},{"slug":"dulet","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"250 g beef honeycomb tripe"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g lamb liver"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g lean beef, top round"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g niter kibbeh"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g red onion, chopped very fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g mitmita"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g korerima (Ethiopian cardamom), ground"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 green chilli, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 sheets injera, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scrub the tripe with salt and lemon, rinse it, then simmer it in fresh water until a knife enters without that faint squeak, about 90 minutes, and cool it before you cut it.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":5400,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hand-chop the tripe, liver and beef separately to an even 5 mm dice; a machine smears the liver and the dish loses its grain.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Sweat the onion in the niter kibbeh over low heat until it collapses and the butter runs clear at the edge of the pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Raise the heat, add the beef and tripe and stir until the beef loses its red, then the liver for one final minute, taking everything to 71C (160F).","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Off the heat, fold in the mitmita, korerima, salt, lemon and chilli, because those spices scorch and turn bitter in a hot dry pan.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tripe and liver run on opposite clocks. Tripe is smooth muscle sheathed in collagen and wants 90 minutes of wet heat; liver sets in under a minute and then turns to sand. Cooking the tripe ahead and cutting everything to the same 5 mm dice is what lets three minutes in the pan finish the dish. Niter kibbeh is clarified, so it takes that heat without milk solids burning. Hand-chopped meat is cut surfaces throughout, so it goes to 71C (160F), not the traditional barely warm.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Injera","Sweating aromatics: soft, never browned","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["fiery","rich","sweet-edged","warm-spiced"],"sentence":"Leads with real fire that builds and stays, richness that coats and comforts, fading in warm spice."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Injera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20make%20injera%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Dulet%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Dulet"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Ethiopian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Ethiopian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Lamb","Liver & Offal","Onion","Chiles","Lemon","Honey","Cardamom"],"judgedBy":["knife-cuts-dice-julienne-bias","sweating-aromatics-soft-never-browned"]},{"slug":"mishkaki","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g beef sirloin, in 3 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g green papaya, grated fine with its skin"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ginger, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 ml lemon juice"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml vegetable oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g curry powder"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g ground black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml beef stock, for basting"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the papaya, garlic, ginger, lemon, oil, curry powder, salt and pepper, and turn the beef through it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Marinate it 2 hours in the fridge and no longer. If you want the warmth that speeds the enzyme, bring it out for the last 20 minutes only.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":8400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Thread the cubes onto skewers touching each other rather than spaced, so they shield one another from the fire.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot coals, turning every 45 seconds and brushing with the stock, until the outside is crusted and the centre reads 63C (145F), about 8 minutes.","durationSec":480,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest 3 minutes on a warm plate so the juices settle back through the cubes.","durationSec":180,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":180,"estimated":true}],"note":"Green papaya carries papain, an enzyme that cuts both collagen and muscle protein. It travels only a few millimetres in and it does not switch itself off: two hours cold gives tender, a whole day gives a grey mushy surface over raw-tasting middles. Cold slows it and heat speeds it up before finally killing it, and it is at its fiercest around 60C to 70C (140F to 158F), so the tenderising carries on over the fire. Short marinade, hot coals, fast turns.","equipment":["Grill","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["aromatic","bright","charred"],"sentence":"Leads with aromatics blooming from the first bite, acid holding everything up to the light, with a bitter-edged char at the rim."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mishkaki%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Mishkaki"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=East%20African%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the East African kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Ginger","Lemon","Stock/Broth","Curry powder"],"judgedBy":["marinating-acid-salt-and-time","grilling-over-live-coals"]},{"slug":"colonial-goose","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1 leg of lamb, 2.2 kg, tunnel-boned with the cavity left whole"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml dry red wine"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml red wine vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g soft brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml vegetable oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g dried apricots, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g coarse fresh breadcrumbs"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, chopped fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"50 g butter"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g thyme leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"zest of 1 lemon"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml lamb or chicken stock"}],"steps":[{"text":"Mix the wine, vinegar, sugar and oil, and marinate the boned leg in it for 12 hours in the fridge, turning it twice.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cook the onion in the butter until soft, cool it, then work in the apricots, breadcrumbs, thyme, zest, salt and pepper.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack the stuffing into the cavity without forcing it, and tie the leg at 3 cm intervals into an even cylinder.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pour the marinade into a pan and boil it for a full minute before it goes anywhere near the roast, because it held raw lamb for 12 hours. Roast at 160C (325F), basting with the boiled marinade every 20 minutes, until the centre of the stuffing reads 74C (165F), 2 hours to 2.5 hours.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":480,"unattendedSec":45000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest it 20 minutes, then boil the roasting juices with the stock and whatever baste is left down to a light gravy.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":false}],"note":"A stuffed leg cannot be a pink roast. The stuffing sits at the slowest point in the joint and has to reach 74C (165F), so the lamb around it will be cooked through. That is the trade, and what keeps it juicy is the acid marinade, which shifts the meat's pH away from the point where its proteins hold least water, plus the butter and fruit inside. Boning does more than make room: it turns the leg into an even cylinder, so the ends are not dry by the time the middle is safe.","equipment":["Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Roasting in a hot oven","Marinating: acid, salt and time"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","bright","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, acid holding everything up to the light, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":29,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colonial%20Goose%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Colonial Goose"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Aotearoa%20%26%20Maori%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Aotearoa & Maori kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Lamb","Onion","Lemon","Dried fruit","Stone fruit","Thyme","Butter","Breadcrumbs/Panko","Vinegar","Wine","Stock/Broth","Sugar"],"judgedBy":["roasting-in-a-hot-oven","marinating-acid-salt-and-time"]},{"slug":"kangaroo-tail-soup","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg kangaroo tail, cut through the joints into 5 cm lengths"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml vegetable oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g onion, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g carrot, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g celery, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g plain flour"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L beef stock"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g tinned tomatoes, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml dry sherry"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 ml Worcestershire sauce"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dry the tail pieces and brown them hard in the oil in batches until the cut faces are deep brown, because a crowded pan steams them and there is no fond.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Soften the vegetables in the same pot, stir in the flour and cook it until it smells of biscuit, then pour in the stock and tomatoes and add the bay.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Return the tail, bring it to a bare shiver and hold it there for 3 hours; at a rolling boil the fat shears into the broth and it turns cloudy and greasy.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lift the tail out, strip the meat from the bones and return it, then spread the soup into shallow trays so it cools quickly, refrigerate it and lift off the set fat. Never put the whole deep pot in the fridge: gelatine slows convection and the middle sits warm for hours.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Reheat with the sherry and Worcestershire and taste again for salt, because gelatine dulls seasoning.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Kangaroo is the leanest red meat in common use, which is why the loin has to be served rare and the tail has to be simmered. The tail is a rope of small joints sheathed in collagen and worked with every hop. Held at a shiver, around 85C (185F), that collagen becomes gelatine and gives the broth a body that flour cannot imitate. Boil it and the agitation shears the fat into droplets the gelatine then holds suspended, which is what clouds it. Oxtail behaves the same way and takes the same time.","equipment":["Sheet pan"],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["umami-deep"],"sentence":"Leads with savory depth stacked from the pantry’s bass notes."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Kangaroo%20Tail%20Soup%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Kangaroo Tail Soup"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Australian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Australian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Tomato","Carrot","Celery","Bay leaf","Flour","Wine","Stock/Broth"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"povi-masima","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 kg beef brisket, in one piece"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g coarse sea salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g soft brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 garlic cloves, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 kg taro or green banana, peeled in gloves (raw taro sap carries the same needle crystals as the leaf and will burn bare skin)"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 g young taro leaves, or 300 g spinach"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 ml coconut cream"}],"steps":[{"text":"Boil the water with the salt, sugar, peppercorns and garlic until it runs clear, then chill it to fridge temperature; warm brine on raw meat is the one way this goes wrong.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Submerge the brisket, weight it under a plate and hold it at 4C (39F) for 5 days, turning it daily.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rinse the meat, cover it with fresh cold water, bring it to the boil and tip that water away, then do it a second time before simmering in fresh water for 2.5 hours until a skewer slides out clean.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":9000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Drop the taro into the pot for the last 40 minutes.","durationSec":2400,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":2400,"estimated":false},{"text":"Simmer the leaves and onion in the coconut cream with the lid on and the heat bare, adding a splash of water if it tightens, for the full 45 minutes it takes the needle crystals in taro leaf to break down. Do not cut that time short. Serve the sliced beef over them.","durationSec":2700,"handsOnSec":2940,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Salt travels into meat down a concentration gradient, and after 5 days in a brine this strong the brisket is far saltier than anyone would eat. At 250 g in 2 L this brine is much too strong to help the meat hold water; that strong, it pulls water out. The two changes of boiling water run the cure backwards and bring the meat down to the salt level where water-holding does improve. What keeps it juicy after 2.5 hours in the pot is gelatine from the wet heat. Cured without nitrite it cooks grey.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Brining & curing"],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","aromatic","fiery","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, aromatics blooming from the first bite, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Brining & curing","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=brining%20curing%20meat%20technique%20explained","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Povi%20Masima%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Povi Masima"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Onion","Garlic","Spinach","Banana","Coconut","Coconut milk","Sugar","Clove"],"judgedBy":["brining-and-curing"]},{"slug":"laplap-with-chicken","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg taro or yam, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g green banana, peeled"},{"kind":"item","text":"800 g chicken thighs, bone in and skin on"},{"kind":"item","text":"500 ml coconut cream"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g fresh chilli, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 large banana leaves, passed over a flame until they turn glossy and pliable"}],"steps":[{"text":"Wearing gloves, because raw taro sap drives calcium oxalate needles into the skin and this is a lot of grating, grate the taro and green banana on the fine side of the grater into a wet paste that mounds on the spoon rather than running off it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Beat in the coconut cream, water and salt until it is the thickness of a heavy batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line a deep tray with overlapping banana leaves and build it no more than 5 cm deep: half the batter, then the chicken, onion and chilli, then the rest of the batter.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fold the leaves into a sealed parcel and bake at 180C (350F) until the chicken reads 74C (165F) at the bone and the batter is set enough to cut, 90 minutes to 2 hours 30 minutes depending on how deep the tray is. The thermometer decides this, not the clock.","durationSec":5400,"handsOnSec":2040,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rest the parcel 15 minutes before opening it, because the starch firms as it cools and it will then slice instead of slumping.","durationSec":900,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":900,"estimated":true}],"note":"Grated taro is starch granules suspended in their own water. Held above about 70C (158F) they swell and lock into a cake that sets around the chicken and catches every drop of juice and fat it gives up. The banana leaf is the lid: it keeps the parcel saturated so the batter steams and never skins over, which is exactly what the earth oven does. Coconut fat coats the granules and keeps the crumb short. Leave it out and the cake turns to glue.","equipment":["Thermometer","Sheet pan"],"techniques":[],"flavor":{"tags":["rich","fiery"],"sentence":"Leads with richness that coats and comforts, real fire that builds and stays."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Laplap%20with%20Chicken%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Laplap with Chicken"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Pacific%20Islands%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Pacific Islands kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Chicken","Onion","Sweet potato","Chiles","Banana","Coconut","Cream","Coconut milk"]},{"slug":"barreado","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2 kg beef chuck, in 4 cm cubes"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g smoked bacon, diced"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g onion, sliced"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g garlic, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"12 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 bay leaves"},{"kind":"item","text":"18 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"300 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g cassava flour, for the seal"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g cassava flour, toasted, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 ripe bananas, to serve"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 oranges, in segments, to serve"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toss the beef with the bacon, onion, garlic, cumin, bay, salt and pepper and leave it 2 hours in the fridge.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Pack it into a heavy pot, pour in the water and put the lid on.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mix the 250 g of cassava flour with enough water to make a stiff putty and press it right around the join of lid and pot so no steam can leave.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cook at 130C (265F) for 10 hours to 12 hours without opening it; the meat is done when it collapses into threads under the back of a spoon.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Mash the meat into its own fat and juices to a rough purée and serve it with the toasted flour, the banana and the orange.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Nothing in the pot thickens anything, and there is no sauce until you make one. The long cook turns the chuck's collagen to gelatine and renders the bacon fat; mashing the meat while it is hot drives gelatine, fat and juices together, and the gelatine is the emulsifier that keeps the fat in suspension instead of floating clear. Work it warm, because gelatine stiffens as it cools. The toasted farinha at the table is the second thickener, drinking up what the meat cannot hold.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Steaming: basket, leaf and lid","Mashing & puréeing"],"flavor":{"tags":["nutty-toasted","earthy","aromatic","silky"],"sentence":"Leads with toasted, nutty warmth throughout, an earthy, grounding base, melting away silken."},"pairingId":27,"films":{"techniques":[{"label":"Technique: Building an emulsion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=how%20to%20emulsify%20sauce%20technique","sub":"The skill inside this recipe"}],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Barreado%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Barreado"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Brazilian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Brazilian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Bacon","Onion","Garlic","Orange","Banana","Bay leaf","Flour","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["mashing-and-pureeing","steaming-basket-leaf-and-lid"]},{"slug":"cau-cau","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"800 g honeycomb tripe"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 limes, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g coarse salt, for scrubbing"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 ml vegetable oil"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 g red onion, diced fine"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, chopped"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g ají amarillo paste"},{"kind":"item","text":"4 g palillo or ground turmeric"},{"kind":"item","text":"700 g yellow potatoes, in 2 cm dice"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 ml of the tripe cooking liquid"},{"kind":"item","text":"120 g peas"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g hierbabuena (fresh spearmint), chopped, or 15 g huacatay leaves for the regional version"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 g fine salt"}],"steps":[{"text":"Scrub the tripe with the coarse salt and the cut limes, rinse it, then blanch it 5 minutes in boiling water and pour that water down the sink.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":300,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cook the tripe in fresh water as for Dulet, about 2 hours, then cut it into 2 cm squares and keep the liquid.","durationSec":7200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":7200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Fry the onion and garlic in the oil until they have lost all their water and start to catch, then stir in the ají amarillo and palillo and fry until the oil at the edge runs orange.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Add the tripe, potatoes and 600 ml of the reserved liquid and simmer uncovered until the potatoes are soft and their starch has thickened the sauce, about 25 minutes.","durationSec":1500,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":1500,"estimated":false},{"text":"Stir in the peas and the hierbabuena off the heat, because its oils cook away within a minute.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Tripe forgives a long cook far better than a short one. It is smooth muscle sheathed in collagen with no fibre bundles to wring dry, so two hours only takes it from rubber to silk, while twenty minutes short leaves it rubber. The blanching water goes down the sink because the barnyard smell sits in water-soluble compounds. Nothing else thickens this stew, so use a floury yellow potato and cook it in the sauce rather than beside it, where its starch can leach out and body the liquid.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Blanching & shocking","Keeping the cooking liquid","Knife cuts: dice, julienne, bias"],"flavor":{"tags":["bright","aromatic","sweet-edged","herb-fresh"],"sentence":"Leads with acid holding everything up to the light, aromatics blooming from the first bite, lifted green at the very end."},"pairingId":38,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Cau%20Cau%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Cau Cau"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Peruvian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Peruvian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Liver & Offal","Onion","Garlic","Potato","Peas","Lime","Honey","Turmeric"],"judgedBy":["keeping-the-cooking-liquid","blanching-and-shocking"]},{"slug":"barbacoa-de-borrego-en-penca-de-maguey","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"2.5 kg lamb shoulder and leg on the bone, in large pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g guajillo chillies, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g ancho chillies, stemmed and seeded"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 garlic cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g cumin seed, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g black peppercorns"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 ml cider vinegar"},{"kind":"item","text":"25 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 maguey leaves, spines trimmed and the leaves held over a flame until they soften and darken"},{"kind":"item","text":"8 avocado leaves, toasted"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"200 g chickpeas, soaked overnight"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 g carrot, in chunks"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 onion, 150 g, halved"},{"kind":"item","text":"80 g long grain rice"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g mint leaves"}],"steps":[{"text":"Toast the chillies on a dry pan until they smell of raisins and no longer, soak them 20 minutes in hot water, then blend with the garlic, cumin, peppercorns, cloves, vinegar and 15 g of the salt to a thick adobo.","durationSec":1200,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":1200,"estimated":true},{"text":"Rub the lamb all over with it and leave it overnight in the fridge.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":28800,"estimated":true},{"text":"Put the water, chickpeas, carrot, onion, mint and remaining salt in the base of a deep pot and set a rack above the liquid, high enough that the meat can never touch it. The rice waits until the end.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Line the rack with the softened maguey leaves, lay in the lamb and the avocado leaves, fold the leaves over, cover with foil and the lid and cook at 150C (300F) for 5 hours, until the meat leaves the bone with no resistance. At the four hour mark, open it just far enough to stir the rice into the broth below, then seal it again.","durationSec":18000,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":18000,"estimated":true},{"text":"Shred the lamb, skim the fat from the broth beneath and serve that broth alongside as the consomé.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true}],"note":"Barbacoa is steam, not roast. Sealed under leaves the chamber stays saturated, the surface never climbs past 100C (212F) and so never browns, and the collagen converts while the outside stays soft: that is why it shreds rather than slices. The rack is not optional. Meat sitting in the liquid boils, and the adobo washes off it into the pot. Everything that drips down is the consomé, which is why the chickpeas wait in it and the rice goes in only at the end, before it can turn to porridge.","equipment":[],"techniques":["Skimming: foam, fat and a clear broth","Soaking dried goods back to life","The overnight rest: letting a dish marry"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","bright","herb-fresh","earthy"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, acid holding everything up to the light, settling low and earthy."},"pairingId":26,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Barbacoa%20de%20Borrego%20en%20Penca%20de%20Maguey%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Barbacoa de Borrego en Penca de Maguey"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Rick Bayless on Barbacoa de Borrego en Penca de Maguey","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Rick%20Bayless%20Barbacoa%20de%20Borrego%20en%20Penca%20de%20Maguey","sub":"A YouTube search for Rick Bayless and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Lamb","Onion","Garlic","Carrot","Chiles","Avocado","Mint","Rice","Chickpeas","Vinegar","Beer/Cider","Cumin","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-overnight-rest-letting-a-dish-marry","soaking-dried-goods-back-to-life"]},{"slug":"guyanese-pepperpot","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg beef shin, in 5 cm pieces"},{"kind":"item","text":"600 g oxtail, cut through the joints"},{"kind":"item","text":"400 g pork trotter, split"},{"kind":"item","text":"1.5 L water"},{"kind":"item","text":"250 ml cassareep"},{"kind":"item","text":"60 g soft brown sugar"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 cinnamon stick"},{"kind":"item","text":"6 cloves"},{"kind":"item","text":"1 strip of orange peel"},{"kind":"item","text":"10 g thyme"},{"kind":"item","text":"15 g fine salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"2 wiri wiri peppers, or 1 scotch bonnet, left whole and unpierced"}],"steps":[{"text":"Cover the meats with the water, bring them to the boil and skim off the grey foam until the surface runs clear.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Stir in the cassareep, sugar, cinnamon, cloves, orange peel, thyme and salt, and lay the peppers on top unbroken.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Hold it at a bare simmer for 3 hours, until the trotter is sticky, the oxtail slides off its bone and the liquid has gone black and glossy.","durationSec":10800,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":10800,"estimated":false},{"text":"Lift the peppers out before they burst, then taste: cassareep should read bitter, sweet and smoky at once, and it takes more salt than you expect.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Cool it quickly and keep it in the fridge no longer than 3 days, bringing it back to a rolling boil every time you reheat it. It is better on the second day and better again on the third.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":true}],"note":"Cassareep is the juice of bitter cassava boiled down to a black syrup. The boiling drives off the cyanide that makes the raw juice poisonous and concentrates sugars and phenols that hold bacteria back, which is why a pepperpot traditionally lives on the stove for weeks and is brought back to the boil each morning. Buy it made rather than pressing your own, and at home use the fridge and that daily boil. The trotter and oxtail are in the pot for gelatine: it makes the sauce cling.","equipment":[],"techniques":["The bare simmer: holding liquid below the boil","Salted water & the float test"],"flavor":{"tags":["warm-spiced","fiery","sweet-edged"],"sentence":"Leads with warm spice humming through the middle, real fire that builds and stays, trailing gentle sweetness."},"pairingId":18,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Guyanese%20Pepperpot%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Guyanese Pepperpot"},"teacher":{"label":"Search YouTube: Chris De La Rosa on Guyanese Pepperpot","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Chris%20De%20La%20Rosa%20Guyanese%20Pepperpot","sub":"A YouTube search for Chris De La Rosa and this dish. No affiliation."}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Pork","Chiles","Orange","Thyme","Sugar","Cinnamon","Clove"],"judgedBy":["the-bare-simmer-holding-liquid-below-the-boil","salted-water-and-the-float-test"]},{"slug":"carne-oreada-santandereana","ingredients":[{"kind":"item","text":"1.2 kg beef flank, butterflied into a sheet about 1 cm thick"},{"kind":"item","text":"30 g coarse salt"},{"kind":"item","text":"40 g panela, grated"},{"kind":"item","text":"150 ml water"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 g garlic, crushed"},{"kind":"item","text":"5 g ground cumin"},{"kind":"item","text":"3 g coarse black pepper"},{"kind":"item","text":"20 ml vegetable oil"}],"steps":[{"text":"Dissolve the salt and panela in the water with the garlic, cumin and pepper, and turn the sheet of beef through it.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Lay the sheet flat on a rack over a tray and dry it uncovered in the fridge, and only in the fridge, for 24 hours to 36 hours, turning it once, until the surface is matte and tacky and the sheet has stiffened but still bends.","durationSec":36000,"handsOnSec":0,"unattendedSec":36000,"estimated":false},{"text":"Cut it into hand-sized pieces and rub them lightly with the oil.","durationSec":null,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":true},{"text":"Grill over hot coals, about 2 minutes a side, until the edges are dark and crisp and the middle reads 63C (145F).","durationSec":120,"handsOnSec":120,"unattendedSec":0,"estimated":false},{"text":"Rest it 5 minutes and cut across the grain.","durationSec":300,"handsOnSec":240,"unattendedSec":300,"estimated":true}],"note":"Oreada means aired, not dried. Losing 15 percent to 20 percent of its water concentrates the beef and gives the salt time to travel through and dissolve surface protein into the tacky film that browns in seconds over coals. Stop there: further and you have jerky, which no longer cooks like meat. The traditional rack is a Santander roof in the sun, but the fridge is not a stylistic substitution for it. At 2.5 percent salt and no nitrite, this cure is safe cold and nowhere else.","equipment":["Grill","Sheet pan","Grater / zester"],"techniques":["Grilling over live coals","Resting meat & slicing against the grain"],"flavor":{"tags":["charred","earthy","aromatic","rich"],"sentence":"Leads with char used as punctuation, an earthy, grounding base, on a rich, rounded finish."},"pairingId":16,"films":{"techniques":[],"search":{"label":"This dish, cooked on camera","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Carne%20Oreada%20Santandereana%20recipe","sub":"Search films of Carne Oreada Santandereana"},"teacher":{"label":"The cuisine, deeper","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Colombian%20cooking%20techniques","sub":"Technique films from the Colombian kitchen"}},"lexiconTerms":[],"pantryItems":["Beef","Garlic","Butter","Cumin"],"judgedBy":["resting-meat-and-slicing-against-the-grain","grilling-over-live-coals"]}]`);export{e as default};